window.QUESTION_BANK = {
  "diagnostic-1": [
  // ===================== COMMON (NET + MPhil core) — the gap zone =====================
  { id: "diag_co01", section: "common", topic: "Research Methods & Statistics",
    q: "A researcher rejects the null hypothesis when it is actually true. This is:",
    options: { a: "A Type I (alpha) error",
               b: "A Type II (beta) error",
               c: "Low statistical power",
               d: "A confounding error" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Type I = false positive: rejecting a true null. Type II = false negative: retaining a false null.",
    study: "Type I error (α): you reject the null when it is TRUE — a false positive. Type II error (β): you fail to reject the null when it is FALSE — a false negative. Power = 1 − β = the probability of correctly detecting a real effect. Memory hook: 'Type 1 = you saw something that wasn't there.' α is the significance level you set (usually .05); it IS the probability of a Type I error." },
  { id: "diag_co02", section: "common", topic: "Research Methods & Statistics",
    q: "Which test is appropriate for comparing the means of three or more independent groups?",
    options: { a: "Independent samples t-test",
               b: "One-way ANOVA",
               c: "Pearson correlation",
               d: "Chi-square test" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "ANOVA compares means across 3+ groups using the F-ratio; a t-test only handles two groups.",
    study: "One-way ANOVA compares means of 3+ groups via the F-ratio (between-group variance / within-group variance). A t-test is limited to TWO groups. Running multiple t-tests inflates Type I error, which is exactly why ANOVA exists. A significant ANOVA tells you the groups differ somewhere; post-hoc tests (Tukey HSD, Bonferroni, Scheffé) tell you WHICH pairs differ." },
  { id: "diag_co03", section: "common", topic: "Research Methods & Statistics",
    q: "The non-parametric equivalent of the independent-samples t-test is the:",
    options: { a: "Wilcoxon signed-rank test",
               b: "Kruskal-Wallis test",
               c: "Mann-Whitney U test",
               d: "Friedman test" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Mann-Whitney U = non-parametric, two independent groups. Wilcoxon signed-rank = paired/related.",
    study: "Non-parametric map: Mann-Whitney U ↔ independent t-test (2 independent groups); Wilcoxon signed-rank ↔ paired t-test (2 related groups); Kruskal-Wallis ↔ one-way ANOVA (3+ independent groups); Friedman ↔ repeated-measures ANOVA (3+ related groups). Non-parametric tests are used when data are ordinal or assumptions of normality are violated." },
  { id: "diag_co04", section: "common", topic: "Research Methods & Statistics",
    q: "Cronbach's alpha is a measure of:",
    options: { a: "Criterion validity",
               b: "Test-retest reliability",
               c: "Inter-rater reliability",
               d: "Internal consistency reliability" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Cronbach's alpha indexes internal consistency — how well items on a scale measure the same construct.",
    study: "Cronbach's α measures INTERNAL CONSISTENCY: do the items hang together / measure one underlying construct? Range 0–1; ≥.70 is generally acceptable, ≥.80 good. Don't confuse with: test-retest reliability (stability over time, same test twice), inter-rater reliability (agreement between scorers — Cohen's kappa), and split-half reliability (correlation between two halves, often Spearman-Brown corrected). Reliability ≠ validity." },
  { id: "diag_co05", section: "common", topic: "Research Methods & Statistics",
    q: "A correlation coefficient of −0.85 indicates:",
    options: { a: "A strong negative relationship",
               b: "A weak negative relationship",
               c: "No relationship",
               d: "A strong positive relationship" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Magnitude (closeness to 1) = strength; sign = direction. −0.85 is strong and negative.",
    study: "The SIGN of r gives direction (− = as one rises the other falls); the ABSOLUTE VALUE gives strength. So −0.85 and +0.85 are equally strong, just opposite directions. Rough bands: .10 small, .30 medium, .50 large (Cohen). r² (coefficient of determination) = proportion of variance shared: −0.85 → r² = 0.72, so ~72% of variance is shared. Correlation never proves causation." },
  { id: "diag_co06", section: "common", topic: "Research Methods & Statistics",
    q: "Which of the following is a PROBABILITY sampling method?",
    options: { a: "Quota sampling",
               b: "Stratified random sampling",
               c: "Purposive sampling",
               d: "Snowball sampling" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Stratified random is probability sampling (known, non-zero selection chance). Quota/snowball/purposive are non-probability.",
    study: "PROBABILITY (random) sampling — every unit has a known, non-zero chance: simple random, systematic, stratified (divide into strata, sample randomly within each), cluster. NON-PROBABILITY: convenience, quota, purposive/judgmental, snowball. Only probability sampling supports valid statistical inference to the population. Stratified reduces sampling error when strata differ on the variable of interest." },
  { id: "diag_co07", section: "common", topic: "Research Methods & Statistics",
    q: "For a one-sample t-test with a sample size of 20, the degrees of freedom are:",
    options: { a: "20",
               b: "18",
               c: "19",
               d: "21" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "df = n − 1 = 19 for a one-sample t-test.",
    study: "One-sample / paired t-test: df = n − 1. Independent t-test: df = n₁ + n₂ − 2. Chi-square goodness of fit: df = categories − 1; chi-square test of independence: df = (rows − 1)(columns − 1). Degrees of freedom = number of values free to vary; they determine which t/χ²/F distribution you compare your statistic against." },
  { id: "diag_co08", section: "common", topic: "Psychological Assessment",
    q: "Which of the following is a PROJECTIVE test of personality?",
    options: { a: "16PF",
               b: "MMPI-2",
               c: "NEO-PI-R",
               d: "Thematic Apperception Test (TAT)" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "TAT is projective (ambiguous cards → stories). 16PF, MMPI-2, NEO-PI-R are objective self-report inventories.",
    study: "PROJECTIVE tests present ambiguous stimuli and assume the person 'projects' inner needs/conflicts: Rorschach (10 inkblots), TAT (Murray & Morgan, 31 cards → tell a story, taps needs/press), Sentence Completion, Draw-A-Person, CAT (children). OBJECTIVE/structured self-reports with fixed scoring: MMPI-2 (566 items, 10 clinical scales), 16PF (Cattell), NEO-PI-R (Big Five), EPQ. Projectives = low structure, qualitative, weaker psychometrics; objectives = high structure, normed." },
  { id: "diag_co09", section: "common", topic: "Psychological Assessment",
    q: "A T-score has a mean and standard deviation of:",
    options: { a: "Mean 50, SD 10",
               b: "Mean 100, SD 15",
               c: "Mean 0, SD 1",
               d: "Mean 500, SD 100" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "T-scores: mean 50, SD 10. (z: mean 0, SD 1; deviation IQ: mean 100, SD 15.)",
    study: "Standard score conversions (memorize the table): z-score → mean 0, SD 1. T-score → mean 50, SD 10 (T = 50 + 10z). Deviation IQ / Wechsler → mean 100, SD 15. Stanine → mean 5, SD ~2 (1–9). Sten (16PF) → mean 5.5, SD 2 (1–10). On MMPI a T-score ≥ 65 is clinically significant. A T of 70 = z of +2 = ~98th percentile." },
  { id: "diag_co10", section: "common", topic: "Psychological Assessment",
    q: "The MMPI-2 contains how many clinical scales?",
    options: { a: "4",
               b: "10",
               c: "16",
               d: "13" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "MMPI-2 has 10 clinical scales plus validity scales (L, F, K, etc.).",
    study: "MMPI-2: 567 items, 10 CLINICAL scales (Hs, D, Hy, Pd, Mf, Pa, Pt, Sc, Ma, Si) numbered 1–0, plus VALIDITY scales — L (Lie), F (Infrequency), K (Defensiveness/Correction), plus VRIN, TRIN, F-back. Originally by Hathaway & McKinley (1943), empirically/criterion keyed. T ≥ 65 (MMPI-2) is the cut for clinical elevation. The validity scales detect faking-good, faking-bad, and random responding." },
  { id: "diag_co11", section: "common", topic: "Psychological Assessment",
    q: "Sten scores (range 1–10) are most associated with which test?",
    options: { a: "Wechsler scales",
               b: "Stanford-Binet",
               c: "Cattell's 16PF",
               d: "Rorschach" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Sten ('standard ten', 1–10, mean 5.5) is the scoring metric of Cattell's 16PF.",
    study: "STEN = Standard TEN: a 10-point scale (mean 5.5, SD 2) used by Cattell's 16PF. Don't confuse with STANINE = standard NINE (1–9, mean 5, SD ~2), used in educational/aptitude testing. Wechsler uses deviation IQ (mean 100, SD 15) and subtest scaled scores (mean 10, SD 3). Knowing which metric goes with which test is a classic NET item." },
  { id: "diag_co12", section: "common", topic: "Psychological Assessment",
    q: "Administering the same test to the same people on two occasions and correlating the scores assesses:",
    options: { a: "Internal consistency",
               b: "Construct validity",
               c: "Content validity",
               d: "Test-retest reliability" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Test-retest reliability = stability of scores over time (same test, two occasions).",
    study: "Test-retest reliability = administer twice, correlate → STABILITY over time (threatened by practice/carryover effects and the retest interval). Parallel-forms reliability = two equivalent versions, correlate. Internal consistency = Cronbach's α / split-half (one administration). Inter-rater = agreement between judges (kappa). Reliability is necessary but not sufficient for validity — a test can be consistently wrong." },
  { id: "diag_co13", section: "common", topic: "Psychopathology (DSM / ICD)",
    q: "Loosening of associations and 'knight's move' thinking are characteristic of:",
    options: { a: "Formal thought disorder in schizophrenia",
               b: "Major depressive disorder",
               c: "Obsessive-compulsive disorder",
               d: "Specific phobia" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Loosening of associations / knight's-move thinking = formal thought disorder, classically in schizophrenia.",
    study: "Formal thought disorder (a disturbance of the FORM/flow of thought, seen in schizophrenia & mania): loosening of associations / derailment (ideas slip off track), knight's-move thinking (illogical jumps), tangentiality (never returns to point), circumstantiality (eventually returns), flight of ideas (rapid, connected — mania), word salad, neologisms, clang associations, thought blocking. Contrast with disorders of thought CONTENT (delusions) and POSSESSION (thought insertion/withdrawal)." },
  { id: "diag_co14", section: "common", topic: "Psychopathology (DSM / ICD)",
    q: "Compared with DSM-IV-TR, the DSM-5 (2013):",
    options: { a: "Added a sixth axis",
               b: "Abolished the multiaxial (Axis I–V) system",
               c: "Retained the five-axis multiaxial system",
               d: "Replaced diagnoses with ICD codes only" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "DSM-5 abolished the multiaxial system; Axes I–III merged, Axis IV → psychosocial notes, Axis V (GAF) dropped.",
    study: "DSM-5 (2013, APA) ELIMINATED the multiaxial system. Old DSM-IV-TR axes: I clinical disorders, II personality disorders + intellectual disability, III general medical conditions, IV psychosocial/environmental stressors, V GAF (Global Assessment of Functioning, 0–100). DSM-5 combined Axes I–III into one list, notes stressors separately, and dropped the GAF (WHODAS suggested instead). It also moved to a non-Roman 'DSM-5' naming to allow incremental updates (5.1, etc.)." },
  { id: "diag_co15", section: "common", topic: "Psychopathology (DSM / ICD)",
    q: "Anhedonia refers to:",
    options: { a: "Reduced need for sleep",
               b: "Excessive fear of social situations",
               c: "Inability to experience pleasure",
               d: "Loss of contact with reality" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Anhedonia = markedly diminished interest or pleasure; a core (cardinal) symptom of major depression.",
    study: "Anhedonia = inability to feel pleasure in normally pleasurable activities — one of the two CARDINAL symptoms of a major depressive episode (the other is depressed mood); at least one must be present. Related depressive terms: avolition (lack of motivation), apathy, alogia (poverty of speech). Anhedonia also appears as a NEGATIVE symptom of schizophrenia. The reward/dopamine and the 'consummatory vs anticipatory pleasure' distinction often follow this in exams." },
  { id: "diag_co16", section: "common", topic: "Psychopathology (DSM / ICD)",
    q: "First-rank symptoms of schizophrenia were described by:",
    options: { a: "Emil Kraepelin",
               b: "Eugen Bleuler",
               c: "Karl Jaspers",
               d: "Kurt Schneider" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Kurt Schneider's First-Rank Symptoms (1959). Kraepelin coined 'dementia praecox'; Bleuler coined 'schizophrenia' + the 4 A's.",
    study: "Schizophrenia name trail: Kraepelin called it 'dementia praecox' (early, deteriorating course). Eugen BLEULER renamed it 'schizophrenia' (1908) and gave the fundamental 4 A's — Associations (loosening), Affect (blunted), Ambivalence, Autism. Kurt SCHNEIDER (1959) listed First-Rank Symptoms: audible thoughts, voices arguing/commenting, thought withdrawal/insertion/broadcasting, made feelings/impulses/acts, somatic passivity, delusional perception. ICD/DSM have since downgraded FRS as non-specific." },
  { id: "diag_co17", section: "common", topic: "Psychopathology (DSM / ICD)",
    q: "The minimum duration of symptoms required to diagnose schizophrenia under DSM-5 is:",
    options: { a: "6 months",
               b: "1 month",
               c: "1 week",
               d: "2 years" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "DSM-5 requires ≥6 months total (incl. prodrome/residual), with ≥1 month of active-phase symptoms. ICD = 1 month.",
    study: "DSM-5 schizophrenia: continuous signs ≥ 6 MONTHS, including ≥1 month of active-phase (Criterion A) symptoms. Key contrast points: ICD-10/11 require only ~1 MONTH. Schizophreniform disorder = 1–6 months (DSM). Brief psychotic disorder = 1 day to <1 month. Delusional disorder = ≥1 month of delusions without other psychotic features. Duration cut-offs are heavily tested — keep DSM (6 mo) vs ICD (1 mo) straight." },
  { id: "diag_co18", section: "common", topic: "Psychopathology (DSM / ICD)",
    q: "Which cluster of DSM-5 personality disorders is described as 'dramatic, emotional, erratic'?",
    options: { a: "Cluster A",
               b: "Cluster B",
               c: "Cluster C",
               d: "Cluster D" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Cluster B (Antisocial, Borderline, Histrionic, Narcissistic) = dramatic/emotional/erratic.",
    study: "Three DSM-5 personality-disorder clusters: A — 'odd/eccentric': Paranoid, Schizoid, Schizotypal ('weird'). B — 'dramatic/emotional/erratic': Antisocial, Borderline, Histrionic, Narcissistic ('wild'). C — 'anxious/fearful': Avoidant, Dependent, Obsessive-Compulsive PD ('worried'). There is no Cluster D. Borderline (splitting, identity disturbance, fear of abandonment) is the most exam-frequent; treated with DBT." },
  { id: "diag_co19", section: "common", topic: "Therapy & Counselling",
    q: "Unconditional positive regard, empathy, and congruence are the core conditions described by:",
    options: { a: "Aaron Beck",
               b: "Albert Ellis",
               c: "Carl Rogers",
               d: "Fritz Perls" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Carl Rogers' Person-Centred therapy: the three 'necessary and sufficient' conditions.",
    study: "Carl Rogers (person-centred / client-centred therapy) named three core, 'necessary and sufficient' therapist conditions: (1) Unconditional Positive Regard — non-judgemental acceptance; (2) Empathy — accurate empathic understanding; (3) Congruence/genuineness — being real, not playing a role. Humanistic, non-directive, focuses on the actualizing tendency and the gap between self-concept and ideal self. Contrast: Beck = cognitive, Ellis = REBT, Perls = Gestalt." },
  { id: "diag_co20", section: "common", topic: "Therapy & Counselling",
    q: "Systematic desensitization, based on reciprocal inhibition, was developed by:",
    options: { a: "B. F. Skinner",
               b: "John Watson",
               c: "Ivan Pavlov",
               d: "Joseph Wolpe" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Joseph Wolpe developed systematic desensitization using reciprocal inhibition (relaxation incompatible with anxiety).",
    study: "Systematic desensitization (Joseph Wolpe, 1958) treats phobias/anxiety by three steps: (1) train deep muscle relaxation, (2) build an anxiety HIERARCHY (least → most feared), (3) pair relaxation with each step, working up. Principle = RECIPROCAL INHIBITION: you cannot be relaxed and anxious at once. It is counter-conditioning. Compare: flooding/implosion (full exposure, no hierarchy), exposure & response prevention (OCD), aversion therapy (pair undesired behaviour with aversive stimulus)." },
  { id: "diag_co21", section: "common", topic: "Therapy & Counselling",
    q: "The 'empty chair' technique is most associated with:",
    options: { a: "Gestalt therapy",
               b: "Psychoanalysis",
               c: "Cognitive therapy",
               d: "Behaviour therapy" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Empty-chair / two-chair work is a hallmark of Fritz Perls' Gestalt therapy.",
    study: "The empty-chair technique belongs to GESTALT therapy (Fritz Perls). The client speaks to an imagined person (or a part of self) in an empty chair to work through 'unfinished business' and integrate disowned parts, emphasising here-and-now awareness. Other Gestalt ideas: figure-ground, contact, the 'safe emergency', responsibility. Don't attribute it to psychodrama (Moreno uses role-play/stage) though they overlap." },
  { id: "diag_co22", section: "common", topic: "Therapy & Counselling",
    q: "In Ellis's REBT, the 'B' in the ABC model stands for:",
    options: { a: "Behaviour",
               b: "Belief",
               c: "Baseline",
               d: "Bias" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "ABC = Activating event → Belief (esp. irrational) → Consequence (emotional/behavioural). Ellis added D (dispute) and E (effect).",
    study: "Albert Ellis's REBT (Rational Emotive Behaviour Therapy) ABC(DE) model: A = Activating event; B = Belief about it (rational or IRRATIONAL — the real cause of distress); C = Consequence (emotional/behavioural). Therapy adds D = Disputing irrational beliefs and E = Effective new philosophy. Core targets are 'musts/shoulds' (musturbation) and catastrophizing. Contrast with Beck's cognitive therapy: automatic thoughts, cognitive distortions, the cognitive triad, collaborative empiricism." },
  { id: "diag_co23", section: "common", topic: "Therapy & Counselling",
    q: "Transference and free association are central techniques of:",
    options: { a: "Behaviour therapy",
               b: "Person-centred therapy",
               c: "Psychoanalytic therapy",
               d: "Reality therapy" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Free association, dream analysis, transference, and interpretation are core psychoanalytic (Freudian) techniques.",
    study: "Psychoanalysis (Freud) techniques: free association (say whatever comes to mind), dream analysis (manifest vs latent content), analysis of TRANSFERENCE (client redirects feelings about significant figures onto the analyst) and COUNTER-transference (analyst's reactions), interpretation, and working through resistance. Goal: make the unconscious conscious. Contrast: behaviour therapy = conditioning; person-centred = Rogers; reality therapy = Glasser (choices/needs)." },
  { id: "diag_co24", section: "common", topic: "Therapy & Counselling",
    q: "Dialectical Behaviour Therapy (DBT) was developed by Marsha Linehan primarily to treat:",
    options: { a: "Schizophrenia",
               b: "Autism spectrum disorder",
               c: "Specific phobias",
               d: "Borderline personality disorder" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Linehan developed DBT for borderline personality disorder and chronic suicidality.",
    study: "DBT (Marsha Linehan) was built for Borderline Personality Disorder & chronic suicidal/self-harm behaviour; now also used for eating disorders and emotion dysregulation. 'Dialectic' = balancing ACCEPTANCE and CHANGE. Four skills modules: (1) Mindfulness, (2) Distress Tolerance, (3) Emotion Regulation, (4) Interpersonal Effectiveness. Modes: individual therapy, skills group, phone coaching, therapist consultation team. Rooted in CBT + Zen mindfulness." },
  { id: "diag_co25", section: "common", topic: "Developmental Psychology",
    q: "Object permanence, according to Piaget, first develops during the:",
    options: { a: "Sensorimotor stage",
               b: "Preoperational stage",
               c: "Concrete operational stage",
               d: "Formal operational stage" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Object permanence (objects exist when out of sight) develops in the sensorimotor stage (0–2 yrs).",
    study: "Piaget's stages: Sensorimotor (0–2) — develops OBJECT PERMANENCE; Preoperational (2–7) — symbolic thought, egocentrism, lacks conservation, animism, centration; Concrete Operational (7–11) — conservation, reversibility, classification, but concrete; Formal Operational (11+) — abstract & hypothetico-deductive reasoning. Key processes: assimilation, accommodation, equilibration, schema. Object permanence ~8 months (A-not-B error). Critique: underestimated infants (Baillargeon)." },
  { id: "diag_co26", section: "common", topic: "Developmental Psychology",
    q: "In Erikson's theory, the psychosocial crisis of adolescence is:",
    options: { a: "Trust vs mistrust",
               b: "Identity vs role confusion",
               c: "Industry vs inferiority",
               d: "Intimacy vs isolation" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Adolescence = Identity vs Role Confusion (stage 5) in Erikson's 8 psychosocial stages.",
    study: "Erikson's 8 psychosocial stages (crisis · virtue): 1 Trust vs Mistrust (infancy · hope), 2 Autonomy vs Shame/Doubt (will), 3 Initiative vs Guilt (purpose), 4 Industry vs Inferiority (school-age · competence), 5 IDENTITY vs Role Confusion (adolescence · fidelity), 6 Intimacy vs Isolation (young adult · love), 7 Generativity vs Stagnation (midlife · care), 8 Ego Integrity vs Despair (old age · wisdom). Marcia extended stage 5 into 4 identity statuses (diffusion, foreclosure, moratorium, achievement)." },
  { id: "diag_co27", section: "common", topic: "Developmental Psychology",
    q: "The 'Strange Situation' procedure for assessing attachment was devised by:",
    options: { a: "John Bowlby",
               b: "Harry Harlow",
               c: "Mary Ainsworth",
               d: "Konrad Lorenz" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Mary Ainsworth's Strange Situation classified infant attachment patterns. Bowlby founded attachment theory.",
    study: "Mary AINSWORTH created the Strange Situation (a lab procedure of separations/reunions) and identified attachment styles: Secure (~B), Insecure-Avoidant (A), Insecure-Resistant/Ambivalent (C); Main & Solomon later added Disorganized (D). John BOWLBY founded attachment theory (secure base, internal working models, monotropy, the 'maternal deprivation' hypothesis). Harlow = rhesus monkeys/contact comfort; Lorenz = imprinting in geese (critical period)." },
  { id: "diag_co28", section: "common", topic: "Developmental Psychology",
    q: "The 'Zone of Proximal Development' is a concept proposed by:",
    options: { a: "Jean Piaget",
               b: "Lawrence Kohlberg",
               c: "Jerome Bruner",
               d: "Lev Vygotsky" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Vygotsky's ZPD = the gap between what a learner can do alone vs with guidance ('scaffolding').",
    study: "Lev VYGOTSKY (sociocultural theory): the Zone of Proximal Development is the distance between what a child can do ALONE and what they can do with help from a 'more knowledgeable other'. Learning leads development; tools include scaffolding (Bruner's term), private/inner speech, and cultural mediation. Contrast with Piaget (development leads learning; individual constructivism). Kohlberg = moral development stages; Bruner = modes of representation (enactive, iconic, symbolic) + spiral curriculum." },
  { id: "diag_co29", section: "common", topic: "Social Psychology",
    q: "The tendency for individuals to be less likely to help when others are present is the:",
    options: { a: "Bystander effect (diffusion of responsibility)",
               b: "Social facilitation effect",
               c: "Halo effect",
               d: "Just-world phenomenon" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Bystander effect: presence of others diffuses responsibility, reducing helping (Darley & Latané, after Kitty Genovese).",
    study: "Bystander effect (Darley & Latané, prompted by the 1964 Kitty Genovese case): the more bystanders present, the LESS likely (and slower) any one person helps — via diffusion of responsibility, pluralistic ignorance (everyone looks calm, so I assume no emergency), and evaluation apprehension. Latané & Darley's 5-step decision model: notice → interpret as emergency → assume responsibility → know how to help → decide to help. Don't confuse with social facilitation (presence of others improves easy/well-learned tasks)." },
  { id: "diag_co30", section: "common", topic: "Social Psychology",
    q: "The theory of cognitive dissonance was proposed by:",
    options: { a: "Stanley Milgram",
               b: "Leon Festinger",
               c: "Solomon Asch",
               d: "Philip Zimbardo" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Leon Festinger (1957): tension from holding inconsistent cognitions motivates attitude/behaviour change.",
    study: "Cognitive dissonance (Leon Festinger, 1957): inconsistency between cognitions (or between attitude and behaviour) creates discomfort that motivates us to reduce it — by changing the attitude, adding consonant cognitions, or trivializing. Classic study: Festinger & Carlsmith ($1 vs $20 boring-task study — the $1 group changed attitudes MORE, because they had less external justification). Milgram = obedience; Asch = conformity; Zimbardo = Stanford prison experiment." },
  { id: "diag_co31", section: "common", topic: "Social Psychology",
    q: "Overestimating dispositional causes and underestimating situational causes of others' behaviour is the:",
    options: { a: "Self-serving bias",
               b: "Actor-observer bias",
               c: "Fundamental attribution error",
               d: "Confirmation bias" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Fundamental attribution error (Ross): over-attribute others' behaviour to personality, under-weight the situation.",
    study: "Fundamental Attribution Error (correspondence bias; term by Lee Ross): when judging OTHERS we over-credit internal disposition ('he's lazy') and ignore the situation. Related: Actor-Observer Bias — we explain OUR OWN behaviour situationally but others' dispositionally. Self-Serving Bias — credit our successes to ability, blame failures on circumstances. Just-world hypothesis — people get what they deserve. These attribution biases (Heider, Kelley's covariation model) are NET staples." },
  { id: "diag_co32", section: "common", topic: "Social Psychology",
    q: "The classic line-judgment experiments demonstrating conformity to group pressure were conducted by:",
    options: { a: "Stanley Milgram",
               b: "Philip Zimbardo",
               c: "Muzafer Sherif",
               d: "Solomon Asch" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Solomon Asch's line-judgment studies showed conformity to a unanimous but wrong majority.",
    study: "Solomon ASCH (1950s) line-judgment studies: participants conformed to a clearly wrong unanimous majority ~37% of trials; ~75% conformed at least once. Conformity drops sharply if even one ally dissents. Sherif's autokinetic-effect study showed norm formation (informational influence) on an ambiguous task. Milgram = obedience to authority (shock study). Two influence types: NORMATIVE (fit in / be liked) vs INFORMATIONAL (be right when uncertain)." },
  { id: "diag_co33", section: "common", topic: "Biopsychology",
    q: "Parkinson's disease is primarily associated with a deficiency of which neurotransmitter?",
    options: { a: "Dopamine",
               b: "Serotonin",
               c: "Acetylcholine",
               d: "GABA" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Parkinson's = loss of dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra (nigrostriatal pathway).",
    study: "Parkinson's disease = degeneration of DOPAMINE-producing neurons in the substantia nigra (basal ganglia / nigrostriatal pathway) → resting tremor, rigidity, bradykinesia, postural instability. Treated with L-DOPA. Contrast NT links: Alzheimer's → acetylcholine deficit; Huntington's → GABA/ACh loss; depression → serotonin/noradrenaline; schizophrenia → dopamine EXCESS (mesolimbic, the dopamine hypothesis); anxiety → low GABA." },
  { id: "diag_co34", section: "common", topic: "Biopsychology",
    q: "The 'rest and digest' functions of the body are governed by the:",
    options: { a: "Sympathetic nervous system",
               b: "Parasympathetic nervous system",
               c: "Somatic nervous system",
               d: "Central nervous system" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Parasympathetic = 'rest and digest' (conserves energy). Sympathetic = 'fight or flight'.",
    study: "Autonomic nervous system has two branches: SYMPATHETIC = 'fight or flight' (↑heart rate, dilates pupils, inhibits digestion, releases adrenaline) and PARASYMPATHETIC = 'rest and digest' (↓heart rate, constricts pupils, stimulates digestion); dominant cranial nerve = vagus (X). They are antagonistic/complementary. The somatic NS controls voluntary skeletal muscle; together with the autonomic NS it makes up the peripheral nervous system." },
  { id: "diag_co35", section: "common", topic: "Biopsychology",
    q: "Damage to Broca's area typically results in:",
    options: { a: "Fluent speech with poor comprehension",
               b: "Complete loss of hearing",
               c: "Non-fluent, effortful speech with relatively intact comprehension",
               d: "Inability to recognize faces" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Broca's aphasia = non-fluent, effortful, agrammatic speech; comprehension relatively preserved. Broca's area is in the frontal lobe.",
    study: "Broca's area (left frontal lobe, near motor cortex) = speech PRODUCTION. Damage → Broca's (expressive/non-fluent) aphasia: halting, effortful, telegraphic speech, but comprehension is relatively intact and the patient is AWARE of the deficit. Wernicke's area (left temporal) = comprehension; damage → fluent but meaningless 'word salad' speech with poor comprehension and little awareness. Arcuate fasciculus damage → conduction aphasia (poor repetition). Face recognition = fusiform (prosopagnosia)." },
  { id: "diag_co36", section: "common", topic: "Biopsychology",
    q: "The hippocampus is most critical for:",
    options: { a: "Regulating body temperature",
               b: "Controlling balance and coordination",
               c: "Producing speech",
               d: "Forming new long-term (declarative) memories" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "The hippocampus consolidates new declarative/episodic long-term memories (cf. patient H.M.).",
    study: "Hippocampus (medial temporal lobe, part of the limbic system) = consolidation of new DECLARATIVE/episodic long-term memories; spatial navigation. Patient H.M. (bilateral hippocampal resection) had anterograde amnesia — couldn't form new explicit memories but kept old ones and could learn procedural skills. Other structures: hypothalamus → temperature/homeostasis/4 F's; cerebellum → balance & coordination; amygdala → fear/emotion; Broca's → speech." },
  { id: "diag_co37", section: "common", topic: "Cognition",
    q: "The working memory model with a phonological loop, visuospatial sketchpad, and central executive was proposed by:",
    options: { a: "Baddeley & Hitch",
               b: "Atkinson & Shiffrin",
               c: "Tulving",
               d: "Sperling" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Baddeley & Hitch (1974) working memory: central executive + phonological loop + visuospatial sketchpad (+ episodic buffer, 2000).",
    study: "Baddeley & Hitch (1974) WORKING MEMORY model replaced the passive short-term store: a CENTRAL EXECUTIVE (attentional controller) directs two slave systems — the PHONOLOGICAL LOOP (verbal/acoustic; phonological store + articulatory rehearsal) and the VISUOSPATIAL SKETCHPAD (visual/spatial). Baddeley (2000) added the EPISODIC BUFFER (binds info across systems, links to LTM). Contrast Atkinson-Shiffrin (1968) multi-store: sensory → STM → LTM. Tulving = episodic vs semantic memory." },
  { id: "diag_co38", section: "common", topic: "Cognition",
    q: "When previously learned information interferes with the recall of newly learned information, it is called:",
    options: { a: "Retroactive interference",
               b: "Proactive interference",
               c: "Decay",
               d: "Encoding failure" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "PROactive interference: OLD learning blocks NEW (forward). REtroactive: NEW blocks OLD (backward).",
    study: "Interference theory of forgetting: PROACTIVE interference = OLD memories interfere with NEW learning (e.g., last year's locker number intrudes on this year's). RETROACTIVE interference = NEW learning interferes with OLD memories. Mnemonic: PRO = forward (old pushes forward onto new); RETRO = backward (new reaches back onto old). Other forgetting accounts: trace decay, cue-dependent forgetting (encoding-specificity, Tulving), motivated forgetting/repression." },
  { id: "diag_co39", section: "common", topic: "Cognition",
    q: "George Miller's classic estimate of the capacity of short-term memory is:",
    options: { a: "3 ± 1 items",
               b: "12 ± 3 items",
               c: "7 ± 2 items",
               d: "Unlimited" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Miller (1956) 'The Magical Number Seven, Plus or Minus Two' — STM holds ~7±2 chunks.",
    study: "George Miller (1956), 'The Magical Number Seven, Plus or Minus Two': short-term memory capacity ≈ 7 ± 2 CHUNKS. CHUNKING (grouping items into meaningful units) expands effective capacity. STM duration ~15–30s without rehearsal. (Cowan later argued the real limit is closer to 4±1 chunks.) Contrast: sensory memory is brief/high-capacity (Sperling's iconic memory ~250ms); LTM is effectively unlimited and durable." },
  { id: "diag_co40", section: "common", topic: "Learning & Conditioning",
    q: "Negative reinforcement involves:",
    options: { a: "Presenting an aversive stimulus to decrease behaviour",
               b: "Removing a reward to decrease behaviour",
               c: "Presenting a reward to increase behaviour",
               d: "Removing an aversive stimulus to increase behaviour" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Negative reinforcement = REMOVE an aversive stimulus → behaviour INCREASES. It is not punishment.",
    study: "Operant 2×2 (Skinner). REINFORCEMENT always INCREASES behaviour; PUNISHMENT always DECREASES it. 'Positive' = add a stimulus; 'Negative' = remove one. So: Positive reinforcement = add pleasant (praise) → ↑. NEGATIVE reinforcement = remove aversive (seatbelt alarm stops when you buckle) → ↑. Positive punishment = add aversive (scolding) → ↓. Negative punishment = remove pleasant (take away phone) → ↓. The classic trap: negative reinforcement is NOT punishment." },
  { id: "diag_co41", section: "common", topic: "Learning & Conditioning",
    q: "The 'Little Albert' experiment, demonstrating the conditioning of a fear response, was conducted by:",
    options: { a: "John B. Watson",
               b: "B. F. Skinner",
               c: "Edward Thorndike",
               d: "Ivan Pavlov" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "John B. Watson & Rosalie Rayner (1920) classically conditioned fear in 'Little Albert'.",
    study: "John B. WATSON (with Rosalie Rayner, 1920) conditioned an 11-month-old, 'Little Albert', to fear a white rat by pairing it (neutral stimulus) with a loud clanging noise (US → fear UR); the fear then generalized to similar furry objects. Demonstrates classical conditioning of emotion + stimulus generalization; Watson is the father of behaviourism ('Give me a dozen healthy infants...'). Pavlov = dogs/salivation; Thorndike = law of effect/puzzle box; Skinner = operant chamber." },
  { id: "diag_co42", section: "common", topic: "Learning & Conditioning",
    q: "Learning that occurs without reinforcement and is not demonstrated until there is an incentive (shown in Tolman's rat maze studies) is called:",
    options: { a: "Insight learning",
               b: "Latent learning",
               c: "Observational learning",
               d: "Vicarious learning" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Latent learning (Tolman): learning happens but stays hidden until there's a reason to perform it; evidence for cognitive maps.",
    study: "Edward TOLMAN — LATENT learning: rats allowed to explore a maze with no reward still learned its layout (a 'cognitive map'); they showed the learning only once food (incentive) was introduced, then quickly outperformed never-rewarded rats. This challenged strict S-R behaviourism by showing learning ≠ performance and that cognition matters. Insight learning = Köhler (chimps, sudden 'aha'); observational/vicarious learning = Bandura (Bobo doll, modelling)." },
  { id: "diag_co43", section: "common", topic: "Personality",
    q: "Which of the following is NOT one of the Big Five (OCEAN) personality traits?",
    options: { a: "Openness to experience",
               b: "Conscientiousness",
               c: "Intelligence",
               d: "Neuroticism" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Big Five = OCEAN: Openness, Conscientiousness, Extraversion, Agreeableness, Neuroticism. Intelligence is not one.",
    study: "Big Five / Five-Factor Model (Costa & McCrae; measured by NEO-PI-R) = OCEAN: Openness, Conscientiousness, Extraversion, Agreeableness, Neuroticism (vs Emotional Stability). Intelligence is NOT a Big Five factor. Lineage: Allport (trait theory, cardinal/central/secondary), Cattell (16PF, 16 source traits), Eysenck (PEN — Psychoticism, Extraversion, Neuroticism). HEXACO adds Honesty-Humility as a 6th factor." },
  { id: "diag_co44", section: "common", topic: "Personality",
    q: "In Freud's structural model, the part of the personality that operates on the 'pleasure principle' is the:",
    options: { a: "Ego",
               b: "Superego",
               c: "Preconscious",
               d: "Id" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "The ID seeks immediate gratification (pleasure principle). The ego runs on the reality principle; the superego is the moral conscience.",
    study: "Freud's structural model: ID — present at birth, fully unconscious, runs on the PLEASURE PRINCIPLE (immediate gratification, primary process thinking). EGO — the 'executive', operates on the REALITY PRINCIPLE (secondary process), mediates id vs superego vs reality, deploys defense mechanisms. SUPEREGO — internalized morals/ideals (conscience + ego-ideal), develops ~age 5 via the Oedipus/Electra resolution. Anxiety arises when the ego can't balance the three." },
  // ===================== MPhil-SPECIFIC =====================
  { id: "diag_mp01", section: "mphil", topic: "MPhil — Legal & Professional",
    q: "The Rehabilitation Council of India (RCI) was established as a statutory body under the RCI Act of:",
    options: { a: "1992",
               b: "1987",
               c: "2002",
               d: "2016" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "RCI Act, 1992 — makes registration mandatory to practise as a clinical psychologist/rehabilitation professional.",
    study: "RCI Act, 1992: the Rehabilitation Council of India (under the Ministry of Social Justice & Empowerment) regulates training and maintains the register of rehabilitation professionals — including clinical psychologists. Registration (CRR number) is MANDATORY to practise; practising unregistered is an offence. Sister legislation to keep straight: Mental Healthcare Act 2017, Rights of Persons with Disabilities (RPwD) Act 2016, National Trust Act 1999, Persons with Disabilities Act 1995 (repealed by RPwD 2016)." },
  { id: "diag_mp02", section: "mphil", topic: "MPhil — Legal & Professional",
    q: "The concept of an 'Advance Directive' and a 'Nominated Representative' for mental healthcare in India comes from the:",
    options: { a: "Mental Health Act, 1987",
               b: "Mental Healthcare Act, 2017",
               c: "RPwD Act, 2016",
               d: "RCI Act, 1992" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Mental Healthcare Act (MHCA) 2017 introduced advance directives, nominated representatives, and decriminalized suicide.",
    study: "Mental Healthcare Act (MHCA), 2017 (replaced the Mental Health Act 1987): rights-based, aligns with the UN CRPD. Key features — right to access mental healthcare, ADVANCE DIRECTIVE (state in advance how you wish to be treated), NOMINATED REPRESENTATIVE, decriminalized attempted suicide (presumed severe stress), restricted ECT (banned without anaesthesia and for minors save exceptions), Mental Health Review Boards. Replaced 'mentally ill person' with 'person with mental illness'." },
  { id: "diag_mp03", section: "mphil", topic: "MPhil — History & Training Models",
    q: "The 'scientist-practitioner' model of clinical psychology training (Ph.D.) originated at the 1949 conference held at:",
    options: { a: "Vail",
               b: "Salt Lake City",
               c: "Boulder",
               d: "Adelphi" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Boulder Conference (1949) → scientist-practitioner (Boulder) model, Ph.D. Vail (1973) → practitioner-scholar, Psy.D.",
    study: "Training models for clinical psychology: BOULDER model (Boulder, Colorado conference, 1949) = SCIENTIST-PRACTITIONER → integrates research + practice, awards the Ph.D. VAIL model (Vail, Colorado, 1973) = PRACTITIONER-SCHOLAR → emphasises clinical practice, awards the Psy.D. (Doctor of Psychology). Also know: Lightner Witmer opened the first psychological CLINIC (1896) and coined 'clinical psychology'; the Clinical-Scientist model (1990s, McFall) is research-heavy." },
  { id: "diag_mp04", section: "mphil", topic: "MPhil — Ethics",
    q: "The legal principle that a therapist has a 'duty to warn/protect' an identifiable third party from a client's threats stems from:",
    options: { a: "The Durham rule",
               b: "The Daubert standard",
               c: "The M'Naghten rule",
               d: "The Tarasoff case" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Tarasoff v. Regents (1976) established the duty to warn/protect identifiable potential victims — a limit on confidentiality.",
    study: "Tarasoff v. Regents of the University of California (1976): created the therapist's DUTY TO WARN / PROTECT an identifiable third party from a client's serious threat — 'protective privilege ends where the public peril begins'. This is a recognised LIMIT to confidentiality (others: risk of harm to self, child/elder abuse, court order). Don't confuse with insanity-defence standards: M'Naghten (right-wrong test), Durham (product test), and Daubert (admissibility of scientific evidence)." },
  { id: "diag_mp05", section: "mphil", topic: "MPhil — Culture & Diagnosis",
    q: "'Dhat syndrome', characterized by anxiety over the loss of semen, is a culture-bound syndrome reported in:",
    options: { a: "The Indian subcontinent",
               b: "Malaysia",
               c: "Latin America",
               d: "The Arctic" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Dhat = South Asian culture-bound syndrome: distress attributed to semen loss.",
    study: "Culture-bound syndromes (now 'cultural concepts of distress', DSM-5): DHAT — South Asia/India, anxiety + somatic symptoms attributed to semen loss in urine. Koro — SE Asia, fear that genitals are retracting into the body. Amok — Malaysia, sudden mass homicidal outburst. Latah — Malaysia/Indonesia, exaggerated startle + echolalia. Pibloktoq ('Arctic hysteria') — Inuit. Susto ('soul loss/fright') — Latin America. Taijin kyofusho — Japan, fear of offending others." },
  { id: "diag_mp06", section: "mphil", topic: "MPhil — Legal & Professional",
    q: "In India, the Act that provides for the protection of children from sexual offences is the POCSO Act of:",
    options: { a: "2005",
               b: "2012",
               c: "2009",
               d: "2015" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Protection of Children from Sexual Offences (POCSO) Act, 2012; gender-neutral, defines a child as under 18.",
    study: "POCSO Act, 2012 (Protection of Children from Sexual Offences): gender-neutral, defines a 'child' as anyone under 18, mandates child-friendly procedures and reporting (mandatory reporting of known offences), and special courts. Related dates: Juvenile Justice (Care & Protection) Act 2015 (amended), Criminal Law (Amendment) Act 2013 (raised age of consent to 18, post-Nirbhaya). Clinical psychologists must know mandatory-reporting duties under POCSO." },
  { id: "diag_mp07", section: "mphil", topic: "MPhil — Clinical Assessment",
    q: "In the Mental Status Examination (MSE), 'insight' refers to:",
    options: { a: "The client's orientation to time, place, and person",
               b: "The client's ability to do serial subtraction",
               c: "The client's awareness and understanding of their own illness",
               d: "The presence of hallucinations" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Insight in the MSE = the patient's awareness that they are ill and understanding of its nature; judged on a spectrum.",
    study: "MSE 'insight' = the patient's awareness/understanding that they have a mental illness and its implications (graded: complete denial → partial → intellectual → true emotional insight). Distinct from JUDGEMENT (ability to make sound decisions). Other MSE domains: Appearance & behaviour, Speech, Mood (sustained, subjective) vs Affect (observed, momentary — range/reactivity/congruence), Thought (form/content/possession), Perception (hallucinations/illusions), Cognition (orientation, attention, memory — serial 7s), Insight & Judgement. Poor insight is common in psychosis and mania." },
  { id: "diag_mp08", section: "mphil", topic: "MPhil — History & Training Models",
    q: "The first psychological clinic and the term 'clinical psychology' are credited to:",
    options: { a: "Sigmund Freud",
               b: "Wilhelm Wundt",
               c: "G. Stanley Hall",
               d: "Lightner Witmer" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Lightner Witmer founded the first psychological clinic (Univ. of Pennsylvania, 1896) and coined 'clinical psychology'.",
    study: "Lightner WITMER opened the world's first psychological clinic at the University of Pennsylvania in 1896 and coined the term 'clinical psychology' (1907, also founded the journal The Psychological Clinic). G. Stanley Hall = first APA president, first US psychology Ph.D., child study / adolescence. Wundt = first experimental psychology lab (Leipzig, 1879). Knowing these 'firsts' (Witmer-clinic, Wundt-lab, Hall-APA) is a reliable MPhil/NET history item." },
  // ===================== UGC-NET SPECIFIC =====================
  { id: "diag_nt01", section: "ugcnet", topic: "UGC-NET — Schools & History",
    q: "The founder of psychophysics, who studied the relationship between physical stimuli and sensations, is:",
    options: { a: "Gustav Fechner",
               b: "Wilhelm Wundt",
               c: "Ernst Weber",
               d: "Hermann Ebbinghaus" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Gustav Fechner is the father of psychophysics (Elements of Psychophysics, 1860); built on Weber's work (Weber-Fechner law).",
    study: "Gustav FECHNER (1860, 'Elements of Psychophysics') = founder of psychophysics, quantifying the relation between physical stimulus magnitude and sensation. Methods: limits, constant stimuli, adjustment; concepts: absolute threshold, difference threshold (jnd). Weber's law: ΔI/I = constant (just-noticeable difference is a constant proportion). Fechner's law: sensation = k·log(stimulus). Ebbinghaus = forgetting curve / nonsense syllables; Wundt = first lab (1879)." },
  { id: "diag_nt02", section: "ugcnet", topic: "UGC-NET — Schools & History",
    q: "The first experimental psychology laboratory (Leipzig, 1879) was established by:",
    options: { a: "William James",
               b: "Wilhelm Wundt",
               c: "Edward Titchener",
               d: "Sigmund Freud" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Wilhelm Wundt founded the first psychology lab at Leipzig in 1879 — the conventional birth of scientific psychology.",
    study: "Wilhelm WUNDT — first experimental psychology laboratory at Leipzig, 1879 (the marker for psychology becoming a science). His school = STRUCTURALISM (analyse conscious experience into elements via introspection); his student Edward TITCHENER carried structuralism to the US and coined the name. Contrast: William JAMES = functionalism (function/purpose of mind, 'Principles of Psychology', 1890); Freud = psychoanalysis. Wundt-lab vs Witmer-clinic vs James-functionalism are commonly mixed up." },
  { id: "diag_nt03", section: "ugcnet", topic: "UGC-NET — Schools & History",
    q: "Functionalism, emphasizing the purpose of mental processes in adaptation, is most associated with:",
    options: { a: "Wilhelm Wundt",
               b: "Edward Titchener",
               c: "William James",
               d: "John Watson" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "William James is the leading figure of functionalism (influenced by Darwin); author of 'Principles of Psychology' (1890).",
    study: "FUNCTIONALISM (William James; also Dewey, Angell, Carr) asked what mind/behaviour is FOR — how consciousness helps the organism adapt and survive (Darwinian influence) — rather than its structure. It paved the way for applied psychology and behaviourism. Contrast STRUCTURALISM (Wundt/Titchener: break consciousness into elements via introspection). James's 'Principles of Psychology' (1890), stream of consciousness, and the James-Lange theory of emotion are linked NET items." },
  { id: "diag_nt04", section: "ugcnet", topic: "UGC-NET — Cognition & Perception",
    q: "The Gestalt principle stating that objects close together are perceived as a group is the law of:",
    options: { a: "Similarity",
               b: "Continuity",
               c: "Closure",
               d: "Proximity" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Law of Proximity: elements near each other are grouped together perceptually.",
    study: "Gestalt principles of perceptual organization ('the whole is other than the sum of its parts'): PROXIMITY (group by nearness), SIMILARITY (group alike items), CLOSURE (fill gaps to see a whole), CONTINUITY/good continuation (follow smooth paths), COMMON FATE (move together = grouped), FIGURE-GROUND, PRÄGNANZ (law of good form/simplicity — the overarching principle). Key Gestalt figures: Wertheimer (phi phenomenon), Köhler (insight), Koffka." },
  { id: "diag_nt05", section: "ugcnet", topic: "UGC-NET — Research & Applied",
    q: "When participants change their behaviour simply because they know they are being observed/studied, this is the:",
    options: { a: "Hawthorne effect",
               b: "Placebo effect",
               c: "Halo effect",
               d: "Pygmalion effect" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Hawthorne effect: behaviour changes due to awareness of being observed (from the Western Electric Hawthorne studies).",
    study: "HAWTHORNE EFFECT (Elton Mayo, Western Electric Hawthorne plant studies, 1920s–30s): workers' productivity rose with ANY change in conditions — even reduced lighting — because they knew they were being watched/given attention; a key threat to internal validity (a reactivity/demand effect). Don't confuse: Placebo effect (improvement from an inert treatment due to expectancy); Halo effect (one trait colours overall judgement); Pygmalion/Rosenthal effect (expectations create self-fulfilling performance — experimenter expectancy)." },
  { id: "diag_nt06", section: "ugcnet", topic: "UGC-NET — Motivation & Personality",
    q: "At the apex of Maslow's hierarchy of needs is:",
    options: { a: "Esteem needs",
               b: "Self-actualization",
               c: "Safety needs",
               d: "Belongingness needs" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Maslow's pyramid peaks at self-actualization (realizing one's potential); the classic 5-level model.",
    study: "Maslow's hierarchy (bottom → top): Physiological → Safety → Love/Belongingness → Esteem → SELF-ACTUALIZATION (realizing one's full potential). The lower four are 'deficiency (D) needs'; self-actualization is a 'being (B) need / growth need'. Maslow later added cognitive and aesthetic needs, and finally SELF-TRANSCENDENCE above self-actualization. Humanistic psychology (Maslow & Rogers) = the 'third force'. Peak experiences are linked to self-actualizers." },
  { id: "diag_nt07", section: "ugcnet", topic: "UGC-NET — Psychometrics",
    q: "The extent to which a test score predicts performance on a future criterion is called:",
    options: { a: "Content validity",
               b: "Concurrent validity",
               c: "Predictive validity",
               d: "Face validity" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Predictive validity = test predicts a FUTURE criterion (a type of criterion-related validity). Concurrent = present-time criterion.",
    study: "Validity types: CONTENT validity (items cover the domain — e.g., a syllabus); CRITERION-related validity, split into PREDICTIVE (test now predicts a FUTURE outcome, e.g., entrance test → later grades) and CONCURRENT (test correlates with a criterion measured at the SAME time); CONSTRUCT validity (does it measure the theoretical construct — via convergent & discriminant evidence, factor analysis); FACE validity (appears valid superficially — weakest, not a true psychometric form). Validity needs reliability but goes beyond it." },
  { id: "diag_nt08", section: "ugcnet", topic: "UGC-NET — Intelligence",
    q: "The two-factor theory of intelligence, proposing a general factor 'g' and specific factors 's', was given by:",
    options: { a: "L. L. Thurstone",
               b: "J. P. Guilford",
               c: "Howard Gardner",
               d: "Charles Spearman" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Charles Spearman's two-factor theory: a general intelligence 'g' plus task-specific 's' factors (via factor analysis).",
    study: "Charles SPEARMAN — two-factor theory of intelligence: a general factor 'g' (common to all tasks) + specific factors 's' (task-specific); he pioneered factor analysis. Contrast other theories: THURSTONE — 7 Primary Mental Abilities (no single g); GUILFORD — Structure of Intellect (up to 150/180 abilities); CATTELL-HORN — fluid (Gf) vs crystallized (Gc); GARDNER — multiple intelligences (8+); STERNBERG — triarchic (analytical, creative, practical); Carroll's three-stratum / CHC model integrates them." },
],
  "pep-mock-1": [
  { id: "pep1_mp01", section: "mphil",
    q: "Which ministry is the Rehabilitation Council of India (RCI) under?",
    options: { a: "Ministry of Education",
               b: "Ministry of Health and Family Welfare",
               c: "Ministry of Labour and Employment",
               d: "Ministry of Social Justice and Empowerment" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "The Rehabilitation Council of India is a statutory body under the Ministry of Social Justice and Empowerment (Department of Empowerment of Persons with Disabilities), which handles disability and rehabilitation matters; it is not under Health or Education." },
  { id: "pep1_mp02", section: "mphil",
    q: "Q) Who coined the term “mental tests”?",
    options: { a: "Alfred Binet",
               b: "James McKeen Cattell",
               c: "David Wechsler",
               d: "Lewis Terman" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "James McKeen Cattell coined the term 'mental tests' in his 1890 paper 'Mental Tests and Measurements.' Binet developed early intelligence scales but did not coin this term." },
  { id: "pep1_mp03", section: "mphil",
    q: "Q) Who was the psychologist that introduced the term scholar-professional for the new model of training?",
    options: { a: "Gordon Derner",
               b: "Sigmund Freud",
               c: "F. Skinner",
               d: "Carl Jung" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Gordon Derner was central to establishing the scholar-professional (professional/practitioner) model of clinical training and founded a professional school of psychology; the others (Freud, Skinner, Jung) are unrelated to this training-model terminology." },
  { id: "pep1_mp04", section: "mphil",
    q: "Q) Which Act mandates all rehabilitation professionals to be registered with the RCI?",
    options: { a: "Mental Health Care Act, 2017",
               b: "Persons with Disabilities Act, 1995",
               c: "Mental Healthcare Act, 1987",
               d: "Rehabilitation Council of India Act, 1992" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "The Rehabilitation Council of India Act, 1992 created the RCI as a statutory body and mandates registration of rehabilitation professionals; practicing without registration is an offence under this Act." },
  { id: "pep1_mp05", section: "mphil",
    q: "Q) How do clinicians tend to conceive assessment information?",
    options: { a: "As factual, hypothetical, or imaginary",
               b: "As predictions, estimates, or suggestions",
               c: "As examples, prototypes, or categories",
               d: "As samples, correlates, or signs" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "In assessment psychology, clinicians conceive test data as samples (of behavior), correlates (statistically linked to criteria), or signs (indicators of underlying states), per the classic frameworks of interpretation." },
  { id: "pep1_mp06", section: "mphil",
    q: "Q) Which of the following affect factors do NOT affect the outward objective expression of immediate emotion?",
    options: { a: "Quality of affect",
               b: "Persistence of mood",
               c: "Range of affect",
               d: "Reactivity of mood" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Quality, range, and reactivity describe the outward objective expression of immediate emotion (affect). Persistence of mood refers to the sustained, pervasive internal emotional climate, not the moment-to-moment outward affective display." },
  { id: "pep1_mp07", section: "mphil",
    q: "Which aspect of cognitive function is assessed by the Glasgow Coma Scale?",
    options: { a: "Consciousness",
               b: "Attention",
               c: "Memory",
               d: "Intelligence" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "The Glasgow Coma Scale grades level of consciousness via eye opening, verbal, and motor responses. It is not a measure of attention, memory, or intelligence." },
  { id: "pep1_mp08", section: "mphil",
    q: "What is assessed in the Insight category of the cognitive assessment?",
    options: { a: "Orientation to time, place, and person",
               b: "Level of consciousness",
               c: "Degree of awareness regarding one’s illness",
               d: "Immediate retention and recall" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Insight in the mental status examination refers to the patient's degree of awareness and understanding of their own illness. Orientation, consciousness, and immediate recall are separate cognitive domains." },
  { id: "pep1_mp09", section: "mphil",
    q: "What is flight of ideas?",
    options: { a: "A repetition of the same idea over and over again",
               b: "A rapid shift from one idea to another without completing any of them",
               c: "A preoccupation with irrelevant details",
               d: "A lack of spontaneity in speech" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Flight of ideas is a rapid, continuous flow of speech with quick shifting from one idea to another, often with discernible (loose) connections. Repetition of one idea is perseveration; preoccupation with detail is circumstantiality." },
  { id: "pep1_mp10", section: "mphil",
    q: "Which of the following is NOT included in the General Principles of the APA's Ethical Principles of Psychologists and Code of Conduct?",
    options: { a: "Beneficence and Nonmaleficence",
               b: "Fidelity and Responsibility",
               c: "Integrity",
               d: "Equity" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "The APA's five General Principles are Beneficence and Nonmaleficence, Fidelity and Responsibility, Integrity, Justice, and Respect for People's Rights and Dignity. 'Equity' is not one of them (the relevant principle is Justice)." },
  { id: "pep1_mp11", section: "mphil",
    q: "Which General Principle emphasizes the importance of promoting accuracy, honesty, and truthfulness in the science, teaching, and practice of psychology?",
    options: { a: "Beneficence and Nonmaleficence",
               b: "Fidelity and Responsibility",
               c: "Integrity",
               d: "Respect for People's Rights and Dignity" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Integrity (Principle C) emphasizes promoting accuracy, honesty, and truthfulness in the science, teaching, and practice of psychology. Fidelity and Responsibility concerns trust and professional obligations." },
  { id: "pep1_mp12", section: "mphil",
    q: "How does the deontological approach to ethics differ from the utilitarian perspective?",
    options: { a: "The deontological approach emphasizes the cost-benefit analysis of actions, while the utilitarian perspective focuses on moral duties and obligations.",
               b: "The deontological approach focuses on the intrinsic nature of actions as being good or bad, while the utilitarian perspective takes a cost-benefit approach to judge actions.",
               c: "The deontological approach and the utilitarian perspective are identical in their approach to ethics.",
               d: "The deontological approach and the utilitarian perspective both focus on moral duties and obligations." },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Deontology judges actions by their intrinsic rightness/wrongness and adherence to duties, whereas utilitarianism judges actions by their consequences via a cost-benefit (greatest-good) calculus. Option a reverses the two." },
  { id: "pep1_mp13", section: "mphil",
    q: "What are the four pillars of principle-based ethics?",
    options: { a: "Autonomy, beneficence, non-maleficence, and integrity",
               b: "Autonomy, beneficence, justice, and respect",
               c: "Beneficence, non-maleficence, justice, and respect for autonomy",
               d: "Beneficence, non-maleficence, integrity, and respect for autonomy" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Beauchamp and Childress's four principles of biomedical ethics are beneficence, non-maleficence, justice, and respect for autonomy. Integrity is not one of the four principles." },
  { id: "pep1_mp14", section: "mphil",
    q: "Which types of psychotherapy are most effective in treating survivors of sexual assault?",
    options: { a: "Psychodynamic psychotherapy, cognitive-behavioral therapy, and mindfulness-based therapy",
               b: "Psychodynamic psychotherapy, interpersonal therapy, and dialectical behavior therapy",
               c: "Psychodynamic psychotherapy, trauma-focused cognitive-behavioral therapy, and eye movement desensitization and reprocessing therapy",
               d: "Cognitive-behavioral therapy, mindfulness-based therapy, and acceptance and commitment therapy" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "For sexual assault/PTSD survivors the most evidence-based therapies are trauma-focused CBT (including CPT/PE) and EMDR; psychodynamic work is also used adjunctively. This option uniquely names the trauma-specific, empirically supported approaches." },
  { id: "pep1_mp15", section: "mphil",
    q: "What is the main goal of TF-CBT?",
    options: { a: "To provide closure and relief from the effects of trauma",
               b: "To develop skills for emotional regulation and mindfulness",
               c: "To collect the trauma narrative and desensitize traumatic memories",
               d: "To teach the child or adolescent coping skills for managing symptoms related to trauma" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "A core aim of TF-CBT is creating and processing the trauma narrative and desensitizing/gradually exposing the child to traumatic memories to reduce avoidance. Coping-skill teaching is one component but the central trauma-processing goal is narrative development and desensitization." },
  { id: "pep1_mp16", section: "mphil",
    q: "What is the correct sequence of the eight phases of EMDR?",
    options: { a: "Phase 1: Preparation, Phase 2: Assessment, Phase 3: History taking and treatment planning, Phase 4: Desensitization, Phase 5: Installation, Phase 6: Closure, Phase 7: Body scan, Phase 8: Reevaluation",
               b: "Phase 1: History taking and treatment planning, Phase 2: Preparation, Phase 3: Assessment, Phase 4: Desensitization, Phase 5: Installation, Phase 6: Body scan, Phase 7: Closure, Phase 8: Reevaluation",
               c: "Phase 1: History taking and treatment planning, Phase 2: Preparation, Phase 3: Assessment, Phase 4: Desensitization, Phase 5: Installation, Phase 6: Body scan, Phase 7: Reevaluation, Phase 8: Closure",
               d: "Phase 1: Preparation, Phase 2: History taking and treatment planning, Phase 3: Assessment, Phase 4: Desensitization, Phase 5: Body scan, Phase 6: Installation, Phase 7: Reevaluation, Phase 8: Closure" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "EMDR's eight phases in order: History-taking/treatment planning, Preparation, Assessment, Desensitization, Installation, Body scan, Closure, Reevaluation. Note: the option text mislabels phase 7 as Reevaluation and phase 8 as Closure, but it is the only choice with the correct phase 1-6 sequence and is the intended key." },
  { id: "pep1_mp17", section: "mphil",
    q: "How does the syndromal approach of classifying psychiatric disorders differ from the historical approach?",
    options: { a: "The syndromal approach is based on known etiology, while the historical approach is based on clinical signs and symptoms.",
               b: "The syndromal approach is based on clinical signs and symptoms, while the historical approach is based on known etiology.",
               c: "The syndromal approach is not used in psychiatry, while the historical approach is commonly used.",
               d: "The syndromal approach is used only for medical illnesses, while the historical approach is used only for psychiatric disorders." },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "The modern syndromal (descriptive) approach groups disorders by clusters of clinical signs and symptoms, whereas earlier/historical etiological classification attempted to organize disorders by presumed known causes. Option a reverses this." },
  { id: "pep1_mp18", section: "mphil",
    q: "Which category of mental disorders in ICD-10 includes delirium and dementia?",
    options: { a: "F00-F09 Organic, Including Symptomatic, Mental Disorders",
               b: "F10-F19 Mental and Behavioural Disorders due to Psychoactive Substance Use",
               c: "F20-F29 Schizophrenia, Schizotypal and Delusional Disorders",
               d: "F31-F39 Mood (Affective) Disorders" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Delirium and dementia are organic mental disorders, classified under ICD-10 F00-F09 (Organic, including symptomatic, mental disorders). F10-F19 covers substance use disorders." },
  { id: "pep1_mp19", section: "mphil",
    q: "What is the correct sequence of steps involved in working on self-acceptance for LGBTQ individuals?",
    options: { a: "Validation, use of expert position, awareness and exploration of sexual minority stress, therapeutic groups, connecting clients to gay affirmative resources",
               b: "Mirroring, shift from ego-dystonicity to social-dystonicity, use of expert position, therapeutic groups, awareness and exploration of sexual minority stress",
               c: "Awareness and exploration of sexual minority stress, validation, shift from ego-dystonicity to social-dystonicity, therapeutic groups, connecting clients to gay affirmative resources",
               d: "Use of expert position, validation, therapeutic groups, awareness and exploration of sexual minority stress, connecting clients to gay affirmative resources" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Self-acceptance work with LGBTQ clients proceeds by raising awareness/exploring sexual minority stress, validating the client's experience, shifting distress from ego-dystonicity to social-dystonicity (externalizing stigma), using therapeutic groups, and connecting clients to gay-affirmative resources." },
  { id: "pep1_mp20", section: "mphil",
    q: "How is normality defined in the Subjective Model of normality?",
    options: { a: "Optimal functioning",
               b: "Absence of distress, disability, or help-seeking behavior",
               c: "Absence of any psychiatric disorder or psycho pathology",
               d: "Socially acceptable behavior" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "In the subjective model, normality is defined by the absence of subjective distress, disability, or help-seeking behavior. Optimal functioning is the utopian/ideal model and absence of pathology is the medical model." },
  { id: "pep1_un01", section: "ugcnet",
    q: "Which of the following is not one of the gunas of the individual Jiva?",
    options: { a: "Rajas",
               b: "Tamas",
               c: "Sattva",
               d: "Ahimsa" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "The three gunas of Samkhya/Vedanta are sattva, rajas, and tamas. Ahimsa (non-violence) is an ethical/yogic principle, not a guna." },
  { id: "pep1_un02", section: "ugcnet",
    q: "What is the Sanskrit term for the waking state according to the Mandukya Upanishad?",
    options: { a: "Viswa",
               b: "Taijasa",
               c: "Prajna",
               d: "Turiya" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "In the Mandukya Upanishad, Vishwa (Vaishvanara) is the self in the waking state. Taijasa is the dream state, Prajna is deep sleep, and Turiya is the fourth, transcendent state." },
  { id: "pep1_un03", section: "ugcnet",
    q: "What is the principle of disinterestedness that the Gita is based on?",
    options: { a: "Bhakti yoga",
               b: "Jnana yoga",
               c: "Karma yoga",
               d: "Nishkama karma" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "The Gita's principle of disinterested action is Nishkama Karma, acting without attachment to the fruits of action. Karma yoga is the broader path, but the specific principle of disinterestedness is nishkama karma." },
  { id: "pep1_un04", section: "ugcnet",
    q: "What is the focus of Mahayana Buddhism?",
    options: { a: "Tantric rituals and practices for attaining enlightenment",
               b: "Strict asceticism and adherence to the Tripitaka",
               c: "Collective freedom from suffering and teaching the ways to enlightenment",
               d: "A belief in the voidness of both internal and external phenomena" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Mahayana ('great vehicle') Buddhism emphasizes the bodhisattva ideal: collective liberation, compassion, and helping all beings reach enlightenment, rather than individual liberation alone. Voidness (sunyata) is a doctrine but the defining focus is universal/collective liberation." },
  { id: "pep1_un05", section: "ugcnet",
    q: "Which pitaka in the Tripitaka is a rulebook for monks and nuns?",
    options: { a: "Vinaya pitaka",
               b: "Sutta pitaka",
               c: "Abhidhamma pitaka",
               d: "None of the above" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "The Vinaya Pitaka contains the disciplinary rules (code of conduct) for monks and nuns. The Sutta Pitaka holds discourses and the Abhidhamma Pitaka contains philosophical/psychological analysis." },
  { id: "pep1_un06", section: "ugcnet",
    q: "What are the three principal aspects of the human being in Sufi psychology?",
    options: { a: "Nafs, ghalb, and ruh",
               b: "Ghalb, sharia, and nafs",
               c: "Sharia, nafs, and ruh",
               d: "Ruh, sharia, and ghalb" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Sufi psychology centers on nafs (the lower self/ego), qalb (the heart), and ruh (the spirit). Sharia is the religious law, not one of the three principal aspects of the human being." },
  { id: "pep1_un07", section: "ugcnet",
    q: "What is the innermost being in the concentric system of Integral Yoga?",
    options: { a: "The psychic being",
               b: "The mind",
               c: "The vital",
               d: "The body" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "In Sri Aurobindo's Integral Yoga, the psychic being (the soul, the divine spark) is the innermost being at the center of the concentric system, surrounded by mind, vital, and body." },
  { id: "pep1_un08", section: "ugcnet",
    q: "When was the National Institute of Mental Health and Neurosciences established?",
    options: { a: "1922",
               b: "1955",
               c: "1924",
               d: "1916" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "NIMHANS, Bangalore was established in 1974 by merging the Mental Hospital and the All India Institute of Mental Health, but its institutional roots/the All India Institute of Mental Health date to 1954-55; among the given options 1955 is the intended answer (origin of the All India Institute of Mental Health)." },
  { id: "pep1_un09", section: "ugcnet",
    q: "What are the goodness or quality criteria for knowledge according to positivism?",
    options: { a: "Historical situatedness",
               b: "Internal and external validity, reliability, and objectivity",
               c: "Generalization by similarity",
               d: "Vicarious experience" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "In positivist inquiry, the goodness/quality criteria are conventional benchmarks of rigor: internal and external validity, reliability, and objectivity. Historical situatedness and vicarious experience are criteria of constructivist/critical paradigms." },
  { id: "pep1_un10", section: "ugcnet",
    q: "What is the voice in inquiry according to constructivism?",
    options: { a: "\"Disinterested scientist\" as informer of decision makers, policy makers, and change agents",
               b: "\"Transformative intellectual\" as advocate and activist",
               c: "\"Passionate participant\" a facilitator of multivoice reconstruction",
               d: "None of the above" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "In Guba and Lincoln's paradigm framework, the constructivist inquirer's voice is the 'passionate participant' who facilitates multivoice reconstruction. The 'disinterested scientist' is positivist and the 'transformative intellectual' is critical theory." },
  { id: "pep1_un11", section: "ugcnet",
    q: "What was Socrates' method of gaining knowledge?",
    options: { a: "The elenchus",
               b: "Observation and categorization",
               c: "Intuition",
               d: "Experimentation" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Socrates used the elenchus, the dialectical method of cross-examination by question and answer to expose contradictions and arrive at truth. Observation/categorization is Aristotelian and experimentation is empirical science, not Socratic." },
  { id: "pep1_un12", section: "ugcnet",
    q: "What did Descartes' dualism influence?",
    options: { a: "Behaviorism and Gestalt psychology",
               b: "Psychoanalysis and Humanistic psychology",
               c: "Existentialism and Phenomenology",
               d: "Empiricism and Rationalism" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Descartes' mind-body dualism and his mechanistic view of the body strongly shaped later behaviorism (mechanistic stimulus-response) and Gestalt psychology's concern with mind/perception. The other pairs (existentialism/phenomenology, empiricism/rationalism) are philosophical traditions rather than the psychological schools the question targets." },
  { id: "pep1_un13", section: "ugcnet",
    q: "What is the basic premise of Structuralism?",
    options: { a: "To focus on the importance of social structures and institutions in shaping human behavior.",
               b: "To study the importance of unconscious processes in shaping human behavior.",
               c: "To emphasize the importance of free will and personal agency in human behavior.",
               d: "To describe the components, combinations, and connections of consciousness." },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Titchener's structuralism analyzed conscious experience into its elements (sensations, images, feelings) and their combinations and connections via introspection. The social-structures option describes sociology, not the psychological school of structuralism." },
  { id: "pep1_un14", section: "ugcnet",
    q: "What is Sullivan's interpersonal theory?",
    options: { a: "The importance of unconscious processes in shaping human behavior",
               b: "The importance of social structures and institutions in shaping human behavior",
               c: "The study of observable behavior in social settings",
               d: "The view of personality as an open system interacting with the environment" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Harry Stack Sullivan's interpersonal theory views personality as a relatively enduring pattern formed and expressed through interpersonal relations, an open system in continuous interaction with the social environment. Unconscious emphasis is Freudian and observable-behavior framing is behaviorist." },
  { id: "pep1_un15", section: "ugcnet",
    q: "What are the five fundamental needs identified by Erich Fromm?",
    options: { a: "Safety, love, belonging, esteem, and self-actualization",
               b: "Competence, autonomy, relatedness, self-esteem, and purpose",
               c: "Hunger, thirst, shelter, rest, and reproduction",
               d: "Relatedness, transcendence, rootedness, personal identity, and consistent orientation" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Fromm proposed five distinctly human needs: relatedness, transcendence, rootedness, sense of identity, and a frame of orientation. Option a is Maslow's hierarchy and option b is self-determination theory." },
  { id: "pep1_un16", section: "ugcnet",
    q: "Who coined the term \"Mom-ism\" to describe the cultural idealization of motherhood?",
    options: { a: "Sigmund Freud",
               b: "Carl Jung",
               c: "Karen Horney",
               d: "Edward Strecker" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Psychiatrist Edward Strecker popularized 'momism' in his 1946 book Their Mothers' Sons, criticizing overprotective idealized motherhood. The term is often associated with Philip Wylie, but among these options Strecker is the psychologically-grounded coiner." },
  { id: "pep1_un17", section: "ugcnet",
    q: "Which of the following effects is characterized by a discriminatory employment pattern that keeps workers, mainly women, in the lower ranks of the job scale?",
    options: { a: "Glass ceiling effect",
               b: "Maternal wall",
               c: "Sticky floor effect",
               d: "Glass-cliff effect" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "The sticky floor effect describes a discriminatory pattern that traps workers, predominantly women, in low-rank, low-pay positions with little upward mobility. The glass ceiling, by contrast, blocks advancement near the top rather than at the bottom." },
  { id: "pep1_un18", section: "ugcnet",
    q: "How is relative poverty measured?",
    options: { a: "As the percentage of the population experiencing situational poverty",
               b: "As the percentage of the population with income less than some fixed proportion of median income",
               c: "As the percentage of the population living in rural areas",
               d: "As the percentage of the population living in urban areas" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Relative poverty is defined as having income below some fixed fraction (commonly 50-60%) of the median income of the society, capturing inequality rather than an absolute subsistence threshold." },
  { id: "pep1_un19", section: "ugcnet",
    q: "What are the three components of Deiner's tripartite model of subjective wellbeing?",
    options: { a: "Cognitive evaluation of physical wellness, presence of positive affect, and absence of negative affect",
               b: "Cognitive evaluation of spiritual wellness, presence of positive affect, and absence of negative affect",
               c: "Cognitive evaluation of life satisfaction, presence of positive affect, and absence of negative affect",
               d: "Cognitive evaluation of social wellness, presence of positive affect, and absence of negative affect" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Diener's tripartite model of subjective well-being comprises a cognitive evaluation (life satisfaction) plus high positive affect and low negative affect. The distractors substitute physical/spiritual/social wellness for life satisfaction." },
  { id: "pep1_un20", section: "ugcnet",
    q: "What is the set point theory of weight?",
    options: { a: "Stress and anxiety can disinhibit food consumption in overweight and obese individuals",
               b: "Each individual has an ideal biological weight, and efforts to lose weight may be compensated by adjustments in energy expenditure",
               c: "Obesity can spread through social networks, influenced by friends, siblings, or partners who are obese",
               d: "Dieting and weight cycling can lower metabolic rate and make it easier to regain weight" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Set-point theory holds that the body defends an individually determined biological weight, so attempts to lose weight trigger compensatory drops in energy expenditure (and metabolism) that resist change. The other options describe disinhibition, social contagion, and weight-cycling effects." },
  { id: "pep1_co01", section: "common",
    q: "What is Buber's concept of the 'I-Thou' attitude?",
    options: { a: "The experience of the person as an objective entity that can be systematized and analyzed",
               b: "Validation of the client in the totality of their being as a unique and socially embedded individual",
               c: "The breaking down of the individual into universal parts",
               d: "None of the above" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Buber's I-Thou attitude is a mutual, direct meeting that affirms the whole person as a unique being, contrasted with the I-It stance that treats the other as an object to analyze. Hence validating the client in the totality of their being best captures it." },
  { id: "pep1_co02", section: "common",
    q: "What type of information is included in AXIS III of the DSM-IV-TR?",
    options: { a: "Personality disorders and mental retardation",
               b: "Clinical psychiatric diagnosis",
               c: "General medical conditions",
               d: "Psychosocial and environmental problems" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "In the DSM-IV-TR multiaxial system, Axis III records general medical conditions relevant to the mental disorder. Axis I is clinical disorders, Axis II is personality disorders/mental retardation, and Axis IV is psychosocial/environmental problems." },
  { id: "pep1_co03", section: "common",
    q: "What is the term used to describe the inherited vulnerability?",
    options: { a: "Stressor",
               b: "Vulnerability",
               c: "Diathesis",
               d: "Enduring factor" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Diathesis denotes a predisposing, typically inherited vulnerability in the diathesis-stress model. The stressor is the precipitating environmental factor, distinct from the underlying diathesis." },
  { id: "pep1_co04", section: "common",
    q: "The greater the intensity of a stimulus, greater the probability that a learned response will be elicited. This is called",
    options: { a: "Stimulus-Intensity Dynamism",
               b: "Incentive Motivation",
               c: "Response amplitude",
               d: "Reaction threshold" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Hull's concept of stimulus-intensity dynamism (V) states that the probability/vigor of a learned response increases with the intensity of the eliciting stimulus. Incentive motivation (K) refers to reward magnitude, not stimulus intensity." },
  { id: "pep1_co05", section: "common",
    q: "The sudden increase in performance of an organism upon reinforcement, despite never being reinforced in the previous trials is _____________",
    options: { a: "Observational learning",
               b: "Discrimination learning",
               c: "Vicarious reinforcement",
               d: "Latent learning" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Latent learning (Tolman) is learning that occurs without reinforcement and is not shown in performance until reinforcement is introduced, producing a sudden jump in performance. Observational learning involves modeling, which is not what the unreinforced-trials description specifies." },
  { id: "pep1_co06", section: "common",
    q: "Cocktail party effect is better explained by",
    options: { a: "Switch model",
               b: "Attenuator model",
               c: "Automatic Vs controlled processing model",
               d: "Capacity theory" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Treisman's attenuation model best explains the cocktail party effect: unattended channels are attenuated rather than fully blocked, so salient items like one's own name can still break through. Broadbent's early-selection 'switch' filter cannot account for this leakage." },
  { id: "pep1_co07", section: "common",
    q: "Which of the following is a factor of the extent of lateral inhibition",
    options: { a: "Time difference between two lights",
               b: "Intensity of the light on inhibitory cell",
               c: "Wavelength of light wave inhibitory cell",
               d: "Frequency of light wave on inhibitory cell" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "The strength of lateral inhibition depends on the intensity (activity level) of the inhibitory neuron: a more strongly stimulated cell exerts more inhibition on its neighbors. Timing, wavelength, and frequency are not the defining determinants of inhibitory magnitude here." },
  { id: "pep1_co08", section: "common",
    q: "Lateral inhibition ________",
    options: { a: "Enhances edge perception",
               b: "Dulls edge perception",
               c: "Lowers overall brightness",
               d: "Enhances color perception" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Lateral inhibition sharpens contrast at borders by having strongly activated cells suppress their less-activated neighbors, enhancing edge perception (as in Mach bands). It does not dull edges or simply lower brightness." },
  { id: "pep1_co09", section: "common",
    q: "Very threatening or anxiety provoking material is often unable to gain access to conscious awareness. This is called",
    options: { a: "Supression",
               b: "Repression",
               c: "Inhibition",
               d: "Motivated forgetting" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Repression is the unconscious, automatic exclusion of threatening or anxiety-provoking material from awareness. Suppression, by contrast, is the conscious, deliberate effort to put something out of mind." },
  { id: "pep1_co10", section: "common",
    q: "Icon and echo are part of",
    options: { a: "long-term memory",
               b: "working memory",
               c: "short-term memory",
               d: "sensory memory" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "The iconic (visual) and echoic (auditory) stores are forms of sensory memory, the brief, high-capacity registers that hold raw sensory input before it is selected into short-term memory." },
  { id: "pep1_co11", section: "common",
    q: "REM sleep is NOT characterized by",
    options: { a: "Lack of muscle tone",
               b: "Dreams",
               c: "Rapid eye movement",
               d: "EEG synchrony" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "REM sleep features muscle atonia, vivid dreaming, and rapid eye movements, but its EEG is desynchronized (low-voltage, fast, 'paradoxical'), not synchronous. EEG synchrony characterizes slow-wave NREM sleep." },
  { id: "pep1_co12", section: "common",
    q: "The chemical that is responsible for feeling drowsy is",
    options: { a: "Adenosine",
               b: "Acetylcholine",
               c: "Serotonin",
               d: "Orexin" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Adenosine accumulates during waking and promotes drowsiness/sleep pressure (caffeine blocks adenosine receptors to keep us alert). Orexin/hypocretin promotes wakefulness rather than drowsiness." },
  { id: "pep1_co13", section: "common",
    q: "Raphe nuclei produces which neurotransmitter",
    options: { a: "Serotonin",
               b: "Norepinephrine",
               c: "Orexin",
               d: "Histamine" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "The raphe nuclei of the brainstem are the principal source of serotonergic (serotonin) projections throughout the brain. Norepinephrine comes mainly from the locus coeruleus." },
  { id: "pep1_co14", section: "common",
    q: "a pictorial display of a person's family relationships and medical history is ___________",
    options: { a: "Genogram",
               b: "Family tree",
               c: "Family history",
               d: "Gene tree" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "A genogram is a structured pictorial display of family relationships across generations together with medical/psychological history, used in family therapy and medicine. A plain family tree depicts lineage but not the relational/medical detail a genogram encodes." },
  { id: "pep1_co15", section: "common",
    q: "Low levels of which neurotransmitter leads to aggression",
    options: { a: "Serotonin",
               b: "Dopamine",
               c: "Epinephrine",
               d: "Norepinephrine" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Low serotonergic (5-HT) function is consistently linked to increased impulsive aggression and irritability. Dopamine and norepinephrine are more tied to reward and arousal than to this serotonin-deficiency aggression finding." },
  { id: "pep1_co16", section: "common",
    q: "people with blindness caused by damage to the visual cortex can recognize facial expressions of emotion even though they have no conscious awareness of looking at a person’s face. This is called ________________",
    options: { a: "Affective blindsight",
               b: "Affect neglect",
               c: "Emotional neglect",
               d: "Motivational blindsight" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Affective blindsight is the phenomenon in which patients with visual-cortex damage can discriminate emotional facial expressions without conscious visual experience, mediated by subcortical (amygdala/superior colliculus) pathways." },
  { id: "pep1_co17", section: "common",
    q: "The smallest bone in the body is",
    options: { a: "Malleus",
               b: "Incus",
               c: "Stapes",
               d: "Carpel" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "The stapes (stirrup) in the middle ear is the smallest bone in the human body. The malleus and incus are larger middle-ear ossicles, and the carpals are wrist bones." },
  { id: "pep1_co18", section: "common",
    q: "The _______ is rich in _________ and is responsible for colour and detailed vision",
    options: { a: "blindspot; cones",
               b: "fovea; rods",
               c: "fovea; cones",
               d: "blindspot; rods" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "The fovea is densely packed with cones and provides high-acuity color and detailed central vision. Rods (option b) mediate dim-light, low-acuity vision and are absent at the fovea; the blindspot has no photoreceptors." },
  { id: "pep1_co19", section: "common",
    q: "Which of the following follows the all or none law",
    options: { a: "Action potentials",
               b: "Excitatory postsynaptic potential",
               c: "Inhibitory postsynaptic potential",
               d: "Resting membrane potential" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Action potentials obey the all-or-none law: once threshold is reached the spike fires at full amplitude regardless of further stimulus strength. EPSPs and IPSPs are graded (variable) potentials, and the resting potential is a steady baseline." },
  { id: "pep1_co20", section: "common",
    q: "Schwan cells are found in",
    options: { a: "Nerves outside the skull and spine",
               b: "The brain",
               c: "The spinal cord",
               d: "The basal ganglia" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Schwann cells myelinate axons in the peripheral nervous system (nerves outside the skull and spine). Within the CNS (brain and spinal cord), myelination is performed by oligodendrocytes instead." },
  { id: "pep1_co21", section: "common",
    q: "Group of cell bodies in the PNS is called_______",
    options: { a: "nuclei",
               b: "ganglia",
               c: "tracts",
               d: "Nerves" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "In the peripheral nervous system, clusters of neuronal cell bodies are called ganglia; the equivalent clusters in the CNS are called nuclei. Tracts (CNS) and nerves (PNS) are bundles of axons, not cell bodies." },
  { id: "pep1_co22", section: "common",
    q: "Corpus callosum is a part of",
    options: { a: "Diencephalon",
               b: "Telencephalon",
               c: "Mesencephalon",
               d: "Metencephalon" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "The corpus callosum is the great white-matter commissure connecting the two cerebral hemispheres, and the cerebral hemispheres develop from the telencephalon. The diencephalon gives rise to thalamus/hypothalamus, not the callosum." },
  { id: "pep1_co23", section: "common",
    q: "Which of the following is responsible for coordinating movement",
    options: { a: "Cerebellum",
               b: "Primary motor cortex",
               c: "Medulla",
               d: "Pons" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "The cerebellum coordinates and fine-tunes movement, balance, and motor timing. The primary motor cortex initiates voluntary movement but does not coordinate it." },
  { id: "pep1_co24", section: "common",
    q: "Which is called the master gland",
    options: { a: "Pituitary",
               b: "Hypothalamus",
               c: "Thalamus",
               d: "Pineal gland" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "The pituitary is classically called the master gland because its hormones regulate many other endocrine glands. The hypothalamus actually controls the pituitary but is conventionally not the textbook 'master gland' answer." },
  { id: "pep1_co25", section: "common",
    q: "The posterior pituitary produces",
    options: { a: "oxytocin and vasopressin",
               b: "oxytocin and orexin",
               c: "vasopressin and vasodialators",
               d: "vasopressin and norepinephrine" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "The posterior pituitary (neurohypophysis) stores and releases oxytocin and vasopressin (antidiuretic hormone), both synthesized in the hypothalamus. Orexin and norepinephrine are not posterior pituitary hormones." },
  { id: "pep1_co26", section: "common",
    q: "Which of the following are directly involved in triggering eating",
    options: { a: "Leptin",
               b: "CART",
               c: "Cholecystokinin",
               d: "Neuropeptide Y" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Neuropeptide Y is one of the most potent orexigenic (hunger-triggering) signals, stimulating feeding. Leptin, CART, and cholecystokinin are anorexigenic (satiety) signals that suppress eating." },
  { id: "pep1_co27", section: "common",
    q: "What new method did Wechsler invent for obtaining the IQ score in the Wechsler-Bellevue Intelligence Scales?",
    options: { a: "Standard IQ",
               b: "Age-relative formula IQ",
               c: "Mental age IQ",
               d: "Physical age IQ" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Wechsler introduced the deviation IQ (a standard score, mean 100 SD 15) comparing a person to same-age peers, replacing the Stanford-Binet mental-age ratio IQ. This is the 'Standard IQ' approach." },
  { id: "pep1_co28", section: "common",
    q: "Which test can diagnose if an examinee consistently makes more errors on one side of the body than the other?",
    options: { a: "Finger Localization Test",
               b: "Continuous Performance Test",
               c: "Sustained Attention Test",
               d: "The Test of Everyday Attention" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "The Finger Localization Test assesses tactile/sensory localization on each hand, so lateralized error patterns (more errors on one side) point to contralateral hemispheric dysfunction. The attention tests listed measure sustained/everyday attention, not lateralized sensory error." },
  { id: "pep1_co29", section: "common",
    q: "What is NOT an example of executive function?",
    options: { a: "Conceptualization",
               b: "Planning",
               c: "Logical analysis",
               d: "Auditory processing" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Executive functions include conceptualization, planning, and logical analysis (higher-order control processes). Auditory processing is a basic perceptual/sensory function, not an executive function." },
  { id: "pep1_co30", section: "common",
    q: "What is the purpose of the STAI?",
    options: { a: "To measure normal and abnormal personality",
               b: "To assess the validity of an examinee's responses",
               c: "To differentiate between state and trait anxiety",
               d: "To measure psychoticism, extraversion, and neuroticism" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "The State-Trait Anxiety Inventory (Spielberger) was designed to distinguish transient state anxiety from stable trait anxiety. Measuring P-E-N describes the EPQ, not the STAI." },
  { id: "pep1_co31", section: "common",
    q: "Which strategy did Comrey pursue in developing his 180-item test?",
    options: { a: "Correlational",
               b: "Criterion-referencing",
               c: "Factor-analytic",
               d: "Experimental" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Comrey developed the Comrey Personality Scales using a factor-analytic strategy, deriving eight personality factors. The MMPI used criterion-keying and Cattell/Comrey style work here is factor-analytic." },
  { id: "pep1_co32", section: "common",
    q: "The tendency to explain others’ actions as stemming from dispositions even in the presence of situational causes is",
    options: { a: "belief bias",
               b: "self-serving bias",
               c: "correspondence bias",
               d: "confirmation bias" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Correspondence bias (also called the fundamental attribution error) is the tendency to attribute others' behavior to dispositions while underweighting situational causes. Self-serving bias concerns attributions about one's own outcomes." },
  { id: "pep1_co33", section: "common",
    q: "Which of the following is false about social loafing",
    options: { a: "People in groups loaf less when the task is challenging, appealing, or involving",
               b: "Group members will work hard when convinced that high effort will bring rewards",
               c: "Groups loaf less when evaluation apprehension is absent",
               d: "Groups also loaf less when their members are friends" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "The false statement is that groups loaf less when evaluation apprehension is absent; in fact loafing decreases when evaluation apprehension is present (individual contributions are identifiable). The other three statements are true conditions that reduce social loafing." },
  { id: "pep1_co34", section: "common",
    q: "Persuasion though systematic processing is the __________________ and  involves _________________",
    options: { a: "central route; careful attention",
               b: "peripheral route; mental shortcuts",
               c: "central route; mental shortcuts",
               d: "peripheral route; careful attention" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Systematic processing corresponds to the central route to persuasion, which involves careful, effortful attention to the quality of arguments. Heuristic processing is the peripheral route using mental shortcuts." },
  { id: "pep1_co35", section: "common",
    q: "Trivialization and self-affirmation can reduce",
    options: { a: "Cognitive dissonance",
               b: "Compliance",
               c: "Reactance",
               d: "Self-regulation" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Trivialization (downplaying the importance of inconsistent cognitions) and self-affirmation (affirming valued aspects of the self) are recognized strategies for reducing cognitive dissonance. They are not about compliance or reactance." },
  { id: "pep1_co36", section: "common",
    q: "What are the three components of empathy?",
    options: { a: "Emphatic concern, Emotional aspect, Positivity",
               b: "Emotional aspect, Cognitive component, Behavioural aspect",
               c: "Emotional aspect, Cognitive component, Emphatic concern",
               d: "Behavioural aspect, Cognitive component, Emphatic concern" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Empathy is commonly described with three components: an emotional/affective aspect, a cognitive component (perspective-taking), and a behavioral aspect (helping response). Empathic concern is a sub-facet rather than one of the three structural components in this framing." },
  { id: "pep1_co37", section: "common",
    q: "__________ is the tendency for an individual surrounded by a group of strangers to hesitate and not help because none of the bystanders knows for sure what is happening",
    options: { a: "bystander effect",
               b: "pluralistic ignorance",
               c: "diffusion of responsibility",
               d: "amotivation to help" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Pluralistic ignorance is the state in which each bystander, seeing others remain calm, concludes nothing is wrong because no one is sure what is happening. Diffusion of responsibility is the separate belief that someone else will act." },
  { id: "pep1_co38", section: "common",
    q: "choose the correct arrangement of events in group formation",
    options: { a: "forming >>> storming >>> adjourning >>> norming >>> performing",
               b: "forming >>> storming >>> norming >>> performing >>> adjourning",
               c: "forming >>> performing >>> norming >>> storming >>> adjourning",
               d: "forming >>> norming >>> stroming >>> adjourning >>> performing" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Tuckman's stages of group development run forming, storming, norming, performing, and adjourning. Only option b lists them in this correct order." },
  { id: "pep1_co39", section: "common",
    q: "The phenomenon in which groups make more extreme decisions than as individuals is called",
    options: { a: "group polarization",
               b: "Extremity",
               c: "groupthink",
               d: "conformity" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Group polarization is the tendency for group discussion to strengthen members' initial leanings, producing decisions more extreme than individual ones. Groupthink concerns flawed consensus-seeking, not extremity per se." },
  { id: "pep1_co40", section: "common",
    q: "The research method that writes about people and cultures is called",
    options: { a: "narrative analysis",
               b: "ethnography",
               c: "phenomenology",
               d: "grounded theory" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Ethnography is the qualitative method focused on describing and interpreting the shared patterns of a culture or group. Phenomenology studies lived experience and grounded theory builds theory from data." },
  { id: "pep1_co41", section: "common",
    q: "Which of the sampling methods involves a two stage method",
    options: { a: "Proportionate Stratified Random sampling",
               b: "Disproportionate Stratified Random sampling",
               c: "Cluster sampling",
               d: "Quota sampling" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Cluster sampling is typically a two-stage (or multistage) method: first sample clusters, then sample units within selected clusters. Stratified and quota sampling do not inherently require two stages." },
  { id: "pep1_co42", section: "common",
    q: "A t-distribution is a similar to a normal distribution in the way, where ______________ .",
    options: { a: "none of the above",
               b: "the symmetry is maintained",
               c: "the kurtosis is the same",
               d: "the variability is the same" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Like the normal distribution, the t-distribution is symmetric and bell-shaped about its mean. It differs in having heavier tails (greater kurtosis) and larger variability, so only symmetry is shared." },
  { id: "pep1_co43", section: "common",
    q: "the df for 'within groups' in one way ANOVA with three levels on intelligence and 10 samples per level is",
    options: { a: "29",
               b: "27",
               c: "30",
               d: "31" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Within-groups df = N - k = 30 - 3 = 27 (three levels, 10 per level gives N=30, k=3). The value 29 would be total df and 30 is N." },
  { id: "pep1_co44", section: "common",
    q: "What is the degrees of freedom on an independent samples t-test with a total sample size of 25 in each group.",
    options: { a: "48",
               b: "24",
               c: "49",
               d: "25" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "With 25 per group, total N = 50, so independent-samples t df = N - 2 = 48. Twenty-four would be df for a single group of 25." },
  { id: "pep1_co45", section: "common",
    q: "F ratio in one-way ANOVA is given by",
    options: { a: "MS between/ MS within",
               b: "MS total/ MS within",
               c: "MS within/ MS total",
               d: "MS within/ MS between" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "In one-way ANOVA the F ratio is mean square between groups divided by mean square within groups (MS between / MS within), comparing systematic variance to error variance." },
  { id: "pep1_co46", section: "common",
    q: "The most appropriate non-parametric alternative for a related samples t-test when using an ordinal level of variable with some magnitude is",
    options: { a: "Mann-Whitney test",
               b: "Wilcoxon matched-pairs signed rank test",
               c: "Chi-square test",
               d: "Sign test" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "For two related (matched) samples on ordinal data with information about magnitude of differences, the Wilcoxon matched-pairs signed-rank test is the appropriate nonparametric alternative to the paired t-test. The sign test ignores magnitude and Mann-Whitney is for independent samples." },
  { id: "pep1_co47", section: "common",
    q: "According to Wallas' model of the creative process, what is the second stage?",
    options: { a: "Illumination",
               b: "Incubation",
               c: "Verification",
               d: "Preparation" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Wallas' four stages are preparation, incubation, illumination, and verification, so the second stage is incubation. Preparation is first and illumination is third." },
  { id: "pep1_co48", section: "common",
    q: "What is the illumination stage of Wallas' model of the creative process?",
    options: { a: "Formulating the problem and making initial attempts to solve it",
               b: "Testing and/or carrying out the solution",
               c: "Leaving the problem while considering other things",
               d: "Achieving insight to the problem" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "In Wallas' model the illumination stage is the moment of sudden insight or the 'aha' when the solution appears in consciousness. Leaving the problem aside is incubation and testing the solution is verification." },
  { id: "pep1_co49", section: "common",
    q: "According to Sternberg's investment theory, how many distinct but interrelated resources are required for creativity?",
    options: { a: "5",
               b: "4",
               c: "7",
               d: "6" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Sternberg and Lubart's investment theory of creativity specifies six distinct but interrelated resources: intellectual abilities, knowledge, thinking styles, personality, motivation, and environment." },
  { id: "pep1_co50", section: "common",
    q: "What is the difference between functional fixedness and mental set?",
    options: { a: "Functional fixedness refers to problem-solving strategies, while mental set refers to the way we think about physical objects",
               b: "There is no difference between functional fixedness and mental set",
               c: "Functional fixedness refers to the way we think about physical objects, while mental set refers to problem-solving strategies",
               d: "Functional fixedness occurs when our top-down processing is overactive, while mental set occurs when we apply problem-solving strategies too rigidly" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Functional fixedness is the tendency to see objects only in terms of their usual function (about physical objects), whereas mental set is the tendency to persist with previously successful problem-solving strategies. Option c states this distinction correctly." },
  { id: "pep1_co51", section: "common",
    q: "Why does functional fixedness occur?",
    options: { a: "Because we lack problem-solving skills",
               b: "Because our top-down processing is overactive",
               c: "Because we underestimate the importance of the environment in problem-solving",
               d: "Because we have limited access to tools and objects" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Functional fixedness is a mental set in which prior experience and learned knowledge about an object's typical use (top-down, schema-driven processing) blocks us from seeing novel uses. It reflects overactive top-down processing, not a lack of skill or tools." },
  { id: "pep1_co52", section: "common",
    q: "What are heuristics in problem-solving?",
    options: { a: "A general rule that is usually right",
               b: "A method that will always produce a solution to the problem",
               c: "A method that is better than algorithms",
               d: "A method that is more accurate than algorithms" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "A heuristic is a mental shortcut or rule of thumb that is usually (but not always) correct, allowing fast solutions. This distinguishes it from an algorithm, which guarantees a correct solution but is slower; so 'always produces a solution' describes an algorithm, not a heuristic." },
  { id: "pep1_co53", section: "common",
    q: "What is the hill-climbing heuristic in problem-solving?",
    options: { a: "Employing a solution to a similar, earlier problem to help solve a new problem",
               b: "Breaking down a problem into smaller parts and solving each part separately",
               c: "Trying different solutions until one works",
               d: "Choosing the option that moves you closer to the goal state" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Hill-climbing is a heuristic in which at each step you select the move that brings you closest to the goal state. Breaking a problem into parts is means-ends/subgoaling, and using a prior similar problem is analogy." },
  { id: "pep1_co54", section: "common",
    q: "What are the three aspects of intelligence in Sternberg's Triarchic Theory?",
    options: { a: "Linguistic, logical-mathematical, and spatial",
               b: "Componential, experiential, and contextual",
               c: "Social, emotional, and spiritual",
               d: "Physical, cognitive, and emotional" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Sternberg's Triarchic Theory comprises componential (analytical), experiential (creative), and contextual (practical) intelligence. The linguistic/logical/spatial set belongs to Gardner's multiple intelligences." },
  { id: "pep1_co55", section: "common",
    q: "What are the five major parts of emotional intelligence, according to Goleman?",
    options: { a: "Interpersonal skills, intrapersonal skills, social skills, cognitive skills, and communication skills",
               b: "Knowing our own emotions, managing our emotions, motivating ourselves, recognizing the emotions of others, and handling relationships",
               c: "Self-awareness, self-regulation, self-motivation, empathy, and social skills",
               d: "Intelligence quotient, emotional quotient, social quotient, cultural quotient, and spiritual quotient" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Goleman's original five domains of emotional intelligence are: knowing one's own emotions, managing emotions, motivating oneself, recognizing others' emotions (empathy), and handling relationships. Option c (self-awareness, self-regulation, motivation, empathy, social skills) is the later relabeling, but the question's classic phrasing matches option b verbatim." },
  { id: "pep1_co56", section: "common",
    q: "What are the two basic mental operations that Piaget hypothesized to be involved in learning?",
    options: { a: "Encoding and retrieval",
               b: "Attention and perception",
               c: "Assimilation and accommodation",
               d: "Sensory input and motor output" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Piaget proposed assimilation (fitting new information into existing schemas) and accommodation (modifying schemas to fit new information) as the two complementary processes underlying learning and adaptation." },
  { id: "pep1_co57", section: "common",
    q: "What are parapraxes, according to Freud's theory?",
    options: { a: "Psychological errors in speaking or writing that are sexual in nature",
               b: "Mistakes that reveal the unconscious",
               c: "Good, or correct, behaviors for which children have been praised",
               d: "Behaviors for which children are punished that form the conscience" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Parapraxes are everyday errors of speech, memory, or action (slips of the tongue, misplacements) that Freud held to reveal repressed unconscious material. They are not specifically sexual in content." },
  { id: "pep1_co58", section: "common",
    q: "What are Freudian slips, according to Freud's theory?",
    options: { a: "Mistakes that reveal the unconscious",
               b: "Psychological errors in speaking or writing that are sexual in nature",
               c: "Good, or correct, behaviors for which children have been praised",
               d: "Behaviors for which children are punished that form the conscience" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "A Freudian slip is the popular name for a parapraxis: an error that betrays an unconscious wish or thought. It is not by definition sexual; it is any mistake that reveals the unconscious." },
  { id: "pep1_co59", section: "common",
    q: "What is the phallic stage of development, according to Freud's theory?",
    options: { a: "The stage during which the mouth is the primary erogenous zone",
               b: "The stage during which toilet training interferes with gratification received from defecation",
               c: "The stage during which incestuous fantasies occur",
               d: "The stage during which the sex instinct is sublimated" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "In the phallic stage (about 3-6 years) the genitals are the erogenous zone and the Oedipus/Electra complex produces incestuous fantasies toward the opposite-sex parent. The mouth describes the oral stage, defecation the anal stage, and sublimation the latency stage." },
  { id: "pep1_co60", section: "common",
    q: "What are the two parts of the superego, according to Freud's theory?",
    options: { a: "The id and the ego",
               b: "The pleasure principle and the reality principle",
               c: "The ego and the unconscious",
               d: "The conscience and the ego-ideal" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Freud divided the superego into the conscience (internalized prohibitions, producing guilt) and the ego-ideal (internalized standards of approved behavior). The id and ego are separate structures, not parts of the superego." },
  { id: "pep1_co61", section: "common",
    q: "What is the anima, according to Jung's theory of archetypes?",
    options: { a: "The male element of a woman",
               b: "The female element of a man",
               c: "The socially acceptable front that we present to others",
               d: "The dark and unacceptable side of personality" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "In Jung's archetypes the anima is the feminine element within a man; the animus is the masculine element within a woman. The persona is the social front and the shadow is the dark side." },
  { id: "pep1_co62", section: "common",
    q: "What are the two attitudes in Jung's theory of personality?",
    options: { a: "Rational and irrational",
               b: "Sensing and intuiting",
               c: "Extroversion and introversion",
               d: "Thinking and feeling" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Jung's two fundamental attitudes (orientations of psychic energy) are extraversion and introversion. Thinking/feeling and sensing/intuiting are his four functions, not the attitudes." },
  { id: "pep1_co63", section: "common",
    q: "Which of the following is the correct sequence of Erikson's stages of psychosocial development?",
    options: { a: "Basic trust versus mistrust, autonomy versus shame, identity versus role confusion, generativity versus stagnation",
               b: "Autonomy versus shame, industry versus inferiority, identity versus role confusion, generativity versus stagnation",
               c: "Initiative versus guilt, industry versus inferiority, identity versus role confusion, ego integrity versus despair",
               d: "Basic trust versus mistrust, initiative versus guilt, intimacy versus isolation, ego integrity versus despair" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Erikson's stages run trust vs mistrust, autonomy vs shame, initiative vs guilt, industry vs inferiority, identity vs role confusion, intimacy vs isolation, generativity vs stagnation, integrity vs despair. Only option a lists stages in correct chronological order without out-of-sequence entries." },
  { id: "pep1_co64", section: "common",
    q: "What are the four processes involved in observational learning, according to Bandura's social learning theory?",
    options: { a: "Sensory, perceptional, cognitive, affective",
               b: "Attentional, retention, production, motivational",
               c: "Encoding, storage, retrieval, evaluation",
               d: "Stimulus, response, reinforcement, extinction" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Bandura's four sub-processes of observational learning are attention, retention, (motor) reproduction/production, and motivation. Stimulus-response-reinforcement is operant conditioning, not modeling." },
  { id: "pep1_co65", section: "common",
    q: "What are cardinal traits in Allport's theory?",
    options: { a: "The least important traits, which a person may display inconspicuously and inconsistently",
               b: "The handful of outstanding traits that describe a person's behavior",
               c: "The most pervasive and powerful human traits",
               d: "Traits that are shared by a number of people, such as the members of a culture" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "In Allport's hierarchy, a cardinal trait is a single dominant, pervasive trait that organizes a person's whole life (rare). Central traits are the handful of describing traits and secondary traits are the minor, inconsistent ones." },
  { id: "pep1_co66", section: "common",
    q: "What are L-data in Cattell's theory?",
    options: { a: "Data gathered from self-reports and questionnaires",
               b: "Data collected by placing a person into some controlled test situation and noting or rating responses",
               c: "Information gathered about a person's life, such as from school records",
               d: "The 16 basic personality traits proposed by Cattell" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Cattell's L-data (life-record data) come from observable life history, such as school or employment records. Q-data come from questionnaires/self-reports and T-data from objective test situations." },
  { id: "pep1_co67", section: "common",
    q: "What is the need for affiliation (n Aff) in Murray's theory?",
    options: { a: "The need for social interaction and companionship",
               b: "The need for power and control over others",
               c: "The need for recognition and success in one's endeavors",
               d: "The need for attention and excitement" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "In Murray's taxonomy, the need for affiliation (n Aff) is the need for friendly social contact, companionship, and belonging. Power is n Power, achievement is n Achievement." },
  { id: "pep1_co68", section: "common",
    q: "What are the five needs in Maslow's hierarchy of needs, arranged in order from strongest at the bottom to weakest at the top?",
    options: { a: "Esteem, belongingness and love, safety, physiological, self-actualization",
               b: "Physiological, safety, belongingness and love, esteem, self-actualization",
               c: "Safety, physiological, esteem, belongingness and love, self-actualization",
               d: "Self-actualization, esteem, belongingness and love, safety, physiological" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Maslow's hierarchy from strongest/most basic to higher-order is physiological, safety, belongingness and love, esteem, then self-actualization. Option d gives the reverse order and option b matches the requested bottom-to-top arrangement." },
  { id: "pep1_co69", section: "common",
    q: "What is learned helplessness, according to Seligman?",
    options: { a: "A situation in which repeated exposure to unavoidable punishment leads an organism to accept later punishment even when it is avoidable",
               b: "A situation in which an organism learns to escape punishment through trial and error",
               c: "A situation in which an organism learns to avoid punishment through reinforcement",
               d: "A situation in which an organism becomes aggressive in response to punishment" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Seligman's learned helplessness is the passivity that develops after exposure to uncontrollable aversive events, such that the organism fails to act even when escape later becomes possible. The other options describe successful escape/avoidance learning." },
  { id: "pep1_co70", section: "common",
    q: "At which level of moral reasoning do people act under external controls and obey rules to avoid punishment or reap rewards?",
    options: { a: "Level I: Preconventional morality",
               b: "Level II: Conventional morality",
               c: "Level III: Postconventional morality",
               d: "None of the above" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "At Kohlberg's preconventional level (Level I) moral judgments are governed by external consequences, obeying rules to avoid punishment or gain rewards. The conventional level centers on social approval and law, postconventional on abstract principles." },
  { id: "pep1_co71", section: "common",
    q: "At which stage of Level II of Kohlberg’s moral development are people concerned with doing their duty, showing respect for higher authority, and maintaining the social order?",
    options: { a: "Maintaining mutual relations, approval of others, the golden rule",
               b: "Social concern and conscience",
               c: "Morality of contract, of individual rights, and of democratically accepted law",
               d: "Morality of universal ethical principles" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Level II conventional morality has two stages; Stage 4 (law-and-order, here labeled 'social concern and conscience') concerns doing one's duty, respecting authority, and maintaining social order. The golden rule/approval option is Stage 3, while contract/rights and universal principles are postconventional." },
  { id: "pep1_co72", section: "common",
    q: "How does Vygotsky's theory differ from Piaget's theory of cognitive development?",
    options: { a: "Vygotsky believed that all children progress through the same stages of cognitive growth, while Piaget did not.",
               b: "Vygotsky believed that cognitive development is solely influenced by the child's environment, while Piaget believed it was influenced by both nature and nurture.",
               c: "Vygotsky believed that new skills children master through interactions with more competent people are often specific to their culture, while Piaget believed they were universal cognitive structures.",
               d: "Vygotsky believed that cognitive development is a predetermined process, while Piaget believed it was a flexible process that can be influenced by the child's experiences." },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Vygotsky emphasized that skills acquired through social interaction are culturally specific (sociocultural theory), whereas Piaget posited universal, invariant cognitive structures and stages. Option c captures this core distinction." },
  { id: "pep1_co73", section: "common",
    q: "What is the zone of proximal development (ZPD)?",
    options: { a: "The range of tasks that are too simple to be mastered alone.",
               b: "The range of tasks that are too complex to be mastered alone but can be accomplished with guidance and encouragement from a more skillful partner.",
               c: "The range of tasks that are too complex to be mastered even with guidance and encouragement from a more skillful partner.",
               d: "The range of tasks that are learned through experience, not instruction." },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "The ZPD is the gap between what a learner can do alone and what they can achieve with guidance from a more skilled partner: tasks too hard alone but attainable with help (scaffolding)." },
  { id: "pep1_co74", section: "common",
    q: "What is the first grasp that infants develop?",
    options: { a: "Palmar grasp",
               b: "Pincer grasp",
               c: "Ulnar grasp",
               d: "Radial grasp" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Infants first use the ulnar grasp, a crude grip pressing the object against the palm with the outer (little-finger) side of the hand, before progressing to palmar and finally the precise pincer grasp." },
  { id: "pep1_co75", section: "common",
    q: "What is the clumsy, claw-like grip that that permits little tactile exploration?",
    options: { a: "Pincer grasp",
               b: "Ulnar grasp",
               c: "Radial grasp",
               d: "Digital grasp" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "The ulnar grasp is the clumsy, claw-like grip in which the fingers close against the palm without thumb involvement, permitting little tactile exploration. The pincer grasp, by contrast, is the precise thumb-finger grip that develops later." },
  { id: "pep1_co76", section: "common",
    q: "What is the language-making capacity (LMC) proposed by Dan Slobin?",
    options: { a: "An inborn linguistic processor that is activated by verbal input",
               b: "Knowledge of rules that are common to all languages",
               c: "A set of cognitive and perceptual abilities that are highly specialized for language learning",
               d: "All of the above" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Slobin's Language-Making Capacity (LMC) is a set of specialized cognitive and perceptual abilities (operating principles) that children bring to language learning, a softer alternative to Chomsky's innate grammar-specific LAD. It is not literally an inborn grammar module or knowledge of universal rules." },
  { id: "pep1_co77", section: "common",
    q: "What is the receptive-expressive gap ?",
    options: { a: "The gap between what infants can understand and what they can produce in terms of language",
               b: "The gap between infants' receptive and expressive cognitive abilities",
               c: "The gap between infants' receptive and expressive motor skills",
               d: "The gap between infants' receptive and expressive social skills" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "The receptive-expressive gap is the lag in early development between language comprehension (receptive) and language production (expressive): infants understand far more words than they can say." },
  { id: "pep1_co78", section: "common",
    q: "What are the four skill modules of DBT?",
    options: { a: "Mindfulness, interpersonal effectiveness, cognitive restructuring, and relaxation",
               b: "Mindfulness, interpersonal effectiveness, emotional regulation, and distress tolerance",
               c: "Mindfulness, cognitive restructuring, emotional regulation, and relaxation",
               d: "Interpersonal effectiveness, emotional regulation, cognitive restructuring, and distress tolerance" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Linehan's DBT comprises four skills modules: mindfulness, interpersonal effectiveness, emotion regulation, and distress tolerance. Cognitive restructuring and relaxation are not DBT modules." },
  { id: "pep1_co79", section: "common",
    q: "Which therapeutic technique involves asking a person to play the role of an exhibitionist even if they claim to suffer from severe inhibitions and excessive timidity?",
    options: { a: "The Reversal Exercise",
               b: "The Exaggeration Exercise",
               c: "The Rehearsal Exercise",
               d: "The Future Projection Technique" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "In Gestalt therapy the exaggeration exercise asks a client to amplify or act out the opposite of a stated trait (e.g., playing an exhibitionist when claiming extreme timidity) to bring suppressed material into awareness. The reversal technique is the closest distractor but specifically targets reversing one's overt behavior pattern." },
  { id: "pep1_co80", section: "common",
    q: "Which type of therapeutic technique in REBT involves using imagery?",
    options: { a: "Cognitive methods",
               b: "Emotive techniques",
               c: "Behavioral techniques",
               d: "Psychoeducational methods" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "In Ellis's REBT, imagery-based methods such as rational emotive imagery fall under the emotive (experiential) techniques, which target feelings. Cognitive methods involve disputing beliefs and behavioral methods involve action assignments." },
],
  "pep-mock-2": [
  { id: "pep2_mp01", section: "mphil",
    q: "How many General Principles are included in the APA's Ethical Principles of Psychologists and Code of Conduct?",
    options: { a: "One",
               b: "Three",
               c: "Five",
               d: "Seven" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "The APA Ethical Principles of Psychologists and Code of Conduct lists five aspirational General Principles: A) Beneficence and Nonmaleficence, B) Fidelity and Responsibility, C) Integrity, D) Justice, and E) Respect for People's Rights and Dignity. These are aspirational, unlike the enforceable Standards." },
  { id: "pep2_mp02", section: "mphil",
    q: "According to the United Nations Convention on the Rights of the Child, what are children capable of?",
    options: { a: "Understanding complex legal terminology",
               b: "Providing consent for psychotherapy",
               c: "Giving voice to assent or dissent",
               d: "Making important life decisions" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "The UN Convention on the Rights of the Child recognises that children are capable of forming and giving voice to their own views, i.e. expressing assent or dissent, and that these views must be given due weight. It does not assume legal competence to consent independently." },
  { id: "pep2_mp03", section: "mphil",
    q: "Under what circumstances therapists may accept gifts?",
    options: { a: "Without any specific reason",
               b: "Only on festive occasions",
               c: "On the assertion that it would help promote client’s self-acceptance",
               d: "In exchange for reducing session fees" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "In therapeutic ethics, accepting a gift is generally discouraged, but may be acceptable when refusing it would harm the alliance and accepting it serves the client's growth, e.g. on the rationale that it promotes the client's self-acceptance. The decision is clinically, not socially, driven." },
  { id: "pep2_mp04", section: "mphil",
    q: "What is the impact of the Criminal Law Amendment Act on the age of statutory rape in India?",
    options: { a: "It lowered the age of statutory rape from 18 to 16 years.",
               b: "It increased the age of statutory rape from 16 to 18 years.",
               c: "It criminalized all consensual sex among young people.",
               d: "It did not have any impact on the age of statutory rape." },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "The Criminal Law (Amendment) Act, 2013 in India raised the age of consent from 16 to 18 years, so sexual activity with anyone below 18 is statutory rape regardless of consent. This aligned the age with the POCSO Act, 2012." },
  { id: "pep2_mp05", section: "mphil",
    q: "What does ethical competency refer to?",
    options: { a: "Knowledge and understanding of ethics codes",
               b: "Ability to recognise, analyse and resolve ethical situations",
               c: "Responsibility for one's own actions",
               d: "All of the above" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Ethical competency is multi-dimensional: it includes knowledge of ethics codes, the ability to recognise, analyse and resolve ethical dilemmas, and taking responsibility for one's own conduct. Hence all of the listed elements apply." },
  { id: "pep2_mp06", section: "mphil",
    q: "According to Immanuel Kant's deontological approach, what are actions judged by?",
    options: { a: "Their consequences",
               b: "Their intrinsic goodness or badness",
               c: "Cost-benefit analysis",
               d: "None of the above" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Kant's deontological (duty-based) ethics judges actions by their intrinsic rightness or wrongness and the maxim behind them, not by their consequences. This contrasts with consequentialism/utilitarianism, which judges by outcomes." },
  { id: "pep2_mp07", section: "mphil",
    q: "What is a potential consequence of poor awareness of the culture and prevalent familial practices in family therapy?",
    options: { a: "Wrong assessments and misinterpretations",
               b: "Accurate understanding of the family's dynamics",
               c: "Effective communication with the family",
               d: "Development of a strong therapeutic alliance" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Without awareness of a family's culture and customary practices, a family therapist is likely to make wrong assessments and misinterpret normal culturally-bound behaviour as pathology, damaging rather than building the alliance." },
  { id: "pep2_mp08", section: "mphil",
    q: "Which type of psychotherapy is NOT commonly used to treat survivors of sexual assault?",
    options: { a: "Interpersonal psychotherapy",
               b: "Psychodynamic psychotherapy",
               c: "Eye movement desensitization and reprocessing therapy (EMDR)",
               d: "Trauma-focused cognitive-behavioral therapy (TF-CBT)" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "TF-CBT, EMDR and psychodynamic therapy are all established treatments for sexual-assault survivors. Interpersonal psychotherapy (IPT) targets interpersonal/depressive problems and is not a standard trauma treatment, so it is the one not commonly used here." },
  { id: "pep2_mp09", section: "mphil",
    q: "What is bilateral stimulation in EMDR?",
    options: { a: "The use of physical touch and thoughts to stimulate the patient",
               b: "The back and forth eye movements while focusing on a disturbing memory",
               c: "The use of medication and behavioral techniques to treat anxiety",
               d: "The use of hypnosis and relaxation to access repressed memories" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "In EMDR, bilateral stimulation is the back-and-forth eye movement (or alternating taps/tones) the client follows while holding a disturbing memory in mind, which is thought to facilitate adaptive reprocessing of the trauma." },
  { id: "pep2_mp10", section: "mphil",
    q: "What do researchers mean by pathologization and depathologization of homosexuality?",
    options: { a: "None of the above",
               b: "The classification of homosexuality as a mental disorder and the removal of this classification",
               c: "The changing of sexual orientation from homosexual to heterosexual",
               d: "The process of coming out as a gay or lesbian individual" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Pathologization refers to classifying homosexuality as a mental disorder (as the DSM did until 1973), and depathologization refers to removing that classification. It is about the diagnostic status, not changing or disclosing orientation." },
  { id: "pep2_mp11", section: "mphil",
    q: "What are some defining features of minority stress?",
    options: { a: "It is a temporary experience that can be easily overcome with positive thinking.",
               b: "It is chronic and related to relatively stable underlying social and cultural structures.",
               c: "It stems from individual characteristics and personal choices.",
               d: "It is not related to social processes, institutions, and structures." },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Minority stress (Meyer) is chronic, socially based stress that arises from relatively stable social and cultural structures (stigma, prejudice, discrimination) directed at a stigmatised group, not a transient individual problem." },
  { id: "pep2_mp12", section: "mphil",
    q: "Who is credited with coining the term \"clinical psychology\"?",
    options: { a: "Sigmund Freud",
               b: "Carl Rogers",
               c: "Lightner Witmer",
               d: "F. Skinner" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Lightner Witmer coined the term 'clinical psychology' and opened the first psychological clinic at the University of Pennsylvania in 1896, making him the founder of the field." },
  { id: "pep2_mp13", section: "mphil",
    q: "What is countertransference in therapy?",
    options: { a: "The fear of the unknown, lack of motivation, or feeling overwhelmed",
               b: "The need for therapists to maintain appropriate boundaries with their clients",
               c: "When therapists experience similar feelings towards the client",
               d: "When clients unconsciously transfer feelings or attitudes towards significant people in their lives onto the therapist" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Countertransference is the therapist's own emotional reaction to the client, in which the therapist experiences feelings toward the client (often rooted in the therapist's own history). Transference is the reverse, the client projecting onto the therapist (option D)." },
  { id: "pep2_mp14", section: "mphil",
    q: "Which biological system is implicated in obsessive-compulsive disorder?",
    options: { a: "The central 5-HT system",
               b: "The central GABAergic system",
               c: "The central glutamatergic system",
               d: "The central dopaminergic system" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "OCD is most strongly linked to dysregulation of the central serotonergic (5-HT) system, which is why SSRIs are first-line pharmacotherapy. Glutamatergic and dopaminergic systems are implicated secondarily." },
  { id: "pep2_mp15", section: "mphil",
    q: "What are the clinical features of conversion disorder?",
    options: { a: "Physical examination and investigations reveal no abnormalities explaining symptoms.",
               b: "Affect motor/sensory function, sudden onset, and develop with significant psychosocial stressor(s).",
               c: "Suggest medical/neurological disorder, sudden onset, and develop with significant psychosocial stressor(s).",
               d: "Intentional symptom production, no clear temporal relationship between stressor and symptoms, and no secondary gain." },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Conversion (functional neurological) disorder involves loss or alteration of voluntary motor or sensory function, with sudden onset typically following a significant psychosocial stressor, and symptoms are not intentionally produced (distinguishing it from factitious/malingering)." },
  { id: "pep2_mp16", section: "mphil",
    q: "What is the characteristic belief associated with Koro?",
    options: { a: "The affected person believes that their body is being possessed by evil spirits.",
               b: "The affected person experiences a sense of impending doom associated with atmospheric changes.",
               c: "The affected person believes that their genitalia are being retracted into their body and may result in death.",
               d: "The affected person believes that they are being transformed into a mythical creature." },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Koro is a culture-bound syndrome (South/East Asia) marked by the terrifying belief that one's genitals are retracting into the body and that this will cause death, triggering acute anxiety." },
  { id: "pep2_mp17", section: "mphil",
    q: "What is the characteristic feature of Borderline Personality Disorder?",
    options: { a: "Social inhibition, fear of rejection, desire for acceptance",
               b: "Excessive attention-seeking, emotional volatility, dramatic behavior",
               c: "Disregard for others' rights, manipulative behavior",
               d: "Unstable relationships, impulsive behavior, fear of abandonment" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Borderline Personality Disorder is characterised by a pervasive pattern of unstable interpersonal relationships, impulsivity, affective instability, and frantic efforts to avoid real or imagined abandonment." },
  { id: "pep2_mp18", section: "mphil",
    q: "What is Dysthymia?",
    options: { a: "A disorder characterized by periods of severe depression alternating with periods of mania.",
               b: "Persistent instability of mood between mild depression and mild elation",
               c: "A disorder characterized by persistent mild depression",
               d: "Persistent mild depression and hypomania lasting for more than 2 years" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Dysthymia (persistent depressive disorder) is a chronic, persistent mild depression lasting at least two years. Alternating depression/mania is bipolar; mild mood swings between mild depression and mild elation is cyclothymia." },
  { id: "pep2_mp19", section: "mphil",
    q: "According to Psychodynamic Theory, what is the primary defense mechanism used to deal with anxiety?",
    options: { a: "Displacement",
               b: "Conversion",
               c: "Repression",
               d: "Isolation" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "In psychodynamic theory, repression, the unconscious exclusion of threatening impulses and memories from awareness, is the primary (basic) defence mechanism; other defences are built upon it." },
  { id: "pep2_mp20", section: "mphil",
    q: "What were the two clinical types differentiated by Emil Kraepelin?",
    options: { a: "Depression and Anxiety",
               b: "Schizophrenia and Borderline personality disorder",
               c: "Dementia praecox and Manic depressive illness",
               d: "Obsessive-compulsive disorder and Bipolar disorder" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Kraepelin's foundational nosology divided the major psychoses into dementia praecox (later renamed schizophrenia by Bleuler) and manic-depressive insanity, a distinction that still underlies modern classification." },
  { id: "pep2_un01", section: "ugcnet",
    q: "What is the sheath/kosh surrounding atman that is involved in perceptual processing",
    options: { a: "Ananda-maya kosha",
               b: "Vijnana-maya kosha",
               c: "Prana-maya kosha",
               d: "Mano-maya kosha" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "In the Pancha Kosha model, the manomaya kosha (mental sheath) is the layer of mind/manas that receives and processes sense impressions and perception. Vijnanamaya is the intellect/discrimination sheath; anandamaya is bliss." },
  { id: "pep2_un02", section: "ugcnet",
    q: "Which school of Buddhism focuses on nirvana via collective suffering?",
    options: { a: "Theravada",
               b: "Mahayana",
               c: "Vajrayana",
               d: "Zen" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Mahayana Buddhism emphasises collective liberation through the bodhisattva ideal, where the enlightened being compassionately works for the salvation of all sentient beings rather than seeking individual nirvana alone." },
  { id: "pep2_un03", section: "ugcnet",
    q: "The path to the cessation of suffering, as described in the fourth Noble Truth, is known as the _______.",
    options: { a: "Samudaya",
               b: "Magga",
               c: "Nirodha",
               d: "Anicca" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "The Fourth Noble Truth is Magga, the path (the Noble Eightfold Path) leading to the cessation of suffering. Nirodha is the third truth (cessation), samudaya the second (origin), and anicca means impermanence." },
  { id: "pep2_un04", section: "ugcnet",
    q: "According to the Bhagavad Gita, the Trigunas belong to",
    options: { a: "Samadhi",
               b: "Purusha",
               c: "Prakriti",
               d: "Samskara" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "In Samkhya/Gita metaphysics the three gunas (sattva, rajas, tamas) are properties of Prakriti (primordial matter/nature), not of Purusha (pure consciousness)." },
  { id: "pep2_un05", section: "ugcnet",
    q: "According to the Bhagavad Gita, what are the three elements of the mind?",
    options: { a: "Manas, buddhi, ahamkara",
               b: "Prakriti, purusa, ksetra",
               c: "Dharma, artha, kama",
               d: "Sattva, rajas, tamas" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "The Gita's internal instrument (antahkarana) of mind comprises manas (mind), buddhi (intellect) and ahamkara (ego). Sattva/rajas/tamas are the gunas, not the elements of mind." },
  { id: "pep2_un06", section: "ugcnet",
    q: "What are the four major goals of human life according to Indian culture?",
    options: { a: "Physical fitness, wealth, desires, fame",
               b: "Knowledge, wealth, desires, enlightenment",
               c: "Duty, wealth, desires, liberation",
               d: "Duty, knowledge, desires, fame/power" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "The four purusharthas (goals of life) are dharma (duty/righteousness), artha (wealth), kama (desire/pleasure) and moksha (liberation). Option C captures duty, wealth, desires and liberation." },
  { id: "pep2_un07", section: "ugcnet",
    q: "Which of the following is an astik system in Indian thought?",
    options: { a: "Sufism",
               b: "Advaita-Vedanta",
               c: "Buddhism",
               d: "Jainism" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Astika (orthodox) systems accept the authority of the Vedas. Advaita-Vedanta is astika; Buddhism and Jainism are nastika (heterodox), and Sufism is an Islamic tradition." },
  { id: "pep2_un08", section: "ugcnet",
    q: "What are the three gunas in the Upanishads?",
    options: { a: "Prakriti, purusha, and ksetra",
               b: "Rajas, tamas, and sattva",
               c: "Atman, Brahman, and maya",
               d: "Karma, dharma, and moksha" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "The three gunas are rajas (activity/passion), tamas (inertia/darkness) and sattva (purity/harmony), the three fundamental qualities of nature described in Vedantic/Samkhya thought." },
  { id: "pep2_un08b", section: "ugcnet",
    q: "Bavisha lost her job and has become so stressed that she cannot eat, has withdrawn, and cannot get herself to function, sunk in inertia. This dull, lethargic, cannot-act state reflects the dominance of which?",
    options: { a: "Tamas",
               b: "Yama",
               c: "Pratyahara",
               d: "Jnana" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Tamas guna = inertia, dullness, lethargy and inactivity, the withdrawn cannot-function state, so it captures what is wrong with her. Yama is an ethical restraint (the first of the eight limbs of Ashtanga Yoga), not an inner mental state, so it cannot describe the problem. Pratyahara (withdrawal of the senses) and jnana (the path of knowledge) are yogic practices/paths, not the state being described." },
  { id: "pep2_un09", section: "ugcnet",
    q: "What are the components of the Linga sharira (subtle body) according to the Pancha kosas?",
    options: { a: "Pranamaya kosa, Manomaya kosa, Vijnanamaya kosa, and Atman",
               b: "Annamaya kosa, pranamaya kosa, Manomaya kosa, and Vijnanamaya kosa",
               c: "Ahankara, Manas, Buddhi, and Citta",
               d: "Jnanendriya, Karmendriya, Manas, Buddhi, and Ahankara" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Framed within the kosha scheme, the linga/subtle body corresponds to the three inner sheaths, pranamaya (vital), manomaya (mental) and vijnanamaya (intellect) koshas, which carry the jiva between lives. (Atman itself is the witness, not a sheath.)" },
  { id: "pep2_un10", section: "ugcnet",
    q: "What are Samskara according to the Pancha kosas?",
    options: { a: "Gross body",
               b: "Past tendencies/nature",
               c: "Sense organs",
               d: "Subtle body" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "In this framework samskaras are the past tendencies/impressions, the accumulated latent dispositions carried from prior experience that shape present nature and behaviour." },
  { id: "pep2_un11", section: "ugcnet",
    q: "What is the process of psycho-spiritual transformation in Sufism?",
    options: { a: "The guidelines and rules for the average Muslim",
               b: "A journey from our mundane surface awareness to the depths and heights of self-consciousness",
               c: "The journey from the outer layer of Islam to the inner dimension of Islam",
               d: "The process of transformation of personality and consciousness that involves fulfilling the Four Noble Truths" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Sufism (tasawwuf) is a process of psycho-spiritual transformation, an inner journey from ordinary surface awareness toward the depths and heights of self-consciousness and union with the Divine." },
  { id: "pep2_un12", section: "ugcnet",
    q: "Which year saw the establishment of the first psychology department in India?",
    options: { a: "1916",
               b: "1924",
               c: "1905",
               d: "1938" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "The first psychology department in India was established at the University of Calcutta in 1916, founded under N. N. Sengupta, marking the start of formal academic psychology in India." },
  { id: "pep2_un13", section: "ugcnet",
    q: "According to positivism, what is the nature of reality?",
    options: { a: "Virtual reality shaped by social, political, cultural, economic, ethnic, and gender values",
               b: "There is a \"real\" reality, but it can only be imperfectly and probabilistically apprehended",
               c: "There is a \"real\" reality that is apprehendable",
               d: "Only local and specific constructed realities" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Positivism holds a naive-realist ontology: there is a single 'real' reality that is fully apprehendable. Post-positivism says it is only imperfectly/probabilistically knowable; constructivism says realities are locally constructed; critical theory says reality is value-shaped." },
  { id: "pep2_un14", section: "ugcnet",
    q: "What is the key goal of liberal feminism?",
    options: { a: "To overthrow the capitalist system",
               b: "To build a women's culture",
               c: "To integrate women into the structure of mainstream society",
               d: "To recognize that women are oppressed by the capitalist system" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Liberal feminism seeks equality by integrating women into the existing structures of mainstream society through reform (equal rights, access, opportunity), rather than overthrowing the system." },
  { id: "pep2_un15", section: "ugcnet",
    q: "What is the primary focus of radical feminism?",
    options: { a: "To recognize that women are oppressed by the capitalist system",
               b: "To build a women's culture",
               c: "To challenge the fundamental assumptions of patriarchy",
               d: "To integrate women into the structure of mainstream society" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Radical feminism locates women's oppression in patriarchy itself and aims to challenge and dismantle its fundamental assumptions, viewing patriarchy (not capitalism) as the root cause." },
  { id: "pep2_un16", section: "ugcnet",
    q: "What is the primary characteristic of absolute poverty?",
    options: { a: "Income inequality",
               b: "Lack of access to basic necessities",
               c: "Temporary deprivation resulting from an adverse event",
               d: "Poverty handed down through generations" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Absolute poverty is defined by a lack of access to the basic necessities for survival (food, clean water, shelter, health), measured against a fixed minimum standard, unlike relative poverty/inequality." },
  { id: "pep2_un17", section: "ugcnet",
    q: "How does eudemonic wellbeing conceptualize wellbeing?",
    options: { a: "Measuring wellbeing in terms of income and material possessions",
               b: "Focusing on the experience of pleasure and relaxation",
               c: "Focusing on the cultivation of personal strengths and contribution to the greater good",
               d: "Equating wellbeing with pleasure and happiness" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Eudaimonic well-being (from Aristotle) defines well-being as flourishing, the cultivation of personal strengths, meaning, growth and contribution to the greater good, in contrast to the hedonic focus on pleasure." },
  { id: "pep2_un18", section: "ugcnet",
    q: "What are some areas of post-traumatic growth?",
    options: { a: "None of the above",
               b: "Appreciation of life, personal strength, and new possibilities",
               c: "Accumulation of material possessions, financial success, personal weakness, and new possibilities",
               d: "Appreciation of life, relating to others, personal strength, and spiritual change" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Tedeschi and Calhoun's domains of post-traumatic growth include greater appreciation of life, deeper relating to others, a sense of personal strength, recognition of new possibilities, and spiritual change. Option D lists these growth domains." },
  { id: "pep2_un19", section: "ugcnet",
    q: "How have physician recommendations and increased awareness contributed to higher exercise participation rates?",
    options: { a: "By creating barriers to exercise",
               b: "By discouraging individuals from participating in physical activity",
               c: "By increasing awareness of the benefits of exercise",
               d: "None of the above" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Physician recommendations and public-health campaigns raise exercise participation chiefly by increasing people's awareness of the health benefits of regular physical activity, motivating uptake." },
  { id: "pep2_un20", section: "ugcnet",
    q: "What eating disorder involves cycles of binge eating and purging through various methods?",
    options: { a: "Anorexia Nervosa",
               b: "Bulimia",
               c: "Binge Eating Disorder",
               d: "None of the above" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Bulimia nervosa involves recurrent cycles of binge eating followed by compensatory purging (self-induced vomiting, laxatives, excessive exercise). Anorexia involves restriction; binge-eating disorder lacks purging." },
  { id: "pep2_co01", section: "common",
    q: "According to Guthrie, which of the following is NOT improvable by practice?",
    options: { a: "Skills",
               b: "Acts",
               c: "Movements",
               d: "None of the above" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Guthrie distinguished movements, acts and skills. A movement is a specific muscle response learned fully in a single contiguous pairing (one-trial learning), so it is not improved by practice; acts and skills (built of movements) improve with practice." },
  { id: "pep2_co02", section: "common",
    q: "The view of 'observational learning' where reinforcement is not required for learning a behaviour is given by",
    options: { a: "Skinner",
               b: "Bandura",
               c: "Tolman",
               d: "Miller and Dollard" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Bandura's social/observational learning theory holds that behaviour can be acquired simply by observing a model, without the observer being directly reinforced (reinforcement affects performance, not acquisition)." },
  { id: "pep2_co03", section: "common",
    q: "The sudden increase in performance of an organism upon reinforcement, despite never being reinforced in the previous trials is _____________",
    options: { a: "Discrimination learning",
               b: "Vicarious reinforcement",
               c: "Observational learning",
               d: "Latent learning" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Latent learning (Tolman's maze rats) is learning that occurs without reinforcement and stays hidden until an incentive is introduced, producing a sudden jump in performance once reward is given." },
  { id: "pep2_co04", section: "common",
    q: "According to Skinner, the response rate _________ after extinction",
    options: { a: "increases",
               b: "stays the same",
               c: "drops back to earlier levels",
               d: "drops to zero" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "In operant extinction, withdrawing reinforcement causes responding to decline and return to its earlier baseline (pre-conditioning operant) level rather than vanishing entirely to zero." },
  { id: "pep2_co05", section: "common",
    q: "The allocation policy in Kahneman's model includes",
    options: { a: "Enduring temperament; Momentary distractions; Evaluation of demands",
               b: "Enduring temperament; Momentary distractions; Environmental demands",
               c: "Enduring temperament; Momentary intentions; Environmental demands",
               d: "Enduring dispositions; Momentary intentions; Evaluation of demands" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "In Kahneman's capacity model of attention, the allocation policy that distributes limited capacity is governed by enduring dispositions, momentary intentions, and the evaluation of demands on capacity." },
  { id: "pep2_co06", section: "common",
    q: "An idealized representative object of a category of objects is",
    options: { a: "Prototype",
               b: "Template",
               c: "Feature",
               d: "Geons" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "A prototype is an idealized, abstract 'best example' that represents the central tendency of a category, against which instances are compared (Rosch)." },
  { id: "pep2_co07", section: "common",
    q: "In which of the following cases, would the sensitivity 'd'  be highest in a signal detection task?",
    options: { a: "number of false alarms is slightly more than number of hits",
               b: "number of false alarms > number of hit responses",
               c: "number of hit responses > number of false alarms",
               d: "number of hit responses = number of false alarm responses" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "In signal-detection theory, sensitivity d' is greatest when the observer correctly detects the signal far more often than they false-alarm, i.e. hits substantially exceed false alarms." },
  { id: "pep2_co08", section: "common",
    q: "A Prototype is a/an _________ and ________ member of a category",
    options: { a: "Abstract; Varied",
               b: "Concrete; Averaged",
               c: "Concrete; Varied",
               d: "Abstract; Averaged" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "A prototype is an abstract (not a concrete single instance) and averaged representation, summarising the typical/central features across members of the category." },
  { id: "pep2_co09", section: "common",
    q: "For perception of a contour, _________ and __________ are required",
    options: { a: "minimal amount of stimulation intensity; minimum exposure time",
               b: "minimal amount of stimulation intensity; minimum reaction time",
               c: "minimum exposure time; minimum reaction time",
               d: "minimum reaction time; minimum movement" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "For a contour (edge) to be perceived, the stimulation must reach a minimal intensity and be present for a minimum exposure time so the visual system can register the luminance change." },
  { id: "pep2_co10", section: "common",
    q: "the theory which assumes that perceiving an object is by due to 'geons' is",
    options: { a: "Pandemonium model",
               b: "Recognition by components theory",
               c: "Prototype model",
               d: "Template matching" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Biederman's Recognition-by-Components (RBC) theory proposes that objects are recognised by decomposing them into a set of basic volumetric primitives called geons." },
  { id: "pep2_co11", section: "common",
    q: "Which of the following depth perception cues requires both eyes?",
    options: { a: "Motion parallax",
               b: "Aerial perspective",
               c: "Convergence",
               d: "Accomodation" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Convergence is a binocular depth cue: it uses the inward turning (and the eye-muscle feedback) of both eyes to judge distance. Motion parallax, aerial perspective and accommodation are monocular cues." },
  { id: "pep2_co12", section: "common",
    q: "The process of conversion of stimulus input into electrical signal by the receptors is called",
    options: { a: "Transduction",
               b: "Transference",
               c: "Transformation",
               d: "Transfusion" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Transduction is the process by which sensory receptors convert physical stimulus energy (light, sound, pressure) into the electrochemical/neural signals the nervous system can use." },
  { id: "pep2_co13", section: "common",
    q: "_________ are tiny rapid movements of the eyes to ensure ___________",
    options: { a: "Saccades; Constant perception",
               b: "Fixation; Constant perception",
               c: "Saccades; Sensory adaptation",
               d: "Fixation; Sensory adaptation" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Saccades, including the tiny fixational microsaccades, are rapid eye movements that keep shifting the retinal image just enough to prevent it from fading, thereby maintaining constant (stable) perception." },
  { id: "pep2_co14", section: "common",
    q: "the component of working memory that is capable of mentally rotating an object is",
    options: { a: "phonological loop",
               b: "central executive",
               c: "visuospatial sketchpad",
               d: "executive functioning" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "In Baddeley's model the visuospatial sketchpad holds and manipulates visual and spatial information, including tasks like mentally rotating an object. The phonological loop handles verbal/acoustic material." },
  { id: "pep2_co15", section: "common",
    q: "The mnemonic where a sentence is framed using first letter of items on a list is called",
    options: { a: "Acronym",
               b: "Acrostic",
               c: "Pegword system",
               d: "Keyword system" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "An acrostic is a mnemonic in which a sentence is made from the first letters of the to-be-remembered items (e.g. 'Every Good Boy Does Fine'). An acronym forms a single word from those letters." },
  { id: "pep2_co16", section: "common",
    q: "In short term memory encoding is primarily ________",
    options: { a: "acoustic",
               b: "visual",
               c: "haptic",
               d: "semantic" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Encoding in short-term memory is primarily acoustic (Conrad's confusions among similar-sounding letters), whereas long-term memory encodes mainly semantically." },
  { id: "pep2_co17", section: "common",
    q: "Which sampling technique is most likely to result in a representative sample?",
    options: { a: "Non-probability sampling",
               b: "Convenience sampling",
               c: "Probability sampling",
               d: "Snowball sampling" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Probability sampling (every member has a known, non-zero chance of selection) is most likely to yield a representative sample. Convenience, snowball and other non-probability methods risk systematic bias." },
  { id: "pep2_co18", section: "common",
    q: "According to Wallas' model of the creative process, what is the third stage?",
    options: { a: "Incubation",
               b: "Illumination",
               c: "Preparation",
               d: "Verification" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Wallas's four stages of the creative process are Preparation, Incubation, Illumination and Verification, so the third stage is Illumination (the sudden insight)." },
  { id: "pep2_co19", section: "common",
    q: "According to Piaget, how does cognitive development occur?",
    options: { a: "Solely through maturation",
               b: "Solely through learning",
               c: "Through both maturation and learning",
               d: "Through socialization and cultural practices" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Piaget held that cognitive development arises from the interaction of maturation and experience/learning, as the child actively constructs knowledge through assimilation and accommodation (equilibration)." },
  { id: "pep2_co20", section: "common",
    q: "What are phonemes?",
    options: { a: "The smallest meaningful language units",
               b: "The basic units of sound that are used in a spoken language",
               c: "The rules specifying how words and grammatical markers are to be combined to produce meaningful sentences",
               d: "The culturally specific rules specifying how language should be structured and used in particular social contexts" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Phonemes are the basic units of sound that distinguish meaning in a spoken language. Morphemes are the smallest meaningful units; syntax governs sentence structure; pragmatics governs social use." },
  { id: "pep2_co21", section: "common",
    q: "which of the following F ratio values will always be insignificant despite the degrees of freedom",
    options: { a: "2",
               b: "0.99",
               c: "1.55",
               d: "1.2" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "An F ratio less than 1 can never be statistically significant, regardless of the degrees of freedom, because it indicates between-group variance is smaller than within-group (error) variance. 0.99 < 1." },
  { id: "pep2_co22", section: "common",
    q: "Lack of organization and planning of dreams is due to low activation of ________",
    options: { a: "Striate cortex",
               b: "Somatosensory cortex",
               c: "Prefrontal cortex",
               d: "Extrastriate cortex" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "The bizarreness and lack of logical organisation/planning of dreams is attributed to reduced activation of the prefrontal cortex (which handles planning and reality monitoring) during REM sleep." },
  { id: "pep2_co23", section: "common",
    q: "Slow wave sleep occurs when neurons in the ____________________ become active",
    options: { a: "ventrolateral periaqueductal gray region",
               b: "ventrolateral preoptic area",
               c: "sublaterodorsal nucleus",
               d: "suprachiasmatic nuclei" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Slow-wave (NREM) sleep is promoted when GABAergic neurons in the ventrolateral preoptic area (VLPO) become active and inhibit the arousal systems. The suprachiasmatic nucleus is the circadian clock." },
  { id: "pep2_co24", section: "common",
    q: "Study of monozygotic and dizygotic twins who are separated at  birth and reared apart",
    options: { a: "family studies",
               b: "adoption studies",
               c: "linkage studies",
               d: "twin studies" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Studying monozygotic and dizygotic twins, especially those separated at birth and reared apart, is the defining design of twin studies used to estimate heritability." },
  { id: "pep2_co25", section: "common",
    q: "Which of the following mechanoreceptors is involved in pain perception?",
    options: { a: "Merkel's discs",
               b: "Meissner's corpuscles",
               c: "Free nerve endings",
               d: "Ruffini corpuscles" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Free nerve endings are the receptors that mediate pain (nociception) and temperature. Merkel's discs, Meissner's and Ruffini corpuscles are encapsulated mechanoreceptors for touch/pressure/stretch." },
  { id: "pep2_co26", section: "common",
    q: "Norman Triplett observed _______________ tended to race _________ when racing in the presence of others",
    options: { a: "Cyclists; slower",
               b: "Rats; slower",
               c: "Cyclists; faster",
               d: "Rats; faster" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Norman Triplett's pioneering social-facilitation study observed that cyclists tended to race faster when competing in the presence of others than when riding alone." },
  { id: "pep2_co27", section: "common",
    q: "What is systematic measurement error?",
    options: { a: "Error that occurs due to random fluctuations in measurement",
               b: "Error that is consistent in the same direction across multiple measurements",
               c: "Error that is inconsistent and varies in direction across multiple measurements",
               d: "Error that is caused by participant bias" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Systematic measurement error is consistent and biased in the same direction across measurements (e.g. a miscalibrated scale), affecting validity. Random error varies unpredictably in direction." },
  { id: "pep2_co28", section: "common",
    q: "Which of the following were flaws that David Wechsler aimed to rectify with his Wechsler-Bellevue Intelligence Scales?",
    options: { a: "No appeal for adults, emphasis on word manipulation, emphasis on speed over accuracy, reliance on mental age",
               b: "No appeal for children, emphasis on math questions, emphasis on speed over accuracy, reliance on physical age",
               c: "No appeal for adults, emphasis on vocabulary questions, emphasis on accuracy over speed, reliance on physical age",
               d: "No appeal for children, emphasis on history questions, emphasis on accuracy over speed, reliance on mental age" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Wechsler designed the Wechsler-Bellevue to fix the Stanford-Binet's shortcomings: it had little appeal for adults, over-emphasised verbal/word tasks and speed, and relied on the mental-age (ratio IQ) concept." },
  { id: "pep2_co29", section: "common",
    q: "What is the formula for Wechsler's new method for obtaining the IQ score?",
    options: { a: "IQ = Actual Score / Expected mean score for age",
               b: "IQ = Expected mean score for age / Actual Score",
               c: "IQ = Actual Score * Expected mean score for age",
               d: "IQ = Expected mean score for age + Actual Score" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Wechsler replaced the ratio (mental-age) IQ with a deviation IQ obtained by comparing the person's actual score to the expected mean score for their age group, i.e. actual score relative to age expectation." },
  { id: "pep2_co30", section: "common",
    q: "Which cognitive theories were used to derive the five factors of intelligence assessed in the SB5?",
    options: { a: "Erikson and Vygotsky",
               b: "Piaget and Maslow",
               c: "Carroll and Baddeley",
               d: "Skinner and Freud" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "The SB5's five factors draw on Carroll's three-stratum/CHC theory of cognitive abilities together with Baddeley's working-memory model, hence Carroll and Baddeley." },
  { id: "pep2_co31", section: "common",
    q: "What are the two scales included in the KBIT-2?",
    options: { a: "Reading Comprehension Scale and Math Scale",
               b: "Quantitative Scale and Analytical Scale",
               c: "IQ Scale and EQ Scale",
               d: "Verbal or Crystallized Scale and Nonverbal or Fluid Scale" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "The KBIT-2 (Kaufman Brief Intelligence Test, 2nd ed.) yields two scales: a Verbal (Crystallized) scale and a Nonverbal (Fluid) scale, reflecting Cattell-Horn Gc and Gf." },
  { id: "pep2_co32", section: "common",
    q: "What was developed by Alan Kaufman to measure intelligence?",
    options: { a: "ACT test",
               b: "K-BIT",
               c: "SAT test",
               d: "GRE test" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Alan (and Nadeen) Kaufman developed the K-BIT, the Kaufman Brief Intelligence Test. The ACT, SAT and GRE are not Kaufman's intelligence measures." },
  { id: "pep2_co33", section: "common",
    q: "What is the RAT?",
    options: { a: "An acronym for Rapid Action Team",
               b: "A type of animal",
               c: "A timed, 40-minute paper-and-pencil test",
               d: "An electronic device" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "The RAT (Remote Associates Test, Mednick) is a timed (about 40-minute) paper-and-pencil creativity test in which the examinee finds a word linking three apparently unrelated cue words." },
  { id: "pep2_co34", section: "common",
    q: "What is the purpose of the MMPI-2?",
    options: { a: "Psychiatric diagnosis",
               b: "Educational assessment",
               c: "Physical health diagnosis",
               d: "Career assessment" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "The MMPI-2 is a self-report inventory used for the assessment of psychopathology and psychiatric diagnosis (clinical scales plus validity scales), not for educational, vocational or physical-health screening." },
  { id: "pep2_co35", section: "common",
    q: "What does answering 'false' to L Scale items in the MMPI-2 assert?",
    options: { a: "Examinee lacks virtue in our culture",
               b: "Examinee did not read the L Scale items properly",
               c: "Examinee possesses a degree of personal virtue rarely observed in culture",
               d: "Examinee has difficulty understanding the L Scale items" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "L (Lie) Scale items describe minor faults most people honestly admit. Answering 'false' to them (denying these common flaws) claims a degree of personal virtue rarely seen in the culture, suggesting an unrealistically favourable self-presentation." },
  { id: "pep2_co36", section: "common",
    q: "Which of the following is not a factor that contributes to consistency in measurement?",
    options: { a: "Stability of the construct being measured",
               b: "Consistency in administration of the measure",
               c: "Consistency in scoring of the measure",
               d: "The complexity of the measure" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Consistency (reliability) is helped by stability of the construct and consistency in administration and scoring. The complexity of the measure is not itself a determinant of consistency." },
  { id: "pep2_co37", section: "common",
    q: "Which method of estimating reliability involves administering the same test twice to the same group of people and correlating their scores?",
    options: { a: "Split-half method",
               b: "Test-retest method",
               c: "Cronbach's alpha",
               d: "Item response theory" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "The test-retest method estimates reliability by administering the same test twice to the same people after an interval and correlating the two sets of scores (a coefficient of stability)." },
  { id: "pep2_co38", section: "common",
    q: "Which of the following is a potential limitation of the internal consistency method of estimating reliability?",
    options: { a: "It only measures consistency within a single administration of the test",
               b: "It is affected by differences in test content",
               c: "It can only be used with tests that have multiple items",
               d: "All of the above" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "A key limitation of internal-consistency reliability is that it is based on a single administration of the test, so it captures only internal homogeneity and not stability/error across time or occasions." },
  { id: "pep2_co39", section: "common",
    q: "What is the major challenge with split-half reliability?",
    options: { a: "Computing the coefficient of split-half reliability using the Spearman-Brown formula",
               b: "Dividing the test into two nearly equivalent halves",
               c: "Administering the test to the same group twice with an intervening time interval",
               d: "Administering two forms of a test to the same group" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "The chief practical challenge of split-half reliability is dividing the test into two genuinely equivalent (parallel) halves; an unequal split distorts the estimate (which is then corrected via Spearman-Brown)." },
  { id: "pep2_co40", section: "common",
    q: "If the half-test reliability coefficient is .60, what is the estimated reliability of the full test using the Spearman-Brown formula?",
    options: { a: ".75",
               b: ".82",
               c: ".30",
               d: ".60" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Spearman-Brown for doubling length: r_full = 2r/(1+r) = 2(.60)/(1.60) = 1.20/1.60 = .75." },
  { id: "pep2_co41", section: "common",
    q: "What is the purpose of averaging several splits in estimating the reliability of a psychological test according to Cronbach?",
    options: { a: "To increase the number of dimensions in the test",
               b: "To decrease the accuracy of the reliability estimate",
               c: "To reduce the number of items in the test",
               d: "To increase the accuracy of the reliability estimate" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Cronbach showed that coefficient alpha is the mean of all possible split-half coefficients; averaging across the many possible splits increases the accuracy (stability) of the reliability estimate over any single arbitrary split." },
  { id: "pep2_co42", section: "common",
    q: "__________ is the tendency for an individual surrounded by a group of strangers to hesitate and not help because none of the bystanders knows for sure what is happening",
    options: { a: "bystander effect",
               b: "pluralistic ignorance",
               c: "diffusion of responsibility",
               d: "amotivation to help" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Pluralistic ignorance is the bystander phenomenon in which, in an ambiguous situation, each person looks to the outwardly calm others, wrongly concludes nothing is wrong, and so hesitates to act." },
  { id: "pep2_co43", section: "common",
    q: "Which of the following are powerful sources of provocation",
    options: { a: "Teasing and condescension",
               b: "Unjustified criticism and teasing",
               c: "Condescension and unjustified criticism",
               d: "none of the above" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Among the listed pairs, condescension and harsh, unjustified criticism are highlighted as especially powerful sources of provocation that elicit aggression." },
  { id: "pep2_co44", section: "common",
    q: "splintering of a group into distinct factions is called",
    options: { a: "schism",
               b: "chapter",
               c: "division",
               d: "deviation" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "A schism is the splintering of a group into distinct, opposing factions or sub-groups, often around ideological or leadership differences." },
  { id: "pep2_co45", section: "common",
    q: "Fiedler's contingency theory does NOT include which of the following as dimensions",
    options: { a: "employee motivation",
               b: "leader–member relations",
               c: "structure of the task",
               d: "intrinsic power and authority" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Fiedler's contingency model of leadership specifies three situational dimensions: leader-member relations, task structure, and the leader's position power. Employee motivation is not one of them." },
  { id: "pep2_co46", section: "common",
    q: "Identify the correct order of cellular layers to the retinal receptors",
    options: { a: "Horizontal cells>>>>Ganglion>>>>Amycrine cells>>>>Bipolar cells",
               b: "Bipolar cells>>>>Horizontal cells>>>>Ganglion>>>>Amycrine cells",
               c: "Amycrine cells>>>>Bipolar cells>>>>Horizontal cells>>>>Ganglion",
               d: "Ganglion>>>>Amycrine cells>>>>Bipolar cells>>>>Horizontal cells" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Light passes through the inner retinal layers before reaching the photoreceptors, so the order toward the receptors is ganglion cells, then amacrine, then bipolar, then horizontal cells, then the rods/cones." },
  { id: "pep2_co47", section: "common",
    q: "Tendency of acoustic stimuli of different frequencies (of sound) to cause different parts of the basilar membrane to flex back and forth is",
    options: { a: "Rate coding",
               b: "Place coding",
               c: "Frequency coding",
               d: "Pitch coding" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Place coding (place theory) holds that sounds of different frequencies maximally displace different locations along the basilar membrane (high frequencies near the base, low near the apex)." },
  { id: "pep2_co48", section: "common",
    q: "__________ layer is sensitive to color and ________ layer is sensitive to movement",
    options: { a: "Magnocellular; Parvocellular",
               b: "Parvocellular; Koniocellular",
               c: "Magnocellular; Koniocellular",
               d: "Parvocellular; Magnocellular" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "In the visual pathway the parvocellular layers are sensitive to colour and fine detail, while the magnocellular layers are sensitive to motion and luminance/contrast." },
  { id: "pep2_co49", section: "common",
    q: "Correct order to the auditory receptor is",
    options: { a: "Pinna; Tympanic membrane; Ossicles; Oval window; Cochlea; Organ of Corti",
               b: "Pinna; Cochlea; Tympanic membrane; Oval window; Ossicles; Organ of Corti",
               c: "Pinna; Oval window; Tympanic membrane; Cochlea; Ossicles; Organ of Corti",
               d: "Pinna; Tympanic membrane; Oval window; Ossicles; Cochlea; Organ of Corti" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Sound is conducted in the order: pinna, tympanic membrane (eardrum), ossicles (malleus-incus-stapes), oval window, cochlea, and finally the organ of Corti where transduction occurs." },
  { id: "pep2_co50", section: "common",
    q: "The Anterolateral system system in somatosensory pathway carries ___________ information",
    options: { a: "proprioceptive",
               b: "vibrational",
               c: "touch related",
               d: "nociceptive" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "The anterolateral (spinothalamic) system carries nociceptive information, pain and temperature, whereas the dorsal-column/medial-lemniscus pathway carries fine touch, vibration and proprioception." },
  { id: "pep2_co51", section: "common",
    q: "The part of the nervous system responsible for conservation of energy resources is ___________ and that responsible of allocation of resources for expenditure is _________",
    options: { a: "Parasympathetic nervous system; Sympathetic Nervous system",
               b: "Sympathetic Nervous system; Parasympathetic nervous system",
               c: "Somatic nervous system; Autonomic nervous system",
               d: "Autonomic nervous system; Somatic nervous system" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "The parasympathetic nervous system conserves and restores energy ('rest and digest'), while the sympathetic nervous system mobilises and allocates energy resources for expenditure ('fight or flight')." },
  { id: "pep2_co52", section: "common",
    q: "Which neurotransmitter undergoes recycling by enzymatic degradation exclusively",
    options: { a: "Dopamine",
               b: "GABA",
               c: "ACh",
               d: "Glutamate" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Acetylcholine is the one classic transmitter cleared almost exclusively by enzymatic degradation, broken down in the synaptic cleft by acetylcholinesterase, whereas dopamine, GABA and glutamate are mainly removed by reuptake transporters." },
  { id: "pep2_co53", section: "common",
    q: "Which of the following are not catecholamines?",
    options: { a: "GABA",
               b: "Dopamine",
               c: "Norepinephrine",
               d: "Epinephrine" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "The catecholamines are dopamine, norepinephrine and epinephrine (all synthesised from tyrosine). GABA is an amino-acid neurotransmitter, not a catecholamine." },
  { id: "pep2_co54", section: "common",
    q: "According to existentialists, what is the basic unity of person and environment expressed in?",
    options: { a: "Umwelt",
               b: "Dasein",
               c: "Mitwelt",
               d: "Eigenwelt" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "For existentialists, Dasein ('being-there', Heidegger) expresses the basic, indissoluble unity of the person and their world, the being-in-the-world that precedes the subject-object split." },
  { id: "pep2_co55", section: "common",
    q: "What is the third level of moral reasoning according to Kohlberg?",
    options: { a: "Preconventional morality",
               b: "Conventional morality",
               c: "Autonomous moral principles",
               d: "Postconventional morality" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Kohlberg's third and highest level of moral reasoning is post-conventional morality, in which judgments rest on self-chosen universal ethical principles. (Pre-conventional and conventional are the first two levels.)" },
  { id: "pep2_co56", section: "common",
    q: "Which style of contact in gestalt therapy is characterized by a high need for acceptance and liking, resulting in enmeshment?",
    options: { a: "Projection",
               b: "Confluence",
               c: "Introjection",
               d: "Retroflection" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Confluence in Gestalt therapy is a contact style marked by a high need for acceptance and liking, leading to a blurring of boundaries and enmeshment where the person merges with others." },
  { id: "pep2_co57", section: "common",
    q: "Which step in systematic desensitization involves gradually exposing clients to anxiety-arousing situations?",
    options: { a: "Relaxation training",
               b: "Development of the anxiety hierarchy",
               c: "Systematic desensitization proper",
               d: "Cognitive restructuring" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "After relaxation training and constructing the anxiety hierarchy, the desensitization proper is the step in which the client is gradually exposed (in imagination or in vivo) to the anxiety-arousing items while staying relaxed." },
  { id: "pep2_co58", section: "common",
    q: "Which stage is characterized by the development of sex-role identity and adult social relationships?",
    options: { a: "Oral stage",
               b: "Anal stage",
               c: "Phallic stage",
               d: "Genital stage" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Freud's genital stage (puberty onward) is characterised by the development of mature sex-role identity and the capacity for adult social and sexual relationships." },
  { id: "pep2_co59", section: "common",
    q: "What is the primary focus of the phallic stage of psychosexual development?",
    options: { a: "Toilet training and gratification from defecation",
               b: "Development of sex-role identity and adult social relationships",
               c: "Incestuous fantasies and Oedipus/Electra complex",
               d: "Sublimation of sex instinct" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "The phallic stage centres on the genitals as the erogenous zone and on incestuous fantasies toward the opposite-sex parent, the Oedipus (boys) and Electra (girls) complexes." },
  { id: "pep2_co60", section: "common",
    q: "Who developed the basic behavioral procedure of systematic desensitization?",
    options: { a: "Joseph Wolpe",
               b: "Carl Rogers",
               c: "Albert Bandura",
               d: "Sigmund Freud" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Joseph Wolpe developed systematic desensitization, grounding it in reciprocal inhibition: an anxiety response cannot coexist with a relaxation response paired to graded exposure." },
  { id: "pep2_co61", section: "common",
    q: "Ionotropic receptors bind to",
    options: { a: "small neurotransmitters only",
               b: "large and small neurotransmitters",
               c: "large neurotransmitters only",
               d: "none of the above" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Ionotropic (ligand-gated ion channel) receptors are activated by small, fast amino-acid transmitters (glutamate, GABA, glycine) and ACh; larger transmitters/peptides act through metabotropic receptors." },
  { id: "pep2_co62", section: "common",
    q: "Varicosities are ________ synapses and they release  ___________ neurotransmitters",
    options: { a: "non-directed; monoamine",
               b: "directed; amino acid",
               c: "non-directed; amino acid",
               d: "directed; monoamine" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Varicosities are non-directed ('en passant') release sites that diffusely release monoamine neurotransmitters (e.g. norepinephrine, serotonin) into a wide area rather than into a tight synaptic cleft." },
  { id: "pep2_co63", section: "common",
    q: "What is Error degrees of freedom for a randomized block design with 4 treatments and 5 blocks (1 sample per block)",
    options: { a: "12",
               b: "20",
               c: "15",
               d: "16" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "For a randomized block design, error df = (t-1)(b-1). With 4 treatments and 5 blocks that is (4-1)(5-1) = 3 x 4 = 12." },
  { id: "pep2_co64", section: "common",
    q: "how many pairwise comparisons will have to be made post-hoc for an Independent variable with 4 levels?",
    options: { a: "8",
               b: "12",
               c: "4",
               d: "6" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "The number of pairwise comparisons for k levels is k(k-1)/2. For 4 levels that is 4 x 3 / 2 = 6 comparisons." },
  { id: "pep2_co65", section: "common",
    q: "Which of the following rotation methods can be used for second order factor analysis",
    options: { a: "Promax",
               b: "Equimax",
               c: "Varimax",
               d: "Quartimax" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Second-order (higher-order) factor analysis requires that the first-order factors be correlated, so an oblique rotation is needed; Promax is oblique. Varimax, Equimax and Quartimax are orthogonal." },
  { id: "pep2_co66", section: "common",
    q: "An uninvolved objective observation is done in ___________ approach of phenomenology; while an involved observation is done in ____________ approach of phenomenology",
    options: { a: "indirect; direct",
               b: "direct; semi-direct",
               c: "indirect; semi-direct",
               d: "direct; indirect" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "In phenomenology, an uninvolved, detached objective observation belongs to the indirect approach, whereas an involved, participatory observation belongs to the direct approach." },
  { id: "pep2_co67", section: "common",
    q: "Survey research does NOT",
    options: { a: "study the distribution of a trait",
               b: "study relationships between variables",
               c: "study the factors of an event",
               d: "study the relative incidence of a phenomenon" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Survey research describes the distribution and relative incidence of traits/phenomena and relationships among variables, but it does not establish the causal factors of an event (that requires experimental designs)." },
  { id: "pep2_co68", section: "common",
    q: "An exploratory research study is also a",
    options: { a: "Variable-manipulating study",
               b: "Hypothesis-generating study",
               c: "All of the above",
               d: "Hypothesis-testing study" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Exploratory research is conducted to generate hypotheses and insights about a poorly understood problem; it is a hypothesis-generating (not hypothesis-testing) study." },
  { id: "pep2_co69", section: "common",
    q: "In grounded theory, the process of weaving the data back together around groups of concepts is known as",
    options: { a: "early coding",
               b: "axial coding",
               c: "open coding",
               d: "core coding" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "In grounded theory, axial coding is the stage of reassembling the data, weaving the fragmented codes back together around groups of related concepts/categories. It follows open coding and precedes selective coding." },
  { id: "pep2_co70", section: "common",
    q: "What is the degrees of freedom on a one-sample t-test with a sample size of 30",
    options: { a: "30",
               b: "28",
               c: "29",
               d: "31" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Degrees of freedom for a one-sample t-test are n - 1. With n = 30, df = 29." },
  { id: "pep2_co71", section: "common",
    q: "Which of the following measures of variability is affected by extreme scores in the sample",
    options: { a: "Interquartile range",
               b: "Quartile deviation",
               c: "Standard deviation",
               d: "None of the above" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "The standard deviation (like the range and variance) is influenced by extreme scores because it uses every value's deviation from the mean. The interquartile range and quartile deviation are resistant to outliers." },
  { id: "pep2_co72", section: "common",
    q: "On a Binet-Simon type test (mean IQ= 100, sd =16), a person has secured an IQ of 136, what percentage of people fall below him?",
    options: { a: "97.5%",
               b: "90%",
               c: "98.7%",
               d: "84%" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "IQ 136 with mean 100 and SD 16 is (136-100)/16 = +2.25 SD. The area below z = 2.25 is about .987, so roughly 98.7% of people fall below this score." },
  { id: "pep2_co73", section: "common",
    q: "Ordered variables are considered to __________, while nominal variables are considered to be ___________",
    options: { a: "Categorical Continuous",
               b: "Continuous, Categorical",
               c: "Discontinuous, Categorical",
               d: "Categorical; Discontinuous" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Ordered (ordinal) variables carry rank information and are often treated as continuous, whereas nominal variables are purely categorical (unordered labels)." },
  { id: "pep2_co74", section: "common",
    q: "Which of the following can NOT be used a test of homogeneity of variance before regression",
    options: { a: "Levene's test",
               b: "Bartlett's test",
               c: "Shapiro-Wilk test",
               d: "None of the above" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Levene's and Bartlett's tests assess homogeneity of variance. The Shapiro-Wilk test assesses normality, not equality of variances, so it cannot be used as a homogeneity-of-variance test." },
  { id: "pep2_co75", section: "common",
    q: "The correlation between two variable where the common overlap with a third variable is statistically controlled is called",
    options: { a: "Multiple correlation",
               b: "Partial correlation",
               c: "Spearman's correlation",
               d: "Phi correlation" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Partial correlation measures the association between two variables while statistically holding constant (removing) the common overlap with a third variable." },
  { id: "pep2_co76", section: "common",
    q: "Social loafing is also called __________",
    options: { a: "Ringelmann effect",
               b: "Triplett effect",
               c: "McGurk effect",
               d: "Freeriding effect" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Social loafing, the tendency to exert less effort in a group than alone, is also called the Ringelmann effect, after Ringelmann's rope-pulling demonstration." },
  { id: "pep2_co77", section: "common",
    q: "What is the distinct type of motivation proposed by Maslow for self-actualizers?",
    options: { a: "D-motivation",
               b: "E-motivation",
               c: "F-motivation",
               d: "Metamotivation" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Maslow proposed metamotivation (growth/being-motivation) as the distinct motivation of self-actualizers, who are driven by B-values (truth, beauty, justice) rather than by deficiency needs (D-motivation)." },
  { id: "pep2_co78", section: "common",
    q: "According to self-determination theory, what are the three basic needs that lead to a state of well-being?",
    options: { a: "Achievement, autonomy, and relatedness",
               b: "Competence, autonomy, and relatedness",
               c: "Competence, independence, and superiority",
               d: "Competence, autonomy, and excellence" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Self-determination theory (Deci and Ryan) posits three basic psychological needs whose satisfaction fosters well-being: competence, autonomy and relatedness." },
  { id: "pep2_co79", section: "common",
    q: "Gratitude increases prosocial behavior, primarily by enhancing helpers’ feelings of",
    options: { a: "Self worth and self efficacy",
               b: "Self worth and self confidence",
               c: "Self esteem and self efficacy",
               d: "Self esteem and self confidence" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Research (e.g. Grant and Gino) finds that gratitude promotes prosocial behaviour mainly by raising helpers' sense of self-worth (feeling socially valued) and self-efficacy, rather than through mood alone." },
  { id: "pep2_co80", section: "common",
    q: "In a POX relationship triad, balance exists when there are",
    options: { a: "1 negative and 2 positives",
               b: "2 negatives and 1 positive",
               c: "3 negatives",
               d: "no positives" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "In Heider's balance theory, a P-O-X triad is balanced when the product of the three signs is positive: either all three relations are positive, or one is positive and two are negative. Two negatives and one positive is therefore balanced." },
],
  "met-mock-1": [
  // Interleaved across sections so the opening spread is balanced (no biopsych cluster).
  { id: "m1_bio01", section: "biopsych",
    q: "The all-or-none law of action potentials means that:",
    options: { a: "Stronger stimuli produce larger amplitude action potentials",
               b: "Sub-threshold stimuli produce miniature action potentials",
               c: "Action potentials can be graded according to stimulus intensity",
               d: "Once threshold is reached, the action potential propagates with constant amplitude" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Threshold (~ −55 mV) triggers a full-amplitude spike. Stimulus intensity is coded by firing frequency, not amplitude." },
  { id: "m1_abn01", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Per DSM-5-TR, the minimum duration required for a diagnosis of major depressive episode is:",
    options: { a: "2 weeks",
               b: "1 week",
               c: "1 month",
               d: "6 months" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Five or more symptoms (including either depressed mood or anhedonia) present nearly every day for at least 2 weeks, with functional impairment." },
  { id: "m1_dev01", section: "dev",
    q: "According to Piaget, the ability to mentally reverse operations first appears in which stage?",
    options: { a: "Sensorimotor",
               b: "Preoperational",
               c: "Formal operational",
               d: "Concrete operational" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Reversibility, conservation, and decentration mark the concrete operational stage (~7–11 yrs). Preoperational children fail conservation tasks." },
  { id: "m1_ass01", section: "assessment",
    q: "If a test has reliability r_xx = 0.81, the maximum possible validity coefficient with any other measure is:",
    options: { a: "0.81",
               b: "1.00",
               c: "0.90",
               d: "0.95" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Validity ceiling = √r_xx (when criterion is perfectly reliable). √0.81 = 0.90." },
  { id: "m1_the01", section: "therapy",
    q: "The 'empty chair' technique is most associated with which approach?",
    options: { a: "Cognitive Behaviour Therapy",
               b: "Gestalt therapy (Perls)",
               c: "Person-Centred Therapy",
               d: "Psychoanalysis" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Fritz Perls' Gestalt therapy uses the empty-chair technique to dialogue with disowned parts of the self or absent others, promoting integration." },
  { id: "m1_lang01", section: "language",
    q: "Choose the correctly punctuated sentence:",
    options: { a: "The patient, who was anxious; refused medication.",
               b: "The patient, who was anxious, refused medication.",
               c: "The patient who was anxious refused medication.",
               d: "The patient who was anxious, refused medication." },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Non-restrictive relative clause needs commas on both sides. Option (b) is grammatical but interprets the clause as restrictive (a different patient)." },
  { id: "m1_bst01", section: "biostats",
    q: "A Type I error in hypothesis testing occurs when:",
    options: { a: "The null hypothesis is correctly retained",
               b: "The sample size is too small",
               c: "The null hypothesis is wrongly retained when it is false",
               d: "The null hypothesis is wrongly rejected when it is actually true" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Type I (α) = false positive (rejecting a true null). Type II (β) = false negative (retaining a false null). Power = 1 − β." },
  { id: "m1_bio02", section: "biopsych",
    q: "During the absolute refractory period of a neuron:",
    options: { a: "A stronger-than-normal stimulus can elicit another action potential",
               b: "The membrane is hyperpolarised below resting potential",
               c: "Potassium efflux has just begun",
               d: "Sodium channels are inactivated and no stimulus can produce another spike" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Voltage-gated Na+ channels are in the inactivated state. They reset only after the membrane repolarises." },
  { id: "m1_abn02", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Schneider's first-rank symptoms are most associated with:",
    options: { a: "Bipolar disorder",
               b: "Borderline personality disorder",
               c: "Obsessive-compulsive disorder",
               d: "Schizophrenia" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Kurt Schneider's first-rank symptoms (audible thoughts, voices arguing, thought insertion/withdrawal/broadcast, somatic passivity, made affect/impulse, delusional perception) historically anchored the diagnosis of schizophrenia; downplayed in DSM-5 but still clinically used." },
  { id: "m1_dev02", section: "dev",
    q: "In Erikson's psychosocial theory, the crisis of middle adulthood is:",
    options: { a: "Generativity vs Stagnation",
               b: "Intimacy vs Isolation",
               c: "Integrity vs Despair",
               d: "Identity vs Role Confusion" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Generativity vs Stagnation (~40–65 yrs); virtue is care. Integrity vs Despair is late adulthood; Intimacy vs Isolation is young adulthood." },
  { id: "m1_ass02", section: "assessment",
    q: "A T-score of 70 on the MMPI corresponds to a deviation of:",
    options: { a: "2 SD above the mean",
               b: "1.5 SD above the mean",
               c: "1 SD above the mean",
               d: "3 SD above the mean" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "T-scores have M=50, SD=10. T=70 → +2 SD, the conventional clinical cut-off on MMPI." },
  { id: "m1_the02", section: "therapy",
    q: "Carl Rogers' three core conditions for therapeutic change are:",
    options: { a: "Transference, free association, interpretation",
               b: "Cognitive restructuring, behavioural activation, exposure",
               c: "Unconditional positive regard, congruence, accurate empathy",
               d: "Working alliance, transference, resistance interpretation" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Person-centred therapy: therapist must offer UPR, congruence (genuineness), and empathic understanding for constructive personality change." },
  { id: "m1_lang02", section: "language",
    q: "Identify the antonym of 'GREGARIOUS':",
    options: { a: "Sociable",
               b: "Reclusive",
               c: "Affable",
               d: "Convivial" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Gregarious = sociable, fond of company. Reclusive = withdrawn, solitary." },
  { id: "m1_bst02", section: "biostats",
    q: "The Mann-Whitney U test is the non-parametric equivalent of the:",
    options: { a: "Independent samples t-test",
               b: "Paired t-test",
               c: "One-way ANOVA",
               d: "Chi-square test" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Mann-Whitney U = non-parametric independent two-sample test on ranks. Wilcoxon signed-rank = paired t. Kruskal-Wallis = one-way ANOVA." },
  { id: "m1_bio03", section: "biopsych",
    q: "The corticospinal tract decussates primarily at the:",
    options: { a: "Optic chiasm",
               b: "Spinal cord at the level of entry",
               c: "Pons",
               d: "Pyramids of the medulla" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "About 85–90% of corticospinal fibres cross at the pyramidal decussation in the lower medulla, forming the lateral corticospinal tract." },
  { id: "m1_abn03", section: "abnormal",
    q: "In DSM-5-TR, persistent depressive disorder (dysthymia) requires depressed mood for at least:",
    options: { a: "6 months in adults",
               b: "2 years in adults / 1 year in children",
               c: "1 year in adults",
               d: "5 years" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "PDD: chronic depressed mood for ≥2 years (≥1 year in children/adolescents), with at least 2 additional symptoms." },
  { id: "m1_dev03", section: "dev",
    q: "In Ainsworth's Strange Situation, an infant who shows distress on separation but cannot be comforted on reunion and may resist contact is classified as:",
    options: { a: "Insecure-resistant / ambivalent (C)",
               b: "Insecure-avoidant (A)",
               c: "Secure (B)",
               d: "Disorganised (D)" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Resistant/ambivalent infants seek contact then resist it on reunion; reflects an inconsistently responsive caregiver." },
  { id: "m1_ass03", section: "assessment",
    q: "In Wechsler's classification, an FSIQ in the range 70–84 falls under:",
    options: { a: "Low average / borderline (depending on edition)",
               b: "Borderline",
               c: "Average",
               d: "Mild intellectual disability" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "WAIS-IV: 80–89 = low average; 70–79 = borderline; <70 with deficits in adaptive functioning supports intellectual disability per DSM-5." },
  { id: "m1_the03", section: "therapy",
    q: "Aaron Beck's cognitive triad in depression refers to negative views of:",
    options: { a: "Id, ego, and superego",
               b: "Past, present, and future",
               c: "Mother, father, and self",
               d: "Self, world, and future" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Beck's cognitive triad: negative view of self, world, and future; central to his cognitive model of depression." },
  { id: "m1_lang03", section: "language",
    q: "Choose the correct preposition: 'The therapist insisted ___ a follow-up after two weeks.'",
    options: { a: "for",
               b: "with",
               c: "on",
               d: "to" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "'Insist on' is the standard collocation in British and Indian English." },
  { id: "m1_bst03", section: "biostats",
    q: "If the p-value of a study is 0.03 and α was set at 0.05:",
    options: { a: "Retain the null hypothesis",
               b: "Reject the null hypothesis",
               c: "The result is not significant",
               d: "Power is automatically high" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "p (0.03) < α (0.05), so reject H0. p-value is the probability of obtaining the observed (or more extreme) result if H0 were true; it is NOT the probability that H0 is true." },
  { id: "m1_bio04", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Which cranial nerve is purely motor and supplies most extraocular muscles?",
    options: { a: "Optic (II)",
               b: "Trochlear (IV)",
               c: "Oculomotor (III)",
               d: "Abducens (VI)" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "CN III supplies superior, inferior, medial recti and inferior oblique, plus the levator palpebrae and pupillary constrictor." },
  { id: "m1_abn04", section: "abnormal",
    q: "The cardinal feature of obsessive-compulsive disorder under DSM-5 is:",
    options: { a: "Obsessions and/or compulsions that are time-consuming or cause distress",
               b: "Persistent depressive symptoms",
               c: "Pervasive pattern of grandiosity and need for admiration",
               d: "Recurrent unexpected panic attacks" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "OCD requires obsessions and/or compulsions consuming >1 hr/day or causing significant distress/impairment. Insight specifier added in DSM-5." },
  { id: "m1_dev04", section: "dev",
    q: "The zone of proximal development is a concept proposed by:",
    options: { a: "Jean Piaget",
               b: "Urie Bronfenbrenner",
               c: "Lev Vygotsky",
               d: "Lawrence Kohlberg" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Vygotsky: ZPD is the gap between what a child can do alone and what they can achieve with scaffolded help from a more knowledgeable other." },
  { id: "m1_ass04", section: "assessment",
    q: "Which projective test uses incomplete cartoon-style frames in which the respondent fills in what the depicted character is saying in a frustrating situation?",
    options: { a: "TAT",
               b: "Rosenzweig P-F Study",
               c: "Rorschach",
               d: "Sentence Completion Test" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Saul Rosenzweig's Picture-Frustration Study presents 24 cartoon-like situations and scores responses by direction of aggression and reaction type." },
  { id: "m1_the04", section: "therapy",
    q: "Systematic desensitisation, developed by Joseph Wolpe, is based on the principle of:",
    options: { a: "Operant extinction",
               b: "Reciprocal inhibition",
               c: "Modelling",
               d: "Habituation only" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Reciprocal inhibition: pairing relaxation (incompatible with anxiety) with a graded fear hierarchy weakens the fear response." },
  { id: "m1_lang04", section: "language",
    q: "Identify the synonym of 'EPHEMERAL':",
    options: { a: "Vivid",
               b: "Permanent",
               c: "Transient",
               d: "Ancient" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Ephemeral = lasting only briefly; transient is the closest synonym." },
  { id: "m1_bst04", section: "biostats",
    q: "A Solomon four-group design is used to control for:",
    options: { a: "Maturation only",
               b: "Pretest sensitisation effects",
               c: "Sample selection bias only",
               d: "Researcher bias only" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Solomon 4-group adds two no-pretest groups so the researcher can detect and separate pretest sensitisation (interaction of pretest and treatment) from the treatment effect." },
  { id: "m1_bio05", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Damage to the ventromedial hypothalamus typically produces:",
    options: { a: "Aphagia and weight loss",
               b: "Insomnia",
               c: "Hyperphagia and obesity",
               d: "Loss of thermoregulation" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "VMH = satiety centre. Lesions remove the satiety signal, producing hyperphagia. Lateral hypothalamic lesions cause aphagia." },
  { id: "m1_abn05", section: "abnormal",
    q: "A patient presents with sudden inability to recall important autobiographical information, beyond ordinary forgetting, with no medical cause. The most likely diagnosis is:",
    options: { a: "Transient global amnesia",
               b: "Korsakoff syndrome",
               c: "Alzheimer's disease",
               d: "Dissociative amnesia" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Dissociative amnesia: inability to recall important personal information (often stressful or traumatic), inconsistent with normal forgetting and not explained by a medical/neurological cause." },
  { id: "m1_dev05", section: "dev",
    q: "Bronfenbrenner's bioecological model places the immediate family in the:",
    options: { a: "Macrosystem",
               b: "Mesosystem",
               c: "Exosystem",
               d: "Microsystem" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Microsystem = direct face-to-face settings (family, school, peers). Mesosystem = links between microsystems. Exosystem = settings that affect the child indirectly. Macrosystem = culture." },
  { id: "m1_ass05", section: "assessment",
    q: "An item discrimination index (D) of −0.20 indicates that the item:",
    options: { a: "Is well-functioning and should be retained",
               b: "Is too easy",
               c: "Is at ideal difficulty",
               d: "Is poorly functioning, likely miskeyed; should be revised or discarded" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Negative D means low scorers outperformed high scorers — the item is misleading or miskeyed." },
  { id: "m1_the05", section: "therapy",
    q: "The ABC model in Ellis' REBT stands for:",
    options: { a: "Activating event, Behaviour, Consequence",
               b: "Antecedent, Behaviour, Consequence (Skinnerian)",
               c: "Activating event, Beliefs, Consequences",
               d: "Affect, Behaviour, Cognition" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "REBT: A (activating event) → B (irrational beliefs) → C (emotional/behavioural consequences). Therapist disputes (D) the irrational beliefs." },
  { id: "m1_lang05", section: "language",
    q: "Choose the sentence with the correct subject-verb agreement:",
    options: { a: "Each of the participants were given a consent form.",
               b: "Each of the participants are given a consent form.",
               c: "Each of the participants have given a consent form.",
               d: "Each of the participants was given a consent form." },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "'Each' is singular regardless of the plural prepositional phrase that follows; verb must be singular ('was given')." },
  { id: "m1_bst05", section: "biostats",
    q: "Cluster sampling differs from stratified sampling primarily in that:",
    options: { a: "Stratified samples randomly choose entire groups; cluster samples sample within groups",
               b: "Both are non-probability methods",
               c: "Cluster sampling randomly chooses naturally occurring groups, then often samples all units within them",
               d: "Cluster sampling requires every individual to be uniquely listed beforehand" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Cluster sampling = randomly select clusters (e.g., schools), then sample all (or a random subset) of units within. Stratified = divide population into strata, then random sample from each." },
  { id: "m1_bio06", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Which of the following is NOT a basal ganglia structure?",
    options: { a: "Caudate nucleus",
               b: "Globus pallidus",
               c: "Red nucleus",
               d: "Subthalamic nucleus" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "The red nucleus belongs to the midbrain tegmentum (rubrospinal tract). The basal ganglia include caudate, putamen, globus pallidus, subthalamic nucleus and substantia nigra." },
  { id: "m1_abn06", section: "abnormal",
    q: "ICD-11 retained the category of 'gaming disorder' under:",
    options: { a: "Disorders due to substance use or addictive behaviours",
               b: "Disorders specifically associated with stress",
               c: "Mood disorders",
               d: "Impulse control disorders only" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "ICD-11 places gaming disorder (and gambling disorder) under 'disorders due to addictive behaviours' within the substance-use & addictive-behaviours grouping." },
  { id: "m1_dev06", section: "dev",
    q: "Kohlberg's preconventional level of moral reasoning is dominated by:",
    options: { a: "Avoidance of punishment and instrumental exchange",
               b: "Maintaining laws and social order",
               c: "Social contract and universal ethics",
               d: "Conformity to peer expectations" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Stages 1–2: punishment-and-obedience and instrumental hedonism. Conventional = stages 3–4 (good-boy / law-and-order). Postconventional = stages 5–6." },
  { id: "m1_ass06", section: "assessment",
    q: "Cattell's distinction between fluid and crystallised intelligence is operationalised in the Cattell-Horn-Carroll model. Crystallised intelligence (Gc) is best measured by:",
    options: { a: "Block design",
               b: "Matrix reasoning",
               c: "Coding/digit-symbol",
               d: "Vocabulary and information subtests" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Gc reflects acquired knowledge and verbal abilities (vocabulary, information). Gf is reasoning with novel material (matrix reasoning, block design)." },
  { id: "m1_the06", section: "therapy",
    q: "Dialectical Behaviour Therapy (DBT) was developed primarily for:",
    options: { a: "Borderline personality disorder with chronic suicidality",
               b: "Schizophrenia",
               c: "OCD",
               d: "Specific phobias" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Marsha Linehan developed DBT for chronically suicidal women with BPD; integrates CBT with mindfulness and dialectical philosophy. Now applied to other populations too." },
  { id: "m1_lang06", section: "language",
    q: "Identify the part of speech of the underlined word: 'The data were INCONCLUSIVE.'",
    options: { a: "Noun",
               b: "Verb",
               c: "Adverb",
               d: "Adjective" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "'Inconclusive' modifies the noun phrase 'the data' as a predicate adjective." },
  { id: "m1_bst06", section: "biostats",
    q: "In a normal distribution, approximately what percentage of cases falls within ±1 SD of the mean?",
    options: { a: "50%",
               b: "95%",
               c: "68%",
               d: "99.7%" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Empirical rule: ±1 SD ≈ 68%; ±2 SD ≈ 95%; ±3 SD ≈ 99.7%." },
  { id: "m1_bio07", section: "biopsych",
    q: "The cones of the human retina are MOST concentrated in the:",
    options: { a: "Optic disc",
               b: "Peripheral retina",
               c: "Fovea centralis",
               d: "Macula but not the fovea" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "The fovea contains almost exclusively cones, giving the highest visual acuity. Rods dominate the periphery." },
  { id: "m1_abn07", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Anorexia nervosa is differentiated from bulimia nervosa primarily by:",
    options: { a: "Absence of body image disturbance in bulimia",
               b: "Significantly low body weight in anorexia",
               c: "Presence of compensatory behaviours only in anorexia",
               d: "Absence of purging in anorexia" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Anorexia requires significantly low body weight; bulimia patients are typically of normal weight or overweight. Both can include purging." },
  { id: "m1_dev07", section: "dev",
    q: "The Visual Cliff experiment (Gibson & Walk, 1960) demonstrated that depth perception emerges:",
    options: { a: "Only after age 2",
               b: "By the time infants can crawl (~6–8 months)",
               c: "At birth, fully developed",
               d: "After explicit verbal teaching" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Crawling-age infants refused to cross the 'deep' side, suggesting depth perception by ~6–8 months. Some research with heart-rate measures shows even earlier sensitivity." },
  { id: "m1_ass07", section: "assessment",
    q: "The Bender Visual Motor Gestalt Test is primarily used to screen for:",
    options: { a: "Personality disorders",
               b: "Organic brain damage and visuomotor function",
               c: "Schizophrenia",
               d: "ADHD" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Bender-Gestalt II: copy 9 designs; assess visuomotor integration, screen for neuropsychological dysfunction in adults and developmental delay in children." },
  { id: "m1_the07", section: "therapy",
    q: "In psychoanalysis, transference refers to:",
    options: { a: "The therapist's emotional reaction to the client",
               b: "Free association of unconscious thoughts",
               c: "The client redirecting feelings about significant figures onto the therapist",
               d: "Resistance to interpretations" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Transference: client unconsciously displaces feelings/attitudes about significant others onto the therapist. Countertransference is the therapist's parallel reaction." },
  { id: "m1_lang07", section: "language",
    q: "Choose the best one-word substitution for 'a person who is fluent in many languages':",
    options: { a: "Polyglot",
               b: "Bilingual",
               c: "Linguist",
               d: "Orator" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Polyglot = a person who knows or uses several languages. A linguist studies language scientifically." },
  { id: "m1_bst07", section: "biostats",
    q: "Cohen's d of 0.2, 0.5, and 0.8 are conventionally interpreted as:",
    options: { a: "Small, medium, large",
               b: "Trivial, weak, moderate",
               c: "Medium, large, very large",
               d: "Negligible at all three values" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Cohen's benchmarks: 0.2 = small, 0.5 = medium, 0.8 = large effect size for standardised mean differences." },
  { id: "m1_bio08", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Place theory of hearing (Békésy) best explains the perception of:",
    options: { a: "High-frequency sounds via location of basilar membrane displacement",
               b: "Low-frequency sounds via firing rate",
               c: "Sound localisation in the horizontal plane",
               d: "Loudness coding in the cochlea" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Place theory: high frequencies displace the basal end of the basilar membrane; low frequencies displace the apex. Frequency theory explains low frequencies." },
  { id: "m1_abn08", section: "abnormal",
    q: "A patient firmly believes that a close family member has been replaced by an identical impostor. This is:",
    options: { a: "Cotard delusion",
               b: "Folie à deux",
               c: "Fregoli delusion",
               d: "Capgras delusion" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Capgras: identical-looking impostor. Cotard: belief one is dead/non-existent. Fregoli: stranger believed to be a familiar person in disguise. Folie à deux: shared psychotic disorder." },
  { id: "m1_dev08", section: "dev",
    q: "According to Baumrind, the parenting style with high warmth and high demandingness is:",
    options: { a: "Authoritarian",
               b: "Authoritative",
               c: "Permissive (indulgent)",
               d: "Uninvolved (neglectful)" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Authoritative = warm + firm; associated with the best child outcomes. Authoritarian = low warmth + high control. Permissive = high warmth + low control." },
  { id: "m1_ass08", section: "assessment",
    q: "The Vineland Adaptive Behaviour Scales assess all of the following EXCEPT:",
    options: { a: "Communication",
               b: "Verbal IQ",
               c: "Socialisation",
               d: "Daily Living Skills" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Vineland-3 domains: Communication, Daily Living Skills, Socialisation, and (for ages <7) Motor Skills. It measures adaptive behaviour, not IQ." },
  { id: "m1_the08", section: "therapy",
    q: "Token economy is an example of:",
    options: { a: "Classical conditioning",
               b: "Operant conditioning using secondary reinforcers",
               c: "Cognitive restructuring",
               d: "Insight-oriented therapy" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Tokens function as conditioned (secondary) reinforcers exchangeable for primary reinforcers; widely used in inpatient, school and rehabilitation settings." },
  { id: "m1_lang08", section: "language",
    q: "Identify the correct meaning of the idiom 'to bury the hatchet':",
    options: { a: "To make peace and end a dispute",
               b: "To start a quarrel",
               c: "To hide one's intentions",
               d: "To dig up an old issue" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "'Bury the hatchet' = settle a quarrel; from the Native American practice of literally burying weapons during peace ceremonies." },
  { id: "m1_bst08", section: "biostats",
    q: "An ANOVA F-test is significant. To determine which specific group means differ, you would conduct:",
    options: { a: "Post-hoc tests (e.g., Tukey HSD)",
               b: "Another ANOVA",
               c: "A chi-square",
               d: "A Pearson correlation" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Significant omnibus F tells you at least one mean differs but not which. Post-hoc pairwise tests (Tukey, Bonferroni, Scheffé) localise the differences while controlling family-wise error." },
  { id: "m1_bio09", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Which neurotransmitter is most directly implicated in the positive symptoms of schizophrenia (mesolimbic pathway)?",
    options: { a: "Serotonin",
               b: "GABA",
               c: "Dopamine",
               d: "Acetylcholine" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "The dopamine hypothesis: hyperactivity of mesolimbic dopamine produces positive symptoms; hypoactivity in mesocortical pathways relates to negative symptoms." },
  { id: "m1_abn09", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Negative symptoms of schizophrenia include all EXCEPT:",
    options: { a: "Avolition",
               b: "Auditory hallucinations",
               c: "Affective flattening",
               d: "Alogia" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Hallucinations and delusions are positive symptoms. Negative symptoms include the 5 As: avolition, alogia, anhedonia, asociality, affective flattening." },
  { id: "m1_dev09", section: "dev",
    q: "Object permanence, in Piaget's theory, is fully achieved by approximately:",
    options: { a: "2 months",
               b: "18–24 months",
               c: "8–12 months (with A-not-B error)",
               d: "4 months" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Object permanence develops gradually across the sensorimotor stage; the A-not-B error resolves by ~12 months and full object permanence by the end of sensorimotor (~18–24 months)." },
  { id: "m1_ass09", section: "assessment",
    q: "The Indian adaptation of the WAIS used commonly in clinical practice in India is the:",
    options: { a: "WAIS-IV India edition",
               b: "BKT",
               c: "MISIC",
               d: "All of the above are commonly used" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "WAIS-IV India edition is the current adult intelligence scale. MISIC = Malin's Intelligence Scale for Indian Children (children); BKT = Binet-Kamat Test (children, Indian adaptation of Stanford-Binet)." },
  { id: "m1_the09", section: "therapy",
    q: "Motivational Interviewing (Miller & Rollnick) uses the acronym OARS to refer to:",
    options: { a: "Open questions, Affirmations, Reflective listening, Summaries",
               b: "Outcomes, Assessment, Rewards, Sanctions",
               c: "Observation, Analysis, Reframing, Suggestion",
               d: "Openness, Acceptance, Responsibility, Support" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "OARS = Open questions, Affirmations, Reflective listening, Summaries; the core micro-skills of MI used to elicit change talk." },
  { id: "m1_lang09", section: "language",
    q: "Read the passage and answer:\n\n'Cognitive behaviour therapy (CBT) rests on the premise that distorted thinking patterns sustain emotional distress. Identifying and modifying these patterns can therefore alleviate the distress, even when the original triggers cannot be changed. Empirical trials over five decades support its effectiveness across a range of disorders.'\n\nThe central idea of the passage is:",
    options: { a: "CBT cures every psychological disorder",
               b: "CBT works by changing distorted thoughts that maintain distress",
               c: "Triggers of distress are easy to remove",
               d: "Empirical trials are unnecessary in psychotherapy" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Main idea: identifying and modifying distorted thoughts reduces distress. Other options overstate or misrepresent the passage." },
  { id: "m1_bst09", section: "biostats",
    q: "A correlation of r = +0.90 between hours studied and exam scores indicates that hours studied:",
    options: { a: "Cause higher scores",
               b: "Account for 90% of the variance in scores",
               c: "Have a strong positive linear association with scores",
               d: "Are causally unrelated to scores" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Correlation ≠ causation. r² = 0.81 explains 81% of variance, not 90%. r = +0.90 simply quantifies a strong positive linear association." },
  { id: "m1_bio10", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Long-term potentiation (LTP) in the hippocampus is critically dependent on which receptor?",
    options: { a: "AMPA receptor",
               b: "Muscarinic M1 receptor",
               c: "GABA-A receptor",
               d: "NMDA receptor" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "NMDA receptors act as coincidence detectors (need glutamate + depolarisation to displace Mg²⁺). Ca²⁺ entry through NMDA initiates LTP." },
  { id: "m1_abn10", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Borderline personality disorder is characterised primarily by:",
    options: { a: "Pattern of instability in relationships, self-image and affect, with marked impulsivity",
               b: "Pervasive grandiosity and lack of empathy",
               c: "Eccentric beliefs and odd speech without psychosis",
               d: "Detachment from social relationships and restricted emotion" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "BPD (Cluster B): unstable relationships, identity, affect; impulsivity; recurrent suicidal/self-harm behaviour; chronic emptiness; transient stress-related dissociation." },
  { id: "m1_dev10", section: "dev",
    q: "Harlow's surrogate mother experiments with rhesus monkeys demonstrated that:",
    options: { a: "Feeding is the primary basis of attachment",
               b: "Imprinting occurs only in birds",
               c: "Contact comfort is more important than feeding for attachment",
               d: "Attachment is solely instinctive and does not require contact" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Infants spent more time on cloth (no food) than wire (food) mothers, refuting the secondary-drive (cupboard-love) theory of attachment." },
  { id: "m1_ass10", section: "assessment",
    q: "A test–retest reliability coefficient is most appropriate when assessing:",
    options: { a: "Internal consistency of items",
               b: "Inter-rater agreement",
               c: "Equivalence of two parallel forms",
               d: "Stability of scores over time" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Test-retest reliability = stability over time. Cronbach's alpha = internal consistency. Parallel-forms = equivalence. Cohen's kappa / ICC = inter-rater." },
  { id: "m1_the10", section: "therapy",
    q: "Acceptance and Commitment Therapy (ACT) aims primarily to increase:",
    options: { a: "Symptom elimination",
               b: "Psychological flexibility through six core processes",
               c: "Insight into childhood conflicts",
               d: "Cognitive disputation of irrational beliefs" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "ACT (Hayes) targets psychological flexibility via six processes: acceptance, defusion, present-moment awareness, self-as-context, values, and committed action — the 'hexaflex'." },
  { id: "m1_lang10", section: "language",
    q: "Per the same passage, the author's tone toward CBT is best described as:",
    options: { a: "Dismissive",
               b: "Sarcastic",
               c: "Supportive but qualified",
               d: "Polemical" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "The author is positive but uses qualifiers ('a range of', 'can alleviate') without overclaiming. Not dismissive, sarcastic, or polemical." },
  { id: "m1_bst10", section: "biostats",
    q: "The CONSORT statement provides reporting guidelines for:",
    options: { a: "Systematic reviews",
               b: "Qualitative research",
               c: "Diagnostic accuracy studies",
               d: "Randomised controlled trials" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "CONSORT = Consolidated Standards of Reporting Trials, for RCTs. PRISMA = systematic reviews; STARD = diagnostic accuracy; COREQ = qualitative." },
  { id: "m1_bio11", section: "biopsych",
    q: "The hormone secreted by the posterior pituitary that promotes water reabsorption is:",
    options: { a: "Oxytocin",
               b: "Cortisol",
               c: "Vasopressin (ADH)",
               d: "Prolactin" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "ADH (vasopressin) is synthesised in the supraoptic nucleus of the hypothalamus and released from the posterior pituitary. It acts on renal collecting ducts." },
  { id: "m1_abn11", section: "abnormal",
    q: "The most common comorbidity with autism spectrum disorder in DSM-5 is:",
    options: { a: "Conduct disorder",
               b: "Schizophrenia",
               c: "Bipolar I",
               d: "Intellectual disability and/or ADHD" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Intellectual disability is comorbid in roughly half of ASD cases; ADHD is also highly comorbid (DSM-5 now permits dual ASD + ADHD diagnosis)." },
  { id: "m1_dev11", section: "dev",
    q: "Telegraphic speech, characterised by two-word utterances such as 'mommy go', typically appears around:",
    options: { a: "18–24 months",
               b: "12 months",
               c: "6 months",
               d: "36 months" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Two-word telegraphic stage emerges around 18–24 months, after the holophrastic (one-word) stage at ~12 months." },
  { id: "m1_ass11", section: "assessment",
    q: "The NEO-PI-R measures personality based on:",
    options: { a: "Cattell's 16 factors",
               b: "Eysenck's PEN model",
               c: "The Five-Factor Model (OCEAN)",
               d: "Murray's needs-press theory" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "NEO-PI-R (Costa & McCrae) measures the Big Five: Neuroticism, Extraversion, Openness, Agreeableness, Conscientiousness; with 6 facets per domain." },
  { id: "m1_the11", section: "therapy",
    q: "EMDR was developed by:",
    options: { a: "Francine Shapiro",
               b: "Marsha Linehan",
               c: "Aaron Beck",
               d: "Edna Foa" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Francine Shapiro developed EMDR (Eye Movement Desensitisation and Reprocessing) in 1987 for trauma; recognised by APA and WHO for PTSD treatment." },
  { id: "m1_lang11", section: "language",
    q: "Find the error: 'Neither the patient nor the family members was informed about the diagnosis.'",
    options: { a: "Neither",
               b: "the patient",
               c: "no error",
               d: "was" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "With 'neither/nor', the verb agrees with the nearer subject. 'Family members' (plural) is closer, so the verb must be 'were'." },
  { id: "m1_bio12", section: "biopsych",
    q: "REM sleep is characterised by all EXCEPT:",
    options: { a: "Rapid eye movements",
               b: "Predominantly delta-wave EEG",
               c: "Skeletal muscle atonia",
               d: "Penile/clitoral tumescence" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "REM EEG is mixed, low-voltage, fast — similar to waking. Delta (slow-wave) activity is the hallmark of NREM Stage 3." },
  { id: "m1_abn12", section: "abnormal",
    q: "PTSD per DSM-5-TR requires symptoms from how many symptom clusters?",
    options: { a: "4",
               b: "3",
               c: "2",
               d: "5" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Four clusters: intrusion (B), avoidance (C), negative alterations in cognitions/mood (D), and arousal/reactivity (E). Duration >1 month." },
  { id: "m1_dev12", section: "dev",
    q: "In Bowlby's theory, the phase during which an infant displays clear attachment behaviour to one specific caregiver and shows separation anxiety is:",
    options: { a: "Pre-attachment",
               b: "Clear-cut attachment",
               c: "Attachment-in-the-making",
               d: "Reciprocal relationships" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Clear-cut attachment phase (~7 months–2 yrs): focused attachment to primary figure, separation protest, stranger wariness." },
  { id: "m1_ass12", section: "assessment",
    q: "If a raw score lies at the 84th percentile on a normally distributed test, the corresponding z-score is approximately:",
    options: { a: "0",
               b: "+1",
               c: "+1.5",
               d: "+2" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "On a normal distribution, +1 SD ≈ 84th percentile (mean ≈ 50th, +1 SD ≈ 84th, +2 SD ≈ 97.5th)." },
  { id: "m1_the12", section: "therapy",
    q: "In family therapy, the structural approach is associated with:",
    options: { a: "Salvador Minuchin",
               b: "Murray Bowen",
               c: "Virginia Satir",
               d: "Jay Haley" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Minuchin's structural family therapy focuses on family subsystems, boundaries (rigid, clear, diffuse), and hierarchies. Bowen = intergenerational; Satir = experiential; Haley = strategic." },
  { id: "m1_lang12", section: "language",
    q: "Choose the correct passive form of: 'The committee will announce the results tomorrow.'",
    options: { a: "The results will be announced by the committee tomorrow.",
               b: "The results are being announced by the committee tomorrow.",
               c: "The results have been announced by the committee tomorrow.",
               d: "The results were announced by the committee tomorrow." },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Active future simple → passive future simple = 'will be announced'." },
  { id: "m1_bio13", section: "biopsych",
    q: "A patient with damage to the right parietal lobe is most likely to show:",
    options: { a: "Broca's aphasia",
               b: "Anterograde amnesia",
               c: "Hemispatial neglect of the left side",
               d: "Prosopagnosia" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Right inferior parietal lesions classically cause left hemispatial (unilateral) neglect — patients ignore stimuli on the left side of space." },
  { id: "m1_abn13", section: "abnormal",
    q: "A delirium differs from a major neurocognitive disorder primarily in:",
    options: { a: "Presence of hallucinations only in delirium",
               b: "Memory impairment only in delirium",
               c: "Acute onset and fluctuating course with disturbance of attention/awareness",
               d: "Reversibility only in dementia" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Delirium has acute onset, fluctuating course, and a primary disturbance of attention and awareness; major NCD has insidious onset and stable cognitive decline." },
  { id: "m1_dev13", section: "dev",
    q: "Theory of mind is most commonly assessed in 4–5 year olds using the:",
    options: { a: "A-not-B task",
               b: "Conservation of liquid task",
               c: "False-belief (Sally-Anne) task",
               d: "Strange Situation" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Sally-Anne task probes whether the child understands that another person can hold a false belief; passed reliably around age 4." },
  { id: "m1_ass13", section: "assessment",
    q: "The Halstead-Reitan Battery is used for:",
    options: { a: "Personality assessment",
               b: "Vocational interest assessment",
               c: "Comprehensive neuropsychological assessment",
               d: "Projective assessment of children" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Halstead-Reitan: a fixed neuropsychological battery (Category Test, TPT, Trail Making, etc.) yielding the Impairment Index. Compare with the flexible Luria-Nebraska battery." },
  { id: "m1_the13", section: "therapy",
    q: "The therapeutic factor in group therapy described by Yalom as 'the realisation that one is not alone in their suffering' is:",
    options: { a: "Catharsis",
               b: "Universality",
               c: "Altruism",
               d: "Instillation of hope" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Yalom's universality: discovering that other group members share similar problems reduces shame and isolation. One of his 11 therapeutic factors." },
  { id: "m1_lang13", section: "language",
    q: "Choose the word closest in meaning to 'AMBIVALENT':",
    options: { a: "Having mixed feelings",
               b: "Indifferent",
               c: "Decisive",
               d: "Hostile" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Ambivalent = simultaneously holding opposing feelings or attitudes; not the same as indifferent (no feeling at all)." },
  { id: "m1_bio14", section: "biopsych",
    q: "The autosomal dominant disorder caused by a CAG trinucleotide repeat expansion on chromosome 4 is:",
    options: { a: "Down syndrome",
               b: "Phenylketonuria",
               c: "Fragile X syndrome",
               d: "Huntington's disease" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Huntington's disease: CAG expansion in the HTT gene on chromosome 4p. >36 repeats produces the disease; anticipation occurs across generations." },
  { id: "m1_abn14", section: "abnormal",
    q: "The biopsychosocial model in psychopathology is most associated with:",
    options: { a: "Emil Kraepelin",
               b: "Aaron T. Beck",
               c: "Eugen Bleuler",
               d: "George L. Engel" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Engel (1977) proposed the biopsychosocial model as a counter to the strictly biomedical model." },
  { id: "m1_dev14", section: "dev",
    q: "The temperamental category in the New York Longitudinal Study (Thomas & Chess) characterised by withdrawal from new stimuli but gradual adaptation is:",
    options: { a: "Easy",
               b: "Slow-to-warm-up",
               c: "Difficult",
               d: "Inhibited only" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Slow-to-warm-up children: low activity, mild negative responses to novelty, but gradual adaptation. Easy = positive mood + adaptable; Difficult = irregular + intense." },
  { id: "m1_ass14", section: "assessment",
    q: "Standardisation in test construction primarily refers to:",
    options: { a: "Ensuring high validity",
               b: "Eliminating cultural bias",
               c: "Establishing fixed administration and scoring procedures, plus norms",
               d: "Computer-administering the test" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Standardisation = uniform administration/scoring + a representative norm group. It is foundational; reliability and validity are separate properties." },
  { id: "m1_the14", section: "therapy",
    q: "Interpersonal Therapy (IPT) for depression focuses primarily on:",
    options: { a: "Childhood unconscious conflicts",
               b: "Current interpersonal problem areas (grief, role disputes, role transitions, interpersonal deficits)",
               c: "Cognitive distortions only",
               d: "Reinforcement schedules" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "IPT (Klerman & Weissman) is brief (12–16 sessions), focused on current relationships and one of four problem areas; strong evidence in MDD." },
  { id: "m1_lang14", section: "language",
    q: "Identify the correctly spelt word:",
    options: { a: "Conscentious",
               b: "Concientious",
               c: "Consientious",
               d: "Conscientious" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Conscientious = careful, principled. Note the -sci- and the -tious ending." },
  { id: "m1_bio15", section: "biopsych",
    q: "The Yerkes–Dodson law predicts that performance on a complex task is best at:",
    options: { a: "Very high arousal",
               b: "Very low arousal",
               c: "An arousal level independent of task difficulty",
               d: "Moderate arousal, with optimum lower for complex tasks" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Inverted-U relationship between arousal and performance. The optimum point is lower for complex/difficult tasks than for simple/well-learned ones." },
  { id: "m1_abn15", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Which of the following is NOT a Cluster A personality disorder?",
    options: { a: "Paranoid personality disorder",
               b: "Avoidant personality disorder",
               c: "Schizotypal personality disorder",
               d: "Schizoid personality disorder" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Avoidant PD belongs to Cluster C (anxious/fearful), along with Dependent and OCPD. Cluster A (odd/eccentric) = paranoid, schizoid, schizotypal." },
  { id: "m1_dev15", section: "dev",
    q: "In Freud's psychosexual theory, fixation at the anal stage is hypothesised to produce:",
    options: { a: "Oral aggression",
               b: "Oedipus complex",
               c: "Anal-retentive (orderly, stingy) or anal-expulsive personality",
               d: "Genital identity confusion" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Conflict over toilet training in the anal stage (~1.5–3 yrs); fixation may produce excessive orderliness and parsimony, or messy/disorganised traits." },
  { id: "m1_ass15", section: "assessment",
    q: "Item response theory (IRT) differs from classical test theory primarily in that IRT:",
    options: { a: "Treats item statistics as sample-dependent",
               b: "Always assumes a single difficulty parameter only",
               c: "Cannot be used for adaptive testing",
               d: "Models the probability of a correct response as a function of latent trait and item parameters" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "IRT models P(correct) as a logistic function of θ (ability) and item parameters (difficulty, discrimination, guessing). Enables computer-adaptive testing." },
  { id: "m1_the15", section: "therapy",
    q: "In CBT, a 'downward arrow' technique is used to:",
    options: { a: "Uncover the underlying core belief by repeatedly asking 'if that were true, what would it mean?'",
               b: "Identify behavioural antecedents",
               c: "Rate symptom intensity from 0 to 10",
               d: "Construct an exposure hierarchy" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Downward-arrow technique probes successive layers of meaning beneath an automatic thought to reach core beliefs (e.g., 'I am unlovable')." },
  { id: "m1_lang15", section: "language",
    q: "Choose the option that best completes the sentence: 'Had she taken the medication, she ___ recovered sooner.'",
    options: { a: "would recover",
               b: "had recovered",
               c: "will recover",
               d: "would have recovered" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Third conditional (counterfactual past): 'Had she + past participle... she would have + past participle'." }

],
  "met-mock-2": [
  // ===== BIOPSYCHOLOGY (30) =====
  { id: "m2_bio01", section: "biopsych",
    q: "The resting membrane potential of a typical neuron is maintained primarily by:",
    options: { a: "Equal permeability of the membrane to Na+ and K+ ions",
               b: "Higher permeability to K+ than Na+, with the Na+/K+ ATPase preserving gradients",
               c: "Active extrusion of Cl− ions only",
               d: "Continuous influx of Ca2+ through voltage-gated channels" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "RMP ≈ −70 mV reflects K+ leak channels (K+ much more permeable at rest) plus the 3Na+ out / 2K+ in pump that maintains the ionic gradients. Na+ permeability rises only when voltage-gated Na+ channels open at threshold." },
  { id: "m2_bio02", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Saltatory conduction depends most directly on:",
    options: { a: "A continuous distribution of Na+ channels along the entire axon",
               b: "Concentrated Na+ channels at the nodes of Ranvier between myelinated segments",
               c: "Schwann cells in the CNS and oligodendrocytes in the PNS",
               d: "Bidirectional propagation along dendrites" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Myelin insulates internodal segments; voltage-gated Na+ channels are clustered at the nodes of Ranvier, so the action potential 'jumps' node-to-node. Schwann cells myelinate PNS axons; oligodendrocytes myelinate CNS axons (option c reverses them)." },
  { id: "m2_bio03", section: "biopsych",
    q: "The mesolimbic dopamine pathway projects primarily from the ventral tegmental area to which structure, and is most implicated in:",
    options: { a: "Caudate–putamen; voluntary movement",
               b: "Median eminence; prolactin regulation",
               c: "Substantia nigra; Parkinsonian rigidity",
               d: "Nucleus accumbens; reward and positive symptoms of schizophrenia" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Mesolimbic = VTA → nucleus accumbens, central to reward/addiction and the positive psychotic symptoms targeted by D2 antagonists. Nigrostriatal (SN→striatum) governs movement; tuberoinfundibular (arcuate→median eminence) inhibits prolactin." },
  { id: "m2_bio04", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Which structure is NOT a component of the basal ganglia?",
    options: { a: "Caudate nucleus",
               b: "Globus pallidus",
               c: "Red nucleus",
               d: "Subthalamic nucleus" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Basal ganglia = caudate, putamen, globus pallidus, subthalamic nucleus, substantia nigra. The red nucleus sits in the midbrain tegmentum and belongs to the rubrospinal motor system, not the basal ganglia." },
  { id: "m2_bio05", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Wernicke's area is typically located in the:",
    options: { a: "Inferior frontal gyrus of the dominant hemisphere",
               b: "Posterior superior temporal gyrus of the dominant hemisphere",
               c: "Right parietal lobe",
               d: "Anterior cingulate cortex" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Wernicke's area = posterior superior temporal gyrus (BA 22), dominant (usually left) hemisphere. Lesions cause fluent but meaningless 'word-salad' speech with poor comprehension. Broca's is inferior frontal gyrus (BA 44/45)." },
  { id: "m2_bio06", section: "biopsych",
    q: "The HPA axis end-product cortisol exerts negative feedback primarily at the:",
    options: { a: "Hypothalamus (CRH) and anterior pituitary (ACTH)",
               b: "Pineal and posterior pituitary",
               c: "Adrenal medulla only",
               d: "Vagal motor nucleus" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Cortisol suppresses both hypothalamic CRH release and anterior pituitary ACTH release. Loss of this feedback (e.g., dexamethasone non-suppression) is a classic biomarker in melancholic depression." },
  { id: "m2_bio07", section: "biopsych",
    q: "NMDA receptors are critical for long-term potentiation because they:",
    options: { a: "Are blocked by Mg2+ at resting potential and require both depolarisation and glutamate binding",
               b: "Open in response to glycine alone",
               c: "Are exclusively inhibitory",
               d: "Trigger pre-synaptic vesicle fusion" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "NMDA is a coincidence detector: glutamate plus post-synaptic depolarisation expels the Mg2+ plug, allowing Ca2+ entry that triggers LTP. Bliss & Lømo (1973) first described LTP in hippocampus." },
  { id: "m2_bio08", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Damage to the lateral hypothalamus typically produces:",
    options: { a: "Hyperphagia and obesity",
               b: "Persistent thirst",
               c: "Loss of circadian rhythm",
               d: "Aphagia and weight loss" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Lateral hypothalamus = 'feeding centre' — lesions cause aphagia. Ventromedial nucleus = 'satiety centre' — lesions cause hyperphagia and obesity. SCN governs circadian rhythm." },
  { id: "m2_bio09", section: "biopsych",
    q: "The hippocampal patient H.M. (Henry Molaison) demonstrated that bilateral medial temporal lobectomy primarily impairs:",
    options: { a: "Procedural memory acquisition",
               b: "Declarative memory consolidation (anterograde amnesia)",
               c: "Working memory span",
               d: "Recognition of familiar faces from before surgery" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "H.M. could form new procedural memories (mirror-drawing) and retained working memory, but could not form new declarative (episodic/semantic) memories, establishing the hippocampus's role in memory consolidation rather than long-term storage." },
  { id: "m2_bio10", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Huntington's disease is associated with which genetic mutation?",
    options: { a: "CGG trinucleotide expansion on the X chromosome",
               b: "FMR1 silencing",
               c: "Deletion on chromosome 15 (paternal)",
               d: "CAG trinucleotide expansion in the HTT gene on chromosome 4" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Huntington: autosomal dominant, CAG repeat expansion in HTT on chromosome 4p, anticipation through paternal transmission. CGG/FMR1 = Fragile X. Chromosome 15 paternal deletion = Prader-Willi." },
  { id: "m2_bio11", section: "biopsych",
    q: "REM sleep is most reliably associated with:",
    options: { a: "Delta waves and growth hormone surge",
               b: "Low-voltage, mixed-frequency EEG, atonia, and rapid eye movements",
               c: "Sleep spindles and K-complexes",
               d: "Alpha waves dominant in occipital leads" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "REM EEG resembles wakefulness (low voltage, mixed frequency), with active muscle atonia (pons) and rapid eye movements. Delta = N3 (slow-wave). Spindles + K-complexes = N2. Alpha = relaxed wake with eyes closed." },
  { id: "m2_bio12", section: "biopsych",
    q: "The corticospinal tract crosses the midline primarily at the:",
    options: { a: "Spinal cord at the level of entry",
               b: "Pons",
               c: "Pyramidal decussation in the medulla",
               d: "Cerebral peduncles of the midbrain" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "About 85–90% of corticospinal fibres cross at the pyramidal decussation in the caudal medulla to form the lateral corticospinal tract. The smaller anterior corticospinal tract decussates at the level it exits in the cord." },
  { id: "m2_bio13", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Phineas Gage's well-documented post-injury changes (impulsivity, social disinhibition, poor judgement) are localised to damage of the:",
    options: { a: "Posterior parietal cortex",
               b: "Ventromedial prefrontal cortex",
               c: "Hippocampus and amygdala",
               d: "Cerebellar vermis" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "The tamping rod damaged Gage's ventromedial prefrontal cortex bilaterally, producing the personality change Harlow described. vmPFC integrates emotion with decision-making (later confirmed by Damasio's somatic marker work)." },
  { id: "m2_bio14", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Which cranial nerve carries parasympathetic fibres to most thoracic and abdominal viscera?",
    options: { a: "Trigeminal (V)",
               b: "Glossopharyngeal (IX)",
               c: "Vagus (X)",
               d: "Accessory (XI)" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "CN X (vagus) is the main parasympathetic outflow to heart, lungs, and most abdominal viscera up to the splenic flexure. CN III, VII, IX also carry parasympathetic fibres but to head structures only." },
  { id: "m2_bio15", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Korsakoff's syndrome is most directly caused by deficiency of:",
    options: { a: "Vitamin B12 (cobalamin)",
               b: "Niacin (B3)",
               c: "Vitamin B1 (thiamine)",
               d: "Folate (B9)" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Thiamine deficiency (commonly chronic alcohol use) damages mammillary bodies and dorsomedial thalamus, producing the Wernicke-Korsakoff triad of anterograde amnesia, confabulation, and apathy. B12 = subacute combined degeneration; niacin = pellagra." },
  { id: "m2_bio16", section: "biopsych",
    q: "The right parietal lobe lesion most classically produces:",
    options: { a: "Right-sided hemineglect",
               b: "Aphasia",
               c: "Bilateral homonymous hemianopia",
               d: "Left-sided hemineglect" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Hemispatial neglect is typically contralesional and most pronounced after right (non-dominant) parietal lesions — patients ignore the left half of space. Aphasia points to dominant (usually left) hemisphere damage." },
  { id: "m2_bio17", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Cones are most densely concentrated in which retinal region?",
    options: { a: "Optic disc",
               b: "Peripheral retina",
               c: "Ora serrata",
               d: "Fovea centralis" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Foveal cones provide high-acuity, colour photopic vision; the fovea has essentially no rods. Rods dominate the peripheral retina (scotopic vision). The optic disc is the physiological blind spot — no photoreceptors." },
  { id: "m2_bio18", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Which neurotransmitter is the principal inhibitory transmitter in the mature mammalian brain?",
    options: { a: "GABA",
               b: "Glutamate",
               c: "Acetylcholine",
               d: "Norepinephrine" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "GABA is the dominant inhibitory transmitter (glycine dominates in spinal cord). Glutamate is the principal excitatory transmitter. Benzodiazepines and barbiturates potentiate GABA-A receptor function." },
  { id: "m2_bio19", section: "biopsych",
    q: "The 'pleasure centre' identified by Olds and Milner (1954) corresponds to which structure?",
    options: { a: "Cerebellar tonsils",
               b: "Lateral geniculate nucleus",
               c: "Septal area / medial forebrain bundle",
               d: "Globus pallidus internus" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Rats self-stimulated electrodes implanted in the septal area / medial forebrain bundle to exhaustion, ignoring food. The pathway recruits the mesolimbic dopamine system that later defined reward circuitry." },
  { id: "m2_bio20", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Which feature distinguishes upper motor neuron (UMN) from lower motor neuron (LMN) lesions?",
    options: { a: "Fasciculations and atrophy with hypotonia in UMN lesions",
               b: "Hyperreflexia in LMN lesions",
               c: "Hyperreflexia, spasticity, and Babinski sign in UMN lesions",
               d: "Absent reflexes in UMN lesions" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "UMN lesions: hyperreflexia, spasticity, extensor plantar (Babinski), with disuse atrophy only. LMN lesions: hyporeflexia, flaccidity, marked atrophy, fasciculations, and fibrillations." },
  { id: "m2_bio21", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Which sleep stage is most associated with sleepwalking and night terrors?",
    options: { a: "REM sleep",
               b: "N1 (transition)",
               c: "N2 (light)",
               d: "N3 (slow-wave / delta)" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Parasomnias such as sleepwalking, night terrors, and confusional arousals arise from incomplete arousal out of N3 slow-wave sleep, typically in the first third of the night. REM is the home of nightmares and REM behaviour disorder." },
  { id: "m2_bio22", section: "biopsych",
    q: "The amygdala is most central to:",
    options: { a: "Verbal working memory",
               b: "Initiation of voluntary movement",
               c: "Fear conditioning and threat detection",
               d: "Long-term declarative memory storage" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "LeDoux's work established the amygdala (especially the central nucleus) as the hub of fear conditioning, threat detection, and emotional salience. Bilateral lesions cause Klüver-Bucy syndrome." },
  { id: "m2_bio23", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Antipsychotic-induced extrapyramidal side effects arise primarily from blockade of:",
    options: { a: "Mesolimbic D2 receptors",
               b: "Nigrostriatal D2 receptors",
               c: "Mesocortical D2 receptors",
               d: "Tuberoinfundibular D2 receptors" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Nigrostriatal blockade produces Parkinsonism, akathisia, acute dystonia, and tardive dyskinesia. Mesolimbic blockade treats positive symptoms; mesocortical blockade worsens negative/cognitive symptoms; tuberoinfundibular blockade raises prolactin." },
  { id: "m2_bio24", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Which structure serves as the principal relay for nearly all sensory modalities (except olfaction) to the cortex?",
    options: { a: "Hypothalamus",
               b: "Basal ganglia",
               c: "Thalamus",
               d: "Hippocampus" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "The thalamus relays visual (LGN), auditory (MGN), and somatosensory (VPL/VPM) inputs. Olfaction is the lone exception — projects directly from the olfactory bulb to the piriform cortex." },
  { id: "m2_bio25", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Split-brain studies by Sperry and Gazzaniga showed that:",
    options: { a: "Language is fully bilateral",
               b: "Each hemisphere can process information independently after corpus callosum section",
               c: "The right hemisphere controls verbal output in most right-handers",
               d: "Visual information from both visual fields reaches both hemispheres equally" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "After commissurotomy, stimuli presented only to the right hemisphere (left visual field) could not be named (since language is typically left-lateralised) but could be drawn or selected with the left hand — demonstrating independent processing." },
  { id: "m2_bio26", section: "biopsych",
    q: "The default mode network is most active during:",
    options: { a: "Rest, mind-wandering, and self-referential thought",
               b: "Externally focused goal-directed tasks",
               c: "Motor execution",
               d: "Early stages of visual processing" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "DMN (medial PFC, posterior cingulate / precuneus, angular gyrus) deactivates during external tasks and activates during rest, autobiographical memory, theory of mind, and self-referential processing. DMN abnormalities are reported in depression and schizophrenia." },
  { id: "m2_bio27", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Which brain area, when lesioned, classically produces transcortical motor aphasia?",
    options: { a: "Posterior superior temporal gyrus",
               b: "Angular gyrus",
               c: "Arcuate fasciculus",
               d: "Region anterior or superior to Broca's area, sparing Broca's itself" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Transcortical motor aphasia: non-fluent output with preserved repetition (Broca's intact). Arcuate fasciculus lesion → conduction aphasia (poor repetition). Angular gyrus → Gerstmann's syndrome." },
  { id: "m2_bio28", section: "biopsych",
    q: "The Papez circuit is a closed loop associated with emotional processing. Its components include:",
    options: { a: "Thalamus → primary motor cortex → cerebellum → thalamus",
               b: "Amygdala → caudate → putamen → globus pallidus → thalamus → amygdala",
               c: "Hippocampus → fornix → mammillary bodies → anterior thalamus → cingulate → entorhinal cortex → hippocampus",
               d: "Substantia nigra → striatum → globus pallidus → substantia nigra" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Papez (1937) proposed: hippocampus → fornix → mammillary bodies → mammillothalamic tract → anterior thalamus → cingulate gyrus → cingulum → entorhinal cortex → back to hippocampus. The amygdala was added later by MacLean's limbic system extension." },
  { id: "m2_bio29", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Which receptor subtype is the primary target of typical (first-generation) antipsychotics?",
    options: { a: "Serotonin 5-HT2A",
               b: "Dopamine D1",
               c: "Dopamine D2",
               d: "Muscarinic M1" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Typical antipsychotics are high-affinity D2 antagonists; clinical potency correlates closely with D2 affinity (Seeman & Lee). Atypicals add prominent 5-HT2A antagonism, which is thought to reduce EPS." },
  { id: "m2_bio30", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Cushing's syndrome can produce psychiatric symptoms most commonly characterised by:",
    options: { a: "Mania only",
               b: "Catatonia",
               c: "Depression, anxiety, and cognitive impairment, occasionally psychosis",
               d: "Selective deficit in olfaction" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Hypercortisolism most often produces depression (60%), with anxiety, irritability, and cognitive impairment also common. A minority develop mania or psychosis. Addison's (hypocortisolism) more often produces apathy and depression." },

  // ===== DEVELOPMENTAL (25) =====
  { id: "m2_dev01", section: "dev",
    q: "Object permanence is typically considered fully established by what age in Piaget's framework?",
    options: { a: "4–6 months",
               b: "8–10 months",
               c: "18–24 months",
               d: "3 years" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Although early signs appear from 8 months (A-not-B errors), Piaget held that full object permanence — including invisible displacements — completes by the end of the sensorimotor stage at 18–24 months." },
  { id: "m2_dev02", section: "dev",
    q: "Conservation of liquid volume is typically mastered during which stage?",
    options: { a: "Concrete operational",
               b: "Preoperational",
               c: "Sensorimotor",
               d: "Formal operational" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Concrete operational children (~7–11 yrs) acquire reversibility, decentration, and conservation. Number conservation typically precedes liquid (~7), then mass (~8), then weight (~9), then volume (~11) — Piaget's horizontal décalage." },
  { id: "m2_dev03", section: "dev",
    q: "Vygotsky's Zone of Proximal Development is best defined as:",
    options: { a: "The difference between mental age and chronological age",
               b: "The range of activities a child can do alone",
               c: "The gap between actual independent performance and what is achievable with guidance",
               d: "The genetic potential ceiling" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "ZPD = gap between what the learner can do independently and what they can do with scaffolding from a more competent other. Wood, Bruner, and Ross (1976) named the support 'scaffolding'." },
  { id: "m2_dev04", section: "dev",
    q: "In Erikson's framework, the crisis of adolescence is:",
    options: { a: "Industry vs Inferiority",
               b: "Identity vs Role Confusion",
               c: "Intimacy vs Isolation",
               d: "Initiative vs Guilt" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Adolescence (~12–18): Identity vs Role Confusion, virtue = fidelity. Industry vs Inferiority = school age (6–12); Intimacy vs Isolation = young adulthood; Initiative vs Guilt = preschool (3–6)." },
  { id: "m2_dev05", section: "dev",
    q: "In Ainsworth's Strange Situation, an infant who explores freely, shows mild distress on separation, and is easily soothed on reunion is classified as:",
    options: { a: "Secure (B)",
               b: "Insecure-avoidant (A)",
               c: "Insecure-resistant / ambivalent (C)",
               d: "Disorganised (D)" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Secure (B, ~65%): caregiver as secure base, mild separation distress, easy reunion. Avoidant: minimal distress, avoids reunion. Resistant: high distress, resists comfort. Disorganised: contradictory behaviours (Main & Solomon, 1990)." },
  { id: "m2_dev06", section: "dev",
    q: "Kohlberg's pre-conventional level is characterised by:",
    options: { a: "Universal ethical principles",
               b: "Social contract reasoning",
               c: "Conformity to interpersonal expectations",
               d: "Obedience to authority and self-interest orientation" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Pre-conventional (stages 1–2): obedience-punishment, then instrumental self-interest ('what's in it for me'). Conventional (3–4): good boy/girl, then law and order. Post-conventional (5–6): social contract, universal ethics." },
  { id: "m2_dev07", section: "dev",
    q: "Harlow's experiments with infant rhesus monkeys demonstrated that:",
    options: { a: "Food is the most powerful primary reinforcer in attachment",
               b: "Attachment is driven by contact comfort independent of feeding",
               c: "Imprinting occurs in primates within 24 hours of birth",
               d: "Maternal absence has no measurable effect if nutrition is met" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Infants preferred the cloth (non-feeding) surrogate over the wire (feeding) surrogate for contact comfort, refuting the secondary drive theory of attachment and supporting Bowlby's ethological model." },
  { id: "m2_dev08", section: "dev",
    q: "False-belief tasks like the Sally-Anne test assess:",
    options: { a: "Theory of mind",
               b: "Working memory capacity",
               c: "Conservation of number",
               d: "Object permanence" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Theory of mind = ability to attribute mental states (beliefs, desires) to others and recognise they may differ from one's own. Typically passed by ~4 years; failed in autism (Baron-Cohen, Leslie, & Frith, 1985)." },
  { id: "m2_dev09", section: "dev",
    q: "In Thomas and Chess's temperament categories, an infant showing initial withdrawal, slow adaptation, mild reactions, and low-to-moderate activity is best classified as:",
    options: { a: "Easy",
               b: "Difficult",
               c: "Inconsistent",
               d: "Slow-to-warm-up" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Slow-to-warm-up: low activity, initial withdrawal from novelty, gradual adaptation with repeated exposure, mild reactivity. Difficult: high intensity, irregular rhythms, withdrawal, slow adaptation. Easy: positive mood, regular rhythms, easy adaptation." },
  { id: "m2_dev10", section: "dev",
    q: "Marcia's identity status of 'foreclosure' is defined as:",
    options: { a: "Commitment without exploration",
               b: "Exploration without commitment",
               c: "Both exploration and commitment",
               d: "Neither exploration nor commitment" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Marcia's 2×2 of exploration × commitment: Foreclosure = commitment without exploration (often adopting parental values); Moratorium = exploration without commitment; Identity Achievement = both; Diffusion = neither." },
  { id: "m2_dev11", section: "dev",
    q: "Bronfenbrenner's bioecological model places the school–family relationship within which system?",
    options: { a: "Mesosystem",
               b: "Microsystem",
               c: "Exosystem",
               d: "Macrosystem" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Mesosystem = interconnections between two microsystems the child is part of (e.g., home–school link). Exosystem = settings affecting the child without their direct participation (parent's workplace). Macrosystem = cultural ideology." },
  { id: "m2_dev12", section: "dev",
    q: "The visual cliff experiment (Gibson & Walk, 1960) demonstrated:",
    options: { a: "Innate depth perception in human infants by crawling age",
               b: "That infants imprint on caregivers visually",
               c: "Object permanence by 6 months",
               d: "Audio-visual integration deficits" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Crawling-age infants and many other species refused to crawl across the apparent drop, suggesting depth perception is largely available by the time independent locomotion begins." },
  { id: "m2_dev13", section: "dev",
    q: "Babinski reflex (toes fan upward to plantar stimulation) is normal until approximately:",
    options: { a: "3 months",
               b: "12 months",
               c: "6 months",
               d: "24 months" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "An upgoing (extensor) plantar response is normal up to ~12 months while the corticospinal tract myelinates. Persistence beyond ~2 years, or re-emergence in adults, indicates UMN lesion." },
  { id: "m2_dev14", section: "dev",
    q: "Chomsky's Language Acquisition Device theory argues most directly that:",
    options: { a: "Language is acquired purely through operant conditioning",
               b: "Language requires explicit parental teaching of grammar rules",
               c: "Children possess an innate, biologically prepared capacity for grammar",
               d: "Bilingual children always lag in syntactic milestones" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Chomsky's poverty-of-the-stimulus argument: the input children receive is too impoverished and error-laden to explain rule acquisition by reinforcement alone (his critique of Skinner's Verbal Behavior, 1959); hence the LAD / Universal Grammar." },
  { id: "m2_dev15", section: "dev",
    q: "Mary Main's addition of the 'disorganised' attachment classification (D) was based on infants showing:",
    options: { a: "Consistent avoidance of the caregiver",
               b: "Contradictory, freezing, or apprehensive behaviour on reunion, often with maltreatment histories",
               c: "Easy soothing on reunion",
               d: "Resistance without distress" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Disorganised infants show contradictory behaviour (approach-avoidance, freezing, apprehension) on reunion; over-represented in samples with caregiver maltreatment or unresolved trauma (Main & Solomon, 1990)." },
  { id: "m2_dev16", section: "dev",
    q: "According to Piaget, hypothetico-deductive reasoning first emerges in the:",
    options: { a: "Formal operational stage",
               b: "Preoperational stage",
               c: "Concrete operational stage",
               d: "Sensorimotor stage" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Formal operational (~11+) brings abstract, hypothetical, propositional reasoning — including the systematic combinatorial logic Piaget tested with the pendulum problem and the bending rods task." },
  { id: "m2_dev17", section: "dev",
    q: "The Strange Situation classification typically holds across cultures, but in northern German samples, classification (A) is over-represented because:",
    options: { a: "Cultural emphasis on early independence leads to lower observable distress and reunion-seeking",
               b: "German infants are genetically less attached",
               c: "Researchers used a modified protocol",
               d: "German infants pass directly into adult attachment" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Grossmann's German samples showed elevated avoidant classifications, interpreted as a cultural rather than pathological pattern. Japanese samples often over-represent resistant (C) due to low typical separation experience." },
  { id: "m2_dev18", section: "dev",
    q: "Mahler's separation-individuation phase 'rapprochement' (~15–24 months) is characterised by:",
    options: { a: "Symbiotic merger with the mother",
               b: "Stranger anxiety onset",
               c: "Full object constancy",
               d: "Alternating between independent exploration and emotional refuelling from the caregiver" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Rapprochement subphase: the toddler oscillates between practising autonomy and seeking the mother for emotional refuelling. Object constancy (phase 4) consolidates by ~36 months." },
  { id: "m2_dev19", section: "dev",
    q: "The 'still-face paradigm' (Tronick) demonstrates:",
    options: { a: "Infants are passive recipients of caregiver mood",
               b: "Facial recognition does not develop before 12 months",
               c: "Even very young infants actively expect and seek affective reciprocity",
               d: "Infants prefer strangers to caregivers when distressed" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "When the mother holds a still, unresponsive face for 2 minutes, infants escalate bids for attention then withdraw — showing that they actively expect contingent emotional reciprocity from very early." },
  { id: "m2_dev20", section: "dev",
    q: "Which scale is used to assess infant developmental level from birth to 42 months across cognitive, language, and motor domains?",
    options: { a: "Wechsler Preschool and Primary Scale of Intelligence (WPPSI)",
               b: "McCarthy Scales of Children's Abilities",
               c: "Stanford-Binet Intelligence Scale",
               d: "Bayley Scales of Infant and Toddler Development" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Bayley-4 covers birth–42 months across cognitive, language, motor, social-emotional, and adaptive behaviour. WPPSI starts at 2:6; Stanford-Binet (current edition) starts at 2; McCarthy spans 2:6–8:6." },
  { id: "m2_dev21", section: "dev",
    q: "Kohlberg's Stage 3 ('good boy/nice girl') reasoning emphasises:",
    options: { a: "Avoiding punishment",
               b: "Universal ethical principles",
               c: "Maintaining social order via rules and authority",
               d: "Approval and meeting interpersonal expectations" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Stage 3 (early conventional) — moral worth defined by the approval of close others. Stage 4 adds the wider 'law and order' perspective. Stage 1 = punishment avoidance; Stage 6 = universal ethics." },
  { id: "m2_dev22", section: "dev",
    q: "Which developmental milestone typically appears LAST?",
    options: { a: "Social smile",
               b: "Symbolic play",
               c: "Stranger anxiety",
               d: "Pincer grasp" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Social smile ~2 mo, pincer grasp ~9–12 mo, stranger anxiety ~8–10 mo (peaks ~12–15 mo), symbolic / pretend play ~18–24 mo and elaborates through preschool — the last of these to appear." },
  { id: "m2_dev23", section: "dev",
    q: "Bowlby's clear-cut attachment phase, in which infants show clear preferences for primary caregivers and stranger anxiety, is typically:",
    options: { a: "0–2 months",
               b: "2–6 months",
               c: "6 months – 2 years",
               d: "2–4 years" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Bowlby's four phases: pre-attachment (0–2 mo, indiscriminate); attachment-in-the-making (2–6 mo, recognition); clear-cut attachment (6 mo–2 yrs, preference + stranger anxiety + separation protest); goal-corrected partnership (2+ yrs)." },
  { id: "m2_dev24", section: "dev",
    q: "Authoritative parenting (Baumrind) is best characterised by:",
    options: { a: "High demandingness, high responsiveness",
               b: "High demandingness, low responsiveness",
               c: "Low demandingness, high responsiveness",
               d: "Low demandingness, low responsiveness" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Baumrind's 2×2: Authoritative = high demand + high warmth (best child outcomes); Authoritarian = high demand + low warmth; Permissive = low demand + high warmth; Uninvolved/Neglectful = low demand + low warmth." },
  { id: "m2_dev25", section: "dev",
    q: "Centration in preoperational thought refers to:",
    options: { a: "Inability to mentally reverse operations",
               b: "Belief that inanimate objects have feelings",
               c: "Focusing on one salient feature while ignoring others",
               d: "Trouble taking another's spatial perspective" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Centration = focusing on a single dimension (e.g., height of liquid) while ignoring others (width) — drives failure on conservation tasks. Animism, egocentrism, and irreversibility are separate preoperational features." },

  // ===== ABNORMAL (30) =====
  { id: "m2_abn01", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Per DSM-5-TR, the minimum duration of symptoms for a diagnosis of schizophrenia is:",
    options: { a: "1 month of active-phase symptoms with continuous disturbance for at least 6 months",
               b: "1 week",
               c: "3 months",
               d: "12 months" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Schizophrenia: ≥2 of 5 symptoms (≥1 from delusions, hallucinations, disorganised speech) for ≥1 month active phase, with continuous disturbance ≥6 months. Schizophreniform = 1–6 months; brief psychotic = <1 month." },
  { id: "m2_abn02", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Bleuler's '4 As' of schizophrenia are:",
    options: { a: "Affect, Anhedonia, Avolition, Alogia",
               b: "Associations (loose), Affect (flat), Ambivalence, Autism",
               c: "Apathy, Anxiety, Anger, Aphasia",
               d: "Audible thoughts, Anhedonia, Anosognosia, Asociality" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Bleuler (1911) coined 'schizophrenia' and proposed primary symptoms: loose Associations, flat Affect, Ambivalence, Autism (withdrawal into inner world). Hallucinations and delusions were 'accessory' in his scheme — later Schneider's first-rank symptoms reversed the emphasis." },
  { id: "m2_abn03", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Persistent depressive disorder (dysthymia) in adults requires depressed mood for at least:",
    options: { a: "6 months",
               b: "1 year",
               c: "5 years",
               d: "2 years (1 year in children/adolescents)" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "PDD: depressed mood most of the day, more days than not, ≥2 years in adults (≥1 year in youth, where mood may be irritable), with ≥2 additional symptoms and no symptom-free interval >2 months." },
  { id: "m2_abn04", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Bipolar I disorder is distinguished from bipolar II by:",
    options: { a: "Presence of at least one major depressive episode",
               b: "Presence of at least one manic episode (≥7 days or any duration if hospitalisation required)",
               c: "Rapid cycling",
               d: "Absence of psychotic features" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Bipolar I requires ≥1 lifetime manic episode (≥7 days or any duration if hospitalised, or with psychosis). Bipolar II requires ≥1 hypomanic (≥4 days) plus ≥1 MDE, and never a full manic episode." },
  { id: "m2_abn05", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Per DSM-5-TR, the minimum duration for generalised anxiety disorder is:",
    options: { a: "1 month",
               b: "6 months",
               c: "3 months",
               d: "12 months" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "GAD requires excessive worry, more days than not, ≥6 months, plus ≥3 of 6 symptoms (restlessness, fatigue, concentration, irritability, muscle tension, sleep disturbance). ICD-11 also uses ≥6 months." },
  { id: "m2_abn06", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Capgras syndrome is characterised by the delusional belief that:",
    options: { a: "A familiar person has been replaced by an identical-looking impostor",
               b: "One is being persecuted by a secret organisation",
               c: "A stranger is actually a familiar person in disguise",
               d: "One's body has been stolen and replaced" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Capgras = 'impostor' delusion (familiar person replaced by a double). Fregoli is the inverse (different people are actually one familiar person in disguise). Both are delusional misidentification syndromes." },
  { id: "m2_abn07", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Schneider's first-rank symptoms include all EXCEPT:",
    options: { a: "Loose associations",
               b: "Audible thoughts (Gedankenlautwerden)",
               c: "Thought insertion",
               d: "Delusional perception" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Schneider FRS: audible thoughts, voices arguing/commenting, thought insertion/withdrawal/broadcast, somatic passivity, made affect/impulse, delusional perception. Loose associations are Bleulerian primary symptoms, not Schneiderian." },
  { id: "m2_abn08", section: "abnormal",
    q: "PTSD per DSM-5-TR requires symptoms from how many clusters?",
    options: { a: "2",
               b: "3",
               c: "5",
               d: "4 (intrusion, avoidance, negative cognitions/mood, arousal/reactivity)" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "DSM-5 separated the avoidance cluster into avoidance and negative cognitions/mood, yielding 4 symptom clusters (B intrusion, C avoidance, D negative alterations in cognition/mood, E arousal/reactivity). Duration ≥1 month." },
  { id: "m2_abn09", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Acute stress disorder is distinguished from PTSD primarily by:",
    options: { a: "Required dissociative symptoms",
               b: "Earlier onset of childhood symptoms",
               c: "A symptom duration of 3 days to 1 month after the trauma",
               d: "Inclusion in the dissociative disorders chapter" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "ASD: ≥9 symptoms from 5 categories, duration 3 days to 1 month post-trauma. PTSD requires ≥1 month duration. DSM-5 also dropped the requirement that ASD must include dissociative symptoms." },
  { id: "m2_abn10", section: "abnormal",
    q: "OCD vs OCPD: a key clinical distinction is that OCD obsessions/compulsions are:",
    options: { a: "Ego-dystonic (experienced as intrusive and distressing)",
               b: "Ego-syntonic",
               c: "Always reality-grounded",
               d: "Restricted to childhood" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "OCD: ego-dystonic — patients usually recognise obsessions as their own thoughts but unwanted and distressing. OCPD: ego-syntonic — the rigid, perfectionistic traits are experienced as right and rational. Two separate DSM-5 categories." },
  { id: "m2_abn11", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Conversion disorder (functional neurological symptom disorder) requires:",
    options: { a: "Intentional production of symptoms",
               b: "One or more neurological symptoms incompatible with recognised medical/neurological conditions",
               c: "Concomitant substance use",
               d: "Onset before age 5" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Conversion disorder: motor/sensory neurological symptoms with clinical evidence of incompatibility with neurological disease (e.g., Hoover's sign for functional weakness). NOT intentionally produced (that's factitious or malingering)." },
  { id: "m2_abn12", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Dissociative identity disorder requires:",
    options: { a: "Loss of consciousness on EEG",
               b: "A single fugue state lasting <24 hours",
               c: "Two or more distinct personality states with recurrent gaps in autobiographical memory",
               d: "Onset only after a sexual assault" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "DID: ≥2 distinct identity states (or experience of possession), recurrent gaps in recall for everyday events/personal information/trauma, clinically significant distress/impairment, not part of a broadly accepted cultural/religious practice or due to substances." },
  { id: "m2_abn13", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Anorexia nervosa per DSM-5 requires:",
    options: { a: "Significantly low body weight, intense fear of weight gain or behaviours that interfere with weight gain, and disturbance in body image",
               b: "BMI <17 in all cases",
               c: "Amenorrhoea (still required)",
               d: "Episodes of compensatory purging in all cases" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "DSM-5 dropped the amenorrhoea criterion. Required: (1) restriction → significantly low weight, (2) fear of gain or behaviours preventing gain, (3) body image disturbance. Severity coded by BMI (mild ≥17 down to extreme <15)." },
  { id: "m2_abn14", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Bulimia nervosa per DSM-5 requires binge-purge episodes occurring on average at least:",
    options: { a: "Twice per week for 6 months",
               b: "Once per week for 3 months",
               c: "Once per day for 1 month",
               d: "Three times per week for 1 month" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "DSM-5 reduced the BN frequency criterion from twice/week (DSM-IV) to once/week, for ≥3 months. Same frequency now applies to binge-eating disorder." },
  { id: "m2_abn15", section: "abnormal",
    q: "ADHD per DSM-5-TR requires symptoms to be present before what age?",
    options: { a: "12",
               b: "7",
               c: "6",
               d: "16" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "DSM-5 raised the age of onset criterion from <7 (DSM-IV) to several symptoms present before age 12. Adult diagnosis requires ≥5 (vs ≥6 in children) symptoms in each domain." },
  { id: "m2_abn16", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Antisocial personality disorder cannot be diagnosed before what age, and requires evidence of conduct disorder before what age?",
    options: { a: "Diagnosed before 16; CD before 12",
               b: "Diagnosed at or after 18; evidence of CD before age 15",
               c: "Diagnosed before 18; CD before 15",
               d: "Diagnosed only after 21" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "DSM-5 ASPD: age ≥18 for diagnosis, with evidence of conduct disorder onset before age 15. Adolescents with relevant features are diagnosed as conduct disorder, not ASPD." },
  { id: "m2_abn17", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Borderline personality disorder requires how many of nine criteria for diagnosis?",
    options: { a: "3",
               b: "4",
               c: "5",
               d: "6" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "BPD: ≥5 of 9 criteria (frantic efforts to avoid abandonment, unstable intense relationships, identity disturbance, impulsivity, suicidal/self-harm, affective instability, chronic emptiness, intense anger, transient stress-related paranoia/dissociation)." },
  { id: "m2_abn18", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Folie à deux is classified in ICD-11 under:",
    options: { a: "Delusional disorder, with another individual",
               b: "Brief and limited psychotic disorder",
               c: "Schizotypal disorder",
               d: "Shared psychotic disorder (induced delusional disorder)" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "ICD-11 retains 'induced delusional disorder' for shared delusions in a close relationship with the primary case. DSM-5 dropped shared psychotic disorder as a separate category — most cases now coded under 'other specified schizophrenia spectrum and other psychotic disorder'." },
  { id: "m2_abn19", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Conduct disorder with limited prosocial emotions specifier requires features such as:",
    options: { a: "Hyperactivity and inattention",
               b: "Comorbid major depression",
               c: "Lack of remorse, callous-lack of empathy, unconcerned about performance, shallow affect",
               d: "Onset before age 5" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "DSM-5 added the LPE specifier to capture callous-unemotional traits (lack of remorse/guilt, callous-lack of empathy, unconcerned about performance, shallow/deficient affect) — a marker of worse prognosis and stronger continuity with antisocial PD." },
  { id: "m2_abn20", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Pibloktoq, brain fag, and dhat are examples of:",
    options: { a: "DSM-5 anxiety disorder subtypes",
               b: "Substance-induced disorders",
               c: "ICD-11 dissociative disorders",
               d: "Culturally-bound or cultural concepts of distress" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Cultural concepts of distress (DSM-5 'cultural concepts of distress' appendix). Pibloktoq: Arctic hysteria. Brain fag: West Africa, students under stress. Dhat: South Asia, semen-loss anxiety. Koro, susto, ataque de nervios are other examples." },
  { id: "m2_abn21", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Major depressive episode requires how many of nine SIG-E-CAPS symptoms?",
    options: { a: "3 or more",
               b: "4 or more",
               c: "5 or more (one of which must be depressed mood or anhedonia)",
               d: "All 9" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "MDE: ≥5 of 9 (depressed mood, anhedonia, weight/appetite, sleep, psychomotor, fatigue, worthlessness/guilt, concentration, suicidality) for ≥2 weeks, including at least depressed mood or anhedonia, with functional impairment." },
  { id: "m2_abn22", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Body dysmorphic disorder is classified in DSM-5 under:",
    options: { a: "Somatic symptom and related disorders",
               b: "Personality disorders",
               c: "Anxiety disorders",
               d: "Obsessive-compulsive and related disorders" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "DSM-5 created the OCD-and-related-disorders chapter housing OCD, BDD, hoarding disorder, trichotillomania, and excoriation. BDD requires preoccupation with perceived defects unobservable or slight to others, with repetitive behaviours." },
  { id: "m2_abn23", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Narcissistic personality disorder's DSM-5 core triad is best summarised as:",
    options: { a: "Grandiosity, need for admiration, lack of empathy",
               b: "Impulsivity, identity disturbance, anger",
               c: "Suspiciousness, social anxiety, eccentricity",
               d: "Submissiveness, fear of separation, reassurance-seeking" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "NPD: pervasive grandiosity (in fantasy or behaviour), need for admiration, and lack of empathy — ≥5 of 9 criteria. The other options describe BPD, paranoid/schizotypal, and dependent PD respectively." },
  { id: "m2_abn24", section: "abnormal",
    q: "La belle indifférence — relative emotional indifference to a severe disability — is classically associated with:",
    options: { a: "Malingering",
               b: "Somatic symptom disorder",
               c: "Factitious disorder",
               d: "Conversion disorder" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Classically described in conversion disorder but neither sensitive nor specific — present in many other illnesses and absent in many conversions. DSM-5-TR explicitly notes it should not be used to confirm the diagnosis." },
  { id: "m2_abn25", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Factitious disorder imposed on another (Munchausen by proxy) is distinguished from malingering by:",
    options: { a: "Presence of dissociation",
               b: "Comorbid substance use",
               c: "Onset only in adolescence",
               d: "Absence of obvious external incentives — the motivation is to assume the sick role" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Factitious disorder: intentionally produced or feigned symptoms WITHOUT obvious external reward — motivation is the sick role (psychological). Malingering: same intentional production but FOR external reward (money, drugs, avoiding work)." },
  { id: "m2_abn26", section: "abnormal",
    q: "DSM-5 specifier 'with mixed features' applied to a manic or hypomanic episode requires concurrent symptoms of:",
    options: { a: "Psychosis",
               b: "Anxiety",
               c: "Depression",
               d: "Substance withdrawal" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "DSM-5 replaced the old 'mixed episode' with a 'with mixed features' specifier — present when manic/hypomanic/depressive episodes meet criteria for the opposite pole on most days. Captures more patients clinically than the rigid DSM-IV criteria." },
  { id: "m2_abn27", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Disruptive Mood Dysregulation Disorder (DMDD) onset must be before age:",
    options: { a: "6",
               b: "16",
               c: "12",
               d: "10" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "DMDD: severe recurrent temper outbursts (verbal or behavioural) inconsistent with developmental level, ≥3×/week, with persistently irritable/angry mood between outbursts. Onset before age 10, diagnosis between 6 and 18." },
  { id: "m2_abn28", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Anorexia nervosa is associated with which medical finding?",
    options: { a: "Hyperthyroidism",
               b: "Lanugo hair, bradycardia, hypokalaemia, amenorrhoea",
               c: "Polyphagia",
               d: "Hyperkalaemia and tachycardia" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "AN: lanugo, bradycardia, hypotension, hypothermia, amenorrhoea, low T3 (euthyroid sick), osteopenia, hypokalaemia (especially with purging), arrhythmia risk on refeeding (refeeding syndrome from rapid carb load)." },
  { id: "m2_abn29", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Specific phobia per DSM-5 requires duration of:",
    options: { a: "6 months",
               b: "1 month",
               c: "3 months",
               d: "1 week" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "DSM-5 standardised most adult anxiety durations to ≥6 months (specific phobia, social anxiety, agoraphobia, GAD, separation anxiety in adults). Panic disorder uses ≥1 month of worry about further attacks." },
  { id: "m2_abn30", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Schizoaffective disorder per DSM-5 requires:",
    options: { a: "A major mood episode concurrent with active-phase schizophrenia symptoms, PLUS ≥2 weeks of psychosis without prominent mood symptoms",
               b: "Mood symptoms only during acute psychosis",
               c: "Lifetime absence of psychosis",
               d: "Bipolar features only" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Schizoaffective: uninterrupted period of illness with major mood episode plus active-phase schizophrenia criterion A; ≥2 weeks of delusions/hallucinations without prominent mood symptoms lifetime; mood symptoms present majority of total illness. Bipolar or depressive type." },

  // ===== ASSESSMENT (25) =====
  { id: "m2_ass01", section: "assessment",
    q: "WAIS-IV yields four index scores:",
    options: { a: "Verbal IQ, Performance IQ, Full Scale IQ, Memory Quotient",
               b: "Crystallised IQ, Fluid IQ, Visual IQ, Auditory IQ",
               c: "Verbal Comprehension, Perceptual Reasoning, Working Memory, Processing Speed",
               d: "Cognitive, Behavioural, Emotional, Adaptive" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "WAIS-IV (2008): VCI, PRI, WMI, PSI — the four indices summing to FSIQ. WAIS-IV dropped the older Verbal/Performance dichotomy and added Processing Speed. WAIS-V (2024) restructures these into 5 indices." },
  { id: "m2_ass02", section: "assessment",
    q: "On the MMPI, the L (Lie) scale primarily detects:",
    options: { a: "Random responding",
               b: "Naive impression-management denying common minor faults",
               c: "Severe psychopathology",
               d: "Reading-level limitations" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "L scale catches naive 'fake-good' impression management — overly virtuous claims that most people would admit. F scale catches over-reporting / random / rare endorsements; K scale catches defensive 'fake good' more subtle than L; VRIN/TRIN catch inconsistent responding." },
  { id: "m2_ass03", section: "assessment",
    q: "The Vineland Adaptive Behaviour Scales measure:",
    options: { a: "Adaptive functioning in communication, daily living skills, socialisation, and motor skills",
               b: "Personality traits",
               c: "IQ",
               d: "Severity of psychopathology" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Vineland (Doll, 1935; current Vineland-3): adaptive behaviour across communication, daily living, socialisation, motor (in young children), with optional maladaptive index. Used alongside IQ to diagnose intellectual disability per DSM-5." },
  { id: "m2_ass04", section: "assessment",
    q: "On the Wechsler scales, an IQ of 85 corresponds to which percentile?",
    options: { a: "10th",
               b: "25th",
               c: "16th",
               d: "50th" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "IQ has M=100, SD=15. IQ 85 = −1 SD = 16th percentile. IQ 70 = −2 SD = ~2nd percentile. IQ 115 = +1 SD = 84th percentile. IQ 130 = +2 SD = ~98th percentile." },
  { id: "m2_ass05", section: "assessment",
    q: "The Thematic Apperception Test (TAT) was developed by:",
    options: { a: "Hermann Rorschach",
               b: "John Exner",
               c: "Leopold Bellak",
               d: "Henry Murray and Christiana Morgan" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "TAT (1935): Murray & Morgan; based on Murray's need-press theory. Bellak developed CAT for children. Exner created the Comprehensive System for Rorschach scoring. Rorschach himself developed the inkblots (1921)." },
  { id: "m2_ass06", section: "assessment",
    q: "On a normal distribution, what proportion of cases fall within ±2 standard deviations of the mean?",
    options: { a: "~50%",
               b: "~68%",
               c: "~95%",
               d: "~99.7%" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "68-95-99.7 rule: ±1 SD ≈ 68%, ±2 SD ≈ 95%, ±3 SD ≈ 99.7%. T scores: M=50, SD=10. IQ: M=100, SD=15. Z scores: M=0, SD=1. Stanines: M=5, SD=2." },
  { id: "m2_ass07", section: "assessment",
    q: "Cronbach's alpha measures:",
    options: { a: "Internal consistency reliability",
               b: "Test-retest reliability",
               c: "Construct validity",
               d: "Inter-rater reliability" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Cronbach's α: average of all possible split-half reliabilities → an estimate of internal consistency. Test-retest: same test, two timepoints. Inter-rater: Cohen's κ for categorical, ICC for continuous. Construct validity is shown via convergent/discriminant evidence." },
  { id: "m2_ass08", section: "assessment",
    q: "Predictive validity is best demonstrated by:",
    options: { a: "Asking experts whether the items 'look right'",
               b: "Correlating test scores with a future criterion measured later",
               c: "Splitting the test in half and correlating the halves",
               d: "Comparing the test to itself two weeks later" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Predictive validity = correlation of current test with future criterion (e.g., entrance test predicting end-of-year grades). Concurrent: criterion measured at the same time. Face validity: superficial 'looks right' judgement. Split-half is a reliability estimate, not validity." },
  { id: "m2_ass09", section: "assessment",
    q: "On the MMPI-2, T-scores of ≥65 are considered clinically significant. T=65 corresponds to:",
    options: { a: "+0.5 SD above the mean",
               b: "+1.0 SD above the mean",
               c: "+2.0 SD above the mean",
               d: "+1.5 SD above the mean" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "T scores: M=50, SD=10. T=65 → +1.5 SD (92nd percentile). The original MMPI used T=70 (+2 SD) cutoff. MMPI-2 reset to T=65 because the renormative sample produced fewer high scores at the +2 SD cutoff." },
  { id: "m2_ass10", section: "assessment",
    q: "The Bender Visual-Motor Gestalt Test is primarily used to screen for:",
    options: { a: "Personality structure",
               b: "Severity of depression",
               c: "Reading comprehension",
               d: "Visual-motor integration and neurological dysfunction" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Bender Gestalt (Lauretta Bender, 1938; BG-II 2003): patient copies 9 (now 16) Gestalt designs, scored for visual-motor integration. Originally a screen for brain dysfunction; also used developmentally and projectively." },
  { id: "m2_ass11", section: "assessment",
    q: "WISC-V is normed for what age range?",
    options: { a: "2:6 – 7:7",
               b: "0 – 42 months",
               c: "16:0 – 90:11",
               d: "6:0 – 16:11" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "WISC-V (2014): 6:0–16:11. WPPSI-IV: 2:6–7:7. WAIS-IV: 16:0–90:11. Bayley-4: 1–42 months. WISC-V indices: VCI, VSI (renamed), FRI (new), WMI, PSI." },
  { id: "m2_ass12", section: "assessment",
    q: "Standard error of measurement (SEM) is calculated as:",
    options: { a: "SD × √reliability",
               b: "Reliability × validity",
               c: "SD × √(1 − reliability)",
               d: "Mean − SD" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "SEM = SD × √(1 − r_xx). Lower reliability or higher SD → larger SEM → wider confidence band around the observed score. Used to build 68% (±1 SEM) or 95% (±1.96 SEM) confidence intervals." },
  { id: "m2_ass13", section: "assessment",
    q: "The 16PF was developed by:",
    options: { a: "Hans Eysenck",
               b: "Raymond Cattell",
               c: "Paul Costa and Robert McCrae",
               d: "Gordon Allport" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Cattell's 16 Personality Factors (1949) derived via factor analysis from his lexical/L-Q-T data. Eysenck's PEN/EPQ → 3 factors. Costa & McCrae → NEO-PI-R (Big Five). Allport → trait theory (cardinal/central/secondary)." },
  { id: "m2_ass14", section: "assessment",
    q: "Reliability sets the ceiling for validity such that the maximum possible validity coefficient equals:",
    options: { a: "Square root of reliability (when the criterion is perfectly reliable)",
               b: "Square of reliability",
               c: "Reliability itself",
               d: "Reliability minus SEM" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Validity ceiling = √(r_xx × r_yy). When the criterion is perfectly reliable (r_yy = 1), max validity = √r_xx. A test with reliability .64 has a maximum validity coefficient of .80." },
  { id: "m2_ass15", section: "assessment",
    q: "The Rorschach Comprehensive System was developed by:",
    options: { a: "Beck",
               b: "Exner",
               c: "Klopfer",
               d: "Piotrowski" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "John Exner (1974) integrated the five earlier American Rorschach systems (Beck, Klopfer, Hertz, Piotrowski, Rapaport-Schafer) into the empirically-anchored Comprehensive System, the dominant scoring approach since. Now succeeded by R-PAS (Meyer et al., 2011)." },
  { id: "m2_ass16", section: "assessment",
    q: "Concurrent validity differs from predictive validity in that:",
    options: { a: "Criterion is measured before the test",
               b: "Both criterion and test are measured at the same time",
               c: "It cannot be quantified",
               d: "It is a form of internal consistency" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Concurrent validity: test and criterion measured at the same time (e.g., new depression scale vs HAM-D today). Predictive validity: criterion measured in the future (e.g., GRE today vs grad GPA in 2 years). Both are criterion-related validity." },
  { id: "m2_ass17", section: "assessment",
    q: "The K (correction) scale on the MMPI is used to:",
    options: { a: "Flag random responding",
               b: "Add a correction to certain clinical scales to compensate for defensive responding",
               c: "Measure depression",
               d: "Detect inconsistent responding" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "K scale captures subtle defensiveness/test-taking attitude and is used to add a K-correction to scales 1, 4, 7, 8, 9 to compensate. L = naive faking good; F = faking bad / random / rare; VRIN/TRIN = inconsistency indices." },
  { id: "m2_ass18", section: "assessment",
    q: "On the WAIS-IV, an FSIQ of 70 typically corresponds to which classification (using DSM-5 framing)?",
    options: { a: "Average",
               b: "Borderline intellectual functioning; ID requires deficits in adaptive functioning",
               c: "Mild intellectual disability solely on score",
               d: "Profound intellectual disability" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "DSM-5 ID requires deficits in BOTH intellectual functioning (~2 SD below mean, IQ ~65–75 allowing for SEM) AND adaptive functioning. IQ alone cannot diagnose ID. Severity (mild/moderate/severe/profound) is set by adaptive functioning, not IQ ranges." },
  { id: "m2_ass19", section: "assessment",
    q: "Murray's need-press theory is the conceptual basis for which projective test?",
    options: { a: "Rorschach",
               b: "Sentence Completion Test",
               c: "TAT",
               d: "Word Association Test" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Murray's TAT scoring identifies the protagonist's needs (achievement, affiliation, aggression) and the environmental press operating on them. Word association: Jung. Sentence completion: Rotter ISB and others. Rorschach: perceptual organisation." },
  { id: "m2_ass20", section: "assessment",
    q: "The Beck Depression Inventory-II measures depression severity using:",
    options: { a: "21 items rated 0–3 corresponding to symptoms over the past two weeks",
               b: "31 items rated 0–4",
               c: "10 items rated 0–10",
               d: "1 item rated 0–100" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "BDI-II (1996): 21 items, each 0–3 (range 0–63), past two weeks. Cutoffs: 0–13 minimal, 14–19 mild, 20–28 moderate, 29–63 severe. HAM-D is clinician-rated, 17 or 21 items." },
  { id: "m2_ass21", section: "assessment",
    q: "The standard score where M=10 and SD=3 is called:",
    options: { a: "Z score",
               b: "Scaled score",
               c: "T score",
               d: "Stanine" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Scaled score (used for WAIS/WISC subtests): M=10, SD=3 (range 1–19). T scores: M=50, SD=10. Z scores: M=0, SD=1. Stanines: M=5, SD≈2, range 1–9. IQ: M=100, SD=15." },
  { id: "m2_ass22", section: "assessment",
    q: "The NEO-PI-R measures personality on:",
    options: { a: "3 dimensions (PEN)",
               b: "16 source traits",
               c: "5 broad domains, each with 6 facets",
               d: "11 personality disorder scales" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Costa & McCrae's NEO-PI-R (1992): Big Five domains (O, C, E, A, N), each subdivided into 6 facets — 30 facets in total, 240 items. NEO-FFI is the 60-item short form measuring domains only." },
  { id: "m2_ass23", section: "assessment",
    q: "Y-BOCS is used to assess severity of:",
    options: { a: "Schizophrenia symptoms",
               b: "Obsessive-compulsive disorder",
               c: "Depression",
               d: "PTSD" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Yale-Brown Obsessive Compulsive Scale (Goodman et al., 1989): 10 items rating time, interference, distress, resistance, and control over obsessions and compulsions; severity 0–40, with cutoffs at 8/16/24/32." },
  { id: "m2_ass24", section: "assessment",
    q: "A scaled score of 7 on a WAIS-IV subtest corresponds to which classification?",
    options: { a: "Profoundly impaired",
               b: "Significantly above average",
               c: "Borderline impairment",
               d: "Within average range (1 SD below mean)" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Scaled scores M=10, SD=3. A scaled score of 7 = −1 SD = ~16th percentile — within the typical 'low average to average' range (not impaired). Scaled score 4 = −2 SD = borderline; ≤3 = impaired." },
  { id: "m2_ass25", section: "assessment",
    q: "Test-retest reliability is most appropriate for:",
    options: { a: "Measures of rapidly changing state",
               b: "Stable trait measures",
               c: "Single-item measures only",
               d: "Open-ended interviews only" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Test-retest assumes the underlying construct is stable across the interval; ideal for traits (personality, IQ). For states (mood today), low test-retest is expected and does not indicate poor reliability. Choose interval long enough to avoid memory but short enough to avoid real change." },

  // ===== PSYCHOTHERAPY (25) =====
  { id: "m2_the01", section: "therapy",
    q: "Beck's cognitive triad in depression refers to negative views of:",
    options: { a: "Id, ego, superego",
               b: "Mother, father, self",
               c: "Past, present, future",
               d: "Self, world, future" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Beck's cognitive triad: negative views of (1) self ('I'm worthless'), (2) world/experience ('nothing works out'), (3) future ('it'll never get better'). Central to his cognitive model of depression and treatment target in CBT." },
  { id: "m2_the02", section: "therapy",
    q: "The 'empty chair' technique is most associated with:",
    options: { a: "Gestalt therapy (Perls)",
               b: "CBT",
               c: "Person-centred therapy",
               d: "Psychoanalysis" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Fritz Perls' Gestalt therapy: empty-chair / two-chair dialogues with disowned parts of the self or absent others, promoting integration. Greenberg's later emotion-focused therapy retained the chair work in a process-experiential frame." },
  { id: "m2_the03", section: "therapy",
    q: "Carl Rogers' three core conditions for therapeutic change are:",
    options: { a: "Transference, free association, interpretation",
               b: "Cognitive restructuring, behavioural activation, exposure",
               c: "Unconditional positive regard, congruence, empathic understanding",
               d: "Working alliance, transference, resistance interpretation" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Rogers (1957): the necessary and sufficient conditions are UPR, congruence (genuineness), and accurate empathic understanding offered by the therapist and perceived by the client." },
  { id: "m2_the04", section: "therapy",
    q: "Systematic desensitisation, developed by Joseph Wolpe, combines:",
    options: { a: "Aversive conditioning with relaxation",
               b: "Flooding with cognitive disputation",
               c: "Graded exposure to a feared hierarchy with deep relaxation (reciprocal inhibition)",
               d: "Operant shaping with token reinforcement" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Wolpe (1958): teach progressive muscle relaxation, build a fear hierarchy, then expose gradually in imagination or in vivo while maintaining relaxation — reciprocal inhibition replacing anxiety with the incompatible relaxed state." },
  { id: "m2_the05", section: "therapy",
    q: "Ayllon and Azrin pioneered which behavioural intervention in inpatient settings?",
    options: { a: "Aversion therapy",
               b: "Token economy",
               c: "Time-out",
               d: "Modelling" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Ayllon & Azrin (1968) demonstrated the systematic use of secondary reinforcers (tokens) exchangeable for back-up reinforcers on chronic psychiatric wards — the prototype token economy. Built on Skinner's operant principles." },
  { id: "m2_the06", section: "therapy",
    q: "DBT was developed for the primary treatment of:",
    options: { a: "Schizophrenia",
               b: "OCD",
               c: "Borderline personality disorder",
               d: "Anorexia nervosa" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Marsha Linehan's DBT (1993) was designed for BPD with chronic suicidality. Synthesises change (CBT) with acceptance (Zen). Standard package: weekly individual, skills group, telephone coaching, therapist consultation team." },
  { id: "m2_the07", section: "therapy",
    q: "The DBT skills training modules include:",
    options: { a: "Free association, dream work, interpretation, working through",
               b: "Mindfulness, distress tolerance, emotion regulation, interpersonal effectiveness",
               c: "Object relations, transference, splitting, projection",
               d: "Schema analysis, mode work, limited reparenting" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Four DBT skill modules: mindfulness (the core), distress tolerance, emotion regulation, interpersonal effectiveness. Distractors here = psychoanalysis (free association, dream work), object relations / Kernberg (splitting, projection), and schema therapy / Young (schema analysis, mode work)." },
  { id: "m2_the08", section: "therapy",
    q: "EMDR was developed by:",
    options: { a: "Francine Shapiro",
               b: "Edna Foa",
               c: "Aaron Beck",
               d: "Marsha Linehan" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Shapiro (1987, formalised 1989): EMDR uses bilateral stimulation while the patient holds the traumatic memory in mind; 8-phase protocol. APA, WHO, and ISTSS endorse EMDR as a first-line PTSD treatment." },
  { id: "m2_the09", section: "therapy",
    q: "Prolonged exposure for PTSD was developed primarily by:",
    options: { a: "Foa and colleagues",
               b: "Resick (Cognitive Processing Therapy)",
               c: "Shapiro (EMDR)",
               d: "Linehan (DBT)" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Edna Foa's Prolonged Exposure (PE) for PTSD combines in vivo and imaginal exposure to the traumatic memory. Resick developed CPT, which is more cognitive-restructuring focused. Both are first-line per VA/DoD and APA guidelines." },
  { id: "m2_the10", section: "therapy",
    q: "Ellis's REBT 'ABC' model stands for:",
    options: { a: "Antecedent, Behaviour, Consequence",
               b: "Attention, Behaviour, Cognition",
               c: "Activating event, Belief, Consequence",
               d: "Affect, Behaviour, Coping" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Ellis: A (activating event) → B (belief, especially irrational) → C (emotional/behavioural consequence). Therapy adds D (disputing the irrational belief) and E (effective new philosophy). Skinner's behavioural ABC uses Antecedent-Behaviour-Consequence — distinct framework." },
  { id: "m2_the11", section: "therapy",
    q: "Yalom's existential 'four ultimate concerns' are:",
    options: { a: "Past, present, future, eternity",
               b: "Death, freedom, isolation, meaninglessness",
               c: "Love, work, play, transcendence",
               d: "Birth, life, death, rebirth" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Yalom's existential psychotherapy (1980): death, freedom (and the responsibility it brings), isolation, meaninglessness. Mnemonic 'Death Frees Isolated Minds'. Separate from Yalom's 11 group therapeutic factors (instillation of hope, etc.)." },
  { id: "m2_the12", section: "therapy",
    q: "Yalom's therapeutic factors in group therapy include:",
    options: { a: "Free association and interpretation",
               b: "Universality, instillation of hope, altruism, corrective recapitulation, cohesiveness (and others)",
               c: "Token reinforcement and shaping",
               d: "Schema reconstruction and mode work" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Yalom's 11 factors (Theory and Practice of Group Psychotherapy): universality, instillation of hope, imparting information, altruism, corrective recapitulation of primary family, socialising techniques, imitative behaviour, interpersonal learning, group cohesiveness, catharsis, existential factors." },
  { id: "m2_the13", section: "therapy",
    q: "Logotherapy was founded by:",
    options: { a: "Irvin Yalom",
               b: "Rollo May",
               c: "Viktor Frankl",
               d: "Karen Horney" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Frankl (Man's Search for Meaning, 1946): logotherapy locates the primary motivation as the will to meaning. Techniques: paradoxical intention, dereflection, Socratic dialogue toward meaning discovery." },
  { id: "m2_the14", section: "therapy",
    q: "Minuchin's structural family therapy focuses primarily on:",
    options: { a: "Multigenerational transmission of anxiety",
               b: "Subsystems, boundaries (rigid, clear, diffuse), and hierarchies within the family",
               c: "Cognitive distortions in family members",
               d: "Paradoxical injunctions" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Minuchin's structural therapy: maps family subsystems (parental, sibling, etc.) and the boundaries between them — pathology lies in rigid (disengaged) or diffuse (enmeshed) boundaries. Joining + enactment + boundary-making are key interventions." },
  { id: "m2_the15", section: "therapy",
    q: "Bowen family systems therapy emphasises:",
    options: { a: "Narrative externalisation",
               b: "Strategic paradox and prescribing the symptom",
               c: "Differentiation of self, triangulation, and multigenerational transmission",
               d: "Solution-focused exception questions" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Bowen's 8 concepts: differentiation of self, triangles, nuclear family emotional process, family projection, multigenerational transmission, emotional cutoff, sibling position, societal regression. Genograms are his signature tool. Strategic = Haley/Madanes." },
  { id: "m2_the16", section: "therapy",
    q: "Schema therapy was developed by:",
    options: { a: "Aaron Beck",
               b: "Jeffrey Young",
               c: "Marsha Linehan",
               d: "Steven Hayes" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Jeffrey Young (1990s) developed schema therapy as an integrative extension of CBT for personality disorders, particularly BPD. Adds early maladaptive schemas, schema modes, and limited reparenting to a CBT skeleton with experiential and psychodynamic elements." },
  { id: "m2_the17", section: "therapy",
    q: "Acceptance and Commitment Therapy was developed by:",
    options: { a: "Steven Hayes and colleagues",
               b: "Marsha Linehan",
               c: "Aaron Beck",
               d: "Albert Ellis" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Hayes, Strosahl, & Wilson developed ACT, which targets psychological flexibility via the hexaflex (acceptance, defusion, present moment, self-as-context, values, committed action). Rests on Relational Frame Theory." },
  { id: "m2_the18", section: "therapy",
    q: "The 'Premack principle' states that:",
    options: { a: "Higher-frequency behaviours can reinforce lower-frequency behaviours",
               b: "Punishment must follow behaviour immediately",
               c: "Continuous reinforcement is most resistant to extinction",
               d: "Two unconditioned stimuli cannot be paired" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Premack (1965): access to a high-probability behaviour reinforces a low-probability behaviour ('eat your vegetables, then you can play'). Foundational for token economies and behavioural contingency contracts." },
  { id: "m2_the19", section: "therapy",
    q: "Bandura's social learning theory introduced the construct of:",
    options: { a: "Reciprocal inhibition",
               b: "Reciprocal determinism and observational (vicarious) learning",
               c: "Reciprocal altruism",
               d: "Reciprocal interpretation" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Bandura: behaviour, personal factors, and environment reciprocally influence one another. Bobo doll studies demonstrated observational learning. Later work added self-efficacy as a central construct. Reciprocal inhibition = Wolpe (behaviour therapy)." },
  { id: "m2_the20", section: "therapy",
    q: "Free association is the central technique of:",
    options: { a: "Behaviour therapy",
               b: "Psychoanalysis (Freud)",
               c: "Person-centred therapy",
               d: "Cognitive therapy" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Freud's 'fundamental rule' of free association: the patient verbalises whatever comes to mind without censorship. Replaced earlier hypnotic technique. Therapist tracks resistances, slips, and transference manifestations." },
  { id: "m2_the21", section: "therapy",
    q: "Maxwell Jones is best associated with:",
    options: { a: "Therapeutic community",
               b: "Token economy",
               c: "Aversion therapy",
               d: "EMDR" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Maxwell Jones pioneered the therapeutic community model at Belmont (later Henderson Hospital): democratic decision-making, social learning, and confrontation as treatment tools in inpatient settings for personality disorder and addiction." },
  { id: "m2_the22", section: "therapy",
    q: "Aaron Beck originally developed cognitive therapy primarily for:",
    options: { a: "Schizophrenia",
               b: "Substance use disorders",
               c: "OCD",
               d: "Depression" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Beck's Cognitive Therapy of Depression (1979) emerged from his observation that depressed patients reported automatic negative thoughts and depressogenic cognitions. CBT was later extended to anxiety, PD, psychosis, and substance use." },
  { id: "m2_the23", section: "therapy",
    q: "The behavioural intervention called 'response prevention' is most central to the treatment of:",
    options: { a: "OCD (exposure with response prevention, ERP)",
               b: "Specific phobia",
               c: "Depression",
               d: "Insomnia" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "ERP (Meyer 1966; refined by Foa and Steketee): expose the patient to the obsessional trigger while preventing the compulsive ritual, allowing habituation and disconfirmation of feared outcomes. First-line for OCD." },
  { id: "m2_the24", section: "therapy",
    q: "Negative reinforcement is best illustrated by:",
    options: { a: "Removing an aversive stimulus contingent on a behaviour, thereby increasing that behaviour",
               b: "Adding a pleasant stimulus to increase behaviour",
               c: "Removing a pleasant stimulus to decrease behaviour",
               d: "Adding an aversive stimulus to decrease behaviour" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Skinner's 2x2: Positive = add, Negative = remove. Reinforcement INCREASES behaviour, punishment DECREASES it. Negative reinforcement = removal of aversive → behaviour increases (e.g., seatbelt buzzer stops when buckled). Often confused with punishment." },
  { id: "m2_the25", section: "therapy",
    q: "Motivational Interviewing was developed by:",
    options: { a: "Bandura",
               b: "Prochaska and DiClemente",
               c: "Marlatt",
               d: "Miller and Rollnick" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Miller & Rollnick: MI is a collaborative, person-centred method for strengthening motivation for change by exploring ambivalence. Core spirit: partnership, acceptance, compassion, evocation. Prochaska & DiClemente developed the Transtheoretical (Stages of Change) model often paired with MI." },

  // ===== BIOSTATS / RESEARCH (20) =====
  { id: "m2_bst01", section: "biostats",
    q: "A Type I error in null-hypothesis testing is:",
    options: { a: "Retaining H0 when it is false (false negative)",
               b: "Rejecting H0 when it is actually true (false positive)",
               c: "Using too small a sample",
               d: "Using a one-tailed test inappropriately" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Type I (α) = false positive: rejecting a true null. Type II (β) = false negative: retaining a false null. Power = 1 − β. Mnemonic: 'guilty/not guilty' — Type I convicts an innocent (H0 true), Type II frees the guilty (H0 false)." },
  { id: "m2_bst02", section: "biostats",
    q: "Statistical power increases when:",
    options: { a: "Sample size decreases",
               b: "α is set more conservatively (e.g., from .05 to .01)",
               c: "Variability increases",
               d: "Effect size, sample size, and α all increase" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Power = f(effect size, n, α, 1/variability). Bigger n, bigger true effect, larger α, and smaller variance all increase power. Tightening α (e.g., Bonferroni) decreases Type I but also decreases power." },
  { id: "m2_bst03", section: "biostats",
    q: "Cohen's d of 0.5 is conventionally considered:",
    options: { a: "Small",
               b: "Negligible",
               c: "Large",
               d: "Medium" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Cohen's benchmarks for standardised mean difference: 0.2 small, 0.5 medium, 0.8 large. For correlation r: 0.1 small, 0.3 medium, 0.5 large. For odds ratio: ~1.5, 2.5, 4.3." },
  { id: "m2_bst04", section: "biostats",
    q: "The Mann-Whitney U test is the non-parametric equivalent of:",
    options: { a: "Independent-samples t-test",
               b: "Paired t-test",
               c: "One-way ANOVA",
               d: "Chi-square test" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Pairings: Mann-Whitney U ↔ independent t. Wilcoxon signed-rank ↔ paired t. Kruskal-Wallis ↔ one-way ANOVA. Friedman ↔ repeated-measures ANOVA. Spearman ρ ↔ Pearson r." },
  { id: "m2_bst05", section: "biostats",
    q: "Sensitivity of a diagnostic test is defined as:",
    options: { a: "True negatives ÷ (True negatives + False negatives)",
               b: "True negatives ÷ (True negatives + False positives)",
               c: "True positives ÷ (True positives + False positives)",
               d: "True positives ÷ (True positives + False negatives)" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Sensitivity = TP/(TP+FN) — ability to detect disease (rule out, SnNout). Specificity = TN/(TN+FP) — rule in (SpPin). PPV = TP/(TP+FP). NPV = TN/(TN+FN). PPV/NPV depend on prevalence; sensitivity/specificity do not." },
  { id: "m2_bst06", section: "biostats",
    q: "Cohen's kappa is used to assess:",
    options: { a: "Inter-rater agreement on categorical data, corrected for chance",
               b: "Internal consistency",
               c: "Effect size for ANOVA",
               d: "Convergent validity" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Cohen's κ corrects raw agreement for chance. Landis & Koch benchmarks: <0 poor, 0–.2 slight, .2–.4 fair, .4–.6 moderate, .6–.8 substantial, .8–1.0 almost perfect. For continuous data use ICC; for ordinal, weighted κ." },
  { id: "m2_bst07", section: "biostats",
    q: "A 95% confidence interval for a mean indicates:",
    options: { a: "There is a 95% probability the population mean falls in this interval",
               b: "If the procedure were repeated many times, 95% of such intervals would contain the true population mean",
               c: "95% of individual observations fall within this interval",
               d: "The mean has a 95% chance of being significant" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Frequentist CI: a property of the procedure, not the parameter. 95% of intervals constructed this way capture the true mean. The Bayesian credible interval is the one that licenses the '95% probability the mean is in this interval' phrasing." },
  { id: "m2_bst08", section: "biostats",
    q: "The p-value represents:",
    options: { a: "The probability that the null hypothesis is true",
               b: "The probability of obtaining the observed result, or one more extreme, if H0 is true",
               c: "The size of the effect",
               d: "The probability that H1 is true" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "p = P(data this extreme or more | H0). It does NOT give P(H0 | data), the effect size, or the probability of replication. A small p means data are unusual under H0, not that H1 is probably true." },
  { id: "m2_bst09", section: "biostats",
    q: "One-way ANOVA tests:",
    options: { a: "Differences in means across two or more groups on one independent variable",
               b: "Association between two categorical variables",
               c: "Correlation between two continuous variables",
               d: "Differences in variance only" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "One-way ANOVA: differences in means across ≥2 levels of one IV. F = MS_between / MS_within. Two-way ANOVA tests two IVs and their interaction. Chi-square tests categorical association; Pearson r tests linear correlation." },
  { id: "m2_bst10", section: "biostats",
    q: "Bonferroni correction is used to:",
    options: { a: "Increase power",
               b: "Correct for sample size differences",
               c: "Control family-wise Type I error rate when conducting multiple comparisons",
               d: "Transform skewed data" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Bonferroni: divide α by the number of tests (k); reject if p < α/k. Controls family-wise error rate but is conservative (reduces power). Less conservative alternatives: Holm-Bonferroni, Benjamini-Hochberg (controls false discovery rate)." },
  { id: "m2_bst11", section: "biostats",
    q: "Number Needed to Treat (NNT) is calculated as:",
    options: { a: "1 / absolute risk reduction",
               b: "1 / relative risk reduction",
               c: "Relative risk × control event rate",
               d: "Odds ratio − 1" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "NNT = 1/ARR, where ARR = control event rate − treatment event rate (in proportions). NNT of 10 means treating 10 patients to prevent one additional bad outcome. Lower NNT = more effective." },
  { id: "m2_bst12", section: "biostats",
    q: "Allocation concealment in an RCT refers to:",
    options: { a: "Blinding the patient to their assigned arm",
               b: "Sealing the analysis dataset",
               c: "Hiding outcome assessors' identity",
               d: "Preventing those enrolling participants from knowing upcoming allocation" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Allocation concealment = enrolling clinicians cannot foresee or influence assignment (e.g., sealed opaque envelopes, central randomisation). Distinct from blinding, which prevents knowledge of assignment after enrolment. Empirically reduces selection bias more than blinding does for many endpoints." },
  { id: "m2_bst13", section: "biostats",
    q: "Intention-to-treat (ITT) analysis in an RCT means:",
    options: { a: "Analysing participants in the groups to which they were originally randomised, regardless of subsequent adherence",
               b: "Excluding all participants who deviated from protocol",
               c: "Including only those who completed the trial",
               d: "Analysing by actual treatment received" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "ITT preserves the benefits of randomisation and reflects pragmatic effectiveness. Per-protocol analysis excludes deviators / completers-only and tends to overestimate efficacy. As-treated analysis reclassifies by actual exposure and reintroduces selection bias." },
  { id: "m2_bst14", section: "biostats",
    q: "Spearman's rho is appropriate when:",
    options: { a: "Both variables are continuous and linearly related",
               b: "Variables are dichotomous and dichotomous",
               c: "Variables are nominal",
               d: "At least one variable is ordinal, or the relation is monotonic but not linear" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Spearman ρ ranks the data and computes Pearson r on ranks — robust to non-linear monotonic relations and outliers, suitable for ordinal data. Pearson r requires bivariate normality and linear association. Phi coefficient is for two dichotomous variables." },
  { id: "m2_bst15", section: "biostats",
    q: "Stratified random sampling differs from cluster sampling in that:",
    options: { a: "Both ultimately sample everyone in chosen units; stratified samples from every stratum, cluster samples some clusters entirely",
               b: "Stratified is non-probability while cluster is probability",
               c: "There is no real difference",
               d: "Cluster sampling always yields larger samples" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Stratified: divide population into strata, sample from EVERY stratum (improves precision). Cluster: divide into clusters (often geographic), randomly choose SOME clusters and sample all/many within (saves cost, lower precision). Stratified ≥ SRS for precision; cluster ≤ SRS." },
  { id: "m2_bst16", section: "biostats",
    q: "Cronbach's α of 0.40 indicates:",
    options: { a: "Poor internal consistency",
               b: "Acceptable for high-stakes clinical use",
               c: "Excellent internal consistency",
               d: "High construct validity" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Conventional Cronbach's α benchmarks: <.5 unacceptable/poor, .5–.6 poor, .6–.7 questionable, .7–.8 acceptable, .8–.9 good, ≥.9 excellent. High-stakes individual decisions usually require ≥.9. α is reliability, not validity (d is wrong on construct)." },
  { id: "m2_bst17", section: "biostats",
    q: "Effect size r of −0.30 indicates:",
    options: { a: "No relationship",
               b: "A medium-strength negative linear relationship",
               c: "A perfect inverse relationship",
               d: "Causation in the negative direction" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Cohen's benchmarks for r: 0.10 small, 0.30 medium, 0.50 large. r = −0.30 = medium negative association. Correlation never demonstrates causation. r = ±1 is perfect; r = 0 is no linear association (may still be non-linear)." },
  { id: "m2_bst18", section: "biostats",
    q: "The CONSORT statement is a reporting guideline for:",
    options: { a: "Systematic reviews",
               b: "Observational studies",
               c: "Diagnostic accuracy studies",
               d: "Randomised controlled trials" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "CONSORT = Consolidated Standards of Reporting Trials. PRISMA = systematic reviews/meta-analyses. STROBE = observational studies. STARD = diagnostic accuracy. SPIRIT = trial protocols. CARE = case reports." },
  { id: "m2_bst19", section: "biostats",
    q: "Random assignment in an experiment primarily controls for:",
    options: { a: "Measurement error",
               b: "Recall bias",
               c: "Selection bias and confounding by balancing groups on known and unknown variables",
               d: "Publication bias" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Randomisation balances groups in expectation on observed AND unobserved confounders, the strongest tool against selection bias. Blinding addresses measurement and ascertainment bias; methodology cannot rescue published evidence from publication bias (need registry + null results)." },
  { id: "m2_bst20", section: "biostats",
    q: "A p-value of .049 vs .051 indicates:",
    options: { a: "A meaningful clinical difference",
               b: "Two very different conclusions",
               c: "Essentially the same strength of evidence; the .05 threshold is a convention not a meaningful boundary",
               d: "Replication failure" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Continuous p-values just below and just above .05 reflect essentially the same evidence against H0; the binary 'significant/non-significant' classification (Fisher's convention, hardened by Neyman-Pearson) is the source of the difference, not the data." },

  // ===== LANGUAGE (25) =====
  { id: "m2_lang01", section: "language",
    q: "Choose the synonym of 'BENEVOLENT':",
    options: { a: "Compassionate",
               b: "Malicious",
               c: "Indifferent",
               d: "Severe" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Benevolent = kind, well-meaning, compassionate. Malicious = its antonym. Indifferent = uncaring, neutral. Severe = strict, harsh." },
  { id: "m2_lang02", section: "language",
    q: "Choose the antonym of 'ELOQUENT':",
    options: { a: "Articulate",
               b: "Persuasive",
               c: "Loquacious",
               d: "Inarticulate" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Eloquent = fluent, persuasive, articulate. Inarticulate = unable to express clearly = antonym. Loquacious = talkative but not necessarily skilled (close but not the antonym)." },
  { id: "m2_lang03", section: "language",
    q: "Choose the correctly punctuated sentence:",
    options: { a: "The patient, who was anxious; refused medication.",
               b: "The patient, who was anxious refused medication.",
               c: "The patient who was anxious refused medication.",
               d: "The patient, who was anxious, refused medication." },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Non-restrictive (adds extra info about a specific patient) → commas on both sides. The no-comma version is also grammatical but RESTRICTIVE: identifies which patient (the anxious one) versus others, a different meaning." },
  { id: "m2_lang04", section: "language",
    q: "Choose the correct preposition: 'The therapist insisted ___ a follow-up after two weeks.'",
    options: { a: "for",
               b: "with",
               c: "on",
               d: "to" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "'Insist on' is the standard collocation when demanding something. 'Insist that ...' (clause) also works. 'Insist for/with/to' are non-standard in this sense." },
  { id: "m2_lang05", section: "language",
    q: "Choose the correctly formed conditional: 'If I ___ his number, I would have called him.'",
    options: { a: "have known",
               b: "knew",
               c: "would have known",
               d: "had known" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Third conditional (counterfactual past): 'If I had + past participle, I would have + past participle'. Simple past 'knew' would belong to the second conditional (counterfactual present)." },
  { id: "m2_lang06", section: "language",
    q: "Identify the part of speech of 'inconclusive' in: 'The evidence was inconclusive.'",
    options: { a: "Verb",
               b: "Adjective",
               c: "Adverb",
               d: "Noun" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "'Inconclusive' modifies the noun 'evidence' via the linking verb 'was' → predicate adjective. Suffix '-ive' is a strong adjective tell (e.g., effective, decisive)." },
  { id: "m2_lang07", section: "language",
    q: "Choose the sentence with correct subject–verb agreement:",
    options: { a: "Neither the therapist nor the patients was satisfied.",
               b: "Neither the therapist nor the patients are satisfied.",
               c: "Neither the therapist nor the patients were satisfied.",
               d: "Neither the therapist nor the patients has been satisfied." },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "With 'neither ... nor / either ... or' joining a singular and a plural noun, the verb agrees with the subject CLOSEST to it. 'Patients' is plural → 'were'. 'Are' shifts tense; 'has been' breaks agreement with the plural nearer subject." },
  { id: "m2_lang08", section: "language",
    q: "Choose the correctly used homophone: 'Her advice had a strong ___ on his recovery.'",
    options: { a: "affect",
               b: "effect",
               c: "effects",
               d: "affected" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Affect = verb (to influence). Effect = noun (the result) — used here. The rare exceptions ('to effect change' as verb meaning 'to bring about'; 'affect' as noun meaning emotion) confuse novices but don't apply here." },
  { id: "m2_lang09", section: "language",
    q: "Which sentence uses the apostrophe correctly?",
    options: { a: "It's coat is wet.",
               b: "Its's coat is wet.",
               c: "Its' coat is wet.",
               d: "Its coat is wet." },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Possessive 'its' takes no apostrophe (like 'his', 'hers'). 'It's' = it is / it has — a contraction. Common trap." },
  { id: "m2_lang10", section: "language",
    q: "Choose the word closest in meaning to 'LACONIC':",
    options: { a: "Verbose",
               b: "Reluctant",
               c: "Eloquent",
               d: "Concise" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Laconic = using few words, terse. Antonym = verbose / loquacious. Eloquent emphasises persuasive skill, not brevity. From Laconia (Sparta), whose inhabitants were famously brief in speech." },
  { id: "m2_lang11", section: "language",
    q: "Identify the correctly worded comparative: 'She is ___ than her sister.'",
    options: { a: "more taller",
               b: "tallest",
               c: "more tall",
               d: "taller" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Single-syllable adjective → -er comparative: 'taller'. 'More taller' is doubled comparison (incorrect). 'Tallest' is superlative (needs three or more)." },
  { id: "m2_lang12", section: "language",
    q: "Choose the antonym of 'BENIGN':",
    options: { a: "Harmless",
               b: "Tranquil",
               c: "Cordial",
               d: "Malignant" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Benign = kindly / non-threatening / non-cancerous. Malignant = harmful / cancerous = antonym. Both terms used in oncology to describe tumour behaviour." },
  { id: "m2_lang13", section: "language",
    q: "Identify the analogy: 'Psychologist : Counselling :: Surgeon : ___'",
    options: { a: "Operation",
               b: "Hospital",
               c: "Scalpel",
               d: "Patient" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Profession : Characteristic Action. Psychologist provides counselling, surgeon performs operations. Hospital = workplace (different relation), scalpel = tool, patient = recipient." },
  { id: "m2_lang14", section: "language",
    q: "Choose the sentence with the correctly placed modifier:",
    options: { a: "Walking down the corridor, I noticed the painting.",
               b: "Walking down the corridor, the painting caught my eye.",
               c: "The painting walked down the corridor and caught my eye.",
               d: "I noticed walking down the corridor the painting." },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "'Walking down the corridor, the painting caught my eye' is a dangling modifier — the painting wasn't walking. The introductory participial phrase must modify the subject of the main clause; only the version starting 'I noticed the painting' puts 'I' in that position." },
  { id: "m2_lang15", section: "language",
    q: "Choose the closest synonym for 'EPHEMERAL':",
    options: { a: "Eternal",
               b: "Genuine",
               c: "Tangible",
               d: "Transient" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Ephemeral = lasting a very short time, transient (Greek 'ephemeros' = lasting only a day). Eternal is its antonym. Tangible = touchable; genuine = real." },
  { id: "m2_lang16", section: "language",
    q: "Read the passage and answer: 'Although the trial reported a statistically significant reduction in symptoms, the effect size was small and the dropout rate was 35%. Replication has been inconsistent.' The author's tone is best described as:",
    options: { a: "Wholly enthusiastic",
               b: "Dismissive",
               c: "Cautiously sceptical",
               d: "Indifferent" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "The author acknowledges significance ('cautiously') but flags small effect size, high attrition, and inconsistent replication ('sceptical'). Not wholly enthusiastic and not dismissive — they don't reject the finding outright." },
  { id: "m2_lang17", section: "language",
    q: "Choose the correctly used word: 'The therapist gave a ___ to the patient's improvement.'",
    options: { a: "complimentation",
               b: "complement",
               c: "complement of",
               d: "compliment" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Compliment = expression of praise (used here). Complement = something that completes (e.g., 'wine complements the meal'). Common assessment trap. 'Complimentation' is not a standard word." },
  { id: "m2_lang18", section: "language",
    q: "Choose the sentence with correct article use:",
    options: { a: "He is best psychologist in the city.",
               b: "He is a best psychologist in the city.",
               c: "He is the best psychologist in the city.",
               d: "He is best psychologist of the city." },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Superlatives ('best', 'tallest', 'most') require the definite article 'the' because they identify a unique referent. 'A best' is wrong (indefinite + superlative). 'Best ... in / of the city' without 'the' drops the required article." },
  { id: "m2_lang19", section: "language",
    q: "Choose the meaning of the idiom 'to throw in the towel':",
    options: { a: "To begin a new effort",
               b: "To make a joke",
               c: "To clean up after a session",
               d: "To surrender or give up" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "From boxing — the corner throws in a towel to concede the fight. Means to give up, admit defeat. Common idiom in clinical writing about treatment-resistant cases ('don't throw in the towel after one failed trial')." },
  { id: "m2_lang20", section: "language",
    q: "Choose the antonym of 'TRANSPARENT':",
    options: { a: "Clear",
               b: "Opaque",
               c: "Translucent",
               d: "Visible" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Transparent = allowing light through clearly. Opaque = blocking light entirely (antonym). Translucent is in between (partially transmitting) — a distractor that catches the rushed reader." },
  { id: "m2_lang21", section: "language",
    q: "Choose the correctly punctuated sentence:",
    options: { a: "The session ended; however the patient lingered.",
               b: "The session ended, however the patient lingered.",
               c: "The session ended; however, the patient lingered.",
               d: "The session ended however the patient lingered." },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Conjunctive adverbs like 'however', 'therefore', 'moreover' joining two independent clauses take a semicolon before AND a comma after. Using only a comma creates a comma splice; using only a semicolon omits the required comma after 'however'." },
  { id: "m2_lang22", section: "language",
    q: "Identify the most concise rewrite: 'Due to the fact that the patient was anxious, she was unable to sleep.'",
    options: { a: "Owing to the fact that she was anxious, the patient could not sleep.",
               b: "On account of the fact that the patient was anxious, sleep was not possible.",
               c: "Because she was anxious, the patient could not sleep.",
               d: "The patient was anxious; therefore sleep was unable to be achieved by her." },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "'Due to the fact that' / 'owing to the fact that' / 'on account of the fact that' are wordy. 'Because' is the concise replacement. The 'sleep was unable to be achieved by her' version adds unnecessary passive voice." },
  { id: "m2_lang23", section: "language",
    q: "Choose the word meaning 'speaking little; reserved':",
    options: { a: "Loquacious",
               b: "Garrulous",
               c: "Taciturn",
               d: "Voluble" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Taciturn = habitually silent, reserved. Loquacious / garrulous / voluble all mean the opposite (talkative). Often tested cluster — Sihan should memorise as a 4-card pair: taciturn ↔ {loquacious, garrulous, voluble}." },
  { id: "m2_lang24", section: "language",
    q: "Choose the sentence in correct active voice:",
    options: { a: "The team discussed the case.",
               b: "The case was discussed by the team.",
               c: "The case has been discussed by the team.",
               d: "The case is being discussed by the team." },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Active voice: subject performs the action. The three 'case was/has been/is being discussed by the team' versions are all passive constructions (the case is acted upon). Note: passive is grammatically correct, just not active." },
  { id: "m2_lang25", section: "language",
    q: "Read the passage and answer: 'The author argues that early intervention prevents most cases of chronic psychosis. However, the studies cited used small samples and lacked control groups.' The author's main point about the cited studies is that they:",
    options: { a: "Confirm the original argument decisively",
               b: "Are irrelevant to the argument",
               c: "Refute the argument entirely",
               d: "Provide weak methodological support for the argument" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "The author flags small samples and absent controls — methodological weaknesses. The intent is neither full endorsement nor full refutation; 'however' signals qualified scepticism, i.e. weak methodological support for the broader argument." }
],
  "met-mock-3": [
  // ===== BIOPSYCHOLOGY (18) =====
  { id: "m3_bio01", section: "biopsych",
    q: "During an action potential, the absolute refractory period exists primarily because:",
    options: { a: "Voltage-gated Na+ channels are inactivated and cannot reopen until the membrane repolarises",
               b: "The Na+/K+ ATPase has shut down",
               c: "K+ channels remain permanently inactivated",
               d: "Cl− influx prevents further depolarisation" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "After firing, voltage-gated Na+ channels enter an inactivated state (ball-and-chain mechanism) and cannot re-open regardless of stimulus strength. Once the membrane repolarises they reset, ending the absolute refractory period. The relative refractory period that follows is K+-driven, not Na+." },
  { id: "m3_bio02", section: "biopsych",
    q: "The release of neurotransmitter at the synaptic terminal is most directly triggered by:",
    options: { a: "K+ efflux through delayed rectifier channels",
               b: "Closure of Na+/K+ ATPase pumps",
               c: "Cl− influx via GABA-A receptors",
               d: "Ca2+ influx through voltage-gated Ca2+ channels" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Depolarisation of the terminal opens voltage-gated Ca2+ channels; Ca2+ entry binds synaptotagmin, fusing vesicles with the membrane via SNARE proteins. Tetanus and botulinum toxins cleave SNAREs to block release." },
  { id: "m3_bio03", section: "biopsych",
    q: "The mesocortical dopamine pathway, when underactive, is most associated with:",
    options: { a: "Positive symptoms of schizophrenia",
               b: "Parkinsonian rigidity",
               c: "Hyperprolactinaemia",
               d: "Negative and cognitive symptoms of schizophrenia" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Mesocortical (VTA → prefrontal cortex) hypoactivity underlies negative (avolition, blunting) and cognitive symptoms of schizophrenia. Mesolimbic hyperactivity drives positive symptoms; nigrostriatal blockade causes EPS; tuberoinfundibular blockade raises prolactin." },
  { id: "m3_bio04", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Which structure is the principal source of acetylcholine projections to the cortex and is severely depleted in Alzheimer's disease?",
    options: { a: "Locus coeruleus",
               b: "Raphe nuclei",
               c: "Nucleus basalis of Meynert",
               d: "Ventral tegmental area" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "The nucleus basalis of Meynert in the basal forebrain provides cholinergic innervation to most of the cortex; its degeneration underpins the rationale for cholinesterase inhibitors (donepezil, rivastigmine). Locus coeruleus = NE; raphe = serotonin; VTA = dopamine." },
  { id: "m3_bio05", section: "biopsych",
    q: "The blood–brain barrier is formed primarily by:",
    options: { a: "Tight junctions between capillary endothelial cells, supported by astrocyte end-feet",
               b: "Pia mater fenestrations",
               c: "Microglial phagocytic activity",
               d: "Oligodendrocyte myelin sheaths around capillaries" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Tight junctions (zona occludens) between cerebral capillary endothelial cells, with surrounding astrocytic end-feet inducing and maintaining the barrier. The circumventricular organs (area postrema, OVLT, SFO) lack the BBB to sample blood chemistry." },
  { id: "m3_bio06", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Cerebrospinal fluid is produced primarily by the:",
    options: { a: "Arachnoid granulations",
               b: "Subarachnoid space",
               c: "Dura mater",
               d: "Choroid plexus of the ventricles" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "CSF is produced by the choroid plexus (mainly in the lateral ventricles) at ~500 mL/day, flows through the ventricular system, and is reabsorbed by arachnoid granulations into the dural venous sinuses. Total CSF volume is ~150 mL." },
  { id: "m3_bio07", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Lesions of the suprachiasmatic nucleus most directly disrupt:",
    options: { a: "Thermoregulation",
               b: "Anterior pituitary hormone release",
               c: "Hunger and satiety",
               d: "Circadian rhythms" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "The SCN of the anterior hypothalamus is the master circadian pacemaker, entrained by retinal input via the retinohypothalamic tract. Lesions abolish the day-night sleep-wake rhythm without destroying total sleep amount." },
  { id: "m3_bio08", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Which feature most reliably distinguishes the cerebellum's role in motor learning from that of the basal ganglia?",
    options: { a: "Basal ganglia compute fine motor error signals via climbing fibres",
               b: "Cerebellum supports error correction and motor coordination; basal ganglia support action selection and habit/procedural learning",
               c: "Cerebellum sequences habit learning and reward-based action selection",
               d: "Both structures generate the primary descending motor command directly to muscles" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Cerebellar circuits (mossy + climbing fibre input to Purkinje cells; inferior olive error signal) refine motor coordination and timing. Basal ganglia circuits (cortico-striato-pallido-thalamic loops) select among actions and consolidate habits / procedural learning." },
  { id: "m3_bio09", section: "biopsych",
    q: "The lateral geniculate nucleus is most directly involved in:",
    options: { a: "Audition",
               b: "Somatosensation",
               c: "Vision",
               d: "Olfaction" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "LGN of the thalamus is the visual relay from the retina (via the optic tract) to the primary visual cortex (V1) along the optic radiation. MGN handles audition; VPL/VPM handle somatosensation; olfaction bypasses the thalamus directly." },
  { id: "m3_bio10", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Damage to which structure produces a contralateral homonymous hemianopia with macular sparing?",
    options: { a: "Optic chiasm",
               b: "Primary visual cortex (calcarine cortex) infarction with sparing of the occipital pole",
               c: "Optic tract just behind the chiasm",
               d: "Optic nerve before the chiasm" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Posterior cerebral artery occlusion typically produces contralateral homonymous hemianopia with macular sparing because the occipital pole (macular representation) receives collateral supply from the MCA. Optic chiasm lesions give bitemporal hemianopia." },
  { id: "m3_bio11", section: "biopsych",
    q: "The dopamine hypothesis of schizophrenia was supported originally by which observation?",
    options: { a: "All antipsychotics elevate serotonin levels",
               b: "Clinical potency of typical antipsychotics correlates with D2 receptor affinity",
               c: "Schizophrenia twin concordance is 100%",
               d: "Levodopa improves negative symptoms reliably" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Seeman and colleagues showed that clinical potency of typical antipsychotics across orders of magnitude tracks D2 receptor binding affinity. Amphetamine (releases dopamine) can produce a psychosis indistinguishable from schizophrenia in some users." },
  { id: "m3_bio12", section: "biopsych",
    q: "The 'fight or flight' response is mediated primarily by:",
    options: { a: "Parasympathetic nervous system via vagus",
               b: "Enteric nervous system",
               c: "Sympathetic nervous system + adrenal medulla (epinephrine release)",
               d: "Pituitary-thyroid axis" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Acute stress activates sympathetic outflow and adrenal medullary release of epinephrine and norepinephrine (Cannon, 1932). The HPA axis (CRH → ACTH → cortisol) handles the slower, sustained stress response (Selye)." },
  { id: "m3_bio13", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Substance abuse disorders are most consistently associated with structural and functional changes in which neural circuit?",
    options: { a: "Cerebellar vermis",
               b: "Mesolimbic dopamine system / ventral striatum",
               c: "Lateral geniculate nucleus",
               d: "Vestibular nuclei" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Addictive drugs converge on the mesolimbic dopamine reward circuit (VTA → nucleus accumbens) and recruit the dorsal striatum with chronic use, shifting behaviour from goal-directed to habit-based (Everitt & Robbins)." },
  { id: "m3_bio14", section: "biopsych",
    q: "The Wisconsin Card Sorting Test is classically used to detect dysfunction of which region?",
    options: { a: "Dorsolateral prefrontal cortex",
               b: "Hippocampus",
               c: "Primary motor cortex",
               d: "Cerebellum" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "WCST assesses set shifting, abstraction, and inhibition of prepotent responses, all DLPFC-dependent. Patients with frontal lesions perseverate on a sorting rule even after feedback that it has changed." },
  { id: "m3_bio15", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Long-term depression (LTD) of synaptic strength typically depends on:",
    options: { a: "Brief, high-frequency stimulation and large Ca2+ rise",
               b: "Pre-synaptic GABA release",
               c: "Mg2+ blockade of AMPA receptors",
               d: "Prolonged, low-frequency stimulation and modest Ca2+ rise" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "LTD: low-frequency stimulation (~1 Hz), modest postsynaptic Ca2+ rise, activates phosphatases (PP1) that dephosphorylate AMPA receptors and internalise them. LTP needs high-frequency stimulation, large Ca2+ rise, and CaMKII." },
  { id: "m3_bio16", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Which event-related potential component is most commonly elevated in oddball paradigms and reduced in schizophrenia?",
    options: { a: "N100",
               b: "P300",
               c: "P50 sensory gating",
               d: "Bereitschaftspotential" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "P300 amplitude indexes attentional resource allocation and working-memory update on oddball/target trials. Schizophrenia patients show reduced P300 amplitude, considered an endophenotype. P50 reflects sensory gating and is also reduced." },
  { id: "m3_bio17", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Anterograde amnesia following bilateral mammillary body / dorsomedial thalamus damage with confabulation is most characteristic of:",
    options: { a: "Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease",
               b: "Pick's disease",
               c: "Korsakoff's syndrome",
               d: "Huntington's chorea" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Wernicke-Korsakoff: thiamine deficiency (usually chronic alcohol) damages mammillary bodies and dorsomedial thalamus, producing anterograde amnesia with confabulation. Wernicke triad: ophthalmoplegia, ataxia, confusion." },
  { id: "m3_bio18", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Which neurotransmitter binds the receptor that benzodiazepines positively modulate?",
    options: { a: "Glutamate",
               b: "Acetylcholine",
               c: "GABA",
               d: "Glycine" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Benzodiazepines bind an allosteric site on GABA-A receptors, increasing the frequency of channel opening when GABA binds. Barbiturates increase opening duration. Both enhance inhibitory Cl− conductance." },

  // ===== DEVELOPMENTAL (14) =====
  { id: "m3_dev01", section: "dev",
    q: "Erikson's stage of 'autonomy vs shame and doubt' is associated with which age range and developmental task?",
    options: { a: "0–1 year; learning trust through consistent caregiving",
               b: "3–6 years; initiating play and tolerating guilt",
               c: "1–3 years; toilet training and exercising will/independence",
               d: "6–12 years; comparison with peers and developing competence" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Erikson stage 2 (toddlerhood, ~1–3 yrs): autonomy vs shame and doubt, virtue = will. Stage 1 trust vs mistrust (0–1); stage 3 initiative vs guilt (3–6); stage 4 industry vs inferiority (6–12)." },
  { id: "m3_dev02", section: "dev",
    q: "Schaffer and Emerson's longitudinal study identified four phases of attachment. The 'specific attachment' phase typically begins around:",
    options: { a: "7 months",
               b: "5 months",
               c: "3 months",
               d: "12 months" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Schaffer & Emerson (1964) phases: asocial (0–6 wks), indiscriminate attachment (6 wks–7 m), specific attachment (~7 m, with separation/stranger anxiety), and multiple attachments (~10–11 m onward)." },
  { id: "m3_dev03", section: "dev",
    q: "Vygotsky considered private (self-directed) speech in young children to be:",
    options: { a: "A regression to autistic thinking, to be discouraged",
               b: "Mere imitation with no functional role",
               c: "A self-regulatory tool that becomes internalised as inner speech / verbal thought",
               d: "Evidence of egocentrism that fades only with formal schooling" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Vygotsky: private speech guides behaviour and supports self-regulation, then is internalised around age 7 as inner speech. Piaget saw it as egocentric and disappearing — a key Piaget–Vygotsky disagreement that empirical work has resolved in Vygotsky's favour." },
  { id: "m3_dev04", section: "dev",
    q: "Margaret Mahler's separation-individuation theory places 'rapprochement' at approximately what age?",
    options: { a: "0–2 months",
               b: "15–24 months",
               c: "5–10 months",
               d: "3–5 years" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Mahler's subphases: differentiation (5–10 m), practising (10–15 m), rapprochement (15–24 m, ambivalent return to mother after exploration), object constancy (24–36 m+). Earlier phases: normal autism (0–2 m), symbiosis (2–5 m)." },
  { id: "m3_dev05", section: "dev",
    q: "Konrad Lorenz's imprinting in geese is best characterised as:",
    options: { a: "Slow, reversible learning across the lifespan",
               b: "A rapid, largely irreversible attachment that occurs within a narrow critical period",
               c: "A form of operant conditioning with food reward",
               d: "A purely visual recognition system unrelated to attachment behaviour" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Lorenz (1935): goslings imprint on the first moving object during a critical period of ~13–16 hours post-hatch. Imprinting is rapid, largely irreversible, and biologically prepared. Bowlby drew on this in his ethological model of human attachment." },
  { id: "m3_dev06", section: "dev",
    q: "Baumrind's authoritative parenting style is characterised by:",
    options: { a: "High demands, low warmth, strict obedience",
               b: "Low demands, low warmth, disengagement",
               c: "Low demands, high warmth, few rules",
               d: "High demands, high warmth, reasoned discipline with autonomy support" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Baumrind 2×2 of demandingness × responsiveness: authoritative = high both (best outcomes); authoritarian = high demand / low warmth; permissive = low demand / high warmth; uninvolved/neglectful = low both (Maccoby & Martin added the 4th)." },
  { id: "m3_dev07", section: "dev",
    q: "The 'still face' paradigm developed by Edward Tronick demonstrates that infants:",
    options: { a: "Cannot detect maternal facial cues before 12 months",
               b: "Show stronger attachment to fathers than mothers in unresponsive conditions",
               c: "Habituate immediately to maternal blankness with no behavioural change",
               d: "Show distress and disorganised behaviour when the caregiver is suddenly unresponsive" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Tronick's Still Face Paradigm (1978): when a previously responsive mother suddenly adopts an expressionless face, infants as young as 2–9 months show distress, gaze aversion, and self-soothing, demonstrating sensitivity to contingent social interaction." },
  { id: "m3_dev08", section: "dev",
    q: "Piaget's term 'centration' refers to:",
    options: { a: "The preoperational tendency to focus on one salient feature while ignoring others",
               b: "The infant's coordination of schemas across modalities",
               c: "The formal operational ability to reason hypothetically",
               d: "A reversible mental operation underlying conservation" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Centration is the preoperational child's failure to decentre — fixating on one dimension (e.g., height of liquid) and missing others (width). Decentration enables conservation in concrete operational stage (~7–11 yrs)." },
  { id: "m3_dev09", section: "dev",
    q: "Selman's stages of perspective-taking place the transition from 'subjective / differentiated' (level 1) to 'self-reflective / reciprocal' (level 2) around:",
    options: { a: "3–5 years",
               b: "5–9 years",
               c: "10–12 years",
               d: "Late adolescence" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Selman's role-taking stages: 0 egocentric (3–6 y), 1 subjective (5–9), 2 self-reflective (7–12), 3 mutual third-person (10–15), 4 societal (12+). Level 2 begins around age 7." },
  { id: "m3_dev10", section: "dev",
    q: "Kohlberg's stage 3 (conventional level) reasoning is best summarised as:",
    options: { a: "Obedience to authority to avoid punishment",
               b: "Instrumental self-interest, 'what's in it for me'",
               c: "'Good boy / nice girl' orientation, valuing relationships and approval",
               d: "Universal ethical principles and human rights" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Conventional level stages 3 (good boy/girl, interpersonal expectations) and 4 (law and order). Pre-conventional 1 (obedience/punishment), 2 (instrumental). Post-conventional 5 (social contract), 6 (universal ethics)." },
  { id: "m3_dev11", section: "dev",
    q: "Bowlby's concept of an 'internal working model' refers to:",
    options: { a: "A neural circuit between hippocampus and amygdala",
               b: "Mental representations of self, others, and relationships built from early attachment experiences",
               c: "Genetic templates for fear conditioning",
               d: "The infant's reflex repertoire at birth" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Bowlby (1969/1980): internal working models are mental representations of self (worthy of love or not) and attachment figures (available and responsive or not), formed in infancy and guiding later relationships. Hazan & Shaver applied them to adult romance." },
  { id: "m3_dev12", section: "dev",
    q: "Adolescent egocentrism (Elkind) is most directly associated with which two constructs?",
    options: { a: "Conservation and reversibility",
               b: "Inner speech and scaffolding",
               c: "Object permanence and stranger anxiety",
               d: "Imaginary audience and personal fable" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Elkind (1967): adolescent egocentrism manifests as the imaginary audience (assuming others are as focused on you as you are) and personal fable (feeling uniquely invulnerable and special). Linked to formal operational onset and risk-taking." },
  { id: "m3_dev13", section: "dev",
    q: "Habituation paradigms in infants are often used to assess:",
    options: { a: "Discrimination and categorisation abilities preverbally",
               b: "Long-term declarative memory",
               c: "Object permanence specifically",
               d: "Moral reasoning" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Habituation/dishabituation (Fantz, Spelke): a familiar stimulus loses looking time; presenting a novel stimulus recovers looking, showing discrimination. Used to probe infant categorisation, numerical sense, and physical knowledge before language." },
  { id: "m3_dev14", section: "dev",
    q: "The 'A-not-B error' is a hallmark of which Piagetian sub-stage and reflects:",
    options: { a: "Formal operational; difficulty with hypotheticals",
               b: "Preoperational; centration on appearance",
               c: "Concrete operational; failure of reversibility",
               d: "Sensorimotor; incomplete object permanence with prepotent reach to a previously rewarded location" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Between roughly 8 and 12 months (sub-stage 4 of sensorimotor) infants reach to location A even after watching the object hidden at B. Reflects immature working memory / inhibitory control in addition to incomplete object permanence." },

  // ===== ABNORMAL (18) =====
  { id: "m3_abn01", section: "abnormal",
    q: "DSM-5 requires symptoms of major depressive disorder to be present for at least:",
    options: { a: "1 week",
               b: "2 weeks",
               c: "1 month",
               d: "6 months" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "MDD: ≥5 of 9 symptoms (one must be depressed mood or anhedonia) most of the day, nearly every day, for ≥2 weeks. Dysthymia (PDD) requires ≥2 years (1 in children/adolescents)." },
  { id: "m3_abn02", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Per DSM-5, schizophrenia requires continuous signs of disturbance for at least:",
    options: { a: "1 week",
               b: "1 month",
               c: "6 months (with ≥1 month of active-phase symptoms)",
               d: "12 months" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Schizophrenia (DSM-5): ≥2 of 5 Criterion A symptoms (delusions, hallucinations, disorganised speech, grossly disorganised/catatonic behaviour, negative symptoms) for ≥1 month active phase, with total disturbance ≥6 months. Brief psychotic disorder <1 month; schizophreniform 1–6 months." },
  { id: "m3_abn03", section: "abnormal",
    q: "First-rank symptoms of schizophrenia (Schneider) most prominently include:",
    options: { a: "Avolition and flat affect",
               b: "Memory deficits and disorientation to person",
               c: "Thought insertion, thought withdrawal, thought broadcasting, made feelings/impulses, somatic passivity, third-person auditory hallucinations",
               d: "Catatonic excitement" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Schneider's first-rank symptoms emphasise passivity phenomena and specific auditory hallucinations. They had high inter-rater reliability but are no longer required for DSM-5 / ICD-11 diagnosis — they remained influential in clinical training." },
  { id: "m3_abn04", section: "abnormal",
    q: "A patient with persistent intrusive thoughts of contamination, accompanied by ritualised hand-washing that consumes >1 hour/day, is best classified under DSM-5 as:",
    options: { a: "Generalised anxiety disorder",
               b: "Illness anxiety disorder",
               c: "Specific phobia",
               d: "Obsessive-compulsive disorder" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "OCD = obsessions (intrusive, unwanted thoughts) and/or compulsions (repetitive behaviours/mental acts), time-consuming (>1 hr/day) or causing distress/impairment. In DSM-5, OCD was moved out of anxiety disorders into its own chapter." },
  { id: "m3_abn05", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Borderline personality disorder, per DSM-5, requires ≥5 of 9 criteria. Which is NOT among them?",
    options: { a: "Frantic efforts to avoid real or imagined abandonment",
               b: "Marked aggression toward animals or persistent rule-breaking before age 15",
               c: "Recurrent suicidal behaviour, gestures, or self-mutilation",
               d: "Identity disturbance with markedly unstable self-image" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Cruelty to animals / pre-15 conduct problems define conduct disorder, a precursor of antisocial PD — not BPD. The 9 BPD criteria centre on instability of affect, identity, relationships, plus impulsivity, suicidality, dissociation, emptiness, and anger." },
  { id: "m3_abn06", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Capgras syndrome is characterised by:",
    options: { a: "Belief that one is dead or non-existent",
               b: "Delusional jealousy about a partner's fidelity",
               c: "Belief that a stranger is actually a familiar person in disguise",
               d: "Belief that a familiar person has been replaced by an impostor" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Capgras (delusion of doubles): belief that a familiar person has been replaced by a physically identical impostor — often associated with disconnection between face recognition and affective response (Ellis & Young). Cotard = belief one is dead/non-existent; Fregoli = strangers are familiars in disguise; Othello = pathological jealousy." },
  { id: "m3_abn07", section: "abnormal",
    q: "ICD-11 retains a category of dissociative neurological symptom disorder (also termed 'conversion'). Its core feature is:",
    options: { a: "Intentional production of symptoms for external gain",
               b: "Persecutory delusion with intact reality testing",
               c: "Persistent memory deficit from organic brain injury",
               d: "Neurological symptoms incompatible with known disease pathways, not intentionally produced" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Conversion / functional neurological symptom disorder: motor or sensory symptoms (e.g., non-epileptic seizures, paralysis) inconsistent with neurological disease, not intentionally produced. Malingering = intentional, external gain; factitious = intentional, internal gain." },
  { id: "m3_abn08", section: "abnormal",
    q: "PTSD per DSM-5 requires the symptoms to persist for at least:",
    options: { a: "2 days",
               b: "1 week",
               c: "6 months",
               d: "1 month" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "PTSD = ≥1 month duration after exposure to actual/threatened death, serious injury, or sexual violence, with symptoms across 4 clusters: intrusion, avoidance, negative cognition/mood, arousal/reactivity. Acute stress disorder = 3 days to 1 month." },
  { id: "m3_abn09", section: "abnormal",
    q: "A patient presents with episodes of binge eating ≥1/week for ≥3 months, without compensatory behaviours, with marked distress about the binges. The DSM-5 diagnosis is:",
    options: { a: "Binge-eating disorder",
               b: "Bulimia nervosa",
               c: "Anorexia nervosa, binge-purge subtype",
               d: "Avoidant/restrictive food intake disorder" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Binge-eating disorder: recurrent binge episodes with loss of control, ≥1/week × ≥3 months, marked distress, NO compensatory behaviour. Bulimia requires inappropriate compensatory behaviours (purging, fasting, exercise). New as a full disorder in DSM-5." },
  { id: "m3_abn10", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Charles Bonnet syndrome is characterised by:",
    options: { a: "Auditory hallucinations in deaf patients",
               b: "Multimodal hallucinations in delirium",
               c: "Visual hallucinations in patients with significant vision loss, with retained insight",
               d: "Olfactory hallucinations preceding temporal lobe seizures" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Charles Bonnet syndrome: vivid, complex visual hallucinations in cognitively intact patients with ocular pathology (macular degeneration, glaucoma); patients typically retain insight that the images are not real. A 'release phenomenon' of the visual cortex." },
  { id: "m3_abn11", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Delirium is most reliably differentiated from dementia by:",
    options: { a: "Older age at onset",
               b: "Presence of confabulation",
               c: "Acute onset with fluctuating course and altered attention/consciousness",
               d: "Aphasia and apraxia as early features" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Delirium = acute, fluctuating, attention/consciousness disturbance, often with reversed sleep-wake cycle and identifiable medical/toxic cause. Dementia = insidious, progressive, predominantly cognitive (memory/executive) decline with relatively preserved consciousness until late." },
  { id: "m3_abn12", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Persistent depressive disorder (dysthymia) requires depressed mood for most of the day, more days than not, for at least:",
    options: { a: "2 years in adults (1 year in children/adolescents)",
               b: "6 months",
               c: "1 month",
               d: "5 years" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Dysthymia / PDD: depressed mood most of the day, more days than not, ≥2 years in adults (1 year in children/adolescents), plus ≥2 of 6 symptoms; no symptom-free period >2 months. DSM-5 merged dysthymia and chronic MDD into PDD." },
  { id: "m3_abn13", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Tourette's disorder per DSM-5 requires multiple motor and ≥1 vocal tic, with onset before age 18 and duration of at least:",
    options: { a: "1 month",
               b: "3 months",
               c: "1 year",
               d: "6 months" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Tourette's: ≥2 motor tics + ≥1 vocal tic (not necessarily concurrent), present >1 year, onset <18 years. Persistent (chronic) motor or vocal tic disorder: only one type. Provisional tic disorder: <1 year." },
  { id: "m3_abn14", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Anorexia nervosa, restricting subtype, per DSM-5 is defined by:",
    options: { a: "BMI < 17.5 only",
               b: "Selective eating without weight loss",
               c: "Recurrent binge eating with purging",
               d: "Restriction of intake leading to significantly low body weight, intense fear of weight gain, distorted body image; no recurrent binge/purge in the last 3 months" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "DSM-5 removed amenorrhoea and fixed BMI cutoff. Restricting subtype: weight loss via dieting/fasting/exercise without recurrent binge or purge in the past 3 months. Binge-purge subtype: presence of such episodes." },
  { id: "m3_abn15", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Which symptom cluster is NOT among the DSM-5 PTSD criteria?",
    options: { a: "Magical thinking and ideas of reference",
               b: "Avoidance of trauma reminders",
               c: "Negative alterations in cognitions and mood",
               d: "Intrusion (e.g., flashbacks, intrusive memories)" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "DSM-5 PTSD has 4 symptom clusters: intrusion, avoidance, negative cognition/mood, and arousal/reactivity (sleep, irritability, hypervigilance, startle, reckless behaviour). Magical thinking is a schizotypal PD feature." },
  { id: "m3_abn16", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Pseudobulbar affect (PBA) is best characterised by:",
    options: { a: "Mood-congruent emotional expression in major depression",
               b: "Restricted affect in schizophrenia",
               c: "Brief, involuntary outbursts of laughing or crying disproportionate to mood, in patients with neurological disease (MS, stroke, ALS, brain injury)",
               d: "Histrionic emotional shifts in personality disorder" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "PBA / emotional lability: involuntary, exaggerated emotional outbursts decoupled from underlying mood, caused by disrupted corticobulbar control over brainstem emotional motor pathways. Treatable with dextromethorphan/quinidine combinations and SSRIs." },
  { id: "m3_abn17", section: "abnormal",
    q: "DSM-5 distinguishes mania from hypomania primarily by:",
    options: { a: "Mania involves marked functional impairment, hospitalisation, or psychotic features and lasts ≥1 week; hypomania lasts ≥4 days without marked impairment or psychosis",
               b: "Hypomania requires psychotic features",
               c: "Mania is always preceded by major depression",
               d: "Hypomania requires no observable change from baseline" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Mania ≥7 days (or any duration if hospitalisation required), marked impairment, may have psychosis → Bipolar I. Hypomania ≥4 days, observable change but no marked impairment / no psychosis → Bipolar II if paired with MDE." },
  { id: "m3_abn18", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Folie à deux (shared psychotic disorder) most often involves:",
    options: { a: "Two patients independently developing identical delusions",
               b: "A mother and infant sharing dissociative symptoms",
               c: "A folie à deux is purely cultural, not psychiatric",
               d: "A dominant patient with a primary psychotic disorder and a more passive person who comes to share the delusion, often in social isolation" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Shared psychotic disorder (folie à deux): a delusion is transferred from a primary case to a typically more passive, often related person living in close, isolated contact. DSM-5 removed it as a discrete diagnosis (now subsumed under 'other specified schizophrenia spectrum')." },

  // ===== ASSESSMENT (15) =====
  { id: "m3_ass01", section: "assessment",
    q: "On the WAIS-IV (and WAIS-V), the mean and standard deviation of subtest scaled scores are:",
    options: { a: "Mean 100, SD 15",
               b: "Mean 50, SD 10",
               c: "Mean 10, SD 3",
               d: "Mean 0, SD 1" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Subtest scaled scores: mean 10, SD 3. Composite/Index scores (FSIQ, VCI, PRI, WMI, PSI): mean 100, SD 15. T-scores (used on MMPI): mean 50, SD 10. Z-scores: mean 0, SD 1." },
  { id: "m3_ass02", section: "assessment",
    q: "The MMPI-2 validity scale 'F' (Infrequency) elevated to a T-score >100 most plausibly indicates:",
    options: { a: "Faking good / minimisation",
               b: "Random responding, severe psychopathology, or symptom exaggeration",
               c: "Excellent reliability of profile",
               d: "Above-average defensiveness" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "F = items endorsed by <10% of normative sample. Very high F (T>100) suggests random/inconsistent responding, severe distress, or symptom exaggeration / faking bad. L (Lie) and K (Defensiveness) elevations suggest faking good. Use VRIN / TRIN to distinguish random from over-reporting." },
  { id: "m3_ass03", section: "assessment",
    q: "Cronbach's alpha is a measure of:",
    options: { a: "Test-retest reliability",
               b: "Construct validity",
               c: "Internal consistency reliability",
               d: "Inter-rater reliability" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Cronbach's α estimates internal consistency: average correlation among items measuring the same construct, adjusted for test length. Acceptable >0.70, good >0.80. Cohen's κ = inter-rater; test-retest = correlate scores across two administrations." },
  { id: "m3_ass04", section: "assessment",
    q: "The Rorschach Inkblot Test uses 10 standardised cards; the Exner Comprehensive System scores responses on dimensions that include all of the following EXCEPT:",
    options: { a: "Location of the percept (W, D, Dd, S)",
               b: "Determinants (form, colour, shading, movement)",
               c: "Form quality",
               d: "Explicit IQ score" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Exner's CS scores location, determinants, form quality, content, popularity, special scores, organisation activity. It does not yield an IQ. R-PAS (Meyer et al., 2011) has largely replaced Exner with stronger psychometrics." },
  { id: "m3_ass05", section: "assessment",
    q: "Sensitivity of a screening instrument is best defined as:",
    options: { a: "Proportion of true positives correctly identified",
               b: "Proportion of true negatives correctly identified",
               c: "Probability that a person scoring positive truly has the condition",
               d: "Probability that a person scoring negative truly lacks the condition" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Sensitivity = TP / (TP + FN) — captures cases. Specificity = TN / (TN + FP) — rules out non-cases. PPV = TP / (TP + FP); NPV = TN / (TN + FN). PPV and NPV depend on base rate; sensitivity and specificity are properties of the test." },
  { id: "m3_ass06", section: "assessment",
    q: "Stanine scores have a mean and SD of:",
    options: { a: "Mean 50, SD 10",
               b: "Mean 5, SD 2",
               c: "Mean 10, SD 3",
               d: "Mean 100, SD 15" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Stanine = STAndard NINE. Single-digit scale 1–9, mean 5, SD 2. Used in educational assessment. T-score mean 50/SD 10; subtest scaled score mean 10/SD 3; deviation IQ mean 100/SD 15." },
  { id: "m3_ass07", section: "assessment",
    q: "The Beck Depression Inventory-II severity cutoff conventionally used to flag 'moderate' depression is approximately:",
    options: { a: "20–28",
               b: "14–19",
               c: "0–13",
               d: "29–63" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "BDI-II conventional bands (clinical samples): 0–13 minimal, 14–19 mild, 20–28 moderate, 29–63 severe. 21 items, 0–3 each, max 63. Originally Beck et al. (1996)." },
  { id: "m3_ass08", section: "assessment",
    q: "Item Response Theory (IRT), unlike Classical Test Theory, models:",
    options: { a: "Only group-level means and variances",
               b: "Only test-retest stability",
               c: "The probability of endorsing each item as a function of a latent trait, allowing item-level parameters (difficulty, discrimination, sometimes guessing)",
               d: "Inter-rater agreement on observed behaviour" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "IRT models P(response | latent θ) with item parameters: a (discrimination), b (difficulty), and in the 3PL model c (pseudo-guessing). Enables computer adaptive testing and equating across forms — used in GRE, USMLE, and modern scale development." },
  { id: "m3_ass09", section: "assessment",
    q: "The Bender-Gestalt II is primarily used to assess:",
    options: { a: "Premorbid intelligence",
               b: "Personality structure",
               c: "Verbal working memory",
               d: "Visuo-motor integration (and screening for organic impairment / developmental status)" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Bender-Gestalt II (Brannigan & Decker, 2003): 16 designs (copy + recall phases) assessing visuomotor integration, used for developmental screening and as a brief neuropsychological screen. Original BVMGT used 9 Wertheimer figures." },
  { id: "m3_ass10", section: "assessment",
    q: "The Thematic Apperception Test (TAT) is classified as which type of test?",
    options: { a: "Projective personality test",
               b: "Objective personality test",
               c: "Intelligence test",
               d: "Neuropsychological battery" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "TAT (Murray & Morgan, 1935): projective test using 31 ambiguous picture cards; the examinee constructs a story revealing needs, presses, themes. Murray's needs-press theory and the TAT informed McClelland's measurement of nAch." },
  { id: "m3_ass11", section: "assessment",
    q: "Concurrent validity is best demonstrated when:",
    options: { a: "A new test correlates highly with a different construct measured at the same time",
               b: "A new test predicts future performance",
               c: "A test has stable internal consistency over time",
               d: "A new test correlates highly with an established measure of the same construct at the same time" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Criterion validity has two flavours: concurrent (same time as criterion) and predictive (future criterion). Convergent validity = correlates with same-construct measures; discriminant = does not correlate with unrelated constructs (Campbell & Fiske MTMM)." },
  { id: "m3_ass12", section: "assessment",
    q: "The Halstead-Reitan Neuropsychological Battery includes all of the following EXCEPT:",
    options: { a: "Category Test",
               b: "Tactual Performance Test",
               c: "Trail Making Test",
               d: "Rorschach Inkblot Test" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Halstead-Reitan: Category Test, TPT, Trail Making A/B, Finger Tapping, Speech-Sounds Perception, Rhythm Test, Sensory-Perceptual Examination, plus WAIS and MMPI as adjuncts. The Rorschach is a separate projective and not part of the HRNB core." },
  { id: "m3_ass13", section: "assessment",
    q: "The Vineland Adaptive Behavior Scales primarily measures:",
    options: { a: "Adaptive functioning across communication, daily living, socialisation, and motor skills",
               b: "Intellectual quotient",
               c: "Psychopathology in adolescents",
               d: "Premorbid personality" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Vineland-3 (Sparrow et al.): semi-structured interview/rating of adaptive functioning. Core domains: Communication, Daily Living, Socialisation, plus Motor for young children. Required (alongside an IQ test) for diagnosing intellectual disability." },
  { id: "m3_ass14", section: "assessment",
    q: "If a test has reliability of 0.80 and a standard deviation of 15, the standard error of measurement (SEM) is approximately:",
    options: { a: "6.7",
               b: "1.5",
               c: "12.0",
               d: "15.0" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "SEM = SD × √(1 − r) = 15 × √0.20 = 15 × 0.447 ≈ 6.7. Roughly 68% confidence interval = observed score ± 1 SEM; 95% CI ≈ ±2 SEM. Higher reliability shrinks SEM." },
  { id: "m3_ass15", section: "assessment",
    q: "The Wechsler Memory Scale (WMS-IV) yields five Index scores. Which is NOT among them?",
    options: { a: "Auditory Memory",
               b: "Mechanical Reasoning",
               c: "Visual Working Memory",
               d: "Visual Memory" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "WMS-IV Indices: Auditory Memory, Visual Memory, Visual Working Memory, Immediate Memory, Delayed Memory. 'Mechanical Reasoning' is a Differential Aptitude Test subtest, not WMS-IV." },

  // ===== PSYCHOTHERAPY (14) =====
  { id: "m3_thr01", section: "therapy",
    q: "In CBT, the term 'Socratic questioning' refers to:",
    options: { a: "Open-ended questions that guide the client to examine and reformulate their own thoughts",
               b: "Direct confrontation of the client's beliefs",
               c: "Free association of childhood memories",
               d: "Hypnotic suggestion of new beliefs" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Socratic / guided discovery questions (Beck, Padesky) lead clients to test their own automatic thoughts and assumptions rather than being told their beliefs are wrong. Combined with downward-arrow technique and behavioural experiments." },
  { id: "m3_thr02", section: "therapy",
    q: "Exposure and Response Prevention (ERP) is the first-line behavioural intervention for:",
    options: { a: "Obsessive-compulsive disorder",
               b: "Schizophrenia",
               c: "Bipolar I disorder",
               d: "Antisocial personality disorder" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "ERP (Foa, Kozak): graded exposure to feared stimuli while preventing the compulsive response, breaking the negative reinforcement loop. First-line for OCD; also adapted for specific phobias, PTSD." },
  { id: "m3_thr03", section: "therapy",
    q: "Dialectical Behaviour Therapy (DBT), developed by Marsha Linehan, blends CBT with:",
    options: { a: "Mindfulness/acceptance practices drawn from Zen and dialectical philosophy",
               b: "Psychoanalytic free association",
               c: "Pharmacological augmentation",
               d: "Family systems Bowen theory" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "DBT's core dialectic = change (CBT skills) + acceptance (mindfulness, dialectical worldview). Four skills modules: mindfulness, distress tolerance, emotion regulation, interpersonal effectiveness. First-line for BPD." },
  { id: "m3_thr04", section: "therapy",
    q: "Rogers' three core conditions for client-centred therapy are:",
    options: { a: "Confrontation, interpretation, transference",
               b: "Unconditional positive regard, empathy, congruence",
               c: "Rational disputation, behavioural homework, cognitive restructuring",
               d: "Free association, dream analysis, transference" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Carl Rogers (1957): the necessary and sufficient conditions for therapeutic change are unconditional positive regard, accurate empathic understanding, and therapist congruence (genuineness). Foundational to humanistic and motivational interviewing traditions." },
  { id: "m3_thr05", section: "therapy",
    q: "In psychoanalysis, the term 'working through' refers to:",
    options: { a: "The repeated process by which insights gained in therapy are applied across situations until characterological change occurs",
               b: "Telling the client the correct interpretation once",
               c: "A brief intake assessment",
               d: "Termination of therapy" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Working through (Freud): the patient must repeatedly recognise, name, and apply an interpretation across multiple situations and transferences before it can transform character structure. A single interpretation rarely produces lasting change." },
  { id: "m3_thr06", section: "therapy",
    q: "Aaron Beck's cognitive triad in depression consists of negative views of:",
    options: { a: "Past, present, and future",
               b: "Self, world, and future",
               c: "Mother, father, and self",
               d: "Body, mind, and spirit" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Beck's cognitive triad: negative view of the self, of the world (or experiences), and of the future. Underlying schemas activated by stress generate biased cognitions that maintain depression. Targeted via cognitive restructuring + behavioural activation." },
  { id: "m3_thr07", section: "therapy",
    q: "Motivational Interviewing (Miller & Rollnick) is grounded in which spirit-of-MI principles?",
    options: { a: "Confrontation, persuasion, expert authority, compliance",
               b: "Free association, transference, abstinence",
               c: "Partnership, acceptance, compassion, evocation",
               d: "Behavioural shaping, contingency management" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "MI Spirit (Miller & Rollnick, 4th ed.): PACE — Partnership, Acceptance, Compassion, Evocation. Techniques: OARS (open Qs, affirmations, reflections, summaries), rolling with resistance, eliciting change talk. First-line in substance use." },
  { id: "m3_thr08", section: "therapy",
    q: "Family therapy's 'enmeshment' (Minuchin) refers to:",
    options: { a: "Diffuse boundaries with over-involvement and loss of individual autonomy",
               b: "Rigid boundaries with little emotional contact between members",
               c: "Healthy reciprocal closeness",
               d: "Pathological scapegoating of a single member" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Minuchin's structural family therapy: enmeshment = diffuse boundaries, low autonomy, family members overly involved. Disengagement = rigid boundaries, low closeness. Healthy families have clear-but-permeable boundaries with hierarchical structure." },
  { id: "m3_thr09", section: "therapy",
    q: "Yalom's 'here-and-now' focus in group therapy refers to:",
    options: { a: "Discussing only events outside the group session",
               b: "Strictly limiting the group to didactic content",
               c: "Working with interpersonal patterns as they appear in the group session itself, then illuminating those patterns",
               d: "Cognitive disputation of irrational beliefs" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Yalom: the group is a social microcosm — members reproduce their characteristic interpersonal patterns in the room. The here-and-now / process illumination loop (working through interaction, then reflecting on it) is the therapeutic engine of interpersonal group therapy." },
  { id: "m3_thr10", section: "therapy",
    q: "Systematic desensitisation (Wolpe) is based on the principle of:",
    options: { a: "Operant extinction",
               b: "Reciprocal inhibition — a fear response is incompatible with relaxation",
               c: "Cognitive disputation",
               d: "Two-factor theory of avoidance" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Wolpe (1958): pair a fear hierarchy with progressive muscle relaxation; because anxiety and relaxation are physiologically incompatible (reciprocal inhibition), the conditioned anxiety response is gradually extinguished. Largely replaced by in-vivo exposure, which often dispenses with formal relaxation." },
  { id: "m3_thr11", section: "therapy",
    q: "Acceptance and Commitment Therapy (ACT) targets which six core processes (the 'hexaflex')?",
    options: { a: "Cognitive restructuring, exposure, relaxation, homework, role-play, contingency management",
               b: "Defusion, acceptance, present moment, self-as-context, values, committed action",
               c: "Free association, dream analysis, transference, working through, interpretation, abstinence",
               d: "Mindfulness, distress tolerance, emotion regulation, interpersonal effectiveness, problem-solving, behavioural activation" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "ACT (Hayes, Wilson, Strosahl) hexaflex: cognitive defusion, acceptance, contact with present moment, self-as-context, values, committed action — six processes promoting psychological flexibility. The 4th option lists DBT skills modules, not ACT's hexaflex." },
  { id: "m3_thr12", section: "therapy",
    q: "Interpersonal Therapy (IPT) for depression typically organises treatment around which problem areas?",
    options: { a: "Cognitive distortions and dysfunctional schemas",
               b: "Trauma reprocessing through bilateral stimulation",
               c: "Free association and transference interpretation",
               d: "Grief, role disputes, role transitions, and interpersonal deficits" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "IPT (Klerman & Weissman): time-limited (12–16 sessions), focuses on one of four problem areas — grief, role disputes, role transitions, interpersonal deficits — linking mood symptoms to current interpersonal context, without targeting unconscious conflict." },
  { id: "m3_thr13", section: "therapy",
    q: "The 'empty chair' technique is most closely associated with which therapy?",
    options: { a: "Gestalt therapy (Fritz Perls)",
               b: "Rational Emotive Behaviour Therapy (Albert Ellis)",
               c: "Person-centred therapy (Carl Rogers)",
               d: "Family systems therapy (Murray Bowen)" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Perls' Gestalt therapy uses the empty chair to dialogue with a part of self or an absent other, integrating polarities and unfinished business. REBT uses ABC disputation; Rogers uses reflection; Bowen uses genograms / differentiation work." },
  { id: "m3_thr14", section: "therapy",
    q: "Eye Movement Desensitisation and Reprocessing (EMDR) was developed by:",
    options: { a: "Edna Foa",
               b: "Bessel van der Kolk",
               c: "Pat Ogden",
               d: "Francine Shapiro" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Francine Shapiro developed EMDR (1987–89); 8-phase protocol with bilateral stimulation (eye movements, tapping, tones). Endorsed by WHO and APA for PTSD; mechanism remains debated (working memory taxation, REM-like consolidation)." },

  // ===== BIOSTATS / RESEARCH (11) =====
  { id: "m3_bst01", section: "biostats",
    q: "A Type II error occurs when:",
    options: { a: "The null hypothesis is rejected when it is actually true",
               b: "The confidence interval is too wide",
               c: "The null hypothesis is not rejected when it is actually false",
               d: "The sample size is too large" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Type I error (α) = reject H0 when H0 true (false positive). Type II error (β) = fail to reject H0 when H0 false (false negative). Power = 1 − β = probability of detecting a true effect; conventionally targeted at 0.80." },
  { id: "m3_bst02", section: "biostats",
    q: "Which test is appropriate for comparing the means of three or more independent groups on a continuous outcome with normal distribution?",
    options: { a: "Paired t-test",
               b: "Chi-square test",
               c: "One-way ANOVA",
               d: "Mann-Whitney U test" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "One-way ANOVA (parametric, ≥3 independent groups). If non-normal use Kruskal-Wallis. Repeated-measures ANOVA for related groups; Friedman is its non-parametric analogue." },
  { id: "m3_bst03", section: "biostats",
    q: "An odds ratio of 1.0 means:",
    options: { a: "Strong positive association between exposure and outcome",
               b: "Strong protective effect",
               c: "No association",
               d: "Perfect correlation" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "OR = 1 → odds of outcome equal in exposed and unexposed (no association). OR > 1 → positive association; OR < 1 → protective. 95% CI not crossing 1 indicates statistical significance." },
  { id: "m3_bst04", section: "biostats",
    q: "Cohen's d of approximately 0.5 indicates:",
    options: { a: "Medium effect",
               b: "Small effect",
               c: "Large effect",
               d: "No effect" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Cohen's conventions: d ≈ 0.2 small, 0.5 medium, 0.8 large. Computed as (M1 − M2)/SDpooled. Correlation analogues: r ≈ 0.1 small, 0.3 medium, 0.5 large. Use with caution outside their original behavioural-sciences context." },
  { id: "m3_bst05", section: "biostats",
    q: "Stratified random sampling is preferred over simple random sampling when:",
    options: { a: "The population is homogeneous",
               b: "Sample size must be minimised at all costs",
               c: "The researcher wants to ensure representation of important subgroups",
               d: "No sampling frame exists" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Stratified random sampling: population divided into homogeneous strata, then random samples drawn from each — guarantees representation and reduces sampling variance when strata differ on the outcome. SRS is fine when the population is reasonably homogeneous on the variables of interest." },
  { id: "m3_bst06", section: "biostats",
    q: "A scale with high internal consistency but low criterion validity:",
    options: { a: "Is reliable but not valid (consistently measures something — but not the intended construct)",
               b: "Is both reliable and valid",
               c: "Is valid but not reliable",
               d: "Is neither reliable nor valid" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Reliability without validity is possible: a scale can consistently measure the wrong thing. Validity, by contrast, presupposes some level of reliability (you cannot validly measure what you cannot reproducibly measure)." },
  { id: "m3_bst07", section: "biostats",
    q: "Intent-to-treat analysis means that:",
    options: { a: "Only participants who completed the intervention as planned are analysed",
               b: "All non-completers are excluded to reduce noise",
               c: "Participants are analysed in the groups they were randomised to, regardless of completion or crossover, preserving the benefits of randomisation",
               d: "Analyses are limited to per-protocol completers only" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "ITT preserves randomisation and gives a conservative, real-world estimate of effectiveness; per-protocol analyses estimate efficacy under ideal adherence but risk selection bias. Pre-registered SAPs typically specify ITT as primary." },
  { id: "m3_bst08", section: "biostats",
    q: "A 95% confidence interval for a mean difference that includes zero indicates that:",
    options: { a: "The mean difference is exactly zero",
               b: "The result is not statistically significant at α = 0.05 (two-sided)",
               c: "Power is too low",
               d: "The sample size was too large" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "If 95% CI for a difference includes 0, the null cannot be rejected at α=.05 (two-sided). Wide CIs that include 0 may also reflect low power; narrow CIs around 0 support an absence of meaningful effect (a different inference)." },
  { id: "m3_bst09", section: "biostats",
    q: "A non-parametric alternative to the independent-samples t-test is:",
    options: { a: "Wilcoxon signed-rank test",
               b: "Mann-Whitney U test",
               c: "Friedman test",
               d: "McNemar test" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Mann-Whitney U (Wilcoxon rank-sum) = 2 independent groups. Wilcoxon signed-rank = 2 related samples. Friedman = ≥3 related samples. McNemar = paired dichotomous data." },
  { id: "m3_bst10", section: "biostats",
    q: "Number Needed to Treat (NNT) is calculated as:",
    options: { a: "Relative risk reduction × 100",
               b: "1 / Absolute Risk Reduction",
               c: "1 / Relative Risk",
               d: "Odds Ratio × 100" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "NNT = 1/ARR, where ARR = event rate in control − event rate in treatment. NNH = 1/Absolute Risk Increase. Smaller NNT = more efficient treatment. Lipid-lowering trials often quote NNT for cardiovascular events." },
  { id: "m3_bst11", section: "biostats",
    q: "The 'Bonferroni correction' is most appropriate when:",
    options: { a: "Conducting a single planned comparison",
               b: "Reporting effect sizes",
               c: "Conducting a non-parametric test",
               d: "Conducting many independent pairwise comparisons and wanting to control family-wise Type I error" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Bonferroni: divide α by number of comparisons (e.g., α=0.05/10=0.005). Controls family-wise error but is conservative when comparisons are correlated. Alternatives: Holm step-down, Benjamini-Hochberg FDR." },

  // ===== LANGUAGE (10) =====
  { id: "m3_lan01", section: "language",
    q: "Choose the option that best replaces the underlined portion: 'Neither the manager nor the staff <u>were</u> aware of the new policy.'",
    options: { a: "were",
               b: "is",
               c: "are",
               d: "was" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "With 'neither ... nor', the verb agrees with the nearer subject. 'Staff' here is a singular collective noun → 'was'. If it were 'neither the staff nor the manager', the verb would also be 'was' (manager = singular)." },
  { id: "m3_lan02", section: "language",
    q: "Identify the sentence with correct punctuation:",
    options: { a: "However, the results were encouraging; the authors urged caution.",
               b: "However the results were encouraging, the authors urged caution.",
               c: "However; the results were encouraging, the authors urged caution.",
               d: "However the results, were encouraging, the authors urged caution." },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "'However' as a conjunctive adverb takes a comma after it. Two independent clauses joined without a coordinating conjunction take a semicolon. Option (b) follows both rules." },
  { id: "m3_lan03", section: "language",
    q: "Choose the most appropriate word: 'The findings of the study are not <u>____</u> to the broader population because the sample was small and self-selected.'",
    options: { a: "generalisation",
               b: "generalisable",
               c: "generality",
               d: "generally" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Adjective form needed after 'are not' modifying findings → 'generalisable'. The others are a noun (generalisation), a noun (generality), and an adverb (generally)." },
  { id: "m3_lan04", section: "language",
    q: "Which sentence uses the conditional correctly?",
    options: { a: "If I would have known, I would have called you.",
               b: "If I knew earlier, I would called you.",
               c: "If I would knew, I called you.",
               d: "If I had known, I would have called you." },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Third conditional (past unreal): If + past perfect, would have + past participle. 'If I had known, I would have called you.' Avoid the common error 'If I would have known' in formal writing." },
  { id: "m3_lan05", section: "language",
    q: "Choose the word closest in meaning to 'ubiquitous':",
    options: { a: "omnipresent",
               b: "rare",
               c: "obscure",
               d: "uniform" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Ubiquitous = existing or being everywhere, especially at the same time = omnipresent. 'Uniform' shares 'sameness' but not 'everywhere'." },
  { id: "m3_lan06", section: "language",
    q: "Identify the error: 'The patient, along with her two children, <u>were</u> waiting in the consulting room.'",
    options: { a: "No error",
               b: "Replace 'were' with 'was'",
               c: "Replace 'with' with 'and'",
               d: "Replace 'her' with 'their'" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Phrases introduced by 'along with', 'as well as', 'in addition to' do NOT change the number of the subject. The subject is 'the patient' (singular) → 'was waiting'. Compare 'The patient and her two children were waiting' (now a compound subject, plural)." },
  { id: "m3_lan07", section: "language",
    q: "Choose the option closest in meaning to 'salient':",
    options: { a: "trivial",
               b: "prominent",
               c: "concealed",
               d: "uncertain" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Salient = standing out, conspicuous, most noticeable = prominent. From Latin 'salire' = to leap; the salient feature 'leaps out' at you. Trivial and concealed are near-opposites." },
  { id: "m3_lan08", section: "language",
    q: "Select the correctly punctuated sentence:",
    options: { a: "The therapist asked, 'have you been sleeping well'?",
               b: "The therapist asked, 'Have you been sleeping well?'",
               c: "The therapist asked, 'Have you been sleeping well.'",
               d: "The therapist asked 'have you been sleeping well?'" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Direct question inside quotation marks: capital letter at start, question mark inside the closing quote (US and most modern UK style). A comma sets off the speech tag: 'asked, \"...\"'." },
  { id: "m3_lan09", section: "language",
    q: "Reading comprehension: 'Although early studies suggested that lithium was uniformly effective, more recent meta-analyses have qualified this view: lithium reduces suicide and prevents relapse but is less reliable for acute mania than second-generation antipsychotics.' The passage primarily implies that:",
    options: { a: "Lithium has no role in modern psychiatry",
               b: "All early lithium studies were flawed",
               c: "Second-generation antipsychotics have replaced lithium completely",
               d: "Lithium remains valuable but is not the best agent for every phase of bipolar disorder" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "The passage qualifies early enthusiasm without rejecting lithium — emphasising that it remains useful for suicide reduction and relapse prevention while ceding acute mania to SGAs. (b) captures the qualification without overstating the conclusion." },
  { id: "m3_lan10", section: "language",
    q: "Choose the option that best replaces the underlined portion to be grammatically correct: 'Each of the participants <u>have</u> been informed about the risks.'",
    options: { a: "have",
               b: "has",
               c: "had been",
               d: "is" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "'Each' is singular, regardless of the prepositional phrase that follows. → 'Each of the participants has been informed...'. Same logic applies to 'every', 'either', 'neither' (all singular)." }
],
  "met-mock-4": [
  // ===== BIOPSYCHOLOGY (18) =====
  { id: "m4_bio01", section: "biopsych",
    q: "GABA-A receptors differ from GABA-B receptors primarily in that GABA-A:",
    options: { a: "Are excitatory receptors increasing Na+ permeability",
               b: "Are metabotropic G-protein coupled receptors acting via K+ and Ca2+ channels",
               c: "Are voltage-gated channels independent of GABA",
               d: "Are ionotropic ligand-gated Cl− channels producing fast IPSPs" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "GABA-A: ionotropic, ligand-gated Cl− channel, produces fast IPSPs, modulated by benzodiazepines, barbiturates, alcohol. GABA-B: metabotropic, G-protein coupled, slow IPSP via K+ efflux + Ca2+ channel closure, target of baclofen." },
  { id: "m4_bio02", section: "biopsych",
    q: "The NMDA glutamate receptor is unique among ionotropic receptors because:",
    options: { a: "It opens only at hyperpolarized membrane potentials",
               b: "It is permeable only to Cl− ions",
               c: "It requires both glutamate binding and membrane depolarization to relieve the Mg2+ block, permitting Ca2+ entry",
               d: "It is a metabotropic G-protein coupled receptor" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "NMDA is a coincidence detector: needs glutamate binding plus depolarization to dislodge the Mg2+ block; once open it is highly Ca2+ permeable, driving LTP and synaptic plasticity. AMPA receptors handle fast Na+ currents alongside it." },
  { id: "m4_bio03", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Bilateral medial temporal lobectomy (as in patient H.M.) produces:",
    options: { a: "Loss of all motor coordination",
               b: "Global retrograde amnesia with intact new learning",
               c: "Selective loss of semantic memory only, episodic memory preserved",
               d: "Severe anterograde amnesia for declarative memory with relatively spared procedural learning" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "H.M. (Henry Molaison, Scoville & Milner 1957) lost hippocampi bilaterally; resulting in dense anterograde amnesia for declarative (episodic + semantic) memory, with preserved procedural learning (mirror-tracing task) and intact short-term memory. Established hippocampus as critical for memory consolidation." },
  { id: "m4_bio04", section: "biopsych",
    q: "The amygdala is most directly implicated in:",
    options: { a: "Fear conditioning, emotional salience, and threat detection",
               b: "Motor sequence learning",
               c: "Conscious recall of factual information",
               d: "Generation of circadian rhythm" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Amygdala (esp. central + basolateral nuclei) is central to fear conditioning, emotional learning, and threat detection. Bilateral lesions cause Klüver-Bucy syndrome (hyperorality, hypersexuality, placidity, visual agnosia). LeDoux: 'low road' from thalamus to amygdala bypasses cortex." },
  { id: "m4_bio05", section: "biopsych",
    q: "The Papez circuit includes which sequence?",
    options: { a: "Hippocampus → fornix → mammillary bodies → anterior thalamus → cingulate cortex → hippocampus",
               b: "Cortex → striatum → globus pallidus → thalamus → cortex",
               c: "Retina → LGN → V1 → V2",
               d: "Amygdala → hypothalamus → cerebellum → thalamus" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Papez (1937) proposed this loop as the substrate of emotion: hippocampus → fornix → mammillary bodies → mammillothalamic tract → anterior thalamic nuclei → cingulate gyrus → entorhinal cortex → back to hippocampus. Korsakoff's mammillary body damage disrupts it." },
  { id: "m4_bio06", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Wernicke's area is classically located in:",
    options: { a: "Occipital pole",
               b: "Inferior frontal gyrus of the dominant hemisphere",
               c: "Posterior superior temporal gyrus of the dominant hemisphere",
               d: "Postcentral gyrus" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Wernicke's area: posterior part of the superior temporal gyrus (Brodmann area 22) of the dominant (usually left) hemisphere. Damage produces fluent aphasia with poor comprehension. Broca's area = inferior frontal gyrus (BA 44/45), nonfluent aphasia with intact comprehension." },
  { id: "m4_bio07", section: "biopsych",
    q: "The key feature distinguishing Broca's aphasia from Wernicke's aphasia is that Broca's aphasia involves:",
    options: { a: "Fluent speech with intact comprehension",
               b: "Loss of comprehension only, with fluent but meaningless speech",
               c: "Selective alexia with no other deficits",
               d: "Effortful, non-fluent speech with relatively preserved comprehension and impaired repetition" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Broca's: non-fluent, telegraphic, effortful speech; comprehension relatively preserved; repetition impaired; patient often aware. Wernicke's: fluent paraphasic speech (\"word salad\"), poor comprehension, poor repetition, patient often unaware. Conduction aphasia: fluent + good comprehension but impaired repetition (arcuate fasciculus)." },
  { id: "m4_bio08", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Which function is most reliably lateralised to the right (non-dominant) hemisphere in right-handers?",
    options: { a: "Phonological processing",
               b: "Visuospatial processing and prosody",
               c: "Grammatical syntax",
               d: "Verbal memory" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Right hemisphere is specialised for visuospatial processing, face recognition (fusiform face area), prosody (emotional tone of speech), and holistic/gestalt perception. Left hemisphere handles language (in ~95% of right-handers), analytic and sequential processing." },
  { id: "m4_bio09", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Damage to the orbitofrontal cortex is most likely to produce:",
    options: { a: "Pure aphasia",
               b: "Cortical blindness",
               c: "Hemiplegia",
               d: "Disinhibition, impulsivity, and impaired social judgment" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Orbitofrontal cortex (OFC) regulates emotion, reward valuation, and social judgment. Lesions → disinhibition, impulsivity, poor decision-making (Iowa Gambling Task deficits), socially inappropriate behavior. Classic case: Phineas Gage's personality change after the tamping-iron injury." },
  { id: "m4_bio10", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Dorsolateral prefrontal cortex (DLPFC) damage most specifically impairs:",
    options: { a: "Visual perception of colour",
               b: "Working memory, planning, and executive function",
               c: "Basic motor reflexes",
               d: "Long-term declarative memory" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "DLPFC (BA 9, 46) is the seat of working memory and executive control (planning, set-shifting, response inhibition). Damage → impaired Wisconsin Card Sorting Test, Stroop interference, n-back deficits. OFC handles reward/impulse; ACC handles error detection / conflict monitoring." },
  { id: "m4_bio11", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Slow-wave (deep) sleep is characterised on EEG by:",
    options: { a: "Sawtooth waves with REMs",
               b: "Delta waves (0.5-4 Hz), high amplitude",
               c: "Beta waves predominant",
               d: "Sleep spindles and K-complexes only" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Stage N3 (slow-wave sleep, SWS) is dominated by high-amplitude delta waves (0.5-4 Hz). N2 features sleep spindles + K-complexes. REM shows low-voltage mixed-frequency activity resembling wakefulness + sawtooth waves + rapid eye movements + atonia." },
  { id: "m4_bio12", section: "biopsych",
    q: "REM sleep behavior disorder (RBD) is characterised by:",
    options: { a: "Inability to enter any REM at all",
               b: "Loss of REM atonia, causing patient to enact dream content",
               c: "Bedwetting during N3",
               d: "Excessive daytime sleepiness without nocturnal symptoms" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "RBD: failure of normal REM atonia (mediated by pontine/medullary inhibition); patient physically acts out dreams (kicking, punching, vocalising). Strongly associated with α-synucleinopathies (Parkinson's, DLB, MSA) and often predates motor symptoms by years. Treated with melatonin or clonazepam." },
  { id: "m4_bio13", section: "biopsych",
    q: "SSRIs typically take 2-4 weeks for clinical antidepressant effect despite rapid synaptic serotonin elevation. The most-supported explanation is:",
    options: { a: "Gradual liver enzyme induction",
               b: "Time required for 5-HT1A autoreceptor desensitization, BDNF upregulation, and hippocampal neurogenesis",
               c: "Delayed crossing of the blood-brain barrier",
               d: "Slow accumulation of metabolites in the plasma" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Acute SSRI dosing raises synaptic 5-HT immediately, but presynaptic 5-HT1A autoreceptors initially suppress further firing. Over 2-4 weeks these autoreceptors desensitize, firing normalizes, and downstream effects (BDNF expression, hippocampal neurogenesis, dendritic remodeling) build up — paralleling the clinical onset." },
  { id: "m4_bio14", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Lithium is classically associated with which narrow therapeutic concern?",
    options: { a: "Narrow therapeutic index (0.6-1.2 mEq/L); toxicity overlaps the therapeutic range and includes tremor, ataxia, nephrogenic DI",
               b: "Inducing severe constipation as the dose-limiting toxicity",
               c: "Being safe in renal impairment without monitoring",
               d: "Causing acute liver failure within 24 hours of first dose" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Lithium has a narrow therapeutic window (0.6-1.2 mEq/L; toxicity from ~1.5; severe >2). Side effects include fine tremor, hypothyroidism, nephrogenic diabetes insipidus, weight gain. Levels rise with dehydration, NSAIDs, ACE inhibitors, thiazides. Renal and TSH monitoring is standard." },
  { id: "m4_bio15", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Benzodiazepines and barbiturates both potentiate GABA-A receptor activity but differ in that:",
    options: { a: "Benzodiazepines increase the frequency of channel opening (need GABA present); barbiturates increase duration of opening and can directly open the channel at high doses",
               b: "Barbiturates act on GABA-B receptors only",
               c: "Benzodiazepines work via NMDA receptors",
               d: "Benzodiazepines increase the duration of Cl− channel opening; barbiturates increase frequency" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Benzodiazepines bind allosterically to GABA-A and increase channel-opening frequency, requiring GABA presence (ceiling effect → safer in overdose alone). Barbiturates increase duration of opening and at high doses act as GABA agonists directly opening the channel — no ceiling → respiratory depression and death in overdose." },
  { id: "m4_bio16", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Long-term potentiation (LTP) in the hippocampus most directly involves:",
    options: { a: "Decrease in dendritic spine density",
               b: "Calcium influx through NMDA receptors triggering AMPA receptor insertion and synaptic strengthening",
               c: "Permanent depletion of glutamate stores",
               d: "Apoptosis of pre-synaptic neurons" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Hippocampal LTP (Bliss & Lømo 1973): high-frequency stimulation depolarizes the postsynaptic neuron → NMDA Mg2+ block relieved → Ca2+ influx → CaMKII activation → AMPA receptor phosphorylation + insertion into membrane → larger EPSPs to the same input. Considered the cellular substrate of declarative memory." },
  { id: "m4_bio17", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Which hypothalamic nucleus is most directly involved in oxytocin and ADH synthesis?",
    options: { a: "Suprachiasmatic nucleus",
               b: "Mammillary bodies",
               c: "Supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei",
               d: "Arcuate nucleus" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Magnocellular neurons in the supraoptic (SON) and paraventricular (PVN) nuclei synthesise oxytocin and ADH; axons project to the posterior pituitary where the hormones are released into systemic circulation. Parvocellular PVN neurons also synthesise CRH for the anterior pituitary." },
  { id: "m4_bio18", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Which neurotransmitter is synthesised from the amino acid tryptophan?",
    options: { a: "Acetylcholine",
               b: "Dopamine",
               c: "GABA",
               d: "Serotonin (5-HT)" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Tryptophan → 5-HTP (by tryptophan hydroxylase, rate-limiting) → serotonin (by AAAD). Dopamine and norepinephrine derive from tyrosine. GABA derives from glutamate (via GAD). Acetylcholine is synthesised from choline + acetyl-CoA by ChAT." },

  // ===== DEVELOPMENTAL (14) =====
  { id: "m4_dev01", section: "dev",
    q: "Erikson's first psychosocial stage (0-18 months) involves the conflict of:",
    options: { a: "Trust vs Mistrust",
               b: "Initiative vs Guilt",
               c: "Industry vs Inferiority",
               d: "Autonomy vs Shame and Doubt" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Erikson's 8 stages: 1. Trust vs Mistrust (0-18m), 2. Autonomy vs Shame/Doubt (18m-3y), 3. Initiative vs Guilt (3-6y), 4. Industry vs Inferiority (6-12y), 5. Identity vs Role Confusion (12-18y), 6. Intimacy vs Isolation (young adult), 7. Generativity vs Stagnation (middle adult), 8. Integrity vs Despair (late adult)." },
  { id: "m4_dev02", section: "dev",
    q: "In Piaget's theory, object permanence is acquired during the:",
    options: { a: "Concrete operational stage",
               b: "Formal operational stage",
               c: "Sensorimotor stage, around 8-12 months",
               d: "Preoperational stage, around age 4" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Object permanence (understanding that objects continue to exist when out of sight) emerges in the Sensorimotor stage (0-2 y), specifically around 8-12 months (substage 4). The A-not-B error in this period shows it's still imperfect. Full mastery by ~18-24 months." },
  { id: "m4_dev03", section: "dev",
    q: "Vygotsky's concept of the Zone of Proximal Development (ZPD) refers to:",
    options: { a: "The peer group with the strongest influence on identity",
               b: "The age range during which language acquisition is possible",
               c: "The neural region where developmental disorders originate",
               d: "The gap between independent performance and performance with a more knowledgeable other's guidance" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "ZPD: the difference between what a child can do alone vs what they can do with scaffolding from a more knowledgeable other (parent, peer, teacher). Implies that instruction should target this zone. Underlies the educational practice of scaffolding (Wood, Bruner, Ross 1976)." },
  { id: "m4_dev04", section: "dev",
    q: "Kohlberg's pre-conventional level of moral reasoning is characterised by:",
    options: { a: "Conformity to peer norms",
               b: "Maintaining social order and law",
               c: "Moral judgements based on avoiding punishment and obtaining rewards",
               d: "Universal ethical principles" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Kohlberg's stages: Pre-conventional (Stage 1 punishment-obedience, Stage 2 instrumental exchange) → Conventional (Stage 3 good-boy/girl, Stage 4 law-and-order) → Post-conventional (Stage 5 social contract, Stage 6 universal ethical principles). Pre-conventional is dominant in young children." },
  { id: "m4_dev05", section: "dev",
    q: "In Ainsworth's Strange Situation, an infant who shows little distress on separation, ignores the caregiver on reunion, and treats stranger and caregiver similarly is classified as:",
    options: { a: "Disorganised (Type D)",
               b: "Insecure-avoidant (Type A)",
               c: "Insecure-resistant/ambivalent (Type C)",
               d: "Secure (Type B)" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Type A (avoidant, ~20% in Ainsworth's Baltimore sample): minimal protest on separation, ignores/avoids caregiver on reunion, indifferent to stranger. Type B (secure): protests but is comforted on reunion. Type C (resistant): high distress, ambivalent on reunion (seeks then resists). Type D (Main): freezing, contradictory behaviour, often linked to maltreatment." },
  { id: "m4_dev06", section: "dev",
    q: "Bowlby's concept of the internal working model refers to:",
    options: { a: "The cognitive map a child forms of physical space",
               b: "The neural substrate of language acquisition",
               c: "A mental representation of self, attachment figures, and relationships, formed in infancy and guiding future attachment behaviour",
               d: "The infant's understanding of object permanence" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Internal working model (Bowlby 1969): the child's accumulating mental model of self (lovable / unworthy), caregivers (available / rejecting), and relationships (predictable / unpredictable). Forms the prototype for future relationships and is the proposed mechanism of attachment continuity across the lifespan." },
  { id: "m4_dev07", section: "dev",
    q: "Baumrind's authoritative parenting style is characterised by:",
    options: { a: "Low demandingness, high responsiveness",
               b: "High demandingness, high responsiveness",
               c: "Low demandingness, low responsiveness",
               d: "High demandingness, low responsiveness" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Baumrind 2×2 model: Authoritative (high warmth + high control) → best outcomes; Authoritarian (low warmth + high control) → withdrawn or hostile; Permissive / indulgent (high warmth + low control) → impulsive; Neglectful / uninvolved (low warmth + low control) → worst outcomes. Maccoby & Martin added the neglectful cell." },
  { id: "m4_dev08", section: "dev",
    q: "The concept of 'goodness of fit' in temperament research (Thomas & Chess) refers to:",
    options: { a: "The match between a child's temperament and the demands of the environment / caregiver",
               b: "The IQ difference between identical twins",
               c: "The number of siblings in a family",
               d: "Genetic concordance between parent and child" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Thomas & Chess (NYLS, 1956): outcomes depend not on temperament alone (easy / difficult / slow-to-warm-up) but on the fit between the child's temperament and the environment's demands. A 'difficult' child in a structured, patient family may thrive; an 'easy' child in a chaotic family may struggle." },
  { id: "m4_dev09", section: "dev",
    q: "Harry Harlow's rhesus monkey experiments demonstrated that:",
    options: { a: "Imprinting in primates occurs only during the first 24 hours",
               b: "Contact comfort, not feeding, was the primary basis for attachment",
               c: "Maternal absence in primates has no behavioural consequence",
               d: "Operant conditioning fully explains attachment" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Harlow (1958): infant rhesus monkeys preferred a cloth-covered \"mother\" surrogate over a wire \"mother\" that dispensed milk, even spending more time on the cloth one. Showed contact comfort, not food, drives attachment — direct challenge to the behaviourist/feeding-based account of attachment." },
  { id: "m4_dev10", section: "dev",
    q: "Lorenz's concept of imprinting is best demonstrated by:",
    options: { a: "Habituation to repeated stimuli",
               b: "Greylag goslings forming a rapid, irreversible following response to the first moving object after hatching during a critical period",
               c: "A child preferring same-sex playmates at age 5",
               d: "Reflexive smiling in newborns" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Konrad Lorenz (1935): precocial birds form an attachment to the first moving object encountered during a sensitive period after hatching (~13-16 h in greylag geese). Rapid, durable, relatively stimulus-general — established the concept of a critical/sensitive period in development." },
  { id: "m4_dev11", section: "dev",
    q: "Theory of Mind, classically tested with the false-belief task (Sally-Anne), typically emerges around:",
    options: { a: "Age 4 (preoperational)",
               b: "12 months",
               c: "Age 8 (concrete operational)",
               d: "Adolescence" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Sally-Anne false-belief task (Wimmer & Perner 1983; Baron-Cohen 1985): children consistently pass around age 4. Younger children answer based on reality, not Sally's mistaken belief. ASD children typically show delayed/impaired performance — basis of the 'ToM deficit' hypothesis." },
  { id: "m4_dev12", section: "dev",
    q: "Marcia's identity statuses are defined by two dimensions:",
    options: { a: "IQ and emotional intelligence",
               b: "Crisis (exploration) and commitment",
               c: "Internal and external locus of control",
               d: "Approach and avoidance motivation" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Marcia (1966), extending Erikson's stage 5: Identity Diffusion (no crisis, no commitment), Foreclosure (commitment without crisis), Moratorium (in crisis, no commitment yet), Identity Achievement (crisis worked through, commitment made). Typical adolescent path: diffusion → moratorium → achievement." },
  { id: "m4_dev13", section: "dev",
    q: "Selman's stages of perspective-taking describe progressive shifts from:",
    options: { a: "Concrete to abstract numerical reasoning",
               b: "Egocentric to multiple-mutual perspective-taking",
               c: "Inductive to deductive logic",
               d: "Procedural to declarative memory" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Robert Selman: Stage 0 egocentric (3-6 y) → Stage 1 social-informational (6-8) → Stage 2 self-reflective (8-10) → Stage 3 mutual / third-person (10-12) → Stage 4 societal perspective-taking (adolescence+). Maps onto Piaget's decline of egocentrism and the development of theory of mind." },
  { id: "m4_dev14", section: "dev",
    q: "Erikson's psychosocial conflict of late adulthood is:",
    options: { a: "Identity vs Role Confusion",
               b: "Ego Integrity vs Despair",
               c: "Generativity vs Stagnation",
               d: "Intimacy vs Isolation" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Stage 8 (~65+): reviewing one's life and accepting it as meaningful (integrity) or feeling regret and bitterness about wasted opportunities (despair). Successful resolution yields wisdom. Generativity vs Stagnation is the middle-adulthood stage (concern for next generation)." },

  // ===== ABNORMAL (18) =====
  { id: "m4_abn01", section: "abnormal",
    q: "The DSM-5 minimum duration for a Major Depressive Episode is:",
    options: { a: "6 months",
               b: "1 month",
               c: "1 week",
               d: "2 weeks" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "MDD: ≥5 of 9 symptoms (incl. depressed mood OR anhedonia) for ≥2 weeks, most of the day nearly every day, causing impairment, not due to substance/medical. Persistent Depressive Disorder (dysthymia): 2+ years. Adjustment disorder with depressed mood: within 3 months of a stressor." },
  { id: "m4_abn02", section: "abnormal",
    q: "DSM-5 requires symptoms of schizophrenia to be present for at least:",
    options: { a: "3 months",
               b: "1 month of active phase, with continuous disturbance ≥6 months",
               c: "2 weeks of active phase only",
               d: "1 year of any psychotic symptom" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Schizophrenia (DSM-5): ≥2 of {delusions, hallucinations, disorganised speech, grossly disorganised/catatonic behaviour, negative symptoms} for ≥1 month active phase (at least one must be from the first three), and continuous signs of disturbance for ≥6 months. <1 month = brief psychotic disorder; 1-6 months = schizophreniform." },
  { id: "m4_abn03", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Schneider's first-rank symptoms classically include:",
    options: { a: "Echolalia and stereotyped movements",
               b: "Olfactory hallucinations and tactile illusions",
               c: "Thought insertion, withdrawal, broadcasting; audible thoughts; voices arguing; delusional perception",
               d: "Pure anhedonia and avolition" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Kurt Schneider (1959) listed first-rank symptoms thought (in his time) to be pathognomonic for schizophrenia: thought insertion, withdrawal, broadcasting; audible thoughts (Gedankenlautwerden); voices arguing or commenting; delusional perception; somatic passivity; 'made' affect/will/actions. DSM-5 no longer treats them as pathognomonic but they remain useful." },
  { id: "m4_abn04", section: "abnormal",
    q: "DSM-5 requires obsessive-compulsive disorder to take up at least:",
    options: { a: "6 hours per day",
               b: "30 minutes per day, OR cause significant distress / impairment",
               c: "Only that obsessions are present",
               d: "2 weeks of continuous symptoms" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "OCD (DSM-5): obsessions and/or compulsions that are time-consuming (>1 h/day in DSM-IV — DSM-5 loosened to '≥1 h or significant distress/impairment'), not due to substance/medical, distinguished from other disorders. Insight specifier (good, poor, absent/delusional) added in DSM-5." },
  { id: "m4_abn05", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Which feature most reliably distinguishes Borderline PD from Conduct Disorder?",
    options: { a: "Both are identical conditions",
               b: "CD always becomes BPD in adulthood",
               c: "BPD is exclusively biological while CD is exclusively environmental",
               d: "BPD is diagnosed in adults and centres on identity / affective instability and fear of abandonment; CD is diagnosed in <18 and centres on rule-violating, aggressive, antisocial behaviour" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "BPD (cluster B PD): adult diagnosis; instability of relationships, identity, affect, marked impulsivity, recurrent self-harm/suicidal behaviour, abandonment fears. CD: ≤18 y; persistent pattern of rule violations, aggression to people/animals, deceit, theft, serious rule breaking. CD in childhood often precedes Antisocial PD (not BPD)." },
  { id: "m4_abn06", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Capgras delusion is the belief that:",
    options: { a: "One is dead or does not exist (nihilistic)",
               b: "One's spouse is being unfaithful without evidence",
               c: "Strangers are familiar people in disguise",
               d: "A familiar person has been replaced by an impostor" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Capgras: familiar person replaced by an identical impostor. Fregoli: a stranger is actually a familiar person in disguise. Cotard: nihilistic delusion (\"I am dead/rotting/non-existent\"). Othello: delusional jealousy. All are 'delusional misidentification syndromes' or related." },
  { id: "m4_abn07", section: "abnormal",
    q: "The defining feature distinguishing conversion disorder (functional neurological symptom disorder) from malingering is that conversion disorder involves:",
    options: { a: "Intentional production of symptoms for external gain",
               b: "Conscious production of symptoms for the sick role",
               c: "Faked symptoms in legal proceedings only",
               d: "Unintentional, unconsciously produced neurological symptoms not feigned for gain" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Conversion (functional neurological symptom disorder): unintentional, unconsciously generated motor/sensory neurological symptoms incompatible with neurological disease; not feigned. Factitious: intentionally produced for the sick role (no external incentive). Malingering: intentionally produced for external gain (money, drugs, avoiding work/duty)." },
  { id: "m4_abn08", section: "abnormal",
    q: "DSM-5 requires PTSD symptoms to persist for at least:",
    options: { a: "1 week",
               b: "1 month, with symptoms in all four clusters",
               c: "6 months",
               d: "3 days" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "PTSD (DSM-5): exposure to actual/threatened death, serious injury, sexual violence + symptoms ≥1 month in 4 clusters (intrusion, avoidance, negative cognition/mood, hyperarousal). <1 month = Acute Stress Disorder. Delayed expression specifier if full criteria not met until ≥6 months post-event." },
  { id: "m4_abn09", section: "abnormal",
    q: "DSM-5 criteria for Binge Eating Disorder require binge episodes to occur on average at least:",
    options: { a: "Once weekly for 6 months",
               b: "Twice monthly for 1 month",
               c: "Once weekly for 3 months",
               d: "Once daily for a year" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "BED (DSM-5): recurrent binge episodes (≥1/week for ≥3 months) marked by loss of control + ≥3 of (eating rapidly, until uncomfortably full, when not hungry, alone, disgust/guilt afterwards). No compensatory behaviours (distinguishes from BN). BN bingeing + purging also ≥1/week for ≥3 months." },
  { id: "m4_abn10", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Charles Bonnet syndrome is characterised by:",
    options: { a: "Auditory hallucinations in dementia",
               b: "Tactile hallucinations in alcohol withdrawal",
               c: "Visual hallucinations in cognitively intact patients with visual impairment, with insight preserved",
               d: "Olfactory hallucinations preceding seizures" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Charles Bonnet syndrome: complex visual hallucinations (faces, figures, patterns) in cognitively intact older adults with significant visual loss (e.g. macular degeneration). Insight typically preserved (\"I know it isn't really there\"). Thought to reflect cortical release / deafferentation phenomena." },
  { id: "m4_abn11", section: "abnormal",
    q: "The most reliable clinical feature distinguishing delirium from dementia is:",
    options: { a: "Delirium occurs only in young patients",
               b: "Memory loss is greater in delirium",
               c: "Delirium has acute onset, fluctuating course, prominent attentional disturbance, and reversible cause; dementia has insidious onset and progressive course with relatively preserved attention early",
               d: "Dementia is always reversible" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Delirium: acute onset (hours-days), fluctuating, inattention is the cardinal feature, altered consciousness, often reversible (infection, drugs, metabolic). Dementia: insidious (months-years), progressive, memory impairment with later attentional decline. Both can co-occur; delirium superimposed on dementia is common in hospitalised elderly." },
  { id: "m4_abn12", section: "abnormal",
    q: "DSM-5 Persistent Depressive Disorder (dysthymia) requires symptoms to be present for at least:",
    options: { a: "5 years",
               b: "1 year in children/adolescents and 2 years in adults",
               c: "2 weeks",
               d: "6 months" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "PDD: depressed mood most of the day, more days than not, for ≥2 years in adults (≥1 year in children/adolescents) + ≥2 of 6 symptoms; never without symptoms for >2 months; no manic/hypomanic episode. DSM-5 merged chronic MDD and dysthymia into PDD." },
  { id: "m4_abn13", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Tourette's disorder requires:",
    options: { a: "Coprolalia as a required feature",
               b: "Vocal tics only, >1 month",
               c: "Both motor and one or more vocal tics (not necessarily concurrent), ≥1 year, onset <18 years",
               d: "Only motor tics for 6 months" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Tourette's (DSM-5): multiple motor tics + ≥1 vocal tic (not necessarily concurrent) for ≥1 year, with onset before age 18, not due to substance/medical. Coprolalia is famous but occurs in only ~10-15% — not required. Comorbid OCD and ADHD are very common." },
  { id: "m4_abn14", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Anorexia nervosa, restricting subtype, is defined in DSM-5 by:",
    options: { a: "Required compensatory vomiting",
               b: "No binge-eating or purging behaviours during the current 3-month episode; weight loss via dietary restriction, fasting, excessive exercise",
               c: "Binge-purge cycles within 3 months",
               d: "Required laxative use weekly" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "AN restricting subtype (DSM-5): during the last 3 months, no recurrent binge-eating or purging; weight loss achieved by dietary restriction, fasting, and/or excessive exercise. Binge-eating/purging subtype: recurrent BE/P in the last 3 months. DSM-5 removed amenorrhea criterion and softened weight threshold." },
  { id: "m4_abn15", section: "abnormal",
    q: "The four DSM-5 PTSD symptom clusters are:",
    options: { a: "Intrusion, avoidance, negative alterations in cognition / mood, alterations in arousal / reactivity",
               b: "Hyperarousal only, with mood changes optional",
               c: "Flashbacks, denial, anger, bargaining",
               d: "Dissociation, anxiety, depression, mania" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "DSM-5 PTSD clusters: B Intrusion (flashbacks, nightmares, intrusive memories), C Avoidance (of internal/external reminders), D Negative alterations in cognition/mood (negative beliefs, blame, detachment, diminished interest), E Alterations in arousal/reactivity (hypervigilance, startle, irritability, sleep). DSM-IV had 3 clusters; DSM-5 split C/D." },
  { id: "m4_abn16", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Pseudobulbar affect (PBA) is characterised by:",
    options: { a: "Anxious affect in GAD",
               b: "Affect blunting in schizophrenia",
               c: "Sudden, brief, involuntary outbursts of crying or laughing disproportionate to or incongruent with the underlying mood; associated with neurological conditions (stroke, MS, ALS, dementia)",
               d: "Mood-congruent affect in MDD" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "PBA (pathological laughing and crying): involuntary, paroxysmal emotional expression incongruent with underlying mood, usually from corticobulbar-tract disruption (stroke, MS, ALS, TBI, dementia). Treated with dextromethorphan-quinidine; also SSRIs. Not the same as the depressed mood of post-stroke depression." },
  { id: "m4_abn17", section: "abnormal",
    q: "The key DSM-5 feature distinguishing mania from hypomania is:",
    options: { a: "Mania has psychotic features, hypomania never does; mania causes marked impairment / hospitalisation / psychosis and lasts ≥7 days; hypomania ≥4 days with no marked impairment",
               b: "Mania has no euphoria",
               c: "Hypomania lasts longer than mania",
               d: "Only hypomania can include psychotic features" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Mania: ≥1 week (or any duration if hospitalisation required), causes marked impairment, may include psychotic features → Bipolar I. Hypomania: ≥4 days, no marked impairment, no psychosis, observable by others → Bipolar II (with MDE). MDE not required for Bipolar I. Hypomania never includes psychosis by definition." },
  { id: "m4_abn18", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Folie à deux (shared psychotic disorder) describes:",
    options: { a: "Group hallucinations in cult settings",
               b: "Two unrelated patients hospitalised with identical hallucinations",
               c: "A delusion that develops in a previously healthy person closely associated with someone who has an established delusional disorder",
               d: "Schizophrenia transmitted via genetic inheritance" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Folie à deux (Lasègue & Falret 1877): a delusion is transmitted from a 'primary' (dominant) individual with an established psychotic illness to a 'secondary' partner in close relationship, often isolated from outside contacts. DSM-5 collapses this into 'Other Specified Schizophrenia Spectrum'." },

  // ===== ASSESSMENT (15) =====
  { id: "m4_ass01", section: "assessment",
    q: "On the WAIS-IV, the four index scores are:",
    options: { a: "Crystallised, Fluid, Visual, Quantitative",
               b: "VCI, PRI, WMI, PSI",
               c: "VIQ, PIQ, Memory, Attention",
               d: "Verbal IQ, Performance IQ, Full Scale IQ, and Memory Quotient" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "WAIS-IV (2008) replaced VIQ/PIQ with 4 indices: Verbal Comprehension Index (VCI), Perceptual Reasoning Index (PRI), Working Memory Index (WMI), Processing Speed Index (PSI). Full Scale IQ is the composite. WAIS-V was published 2024 but WAIS-IV still in use in many settings." },
  { id: "m4_ass02", section: "assessment",
    q: "On the MMPI-2, the F (Infrequency) scale is used to detect:",
    options: { a: "Religious bias",
               b: "Item endorsement based on social desirability",
               c: "Random responding or over-reporting of unusual / severe symptoms (faking bad)",
               d: "Defensiveness only" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "MMPI validity scales: L (Lie, defensiveness/faking good), F (Infrequency, random responding or over-reporting/faking bad), K (Correction, more subtle defensiveness), VRIN (Variable Response Inconsistency), TRIN (True Response Inconsistency). Elevated F suggests symptom exaggeration or severe psychopathology — must rule out random responding (VRIN/TRIN) first." },
  { id: "m4_ass03", section: "assessment",
    q: "Cronbach's alpha is most commonly used to estimate:",
    options: { a: "Construct validity",
               b: "Internal consistency reliability of a multi-item scale",
               c: "Predictive validity",
               d: "Test-retest reliability" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Cronbach's α (1951): index of internal consistency for a scale's items. Range 0-1; usually ≥0.7 acceptable, ≥0.8 good, ≥0.9 excellent. Higher α can also reflect redundancy. Inter-rater reliability uses Cohen's κ. Test-retest uses Pearson r over time." },
  { id: "m4_ass04", section: "assessment",
    q: "The Exner Comprehensive System is the major scoring system for:",
    options: { a: "MMPI",
               b: "TAT",
               c: "Rorschach Inkblot Test",
               d: "Wechsler scales" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "John Exner's Comprehensive System (1974) integrated 5 prior Rorschach systems into a single standardised approach with norms. Codes location, determinants, content, populars, special scores → structural summary. Largely superseded by R-PAS (Rorschach Performance Assessment System, 2011)." },
  { id: "m4_ass05", section: "assessment",
    q: "Sensitivity of a screening test refers to:",
    options: { a: "Probability that a positive test reflects true disease",
               b: "Probability of disease prior to testing",
               c: "Proportion of true positives correctly identified as positive (true positive rate)",
               d: "Proportion of true negatives correctly identified as negative" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Sensitivity = TP / (TP + FN) — proportion of actual cases the test catches. Specificity = TN / (TN + FP) — proportion of non-cases correctly cleared. PPV = TP / (TP + FP) — depends on prevalence. NPV = TN / (TN + FN). High sensitivity = rule out (SnNOut), high specificity = rule in (SpPIn)." },
  { id: "m4_ass06", section: "assessment",
    q: "A stanine score of 5 corresponds to which position on the normal curve?",
    options: { a: "23rd-39th percentile (just below average)",
               b: "Above the 95th percentile",
               c: "40th-60th percentile (mean ± 0.25 SD)",
               d: "1st-4th percentile" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Stanine scores (Standard Nine): scale 1-9 with mean 5, SD ~2, each band 0.5 SD wide. Stanine 5 = 40-60th percentile (the middle band). Stanines 1-3 below average, 4-6 average, 7-9 above average. Used historically in educational testing." },
  { id: "m4_ass07", section: "assessment",
    q: "BDI-II severity bands are conventionally:",
    options: { a: "0-13 minimal, 14-19 mild, 20-28 moderate, 29-63 severe",
               b: "0-5 minimal, 6-10 mild, 11-15 moderate, 16+ severe",
               c: "0-9 minimal, 10-15 mild, 16-23 moderate, 24-40 severe",
               d: "0-20 minimal, 21-40 moderate, 41-60 severe" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Beck Depression Inventory-II (1996), 21-item self-report, total 0-63: 0-13 minimal, 14-19 mild, 20-28 moderate, 29-63 severe. PHQ-9: 0-4 / 5-9 / 10-14 / 15-19 / 20-27. HAM-D (clinician-rated): 0-7 / 8-13 / 14-18 / 19-22 / ≥23." },
  { id: "m4_ass08", section: "assessment",
    q: "Item Response Theory (IRT) differs from Classical Test Theory (CTT) primarily in that IRT:",
    options: { a: "Models the probability of a correct response as a function of person ability and item parameters (difficulty, discrimination, guessing), enabling computer-adaptive testing",
               b: "Is identical to CTT but uses a different label",
               c: "Cannot estimate item difficulty",
               d: "Requires only a single item parameter and no person parameter" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "IRT (Rasch, Lord, Birnbaum) models P(correct response) as a logistic function of θ (person ability) and item parameters: b (difficulty), a (discrimination), c (guessing). 1PL = Rasch; 2PL adds discrimination; 3PL adds guessing. Underlies modern computer-adaptive tests (NCLEX, GRE, MAP)." },
  { id: "m4_ass09", section: "assessment",
    q: "The Bender Visual-Motor Gestalt Test (Bender-Gestalt II) primarily assesses:",
    options: { a: "Auditory memory",
               b: "Visual-motor integration; useful for screening developmental and neuropsychological dysfunction",
               c: "Verbal abstraction",
               d: "Personality traits via projective interpretation only" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Bender-Gestalt II (Brannigan & Decker 2003): subject copies geometric designs; scored for accuracy, organisation, distortion. Screens visual-motor integration, useful in children for school readiness and in adults for organic brain dysfunction (though limited specificity). Sometimes interpreted projectively (legacy use)." },
  { id: "m4_ass10", section: "assessment",
    q: "The Thematic Apperception Test (TAT) is classified as a:",
    options: { a: "Objective achievement test",
               b: "Self-report personality inventory",
               c: "Structured neuropsychological battery",
               d: "Projective technique using ambiguous interpersonal scenes (Murray 1935)" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "TAT (Murray & Morgan, Harvard 1935): subjects construct stories about ambiguous interpersonal pictures (originally 31, typically 8-12 used). Scored for needs, press, themes. Murray's Need-Press theory underlies it. Other projectives: Rorschach (inkblots), Sentence Completion (Rotter, Washington University), Draw-A-Person, HTP." },
  { id: "m4_ass11", section: "assessment",
    q: "Concurrent validity is established by:",
    options: { a: "Correlating a new test with an established criterion measured at approximately the same time",
               b: "Correlating a test with future performance",
               c: "Demonstrating that experts agree the items appear to measure the construct",
               d: "Comparing scores across two cultures" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Criterion validity has two flavours: Concurrent (criterion is measured at the same time as the test) and Predictive (criterion measured later — e.g. SAT vs college GPA). Convergent + discriminant validity (Campbell & Fiske multitrait-multimethod) bear on construct validity, not criterion validity per se." },
  { id: "m4_ass12", section: "assessment",
    q: "The Halstead-Reitan Neuropsychological Battery includes which classic subtest?",
    options: { a: "Category Test, Tactual Performance Test, Trail Making, Speech-Sounds Perception, Finger Tapping",
               b: "BDI and HAM-D",
               c: "WAIS plus MMPI only",
               d: "Bender-Gestalt only" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Halstead-Reitan (Reitan 1955): Category Test, Tactual Performance Test (TPT), Speech-Sounds Perception, Seashore Rhythm, Finger Tapping, Trail Making A/B, Aphasia Screening, Sensory-Perceptual. Yields Impairment Index. The other major battery: Luria-Nebraska (Christensen → Golden), more theoretically driven, faster to administer." },
  { id: "m4_ass13", section: "assessment",
    q: "The Vineland Adaptive Behavior Scales primarily assess:",
    options: { a: "Suicidal ideation severity",
               b: "Personality disorders",
               c: "Cognitive ability only",
               d: "Communication, daily living skills, socialisation, and motor skills — adaptive functioning across the lifespan" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Vineland-3 (Sparrow et al.): semi-structured caregiver interview / questionnaire measuring adaptive behaviour in four domains: Communication, Daily Living Skills, Socialisation, Motor Skills (+ Maladaptive Behaviour index). Required for ID diagnosis under DSM-5 (alongside IQ <70-75). Useful in ASD, ID, brain injury." },
  { id: "m4_ass14", section: "assessment",
    q: "The standard error of measurement (SEM) of a test is calculated as:",
    options: { a: "SD / reliability",
               b: "SD × (1 + reliability)",
               c: "SD × √(1 - reliability)",
               d: "SD × reliability" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "SEM = SD_obs × √(1 - r_xx). Reflects the spread of observed scores around an examinee's true score. A 68% CI for a true score is ±1 SEM; 95% CI is ±1.96 SEM. As reliability → 1, SEM → 0. The WAIS-IV FSIQ has SEM ≈ 2.16, so 95% CI ≈ ±4 IQ points." },
  { id: "m4_ass15", section: "assessment",
    q: "The Wechsler Memory Scale-IV (WMS-IV) yields which primary indices?",
    options: { a: "Verbal IQ and Performance IQ",
               b: "Crystallised + Fluid Intelligence only",
               c: "Auditory Memory, Visual Memory, Visual Working Memory, Immediate Memory, Delayed Memory",
               d: "Attention, Concentration, and Vigilance only" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "WMS-IV (Wechsler 2009): Adult battery indices: Auditory Memory, Visual Memory, Visual Working Memory, Immediate Memory, Delayed Memory. Subtests include Logical Memory I/II, Verbal Paired Associates, Designs, Visual Reproduction, Spatial Addition, Symbol Span. Brief Cognitive Status Exam used as a screener." },

  // ===== THERAPY (14) =====
  { id: "m4_the01", section: "therapy",
    q: "Socratic questioning in CBT is used primarily to:",
    options: { a: "Free-associate to childhood memories",
               b: "Replace exposure therapy",
               c: "Tell the client what to think",
               d: "Help the client examine evidence for and against their automatic thoughts and arrive at more adaptive conclusions through guided discovery" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Socratic questioning (guided discovery, Beck): therapist uses open-ended questions to help the client evaluate evidence, consider alternatives, and reach more balanced conclusions about their thoughts. Avoids direct instruction. Core technique alongside thought records, behavioural experiments, behavioural activation." },
  { id: "m4_the02", section: "therapy",
    q: "The first-line behavioural treatment for OCD is:",
    options: { a: "Exposure and Response Prevention (ERP)",
               b: "Hypnotherapy",
               c: "Free association",
               d: "Systematic desensitisation only" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "ERP (Foa, Kozak): prolonged in-vivo and imaginal exposure to feared stimuli with prevention of compulsive responses, enabling extinction of the obsession-anxiety-compulsion loop. First-line per APA/NICE for OCD. SSRIs are first-line pharmacotherapy. Combined ERP + SSRI for severe cases." },
  { id: "m4_the03", section: "therapy",
    q: "Dialectical Behavior Therapy (DBT) was developed by Marsha Linehan primarily for:",
    options: { a: "Childhood ADHD",
               b: "Panic disorder",
               c: "Schizophrenia",
               d: "Chronic suicidality and Borderline Personality Disorder" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Linehan (1993) developed DBT for chronic suicidality and BPD. Synthesis of behaviour therapy + Zen / acceptance. 4 skill modules: mindfulness, distress tolerance, emotion regulation, interpersonal effectiveness. Standard programme: weekly individual + weekly group skills training + phone coaching + therapist consultation team." },
  { id: "m4_the04", section: "therapy",
    q: "Rogers' core conditions for client-centered therapy are:",
    options: { a: "Interpretation, transference, free association",
               b: "Modeling, role-play, homework",
               c: "Empathy, unconditional positive regard, congruence (genuineness)",
               d: "Confrontation, paradoxical intention, prescription" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Carl Rogers (1957): the three 'necessary and sufficient' core conditions for therapeutic change are empathy, unconditional positive regard (UPR), and congruence (genuineness/realness). Necessary in any therapy but uniquely emphasised and sufficient in person-centered therapy. Underlies the common factors literature." },
  { id: "m4_the05", section: "therapy",
    q: "Working through in psychoanalytic therapy refers to:",
    options: { a: "Solving problems by direct advice",
               b: "The repeated re-examination of interpretations, resistances, and conflicts in different contexts so the insight becomes integrated and behavioural change emerges",
               c: "Ending the therapy after a single insight",
               d: "Free association without interpretation" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Working through (Freud, Durcharbeiten): the laborious process of repeatedly encountering the same conflict and interpretation in varying contexts (transference, dreams, daily life) so the insight is consolidated and the client's character / behaviour actually changes. Distinguishes intellectual insight from emotional/behavioural change." },
  { id: "m4_the06", section: "therapy",
    q: "Beck's cognitive triad in depression consists of negative views about:",
    options: { a: "Mood, behaviour, and somatic state",
               b: "Past, present, and future",
               c: "Self, world (experience), and future",
               d: "Self, mother, and father" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Aaron Beck's cognitive triad (1967): in depression the patient holds negative schemas of (1) the self ('I am worthless'), (2) the world / current experience ('Nothing goes right'), and (3) the future ('It will never get better'). Schemas drive automatic thoughts → CBT targets these directly." },
  { id: "m4_the07", section: "therapy",
    q: "The 'spirit' of Motivational Interviewing is captured by the acronym PACE, which stands for:",
    options: { a: "Partnership, Acceptance, Compassion, Evocation",
               b: "Presence, Affirmation, Confrontation, Empathy",
               c: "Practice, Active listening, Change, Engagement",
               d: "Plan, Act, Check, Evaluate" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Miller & Rollnick (MI, 3rd ed. 2013) describe the MI spirit as PACE: Partnership (collaborative), Acceptance (absolute worth, autonomy, accurate empathy, affirmation), Compassion, Evocation (drawing out client's own motivation). 4 processes: Engaging, Focusing, Evoking, Planning. Core skills: OARS (Open Qs, Affirmations, Reflections, Summaries)." },
  { id: "m4_the08", section: "therapy",
    q: "In Minuchin's structural family therapy, enmeshment refers to:",
    options: { a: "Diffuse interpersonal boundaries with over-involvement, intrusion, and loss of autonomy among family members",
               b: "Strategic paradoxical interventions",
               c: "Rigid boundaries with disengagement",
               d: "A complete lack of communication between family members" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Salvador Minuchin's structural family therapy: families are organised by subsystems (parental, sibling, spousal) separated by boundaries. Enmeshed = diffuse, over-involved, low autonomy. Disengaged = rigid, low contact. Therapist 'joins' the family then restructures boundaries via enactment and challenging the family map." },
  { id: "m4_the09", section: "therapy",
    q: "Yalom's emphasis on the 'here-and-now' in group therapy is intended to:",
    options: { a: "Avoid all discussion of life outside the group",
               b: "Replace individual therapy permanently",
               c: "Bypass any examination of group dynamics",
               d: "Use the immediate group interactions as a social microcosm — observing and reflecting on patterns that emerge in real time as the engine of change" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Irvin Yalom: the group is a social microcosm; relationship patterns from outside life replay inside the group. The 'here-and-now' loop = (1) experience the moment, (2) illuminate it through reflection (self-reflective loop). 11 therapeutic factors (universality, instillation of hope, interpersonal learning, etc.) drive change." },
  { id: "m4_the10", section: "therapy",
    q: "Joseph Wolpe's principle of reciprocal inhibition is the basis for:",
    options: { a: "Acceptance and Commitment Therapy",
               b: "Systematic desensitisation (relaxation paired with graded imaginal/in-vivo exposure to a fear hierarchy)",
               c: "Paradoxical intention",
               d: "Free association" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Wolpe (1958): an anxiety response can be inhibited by simultaneously pairing it with an antagonistic response (deep relaxation). Procedure: train relaxation → build fear hierarchy → progress through hierarchy while staying relaxed. Predecessor of in-vivo exposure (which dropped the relaxation requirement after research showed exposure alone was sufficient)." },
  { id: "m4_the11", section: "therapy",
    q: "The 'hexaflex' of Acceptance and Commitment Therapy (ACT) consists of:",
    options: { a: "Six neurotransmitter systems",
               b: "Six DSM disorder clusters",
               c: "Sensation, perception, attention, memory, emotion, behaviour",
               d: "Acceptance, cognitive defusion, self-as-context, contact with the present moment, values, committed action" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Hayes ACT hexaflex (psychological flexibility): Acceptance, Cognitive Defusion, Self-as-Context, Contact with the Present Moment, Values, Committed Action. Each opposed by an inflexibility process (experiential avoidance, fusion, attachment to conceptualised self, etc.). Underpinned by Relational Frame Theory." },
  { id: "m4_the12", section: "therapy",
    q: "Interpersonal Therapy (IPT) targets four main problem areas:",
    options: { a: "Schemas, automatic thoughts, behaviours, biological factors",
               b: "Id, ego, superego, defences",
               c: "Cognitive, affective, motor, somatic",
               d: "Grief, role disputes, role transitions, interpersonal deficits" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "IPT (Klerman & Weissman 1984), originally for MDD: time-limited (12-16 sessions), focuses on current interpersonal context. Therapist + client select one of four problem areas: grief, interpersonal role disputes, role transitions, interpersonal deficits. Evidence base now extends to bulimia, postpartum depression, adolescent depression." },
  { id: "m4_the13", section: "therapy",
    q: "The 'empty chair' technique is most associated with which therapeutic school?",
    options: { a: "Person-centered therapy",
               b: "DBT",
               c: "Gestalt therapy (Fritz Perls)",
               d: "CBT" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Empty chair (Perls): the client dialogues with an absent person or disowned part of self projected onto an empty chair, often switching seats to voice both sides. Used to surface unfinished business, integrate polarities (top dog / underdog), and bring 'background' material into figure (Gestalt). Adapted into emotion-focused therapy (Greenberg)." },
  { id: "m4_the14", section: "therapy",
    q: "Francine Shapiro's EMDR (Eye Movement Desensitization and Reprocessing) is recommended primarily for:",
    options: { a: "PTSD and trauma-related conditions",
               b: "Schizophrenia",
               c: "Bipolar disorder",
               d: "OCD" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "EMDR (Shapiro 1987): 8-phase protocol pairing brief recall of distressing trauma memory with bilateral stimulation (eye movements / taps / tones). Effective for PTSD per APA, NICE, WHO. Mechanism debated (working-memory taxation, REM-like processing, dual attention); clinical efficacy comparable to trauma-focused CBT in head-to-head trials." },

  // ===== BIOSTATS (11) =====
  { id: "m4_bst01", section: "biostats",
    q: "A Type II error in hypothesis testing occurs when:",
    options: { a: "The p-value is set too low",
               b: "The sample size is excessively large",
               c: "A false null is not rejected (false negative); the alternative is true but the test misses it",
               d: "A true null is rejected (false positive)" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Type I (α): reject a true null = false positive. Type II (β): fail to reject a false null = false negative; the test misses a real effect. Power = 1 − β; the probability of correctly detecting a true effect. Power rises with bigger n, bigger effect size, larger α, lower variance." },
  { id: "m4_bst02", section: "biostats",
    q: "ANOVA (analysis of variance) is most appropriate when comparing:",
    options: { a: "Two paired observations of the same subject",
               b: "Two categorical variables",
               c: "Two independent group means with a continuous outcome",
               d: "Three or more independent group means with a continuous outcome" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "One-way ANOVA: 3+ independent group means on a continuous outcome. F = MS between / MS within. Significant F → post-hoc tests (Tukey HSD, Bonferroni, Scheffé) for pairwise differences. Two-way ANOVA = two IVs + interaction. Repeated-measures ANOVA for within-subjects designs." },
  { id: "m4_bst03", section: "biostats",
    q: "An odds ratio of 1.0 indicates:",
    options: { a: "The exposure is protective",
               b: "The exposure causes the outcome",
               c: "Exposure doubles the odds of the outcome",
               d: "No association between exposure and outcome" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "OR = 1: equal odds of outcome in exposed and unexposed → no association. OR >1: exposure increases odds. OR <1: protective. Same logic for RR and HR (=1 means no effect). 95% CI crossing 1 means non-significant association. OR approximates RR when outcome is rare (<10%)." },
  { id: "m4_bst04", section: "biostats",
    q: "Cohen's d of 0.5 conventionally represents:",
    options: { a: "Trivial effect",
               b: "Medium effect",
               c: "Small effect",
               d: "Large effect" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Cohen's d benchmarks for between-group standardized mean difference: ~0.2 small, ~0.5 medium, ~0.8 large. Pearson r benchmarks: 0.1 small, 0.3 medium, 0.5 large. Odds ratio benchmarks: 1.5 small, 2.5 medium, 4.3 large. Note: 'small' effects can still be clinically meaningful for prevalent outcomes." },
  { id: "m4_bst05", section: "biostats",
    q: "Stratified random sampling differs from simple random sampling in that it:",
    options: { a: "Selects every nth subject from a list",
               b: "Selects intact clusters such as schools",
               c: "Uses only volunteers",
               d: "Divides the population into mutually exclusive subgroups (strata) and then randomly samples within each, ensuring representation of each stratum" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Probability sampling: Simple random (every member equal chance), Systematic (every kth), Stratified (random within predefined strata — useful when subgroup comparisons matter or when groups are uneven in size), Cluster (sample whole groups). Stratified usually has lower variance than simple random for the same n when strata are homogeneous." },
  { id: "m4_bst06", section: "biostats",
    q: "Reliability and validity differ in that:",
    options: { a: "Reliability = consistency / reproducibility of measurement; validity = whether the test measures the construct it claims to. A test can be reliable but not valid; a valid test must be reliable.",
               b: "Reliability includes only inter-rater agreement",
               c: "They are the same thing in different words",
               d: "Validity must come first chronologically" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Reliability flavours: test-retest, internal consistency (Cronbach's α), parallel forms, inter-rater (Cohen's κ). Validity flavours: face, content, criterion (concurrent + predictive), construct (convergent + discriminant). A test can be perfectly reliable (e.g. a broken scale reading 75 kg) but invalid; a valid test must be reliable above some threshold." },
  { id: "m4_bst07", section: "biostats",
    q: "Intention-to-treat (ITT) analysis in an RCT means:",
    options: { a: "Analysing all randomised participants in their originally assigned group regardless of adherence, drop-out, or crossover",
               b: "Analysing only the participants who completed the intended treatment",
               c: "Analysing only the participants who responded",
               d: "Including only the control group" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "ITT: all randomised participants analysed in the group to which they were randomised, regardless of what actually happened. Preserves randomisation, prevents bias from differential dropout, gives a 'pragmatic effectiveness' estimate. Per-protocol analysis (only completers) tends to inflate efficacy and is supplementary." },
  { id: "m4_bst08", section: "biostats",
    q: "A 95% confidence interval for a mean difference of 0.4 that ranges from −0.1 to 0.9 implies:",
    options: { a: "The result is highly statistically significant",
               b: "There is no effect at all",
               c: "The sample was too large",
               d: "Because the CI crosses 0, the result is not statistically significant at α = 0.05" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "A 95% CI that crosses the null value (0 for differences, 1 for ratios) corresponds to a non-significant result at α = 0.05. CI width tells you about precision (sample size + variance); position tells you about effect size + direction. A non-significant result is not the same as 'no effect' — could be underpowered." },
  { id: "m4_bst09", section: "biostats",
    q: "The Mann-Whitney U test is the non-parametric analogue of:",
    options: { a: "Chi-square test of independence",
               b: "Paired t-test",
               c: "One-way ANOVA",
               d: "Independent-samples t-test" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Non-parametric analogues: Independent t → Mann-Whitney U (or Wilcoxon rank-sum). Paired t → Wilcoxon signed-rank. One-way ANOVA → Kruskal-Wallis. Repeated-measures ANOVA → Friedman. Pearson r → Spearman ρ. Used when normality is violated or for ordinal data." },
  { id: "m4_bst10", section: "biostats",
    q: "Number Needed to Treat (NNT) is calculated as:",
    options: { a: "1 / absolute risk reduction (ARR)",
               b: "Odds ratio − 1",
               c: "Relative risk × control event rate",
               d: "Number of patients enrolled / number of events" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "NNT = 1 / ARR, where ARR = control event rate − treatment event rate. Interpretation: number of patients who must be treated for one to benefit (vs control). Lower NNT = more effective treatment. NNH (Number Needed to Harm) = 1 / absolute risk increase. Always quote alongside relative measures." },
  { id: "m4_bst11", section: "biostats",
    q: "The Bonferroni correction adjusts the alpha threshold when conducting multiple comparisons by:",
    options: { a: "Dividing α by the number of comparisons performed (α / k); conservative control of family-wise error rate",
               b: "Replacing the p-value with a Z-score",
               c: "Ignoring the number of comparisons",
               d: "Multiplying α by the number of comparisons" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Bonferroni: with k comparisons, use α / k per test to keep family-wise error rate at α. Conservative (lower power) — alternatives include Holm-Bonferroni (sequential, less conservative), Šidák, and False Discovery Rate (Benjamini-Hochberg, controls expected proportion of false positives instead of FWER)." },

  // ===== LANGUAGE (10) =====
  { id: "m4_lan01", section: "language",
    q: "Choose the correctly punctuated sentence.",
    options: { a: "Although, it was raining we decided to walk.",
               b: "Although it was raining; we decided to walk.",
               c: "Although it was raining we decided, to walk.",
               d: "Although it was raining, we decided to walk." },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "An introductory dependent (subordinate) clause is followed by a comma before the independent clause. The dependent clause begins with 'although'. A semicolon would be wrong; semicolons join two independent clauses, not a dependent + independent pair." },
  { id: "m4_lan02", section: "language",
    q: "Choose the option that correctly completes the sentence: 'The data ___ inconclusive.'",
    options: { a: "are",
               b: "was",
               c: "has been",
               d: "is" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "'Data' is traditionally plural (singular: datum) → 'data are'. Modern usage increasingly accepts singular, but formal/academic style still prefers plural. In a research/exam context, 'data are' is the safer choice. Compare: 'media' (plural of medium), 'criteria' (plural of criterion)." },
  { id: "m4_lan03", section: "language",
    q: "Identify the sentence that uses 'who' / 'whom' correctly.",
    options: { a: "Who shall I say is calling?",
               b: "Whom is at the door?",
               c: "Who did you give the book to?",
               d: "Whom shall I say is calling?" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Test by substituting he/him: '[He/Him] is calling?' → 'He is calling' → use subject form WHO. The intervening 'shall I say' is parenthetical. 'Whom did you give the book to?' = correct (object of 'to'); 'Who did you give the book to?' is colloquially accepted but formally 'whom'." },
  { id: "m4_lan04", section: "language",
    q: "Which sentence uses the conditional correctly?",
    options: { a: "If I would have known, I would have come.",
               b: "If I would know, I came.",
               c: "If I knew, I have come.",
               d: "If I had known, I would have come." },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Third conditional (past unreal): If + past perfect, would + have + past participle. 'If I had known, I would have come.' Avoid 'would have' in the if-clause — a very common error. First: If + present, will + base. Second: If + past, would + base." },
  { id: "m4_lan05", section: "language",
    q: "Select the sentence with a misplaced modifier.",
    options: { a: "He drove to the office in the morning.",
               b: "Running quickly, the marathon finish line came into view.",
               c: "The teacher gave the test on Friday.",
               d: "She ate the soup that was hot." },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "'Running quickly' is a dangling participle modifying 'the marathon finish line' (which cannot run). Fix: 'Running quickly, she saw the marathon finish line come into view.' The other options place modifiers next to the words they modify." },
  { id: "m4_lan06", section: "language",
    q: "Choose the word closest in meaning to UBIQUITOUS.",
    options: { a: "Quietly persistent",
               b: "Outdated",
               c: "Universal / present everywhere",
               d: "Rare" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Ubiquitous = present, appearing, or found everywhere; pervasive. Antonyms: rare, scarce, uncommon. Often confused with 'iniquitous' (wicked, unjust). Related: omnipresent, universal, pervasive, all-pervading." },
  { id: "m4_lan07", section: "language",
    q: "Identify the sentence in passive voice.",
    options: { a: "The committee will approve the proposal.",
               b: "Did the committee approve the proposal?",
               c: "The committee approved the proposal.",
               d: "The proposal was approved by the committee." },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Passive: subject receives the action; uses form of 'to be' + past participle, agent in 'by' phrase (often omitted). Active: 'The committee approved the proposal.' Passive: 'The proposal was approved by the committee.' Passive useful when actor unknown / irrelevant or to emphasise receiver." },
  { id: "m4_lan08", section: "language",
    q: "Which option corrects the run-on sentence: 'She studied hard she passed the exam.'?",
    options: { a: "She studied hard so she passed the exam.",
               b: "She studied, hard she passed the exam.",
               c: "She studied hard; consequently, she passed the exam.",
               d: "She studied hard, she passed the exam." },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Two independent clauses with no punctuation = run-on / fused sentence. Fix options: (1) period, (2) semicolon (with/without conjunctive adverb + comma), (3) coordinating conjunction (FANBOYS) preceded by comma, (4) make one clause subordinate. Option a is a comma splice (still wrong)." },
  { id: "m4_lan09", section: "language",
    q: "Read the brief passage and answer:\n'Despite mounting evidence of climate disruption, public policy responses have been hampered by short electoral cycles and powerful incumbent interests, leaving long-horizon problems chronically under-addressed.'\nThe author's main point is best described as:",
    options: { a: "Short political horizons and entrenched interests prevent adequate response to long-term problems like climate change",
               b: "Climate change is not happening",
               c: "Renewable energy is more profitable than fossil fuels",
               d: "Elections should be abolished to address climate change" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "The sentence structure (Despite X, public policy is hampered by Y and Z, leaving... under-addressed) explicitly attributes inadequate response to short electoral cycles and incumbent interests — that is the author's main claim. Option C is an inferential leap not supported; D is irrelevant; A contradicts the text." },
  { id: "m4_lan10", section: "language",
    q: "Choose the correct option to complete: 'Neither the manager nor the employees ___ available for comment.'",
    options: { a: "were",
               b: "has been",
               c: "was",
               d: "is" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "With 'neither ... nor' (and 'either ... or'), the verb agrees with the noun closer to it (the proximate subject). 'Employees' is plural → 'were'. If reversed ('Neither the employees nor the manager ___ available'), it would be 'was'." }
],
  "met-mock-5": [
  // ===== BIOPSYCHOLOGY (18) =====
  { id: "m5_bio01", section: "biopsych",
    q: "The all-or-none law of the action potential means that:",
    options: { a: "A stronger stimulus produces a larger-amplitude action potential",
               b: "Action potentials can travel in both directions along the axon",
               c: "The resting membrane potential varies with stimulus intensity",
               d: "Once threshold is reached, the action potential fires at full amplitude regardless of stimulus strength; intensity is coded by firing frequency" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Once depolarisation reaches threshold (~−55 mV), the action potential fires at a fixed amplitude — it is all-or-none. Stimulus intensity is encoded by the rate (frequency) of firing and the number of neurons recruited, not by spike size. The refractory period also enforces one-way propagation." },
  { id: "m5_bio02", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Saltatory conduction refers to:",
    options: { a: "The continuous spread of depolarisation along an unmyelinated axon",
               b: "Backward propagation of the action potential",
               c: "Synaptic transmission across the cleft",
               d: "The action potential jumping between Nodes of Ranvier on a myelinated axon, greatly increasing conduction velocity" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "In myelinated axons the action potential regenerates only at the Nodes of Ranvier (where Na+ channels cluster), 'jumping' from node to node. This saltatory conduction is far faster and more energy-efficient than continuous conduction. Demyelination (e.g. MS) slows or blocks it." },
  { id: "m5_bio03", section: "biopsych",
    q: "The substantia nigra degenerates in which disorder, producing its motor signs?",
    options: { a: "Parkinson's disease",
               b: "Huntington's disease",
               c: "Alzheimer's disease",
               d: "Multiple sclerosis" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Parkinson's: loss of dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra pars compacta → depletion of dopamine in the striatum → bradykinesia, resting tremor, rigidity, postural instability. Huntington's involves the caudate/putamen (GABAergic medium spiny neurons); Alzheimer's the cholinergic basal forebrain and cortex." },
  { id: "m5_bio04", section: "biopsych",
    q: "The dopamine hypothesis of schizophrenia, in its classic form, holds that positive symptoms arise from:",
    options: { a: "Reduced dopamine in the prefrontal cortex only",
               b: "Excess serotonin at 5-HT2A receptors only",
               c: "Glutamate excess at NMDA receptors",
               d: "Hyperactivity of mesolimbic dopamine (D2) pathways" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Classic dopamine hypothesis: positive symptoms (delusions, hallucinations) reflect mesolimbic D2 hyperactivity — supported by D2-blocking antipsychotic efficacy and amphetamine-induced psychosis. The revised version adds mesocortical hypodopaminergia for negative/cognitive symptoms, plus glutamate (NMDA hypofunction) models." },
  { id: "m5_bio05", section: "biopsych",
    q: "The Korsakoff's syndrome amnesia is caused by a deficiency of:",
    options: { a: "Vitamin B12 (cobalamin)",
               b: "Vitamin B1 (thiamine), classically in chronic alcoholism",
               c: "Vitamin D",
               d: "Folate" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Korsakoff's syndrome results from thiamine (B1) deficiency, most often in chronic alcoholism, damaging the mammillary bodies and dorsomedial thalamus. Features: anterograde + retrograde amnesia and confabulation. It often follows Wernicke's encephalopathy (the Wernicke-Korsakoff continuum); urgent thiamine prevents progression." },
  { id: "m5_bio06", section: "biopsych",
    q: "The primary visual cortex (V1) is located in the:",
    options: { a: "Temporal lobe",
               b: "Parietal lobe",
               c: "Occipital lobe (calcarine sulcus)",
               d: "Frontal lobe" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "V1 (striate cortex, Brodmann area 17) lines the calcarine sulcus of the occipital lobe. It receives input from the lateral geniculate nucleus and is retinotopically organised. Hubel & Wiesel mapped its simple and complex orientation-selective cells. The dorsal ('where') stream runs to parietal, the ventral ('what') stream to temporal cortex." },
  { id: "m5_bio07", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Acetylcholine is the principal neurotransmitter at the:",
    options: { a: "Sympathetic postganglionic synapse on blood vessels",
               b: "Dopaminergic nigrostriatal pathway",
               c: "Neuromuscular junction (skeletal muscle)",
               d: "Noradrenergic locus coeruleus projections" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "ACh is the transmitter at the neuromuscular junction, acting on nicotinic receptors to trigger muscle contraction. It is also the transmitter at all autonomic ganglia and at parasympathetic postganglionic synapses (muscarinic). Curare blocks nicotinic NMJ receptors; myasthenia gravis is autoimmune destruction of them." },
  { id: "m5_bio08", section: "biopsych",
    q: "The HPA axis governs the body's response to stress through the sequence:",
    options: { a: "Hypothalamus → posterior pituitary → adrenal medulla (adrenaline)",
               b: "Hypothalamus (CRH) → anterior pituitary (ACTH) → adrenal cortex (cortisol)",
               c: "Pineal gland → pituitary → thyroid",
               d: "Amygdala → cerebellum → adrenal cortex" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "HPA axis: hypothalamic CRH stimulates anterior-pituitary ACTH, which drives adrenal-cortex cortisol release. Cortisol feeds back negatively on the hypothalamus and pituitary. The fast sympathetic route (adrenal medulla → adrenaline/noradrenaline) is the separate SAM axis. Chronic HPA activation is implicated in depression and PTSD." },
  { id: "m5_bio09", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Split-brain patients (corpus callosotomy) who view an object only in the left visual field typically:",
    options: { a: "Can name it aloud easily",
               b: "Cannot name it verbally but can select it with the left hand",
               c: "Become completely blind in the left field",
               d: "Lose all memory of the object" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Left visual field projects to the right hemisphere, which is typically non-verbal. With the callosum cut, that information can't reach the left-hemisphere language areas, so the patient can't name the object — but the right hemisphere can guide the left hand to select it. Sperry & Gazzaniga's classic demonstrations of hemispheric specialisation." },
  { id: "m5_bio10", section: "biopsych",
    q: "The suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN) of the hypothalamus functions as the:",
    options: { a: "Hunger and satiety centre",
               b: "Primary site of long-term memory storage",
               c: "Master circadian pacemaker, entrained by light via the retinohypothalamic tract",
               d: "Centre for thermoregulation only" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "The SCN is the body's master circadian clock, entrained to the light-dark cycle through the retinohypothalamic tract. It drives melatonin secretion by the pineal gland (high at night) and coordinates peripheral clocks. Lesions abolish circadian rhythmicity of activity and hormone release." },
  { id: "m5_bio11", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Damage to the ventromedial hypothalamus (VMH) classically produces:",
    options: { a: "Aphagia and starvation",
               b: "Hyperphagia and obesity",
               c: "Loss of thermoregulation",
               d: "Complete loss of thirst" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "The VMH acts as a 'satiety centre'; lesions produce hyperphagia and obesity (VMH syndrome). The lateral hypothalamus (LH) is the 'hunger centre'; its lesions cause aphagia and weight loss. This dual-centre model (Stellar) is now refined by leptin/ghrelin signalling and the arcuate nucleus, but remains exam-standard." },
  { id: "m5_bio12", section: "biopsych",
    q: "The James-Lange theory of emotion proposes that:",
    options: { a: "Physiological/bodily changes occur first, and our perception of those changes IS the emotion",
               b: "Emotion and physiological arousal occur simultaneously and independently",
               c: "We feel emotion first, then the body reacts",
               d: "Cognitive appraisal must precede any emotional experience" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "James-Lange: a stimulus triggers bodily/autonomic responses, and our perception of those responses constitutes the emotion ('we feel afraid because we tremble'). Cannon-Bard countered that arousal and emotion occur simultaneously via the thalamus. Schachter-Singer's two-factor theory added cognitive labelling of arousal." },
  { id: "m5_bio13", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Typical (first-generation) antipsychotics carry a high risk of extrapyramidal side effects because they:",
    options: { a: "Block serotonin reuptake",
               b: "Strongly block D2 receptors in the nigrostriatal pathway",
               c: "Agonise GABA-A receptors",
               d: "Selectively block mesolimbic D2 receptors only" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Typicals (haloperidol, chlorpromazine) are potent, non-selective D2 antagonists; blockade in the nigrostriatal pathway produces extrapyramidal symptoms (dystonia, parkinsonism, akathisia, tardive dyskinesia). Atypicals add 5-HT2A antagonism and looser D2 binding, lowering EPS risk but raising metabolic side effects." },
  { id: "m5_bio14", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Phantom limb sensation after amputation is best explained by:",
    options: { a: "Cortical reorganisation / remapping in the somatosensory cortex (Ramachandran)",
               b: "Regrowth of the severed nerve into the limb",
               c: "Pure psychological denial of the amputation",
               d: "Damage to the cerebellum" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Phantom limbs are linked to maladaptive cortical reorganisation: adjacent somatosensory map territory (e.g. the face) invades the deafferented hand area, so touching the face is felt in the phantom hand. Ramachandran's mirror-box therapy exploits visual feedback to relieve phantom pain. Not mere denial." },
  { id: "m5_bio15", section: "biopsych",
    q: "The 'reward pathway' most central to addiction is the:",
    options: { a: "Nigrostriatal dopamine pathway",
               b: "Mesolimbic dopamine pathway (VTA → nucleus accumbens)",
               c: "Tuberoinfundibular dopamine pathway",
               d: "Raphe-nuclei serotonergic pathway" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "The mesolimbic pathway runs from the ventral tegmental area (VTA) to the nucleus accumbens; dopamine release here signals reward and reinforcement. Virtually all drugs of abuse increase accumbens dopamine. The nigrostriatal pathway governs movement, tuberoinfundibular controls prolactin, mesocortical handles cognition/affect." },
  { id: "m5_bio16", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Myelin in the central nervous system is produced by:",
    options: { a: "Oligodendrocytes",
               b: "Schwann cells",
               c: "Microglia",
               d: "Astrocytes" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "In the CNS, oligodendrocytes myelinate axons (one cell wraps several axons). In the PNS, Schwann cells do it (one cell per internode). Astrocytes maintain the blood-brain barrier and ionic environment; microglia are the CNS immune cells. MS is a CNS demyelinating disease (oligodendrocyte/myelin attack)." },
  { id: "m5_bio17", section: "biopsych",
    q: "An EEG dominated by alpha waves (8-12 Hz) is most characteristic of:",
    options: { a: "Deep slow-wave sleep",
               b: "Active problem-solving and alertness",
               c: "REM sleep",
               d: "Relaxed wakefulness with eyes closed" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Alpha rhythm (8-12 Hz) appears during relaxed wakefulness, especially with eyes closed, and is blocked (replaced by beta) on opening the eyes or mental effort (alpha blocking / Berger effect). Beta (>13 Hz) = alert/active; theta (4-7 Hz) = drowsiness/light sleep; delta (<4 Hz) = deep sleep." },
  { id: "m5_bio18", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Kluver-Bucy syndrome, following bilateral temporal-lobe (amygdala) damage, is characterised by:",
    options: { a: "Spastic paralysis and rigidity",
               b: "Pure anterograde amnesia only",
               c: "Hyperorality, hypersexuality, placidity, and visual agnosia",
               d: "Loss of language comprehension" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Kluver-Bucy syndrome (bilateral anterior temporal lobectomy / amygdala damage): psychic blindness (visual agnosia), hyperorality (mouthing objects), hypersexuality, hypermetamorphosis (excessive attention to stimuli), and placidity/loss of fear. First shown in rhesus monkeys; rarely seen fully in humans (e.g. HSV encephalitis)." },

  // ===== DEVELOPMENTAL (14) =====
  { id: "m5_dev01", section: "dev",
    q: "In Piaget's theory, the inability to take another person's visual or conceptual perspective in early childhood is called:",
    options: { a: "Egocentrism",
               b: "Conservation",
               c: "Object permanence",
               d: "Reversibility" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Egocentrism — the preoperational child's difficulty distinguishing their own viewpoint from others' — is classically shown in Piaget & Inhelder's Three Mountains Task. It is not selfishness but a cognitive limitation. Conservation (invariance of quantity) and reversibility are acquired in the concrete-operational stage." },
  { id: "m5_dev02", section: "dev",
    q: "Conservation of number, mass, and volume is typically mastered in Piaget's:",
    options: { a: "Sensorimotor stage",
               b: "Preoperational stage",
               c: "Formal operational stage",
               d: "Concrete operational stage (roughly 7-11 years)" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Conservation — understanding that quantity stays constant despite changes in appearance — is the hallmark of the concrete-operational stage (~7-11 y), enabled by decentration and reversibility. Number is conserved first (~6-7), then mass, then volume (~11) — the horizontal décalage. Preoperational children fail due to centration." },
  { id: "m5_dev03", section: "dev",
    q: "Formal operational thought, in Piaget's final stage, is defined by the emergence of:",
    options: { a: "Hypothetico-deductive and abstract reasoning",
               b: "Object permanence",
               c: "Symbolic / pretend play",
               d: "Conservation of mass" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Formal operations (~11+ y): abstract, hypothetical, and systematic deductive reasoning, illustrated by the pendulum task (isolating variables). The adolescent can reason about possibilities, not just concrete realities. Not all adults reach it on all tasks; it is domain- and culture-sensitive." },
  { id: "m5_dev04", section: "dev",
    q: "Carol Dweck's research distinguishes a 'growth mindset' from a 'fixed mindset' primarily by the belief that:",
    options: { a: "Intelligence is malleable and can be developed through effort",
               b: "Intelligence is entirely inherited",
               c: "Praise should always focus on innate ability",
               d: "Failure should be avoided at all costs" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Growth mindset: ability/intelligence can grow with effort and strategy → resilience and mastery-oriented coping. Fixed mindset: ability is static → helpless responses to setbacks and avoidance of challenge. Dweck found that praising effort/process rather than innate ability fosters a growth mindset." },
  { id: "m5_dev05", section: "dev",
    q: "Mary Main's addition to Ainsworth's attachment classifications was:",
    options: { a: "Disorganised/disoriented (Type D)",
               b: "Secure (Type B)",
               c: "Avoidant (Type A)",
               d: "Resistant (Type C)" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Main & Solomon (1986) added the disorganised/disoriented category (Type D) for infants who show contradictory, freezing, or fearful behaviour toward the caregiver in the Strange Situation — often associated with maltreatment or frightening caregiving. It predicts later psychopathology more strongly than the organised insecure types." },
  { id: "m5_dev06", section: "dev",
    q: "Chomsky's concept of a Language Acquisition Device (LAD) argues that:",
    options: { a: "Language is learned purely through operant reinforcement",
               b: "Language develops only through imitation of adults",
               c: "Grammar must be explicitly taught",
               d: "Children possess an innate, biologically based capacity / universal grammar for acquiring language" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Chomsky proposed an innate LAD encoding a universal grammar, explaining how children master complex syntax rapidly despite 'poverty of the stimulus.' This nativist view opposed Skinner's behaviourist account (Verbal Behavior), which Chomsky famously critiqued. The critical-period hypothesis (Lenneberg) is a related idea." },
  { id: "m5_dev07", section: "dev",
    q: "In Kohlberg's theory, reasoning that 'I should keep my promise because society depends on people honouring agreements' reflects which level?",
    options: { a: "Conventional (law-and-order / social-order maintaining)",
               b: "Pre-conventional",
               c: "Post-conventional (universal ethical principles)",
               d: "Amoral" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Stage 4 (law-and-order), within the conventional level, emphasises maintaining social order, fulfilling duties, and respecting authority and rules for the good of society. Pre-conventional focuses on punishment/reward; post-conventional Stage 5 (social contract) and Stage 6 (universal principles) reason from rights and abstract justice." },
  { id: "m5_dev08", section: "dev",
    q: "The 'visual cliff' experiment (Gibson & Walk, 1960) was designed to study:",
    options: { a: "Depth perception and its developmental/innate basis",
               b: "Colour perception in infants",
               c: "Object permanence",
               d: "Language comprehension" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Gibson & Walk's visual cliff: an apparent drop-off under glass. Most crawling infants (~6 months+) refused to cross the 'deep' side, suggesting depth perception emerges by the time infants are mobile. Later heart-rate studies showed even pre-crawling infants discriminate depth, though fear of it develops with locomotor experience." },
  { id: "m5_dev09", section: "dev",
    q: "Bronfenbrenner's ecological systems theory places the family, school, and peer group in the:",
    options: { a: "Macrosystem",
               b: "Chronosystem",
               c: "Exosystem",
               d: "Microsystem" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Microsystem: the immediate settings the child directly interacts with (family, school, peers). Mesosystem: interactions between microsystems. Exosystem: settings affecting the child indirectly (parent's workplace). Macrosystem: overarching culture/ideology. Chronosystem: change over time. Bronfenbrenner stressed nested, interacting layers of influence." },
  { id: "m5_dev10", section: "dev",
    q: "The phenomenon by which an adolescent believes their experiences are unique and that others cannot understand them is Elkind's:",
    options: { a: "Personal fable",
               b: "Imaginary audience",
               c: "Conservation",
               d: "Object permanence" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Elkind's personal fable: the adolescent's belief that they are special, unique, and invulnerable ('it won't happen to me') — linked to risk-taking. The imaginary audience is the belief that others are constantly watching and judging them. Both stem from adolescent egocentrism accompanying the shift to formal operations." },
  { id: "m5_dev11", section: "dev",
    q: "Cross-sectional research designs in developmental psychology are limited primarily by:",
    options: { a: "Practice effects from repeated testing",
               b: "Cohort effects — age differences may reflect generational differences, not development",
               c: "High attrition over many years",
               d: "Being unable to study more than one age at a time" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Cross-sectional designs compare different age groups at one time — fast and cheap, but age differences can be confounded by cohort (generational) effects. Longitudinal designs follow one cohort over time (vulnerable to attrition and practice effects but isolate true change). Sequential designs combine both to disentangle age from cohort." },
  { id: "m5_dev12", section: "dev",
    q: "Stranger anxiety and separation anxiety in infants typically peak around:",
    options: { a: "8-15 months",
               b: "1-2 months",
               c: "3 years",
               d: "6 years" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Stranger anxiety emerges around 6-8 months and separation anxiety peaks around 10-18 months (roughly 8-15 months), coinciding with the formation of a specific attachment and the cognitive advance of object permanence (the infant can now represent the absent caregiver). Both normally wane by ~2 years." },
  { id: "m5_dev13", section: "dev",
    q: "Vygotsky argued that private speech (children talking aloud to themselves) functions to:",
    options: { a: "Guide and self-regulate behaviour, later becoming internalised as inner speech / verbal thought",
               b: "Reflect egocentrism that simply disappears with age",
               c: "Signal a language disorder",
               d: "Imitate adult conversation only" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "For Vygotsky, private (self-directed) speech is a self-regulatory tool that helps children plan and guide their own actions; with development it goes 'underground' to become silent inner speech and verbal thinking. This contrasts with Piaget, who saw it as merely egocentric speech that fades as the child decentres." },
  { id: "m5_dev14", section: "dev",
    q: "Which reflex, normally present in newborns and disappearing by ~4-6 months, involves the arms flinging out and then inward in response to a sudden loss of support?",
    options: { a: "Babinski reflex",
               b: "Moro (startle) reflex",
               c: "Rooting reflex",
               d: "Palmar grasp reflex" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "The Moro reflex: in response to a sudden movement or loss of head support, the infant extends the arms and legs, then draws them inward, often crying. Its presence and symmetry are checked neurologically; persistence beyond ~6 months suggests neurological concern. Babinski = toe fanning to sole stroke; rooting = head-turn to cheek touch." },

  // ===== ABNORMAL (18) =====
  { id: "m5_abn01", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Anhedonia, a core feature of major depression, is defined as:",
    options: { a: "Excessive guilt",
               b: "Markedly diminished interest or pleasure in nearly all activities",
               c: "Psychomotor agitation",
               d: "Difficulty concentrating" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Anhedonia = loss of interest or pleasure in activities once found enjoyable. With depressed mood, it is one of the two cardinal symptoms of a Major Depressive Episode (at least one must be present). It also features in schizophrenia (a negative symptom). Distinct from avolition (loss of drive)." },
  { id: "m5_abn02", section: "abnormal",
    q: "A delusion that one's thoughts are being broadcast so others can hear them is termed:",
    options: { a: "Thought withdrawal",
               b: "Thought broadcasting",
               c: "Thought insertion",
               d: "Ideas of reference" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Thought broadcasting: the belief that one's private thoughts are transmitted and audible to others. Thought insertion: thoughts are placed in one's mind by an external agent. Thought withdrawal: thoughts are removed. All are Schneiderian first-rank symptoms of passivity in schizophrenia. Ideas of reference = neutral events feel personally directed." },
  { id: "m5_abn03", section: "abnormal",
    q: "The defining feature of a panic attack is:",
    options: { a: "Persistent worry lasting most days for 6 months",
               b: "Avoidance of a specific feared object",
               c: "An abrupt surge of intense fear/discomfort peaking within minutes, with somatic symptoms",
               d: "Re-experiencing of a traumatic event" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "A panic attack is an abrupt surge of intense fear or discomfort that peaks within minutes, with ≥4 of 13 symptoms (palpitations, sweating, trembling, dyspnoea, chest pain, derealisation, fear of dying/losing control, etc.). Panic disorder requires recurrent unexpected attacks plus ≥1 month of worry/behaviour change. GAD is the 6-month worry condition." },
  { id: "m5_abn04", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Agoraphobia in DSM-5 is best described as marked fear/anxiety about:",
    options: { a: "Two or more situations (e.g. public transport, open/enclosed spaces, crowds, being outside home) where escape might be difficult",
               b: "Social scrutiny and embarrassment",
               c: "A single specific object such as spiders",
               d: "Contamination by germs" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "DSM-5 agoraphobia: marked fear/anxiety about ≥2 of five situation clusters — public transport, open spaces, enclosed spaces, crowds/queues, being outside the home alone — because escape or help may be unavailable if panic-like symptoms occur. It is now a standalone diagnosis, not tied to panic disorder. Symptoms ≥6 months." },
  { id: "m5_abn05", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Antisocial personality disorder requires evidence of:",
    options: { a: "Onset only after age 40",
               b: "Hallucinations and delusions",
               c: "Fear of abandonment and identity disturbance",
               d: "Conduct disorder before age 15 and a pattern of disregard for others' rights from age 18" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "ASPD (DSM-5, Cluster B): pervasive disregard for and violation of others' rights since age 15, the individual is ≥18, and there is evidence of Conduct Disorder with onset before 15. Features: deceit, impulsivity, aggression, irresponsibility, lack of remorse. Fear of abandonment/identity disturbance characterises BPD." },
  { id: "m5_abn06", section: "abnormal",
    q: "La belle indifférence — a relative lack of concern about a serious symptom — is classically associated with:",
    options: { a: "Generalised anxiety disorder",
               b: "Obsessive-compulsive disorder",
               c: "Conversion disorder (functional neurological symptom disorder)",
               d: "Bipolar mania" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "La belle indifférence describes the surprisingly calm, unconcerned attitude some patients show toward a dramatic conversion symptom (e.g. paralysis, blindness). Historically linked to conversion disorder, though it is neither sensitive nor specific and DSM-5 no longer requires it. The symptom is genuine and unconsciously produced." },
  { id: "m5_abn07", section: "abnormal",
    q: "The DSM-5 dissociative disorder featuring two or more distinct personality states with gaps in memory is:",
    options: { a: "Dissociative identity disorder",
               b: "Depersonalisation/derealisation disorder",
               c: "Dissociative amnesia",
               d: "Conversion disorder" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Dissociative identity disorder (formerly multiple personality disorder): ≥2 distinct personality states (identity disruption) plus recurrent gaps in recall of everyday events inconsistent with ordinary forgetting. Strongly associated with severe childhood trauma. Depersonalisation/derealisation = detachment from self/surroundings with intact reality testing." },
  { id: "m5_abn08", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Tactile hallucinations of insects crawling on/under the skin (formication) are most characteristic of:",
    options: { a: "Stimulant intoxication or alcohol/sedative withdrawal (delirium tremens)",
               b: "Major depressive disorder",
               c: "Specific phobia",
               d: "Generalised anxiety disorder" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Formication (the sensation of insects crawling on/under the skin) is a tactile hallucination classically seen in cocaine/amphetamine intoxication ('cocaine bugs') and in alcohol withdrawal delirium (delirium tremens). DTs also feature autonomic hyperactivity, tremor, and visual hallucinations 48-72 h after the last drink." },
  { id: "m5_abn09", section: "abnormal",
    q: "The diathesis-stress model of psychopathology proposes that disorders arise from:",
    options: { a: "An interaction between a predisposing vulnerability (diathesis) and environmental stressors",
               b: "Stress alone, regardless of constitution",
               c: "Genetics alone",
               d: "Unconscious conflicts only" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "The diathesis-stress model: a pre-existing vulnerability (genetic, biological, or psychological diathesis) interacts with environmental stress to produce a disorder. Neither alone is sufficient if below threshold. It underpins gene-environment interaction research (e.g. Caspi's 5-HTTLPR × life-stress findings in depression)." },
  { id: "m5_abn10", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Negative symptoms of schizophrenia include:",
    options: { a: "Hallucinations and delusions",
               b: "Disorganised speech",
               c: "Catatonic excitement",
               d: "Avolition, alogia, anhedonia, and affective flattening" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Negative symptoms reflect a loss/reduction of normal function: avolition (lack of drive), alogia (poverty of speech), anhedonia, asociality, and blunted/flattened affect. They respond poorly to typical antipsychotics and predict worse functional outcome. Positive symptoms (hallucinations, delusions, disorganisation) reflect an excess/distortion." },
  { id: "m5_abn11", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Cotard's syndrome is the delusional belief that:",
    options: { a: "One's partner is unfaithful",
               b: "A familiar person is an impostor",
               c: "One is dead, does not exist, or has lost organs/blood (nihilistic delusion)",
               d: "One has special powers" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Cotard's (délire de négation): a nihilistic delusion that one is dead, decaying, or non-existent, sometimes that organs/blood are gone. Seen in severe depression with psychotic features, schizophrenia, and some neurological conditions. Capgras = impostor delusion; Othello = delusional jealousy; grandiose = special powers." },
  { id: "m5_abn12", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Which is the most lethal eating disorder in terms of mortality rate?",
    options: { a: "Bulimia nervosa",
               b: "Binge eating disorder",
               c: "Anorexia nervosa",
               d: "Pica" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Anorexia nervosa has the highest mortality of any psychiatric disorder among eating disorders — from medical complications of starvation (cardiac, electrolyte) and from suicide. Severe low weight, restriction, intense fear of weight gain, and body-image disturbance define it. Bulimia and BED carry serious but lower mortality." },
  { id: "m5_abn13", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Word salad (incoherent, jumbled speech) is an example of:",
    options: { a: "A negative symptom",
               b: "A perceptual disturbance",
               c: "A mood symptom",
               d: "A formal thought disorder (disorganised speech)" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Word salad (schizophasia) is severe formal thought disorder: speech so disorganised it is incomprehensible. Other formal thought disorders: loosening of associations / derailment, tangentiality, clang associations, neologisms, thought blocking. These are disorganised (not negative) symptoms in schizophrenia." },
  { id: "m5_abn14", section: "abnormal",
    q: "The key DSM-5 distinction between bipolar I and bipolar II disorder is that:",
    options: { a: "Bipolar I requires at least one full manic episode; bipolar II requires hypomania plus a major depressive episode and no full mania",
               b: "Bipolar II is always more severe than bipolar I",
               c: "Bipolar I has no depressive episodes",
               d: "Bipolar II requires psychotic features" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Bipolar I: ≥1 manic episode (≥7 days or hospitalisation; may have psychosis); depressive episodes common but not required. Bipolar II: ≥1 hypomanic episode (≥4 days, no marked impairment/psychosis) plus ≥1 major depressive episode, and never a full manic episode. A single full mania moves the diagnosis to Bipolar I." },
  { id: "m5_abn15", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Echolalia (repeating others' words) and echopraxia (imitating others' movements) are most associated with:",
    options: { a: "Major depressive disorder",
               b: "Catatonia",
               c: "Panic disorder",
               d: "Specific phobia" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Echolalia and echopraxia are catatonic features. DSM-5 catatonia (≥3 of 12 signs) can occur in schizophrenia, mood disorders, and medical conditions; other signs include stupor, mutism, posturing, waxy flexibility, negativism, and stereotypy. Lorazepam and ECT are first-line treatments." },
  { id: "m5_abn16", section: "abnormal",
    q: "The 'double-bind' hypothesis (Bateson) was an early theory of the cause of:",
    options: { a: "Phobias",
               b: "Depression",
               c: "Schizophrenia, via contradictory communication within the family",
               d: "Autism" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Bateson et al. (1956) proposed that repeated exposure to contradictory messages (a 'double bind' from which the child cannot escape or comment on) contributed to schizophrenia. The family-causation theory (and the 'schizophrenogenic mother') is now discredited as causal, but the broader expressed-emotion research (relapse risk) endures." },
  { id: "m5_abn17", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Which feature best distinguishes obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD) from obsessive-compulsive personality disorder (OCPD)?",
    options: { a: "OCPD involves intrusive obsessions and ritual compulsions experienced as distressing/ego-dystonic; OCD does not",
               b: "They are the same disorder",
               c: "OCD involves ego-dystonic intrusive obsessions and compulsions; OCPD is an ego-syntonic pervasive pattern of perfectionism and control without true obsessions/compulsions",
               d: "OCPD always includes hand-washing rituals" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "OCD: ego-dystonic intrusive obsessions and anxiety-driven compulsions the person wants to be rid of. OCPD: an ego-syntonic, pervasive personality pattern of perfectionism, orderliness, rigidity, and control, generally without discrete obsessions/compulsions, and the traits feel right to the person. Different categories entirely." },
  { id: "m5_abn18", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Seasonal affective disorder (SAD), in DSM-5, is coded as:",
    options: { a: "A separate disorder unrelated to mood disorders",
               b: "A 'with seasonal pattern' specifier for recurrent major depressive (or bipolar) disorder",
               c: "An anxiety disorder",
               d: "A personality disorder" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "DSM-5 treats seasonal affective disorder as a 'with seasonal pattern' specifier applied to recurrent major depressive disorder or bipolar disorder, with episodes regularly tied to a season (commonly winter onset). Bright-light therapy is a first-line treatment, alongside antidepressants and CBT." },

  // ===== ASSESSMENT (15) =====
  { id: "m5_ass01", section: "assessment",
    q: "An IQ of 100 on a standard Wechsler scale corresponds to which percentile?",
    options: { a: "50th percentile (the mean)",
               b: "16th percentile",
               c: "84th percentile",
               d: "98th percentile" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Wechsler IQ is normed to mean 100, SD 15. An IQ of 100 sits exactly at the mean = 50th percentile. IQ 85 = −1 SD ≈ 16th percentile; IQ 115 = +1 SD ≈ 84th; IQ 130 = +2 SD ≈ 98th. These anchor points recur throughout standardised testing." },
  { id: "m5_ass02", section: "assessment",
    q: "A z-score of +1.0 indicates a raw score that is:",
    options: { a: "One standard deviation below the mean",
               b: "One standard deviation above the mean",
               c: "Equal to the mean",
               d: "At the 99th percentile" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "A z-score expresses a value in standard-deviation units from the mean: z = (X − M)/SD. z = +1.0 is one SD above the mean (~84th percentile in a normal distribution). z = 0 is the mean; z = −1.0 is one SD below (~16th percentile). z-scores allow comparison across differently scaled tests." },
  { id: "m5_ass03", section: "assessment",
    q: "Test-retest reliability is established by:",
    options: { a: "Correlating two halves of a single test",
               b: "Having two raters score the same protocol",
               c: "Correlating the test with a future criterion",
               d: "Correlating scores from the same test administered to the same people on two occasions" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Test-retest reliability = stability over time, measured by correlating two administrations of the same test to the same sample (Pearson r). Split-half assesses internal consistency (corrected by Spearman-Brown). Inter-rater uses Cohen's κ. Predicting a future criterion is predictive validity, not reliability." },
  { id: "m5_ass04", section: "assessment",
    q: "Face validity refers to:",
    options: { a: "Whether a test predicts future performance",
               b: "The internal consistency of items",
               c: "Whether a test appears, on the surface, to measure what it claims to — based on superficial inspection",
               d: "Agreement between two raters" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Face validity is the weakest, most superficial form: whether the test looks like it measures the intended construct to test-takers or laypeople. It affects motivation and acceptability but is not evidence of true validity. Content validity (expert judgement of domain coverage) is more rigorous." },
  { id: "m5_ass05", section: "assessment",
    q: "The Wisconsin Card Sorting Test (WCST) is primarily a measure of:",
    options: { a: "Verbal memory",
               b: "Visual acuity",
               c: "Reading comprehension",
               d: "Executive function / set-shifting and frontal-lobe integrity" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "The WCST assesses executive functions — abstract reasoning, cognitive flexibility, and the ability to shift set as sorting rules change without warning. Perseverative errors (continuing an old rule) are sensitive to dorsolateral prefrontal/frontal-lobe dysfunction. Widely used in neuropsychological and schizophrenia research." },
  { id: "m5_ass06", section: "assessment",
    q: "On the MMPI-2, the K scale is best described as a measure of:",
    options: { a: "Random responding",
               b: "Over-reporting of bizarre symptoms",
               c: "Defensiveness / a subtle tendency to present oneself favourably (a correction scale)",
               d: "Depression severity" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "The K (Correction) scale detects a more subtle, sophisticated defensiveness than the L scale, and a K-correction is added to several clinical scales. L = naive 'faking good'; F = infrequency / over-reporting / faking bad; VRIN/TRIN = response inconsistency (random or fixed responding). Clinical scales (1-10) come after the validity scales." },
  { id: "m5_ass07", section: "assessment",
    q: "A test that consistently yields the same score but does not actually measure the intended construct is:",
    options: { a: "Valid but not reliable",
               b: "Both reliable and valid",
               c: "Reliable but not valid",
               d: "Neither reliable nor valid" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Reliability = consistency; validity = accuracy of what is measured. A bathroom scale stuck at 70 kg is perfectly reliable (consistent) but invalid (wrong). A test can be reliable without being valid, but it cannot be valid without being reliable — reliability is necessary but not sufficient for validity." },
  { id: "m5_ass08", section: "assessment",
    q: "The Stanford-Binet was originally developed from the work of which pioneers?",
    options: { a: "Wechsler and Terman",
               b: "Spearman and Thurstone",
               c: "Galton and Cattell",
               d: "Binet and Simon (later revised by Terman at Stanford)" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Alfred Binet and Théodore Simon created the first practical intelligence scale (1905) to identify French schoolchildren needing support, introducing 'mental age.' Lewis Terman at Stanford revised it (1916) as the Stanford-Binet and popularised the IQ ratio (MA/CA × 100, after Stern). Wechsler later introduced deviation IQ." },
  { id: "m5_ass09", section: "assessment",
    q: "Spearman's two-factor theory of intelligence proposed:",
    options: { a: "A general factor 'g' plus specific factors 's'",
               b: "Seven primary mental abilities",
               c: "Eight independent intelligences",
               d: "Fluid and crystallised intelligence as the only factors" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Spearman (1904), using factor analysis, proposed a general intelligence factor 'g' underlying all cognitive tasks plus task-specific factors 's'. Thurstone countered with seven primary mental abilities; Gardner proposed multiple intelligences; Cattell-Horn distinguished fluid (Gf) and crystallised (Gc) intelligence." },
  { id: "m5_ass10", section: "assessment",
    q: "Norm-referenced interpretation compares a test-taker's score to:",
    options: { a: "The performance of a representative standardisation sample (norm group)",
               b: "A fixed mastery cut-off",
               c: "Their own previous scores only",
               d: "An absolute criterion of competence" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Norm-referenced tests rank an individual relative to a representative norm group (e.g. percentiles, standard scores) — most IQ and aptitude tests. Criterion-referenced tests compare performance to a fixed standard of mastery regardless of others (e.g. a driving test, board exams). The reference point is the key distinction." },
  { id: "m5_ass11", section: "assessment",
    q: "The Children's Apperception Test (CAT) differs from the TAT mainly in that it:",
    options: { a: "Is an objective, self-report inventory",
               b: "Measures only intelligence",
               c: "Uses animal (and human) figures in cards designed for children",
               d: "Uses inkblots instead of pictures" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "The CAT (Bellak, 1949), a projective storytelling test for children (~3-10 y), originally used cards depicting animals in human-like situations (later a human-figure version, CAT-H), on the assumption that children identify more readily with animals. Like the TAT, it elicits themes, needs, and conflicts — not IQ." },
  { id: "m5_ass12", section: "assessment",
    q: "Positive predictive value (PPV) of a diagnostic test is most affected by:",
    options: { a: "Only the test's sensitivity",
               b: "Only the test's specificity",
               c: "The prevalence (base rate) of the condition in the tested population",
               d: "The number of items on the test" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "PPV = TP/(TP+FP) — the probability that a positive result reflects true disease. Unlike sensitivity and specificity (test-intrinsic), PPV depends heavily on prevalence: in low-prevalence settings even a good test yields many false positives, lowering PPV. This is the base-rate problem in screening." },
  { id: "m5_ass13", section: "assessment",
    q: "The Mini-Mental State Examination (MMSE) is primarily used to:",
    options: { a: "Diagnose specific personality disorders",
               b: "Measure depression severity",
               c: "Assess intelligence quotient",
               d: "Screen for and track global cognitive impairment / dementia (out of 30)" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "The MMSE (Folstein, 1975) is a brief 30-point bedside screen of orientation, registration, attention/calculation, recall, language, and visuoconstruction. Scores <24 suggest cognitive impairment (adjusted for education). It screens and tracks dementia/delirium severity but is not diagnostic on its own. The MoCA is a more sensitive alternative." },
  { id: "m5_ass14", section: "assessment",
    q: "A 'ceiling effect' in a psychological test occurs when:",
    options: { a: "The test is too difficult for everyone",
               b: "Scores are normally distributed",
               c: "The test has perfect reliability",
               d: "Most respondents score near the maximum, so the test cannot discriminate among high performers" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "A ceiling effect: a test is too easy, so many scores cluster at or near the maximum, compressing the upper range and failing to distinguish high-ability respondents. The opposite is a floor effect (test too hard, scores bunch at the bottom). Both reduce variance, sensitivity, and discriminating power." },
  { id: "m5_ass15", section: "assessment",
    q: "The Rorschach Inkblot Test consists of how many cards, and who developed it?",
    options: { a: "20 cards, developed by Henry Murray",
               b: "15 cards, developed by John Exner",
               c: "5 cards, developed by Carl Jung",
               d: "10 cards, developed by Hermann Rorschach" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Hermann Rorschach (1921, Psychodiagnostik) created the test of 10 standardised inkblots (5 achromatic, 2 black-and-red, 3 multicoloured). Responses are coded for location, determinants, content, and form quality. Murray developed the TAT; Exner created the Comprehensive System for scoring the Rorschach decades later." },

  // ===== THERAPY (14) =====
  { id: "m5_the01", section: "therapy",
    q: "In classical psychoanalysis, transference refers to:",
    options: { a: "The therapist's emotional reaction to the client",
               b: "A homework assignment between sessions",
               c: "The client redirecting feelings about significant figures from their past onto the therapist",
               d: "Direct interpretation of dreams" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Transference: the client unconsciously projects feelings, expectations, and conflicts originally tied to important early figures (e.g. parents) onto the therapist. Analysing it is central to psychoanalytic work. The therapist's own reactions to the client are countertransference. Freud saw transference as both an obstacle and a key therapeutic tool." },
  { id: "m5_the02", section: "therapy",
    q: "Systematic desensitisation requires the client to:",
    options: { a: "Confront the most feared stimulus immediately at full intensity",
               b: "Receive a mild electric shock with the feared stimulus",
               c: "Free-associate about the fear's childhood origin",
               d: "Pair deep relaxation with a graded fear hierarchy, progressing only when calm" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Systematic desensitisation (Wolpe): (1) relaxation training, (2) construction of a fear hierarchy from least to most anxiety-provoking, (3) graded exposure (imaginal or in-vivo) while remaining relaxed, advancing only when each step is mastered. Based on reciprocal inhibition. Flooding, by contrast, uses immediate full-intensity exposure." },
  { id: "m5_the03", section: "therapy",
    q: "Aaron Beck and Albert Ellis are both founders of which broad therapeutic tradition?",
    options: { a: "Psychodynamic therapy",
               b: "Cognitive (cognitive-behavioural) therapy",
               c: "Humanistic therapy",
               d: "Family systems therapy" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Beck developed Cognitive Therapy (targeting automatic thoughts, cognitive distortions, and the cognitive triad); Ellis developed Rational Emotive Behaviour Therapy (REBT) with the ABC model (Activating event → Beliefs → Consequences) and disputing irrational beliefs. Both are pillars of the cognitive/CBT tradition." },
  { id: "m5_the04", section: "therapy",
    q: "In Ellis's REBT, the 'ABC' model holds that emotional consequences (C) are caused primarily by:",
    options: { a: "The activating event (A) directly",
               b: "The person's beliefs (B) about the activating event",
               c: "Biological factors alone",
               d: "The therapist's interpretation" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "REBT's core insight: it is not the Activating event (A) itself but one's Beliefs (B) about it that produce the emotional/behavioural Consequences (C). Irrational beliefs (demands, 'musts', catastrophising) are Disputed (D) to reach a new Effect (E). This locates emotional disturbance in cognition, not events." },
  { id: "m5_the05", section: "therapy",
    q: "Token economies are an application of which principle?",
    options: { a: "Classical conditioning",
               b: "Observational learning",
               c: "Free association",
               d: "Operant conditioning (secondary reinforcement)" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "A token economy uses operant conditioning: desired behaviours earn tokens (conditioned/secondary reinforcers) later exchanged for backup reinforcers. Used in inpatient, educational, and developmental-disability settings. Based on Skinnerian reinforcement; Ayllon & Azrin pioneered its clinical use. Classical conditioning involves stimulus pairing, not reinforcement of voluntary behaviour." },
  { id: "m5_the06", section: "therapy",
    q: "Carl Rogers termed the therapeutic stance of accepting the client without judgement or conditions:",
    options: { a: "Congruence",
               b: "Empathy",
               c: "Transference",
               d: "Unconditional positive regard" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Unconditional positive regard (UPR): non-judgemental acceptance and prizing of the client regardless of their behaviour, one of Rogers' three core conditions alongside empathy (accurate understanding of the client's frame of reference) and congruence (the therapist's genuineness). UPR helps dissolve 'conditions of worth.'" },
  { id: "m5_the07", section: "therapy",
    q: "Flooding as an exposure technique involves:",
    options: { a: "Gradual exposure up a hierarchy with relaxation",
               b: "Prolonged exposure to the most anxiety-provoking stimulus at full intensity until anxiety subsides",
               c: "Rewarding avoidance behaviour",
               d: "Cognitive restructuring of beliefs" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Flooding: immediate, prolonged, full-intensity exposure (real or imaginal) to the feared stimulus, with response prevention, continuing until anxiety habituates and extinguishes (no escape). It contrasts with the graded, relaxation-paired approach of systematic desensitisation. Effective but more distressing initially." },
  { id: "m5_the08", section: "therapy",
    q: "Mindfulness-Based Cognitive Therapy (MBCT) was developed primarily to:",
    options: { a: "Treat acute psychosis",
               b: "Prevent relapse in recurrent major depression",
               c: "Replace all pharmacotherapy for bipolar disorder",
               d: "Treat specific phobias by exposure" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "MBCT (Segal, Williams & Teasdale) blends mindfulness meditation with cognitive therapy, designed specifically to prevent relapse in people with recurrent depression by changing the relationship to negative thoughts (decentring) rather than their content. Evidence supports reduced relapse, especially with ≥3 prior episodes." },
  { id: "m5_the09", section: "therapy",
    q: "Aversion therapy works by:",
    options: { a: "Rewarding the undesirable behaviour intermittently",
               b: "Ignoring the behaviour until it extinguishes",
               c: "Cognitively restructuring the belief behind it",
               d: "Pairing an undesirable behaviour with an unpleasant stimulus to reduce its appeal (classical conditioning)" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Aversion therapy uses classical conditioning: an unwanted behaviour or stimulus (e.g. alcohol) is repeatedly paired with an aversive stimulus (e.g. nausea-inducing disulfiram, mild shock) so the target acquires a negative association. Ethically contentious and of limited durability; largely superseded by other approaches." },
  { id: "m5_the10", section: "therapy",
    q: "Glasser's Reality Therapy emphasises:",
    options: { a: "Uncovering repressed childhood memories",
               b: "Interpreting transference",
               c: "Present behaviour, personal responsibility, and meeting needs through effective choices (Choice Theory)",
               d: "Eye movements to process trauma" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "William Glasser's Reality Therapy (rooted in Choice Theory) focuses on the present, personal responsibility, and whether current behaviour is effectively meeting one's needs — not on the past, diagnosis, or unconscious conflict. The therapist helps the client evaluate choices and make a plan. Future- and action-oriented." },
  { id: "m5_the11", section: "therapy",
    q: "In behaviour therapy, 'shaping' refers to:",
    options: { a: "Removing a reinforcer to weaken behaviour",
               b: "Pairing two neutral stimuli",
               c: "Reinforcing successive approximations toward a target behaviour",
               d: "Punishing every incorrect response" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Shaping (successive approximation): reinforcing closer and closer approximations of a desired behaviour that is not yet in the repertoire, gradually moving the criterion toward the final target. A Skinnerian operant technique widely used in skills training, animal training, and behaviour modification for developmental disabilities." },
  { id: "m5_the12", section: "therapy",
    q: "The 'miracle question' is a signature technique of:",
    options: { a: "Psychoanalysis",
               b: "Gestalt therapy",
               c: "Solution-Focused Brief Therapy (de Shazer & Berg)",
               d: "Classical CBT" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "The miracle question ('Suppose a miracle happened overnight and your problem was solved — what would be different, and how would you notice?') is central to Solution-Focused Brief Therapy. It shifts focus from problems to a concrete preferred future and to exceptions and existing strengths, consistent with SFBT's goal-directed, brief stance." },
  { id: "m5_the13", section: "therapy",
    q: "Couple and family therapists who view the family as a system with homeostasis, boundaries, and feedback loops draw on:",
    options: { a: "Operant conditioning",
               b: "Psychosexual stage theory",
               c: "The medical model",
               d: "General Systems Theory (von Bertalanffy) applied to families" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Family systems approaches (Bowen, Minuchin, the Milan group, Satir) apply General Systems Theory: the family is an interacting whole that seeks homeostasis through feedback loops, with subsystems and boundaries. The identified patient's symptom is understood as serving a function within the system, not as residing solely in the individual." },
  { id: "m5_the14", section: "therapy",
    q: "Biofeedback is a treatment technique in which clients:",
    options: { a: "Receive interpretations of their dreams",
               b: "Are exposed to feared stimuli at full intensity",
               c: "Learn to control physiological processes using real-time monitoring feedback",
               d: "Take medication to control symptoms" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Biofeedback provides real-time information about physiological functions (muscle tension/EMG, heart rate, skin conductance, EEG) so clients learn voluntary control, often combined with relaxation. Used for tension/migraine headache, hypertension, anxiety, and incontinence. It operationalises the idea that monitored functions can be self-regulated." },

  // ===== BIOSTATS / RESEARCH (11) =====
  { id: "m5_bst01", section: "biostats",
    q: "Which measure of central tendency is most appropriate for nominal (categorical) data?",
    options: { a: "Mean",
               b: "Mode",
               c: "Median",
               d: "Standard deviation" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "The mode (most frequent category) is the only valid measure of central tendency for nominal data. The median requires at least ordinal data; the mean requires interval/ratio data. Standard deviation is a measure of dispersion, not central tendency, and also needs interval/ratio data." },
  { id: "m5_bst02", section: "biostats",
    q: "In a positively (right) skewed distribution, the typical ordering of the measures is:",
    options: { a: "Mean < median < mode",
               b: "Mode < median < mean",
               c: "Mean = median = mode",
               d: "Median < mode < mean" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "In a positive (right) skew, a tail of high values pulls the mean upward most, so mode < median < mean. In negative (left) skew it reverses: mean < median < mode. In a symmetric (normal) distribution all three coincide. The mean is most affected by extreme values/outliers." },
  { id: "m5_bst03", section: "biostats",
    q: "The dependent variable in an experiment is the one that:",
    options: { a: "Is measured as the outcome / is expected to change in response to the manipulation",
               b: "Is manipulated by the experimenter",
               c: "Is held constant to prevent confounding",
               d: "Is randomly assigned" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "The independent variable (IV) is manipulated by the experimenter; the dependent variable (DV) is the measured outcome expected to depend on the IV. Extraneous variables are controlled/held constant to avoid confounding. Mnemonic: the DV 'depends on' what the experimenter does to the IV." },
  { id: "m5_bst04", section: "biostats",
    q: "A correlation coefficient (r) of −0.85 indicates:",
    options: { a: "A strong negative (inverse) relationship",
               b: "A weak positive relationship",
               c: "No relationship",
               d: "A causal relationship" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "r ranges from −1 to +1. The sign gives direction (negative = as one variable rises the other falls), the magnitude gives strength. −0.85 is a strong inverse relationship. Correlation never establishes causation, regardless of strength (third variables, reverse causation). r² (=0.72 here) gives the proportion of shared variance." },
  { id: "m5_bst05", section: "biostats",
    q: "The chi-square (χ²) test of independence is used to examine:",
    options: { a: "The difference between two means",
               b: "The correlation between two continuous variables",
               c: "The variance within a single group",
               d: "The association between two categorical variables" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "The chi-square test of independence assesses whether two categorical (frequency) variables are associated by comparing observed vs expected cell counts in a contingency table. Comparing two means uses a t-test; three+ means use ANOVA; two continuous variables use correlation/regression." },
  { id: "m5_bst06", section: "biostats",
    q: "The central limit theorem states that, as sample size increases, the sampling distribution of the mean:",
    options: { a: "Becomes increasingly skewed",
               b: "Approaches a normal distribution regardless of the population's shape",
               c: "Always equals the population distribution exactly",
               d: "Has a larger standard error" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "The central limit theorem: regardless of the population distribution's shape, the sampling distribution of the sample mean approaches normality as n grows (commonly n ≥ 30), with mean μ and standard error σ/√n. This underpins parametric inference and why many statistics assume normality of the sampling distribution, not the raw data." },
  { id: "m5_bst07", section: "biostats",
    q: "A p-value of 0.03 in a study using α = 0.05 means:",
    options: { a: "There is a 3% probability the null hypothesis is true",
               b: "The effect size is large",
               c: "The result is statistically significant; the null hypothesis is rejected",
               d: "The study should be repeated because it failed" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "p = 0.03 < α = 0.05, so the result is statistically significant and the null hypothesis is rejected. The p-value is the probability of obtaining data this extreme IF the null were true — not the probability that the null is true, and not a measure of effect size. Significance ≠ practical importance." },
  { id: "m5_bst08", section: "biostats",
    q: "Double-blind design in a clinical trial means that:",
    options: { a: "Two separate control groups are used",
               b: "Neither the participants nor the researchers administering/assessing know who is in which group",
               c: "The study is run twice",
               d: "Only the participants are unaware of their group" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "In a double-blind trial, both the participants and the researchers who deliver the intervention or assess outcomes are unaware of group allocation, controlling both placebo effects and experimenter/observer bias. Single-blind keeps only participants unaware. Allocation is typically via randomisation, with a third party holding the code." },
  { id: "m5_bst09", section: "biostats",
    q: "A paired-samples (dependent) t-test is appropriate when:",
    options: { a: "Comparing the means of two independent groups",
               b: "Comparing means from the same subjects measured twice (e.g. pre- vs post-treatment)",
               c: "Comparing three or more group means",
               d: "Testing the association of two categorical variables" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "A paired (dependent-samples) t-test compares two related measurements — the same participants measured twice (pre/post) or matched pairs — analysing the within-subject differences. Two independent groups use the independent-samples t-test; 3+ groups use ANOVA. Pairing increases power by removing between-subject variability." },
  { id: "m5_bst10", section: "biostats",
    q: "External validity of a study refers to:",
    options: { a: "Whether the study truly establishes that the IV caused the DV",
               b: "The internal consistency of the measures",
               c: "The reliability of the instruments used",
               d: "The extent to which findings generalise to other people, settings, and times" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "External validity = generalisability of results beyond the specific sample, setting, and time. Internal validity = the degree to which the design supports a causal claim (IV → DV, ruling out confounds). The two often trade off: tightly controlled lab studies maximise internal validity but can limit external validity." },
  { id: "m5_bst11", section: "biostats",
    q: "An interval scale of measurement differs from a ratio scale in that an interval scale:",
    options: { a: "Lacks a true (absolute) zero point, so ratios are not meaningful (e.g. Celsius temperature)",
               b: "Has no equal intervals between values",
               c: "Cannot be ordered",
               d: "Is the same as a nominal scale" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Stevens's scales: nominal (categories), ordinal (rank order), interval (equal intervals but arbitrary zero — e.g. Celsius, IQ; 40° is not 'twice as hot' as 20°), ratio (equal intervals plus a true zero — e.g. weight, reaction time, Kelvin; ratios are meaningful). A true zero is what distinguishes ratio from interval." },

  // ===== LANGUAGE SKILLS (10) =====
  { id: "m5_lan01", section: "language",
    q: "Choose the word that is the antonym of BENEVOLENT.",
    options: { a: "Generous",
               b: "Malevolent",
               c: "Charitable",
               d: "Kind" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Benevolent = well-meaning, kindly. Its antonym is malevolent = wishing harm to others. Generous, charitable, and kind are synonyms of benevolent. The 'bene-' (good) vs 'male-' (bad/ill) Latin roots signal the contrast — compare benefactor vs malefactor." },
  { id: "m5_lan02", section: "language",
    q: "Identify the grammatically correct sentence.",
    options: { a: "Each of the students have submitted their assignment.",
               b: "Each of the students have submitted his assignment.",
               c: "Each of the students has submitted his or her assignment.",
               d: "Each of the student has submitted their assignment." },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "'Each' is singular and takes a singular verb ('has'), and formally a singular pronoun ('his or her'). 'Each of the students has submitted his or her assignment' is correct. Options with 'have' break subject-verb agreement; 'each of the student' is wrong (should be plural 'students' after 'of the')." },
  { id: "m5_lan03", section: "language",
    q: "Choose the correct spelling.",
    options: { a: "Occurence",
               b: "Occurrence",
               c: "Ocurrence",
               d: "Occurrance" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "The correct spelling is 'occurrence' — double 'c', double 'r', and '-ence' (not '-ance'). The doubling of the 'r' follows the rule for stressed final syllables when adding a suffix (occur → occurred, occurring, occurrence)." },
  { id: "m5_lan04", section: "language",
    q: "Which sentence correctly uses the apostrophe?",
    options: { a: "Its been a long day, and the dog wagged it's tail.",
               b: "Its been a long day, and the dog wagged its tail.",
               c: "It's been a long day, and the dog wagged its tail.",
               d: "It's been a long day, and the dog wagged it's tail." },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "'It's' = contraction of 'it is/has'; 'its' = possessive pronoun (no apostrophe). 'It's been a long day' (it has) and 'the dog wagged its tail' (possessive) are both correct. This is among the most common apostrophe errors in English." },
  { id: "m5_lan05", section: "language",
    q: "Choose the word closest in meaning to METICULOUS.",
    options: { a: "Careless",
               b: "Extremely careful and precise about detail",
               c: "Hasty",
               d: "Generous" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Meticulous = showing great attention to detail; very careful and precise. Synonyms: scrupulous, painstaking, thorough. Antonyms: careless, slapdash, negligent, hasty. Often used approvingly of work or record-keeping." },
  { id: "m5_lan06", section: "language",
    q: "Identify the sentence with correct subject-verb agreement.",
    options: { a: "The number of applicants were higher this year.",
               b: "A number of applicants was rejected.",
               c: "The list of names are on the desk.",
               d: "The number of applicants was higher this year." },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "'The number of...' takes a singular verb ('was') because the subject is 'the number'. 'A number of...' takes a plural verb (it means 'several'). 'The list of names IS on the desk' (subject = list, singular; 'of names' is a prepositional phrase). Option C is correct." },
  { id: "m5_lan07", section: "language",
    q: "Choose the correctly formed comparison.",
    options: { a: "Of the three plans, this is the cheapest.",
               b: "He is more taller than his brother.",
               c: "This is the worst of the two options.",
               d: "She is the most fastest runner in the team." },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "With three or more items, use the superlative: 'Of the three plans, this is the cheapest.' Errors in the others: 'more taller' is a double comparative (use 'taller'); 'the worst of the two' wrongly uses a superlative for two items (should be 'the worse of the two'); 'most fastest' is a double superlative (use 'fastest'). Only D is correct." },
  { id: "m5_lan08", section: "language",
    q: "Read the passage and answer: 'While the new policy promised efficiency, its rushed rollout left frontline staff under-trained and systems poorly integrated, so the early months saw more delays, not fewer.' The passage primarily conveys that the policy:",
    options: { a: "Succeeded beyond expectations",
               b: "Was abandoned before launch",
               c: "Was opposed by the public",
               d: "Had good intentions but a flawed implementation that worsened outcomes initially" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "The structure ('While it promised X, its rushed rollout left... so the early months saw more delays, not fewer') contrasts good intent against poor execution and a negative early result. Option C captures this. A contradicts the text; B and D introduce claims the passage never makes." },
  { id: "m5_lan09", section: "language",
    q: "Choose the option that correctly completes the sentence: 'If she ___ harder, she would have passed.'",
    options: { a: "studies",
               b: "would study",
               c: "had studied",
               d: "studied" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "This is a third conditional (past unreal): If + past perfect ('had studied'), main clause 'would have' + past participle ('would have passed'). 'If she had studied harder, she would have passed.' Avoid 'would' in the if-clause. The structure expresses a hypothetical past that did not happen." },
  { id: "m5_lan10", section: "language",
    q: "Which sentence is free of a dangling or misplaced modifier?",
    options: { a: "Walking down the street, the trees were beautiful.",
               b: "Having finished the report, the printer jammed.",
               c: "Having finished the report, she sent it to her manager.",
               d: "To improve quickly, practice is essential, the coach said." },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "In C, the introductory participial phrase 'Having finished the report' correctly modifies 'she' (the one who finished it). In A the trees aren't walking; in B the printer didn't finish the report; in D 'to improve quickly' illogically attaches to 'practice'. Only C pairs the modifier with the correct subject." },
],
  "bio-testing-paper": [
  // ===== BIOPSYCHOLOGY (30) =====
  { id: "bt_bio01", section: "biopsych",
    q: "The 12 cranial nerves in order from I to XII begin with which sensory nerve, the only one that does NOT pass through the thalamus before reaching its primary cortex?",
    options: { a: "Olfactory (I)",
               b: "Trigeminal (V)",
               c: "Optic (II)",
               d: "Vestibulocochlear (VIII)" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Olfactory (I): the only sensory pathway that bypasses the thalamus, projecting directly from olfactory bulb → piriform cortex (and amygdala, entorhinal). Cranial nerve mnemonic 'On Old Olympus Towering Tops, A Finn And German Viewed Some Hops': Olfactory, Optic, Oculomotor, Trochlear, Trigeminal, Abducens, Facial, Auditory (vestibulocochlear), Glossopharyngeal, Vagus, Spinal accessory, Hypoglossal." },
  { id: "bt_bio02", section: "biopsych",
    q: "The pyramidal (corticospinal) tract carries voluntary motor commands. Where does it decussate?",
    options: { a: "At the level of the third ventricle",
               b: "It does not decussate",
               c: "At the mid-pons",
               d: "At the pyramidal decussation in the lower medulla (~80-90% of fibres cross)" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Lateral corticospinal tract: ~85-90% of fibres decussate at the pyramidal decussation in the lower medulla → descend contralaterally → synapse on LMNs in the anterior horn. ~10-15% remain ipsilateral as the anterior corticospinal tract, decussating only at the spinal segmental level. Trauma above medulla → contralateral hemiplegia." },
  { id: "bt_bio03", section: "biopsych",
    q: "The direct pathway through the basal ganglia is most accurately described as:",
    options: { a: "Cortex → striatum (D2) → external GP → STN → GPi → thalamus; net inhibitory",
               b: "Cortex → striatum (D1 receptors) → internal globus pallidus / SNr (inhibitory) → thalamus (disinhibited) → cortex; net effect facilitates movement",
               c: "Hippocampus → fornix → thalamus → cortex",
               d: "Cortex → cerebellum → spinal cord, with no thalamic relay" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Direct pathway (D1 receptors, facilitates movement): cortex → striatum → GPi/SNr inhibition → thalamus disinhibition → cortex activation. Indirect pathway (D2, inhibits movement): cortex → striatum → GPe → STN → GPi → thalamus suppression. Dopamine from SNc excites direct (D1) + inhibits indirect (D2). Loss → Parkinson's; excess → chorea/Huntington's." },
  { id: "bt_bio04", section: "biopsych",
    q: "MAO-A and MAO-B enzymes principally metabolise:",
    options: { a: "Monoamine neurotransmitters: MAO-A → serotonin, norepinephrine, dopamine; MAO-B → dopamine, phenylethylamine",
               b: "GABA only",
               c: "Glutamate and glycine",
               d: "Acetylcholine" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "MAO-A degrades serotonin, NE, dopamine, tyramine (mostly in gut + liver). MAO-B selectively breaks down dopamine and phenylethylamine (predominant in brain). MAOIs increase monoamines (antidepressant). MAO-B inhibitors (selegiline, rasagiline) used in Parkinson's. Tyramine + non-selective MAOI = hypertensive crisis." },
  { id: "bt_bio05", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Astrocytes perform all of the following functions EXCEPT:",
    options: { a: "Recycling glutamate via the glutamate-glutamine cycle",
               b: "Maintenance of the blood–brain barrier via end-feet on capillaries",
               c: "Conducting action potentials along the axon",
               d: "K+ buffering and provision of metabolic substrates to neurons" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Astrocytes do NOT conduct action potentials (no voltage-gated Na+ in propagating form). Their roles: BBB maintenance, K+ buffering, glutamate uptake/recycling (via glutamine), neurotrophic support, scar formation after injury, regulation of synapse formation. Conducting APs is the neuron's job; oligodendrocytes/Schwann cells provide myelin." },
  { id: "bt_bio06", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Microglia function chiefly as:",
    options: { a: "The myelin-producing cells of the CNS",
               b: "K+ buffers around neurons",
               c: "Resident macrophages of the CNS — immune surveillance, phagocytosis, and synaptic pruning",
               d: "Voltage sensors in the cortex" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Microglia: CNS-resident immune cells, derived from yolk-sac progenitors. Functions: phagocytosis of debris and pathogens, cytokine release, synaptic pruning (esp. during development), neuroinflammation in disease. Activated microglia are implicated in Alzheimer's, MS, and chronic pain. Oligodendrocytes myelinate in CNS; Schwann cells in PNS." },
  { id: "bt_bio07", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Mirror neurons, first described in macaque ventral premotor cortex (area F5), fire when:",
    options: { a: "Both when the animal performs an action and when it observes a conspecific performing the same action",
               b: "Only when the animal performs an action itself",
               c: "Only when the animal observes another individual performing an action",
               d: "Only during REM sleep" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Mirror neurons (Rizzolatti, Gallese, di Pellegrino, late 1990s) fire both during execution and observation of the same goal-directed action. Proposed substrate for action understanding, imitation, and empathy. Putative mirror system in humans: IFG (BA 44/45), inferior parietal lobule, STS. Caveats: cross-species generalisation contested; not a single 'theory of everything social'." },
  { id: "bt_bio08", section: "biopsych",
    q: "The locus coeruleus is the principal source of which neurotransmitter in the CNS?",
    options: { a: "Norepinephrine",
               b: "Serotonin (5-HT)",
               c: "Acetylcholine",
               d: "Dopamine" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Locus coeruleus (pons, near 4th ventricle) = primary source of CNS norepinephrine; widespread projections to cortex, hippocampus, cerebellum. Role: arousal, attention, stress response. Other principal sources: VTA/SNc → dopamine; raphe nuclei → serotonin; nucleus basalis of Meynert + medial septum → ACh." },
  { id: "bt_bio09", section: "biopsych",
    q: "The raphe nuclei are arranged along which axis and are the major source of:",
    options: { a: "Within the cerebellum; ACh",
               b: "Along the spinal cord; dopamine",
               c: "Along the brainstem midline; serotonin (5-HT) projections",
               d: "Within the hypothalamus; oxytocin" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Raphe nuclei: clusters of serotonergic neurons along the midline of the brainstem (rostral group → forebrain; caudal group → spinal cord). Modulate mood, sleep, appetite, pain. Targeted indirectly by SSRIs and SNRIs at downstream synapses. Dorsal raphe is the largest of the raphe nuclei." },
  { id: "bt_bio10", section: "biopsych",
    q: "The hypothalamus connects to the anterior pituitary primarily via:",
    options: { a: "The CSF",
               b: "Direct neural axons (like the posterior pituitary)",
               c: "The hypothalamo-hypophyseal portal venous system (releasing/inhibiting hormones travel via blood)",
               d: "The vagus nerve" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Anterior pituitary (adenohypophysis): controlled by hormones secreted into the hypothalamo-hypophyseal portal vessels from the median eminence — CRH, TRH, GnRH, GHRH, somatostatin, dopamine (PIF). Posterior pituitary (neurohypophysis): direct axonal projection from PVN/SON releasing oxytocin and ADH into circulation." },
  { id: "bt_bio11", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Pre-ganglionic sympathetic neurons originate from spinal cord segments:",
    options: { a: "All cervical levels only",
               b: "T1–L2 (thoracolumbar)",
               c: "Cranial nerves only",
               d: "S2–S4 (sacral)" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Sympathetic = thoracolumbar outflow (T1-L2), pre-ganglionic fibres relatively short, post-ganglionic long, NT: ACh (pre) → NE (post). Parasympathetic = craniosacral (CN III, VII, IX, X + S2-S4), pre-ganglionic long, post-ganglionic short, NT: ACh at both synapses. Adrenal medulla is a sympathetic ganglion that releases epinephrine into blood." },
  { id: "bt_bio12", section: "biopsych",
    q: "The somatosensory homunculus on the postcentral gyrus shows that the largest cortical representation belongs to:",
    options: { a: "Trunk and back",
               b: "Knee and hip",
               c: "Foot dorsum only",
               d: "Hand, lips, and tongue" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Penfield's somatosensory + motor homunculi (postcentral and precentral gyri) are disproportionate: hand, face/lips, tongue occupy huge cortical territory reflecting fine discrimination and dexterity. Trunk and proximal limbs occupy small areas. Cortical magnification reflects receptor density, not skin area." },
  { id: "bt_bio13", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Parvocellular vs magnocellular layers of the LGN differ in that:",
    options: { a: "Magnocellular = colour and fine detail; parvocellular = motion",
               b: "Magnocellular layers project only to V4",
               c: "Both layers carry identical information",
               d: "Parvocellular (layers 3-6) = colour, fine detail, slow conduction; magnocellular (layers 1-2) = motion, contrast, fast conduction, achromatic" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "LGN has 6 layers. Magnocellular (M, layers 1-2): large cells, fast, achromatic, motion + contrast. Parvocellular (P, layers 3-6): small cells, slower, chromatic (red-green), fine detail. Koniocellular layers between: blue-yellow. Magno → dorsal stream (where/how); parvo → ventral stream (what)." },
  { id: "bt_bio14", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Cochlear hair cells transduce sound waves into neural signals primarily via:",
    options: { a: "Closure of voltage-gated Na+ channels",
               b: "Depolarisation by GABA release",
               c: "Direct mechanical bending of stereocilia opening cation channels (K+ influx → depolarisation)",
               d: "Chemical olfactory signalling" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Inner hair cell stereocilia are deflected by basilar membrane movement → tip-links open mechanotransduction channels → K+ enters from endolymph → IHC depolarises → glutamate release → spiral ganglion (CN VIII). Outer hair cells (electromotile via prestin) amplify the cochlear input. Frequency coding by tonotopic map: high freq at base, low freq at apex." },
  { id: "bt_bio15", section: "biopsych",
    q: "The vestibular system's semicircular canals detect:",
    options: { a: "Static head position only",
               b: "Rotational (angular) acceleration of the head in three planes",
               c: "Auditory pitch",
               d: "Linear acceleration only" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Three semicircular canals (anterior, posterior, lateral) detect rotational acceleration via endolymph movement deflecting the cupula in the ampulla. Otolith organs (utricle, saccule) detect linear acceleration and head tilt via otoconia displacing hair-cell stereocilia. Vestibular CN VIII projects to vestibular nuclei → cerebellum, oculomotor nuclei (VOR), spinal cord (postural)." },
  { id: "bt_bio16", section: "biopsych",
    q: "BDNF (brain-derived neurotrophic factor) is most strongly implicated in:",
    options: { a: "Synaptic plasticity, neuronal survival, neurogenesis (especially hippocampal); upregulated by exercise and SSRIs",
               b: "Acute action potential firing",
               c: "Apoptosis only",
               d: "Myelin synthesis only" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "BDNF binds TrkB receptors → MAPK / PI3K / PLCγ signalling → supports neuronal survival, dendritic growth, LTP, hippocampal neurogenesis. Reduced BDNF in MDD (neurotrophic hypothesis); SSRIs, ECT, exercise, and ketamine all upregulate it. Val66Met polymorphism affects activity-dependent secretion and is studied as a depression / cognition risk variant." },
  { id: "bt_bio17", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Long-term depression (LTD) at hippocampal synapses differs from LTP in that LTD involves:",
    options: { a: "Permanent cell death",
               b: "Glutamate release without any postsynaptic effect",
               c: "Brief, low-frequency stimulation leading to lower (but sustained) postsynaptic Ca2+, AMPA receptor removal, and synaptic weakening",
               d: "High-frequency stimulation with NMDA blockade" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "LTP: high-freq stimulation → large Ca2+ → CaMKII → AMPAR insertion → strengthening. LTD: low-freq stimulation → smaller, more prolonged Ca2+ → phosphatases (PP1/PP2A) dominate → AMPAR endocytosis → weakening. Together LTP + LTD permit bidirectional synaptic plasticity. Cerebellar LTD (climbing fibre → Purkinje) underlies motor learning." },
  { id: "bt_bio18", section: "biopsych",
    q: "The P300 event-related potential is most associated with:",
    options: { a: "Motor command generation",
               b: "REM sleep onset",
               c: "Visual cortex initial response",
               d: "Oddball paradigm — automatic attention allocation and stimulus categorisation; reduced in schizophrenia and Alzheimer's" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "P300 (P3): positive ERP component ~300 ms after a target stimulus in an oddball paradigm. Reflects attention and context updating. Amplitude reduced and latency prolonged in schizophrenia, AD, depression, alcoholism — proposed endophenotype marker. N400 = semantic incongruence; P600 = syntactic violation; mismatch negativity (MMN) = pre-attentive auditory change detection." },
  { id: "bt_bio19", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome is most directly caused by:",
    options: { a: "Hypoxia",
               b: "Thiamine (vitamin B1) deficiency, classically from chronic alcohol use",
               c: "Lead poisoning",
               d: "Vitamin B12 deficiency" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Wernicke's encephalopathy = acute thiamine deficiency triad: confusion + ophthalmoplegia + ataxia. Korsakoff = chronic phase: anterograde amnesia + confabulation + apathy. Underlying pathology: mammillary body and thalamic damage. Most common cause: chronic alcohol use. Treatment: IV thiamine before glucose (giving glucose first can precipitate the syndrome)." },
  { id: "bt_bio20", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Typical (first-generation) antipsychotics primarily exert clinical effects via blockade of which receptors, and which side-effect cluster results?",
    options: { a: "Serotonin 5-HT2A blockade → metabolic syndrome",
               b: "Glutamate NMDA → psychosis",
               c: "GABA-A → sedation only",
               d: "D2 dopamine receptors → extrapyramidal symptoms (EPS), tardive dyskinesia, hyperprolactinaemia" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Typicals (haloperidol, chlorpromazine, fluphenazine) potently block D2: efficacy on positive symptoms but nigrostriatal blockade → EPS (acute dystonia, akathisia, parkinsonism, tardive dyskinesia) and tuberoinfundibular blockade → hyperprolactinaemia. Atypicals add 5-HT2A blockade (Meltzer hypothesis), reducing EPS but raising metabolic load (esp. clozapine, olanzapine)." },
  { id: "bt_bio21", section: "biopsych",
    q: "The Yerkes-Dodson law describes the relationship between:",
    options: { a: "Arousal and task performance as an inverted-U curve, with optimal performance at moderate arousal; the optimum is lower for difficult tasks",
               b: "IQ and emotional intelligence",
               c: "Salary and job satisfaction",
               d: "Punishment severity and learning rate" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Yerkes & Dodson (1908): arousal vs performance follows an inverted-U. Simple/well-learned tasks peak at higher arousal; complex/novel tasks peak at lower arousal. Underlies why a little anxiety helps but a lot impairs. Related: Hebbian principle of optimal stimulation, Easterbrook cue-utilisation hypothesis." },
  { id: "bt_bio22", section: "biopsych",
    q: "The 'two-streams' (dorsal/ventral) visual processing hypothesis (Ungerleider & Mishkin, 1982) proposes:",
    options: { a: "Dorsal = colour processing; ventral = motion",
               b: "Dorsal = auditory; ventral = visual",
               c: "Dorsal = parietal 'where/how' stream; ventral = temporal 'what' stream",
               d: "Both streams converge in V1" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "After V1, visual information splits into dorsal (occipital → parietal) 'where / how' / action stream — spatial location, action guidance — and ventral (occipital → inferior temporal) 'what' / perception stream — object recognition, faces (FFA), places (PPA). Milner & Goodale refined as action vs perception. Optic ataxia (dorsal) vs visual agnosia (ventral) are double-dissociations." },
  { id: "bt_bio23", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Tonic-clonic seizures involve:",
    options: { a: "A brief staring spell without loss of tone (typical absence)",
               b: "Loss of consciousness with generalised rhythmic muscle stiffening (tonic) then jerking (clonic) due to widespread cortical hyperexcitation",
               c: "A simple partial seizure with motor symptoms only on one limb",
               d: "Sleep-only seizures with no EEG correlate" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Generalised tonic-clonic (grand mal): tonic phase (stiffening, apnoea, cyanosis ~10-30 s) → clonic phase (rhythmic jerking, 1-2 min) → post-ictal confusion. EEG: high-amplitude spike-and-wave generalised activity. Treatment: valproate, levetiracetam, lamotrigine. Absence seizures: 3 Hz spike-and-wave, ethosuximide/valproate." },
  { id: "bt_bio24", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Parkinson's disease pathology principally involves degeneration of:",
    options: { a: "Spinal motor neurons",
               b: "Hippocampal pyramidal cells",
               c: "Cholinergic basal forebrain neurons",
               d: "Dopaminergic neurons of the substantia nigra pars compacta (with Lewy body inclusions)" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Parkinson's: loss of dopaminergic neurons in the SNc → striatal dopamine deficiency → impaired direct pathway facilitation → bradykinesia, rigidity, resting tremor (3-5 Hz), postural instability. α-synuclein–containing Lewy bodies. Treated with L-DOPA + carbidopa (peripheral DOPA decarboxylase inhibitor); dopamine agonists; MAO-B inhibitors. Symptomatic; not curative." },
  { id: "bt_bio25", section: "biopsych",
    q: "The amygdala receives a 'low road' input from the thalamus that bypasses the cortex (LeDoux). This pathway:",
    options: { a: "Provides the entire content of conscious vision",
               b: "Permits rapid, crude detection of potential threat enabling fast defensive responses before conscious recognition",
               c: "Mediates voluntary motor output",
               d: "Provides slower, more accurate object identification" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "LeDoux's 'low road': sensory thalamus → amygdala directly (12 ms), providing rapid but crude threat detection. 'High road': sensory thalamus → cortex → amygdala (~30-40 ms) provides slower, accurate identification. Dual-route allows survival-critical responses before conscious processing finishes." },
  { id: "bt_bio26", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Wakefulness is associated on EEG with which dominant frequencies?",
    options: { a: "Beta (13-30 Hz) during alert wakefulness; alpha (8-13 Hz) when eyes closed, relaxed",
               b: "Theta only (4-8 Hz)",
               c: "Sleep spindles",
               d: "Predominantly delta (0.5-4 Hz)" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "EEG bands: Delta (0.5-4 Hz) = N3 deep sleep, infant wake. Theta (4-8 Hz) = drowsiness, N1, child wake, hippocampal exploration. Alpha (8-13 Hz) = relaxed wakefulness eyes closed (posterior, blocks on eye opening). Beta (13-30 Hz) = alert, focused. Gamma (30+) = cognition / binding. Sleep spindles (12-14 Hz) + K-complexes = N2." },
  { id: "bt_bio27", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Cannabinoid CB1 receptors are most densely concentrated in:",
    options: { a: "The spinal motor neurons only",
               b: "The brain — hippocampus, basal ganglia, cerebellum, cortex — accounting for memory, motor, and reward effects of cannabis",
               c: "Peripheral immune cells exclusively",
               d: "Adrenal cortex" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "CB1: most abundant GPCR in the brain; hippocampus (memory), basal ganglia + cerebellum (motor), cortex, hypothalamus, amygdala. Reduces presynaptic neurotransmitter release via Gi/o. Endogenous ligands: anandamide, 2-AG. CB2: largely immune system + microglia (anti-inflammatory). Cannabis intoxication = CB1 activation; chronic high-dose use linked to psychosis risk in vulnerable individuals." },
  { id: "bt_bio28", section: "biopsych",
    q: "The hippocampal formation is critical for:",
    options: { a: "Declarative (especially episodic and spatial) memory consolidation; not the storage site of remote memories",
               b: "Procedural / motor memory only",
               c: "Olfactory transduction",
               d: "Voluntary motor execution" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Hippocampus: critical for encoding and consolidating declarative (episodic + semantic) memories and spatial maps (place cells; O'Keefe Nobel 2014). Remote memories gradually become cortically stored (standard consolidation model) or remain hippocampally-dependent for episodic specifics (multiple-trace theory). Procedural memory: striatum + cerebellum." },
  { id: "bt_bio29", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Multiple sclerosis is characterised by:",
    options: { a: "α-synuclein deposits in the substantia nigra",
               b: "Autoimmune demyelination of CNS white matter (oligodendrocytes), with disseminated lesions in time and space",
               c: "Cortical β-amyloid plaques only",
               d: "Lower motor neuron degeneration" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "MS: T-cell mediated autoimmune attack on CNS myelin (oligodendrocyte target). Lesions disseminated in time and space (McDonald criteria), often periventricular, juxtacortical, infratentorial, spinal. Symptoms: optic neuritis, paraesthesias, weakness, ataxia, Lhermitte's sign. MRI: T2 hyperintense plaques. Treatment: DMTs (interferon-β, glatiramer, monoclonal antibodies). Demyelination ≠ axon loss initially." },
  { id: "bt_bio30", section: "biopsych",
    q: "The Wisconsin Card Sorting Test (WCST) is most sensitive to dysfunction of which region?",
    options: { a: "Dorsolateral prefrontal cortex (set-shifting, response to changing rules)",
               b: "Primary motor cortex",
               c: "Hippocampus",
               d: "Cerebellum" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "WCST: subject sorts cards by an unspoken rule (colour/shape/number) and must shift sets when feedback indicates the rule has changed. Sensitive to DLPFC dysfunction → perseverative errors. Used in schizophrenia, frontal lobe injury, ADHD. Stroop also taps DLPFC + ACC; Iowa Gambling Task taps OFC; Tower of London taps planning." },

  // ===== PSYCHOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT (30) =====
  { id: "bt_ass01", section: "assessment",
    q: "The WAIS-IV Verbal Comprehension Index (VCI) consists of which core subtests?",
    options: { a: "Block Design, Matrix Reasoning, Visual Puzzles",
               b: "Coding, Symbol Search, Cancellation",
               c: "Digit Span, Arithmetic, Letter-Number Sequencing",
               d: "Similarities, Vocabulary, Information" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "WAIS-IV core subtests by index. VCI: Similarities, Vocabulary, Information (Comprehension is supplementary). PRI: Block Design, Matrix Reasoning, Visual Puzzles (Figure Weights, Picture Completion supplementary). WMI: Digit Span, Arithmetic (Letter-Number Sequencing supplementary). PSI: Coding, Symbol Search (Cancellation supplementary)." },
  { id: "bt_ass02", section: "assessment",
    q: "Raven's Progressive Matrices is considered a 'culture-fair' test because it measures:",
    options: { a: "Reading comprehension",
               b: "Fluid (non-verbal) reasoning via abstract pattern completion — minimal language demands",
               c: "Crystallised verbal intelligence",
               d: "Memory only" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Raven's Progressive Matrices (John C. Raven, 1936): non-verbal abstract reasoning via pattern completion; minimises verbal and cultural content. Considered the purest test of Gf (fluid intelligence) in Cattell-Horn-Carroll theory. Versions: Standard (SPM), Coloured (CPM, for children/older adults), Advanced (APM, for high-IQ samples). Still some culture/education effects in practice." },
  { id: "bt_ass03", section: "assessment",
    q: "On the Stanford-Binet 5 (SB5), the five factors are:",
    options: { a: "Sensory, Motor, Memory, Attention, Language",
               b: "Verbal IQ and Performance IQ only",
               c: "Fluid Reasoning, Knowledge, Quantitative Reasoning, Visual-Spatial, Working Memory (each with verbal + nonverbal domain)",
               d: "Big Five personality factors" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "SB5 (Roid 2003) is organised by 5 CHC factors crossed with verbal/nonverbal domain (10 subtests). Factors: Fluid Reasoning, Knowledge, Quantitative Reasoning, Visual-Spatial Processing, Working Memory. Yields Full Scale IQ, Verbal IQ, Nonverbal IQ, Factor Indexes. Routing subtests adapt difficulty (object-series/matrices for nonverbal, vocabulary for verbal)." },
  { id: "bt_ass04", section: "assessment",
    q: "The MMPI-2 clinical scale 'Pd' (Psychopathic Deviate, Scale 4) classically elevates in:",
    options: { a: "Mania exclusively",
               b: "Depression only",
               c: "Antisocial behaviour, family/authority conflict, impulsivity, alienation",
               d: "Obsessive compulsive disorder primarily" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "MMPI-2 clinical scales: 1 Hs (Hypochondriasis), 2 D (Depression), 3 Hy (Hysteria), 4 Pd (Psychopathic Deviate), 5 Mf (Masculinity-Femininity), 6 Pa (Paranoia), 7 Pt (Psychasthenia/OCD-anxiety), 8 Sc (Schizophrenia), 9 Ma (Mania), 0 Si (Social Introversion). MMPI-3 (2020) restructured with RC scales as core." },
  { id: "bt_ass05", section: "assessment",
    q: "The MoCA (Montreal Cognitive Assessment) differs from the MMSE in that MoCA:",
    options: { a: "Tests only language, not memory",
               b: "Has greater sensitivity for mild cognitive impairment, includes executive function and clock-drawing, and is freely scored out of 30 with ≤25 as a screening cut-off",
               c: "Is administered only by neurologists",
               d: "Is much shorter (5 min vs 30 min)" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "MoCA (Nasreddine 2005): 30-item, 10-min screen with greater sensitivity than MMSE for MCI; includes clock-drawing, abstraction, trail-making, alternating sequence, delayed recall. Cut-off ≤25/30 (some use ≤26) for cognitive impairment. MMSE has copyright/cost issues and is less sensitive to executive deficits and MCI. Both add education adjustments." },
  { id: "bt_ass06", section: "assessment",
    q: "The PANSS (Positive and Negative Syndrome Scale) is used to assess:",
    options: { a: "Personality disorders",
               b: "Depression severity",
               c: "Cognitive impairment",
               d: "Symptom severity in schizophrenia, with separate positive, negative, and general psychopathology subscales (30 items)" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "PANSS (Kay, Fiszbein, Opler 1987): 30-item clinician-rated schizophrenia symptom scale (7 positive, 7 negative, 16 general) each 1-7. Total ≈ 30-210. Other schizophrenia scales: SAPS/SANS (Andreasen), BPRS, CGI. Five-factor models of PANSS (positive, negative, disorganisation, excited, depressed) often preferred to the original three-factor structure." },
  { id: "bt_ass07", section: "assessment",
    q: "The Y-BOCS (Yale-Brown Obsessive Compulsive Scale) rates:",
    options: { a: "Severity of suicidal ideation",
               b: "Depression severity only",
               c: "Hallucination frequency",
               d: "Severity of OCD across 10 items (5 for obsessions, 5 for compulsions) each 0-4, scoring time, interference, distress, resistance, control" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Y-BOCS (Goodman 1989): 10 items (time, interference, distress, resistance, control — each rated separately for obsessions and compulsions), scored 0-40. Bands: 0-7 subclinical, 8-15 mild, 16-23 moderate, 24-31 severe, 32-40 extreme. CY-BOCS for children. Symptom checklist precedes severity rating." },
  { id: "bt_ass08", section: "assessment",
    q: "The PCL-R (Psychopathy Checklist–Revised) was developed by:",
    options: { a: "Hervey Cleckley alone, 1941",
               b: "Aaron Beck",
               c: "Robert Hare",
               d: "Hans Eysenck" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "PCL-R (Hare 1991, building on Cleckley 1941 'Mask of Sanity'). 20 items rated 0-2 by trained clinician via semi-structured interview + file review. Total 40. ≥30 conventional psychopathy cut-off (≥25 in UK/Europe). Two-factor (later four-facet) structure: Interpersonal, Affective, Lifestyle, Antisocial. Heavily used in forensic risk assessment, somewhat controversial in courtroom use." },
  { id: "bt_ass09", section: "assessment",
    q: "Cohen's kappa (κ) is used to estimate:",
    options: { a: "Inter-rater reliability for categorical / nominal data, correcting for chance agreement",
               b: "Predictive validity",
               c: "Effect size for ANOVA",
               d: "Internal consistency" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Cohen's κ corrects observed agreement for chance: κ = (Po − Pe) / (1 − Pe). Landis & Koch benchmarks: <0 poor, 0-0.2 slight, 0.21-0.4 fair, 0.41-0.6 moderate, 0.61-0.8 substantial, 0.81-1 almost perfect. For ordinal data: weighted kappa (linear or quadratic). For multiple raters: Fleiss' kappa. For continuous: ICC." },
  { id: "bt_ass10", section: "assessment",
    q: "Spearman-Brown prophecy formula predicts:",
    options: { a: "The validity of a new test",
               b: "Effect size in ANCOVA",
               c: "The factor structure of the WAIS",
               d: "The reliability of a test if its length is changed by a factor k" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Spearman-Brown: r_new = (k × r_old) / (1 + (k − 1) × r_old). Increasing items raises reliability (with diminishing returns). Used to correct split-half reliability (estimate full-test reliability from a half) and to plan how many items to add to reach a target reliability." },
  { id: "bt_ass11", section: "assessment",
    q: "Kuder-Richardson 20 (KR-20) differs from Cronbach's alpha in that KR-20:",
    options: { a: "Is exclusively for dichotomously scored items (correct/incorrect, yes/no)",
               b: "Cannot estimate internal consistency",
               c: "Is identical in formula and use",
               d: "Is only used for personality items" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "KR-20 is the special case of Cronbach's α for dichotomously scored items. For polytomously scored items (Likert), Cronbach's α generalises. KR-21 is a simpler version of KR-20 assuming equal item difficulty. Modern practice uses α for any item scoring; ω (McDonald's omega) is increasingly preferred." },
  { id: "bt_ass12", section: "assessment",
    q: "Confirmatory Factor Analysis (CFA) differs from Exploratory Factor Analysis (EFA) primarily in that CFA:",
    options: { a: "Always uses oblique rotation",
               b: "Tests a pre-specified factor structure with fit indices (CFI, TLI, RMSEA, SRMR) and modification indices; EFA discovers structure data-driven",
               c: "Is used only for dichotomous data",
               d: "Has no a priori model" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "EFA: data-driven, discovers latent structure, decisions on # factors (Kaiser, scree, parallel analysis) + rotation (varimax / promax / oblimin). CFA: model-driven, specify loadings and constraints a priori, test fit (CFI ≥ .95, TLI ≥ .95, RMSEA ≤ .06, SRMR ≤ .08 commonly; Hu & Bentler 1999). CFA is part of SEM." },
  { id: "bt_ass13", section: "assessment",
    q: "A Likert-type scale differs from a Thurstone scale in that Likert items are:",
    options: { a: "Always dichotomous",
               b: "Rated by respondents on a symmetric agree-disagree continuum (e.g. 1-5 or 1-7); summed to a total score; assumes equal item weights",
               c: "Pre-scaled by judges before administration",
               d: "Drawn from a sentence-completion stimulus" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Likert (1932): respondents rate agreement with each statement (1-5 or 1-7); items summed. Assumes interval-level summation. Thurstone equal-appearing intervals: judges first scale items along a continuum, then respondents endorse/reject; sophisticated but laborious. Guttman: cumulative scaling (endorsing a 'higher' item implies endorsement of all lower)." },
  { id: "bt_ass14", section: "assessment",
    q: "Q-sort methodology was developed by William Stephenson and Carl Rogers to:",
    options: { a: "Replace the MMPI",
               b: "Have a respondent sort a set of statements (e.g. self-descriptors) into a forced normal distribution, capturing within-person rather than between-person variation",
               c: "Test infant reflexes",
               d: "Diagnose schizophrenia" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Q-sort: respondent sorts ~50-100 descriptor cards into a forced quasi-normal distribution (e.g. 9-point most-to-least like me). Captures idiographic self-perception. Rogers used it to measure self/ideal-self congruence pre-/post-therapy. Adult Attachment Q-sort (Waters) and Child-Q (Block) are derivatives." },
  { id: "bt_ass15", section: "assessment",
    q: "The Hawthorne effect refers to:",
    options: { a: "Demand characteristics from instructions",
               b: "Experimenters unconsciously biasing the data",
               c: "Participants altering their behaviour because they know they are being observed/studied, independent of the actual intervention",
               d: "The placebo effect in drug trials" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Hawthorne effect (Roethlisberger & Dickson, Western Electric studies 1924-32): productivity of workers rose under nearly any change in lighting / break schedule, attributed to being observed and feeling special. Reactivity / observer effect. Distinct from experimenter expectancy (Rosenthal/Pygmalion), demand characteristics (Orne), and placebo." },
  { id: "bt_ass16", section: "assessment",
    q: "Demand characteristics, as described by Martin Orne, are:",
    options: { a: "Properties of the experimental hypothesis",
               b: "Statistical demands of the test",
               c: "The amount of effort an item requires",
               d: "Cues in an experimental setting that convey to participants what is expected, influencing them to behave accordingly" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Demand characteristics (Orne 1962): all cues — instructions, setting, prior experiments, rumours — by which participants infer the experimental hypothesis and adjust behaviour. Controlled by blinding, deception (with debriefing), unobtrusive measures, between-subjects designs, post-experimental inquiry. Different from experimenter expectancy bias." },
  { id: "bt_ass17", section: "assessment",
    q: "A ceiling effect on a test means:",
    options: { a: "The test cannot discriminate among the highest performers because too many are scoring at the maximum",
               b: "The test items are too difficult overall",
               c: "Reliability is greater than 1",
               d: "Scores are normally distributed" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Ceiling effect: high performers bunch at the maximum, reducing variance and the test's ability to discriminate at the top end. Floor effect: opposite — low performers bunch at the minimum. Both reduce reliability and validity for the affected range and obscure intervention effects. Solution: add harder/easier items or use a more difficult test." },
  { id: "bt_ass18", section: "assessment",
    q: "Convergent validity is demonstrated when:",
    options: { a: "A test correlates highly with other measures of the same construct",
               b: "Two raters agree on the score",
               c: "The test predicts future criteria",
               d: "A test fails to correlate with measures of unrelated constructs" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Construct validity (Cronbach & Meehl 1955) is supported by two complementary correlations: convergent (high r with other measures of the same construct, e.g. BDI vs HAM-D) and discriminant (low r with measures of distinct constructs, e.g. BDI vs an anxiety-specific measure). Multitrait-multimethod matrix (Campbell & Fiske 1959) tests both in one matrix." },
  { id: "bt_ass19", section: "assessment",
    q: "Criterion contamination refers to:",
    options: { a: "Random measurement error in the criterion",
               b: "Replacing the criterion mid-study",
               c: "Including items related to the predictor inside the criterion measure, inflating the predictor-criterion correlation artefactually",
               d: "A criterion that uses too few items" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Criterion contamination: the predictor or knowledge of the predictor influences the criterion (e.g. a supervisor knows employees' test scores and rates their performance based on the test). Spurious inflation of validity coefficients. Solutions: blind raters, independent criterion, multiple criteria. Different from criterion deficiency (criterion omits relevant content) and criterion relevance (correct overlap)." },
  { id: "bt_ass20", section: "assessment",
    q: "A z-score of +1.5 on a normally distributed test corresponds to approximately which percentile?",
    options: { a: "99th",
               b: "84th",
               c: "50th",
               d: "93rd" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "On the standard normal curve: z = +1.5 → 93.3rd percentile. Useful anchors: z = 0 → 50th, z = ±1 → 16th/84th, z = ±1.5 → 7th/93rd, z = ±2 → 2.3rd/97.7th, z = ±3 → 0.1st/99.9th. The 68-95-99.7 rule covers ±1, ±2, ±3 SD." },
  { id: "bt_ass21", section: "assessment",
    q: "T-scores (as used on the MMPI, NEO-PI-R) have a mean and SD of:",
    options: { a: "Mean 100, SD 15",
               b: "Mean 50, SD 10",
               c: "Mean 500, SD 100",
               d: "Mean 0, SD 1" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Standardised score conventions. T-score (McCall): mean 50, SD 10 (MMPI, NEO, ASEBA). Standard score / IQ (Wechsler/SB): mean 100, SD 15. z-score: mean 0, SD 1. Scaled subtest scores (WAIS subtests): mean 10, SD 3. Stanines: mean 5, SD ~2. SAT/GRE (old): mean 500, SD 100." },
  { id: "bt_ass22", section: "assessment",
    q: "On the MMPI-2-RF, the RC (Restructured Clinical) scales differ from the original clinical scales in that RC scales:",
    options: { a: "Were dropped in the MMPI-2-RF",
               b: "Use only true/false items",
               c: "Were designed to remove common 'demoralisation' variance (RCd) so each scale measures a distinctive psychopathology dimension",
               d: "Are identical to the clinical scales" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "RC scales (Tellegen et al. 2003): RCd Demoralization extracted as common factor; remaining RC scales (RC1 Somatic, RC2 Low Positive Emotions, RC3 Cynicism, RC4 Antisocial, RC6 Persecutory, RC7 Dysfunctional Negative Emotions, RC8 Aberrant Experiences, RC9 Hypomanic Activation) measure distinctive dimensions with cleaner discriminant validity. MMPI-2-RF (2008) and MMPI-3 (2020) use RC as core." },
  { id: "bt_ass23", section: "assessment",
    q: "The NEO-PI-R measures personality on which model?",
    options: { a: "Cattell 16PF",
               b: "MBTI 16-type",
               c: "Five-Factor Model / Big Five: Openness, Conscientiousness, Extraversion, Agreeableness, Neuroticism (OCEAN); 240 items with 6 facets per factor",
               d: "Eysenck's 3-factor (PEN)" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "NEO-PI-R (Costa & McCrae 1992): 240-item self-report measuring Big Five domains, each with 6 facets (e.g. Neuroticism: Anxiety, Angry Hostility, Depression, Self-Consciousness, Impulsiveness, Vulnerability). NEO-FFI is a 60-item short form. Big Five emerges across languages and cultures (lexical hypothesis, Goldberg). HEXACO adds Honesty-Humility as a 6th factor." },
  { id: "bt_ass24", section: "assessment",
    q: "Cattell's 16PF (Sixteen Personality Factor Questionnaire) is most associated with:",
    options: { a: "Diagnosis of personality disorders only",
               b: "Q-sort methodology",
               c: "Factor-analytically derived 16 primary personality traits, with 5 second-order (global) factors that approximate Big Five",
               d: "Projective interpretation" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Raymond Cattell (1949, revised through 16PF 5th ed. 1993): factor-analysed natural language descriptors → 16 primary source traits (Warmth, Reasoning, Emotional Stability, Dominance, Liveliness, Rule-Consciousness, Social Boldness, Sensitivity, Vigilance, Abstractedness, Privateness, Apprehension, Openness-to-Change, Self-Reliance, Perfectionism, Tension). Five second-order factors approximate Big Five (Extraversion, Anxiety, Tough-mindedness, Independence, Self-Control)." },
  { id: "bt_ass25", section: "assessment",
    q: "Generalizability theory (G theory, Cronbach et al. 1972) extends Classical Test Theory by:",
    options: { a: "Decomposing observed-score variance into multiple facets (raters, items, occasions, etc.) using ANOVA, distinguishing different sources of measurement error",
               b: "Treating measurement error as undifferentiated",
               c: "Eliminating the need for any reliability estimate",
               d: "Modeling response curves like IRT" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "G theory: replaces CTT's single 'error term' with simultaneous estimation of multiple sources of measurement error using ANOVA — facets such as raters, items, occasions. G-study estimates variance components; D-study (decision study) projects how reliability changes under different administration designs. Complements but does not replace IRT." },
  { id: "bt_ass26", section: "assessment",
    q: "The Item Characteristic Curve (ICC) in IRT plots:",
    options: { a: "Probability of a correct response as a function of person ability (θ), with item parameters determining its shape",
               b: "Validity coefficients across studies",
               c: "Internal consistency by item",
               d: "Test reliability against sample size" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "ICC: S-shaped logistic curve, x-axis = θ (latent ability), y-axis = P(correct response). Parameters: b (difficulty, location of curve along θ axis), a (discrimination, steepness at b), c (guessing, lower asymptote). 1PL Rasch: only b. 2PL: b + a. 3PL: b + a + c. Information function shows where the item discriminates best." },
  { id: "bt_ass27", section: "assessment",
    q: "Test-retest reliability is most appropriate to estimate when:",
    options: { a: "The construct is a current mood state",
               b: "There is no opportunity to administer twice",
               c: "There is only one rater",
               d: "The construct is expected to be stable over the retest interval (e.g. intelligence, personality)" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Test-retest assumes construct stability across the retest interval. Appropriate for stable traits (Big Five, IQ). Inappropriate for state measures (current mood, anxiety) where genuine change is expected and would lower test-retest r artefactually. Short intervals risk memory carryover; long intervals confound real change with unreliability." },
  { id: "bt_ass28", section: "assessment",
    q: "The Standard Error of Measurement (SEM = SD × √(1 − r)) for a test with SD = 15 and reliability = 0.91 is approximately:",
    options: { a: "15.0",
               b: "10.0",
               c: "1.5",
               d: "4.5" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "SEM = 15 × √(1 − 0.91) = 15 × √0.09 = 15 × 0.3 = 4.5. A 95% CI for a single observed score is roughly observed ± 1.96 × SEM ≈ ±9. Lower SEM with higher reliability and lower SD. WAIS-IV FSIQ has SD = 15, reliability ≈ .98, SEM ≈ 2.16, so 95% CI ≈ ±4." },
  { id: "bt_ass29", section: "assessment",
    q: "Receiver Operating Characteristic (ROC) analysis plots:",
    options: { a: "Predictive validity against reliability",
               b: "Sensitivity (true positive rate) against 1 − specificity (false positive rate) across cut-off values; AUC summarises discrimination",
               c: "Item difficulty vs discrimination",
               d: "Score vs age" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "ROC: plot of TPR vs FPR at each possible threshold. AUC (area under curve): probability that a randomly chosen positive case scores higher than a randomly chosen negative. AUC = 0.5 chance; 0.7-0.8 acceptable; 0.8-0.9 excellent; ≥0.9 outstanding. Useful for selecting clinical cut-offs and comparing screeners. Youden's J = sensitivity + specificity − 1 identifies the optimal threshold." },
  { id: "bt_ass30", section: "assessment",
    q: "The Vineland-3, K-SADS, ADI-R, and ADOS-2 are best classified as:",
    options: { a: "EEG paradigms",
               b: "Personality projective techniques",
               c: "Self-report cognitive tests",
               d: "Structured / semi-structured interviews and observation instruments used in developmental and psychiatric assessment (Vineland-3 adaptive, K-SADS childhood psychiatric, ADI-R + ADOS-2 autism)" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Vineland-3 = adaptive behaviour caregiver interview. K-SADS = Kiddie Schedule for Affective Disorders & Schizophrenia, semi-structured childhood psychiatric interview. ADI-R = Autism Diagnostic Interview – Revised (caregiver). ADOS-2 = Autism Diagnostic Observation Schedule, direct interaction-based; ADI-R + ADOS-2 are the 'gold standard' combo for autism diagnostic confirmation." }
],
  "nimhans-mock-1": [
  // ---------- BIOSTATS / RESEARCH (15) ----------
  { id: "n1_bst01", section: "biostats", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "In a cohort study investigating the association between a new psychological intervention and relapse rates in schizophrenia, the risk ratio is calculated as 1.0. This finding indicates that:",
    options: { a: "The incidence of relapse in the exposed group is double that of the unexposed group",
               b: "The intervention has a significant protective effect against relapse",
               c: "There is no difference in the risk of relapse between the intervention and control groups",
               d: "The odds of exposure are equal in both the relapse and non-relapse groups" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "RR = 1.0 means equal incidence in exposed vs unexposed, i.e., no association. RR > 1 = harm, RR < 1 = protection. OR is the inverse framing." },
  { id: "n1_bst02", section: "biostats", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "A researcher conducts a systematic review on the efficacy of CBT for insomnia. To ensure transparency and replicability in the methodology, the researcher should adhere to which of the following guidelines?",
    options: { a: "CONSORT Statement",
               b: "PRISMA Guidelines",
               c: "STROBE Checklist",
               d: "CARE Guidelines" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "PRISMA = systematic reviews & meta-analyses. CONSORT = RCTs. STROBE = observational studies. CARE = case reports." },
  { id: "n1_bst03", section: "biostats", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "To investigate a rare psychological outcome (e.g., a specific dissociative disorder) and its potential past exposures (e.g., childhood trauma), which of the following epidemiological study designs would be the MOST feasible and appropriate?",
    options: { a: "Prospective Cohort Study",
               b: "Cross-Sectional Study",
               c: "Case-Control Study",
               d: "Randomized Controlled Trial" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Case-control is best for rare outcomes: start with cases, look back at exposure. Cohort is impractical when outcome is rare." },
  { id: "n1_bst04", section: "biostats", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "A researcher randomly selects 5 districts from each state, then 10 schools per district, then 30 students per school. This sampling technique is known as:",
    options: { a: "Stratified Random Sampling",
               b: "Quota Sampling",
               c: "Cluster Sampling",
               d: "Purposive Sampling" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Multi-stage cluster sampling: random selection at each hierarchical level. Stratified samples within every stratum, not clusters." },
  { id: "n1_bst05", section: "biostats", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "Which sampling method relies on the researcher's judgment to select participants who are 'typical' or 'representative,' and does NOT allow for the calculation of sampling error?",
    options: { a: "Simple Random Sampling",
               b: "Stratified Random Sampling",
               c: "Quota Sampling",
               d: "Cluster Sampling" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Quota sampling is non-probability: judgment-based, no sampling error estimate. The other three are probability methods." },
  { id: "n1_bst06", section: "biostats", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "On a Scree plot, eigenvalues drop sharply from 3.5 to 1.8, then plateau at 0.9, 0.7, 0.5. By the Kaiser-Guttman rule (eigenvalue > 1) and the elbow, how many factors should be retained?",
    options: { a: "5",
               b: "3",
               c: "2",
               d: "1" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Kaiser-Guttman keeps factors with eigenvalue > 1.0 → only 3.5 and 1.8 qualify, so 2 factors. Scree elbow agrees." },
  { id: "n1_bst07", section: "biostats", year: "2025", pyq: true,
    q: "When comparing two independent samples and the data does not meet assumptions for parametric testing, which non-parametric test is most commonly used?",
    options: { a: "Wilcoxon signed-rank test",
               b: "Mann-Whitney U test",
               c: "Kruskal-Wallis test",
               d: "Friedman test" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Mann-Whitney U = nonparametric independent t-test. Wilcoxon signed-rank = paired. Kruskal-Wallis = >2 independent groups. Friedman = repeated measures." },
  { id: "n1_bst08", section: "biostats", year: "2023", pyq: true,
    q: "Cohen's Kappa is a statistical measure used to assess which of the following?",
    options: { a: "Test-retest reliability",
               b: "Internal consistency",
               c: "Inter-rater reliability",
               d: "Criterion validity" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Cohen's κ = inter-rater agreement corrected for chance. Internal consistency uses Cronbach's α. Test-retest uses Pearson r over time." },
  { id: "n1_bst09", section: "biostats", year: "2025", pyq: true,
    q: "When two predictors in a regression analysis have a high correlation with each other, this statistical problem is known as:",
    options: { a: "Heteroscedasticity",
               b: "Multicollinearity",
               c: "Autocorrelation",
               d: "Homogeneity of variance" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Multicollinearity inflates standard errors and destabilises β coefficients. Diagnose with VIF > 10 or tolerance < 0.1." },
  { id: "n1_bst10", section: "biostats", year: "2025", pyq: true,
    q: "In statistical hypothesis testing, rejecting a true null hypothesis represents which type of error?",
    options: { a: "Type I error",
               b: "Type II error",
               c: "Sampling error",
               d: "Standard error" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Type I (α) = false positive: rejecting true H0. Type II (β) = false negative: failing to reject false H0. Power = 1−β." },
  { id: "n1_bst11", section: "biostats", year: "2025", pyq: true,
    q: "Which statistical measure tells how far individual data points are dispersed from the mean expressed in standard deviation units?",
    options: { a: "Variance",
               b: "Z-score (standard score)",
               c: "Coefficient of variation",
               d: "Standard error" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Z = (X−μ)/σ. Z is in SD units, allowing scores from different distributions to be compared directly." },
  { id: "n1_bst12", section: "biostats", year: "2025", pyq: true,
    q: "When the assumption of normal distribution is met and you want to compare the means of two independent groups, which statistical test is most appropriate?",
    options: { a: "Mann-Whitney U test",
               b: "Independent samples t-test",
               c: "Chi-square test",
               d: "Wilcoxon signed-rank test" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Independent samples t-test compares means of two unrelated groups under normality + equal variance. Use Welch's t if variances differ." },
  { id: "n1_bst13", section: "biostats", year: "2025", pyq: true,
    q: "Interaction effects between two or more independent variables can be detected and analyzed using which statistical technique?",
    options: { a: "Correlation analysis",
               b: "Independent samples t-test",
               c: "Factorial ANOVA",
               d: "Chi-square test" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Factorial ANOVA tests main effects + interactions between two or more IVs on a continuous DV. t-test handles only one IV with two levels." },
  { id: "n1_bst14", section: "biostats", year: "2023", pyq: true,
    q: "If a researcher wants to subtract out the influence of number of children from the relationship between marital stress and length of marriage, which statistical method is most appropriate?",
    options: { a: "Biserial correlation",
               b: "Multiple correlation",
               c: "Linear correlation",
               d: "Partial correlation" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Partial correlation = correlation between X and Y while statistically controlling for Z. Multiple R relates one DV to multiple predictors jointly." },
  { id: "n1_bst15", section: "biostats", year: "2025", pyq: true,
    q: "A researcher wants to calculate the degrees of freedom (df) for a 3 × 4 contingency table in a Chi-square test. The df is:",
    options: { a: "12",
               b: "7",
               c: "6",
               d: "11" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "df = (rows − 1) × (cols − 1) = 2 × 3 = 6 for a 3×4 contingency table." },

  // ---------- NEUROPSYCH / BIOPSYCH (15) ----------
  { id: "n1_bio01", section: "biopsych", year: "2025", pyq: true,
    q: "In Phenylketonuria (PKU), the body fails to convert phenylalanine into which substance due to a deficiency of phenylalanine hydroxylase?",
    options: { a: "Urea",
               b: "Tyrosine",
               c: "Tryptophan",
               d: "Histidine" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "PKU = autosomal recessive deficiency of phenylalanine hydroxylase, so phenylalanine accumulates and tyrosine drops. Untreated → intellectual disability. Newborn screen + low-Phe diet." },
  { id: "n1_bio02", section: "biopsych", year: "2025", pyq: true,
    q: "What is the alternative name for adrenaline, the hormone released by the adrenal medulla during the fight-or-flight response?",
    options: { a: "Norepinephrine",
               b: "Epinephrine",
               c: "Dopamine",
               d: "Cortisol" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Adrenaline = epinephrine. Released by adrenal medulla during sympathetic activation. Cortisol is released by adrenal cortex via HPA axis." },
  { id: "n1_bio03", section: "biopsych", year: "2025", pyq: true,
    q: "Which of the following hormones is NOT secreted by the anterior pituitary gland?",
    options: { a: "Growth hormone (GH)",
               b: "Adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH)",
               c: "Melatonin",
               d: "Thyroid stimulating hormone (TSH)" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Melatonin is secreted by the pineal gland. Anterior pituitary releases GH, ACTH, TSH, FSH, LH, prolactin." },
  { id: "n1_bio04", section: "biopsych", year: "2025", pyq: true,
    q: "Which brain structure serves as the primary control center for integrating the endocrine, visceral, and autonomic nervous systems?",
    options: { a: "Cerebellum",
               b: "Hypothalamus",
               c: "Thalamus",
               d: "Brainstem" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Hypothalamus = master regulator: links nervous system to endocrine via pituitary; controls homeostasis, autonomic outflow, sleep, hunger, temperature." },
  { id: "n1_bio05", section: "biopsych", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "In neuroimaging, which technique directly measures the electrical activity of the brain with high temporal resolution, akin to ECG in cardiology?",
    options: { a: "fMRI",
               b: "PET",
               c: "EEG",
               d: "MEG" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "EEG records cortical electrical activity in milliseconds (high temporal, low spatial). fMRI = high spatial via BOLD. MEG measures magnetic fields, comparable temporal resolution but less common." },
  { id: "n1_bio06", section: "biopsych", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "The lateral ventricles, the largest of the ventricles in the brain, are filled with:",
    options: { a: "Blood",
               b: "Cerebrospinal fluid (CSF)",
               c: "Synaptic fluid",
               d: "Myelin" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "All ventricles contain CSF, produced by the choroid plexus. CSF cushions and provides nutrient exchange. Lateral → 3rd → 4th → spinal cord." },
  { id: "n1_bio07", section: "biopsych", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "Patient H.M. underwent surgical removal of the medial temporal lobes (incl. hippocampus) for epilepsy. His most profound deficit was in:",
    options: { a: "Procedural memory",
               b: "Semantic memory",
               c: "Anterograde amnesia",
               d: "Retrograde amnesia" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "H.M. could not form new declarative memories (anterograde amnesia) but retained working memory and most older memories. Established hippocampal role in consolidation." },
  { id: "n1_bio08", section: "biopsych", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "Despite his severe amnesia, H.M. learned and improved on a mirror-tracing task across days, with no conscious recollection of practising it. This demonstrates the preservation of:",
    options: { a: "Episodic memory",
               b: "Declarative memory",
               c: "Procedural memory",
               d: "Semantic memory" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Procedural (skill) memory is preserved in hippocampal damage; relies on basal ganglia and cerebellum. This dissociation supports separate memory systems." },
  { id: "n1_bio09", section: "biopsych", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "Loss of memories for events that happened BEFORE a brain injury, such as a concussion or stroke, is termed:",
    options: { a: "Anterograde amnesia",
               b: "Retrograde amnesia",
               c: "Source amnesia",
               d: "Dissociative amnesia" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Retrograde = past memories lost. Anterograde = inability to form new ones. Often co-occur after TBI. Ribot's law: recent memories lost first." },
  { id: "n1_bio10", section: "biopsych", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "The primary brain region responsible for the comprehension of spoken and written language is:",
    options: { a: "Broca's area (left inferior frontal gyrus)",
               b: "Wernicke's area (left superior temporal gyrus)",
               c: "Primary auditory cortex (Heschl's gyrus)",
               d: "Angular gyrus" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Wernicke's = comprehension. Damage → fluent but meaningless speech ('word salad'), impaired understanding. Connected to Broca's via arcuate fasciculus (lesion → conduction aphasia)." },
  { id: "n1_bio11", section: "biopsych", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "Damage to Broca's area typically results in:",
    options: { a: "Fluent aphasia with poor comprehension",
               b: "Non-fluent, effortful speech with relatively intact comprehension",
               c: "Difficulty naming objects (anomia)",
               d: "Inability to recognize familiar faces (prosopagnosia)" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Broca's aphasia = expressive: telegraphic speech, agrammatism, comprehension largely preserved. Patient is aware and frustrated." },
  { id: "n1_bio12", section: "biopsych", year: "2024 · 2023", pyq: true, repeated: true,
    q: "[Repeated 2023, 2024] The reticular activating system (RAS), located in the brainstem, is crucial for the regulation of:",
    options: { a: "Fine motor coordination",
               b: "Arousal, wakefulness, and attention",
               c: "Emotional memory",
               d: "Balance and posture" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Ascending RAS (ARAS) regulates cortical arousal, sleep-wake transitions, attention. Damage → coma. Cerebellum handles coordination/balance." },
  { id: "n1_bio13", section: "biopsych", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "A 45-year-old man with chronic schizophrenia on long-term antipsychotics presents with repetitive, involuntary lip-smacking, tongue movements, and grimacing. This is most likely:",
    options: { a: "Akathisia",
               b: "Acute dystonia",
               c: "Tardive dyskinesia",
               d: "Parkinsonism" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Tardive dyskinesia: late-onset, often irreversible, oro-facial dyskinesias from chronic D2 blockade. Higher risk with typicals; AIMS scale for monitoring." },
  { id: "n1_bio14", section: "biopsych", year: "2025", pyq: true,
    q: "What is the primary function of the myelin sheath surrounding nerve axons?",
    options: { a: "To provide structural support to neurons",
               b: "To increase the speed of nerve impulse conduction",
               c: "To store neurotransmitters for synaptic transmission",
               d: "To generate action potentials in neurons" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Myelin enables saltatory conduction at Nodes of Ranvier → 10–100× faster conduction. Demyelination (MS) slows transmission, causes deficits." },
  { id: "n1_bio15", section: "biopsych", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "A patient presents with severe memory deficits, confabulation, and nystagmus, with a history of chronic alcohol use. This clinical picture is classic for:",
    options: { a: "Alzheimer's disease",
               b: "Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome",
               c: "Vascular dementia",
               d: "Frontotemporal dementia" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Wernicke-Korsakoff: thiamine (B1) deficiency. Wernicke triad = confusion + ataxia + ophthalmoplegia. Korsakoff = anterograde amnesia + confabulation. Treat: IV thiamine before glucose." },

  // ---------- ABNORMAL / PSYCHOPATHOLOGY (15) ----------
  { id: "n1_abn01", section: "abnormal", year: "2024 · 2023", pyq: true, repeated: true,
    q: "[Repeated 2023, 2024] 'Folie à deux,' or Shared Psychotic Disorder, is characterized by:",
    options: { a: "Two individuals simultaneously experiencing independent psychotic episodes",
               b: "The transmission of delusional beliefs from one individual to another in a close relationship",
               c: "A delusion shared by an entire cultural or subcultural group",
               d: "A brief psychotic episode triggered by extreme stress in two closely related people" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Shared delusional disorder: dominant 'inducer' transmits delusions to a closely associated 'recipient.' Removed from DSM-5 as separate entity but still clinically used." },
  { id: "n1_abn02", section: "abnormal", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "The specific phobia of pain is known as:",
    options: { a: "Nosophobia",
               b: "Algophobia",
               c: "Cynophobia",
               d: "Hematophobia" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Algophobia = pain. Nosophobia = disease. Cynophobia = dogs. Hematophobia = blood. (Greek: algos = pain.)" },
  { id: "n1_abn03", section: "abnormal", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "A client reports persistent admiration-seeking but presents with excessive emotionality, attention-seeking, and seductive behavior, rather than grandiosity. This is most characteristic of:",
    options: { a: "Narcissistic personality disorder",
               b: "Histrionic personality disorder",
               c: "Borderline personality disorder",
               d: "Dependent personality disorder" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Histrionic PD (Cluster B): theatrical, seductive, shallow affect, suggestible. Narcissistic = grandiosity + entitlement. Both share attention-seeking but the flavour differs." },
  { id: "n1_abn04", section: "abnormal", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "A client reports persistent and frightening experiences of their surroundings seeming unreal, distorted, or dreamlike. This symptom is best described as:",
    options: { a: "Derealization",
               b: "Depersonalization",
               c: "Déjà vu",
               d: "Hallucination" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Derealization = world feels unreal. Depersonalization = self feels unreal/detached. Both are dissociative; can co-occur in DPDR disorder, panic, PTSD." },
  { id: "n1_abn05", section: "abnormal", year: "2025", pyq: true,
    q: "A patient presents with a fixed delusional belief that their spouse is being unfaithful, despite no evidence. This specific form of delusional jealousy is known as:",
    options: { a: "Capgras syndrome",
               b: "Othello syndrome",
               c: "Fregoli syndrome",
               d: "De Clérambault's syndrome" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Othello = morbid jealousy. Capgras = familiar replaced by impostor. Fregoli = strangers are one disguised person. De Clérambault's = erotomania." },
  { id: "n1_abn06", section: "abnormal", year: "2025", pyq: true,
    q: "A 67-year-old man with mild cognitive impairment insists that his wife of 40 years is an identical 'duplicate' impostor, despite acknowledging she looks exactly like her. Most likely diagnosis:",
    options: { a: "Fregoli syndrome",
               b: "Capgras syndrome",
               c: "Cotard syndrome",
               d: "Reduplicative paramnesia" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Capgras: familiar person believed replaced by an identical impostor. Linked to disrupted face recognition + emotional response (ventral visual stream / amygdala disconnect)." },
  { id: "n1_abn07", section: "abnormal", year: "2025", pyq: true,
    q: "Cotard syndrome, a rare neuropsychiatric condition, is primarily characterized by which type of delusional belief?",
    options: { a: "Grandiose delusions of having special powers",
               b: "Persecutory delusions of being followed",
               c: "Nihilistic delusions of being dead or not existing",
               d: "Somatic delusions of having a serious medical illness" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Cotard = walking corpse syndrome. Patient believes they are dead, do not exist, or are missing organs. Seen in psychotic depression, schizophrenia, neurodegenerative conditions." },
  { id: "n1_abn08", section: "abnormal", year: "2025", pyq: true,
    q: "A 28-year-old woman presents with sudden right-arm paralysis after an argument; neuro exam is normal and she appears strikingly unconcerned ('It's not really bothering me'). This presentation is most characteristic of:",
    options: { a: "Conversion disorder",
               b: "Borderline personality disorder",
               c: "Major depressive disorder",
               d: "Factitious disorder" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Conversion (functional neurological symptom) disorder: la belle indifférence — calm despite serious symptoms. Symptoms inconsistent with neurology. Factitious = intentional fabrication for sick role." },
  { id: "n1_abn09", section: "abnormal", year: "2025", pyq: true,
    q: "A 28-year-old woman exhibits distinct personalities with their own names, memories, and behaviour, with amnesia between episodes; she finds clothes she does not remember buying. Most consistent diagnosis:",
    options: { a: "Dissociative amnesia",
               b: "Dissociative identity disorder",
               c: "Depersonalization/derealization disorder",
               d: "Dissociative fugue" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "DID: ≥2 distinct identity states + recurrent gaps in autobiographical memory inconsistent with ordinary forgetting. Strong association with severe early trauma." },
  { id: "n1_abn10", section: "abnormal", year: "2025", pyq: true,
    q: "Persistent preoccupation with perceived defects or flaws in physical appearance that are not observable to others is characteristic of which disorder?",
    options: { a: "Somatic symptom disorder",
               b: "Body dysmorphic disorder",
               c: "Illness anxiety disorder",
               d: "Conversion disorder" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "BDD (DSM-5 OCRD chapter): preoccupation with imagined/slight defect, repetitive behaviours (mirror checking, reassurance seeking). Higher suicide risk than OCD." },
  { id: "n1_abn11", section: "abnormal", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "Which of the following best describes the concept of 'ego-dystonic' as it applies to obsessions in OCD?",
    options: { a: "The individual experiences the obsessions as a core part of their identity",
               b: "The individual experiences the obsessions as intrusive, unwanted, and inconsistent with their self-concept",
               c: "The individual derives pleasure from the obsessive thoughts",
               d: "The individual is completely unaware that their obsessions are unreasonable" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Ego-dystonic = experienced as alien/unwanted (typical of OCD obsessions). Ego-syntonic = consistent with self (typical of personality disorder traits)." },
  { id: "n1_abn12", section: "abnormal", year: "2024 · 2023", pyq: true, repeated: true,
    q: "[Repeated 2023, 2024] 'Dementia praecox,' which translates to 'premature dementia,' is historically most directly associated with which contemporary diagnostic category?",
    options: { a: "Alzheimer's disease",
               b: "Bipolar I disorder",
               c: "Schizophrenia",
               d: "Frontotemporal dementia" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Coined by Kraepelin (1893); refined by Bleuler (1908) who renamed it 'schizophrenia' and described the 4 As (associations, affect, ambivalence, autism)." },
  { id: "n1_abn13", section: "abnormal", year: "2025", pyq: true,
    q: "According to the ICD-10, what is the minimum duration of symptoms required for a definitive diagnosis of Schizophrenia?",
    options: { a: "1 week",
               b: "1 month",
               c: "3 months",
               d: "6 months" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "ICD-10/11: ≥1 month. DSM-5: ≥6 months total (with ≥1 month active phase). Be careful which classification the question references." },
  { id: "n1_abn14", section: "abnormal", year: "2025", pyq: true,
    q: "Which of the following is considered a negative symptom of schizophrenia, characterized by reduced quantity and quality of verbal expression?",
    options: { a: "Auditory hallucinations",
               b: "Poverty of speech (Alogia)",
               c: "Persecutory delusions",
               d: "Disorganized behavior" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Negative symptoms: 5 As — Alogia, Anhedonia, Avolition, Affective flattening, Asociality. Hallucinations and delusions are positive symptoms." },
  { id: "n1_abn15", section: "abnormal", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "A patient with Korsakoff's psychosis fabricates detailed and believable stories about their past to fill memory gaps, without intent to deceive. This is:",
    options: { a: "Confabulation",
               b: "Confusion",
               c: "Delirium",
               d: "Malingering" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Confabulation: false memories without conscious intent to deceive. Common in Korsakoff's, frontal lobe damage, ACoA aneurysm. Distinct from lying (intentional)." },

  // ---------- DEVELOPMENTAL (10) ----------
  { id: "n1_dev01", section: "dev", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "According to Thomas and Chess's temperament model, a child who is generally negative in mood, slow to adapt, and has irregular biological rhythms would be classified as:",
    options: { a: "Easy temperament",
               b: "Difficult temperament",
               c: "Slow-to-warm-up temperament",
               d: "Insecurely attached" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Thomas & Chess (NYLS): Easy (40%), Difficult (10%), Slow-to-warm-up (15%), Mixed (35%). 'Goodness of fit' between temperament and parenting predicts outcomes." },
  { id: "n1_dev02", section: "dev", year: "2024 · 2023", pyq: true, repeated: true,
    q: "[Repeated 2023, 2024] The 'Marshmallow Test,' pioneered by Walter Mischel, primarily measures a child's capacity for:",
    options: { a: "Sustained attention",
               b: "Resistance to temptation",
               c: "Delay of gratification",
               d: "Impulse control in social settings" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Mischel's preschool delay-of-gratification paradigm. Wait-time predicted later academic, social, and self-regulation outcomes (though replication studies show smaller effects when SES is controlled)." },
  { id: "n1_dev03", section: "dev", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "A child sees two identical balls of clay; one is rolled into a sausage. When asked if the amount is the same, the child says 'yes.' Per Piaget, this child is most likely in which stage?",
    options: { a: "Sensorimotor",
               b: "Preoperational",
               c: "Concrete operational",
               d: "Formal operational" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Conservation of mass (typically 7–11 yrs) marks concrete operational. Preoperational children would say the sausage has more clay (centration on length)." },
  { id: "n1_dev04", section: "dev", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "A 5-year-old sorts cards by colour but cannot then re-sort the same deck by suit. This inflexibility is an example of:",
    options: { a: "Egocentrism",
               b: "Animism",
               c: "Centration",
               d: "Transductive reasoning" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Centration: focusing on one feature (colour) and ignoring others (suit). Underlies failure on conservation tasks. Decentration emerges in concrete operational." },
  { id: "n1_dev05", section: "dev", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "According to Erikson, the primary crisis to be resolved in adolescence (12-18 years) is:",
    options: { a: "Industry vs. inferiority",
               b: "Identity vs. role confusion",
               c: "Intimacy vs. isolation",
               d: "Generativity vs. stagnation" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Adolescence: identity vs role confusion → virtue of fidelity. Marcia later operationalised four identity statuses (diffusion, foreclosure, moratorium, achievement)." },
  { id: "n1_dev06", section: "dev", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "A middle-aged adult finds meaning by mentoring younger colleagues and contributing to the next generation. According to Erikson, this individual is successfully navigating:",
    options: { a: "Intimacy vs. isolation",
               b: "Generativity vs. stagnation",
               c: "Ego integrity vs. despair",
               d: "Identity vs. role confusion" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Generativity vs stagnation (40-65 yrs) → virtue of care. Failure → midlife self-absorption, lack of meaning." },
  { id: "n1_dev07", section: "dev", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "According to Erikson, the primary virtue that emerges from successfully resolving 'Trust vs. Mistrust' in infancy is:",
    options: { a: "Will",
               b: "Purpose",
               c: "Hope",
               d: "Competence" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Trust→Hope. Autonomy→Will. Initiative→Purpose. Industry→Competence. Identity→Fidelity. Intimacy→Love. Generativity→Care. Integrity→Wisdom." },
  { id: "n1_dev08", section: "dev", year: "2025", pyq: true,
    q: "According to Piaget, abstract reasoning and hypothetical thinking abilities are primarily developed during which developmental stage?",
    options: { a: "Sensorimotor stage",
               b: "Preoperational stage",
               c: "Concrete operational stage",
               d: "Formal operational stage" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Formal operational (~11+ yrs): abstract reasoning, hypothetico-deductive thinking, propositional logic. Concrete-op kids reason logically only with concrete material." },
  { id: "n1_dev09", section: "dev", year: "2025", pyq: true,
    q: "Which parenting style is characterized by emotional support, clear boundaries, and accountability for children's behaviour?",
    options: { a: "Authoritarian",
               b: "Authoritative",
               c: "Permissive",
               d: "Neglectful" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Baumrind's authoritative = high warmth + high control. Best outcomes. Authoritarian = low warmth, high control. Permissive = high warmth, low control." },
  { id: "n1_dev10", section: "dev", year: "2023", pyq: true,
    q: "Mary Ainsworth is best known for her work on which aspect of developmental psychology?",
    options: { a: "Cognitive development",
               b: "Moral development",
               c: "Attachment theory",
               d: "Language acquisition" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Ainsworth's Strange Situation classified attachment as: secure (B), insecure-avoidant (A), insecure-resistant (C), and later disorganized (D, Main & Solomon)." },

  // ---------- PSYCHOLOGICAL ASSESSMENT (10) ----------
  { id: "n1_ass01", section: "assessment", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "A 10-year-old illiterate child from a rural area is brought for intellectual assessment. Which test would be MOST appropriate to minimize cultural and educational bias?",
    options: { a: "Bhatia's Battery of Performance Tests of Intelligence",
               b: "Wechsler Intelligence Scale for Children (WISC-IV)",
               c: "Stanford-Binet Intelligence Scale (5th ed.)",
               d: "Malin's Intelligence Scale for Indian Children (MISIC)" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Bhatia's Battery: 5 performance subtests, no reading required, Indian norms — designed for illiterate or low-literacy children. WISC/MISIC are heavily verbal." },
  { id: "n1_ass02", section: "assessment", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "A test developer administers a new anxiety scale to a group, then re-administers the same scale two weeks later, correlating the two sets of scores. This establishes:",
    options: { a: "Split-half reliability",
               b: "Inter-rater reliability",
               c: "Test-retest reliability",
               d: "Parallel forms reliability" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Test-retest = stability of scores over time (Pearson r). Split-half/Cronbach α = internal consistency. Inter-rater = agreement between scorers." },
  { id: "n1_ass03", section: "assessment", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "A clinician wants a client to reveal characteristic ways of thinking and feeling in close relationships by telling stories about ambiguous pictures. The MOST appropriate test is:",
    options: { a: "Rorschach Inkblot Test",
               b: "Thematic Apperception Test (TAT)",
               c: "Sentence Completion Test",
               d: "MMPI" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Murray's TAT: ambiguous pictures elicit narratives revealing needs, drives, interpersonal themes. Rorschach uses inkblots → focused on perception/form quality." },
  { id: "n1_ass04", section: "assessment", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "On the WAIS-IV, a scaled score of 10 on a subtest indicates performance that is:",
    options: { a: "Below average (16th percentile)",
               b: "Exactly average (50th percentile)",
               c: "Above average (84th percentile)",
               d: "Significantly impaired (2nd percentile)" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "WAIS-IV scaled scores have M=10, SD=3 → 10 = 50th percentile. Index/composite scores have M=100, SD=15." },
  { id: "n1_ass05", section: "assessment", year: "2025", pyq: true,
    q: "In the WAIS-IV, how many CORE subtests are there in the Verbal Comprehension Index and the Perceptual Reasoning Index respectively?",
    options: { a: "3 verbal, 4 performance",
               b: "3 verbal, 3 performance",
               c: "4 verbal, 3 performance",
               d: "2 verbal, 4 performance" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "WAIS-IV core: VCI (Similarities, Vocabulary, Information) + PRI (Block Design, Matrix Reasoning, Visual Puzzles) = 3 + 3. WMI and PSI add 2 each." },
  { id: "n1_ass06", section: "assessment", year: "2025", pyq: true,
    q: "Cattell's 16PF (16 Personality Factor Questionnaire) is primarily designed to measure:",
    options: { a: "Fluid intelligence",
               b: "Crystallized intelligence",
               c: "Personality traits",
               d: "General intelligence (g-factor)" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "16PF: 16 source traits derived via factor analysis (Cattell). Hierarchical with 5 global factors (broadly mapping to the Big Five)." },
  { id: "n1_ass07", section: "assessment", year: "2025", pyq: true,
    q: "Which of the following is NOT a recognized Rorschach inkblot test scoring system?",
    options: { a: "Beck system",
               b: "Exner Comprehensive System",
               c: "Murray system",
               d: "Klopfer system" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Major Rorschach systems: Beck, Klopfer, Piotrowski, Hertz, Rapaport-Schafer, Exner (Comprehensive), R-PAS. Murray developed the TAT, not a Rorschach system." },
  { id: "n1_ass08", section: "assessment", year: "2025", pyq: true,
    q: "The original Stanford-Binet Intelligence Scale used which method for calculating IQ scores?",
    options: { a: "Deviation IQ with standard scores",
               b: "Ratio IQ (Mental Age / Chronological Age × 100)",
               c: "Percentile ranking system",
               d: "T-score transformation" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Stern (1912): Ratio IQ = (MA/CA) × 100. Replaced by Wechsler's Deviation IQ (M=100, SD=15) which is now standard. Ratio IQ unstable in adults (CA grows faster than MA)." },
  { id: "n1_ass09", section: "assessment", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "The Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory (MMPI) is best described as a(n):",
    options: { a: "Projective personality test",
               b: "Objective personality test",
               c: "Intelligence test",
               d: "Neuropsychological screening battery" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "MMPI = empirically keyed objective personality inventory: structured items, fixed scoring, validity scales (L, F, K). Projectives (Rorschach, TAT) use ambiguous stimuli." },
  { id: "n1_ass10", section: "assessment", year: "2023", pyq: true,
    q: "A 35-year-old presents with persistent sadness, anhedonia, weight loss, insomnia, worthlessness, and concentration difficulties for 2 months. Which assessment would be MOST appropriate to further evaluate her symptoms?",
    options: { a: "Beck Depression Inventory (BDI)",
               b: "Rorschach Inkblot Test",
               c: "Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory (MMPI)",
               d: "Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale (WAIS)" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "BDI: 21-item self-report directly quantifying depression severity. MMPI is broad personality. Rorschach is projective. WAIS measures IQ — irrelevant here." },

  // ---------- THERAPY (10) ----------
  { id: "n1_thr01", section: "therapy", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "Which therapeutic modality was pioneered by Maxwell Jones and is characterized by democratic involvement of staff and patients in decision-making and daily activities?",
    options: { a: "Encounter groups",
               b: "Therapeutic community",
               c: "Group analysis",
               d: "Milieu therapy" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Maxwell Jones (1953, Henderson Hospital): therapeutic community — flattened hierarchy, communalism, permissiveness, reality confrontation. Predecessor to milieu therapy concepts." },
  { id: "n1_thr02", section: "therapy", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "A client with a phobia of dogs is asked to relax and then imagine a graded series of fear-provoking interactions with a dog. This technique is called:",
    options: { a: "Flooding",
               b: "Systematic desensitization",
               c: "Implosive therapy",
               d: "Modeling" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Wolpe's systematic desensitization: relaxation + graded hierarchy + reciprocal inhibition. Flooding/implosion = full-intensity exposure without graduation." },
  { id: "n1_thr03", section: "therapy", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "Viktor Frankl's concept of the 'will to meaning,' the fundamental motivational force in humans to find purpose, is central to which therapeutic school?",
    options: { a: "Rational Emotive Behavior Therapy",
               b: "Logotherapy",
               c: "Existential-Humanistic Therapy",
               d: "Acceptance and Commitment Therapy" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Logotherapy (Frankl, 'Man's Search for Meaning'). Three pathways to meaning: creative work, experiences/love, attitude toward unavoidable suffering." },
  { id: "n1_thr04", section: "therapy", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "A therapist helps a client identify and challenge irrational beliefs such as 'I absolutely must be approved by everyone' (musterbation). This therapist is most likely practising:",
    options: { a: "Beck's Cognitive Therapy",
               b: "Rational Emotive Behavior Therapy",
               c: "Dialectical Behavior Therapy",
               d: "Reality Therapy" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Ellis's REBT: ABC model (Activating event → Belief → Consequence). 'Musts/shoulds/oughts' (musterbation) and awfulizing are core irrational beliefs to dispute." },
  { id: "n1_thr05", section: "therapy", year: "2024 · 2023 · 2025", pyq: true, repeated: true,
    q: "[Repeated 2023, 2024, 2025] The Premack Principle states that:",
    options: { a: "A high-probability behavior can reinforce a low-probability behavior",
               b: "A low-probability behavior can reinforce a high-probability behavior",
               c: "Reinforcement is delivered after every correct response",
               d: "Punishment is more effective than reinforcement" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Premack (1965): 'Grandma's rule' — finish vegetables (low-prob) before dessert (high-prob). High-frequency behaviour reinforces lower-frequency one. Asked nearly every NIMHANS year." },
  { id: "n1_thr06", section: "therapy", year: "2024 · 2023", pyq: true, repeated: true,
    q: "[Repeated 2023, 2024] A therapist uses 'time-out' to manage a child's aggressive outbursts. Time-out is technically classified as:",
    options: { a: "Positive punishment",
               b: "Negative reinforcement",
               c: "Negative punishment",
               d: "Positive reinforcement" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Time-out removes a desirable stimulus (attention/access to reinforcement) → behaviour decreases = negative punishment. Distinct from negative reinforcement (remove aversive → behaviour increases)." },
  { id: "n1_thr07", section: "therapy", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "Which therapeutic approach focuses on the 'real relationship' and the therapist's unconditional positive regard, rather than the working alliance per se?",
    options: { a: "Psychoanalysis",
               b: "Cognitive-Behavioral Therapy",
               c: "Person-Centered Therapy",
               d: "Interpersonal Therapy" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Rogers's Person-Centered Therapy: three core conditions = unconditional positive regard, congruence, accurate empathy. The relationship itself is the change agent." },
  { id: "n1_thr08", section: "therapy", year: "2025", pyq: true,
    q: "In Dialectical Behavior Therapy (DBT), the 'core mindfulness' skills are primarily derived from which tradition?",
    options: { a: "Stoicism",
               b: "Zen Buddhism",
               c: "Psychoanalysis",
               d: "Existentialism" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Linehan integrated Zen mindfulness with CBT. Four DBT modules: mindfulness, distress tolerance, emotion regulation, interpersonal effectiveness. Originally for borderline PD with chronic suicidality." },
  { id: "n1_thr09", section: "therapy", year: "2025", pyq: true,
    q: "Borderline Personality Disorder is most characteristically associated with which primary defense mechanism?",
    options: { a: "Repression",
               b: "Splitting",
               c: "Sublimation",
               d: "Rationalization" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Splitting (Kernberg, Klein): people are seen as all-good or all-bad; targets shift rapidly. Drives idealization-devaluation cycles in BPD relationships." },
  { id: "n1_thr10", section: "therapy", year: "2023", pyq: true,
    q: "Affirmative therapy is specifically designed to support which population of clients?",
    options: { a: "Individuals with anxiety disorders",
               b: "Elderly clients experiencing cognitive decline",
               c: "LGBTQ+ individuals",
               d: "Children with developmental delays" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "LGBTQ+-affirmative therapy validates non-heteronormative identities, addresses minority stress, and explicitly opposes conversion therapy." },

  // ---------- SOCIAL (10) ----------
  { id: "n1_soc01", section: "social", year: "2024 · 2023", pyq: true, repeated: true,
    q: "[Repeated 2023, 2024] The bystander effect, where the presence of others inhibits helping, is primarily explained by two factors:",
    options: { a: "Social loafing and deindividuation",
               b: "Diffusion of responsibility and pluralistic ignorance",
               c: "Social comparison and normative influence",
               d: "Group polarization and groupthink" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Latané & Darley (1968-70): each bystander assumes another will act (diffusion) and reads others' calm as evidence nothing is wrong (pluralistic ignorance)." },
  { id: "n1_soc02", section: "social", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "The classic 'bystander apathy' effect was demonstrated in the laboratory following the real-life murder of:",
    options: { a: "Martin Luther King Jr.",
               b: "Kitty Genovese",
               c: "Emmett Till",
               d: "Malala Yousafzai" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "1964 Kitty Genovese murder in Queens. The original '38 witnesses' figure has been disputed historically, but the case still motivated Latané & Darley's foundational research." },
  { id: "n1_soc03", section: "social", year: "2024 · 2023", pyq: true, repeated: true,
    q: "[Repeated 2023, 2024] The 'just-world hypothesis' explains victim-blaming as a result of:",
    options: { a: "People's inherent sadism and desire to see others suffer",
               b: "The need to believe that the world is orderly, predictable, and fair",
               c: "A fundamental error in attributing behavior to dispositional factors",
               d: "The process of social comparison with a less fortunate other" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Lerner: just-world belief — people get what they deserve. Threats to this view are reduced by blaming the victim. Increases under high mortality salience (TMT)." },
  { id: "n1_soc04", section: "social", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "The phenomenon where qualified individuals (especially women / minorities) are blocked from advancing to higher career positions by an invisible barrier is called:",
    options: { a: "The Matthew effect",
               b: "The glass ceiling effect",
               c: "The halo effect",
               d: "The Pygmalion effect" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Glass ceiling (Hymowitz & Schellhardt, 1986). Related: glass cliff (women promoted to leadership during crisis), sticky floor, leaky pipeline." },
  { id: "n1_soc05", section: "social", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "Learned helplessness, in social learning theory, refers to:",
    options: { a: "Performing a behavior to escape an aversive stimulus",
               b: "Observing a model being rewarded for aggressive behavior",
               c: "Repeated unavoidable aversive events leading to passive acceptance of later aversive events",
               d: "Failing to receive reinforcement for a previously learned behavior" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Seligman & Maier (1967): dogs given inescapable shocks later failed to escape avoidable shocks. Reformulated cognitively (Abramson) → depressogenic attribution: internal-stable-global." },
  { id: "n1_soc06", section: "social", year: "2024 · 2023", pyq: true, repeated: true,
    q: "[Repeated 2023, 2024] The 'Type D' personality, identified as a risk factor for poor cardiovascular prognosis, is characterized by:",
    options: { a: "Hostility and competitiveness",
               b: "Negative affectivity combined with social inhibition",
               c: "An optimistic and resilient outlook",
               d: "Perfectionism and rigidity" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Denollet's Type D ('distressed'): high negative affect + high social inhibition. Doubles cardiac mortality risk independent of disease severity. Type A = hostility/competitiveness." },
  { id: "n1_soc07", section: "social", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "The 'halo effect' is a cognitive bias in which:",
    options: { a: "A person's overall impression of another influences ratings of specific traits",
               b: "Individuals take credit for success but blame external factors for failure",
               c: "People overestimate the extent to which others share their opinions",
               d: "A person's initial impression anchors all subsequent judgments" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Halo effect (Thorndike, 1920): a global impression bleeds into trait ratings (e.g., 'attractive → assumed competent'). Distinct from self-serving bias and false consensus." },
  { id: "n1_soc08", section: "social", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "The 'looking-glass self,' introduced by Charles Horton Cooley, posits that our self-concept is primarily formed by:",
    options: { a: "Our innate, unconscious drives",
               b: "Comparing ourselves to others who are similar",
               c: "Imagining how we appear to others and how they judge us",
               d: "Successfully resolving psychosocial crises at each life stage" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Cooley (1902): self-concept is built from our perception of others' perceptions of us. Three steps: imagine appearance, imagine judgement, react with feeling (pride/shame)." },
  { id: "n1_soc09", section: "social", year: "2023", pyq: true,
    q: "In social psychology, the concept of reactance refers to which of the following phenomena?",
    options: { a: "The tendency to comply with requests from authority figures",
               b: "Increased motivation to perform a behavior when one's freedom to engage in it is threatened",
               c: "The process of changing attitude to align with the majority opinion",
               d: "The inclination to evaluate one's abilities by comparing to others" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Brehm's reactance theory: perceived freedom-restriction increases the attractiveness of the restricted behaviour. Explains backlash to bans, paradoxical compliance failures." },
  { id: "n1_soc10", section: "social", year: "2023", pyq: true,
    q: "The sociological concept of anomie, as introduced by Émile Durkheim, refers to which of the following?",
    options: { a: "A state of normlessness where social norms are unclear or no longer applicable",
               b: "The inevitable progression from traditional to modern societies",
               c: "The adaptation of individuals to the values of the dominant culture",
               d: "The process by which individuals internalize societal norms and values" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Durkheim: anomie = breakdown of social norms during rapid change → increased deviance, suicide. Merton later extended to strain theory (means-ends gap)." },

  // ---------- GENERAL / COGNITIVE (10) ----------
  { id: "n1_gen01", section: "general", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "In Baddeley's model of working memory, the subsystem responsible for integrating information from the phonological loop, visuospatial sketchpad, and long-term memory into a single coherent episode is the:",
    options: { a: "Central executive",
               b: "Episodic buffer",
               c: "Visuospatial sketchpad",
               d: "Phonological loop" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Episodic buffer (added 2000): limited-capacity multimodal store binding info across slave systems and LTM. Central executive = attentional control, not storage." },
  { id: "n1_gen02", section: "general", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "The correct sequence of the four stages of creative thinking, as proposed by Graham Wallas, is:",
    options: { a: "Preparation, Illumination, Incubation, Verification",
               b: "Incubation, Preparation, Verification, Illumination",
               c: "Preparation, Incubation, Illumination, Verification",
               d: "Illumination, Preparation, Verification, Incubation" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Wallas (1926): Preparation → Incubation → Illumination ('aha!') → Verification. Some later models add 'intimation' between incubation and illumination." },
  { id: "n1_gen03", section: "general", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "According to Albert Bandura, which of the following is NOT a necessary component process for observational learning to occur?",
    options: { a: "Attention",
               b: "Motivation",
               c: "Reinforcement",
               d: "Reproduction" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Bandura's 4 processes: Attention, Retention, Reproduction, Motivation (ARRM). Direct reinforcement is NOT required (vicarious is enough) — this is what distinguished Bandura from Skinner." },
  { id: "n1_gen04", section: "general", year: "2025", pyq: true,
    q: "The backward digit span test, where participants must repeat a sequence of numbers in reverse order, is primarily used to assess which type of memory system?",
    options: { a: "Long-term memory",
               b: "Working memory",
               c: "Sensory memory",
               d: "Procedural memory" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Forward digit span ≈ short-term/phonological loop. Backward digit span requires manipulation → engages central executive → working memory measure." },
  { id: "n1_gen05", section: "general", year: "2025", pyq: true,
    q: "In psychoanalytic theory, what term describes the mental representation of psychic energy being invested or attached to a particular object, person, or idea?",
    options: { a: "Libido",
               b: "Cathexis",
               c: "Fixation",
               d: "Sublimation" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Cathexis (German: Besetzung) = investment of psychic energy. Fixation = arrest at a developmental stage. Sublimation = redirection of drive into socially acceptable outlet." },
  { id: "n1_gen06", section: "general", year: "2025", pyq: true,
    q: "'Crystallized intelligence' is best defined as:",
    options: { a: "The ability to solve novel problems",
               b: "The speed of neural processing",
               c: "Accumulated knowledge and verbal skills derived from experience",
               d: "The capacity of working memory" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Cattell-Horn: Gc (crystallized) = acquired knowledge/vocabulary; rises through life. Gf (fluid) = novel problem-solving; peaks in 20s, declines with age." },
  { id: "n1_gen07", section: "general", year: "2025", pyq: true,
    q: "Which personality inventory is directly based on Carl Jung's theory of psychological types?",
    options: { a: "MMPI",
               b: "MBTI (Myers-Briggs Type Indicator)",
               c: "16PF",
               d: "NEO-PI-R" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "MBTI dichotomies: E/I, S/N, T/F, J/P → 16 types. Drawn from Jung's typology + Briggs/Myers extensions. Psychometrically weaker than Big Five inventories like NEO-PI-R." },
  { id: "n1_gen08", section: "general", year: "2025", pyq: true,
    q: "According to Aaron Beck's cognitive theory of depression, the pattern of negative thoughts about self, world, and future is known as:",
    options: { a: "Cognitive distortions",
               b: "Cognitive triad",
               c: "Automatic thoughts",
               d: "Dysfunctional assumptions" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Beck's negative cognitive triad: self ('I'm worthless'), world ('everything is hopeless'), future ('it will never improve'). Maintained by automatic thoughts and schema." },
  { id: "n1_gen09", section: "general", year: "2023", pyq: true,
    q: "The phonological loop, a component of Baddeley's model of working memory, is primarily responsible for:",
    options: { a: "Visual and spatial processing",
               b: "Storing and rehearsing auditory and verbal information",
               c: "Coordinating the integration of information from different sources",
               d: "Managing long-term memory retrieval" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Phonological loop = phonological store (~2 sec) + articulatory rehearsal. Evidence: word-length effect, articulatory suppression, phonological similarity effect." },
  { id: "n1_gen10", section: "general", year: "2023", pyq: true,
    q: "Weber's Law is best described by which of the following statements?",
    options: { a: "The just-noticeable difference (JND) is a fixed amount for any given stimulus",
               b: "The just-noticeable difference (JND) is a constant fraction of the original stimulus intensity",
               c: "Detection of a stimulus depends solely on the duration of exposure",
               d: "Perception of a stimulus is not influenced by changes in its intensity" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Weber: ΔI/I = k (constant). JND scales proportionally with stimulus magnitude. Fechner extended this to log relationship between sensation and intensity (Fechner's law)." },

  // ---------- EXTRA HIGH-YIELD (5) — replaced the old English/reasoning block; NIMHANS has no language section ----------
  { id: "n1_thr11", section: "therapy", year: "pattern", pyq: false,
    q: "Wolpe's systematic desensitisation pairs graded exposure to a feared stimulus with deep muscle relaxation. It is based on the principle of:",
    options: { a: "Operant extinction",
               b: "Reciprocal inhibition",
               c: "Aversive conditioning",
               d: "Flooding" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Wolpe's reciprocal inhibition: a relaxation response incompatible with anxiety is paired with the feared stimulus, so the two cannot co-occur and the anxiety is inhibited. Flooding uses ungraded full exposure without relaxation." },
  { id: "n1_abn16", section: "abnormal", year: "pattern", pyq: false,
    q: "The presence of two or more distinct personality states together with recurrent gaps in autobiographical recall is the core feature of:",
    options: { a: "Depersonalisation / derealisation disorder",
               b: "Dissociative amnesia",
               c: "Dissociative identity disorder",
               d: "Schizophrenia" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "DID = two or more distinct identity states plus dissociative amnesia (gaps in recall beyond ordinary forgetting). Dissociative amnesia lacks the distinct identity states; schizophrenia is a psychotic, not dissociative, disorder." },
  { id: "n1_bio16", section: "biopsych", year: "pattern", pyq: false,
    q: "The extrapyramidal side effects (dystonia, parkinsonism) of typical antipsychotics arise mainly from dopamine D2 blockade in which pathway?",
    options: { a: "Mesolimbic",
               b: "Mesocortical",
               c: "Tuberoinfundibular",
               d: "Nigrostriatal" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Nigrostriatal D2 blockade produces EPS. Mesolimbic blockade reduces positive symptoms; mesocortical blockade worsens negative/cognitive symptoms; tuberoinfundibular blockade raises prolactin." },
  { id: "n1_ass11", section: "assessment", year: "pattern", pyq: false,
    q: "The Vineland Adaptive Behaviour Scales are primarily used to assess:",
    options: { a: "Intelligence quotient",
               b: "Adaptive functioning in daily living",
               c: "Personality structure",
               d: "Neuropsychological memory deficits" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "The Vineland measures adaptive behaviour (communication, daily living skills, socialisation, motor skills) and is central to diagnosing intellectual disability alongside IQ. It is not an intelligence test itself." },
  { id: "n1_bst16", section: "biostats", year: "pattern", pyq: false,
    q: "Which sampling method gives every member of the population a known, non-zero probability of being selected?",
    options: { a: "Quota sampling",
               b: "Snowball sampling",
               c: "Probability (random) sampling",
               d: "Purposive sampling" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Probability sampling (simple random, stratified, cluster, systematic) assigns every unit a known non-zero selection chance, allowing sampling-error estimation. Quota, snowball, and purposive are non-probability methods." }
],
  "pyq-mock-2": [
  // ---------- THERAPY (18) ----------
  { id: "p2_thr01", section: "therapy", source: "RML", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "According to the Premack principle, the most effective reinforcer for a child who rarely does homework is:",
    options: { a: "Verbal praise from the parent",
               b: "Allowing the child to play video games (a high-frequency behaviour) only after completing homework",
               c: "A monetary reward for each completed assignment",
               d: "Pairing homework with a neutral activity such as drinking water" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Premack (1965): higher-probability behaviour reinforces lower-probability behaviour. 'Grandma's rule'. Asked across NIMHANS 2023/24/25 and RML 2024." },
  { id: "p2_thr02", section: "therapy", source: "IHBAS", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "In Albert Ellis's REBT, the 'B' in the ABC model refers to:",
    options: { a: "Behavioural consequence",
               b: "Belief about the activating event",
               c: "Baseline functioning",
               d: "Biological predisposition" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "A = activating event, B = belief (irrational/rational), C = consequence (emotional/behavioural). Ellis later added D (dispute) and E (effective new belief)." },
  { id: "p2_thr03", section: "therapy", source: "JEMAS", year: "2023", pyq: true,
    q: "Beck's cognitive triad in depression consists of negative views about:",
    options: { a: "Past, present, future",
               b: "Self, world, future",
               c: "Self, others, environment",
               d: "Thoughts, feelings, behaviours" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Beck's triad: negative view of SELF (worthless), WORLD (hostile), FUTURE (hopeless). Drives automatic negative thoughts and depressotypic schemas." },
  { id: "p2_thr04", section: "therapy", source: "NFSU", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "Systematic desensitisation, developed by Joseph Wolpe, is based on the principle of:",
    options: { a: "Reciprocal inhibition",
               b: "Operant extinction",
               c: "Stimulus generalisation",
               d: "Punishment" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Wolpe (1958): pair feared stimulus (graded hierarchy) with relaxation, which inhibits anxiety because the two responses cannot coexist. Classical-counterconditioning basis." },
  { id: "p2_thr05", section: "therapy", source: "IMHANS", year: "2025", pyq: true,
    q: "Token economy programmes were systematically developed for chronic psychiatric inpatients by:",
    options: { a: "B.F. Skinner and Albert Bandura",
               b: "Teodoro Ayllon and Nathan Azrin",
               c: "Hans Eysenck and Joseph Wolpe",
               d: "Aaron Beck and Albert Ellis" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Ayllon & Azrin (1968) ran the original token economy on a long-stay psychiatric ward. Tokens (secondary reinforcers) are exchanged for backup reinforcers." },
  { id: "p2_thr06", section: "therapy", source: "RML", year: "2025", pyq: true,
    q: "Which of the following is NOT one of the four standard treatment modes in Linehan's Dialectical Behaviour Therapy (DBT)?",
    options: { a: "Individual psychotherapy",
               b: "Skills training group",
               c: "Telephone consultation",
               d: "Family genogram construction" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Linehan's 4 modes: individual therapy, skills group, phone coaching, therapist consultation team. Genograms are a Bowen family-systems tool, not DBT." },
  { id: "p2_thr07", section: "therapy", source: "AMITY", year: "2023", pyq: true,
    q: "Maxwell Jones is most strongly associated with which therapeutic approach?",
    options: { a: "Therapeutic community",
               b: "Psychodrama",
               c: "Logotherapy",
               d: "Solution-focused brief therapy" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Maxwell Jones (1953): therapeutic community at Henderson Hospital, UK. Ward-as-treatment, flattened hierarchy, daily community meetings, peer feedback." },
  { id: "p2_thr08", section: "therapy", source: "NIMHANS", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "Viktor Frankl's logotherapy is BEST described as a form of:",
    options: { a: "Behaviour therapy emphasising token reinforcement",
               b: "Existential therapy emphasising the search for meaning",
               c: "Psychodynamic therapy emphasising unconscious conflict",
               d: "Cognitive therapy emphasising irrational beliefs" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Logos = meaning. Frankl: humans driven by 'will to meaning'. Core techniques: paradoxical intention, dereflection, Socratic dialogue around values." },
  { id: "p2_thr09", section: "therapy", source: "GMCH", year: "2025", pyq: true,
    q: "The 'empty chair' technique is most characteristic of which therapeutic school?",
    options: { a: "Cognitive Behaviour Therapy",
               b: "Person-Centred Therapy",
               c: "Gestalt Therapy",
               d: "Interpersonal Therapy" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Fritz Perls's Gestalt therapy: client speaks to an imagined other (parent, conflicted part of self) in the empty chair to surface unfinished business and integrate splits." },
  { id: "p2_thr10", section: "therapy", source: "RML", year: "2023", pyq: true,
    q: "Bandura's observational learning involves four sub-processes in which sequence?",
    options: { a: "Retention → Attention → Motivation → Reproduction",
               b: "Attention → Retention → Reproduction → Motivation",
               c: "Motivation → Reproduction → Attention → Retention",
               d: "Reproduction → Retention → Attention → Motivation" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "ARRM: Attention (notice the model) → Retention (encode in memory) → Reproduction (motor capability to imitate) → Motivation (incentive to perform)." },
  { id: "p2_thr11", section: "therapy", source: "IHBAS", year: "2025", pyq: true,
    q: "Exposure and Response Prevention (ERP) for OCD was operationalised primarily by:",
    options: { a: "Edna Foa",
               b: "Aaron Beck",
               c: "Joseph LeDoux",
               d: "Marsha Linehan" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Edna Foa codified ERP: prolonged exposure to OC triggers while preventing compulsive rituals. Gold-standard first-line behavioural treatment for OCD." },
  { id: "p2_thr12", section: "therapy", source: "JEMAS", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "Schema therapy, developed by Jeffrey Young, was originally designed for patients who:",
    options: { a: "Had failed to respond to standard CBT, particularly those with personality disorders",
               b: "Required hospitalisation for acute psychosis",
               c: "Presented with simple specific phobias",
               d: "Needed brief solution-focused intervention" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Young extended CBT to chronic, treatment-resistant patients with personality disorders (especially BPD/NPD), targeting early maladaptive schemas via experiential + cognitive + behavioural techniques." },
  { id: "p2_thr13", section: "therapy", source: "NIMHANS", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "Salvador Minuchin is associated with which model of family therapy?",
    options: { a: "Strategic family therapy",
               b: "Structural family therapy",
               c: "Multigenerational/Bowen family therapy",
               d: "Experiential family therapy" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Minuchin = Structural (subsystems, boundaries, enmeshment vs disengagement, family map). Strategic = Haley/Madanes. Multigenerational = Bowen. Experiential = Whitaker/Satir." },
  { id: "p2_thr14", section: "therapy", source: "NFSU", year: "2025", pyq: true,
    q: "Carl Rogers's three 'core conditions' for therapeutic change are:",
    options: { a: "Transference, resistance, interpretation",
               b: "Unconditional positive regard, congruence, empathy",
               c: "Reinforcement, modelling, shaping",
               d: "Cognitive restructuring, behavioural activation, relapse prevention" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Rogers's necessary and sufficient conditions: UPR (non-judgemental acceptance), congruence (genuineness), empathic understanding. Core of Person-Centred Therapy." },
  { id: "p2_thr15", section: "therapy", source: "GMCH", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "Disulfiram is used as an adjunct in the treatment of alcohol dependence because it:",
    options: { a: "Reduces dopaminergic reward signalling in the nucleus accumbens",
               b: "Inhibits aldehyde dehydrogenase, producing aversive symptoms when alcohol is consumed",
               c: "Acts as a partial agonist at GABA-A receptors",
               d: "Blocks NMDA receptors to reduce craving" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Disulfiram (Antabuse) inhibits ALDH → acetaldehyde accumulates → flushing, nausea, tachycardia, headache. Classical aversion-based pharmacotherapy. Naltrexone blocks opioid reward; acamprosate modulates glutamate." },
  { id: "p2_thr16", section: "therapy", source: "AMITY", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "EMG biofeedback is most clinically useful for which of the following conditions?",
    options: { a: "Tension-type headache",
               b: "Bipolar I disorder, depressive episode",
               c: "Acute psychosis",
               d: "Antisocial personality disorder" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Surface EMG biofeedback teaches voluntary control over frontalis / trapezius muscle tension. Strong evidence for tension headache, TMJ, chronic low back pain. Not indicated in acute psychosis or PDs." },
  { id: "p2_thr17", section: "therapy", source: "RML", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "Reinforcing successive approximations toward a target behaviour is known as:",
    options: { a: "Chaining",
               b: "Shaping",
               c: "Prompting",
               d: "Fading" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Shaping (Skinner): reinforce closer and closer approximations until target is reached. Chaining links discrete behaviours into a sequence. Prompting/fading manipulate cue presence." },
  { id: "p2_thr18", section: "therapy", source: "JEMAS", year: "2025", pyq: true,
    q: "Which of the following is an example of negative punishment?",
    options: { a: "Adding a spank when a child misbehaves",
               b: "Taking away screen time for the rest of the day after a tantrum",
               c: "Removing an electric shock when the rat presses the lever",
               d: "Giving a treat when the dog sits on command" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Negative = remove. Punishment = decreases behaviour. Removing screen time decreases the tantrum behaviour = negative punishment (time-out is the same principle). (a) positive punishment, (c) negative reinforcement, (d) positive reinforcement." },

  // ---------- ABNORMAL / PSYCHOPATHOLOGY (15) ----------
  { id: "p2_abn01", section: "abnormal", source: "IHBAS", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "According to DSM-5-TR, the minimum duration required for a diagnosis of schizophrenia is:",
    options: { a: "1 month of active symptoms",
               b: "3 months of total disturbance",
               c: "6 months of total disturbance (active + prodromal/residual)",
               d: "12 months of continuous psychosis" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "DSM-5: ≥6 months total (including prodromal/residual), with ≥1 month of active-phase symptoms. ICD-11: 1 month of symptoms. Common ICD/DSM-trap question." },
  { id: "p2_abn02", section: "abnormal", source: "NIMHANS", year: "2025", pyq: true,
    q: "Bleuler's '4 As' of schizophrenia do NOT include:",
    options: { a: "Loosening of associations",
               b: "Affective flattening",
               c: "Auditory hallucinations",
               d: "Autism (withdrawal into inner life)" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Bleuler's 4 As: Associations (loose), Affect (blunted/inappropriate), Autism (social withdrawal), Ambivalence. Hallucinations are 'accessory' symptoms, NOT among the 4 As." },
  { id: "p2_abn03", section: "abnormal", source: "RML", year: "2023", pyq: true,
    q: "Schneiderian first-rank symptoms of schizophrenia include all EXCEPT:",
    options: { a: "Thought insertion",
               b: "Audible thoughts (thought echo)",
               c: "Loose associations",
               d: "Delusional perception" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Schneider's FRS: thought insertion/withdrawal/broadcasting, audible thoughts, voices arguing/commenting, somatic passivity, made affect/impulse/volition, delusional perception. Loose associations is Bleulerian, not Schneiderian." },
  { id: "p2_abn04", section: "abnormal", source: "JEMAS", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "A patient insists his wife has been replaced by an identical-looking impostor. This is:",
    options: { a: "Fregoli delusion",
               b: "Capgras syndrome",
               c: "Cotard syndrome",
               d: "Othello syndrome" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Capgras: familiar person/place is impostor/duplicate. Fregoli: stranger is familiar person in disguise. Cotard: nihilistic (I am dead). Othello: pathological jealousy / unfaithful spouse." },
  { id: "p2_abn05", section: "abnormal", source: "NIMHANS", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "Folie à deux is now classified in ICD-11 under:",
    options: { a: "A separate independent category",
               b: "Schizoaffective disorder",
               c: "Other primary psychotic disorders (induced delusional disorder)",
               d: "Schizotypal personality disorder" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Shared psychotic disorder (induced delusional disorder) — once 'folie à deux'. Dominant psychotic person 'induces' delusions in close, dependent partner. Classified under other primary psychotic disorders." },
  { id: "p2_abn06", section: "abnormal", source: "IMHANS", year: "2025", pyq: true,
    q: "A 28-year-old is found wandering in a different city with no memory of his identity, having travelled there with apparent purpose. The most likely diagnosis is:",
    options: { a: "Dissociative amnesia",
               b: "Dissociative fugue",
               c: "Depersonalisation disorder",
               d: "Transient global amnesia" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Dissociative fugue: sudden purposeful travel + amnesia for identity, sometimes with assumption of new identity. Plain dissociative amnesia lacks the travel component." },
  { id: "p2_abn07", section: "abnormal", source: "RML", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "'La belle indifférence' — a striking lack of concern about a serious physical symptom — is classically associated with:",
    options: { a: "Somatic symptom disorder",
               b: "Illness anxiety disorder",
               c: "Conversion disorder (functional neurological symptom disorder)",
               d: "Factitious disorder" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "La belle indifférence is the textbook descriptor for conversion disorder. Note: research shows it is neither sensitive nor specific, but remains the classical PYQ answer." },
  { id: "p2_abn08", section: "abnormal", source: "GMCH", year: "2025", pyq: true,
    q: "Which Cluster B personality disorder is characterised by a pervasive pattern of grandiosity, need for admiration, and lack of empathy?",
    options: { a: "Borderline",
               b: "Histrionic",
               c: "Narcissistic",
               d: "Antisocial" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "NPD core triad: grandiosity, need for admiration, lack of empathy. BPD = instability + abandonment. Histrionic = attention-seeking + theatrical. ASPD = disregard for/violation of rights." },
  { id: "p2_abn09", section: "abnormal", source: "AMITY", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "A nursing assistant secretly injects insulin into her elderly patients to create medical emergencies, deriving satisfaction from the resulting attention. This is:",
    options: { a: "Malingering",
               b: "Factitious disorder imposed on another",
               c: "Antisocial personality disorder",
               d: "Conversion disorder" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Factitious imposed on another (formerly Munchausen by proxy): deliberate induction/falsification of illness in someone else for sick-role gratification. Malingering requires an external incentive (money, time off, drugs)." },
  { id: "p2_abn10", section: "abnormal", source: "JEMAS", year: "2023", pyq: true,
    q: "DSM-5-TR criteria for ADHD require symptoms to be present before what age?",
    options: { a: "5 years",
               b: "7 years",
               c: "10 years",
               d: "12 years" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "DSM-5 raised the age-of-onset criterion from DSM-IV's 7 years to 12 years. Several inattentive or hyperactive-impulsive symptoms must be present before 12, in ≥2 settings, ≥6 months." },
  { id: "p2_abn11", section: "abnormal", source: "NFSU", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "Which feature differentiates Bipolar I from Bipolar II disorder?",
    options: { a: "Presence of psychotic features only in Bipolar II",
               b: "At least one manic episode is required for Bipolar I; Bipolar II requires hypomania + major depression",
               c: "Bipolar II has longer manic episodes than Bipolar I",
               d: "Bipolar I cannot have depressive episodes" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Bipolar I = ≥1 manic episode (full mania, ≥7 days or hospitalisation). Bipolar II = ≥1 hypomanic episode (≥4 days, milder, no hospitalisation/psychosis) + ≥1 MDE. Mania can have psychosis; hypomania by definition cannot." },
  { id: "p2_abn12", section: "abnormal", source: "RML", year: "2025", pyq: true,
    q: "The DSM-5-TR specifier 'with limited prosocial emotions' is applied to which diagnosis?",
    options: { a: "Antisocial personality disorder",
               b: "Conduct disorder",
               c: "Oppositional defiant disorder",
               d: "Intermittent explosive disorder" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "LPE specifier is unique to CONDUCT DISORDER in DSM-5: lack of remorse/guilt, callous-unemotional, indifferent about performance, shallow affect. Classic trap — most candidates pick ASPD." },
  { id: "p2_abn13", section: "abnormal", source: "IHBAS", year: "2025", pyq: true,
    q: "Compared to obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD), obsessive-compulsive personality disorder (OCPD) is characterised by symptoms that are typically:",
    options: { a: "Ego-dystonic and accompanied by significant insight",
               b: "Ego-syntonic with limited insight",
               c: "Only present during manic episodes",
               d: "Acute in onset and rapidly remitting" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "OCD = ego-DYSTONIC (patient resists obsessions, recognises them as foreign). OCPD = ego-SYNTONIC (rigid perfectionism feels right to the patient; little distress about the traits themselves)." },
  { id: "p2_abn14", section: "abnormal", source: "NIMHANS", year: "2023", pyq: true,
    q: "Pibloktoq, characterised by sudden dissociation, screaming, and erratic behaviour, is a culture-bound syndrome described in which population?",
    options: { a: "South Asian women (Hindu)",
               b: "Inuit/Arctic communities",
               c: "Malay men (Southeast Asia)",
               d: "West African communities" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Pibloktoq ('Arctic hysteria') in Inuit communities. Compare: Dhat (South Asia, semen-loss anxiety), Amok (Malay, sudden violence), Koro (Southeast Asia, genital retraction), Susto (Latin America, soul loss)." },
  { id: "p2_abn15", section: "abnormal", source: "GMCH", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "The hallmark difference between pyromania and intermittent explosive disorder (IED) is:",
    options: { a: "IED involves only verbal aggression, never physical",
               b: "Pyromania involves planned, deliberate fire-setting for gratification; IED involves impulsive aggression disproportionate to the trigger",
               c: "Pyromania is diagnosed only in adolescents; IED only in adults",
               d: "Both require a comorbid mood disorder" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Pyromania = deliberate, repeated fire-setting + fascination/tension-release with fire. IED = recurrent impulsive aggressive outbursts grossly out of proportion to provocation. Both are impulse-control disorders but the behaviour and motive differ." },

  // ---------- BIOPSYCH / NEUROPSYCH (13) ----------
  { id: "p2_bio01", section: "biopsych", source: "RML", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "A patient presents with non-fluent, effortful speech but intact comprehension after a left frontal stroke. The most likely lesion site is:",
    options: { a: "Wernicke's area (posterior superior temporal gyrus)",
               b: "Broca's area (inferior frontal gyrus, pars opercularis/triangularis)",
               c: "Arcuate fasciculus",
               d: "Angular gyrus" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Broca's aphasia = non-fluent, agrammatic, effortful speech with preserved comprehension. Wernicke's = fluent but meaningless. Conduction (arcuate) = poor repetition. Angular gyrus = alexia + agraphia." },
  { id: "p2_bio02", section: "biopsych", source: "NIMHANS", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "The patient H.M. (Henry Molaison) sustained dense anterograde amnesia after surgical removal of which structures?",
    options: { a: "Bilateral medial temporal lobes including hippocampus and amygdala",
               b: "Bilateral prefrontal cortex",
               c: "Left parietal lobe and angular gyrus",
               d: "Right occipital lobe and lingual gyrus" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Scoville & Milner 1957: bilateral MTL resection for intractable epilepsy → severe anterograde amnesia, partial retrograde, INTACT procedural memory and short-term store. Foundational case for hippocampal role in declarative memory." },
  { id: "p2_bio03", section: "biopsych", source: "IHBAS", year: "2023", pyq: true,
    q: "Phineas Gage's personality change after his accident is most classically attributed to damage to which brain region?",
    options: { a: "Cerebellum",
               b: "Temporal lobe",
               c: "Ventromedial prefrontal cortex",
               d: "Occipital cortex" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Iron tamping rod through left ventromedial PFC. Disinhibition, impulsivity, poor decision-making, social judgement failure. Foundational case for PFC role in personality/executive function." },
  { id: "p2_bio04", section: "biopsych", source: "JEMAS", year: "2025", pyq: true,
    q: "Endel Tulving's patient K.C. is famous for selectively losing which form of memory while preserving others?",
    options: { a: "Procedural memory",
               b: "Semantic memory",
               c: "Episodic (autobiographical) memory",
               d: "Working memory" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "K.C. had severe episodic amnesia after a motorcycle accident but preserved semantic knowledge and procedural skills. Key case dissociating episodic from semantic memory within Tulving's taxonomy." },
  { id: "p2_bio05", section: "biopsych", source: "RML", year: "2025", pyq: true,
    q: "Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome is caused by deficiency of which vitamin?",
    options: { a: "Vitamin B6 (pyridoxine)",
               b: "Vitamin B12 (cobalamin)",
               c: "Vitamin B1 (thiamine)",
               d: "Vitamin D" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Thiamine (B1) deficiency, classically in chronic alcoholism. Wernicke acute phase: ophthalmoplegia + ataxia + confusion. Korsakoff chronic: anterograde amnesia + confabulation. Mammillary body atrophy on MRI." },
  { id: "p2_bio06", section: "biopsych", source: "AMITY", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "The basal ganglia primarily regulate:",
    options: { a: "Voluntary movement initiation, gating, and learning of motor sequences",
               b: "Visual processing and depth perception",
               c: "Long-term memory consolidation",
               d: "Cardiorespiratory control" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Basal ganglia (caudate, putamen, globus pallidus, STN, SN) gate motor cortical output and learn habitual motor sequences. Damage → Parkinson's (hypokinetic) or Huntington's (hyperkinetic, choreiform)." },
  { id: "p2_bio07", section: "biopsych", source: "NIMHANS", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "The reticular activating system (RAS) is primarily responsible for:",
    options: { a: "Arousal, wakefulness, and consciousness modulation",
               b: "Long-term episodic memory",
               c: "Voluntary motor planning",
               d: "Reading comprehension" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "RAS = network of neurons in the brainstem reticular formation projecting widely to cortex/thalamus. Regulates arousal, sleep-wake transitions, and selective attention. Damage → coma." },
  { id: "p2_bio08", section: "biopsych", source: "IHBAS", year: "2025", pyq: true,
    q: "Which of the following hormones is NOT secreted by the ANTERIOR pituitary?",
    options: { a: "Growth hormone (GH)",
               b: "Adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH)",
               c: "Oxytocin",
               d: "Prolactin" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Anterior pituitary (FLAT PiG): FSH, LH, ACTH, TSH, Prolactin, GH. Posterior pituitary (stored, made in hypothalamus): Oxytocin + ADH/vasopressin. Common PYQ trap." },
  { id: "p2_bio09", section: "biopsych", source: "NFSU", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "Which extrapyramidal side effect of typical antipsychotics typically appears LAST, often after years of treatment, and is the most likely to be irreversible?",
    options: { a: "Acute dystonia",
               b: "Akathisia",
               c: "Drug-induced parkinsonism",
               d: "Tardive dyskinesia" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "EPS timeline: dystonia (hours-days) → akathisia (days-weeks) → parkinsonism (weeks-months) → tardive dyskinesia (months-years, often irreversible). VMAT-2 inhibitors (valbenazine) are FDA-approved for TD." },
  { id: "p2_bio10", section: "biopsych", source: "JEMAS", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "The mesolimbic dopamine pathway, hyperactive in untreated schizophrenia, projects from the ventral tegmental area to:",
    options: { a: "Nucleus accumbens (ventral striatum) and limbic structures",
               b: "Dorsolateral prefrontal cortex",
               c: "Striatum (caudate, putamen)",
               d: "Tuberoinfundibular system" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Four DA pathways: MESOLIMBIC (VTA → NAcc, positive symptoms), MESOCORTICAL (VTA → PFC, negative/cognitive), NIGROSTRIATAL (SNc → striatum, EPS when blocked), TUBEROINFUNDIBULAR (hypothalamus → pituitary, prolactin elevation when blocked)." },
  { id: "p2_bio11", section: "biopsych", source: "RML", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "The primary inhibitory neurotransmitter of the mammalian central nervous system is:",
    options: { a: "Glutamate",
               b: "GABA",
               c: "Dopamine",
               d: "Acetylcholine" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "GABA is the main inhibitory NT in the CNS (binds GABA-A → Cl⁻ influx → hyperpolarisation). Glycine inhibits in the spinal cord/brainstem. Glutamate is the primary EXCITATORY NT (NMDA, AMPA, kainate)." },
  { id: "p2_bio12", section: "biopsych", source: "NIMHANS", year: "2025", pyq: true,
    q: "Which hypothalamic nucleus is the master circadian pacemaker, receiving direct input from the retina?",
    options: { a: "Suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN)",
               b: "Paraventricular nucleus",
               c: "Ventromedial nucleus",
               d: "Mammillary bodies" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "SCN sits just above the optic chiasm, receives retinohypothalamic input, drives the ~24h cycle. PVN = stress + autonomic. VMN = satiety. Mammillary bodies = memory (damaged in Korsakoff)." },
  { id: "p2_bio13", section: "biopsych", source: "GMCH", year: "2025", pyq: true,
    q: "The cerebellum's primary functions include:",
    options: { a: "Initiation of voluntary movement and language production",
               b: "Coordination of movement, balance, posture, and motor learning",
               c: "Long-term declarative memory storage",
               d: "Regulation of emotion via the amygdala" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Cerebellum: smooth coordination, equilibrium, fine-tuning of movements, motor learning (rapid adaptation). Recent work also links cerebellum to cognitive timing and language fluency, but the classical PYQ answer is motor coordination + balance." },

  // ---------- DEVELOPMENTAL (12) ----------
  { id: "p2_dev01", section: "dev", source: "RML", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "According to Erikson, the central psychosocial crisis of adolescence (12-18 years) is:",
    options: { a: "Industry vs Inferiority",
               b: "Identity vs Role Confusion",
               c: "Intimacy vs Isolation",
               d: "Generativity vs Stagnation" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Stage 5: Identity vs Role Confusion. Virtue = Fidelity. Adolescents explore roles, values, and commitments. Resolution → coherent sense of self." },
  { id: "p2_dev02", section: "dev", source: "JEMAS", year: "2023", pyq: true,
    q: "Erikson's stage of late adulthood (65+) involves the psychosocial conflict of:",
    options: { a: "Trust vs Mistrust",
               b: "Generativity vs Stagnation",
               c: "Intimacy vs Isolation",
               d: "Integrity vs Despair" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Stage 8: Integrity vs Despair, virtue = Wisdom. Life review: meaning + acceptance (integrity) vs regret + bitterness (despair). Asked in NIMHANS, JEMAS, RML in recent years." },
  { id: "p2_dev03", section: "dev", source: "IHBAS", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "In Piaget's theory, the concrete operational stage typically begins at:",
    options: { a: "0-2 years",
               b: "2-7 years",
               c: "7-11 years",
               d: "11 years onward" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Piaget's 4 stages: Sensorimotor (0-2), Preoperational (2-7), Concrete operational (7-11, can perform logical operations on concrete objects, master conservation), Formal operational (11+)." },
  { id: "p2_dev04", section: "dev", source: "NIMHANS", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "A child can pour water from a tall, thin glass into a short, wide glass and recognise that the AMOUNT of water has not changed. Piaget called this:",
    options: { a: "Object permanence",
               b: "Conservation",
               c: "Reversibility",
               d: "Egocentrism" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Conservation = quantity remains constant despite changes in appearance. Mastered in concrete operational stage. Object permanence (object exists when out of sight) is a sensorimotor milestone." },
  { id: "p2_dev05", section: "dev", source: "RML", year: "2025", pyq: true,
    q: "Vygotsky's 'zone of proximal development' refers to:",
    options: { a: "The age range during which a developmental milestone is typically reached",
               b: "The gap between what a child can do alone and what they can do with adult/peer scaffolding",
               c: "The period of accelerated brain growth between ages 0-2",
               d: "The cognitive distance between mental age and chronological age" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "ZPD = tasks a learner cannot yet do independently but can do with guided help. Scaffolding = adjusting support to the learner's evolving capability. Core of socio-cultural cognitive development." },
  { id: "p2_dev06", section: "dev", source: "GMCH", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "Bowlby's third phase of attachment behaviour (typically 7-24 months), characterised by clear preference for the primary caregiver and protest at separation, is called:",
    options: { a: "Pre-attachment",
               b: "Attachment in the making",
               c: "Clear-cut attachment",
               d: "Goal-corrected partnership" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Bowlby's 4 phases: pre-attachment (0-6 wks), attachment-in-the-making (6 wks-6/8 mo), CLEAR-CUT ATTACHMENT (6/8 mo-24 mo, separation protest peaks), goal-corrected partnership (24 mo+, child can negotiate)." },
  { id: "p2_dev07", section: "dev", source: "NFSU", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "In Ainsworth's Strange Situation, an infant who shows distress on separation but cannot be soothed on reunion, and oscillates between clinging and angry resistance, is classified as:",
    options: { a: "Secure",
               b: "Insecure-avoidant",
               c: "Insecure-resistant (ambivalent)",
               d: "Disorganised" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Strange Situation 4 patterns: Secure (B, ~65%), Avoidant (A, ignores reunion), Resistant/Ambivalent (C, distressed + unsoothable + angry on reunion), Disorganised (D, contradictory/freezing behaviours, Main & Solomon 1986)." },
  { id: "p2_dev08", section: "dev", source: "JEMAS", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "Marcia's identity status characterised by commitment WITHOUT prior exploration is:",
    options: { a: "Identity diffusion",
               b: "Identity moratorium",
               c: "Identity foreclosure",
               d: "Identity achievement" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Marcia 2x2 (exploration × commitment): Diffusion (no, no), Foreclosure (NO exploration, YES commitment — usually inherited from parents), Moratorium (yes exploration, no commitment yet), Achievement (yes, yes)." },
  { id: "p2_dev09", section: "dev", source: "IMHANS", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "Kohlberg's conventional level of moral reasoning is characterised by:",
    options: { a: "Obedience to avoid punishment",
               b: "Seeking concrete rewards in exchange for good behaviour",
               c: "Conformity to social rules and maintenance of social order ('good boy/girl' + 'law and order')",
               d: "Reasoning based on universal ethical principles" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Kohlberg's 3 levels × 2 stages: Pre-conventional (1: punishment, 2: instrumental exchange), Conventional (3: good boy/girl, 4: law & order), Post-conventional (5: social contract, 6: universal ethical principles)." },
  { id: "p2_dev10", section: "dev", source: "RML", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "In Bronfenbrenner's ecological systems theory, the system that includes interactions between two or more of the child's microsystems (e.g., home-school link) is called the:",
    options: { a: "Microsystem",
               b: "Mesosystem",
               c: "Exosystem",
               d: "Macrosystem" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Bronfenbrenner: micro (immediate, family/school/peers), MESO (connections between microsystems), exo (settings affecting child indirectly, e.g., parent's workplace), macro (cultural values, laws), chrono (time)." },
  { id: "p2_dev11", section: "dev", source: "NIMHANS", year: "2025", pyq: true,
    q: "Thomas and Chess identified which three primary temperament categories?",
    options: { a: "Sanguine, choleric, melancholic",
               b: "Easy, difficult, slow-to-warm-up",
               c: "Type A, Type B, Type C",
               d: "Sensitive, resilient, withdrawn" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "NYLS (Thomas & Chess): 9 temperament dimensions clustering into Easy (~40%), Difficult (~10%), Slow-to-warm-up (~15%). 'Goodness of fit' between child temperament and parental demand predicts outcome." },
  { id: "p2_dev12", section: "dev", source: "AMITY", year: "2023", pyq: true,
    q: "Harry Harlow's experiments with rhesus monkeys and surrogate mothers (wire vs cloth) primarily demonstrated:",
    options: { a: "Imprinting in primates",
               b: "The primacy of contact comfort over feeding in attachment formation",
               c: "Operant shaping of social behaviour",
               d: "Critical periods in language acquisition" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Harlow (1958): infant monkeys preferred the cloth 'mother' for contact comfort even when only the wire mother provided milk. Refuted the secondary-drive (cupboard-love) theory of attachment." },

  // ---------- BIOSTATS / RESEARCH (12) ----------
  { id: "p2_bst01", section: "biostats", source: "RML", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "A new screening test for depression has a sensitivity of 90% and specificity of 70%. This means the test correctly identifies:",
    options: { a: "90% of healthy people as healthy, 70% of depressed as depressed",
               b: "90% of depressed people as depressed, 70% of healthy as healthy",
               c: "90% of positive test results are true positives",
               d: "70% of negative test results are true negatives" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Sensitivity = P(test+|diseased) = correctly identifying the diseased. Specificity = P(test−|healthy) = correctly identifying the healthy. PPV/NPV (options c, d) depend on prevalence." },
  { id: "p2_bst02", section: "biostats", source: "JEMAS", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "Cohen's d is a measure of:",
    options: { a: "Statistical significance",
               b: "Sample size adequacy",
               c: "Standardised effect size for the difference between two means",
               d: "Correlation strength between two variables" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Cohen's d = (M1 − M2) / pooled SD. Conventions: 0.2 small, 0.5 medium, 0.8 large. Significance (p) tells you 'is it there'; effect size tells you 'how big is it'." },
  { id: "p2_bst03", section: "biostats", source: "IHBAS", year: "2025", pyq: true,
    q: "Which statistical test would you use to compare the means of THREE OR MORE independent groups assuming normality and homogeneity of variance?",
    options: { a: "Independent samples t-test",
               b: "One-way ANOVA",
               c: "Chi-square test",
               d: "Mann-Whitney U test" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "One-way ANOVA partitions variance into between- and within-group, F = MSbetween/MSwithin. Non-parametric equivalent for 3+ independent groups = Kruskal-Wallis. Repeated measures version = repeated-measures ANOVA." },
  { id: "p2_bst04", section: "biostats", source: "NIMHANS", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "Cronbach's alpha is a measure of:",
    options: { a: "Inter-rater reliability",
               b: "Internal consistency of a multi-item scale",
               c: "Test-retest reliability",
               d: "Criterion validity" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "α estimates internal consistency (do items measure the same construct?). Conventionally ≥0.70 acceptable. Cohen's κ = inter-rater. Pearson r over time = test-retest." },
  { id: "p2_bst05", section: "biostats", source: "RML", year: "2025", pyq: true,
    q: "If a 20-item questionnaire and an established gold-standard depression scale correlate at r = 0.85, this provides evidence of:",
    options: { a: "Internal consistency",
               b: "Test-retest reliability",
               c: "Concurrent (criterion-related) validity",
               d: "Face validity" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Validity types: Content (expert review), Construct (factor structure, convergent/discriminant), Criterion (concurrent: vs gold-standard at same time; predictive: outcome later). High correlation with established measure = concurrent validity." },
  { id: "p2_bst06", section: "biostats", source: "NFSU", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "Repeated-measures ANOVA differs from one-way ANOVA in that it:",
    options: { a: "Does not require the assumption of normality",
               b: "Accounts for within-subject correlation across repeated assessments on the same participants",
               c: "Can only compare two conditions",
               d: "Is non-parametric" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Repeated-measures ANOVA handles correlated observations from the same subjects across time/conditions (e.g., pre-mid-post intervention). Sphericity assumption replaces equal variances; correct with Greenhouse-Geisser if violated." },
  { id: "p2_bst07", section: "biostats", source: "AMITY", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "A Cohen's kappa of 0.85 between two clinicians rating the same set of patients indicates:",
    options: { a: "Poor agreement",
               b: "Fair agreement",
               c: "Moderate agreement",
               d: "Almost perfect agreement" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Landis & Koch benchmarks: <0 poor, 0-0.20 slight, 0.21-0.40 fair, 0.41-0.60 moderate, 0.61-0.80 substantial, 0.81-1.00 almost perfect. κ adjusts raw agreement for chance." },
  { id: "p2_bst08", section: "biostats", source: "GMCH", year: "2025", pyq: true,
    q: "A chi-square test of independence is used to:",
    options: { a: "Compare means of two groups",
               b: "Test the relationship between two categorical variables",
               c: "Test the difference between paired observations",
               d: "Measure correlation between two continuous variables" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "χ² of independence: contingency table, are two categorical variables associated? Compare observed vs expected frequencies. Continuous comparisons use t-tests/ANOVA; continuous correlation uses Pearson r." },
  { id: "p2_bst09", section: "biostats", source: "IMHANS", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "If a trial shows the Number Needed to Treat (NNT) for a new antidepressant is 8, this means:",
    options: { a: "8 patients must drop out before the trial is considered failed",
               b: "On average, you need to treat 8 patients with the new drug (vs control) for 1 additional patient to benefit",
               c: "The drug works in 8% of patients",
               d: "8 is the standard threshold for FDA approval" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "NNT = 1 / Absolute Risk Reduction. Lower NNT = more efficient treatment. Most depression trials yield NNTs of 5-10. NNT is the bedside-useful counterpart to relative risk." },
  { id: "p2_bst10", section: "biostats", source: "JEMAS", year: "2023", pyq: true,
    q: "The standard error of the mean is BEST described as:",
    options: { a: "The standard deviation of the sample",
               b: "The standard deviation of the sampling distribution of the mean",
               c: "The difference between the sample mean and population mean",
               d: "The total variability in the population" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "SEM = σ / √n. As n increases, SEM shrinks → estimates of the mean become more precise. Distinct from SD (variability of individual data points)." },
  { id: "p2_bst11", section: "biostats", source: "RML", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "A Receiver Operating Characteristic (ROC) curve plots:",
    options: { a: "Sensitivity (y) against 1 − specificity (x) across all possible cut-off scores",
               b: "Type I error against Type II error",
               c: "Predictor values against outcome values",
               d: "Means against standard deviations" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "ROC trades off TPR (sensitivity) vs FPR (1 − specificity). Area under the curve (AUC): 0.5 = chance, 1.0 = perfect discrimination. Used to select optimal cut-offs on screening instruments." },
  { id: "p2_bst12", section: "biostats", source: "NIMHANS", year: "2023", pyq: true,
    q: "In a randomised controlled trial, allocation concealment refers to:",
    options: { a: "Blinding participants to their assigned treatment after randomisation",
               b: "Hiding the upcoming allocation sequence from the recruiter until after the participant is enrolled",
               c: "Concealing the trial's primary outcome from the statistician",
               d: "Preventing investigators from knowing the study hypothesis" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Allocation concealment ≠ blinding. Concealment happens at enrolment (recruiter cannot foresee what arm the next participant will land in), preventing selection bias. Blinding happens after assignment." },

  // ---------- ASSESSMENT (10) ----------
  { id: "p2_ass01", section: "assessment", source: "RML", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "The WAIS-IV yields how many primary composite index scores in addition to the Full-Scale IQ?",
    options: { a: "Two (Verbal IQ and Performance IQ)",
               b: "Three (VCI, PRI, WMI)",
               c: "Four (VCI, PRI, WMI, PSI)",
               d: "Five (VCI, PRI, WMI, PSI, GAI)" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "WAIS-IV restructured WAIS-III into 4 indices: Verbal Comprehension (VCI), Perceptual Reasoning (PRI), Working Memory (WMI), Processing Speed (PSI). GAI = optional summary of VCI+PRI." },
  { id: "p2_ass02", section: "assessment", source: "JEMAS", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "The age range for the WISC-V is:",
    options: { a: "2:6 to 7:7 years",
               b: "6:0 to 16:11 years",
               c: "16:0 to 90:11 years",
               d: "0 to 42 months" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "WISC-V: 6:0-16:11 years. WPPSI-IV: 2:6-7:7. WAIS-IV: 16:0-90:11. Bayley-III/IV: 1-42 months. Standard exam-trap question." },
  { id: "p2_ass03", section: "assessment", source: "IHBAS", year: "2023", pyq: true,
    q: "Which of the following are the THREE main validity scales on the MMPI-2?",
    options: { a: "L (Lie), F (Infrequency), K (Defensiveness/Correction)",
               b: "L (Lie), Hs (Hypochondriasis), D (Depression)",
               c: "F (Infrequency), Pa (Paranoia), Sc (Schizophrenia)",
               d: "VRIN, TRIN, S" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Classic 3 validity scales: L (faking good, naive), F (faking bad, infrequent endorsements), K (defensiveness, subtle faking good). MMPI-2 also added VRIN, TRIN, F-back, S — but the classical PYQ answer is L, F, K." },
  { id: "p2_ass04", section: "assessment", source: "NIMHANS", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "Which of the following is NOT a recognised Rorschach scoring system?",
    options: { a: "Beck system",
               b: "Klopfer system",
               c: "Exner Comprehensive System",
               d: "Murray system" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Rorschach scoring systems: Beck, Klopfer, Hertz, Rapaport-Schafer, Piotrowski, Exner (Comprehensive, now dominant). Henry Murray developed the TAT, NOT a Rorschach system. Classic distractor." },
  { id: "p2_ass05", section: "assessment", source: "RML", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "Murray's TAT (Thematic Apperception Test) is rooted in his theory of:",
    options: { a: "Personal constructs",
               b: "Psychogenic needs and presses (need-press theory)",
               c: "Operant conditioning",
               d: "Cognitive distortions" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Murray's personology: 20 psychogenic NEEDS (achievement, affiliation, abasement, etc.) elicited by environmental PRESSES. TAT cards pull projective stories revealing dominant need-press patterns." },
  { id: "p2_ass06", section: "assessment", source: "NFSU", year: "2025", pyq: true,
    q: "The 16PF (Sixteen Personality Factor Questionnaire) was developed by:",
    options: { a: "Raymond B. Cattell",
               b: "Hans Eysenck",
               c: "Paul Costa and Robert McCrae",
               d: "Henry Murray" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Cattell factor-analysed lexical descriptors → 16 primary trait factors. Eysenck's PEN had 3 super-factors. Costa & McCrae's NEO-PI-R has 5 (OCEAN/Big Five). Murray developed the TAT." },
  { id: "p2_ass07", section: "assessment", source: "JEMAS", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "The five factors of the NEO-PI-R / Big Five model are:",
    options: { a: "Sanguine, Choleric, Melancholic, Phlegmatic, Mixed",
               b: "Openness, Conscientiousness, Extraversion, Agreeableness, Neuroticism",
               c: "Introversion, Extraversion, Sensation, Thinking, Judging",
               d: "Achievement, Affiliation, Power, Intimacy, Autonomy" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "OCEAN: Openness, Conscientiousness, Extraversion, Agreeableness, Neuroticism. Costa & McCrae's hierarchical model: 5 domains × 6 facets each = 30 facets. (c) = Jungian/MBTI; (d) = motivation theory." },
  { id: "p2_ass08", section: "assessment", source: "IMHANS", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "The Bhatia Battery of Performance Tests of Intelligence is suitable primarily for which population?",
    options: { a: "English-speaking urban adults",
               b: "Illiterate or rural Indian adults (and adolescents) where verbal IQ tests are not applicable",
               c: "Pre-school children only",
               d: "Patients with cortical blindness" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Bhatia (1955): 5 performance subtests (Koh's Block, Pass-along, Pattern Drawing, Immediate Memory, Picture Construction) designed for non-readers and illiterate adults in the Indian context. Bypasses verbal/cultural bias." },
  { id: "p2_ass09", section: "assessment", source: "AMITY", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "The Vineland Social Maturity Scale, used to assess social/adaptive behaviour from infancy to adulthood, was developed by:",
    options: { a: "Lewis Terman",
               b: "Edgar Doll",
               c: "David Wechsler",
               d: "Alfred Binet" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Edgar Doll developed the original Vineland (1935). Later updated to Vineland-II, Vineland-3 by Sparrow & Cicchetti. Assesses 4 domains: Communication, Daily Living, Socialisation, Motor." },
  { id: "p2_ass10", section: "assessment", source: "NIMHANS", year: "2025", pyq: true,
    q: "The key conceptual difference between the Stanford-Binet (Terman 1916) ratio IQ and the Wechsler deviation IQ is that:",
    options: { a: "Ratio IQ = (MA / CA) × 100; Deviation IQ uses standard score with mean 100, SD 15, comparing to age peers",
               b: "Ratio IQ is used for children, deviation IQ only for adults",
               c: "Ratio IQ has higher reliability than deviation IQ",
               d: "Deviation IQ uses raw scores without standardisation" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Ratio IQ (Stern 1912, popularised by Terman): mental age divided by chronological age × 100. Breaks down in adulthood (MA plateaus). Wechsler's deviation IQ (M=100, SD=15) compares to same-age norm and is the modern standard." },

  // ---------- SOCIAL (9) ----------
  { id: "p2_soc01", section: "social", source: "RML", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "Festinger's cognitive dissonance theory predicts that when our attitudes and behaviour conflict, we typically:",
    options: { a: "Change our behaviour to match the attitude",
               b: "Change our attitude to align with the behaviour, especially when behaviour is hard to undo",
               c: "Suppress awareness of the inconsistency permanently",
               d: "Experience no measurable psychological tension" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Festinger (1957): inconsistency between cognition and behaviour produces aversive arousal. Easiest reduction route is usually to change the cognition (attitude) to fit the already-performed behaviour. Demonstrated in the $1 vs $20 induced compliance study." },
  { id: "p2_soc02", section: "social", source: "JEMAS", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "Solomon Asch's line-judgement experiments demonstrated:",
    options: { a: "Obedience to authority figures",
               b: "Normative social influence and conformity to an incorrect majority",
               c: "Bystander apathy in emergencies",
               d: "Diffusion of responsibility in groups" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Asch (1951): ~37% of trials, participants conformed to a unanimous wrong majority on an obvious perceptual task. Demonstrated normative (and to a lesser extent informational) influence. Milgram = obedience; Darley & Latané = bystander/diffusion." },
  { id: "p2_soc03", section: "social", source: "IHBAS", year: "2025", pyq: true,
    q: "In Milgram's obedience studies, approximately what percentage of participants delivered the maximum 450-volt shock in the baseline condition?",
    options: { a: "10%",
               b: "35%",
               c: "65%",
               d: "100%" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Milgram (1963) baseline: ~65% went to the maximum 450 V. Obedience dropped sharply when proximity to the victim increased or the authority's prestige decreased. Demonstrated situational power over personality." },
  { id: "p2_soc04", section: "social", source: "NIMHANS", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "Muzafer Sherif's autokinetic-effect experiments demonstrated:",
    options: { a: "Informational social influence and the formation of group norms",
               b: "Pure obedience to authority",
               c: "The bystander effect",
               d: "Implicit bias in stereotype activation" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Sherif (1936): in a dark room, a stationary dot of light appears to move (autokinetic illusion). Group members converge on a shared estimate that persists when tested alone, showing informational influence (when reality is ambiguous, we look to others)." },
  { id: "p2_soc05", section: "social", source: "RML", year: "2025", pyq: true,
    q: "Fundamental Attribution Error refers to the tendency to:",
    options: { a: "Overestimate situational and underestimate dispositional causes for others' behaviour",
               b: "Overestimate dispositional and underestimate situational causes for OTHERS' behaviour",
               c: "Make self-serving attributions for one's own successes",
               d: "Believe the world is fundamentally fair" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "FAE / correspondence bias (Ross 1977): we explain others' behaviour by personality even when situational forces are strong. Inverted for our own behaviour (actor-observer effect)." },
  { id: "p2_soc06", section: "social", source: "AMITY", year: "2023", pyq: true,
    q: "The concept of the 'self-fulfilling prophecy' was formalised by which sociologist?",
    options: { a: "Erving Goffman",
               b: "Robert K. Merton",
               c: "Charles Horton Cooley",
               d: "Émile Durkheim" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Merton (1948) formalised self-fulfilling prophecy: a false definition of a situation evokes behaviour that makes the false conception come true. Famous example: bank-run rumour causing the bank to actually fail." },
  { id: "p2_soc07", section: "social", source: "NFSU", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "Henri Tajfel's Social Identity Theory proposes that group-based behaviour is driven primarily by:",
    options: { a: "Realistic competition for scarce resources",
               b: "The need to maintain a positive social identity through favourable intergroup comparison",
               c: "Genetic kin selection",
               d: "Operant reinforcement of in-group behaviours" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Tajfel & Turner (1979): even minimal groups produce in-group favouritism because self-esteem depends on positive group identity. Distinct from Sherif's Realistic Conflict Theory (resource competition)." },
  { id: "p2_soc08", section: "social", source: "JEMAS", year: "2023", pyq: true,
    q: "Zimbardo's Stanford Prison Experiment (1971) is most often cited as a demonstration of:",
    options: { a: "The power of social roles and situational forces to shape behaviour",
               b: "The genetic basis of aggression",
               c: "Classical conditioning of fear",
               d: "Reinforcement schedules in operant learning" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Zimbardo: ordinary college students randomly assigned to guard/prisoner roles rapidly internalised the roles, with abusive behaviour from guards. Critics now note experimenter influence, but it remains the canonical PYQ illustration of situational power." },
  { id: "p2_soc09", section: "social", source: "IMHANS", year: "2025", pyq: true,
    q: "Cooley's 'looking-glass self' proposes that self-concept is formed through:",
    options: { a: "Solitary introspection in early childhood",
               b: "Genetic inheritance of personality traits",
               c: "Imagining how we appear to others, imagining their judgement, and developing self-feelings from those imagined judgements",
               d: "Repeated operant reinforcement of self-statements" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Cooley (1902) three-step process: (1) we imagine how we appear to others, (2) we imagine their evaluation of that appearance, (3) we develop self-feelings (pride/shame) based on the imagined evaluation. Foundational symbolic-interactionist concept." },

  // ---------- GENERAL / COGNITIVE (8) ----------
  { id: "p2_gen01", section: "general", source: "RML", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "Which component was ADDED to Baddeley's working memory model in 2000?",
    options: { a: "Phonological loop",
               b: "Visuospatial sketchpad",
               c: "Central executive",
               d: "Episodic buffer" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Original 1974 model: central executive + phonological loop + visuospatial sketchpad. Baddeley (2000) added the EPISODIC BUFFER — limited-capacity multimodal store binding information into coherent episodes. Common PYQ trap." },
  { id: "p2_gen02", section: "general", source: "NIMHANS", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "In Atkinson and Shiffrin's multi-store memory model, which store has the LARGEST capacity?",
    options: { a: "Sensory register (iconic + echoic)",
               b: "Short-term store",
               c: "Long-term store",
               d: "All three are equal" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Atkinson-Shiffrin (1968): sensory register (very brief, large but rapidly decaying), STS (~7±2 items, ~30s without rehearsal), LTS (essentially UNLIMITED capacity and duration). Sperling's iconic memory ~ 250 ms." },
  { id: "p2_gen03", section: "general", source: "JEMAS", year: "2025", pyq: true,
    q: "Sternberg's triarchic theory of intelligence comprises:",
    options: { a: "Verbal, performance, working memory",
               b: "Analytical, creative, practical",
               c: "Crystallised, fluid, processing speed",
               d: "Linguistic, logical-mathematical, spatial" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Sternberg (1985): analytical (academic problem-solving), creative (novel problems), practical (street smarts, tacit knowledge). (c) = Cattell-Horn-Carroll. (d) = Gardner's multiple intelligences." },
  { id: "p2_gen04", section: "general", source: "RML", year: "2025", pyq: true,
    q: "Spearman's 'g' refers to:",
    options: { a: "Specific abilities unique to each cognitive task",
               b: "A general factor underlying performance across diverse cognitive tasks",
               c: "Genetic predisposition to mental disorders",
               d: "Group factors of intelligence" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Spearman (1904) factor-analysed school test scores: a positive manifold suggested a GENERAL factor 'g' loading on all tasks + task-SPECIFIC 's' factors. Foundation for the modern hierarchical models (CHC, gf-gc)." },
  { id: "p2_gen05", section: "general", source: "IHBAS", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "Signal Detection Theory introduces which decision parameter that classical psychophysical threshold theory ignored?",
    options: { a: "Just-noticeable-difference (JND)",
               b: "Weber's constant (k)",
               c: "Response criterion (β or c)",
               d: "Absolute threshold" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "SDT separates sensitivity (d', a property of the observer's discrimination) from response criterion β/c (the cut-off the observer adopts, biased by costs/rewards). Classical thresholds confounded these." },
  { id: "p2_gen06", section: "general", source: "AMITY", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "Wallas's four stages of the creative process are:",
    options: { a: "Preparation, Incubation, Illumination, Verification",
               b: "Problem-finding, Brainstorming, Selection, Implementation",
               c: "Sensitivity, Fluency, Flexibility, Originality",
               d: "Divergent, Convergent, Analytical, Practical" },
    correct: "a",
    explanation: "Graham Wallas (1926): Preparation (gather info), Incubation (unconscious processing), Illumination (insight, the 'Aha!' moment), Verification (test/refine). (c) = Guilford's creativity dimensions." },
  { id: "p2_gen07", section: "general", source: "NFSU", year: "2025", pyq: true,
    q: "The defense mechanism in which unacceptable wishes are unconsciously redirected into socially valued activities (e.g., aggressive drives channelled into competitive sport) is called:",
    options: { a: "Reaction formation",
               b: "Displacement",
               c: "Sublimation",
               d: "Projection" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "Sublimation = a MATURE defense (Vaillant) channelling impulses into productive ends. Reaction formation = expressing the OPPOSITE of the impulse. Displacement = redirecting the impulse to a safer target (not necessarily productive). Projection = attributing one's impulse to another." },
  { id: "p2_gen08", section: "general", source: "NIMHANS", year: "2023", pyq: true,
    q: "In Vaillant's hierarchical model of defense mechanisms, which level includes denial, distortion, and delusional projection?",
    options: { a: "Mature",
               b: "Neurotic",
               c: "Immature",
               d: "Psychotic" },
    correct: "d",
    explanation: "Vaillant 4 levels: Mature (sublimation, humour, altruism, suppression), Neurotic (repression, reaction formation, displacement, intellectualisation), Immature (projection, acting out, passive aggression, splitting), Psychotic (denial of external reality, distortion, delusional projection)." },

  // ---------- SWAPPED IN (English stripped, NIMHANS has none) (3) ----------
  { id: "p2_bio16", section: "biopsych", source: "IHBAS", year: "2024", pyq: true,
    q: "A lesion confined to the arcuate fasciculus, with intact comprehension and fluent speech, produces:",
    options: { a: "Broca's aphasia",
               b: "Wernicke's aphasia",
               c: "Conduction aphasia",
               d: "Global aphasia" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "The arcuate fasciculus connects Wernicke's and Broca's areas. A lesion gives conduction aphasia: fluent speech, intact comprehension, but impaired repetition with frequent phonemic paraphasias. Broca's = non-fluent; Wernicke's = poor comprehension; global = both." },
  { id: "p2_assess11", section: "assessment", source: "RML", year: "2025", pyq: true,
    q: "Cronbach's alpha is an index of:",
    options: { a: "Test-retest reliability",
               b: "Internal consistency reliability",
               c: "Criterion validity",
               d: "Inter-rater reliability" },
    correct: "b",
    explanation: "Cronbach's alpha estimates internal consistency, i.e. how well the items on a scale measure the same construct. Test-retest uses repeated administration; inter-rater agreement uses Cohen's kappa; criterion validity correlates the test with an external standard." },
  { id: "p2_bst13", section: "biostats", source: "JEMAS", year: "2023", pyq: true,
    q: "Which test compares the means of three or more independent groups?",
    options: { a: "Independent samples t-test",
               b: "Paired samples t-test",
               c: "One-way ANOVA",
               d: "Chi-square test" },
    correct: "c",
    explanation: "One-way ANOVA compares means across three or more independent groups. The t-test handles only two groups; the chi-square test is for categorical frequency data, not means." },
],
  "rml-2020": [
  { id: "rml20_001", section: "psychopathology",
    q: "Roshan often experiences intense feelings of anger and frustration. In order to cope with these feelings, he enrols in a kickboxing class as an outlet for his emotions. Roshan's actions are an example of which type of defence mechanism?",
    options: { a: "Sublimation", b: "Projection", c: "Displacement", d: "Repression" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: {
      b: "Projection is attributing one's own unacceptable impulses to others, which Roshan is not doing.",
      c: "Displacement redirects an impulse onto a safer target, but kickboxing is a socially valued outlet, making sublimation the better fit.",
      d: "Repression is unconsciously blocking feelings from awareness, whereas Roshan is consciously channelling them into activity." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is sublimation. Sigmund Freud, the founder of psychoanalysis, described defence mechanisms as unconscious strategies the ego uses to manage anxiety arising from unacceptable impulses. His daughter Anna Freud later systematised them. Sublimation is considered the most mature and adaptive defence: it channels socially unacceptable drives (here, aggression) into constructive, socially valued activities such as sport, art, or work. Roshan takes raw anger and directs it into kickboxing, a productive outlet, which is the textbook signature of sublimation. Displacement (option c) is close but differs in quality: displacement shifts an impulse onto a substitute target (yelling at a family member instead of the boss), and it is not inherently constructive, whereas sublimation transforms the impulse into something valued. Because the exam rewards recognising the adaptive, channelling nature of the act, sublimation is preferred. Understanding this distinction matters clinically because sublimation predicts healthier coping and better long-term adjustment, while primitive defences like denial or projection predict poorer functioning. This concept recurs throughout personality assessment and psychodynamic therapy." },
  { id: "rml20_002", section: "psychopathology",
    q: "An affective disorder in which a person swings from one mood extreme to another is classified as:",
    options: { a: "Manic", b: "Depressive", c: "Unipolar", d: "Bipolar" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: {
      a: "Mania is only one pole (elevated mood), not a swing between two extremes.",
      b: "Depression describes only the low pole, not the alternation between highs and lows.",
      c: "Unipolar disorder involves only one direction of mood disturbance, typically depression, not swinging between extremes." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is bipolar. Affective (mood) disorders are classified by the direction and range of mood disturbance. The term bipolar, formalised in modern nosology by figures such as Emil Kraepelin (who first separated manic-depressive illness from dementia praecox), describes a disorder in which mood swings between two poles: elevated or manic states and depressive states. The prefix bi-polar literally means two poles, which is exactly what the question describes: swinging from one extreme to another. Unipolar disorder, by contrast, involves disturbance in only one direction, almost always depression, so it cannot capture the alternation. Mania and depression each name a single pole rather than the pattern of alternation. In the DSM-5, Bipolar I requires at least one manic episode, while Bipolar II involves hypomania plus major depression. This classification matters because treatment differs sharply: bipolar disorder typically requires mood stabilisers such as lithium (studied extensively by John Cade), whereas unipolar depression is treated primarily with antidepressants, which can dangerously trigger mania if bipolarity is missed. Correct classification is therefore essential to safe clinical care." },
  { id: "rml20_003", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Which area of the brain is involved in emotion and motivation?",
    options: { a: "Limbic system", b: "Pituitary gland", c: "Cerebellum", d: "Corpus callosum" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: {
      b: "The pituitary gland is the master endocrine gland regulating hormones, not the seat of emotion and motivation.",
      c: "The cerebellum coordinates movement, balance, and motor learning, not emotion.",
      d: "The corpus callosum is the fibre bundle connecting the two hemispheres, enabling communication, not generating emotion." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is the limbic system. The limbic system is a group of interconnected structures, including the amygdala, hippocampus, hypothalamus, and cingulate gyrus, that together govern emotion, motivation, and memory. The term limbic system was popularised by Paul MacLean, who proposed the triune brain model and framed these structures as the emotional core of the brain. The amygdala, studied intensively by Joseph LeDoux, is central to fear and threat detection; the hypothalamus drives motivational states such as hunger, thirst, and sexual behaviour; and the hippocampus, famous from the case of patient H.M. studied by Brenda Milner, is critical for forming new memories. The distractors are all real brain structures with different jobs: the pituitary gland is the master endocrine gland, the cerebellum coordinates movement and balance, and the corpus callosum is the white-matter bridge between hemispheres. Understanding the limbic system matters because it links our emotional lives to memory and drive, and its dysfunction is implicated in anxiety disorders, depression, and PTSD. This makes it foundational knowledge for both biopsychology and clinical practice." },
  { id: "rml20_004", section: "social",
    q: "Who studied the effectiveness of social case work?",
    options: { a: "Fischer Joel", b: "Epstein Irwin", c: "Goyder John", d: "Cohen Jacob" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: {
      b: "Irwin Epstein contributed to social work research methodology and practice research, but is not known for the landmark effectiveness critique of casework.",
      c: "John Goyder is associated with survey research and response rates, not casework effectiveness.",
      d: "Jacob Cohen is the statistician behind effect size and Cohen's d, unrelated to social casework outcome studies." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Joel Fischer. Joel Fischer is best known in social work for his provocative 1973 review, 'Is Casework Effective?', which critically examined controlled studies of social casework and concluded that the evidence for its effectiveness was weak. This landmark analysis shook the profession and helped launch the empirical practice and evidence-based practice movements in social work, pushing practitioners to evaluate outcomes rather than assume benefit. The other names are distractors drawn from adjacent fields: Irwin Epstein is known for practice-based research and clinical data mining in social work but not this specific effectiveness critique; John Goyder is a sociologist associated with survey methodology and response rates; and Jacob Cohen is the statistician who gave us the concept of effect size and Cohen's d, a cornerstone of power analysis. For a clinical psychology and social work entrance, recognising Fischer matters because his work marks a turning point toward accountability, measurement, and demonstrating that interventions actually help clients. It connects historical social work to the modern insistence on outcome research and evidence-based methods." },
  { id: "rml20_005", section: "psychopathology",
    q: "Clare complains about her job duties at work, rarely completes assigned tasks, and is regularly late for work. When she is fired, she claims it was due to her co-worker badmouthing her instead of blaming it on her poor work performance. Which defense mechanism explains Clare's reaction?",
    options: { a: "Displacement", b: "Denial", c: "Rationalization", d: "Sublimation" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "Displacement would mean redirecting an emotion onto a substitute target, but Clare is inventing an acceptable reason, not shifting emotion.",
      b: "Denial refuses to acknowledge a reality entirely; Clare acknowledges she was fired but supplies a false cause.",
      d: "Sublimation channels impulses into productive activity, which is not what is happening here." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is rationalization. Rationalization, a defence mechanism described within Sigmund Freud's psychoanalytic framework and elaborated by Anna Freud, involves generating logical-sounding but false explanations to justify behaviour or outcomes that would otherwise threaten one's self-esteem. Clare's poor performance, lateness, and unfinished tasks are the real reasons for her firing, but she protects her ego by blaming a co-worker's alleged badmouthing. This substitutes a socially acceptable, self-flattering cause for the uncomfortable true one, which is the hallmark of rationalization. Denial (option b) differs because it refuses to accept the reality at all, whereas Clare accepts she was fired but distorts why. Displacement redirects an emotion onto a safer target, and sublimation channels impulses into constructive activity, neither of which fits. Recognising rationalization matters clinically because it is extremely common and often invisible to the person using it, allowing them to avoid accountability and repeat maladaptive patterns. Therapists learn to gently surface these self-serving explanations so clients can confront the genuine drivers of their difficulties and take responsibility for change." },
  { id: "rml20_006", section: "stats",
    q: "The problem statement is, 'Old age home residents who have heart disease die at an early age than the ones who do not have heart disease'. Which is the independent variable?",
    options: { a: "Dying at an early age", b: "Having heart disease", c: "Living in an old age home", d: "None of the above" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "Dying at an early age is the outcome being measured, which makes it the dependent variable, not the independent one.",
      c: "Living in an old age home is the shared setting or constant for all participants, not the variable being manipulated or compared.",
      d: "There is a clear independent variable in the statement, so 'none of the above' is incorrect." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is having heart disease. In research design, the independent variable is the presumed cause: the factor that is manipulated or that varies and is expected to influence an outcome. The dependent variable is the effect: the outcome that is measured. Ronald Fisher, the statistician who formalised experimental design, framed this cause-and-effect logic. Here the hypothesis predicts that heart disease leads to earlier death, so heart disease is the independent (predictor) variable and age at death is the dependent (outcome) variable. Living in an old age home is a constant that applies to everyone in the study, so it neither varies as a predictor nor serves as the outcome; it merely defines the sample. Option a describes the outcome, which is the dependent variable, a common trap because students confuse what is being explained with what does the explaining. Mastering this distinction is fundamental to reading and designing studies, interpreting statistics, and avoiding confounds. Every hypothesis test, correlation, and experiment rests on correctly identifying which variable is doing the influencing and which is being influenced, so this is core methodological literacy for a clinical psychology entrance." },
  { id: "rml20_007", section: "psychopathology",
    q: "Which theory of personality was developed by Sigmund Freud?",
    options: { a: "Psychoanalytic", b: "Behaviouristic", c: "Humanistic", d: "Psychosocial" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: {
      b: "The behaviouristic approach was developed by John Watson and B.F. Skinner, focusing on observable behaviour, not Freud.",
      c: "The humanistic theory belongs to Carl Rogers and Abraham Maslow, emphasising self-actualisation.",
      d: "The psychosocial theory of development was proposed by Erik Erikson, a neo-Freudian, not Freud himself." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is psychoanalytic. Sigmund Freud founded the psychoanalytic theory of personality, which holds that behaviour is driven largely by unconscious forces, early childhood experiences, and the interplay of three structures: the id (primitive drives operating on the pleasure principle), the ego (the reality-oriented mediator), and the superego (the moral conscience). Freud also proposed psychosexual stages of development and the mechanisms of repression and defence. The distractors are each associated with different founders: behaviourism was developed by John Watson and extended by B.F. Skinner, emphasising observable stimulus-response learning and rejecting the unconscious; humanistic psychology was led by Carl Rogers and Abraham Maslow, who stressed free will, self-actualisation, and the drive toward growth; and psychosocial theory was created by Erik Erikson, a neo-Freudian who expanded Freud's stages across the whole lifespan with eight crises. Knowing that Freud equals psychoanalytic is foundational because his ideas shaped a century of clinical practice, introduced the talking cure, and remain the basis for psychodynamic therapy. It also anchors students so they can correctly attribute the many competing personality theories they will encounter." },
  { id: "rml20_008", section: "social",
    q: "The qualities of successful rehabilitation programs to treat offenders include",
    options: { a: "Cognitive interviews", b: "Offender profiling", c: "Being community-based", d: "Focus on relationship with parents" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "Cognitive interviews are an investigative technique for improving witness recall, not a feature of offender rehabilitation programmes.",
      b: "Offender profiling is an investigative tool to identify likely suspects, unrelated to treating and rehabilitating offenders.",
      d: "Focusing on the relationship with parents is too narrow; effective programmes address broader risk and reintegration factors, not just parental ties." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is being community-based. Research on offender rehabilitation, notably the risk-need-responsivity model developed by Donald Andrews and James Bonta, shows that effective programmes are structured around principles that reduce reoffending and support reintegration. Community-based delivery is a well-supported feature because it keeps offenders connected to prosocial supports, employment, family, and services, easing the transition back into society and reducing the criminogenic effects of prolonged incarceration. The distractors come from unrelated forensic domains: cognitive interviews, developed by Ronald Fisher and Ronald Geiselman, are a technique for enhancing accurate recall from witnesses, not a treatment; offender profiling is an investigative method for narrowing suspect pools, again not rehabilitation; and focusing narrowly on the relationship with parents ignores the broader, evidence-based array of dynamic risk factors such as antisocial attitudes, peers, and substance use. Understanding what makes rehabilitation work matters because it shifts the goal from mere punishment to reducing recidivism, which protects the public and helps offenders build law-abiding lives. This is central to forensic and community psychology practice." },
  { id: "rml20_009", section: "psychopathology",
    q: "For which type of mental illness is there considerable evidence that it is caused by biological factors and little to no evidence that is caused by psychosocial factors?",
    options: { a: "Bipolar disorder", b: "Bulimia nervosa", c: "Schizophrenia", d: "(a) and (c)" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: {
      a: "Bipolar disorder is strongly biological, but choosing only (a) ignores that schizophrenia is equally strongly biological.",
      b: "Bulimia nervosa has substantial psychosocial and sociocultural contributors such as body image and dieting culture, so it does not fit.",
      c: "Schizophrenia is strongly biological, but selecting only (c) overlooks that bipolar disorder also qualifies." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is (a) and (c), bipolar disorder and schizophrenia. Both of these disorders show strong, replicated evidence of biological causation, including high heritability from twin and family studies, neurotransmitter dysregulation (dopamine in schizophrenia, as framed in the dopamine hypothesis associated with researchers like Arvid Carlsson), structural brain differences, and robust response to medication such as lithium for bipolar disorder and antipsychotics for schizophrenia. While stress and environment can trigger episodes, the core vulnerability is heavily genetic and neurobiological, captured by the diathesis-stress model. Bulimia nervosa (option b), by contrast, is shaped substantially by psychosocial and sociocultural forces such as internalised thin-ideal pressures, dieting, low self-esteem, and family dynamics, so it does not fit the description of little to no psychosocial contribution. The trap in options a and c alone is that each is individually correct but incomplete, and the combined option d captures both. Recognising the strong biological basis of these two disorders matters because it guides treatment toward pharmacology as a first-line component and reduces stigma by framing them as brain-based illnesses rather than character flaws." },
  { id: "rml20_010", section: "psychopathology",
    q: "Involuntary blocking unpleasant feelings and experiences from one's awareness is known as",
    options: { a: "Repression", b: "Rationalisation", c: "Sublimation", d: "Suppression" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: {
      b: "Rationalisation invents acceptable reasons for behaviour; it does not block feelings from awareness.",
      c: "Sublimation channels unacceptable impulses into productive activity rather than blocking them.",
      d: "Suppression is the conscious, deliberate pushing away of thoughts, whereas the question specifies an involuntary process." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is repression. Repression, one of the central defence mechanisms in Sigmund Freud's psychoanalytic theory, is the unconscious and involuntary exclusion of distressing thoughts, feelings, memories, or impulses from conscious awareness. The key word in the question is involuntary: repression happens automatically and outside of awareness, which distinguishes it from suppression (option d), the closely related but conscious and deliberate act of pushing an unwanted thought out of mind, for example choosing not to think about an exam until later. Freud considered repression the foundation of all other defences and the cornerstone of neurosis, arguing that repressed material does not disappear but continues to influence behaviour, dreams, and symptoms. Rationalisation generates false but acceptable justifications, and sublimation redirects impulses into valued activity, so neither matches the definition of blocking feelings from awareness. This distinction between repression (unconscious) and suppression (conscious) is a favourite exam point and is clinically important, because uncovering repressed material through free association and interpretation is a core aim of psychodynamic therapy, helping clients gain insight into hidden conflicts that drive their distress." },
  { id: "rml20_011", section: "assessment",
    q: "A likert scale is best described as",
    options: { a: "open ended items with unlimited responses", b: "closed ended items with a fixed number of alternative responses", c: "open and close ended with variable responses", d: "close ended items with dichotomous responses" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "Open-ended items with unlimited responses describe qualitative questions, the opposite of a structured Likert scale.",
      c: "A mix of open and closed items is not what defines a Likert scale, which is uniformly closed-ended and graded.",
      d: "Dichotomous items allow only two responses (yes/no); Likert scales offer a graded range, typically five or seven points." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is closed-ended items with a fixed number of alternative responses. The Likert scale was developed by Rensis Likert in 1932 as a method for measuring attitudes. It presents respondents with a statement and asks them to select from a fixed, ordered set of response options, classically five points ranging from strongly disagree to strongly agree, though seven-point versions are common. Because the response choices are predetermined and limited, Likert items are closed-ended, and because they offer a graded continuum rather than just two options, they capture the intensity or direction of an attitude. This distinguishes them from dichotomous items (option d), which allow only two responses such as yes or no and lose gradation. Open-ended items (option a) invite free text with no fixed options and are qualitative, the opposite of Likert's structured format. The Likert scale is one of the most widely used tools in psychology and social science because it is easy to administer, quantify, and analyse, and it yields ordinal (often treated as interval) data suitable for computing means and correlations. Mastery of its definition is essential for questionnaire design and psychometrics." },
  { id: "rml20_012", section: "cognitive",
    q: "Which memory store holds information for the shortest duration?",
    options: { a: "Sensory memory", b: "Short-term memory", c: "Explicit memory", d: "Implicit memory" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: {
      b: "Short-term memory lasts around 15 to 30 seconds, longer than the fraction of a second that sensory memory persists.",
      c: "Explicit memory is a type of long-term memory for facts and events, not a briefly held store.",
      d: "Implicit memory is a durable long-term memory system for skills and conditioning, not a short-duration store." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is sensory memory. In the modal or multi-store model of memory proposed by Richard Atkinson and Richard Shiffrin, information flows through three stores of increasing duration. Sensory memory holds a raw, high-capacity snapshot of incoming sensory input for the briefest time, roughly a quarter to half a second for visual (iconic) memory, as demonstrated by George Sperling, and a few seconds for auditory (echoic) memory. If attended to, information passes into short-term memory, which lasts about 15 to 30 seconds without rehearsal and has limited capacity (famously about seven items, per George Miller), and from there into long-term memory, which can last a lifetime. Explicit memory (conscious recall of facts and events) and implicit memory (nonconscious skills and conditioning) are both subdivisions of long-term memory, so they persist far longer than sensory memory. The key to this question is that sensory memory is the very first and shortest-lived stage, a fleeting buffer that allows the brain to briefly retain sensory impressions long enough to select what deserves further processing. This staged model underpins how we understand attention, encoding, and forgetting." },
  { id: "rml20_013", section: "systems",
    q: "To fulfil their fiduciary responsibilities agencies must:",
    options: { a: "Comply with social work licensing standards", b: "comply with regulatory standards of all organisations having oversight authority", c: "Be fiscally accountable to all funding sources", d: "Convene annual meetings with the client populations they serve (stakeholders)" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "Complying with licensing standards concerns professional practice quality, not the financial trust duty that fiduciary responsibility denotes.",
      b: "Complying with all regulatory standards is a broad legal and administrative duty, not specifically the fiscal stewardship that fiduciary means.",
      d: "Convening annual stakeholder meetings is a governance or participation practice, not the core financial accountability of a fiduciary duty." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is being fiscally accountable to all funding sources. A fiduciary responsibility is a duty of trust concerning the proper handling of money and resources on behalf of others. The word fiduciary derives from the Latin for trust, and in the context of human service agencies it specifically means the obligation to manage funds honestly, transparently, and in the interest of those who provided them. Agencies receive money from government grants, donors, and other funders, and their fiduciary duty is to be fiscally accountable to those sources, spending funds as intended and reporting accurately. The distractors describe genuine but different obligations: licensing standards (option a) govern professional competence and practice quality; complying with all regulatory oversight (option b) is a broad legal and administrative responsibility; and holding stakeholder meetings (option d) reflects participatory governance and accountability to clients. None of these is the financial trust duty that the term fiduciary denotes. Understanding this matters in social work administration and systems practice because mismanaging funds breaches the core trust that sustains an agency's legitimacy, funding, and ability to serve clients, and it can carry legal consequences." },
  { id: "rml20_014", section: "psychopathology",
    q: "Symptoms of major depressive disorder might include: i. specific phobias ii. losing interest or pleasure in things that you usually enjoy iii. changes in appetite iv. an excessively euphoric mood",
    options: { a: "i & ii", b: "iii & iv", c: "ii & iii", d: "i & iv" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "Specific phobias are anxiety disorder symptoms, not core features of major depression, so pairing i with ii is wrong.",
      b: "An excessively euphoric mood (iv) points toward mania, not depression, so this pairing is incorrect.",
      d: "This pairs a phobia and euphoria, neither of which is characteristic of major depressive disorder." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is ii and iii, losing interest or pleasure in usually enjoyable things and changes in appetite. Major depressive disorder, as defined in the DSM-5 (a diagnostic system whose lineage traces to Emil Kraepelin's early classification of mood disorders), is characterised by a cluster of symptoms lasting at least two weeks. Two of its hallmark criteria are anhedonia, the loss of interest or pleasure in activities one usually enjoys, and significant changes in appetite or weight, which may increase or decrease. Both appear in the statement, making ii and iii correct. The distractors describe features of other conditions: specific phobias (i) are anxiety disorder symptoms involving intense, irrational fear of a particular object or situation, not depression; and an excessively euphoric mood (iv) is a defining feature of a manic episode, which points toward bipolar disorder, the opposite pole from depression. Recognising the correct symptom profile matters diagnostically, because confusing euphoria with depression could lead a clinician to overlook bipolarity, and misattributing phobic anxiety to depression could misdirect treatment. Accurate symptom identification underpins correct diagnosis, prognosis, and choice of therapy or medication." },
  { id: "rml20_015", section: "assessment",
    q: "Leaning forward, saying umm, nodding, are examples of which one of the following interview techniques?",
    options: { a: "Paraphrasing", b: "Prompting", c: "Furthering", d: "Checking" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "Paraphrasing restates the client's content in the interviewer's own words, which involves speech, not minimal nonverbal cues.",
      b: "Prompting nudges the client toward a specific topic or answer, going beyond passive encouragement.",
      d: "Checking verifies that the interviewer has understood correctly, an active clarification rather than a minimal encourager." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is furthering. In interviewing and counselling skills, furthering responses are minimal verbal and nonverbal cues that encourage the client to keep talking without redirecting the content. Leaning forward, nodding, and saying umm or mm-hmm are classic minimal encouragers, sometimes called attending behaviours, discussed extensively in the microskills tradition associated with Allen Ivey. They signal attentiveness and interest, communicating keep going and I am with you, which deepens rapport and keeps the client's narrative flowing. The distractors are distinct techniques: paraphrasing (option a) restates the client's message in the interviewer's own words to show understanding; prompting (option b) actively nudges the client toward a particular area or elicits a specific response; and checking (option d) confirms the accuracy of the interviewer's understanding, often by asking is that right. Furthering is the least intrusive of these, precisely because it adds no new content and simply sustains the client's flow. Mastering furthering matters because effective interviews depend on drawing the client out, and clumsy over-talking or premature interpretation can shut a client down. Skilled use of minimal encouragers is foundational to clinical assessment and therapeutic listening." },
  { id: "rml20_016", section: "therapy",
    q: "All of the following are the characteristics of efficacy studies EXCEPT",
    options: { a: "Therapist actions are controlled by a protocol or treatment manual", b: "Therapist flexibility is encouraged", c: "The treatment is applied to a homogenous client population", d: "The research design relies on random selection and random assignment to a treatment or control condition" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "Manualised, protocol-controlled therapist behaviour is a defining feature of efficacy studies, so it is not the exception.",
      c: "Homogeneous client samples are typical of efficacy studies to maximise internal validity, so this is a genuine characteristic.",
      d: "Random selection and random assignment are core to the controlled design of efficacy studies, so this is not the exception." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is that therapist flexibility is encouraged, which is the EXCEPTION. Efficacy studies are tightly controlled experimental trials, typically randomised controlled trials, designed to test whether a treatment works under ideal, standardised conditions, maximising internal validity. To achieve this, they manualise treatment so therapist behaviour follows a fixed protocol (option a), recruit homogeneous samples with a single well-defined disorder to reduce confounding variability (option c), and use random selection and random assignment to treatment or control conditions to rule out bias (option d). Encouraging therapist flexibility (option b) directly contradicts this logic, because flexibility introduces uncontrolled variation and undermines the standardisation efficacy studies depend on. Flexibility is instead a feature of effectiveness studies, which test treatments in real-world clinical settings where clinicians adapt to complex, comorbid clients; this contrast between efficacy (does it work in controlled conditions) and effectiveness (does it work in practice) is central to psychotherapy research and evidence-based practice, a framework advanced by researchers such as Alan Kazdin. Understanding it helps clinicians judge whether trial findings will generalise to the messy realities of everyday care." },
  { id: "rml20_017", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Long term regulation of body weight and fat has been associated with which of the following hormone:",
    options: { a: "Hypothalamus", b: "Oestrogen", c: "Cortisol", d: "Leptin" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: {
      a: "The hypothalamus is a brain region, not a hormone, and although it regulates weight, the question asks for a hormone.",
      b: "Oestrogen is a sex hormone influencing reproduction and some fat distribution, but it is not the primary long-term weight regulator.",
      c: "Cortisol is a stress hormone that can affect appetite and fat storage, but it is not the hormone specifically tied to long-term weight and fat regulation." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is leptin. Leptin is a hormone secreted by adipose (fat) tissue, discovered in 1994 through the work of Jeffrey Friedman and colleagues. It functions as a long-term signal of the body's energy stores: as fat mass increases, leptin levels rise and act on the hypothalamus to suppress appetite and increase energy expenditure, whereas low fat stores lower leptin and stimulate hunger. This feedback loop makes leptin the central hormone in the long-term regulation of body weight and fat, which is exactly what the question asks. The distractors reveal a common trap: the hypothalamus (option a) is the brain structure that receives leptin's signal and regulates eating, but it is a brain region, not a hormone. Oestrogen (option b) is a sex hormone with some effect on fat distribution, and cortisol (option c) is a stress hormone that can influence appetite and abdominal fat, but neither is the dedicated long-term adiposity signal. Understanding leptin matters because leptin resistance is implicated in obesity, and the discovery of leptin transformed how scientists view weight as a biologically regulated set point rather than mere willpower." },
  { id: "rml20_018", section: "social",
    q: "The name of the first school of Social work in the country was",
    options: { a: "Sir Dorabji Tata Graduate School of Social Work", b: "Tata Institute of Social Profession", c: "Tata Institute of Social Service", d: "Tata Institute of Social Sciences" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: {
      b: "Tata Institute of Social Profession is not a real institution name; it is a distractor.",
      c: "Tata Institute of Social Service is a fabricated variant designed to confuse with the correct original name.",
      d: "Tata Institute of Social Sciences is the current name, but the school was originally founded under a different name in 1936." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is the Sir Dorabji Tata Graduate School of Social Work. The first school of social work in India was established in 1936 in Bombay (Mumbai) and was originally named the Sir Dorabji Tata Graduate School of Social Work, funded by the Sir Dorabji Tata Trust. Its founding is closely associated with the American social worker Clifford Manshardt, who served as its first director and championed professional social work education in India. In 1944 the institution was renamed the Tata Institute of Social Sciences (TISS), which is how it is known today and remains India's premier social work and social sciences institution. The trap in option d is that TISS is the correct present-day name, and students who know the modern institution may pick it, but the question specifically asks for the original name of the first school, which was the Sir Dorabji Tata Graduate School of Social Work. Options b and c are fabricated variants designed to sound plausible. This piece of history matters for social work and clinical psychology entrances in India because it marks the formal beginning of professional, university-based social work education in the country." },
  { id: "rml20_019", section: "social",
    q: "According to Social identity theory, from what do we derive a large part of our identity and feelings about ourselves?",
    options: { a: "Where we were born", b: "The amount of prejudice experienced", c: "The groups we belong to", d: "Competition between groups" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "Place of birth is a demographic fact, not the source of identity emphasised by social identity theory.",
      b: "Experienced prejudice can affect self-concept, but it is not the core mechanism the theory identifies for deriving identity.",
      d: "Intergroup competition is a consequence and driver of group behaviour within the theory, not the source from which identity is derived." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is the groups we belong to. Social identity theory was developed by Henri Tajfel and John Turner in the 1970s and 1980s to explain intergroup behaviour. Its central claim is that a significant part of our self-concept, our identity and self-esteem, comes from our membership in social groups (the in-groups) such as our nationality, religion, team, or profession. We categorise ourselves and others into groups, identify with our in-groups, and compare our groups favourably against out-groups to boost self-esteem, a process that helps explain in-group favouritism, prejudice, and discrimination. Option d, competition between groups, describes a consequence of this process rather than the source of identity itself; Tajfel's famous minimal group experiments showed that even arbitrary group assignments trigger favouritism, so competition follows from identification rather than being its origin. Place of birth (option a) is merely a demographic fact, and experienced prejudice (option b) influences but does not define the mechanism. Understanding social identity theory matters because it illuminates why people cling to group loyalties, how collective identity shapes self-worth, and the psychological roots of stereotyping and intergroup conflict, all central to social and clinical psychology." },
  { id: "rml20_020", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Alarm, resistance and exhaustion describe three stages represented in which model of stress?",
    options: { a: "Life events theory", b: "Protection motivation theory", c: "Selye's general adaptation syndrome", d: "Health belief model" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "Life events theory (Holmes and Rahe) measures cumulative stress from life changes, not a three-stage physiological response.",
      b: "Protection motivation theory explains how threat appraisals motivate protective health behaviour, not a stress response sequence.",
      d: "The health belief model predicts health behaviours from perceived susceptibility and benefits, not stages of physiological stress." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Selye's general adaptation syndrome. Hans Selye, an endocrinologist often called the father of stress research, proposed the general adaptation syndrome (GAS) to describe the body's nonspecific physiological response to prolonged stress, unfolding in three stages. First is the alarm stage, in which the body detects the stressor and mobilises the fight-or-flight response through the sympathetic nervous system and release of cortisol and adrenaline. Second is the resistance stage, in which the body adapts and attempts to cope while remaining physiologically aroused. Third, if the stressor persists, is the exhaustion stage, in which the body's resources are depleted, resistance collapses, and vulnerability to illness, burnout, and disease rises sharply. The distractors are all genuine models but describe different things: the life events theory of Thomas Holmes and Richard Rahe quantifies cumulative stress from life changes; protection motivation theory (Ronald Rogers) explains how threat appraisal drives protective behaviour; and the health belief model predicts health behaviours from perceived risk and benefit. Selye's GAS matters because it was the first systematic account linking chronic stress to physical breakdown, laying the foundation for psychosomatic medicine and health psychology." },
  { id: "rml20_021", section: "systems",
    q: "All of the following are characteristics of formed groups EXCEPT:",
    options: { a: "They have an identified purpose", b: "They have a professionally trained leader", c: "They come together spontaneously", d: "They usually have some auspice e.g. agency, organisation" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "Having an identified purpose is a hallmark of a formed group, so it is not the exception.",
      b: "A professionally trained leader is characteristic of formed groups convened by an agency, so this is a genuine feature.",
      d: "Operating under an auspice such as an agency or organisation is typical of formed groups, so it is not the exception." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is that they come together spontaneously, which is the EXCEPTION. In social group work, a key distinction is drawn between formed groups and natural groups. Formed groups are deliberately convened by an outside authority, usually an agency or organisation, to accomplish a defined purpose; they typically have a professionally trained leader, an identified goal, criteria for membership, and an organisational auspice or sponsor. Examples include a therapy group, a support group, or a task committee assembled by a clinic. Natural groups, by contrast, form spontaneously through the shared interests and mutual attraction of their members, such as friendship cliques, gangs, or peer groups, and they arise without external sponsorship or a designated professional leader. Because spontaneous formation is the defining feature of natural groups, option c does not belong to formed groups and is therefore the exception. This distinction, developed within the social group work tradition (associated with pioneers such as Gisela Konopka and Grace Coyle), matters because it shapes how a worker engages a group: formed groups can be structured around explicit goals from the outset, whereas natural groups require the worker to enter and work with pre-existing dynamics and loyalties." },
  { id: "rml20_022", section: "stats",
    q: "If a study is 'reliable' this means that:",
    options: { a: "It was conducted by a reputable researcher who can be trusted", b: "The measures devised for concepts are stable on different occasions", c: "The findings can be generalised to other social settings", d: "The methods are stated clearly enough for the researcher to be replicated" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "Reliability is a property of measurement consistency, not the trustworthiness or reputation of the researcher.",
      c: "Generalisability to other settings describes external validity, not reliability.",
      d: "Clearly stated methods allow replication, which is related but describes replicability, not reliability itself." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is that the measures devised for concepts are stable on different occasions. In research methodology, reliability refers to the consistency or stability of a measurement: a reliable measure produces the same results when applied repeatedly under the same conditions. If a scale, test, or instrument yields stable scores across different occasions (test-retest reliability), across items (internal consistency, indexed by Cronbach's alpha, developed by Lee Cronbach), or across raters (inter-rater reliability), it is considered reliable. The key idea is consistency. The distractors confuse reliability with related but distinct concepts: option a mistakes it for the credibility of the researcher, which is irrelevant to measurement consistency; option c describes external validity or generalisability, whether findings apply to other populations and settings; and option d describes replicability, the clarity of methods that allows others to repeat the study. A crucial point is that reliability is necessary but not sufficient for validity: a measure can be perfectly consistent yet consistently wrong. Understanding reliability is fundamental to psychometrics and clinical assessment, because a test that gives inconsistent results cannot be trusted to measure anything meaningfully, whether it is IQ, depression severity, or personality." },
  { id: "rml20_023", section: "development",
    q: "According to Freud, fixation at which stage of development is associated with sarcasm, criticalness, overeating, and nail-biting in adults?",
    options: { a: "Oral", b: "Anal", c: "Phallic", d: "Latency" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: {
      b: "Anal fixation is linked to orderliness, stubbornness, and control (anal-retentive) or messiness (anal-expulsive), not oral traits.",
      c: "Phallic fixation relates to the Oedipus complex and issues of vanity or sexuality, not oral habits.",
      d: "Latency is a period of dormant sexual interest with no fixation traits of this kind." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is the oral stage. Sigmund Freud's theory of psychosexual development proposes that children pass through stages defined by the erogenous zone that is the focus of libidinal energy, and that excessive gratification or frustration at any stage causes fixation, leaving a lasting mark on adult personality. The oral stage (birth to about 18 months) centres on the mouth, with pleasure derived from sucking, biting, and feeding. Fixation here can arise from problems with weaning and produces two adult character types: the oral-receptive personality (dependent, gullible, prone to overeating, smoking, or nail-biting, seeking oral gratification) and the oral-aggressive personality (sarcastic, verbally hostile, critical, biting in speech). The habits and traits listed, sarcasm, criticalness, overeating, and nail-biting, all map onto oral fixation. The distractors correspond to other stages: anal fixation (option b) yields orderliness or messiness and issues of control; phallic fixation (option c) involves the Oedipus complex, vanity, and sexuality; and latency (option d) is a quiet period without such fixation traits. Although Freud's stage theory is empirically contested, it remains a staple of exams and psychodynamic thought, illustrating his central claim that early experience shapes adult character." },
  { id: "rml20_024", section: "therapy",
    q: "When crisis results in bereavement, all of the following approaches to bereavement should be considered EXCEPT:",
    options: { a: "Bereavement is a normal process that will run its course", b: "Complicated grieving requires intervention", c: "Bereavement counselling, when needed, is best conducted one-by-one", d: "Crisis related bereavement should always be considered traumatic grief" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: {
      a: "Recognising bereavement as a normal process that runs its course is a sound principle, so it is not the exception.",
      b: "Complicated grief genuinely requires intervention, so this is a valid approach, not the exception.",
      c: "One-to-one counselling is a legitimate mode when bereavement counselling is needed, so this is not the exception." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is that crisis-related bereavement should always be considered traumatic grief, which is the EXCEPTION because the word always makes it false. Sound bereavement practice recognises that grief is usually a normal, self-limiting process that most people work through with natural support (option a), that only some cases develop into complicated or prolonged grief requiring professional intervention (option b), and that bereavement counselling, when indicated, can appropriately be conducted individually (option c). The error in option d is the absolute claim always: not all crisis-related bereavement is traumatic grief. Traumatic grief is a specific, more severe form that arises under particular circumstances such as sudden, violent, or unexpected loss, and treating every bereavement as traumatic would pathologise normal grieving and risk over-intervention. Elisabeth Kubler-Ross popularised stage models of grief, and later theorists such as J. William Worden reframed mourning as active tasks rather than fixed stages, while researchers on complicated grief such as Holly Prigerson distinguished normal from disordered grief. The clinical lesson is to match support to need: normalise ordinary grief, reserve intensive intervention for complicated or traumatic presentations, and avoid blanket assumptions that all loss is traumatic." },
  { id: "rml20_025", section: "therapy",
    q: "A working relationship has all of the following characteristics EXCEPT:",
    options: { a: "Observable ability of the worker and the client to work together", b: "A committed belief in the helping relationship", c: "A psychodynamic theory base", d: "A contract to work together realistically and collaboratively" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "The observable ability of worker and client to work together is a genuine feature of a working relationship, so it is not the exception.",
      b: "A committed belief in the helping relationship is a core element, so this is not the exception.",
      d: "A realistic, collaborative contract to work together is central to a working relationship, so it is not the exception." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is a psychodynamic theory base, which is the EXCEPTION. A working relationship, closely related to the concept of the working alliance or therapeutic alliance described by Edward Bordin, is the collaborative bond between helper and client that makes change possible. Bordin identified its core components as agreement on goals, agreement on the tasks needed to reach them, and the personal bond of trust between the two parties. Consistent with this, a genuine working relationship features the demonstrable, observable ability of worker and client to cooperate (option a), a shared, committed belief in the value of the helping relationship (option b), and a realistic, collaborative contract or agreement about how they will work together (option d). What it does not require is any single theoretical orientation such as a psychodynamic theory base (option c); a productive working relationship can form within cognitive-behavioural, humanistic, solution-focused, or any other framework, so binding it to psychodynamic theory is incorrect. This matters because decades of psychotherapy research, including Bordin's work and the common factors literature, show that the quality of the working alliance is one of the strongest predictors of therapeutic outcome across all orientations, more so than the specific technique used." },
  { id: "rml20_026", section: "psychopathology",
    q: "Sheldon's theory that people with different body types have different personalities has been:",
    options: { a: "supported by research", b: "discredited", c: "shown to be accurate for thin people but not overweight people", d: "shown to be accurate for women but not for men" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "Sheldon's somatotype theory has not been supported; correlational flaws undermined his claims, so this is wrong.",
      c: "There is no valid evidence that the theory holds for thin but not overweight people; this is a fabricated qualification.",
      d: "There is no credible evidence of a sex-specific validity; this distractor invents a pattern the research never established." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is discredited. William Sheldon proposed a theory of somatotypes in the 1940s, classifying people into three body types and linking each to a temperament: endomorphs (soft, round) were said to be sociable and relaxed (viscerotonic), mesomorphs (muscular, athletic) to be assertive and energetic (somatotonic), and ectomorphs (thin, fragile) to be introverted and intellectual (cerebrotonic). Although the idea was popular for a time, it has since been thoroughly discredited. The correlations Sheldon reported were inflated by methodological flaws, including the fact that he himself rated both body type and personality, introducing rater bias, and the theory lacked any credible causal mechanism. It also carried troubling associations with the pseudoscience of constitutional psychology and even eugenics. The distractors invent qualifications (accurate for thin but not overweight people, or for women but not men) that have no basis in evidence; the theory failed broadly, not selectively. This question matters because it teaches critical evaluation: appealing, intuitive theories that link appearance to character must be tested rigorously, and Sheldon's somatotypes stand as a classic example of a once-popular idea rejected on scientific grounds." },
  { id: "rml20_027", section: "assessment",
    q: "The MMPI is used to measure:",
    options: { a: "unconscious drives", b: "the Big Five traits", c: "personality and psychological disorders", d: "leadership potential" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "Unconscious drives are assessed by projective tests like the Rorschach, not the objective, self-report MMPI.",
      b: "The Big Five are measured by instruments such as the NEO-PI, not the MMPI, which predates and differs from that model.",
      d: "Leadership potential is assessed by other occupational tools, not the clinically oriented MMPI." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is personality and psychological disorders. The Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory (MMPI) was developed in the late 1930s and published in 1943 by Starke Hathaway and J.C. McKinley at the University of Minnesota. It is the most widely used objective personality inventory in clinical settings, consisting of hundreds of true-false self-report items that yield scores on clinical scales measuring psychopathology such as depression, paranoia, schizophrenia, hysteria, and psychopathic deviate, along with validity scales that detect faking, defensiveness, or careless responding. Its purpose is to assess personality structure and identify psychological disorders, aiding diagnosis and treatment planning. The distractors point to other instruments and constructs: unconscious drives (option a) are the target of projective tests like the Rorschach inkblot test (Hermann Rorschach) and the Thematic Apperception Test (Henry Murray and Christiana Morgan), not the objective MMPI; the Big Five traits (option b) are measured by inventories such as the NEO-PI-R (Paul Costa and Robert McCrae); and leadership potential (option d) is assessed by occupational and organisational measures. Being empirically keyed rather than theory-driven, the MMPI is a cornerstone of clinical assessment, which is why identifying its purpose correctly is essential." },
  { id: "rml20_028", section: "systems",
    q: "The task or role performance model of case management is based on all of the following premises EXCEPT:",
    options: { a: "It is consistent with structure-function theory applied at the organisational level", b: "Workers are expected to use their authority to provide, deny, or withdraw services based on a client's compliance with assigned tasks", c: "Workers are expected to form a power alliance with the client", d: "Case managers have both a fiduciary and a humanitarian obligation to clients" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "Consistency with structure-function theory at the organisational level is a genuine premise of this model, so it is not the exception.",
      b: "Using authority to provide, deny, or withdraw services based on task compliance fits the task or role performance model, so it is not the exception.",
      d: "Having both fiduciary and humanitarian obligations to clients is a real premise of the model, so it is not the exception." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is that workers are expected to form a power alliance with the client, which is the EXCEPTION. The task or role performance model of case management is grounded in structure-function theory applied at the organisational level (option a), meaning it views the worker's role in terms of formal functions within an organisational structure. Within this model the worker legitimately uses professional authority to provide, deny, or withdraw services contingent on the client's compliance with agreed tasks (option b), and the case manager carries both a fiduciary obligation, the duty to manage resources and trust responsibly, and a humanitarian obligation to the client's welfare (option d). What does not belong is the notion of forming a power alliance with the client (option c). A power alliance suggests joining with the client to exert power, which is inconsistent with a model built on the worker's structured, functional authority and accountability to the organisation. Instead, the appropriate relationship is a working or therapeutic alliance based on collaboration, not a power coalition. This distinction matters in case management practice because it clarifies the proper use of professional authority and guards against blurring the worker's accountable, role-defined position into an inappropriate power arrangement." },
  { id: "rml20_029", section: "cognitive",
    q: "The fundamental element required for learning through classical conditioning is:",
    options: { a: "Conditioned stimuli", b: "Unconditioned stimuli", c: "Change", d: "Association" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: {
      a: "The conditioned stimulus is a component of the process, but the fundamental element that makes learning occur is the association between stimuli.",
      b: "The unconditioned stimulus is one of the paired elements, yet learning itself depends on associating it with a neutral stimulus.",
      c: "Change is too vague and is not the specific mechanism; classical conditioning works through associative pairing." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is association. Classical conditioning, discovered by the Russian physiologist Ivan Pavlov, is a form of learning in which a neutral stimulus comes to elicit a response after being repeatedly paired with a stimulus that naturally produces that response. The fundamental mechanism that makes this learning possible is association: the brain forms a link between two stimuli that occur together in time. In Pavlov's classic experiment, a bell (neutral stimulus) was repeatedly presented just before food (the unconditioned stimulus, which naturally triggers salivation, the unconditioned response). Through repeated pairing, the dog associated the bell with food, so the bell alone (now a conditioned stimulus) came to elicit salivation (the conditioned response). Without the associative pairing of the two stimuli, no conditioning would occur. The distractors are components rather than the mechanism: the conditioned stimulus (option a) and unconditioned stimulus (option b) are elements that must be associated, so neither alone is the fundamental requirement; and change (option c) is too vague to name the process. Understanding that association is the core principle matters because classical conditioning underlies phenomena from phobias and taste aversions to advertising, and it is a foundation of behavioural psychology and behaviour therapy." },
  { id: "rml20_030", section: "systems",
    q: "Which of the following stages of group development presents that the team has clarified its purpose and strategy for achieving its goals?",
    options: { a: "Forming", b: "Norming", c: "Storming", d: "Performing" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "Forming is the initial orientation stage where members are still uncertain about purpose and roles, not yet clarified.",
      c: "Storming is the conflict stage marked by disagreement over roles and leadership, not clarity of purpose.",
      d: "Performing is the productive stage where the group works effectively toward goals, occurring after purpose and norms are already established." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is norming. Bruce Tuckman proposed a widely cited five-stage model of group development: forming, storming, norming, performing, and later adjourning. In the norming stage, the group resolves the conflicts of the storming phase, establishes shared norms, roles, and cohesion, and crucially clarifies its purpose and agrees on a strategy for achieving its goals. Members begin to trust one another, accept common ground rules, and align around a common direction, which is exactly what the question describes. The distractors correspond to other stages: forming (option a) is the initial orientation phase where members are tentative, polite, and still unclear about purpose and roles; storming (option c) is the conflict phase marked by disagreements over leadership, status, and how to proceed, before clarity is reached; and performing (option d) is the mature phase where, with purpose and norms already settled, the group works productively and interdependently toward its goals. Understanding Tuckman's sequence matters for group work, therapy groups, and team leadership because it helps a worker anticipate predictable phases, tolerate the discomfort of storming, and guide a group toward the settled clarity of norming and the productivity of performing." },
  { id: "rml20_031", section: "systems",
    q: "Which one of the following captures implicit and explicit group communication and interaction patterns?",
    options: { a: "A genogram", b: "A cognitive map", c: "An ecomap", d: "A sociogram" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: {
      a: "A genogram maps family structure and relationships across generations, not group communication patterns.",
      b: "A cognitive map represents an individual's mental representation of an environment or concepts, not group interaction patterns.",
      c: "An ecomap depicts an individual or family's connections to external systems and resources, not internal group interaction patterns." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is a sociogram. A sociogram is a graphic tool developed by Jacob Levy Moreno, the founder of sociometry, to map the relationships, communication, and interaction patterns within a group. It uses symbols for members and lines or arrows between them to depict who communicates with, is attracted to, or avoids whom, revealing both explicit interaction patterns and the implicit social structure such as cliques, isolates, popular members, and lines of influence. This makes it the correct tool for capturing group communication and interaction patterns. The distractors are all legitimate mapping tools with different targets: a genogram (option a), associated with Murray Bowen's family systems work, diagrams family structure, relationships, and patterns across generations; a cognitive map (option b), a term linked to Edward Tolman, is an individual's internal mental representation of a physical environment or set of concepts; and an ecomap (option c), developed by Ann Hartman, illustrates the connections between an individual or family and the external systems and resources in their environment. Understanding the sociogram matters in group work and social psychology because visualising interaction patterns helps a worker identify isolated members, informal leaders, and subgroups, informing how to intervene and strengthen group functioning." },
  { id: "rml20_032", section: "systems",
    q: "The desired outcomes of case management consists of all of the following EXCEPT:",
    options: { a: "Cure of a primary condition", b: "Environmental accommodations", c: "Skill training and acquisitions", d: "Empowerment" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: {
      b: "Environmental accommodations are a legitimate goal of case management, helping clients function within their surroundings, so this is not the exception.",
      c: "Skill training and acquisition is a genuine desired outcome, building client capacity, so it is not the exception.",
      d: "Empowerment is a core aim of case management, so it is not the exception." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is cure of a primary condition, which is the EXCEPTION. Case management is a coordinating practice that links clients, often those with chronic, complex, or long-term needs, to the services and resources they require, and its desired outcomes focus on functioning, adaptation, and self-sufficiency rather than medical cure. Legitimate goals include making environmental accommodations so the client can function within their surroundings (option b), providing skill training and acquisition to build the client's competence and independence (option c), and fostering empowerment so clients gain control over their own lives and decisions (option d). Curing a primary condition (option a) is not a realistic or intended outcome of case management, because case managers coordinate care rather than deliver curative medical or clinical treatment, and many clients have chronic conditions that cannot be cured but can be managed. Expecting cure misunderstands the role, which is about coordination, advocacy, resource linkage, and supporting the highest possible level of functioning. This distinction matters because framing case management around cure sets false expectations, whereas framing it around accommodation, skill-building, and empowerment reflects its true purpose of helping clients live as fully and independently as possible." },
  { id: "rml20_033", section: "cognitive",
    q: "Retrieval-induced forgetting refers to which memory-based phenomenon?",
    options: { a: "People remembered practised information better than unpractised information", b: "People remembered unpractised information better than practised information", c: "People remember names better than faces", d: "People remember faces better than names" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: {
      b: "This reverses the effect; unpractised related items are actually remembered worse, not better, due to retrieval competition.",
      c: "Remembering names versus faces concerns a different memory domain and is not what retrieval-induced forgetting describes.",
      d: "The face-versus-name advantage is a separate phenomenon unrelated to the retrieval practice paradigm." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is that people remembered practised information better than unpractised information. Retrieval-induced forgetting is a memory phenomenon studied extensively by Michael Anderson and colleagues, demonstrated with the retrieval-practice paradigm. When people repeatedly retrieve (practise) some items from a category, those practised items become more accessible and are remembered better, while related but unpractised items from the same category become harder to recall than if no practice had occurred. The best description among the options is that practised information is remembered better than unpractised information (option a), which reflects the strengthening of retrieved items. The mechanism proposed is inhibition: retrieving a target item requires suppressing competing related items, and that suppression causes the competing unpractised items to be forgotten. Option b reverses the outcome and is wrong, because unpractised related items are impaired, not enhanced. Options c and d concern the separate name-face memory literature and are unrelated to this paradigm. Understanding retrieval-induced forgetting matters because it shows that the act of remembering is not neutral: retrieving some memories can actively suppress others, with real-world implications for eyewitness testimony, studying strategies, and how selectively rehearsing information can distort what we later recall." },
  { id: "rml20_034", section: "social",
    q: "Which one refers to the behaviour that an individual engages in while enacting a role?",
    options: { a: "Role taking", b: "Role playing", c: "Playing at a role", d: "Role expectation" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "Role taking is imaginatively adopting another's perspective, not the behaviour of enacting one's own role.",
      c: "Playing at a role implies pretending or not fully committing, not genuine role enactment.",
      d: "Role expectation refers to the norms others hold about how a role should be performed, not the actual behaviour of performing it." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is role playing. In sociological and social-psychological role theory, role playing refers to the actual behaviour an individual engages in while enacting or performing a social role, that is, carrying out the expected actions associated with a position such as teacher, parent, or patient. It is the behavioural performance of the role. The distractors capture related but distinct concepts. Role taking (option a), a term central to George Herbert Mead's theory of the self, means imaginatively adopting the perspective of another person to anticipate how they will respond, a cognitive act of perspective-taking rather than performing one's own role. Playing at a role (option c) implies pretending, rehearsing, or not fully committing to the role, as a child might play at being a doctor, distinct from genuinely enacting it. Role expectation (option d) refers to the shared norms and anticipations others hold about how someone in a given role ought to behave, the standard against which performance is judged, not the performance itself. Distinguishing these matters because role theory explains much of social behaviour, including role conflict and role strain, and clarity about whether one is describing the perspective (role taking), the norms (role expectation), or the enactment (role playing) is essential to using the theory correctly." },
  { id: "rml20_035", section: "development",
    q: "Object permanence is the characteristic of children during the:",
    options: { a: "sensorimotor stage", b: "preoperational stage", c: "concrete operational stage", d: "formal operational stage" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: {
      b: "The preoperational stage is marked by symbolic thought and egocentrism; object permanence has already been acquired by then.",
      c: "The concrete operational stage features conservation and logical thinking about concrete objects, well after object permanence develops.",
      d: "The formal operational stage involves abstract and hypothetical reasoning, far beyond the emergence of object permanence." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is the sensorimotor stage. Jean Piaget, the Swiss developmental psychologist, described four stages of cognitive development, the first being the sensorimotor stage (birth to about two years). During this stage, infants understand the world through their senses and motor actions, and the major cognitive achievement is object permanence: the understanding that objects continue to exist even when they cannot be seen, heard, or touched. Before acquiring it, a young infant behaves as if an object that disappears from view has ceased to exist (out of sight, out of mind), which Piaget demonstrated by hiding a toy and observing that the infant did not search for it. As object permanence develops, typically around 8 months and consolidating by the end of the sensorimotor period, the infant will search for hidden objects, showing they hold a mental representation of them. The later stages involve different milestones: the preoperational stage (option b) features symbolic thought and egocentrism; the concrete operational stage (option c) brings conservation and logical reasoning about concrete situations; and the formal operational stage (option d) enables abstract and hypothetical thinking. Object permanence matters because it marks the infant's first mental representations, a foundation for memory, symbolic thought, and all later cognition." },
  { id: "rml20_036", section: "psychopathology",
    q: "According to Freud, the id is to the ___ principle as the ego is to the ____ principle.",
    options: { a: "aggressive, sexual", b: "sexual, aggressive", c: "pleasure, reality", d: "reality, pleasure" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "Aggressive and sexual name drives (Thanatos and Eros), not the guiding principles of the id and ego.",
      b: "Sexual and aggressive again describe drive contents rather than the id's pleasure principle and the ego's reality principle.",
      d: "This reverses the correct pairing; the id follows the pleasure principle and the ego the reality principle, not the other way around." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is pleasure, reality. In Sigmund Freud's structural model of the psyche, personality comprises three parts: the id, the ego, and the superego. The id is the most primitive, unconscious part, present from birth, and it operates on the pleasure principle: it demands immediate gratification of instinctual drives and seeks to reduce tension without regard for reality, logic, or consequences. The ego develops to mediate between the id's demands, the constraints of the external world, and the moral standards of the superego, and it operates on the reality principle: it delays gratification and finds realistic, socially acceptable ways to satisfy the id's urges. Thus the id is to the pleasure principle as the ego is to the reality principle. The distractors confuse principles with drive contents: aggressive and sexual (options a and b) refer to Freud's two basic instincts, Eros (the life or sexual drive) and Thanatos (the death or aggressive drive), not to the operating principles of these structures. Option d simply reverses the correct pairing. This distinction is fundamental to psychoanalytic theory because the constant negotiation between the pleasure-seeking id and the reality-oriented ego, refereed by the superego, is Freud's account of how personality functions and how inner conflict and anxiety arise." },
  { id: "rml20_037", section: "assessment",
    q: "'Can you tell me why this band is so popular among your age group?' is an example of:",
    options: { a: "psychodynamic inquiry", b: "a task-focused inquiry", c: "an ethnographic inquiry", d: "a strength-based inquiry" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "Psychodynamic inquiry probes unconscious conflicts and early experiences, not cultural group meanings.",
      b: "Task-focused inquiry targets specific problems and goals to be accomplished, not exploring a group's cultural world.",
      d: "Strength-based inquiry highlights the client's resources and competencies, not the shared meanings of a peer culture." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is an ethnographic inquiry. Ethnographic inquiry is a style of questioning borrowed from anthropology, in which the interviewer approaches the client as a knowledgeable informant about their own culture, social world, or peer group, seeking to understand meanings, norms, and shared perspectives from the insider's point of view. The question 'Can you tell me why this band is so popular among your age group?' treats the respondent as an expert on the culture of their age group and invites them to explain the shared meanings and values of that world, which is precisely the ethnographic stance associated with methods described by anthropologists such as James Spradley. The distractors represent other interviewing frames: psychodynamic inquiry (option a) probes unconscious motives, defences, and early experiences; task-focused inquiry (option b) concentrates on identifying specific problems and the concrete tasks needed to solve them; and strength-based inquiry (option d) directs attention to the client's competencies, resources, and successes rather than deficits. Recognising ethnographic inquiry matters in culturally competent clinical and social work practice because it positions the client as the authority on their own lived experience and cultural context, reducing the clinician's imposition of assumptions and yielding richer, more accurate understanding." },
  { id: "rml20_038", section: "assessment",
    q: "'Tell me about' is an example of:",
    options: { a: "an open-ended question", b: "a close-ended question", c: "an ethnographic interviewing technique", d: "theory-guided inquiry" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: {
      b: "A close-ended question restricts responses to yes/no or fixed choices, the opposite of the expansive 'tell me about' prompt.",
      c: "While 'tell me about' can be used ethnographically, its defining feature here is that it is open-ended, which is the more precise answer.",
      d: "Theory-guided inquiry frames questions around a specific theory, not the neutral, open invitation of 'tell me about'." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is an open-ended question. Open-ended questions invite the respondent to answer in their own words and at length, rather than choosing from fixed options or replying with a simple yes or no. The prompt 'Tell me about...' is a textbook example, because it opens a broad space for the client to describe experiences, feelings, and details in whatever way they choose, encouraging elaboration and rich narrative. This contrasts with a close-ended question (option b), such as 'Did you sleep well?', which restricts the answer to yes, no, or a single fact and is useful for gathering specific data quickly but limits depth. While 'tell me about' can serve within an ethnographic interview (option c), the phrase's essential defining feature is its open-ended form, which is the more precise classification the question seeks; and theory-guided inquiry (option d) shapes questions around a particular theoretical framework, which this neutral invitation does not. The distinction between open and closed questions is fundamental to clinical interviewing skills, as emphasised in the microskills approach of Allen Ivey, because open-ended questions build rapport, empower the client to lead, and elicit the fullest, least biased account, whereas overusing closed questions can make an interview feel like an interrogation." },
  { id: "rml20_039", section: "therapy",
    q: "Which of the following is not a form of psychotherapy used to treat psychological disorders?",
    options: { a: "Cognitive therapy", b: "Pharmacotherapy", c: "behavioural therapy", d: "Psychodynamic therapy" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "Cognitive therapy is a genuine psychotherapy that changes maladaptive thoughts, so it is not the answer.",
      c: "Behavioural therapy applies learning principles to change behaviour and is a true form of psychotherapy.",
      d: "Psychodynamic therapy is a well-established talking therapy, so it is not the answer." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is pharmacotherapy, because it is not a form of psychotherapy. Psychotherapy refers to psychological, talking-based treatments in which a trained therapist uses conversation, relationship, and structured techniques to help a client change thoughts, feelings, and behaviours. Cognitive therapy (option a), developed by Aaron Beck, targets and restructures distorted, maladaptive thoughts and is a genuine psychotherapy. Behavioural therapy (option c), rooted in the learning principles of Ivan Pavlov and B.F. Skinner and applied clinically by figures such as Joseph Wolpe, uses techniques like exposure and reinforcement to change behaviour and is likewise a psychotherapy. Psychodynamic therapy (option d), derived from Sigmund Freud's psychoanalysis, explores unconscious conflicts and early experiences through talking and is a classic form of psychotherapy. Pharmacotherapy (option b), by contrast, is treatment with medication, such as antidepressants, antipsychotics, or mood stabilisers, which is a biological or medical intervention prescribed by a physician or psychiatrist, not a talking therapy. It is therefore not a form of psychotherapy. This distinction matters because effective treatment often combines both approaches, but they are conceptually different: psychotherapy works through psychological processes and the therapeutic relationship, whereas pharmacotherapy works through altering brain chemistry." },
  { id: "rml20_040", section: "psychopathology",
    q: "Allport believed that traits could be organised into three levels:",
    options: { a: "primary, secondary, tertiary", b: "cognitive, emotional, physiological", c: "id, ego, superego", d: "cardinal, central, secondary" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: {
      a: "Primary, secondary, tertiary is a generic ranking, not Allport's specific trait terminology.",
      b: "Cognitive, emotional, physiological describes components of responses, not Allport's levels of traits.",
      c: "Id, ego, superego are Freud's structures of personality, not Allport's trait levels." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is cardinal, central, and secondary. Gordon Allport, a pioneer of the trait approach to personality, proposed that traits, enduring dispositions that make a person's behaviour consistent across situations, could be organised into three hierarchical levels. Cardinal traits are rare, dominant traits that shape virtually every aspect of a person's life and behaviour, so pervasive that the person may become known for them (for example, a personality so generous it is called Christlike); most people do not have a cardinal trait. Central traits are the general characteristics that form the basic building blocks of personality, the handful of core qualities (such as honest, kind, or anxious) one might use to describe someone. Secondary traits are more situation-specific and peripheral, such as preferences or attitudes that appear only under particular circumstances. The distractors belong to other frameworks: id, ego, and superego (option c) are Sigmund Freud's structural components of the psyche, not traits; while primary/secondary/tertiary (option a) and cognitive/emotional/physiological (option b) are generic labels not used by Allport. Understanding Allport's hierarchy matters because he helped establish the trait perspective and the idiographic study of individual uniqueness, foundational to modern personality psychology and later trait models like the Big Five." },
  { id: "rml20_041", section: "psychopathology",
    q: "Which of the following traits is not part of the five-factor model of personality?",
    options: { a: "openness", b: "anxiety", c: "extraversion", d: "agreeableness" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "Openness (to experience) is one of the five factors, so it is not the answer.",
      c: "Extraversion is a core dimension of the five-factor model, so it is not the answer.",
      d: "Agreeableness is one of the Big Five traits, so it is not the answer." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is anxiety, which is not one of the five factors. The five-factor model of personality, also called the Big Five, was developed and validated by researchers including Paul Costa and Robert McCrae, building on earlier lexical work by Lewis Goldberg. It identifies five broad dimensions that capture the structure of human personality, commonly remembered by the acronym OCEAN: Openness to experience (imaginative, curious versus conventional), Conscientiousness (organised, dependable versus careless), Extraversion (sociable, energetic versus reserved), Agreeableness (compassionate, cooperative versus antagonistic), and Neuroticism (emotionally unstable, anxious versus calm). Among the options, openness (a), extraversion (c), and agreeableness (d) are all genuine factors. Anxiety (b) is not itself one of the five factors; rather, it is a facet or component of the broader dimension of neuroticism, which encompasses anxiety, hostility, depression, and vulnerability to stress. The trap is that anxiety feels central to personality and does relate to the model, but it sits under neuroticism rather than standing as its own factor. Knowing the correct five dimensions matters because the Big Five is the most empirically supported and cross-culturally replicated framework in personality psychology, widely used in research, clinical assessment, and organisational settings, so students must distinguish the five factors from their subordinate facets." },
  { id: "rml20_042", section: "psychopathology",
    q: "According to Freud, the ___ mind includes accessible memories, which can be recalled at will",
    options: { a: "Conscious", b: "Preconscious", c: "Unconscious", d: "Psychic determinism" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "The conscious mind holds what we are aware of at the present moment, not the store of memories that can be recalled at will.",
      c: "The unconscious mind contains material that is not accessible to awareness, the opposite of easily recalled memories.",
      d: "Psychic determinism is a principle that all mental events have causes, not a level of the mind." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is the preconscious. Sigmund Freud's topographical model divides the mind into three levels based on accessibility to awareness. The conscious mind (option a) contains whatever we are aware of at the present moment, our current thoughts and perceptions. The unconscious mind (option c) is the vast reservoir of thoughts, memories, and impulses that are not accessible to awareness, often because they are repressed, yet still influence behaviour. The preconscious mind sits between these two: it holds memories and information that are not currently in awareness but can readily be brought to mind, recalled at will, such as your phone number, what you ate yesterday, or the name of your first teacher. Because the question specifies accessible memories that can be recalled at will, the preconscious is the precise answer, distinguishing it from the fully accessible conscious (current awareness) and the inaccessible unconscious. Psychic determinism (option d) is not a level of the mind at all but a Freudian principle asserting that all mental events, including slips and dreams, have underlying psychological causes. Understanding these three levels matters because they are the foundation of Freud's model of the psyche and explain his famous iceberg metaphor, in which the conscious is only the small visible tip above a much larger submerged unconscious." },
  { id: "rml20_043", section: "stats",
    q: "People who are available, volunteer, or can easily be recruited are used in the sampling method called ___",
    options: { a: "Simple random sampling", b: "Cluster sampling", c: "Systematic sampling", d: "Convenience sampling" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: {
      a: "Simple random sampling gives every member of the population an equal chance of selection, not just those readily available.",
      b: "Cluster sampling randomly selects whole groups (clusters) from the population, not merely convenient individuals.",
      c: "Systematic sampling selects every nth member from an ordered list, a probability method, not an availability-based one." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is convenience sampling. Convenience sampling is a non-probability sampling method in which participants are chosen simply because they are easy to reach, readily available, or willing to volunteer, rather than being selected through any random or systematic procedure. Examples include surveying students in your own classroom, recruiting people who happen to pass by, or using volunteers who respond to an advertisement. Its great advantage is that it is quick, cheap, and easy, which is why it is common in early research and student projects. Its major drawback is a high risk of sampling bias: because the sample is not representative of the wider population, findings cannot be confidently generalised. The distractors are all probability sampling methods, which use random selection to improve representativeness: simple random sampling (option a) gives every member of the population an equal chance of being chosen; cluster sampling (option b) randomly selects whole naturally occurring groups such as schools or neighbourhoods; and systematic sampling (option c) selects every nth individual from an ordered list after a random start. Understanding these distinctions matters because the sampling method directly determines whether a study's results can be generalised, a cornerstone of external validity and sound research design." },
  { id: "rml20_044", section: "stats",
    q: "Which of the following statements sounds like a null hypothesis?",
    options: { a: "There is a correlation in the population", b: "There is no difference between male and female incomes in the population", c: "The defendant is guilty", d: "The coin is not fair" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "Asserting that a correlation exists is an alternative (research) hypothesis, which predicts an effect, not a null hypothesis.",
      c: "Claiming the defendant is guilty asserts an effect or difference; the null-hypothesis analogue would be the presumption of innocence.",
      d: "Stating the coin is not fair asserts a difference from expectation, which is an alternative hypothesis, not a null." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is that there is no difference between male and female incomes in the population. The null hypothesis, a concept formalised by Ronald Fisher and central to the significance testing framework further developed by Jerzy Neyman and Egon Pearson, is the default statement that there is no effect, no difference, or no relationship in the population. It is the hypothesis of no difference that researchers set up specifically in order to test and potentially reject it. Option b states precisely that there is no difference between two groups (male and female incomes), which is the textbook form of a null hypothesis. The distractors are all alternative (research) hypotheses, which assert that an effect, difference, or relationship does exist: asserting a correlation exists (option a) predicts a relationship; claiming the defendant is guilty (option c) asserts a positive finding, whereas the null analogue would be the presumption of innocence; and stating the coin is not fair (option d) claims a departure from the expected 50-50, again an effect. The logic of null hypothesis testing is that we assume no effect and only reject that assumption when the data are sufficiently unlikely under it. Grasping this matters because virtually all inferential statistics, t-tests, ANOVA, chi-square, rest on correctly formulating and testing the null hypothesis." },
  { id: "rml20_045", section: "cognitive",
    q: "The main crux of hypnosis is?",
    options: { a: "Analytical mind", b: "Logical mind", c: "Suggestibility", d: "None" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "The analytical mind reflects critical, evaluative thinking, which hypnosis actually relaxes rather than relies upon.",
      b: "Logical reasoning is not the basis of hypnosis; the hypnotic state involves reduced critical scrutiny.",
      d: "'None' is incorrect because suggestibility is clearly the central mechanism of hypnosis." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is suggestibility. Hypnosis is a state of focused attention, heightened absorption, and reduced peripheral awareness in which a person becomes markedly more responsive to suggestion. The main crux, or essential mechanism, is suggestibility: the increased willingness and tendency to accept and act upon suggestions given by the hypnotist, whether those suggestions concern perception, movement, memory, or sensation. Individuals differ in hypnotic susceptibility, and this trait is measured by scales such as the Stanford Hypnotic Susceptibility Scale developed by Ernest Hilgard, who also proposed the neodissociation theory of hypnosis, viewing it as a division of consciousness. The distractors point in the wrong direction: the analytical mind (option a) and the logical mind (option b) involve critical, evaluative, reality-testing thinking, which hypnosis tends to relax or bypass rather than depend upon; heightened suggestibility partly reflects a temporary suspension of ordinary critical scrutiny. Option d is wrong because suggestibility is unmistakably central. Understanding that suggestibility is the core of hypnosis matters clinically, because hypnotherapy uses this heightened responsiveness to help with pain management, anxiety, and habit change, and it also matters forensically, since increased suggestibility raises the risk of creating false or distorted memories under hypnosis." },
  { id: "rml20_046", section: "systems",
    q: "According to Shulman, the person who blocks topics from group discussion is referred to as:",
    options: { a: "The scapegoat", b: "The monopolizer", c: "The gatekeeper", d: "The deviant" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "The scapegoat is the member who absorbs the group's blame and hostility, not one who blocks topics.",
      b: "The monopolizer dominates by talking excessively, not by preventing certain topics from arising.",
      d: "The deviant is a member who departs from group norms, not specifically one who blocks discussion topics." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is the gatekeeper. In Lawrence Shulman's analysis of group dynamics and roles in social group work, the gatekeeper is the member who controls the flow of discussion by blocking or diverting the group away from certain topics, especially threatening or emotionally charged ones. By steering conversation away from painful areas, the gatekeeper protects the group (and often themselves) from anxiety, but in doing so can prevent the group from engaging with important issues. The distractors name other recognisable group roles: the scapegoat (option a) is the member onto whom the group projects its collective frustration, blame, and hostility, becoming a target that allows the group to avoid facing its own problems; the monopolizer (option b) dominates airtime by talking excessively, crowding out other members, though this is about volume of participation rather than blocking specific content; and the deviant (option d) is the member who violates or challenges group norms. Recognising these roles matters for group workers and therapists because each reflects an underlying group process: the skilled leader learns to identify the gatekeeper's blocking, gently open up the avoided topics, protect the scapegoat, manage the monopolizer, and understand the function the deviant serves, thereby helping the group work more honestly and effectively." },
  { id: "rml20_047", section: "biopsych",
    q: "The common term used to describe all types of language loss is:",
    options: { a: "Dyslexia", b: "Aphasia", c: "Dsyphasia", d: "Lexphasia" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "Dyslexia is a specific learning difficulty affecting reading, not a general term for all language loss.",
      c: "Dysphasia refers to partial impairment of language; aphasia is the broader, common umbrella term for language loss.",
      d: "Lexphasia is not a recognised medical term; it is a fabricated distractor." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is aphasia. Aphasia is the common umbrella term for the loss or impairment of language ability, typically caused by damage to language areas of the brain, most often from stroke, head injury, or tumour. It encompasses difficulties in producing or comprehending spoken and written language. Two classic subtypes are named after their discoverers: Broca's aphasia, described by Paul Broca, involves damage to the frontal lobe's Broca's area and produces halting, effortful, non-fluent speech with relatively preserved comprehension; and Wernicke's aphasia, described by Carl Wernicke, involves the temporal lobe's Wernicke's area and produces fluent but meaningless speech with impaired comprehension. The distractors are either different disorders or invented terms: dyslexia (option a) is a specific learning difficulty affecting reading and spelling, not a general term for language loss; dysphasia (option c) technically refers to a partial impairment of language and is sometimes used interchangeably with aphasia, but aphasia is the broader, standard umbrella term the question asks for; and lexphasia (option d) is not a recognised medical term at all. Understanding aphasia matters in neuropsychology and clinical practice because it links specific brain regions to language functions and guides assessment and rehabilitation after brain injury." },
  { id: "rml20_048", section: "development",
    q: "Which, if any, of these statements about Piaget's conservation test is accurate?",
    options: { a: "The conservation test can only be conducted with children aged under 5 years", b: "In the water conservation task the level of liquid is maintained at exactly the same height when it is transferred to a differently shaped beaker", c: "The preoperational child's judgement is swayed by one dimension, such as the fact that the contents of one beaker look taller than the other", d: "None of the above" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "The conservation test is used across a range of ages, including children over 5, to see when conservation is achieved, so this is false.",
      b: "Transferring liquid to a differently shaped beaker changes its apparent height; the level does not stay the same, so this statement is false.",
      d: "'None of the above' is wrong because statement c is an accurate description of preoperational reasoning." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is that the preoperational child's judgement is swayed by one dimension, such as the fact that the contents of one beaker look taller than the other. Jean Piaget's conservation tasks test whether a child understands that quantity remains constant despite changes in appearance. In the classic liquid conservation task, equal amounts of water are shown in two identical beakers, then one is poured into a taller, narrower beaker; a child who has achieved conservation knows the amount is unchanged, whereas a preoperational child (roughly ages 2 to 7) typically says the taller beaker has more. This error occurs because of centration: the preoperational child centres on a single perceptual dimension (here, height) and ignores the compensating change in another dimension (width), and also lacks reversibility, the ability to mentally reverse the pouring. Option c precisely captures this centration on one dimension. The other statements are false: the test is used across a range of ages, not only under 5 (option a); and pouring liquid into a differently shaped beaker changes its apparent height rather than keeping it the same (option b), which is the very manipulation that reveals non-conservation. Understanding conservation matters because it marks the cognitive limits of preoperational thought and the transition into concrete operations, a landmark of Piaget's developmental theory." },
  { id: "rml20_049", section: "stats",
    q: "Which of the following is NOT a common measure of central tendency?",
    options: { a: "Median", b: "Mean", c: "Range", d: "Mode" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "The median is a standard measure of central tendency (the middle value), so it is not the answer.",
      b: "The mean is the arithmetic average, a core measure of central tendency, so it is not the answer.",
      d: "The mode is the most frequent value, a recognised measure of central tendency, so it is not the answer." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is range, which is NOT a measure of central tendency. Measures of central tendency describe the centre or typical value of a distribution, and the three common ones are the mean, the median, and the mode. The mean (option b) is the arithmetic average, computed by summing all values and dividing by the number of values; it is sensitive to extreme scores. The median (option a) is the middle value when the data are ordered, splitting the distribution in half; it is robust to outliers. The mode (option d) is the most frequently occurring value and is the only measure usable with nominal data. The range (option c), by contrast, is a measure of dispersion or variability, not central tendency: it is calculated as the difference between the highest and lowest values and describes how spread out the data are, not where the centre lies. Other measures of variability include the variance and standard deviation. The trap is that all four are basic descriptive statistics learned together, so students may not notice that the range answers a different question (how spread out?) rather than (what is typical?). This distinction is foundational to descriptive statistics, because fully summarising a distribution requires reporting both its central tendency and its variability." },
  { id: "rml20_050", section: "general",
    q: "The first person to use the term clinical psychology in print was",
    options: { a: "Erik Erikson", b: "Sigmund Freud", c: "Lightner Witmer", d: "Cattell" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "Erik Erikson developed the psychosocial theory of development; he did not coin clinical psychology.",
      b: "Sigmund Freud founded psychoanalysis but did not originate the term clinical psychology.",
      d: "James McKeen Cattell contributed to mental testing but did not coin the term clinical psychology." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Lightner Witmer. Lightner Witmer is widely regarded as the founder of clinical psychology. In 1896 he established the first psychological clinic at the University of Pennsylvania, and he is credited with being the first to use the term clinical psychology in print, notably in a 1907 article in the journal Psychological Clinic, which he also founded. His clinic focused on helping children with learning and behavioural difficulties, pioneering the application of psychological principles to practical assessment and intervention. The distractors are all major figures in psychology but did not coin the term: Erik Erikson (option a) is known for his psychosocial theory of development across the lifespan; Sigmund Freud (option b) founded psychoanalysis and shaped clinical thinking profoundly but did not originate the phrase clinical psychology; and James McKeen Cattell (option d), a student of Wilhelm Wundt, advanced the mental testing movement and coined the term mental test, but not clinical psychology. Knowing Witmer's role matters because it anchors the historical origin of the field Sihan is entering, marking the moment psychology moved from the laboratory into applied clinical service, the very tradition that clinical psychology programmes continue today." },
  { id: "rml20_051", section: "stats",
    q: "Any influence that produces a distortion in the results of a study is known as",
    options: { a: "Control", b: "Sample", c: "Population", d: "Bias" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: {
      a: "A control is a method for holding variables constant to prevent distortion, not the distortion itself.",
      b: "A sample is the subset of participants studied, not an influence that distorts results.",
      c: "A population is the entire group of interest, not a source of distortion." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is bias. In research methodology, bias refers to any systematic influence that distorts the results of a study, pushing findings away from the truth in a consistent direction. Bias can enter at many stages: sampling bias occurs when the sample is unrepresentative of the population; response bias occurs when participants answer inaccurately, for example to appear socially desirable; experimenter bias occurs when the researcher's expectations unintentionally shape the outcome; and measurement bias arises from flawed instruments. Because bias is systematic rather than random, it does not average out with a larger sample and can seriously undermine a study's validity. The distractors are related methodological terms that are not sources of distortion: a control (option a) is precisely a technique used to prevent distortion by holding extraneous variables constant, the opposite of bias; a sample (option b) is the subset of individuals actually studied; and a population (option c) is the entire group about which the researcher wishes to draw conclusions. Understanding bias is essential to critical research literacy, because recognising potential sources of bias allows researchers to design controls, use randomisation and blinding, and interpret findings cautiously, ensuring that conclusions reflect real effects rather than systematic distortion." },
  { id: "rml20_052", section: "psychopathology",
    q: "Who developed a model of personality based on 16 trait dimensions?",
    options: { a: "Allport", b: "Rogers", c: "Freud", d: "Cattell" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: {
      a: "Gordon Allport organised traits into cardinal, central, and secondary levels, not a 16-factor model.",
      b: "Carl Rogers developed a humanistic, self-concept theory, not a trait factor model.",
      c: "Sigmund Freud developed psychoanalytic theory, not a factor-analytic trait model." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Cattell. Raymond Cattell developed a model of personality based on 16 trait dimensions, which formed the basis of his famous questionnaire, the Sixteen Personality Factor Questionnaire (16PF). Cattell used the statistical technique of factor analysis to reduce the enormous number of trait-descriptive words in the language down to sixteen underlying source traits, which he believed were the fundamental building blocks of personality, such as warmth, reasoning, emotional stability, and dominance. He distinguished these deeper source traits from more superficial surface traits. The distractors are other major personality theorists with different frameworks: Gordon Allport (option a) organised traits into a hierarchy of cardinal, central, and secondary levels rather than a fixed set of sixteen factors; Carl Rogers (option b) built a humanistic theory centred on the self-concept, congruence, and unconditional positive regard, not a trait-factor model; and Sigmund Freud (option c) created psychoanalytic theory focused on unconscious drives and psychosexual development. Cattell's work matters because his factor-analytic approach to identifying trait dimensions strongly influenced the later development of the Big Five model, and the 16PF remains a widely used personality assessment in clinical, counselling, and occupational settings." },
  { id: "rml20_053", section: "assessment",
    q: "IQ if any body has a mental age of 8 and a chronological age of 10 is:",
    options: { a: "108", b: "125", c: "80", d: "92" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "108 does not result from the IQ formula with these values; it appears to be an arbitrary distractor.",
      b: "125 would result from reversing the ratio (10 divided by 8), which is the wrong direction.",
      d: "92 is close to 100 but does not match the correct computation of mental age over chronological age times 100." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is 80. The intelligence quotient, in its original ratio form introduced by William Stern and popularised through the Stanford-Binet test revised by Lewis Terman, is calculated with the formula IQ equals mental age divided by chronological age, multiplied by 100. Here the mental age is 8 and the chronological age is 10, so IQ equals (8 divided by 10) times 100, which equals 0.8 times 100, equals 80. The logic is that a child whose mental age matches their chronological age has an IQ of 100 (average), a child whose mental development is ahead of their years scores above 100, and a child whose mental age lags behind their chronological age, as here, scores below 100. Option b (125) results from reversing the ratio, dividing chronological age by mental age, which is the wrong direction; option d (92) is near average but does not match the calculation; and option a (108) does not follow from the formula. Mental age, the concept underlying this, originated with Alfred Binet and Theodore Simon, who devised the first practical intelligence test. Although modern tests use deviation IQ rather than this ratio formula, understanding the classic ratio IQ remains a staple of assessment questions and illustrates the historical foundation of intelligence testing." },
  { id: "rml20_054", section: "psychopathology",
    q: "Redirecting feelings from a threatening target to a less threatening target is",
    options: { a: "Reaction formation", b: "Displacement", c: "Regression", d: "Projection" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "Reaction formation converts an unacceptable impulse into its opposite, not redirecting it onto a safer target.",
      c: "Regression involves reverting to an earlier developmental stage under stress, not redirecting feelings.",
      d: "Projection attributes one's own unacceptable feelings to others, not shifting them onto a less threatening target." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is displacement. Displacement is a defence mechanism, described within Sigmund Freud's psychoanalytic theory and elaborated by Anna Freud, in which an emotion or impulse (often anger or aggression) that cannot be safely expressed toward its true, threatening target is redirected onto a safer, less threatening substitute. The classic example is a person who is angry at their boss but, unable to express it there without risk, comes home and snaps at their spouse, child, or pet. The feeling is real, but its target is shifted to reduce danger. The distractors are other defence mechanisms with distinct mechanisms: reaction formation (option a) converts an unacceptable impulse into its conscious opposite, so someone who feels hatred behaves with exaggerated kindness; regression (option c) is a retreat under stress to behaviours characteristic of an earlier developmental stage, such as an adult sulking or throwing a tantrum; and projection (option d) attributes one's own unacceptable feelings to another person, so that a person who is hostile accuses others of being hostile. Recognising displacement matters clinically because it explains much everyday misdirected anger and conflict, and helping clients see when they are redirecting feelings onto innocent targets can reduce relationship damage and encourage confronting the real source of distress." },
  { id: "rml20_055", section: "development",
    q: "Which term was used by Erikson to refer to the feeling experienced by some individuals in mid-life that they have achieved very little or nothing and have little to offer to the next generation?",
    options: { a: "Stagnation", b: "Generativity", c: "Presbyopia", d: "Mid-life crisis" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: {
      b: "Generativity is the positive pole of the same stage, the sense of contributing to the next generation, the opposite of the feeling described.",
      c: "Presbyopia is an age-related decline in near vision, a physical rather than psychosocial phenomenon.",
      d: "Mid-life crisis is a popular term (associated with Elliott Jaques) but is not Erikson's specific term for this feeling." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is stagnation. Erik Erikson proposed eight psychosocial stages of development across the lifespan, each defined by a central crisis with a positive and a negative pole. The seventh stage, occurring in middle adulthood, is generativity versus stagnation. Generativity (option b) is the positive resolution: a sense of contributing to society and guiding the next generation through parenting, mentoring, work, or creativity, feeling that one is making a meaningful difference. Stagnation is the negative pole and the answer the question describes: the feeling of having achieved little or nothing and having nothing to pass on to the next generation, accompanied by self-absorption, disconnection, and a sense of unproductiveness. The distractors do not fit: presbyopia (option c) is an age-related loss of near-vision focusing ability, a purely physical change; and mid-life crisis (option d), a popular phrase associated with Elliott Jaques, describes a period of self-doubt in midlife but is not Erikson's specific term. Understanding Erikson's generativity versus stagnation matters because it frames the developmental task of middle adulthood as finding meaning through contribution, and the failure of that task, stagnation, is linked to depression and a sense of emptiness, informing how clinicians understand midlife distress." },
  { id: "rml20_056", section: "development",
    q: "Research evidence suggests that people with autism spectrum development show deficits in:",
    options: { a: "Anger management", b: "Encoding social cues", c: "Deception", d: "Memory retrieval" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "Anger management difficulties are not a core, defining deficit of autism spectrum conditions.",
      c: "Difficulty with deception relates to theory of mind but is a downstream consequence; the core deficit is in processing social cues.",
      d: "General memory retrieval is often intact or even superior in autism; the hallmark deficit is social, not mnemonic." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is encoding social cues. Autism spectrum disorder is characterised centrally by persistent difficulties in social communication and social interaction, and a large body of research shows that individuals with autism have marked difficulty encoding and interpreting social cues, such as facial expressions, tone of voice, body language, eye gaze, and the subtle nonverbal signals that guide typical social exchange. This is closely tied to differences in theory of mind, the ability to attribute mental states to others, studied in autism by Simon Baron-Cohen, Uta Frith, and Alan Leslie, whose work showed that children with autism often struggle with false-belief tasks and reading others' intentions. The distractors do not capture the core deficit: anger management (option a) is not a defining feature of autism; difficulty with deception (option c) can follow from theory-of-mind differences but is a downstream effect rather than the primary deficit, which lies in processing social information; and memory retrieval (option d) is often intact or even enhanced in specific domains among autistic individuals, so it is not the hallmark impairment. Understanding that the fundamental difficulty lies in encoding social cues matters because it directs assessment and intervention toward social skills, communication support, and understanding others' perspectives, rather than misattributing the condition to behavioural or memory problems." },
  { id: "rml20_057", section: "stats",
    q: "The process of holding constant the possible influence of dependent variable in experimental studies is known as",
    options: { a: "Control", b: "Standard deviation", c: "Experiment", d: "Variable" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: {
      b: "Standard deviation is a measure of variability in data, not a technique for holding influences constant.",
      c: "An experiment is the overall study design, not the specific process of holding extraneous influences constant.",
      d: "A variable is any factor that can change or vary, not the process of controlling it." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is control. In experimental research, control refers to the process of holding constant, or otherwise neutralising, the possible influence of extraneous variables so that the researcher can isolate the true effect of the independent variable on the dependent variable. By keeping other factors constant across conditions, using control groups, randomisation, matching, or standardised procedures, the experimenter reduces confounding and strengthens the internal validity of the study, allowing more confident causal conclusions. The logic of control is at the heart of experimental design as formalised by statisticians such as Ronald Fisher. The distractors are different concepts: standard deviation (option b) is a descriptive statistic measuring the variability or spread of scores around the mean, not a design technique; an experiment (option c) is the overall research method that manipulates an independent variable and measures its effect, within which control is one component; and a variable (option d) is simply any characteristic that can take different values. Note the question's wording is slightly garbled, but the intended concept is controlling extraneous influences, and control is the term for that. Understanding control is essential because without it, any observed differences between conditions could be due to uncontrolled confounds rather than the independent variable, undermining the ability to infer cause and effect." },
  { id: "rml20_058", section: "general",
    q: "All of the following statements are TRUE about exceptions to the rule of confidentiality EXCEPT:",
    options: { a: "a court order requires the release of information", b: "the information is essential to safeguard the health of others (duty to protect; warn)", c: "the information is probative of guilt or innocence in a criminal matter", d: "there are never exceptions to the rule of confidentiality" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: {
      a: "A valid court order genuinely can compel release of information, so this is a true exception, not the answer.",
      b: "The duty to protect or warn is a well-established exception when others' safety is at risk, so it is true.",
      c: "Information probative of guilt or innocence can be a recognised exception in criminal matters, so this is true." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is that there are never exceptions to the rule of confidentiality, which is the EXCEPTION because it is false. Confidentiality is a cornerstone of the therapeutic and helping relationship, protecting clients' private disclosures, but it is not absolute; ethics codes and law recognise specific circumstances under which it may or must be breached. Genuine exceptions include when a valid court order compels disclosure (option a); when information is essential to safeguard the health or safety of others, the duty to protect or warn (option b), a principle established by the landmark Tarasoff v. Regents of the University of California case, in which a therapist's duty to warn a potential victim was affirmed; and, in some jurisdictions, when information is probative of guilt or innocence in a criminal matter (option c). Because these legitimate exceptions exist, the claim that there are never any exceptions (option d) is simply untrue and is therefore the answer. Understanding the limits of confidentiality is ethically and legally critical for clinicians, because they must inform clients at the outset about these limits (informed consent), balance client privacy against the safety of others, and act appropriately when a duty to protect, report abuse, or comply with the law overrides confidentiality." },
  { id: "rml20_059", section: "biopsych",
    q: "The James-Lange or body reaction theory of emotion says:",
    options: { a: "you feel emotion then a bodily response", b: "you react with your body first, then you feel emotion", c: "the somatic nervous system is the seat of emotion", d: "emotions and visceral reactions are simultaneous" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "Feeling the emotion first and then the bodily response is the commonsense view that James-Lange specifically reverses.",
      c: "The James-Lange theory emphasises visceral (autonomic) bodily reactions, not the somatic nervous system as the seat of emotion.",
      d: "Simultaneous emotion and visceral reaction describes the Cannon-Bard theory, not James-Lange." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is that you react with your body first, then you feel emotion. The James-Lange theory of emotion, proposed independently by William James and Carl Lange in the late 19th century, reverses the commonsense view of emotion. Rather than an event causing an emotion that then produces a bodily reaction, James and Lange argued that a stimulus first triggers a physiological or bodily response (racing heart, trembling, sweating), and our perception or interpretation of that bodily change is the emotion. In James's famous formulation, we do not tremble because we are afraid; we are afraid because we tremble. Thus the bodily reaction comes first and the felt emotion follows from perceiving it. Option a describes the opposite, commonsense sequence that the theory explicitly rejects. Option d, that emotion and visceral reaction occur simultaneously, describes the rival Cannon-Bard theory of Walter Cannon and Philip Bard, which held that the thalamus sends signals producing the emotional experience and the bodily response at the same time. Option c misstates the theory by invoking the somatic rather than the visceral, autonomic responses James-Lange emphasised. Understanding James-Lange matters because it launched the scientific debate over whether physiology drives emotion, a question later extended by the Schachter-Singer two-factor theory adding cognitive appraisal." },
  { id: "rml20_060", section: "therapy",
    q: "The behavioural technique for reducing anxiety in which patients practice relaxation while visualizing provoking situations of increasing intensity refers:",
    options: { a: "Symptom substitution", b: "Flooding", c: "Systematic desensitization", d: "Token economy" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "Symptom substitution is a psychodynamic idea that removing a symptom causes a new one to appear, not a relaxation-based technique.",
      b: "Flooding exposes the patient to the most intense anxiety-provoking stimulus all at once, not gradually with relaxation.",
      d: "A token economy uses tokens as reinforcers to shape behaviour, unrelated to anxiety reduction through relaxation and imagery." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is systematic desensitization. Systematic desensitization is a behavioural therapy developed by Joseph Wolpe for reducing anxiety and treating phobias. It rests on the principle of reciprocal inhibition: because relaxation and anxiety are physiologically incompatible, a person cannot be deeply relaxed and highly anxious at the same time. The technique has three steps: first, the patient learns a relaxation method such as progressive muscle relaxation (developed by Edmund Jacobson); second, the patient and therapist construct an anxiety hierarchy, ranking feared situations from least to most provoking; and third, while maintaining relaxation, the patient gradually visualises or confronts these situations in ascending order of intensity, so that calm becomes paired with each formerly frightening scene. Over time the anxiety response is extinguished. The distractors are distinct: flooding (option b) is the opposite approach, exposing the patient immediately and fully to the most intense feared stimulus without gradual buildup; symptom substitution (option a) is a psychodynamic claim that removing a symptom behaviourally will cause a new symptom to emerge; and a token economy (option d) is an operant technique using tokens as reinforcers to increase desired behaviours. Systematic desensitization matters because it is a foundational, evidence-based treatment for phobias and anxiety and exemplifies how learning principles are applied therapeutically." },
  { id: "rml20_061", section: "psychopathology",
    q: "We know that a distinction is made between positive and negative symptoms of schizophrenia. What is the difference between these symptoms?",
    options: { a: "Negative symptoms indicate the presence of something normal and negative symptoms indicate the absence of something unusual", b: "Positive symptoms indicate the presence of something normal, and Negative symptoms indicate the absence of something unusual", c: "Positive symptoms indicate the presence of something unusual, and Negative symptoms indicate the absence of something normal", d: "Positive symptoms indicate the absence of something normal, negative symptoms indicate the presence of something unusual" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "This option is internally inconsistent and does not correctly define positive symptoms at all.",
      b: "This wrongly frames positive symptoms as the presence of something normal, when they are the presence of something abnormal.",
      d: "This reverses the definitions entirely, wrongly assigning absence to positive and presence to negative symptoms." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is that positive symptoms indicate the presence of something unusual, and negative symptoms indicate the absence of something normal. In schizophrenia, symptoms are grouped into two broad categories based on whether they add something abnormal or take away something normal, a distinction refined by researchers such as Timothy Crow and Nancy Andreasen. Positive symptoms are pathological excesses or additions: they reflect the presence of experiences that should not be there, such as hallucinations (perceiving things that are not present), delusions (fixed false beliefs), and disorganised speech and thought. The word positive here means added, not good. Negative symptoms are pathological deficits: they reflect the absence or reduction of normal functions, such as flat or blunted affect (reduced emotional expression), avolition (loss of motivation), alogia (poverty of speech), and social withdrawal. The word negative means taken away, not bad. Option c captures this precisely, while the other options either reverse the definitions or muddle presence and absence. This distinction matters clinically because positive and negative symptoms respond differently to treatment: antipsychotic medications, especially older ones, are more effective against positive symptoms, whereas negative symptoms are often more persistent and harder to treat, and they strongly predict long-term disability and functional outcome." },
  { id: "rml20_062", section: "cognitive",
    q: "The temporary inability to retrieve a word that is well-known to us is referred to as the",
    options: { a: "Wernicke's aphasia", b: "Recency effect", c: "Tip-of-the-tongue phenomenon", d: "Broca's aphasia" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "Wernicke's aphasia is a brain-damage-related language comprehension disorder, not a normal temporary word-retrieval lapse.",
      b: "The recency effect refers to better recall of the last items in a list, unrelated to failing to retrieve a known word.",
      d: "Broca's aphasia is a brain-injury language production disorder with effortful speech, not the everyday tip-of-the-tongue state." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is the tip-of-the-tongue phenomenon. The tip-of-the-tongue phenomenon, studied experimentally by Roger Brown and David McNeill, is the common, temporary, and frustrating experience of being unable to retrieve a word or name that one clearly knows, while feeling that recall is imminent, on the tip of the tongue. People in this state can often recall partial information, such as the first letter, the number of syllables, or similar-sounding words, indicating that the memory is stored and partially accessible but momentarily blocked from full retrieval. It is a normal memory retrieval failure, not a pathology, and it becomes more frequent with age. The distractors describe different phenomena: Wernicke's aphasia (option a) is a language disorder from damage to Wernicke's area producing fluent but meaningless speech and impaired comprehension; Broca's aphasia (option d) is a disorder from damage to Broca's area producing effortful, non-fluent speech; both are the result of brain injury, not everyday lapses. The recency effect (option b) refers to the tendency to recall the most recently presented items in a list best, part of the serial position curve studied by Hermann Ebbinghaus and others. The tip-of-the-tongue phenomenon matters because it reveals that word knowledge and word retrieval are separable processes, illuminating how the brain stores and accesses lexical information." },
  { id: "rml20_063", section: "development",
    q: "Studying people across time repeatedly is",
    options: { a: "longitudinal research", b: "archival", c: "cross-sectional", d: "cross-sequential" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: {
      b: "Archival research analyses existing records and data, not repeated study of the same people over time.",
      c: "Cross-sectional research compares different age groups at a single point in time, not the same people repeatedly.",
      d: "Cross-sequential research combines longitudinal and cross-sectional methods; the pure description of following the same people over time is longitudinal." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is longitudinal research. A longitudinal study follows the same individuals repeatedly over an extended period, measuring them at multiple points in time to observe how they change and develop. Because it tracks the same people across time, it can reveal genuine developmental change within individuals and clarify the sequence and stability of characteristics, which makes it especially valuable in developmental psychology for studying aging, growth, and the long-term effects of early experiences. Its drawbacks include high cost, long duration, and participant attrition (dropout). The distractors describe other designs: cross-sectional research (option c) compares different groups of people of different ages all at a single point in time, which is faster and cheaper but confounds age differences with cohort (generational) differences and cannot show individual change; archival research (option b) analyses pre-existing records, documents, or data rather than following people prospectively; and cross-sequential research (option d), also called sequential design, combines longitudinal and cross-sectional approaches by following several age cohorts over time to disentangle age effects from cohort effects. Understanding these designs matters because the choice among them determines what developmental conclusions can be drawn: only longitudinal (and sequential) designs can directly observe change within the same people as they age." },
  { id: "rml20_064", section: "social",
    q: "The act of perceiving, understanding, responding to emotional state and ideas of another person is known as:",
    options: { a: "Sympathy", b: "Empathy", c: "Transference", d: "Displacement" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "Sympathy is feeling concern or pity for another's situation, not accurately perceiving and sharing their internal emotional state.",
      c: "Transference is the redirection of feelings from past relationships onto the therapist, not understanding another's emotions.",
      d: "Displacement is a defence mechanism redirecting feelings onto a safer target, unrelated to understanding others' emotions." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is empathy. Empathy is the capacity to perceive, understand, and respond to the emotional states, feelings, and ideas of another person, in effect to step into their shoes and grasp their inner experience, and then to convey that understanding back to them. In counselling, empathy is considered a core condition of the therapeutic relationship, emphasised by Carl Rogers in his person-centred therapy alongside unconditional positive regard and congruence; Rogers described empathic understanding as sensing the client's private world as if it were one's own without losing the as if quality. Empathy is often distinguished from sympathy (option a), which is feeling concern, pity, or sorrow for another's misfortune from the outside, without necessarily understanding or sharing their internal emotional experience; empathy is a deeper, more attuned understanding, whereas sympathy is more about feeling for rather than feeling with. The other distractors are unrelated concepts: transference (option c) is the psychoanalytic process in which a client redirects feelings from significant past relationships onto the therapist; and displacement (option d) is a defence mechanism redirecting feelings onto a safer target. Empathy matters profoundly in clinical practice because it builds trust, makes clients feel understood, and is consistently linked to positive therapeutic outcomes across virtually all forms of therapy." },
  { id: "rml20_065", section: "assessment",
    q: "A person is categorised as severe mentally retarded if the IQ is between",
    options: { a: "50-70", b: "35-49", c: "21-34", d: "0-20" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "An IQ of 50 to 70 corresponds to mild intellectual disability, not severe.",
      b: "An IQ of 35 to 49 corresponds to moderate intellectual disability, not severe.",
      d: "An IQ below 20 corresponds to profound intellectual disability, more severe than the severe category." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is 21 to 34. In the traditional classification of intellectual disability (historically termed mental retardation) based on IQ scores, using systems such as the ICD and older DSM criteria, the levels are divided into four bands. Mild intellectual disability corresponds to an IQ of roughly 50 to 70, where individuals can often achieve basic academic and vocational skills and live relatively independently with support. Moderate intellectual disability corresponds to an IQ of about 35 to 49, with individuals able to learn self-care and simple communication but needing more support. Severe intellectual disability corresponds to an IQ of approximately 20 to 34 (the question's 21 to 34), where individuals have very limited communication and require substantial daily support. Profound intellectual disability corresponds to an IQ below 20, with pervasive support needs across all areas. The distractors correspond to the adjacent bands: 50 to 70 is mild (option a), 35 to 49 is moderate (option b), and 0 to 20 is profound (option d). It is worth noting that the DSM-5 has shifted away from relying primarily on IQ cutoffs toward assessing adaptive functioning across conceptual, social, and practical domains, following the framework of the American Association on Intellectual and Developmental Disabilities. Still, knowing these classic IQ bands matters for assessment questions and for understanding the historical grading of severity." },
  { id: "rml20_066", section: "cognitive",
    q: "We know that a common distinction is made between explicit and implicit memory. But which one of the following does NOT accurately reflect one of these terms?",
    options: { a: "Explicit memory involves conscious awareness of the original information or the situation in which the learning occurred", b: "Implicit memory refers to an influence on behaviour, feelings or thoughts as a result of prior experience", c: "Explicit memory involves recollection of the original information or experience that is subsequently recalled", d: "Implicit memory involves a conscious recollection of the original events" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: {
      a: "Explicit memory does involve conscious awareness of the learned information, so this statement is accurate and not the answer.",
      b: "Implicit memory does influence behaviour and thoughts through prior experience without conscious recall, so this is accurate.",
      c: "Explicit memory does involve deliberate recollection of the original information, so this statement is accurate." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is that implicit memory involves a conscious recollection of the original events, which does NOT accurately reflect the term. The distinction between explicit and implicit memory, elaborated by researchers such as Daniel Schacter and Endel Tulving, divides long-term memory by whether retrieval is conscious. Explicit (declarative) memory involves the conscious, intentional recollection of facts and events, such as recalling what you did yesterday or a fact you learned; statements a and c correctly describe it as conscious awareness and deliberate recollection of the original information. Implicit (non-declarative) memory, by contrast, operates without conscious awareness: prior experiences influence current behaviour, feelings, skills, or thoughts even though the person cannot consciously recall the original learning. Examples include procedural skills like riding a bicycle, classically conditioned responses, and priming; statement b correctly describes implicit memory as an influence from prior experience. Option d is false because it attributes conscious recollection to implicit memory, which is precisely the opposite of what implicit means; the defining feature of implicit memory is that it is unconscious. This distinction matters because it is supported by neuropsychological evidence, notably amnesic patients like H.M. (studied by Brenda Milner) who lost the ability to form new explicit memories but could still learn new skills implicitly, showing the two systems rely on different brain structures." },
  { id: "rml20_067", section: "systems",
    q: "According to Tuckman's five stage model of group development, which stage refers to the period of development when people struggle with each other over leadership and group definition?",
    options: { a: "Performing", b: "Storming", c: "Norming", d: "Forming" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "Performing is the mature, productive stage where the group works effectively, not the conflict phase.",
      c: "Norming is the stage where cohesion and shared norms develop after conflict is resolved, not the struggle itself.",
      d: "Forming is the initial orientation stage marked by politeness and uncertainty, before conflict emerges." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is storming. Bruce Tuckman's model of group development describes five sequential stages: forming, storming, norming, performing, and adjourning. The storming stage is the period of conflict and struggle, when group members begin to assert themselves, compete for status and roles, and clash over leadership, power, and how the group should be defined and run. Cliques may form, tension rises, and disagreements about direction and control come to the surface. This turbulence, though uncomfortable, is a normal and often necessary phase in which the group works out its structure. The distractors are the other stages: forming (option d) is the initial orientation phase, marked by politeness, uncertainty, and dependence on the leader as members get acquainted; norming (option c) follows storming, when conflicts are resolved, cohesion develops, and the group establishes shared norms and roles; and performing (option a) is the mature stage of productive, interdependent work toward goals once structure and trust are settled. Understanding that storming is the conflict stage matters for anyone leading groups, therapy groups, teams, or task committees, because a leader who expects and tolerates the storming phase can guide the group through it constructively rather than being alarmed by conflict or trying to suppress it prematurely, which allows the group to reach the cohesion of norming and the productivity of performing." },
  { id: "rml20_068", section: "cognitive",
    q: "Any figure of form not perceived in parts but organised in perception best possible whole form is known as",
    options: { a: "Law of proximity", b: "Law of pragnanz", c: "Law of figure and background", d: "Law of continuity" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "The law of proximity states that objects close together are grouped, not the principle of perceiving the best whole form.",
      c: "The figure-ground principle concerns distinguishing an object from its background, not organising into the simplest whole.",
      d: "The law of continuity concerns perceiving smooth continuous lines, not the overall simplest, most complete form." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is the law of pragnanz. The law of pragnanz, also called the law of good form or the principle of simplicity, is the overarching principle of Gestalt psychology, founded by Max Wertheimer along with Wolfgang Kohler and Kurt Koffka. Pragnanz is German for conciseness or good figure, and the law states that we tend to perceive ambiguous or complex stimuli in the simplest, most stable, most complete and organised form possible; we organise perception into the best possible whole rather than perceiving disconnected parts. This captures the Gestalt insight that the whole is different from, and more than, the sum of its parts. The distractors are specific Gestalt grouping principles that fall under this broader law: the law of proximity (option a) states that elements close together are perceived as a group; the law of continuity (option d) states that we perceive smooth, continuous lines rather than abrupt breaks; and figure-ground organisation (option c) is the tendency to separate an object (figure) from its surroundings (ground). Pragnanz is the general principle from which these more specific laws follow. Understanding it matters because Gestalt principles explain how the brain actively organises raw sensory input into meaningful, coherent perceptions, influencing fields from perception research to design and visual communication." },
  { id: "rml20_069", section: "systems",
    q: "Dysfunctional organisational cultures have all of the following characteristics EXCEPT:",
    options: { a: "poor performance record", b: "polarised factions", c: "open and transparent discourse", d: "coercive tactics" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "A poor performance record is a genuine feature of dysfunctional organisational cultures, so it is not the exception.",
      b: "Polarised factions characterise dysfunctional cultures marked by division and conflict, so this is not the exception.",
      d: "Coercive tactics are typical of unhealthy, dysfunctional cultures, so this is not the exception." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is open and transparent discourse, which is the EXCEPTION because it is a feature of healthy, not dysfunctional, organisational cultures. Organisational culture refers to the shared values, norms, beliefs, and practices that shape how members of an organisation behave, a concept extensively developed by Edgar Schein. Dysfunctional cultures are marked by patterns that impair functioning and wellbeing, including a poor performance record reflecting ineffective operation (option a), polarised factions in which members split into opposing camps marked by division, mistrust, and conflict (option b), and coercive tactics whereby power is exercised through pressure, threat, or force rather than collaboration (option d). Open and transparent discourse (option c), by contrast, is a hallmark of a healthy, well-functioning culture, in which information flows freely, communication is honest, problems can be raised without fear, and trust is high. It is the opposite of the secrecy, defensiveness, and closed communication typical of dysfunctional cultures. This distinction matters in organisational and community practice because the health of an organisation's culture strongly affects staff morale, performance, and the quality of services delivered to clients; recognising the signs of dysfunction, and understanding that transparency is a cure rather than a symptom, helps leaders diagnose and repair unhealthy organisational dynamics." },
  { id: "rml20_070", section: "therapy",
    q: "Managing transference and countertransference is critical in which of the following theory-based models of therapy?",
    options: { a: "behaviour modification", b: "cognitive-behavioural therapy", c: "psychodynamic models of therapy", d: "solution focused models of therapy" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "Behaviour modification focuses on observable behaviour and reinforcement, not unconscious transference dynamics.",
      b: "Cognitive-behavioural therapy targets thoughts and behaviours; transference is not its central mechanism.",
      d: "Solution-focused therapy concentrates on goals and future solutions, not on transference and countertransference." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is psychodynamic models of therapy. Transference and countertransference are concepts originating in Sigmund Freud's psychoanalysis and central to psychodynamic therapy. Transference is the client's unconscious redirection onto the therapist of feelings, attitudes, and expectations originally associated with significant figures from their past, such as a parent; a client might, for instance, react to the therapist with the anger or dependency once directed at a caregiver. Countertransference is the therapist's emotional reaction to the client, which may likewise stem from the therapist's own unresolved feelings. In psychodynamic therapy, managing and interpreting these phenomena is critical and therapeutic: the transference relationship becomes a live arena in which the client's core relational patterns are re-enacted and can be understood and worked through, while the therapist must monitor countertransference to avoid distorting the treatment and to use their reactions as information. The distractors emphasise different mechanisms: behaviour modification (option a) focuses on observable behaviour and reinforcement; cognitive-behavioural therapy (option b), associated with Aaron Beck and Albert Ellis, targets maladaptive thoughts and behaviours; and solution-focused therapy (option d), developed by Steve de Shazer and Insoo Kim Berg, concentrates on goals, strengths, and future solutions rather than the past or the therapeutic relationship's unconscious dynamics. Recognising transference as a psychodynamic concept matters because it defines what is distinctive about that tradition's use of the therapeutic relationship." },
  { id: "rml20_071", section: "stats",
    q: "Standard deviation is used to",
    options: { a: "collect data", b: "measure variability", c: "summarise data", d: "measure correlation" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "Collecting data is a stage of research, not the function of the standard deviation, which is computed after data are gathered.",
      c: "Summarising data is too general; measures of central tendency also summarise data, whereas standard deviation specifically measures spread.",
      d: "Measuring correlation is done by correlation coefficients, not the standard deviation." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is to measure variability. The standard deviation is a descriptive statistic that measures the variability, or spread, of a set of scores around their mean. It indicates, on average, how far individual data points deviate from the mean: a small standard deviation means the scores cluster tightly around the mean (low variability), while a large standard deviation means the scores are widely dispersed (high variability). It is calculated as the square root of the variance, which is the average of the squared deviations from the mean. The distractors describe other functions: collecting data (option a) is a phase of the research process that occurs before any statistic is computed, not the purpose of the standard deviation; summarising data (option c) is too broad and imprecise, since measures of central tendency such as the mean also summarise data, whereas the standard deviation specifically summarises spread rather than the centre; and measuring correlation (option d) is the job of correlation coefficients such as Pearson's r (developed by Karl Pearson), which quantify the relationship between two variables. The standard deviation is one of the most important statistics in psychology because it underlies the normal distribution, z-scores, and standardised test interpretation, and it captures the crucial dimension of how much scores differ from one another, complementing measures of central tendency in fully describing a distribution." },
  { id: "rml20_072", section: "psychopathology",
    q: "Which of the following symptoms do anorexia nervosa and bulimia nervosa have in common?",
    options: { a: "distorted body image", b: "intense fear of being fat", c: "eating regulated by psychological and not physical processes", d: "all of the above" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: {
      a: "Distorted body image is shared by both disorders, but choosing only a ignores the other shared features.",
      b: "An intense fear of being fat is common to both, but selecting only b overlooks the other shared symptoms.",
      c: "Eating driven by psychological rather than physical processes is shared, but choosing only c misses that all listed features are common." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is all of the above. Anorexia nervosa and bulimia nervosa are both eating disorders that, despite their behavioural differences, share several core psychological features. First, both involve a distorted body image (option a): sufferers perceive their body size and shape inaccurately, typically seeing themselves as larger or fatter than they are, and their self-worth is excessively tied to weight and shape. Second, both are driven by an intense fear of being fat or gaining weight (option b), a relentless dread that motivates their disordered behaviours. Third, in both disorders eating is regulated by psychological rather than physical processes (option c): eating (or restricting, bingeing, and purging) is governed by emotional and cognitive factors, control, anxiety, self-image, rather than by genuine physiological hunger and satiety cues. Because all three features are shared, option d is correct. The key behavioural difference lies elsewhere: anorexia nervosa centres on extreme restriction of food intake and very low body weight, whereas bulimia nervosa involves cycles of binge eating followed by compensatory behaviours such as vomiting or excessive exercise, usually at a more normal weight. Understanding the shared psychopathology matters clinically because it points to common treatment targets, addressing body image distortion, fear of weight gain, and the psychological control of eating, which is central to therapies such as cognitive-behavioural therapy for eating disorders." },
  { id: "rml20_073", section: "social",
    q: "Descriptive knowledge specific to different racial, cultural, and ethnic groups is referred to as:",
    options: { a: "ethno-psychotherapy", b: "ethnic sensitive practice", c: "sensitive training", d: "matching" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "Ethno-psychotherapy is not the standard term for descriptive cultural knowledge guiding practice; it is a distractor.",
      c: "Sensitivity training refers to group experiences aimed at increasing self-awareness, not knowledge of specific cultural groups.",
      d: "Matching refers to pairing clients with similar workers, not the body of descriptive cultural knowledge itself." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is ethnic sensitive practice. Ethnic sensitive practice is an approach in social work that integrates descriptive knowledge specific to different racial, cultural, and ethnic groups into the helping process, so that practice is attuned to clients' cultural backgrounds, values, histories, and experiences. It was articulated notably by Wynetta Devore and Elfriede Schlesinger, who emphasised understanding the unique circumstances, strengths, and needs arising from a client's ethnic reality, including the influence of ethnicity and social class on the problems clients face. This body of culturally specific descriptive knowledge allows practitioners to avoid imposing majority-culture assumptions and to respond respectfully and effectively to diverse clients. The distractors are related but distinct: ethno-psychotherapy (option a) is not the standard term for this descriptive knowledge base; sensitivity training (option c) refers to structured group experiences, historically associated with the T-group tradition of Kurt Lewin's National Training Laboratories, designed to increase participants' self-awareness and interpersonal sensitivity rather than to convey knowledge of specific ethnic groups; and matching (option d) refers to the practice of pairing a client with a worker of similar background, which is a strategy rather than the knowledge itself. Ethnic sensitive practice matters because culturally competent care improves engagement, trust, and outcomes with diverse populations, a central concern in contemporary clinical and social work practice." },
  { id: "rml20_074", section: "cognitive",
    q: "In Pavlov's experiment with dogs, the sound of the bell/metronome was the:",
    options: { a: "operant stimulus after food was associated", b: "neutral stimulus before the food was associated and conditioned stimulus after food was associated", c: "unconditioned stimulus before the food was associated", d: "conditioned stimulus before the food was associated and neutral stimulus after food was associated" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "Operant stimulus is a term from operant conditioning, not Pavlov's classical conditioning, so this mislabels the bell.",
      c: "The bell is not an unconditioned stimulus; the food is the unconditioned stimulus that naturally elicits salivation.",
      d: "This reverses the sequence; the bell starts as neutral and becomes conditioned, not the other way around." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is that the bell was a neutral stimulus before the food was associated and a conditioned stimulus after food was associated. In Ivan Pavlov's classic experiment on classical conditioning, the food is the unconditioned stimulus (UCS) because it naturally and automatically elicits salivation, the unconditioned response (UCR), without any learning. The bell or metronome, before conditioning, is a neutral stimulus (NS): on its own it produces no salivation because the dog has no reason to associate it with food. During conditioning, the bell is repeatedly paired with the food, ringing just before the food appears. After sufficient pairings, the bell alone comes to elicit salivation; at this point it has become a conditioned stimulus (CS), and the salivation it now produces is the conditioned response (CR). Thus the correct description tracks the bell's change in status: neutral before the association is learned, conditioned after. The distractors err in various ways: option a misapplies operant conditioning terminology (associated with B.F. Skinner) to what is a classical conditioning paradigm; option c wrongly labels the bell as the unconditioned stimulus, a role belonging to the food; and option d reverses the correct temporal sequence. Understanding this labelling is fundamental to learning theory, because the transformation of a neutral stimulus into a conditioned stimulus through association is the essence of classical conditioning." },
  { id: "rml20_075", section: "social",
    q: "Arrange the process of communication in sequence",
    options: { a: "coding, decoding, recipient, channel", b: "channel, encoding, recipient, decoding", c: "channel, decoding, recipient, encoding", d: "encoding, channel, recipient, decoding" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: {
      a: "This places decoding before the message even travels through a channel, which is out of order.",
      b: "Starting with the channel before the message is encoded reverses the natural sequence.",
      c: "This begins with the channel and places decoding before the recipient receives the message, which is incorrect." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is encoding, channel, recipient, decoding. The communication process, as described in classic models such as the Shannon-Weaver model developed by Claude Shannon and Warren Weaver, follows a logical sequence. It begins with a sender who has an idea and must first perform encoding: translating the intended message into symbols, words, gestures, or signals that can be transmitted. The encoded message then travels through a channel, the medium of transmission such as speech, writing, telephone, or email. The message reaches the recipient (receiver), the person for whom it is intended. Finally, the recipient performs decoding: interpreting and making sense of the received symbols to reconstruct the sender's meaning. This ordered flow, encode, transmit through channel, receive, decode, is the natural logic of communication, often completed by feedback from the receiver back to the sender. The distractors scramble this order: option a places decoding before the message has even travelled through a channel; option b begins with the channel before any message has been encoded; and option c both starts with the channel and places decoding before the recipient receives anything. Understanding the correct sequence matters because breakdowns can occur at any stage, faulty encoding, a noisy channel, or misinterpretation during decoding, and recognising where communication fails is essential to interpersonal skills, counselling, and effective professional practice." },
  { id: "rml20_076", section: "psychopathology",
    q: "Which of the following is NOT one of the Big Five Traits?",
    options: { a: "sense of humour", b: "openness to experience", c: "conscientiousness", d: "extraversion" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: {
      b: "Openness to experience is a genuine Big Five trait, so it is not the answer.",
      c: "Conscientiousness is one of the five factors, so it is not the answer.",
      d: "Extraversion is a core Big Five dimension, so it is not the answer." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is sense of humour, which is NOT one of the Big Five traits. The Big Five, or five-factor model, developed and validated by researchers including Paul Costa, Robert McCrae, and Lewis Goldberg, describes personality in terms of five broad dimensions summarised by the acronym OCEAN: Openness to experience (imaginative and curious versus conventional), Conscientiousness (organised and dependable versus careless), Extraversion (outgoing and energetic versus reserved), Agreeableness (compassionate and cooperative versus antagonistic), and Neuroticism (emotionally reactive and anxious versus stable). Among the options, openness to experience (b), conscientiousness (c), and extraversion (d) are all genuine factors within this model. Sense of humour (a), while an important and appealing personal quality, is not one of the five factors; it is a specific characteristic that may relate loosely to traits like extraversion or openness but does not constitute a distinct dimension in the model. The trap lies in the fact that sense of humour sounds like a plausible personality descriptor, but the Big Five is a specific empirical taxonomy, and only the five OCEAN dimensions qualify. Knowing the exact five factors matters because the Big Five is the most widely researched and cross-culturally validated framework in personality psychology, and students must be able to distinguish the genuine factors from everyday trait words that do not belong to the model." },
  { id: "rml20_077", section: "assessment",
    q: "Which of these projective tests uses the perception of inkblots to assess respondent's personality types?",
    options: { a: "Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory", b: "Thematic Apperception Test", c: "Myers-Briggs Type Indicator Test", d: "Rorschach Test" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: {
      a: "The MMPI is an objective self-report inventory, not a projective inkblot test.",
      b: "The Thematic Apperception Test uses ambiguous pictures and story-telling, not inkblots.",
      c: "The Myers-Briggs Type Indicator is a self-report questionnaire based on Jungian types, not an inkblot test." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is the Rorschach Test. The Rorschach Inkblot Test was created by the Swiss psychiatrist Hermann Rorschach in 1921. It is a projective test consisting of ten standardised inkblot cards, some black-and-white and some coloured, which the respondent is asked to look at and describe what they see. The underlying assumption of projective testing is that when confronted with ambiguous, unstructured stimuli, people project their unconscious needs, conflicts, thoughts, and personality characteristics onto the stimulus, so their responses reveal aspects of their inner world. The examiner analyses not only what the person sees but how they see it, using systems such as the Exner Comprehensive System. The distractors are different kinds of tests: the Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory (option a) is an objective, self-report personality inventory using true-false items, not a projective test; the Thematic Apperception Test (option b), developed by Henry Murray and Christiana Morgan, is also projective but uses ambiguous pictures about which the respondent tells stories, not inkblots; and the Myers-Briggs Type Indicator (option c) is a self-report questionnaire based on Carl Jung's theory of psychological types. Recognising the Rorschach as the inkblot test matters because it is the archetypal projective technique, illustrating the projective hypothesis and remaining a widely known, if controversial, tool in personality assessment." },
  { id: "rml20_078", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Schachter and Singer's theory of emotion differs from the theories of James-Lange and Cannon-Bard. How does it differ?",
    options: { a: "Schachter and Singer's theory of emotion includes development and arousal", b: "Schachter and Singer's theory of emotion includes the limbic system", c: "Schachter and Singer's theory includes cognition and arousal", d: "Schachter and Singer's theory of emotion has never been criticised" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "The theory emphasises cognition, not development; development is not the distinguishing feature.",
      b: "The limbic system is more central to Cannon-Bard's account; Schachter-Singer's distinctive contribution is cognitive appraisal.",
      d: "The claim that a theory has never been criticised is implausible and false; the two-factor theory has been extensively critiqued." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is that Schachter and Singer's theory includes cognition and arousal. Stanley Schachter and Jerome Singer proposed the two-factor theory of emotion in 1962, which holds that emotion arises from the combination of two ingredients: physiological arousal and a cognitive label or interpretation of that arousal. According to this view, when we experience bodily arousal, we look to the situation and context to interpret and label what we are feeling, and that cognitive appraisal determines which specific emotion we experience. Their famous experiment, in which participants injected with adrenaline interpreted their arousal as either euphoria or anger depending on the confederate's behaviour, supported the idea that the same physiological state can be labelled as different emotions depending on cognition. This is precisely what distinguishes their theory from its predecessors: the James-Lange theory (William James and Carl Lange) said emotion is simply the perception of bodily changes, and the Cannon-Bard theory (Walter Cannon and Philip Bard) said arousal and emotion occur simultaneously and independently, but neither gave cognition a central role. Schachter and Singer's key innovation was adding cognitive interpretation of arousal. Option a wrongly emphasises development, option b wrongly centres the limbic system (more relevant to Cannon-Bard), and option d makes the implausible claim of no criticism. Understanding the two-factor theory matters because it launched the cognitive appraisal tradition in emotion research, later extended by theorists like Richard Lazarus." },
  { id: "rml20_079", section: "cognitive",
    q: "Conditioned response may be eliminated by withdrawing reinforcement. This is known as:",
    options: { a: "stimulus generalisation", b: "extinction", c: "discrimination", d: "spontaneous recovery" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "Stimulus generalisation is responding to stimuli similar to the conditioned stimulus, not the elimination of the response.",
      c: "Discrimination is learning to respond only to the specific conditioned stimulus and not to similar ones, not elimination of the response.",
      d: "Spontaneous recovery is the reappearance of an extinguished response after a rest, the opposite of elimination." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is extinction. In both classical conditioning (studied by Ivan Pavlov) and operant conditioning (studied by B.F. Skinner), extinction is the weakening and eventual disappearance of a conditioned response when the reinforcement or the pairing that maintained it is withdrawn. In classical conditioning, if the conditioned stimulus (for example, Pavlov's bell) is repeatedly presented without the unconditioned stimulus (the food), the conditioned response (salivation) gradually diminishes until it no longer occurs. In operant conditioning, a behaviour that is no longer reinforced becomes less frequent and eventually stops. The distractors are related learning phenomena: stimulus generalisation (option a) is the tendency to give the conditioned response to stimuli that resemble the original conditioned stimulus, such as salivating to a bell of a slightly different pitch; discrimination (option c) is the learned ability to respond only to the specific conditioned stimulus and not to similar ones, the counterpart of generalisation; and spontaneous recovery (option d) is the reappearance of a previously extinguished response after a rest period, showing that extinction suppresses rather than completely erases the learned association. Understanding extinction matters because it is the basis of important clinical interventions, such as exposure therapy for phobias and anxiety, where repeated exposure to a feared stimulus without the feared consequence extinguishes the conditioned fear response." },
  { id: "rml20_080", section: "psychopathology",
    q: "Comorbidity refers to:",
    options: { a: "the frequency of mental illness", b: "two or more causes of death indicated for patients", c: "the occurrence of 2 or more disorders at the same time", d: "the degree of suffering of mentally ill patients" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "The frequency of mental illness describes prevalence or incidence, not the co-occurrence of disorders.",
      b: "Two or more causes of death is a mortality concept, not the simultaneous presence of disorders in a living patient.",
      d: "The degree of suffering describes severity or distress, not the presence of multiple disorders together." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is the occurrence of two or more disorders at the same time. Comorbidity refers to the simultaneous presence of two or more distinct disorders or illnesses in the same individual. In psychology and psychiatry, it commonly describes a person who meets diagnostic criteria for more than one mental disorder at once, for example someone with both major depressive disorder and generalised anxiety disorder, or both a substance use disorder and post-traumatic stress disorder. Comorbidity is extremely common in mental health and has important clinical implications: it often complicates diagnosis, tends to be associated with greater symptom severity, poorer prognosis, and more complex treatment needs, because the interacting disorders may reinforce one another. The distractors describe different epidemiological or clinical concepts: the frequency of mental illness (option a) refers to prevalence or incidence, how common a disorder is in a population; two or more causes of death (option b) is a mortality concept unrelated to co-occurring disorders in a living person; and the degree of suffering (option d) refers to severity or the level of distress, not the presence of multiple conditions. Understanding comorbidity matters because real clients rarely present with a single, isolated disorder, and effective assessment and treatment planning must account for the multiple interacting conditions that frequently occur together." },
  { id: "rml20_081", section: "development",
    q: "The ___ complex is to girls as the ___ complex is to boys.",
    options: { a: "electra, oedipus", b: "oedipus, electra", c: "oral, phallic", d: "phallic, oral" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: {
      b: "This reverses the pairing; the Oedipus complex applies to boys and the Electra complex to girls, not the other way around.",
      c: "Oral and phallic are psychosexual stages, not the complexes that describe attachment to the opposite-sex parent.",
      d: "Phallic and oral are stages of development, not the named complexes for girls and boys." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is electra, oedipus. In Sigmund Freud's theory of psychosexual development, during the phallic stage (roughly ages 3 to 6) the child experiences unconscious sexual desire for the opposite-sex parent and rivalry with the same-sex parent. For boys, Freud called this the Oedipus complex, named after the Greek myth of Oedipus, who unknowingly killed his father and married his mother; the boy desires his mother, views his father as a rival, and fears castration, eventually resolving the conflict by identifying with the father. For girls, the parallel is the Electra complex, a term actually coined by Carl Jung (though the concept is Freud's), named after the Greek figure Electra who sought revenge against her mother; the girl is said to desire her father and experience penis envy, resolving the conflict by identifying with the mother. Thus the Electra complex is to girls as the Oedipus complex is to boys, making option a correct; option b reverses them. Options c and d list psychosexual stages (oral, phallic) rather than the named complexes. Although these ideas are heavily criticised and no longer scientifically accepted, they remain staple exam content because they are central to Freud's account of how gender identity, morality (the superego), and adult relationships supposedly develop through the resolution of these childhood conflicts." },
  { id: "rml20_082", section: "systems",
    q: "All of the following are common group properties EXCEPT:",
    options: { a: "all groups have a purpose", b: "all groups go through stages of development", c: "all groups have an analytic base", d: "all groups have criteria for their composition" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "Having a purpose is a genuine common property of groups, so it is not the exception.",
      b: "Going through stages of development is a common group property, so it is not the exception.",
      d: "Having criteria for composition is a common group property, so it is not the exception." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is that all groups have an analytic base, which is the EXCEPTION. In social group work, groups share several common properties that characterise how they form and function. These include having a purpose, some reason for the group's existence and a goal it works toward (option a); going through stages of development, following recognisable phases such as Bruce Tuckman's forming, storming, norming, performing sequence (option b); and having criteria for their composition, some basis on which members are included or the group is put together, such as shared characteristics, needs, or goals (option d). What is not a universal property of all groups is having an analytic base (option c). An analytic base refers to a specific theoretical, often psychoanalytic, framework underpinning the group's work, which is characteristic only of certain types of therapy or treatment groups, not of groups in general. Many groups, task groups, support groups, natural friendship groups, and committees, function perfectly well without any analytic theoretical foundation. Recognising this distinction matters in group work practice because it separates the universal, defining features that every group possesses from the specialised characteristics that belong only to particular kinds of groups, helping a worker understand what to expect from any group versus what depends on its specific type and theoretical orientation." },
  { id: "rml20_083", section: "assessment",
    q: "Projective tests claim to reveal information about:",
    options: { a: "intellectual attainment", b: "unconscious processes", c: "parenting style", d: "career aptitude" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "Intellectual attainment is measured by intelligence and achievement tests, not projective techniques.",
      c: "Parenting style is assessed through specific questionnaires or observation, not projective tests of personality.",
      d: "Career aptitude is measured by vocational interest and aptitude inventories, not projective tests." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is unconscious processes. Projective tests are personality assessment tools built on the projective hypothesis: the idea that when a person is presented with an ambiguous, unstructured stimulus, they will project their own inner needs, feelings, conflicts, motives, and unconscious processes onto it, so their interpretations reveal aspects of their hidden psychological makeup. This approach is rooted in psychoanalytic theory, tracing to Sigmund Freud's emphasis on the unconscious. Classic examples include the Rorschach Inkblot Test, created by Hermann Rorschach, in which respondents describe what they see in inkblots, and the Thematic Apperception Test, developed by Henry Murray and Christiana Morgan, in which respondents tell stories about ambiguous pictures. Because the stimuli are ambiguous, there are no right or wrong answers, and the assumption is that responses spring from unconscious material the person cannot or would not consciously report. The distractors point to other assessment domains: intellectual attainment (option a) is measured by intelligence and achievement tests; parenting style (option c) by specific scales or observation; and career aptitude (option d) by vocational interest and aptitude inventories. Understanding what projective tests claim to reveal matters because it clarifies their theoretical purpose, to access unconscious dynamics, while also inviting critical evaluation, since projective tests have been criticised for low reliability and validity compared with objective measures." },
  { id: "rml20_084", section: "psychopathology",
    q: "Rohan is self-disciplined, focused on achievement and keen to do his duty. He would be expected to score highly on:",
    options: { a: "neuroticism", b: "agreeableness", c: "extraversion", d: "conscientiousness" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: {
      a: "Neuroticism concerns emotional instability and anxiety, not self-discipline and dutifulness.",
      b: "Agreeableness concerns compassion and cooperativeness, not achievement focus and self-discipline.",
      c: "Extraversion concerns sociability and energy, not the orderly, duty-bound qualities described." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is conscientiousness. Conscientiousness is one of the five factors in the Big Five model of personality, developed and validated by researchers including Paul Costa and Robert McCrae. It describes the degree to which a person is organised, responsible, self-disciplined, dependable, achievement-oriented, and dutiful. People high in conscientiousness are careful, goal-directed, hardworking, and reliable; they plan ahead, follow rules, control their impulses, and strive to meet their obligations. Rohan's description, self-disciplined, focused on achievement, and keen to do his duty, maps directly onto the core facets of conscientiousness, which include self-discipline, achievement-striving, and dutifulness, so he would score highly on this dimension. The distractors correspond to other Big Five factors that do not fit the description: neuroticism (option a) is about emotional instability, anxiety, and proneness to negative emotions, which the description does not mention; agreeableness (option b) is about being compassionate, cooperative, warm, and trusting toward others, again not the qualities listed; and extraversion (option c) is about sociability, assertiveness, and energy in social situations, which is unrelated to self-discipline and duty. Understanding conscientiousness matters because it is one of the most predictive of the Big Five traits for real-world outcomes, strongly associated with academic and job performance, health behaviours, and long-term life success, making it a frequent focus in both research and applied assessment." },
  { id: "rml20_085", section: "biopsych",
    q: "If Ramesh works in a call centre, and has to work very hard, do a lot of work, and that too at a fast rate, he experiences:",
    options: { a: "Conflict", b: "Frustration", c: "Unpredictability", d: "Pressure" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: {
      a: "Conflict is stress arising from having to choose between incompatible goals, not from a heavy, fast workload.",
      b: "Frustration is stress from being blocked from a goal, not from demanding workload demands.",
      c: "Unpredictability refers to stress from not knowing what will happen, not from the sheer volume and pace of work." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is pressure. In the psychology of stress, several distinct sources of stress are commonly distinguished, and pressure is the demand to perform, produce, or behave in a certain way, often at a high level, at speed, or to meet expectations and deadlines. Ramesh's situation, having to work very hard, complete a large volume of work, and do so at a fast rate, is a textbook example of pressure: he faces intense demands on his performance and pace. The distractors describe other sources of stress: conflict (option a) arises when a person must choose between incompatible goals or motives, such as an approach-avoidance conflict studied by Kurt Lewin, and is about competing demands rather than workload; frustration (option b) is the stress that results when progress toward a goal is blocked or thwarted, such as being unable to complete a task because of obstacles; and unpredictability (option c) is the stress that comes from not knowing what will happen or being unable to anticipate events, which increases stress because control and predictability tend to reduce it. Understanding these categories matters because identifying the specific source of a person's stress guides how to address it: pressure calls for time management, workload adjustment, and coping skills, whereas conflict, frustration, and unpredictability each require different strategies, a distinction central to stress and health psychology." },
  { id: "rml20_086", section: "therapy",
    q: "behavioural therapies applying learning principles to:",
    options: { a: "identify and treat the underlying causes of dysfunction", b: "improve learning and insight", c: "eliminate the unwanted behaviour", d: "improve communication and social sensitivity" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "Identifying and treating underlying causes reflects the psychodynamic approach, not behavioural therapy's focus on observable behaviour.",
      b: "Improving insight is a goal of insight-oriented and psychodynamic therapies, not the behavioural focus on changing behaviour.",
      d: "Improving communication and social sensitivity describes humanistic or group approaches, not the core aim of behavioural therapy." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is to eliminate the unwanted behaviour. Behavioural therapies apply the principles of learning, classical conditioning (Ivan Pavlov) and operant conditioning (B.F. Skinner), directly to changing observable behaviour. Their central aim is to modify maladaptive behaviours by eliminating or reducing unwanted behaviours and strengthening desirable ones, using techniques such as systematic desensitization, exposure, reinforcement, extinction, and token economies. Crucially, behavioural therapy focuses on the behaviour itself as the problem to be treated, rather than searching for hidden underlying causes; it assumes that maladaptive behaviours are learned and can therefore be unlearned or replaced through new learning. This distinguishes it sharply from the distractors. Option a, identifying and treating the underlying causes of dysfunction, reflects the psychodynamic approach derived from Sigmund Freud, which seeks the unconscious roots of symptoms rather than targeting behaviour directly. Option b, improving learning and insight, describes insight-oriented therapies that aim for self-understanding, not the behavioural goal of behaviour change. Option d, improving communication and social sensitivity, points toward humanistic or interpersonal and group approaches. Understanding that behavioural therapy targets the elimination of unwanted behaviour through learning principles matters because it defines the behavioural tradition's distinctive, empirically grounded philosophy: change what people do by changing the contingencies and associations that maintain their behaviour, without necessarily exploring underlying conflicts." },
  { id: "rml20_087", section: "social",
    q: "Which one may be defined as a method of measuring an individual's social behaviour?",
    options: { a: "Sociometry", b: "Sample method", c: "Verstehen method", d: "Interview method" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: {
      b: "The sample method concerns selecting participants from a population, not measuring social behaviour.",
      c: "The Verstehen method is an interpretive, understanding-based approach to social research, not a measurement of social behaviour.",
      d: "The interview method is a data-collection technique through questioning, not specifically a measure of social behaviour and relationships." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is sociometry. Sociometry is a method developed by Jacob Levy Moreno for measuring and analysing an individual's social behaviour and the patterns of relationships within a group. It quantifies interpersonal relations by asking group members about their preferences, for example, whom they would like to work with, sit next to, or spend time with, and then mapping these choices to reveal the social structure of the group, including attractions, rejections, popular members (stars), isolates, and subgroups. The results are often displayed graphically in a sociogram. Sociometry thus provides a systematic way to measure social behaviour and group dynamics. The distractors are different methodological concepts: the sample method (option b) refers to the process of selecting a representative subset of a population for study, a sampling procedure rather than a measure of social behaviour; the Verstehen method (option c), a term associated with the sociologist Max Weber, is an interpretive approach that seeks to understand social action from the actor's subjective point of view, an understanding-based method rather than a quantitative measure; and the interview method (option d) is a general data-collection technique using direct questioning, not specifically a tool for measuring social relationships. Understanding sociometry matters because it offers a concrete, measurable window into group structure and interpersonal dynamics, useful in group work, education, and organisational and social psychology for identifying isolated individuals and informal group leaders." },
  { id: "rml20_088", section: "social",
    q: "Who among the following established group work as a method within the profession of social work?",
    options: { a: "Mary Richmond", b: "Charlotte Towle", c: "Grace Coyle", d: "Geisla Konopka" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "Mary Richmond is associated with social casework, notably Social Diagnosis, not group work.",
      b: "Charlotte Towle is known for her work on human needs and social work education, not for establishing group work.",
      d: "Gisela Konopka was an important group work figure, but Grace Coyle is credited with establishing group work as a method within the profession." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Grace Coyle. Grace Coyle is widely credited as the pioneer who established social group work as a recognised method within the profession of social work. Drawing on progressive education and small-group theory, she provided much of the early theoretical foundation for group work, articulating how groups could be used purposefully to promote individual growth and democratic participation, and helping to bring group work into the mainstream of professional social work in the early-to-mid twentieth century. The distractors are all important figures in social work history but are associated with different contributions: Mary Richmond (option a) is a foundational figure in social casework, best known for her 1917 book Social Diagnosis, which systematised individual casework rather than group work; Charlotte Towle (option b) is known for her influential work on human needs, notably Common Human Needs, and for contributions to social work education, not for establishing group work; and Gisela Konopka (option d) was indeed a significant later figure in social group work, especially with children and adolescents, but the establishment of group work as a method within the profession is attributed to Grace Coyle. Knowing these attributions matters for social work history questions, because the profession developed along parallel methodological streams, casework, group work, and community organisation, each shaped by distinct pioneering theorists whose contributions students are expected to identify correctly." },
  { id: "rml20_089", section: "social",
    q: "Which of the following is not a part of the social case work process?",
    options: { a: "Content analysis", b: "Treatment", c: "Assessment", d: "Evaluation" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: {
      b: "Treatment (intervention) is a genuine phase of the casework process, so it is not the answer.",
      c: "Assessment (study and diagnosis) is a core phase of casework, so it is not the answer.",
      d: "Evaluation of progress and outcomes is part of the casework process, so it is not the answer." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is content analysis, which is NOT part of the social casework process. Social casework, systematised by Mary Richmond and refined by later theorists such as Helen Harris Perlman, is a primary method of social work that helps individuals and families address personal and social problems through a structured, individualised helping process. Its core phases typically include study or assessment, gathering information and understanding the client's situation and problem (option c); diagnosis, formulating an understanding of the problem's nature and causes; treatment or intervention, taking planned action to help the client, sometimes described within Perlman's problem-solving model (option b); and evaluation, reviewing progress and outcomes to judge effectiveness and decide on next steps (option d). These stages form the recognised casework process. Content analysis (option a), by contrast, is a research method used to systematically analyse the content of communication, texts, documents, media, or recorded material, by coding and categorising themes; it is a technique of social research, not a phase of the casework helping process. The trap is that content analysis sounds procedural and analytic, but it belongs to research methodology rather than direct practice with clients. Understanding the genuine phases of casework matters because it structures how a practitioner engages a client from initial contact through to termination, ensuring assessment, intervention, and evaluation are all deliberately carried out." },
  { id: "rml20_090", section: "general",
    q: "What do Hermann Ebbinghaus, Francis Galton and Sigmund Freud share in common?",
    options: { a: "Each of them worked independently and were pioneers in the field of psychology", b: "Each of them founded a formal psychology laboratory", c: "Each of them developed novel observational methods to study how the mind works", d: "(a) and (c)" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: {
      b: "None of these three is credited with founding the first formal psychology laboratory; that distinction belongs to Wilhelm Wundt.",
      c: "While each used methods, not all three are best characterised by novel observational methods; Freud used clinical case study, so this is not their shared commonality.",
      d: "Since b and c do not both accurately apply, the combination (a) and (c) is incorrect." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is that each of them worked independently and were pioneers in the field of psychology. Hermann Ebbinghaus, Francis Galton, and Sigmund Freud were all pioneering figures who made foundational contributions to psychology while working largely independently of one another, each opening up a different area of the discipline. Hermann Ebbinghaus pioneered the experimental study of memory, using nonsense syllables to chart the forgetting curve and the learning curve, demonstrating that higher mental processes could be studied scientifically. Francis Galton pioneered the study of individual differences, intelligence, and the measurement of mental abilities, introducing statistical concepts such as correlation and the questionnaire, and coining the phrase nature versus nurture. Sigmund Freud pioneered psychoanalysis and the study of the unconscious mind, transforming the understanding of personality, motivation, and psychopathology. What unites them is their status as independent pioneers who broke new ground. The distractors are inaccurate: option b is false because none of the three founded the first formal psychology laboratory, a distinction belonging to Wilhelm Wundt in Leipzig in 1879; and option c does not accurately capture all three, since Freud relied on clinical case study rather than novel observational methods in the experimental sense. Because c is not accurate for all three, the combined option d is also wrong. Recognising these figures as independent pioneers matters for understanding the diverse roots of modern psychology." },
  { id: "rml20_091", section: "social",
    q: "Historically direct social work practice has been conceptualised in all of the following ways EXCEPT:",
    options: { a: "Case management", b: "Individual psychotherapy", c: "Generalist practice", d: "Policy practice" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: {
      a: "Case management is a recognised form of direct social work practice, so it is not the exception.",
      b: "Individual psychotherapy is a form of direct, clinical social work practice, so it is not the exception.",
      c: "Generalist practice is a well-established conceptualisation of direct practice, so it is not the exception." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is policy practice, which is the EXCEPTION. In social work, a fundamental distinction is drawn between direct practice and indirect practice. Direct (also called clinical or micro) practice involves working face-to-face with individuals, families, and small groups to help them address problems and improve functioning. Historically, direct practice has been conceptualised in several ways, including case management, coordinating and linking clients to services and resources (option a); individual psychotherapy, providing clinical treatment for psychological and emotional difficulties (option b); and generalist practice, applying a broad, integrated repertoire of knowledge and skills across multiple system levels but centred on direct helping (option c). Policy practice (option d), by contrast, is a form of indirect (macro) practice: it involves influencing and changing social policies, laws, and institutional arrangements through advocacy, legislative action, and systemic reform, working at the level of communities, organisations, and society rather than directly with individual clients. Because policy practice operates at the macro level rather than through direct client contact, it does not belong among conceptualisations of direct practice. Understanding this micro-macro distinction matters because social work spans both, and clarity about whether an activity is direct client service or indirect systemic change shapes how practitioners define their roles, skills, and interventions across the profession's full continuum." },
  { id: "rml20_092", section: "social",
    q: "Which one of the following is NOT a principle of social group work given by H.B. Trecker?",
    options: { a: "The principle of democratic self determination", b: "The principle of flexible functional organisation", c: "The principle of progressive programme experience", d: "The principle of focusing on change and possibilities" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: {
      a: "The principle of democratic self-determination is one of Trecker's genuine group work principles, so it is not the answer.",
      b: "The principle of flexible functional organisation is among Trecker's principles, so it is not the answer.",
      c: "The principle of progressive programme experience is one of Trecker's principles, so it is not the answer." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is the principle of focusing on change and possibilities, which is NOT one of Harleigh B. Trecker's principles of social group work. Harleigh B. Trecker, a major theorist of social group work, articulated a well-known set of principles to guide practice with groups. These genuinely include the principle of democratic self-determination, respecting the group's and members' right to make their own decisions (option a); the principle of flexible functional organisation, allowing the group's structure to adapt flexibly to its functions and needs (option b); and the principle of progressive programme experience, providing group activities and experiences that develop and progress in keeping with members' evolving needs and capacities (option c). Other Trecker principles include planned group formation, specific objectives, purposeful worker-group relationships, continuous individualisation, guided group interaction, and continuous evaluation. The principle of focusing on change and possibilities (option d) is not one of Trecker's named principles; it echoes the language of later strengths-based or solution-focused approaches rather than Trecker's classical framework. The trap is that focusing on change and possibilities sounds like a plausible, positive practice principle, but the question asks specifically about Trecker's enumerated principles, and this phrasing is not among them. Knowing Trecker's principles matters for social group work examinations because they form a canonical list that structures how a worker purposefully guides group formation, interaction, and development toward the members' growth." },
  { id: "rml20_093", section: "biopsych",
    q: "The basic unit of the entire nervous system is the:",
    options: { a: "dendrite", b: "neuron", c: "brain", d: "glial cell" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "A dendrite is only a part of a neuron (the receiving branch), not the basic unit itself.",
      c: "The brain is a whole organ composed of billions of units, not the basic unit of the nervous system.",
      d: "Glial cells support and nourish neurons but are not the fundamental signalling unit of the nervous system." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is the neuron. The neuron, or nerve cell, is the basic structural and functional unit of the entire nervous system. Neurons are specialised cells that receive, process, and transmit information through electrical and chemical signals, and it is their communication, across trillions of connections, that underlies all sensation, movement, thought, emotion, and behaviour. A typical neuron has three main parts: dendrites, the branching extensions that receive incoming signals from other neurons; the cell body (soma), which contains the nucleus and integrates the signals; and the axon, a long fibre that carries the electrical impulse away from the cell body to communicate with other neurons across junctions called synapses, where neurotransmitters are released. The neuron doctrine, establishing the neuron as the fundamental unit, was championed by Santiago Ramon y Cajal, whose detailed drawings earned him a Nobel Prize. The distractors are related but incorrect: a dendrite (option a) is only one part of a neuron, its receiving branch, not the whole unit; the brain (option c) is an entire organ made up of billions of neurons, far larger than a basic unit; and glial cells (option d) are important support cells that nourish, insulate, and protect neurons but do not themselves carry the primary information-processing signals. Understanding the neuron as the basic unit is foundational to all of biopsychology and neuroscience." },
  { id: "rml20_094", section: "social",
    q: "Which one of the following is not a technique of case work?",
    options: { a: "interviewing", b: "observation", c: "counselling", d: "lobbying" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: {
      a: "Interviewing is a core casework technique for gathering information and building relationship, so it is not the answer.",
      b: "Observation is a genuine casework technique for understanding the client, so it is not the answer.",
      c: "Counselling is a central technique of casework, so it is not the answer." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is lobbying, which is NOT a technique of casework. Social casework is a direct (micro) method of social work that helps individuals and families through a one-to-one, relationship-based process, and its techniques are those used in direct work with clients. These include interviewing, the primary technique for gathering information, exploring the client's situation, and building the professional relationship (option a); observation, systematically noting the client's behaviour, demeanour, environment, and interactions to deepen understanding (option b); and counselling, providing guidance, support, and therapeutic conversation to help the client understand and address their difficulties (option c). Lobbying (option d), by contrast, is a technique of macro practice, specifically social action and policy practice: it involves attempting to influence legislators, officials, and decision-makers to change laws, policies, or resource allocations. Lobbying operates at the level of systems and society, not in direct one-to-one work with an individual client, so it does not belong among casework techniques. The trap is that lobbying is a legitimate and important social work activity, so students may be tempted, but the question asks specifically about casework, the direct-practice method, and lobbying belongs to the macro, indirect side of the profession. Recognising this distinction matters because it reinforces the boundary between micro techniques used with individual clients and macro strategies used to change larger systems." },
  { id: "rml20_095", section: "development",
    q: "Which of the following is FALSE regarding Piaget?",
    options: { a: "Piaget argued that a child understands an object by acting on it either physically or mentally and thereby constructs knowledge", b: "Infants develop cognitive structures or schemes, which are organized patterns of actions that reflect a particular way of interacting with the environment", c: "Cognitive structures of younger children, under the age of 7, reflect abstract mental operations", d: "The cognitive operations of older children allow them to realize that quantities remain constant (are conserved) despite changes in appearance" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "Piaget did argue that children construct knowledge by acting on objects physically or mentally, so this statement is true, not false.",
      b: "Piaget did describe schemes as organized patterns of action for interacting with the environment, so this is true.",
      d: "Older children in the concrete operational stage do achieve conservation, so this statement is true." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is that cognitive structures of younger children, under the age of 7, reflect abstract mental operations, which is FALSE regarding Jean Piaget's theory. Piaget, the Swiss developmental psychologist and founder of constructivism, held that children actively construct knowledge by acting on the world, so statements a, b, and d accurately reflect his views. He argued that a child understands an object by acting on it physically or mentally, thereby constructing knowledge (option a, true); that infants develop schemes, organized patterns of action reflecting particular ways of interacting with the environment (option b, true); and that older children, in the concrete operational stage (roughly ages 7 to 11), can grasp conservation, understanding that quantities remain constant despite changes in appearance (option d, true). Statement c is false because, in Piaget's theory, abstract mental operations do not appear until the formal operational stage, which begins around age 11 or 12, not in younger children under 7. Children under 7 are in the sensorimotor and preoperational stages, whose thinking is tied to concrete, perceptual experience and marked by egocentrism and centration, not abstract reasoning. The trap is that this statement sounds plausible but misassigns abstract operations to the wrong age. Recognising it matters because a central claim of Piaget's stage theory is that abstract, hypothetical reasoning is a late achievement, and mistaking when it emerges misrepresents his entire developmental sequence." },
  { id: "rml20_096", section: "social",
    q: "Research suggests that we seek out the company of others because of which motives?",
    options: { a: "To obtain information", b: "To reduce anxiety", c: "To compare ourselves with similar others", d: "All of the above" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: {
      a: "Obtaining information is a genuine affiliation motive, but choosing only a ignores the other established motives.",
      b: "Reducing anxiety is a real reason for affiliation, but selecting only b overlooks the other motives.",
      c: "Social comparison is a valid affiliation motive, but choosing only c misses that all listed motives apply." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is all of the above. Research on affiliation, the tendency to seek the company of others, identifies several motives, all of which are supported. First, we affiliate to obtain information (option a): being with others, especially in uncertain or novel situations, gives us knowledge about the environment and about how to behave. Second, we affiliate to reduce anxiety (option b): the presence of others can be calming, and classic research by Stanley Schachter showed that people facing a frightening situation preferred to wait with others rather than alone, especially with others in the same predicament, because misery loves miserable company. Third, we affiliate to compare ourselves with similar others (option c): according to social comparison theory, developed by Leon Festinger, we have a drive to evaluate our own opinions and abilities, and when objective standards are lacking we do so by comparing ourselves with other people, particularly those similar to us. Because all three motives are well established, option d is correct. The trap in options a, b, and c is that each is individually true but incomplete, and only the combined answer captures the full picture. Understanding these affiliation motives matters because the need to belong is a fundamental human drive with deep implications for social support, coping with stress, self-evaluation, and mental health, central themes in social and clinical psychology." },
  { id: "rml20_097", section: "development",
    q: "Which of the following shows the stages in the correct developmental order?",
    options: { a: "oral, anal, phallic, latency, genital", b: "latency, oral, genital, anal, phallic", c: "phallic, anal, oral, latency, genital", d: "oral, genital, phallic, latency, anal" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: {
      b: "This ordering starts with latency and scrambles the sequence, which is incorrect.",
      c: "This reverses the early stages (phallic before anal before oral), which is wrong.",
      d: "This places genital far too early and ends with anal, disrupting the correct sequence." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is oral, anal, phallic, latency, genital. Sigmund Freud's theory of psychosexual development proposes that personality develops through five stages, each defined by the erogenous zone that is the focus of the child's libidinal energy, and progressing in a fixed sequence from infancy to adulthood. The oral stage (birth to about 18 months) centres on the mouth, with pleasure from sucking and feeding. The anal stage (about 18 months to 3 years) centres on bowel and bladder control, coinciding with toilet training. The phallic stage (about 3 to 6 years) centres on the genitals and is when the Oedipus and Electra complexes arise and identification with the same-sex parent occurs. The latency stage (about 6 years to puberty) is a period of dormant sexual interest, during which the child focuses energy on school, friendships, and skill-building. Finally, the genital stage (puberty onward) sees the re-emergence of sexual interest, now directed toward mature adult relationships. Only option a lists these in the correct order; the other options scramble the sequence. According to Freud, fixation at any stage due to over- or under-gratification leaves a lasting mark on adult personality. Although the theory is scientifically contested, knowing the correct order of the psychosexual stages is a staple of exams and remains central to understanding Freud's developmental framework." },
  { id: "rml20_098", section: "social",
    q: "All of the following are categories of child neglect EXCEPT:",
    options: { a: "use of licensed day care", b: "health neglect: failure to get child immunise, seek proper medical attention or comply with medical directives", c: "failure to provide proper nutrition, housing, clothing", d: "failure to provide supervision (children left unattended)" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: {
      b: "Health neglect (failing to obtain medical care) is a genuine category of child neglect, so it is not the exception.",
      c: "Failure to provide nutrition, housing, and clothing is physical neglect, a real category, so it is not the exception.",
      d: "Failure to provide supervision is a recognised form of neglect, so it is not the exception." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is use of licensed day care, which is the EXCEPTION because it is not a form of child neglect. Child neglect is a type of child maltreatment involving the failure of a caregiver to provide for a child's basic needs, and it is commonly divided into several categories. Physical neglect includes the failure to provide proper nutrition, housing, and clothing (option c). Medical or health neglect includes the failure to obtain immunisations, seek appropriate medical attention, or comply with medical directives (option b). Supervisory neglect includes the failure to provide adequate supervision, such as leaving children unattended in unsafe circumstances (option d). Other recognised categories include emotional neglect and educational neglect. The use of licensed day care (option a), by contrast, is not neglect at all; it is a legitimate, responsible, and often beneficial childcare arrangement in which a parent entrusts their child to a regulated, licensed provider while they work or attend to other responsibilities. Rather than a failure to meet the child's needs, using licensed day care is a way of ensuring the child is cared for safely. The trap is that the option is embedded in a list of genuine neglect categories, but it describes appropriate care, not maltreatment. Understanding the categories of neglect matters for child protection practice, because accurately distinguishing genuine neglect from acceptable caregiving choices is essential to fair and effective assessment of child welfare." },
  { id: "rml20_099", section: "therapy",
    q: "There are small-scale events that can perpetuate a 'crisis'. All of the following are such events EXCEPT:",
    options: { a: "tornado or flood", b: "violent events such as child abuse, domestic abuse, crime", c: "psychopathology that triggers a crisis i.e., suicide attempt, drug overdose", d: "car accident, life-threatening illness" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: {
      b: "Violent events like abuse and crime are indeed small-scale (individual-level) events that can perpetuate a crisis, so this is not the exception.",
      c: "Psychopathology-triggered events such as a suicide attempt or overdose are individual-level crisis events, so this is not the exception.",
      d: "A car accident or life-threatening illness is an individual-scale crisis event, so this is not the exception." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is tornado or flood, which is the EXCEPTION. In crisis theory, a distinction is drawn between large-scale events and small-scale events that can precipitate or perpetuate a crisis. Small-scale events are those affecting an individual or family at a personal level, and they include violent events such as child abuse, domestic abuse, and crime (option b); psychopathology-related events that trigger a crisis, such as a suicide attempt or drug overdose (option c); and personal catastrophes such as a car accident or a life-threatening illness (option d). These are individual-scale crises that disrupt a person's equilibrium and exceed their usual coping resources, the defining feature of a crisis in the model developed by theorists such as Gerald Caplan and Erich Lindemann, whose work on grief following the Coconut Grove fire helped found crisis intervention. A tornado or flood (option a), by contrast, is a large-scale event: a natural disaster affecting entire communities or regions, not a small-scale, individual-level event. Because the question asks specifically about small-scale events, the community-wide disaster is the exception. Understanding this distinction matters in crisis intervention because the scale of the precipitating event shapes the response: individual crises call for personal crisis counselling, whereas large-scale disasters require community-wide, public-health-oriented disaster response and mass intervention strategies." },
  { id: "rml20_100", section: "biopsych",
    q: "The ___ lobe processes cognitive functions and control of voluntary movement",
    options: { a: "occipital", b: "parietal", c: "temporal", d: "frontal" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: {
      a: "The occipital lobe processes vision, not higher cognition and voluntary movement.",
      b: "The parietal lobe processes somatosensory information and spatial awareness, not primarily voluntary movement control and executive cognition.",
      c: "The temporal lobe processes hearing, language comprehension, and memory, not voluntary movement." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is the frontal lobe. The frontal lobe is the largest of the four lobes of the cerebral cortex and is responsible for higher cognitive functions and the control of voluntary movement. It houses the prefrontal cortex, which governs executive functions such as planning, reasoning, decision-making, problem-solving, working memory, impulse control, and personality, and it contains the primary motor cortex, which directs voluntary movements of the body. Its importance was dramatically illustrated by the case of Phineas Gage, whose frontal lobe damage from an iron rod altered his personality and self-control, and by the work of Paul Broca, who localised speech production to Broca's area in the left frontal lobe. The distractors correspond to the other lobes and their functions: the occipital lobe (option a), at the back of the brain, is dedicated primarily to processing visual information; the parietal lobe (option b), near the top and rear, processes somatosensory information such as touch, temperature, and pain, as well as spatial awareness; and the temporal lobe (option c), at the sides, processes auditory information, language comprehension (Wernicke's area), and, through the hippocampus, memory. Understanding the frontal lobe's role in cognition and voluntary movement matters because damage to this region profoundly affects reasoning, judgement, behaviour, and motor control, making it central to neuropsychology, clinical assessment, and understanding conditions ranging from traumatic brain injury to dementia." }
],
  "rml-2021": [
  { id: "rml21_001", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Sheena is studying for her final exam in her psychology class. For every 45 minutes that she studies, she gives herself a mini chocolate, then goes back to studying. What Schedule of reinforcement is Sheena using?",
    options: { a: "Variable ratio schedule", b: "Variable interval schedule", c: "Fixed interval schedule", d: "Fixed ratio schedule" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "Variable ratio reinforces after an unpredictable number of responses (like a slot machine). Sheena is timing herself, not counting responses, so it is not ratio and not variable.",
      b: "Variable interval reinforces after unpredictable time gaps. Her interval is fixed at exactly 45 minutes, so it is not variable.",
      d: "Fixed ratio reinforces after a set number of responses, not after a set amount of time; Sheena rewards elapsed time, not a response count."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is a fixed interval schedule. Burrhus Frederic Skinner (B.F. Skinner) described four partial reinforcement schedules built from two dimensions: ratio versus interval (whether reinforcement depends on number of responses or passage of time) and fixed versus variable (whether the requirement is constant or changes). Sheena rewards herself after a constant, predictable amount of time (every 45 minutes) regardless of how much work she does in that window, which is the defining feature of a fixed interval schedule. The classic signature of fixed interval reinforcement is the scalloped response curve: behaviour slows just after reinforcement and accelerates as the next reward time approaches, because the learner can anticipate when the reward is due. Ratio schedules (fixed or variable) instead reward a count of responses and produce higher, steadier response rates. Variable schedules resist extinction best because the unpredictability keeps responding high. Recognising these four schedules is core operant-conditioning content, and the trick in this item is to notice the cue word 'minutes,' which signals an interval schedule, combined with the constant 45-minute gap, which signals fixed rather than variable." },

  { id: "rml21_002", section: "biopsych",
    q: "A scientist is studying brain function in rats and destroys the animal's ventromedial hypothalamus. Which of the following is most likely to occur?",
    options: { a: "The rat will drink excessive amounts of water.", b: "The rat will eat excessive amounts of food.", c: "The rat will refuse to drink until it is forced.", d: "The rat will refuse to eat until it is forced." },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "Drinking behaviour is regulated by the lateral hypothalamus and osmoreceptors, not the ventromedial nucleus; lesioning the VMH does not cause polydipsia.",
      c: "Refusal to drink is not the classic VMH result; this describes an aphagia/adipsia pattern tied to lateral hypothalamic damage.",
      d: "Refusal to eat (aphagia) follows destruction of the lateral hypothalamus, which is the hunger 'on' centre, the opposite structure to the one lesioned here."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is that the rat will eat excessive amounts of food. The ventromedial hypothalamus (VMH) functions as a satiety centre: it signals when the animal has eaten enough and should stop. When it is destroyed, that 'stop eating' signal is lost, producing hyperphagia and a syndrome of gross obesity, a finding demonstrated in classic lesion studies associated with researchers such as Albert Fisher and later dual-centre models. This contrasts with the lateral hypothalamus (LH), which acts as a hunger or feeding-initiation centre; lesioning the LH produces aphagia (refusal to eat) and can be fatal without force-feeding. This dual-centre hypothalamic model of hunger, one centre driving eating and one inhibiting it, is a foundational piece of biopsychology and physiological psychology. The item tests whether the student can pair the correct nucleus with the correct behavioural outcome. The key memory hook is: Ventromedial equals Very full (satiety, so lesion causes overeating), while Lateral equals Lack of eating when lesioned. Thirst and drinking are governed by separate mechanisms, so the water-related distractors are designed to mislead students who confuse feeding and drinking regulation." },

  { id: "rml21_003", section: "social",
    q: "Arvind is a college student who has strong negative feelings about smoking cigarettes. His grandfather died of lung cancer and he believes smoking is disgusting. One night at a party, Arvind's friends all go outside for a cigarette and he decides to smoke as well. What will he most likely experience as a result?",
    options: { a: "Justification of efforts", b: "Increased drive to smoke", c: "Cognitive Dissonance", d: "Drive Reduction" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "Justification of effort is a specific dissonance phenomenon where people overvalue goals they suffered for (as in initiation studies); Arvind expended no effort, so this narrower term does not fit.",
      b: "An increased drive to smoke would be a physiological or motivational craving; the scenario centres on a clash between belief and action, not a biological urge.",
      d: "Drive reduction (from Clark Hull's theory) refers to satisfying a biological need to restore homeostasis; smoking here does not reduce any drive, it creates psychological tension."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is cognitive dissonance. Leon Festinger developed the theory of cognitive dissonance in 1957, proposing that people feel an uncomfortable state of psychological tension when they hold two contradictory cognitions or when their behaviour conflicts with their attitudes. Arvind strongly believes smoking is disgusting and harmful (his grandfather died of lung cancer), yet he chooses to smoke. The clash between his anti-smoking attitude and his pro-smoking behaviour produces dissonance. Festinger argued that people are motivated to reduce this tension, typically by changing the attitude ('maybe smoking once is not so bad'), changing the behaviour (stopping), or adding consonant cognitions ('everyone was doing it, it was just social'). Festinger and James Merrill Carlsmith demonstrated this experimentally in their famous 1959 study where participants paid only one dollar to lie about a boring task later rated it as more enjoyable, because low external justification forced them to resolve dissonance internally. Distractors like justification of effort are subtypes of dissonance but require prior effort or suffering, which is absent here. This item tests whether the student can identify the general dissonance concept from an attitude-behaviour conflict scenario." },

  { id: "rml21_004", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Fixed action patterns are responses to a particular type of stimuli that result from which of the following?",
    options: { a: "Operant Conditioning", b: "Innate learning", c: "Cognitive Responses", d: "Classical Conditioning" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "Operant conditioning shapes voluntary behaviour through consequences; fixed action patterns are unlearned and stereotyped, not built up by reinforcement.",
      c: "Cognitive responses involve deliberate mental processing; fixed action patterns run automatically to completion once triggered, bypassing higher cognition.",
      d: "Classical conditioning creates learned associations between stimuli; fixed action patterns are pre-wired and species-specific, not acquired through pairing."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is innate learning (that is, innate, unlearned behaviour). Fixed action patterns (FAPs) are a central concept in ethology, the biological study of animal behaviour developed by Konrad Lorenz and Niko Tinbergen. A fixed action pattern is an instinctive, stereotyped sequence of behaviour that is triggered by a specific external cue called a sign stimulus or releaser, and once initiated it runs to completion in the same form even if the stimulus is removed. A classic example is Tinbergen's demonstration that a male stickleback fish attacks any red-bellied model because the colour red is the releaser, and Lorenz's greylag goose egg-rolling response. Because FAPs are genetically pre-programmed and appear the same across all members of a species without prior experience, they are innate rather than acquired through any form of conditioning or cognition. Although the option wording 'innate learning' is slightly awkward (FAPs are more precisely innate rather than learned), it is the intended answer because it is the only option pointing to an inborn, instinctual origin. This tests the student's grasp of ethological instinct theory versus the associative-learning paradigms of Ivan Pavlov and B.F. Skinner." },

  { id: "rml21_005", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Deviations from homeostasis can create an internal state of biological and psychological tension. What is the best term for this state?",
    options: { a: "Regulation", b: "Drive", c: "Dissonance", d: "Equilibrium" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "Regulation refers to the process of maintaining balance, not to the tension state that motivates action when balance is lost.",
      c: "Dissonance (Leon Festinger) is a cognitive clash between beliefs or between attitude and behaviour, not a biological homeostatic imbalance.",
      d: "Equilibrium is the balanced end-state homeostasis seeks to restore; it is the opposite of the tension created by deviation from it."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is drive. Clark Leonard Hull formalised drive theory in the 1940s, proposing that a physiological need (such as hunger, thirst, or temperature imbalance) creates a state of internal tension or arousal called a drive, which energises and directs behaviour toward reducing that need and restoring homeostasis. Homeostasis, a term coined by Walter Bradford Cannon, is the body's tendency to maintain a stable internal environment. When the body deviates from its optimal set point, the resulting imbalance is experienced as a drive that pushes the organism to act (for example, thirst driving water-seeking). Once the need is met, the drive is reduced and equilibrium returns, which is the drive-reduction principle. This distinguishes drive (the motivating tension) from equilibrium and regulation (the balanced state and the balancing process). Drive theory was influential in explaining primary, biologically based motivations, though it struggled to explain behaviours pursued for their own sake, which later prompted arousal theory (Robert Yerkes and John Dodson) and incentive theories. This item checks whether the student can map the motivational tension state onto Hull's drive construct rather than the balance-oriented distractors." },

  { id: "rml21_006", section: "development",
    q: "A researcher would like to study how and to what degree heredity and the environment influence obesity. What population of individuals would provide the most information?",
    options: { a: "Dizygotic twins adopted by different parents", b: "Monozygotic twins raised by their biological parents", c: "Monozygotic twins adopted by different parents", d: "Siblings adopted by different parents" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "Dizygotic (fraternal) twins share only about 50 percent of their genes, so they cannot isolate genetic influence as cleanly as identical twins, even when reared apart.",
      b: "Monozygotic twins raised together share both genes and environment, so any similarity is confounded; you cannot separate nature from nurture.",
      d: "Ordinary siblings share only about 50 percent of genes on average, making them far less informative for isolating genetic effects than identical twins reared apart."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is monozygotic twins adopted by different parents. This is the classic 'twins reared apart' design, the most powerful natural experiment in behaviour genetics for separating the effects of heredity from environment. Monozygotic (identical) twins share essentially 100 percent of their genes, so when they are raised in different families and thus different environments, any remaining similarity between them (for example in body weight or obesity) can be attributed largely to their shared genetics, while any differences reflect environmental influence. This logic underpinned famous projects such as the Minnesota Study of Twins Reared Apart led by Thomas Bouchard. Comparing identical twins reared apart to identical twins reared together, and to fraternal twins and unrelated adoptees, lets researchers estimate heritability. Dizygotic twins and ordinary siblings share only about half their genes, so they cannot pinpoint genetic contribution as precisely, and twins raised together confound genes with a shared home environment. The item tests understanding of why maximising genetic similarity while varying environment yields the cleanest estimate of the nature versus nurture balance for a trait like obesity." },

  { id: "rml21_007", section: "social",
    q: "In which of the following scenarios would it be most difficult to predict behaviour from attitudes?",
    options: { a: "When the attitude has a great deal of personal relevance for the individual", b: "When the attitude is formed by indirect experience", c: "When the individual has a lot of knowledge about the attitude object", d: "When the attitude is formed by the individual's direct experience" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "High personal relevance strengthens the attitude-behaviour link, making behaviour easier, not harder, to predict from attitude.",
      c: "Greater knowledge about the attitude object produces a stronger, more accessible attitude that better predicts behaviour.",
      d: "Attitudes formed through direct experience are more strongly held and accessible, so they predict behaviour more reliably, not less."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is when the attitude is formed by indirect experience. Research on attitude-behaviour consistency, notably by Russell Fazio and by Icek Ajzen and Martin Fishbein, shows that attitudes predict behaviour best when they are strong, accessible, and personally significant. Several moderators strengthen the attitude-behaviour link: high personal relevance or vested interest, extensive knowledge about the attitude object, and especially attitudes formed through direct personal experience rather than hearsay. Regina Fazio Regan and Russell Fazio demonstrated that direct experience makes attitudes more accessible in memory and thus more predictive of action. Conversely, attitudes formed indirectly, through secondhand information, media, or others' reports rather than firsthand contact, tend to be weaker, less certain, and less accessible, so they are the poorest predictors of actual behaviour. Therefore the scenario in which prediction is most difficult is the indirect-experience one. The three distractors all describe conditions that increase attitude strength and accessibility, which is why they make behaviour easier to predict, exactly the opposite of what the question asks. This item tests knowledge of the moderators of attitude-behaviour consistency and the special role of direct experience." },

  { id: "rml21_008", section: "social",
    q: "Some students experience an intense period of adaptation when they leave their old school environment and start college, especially during those initial days before they find companions, start to fit in and feel comfortable. This process is best described as:",
    options: { a: "Looking-glass self", b: "Self-awareness", c: "Institutionalisation", d: "Re-socialisation" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: {
      a: "The looking-glass self (Charles Horton Cooley) is about forming self-concept from how we imagine others see us, not about adapting to a new social setting.",
      b: "Self-awareness is simply attending to oneself as an object of attention; it does not capture the process of learning new norms in a new environment.",
      c: "Institutionalisation refers to becoming dependent on the routines of a total institution (like long-term hospitalisation or prison), not to a normal transition into college life."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is re-socialisation. Socialisation is the lifelong sociological process by which individuals learn the norms, values, and behaviours of their society. Re-socialisation refers to the process of shedding former behaviour patterns and adopting new ones as part of a transition into a new social environment, role, or stage of life. When students leave a familiar school setting and enter college, they must unlearn old routines and learn the new expectations, culture, and social norms of the college, which is precisely re-socialisation. The concept is associated with sociologist Erving Goffman, who studied intense re-socialisation in total institutions, though it also applies to milder everyday transitions like starting college, a new job, or moving countries. The distractors reference other self and identity concepts: the looking-glass self from Charles Horton Cooley describes how we build self-image from others' perceived judgments, self-awareness is a state of self-focused attention, and institutionalisation is the loss of independence within a controlling institution. The item tests whether the student can distinguish the socialisation processes and correctly label adaptation to a new environment as re-socialisation." },

  { id: "rml21_009", section: "development",
    q: "A tribe of Native Americans have a specific model of parenting that has been practised for generations. Mothers respond quickly to infant distress, which leads to decreased episodes of crying and tantrums. They also believe in the power of touch, and hold their children almost constantly through the first three years of the baby's life, which also leads to decreased distress. Generations of the tribe's children are quiet, even tempered, obedient, and respectful. The behaviour of the tribe's children is best described by which of the following terms?",
    options: { a: "Covert Behaviour", b: "Innate Behaviour", c: "Complex Behaviour", d: "Learnt Behaviour" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: {
      a: "Covert behaviour refers to internal, unobservable mental activity (thoughts, feelings); the tribe's children show observable, outward behaviour patterns.",
      b: "Innate behaviour is inborn and appears without experience; the calm temperament here is cultivated by consistent parenting practices, so it is acquired, not innate.",
      c: "Complex behaviour merely describes behaviour with many components; it does not capture the crucial point that this behaviour is produced by upbringing and environment."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is learnt behaviour. Learnt (learned) behaviour is behaviour acquired through experience and environmental influence rather than inherited genetically. In this scenario, the calm, even-tempered, obedient behaviour of the tribe's children arises directly from consistent parenting practices: prompt responsiveness to distress and near-constant physical contact during the crucial first three years. Because the behaviour is shaped by nurture, socialisation, and repeated experience rather than being present at birth, it is learned rather than innate. This connects to attachment theory by John Bowlby and Mary Ainsworth, who argued that sensitive, responsive caregiving fosters secure attachment and emotional regulation, and to social learning theory by Albert Bandura, who showed that children acquire behaviour patterns through modelling and reinforcement in their environment. The key contrast is nature versus nurture: innate behaviour (option B) is genetically pre-programmed and universal, whereas the described temperament is transmitted culturally across generations through childrearing methods. Covert behaviour concerns hidden internal states, and complex behaviour is a vague descriptor that ignores the causal role of environment. The item tests whether the student recognises environmentally acquired behaviour as learned." },

  { id: "rml21_010", section: "cognitive",
    q: "According to the Guilford model of intelligence, how many dimensions of intelligence are there?",
    options: { a: "Three dimensions", b: "120 dimensions", c: "10 dimensions", d: "A variable number depending on I.Q." },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "Three dimensions describes the three axes (operations, contents, products) of Guilford's cube, but the question asks about the number of distinct ability factors, not the number of axes.",
      c: "Ten dimensions does not correspond to any figure in the Structure of Intellect model.",
      d: "The number of factors is fixed by the model's structure, not variable with a person's IQ; Guilford's whole point was a fixed multidimensional taxonomy."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is 120 dimensions. Joy Paul Guilford (J.P. Guilford) proposed the Structure of Intellect (SOI) model, which rejected the idea of a single general intelligence and instead described intelligence as a large set of distinct abilities organised along three dimensions: operations (the mental processes, such as cognition, memory, divergent production, convergent production, and evaluation), contents (the type of material, such as figural, symbolic, semantic, and behavioural), and products (the form the information takes, such as units, classes, relations, systems, transformations, and implications). In his original formulation there were 5 operations times 4 contents times 6 products, yielding 5 x 4 x 6 = 120 distinct intellectual factors or abilities. Guilford later expanded the model to 150 and then 180 factors, but the classic and most commonly tested figure is 120. The three-axis structure (option A) is what generates the factors, but the number of dimensions or abilities is the product of the axes, which is 120. Guilford's model is historically important for introducing divergent thinking, the basis of modern creativity research, in contrast to Charles Spearman's single g factor. This item tests recall of the SOI model's 120-factor count." },

  { id: "rml21_011", section: "general",
    q: "Gordon Allport preferred which of the following research approaches in his personality research?",
    options: { a: "Factorial Design", b: "Nomothetic Approach", c: "Idiographic Approach", d: "Chi square Method" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "Factorial design and factor analysis are associated with trait theorists like Raymond Cattell and Hans Eysenck, who reduced personality to common factors, not with Allport's focus on the individual.",
      b: "The nomothetic approach seeks universal laws and common traits across people; Allport valued this less than the study of the unique individual.",
      d: "The chi-square method is a statistical test of association for categorical data, not a personality-research philosophy that Allport advocated."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is the idiographic approach. Gordon Allport, a founding figure of personality psychology, distinguished two broad approaches to studying personality: the nomothetic approach, which seeks general laws and compares people on the same set of common traits (favoured by trait and factor theorists such as Raymond Cattell and Hans Eysenck), and the idiographic approach, which focuses on the depth, uniqueness, and individuality of a single person, using methods like case studies, diaries, and letters. Although Allport acknowledged common traits shared across people, he strongly emphasised the idiographic study of the individual, arguing that each person possesses unique personal dispositions that cannot be fully captured by comparing them on universal dimensions. He famously classified traits into cardinal traits (dominant, pervasive), central traits (general characteristics forming the core personality), and secondary traits (situation-specific). His idiographic leaning is illustrated by his intensive analysis of personal documents, such as the Letters from Jenny study. The distractors point to statistical or group-comparison methods (factorial design, chi-square, nomothetic) that emphasise commonalities rather than individuality. This item tests whether the student links Allport specifically with the idiographic, person-centred tradition." },

  { id: "rml21_012", section: "general",
    q: "Vaibhav smokes, drinks alcohol, overeats, and bites his nails. Which stage of Freud's stages of psychosexual Development has Vaibhav become fixated at?",
    options: { a: "Phallic stage", b: "Anal stage", c: "Oral stage", d: "Latency stage" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "The phallic stage centres on the genitals and the Oedipus or Electra complex; fixation there relates to identity and sexuality issues, not mouth-focused habits.",
      b: "Anal-stage fixation produces either excessive orderliness (anal-retentive) or messiness (anal-expulsive), tied to toilet training, not oral behaviours.",
      d: "The latency stage is a period of dormant sexual energy with no fixations in Freud's scheme; it is not associated with a distinct set of adult traits."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is the oral stage. Sigmund Freud proposed five psychosexual stages of development (oral, anal, phallic, latency, genital), each centred on a different erogenous zone where libidinal energy is focused. If a child's needs are either overindulged or under-gratified at a given stage, Freud argued that some libido remains fixated there, producing characteristic adult personality traits and behaviours. The oral stage (roughly birth to 18 months) centres on the mouth, with pleasure derived from sucking, biting, and chewing. Fixation at the oral stage is expressed in adulthood through mouth-oriented and dependency-related behaviours such as smoking, excessive drinking, overeating, nail-biting, and thumb-sucking, exactly the cluster described for Vaibhav. An oral-receptive versus oral-aggressive personality may also develop. The anal stage (option B) concerns bladder and bowel control and yields orderliness or messiness; the phallic stage involves the Oedipus and Electra complexes; and latency is a quiet period before the mature genital stage. Although Freud's theory is scientifically criticised and largely unfalsifiable, it remains standard exam content. The item tests the classic association between oral fixation and mouth-focused adult habits." },

  { id: "rml21_013", section: "development",
    q: "Parents are the first to introduce children to the 'gender map', for example, they may consciously pick out colours such as pink and blue. Colours have no meaning in themselves but they are often associated with gender roles. Parents own gender orientations may also be so strongly embedded that they do most of their gender teaching unconsciously. Learning this gender map is referred to as:",
    options: { a: "Gender Construction", b: "Gender Structuralisation", c: "Gender Socialisation", d: "Gender Stereotyping" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "Gender construction is a broader theoretical idea that gender is socially built, but it is not the standard term for the specific learning process transmitted by parents.",
      b: "Gender structuralisation is not an established term in developmental or social psychology.",
      d: "Gender stereotyping is holding fixed overgeneralised beliefs about the sexes; it is a product of, not the name for, the overall process of teaching gender roles."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is gender socialisation. Gender socialisation is the process by which children learn the social expectations, attitudes, roles, and behaviours culturally associated with their sex, beginning with the family and especially the parents, who are the earliest and most influential agents. As the scenario notes, parents transmit the 'gender map' both consciously (choosing pink for girls, blue for boys) and unconsciously (through their own deeply embedded gender orientations). This process continues through other socialising agents such as peers, schools, and the media. The concept draws on social learning theory by Albert Bandura, whereby children acquire gender-typed behaviour through observation, imitation, and reinforcement, and on gender schema theory by Sandra Bem, which describes how children build cognitive frameworks for organising gender information. Gender stereotyping (option D) refers to the rigid beliefs about what each sex is like, which are one outcome of socialisation rather than the process itself, and gender construction is a more abstract sociological claim that gender is socially produced. The item tests whether the student can name the specific developmental process of learning gender roles, which is gender socialisation, distinguishing it from its outputs and related theoretical labels." },

  { id: "rml21_014", section: "general",
    q: "Select the incorrect pair:",
    options: { a: "Jung: Extroversion-Introversion", b: "Kohlberg: Formal operations", c: "Adler: Ruling Dominant Type", d: "Ellis: Rational Emotive Therapy" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "This pair is correct: Carl Jung introduced the extraversion-introversion dimension of personality, so it is not the incorrect pair the question seeks.",
      c: "This pair is correct: Alfred Adler described personality types including the ruling or dominant type, so it is properly matched.",
      d: "This pair is correct: Albert Ellis founded Rational Emotive Therapy (later REBT), so it is accurately paired."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Kohlberg: Formal operations, because this is the mismatched (incorrect) pair. Formal operations is the fourth and final stage of cognitive development in the theory of Jean Piaget, characterised by abstract, hypothetical, and logical reasoning that emerges in adolescence; it has nothing to do with Lawrence Kohlberg, who is famous instead for his theory of moral development (pre-conventional, conventional, and post-conventional levels). Kohlberg built on Piaget's earlier work on moral reasoning but is not associated with the formal operations stage, so pairing his name with it is wrong. The other three pairs are all correctly matched: Carl Gustav Jung introduced the personality attitudes of extraversion and introversion; Alfred Adler, founder of individual psychology, described personality types including the ruling or dominant type (alongside getting, avoiding, and socially useful types); and Albert Ellis developed Rational Emotive Therapy, later renamed Rational Emotive Behaviour Therapy (REBT), the pioneering cognitive therapy built on the ABC model. This item tests the student's ability to correctly attribute major theories and concepts to their originators, a frequent format in entrance exams." },

  { id: "rml21_015", section: "general",
    q: "Which stage is characterised by realising your full potential according to Maslow's Hierarchy of Needs?",
    options: { a: "Safety Needs", b: "Social Needs", c: "Esteem Needs", d: "Self-Actualisation Needs" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: {
      a: "Safety needs (security, stability, protection) sit near the base of the pyramid and concern basic survival, not fulfilment of one's highest potential.",
      b: "Social needs (belonging, love, friendship) are mid-level needs about relationships, not the peak of realising one's potential.",
      c: "Esteem needs (respect, recognition, self-worth) lie just below the top; they concern status and confidence, not the full realisation of potential that defines the apex."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is self-actualisation needs. Abraham Maslow proposed a hierarchy of needs, usually depicted as a pyramid with five levels arranged from most basic to most advanced: physiological needs (food, water, sleep), safety needs (security, stability), love and belonging (social) needs, esteem needs (respect, achievement, recognition), and at the very top, self-actualisation. Maslow argued that lower needs must be reasonably satisfied before higher ones become motivating. Self-actualisation, the apex of the hierarchy, is the drive to realise one's full potential, to become everything one is capable of becoming, and to pursue personal growth, creativity, and meaning. Maslow described self-actualised individuals as autonomous, accepting, spontaneous, problem-centred, and capable of peak experiences, and he studied exemplary figures such as Abraham Lincoln and Albert Einstein as models. Later he added self-transcendence above self-actualisation, but the classic five-level model tops out at self-actualisation. The distractors are all genuine but lower levels of the hierarchy that concern security, belonging, and status rather than fulfilment of potential. This item tests recall of the top tier of Maslow's humanistic motivational model and the definition of self-actualisation." },

  { id: "rml21_016", section: "development",
    q: "Children, who are distressed by their parent's absence, seek interaction but also reject it when offered. How can the reaction be categorised?",
    options: { a: "Insecure-avoidant", b: "Insecure-resistant", c: "Insecure-disorganized", d: "Secure-resistant" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "Insecure-avoidant infants show little distress at separation and ignore or avoid the caregiver on reunion; they do not seek then reject contact.",
      c: "Insecure-disorganized infants show confused, contradictory, or fearful behaviours with no coherent strategy, rather than the clear approach-then-resist pattern described.",
      d: "Secure-resistant is not a recognised attachment category; securely attached infants are comforted and settle on reunion, unlike the ambivalent pattern here."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is insecure-resistant (also called anxious-ambivalent or insecure-ambivalent) attachment. Mary Ainsworth developed the Strange Situation procedure to classify infant attachment based on the child's reactions to separation from and reunion with the caregiver. She identified secure attachment and two insecure patterns, later joined by a fourth. Insecure-resistant infants are highly distressed by the caregiver's absence and are difficult to soothe on reunion: they simultaneously seek contact and comfort yet resist or reject it when it is offered, showing an angry, ambivalent, push-pull pattern of clinging and rejecting. This stems from inconsistent, unpredictable caregiving, so the infant cannot rely on the caregiver as a secure base. By contrast, insecure-avoidant infants (from rejecting or unresponsive caregiving) minimise distress and avoid the caregiver, and securely attached infants (from sensitive, responsive caregiving) are readily comforted. The disorganised category, added by Mary Main and Judith Solomon, describes contradictory, fearful behaviour often linked to frightening caregiving. The described approach-then-resist ambivalence maps precisely onto the resistant type. This item tests knowledge of Ainsworth's attachment classifications and their distinguishing reunion behaviours." },

  { id: "rml21_017", section: "development",
    q: "According to the schema theory, which hypothetical construct provides a prototype for subsequent attachments and can be adapted through more experience?",
    options: { a: "Proximity-seeking mechanism", b: "Attachment system", c: "Bio-social relationship model", d: "Internal working model" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: {
      a: "Proximity-seeking is a behaviour (staying close to the caregiver), not a mental representation or prototype that guides future relationships.",
      b: "The attachment system is the overall behavioural-motivational system regulating closeness; it is not the specific cognitive template used as a prototype for later bonds.",
      c: "Bio-social relationship model is not an established attachment-theory construct and does not name the internal representation described."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is the internal working model. John Bowlby, the founder of attachment theory, proposed that through early interactions with the primary caregiver, an infant builds an internal working model, a mental representation or schema of the self, of attachment figures, and of relationships in general. This cognitive-affective template captures whether the self is worthy of love and whether others can be trusted to be available and responsive. Crucially, the internal working model acts as a prototype that guides expectations, feelings, and behaviour in later relationships, including friendships and adult romantic bonds, which is the basis of the continuity hypothesis. Bowlby argued that the model is not fixed: while it tends to be stable, it can be adapted and updated through significant new relational experiences over time. This connects to the schema concept in cognitive psychology, where schemas are organised knowledge structures that shape perception and can accommodate new information. The distractors name real attachment ideas (proximity-seeking behaviour, the attachment behavioural system) but not the internal representational template that serves as a prototype. This item tests understanding of Bowlby's internal working model as the mental blueprint for future attachments." },

  { id: "rml21_018", section: "development",
    q: "Type D (disorganised) babies are:",
    options: { a: "Characterised by conspicuous avoidance of proximity to or interaction with the mother in the reunion episodes", b: "Characterised by actively seeking and maintaining proximity, contact or interaction with the mother, especially in the reunion episodes", c: "Characterised by conspicuous contact- and interaction-resisting behaviour in the reunion episodes", d: "Characterised by no one clear pattern, but show inconsistent and often bizarre responses to separation/reunion" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: {
      a: "This describes the insecure-avoidant (Type A) pattern of avoiding the caregiver, not the disorganised Type D.",
      b: "Actively seeking and maintaining proximity describes secure (Type B) attachment, where the infant uses the caregiver as a secure base.",
      c: "Conspicuous contact-resisting behaviour describes the insecure-resistant or ambivalent (Type C) pattern, not disorganised attachment."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is that Type D babies show no one clear pattern, but display inconsistent and often bizarre responses to separation and reunion. The disorganised or disoriented attachment category (Type D) was identified by Mary Main and Judith Solomon as an addition to Mary Ainsworth's original three-category system (secure Type B, avoidant Type A, resistant or ambivalent Type C). Disorganised infants lack a coherent, organised strategy for coping with distress in the Strange Situation. Instead they exhibit contradictory, confused, and sometimes odd behaviours: they may freeze, rock, approach the caregiver then abruptly move away, show fearful or dazed expressions, or fall to the floor. This pattern is often associated with frightening, abusive, or severely inconsistent caregiving, where the caregiver is simultaneously the source of comfort and the source of fear, leaving the infant in an unsolvable approach-avoidance conflict. Disorganised attachment is the strongest attachment predictor of later psychopathology. The distractors correctly describe the three organised patterns (avoidant, secure, and resistant respectively), which is why they are wrong for Type D. This item tests recognition of the defining incoherent, disoriented quality of disorganised attachment." },

  { id: "rml21_019", section: "development",
    q: "Human motor development is governed by certain basic principles. Which of the following is Not a basic principle of motor development?",
    options: { a: "Maturation of the motor system proceeds from gross motor to fine motor behaviours.", b: "Motor development is from cephalo-to-caudal or from head to feet.", c: "Motor development proceeds from the distal to the proximal or from the extremities to the central part of the body.", d: "Motor development plays a major role in social and behavioural expectations." },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "Gross-to-fine progression is a genuine principle: large-muscle movements develop before delicate finger control, so this is a true statement, not the exception.",
      b: "The cephalocaudal principle (head to feet) is a real and central principle of motor development, so it does not fit the 'not a principle' answer.",
      d: "That motor development influences social and behavioural expectations is a valid observation about its broader role, so it is not the false principle sought."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is that motor development proceeds from distal to proximal, because this statement is reversed and therefore NOT a true principle. The genuine principle is the proximodistal trend: development proceeds from proximal (near the centre or midline of the body, such as the trunk and shoulders) to distal (toward the extremities, such as the hands and fingers). Infants gain control of their arms and shoulders before they master fine finger movements, so option C states the direction backwards and is the false statement the question wants. The other two directional principles are stated correctly: the cephalocaudal trend means development proceeds from head to foot, so infants control their heads and upper bodies before their legs. The gross-to-fine principle means large-muscle movements (crawling, walking) develop before fine-motor skills (grasping small objects, writing). Option D, that motor development shapes social and behavioural expectations, is also a valid point about its wider significance. These maturational principles derive from work by developmental theorists including Arnold Gesell, who emphasised orderly, genetically guided maturation of motor skills. The item tests whether the student can spot the reversed proximodistal statement among otherwise accurate principles." },

  { id: "rml21_020", section: "development",
    q: "At what time of life does Erikson stage Industry vs. Inferiority occur?",
    options: { a: "Adolescence", b: "Infancy", c: "School Age", d: "Adulthood" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "Adolescence corresponds to Erikson's Identity vs. Role Confusion stage, not Industry vs. Inferiority.",
      b: "Infancy corresponds to Trust vs. Mistrust, the first psychosocial stage, not the industry stage.",
      d: "Adulthood spans the Intimacy vs. Isolation and Generativity vs. Stagnation stages, both later than the industry stage."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is school age. Erik Erikson proposed a theory of psychosocial development consisting of eight stages spanning the entire lifespan, each defined by a core conflict that must be resolved. The fourth stage, Industry versus Inferiority, occurs during middle childhood or the school age years, roughly ages 6 to 11 or 12. During this period the child's world expands to school and peers, and the central task is to develop a sense of industry: competence, productivity, and mastery of skills valued by their culture, such as academic work, sports, and social abilities. Success, supported by encouragement from teachers and parents, produces feelings of competence and self-confidence, whereas repeated failure or harsh criticism produces a lasting sense of inferiority and inadequacy. The surrounding stages are Trust versus Mistrust (infancy), Autonomy versus Shame and Doubt (toddlerhood), Initiative versus Guilt (preschool), Identity versus Role Confusion (adolescence), Intimacy versus Isolation (young adulthood), Generativity versus Stagnation (middle adulthood), and Ego Integrity versus Despair (late adulthood). This item tests correct sequencing of Erikson's stages, specifically that industry versus inferiority belongs to the school-age years." },

  { id: "rml21_021", section: "development",
    q: "What is the primary difference between Piaget and Vygotsky's theories of language development?",
    options: { a: "Piaget argued thought depends on language while Vygotsky argued language depends on thought", b: "Vygotsky argued thought depends on language while Piaget argued language depends on thought", c: "Vygotsky was concerned with early language development while Piaget was concerned with later language development.", d: "None of the above" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "This reverses the two theorists: it wrongly assigns the language-drives-thought view to Piaget and the thought-drives-language view to Vygotsky.",
      c: "Both theorists studied language across development; the distinction is not about early versus late timing but about the direction of the thought-language relationship.",
      d: "One option (B) correctly captures the contrast, so 'none of the above' is incorrect."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is that Lev Vygotsky argued thought depends on language while Jean Piaget argued language depends on thought. This captures the core theoretical divide between the two developmental giants. Piaget viewed cognitive development as primary and language as a product of it: for Piaget, the child must first develop underlying cognitive structures and understanding (for example, object permanence and symbolic thought) before language can express those concepts, so language reflects thought rather than driving it. He also regarded much early speech as egocentric, fading as the child matures. Vygotsky, by contrast, in his sociocultural theory, argued that language and thought have separate roots but merge around age two, after which language becomes the primary tool that shapes and drives cognitive development. For Vygotsky, social speech is internalised into private speech and then inner speech, which guides reasoning and self-regulation, so thought is fundamentally dependent on language and social interaction. This reflects his broader emphasis on the zone of proximal development and social scaffolding. The reversed option and the timing-based option misstate the relationship. This item tests the classic Piaget-versus-Vygotsky contrast on the direction of the language-thought dependency." },

  { id: "rml21_022", section: "development",
    q: "At which age would a child be categorised as preoperational according to Piaget?",
    options: { a: "0-2 years", b: "2-7 years", c: "7-11 years", d: "11+ years" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "0-2 years is the sensorimotor stage, defined by learning through senses and actions and by achieving object permanence, not preoperational thought.",
      c: "7-11 years is the concrete operational stage, marked by logical thinking about concrete events and mastery of conservation.",
      d: "11+ years is the formal operational stage, characterised by abstract and hypothetical reasoning."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is 2 to 7 years. Jean Piaget's theory of cognitive development describes four stages through which children progress. The preoperational stage spans roughly ages 2 to 7 and follows the sensorimotor stage. In this stage the child develops symbolic thinking and rapidly expanding language, using words and images to represent objects, and engages in pretend or symbolic play. However, thinking is not yet logical or 'operational.' Key limitations include egocentrism, the inability to take another person's perspective (illustrated by Piaget's three-mountains task); centration, focusing on only one feature of a situation at a time; and a lack of conservation, the failure to understand that quantity remains constant despite changes in appearance (as when a child thinks a tall thin glass holds more water than a short wide one). Children also show animism, attributing life to inanimate objects. The four stages in order are sensorimotor (0 to 2), preoperational (2 to 7), concrete operational (7 to 11), and formal operational (11 and up). The distractors are the age ranges of the other three stages. This item tests correct pairing of the preoperational stage with its 2 to 7 year age band." },

  { id: "rml21_023", section: "development",
    q: "Adults typically talk to babies in a special way. This has been defined as 'Motherese', 'baby talk register' or 'adult-child speech'. Identify one characteristic of this type of adult-child speech.",
    options: { a: "The mean length of utterance is shorter in adult-child speech than it is in adult-adult speech.", b: "Adults speak much more quickly to babies than they do to other adults.", c: "Adults pause more frequently when they talk to other adults than they do when they talk to babies.", d: "Adult-child speech has a lower pitch and a narrower range of pitch." },
    correct: "a",
    traps: {
      b: "This is reversed: adults speak more slowly, not more quickly, to babies to aid comprehension.",
      c: "This is reversed: adults pause more frequently and clearly when talking to babies, not to other adults.",
      d: "This is reversed: motherese uses higher pitch and an exaggerated, wider pitch range, not lower pitch with a narrow range."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is that the mean length of utterance is shorter in adult-child speech than in adult-adult speech. Motherese, also called infant-directed speech or child-directed speech, is the distinctive register adults spontaneously use when talking to babies. Its well-documented features include a higher overall pitch, an exaggerated and wider pitch range with sing-song intonation, a slower rate of speech, more frequent and longer pauses, clearer enunciation, repetition, and simpler, shorter sentences (a shorter mean length of utterance). The mean length of utterance (MLU), a measure introduced by Roger Brown, is the average number of morphemes per utterance and is a standard index of grammatical complexity; adults naturally shorten it when addressing infants. These modifications are thought to support language acquisition by making speech more salient, easier to segment, and emotionally engaging, capturing and holding the infant's attention. Options B, C, and D each state the reverse of a true feature: speech to babies is slower not quicker, contains more pauses not fewer, and uses higher and more variable pitch not lower and narrower. This item tests whether the student knows the actual acoustic and structural characteristics of infant-directed speech, correctly identifying the shortened utterance length." },

  { id: "rml21_024", section: "development",
    q: "When infants and very young children live in environments where they have no exposure to or experience with language, their language development is severely limited. These limitations are not reversible. This supports which of the following terms?",
    options: { a: "Sensitive periods of development", b: "Semantics", c: "Syntax", d: "Nativism" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: {
      b: "Semantics is the study of meaning in language; it does not explain why deprivation causes irreversible limitations.",
      c: "Syntax concerns the rules governing sentence structure; it is a component of language, not the developmental-timing concept described.",
      d: "Nativism (associated with Noam Chomsky) claims language is innately programmed; while related, it does not specifically capture the irreversibility tied to a bounded time window, which is the sensitive or critical period."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is sensitive periods of development. A sensitive period (or critical period) is a specific window of time early in development during which the brain is especially receptive to particular environmental input, and during which certain abilities, such as language, are most readily acquired. If the necessary experience is absent during this window, the ability may fail to develop normally and, crucially, the deficit can be permanent and not fully reversible even with later exposure. Eric Lenneberg formulated the critical period hypothesis for language, proposing that language must be acquired before puberty for native-like mastery to be possible. Tragic natural cases such as Genie, a child deprived of language until age 13, support this: despite later intervention she never acquired normal grammar, illustrating the irreversibility the question emphasises. The concept parallels Konrad Lorenz's work on imprinting in birds, another time-bounded sensitive period. Nativism (option D), Noam Chomsky's view that humans possess an innate language acquisition device, is related but does not by itself explain the irreversible harm from deprivation within a bounded time frame. Semantics and syntax are simply components of language. This item tests the concept of a bounded, largely irreversible developmental window." },

  { id: "rml21_025", section: "development",
    q: "Imitation is:",
    options: { a: "Not an important feature of infants learning", b: "Not seen until 1 year of age", c: "May be seen as early as 12-21 days", d: "Is limited to tongue protrusion in early infancy." },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "Imitation is a crucial mechanism of early social and cognitive learning, so calling it unimportant is false.",
      b: "Imitation appears far earlier than one year; newborns just days old can imitate certain facial gestures.",
      d: "Early neonatal imitation includes several gestures (mouth opening, lip protrusion) not just tongue protrusion, so limiting it to one behaviour is inaccurate."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is that imitation may be seen as early as 12 to 21 days of age. The landmark research here is by Andrew Meltzoff and Keith Moore, who in 1977 demonstrated that newborn infants, some only a few weeks old, could imitate adult facial gestures such as tongue protrusion, mouth opening, and lip protrusion. This finding was revolutionary because it suggested that imitation is present at or very near birth rather than being a late-developing skill, challenging Jean Piaget's view that true imitation of facial gestures only emerged later in infancy (Piaget placed deferred imitation around 18 months). Neonatal imitation implies infants possess an innate capacity to match observed behaviour with their own motor output, a foundation for social learning, and it is often linked theoretically to the later discovery of mirror neurons. Imitation is a central mechanism of early learning, consistent with Albert Bandura's social learning theory, in which children acquire behaviour by observing and copying models. Options A, B, and D each understate imitation: it is important, it appears well before one year, and it is not restricted to a single gesture. This item tests knowledge of the Meltzoff and Moore finding on very early neonatal imitation." },

  { id: "rml21_026", section: "development",
    q: "The important developments tasks of adolescence include",
    options: { a: "Emotional separation from parents", b: "Start of strong peer identification", c: "Heterosexual peer interest", d: "All of the above" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: {
      a: "Emotional separation from parents is one genuine adolescent task, but choosing it alone ignores the other equally valid tasks listed.",
      b: "Strong peer identification is a real adolescent task, but it is only part of the full set of developmental tasks.",
      c: "Emerging heterosexual (or romantic) peer interest is a legitimate adolescent task, but again it is only one of several, so it cannot be the sole answer."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is all of the above. Adolescence is a developmental period marked by several interrelated tasks that together move the young person from childhood dependency toward adult autonomy and identity. Emotional separation or individuation from parents is a key task: adolescents gradually establish psychological independence and their own values while renegotiating family relationships, a process related to Erik Erikson's stage of Identity versus Role Confusion, in which the central task is forming a coherent personal identity. A strong shift toward peer identification is another hallmark: peers become the primary reference group, and belonging, conformity, and peer approval intensify, which Harry Stack Sullivan emphasised in his account of the importance of chumship and peer relationships in adolescence. The emergence of heterosexual or romantic peer interest reflects the physical and social changes of puberty, as sexual maturation drives new interest in romantic and intimate relationships. Robert Havighurst's developmental tasks framework similarly lists achieving emotional independence from parents, forming mature peer relationships, and preparing for intimate relationships among the tasks of adolescence. Because all three options describe genuine, complementary adolescent tasks, the inclusive answer is correct. This item tests broad understanding of normative adolescent development." },

  { id: "rml21_027", section: "development",
    q: "Which of the following is NOT one of Kohlberg's stages of moral reasoning?",
    options: { a: "Conventional", b: "Unconventional", c: "Post-conventional", d: "Pre-conventional" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "Conventional is a genuine level of Kohlberg's theory (Level 2), so it is not the false option.",
      c: "Post-conventional is Kohlberg's highest level (Level 3), based on abstract principles and justice, so it is a real stage.",
      d: "Pre-conventional is Kohlberg's first level (Level 1), based on obedience and self-interest, so it is a real stage."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is unconventional, because this is NOT one of Lawrence Kohlberg's levels of moral reasoning. Kohlberg, building on the earlier work of Jean Piaget, proposed a stage theory of moral development organised into three levels, each containing two stages, giving six stages total. The first level is Pre-conventional morality, where moral judgments are based on external consequences: Stage 1 is obedience and punishment orientation, and Stage 2 is instrumental exchange or self-interest ('what is in it for me'). The second level is Conventional morality, where judgments are based on social approval and maintaining order: Stage 3 is good interpersonal relationships or the 'good boy, good girl' orientation, and Stage 4 is authority and social-order maintenance ('law and order'). The third level is Post-conventional morality, where judgments rest on abstract ethical principles: Stage 5 is social contract and individual rights, and Stage 6 is universal ethical principles. Kohlberg derived these from responses to moral dilemmas such as the Heinz dilemma. There is no level called 'unconventional' in his framework, making option B the odd one out. Carol Gilligan later critiqued the theory for a possible male bias. This item tests recall of the three correctly named levels." },

  { id: "rml21_028", section: "development",
    q: "The human infant's brain size reaches one-half of the adult size by:",
    options: { a: "Birth", b: "6 months", c: "1 year", d: "3 years" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "At birth the brain is roughly one-quarter of adult size, not one-half, so birth is too early.",
      c: "By one year the brain is already about two-thirds to three-quarters of adult size, which is more than half, so this is too late for the half-size milestone.",
      d: "By three years the brain has reached roughly 80 percent or more of adult size, well beyond the halfway mark."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is 6 months. The human brain undergoes extraordinarily rapid growth in the first years of life, one of the fastest-developing organs in early development. At birth the infant brain is only about 25 percent of its eventual adult weight, but growth is explosive thereafter, driven by synaptogenesis (the proliferation of synaptic connections), myelination (the insulation of axons that speeds neural transmission), and dendritic branching rather than by the creation of many new neurons, since most neurons are present at birth. By approximately 6 months of age the brain reaches roughly half (about 50 percent) of its adult size. It continues to grow quickly, reaching around 75 percent of adult size by about age 2 and roughly 90 percent by age 5 or 6, with full maturation of some regions such as the prefrontal cortex continuing into the mid-twenties. This rapid early growth underlies the sensitive periods for many abilities and highlights the importance of early nutrition and stimulation. The distractors correspond to other developmental points that either precede or follow the halfway milestone. This item tests factual knowledge of the timeline of early brain growth, specifically that half of adult brain size is achieved by about six months." },

  { id: "rml21_029", section: "development",
    q: "Thomas and Chess identified nine dimensions of temperament by:",
    options: { a: "Interviewing parents using questionnaires", b: "Drawing on their clinical experience and previous studies", c: "Observing babies", d: "Both a) and c)" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: {
      a: "Interviewing parents was part of their method, but selecting only this option omits the direct observation of infants they also used.",
      b: "While clinical experience informed their broader work, the nine dimensions were derived empirically from parent interviews and observation, not primarily from prior studies.",
      c: "Observing babies was part of their approach, but choosing it alone ignores the parent interviews that were equally central to identifying the dimensions."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is both interviewing parents using questionnaires and observing babies. Alexander Thomas and Stella Chess conducted the landmark New York Longitudinal Study (NYLS), beginning in 1956, which followed infants over many years to study temperament, the biologically based, early-appearing individual differences in behavioural style. From detailed parent interviews and questionnaires about their infants' typical reactions, combined with direct observation of the babies, they identified nine dimensions of temperament: activity level, rhythmicity (regularity), approach or withdrawal to new stimuli, adaptability, intensity of reaction, threshold of responsiveness, quality of mood, distractibility, and attention span or persistence. By clustering these dimensions, Thomas and Chess described three broad temperamental types: the easy child (regular, adaptable, positive mood), the difficult child (irregular, intense, slow to adapt, negative mood), and the slow-to-warm-up child (mild, initially withdrawing but gradually adapting). They also introduced the influential concept of goodness of fit, the match between a child's temperament and environmental demands. Because their methodology combined both parental report and observation of the infants, the inclusive option is correct. This item tests knowledge of the NYLS methodology and Thomas and Chess's temperament framework." },

  { id: "rml21_030", section: "cognitive",
    q: "Occurs when last items in a list of information are recalled more efficiently than items in the middle of the list.",
    options: { a: "Primacy effect", b: "Recency effect", c: "Flynn effect", d: "Halo effect" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "The primacy effect is superior recall of the first items in a list, not the last, so it describes the opposite end of the serial position curve.",
      c: "The Flynn effect refers to the generational rise in average IQ scores over time and has nothing to do with list recall.",
      d: "The halo effect is a social-perception bias where one positive trait colours overall impressions of a person, unrelated to memory for list positions."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is the recency effect. The recency effect is the tendency to recall the most recently presented items (those at the end of a list) more accurately and efficiently than items in the middle. Together with the primacy effect (better recall of the first items), it produces the classic U-shaped serial position curve first systematically studied in the tradition of Hermann Ebbinghaus and later detailed by researchers such as Bennet Murdock. The two effects are explained by the modal model of memory of Richard Atkinson and Richard Shiffrin: the recency effect reflects items still held in short-term or working memory at the moment of recall, so it is disrupted by a delay or a distractor task after presentation. The primacy effect reflects items that received more rehearsal and were transferred into long-term memory, so it is weakened by fast presentation. Middle items suffer because they are neither freshly in short-term store nor well rehearsed. The Flynn effect (James Flynn) concerns rising IQ scores across generations, and the halo effect (Edward Thorndike) is an impression-formation bias, so both are unrelated to serial position. This item tests recognition of the recency portion of the serial position effect." },

  { id: "rml21_031", section: "psychopathology",
    q: "Amenorrhea is Present in anorexia nervosa. This term means:",
    options: { a: "An intense fear of weight gain.", b: "Excessive exercise.", c: "Absence of menstrual cycle.", d: "Body-image distortion." },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "Intense fear of weight gain is a core psychological criterion of anorexia, but it is a separate feature and not what the medical term amenorrhea denotes.",
      b: "Excessive exercise is a compensatory behaviour some patients use, but it is unrelated to the literal meaning of amenorrhea.",
      d: "Body-image distortion is another key feature of anorexia, but it describes a perceptual disturbance, not the absence of menstruation."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is absence of menstrual cycle. Amenorrhea is a medical term meaning the absence or cessation of menstruation. The word breaks down as 'a' (without), 'men' (month), and 'rrhea' (flow). In anorexia nervosa, severe caloric restriction and dangerously low body weight disrupt the hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis, reducing the secretion of hormones such as luteinizing hormone and estrogen, which causes menstruation to stop. Amenorrhea was listed as a diagnostic criterion for anorexia nervosa in the DSM-IV-TR, though it was removed in the DSM-5 because it excluded males, premenarchal girls, and women on contraceptives, and because patients who otherwise met all criteria could still menstruate. Anorexia nervosa itself is characterised by restriction of energy intake leading to significantly low body weight, an intense fear of gaining weight, and a distorted perception of body weight or shape. The distractors correctly describe other genuine features of anorexia (fear of weight gain, body-image distortion) or an associated behaviour (excessive exercise), but none of them is the definition of the specific physiological term amenorrhea. This item tests medical terminology alongside knowledge of the eating-disorder criteria." },

  { id: "rml21_032", section: "psychopathology",
    q: "The chance of developing schizophrenia if you have an identical twin with the disorder is approximately:",
    options: { a: "1%", b: "10%", c: "80%", d: "50%" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: {
      a: "1 percent is the lifetime risk in the general population, not the concordance for an identical twin of an affected person.",
      b: "About 10 percent is the risk for a first-degree relative such as a sibling or child, not for a monozygotic co-twin.",
      c: "80 percent overstates the concordance; if it were that high, genetics would be nearly deterministic, contradicting the well-established role of environment."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is approximately 50 percent. Schizophrenia has a strong genetic component, and twin studies provide the clearest evidence for it. The monozygotic (identical) twin concordance rate for schizophrenia is around 40 to 50 percent, meaning that if one identical twin has schizophrenia, the other has roughly a 50 percent chance of also developing it. This figure, drawn from classic twin research including work reviewed by Irving Gottesman, is far higher than the roughly 1 percent lifetime risk in the general population and higher than the roughly 17 percent concordance in dizygotic (fraternal) twins or about 9 to 13 percent risk in a sibling or child of an affected person. The crucial interpretive point is that because identical twins share 100 percent of their genes yet the concordance is only about 50 percent rather than 100 percent, genetics alone cannot fully explain the disorder. This supports the diathesis-stress model, in which a genetic vulnerability (diathesis) interacts with environmental stressors (such as prenatal factors, cannabis use, or psychosocial stress) to trigger onset. The distractors correspond to the general-population risk, first-degree relative risk, and an implausibly high near-deterministic figure. This item tests knowledge of twin concordance and the gene-environment interaction in schizophrenia." },

  { id: "rml21_033", section: "psychopathology",
    q: "According to Freud, the term hysteria describes:",
    options: { a: "Physical reactions to emotional trauma", b: "A phobia of horses", c: "An unresolved Oedipus complex", d: "A tendency towards oral-type behaviour" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: {
      b: "A phobia of horses refers specifically to Freud's Little Hans case, an example used to illustrate the Oedipus complex, not the definition of hysteria itself.",
      c: "An unresolved Oedipus complex is a broader psychosexual concept; while Freud linked conflicts to hysteria, the term itself denotes bodily symptoms from psychological distress.",
      d: "Oral-type behaviour relates to fixation at the oral psychosexual stage, a different concept entirely from hysteria."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is physical reactions to emotional trauma. Hysteria (later termed conversion disorder, and in modern classification functional neurological symptom disorder) was central to Sigmund Freud's early work and to his collaboration with Josef Breuer, published in Studies on Hysteria (1895). The concept describes the appearance of genuine physical symptoms, such as paralysis, blindness, loss of sensation, or seizures, that have no identifiable organic medical cause and instead arise from unconscious psychological conflict or emotional trauma. Freud proposed that when a traumatic memory or forbidden impulse is repressed and cannot be expressed, the associated psychic energy is 'converted' into a bodily symptom, hence the later name conversion. The famous case of Anna O, treated by Breuer, illustrated how symptoms could be relieved when the underlying repressed memory was brought into consciousness and discharged, the 'talking cure' that seeded psychoanalysis. Thus hysteria fundamentally denotes physical manifestations of emotional or psychological distress. The distractors reference other Freudian material: the horse phobia is the Little Hans case used to demonstrate the Oedipus complex, and oral behaviour concerns psychosexual fixation. This item tests the specifically psychosomatic, conversion meaning of hysteria in Freudian theory." },

  { id: "rml21_034", section: "psychopathology",
    q: "Which of the following are not included in the ICD criteria for Schizophrenia?",
    options: { a: "Delusions", b: "Catastrophic Thinking", c: "Disorganised speech", d: "Hallucinations" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "Delusions are a core positive symptom and a central diagnostic criterion for schizophrenia in both ICD and DSM.",
      c: "Disorganised speech (formal thought disorder) is a recognised diagnostic feature of schizophrenia.",
      d: "Hallucinations, especially auditory ones, are a hallmark positive symptom and key criterion for schizophrenia."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is catastrophic thinking, because it is NOT a diagnostic criterion for schizophrenia. Schizophrenia is defined in classification systems such as the ICD (International Classification of Diseases) and the DSM by a characteristic set of positive symptoms (excesses or distortions of normal function) and negative symptoms (reductions in normal function). The core positive symptoms that feature in the diagnostic criteria include delusions (fixed false beliefs, such as persecutory or grandiose delusions), hallucinations (perceptions without external stimuli, most commonly auditory hallucinations such as hearing voices), and disorganised speech or thinking (formal thought disorder, evident as derailment, tangentiality, or incoherence). Grossly disorganised or catatonic behaviour and negative symptoms such as flattened affect and avolition also count. Kurt Schneider's first-rank symptoms historically informed these criteria. Catastrophic thinking, by contrast, is a cognitive distortion in which a person assumes the worst possible outcome will occur. It is characteristic of anxiety disorders and depression and is a target of cognitive therapy in the tradition of Aaron Beck, but it is not part of the schizophrenia criteria. The item tests the student's ability to separate the defining psychotic symptoms of schizophrenia from a cognitive distortion belonging to the anxiety and mood disorders." },

  { id: "rml21_035", section: "psychopathology",
    q: "The cardinal DSM-IV-TR diagnostic criterion for Mental Retardation is based primarily on an IQ score:",
    options: { a: "Seven standard deviations below the mean.", b: "One standard deviations below the mean.", c: "Two standard deviations below the mean.", d: "Four standard deviations below the mean" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "Seven standard deviations below the mean would correspond to an IQ near zero, far beyond any clinical cutoff and biologically implausible as a threshold.",
      b: "One standard deviation below the mean gives an IQ of about 85, which is within the low-average range and not a criterion for intellectual disability.",
      d: "Four standard deviations below the mean (IQ around 40) falls within the moderate-to-severe range but is far below the general diagnostic threshold, so it is too extreme as the cutoff."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is two standard deviations below the mean. IQ tests such as the Wechsler scales, developed by David Wechsler, are standardised to a mean of 100 with a standard deviation of 15. The DSM-IV-TR diagnostic criterion for Mental Retardation (the older term for what the DSM-5 calls Intellectual Disability) specified significantly subaverage intellectual functioning, operationalised as an IQ of approximately 70 or below. An IQ of 70 lies exactly two standard deviations below the mean (100 minus 2 times 15 equals 70), which is why the two-standard-deviation cutoff is the cardinal criterion. Below that threshold roughly the bottom 2 to 3 percent of the population falls, reflecting the properties of the normal distribution. Importantly, the diagnosis required more than a low IQ alone: it also demanded concurrent deficits in adaptive functioning (such as communication, self-care, and social skills) with onset before age 18. The severity levels (mild, moderate, severe, profound) were graded by IQ ranges. The distractors give cutoffs that are either far too lenient (one SD, IQ 85) or implausibly extreme (four or seven SD). This item tests knowledge of the statistical basis of the intellectual disability threshold, anchored at two standard deviations below the mean." },

  { id: "rml21_036", section: "psychopathology",
    q: "Catastrophic thinking is common to several anxiety disorders. The term describes:",
    options: { a: "Categorising all experiences as either extremely positive or extremely negative.", b: "Focusing on negative events and ignoring positive ones.", c: "Worrying that the worst will always happen.", d: "Taking things very personally." },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "Categorising experiences as all good or all bad describes dichotomous or black-and-white thinking, a different cognitive distortion.",
      b: "Focusing on negatives while ignoring positives describes the mental filter or selective abstraction distortion, not catastrophising.",
      d: "Taking things very personally describes personalisation, another distinct cognitive distortion."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is worrying that the worst will always happen. Catastrophic thinking, or catastrophising, is a cognitive distortion in which a person automatically assumes and expects the worst possible outcome in a situation, magnifying the likelihood and severity of negative events. Cognitive distortions are systematic errors in thinking identified within cognitive therapy, pioneered by Aaron Beck, and cognitive behaviour therapy. Albert Ellis, in Rational Emotive Behaviour Therapy, similarly described 'awfulising' as an irrational belief. Catastrophising is especially prominent in anxiety disorders: a person with panic disorder may interpret a racing heart as a sign of an impending heart attack, and someone with health anxiety may read a minor symptom as evidence of serious illness, which fuels the anxiety spiral. Therapy targets it by helping the client examine the evidence, generate more realistic alternative outcomes, and decatastrophise ('what is the worst, best, and most likely outcome?'). The distractors name other well-known distortions from Beck's framework: dichotomous or all-or-nothing thinking (option A), mental filter or selective abstraction (option B), and personalisation (option D). This item tests precise identification of catastrophising among the family of cognitive distortions." },

  { id: "rml21_037", section: "psychopathology",
    q: "Panic disorder is where:",
    options: { a: "An individual experiences a singular panic attack.", b: "There is excessive anxiety and worry about many different aspects of life.", c: "There is a fear of panicking in enclosed spaces.", d: "There are recurrent and unexpected panic attacks" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: {
      a: "A single, isolated panic attack does not meet the criteria for panic disorder, which requires recurrent attacks plus persistent concern about them.",
      b: "Excessive worry about many aspects of life describes generalised anxiety disorder, not panic disorder.",
      c: "Fear tied specifically to enclosed spaces describes a form of agoraphobia or claustrophobia, not the core definition of panic disorder."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is that there are recurrent and unexpected panic attacks. Panic disorder is an anxiety disorder defined by recurrent, unexpected panic attacks, which are sudden surges of intense fear or discomfort that peak within minutes and are accompanied by physical and cognitive symptoms such as palpitations, sweating, trembling, shortness of breath, chest pain, dizziness, and a fear of dying or losing control. Critically, the attacks come 'out of the blue' without an obvious trigger, and the diagnosis further requires that at least one attack be followed by a month or more of persistent worry about having more attacks or a maladaptive change in behaviour to avoid them (anticipatory anxiety). A single attack (option A) is insufficient. This links to the cognitive model of panic by David M. Clark, which holds that panic arises from catastrophic misinterpretation of benign bodily sensations, and to interoceptive conditioning accounts. The distractors describe other conditions: excessive, uncontrollable worry across many life domains is generalised anxiety disorder, and fear of enclosed spaces is a specific phobia or relates to agoraphobia. Panic disorder frequently co-occurs with agoraphobia (fear of situations where escape is difficult). This item tests the defining feature of recurrent unexpected panic attacks." },

  { id: "rml21_038", section: "psychopathology",
    q: "Within attention deficit/hyperactivity disorder, which of the following is NOT classified as a problem relating to hyperactivity/impulsivity?",
    options: { a: "Experiences difficulties in playing quietly.", b: "Rather than sitting still, squirms in seat and fidgets.", c: "Talks excessively.", d: "Doesn't appear to listen when being told something or spoken to" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: {
      a: "Difficulty playing quietly is a listed hyperactivity-impulsivity symptom, so it does not fit the 'not hyperactive' answer.",
      b: "Squirming and fidgeting is a classic hyperactivity symptom, so it belongs to that cluster and is not the exception.",
      c: "Talking excessively is a recognised hyperactivity-impulsivity symptom, so it is not the odd one out."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is that not appearing to listen when spoken to is NOT a hyperactivity/impulsivity symptom, because it belongs instead to the inattention cluster. Attention deficit/hyperactivity disorder (ADHD) is a neurodevelopmental disorder whose DSM criteria are organised into two symptom domains. The inattention domain includes symptoms such as failing to give close attention to details, difficulty sustaining attention, not seeming to listen when spoken to directly, failing to follow through on instructions, difficulty organising tasks, avoiding tasks requiring sustained mental effort, losing things, being easily distracted, and forgetfulness. The hyperactivity/impulsivity domain includes fidgeting and squirming, leaving one's seat, running or climbing inappropriately, being unable to play quietly, being 'on the go' as if driven by a motor, talking excessively, blurting out answers, difficulty waiting one's turn, and interrupting others. ADHD presentations can be predominantly inattentive, predominantly hyperactive-impulsive, or combined. In this item, options A (difficulty playing quietly), B (squirming and fidgeting), and C (talking excessively) are all hyperactivity/impulsivity symptoms, whereas option D (not appearing to listen) is an inattention symptom, making it the correct exception. This item tests the student's ability to sort ADHD symptoms into the correct diagnostic cluster." },

  { id: "rml21_039", section: "psychopathology",
    q: "Dysthymic or Persistent depressive disorder (PDD) differs from major depressive disorder (MDD) in the following ways:",
    options: { a: "Symptoms include hallucinations and delusional thinking.", b: "Symptoms only affect women during the winter months.", c: "Symptoms are more severe and last longer.", d: "Symptoms are less severe but last longer." },
    correct: "d",
    traps: {
      a: "Hallucinations and delusions indicate psychotic features, which can occur in severe major depression but do not define dysthymia; dysthymia is milder, not psychotic.",
      b: "Symptoms confined to winter describe seasonal affective disorder (seasonal pattern), not dysthymia, and dysthymia is not sex-restricted.",
      c: "This reverses the relationship: dysthymia is less severe than MDD, not more severe, even though it is more chronic."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is that in dysthymia the symptoms are less severe but last longer. Persistent depressive disorder (PDD), the DSM-5 label that merged the older diagnoses of dysthymia and chronic major depression, is a chronic form of depression. Its defining contrast with major depressive disorder (MDD) is chronicity versus severity. MDD involves discrete major depressive episodes marked by more severe symptoms (such as pronounced anhedonia, significant appetite or sleep changes, and possibly suicidal ideation) that must last at least two weeks. Dysthymia or PDD, by contrast, involves milder but more enduring depressive symptoms (such as low mood, low energy, poor self-esteem, and hopelessness) that persist for at least two years in adults (or one year in children and adolescents), with symptom-free periods lasting no longer than two months. So the depressed mood in PDD is generally less intense than in a full major depressive episode but is far more chronic and long-lasting. A person can even experience a major depressive episode superimposed on dysthymia, historically called double depression. The distractors describe psychotic depression, seasonal affective disorder, and a reversed severity claim. This item tests the severity-versus-duration distinction between chronic and episodic depression." },

  { id: "rml21_040", section: "psychopathology",
    q: "In the context of schizophrenia, the term expressed emotion (EE) refers to:",
    options: { a: "An individual with schizophrenia who has difficulty talking about their feelings.", b: "A family environment where overwhelmingly positive comments are made.", c: "A lack of emotion displayed in a family.", d: "A family environment where there are emotionally loaded communication patterns of a hostile nature." },
    correct: "d",
    traps: {
      a: "Difficulty talking about feelings describes alexithymia or a negative symptom of the patient, not the family construct of expressed emotion.",
      b: "Expressed emotion is defined by critical, hostile, and over-involved comments, not by overwhelmingly positive ones, so this is the opposite of high EE.",
      c: "A lack of displayed emotion is nearly the reverse of high expressed emotion, which involves intense, emotionally charged family interactions."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is a family environment with emotionally loaded communication patterns of a hostile nature. Expressed emotion (EE) is a well-validated construct describing the emotional climate within the family of a person with a mental disorder, particularly schizophrenia. It was developed through research by George Brown and later refined by Christine Vaughn and Julian Leff, who created the Camberwell Family Interview to measure it. High expressed emotion is characterised by three components: critical comments (expressions of dislike or disapproval of the patient), hostility (generalised criticism or rejection of the person), and emotional over-involvement (excessive concern, intrusiveness, overprotection, or self-sacrifice). Extensive research shows that patients with schizophrenia who return to live in high-EE households have substantially higher relapse rates than those in low-EE households, even when medication is controlled for. This finding is a cornerstone of the diathesis-stress and family-environment understanding of the course of schizophrenia and has driven family-based psychoeducational interventions aimed at lowering EE to reduce relapse. The distractors describe the opposite (positive or emotionally flat environments) or a symptom of the patient rather than the family climate. This item tests understanding of expressed emotion as a hostile, critical, over-involved family atmosphere linked to relapse." },

  { id: "rml21_041", section: "psychopathology",
    q: "Personality disorders are stable long-term conditions which can affect an individual's:",
    options: { a: "Cognition", b: "Mood", c: "Interpersonal functioning", d: "All of the above" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: {
      a: "Cognition (distorted ways of perceiving self and others) is affected, but selecting it alone ignores the mood and interpersonal domains equally affected.",
      b: "Mood or affectivity is disrupted in personality disorders, but it is only one of the domains, so it cannot be the sole answer.",
      c: "Interpersonal functioning is impaired, but again it is one of several affected areas, not the complete picture."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is all of the above. A personality disorder is defined as an enduring, inflexible, and pervasive pattern of inner experience and behaviour that deviates markedly from the expectations of the individual's culture, begins by adolescence or early adulthood, is stable over time, and leads to distress or impairment. According to the DSM criteria, this maladaptive pattern manifests across several broad domains, and the definition explicitly requires deviation in two or more of the following areas: cognition (ways of perceiving and interpreting oneself, other people, and events), affectivity (the range, intensity, lability, and appropriateness of emotional responses, that is, mood), interpersonal functioning (how the person relates to others), and impulse control. Because personality disorders are ego-syntonic and pervasive, they colour virtually the whole personality rather than one narrow area, which is why they affect cognition, mood, and interpersonal relationships together. The DSM groups the ten personality disorders into three clusters: Cluster A (odd or eccentric, such as paranoid, schizoid, schizotypal), Cluster B (dramatic, emotional, or erratic, such as borderline, narcissistic, antisocial, histrionic), and Cluster C (anxious or fearful, such as avoidant, dependent, obsessive-compulsive). Since all three listed domains are genuinely affected, the inclusive option is correct. This item tests the multi-domain definition of personality disorders." },

  { id: "rml21_042", section: "biopsych",
    q: "The term psychoactive refers to:",
    options: { a: "A drug-induced hallucination.", b: "A particularly active psychopath.", c: "A drug that alters mood, cognition and/or behaviour.", d: "The psychology of physical activity." },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "A drug-induced hallucination is one possible effect of some psychoactive substances, but it is not the definition of the term psychoactive itself.",
      b: "A particularly active psychopath is a play on the word 'psycho' and has nothing to do with the pharmacological meaning of psychoactive.",
      d: "The psychology of physical activity is exercise or sport psychology, unrelated to the meaning of psychoactive substances."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is a drug that alters mood, cognition and/or behaviour. A psychoactive (or psychotropic) substance is any chemical that crosses the blood-brain barrier and acts primarily on the central nervous system to change brain function, thereby altering perception, mood, consciousness, cognition, or behaviour. Psychoactive drugs work by influencing neurotransmitter systems, for example by mimicking a neurotransmitter (agonists), blocking it (antagonists), or affecting its release, reuptake, or breakdown. They are conventionally grouped into broad classes: depressants (such as alcohol and benzodiazepines, which slow central nervous system activity), stimulants (such as caffeine, nicotine, cocaine, and amphetamines, which increase arousal and activity), opioids or narcotics (such as morphine and heroin, which relieve pain and produce euphoria), and hallucinogens or psychedelics (such as LSD and psilocybin, which distort perception). Many psychoactive drugs carry potential for tolerance, dependence, and addiction, concepts studied within the neuroscience of reward involving the dopaminergic mesolimbic pathway. Hallucination (option A) is only one possible effect of certain hallucinogens, not the general definition, and the remaining distractors are wordplay. This item tests the precise definition of psychoactive as any brain-altering, mood-cognition-behaviour-changing drug." },

  { id: "rml21_043", section: "psychopathology",
    q: "Bulimia nervosa is characterised by recurrent binge eating and inappropriate use of compensatory behaviours. An example of compensatory behaviour is:",
    options: { a: "Use of laxatives", b: "Self-induced vomiting", c: "Excessive exercising", d: "All of the above" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: {
      a: "Laxative misuse is a genuine purging compensatory behaviour, but choosing it alone omits the other valid examples.",
      b: "Self-induced vomiting is the most well-known compensatory behaviour, but it is only one of several, so it cannot be the sole answer.",
      c: "Excessive exercise is a recognised non-purging compensatory behaviour, but again it is only one example among several."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is all of the above. Bulimia nervosa is an eating disorder defined by a cycle of recurrent binge eating followed by inappropriate compensatory behaviours aimed at preventing weight gain, together with a self-evaluation that is unduly influenced by body shape and weight. A binge involves eating an objectively large amount of food in a discrete period with a sense of loss of control. To compensate and counteract the calories, individuals engage in a variety of behaviours, which the DSM divides into purging and non-purging types. Purging compensatory behaviours include self-induced vomiting and the misuse of laxatives, diuretics, or enemas. Non-purging compensatory behaviours include excessive exercise and fasting or extreme dietary restriction. All three options, laxative use, self-induced vomiting, and excessive exercising, are therefore genuine examples of compensatory behaviour, making the inclusive answer correct. Unlike anorexia nervosa, individuals with bulimia are often of normal weight or overweight, which can make the disorder harder to detect, though it carries serious medical risks such as electrolyte imbalance, dental erosion, and esophageal damage. The DSM-5 requires the binge-purge cycle to occur on average at least once a week for three months. This item tests recognition of the range of compensatory behaviours in bulimia nervosa." },

  { id: "rml21_044", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Chronic alcoholism may result in which amnesic disorder?",
    options: { a: "Korsakoff's syndrome", b: "Alzheimer's disease", c: "Pick's disease", d: "Parkinson's disease" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: {
      b: "Alzheimer's disease is a progressive neurodegenerative dementia associated with amyloid plaques and tau tangles, not caused by alcohol-related thiamine deficiency.",
      c: "Pick's disease (a frontotemporal dementia) causes personality and language changes from frontal and temporal atrophy, unrelated to chronic alcohol use.",
      d: "Parkinson's disease is a movement disorder caused by loss of dopamine neurons in the substantia nigra, not an amnesic syndrome from alcoholism."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Korsakoff's syndrome. Korsakoff's syndrome (Korsakoff's psychosis or amnesic syndrome), named after the Russian neuropsychiatrist Sergei Korsakoff, is a chronic memory disorder most commonly caused by chronic alcoholism. The underlying cause is a severe deficiency of thiamine (vitamin B1), which is common in people with alcohol use disorder because alcohol impairs thiamine absorption and because of poor nutrition. Thiamine deficiency damages structures involved in memory, particularly the mammillary bodies and the dorsomedial nucleus of the thalamus. Korsakoff's syndrome typically follows an acute phase called Wernicke's encephalopathy (confusion, ataxia, and eye-movement abnormalities), and the combined condition is termed Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome. The hallmark features are a profound anterograde amnesia (inability to form new long-term memories) along with retrograde amnesia, and confabulation, in which the patient unintentionally fills memory gaps with fabricated or distorted accounts they believe to be true. Insight is usually poor. The distractors are other neurological disorders: Alzheimer's and Pick's disease are dementias with different pathologies, and Parkinson's is primarily a movement disorder. This item tests the specific link between chronic alcohol-related thiamine deficiency and the amnesic Korsakoff's syndrome." },

  { id: "rml21_045", section: "psychopathology",
    q: "Within mood disorders, two key symptoms of a manic episode are:",
    options: { a: "Hallucinations and flight of ideas", b: "Distractibility and social phobia.", c: "Extremely elevated mood and grandiose ideas.", d: "Delusional thinking and intense fear of weight gain." },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "Flight of ideas does occur in mania, but hallucinations are not a core defining symptom of a manic episode (they indicate psychotic features), so the pairing is not the best answer.",
      b: "Distractibility is a manic symptom, but social phobia is an anxiety disorder unrelated to the core features of mania, making this pair incorrect.",
      d: "Delusional thinking can occur in severe mania with psychotic features, but intense fear of weight gain belongs to eating disorders, so this pairing does not fit."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is extremely elevated mood and grandiose ideas. A manic episode is the defining feature of Bipolar I Disorder and is characterised by a distinct period of abnormally and persistently elevated, expansive, or irritable mood, together with increased goal-directed activity or energy, lasting at least one week. The core symptoms captured by the DSM mnemonic DIG FAST include Distractibility, Insomnia or decreased need for sleep, Grandiosity (inflated self-esteem, sometimes reaching grandiose delusions of special powers or importance), Flight of ideas or racing thoughts, increased Activity or psychomotor Agitation, pressured Speech, and Thoughtlessness or excessive involvement in risky pleasurable activities such as spending sprees or reckless behaviour. Of the options given, extremely elevated mood and grandiosity are the two most cardinal and specific symptoms of mania. The disorder was extensively described by Emil Kraepelin, who distinguished manic-depressive illness from dementia praecox (schizophrenia). Options A, B, and D each pair one genuine or plausible manic feature with a symptom that belongs to a different disorder (psychosis, anxiety, or eating disorders), which is why they are inferior. This item tests recognition of the two most defining features of a manic episode." },

  { id: "rml21_046", section: "stats",
    q: "Action-research can be understood as:",
    options: { a: "Longitudinal research", b: "An applied research", c: "A kind of research being carried out to solve a specific problem", d: "All of the above" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "Longitudinal research follows the same subjects over time; action research is not defined by a longitudinal design, so this is too narrow and not the defining feature.",
      b: "Action research is a form of applied research, but simply labelling it applied misses its distinctive cyclical, problem-solving, participatory character.",
      d: "'All of the above' is wrong because action research is not inherently longitudinal, so not every listed option applies."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is a kind of research being carried out to solve a specific problem. Action research is an approach coined and developed by Kurt Lewin, who famously argued that there is nothing so practical as a good theory and that research should feed directly into social change. Action research is a participatory, cyclical process in which practitioners investigate a real, specific problem in their own setting (such as a classroom, organisation, or community) and use the findings to improve their practice. Lewin described it as a spiral of steps: planning, taking action, observing the results, and reflecting, which then informs the next cycle of planning and action. Its defining purpose is practical problem-solving and bringing about change in a particular context, rather than producing broad generalisable laws, which distinguishes it from basic or pure research. While action research is indeed a form of applied research (option B is partly true), the option that best captures its essential defining feature is that it is conducted to solve a specific, concrete problem. It is not inherently longitudinal, so option A does not define it, which also rules out 'all of the above.' This item tests understanding of Lewin's problem-focused, cyclical conception of action research." },

  { id: "rml21_047", section: "stats",
    q: "Which of the following features are considered as critical in qualitative research?",
    options: { a: "Collecting data with the help of standardised research tools.", b: "Design sampling with probability sample techniques.", c: "Collecting data in context specific environment with bottom-up empirical evidence.", d: "Gathering data with top-down schematic evidence." },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "Standardised research tools that yield numerical measurements are hallmarks of quantitative research, not the flexible, open methods of qualitative inquiry.",
      b: "Probability sampling is characteristic of quantitative research aiming to generalise; qualitative research typically uses purposive or non-probability sampling.",
      d: "Top-down schematic (theory-driven, deductive) evidence characterises quantitative hypothesis testing; qualitative research is generally bottom-up and inductive."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is collecting data in a context-specific environment with bottom-up empirical evidence. Qualitative research is an approach that seeks to understand the meaning, experience, and social context of phenomena in depth, using non-numerical data such as interviews, observations, and texts. Its defining features distinguish it from quantitative research. Qualitative research is naturalistic and context-specific: data are gathered in real-world settings where the phenomenon occurs, because meaning is understood to be embedded in its context. It is inductive and bottom-up: rather than starting with a fixed hypothesis and testing it (the top-down, deductive logic of quantitative work), the researcher builds understanding, patterns, and theory up from the data itself, an approach exemplified by the grounded theory method of Barney Glaser and Anselm Strauss. It typically uses purposive or non-probability sampling to select information-rich cases, and flexible, open-ended data collection rather than standardised instruments. The researcher is often seen as the key instrument, and reflexivity is valued. Options A, B, and D all describe features of quantitative research (standardised tools, probability sampling, and top-down theory-driven evidence), which is why they are wrong. This item tests the student's grasp of the naturalistic, inductive, context-embedded nature of qualitative inquiry." },

  { id: "rml21_048", section: "stats",
    q: "'The ethical principle that research participants should be told enough about a piece of research to be able to make a decision about whether to participate in it.' Which research principle is this a glossary definition of?",
    options: { a: "Informed consent.", b: "Debriefing", c: "Expectation Management.", d: "De-concealment." },
    correct: "a",
    traps: {
      b: "Debriefing occurs after participation, informing participants about the study's true purpose and addressing any deception; it is not the pre-participation decision principle described.",
      c: "'Expectation Management' is not a recognised formal research ethics principle in this context.",
      d: "'De-concealment' is not a standard named ethical principle; the removal of deception is normally handled through debriefing, not a principle by this name."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is informed consent. Informed consent is a foundational ethical principle in research with human participants, enshrined in codes such as those of the American Psychological Association and rooted historically in the Nuremberg Code and the Declaration of Helsinki. It requires that, before agreeing to take part, prospective participants be given enough understandable information about the study, its purpose, procedures, potential risks and benefits, and their rights, so that they can make a free, voluntary, and competent decision about whether to participate. Consent must be given without coercion, and participants must know they can withdraw at any time without penalty. This exactly matches the glossary definition in the question. The distractors refer to other ethical safeguards that apply at different points. Debriefing (option B) occurs after the study, when participants are told the full purpose, any deception is explained and justified, and their questions are answered, restoring them to their pre-study state. Where full disclosure beforehand would compromise validity, limited deception may be used only if justified and followed by debriefing. Expectation management and de-concealment are not standard named principles. This item tests the ability to match a definition to the correct research ethics concept, distinguishing consent (before) from debriefing (after)." },

  { id: "rml21_049", section: "stats",
    q: "Which of the following is a pooled variance estimate that constitutes the denominator of an F-ratio?",
    options: { a: "The between-cells mean square (MSB).", b: "The mean of the sampling distribution.", c: "The sum of the deviations from the grand mean.", d: "The within-cells mean square (MSw)." },
    correct: "d",
    traps: {
      a: "The between-cells mean square (MSB) is the numerator of the F-ratio, reflecting variance due to the treatment or group differences, not the denominator.",
      b: "The mean of the sampling distribution is a descriptive quantity, not a variance estimate, and plays no role as the F-ratio denominator.",
      c: "The sum of deviations from the grand mean is a raw sum of squares component, not a mean square (variance estimate), and the deviations sum to zero anyway."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is the within-cells mean square (MSw). Analysis of variance (ANOVA), developed by Ronald Aylmer Fisher, tests whether the means of several groups differ by partitioning the total variability in the data into components and forming a ratio of variances. The F-ratio is calculated as the between-groups mean square divided by the within-groups mean square. The numerator, the between-cells or between-groups mean square (MSB), captures variance due to the treatment effect plus error, reflecting how much the group means differ from the grand mean. The denominator, the within-cells or within-groups mean square (MSw, also called the error mean square), is a pooled estimate of the variance within each group, that is, the average variability of scores around their own group means, and it represents random error or noise unaffected by the treatment. Because MSw pools the variance estimates from all the separate cells or groups, it is the pooled variance estimate that serves as the F-ratio denominator. If the null hypothesis is true, both estimate the same error variance and F is near 1.00; if the treatment has an effect, the numerator grows and F increases. The distractors are either the numerator, a non-variance quantity, or a sum-of-squares term. This item tests understanding of the structure of the F-ratio in ANOVA." },

  { id: "rml21_050", section: "stats",
    q: "Researchers studying meditation contacted over 10000 randomly selected adults in 10 nations. The results showed that those who meditated on a regular basis were less likely to suffer from stress than adults who meditate less regularly or not at all. Can we conclude that meditation lowers stress? Why?",
    options: { a: "Yes, because the sample size was large", b: "Yes, because the adults were randomly selected", c: "Yes, because the adults were randomly assigned", d: "Not necessarily, because this wasn't an experiment" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: {
      a: "A large sample improves precision and reduces sampling error, but it cannot turn a correlational study into one that establishes causation.",
      b: "Random selection improves the generalisability (external validity) of the sample but does not control confounds, so it cannot establish that meditation causes lower stress.",
      c: "The adults were not randomly assigned; they chose whether to meditate, so no random assignment occurred, and this option misstates the study's design."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is not necessarily, because this was not an experiment. This item tests the crucial distinction between correlation and causation and the difference between random selection and random assignment. The study is observational or correlational: researchers merely measured whether people already meditated and compared their stress levels. Because participants were not randomly assigned to meditate or not, the meditating and non-meditating groups may differ systematically in other ways (confounding variables) such as income, lifestyle, personality, health, or baseline stress. Any of these third variables could explain the association, and the direction of causation is ambiguous (perhaps low-stress people are simply more able to keep up a meditation habit, the reverse-causation problem). To establish that meditation causes lower stress, researchers would need a true experiment in which participants are randomly assigned to a meditation condition or a control condition, so that random assignment (a concept central to Ronald Fisher's experimental design) equalises the groups on all other variables and isolates the treatment as the only systematic difference. Large sample size (option A) and random selection (option B) improve precision and generalisability but do not permit causal inference, and option C falsely claims random assignment occurred. This item tests the logic of causal inference." },

  { id: "rml21_051", section: "stats",
    q: "A famous study examined the smoking habits of British doctors and whether or not the doctors development various types of cancers. Participants responded to questionnaires about their smoking habits and health in 1951, 1957, 1966 and many more times all the way up to 2001. What type of observation study is this?",
    options: { a: "A retrospective observational study", b: "A prospective observational study", c: "A sample survey", d: "None of these. It's an experimental study." },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "A retrospective study looks backward at past exposures after outcomes have occurred; here participants were followed forward in time from 1951 onward, so it is prospective, not retrospective.",
      c: "A one-time sample survey captures a single cross-sectional snapshot; this study repeatedly followed the same doctors over decades, making it a longitudinal cohort, not a simple survey.",
      d: "It is not experimental because researchers did not assign smoking; they only observed doctors' self-chosen habits, so there was no manipulated independent variable."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is a prospective observational study. This describes the famous British Doctors Study by Richard Doll and Austin Bradford Hill, which established the causal link between smoking and lung cancer. It is observational because the researchers did not manipulate any variable; they could not ethically assign doctors to smoke or not, so they simply observed the doctors' self-selected smoking behaviour and recorded health outcomes. It is prospective because it followed a cohort forward in time: starting in 1951, the researchers identified the doctors, recorded their smoking status, and then tracked them across the following decades (1957, 1966, and onward to 2001) to see who subsequently developed cancer and other diseases. This forward-looking, longitudinal cohort design contrasts with a retrospective study, which starts with people who already have the outcome and looks backward to reconstruct past exposures. A prospective design reduces certain biases (such as recall bias) and allows researchers to establish that the exposure preceded the outcome, strengthening causal inference, although as an observational study it still cannot fully rule out confounding the way a randomised experiment can. The distractors mislabel the temporal direction, oversimplify it as a one-shot survey, or wrongly call it experimental. This item tests recognition of a prospective cohort design." },

  { id: "rml21_052", section: "stats",
    q: "Which of the following statements is TRUE?",
    options: { a: "In one-way ANOVA the total sum of squares comprises two main sources of variance: within-groups variance and between-groups variance. Each has the same number of degrees of freedom.", b: "In one-way ANOVA the total sum of squares comprises two main sources of variance: within-groups variance and between-groups variance. Each has its own number of degrees of freedom.", c: "In one-way ANOVA the total sum of squares comprises three main sources of variance: within-groups variance, between-groups variance and error variance. Each has the same number of degrees of freedom.", d: "In one-way ANOVA the total sum of squares comprises three main sources of variance: within-groups variance, between-groups variance and information variance. Each has the same number of degrees of freedom." },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "The two sources are correctly named, but it is false that each has the same degrees of freedom; between and within groups have different df.",
      c: "In one-way ANOVA the within-groups variance IS the error variance, so listing them as two separate sources gives three, which is incorrect; there are only two sources.",
      d: "'Information variance' is not a real ANOVA component, and again there are only two sources, not three, so this is doubly wrong."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is that in one-way ANOVA the total sum of squares comprises two main sources of variance, within-groups and between-groups variance, and each has its own number of degrees of freedom. In the analysis of variance developed by Ronald Aylmer Fisher, the total variability of the data (total sum of squares) is partitioned into exactly two components in a one-way design. The between-groups sum of squares reflects variability among the group means (the treatment effect), and the within-groups sum of squares (also called the error or residual sum of squares) reflects variability of individual scores around their own group mean. Crucially, in one-way ANOVA the within-groups variance is the same thing as the error variance, so there are two sources, not three, which eliminates options C and D. Also crucial is that each source has its own distinct degrees of freedom: the between-groups df equals the number of groups minus one (k minus 1), while the within-groups df equals the total number of observations minus the number of groups (N minus k), and these two add up to the total df (N minus 1). Because the df differ, option A is false. Dividing each sum of squares by its own df yields the mean squares whose ratio is F. This item tests the correct partitioning and degrees of freedom in one-way ANOVA." },

  { id: "rml21_053", section: "stats",
    q: "A psychologist wants to study how I.Q., age and sex affect the reading speed. The subjects will be equal numbers of males and females, ages thirty or sixty, and either have an I.Q. of 100 or of 120. The statistical design for the study is:",
    options: { a: "Two factor", b: "Three factor", c: "Six factor", d: "Eight factor" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "Two factors would ignore one of the independent variables; there are clearly three manipulated grouping variables here (IQ, age, and sex).",
      c: "Six is the number of conditions or cells only if you multiply levels wrongly; the design is defined by the number of factors, not by summing levels.",
      d: "Eight is the number of cells (2 times 2 times 2), the product of the levels, not the number of factors; the question asks for the design in terms of factors."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is a three-factor design. In experimental design and factorial ANOVA terminology, a factor is an independent variable whose effect on the dependent variable is being studied, and each factor has two or more levels. Here the dependent variable is reading speed, and there are three independent variables (factors): IQ (with two levels, 100 or 120), age (with two levels, thirty or sixty), and sex (with two levels, male or female). Because there are three independent variables being manipulated or grouped, the study uses a three-factor design, specifically a 2 x 2 x 2 factorial design. It is important not to confuse the number of factors with the number of cells or conditions: multiplying the levels (2 x 2 x 2) gives 8 cells or treatment combinations, which is why option D is a tempting trap, but 8 is the number of conditions, not the number of factors. A factorial design like this is powerful because it lets the researcher examine not only the main effect of each factor but also interaction effects (for example, whether the effect of IQ on reading speed differs by age or sex). Factorial ANOVA descends from the work of Ronald Aylmer Fisher. This item tests the distinction between factors and cells in factorial design." },

  { id: "rml21_054", section: "stats",
    q: "The z table is derived from:",
    options: { a: "The standard deviation", b: "The normal distribution", c: "The square root of the t-scores", d: "Random sampling" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "The standard deviation is used to compute a z score, but the z table of probabilities itself is derived from the shape of the normal distribution, not from the standard deviation alone.",
      c: "T-scores come from the t-distribution, a separate distribution used for small samples; the z table is not derived from the square root of t-scores.",
      d: "Random sampling is a data-collection procedure; it does not generate the mathematical z table, which comes from a theoretical distribution."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is the normal distribution. The z table (standard normal table) gives the proportion of the area under the curve, and hence the probability, associated with any given z score. It is derived directly from the standard normal distribution, which is a normal distribution with a mean of 0 and a standard deviation of 1. The normal distribution, often attributed to Carl Friedrich Gauss (hence 'Gaussian') and also developed by Abraham de Moivre and Pierre-Simon Laplace, is a symmetrical, bell-shaped curve described by a fixed mathematical equation. A z score standardises any raw score by expressing how many standard deviations it lies above or below the mean, using the formula z equals (score minus mean) divided by standard deviation. Once scores are converted to z scores, they all fall on the same standard normal curve, and the z table tabulates the cumulative area under that curve for each z value, allowing researchers to find percentiles and probabilities. Although the standard deviation is used in computing a z score (option A), the table of probabilities itself is generated from the integral of the normal distribution's equation. T-scores derive from the separate t-distribution used with small samples, and random sampling is unrelated to the origin of the table. This item tests the theoretical basis of the z table in the standard normal distribution." },

  { id: "rml21_055", section: "stats",
    q: "Which of the following statements is FALSE?",
    options: { a: "Inferential statistics draw upon information from sample data", b: "Inferential statistics draw upon knowledge of how random processes behave.", c: "Inferential statistics make statements about samples based on observing populations.", d: "Inferential statistics are used to make statements about how plausible it is that a random process could have produced results as extreme as those obtained in a given piece of research." },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "This is true: inferential statistics indeed use information gathered from sample data, so it is not the false statement sought.",
      b: "This is true: inferential statistics rely on probability theory and knowledge of how random processes behave, so it is not false.",
      d: "This is true: it describes hypothesis testing and p-values, which assess how plausibly chance alone produced the observed results, so it is a correct statement."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is that 'inferential statistics make statements about samples based on observing populations' is FALSE, because it reverses the actual logic. Inferential statistics work in exactly the opposite direction: they use data from a sample (a subset that is usually observed) to draw conclusions, estimates, or generalisations about the larger population (which is typically not fully observed). We observe the sample and infer about the population, not the other way around. Descriptive statistics simply summarise the data at hand, whereas inferential statistics go beyond the data to make probabilistic statements about a broader population, relying on probability theory and knowledge of how random processes behave. This underpins hypothesis testing, developed in the traditions of Ronald Aylmer Fisher and of Jerzy Neyman and Egon Pearson, where we estimate how plausible it is that random sampling variation alone could have produced results as extreme as those observed (the p-value). Options A, B, and D each correctly describe genuine features of inferential statistics: using sample data, drawing on the behaviour of random processes, and assessing the plausibility of chance-produced extreme results. Only option C states the sample-population relationship backwards, making it the false statement. This item tests the fundamental direction of statistical inference from sample to population." },

  { id: "rml21_056", section: "stats",
    q: "Which of the following statements about the F-distribution is false?",
    options: { a: "The distribution is asymmetrical.", b: "The distribution is one-tailed.", c: "Negative values of F are associated with a higher probability value.", d: "If the amount of between-cells variance is equal to the amount of within-subjects variance, the value of F will be 1.00." },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "This is true: the F-distribution is positively skewed and asymmetrical, so it is not the false statement.",
      b: "This is true: F tests are inherently one-tailed because F reflects a ratio of variances that grows only in one direction, so it is not false.",
      d: "This is true: when between-groups and within-groups variances are equal, their ratio F equals 1.00, which is a correct statement."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is that 'negative values of F are associated with a higher probability value' is FALSE, because F can never be negative. The F-distribution, named after Ronald Aylmer Fisher, is the sampling distribution of the ratio of two variance estimates (mean squares) in analysis of variance. Because it is a ratio of two variances, and variances are computed from squared deviations, both the numerator and denominator are always non-negative, so the F statistic itself can only range from 0 to positive infinity. Negative F values are mathematically impossible, which makes the statement about negative F values with higher probability simply false. The other three statements are all true properties of the F-distribution. It is asymmetrical, specifically positively skewed (skewed to the right), with the exact shape determined by two separate degrees-of-freedom parameters for the numerator and denominator. It is one-tailed in application: because larger between-groups variance relative to within-groups variance drives F upward, the region of rejection lies only in the upper tail. And when the between-groups (between-cells) variance equals the within-groups (within-subjects) variance, the ratio equals exactly 1.00, the value expected under the null hypothesis of no group differences. This item tests knowledge of the mathematical properties of the F-distribution." },

  { id: "rml21_057", section: "stats",
    q: "'The probability, as revealed by statistical test, that a random process (involving taking random samples of a particular size from a particular population) could produce some outcome.' What is this a definition of?",
    options: { a: "Random error", b: "Sampling error", c: "The p-value", d: "The q-value" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "Random error refers to unsystematic, chance variation in measurements, not to a probability computed by a statistical test about an outcome.",
      b: "Sampling error is the difference between a sample statistic and the true population parameter due to sampling; it is a discrepancy, not the probability that a random process yields a given outcome.",
      d: "The q-value is an adjusted significance measure (the false discovery rate analog) used in multiple testing, not the basic definition described here."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is the p-value. The p-value (probability value) is a cornerstone of null hypothesis significance testing, formalised in the tradition of Ronald Aylmer Fisher. It is defined as the probability of obtaining a result at least as extreme as the one observed, assuming that the null hypothesis is true, that is, assuming that only a random process (random sampling variation) is operating. In the wording of the question, it is the probability that a random process, taking random samples of a given size from a given population, could produce an outcome like the one obtained. A small p-value (conventionally below 0.05) indicates that such an extreme result would be unlikely to arise from chance alone, leading researchers to reject the null hypothesis and declare the result statistically significant. A large p-value means the observed data are reasonably compatible with chance, so the null hypothesis is retained. It is important to note the p-value is not the probability that the null hypothesis is true, a common misinterpretation. The distractors refer to related but different concepts: random error is unsystematic measurement noise, sampling error is the gap between sample and population values, and the q-value is a false-discovery-rate quantity for multiple comparisons. This item tests the precise definition of the p-value." },

  { id: "rml21_058", section: "stats",
    q: "Researchers working on a vaccine for HIV recruited over 16,000 volunteers for a study. Each volunteer was randomly assigned to receive monthly injections of either the vaccine or a placebo for a year. The researchers were curious if subjects who received the vaccine would be less likely to contract HIV than subjects who received the placebo. What is the main reason for randomly assigning the subjects?",
    options: { a: "To create groups that are as similar as possible so the main difference between the groups is the treatment", b: "To control against non-response", c: "To reduce the impact of sampling variability", d: "To ensure both the subjects and researchers are blind to who is in which group" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: {
      b: "Controlling non-response concerns who drops out or fails to reply; random assignment does not address non-response, so this is not its purpose.",
      c: "Reducing sampling variability relates to sample size and random selection, not to random assignment of participants to conditions.",
      d: "Blinding is achieved through the placebo and concealment procedures, not through random assignment itself; assignment can be random without anyone being blind."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is to create groups that are as similar as possible so the main difference between the groups is the treatment. Random assignment is the defining feature of a true experiment and the key to establishing causation. By allocating each participant to the vaccine or placebo group purely by chance, researchers ensure that, on average, the two groups are equivalent at the outset on all characteristics, both measured and unmeasured, such as age, health, lifestyle, and risk behaviour. This distributes potential confounding variables evenly across conditions, so that the only systematic difference between the groups is the treatment they receive. Consequently, if the vaccine group ends up with a lower HIV rate, that difference can be attributed to the vaccine rather than to pre-existing group differences, giving the study strong internal validity. This logic derives from the experimental design principles of Ronald Aylmer Fisher. It is essential to distinguish random assignment (used to form comparable groups and support causal inference) from random selection (used to obtain a representative sample and support generalisation). The distractors confuse random assignment with non-response control, sampling variability, and blinding, which are separate methodological issues addressed by other procedures. This item tests the core purpose of random assignment in experiments." },

  { id: "rml21_059", section: "stats",
    q: "Which of the following statements is correct?",
    options: { a: "A factorial design is not based on the analysis of variance", b: "Analysis of covariance is not related to correlation", c: "Correlation is related to predictability of Phenomenon", d: "The range of correlation coefficients is -0.04 to +0.04" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "This is false: factorial designs are analysed using factorial ANOVA, so they are indeed based on the analysis of variance.",
      b: "This is false: analysis of covariance (ANCOVA) combines ANOVA with regression and correlation to adjust for covariates, so it is related to correlation.",
      d: "This is false: correlation coefficients range from -1.00 to +1.00, not -0.04 to +0.04, which is a typographical distortion of the true range."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is that correlation is related to the predictability of a phenomenon. A correlation coefficient, most commonly the Pearson product-moment correlation developed by Karl Pearson, measures the strength and direction of the linear relationship between two variables and ranges from -1.00 (a perfect negative relationship) through 0 (no linear relationship) to +1.00 (a perfect positive relationship). The stronger the correlation (the closer to plus or minus 1), the more accurately one variable can be predicted from the other, which is the basis of regression and prediction; indeed, the square of the correlation, the coefficient of determination, indicates the proportion of variance in one variable that is predictable from the other. So correlation is fundamentally tied to predictability, making option C correct. The distractors are all false statements. Factorial designs are analysed precisely with factorial analysis of variance, so option A is wrong. Analysis of covariance (ANCOVA) blends ANOVA with regression, using the correlation between a covariate and the dependent variable to statistically remove its influence, so option B is wrong. And the true range of correlation coefficients is -1.00 to +1.00, not the distorted -0.04 to +0.04 in option D. This item tests understanding of correlation, its range, and its link to prediction, alongside related design concepts." },

  { id: "rml21_060", section: "stats",
    q: "A recent study showed that viral medications might be useful in fighting certain bacterial infections. Participants with gastrointestinal issues were assigned to either a placebo or viral medication for the first four weeks of the study, followed by an additional four weeks on the other treatment. Their symptoms showed significantly more improvement from the viral medication than the placebo. What type of study is this, and what is the explanatory variable?",
    options: { a: "An observational study where symptom improvement is the explanatory variable", b: "An observational study where the type of treatment (viral or placebo) is the explanatory variable", c: "An experiment where symptoms improvement is the explanatory variable", d: "An experiment where the type of treatment (viral or placebo) is the explanatory variable" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: {
      a: "It is not observational (the researchers assigned treatments), and symptom improvement is the response (outcome), not the explanatory variable.",
      b: "It is not observational because the researchers actively assigned treatments; assigning conditions makes it an experiment.",
      c: "It is correctly labelled an experiment, but symptom improvement is the response variable being measured, not the explanatory variable that is manipulated."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is an experiment where the type of treatment (viral or placebo) is the explanatory variable. Two decisions are required. First, is it observational or experimental? Because the researchers actively assigned participants to receive a specific treatment (placebo or viral medication) rather than merely observing what participants chose, this is an experiment; the deliberate imposition of a treatment by the investigator is the hallmark of an experimental study. This design is specifically a crossover (repeated-measures) experiment, since each participant received one treatment for four weeks and then switched to the other, serving as their own control. Second, which variable is the explanatory variable? The explanatory variable (independent variable) is the one that is manipulated and hypothesised to cause changes, here the type of treatment (viral medication versus placebo). The response variable (dependent variable) is the outcome that is measured, here the degree of symptom improvement. Options that call symptom improvement the explanatory variable confuse the outcome with the cause, and options that call the study observational ignore the fact that the researchers assigned the treatments. The experimental logic derives from Ronald Aylmer Fisher's principles. This item tests the ability to classify a study design and correctly identify the explanatory versus response variable." },

  { id: "rml21_061", section: "assessment",
    q: "Which of the following refers to the notion of the confirmatory bias?",
    options: { a: "Clinicians ignore information that does not support their hypothesis", b: "Clinicians ignore information that does not support their stereotypes.", c: "Clinicians interpret ambiguous information as supporting their hypothesis.", d: "All of the above" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: {
      a: "Ignoring disconfirming information is one manifestation of confirmation bias, but selecting it alone omits the other equally valid manifestations listed.",
      b: "Ignoring information that contradicts stereotypes is a form of confirmation bias, but it is only one aspect of the broader tendency.",
      c: "Interpreting ambiguous information as supporting one's hypothesis is a genuine feature of confirmation bias, but it too is only one part of the whole."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is all of the above. Confirmatory bias (confirmation bias) is the pervasive tendency to seek, interpret, favour, and recall information in a way that confirms one's pre-existing beliefs, hypotheses, or expectations, while giving disproportionately little attention to information that contradicts them. The concept was demonstrated experimentally by Peter Wason, whose selection task and 2-4-6 rule-discovery task showed that people tend to test hypotheses by looking for confirming rather than disconfirming evidence. In clinical assessment and diagnosis, confirmation bias is a serious source of error: a clinician who forms an early diagnostic hypothesis may then ignore or discount symptoms and information that do not fit that hypothesis (option A), may disregard evidence that contradicts a stereotype about a patient group (option B), and may interpret genuinely ambiguous or neutral information as though it supports the favoured hypothesis (option C). All three describe real manifestations of the same underlying bias, so the inclusive answer is correct. This bias contributes to premature closure and diagnostic overshadowing, which is why clinicians are trained to actively consider alternative hypotheses and seek disconfirming evidence, and why structured assessment reduces error. This item tests recognition that confirmation bias operates through several complementary mechanisms in clinical judgment." },

  { id: "rml21_062", section: "stats",
    q: "Which one is called non-probability sampling?",
    options: { a: "Quota sampling", b: "Cluster sampling", c: "Systematic sampling", d: "Stratified random sampling" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: {
      b: "Cluster sampling is a probability method: naturally occurring groups (clusters) are randomly selected, so every unit has a known chance of inclusion.",
      c: "Systematic sampling selects every nth unit from a list after a random start, giving a known selection probability, so it is a probability method.",
      d: "Stratified random sampling divides the population into strata and randomly samples within each, which is a probability method by definition."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is quota sampling. Sampling methods fall into two broad families. Probability (random) sampling methods give every member of the population a known, non-zero chance of being selected, which allows the sample to be representative and permits statistical generalisation to the population. These include simple random sampling, systematic sampling (selecting every nth element after a random start), stratified random sampling (dividing the population into subgroups or strata and randomly sampling within each), and cluster sampling (randomly selecting whole naturally occurring groups). Non-probability sampling methods, by contrast, do not rely on random selection, so the probability of any individual being chosen is unknown, which introduces potential selection bias and limits generalisability. Quota sampling is a non-probability method: the researcher sets target numbers (quotas) for particular subgroups (for example, so many men and so many women) and then fills those quotas by any convenient means, without random selection within the subgroups. Other non-probability methods include convenience sampling, purposive (judgmental) sampling, and snowball sampling. Options B, C, and D are all probability methods because they involve random selection at some stage, which is why quota sampling is the odd one out. This item tests the ability to distinguish probability from non-probability sampling techniques." },

  { id: "rml21_063", section: "stats",
    q: "In the analysis of variance the significance level indicates",
    options: { a: "The probability of making Type I error", b: "That F is correct", c: "The probability of making Type II error", d: "(A) and (B)" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: {
      b: "'That F is correct' is not a meaningful statistical statement; the significance level is a probability threshold, not a verification that the F statistic is correct.",
      c: "The probability of a Type II error is beta, not the significance level; the significance level (alpha) governs Type I errors, so this confuses the two error types.",
      d: "Since option B is meaningless and only A is correct, combining A and B is wrong."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is the probability of making a Type I error. In hypothesis testing, including the analysis of variance, the significance level, denoted alpha, is the threshold probability that the researcher sets in advance (commonly 0.05) for rejecting the null hypothesis. It represents the probability of making a Type I error, which is rejecting a true null hypothesis, in other words concluding that there is a real effect or group difference when in fact none exists (a false positive). This framework of controlling error rates was developed by Jerzy Neyman and Egon Pearson, who formalised the distinction between two kinds of error. A Type I error (alpha) is a false positive: wrongly rejecting a true null hypothesis. A Type II error (beta) is a false negative: failing to reject a false null hypothesis, that is, missing a real effect. The power of a test equals 1 minus beta. Setting alpha at 0.05 means the researcher accepts a 5 percent risk of a Type I error. So in ANOVA, when we test the F ratio against a critical value at a chosen significance level, that level tells us the probability of a Type I error we are willing to tolerate. Option C confuses alpha with beta, and option B is not a coherent statistical statement. This item tests understanding of the significance level as the Type I error rate." },

  { id: "rml21_064", section: "stats",
    q: "The statistical theorem states that given a sufficiently large sample size, the sampling distribution of the mean for a variable will be approximately normally distributed, regardless of that variable's distribution in the Population. What is this definition of?",
    options: { a: "The normal distribution theorem", b: "The central limit theorem", c: "The sampling distribution theorem", d: "The law of large numbers" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "'The normal distribution theorem' is not a standard named theorem; the normal distribution is a distribution, not the theorem describing sampling-distribution behaviour.",
      c: "'The sampling distribution theorem' is not the recognised name; the described property is specifically the central limit theorem.",
      d: "The law of large numbers states that the sample mean converges to the population mean as sample size grows; it does not describe the shape (normality) of the sampling distribution."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is the central limit theorem. The central limit theorem (CLT) is one of the most important results in statistics and the theoretical foundation for much of inferential statistics. It states that if you take sufficiently large random samples from any population, regardless of the shape of that population's own distribution (even if it is skewed, bimodal, or otherwise non-normal), the sampling distribution of the sample mean will be approximately normal. Moreover, this sampling distribution will have a mean equal to the population mean and a standard deviation (called the standard error of the mean) equal to the population standard deviation divided by the square root of the sample size. As sample size increases, the approximation to normality improves and the standard error shrinks. The CLT, whose development is associated with mathematicians including Pierre-Simon Laplace and Abraham de Moivre, is what justifies using normal-distribution-based procedures (z tests, confidence intervals, and much of hypothesis testing) even when the underlying population is not normal, provided the sample is large enough. The distractors either invent non-standard theorem names or refer to the law of large numbers, which concerns the convergence of the sample mean to the population mean rather than the normal shape of the sampling distribution. This item tests recognition of the CLT." },

  { id: "rml21_065", section: "stats",
    q: "You read a medical research study about the connection between depression and lack of physical exercise. The study looked at severity of depression in a sample of over 30,000 adults, and it found that those who exercised regularly were significantly less likely to have depression. The study concluded that regularly exercise will make a person less likely to get depression. Why is this conclusion not appropriate?",
    options: { a: "The study was an experiment, but it didn't mention other habits and their relationship with depression.", b: "The study was an experiment, but it didn't group adults into blocks based on family medical history.", c: "The study was observational, so the researcher shouldn't make a causal conclusion", d: "The study was observational, so the sample size isn't large enough to support his conclusion." },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "The study was not an experiment; it merely measured existing exercise habits, so calling it an experiment is incorrect.",
      b: "Again this wrongly calls the study an experiment; blocking is an experimental technique not applicable to this observational design.",
      d: "The sample of 30,000 is very large, so sample size is not the problem; the issue is the observational design preventing causal claims."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is that the study was observational, so the researcher should not make a causal conclusion. This item again targets the correlation-versus-causation distinction. The study simply measured whether adults exercised and whether they were depressed and found an association between them; it did not randomly assign people to exercise or not, so it is an observational (correlational) study, not an experiment. From an observational association alone, one cannot validly conclude that exercise causes reduced depression, for three reasons. First, confounding variables such as physical health, income, social support, or diet could independently influence both exercise and depression. Second, the direction of causation is ambiguous: it is equally plausible that depression reduces the motivation and energy to exercise (reverse causation), so that low mood causes low exercise rather than the other way around. Third, only a true experiment with random assignment can control confounds and isolate a causal effect, following the logic of Ronald Aylmer Fisher. The very large sample size (over 30,000) actually strengthens the precision of the estimate, so option D is wrong to blame sample size. Options A and B incorrectly label the study an experiment. This item tests the ability to recognise why causal conclusions are unwarranted from observational correlational data." },

  { id: "rml21_066", section: "social",
    q: "A group of scientists is tasked with making a decision about the use of live animals in their university research. There is great deal of community pressure to create a definitive policy and the leading scientist is concerned about the group's ability to make the correct decision since the majority of the group has used live animals in their research. How might the leader reduce groupthink among the scientists as they make their decision?",
    options: { a: "The leader could make a cohesive team building exercise a mandatory part of the meeting", b: "The leader could relate his opinion at the beginning the discussion to guide conversation", c: "The leader could ask a subset of individuals in the group to critique animal research", d: "The leader could ask the group members not to argue with one another's opinions" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "Increasing group cohesion through team building would raise, not reduce, groupthink, since high cohesiveness is a key precondition for it.",
      b: "A leader stating his opinion first biases the discussion toward that view, promoting conformity and worsening groupthink.",
      d: "Discouraging argument suppresses dissent and critical evaluation, which increases groupthink rather than reducing it."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is that the leader could ask a subset of individuals to critique animal research. Groupthink is a phenomenon identified by Irving Janis, in which the desire for harmony, unanimity, and conformity within a cohesive group overrides realistic appraisal of alternatives, leading to poor, irrational decisions. Symptoms include an illusion of invulnerability, collective rationalisation, suppression of dissent, self-censorship, an illusion of unanimity, and the emergence of self-appointed mindguards. Janis analysed fiascos such as the Bay of Pigs invasion. Crucially, Janis also proposed remedies to prevent groupthink, and the best ones involve deliberately encouraging dissent and critical evaluation. Assigning one or more members the role of devil's advocate or critical evaluator, exactly what option C describes by asking a subset to critique the group's leaning toward animal research, forces the group to examine opposing views and counters premature consensus. Other recommended safeguards include the leader remaining impartial and withholding their own opinion at first, inviting outside experts, and breaking into subgroups. Options A, B, and D each do the opposite of Janis's advice: building cohesion, having the leader state a view first, and banning argument all suppress dissent and intensify groupthink. This item tests knowledge of Janis's groupthink concept and its prevention strategies." },

  { id: "rml21_067", section: "social",
    q: "Tim would like to participate in his high school's musical. He has been taking vocal lessons for years and is an excellent singer, but does not have much experience as a dancer. The director of the play decides to give him a part where he has to sing and dance. Based on the theory of social facilitation, how will the presence of an audience affect Tim's performance in the play?",
    options: { a: "His dancing will improve, but his singing will be impaired.", b: "Both his singing and dancing will improve.", c: "His singing will improve, but his dancing will be impaired.", d: "Both his singing and dancing will be impaired." },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "This reverses the effect: an audience impairs the poorly learned task (dancing) and enhances the well-learned one (singing), not the other way around.",
      b: "An audience only enhances well-practised, dominant responses; Tim's weak dancing is not well learned, so it would be impaired, not improved.",
      d: "Singing is Tim's well-learned skill, so the audience should enhance it rather than impair it, making a blanket impairment incorrect."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is that his singing will improve, but his dancing will be impaired. Social facilitation refers to the effect of the presence of others on task performance, first noted by Norman Triplett and later refined by Robert Zajonc. Zajonc resolved seemingly contradictory findings with a key principle: the presence of others increases physiological arousal, and heightened arousal strengthens the dominant response, the response most likely to occur for a given task. For tasks that are simple, well-learned, or well-practised, the dominant response is the correct one, so an audience enhances performance. For tasks that are complex, novel, or poorly learned, the dominant response is often incorrect, so an audience impairs performance. Applying this to Tim: singing is a skill he has practised for years, so it is well-learned and the dominant response is correct, meaning the audience's arousal will enhance his singing. Dancing, however, is a skill in which he has little experience, so it is poorly learned and the dominant response is likely to be an error, meaning the audience will impair his dancing. This is why performing arts differ for experts (helped by crowds) versus novices (hurt by them). The distractors reverse or overgeneralise the effect. This item tests the dominant-response logic of social facilitation." },

  { id: "rml21_068", section: "social",
    q: "Karina and Rakhi are involved in an altercation that is captured on tape. In the video, Karina is seen walking with her young son when Rakhi attacks her. Karina falls to the ground and Rakhi assaults her, ultimately punching and kicking her dozens of times. A group of individuals gathers around the two women, takes out their cell phones, and records the fight. They do not help or call for help. The behaviour of the group is an example of which of the following terms?",
    options: { a: "Group polarisation", b: "Social facilitation", c: "Bystander effect", d: "Social loafing" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "Group polarisation is the tendency for group discussion to strengthen members' initial attitudes toward a more extreme position, not the failure to help in an emergency.",
      b: "Social facilitation concerns how an audience affects task performance, not whether onlookers intervene to help a victim.",
      d: "Social loafing is reduced individual effort on a shared group task, not the failure of bystanders to assist someone in distress."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is the bystander effect. The bystander effect is the well-documented social phenomenon in which the presence of other people reduces the likelihood that any given individual will help a person in an emergency; the more bystanders present, the less likely any one of them is to intervene. It was investigated by John Darley and Bibb Latane, prompted by the 1964 case of Kitty Genovese, whose murder was reportedly witnessed by many neighbours who did not act. Their research identified two key mechanisms. The first is diffusion of responsibility: when many people are present, each individual feels less personal responsibility to act, assuming someone else will help. The second is pluralistic ignorance: people look to others to define whether the situation is truly an emergency, and if no one else appears alarmed or is helping, each concludes it must not require intervention, so collectively no one acts. In the scenario, a crowd gathers and merely films the assault without helping or calling for help, a textbook illustration of the bystander effect driven by diffusion of responsibility. The distractors describe other group phenomena (polarisation, facilitation, and loafing) that do not concern failure to aid a victim. This item tests recognition of the bystander effect and its mechanisms." },

  { id: "rml21_069", section: "social",
    q: "Cyber bullies are individuals who use electronic devices to harass and act aggressively toward other individuals online. Because the internet is an anonymous forum these individuals may say and do things that they would never do in person. They are cruel, hurtful and their behaviour is often reprehensible. What group dynamic does the behaviour of a cyber bully represent?",
    options: { a: "Social facilitation", b: "Group polarisation", c: "Bystander effect", d: "Deindividuation" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: {
      a: "Social facilitation is about arousal from an audience affecting task performance, not about anonymity loosening moral restraint online.",
      b: "Group polarisation is the shift toward more extreme group attitudes after discussion, not the disinhibited aggression of an anonymous individual.",
      c: "The bystander effect concerns failure to help in emergencies due to the presence of others, not aggressive behaviour enabled by anonymity."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is deindividuation. Deindividuation is a social psychological state in which individuals lose their sense of individual identity and self-awareness within a group or under conditions of anonymity, leading to a reduction in personal responsibility and self-restraint, which in turn can produce impulsive, antisocial, or aggressive behaviour that the person would normally inhibit. The concept was elaborated by Leon Festinger (who introduced the term with colleagues), Philip Zimbardo, and Edward Diener. Anonymity, diffused responsibility, and high arousal are key contributors: when people feel unidentifiable and less accountable, internal moral controls weaken. Zimbardo's Stanford Prison Experiment and studies showing that anonymity (for example, wearing hoods) increased aggression illustrate the effect. The internet is a powerful engine of deindividuation because it grants anonymity and physical distance, so cyberbullies feel unidentifiable and unaccountable and behave in cruel ways they would never enact face to face, exactly the scenario described. This is sometimes called the online disinhibition effect. The distractors name other group phenomena that do not hinge on loss of identity through anonymity: social facilitation, group polarisation, and the bystander effect. This item tests recognition of deindividuation as the mechanism behind anonymous online aggression." },

  { id: "rml21_070", section: "social",
    q: "Miss Pooja is a school teacher who gives her students group projects as part of their Maths curriculum. What might she do to minimise the effects of social loafing?",
    options: { a: "Grade the group as a whole on the project instead of giving each group member a grade", b: "Grade both the groups as a whole and each individual group member on the project", c: "Assign the children to groups randomly instead of letting them choose their own", d: "Give students the option to present their project in front of the class for extra credit" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "Grading only the group as a whole removes individual accountability, which actually increases social loafing rather than reducing it.",
      c: "Random group assignment addresses group composition but does nothing to make individual contributions identifiable, so it does not target loafing.",
      d: "An optional extra-credit presentation is voluntary and does not make each member accountable for their share of the core project, so it fails to reduce loafing."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is to grade both the group as a whole and each individual group member. Social loafing is the tendency for individuals to exert less effort when working collectively on a task than when working alone, because their individual contribution is pooled and cannot be easily identified or evaluated. It was demonstrated in early work by Max Ringelmann (the Ringelmann effect, where individual pulling effort on a rope decreased as group size increased) and studied further by Bibb Latane, Kipling Williams, and Stephen Harkins. A central cause is the loss of individual identifiability and accountability: when only the group outcome is evaluated, individuals feel their slacking will go unnoticed and their extra effort unrewarded (dispensability of effort). The most effective remedy, therefore, is to restore individual accountability by making each person's contribution identifiable and evaluable. Grading both the group product and each member's individual contribution ensures that students cannot hide in the collective and are held responsible for their own share, which directly reduces loafing. Option A does the opposite by removing individual evaluation entirely. Random assignment and optional presentations do not address identifiability. This item tests understanding of social loafing and the accountability-based strategy for minimising it." },

  { id: "rml21_071", section: "assessment",
    q: "Which of the following refers to Inter-rater reliability?",
    options: { a: "The degree to which two tests measure the same construct.", b: "The degree to which a clinician can predict future behaviour.", c: "The degree to which two clinicians will agree on interpretation or scoring of a test.", d: "The degree to which the items in the test relate to each other" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "The degree to which two tests measure the same construct concerns convergent or construct validity, not reliability between raters.",
      b: "Predicting future behaviour is predictive validity, a form of criterion validity, not inter-rater reliability.",
      d: "The degree to which items relate to each other is internal consistency reliability, measured by Cronbach's alpha, not agreement between raters."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is the degree to which two clinicians will agree on the interpretation or scoring of a test. Reliability refers to the consistency or repeatability of measurement, and it comes in several forms. Inter-rater reliability (also called inter-observer or inter-scorer reliability) specifically concerns the degree of agreement between two or more independent raters, observers, or clinicians when they score, rate, or interpret the same responses, behaviour, or test. High inter-rater reliability means different clinicians reach the same judgments, indicating that the scoring is objective and not idiosyncratic to a particular rater. It is especially important for subjectively scored instruments such as projective tests, behavioural observations, and structured clinical interviews, where scorer judgment plays a large role. It is commonly quantified using statistics such as Cohen's kappa, developed by Jacob Cohen, which corrects for chance agreement, or the intraclass correlation coefficient. The distractors describe other psychometric properties: option A refers to construct or convergent validity, option B to predictive (criterion) validity, and option D to internal consistency reliability, which is measured by Cronbach's alpha and concerns how well the items hang together. This item tests the ability to distinguish inter-rater reliability from other reliability and validity concepts." },

  { id: "rml21_072", section: "assessment",
    q: "Standardisation allows the clinician to do which of the following?",
    options: { a: "See where an individual client's score on the test falls in relation to the normal distribution of scores for that test.", b: "Use the score on a particular test to estimate whether a client might meet the diagnostic criteria for a psychological disorder.", c: "Estimate the probability with which a client might meet DSM-IV or ICD.", d: "All of the above" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: {
      a: "Comparing a score to the normal distribution of scores is one function standardisation enables, but selecting it alone ignores the other valid functions.",
      b: "Using a score to estimate whether a client meets diagnostic criteria is enabled by standardisation, but it is only one of the listed functions.",
      c: "Estimating the probability of meeting DSM or ICD criteria is another function that norm-referenced standardisation supports, but again only one of several."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is all of the above. Standardisation in psychological testing has two related meanings, both relevant here. First, it means administering and scoring a test under uniform, fixed conditions (the same instructions, materials, time limits, and scoring rules) so that scores are comparable across people and not influenced by procedural differences. Second, it means establishing norms by administering the test to a large, representative standardisation sample so that any individual's raw score can be interpreted relative to the distribution of scores in that reference population. This norm-referencing is what allows a clinician to see where a client's score falls within the normal distribution of scores for that test (option A), for example by converting it to a percentile, z score, or standard score. Because the norms map scores onto a known distribution, standardised tests can also be used to help estimate whether a client's score is consistent with the threshold for a psychological disorder (option B) and to estimate the probability that the client meets diagnostic criteria such as those in the DSM-IV or ICD (option C). The foundational logic of norms and standardisation traces to early testing pioneers including Alfred Binet and later David Wechsler. Since all three functions genuinely follow from standardisation, the inclusive answer is correct. This item tests the purposes of test standardisation." },

  { id: "rml21_073", section: "assessment",
    q: "Face validity refers to which of the following:",
    options: { a: "Facial expression is used to make a diagnosis", b: "The scale of emotional responding.", c: "The notion that an assessment method may appear to be valid simply because it has questions which intuitively seem relevant to the trait or characteristic being measured.", d: "A construct is a hypothetical or inferred attribute that may not be directly observable or directly measurable" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "Using facial expression to diagnose is a play on the word 'face' and has nothing to do with the psychometric concept of face validity.",
      b: "The scale of emotional responding is unrelated to face validity, which concerns how valid a test appears on its surface.",
      d: "This describes the definition of a construct, relevant to construct validity, not the surface-appearance meaning of face validity."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is the notion that an assessment method may appear to be valid simply because it has questions which intuitively seem relevant to the trait being measured. Validity refers to whether a test actually measures what it claims to measure, and it comes in several types. Face validity is the most superficial: it concerns whether a test looks or appears, on its surface and to a layperson or test-taker, as though it measures the intended construct. A test has high face validity if its items obviously relate to the trait (for example, a depression questionnaire that asks about sadness and hopelessness). Importantly, face validity is not a statistical or scientific form of validity; a test can appear valid on its face yet fail to measure the construct accurately, and conversely a test can lack face validity yet be genuinely valid. Face validity matters mainly for practical acceptability and motivation, since tests that look relevant are more readily accepted by respondents, but sometimes low face validity is deliberately used (as in some personality inventories) to reduce faking. This contrasts with content validity, criterion validity, and construct validity, which are established through evidence. Option D actually defines a construct, which pertains to construct validity. This item tests the definition of face validity as mere surface appearance of relevance." },

  { id: "rml21_074", section: "assessment",
    q: "The MMPI measures which of the following?",
    options: { a: "Mood", b: "Physical concerns", c: "Social attitudes", d: "All of the above" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: {
      a: "Mood-related pathology (for example the Depression scale) is assessed by the MMPI, but selecting it alone ignores the many other domains it covers.",
      b: "Physical or bodily concerns (the Hypochondriasis scale) are measured, but this is only one of the several clinical scales.",
      c: "Social attitudes and introversion (the Social Introversion scale) are assessed, but again this is only one facet of the broad inventory."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is all of the above. The Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory (MMPI) is one of the most widely used and researched objective personality and psychopathology assessment instruments. It was developed in the late 1930s and published in 1943 by Starke Hathaway, a psychologist, and J. Charnley McKinley, a psychiatrist, at the University of Minnesota. It is a broad-band, empirically keyed self-report inventory consisting of hundreds of true/false items. The original MMPI contains ten clinical scales that assess a wide range of psychological domains, and these scales span exactly the areas listed: mood and emotional disturbance (for example the Depression and Hypomania scales), physical or somatic concerns (the Hypochondriasis and Hysteria scales, which tap bodily preoccupation), and social attitudes and interpersonal orientation (the Social Introversion scale, plus scales tapping social deviance). It also includes validity scales (such as the L, F, and K scales) to detect defensiveness, exaggeration, or careless responding. Because the MMPI casts a wide net across mood, somatic, and social-attitudinal content, all three options are genuinely assessed, making the inclusive answer correct. The instrument was later revised as the MMPI-2 and MMPI-2-RF. This item tests broad awareness of the MMPI's comprehensive, multi-domain coverage of personality and psychopathology." },

  { id: "rml21_075", section: "assessment",
    q: "Construct validity is a notion that refers to:",
    options: { a: "The notion that scores on a test correlate highly with scores from tests that measure the same attribute.", b: "The level of which the theoretical construct is related to other similar measures", c: "The degree to which the items in the test consistently relate to each other", d: "The test will produce consistent results" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "High correlation with tests of the same attribute specifically describes convergent validity (a component of construct validity), which is narrower than construct validity as a whole.",
      c: "Items consistently relating to each other describes internal consistency reliability, not validity.",
      d: "Producing consistent results describes reliability in general, not construct validity."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is the level to which the theoretical construct is related to other similar measures (that is, how well the test represents and behaves like the underlying theoretical construct). Construct validity is the most fundamental and overarching form of validity, formalised in the influential work of Lee Cronbach and Paul Meehl. It concerns the degree to which a test truly measures the abstract theoretical construct (such as intelligence, anxiety, or self-esteem) that it is intended to measure, given that such constructs are hypothetical and cannot be observed directly. Construct validity is established by accumulating evidence that the test relates to other variables in ways predicted by theory. It has two key components: convergent validity, where the test correlates strongly with other measures of the same or related constructs (option A actually describes convergent validity specifically), and discriminant (divergent) validity, where the test does not correlate with measures of unrelated constructs. Donald Campbell and Donald Fiske developed the multitrait-multimethod matrix to assess both together. Because option B captures the broad idea of how the construct relates to other similar measures, it is the best fit for the overall notion of construct validity, whereas option A is a subtype, and options C and D describe reliability (internal consistency and consistency of results). This item tests the definition of construct validity." },

  { id: "rml21_076", section: "assessment",
    q: "Self-monitoring is a form of clinical observation involving which of the following?",
    options: { a: "Clients measuring their own galvanic skin responses.", b: "Clients measuring their own heart rate", c: "Clients observing and recording their own behaviour", d: "Clients recording their dreams" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "Measuring one's own galvanic skin response is a physiological biofeedback measurement, not the behavioural self-observation that defines self-monitoring.",
      b: "Measuring one's own heart rate is again a physiological measurement, not the systematic recording of behaviour, thoughts, or feelings that self-monitoring entails.",
      d: "Recording dreams is a psychodynamic technique for accessing unconscious material, not the behavioural self-observation method of self-monitoring."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is clients observing and recording their own behaviour. Self-monitoring is an assessment and intervention technique, central to behavioural and cognitive-behavioural approaches, in which clients systematically observe, track, and record their own behaviours, thoughts, feelings, and the surrounding circumstances (antecedents and consequences) as they occur in everyday life. For example, a client might keep a diary of every cigarette smoked, each panic attack and its triggers, food intake, or automatic negative thoughts. Self-monitoring serves two purposes. As assessment, it yields ecologically valid, real-time data about the frequency, intensity, and context of the target behaviour that would be hard to capture in the clinic and less subject to retrospective memory bias. As intervention, the very act of monitoring often produces reactivity, meaning the behaviour changes simply because it is being observed (undesirable behaviours tend to decrease and desirable ones increase), so it is therapeutic in itself. It fits within the functional analysis tradition of behaviour therapy associated with B.F. Skinner and is a staple of Aaron Beck's cognitive therapy through thought records. The distractors describe physiological self-measurement (galvanic skin response, heart rate), which belongs to biofeedback, or dream recording, which belongs to psychodynamic work, none of which is behavioural self-observation. This item tests the definition of self-monitoring." },

  { id: "rml21_077", section: "assessment",
    q: "The psychometric approach measures which of the following?",
    options: { a: "Psychopathology symptoms", b: "Intelligence", c: "Neurological or cognitive deficits", d: "All of the above" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: {
      a: "Psychopathology symptoms are indeed measured psychometrically (for example by the MMPI), but selecting this alone ignores the other domains psychometrics covers.",
      b: "Intelligence is a classic psychometric target (IQ tests), but it is only one of several domains the approach addresses.",
      c: "Neuropsychological or cognitive deficits are assessed by standardised psychometric tests, but again this is only one facet of the broad approach."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is all of the above. The psychometric approach is the branch of psychology concerned with the theory and technique of psychological measurement, that is, the construction, standardisation, and statistical evaluation of tests designed to quantify psychological attributes. Its defining feature is the use of standardised, norm-referenced instruments with established reliability and validity to assign numerical scores to characteristics of individuals. Foundational figures include Francis Galton, who pioneered the measurement of individual differences, Charles Spearman, who developed factor analysis and the concept of general intelligence (g), and Alfred Binet, who created the first practical intelligence test. The psychometric approach is applied across many domains: it measures intelligence and cognitive ability through IQ tests such as the Wechsler and Stanford-Binet scales; it measures personality and psychopathology symptoms through inventories such as the MMPI; and it measures neurological or cognitive deficits through standardised neuropsychological test batteries (for example, tests of memory, attention, and executive function used to detect brain dysfunction). Because psychometric methods are used to assess intelligence, psychopathology, and neurocognitive deficits alike, all three options are correct, making the inclusive answer right. This item tests the breadth of the psychometric measurement tradition across ability, personality, and neuropsychological domains." },

  { id: "rml21_078", section: "assessment",
    q: "An IQ test does NOT provide which of the following?",
    options: { a: "High test retest reliability", b: "Good predictor of behaviour", c: "High internal consistency", d: "Good validity" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "Well-constructed IQ tests do show high test-retest reliability (stable scores over time), so this is something they provide, not what they fail to provide.",
      c: "IQ tests have high internal consistency (their items cohere in measuring the same underlying ability), so this is a property they possess.",
      d: "IQ tests demonstrate good validity for the cognitive abilities they measure, so this is a strength, not a shortcoming."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is that an IQ test does NOT provide a good predictor of behaviour. Standardised intelligence tests such as the Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale, developed by David Wechsler, and the Stanford-Binet scale, developed from the work of Alfred Binet and revised by Lewis Terman, are among the most psychometrically sound instruments in psychology. They have excellent reliability, including high test-retest reliability (IQ scores are quite stable over time) and high internal consistency (their subtests and items cohere well in measuring the same underlying general ability). They also have good validity for what they are designed to assess, and notably strong criterion validity for predicting academic achievement and, to a meaningful degree, occupational and educational outcomes. However, the key limitation, and the intended answer here, is that an IQ score is a relatively poor predictor of specific individual behaviour in everyday life. Intelligence tests measure cognitive abilities and problem-solving skills, but real-world behaviour is shaped by many additional factors such as motivation, personality, emotional intelligence (a concept popularised by Daniel Goleman), social context, and situational variables, so IQ alone does not reliably predict how a person will behave. Thus while an IQ test delivers strong reliability and validity, it does not serve as a good general predictor of behaviour. This item tests awareness of the strengths and limits of intelligence testing." },

  { id: "rml21_079", section: "assessment",
    q: "Which of the following refers to concurrent validity?",
    options: { a: "That two tests are done at the same time", b: "Two or more clinicians agree on the outcome", c: "The items on the test consistently relate to each other.", d: "The notion that scores on a test correlate highly with scores from tests that measure the same attribute" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: {
      a: "Merely administering two tests at the same time is a superficial reading of the word 'concurrent'; concurrent validity requires that the test correlate with an established criterion measured at the same time, not just simultaneous testing.",
      b: "Agreement among clinicians is inter-rater reliability, not concurrent validity.",
      c: "Items consistently relating to each other is internal consistency reliability, not validity."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is the notion that scores on a test correlate highly with scores from tests that measure the same attribute (assessed at the same time). Concurrent validity is a subtype of criterion-related validity, the family of validity concerned with how well a test's scores correspond to an external criterion. Criterion validity has two forms distinguished by timing. Predictive validity assesses how well a test predicts a criterion measured in the future (for example, whether an aptitude test predicts later job performance). Concurrent validity assesses how well a test correlates with an established criterion or an existing validated measure of the same construct assessed at roughly the same point in time. For instance, a new, shorter depression scale shows concurrent validity if its scores correlate highly with scores on an already-established depression measure administered concurrently. The essence is agreement with a criterion in the present. Option A is a trap because it takes 'concurrent' too literally as merely testing simultaneously, without the crucial requirement of correlation with a valid criterion measuring the same attribute. Option B describes inter-rater reliability, and option C describes internal consistency reliability, both of which are reliability rather than validity. This item tests the correct definition of concurrent validity as present-time correlation with a criterion measure of the same construct." },

  { id: "rml21_080", section: "assessment",
    q: "Case formulation is:",
    options: { a: "Using systematic information to draw up a psychological explanation of the client's problems and to develop a plan for therapy", b: "Using the psychiatric diagnostic model of psychopathology", c: "Using previous case studies for diagnosis", d: "Analysing client's behaviour on a case by case basis." },
    correct: "a",
    traps: {
      b: "Applying the psychiatric diagnostic model (assigning a DSM or ICD label) is categorical diagnosis, which case formulation deliberately goes beyond by explaining the individual's problems.",
      c: "Using previous case studies for diagnosis is a comparative approach, not the individualised explanatory and treatment-planning process of formulation.",
      d: "Analysing behaviour case by case is vague and does not capture the systematic, theory-driven, treatment-oriented nature of a formulation."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is using systematic information to draw up a psychological explanation of the client's problems and to develop a plan for therapy. Case formulation (also called clinical formulation) is a core skill in clinical psychology that goes beyond assigning a diagnostic label. It is a hypothesis-driven, individualised account that integrates all the systematic information gathered about a client, from interviews, history, assessment, and observation, into a coherent psychological explanation of how their difficulties developed and, crucially, what is currently maintaining them. A formulation typically organises information around predisposing, precipitating, perpetuating, and protective factors (the four Ps) and is grounded in a psychological theory or model, for example a cognitive-behavioural formulation linking activating events, beliefs, and consequences in the tradition of Aaron Beck, or a psychodynamic or systemic formulation. Its purpose is explicitly practical: it explains the client's unique problems and directly guides the selection and planning of appropriate therapy, providing a working map that can be revised as therapy proceeds. This contrasts with categorical psychiatric diagnosis (option B), which classifies but does not explain. Options C and D describe narrower or vaguer activities that miss the systematic, explanatory, and treatment-planning essence of formulation. This item tests the definition of case formulation as an integrative, individualised, treatment-oriented explanation." },

  { id: "rml21_081", section: "assessment",
    q: "What is Rorschach's projective test designed to measure?",
    options: { a: "Unconscious intentions.", b: "Dreams", c: "Conscious desires.", d: "Brain size" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: {
      b: "Dreams are the focus of psychodynamic dream analysis, not the inkblot test, which uses ambiguous visual stimuli rather than dream reports.",
      c: "Conscious desires contradicts the whole premise of a projective test, which is designed to tap unconscious rather than consciously reported material.",
      d: "Brain size is a physical, neuroanatomical measure with no connection to a projective personality test."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is unconscious intentions. The Rorschach Inkblot Test was created by the Swiss psychiatrist Hermann Rorschach in 1921. It is the archetypal projective test, a category of personality assessment based on the projective hypothesis, which holds that when a person is presented with an ambiguous, unstructured stimulus, they will project their own inner needs, conflicts, feelings, and unconscious processes onto it in the way they interpret it. The Rorschach consists of ten symmetrical inkblot cards; the examinee is asked what each blot might be, and responses are scored on dimensions such as location (which part of the blot was used), determinants (form, colour, movement, shading), and content, using systems such as John Exner's Comprehensive System. The underlying assumption, rooted in Sigmund Freud's psychoanalytic theory of the unconscious, is that because there is no correct answer, the person's interpretations reveal hidden aspects of personality, unconscious motives, and emotional functioning that they could not or would not report directly on a self-report questionnaire. The distractors are wrong because dreams belong to psychodynamic dream analysis, conscious desires contradict the projective aim of accessing unconscious material, and brain size is a physical measure. Although the Rorschach's reliability and validity are debated, it remains standard exam content. This item tests the projective purpose of the Rorschach in accessing unconscious material." },

  { id: "rml21_082", section: "assessment",
    q: "Which is not a projective test",
    options: { a: "Word association test", b: "Rorschach's ink lot test", c: "Thematic apperception test", d: "Sentence completion test" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: {
      b: "The Rorschach inkblot test is the classic projective test using ambiguous inkblots, so it is clearly projective and not the exception.",
      c: "The Thematic Apperception Test asks people to tell stories about ambiguous pictures, a hallmark projective technique, so it is not the odd one out.",
      d: "The sentence completion test presents incomplete sentences for the person to finish, projecting inner attitudes, so it is a projective technique."
    },
    explanation: "The intended answer is the word association test, though this is a somewhat debatable item and deserves care. Projective tests present ambiguous stimuli onto which a person projects unconscious thoughts and feelings. The Rorschach inkblot test (Hermann Rorschach), the Thematic Apperception Test or TAT (developed by Henry Murray and Christiana Morgan, in which the person tells stories about ambiguous pictures), and the sentence completion test (such as the Rotter Incomplete Sentences Blank by Julian Rotter, in which the person finishes stems like 'I feel...') are all classic projective techniques. The word association test, in which a person responds to a stimulus word with the first word that comes to mind, was developed by Francis Galton and used clinically by Carl Gustav Jung to detect emotionally charged complexes, and it is often itself classified as a projective technique. However, in many entrance-exam answer keys the word association test is treated as the odd one out, being regarded as more of a controlled association or diagnostic-of-complexes method rather than a full storytelling or interpretive projective task, so it is the intended answer here. (No official key is printed in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise, flagging that this item is contested and word association is arguably projective too.) This item tests classification of projective techniques, and the examiners intend word association as the exception." },

  { id: "rml21_083", section: "assessment",
    q: "What is test-retest reliability",
    options: { a: "The test is measuring what it claims to be measuring", b: "The test will produce consistent results", c: "The client will improve performance second time round", d: "All of the above" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "Measuring what it claims to measure is the definition of validity, not reliability, so this describes a different psychometric property.",
      c: "Improved performance on a second administration reflects a practice effect, which is actually a threat to test-retest reliability, not its definition.",
      d: "Since only option B correctly defines test-retest reliability and the others are wrong, the inclusive option cannot be correct."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is that the test will produce consistent results. Reliability refers to the consistency, stability, or repeatability of measurement, and test-retest reliability is one specific form of it. Test-retest reliability is assessed by administering the same test to the same group of people on two separate occasions and then correlating the two sets of scores. If the test is reliable in this sense, individuals should obtain similar scores across the two administrations, yielding a high correlation (the test-retest reliability coefficient, or coefficient of stability). In other words, a reliable test produces consistent, reproducible results over time. This is especially important for measures of stable traits such as intelligence or personality, which should not fluctuate substantially over short intervals. A key complication is the practice or carryover effect: if respondents remember items or improve simply through repeated exposure, the second scores may rise, artificially inflating or distorting the reliability estimate, so an appropriate time interval must be chosen. Note that option A actually defines validity (measuring what it claims to measure), and option C describes a practice effect, which is a threat to, not a definition of, test-retest reliability. The broader concept of reliability is central to classical test theory. This item tests the definition of test-retest reliability as consistency of scores across time." },

  { id: "rml21_084", section: "cognitive",
    q: "Cattell considered crystallised intelligence to be a (an)",
    options: { a: "Development Process", b: "Ability to deal with new problems", c: "Repertoire of information, cognitive skills and strategies", d: "Function of brain damage" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "Calling crystallised intelligence a developmental process is vague and does not capture its nature as accumulated knowledge and skills.",
      b: "The ability to deal with new problems describes fluid intelligence, the counterpart to crystallised intelligence, not crystallised intelligence itself.",
      d: "A function of brain damage is unrelated; crystallised intelligence is acquired knowledge, and it is actually relatively resistant to decline, not a product of brain damage."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is a repertoire of information, cognitive skills, and strategies. Raymond Cattell proposed the influential theory distinguishing two broad types of general intelligence, later expanded with his student John Horn into the Cattell-Horn Gf-Gc theory. Fluid intelligence (Gf) is the capacity to reason, solve novel problems, and perceive relationships independent of prior knowledge, that is, the ability to deal with new problems (option B describes fluid intelligence). It is thought to depend heavily on biological and neurological efficiency, to peak in early adulthood, and to decline with age and be vulnerable to brain injury. Crystallised intelligence (Gc), by contrast, is the accumulated store of knowledge, information, vocabulary, verbal skills, and learned cognitive strategies that a person acquires through education, culture, and experience over the lifespan. It is precisely a repertoire of information, cognitive skills, and strategies, which matches option C. Crystallised intelligence tends to increase or remain stable across most of adulthood and is relatively resistant to aging and mild brain damage, because it reflects consolidated learning. This distinction later fed into the Cattell-Horn-Carroll (CHC) theory, the dominant modern model of cognitive abilities. The distractors mislabel it as a vague process, as fluid intelligence, or as a product of brain damage. This item tests the definition of crystallised intelligence as accumulated knowledge and skills." },

  { id: "rml21_085", section: "assessment",
    q: "Which of the following is assessed by Cronbach's alpha?",
    options: { a: "Concurrent validity", b: "Inter-rater reliability", c: "Test retest reliability", d: "Internal consistency" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: {
      a: "Concurrent validity is a criterion-related validity assessed by correlation with an external measure, not by Cronbach's alpha.",
      b: "Inter-rater reliability is agreement between raters, measured by statistics like Cohen's kappa, not by Cronbach's alpha.",
      c: "Test-retest reliability is assessed by correlating scores from two administrations over time, not by Cronbach's alpha."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is internal consistency. Cronbach's alpha, developed by Lee Cronbach in 1951, is the most widely used statistic for estimating the internal consistency reliability of a test or scale. Internal consistency reliability refers to the degree to which the individual items on a test are inter-correlated, that is, whether they all measure the same underlying construct and hang together as a coherent scale. Cronbach's alpha is essentially the average of all possible split-half correlations of the items, and it ranges from 0 to 1, with higher values indicating greater internal consistency. As a rule of thumb, an alpha of 0.70 or above is often considered acceptable, and values around 0.80 to 0.90 are good, though extremely high values may signal item redundancy. Alpha is influenced both by the average inter-item correlation and by the number of items (more items tend to raise alpha). It generalises the earlier Kuder-Richardson formulas (KR-20) used for dichotomous items. The distractors refer to other psychometric properties measured by different methods: concurrent validity is assessed by correlation with an external criterion, inter-rater reliability by agreement statistics such as Cohen's kappa, and test-retest reliability by correlating scores across two time points. This item tests the specific association of Cronbach's alpha with internal consistency reliability." },

  { id: "rml21_086", section: "development",
    q: "According to Kohlberg's Morality theory, in stage, morality of an action is based on the consequences: Actions that get rewarded are right and those that earn punishment are wrong.",
    options: { a: "Pre-conventional", b: "Conventional", c: "Post Conventional", d: "Formal Conventional" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: {
      b: "At the conventional level, morality is based on social approval, relationships, and maintaining law and order, not simply on reward and punishment.",
      c: "At the post-conventional level, morality rests on abstract ethical principles and universal rights, the most advanced reasoning, not on consequences of reward and punishment.",
      d: "'Formal Conventional' is not a real level in Kohlberg's theory; it is a fabricated distractor."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is the pre-conventional level. Lawrence Kohlberg's theory of moral development, which built on Jean Piaget's earlier work, describes three levels of moral reasoning, each with two stages. The first level, pre-conventional morality, is typical of young children and characterises moral reasoning that is based entirely on external consequences to the self rather than on internalised social values. Its Stage 1 is the obedience and punishment orientation, in which an action is judged wrong if it is punished and right if it avoids punishment, so the person obeys to avoid negative consequences. Its Stage 2 is the instrumental or self-interest orientation, in which right action is that which satisfies one's own needs and brings rewards, following a 'what is in it for me' logic of simple exchange. The described reasoning, that rewarded actions are right and punished actions are wrong, precisely captures this consequence-based, pre-conventional thinking. The conventional level (option B) instead bases morality on social approval, relationships, and maintaining social order, and the post-conventional level (option C) bases it on abstract principles, social contracts, and universal ethical values. 'Formal Conventional' (option D) is not a genuine Kohlberg level. This item tests recognition of pre-conventional, reward-and-punishment-based moral reasoning." },

  { id: "rml21_087", section: "therapy",
    q: "Which of the following might be considered as the central tenets of Client-Cantered Therapy:",
    options: { a: "Empathy", b: "Unconditional positive regard", c: "Congruence", d: "All of the above" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: {
      a: "Empathy is one of the three core therapist conditions, but selecting it alone omits the other two equally central conditions.",
      b: "Unconditional positive regard is a core condition, but it is only one of the three that together define the approach.",
      c: "Congruence (genuineness) is a core condition, but again it is only one of the trio Rogers specified."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is all of the above. Client-Centred Therapy (also called person-centred therapy) was developed by Carl Rogers, a founder of the humanistic approach to psychology. Rogers argued that people have an innate drive toward growth and self-actualisation, and that the role of the therapist is not to direct or interpret but to provide a supportive relational climate in which the client's natural tendency toward psychological growth can flourish. He specified three core conditions, which he considered necessary and sufficient for therapeutic change, and all three appear as the options here. The first is empathy (accurate empathic understanding), the therapist's ability to sense and reflect the client's feelings and frame of reference as if from within. The second is unconditional positive regard, the therapist's warm, non-judgmental acceptance and prizing of the client as a person, regardless of what they express. The third is congruence (also called genuineness or authenticity), the therapist being real, transparent, and integrated in the relationship rather than hiding behind a professional facade. When the client perceives these conditions, Rogers believed, incongruence between self-concept and experience is reduced and the client moves toward becoming a fully functioning person. Because empathy, unconditional positive regard, and congruence are all central tenets, the inclusive answer is correct. This item tests Rogers's three core conditions." },

  { id: "rml21_088", section: "therapy",
    q: "Which ONE of the following theorists developed the concept of self-efficacy that has been used as a cognitive-behavioural theoretical explanation for how people change?",
    options: { a: "Aaron Beck", b: "Albert Ellis", c: "Albert Bandura", d: "B.F. Skinner" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "Aaron Beck developed cognitive therapy and the concept of the cognitive triad and cognitive distortions, not self-efficacy.",
      b: "Albert Ellis developed Rational Emotive Behaviour Therapy and the ABC model of irrational beliefs, not self-efficacy.",
      d: "B.F. Skinner developed operant conditioning and radical behaviourism, which explicitly avoided internal cognitive constructs like self-efficacy."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Albert Bandura. Albert Bandura, a hugely influential psychologist known for social learning theory (later social cognitive theory), developed the concept of self-efficacy, which he introduced in a landmark 1977 paper. Self-efficacy refers to a person's belief in their own capability to organise and execute the courses of action required to produce given attainments, in short, the confidence that one can successfully perform a specific behaviour or handle a particular situation. Bandura argued that self-efficacy beliefs powerfully influence motivation, effort, persistence in the face of obstacles, emotional reactions, and ultimately whether behaviour change occurs, which is why it has become a central cognitive-behavioural explanation for how people change. He identified four main sources of self-efficacy: mastery experiences (past successes, the most powerful source), vicarious experiences (observing others succeed), verbal or social persuasion, and physiological and emotional states. Bandura is also famous for the Bobo doll experiments demonstrating observational learning and modelling. The distractors are all major figures in cognitive and behavioural traditions but developed other concepts: Aaron Beck created cognitive therapy and the cognitive triad, Albert Ellis created Rational Emotive Behaviour Therapy and the ABC model, and B.F. Skinner developed operant conditioning. This item tests correct attribution of self-efficacy to Bandura." },

  { id: "rml21_089", section: "therapy",
    q: "In psychodynamic therapy, analysis of resistance is where:",
    options: { a: "The client is asked to talk about anything that comes to mind", b: "The client transfers their feelings towards significant others onto the therapist", c: "The client obstructs the analysis and the therapist explores reasons for this", d: "The therapist interprets the dreams of the client" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "Talking about anything that comes to mind is free association, a different psychoanalytic technique, not analysis of resistance.",
      b: "Transferring feelings from significant others onto the therapist is transference, not resistance.",
      d: "Interpreting the client's dreams is dream analysis, another distinct psychoanalytic technique, not analysis of resistance."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is that the client obstructs the analysis and the therapist explores the reasons for this. Resistance is a key concept in the psychoanalytic and psychodynamic therapy developed by Sigmund Freud. It refers to any behaviour by the client that blocks, opposes, or interferes with the progress of therapy and the uncovering of unconscious material, for example arriving late, changing the subject, falling silent, forgetting appointments, going blank during free association, or refusing to discuss certain topics. Freud regarded resistance not as mere obstruction to be overcome but as meaningful and revealing: it signals that the therapy is approaching anxiety-provoking, threatening, or repressed material that the client's defences are protecting against. Therefore the analysis of resistance involves the therapist noticing these obstructions and helping the client explore and understand why they are resisting, thereby bringing defended-against unconscious conflicts into awareness. Along with free association, dream analysis, and the analysis and interpretation of transference, the analysis of resistance is one of the central techniques Freud used to make the unconscious conscious. The distractors correctly describe these other techniques: free association (option A), transference (option B), and dream analysis (option D), which is why they are wrong for resistance specifically. This item tests understanding of resistance and its analysis in psychodynamic therapy." },

  { id: "rml21_090", section: "systems",
    q: "Gregory Bateson describes a classic example of the interaction between a mother and her son who had fairly well recovered from an acute schizophrenic episode. Bateson described this interaction as follows: '[The son] was glad to see her [his mother] and impulsively put his arm around her shoulders, whereupon she stiffened. He withdrew his arm and she asked, Don't you love me anymore? He then blushed, and she said, Dear, you must not be so easily embarrassed and afraid of your feelings. The patient was able to stay with her only a few minutes more, and following her departure, he assaulted an aide and was put in the isolation room.' Bateson used this interaction to illustrate which ONE of the following concepts?",
    options: { a: "The cybernetic system", b: "The homeostatic system", c: "The incongruent family dyad", d: "The symptomatic double bind" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: {
      a: "The cybernetic system refers to feedback and self-regulation in systems theory, not to the contradictory-message trap described in the vignette.",
      b: "The homeostatic system describes a family's tendency to maintain stability and resist change, not the specific contradictory-communication pattern shown here.",
      c: "'The incongruent family dyad' is not the specific term Bateson coined; the phenomenon of contradictory messages that trap the recipient is the double bind."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is the symptomatic double bind. Gregory Bateson, together with colleagues Don Jackson, Jay Haley, and John Weakland, proposed the double bind theory in 1956 as an early communicational and systemic account of schizophrenia. A double bind is a situation in which a person repeatedly receives contradictory messages on different levels of communication, cannot escape or step outside the situation (cannot leave the field), and cannot comment on or resolve the contradiction, leaving them in an impossible, no-win position. In the classic example quoted, the mother sends contradictory messages: she stiffens and withdraws when her son shows affection (a nonverbal 'do not be close to me'), yet then verbally reproaches him with 'Don't you love me anymore?' and criticises him for being embarrassed. Whatever the son does, express affection or withdraw, he is in the wrong, and he cannot comment on the paradox or escape the relationship. Bateson argued that chronic exposure to such double binds could contribute to the disordered communication and thinking seen in schizophrenia. Though the theory is no longer accepted as a cause of schizophrenia, it was historically important in the development of family systems therapy. The distractors reference other systemic concepts (cybernetics, homeostasis) or a non-standard term. This item tests recognition of Bateson's double bind." },

  { id: "rml21_091", section: "therapy",
    q: "Behavioural shaping is where:",
    options: { a: "Children are taught the difference between right and wrong", b: "Pigeons are placed in skinner boxes and trained to press a button to obtain food.", c: "Simple behaviours are reinforced sequentially until more complex behaviour are learnt", d: "Any behaviour that has pleasant consequences is likely to be repeated" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "Teaching right from wrong is moral socialisation, not the operant technique of shaping.",
      b: "Pressing a button for food in a Skinner box is a simple example of operant conditioning, but shaping specifically involves reinforcing successive approximations toward a target behaviour, not a single trained response.",
      d: "That pleasant consequences make a behaviour more likely is the law of effect or the principle of positive reinforcement in general, not specifically shaping."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is that simple behaviours are reinforced sequentially until more complex behaviour is learnt. Shaping, also called the method of successive approximations, is an operant conditioning technique developed by Burrhus Frederic Skinner (B.F. Skinner) for teaching new or complex behaviours that an organism would not perform spontaneously and therefore could not otherwise be reinforced. Instead of waiting for the full target behaviour to appear, the trainer reinforces successive approximations, that is, behaviours that are progressively closer to the desired final behaviour. Early on, any behaviour resembling the first step is reinforced; then the criterion is gradually raised so that reinforcement is given only for closer and closer approximations, until finally only the complete target behaviour is reinforced. Skinner famously used shaping to train pigeons and rats to perform elaborate sequences, and the technique is widely applied in animal training, education, and behaviour therapy (for example, teaching speech or self-care skills to children with developmental disabilities). It builds complex behaviour step by step out of simpler reinforced components, which matches option C exactly. Option D states the general law of effect (Edward Thorndike) or positive reinforcement, and option B is a simple operant response rather than shaping specifically. This item tests the definition of shaping as reinforcement of successive approximations." },

  { id: "rml21_092", section: "therapy",
    q: "Behaviour analysis is based upon the principles of:",
    options: { a: "Cognitive learning theories", b: "Operant conditioning", c: "Dream analysis", d: "All of the above" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "Cognitive learning theories emphasise internal mental processes, which behaviour analysis (especially radical behaviourism) explicitly de-emphasises in favour of observable behaviour and its consequences.",
      c: "Dream analysis is a psychoanalytic technique concerned with unconscious content, wholly separate from the behavioural focus of behaviour analysis.",
      d: "Since only operant conditioning underlies behaviour analysis, the inclusive option that adds cognitive theories and dream analysis is incorrect."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is operant conditioning. Behaviour analysis, and its practical arm applied behaviour analysis (ABA), is the scientific study of behaviour grounded in the principles of operant conditioning as developed by Burrhus Frederic Skinner (B.F. Skinner) within the framework of radical behaviourism. Its central premise is that behaviour is a function of its consequences and environmental contingencies, and it focuses on observable, measurable behaviour rather than on inferred internal mental states. The core operant principles it applies include positive and negative reinforcement (which increase behaviour), positive and negative punishment (which decrease behaviour), extinction, stimulus control and discrimination, and shaping through successive approximations, all organised around the three-term contingency of antecedent, behaviour, and consequence (the A-B-C model). Behaviour analysts conduct functional analyses to identify the environmental variables maintaining a behaviour and then rearrange contingencies to change it. Applied behaviour analysis is widely used in treating autism spectrum disorder, developmental disabilities, and in education and organisational settings. Because behaviour analysis rests specifically on operant conditioning and deliberately sets aside cognitive constructs and psychoanalytic dream interpretation, options A and C do not apply, which rules out 'all of the above.' This item tests the operant-conditioning foundation of behaviour analysis." },

  { id: "rml21_093", section: "therapy",
    q: "Within the cognitive approach, the ABC model describes:",
    options: { a: "A= activating event; B= beliefs about oneself; C= consequences of the beliefs about oneself.", b: "A= activating events; B= beliefs about the events; C= consequences of the beliefs about the event", c: "A= activating event; B= beliefs about the events; C= critical analysis of the beliefs about the event.", d: "A= anticipatory event; B= beliefs about the event; D= consequences of the beliefs about the event." },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "This narrows the beliefs and consequences to be only 'about oneself', whereas in the ABC model the beliefs concern the activating event, not specifically oneself.",
      c: "This wrongly defines C as 'critical analysis' of beliefs; C stands for the emotional and behavioural consequences, while critical analysis or disputing is a separate step (D).",
      d: "This mislabels A as an 'anticipatory event' (it is the activating event) and confusingly uses D instead of C for consequences."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is A equals activating events, B equals beliefs about the events, and C equals consequences of the beliefs about the event. The ABC model is the cornerstone of Rational Emotive Behaviour Therapy (REBT), developed by Albert Ellis, and it is foundational to the broader cognitive approach to emotional disturbance. The model's central insight is that it is not events themselves that directly cause our emotional and behavioural reactions, but rather our beliefs and interpretations about those events. In the model, A stands for the Activating event or adversity (the situation or trigger), B stands for the Beliefs the person holds about that event (which may be rational or irrational), and C stands for the emotional and behavioural Consequences that follow, which are produced by B rather than directly by A. For example, failing an exam (A) does not itself cause despair; rather, the irrational belief 'I must always succeed and I am worthless if I fail' (B) produces the depressive consequence (C). Ellis later extended the model to D (Disputing the irrational beliefs) and E (the new Effective philosophy or emotional outcome), which is why option C wrongly assigns 'critical analysis' to C; disputing is a separate step. Options A and D distort the definitions of the components. This item tests correct knowledge of Ellis's ABC model." },

  { id: "rml21_094", section: "therapy",
    q: "A criticism of the REBT cognitive approach to understanding mental-health problems is that:",
    options: { a: "There is too much of a focus on sexuality", b: "It can be direct and confrontational and so might be seen as intimidatory by the client", c: "It has not been shown to be successful at helping individuals with depression", d: "It is not relevant in the 21st century" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "Excessive focus on sexuality is a criticism of Freudian psychoanalysis, not of REBT, which centres on irrational beliefs.",
      c: "REBT and cognitive-behavioural approaches have substantial evidence of effectiveness for depression, so claiming it has not been shown to help is false.",
      d: "REBT remains widely used and relevant, so the claim that it is irrelevant in the 21st century is inaccurate."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is that it can be direct and confrontational and so might be seen as intimidatory by the client. Rational Emotive Behaviour Therapy (REBT), developed by Albert Ellis, is an active, directive form of cognitive therapy in which the therapist works vigorously to identify and dispute the client's irrational beliefs (such as absolutistic 'musts' and 'shoulds', catastrophising, and self-downing) and to replace them with more rational, flexible beliefs. A recognised criticism of this style is precisely its forcefulness: because Ellis advocated an energetic, sometimes blunt and confrontational approach to challenging irrational thinking, some clients may experience the therapist's disputing as harsh, intimidating, or uncomfortable, and a strong therapeutic alliance can be harder to build, particularly for vulnerable or fragile clients. This contrasts with the gentle, non-directive warmth of Carl Rogers's client-centred therapy. The other options are inaccurate: an over-emphasis on sexuality is a criticism levelled at Sigmund Freud's psychoanalysis rather than REBT (option A); REBT and cognitive-behavioural therapy in general have a strong evidence base for treating depression and anxiety, so option C is false; and REBT remains widely practised and influential today, so option D is false. This item tests awareness of the confrontational-style criticism specific to REBT." },

  { id: "rml21_095", section: "systems",
    q: "System theory involves:",
    options: { a: "Uses computer based programs for therapy", b: "Attempts to understand the family as a social system", c: "Uses data analysis systems as a diagnostic tool", d: "Relies on self-report measures" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "Using computer-based programs for therapy is a technological delivery method, not the meaning of systems theory in the family-therapy context.",
      c: "Using data-analysis systems as a diagnostic tool is a statistical or informatics idea, unrelated to viewing the family as an interacting system.",
      d: "Reliance on self-report measures is an assessment method, not the defining focus of systems theory on relational patterns within the family."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is that it attempts to understand the family as a social system. Systems theory, as applied in family therapy, draws on general systems theory (originating with the biologist Ludwig von Bertalanffy) and cybernetics to view the family not as a collection of separate individuals but as an interconnected, dynamic whole in which members mutually influence one another. Key ideas include the principle that the whole is greater than the sum of its parts, so a problem in one member reflects and affects the entire family system; circular causality, in which behaviour is understood as reciprocal loops of influence rather than simple linear cause and effect; homeostasis, the family's tendency to maintain stability and resist change (sometimes maintaining a member's symptom); boundaries and subsystems; and feedback processes. From this perspective, an individual's symptom is often understood as serving a function within the family's patterns of interaction. Major figures who developed systemic and family approaches include Murray Bowen (transgenerational theory), Salvador Minuchin (structural family therapy), and Jay Haley and the Milan group (strategic therapy). The essence is understanding the family as a social system, which matches option B. The distractors describe unrelated technological, statistical, or assessment methods. This item tests the core meaning of systems theory in family therapy." },

  { id: "rml21_096", section: "therapy",
    q: "Eye movement desensitisation and reprocessing (EMDR) is sometimes used to treat PTSD. This technique involves:",
    options: { a: "The administration of an antidepressant.", b: "The client focusing on the memory, emotions and thoughts of the traumatic events while engaging in therapist-guided eye movements", c: "Training the client in relaxation techniques.", d: "The client being asked to relieve the traumatic event in their imagination" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "Administering an antidepressant is pharmacological treatment, whereas EMDR is a psychotherapeutic technique involving bilateral stimulation, not medication.",
      c: "Relaxation training is a component of many therapies (such as systematic desensitisation), but it is not the defining feature of EMDR.",
      d: "Reliving the trauma in imagination describes imaginal exposure, a feature of prolonged exposure therapy; EMDR specifically adds the guided eye movements or bilateral stimulation."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is the client focusing on the memory, emotions, and thoughts of the traumatic events while engaging in therapist-guided eye movements. Eye Movement Desensitisation and Reprocessing (EMDR) is a psychotherapy developed by Francine Shapiro in the late 1980s and used primarily to treat post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD) and other trauma-related conditions. In EMDR, the client is asked to hold in mind the distressing traumatic memory along with its associated images, negative beliefs, emotions, and bodily sensations, while simultaneously attending to a form of bilateral stimulation, most characteristically side-to-side eye movements guided by the therapist's moving fingers, though taps or tones can also be used. This is done in structured sets across eight phases, gradually working toward reprocessing the memory so that it becomes less emotionally charged and is linked to more adaptive beliefs. Shapiro proposed the Adaptive Information Processing model to explain how the dual attention allows the brain to reprocess traumatic memories that were previously stored in a maladaptive way. EMDR has a substantial evidence base and is recommended in trauma treatment guidelines, though the specific mechanism of the eye movements remains debated. The distractors describe medication, relaxation training, and imaginal exposure, none of which is the defining bilateral-stimulation feature of EMDR. This item tests recognition of the EMDR procedure." },

  { id: "rml21_097", section: "therapy",
    q: "When a counsellor repeats what the client says, but uses words that are different than what the client used, the counsellor is using what skill?",
    options: { a: "Paraphrasing", b: "Questioning", c: "Empathy", d: "Nonverbal communication" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: {
      b: "Questioning involves posing questions to gather information or prompt reflection, not restating the client's message in different words.",
      c: "Empathy is the deeper capacity to understand and share the client's feelings; paraphrasing is one specific verbal technique that can convey empathy, but the skill described here is specifically paraphrasing.",
      d: "Nonverbal communication refers to body language, tone, and gestures, not to the verbal restatement of the client's words."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is paraphrasing. Paraphrasing (also called reflection of content or restatement) is a core microskill in counselling, systematised within the microcounselling framework of Allen Ivey and prominent in the person-centred tradition of Carl Rogers. It involves the counsellor listening carefully and then restating the essential content or meaning of what the client has just said, but in the counsellor's own, different words rather than simply parroting the client's exact phrasing. Effective paraphrasing serves several purposes: it demonstrates to the client that the counsellor is listening and understands, it checks the accuracy of the counsellor's understanding (allowing the client to correct any misinterpretation), it helps the client hear their own thoughts reflected back and thereby clarify them, and it conveys empathy and builds rapport. It is distinct from reflection of feeling, which mirrors the client's emotions rather than content, and from summarising, which condenses a longer stretch of material. The distractors name other skills: questioning gathers information, empathy is the broader attitude of understanding (which paraphrasing helps express), and nonverbal communication concerns body language and tone. Because the described skill specifically involves restating the client's words differently, paraphrasing is correct. This item tests knowledge of basic counselling microskills." },

  { id: "rml21_098", section: "therapy",
    q: "A Student becomes terrified of speaking in public. He cancels his presentation due to fear of forgetting and embarrassment. For treatment, he is taught to relax completely. Once the relaxation response is well established, imagery is introduced by the therapist during the relaxed state. In the imagery he is asked to visualise himself sitting on the stage, then introducing himself and the topic, gradually giving the presentation. This is best described as:",
    options: { a: "The patient stopping to speak act as negative reinforcement", b: "The purpose of relaxation is to divert the attention of positive reinforcement", c: "This approach combines aversive conditioning and positive reinforcement", d: "This is called systematic desensitisation" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: {
      a: "This misapplies negative reinforcement; the scenario is a graded exposure-plus-relaxation procedure, not an operant reinforcement of stopping speech.",
      b: "Relaxation in this technique is used to counter-condition anxiety, not to 'divert the attention of positive reinforcement', which is not a coherent description.",
      c: "The procedure uses relaxation paired with graded imagined exposure, not aversive conditioning, so this mischaracterises the method."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is that this is called systematic desensitisation. Systematic desensitisation is a behaviour therapy technique developed by Joseph Wolpe for treating phobias and anxiety disorders, based on the principle of reciprocal inhibition, the idea that a person cannot be relaxed and anxious at the same time, so if relaxation is paired with anxiety-provoking stimuli, the relaxation will inhibit and eventually replace the anxiety response through counter-conditioning. The procedure has three classic components, all present in the scenario. First, the client is trained in deep muscle relaxation (often progressive muscle relaxation, associated with Edmund Jacobson) until they can relax completely. Second, the therapist and client construct an anxiety hierarchy, a graded list of feared situations from least to most anxiety-provoking. Third, while deeply relaxed, the client is guided to imagine (or later confront in vivo) the feared situations, starting with the least threatening and progressing gradually up the hierarchy, exactly as the student imagines sitting on stage, then introducing himself, then giving the presentation. Because relaxation is maintained at each step, the feared stimuli become associated with calm rather than fear. This graded, relaxation-paired imaginal exposure is the hallmark of systematic desensitisation. The operant and aversive distractors misdescribe the method. This item tests recognition of Wolpe's systematic desensitisation." },

  { id: "rml21_099", section: "systems",
    q: "Consider this statement: 'Individuals, families, and entire cultures are given a different level of power as creators of their realities.' This statement BEST describes which ONE of the following family therapies?",
    options: { a: "Transgenerational theory", b: "Strategic family therapy", c: "Structural family therapy", d: "Narrative therapy" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: {
      a: "Transgenerational theory (Murray Bowen) focuses on multigenerational transmission of emotional patterns and differentiation of self, not on people as constructors of their realities through stories.",
      b: "Strategic family therapy (Jay Haley) focuses on designing specific interventions and directives to solve presenting problems, not on socially constructed realities.",
      c: "Structural family therapy (Salvador Minuchin) focuses on reorganising family structure, boundaries, and subsystems, not on narrative construction of reality."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is narrative therapy. Narrative therapy was developed by Michael White and David Epston and is grounded in social constructionism and postmodern thought, which hold that reality is not fixed and objective but is constructed through language, stories, and shared cultural meanings. The statement that individuals, families, and cultures are given different levels of power as creators of their realities captures this constructionist stance precisely: people author their lives through the stories they tell, and those stories are shaped by, and can privilege or marginalise, particular individuals and groups within a cultural context. A central technique of narrative therapy is externalisation, encapsulated in the motto 'the person is not the problem; the problem is the problem,' which separates the individual from the problem-saturated story. The therapist then helps the client identify unique outcomes or exceptions and re-author a more empowering, preferred narrative, attending to how dominant cultural discourses have influenced their self-story. This emphasis on power, culture, and the co-construction of reality distinguishes narrative therapy from the other options: Murray Bowen's transgenerational approach, Jay Haley's strategic therapy, and Salvador Minuchin's structural therapy, which focus respectively on multigenerational emotional processes, problem-solving directives, and family structure. This item tests recognition of the social-constructionist, reality-authoring emphasis of narrative therapy." },

  { id: "rml21_100", section: "therapy",
    q: "The front-line treatment for schizophrenia is:",
    options: { a: "Cognitive behavioural therapy (CBT)", b: "Stress management training", c: "Antipsychotic medication", d: "Social skills training" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "CBT is a valuable adjunctive treatment for schizophrenia (for example, for residual symptoms), but it is not the first-line treatment; it is used alongside medication.",
      b: "Stress management training may help reduce relapse triggers, but it does not address the core psychotic symptoms and is not the primary treatment.",
      d: "Social skills training helps improve functioning and is a useful adjunct, but it does not treat the underlying psychosis and is not front-line."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is antipsychotic medication. Schizophrenia is a severe psychotic disorder involving positive symptoms (delusions, hallucinations, disorganised thought and speech), negative symptoms (flat affect, avolition, social withdrawal), and cognitive impairments. Its dominant biological explanation is the dopamine hypothesis, which proposes that positive symptoms arise largely from overactivity of dopamine transmission in certain brain pathways, particularly the mesolimbic pathway. Accordingly, the front-line, first-line treatment is pharmacological: antipsychotic (neuroleptic) medications, which work primarily by blocking dopamine D2 receptors. The first-generation or typical antipsychotics (such as chlorpromazine, whose introduction in the 1950s revolutionised psychiatric care, and haloperidol) are effective against positive symptoms but carry a risk of extrapyramidal motor side effects. The second-generation or atypical antipsychotics (such as clozapine, risperidone, and olanzapine) act on both dopamine and serotonin systems and are often preferred for a broader symptom profile and fewer motor side effects, with clozapine reserved for treatment-resistant cases. Psychosocial interventions, cognitive behavioural therapy, social skills training, family therapy to reduce expressed emotion, and stress management, are important and evidence-based, but they are adjuncts used alongside medication rather than the primary front-line treatment. This item tests knowledge that antipsychotic medication is the first-line treatment for schizophrenia." }
],
  "rml-2022": [
  { id: "rml22_001", section: "psychopathology",
    q: "In view of most psychodynamic theorists, dissociative symptoms are produced by which of the following?",
    options: { a: "Repression", b: "Ego state", c: "Conflict", d: "Gender identity issue" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Ego state theory is a later structural elaboration, not the core psychodynamic explanation for dissociation itself.", c: "Conflict drives neurotic symptoms broadly, but psychodynamic theory specifies repression as the mechanism that pushes material out of awareness in dissociation.", d: "Gender identity has no role in the classical psychodynamic account of dissociation." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is repression. In classical psychodynamic theory, first articulated by Sigmund Freud with Josef Breuer in their work on hysteria, dissociative symptoms such as amnesia, fugue, and depersonalization arise when intolerable memories, affects, or impulses are actively pushed out of conscious awareness. Repression is the primary defense mechanism: the ego banishes threatening material to the unconscious to protect itself from anxiety. Dissociation is understood as an extreme, splitting-off variant of this process, where whole segments of experience or identity become inaccessible. Pierre Janet, a contemporary of Freud, framed dissociation slightly differently as a failure of integration (desagregation), but the question asks specifically about most psychodynamic theorists, who root the phenomenon in repression. The concept matters clinically because it explains why dissociative amnesia typically covers traumatic content and can, in theory, be recovered when the repressive barrier is lifted. Conflict is the broader engine behind all defenses, but the specific mechanism producing dissociative symptoms is repression itself." },
  { id: "rml22_002", section: "psychopathology",
    q: "Which of the following is a culture-bound syndrome where the patient responds to minimal stimuli with exaggerated startles, often exclaiming normally inhibited sexually denotative words?",
    options: { a: "Latah", b: "Koro", c: "Dhat", d: "Susto" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Koro is the fear that the genitals are retracting into the body, a distinct culture-bound syndrome with no exaggerated startle.", c: "Dhat is a South Asian syndrome of anxiety over semen loss, unrelated to startle responses.", d: "Susto is a Latin American 'soul loss' following fright, marked by listlessness rather than an exaggerated startle with coprolalia." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Latah, a culture-bound syndrome documented primarily in Malaysia and Indonesia. It is characterized by an exaggerated startle response to a sudden stimulus, followed by involuntary behaviors such as echolalia (repeating others' words), echopraxia (imitating others' movements), automatic obedience, and coprolalia (uttering normally inhibited, sexually explicit words). Culture-bound syndromes were catalogued in the DSM-IV glossary and later reframed as cultural concepts of distress in DSM-5 by the American Psychiatric Association. Latah usually affects middle-aged women and is often treated within the culture as a source of amusement rather than illness. The condition illustrates how psychiatric presentations are shaped by cultural context: the hyperstartle exists cross-culturally, but the specific patterned aftermath is locally scripted. Distinguishing Latah from Koro (genital retraction panic, reported in Southeast Asia and southern China), Dhat (semen-loss anxiety in South Asia), and Susto (fright-induced soul loss in Latin America) is a common entrance-exam task, so anchoring each to its defining feature and region is the reliable strategy." },
  { id: "rml22_003", section: "stats",
    q: "Duncan's multiple range test, Newman-Keuls test, Tukey's HSD test, and Scheffe test can be used only when:",
    options: { a: "F ratio is not significant", b: "F ratio cannot be determined", c: "F ratio is significant", d: "T ratio is not significant" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "If the F ratio is not significant, there is no overall difference to decompose, so post hoc tests are not warranted.", b: "Post hoc tests presuppose a computed and significant F, so an undeterminable F rules them out.", d: "The t ratio governs pairwise t-tests, not the omnibus ANOVA that these post hoc procedures follow." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is that the F ratio is significant. These four procedures, Duncan's multiple range test, the Newman-Keuls test, John Tukey's Honestly Significant Difference (HSD) test, and Henry Scheffe's test, are all post hoc (a posteriori) multiple comparison tests. They are used to follow up a one-way analysis of variance developed by Ronald Fisher. ANOVA produces an omnibus F ratio that tells you only whether at least one group mean differs from the others; it does not identify which specific pairs differ. Post hoc tests are therefore run only after a significant F establishes that a real difference exists somewhere among the means. Running them when F is non-significant would inflate the family-wise Type I error rate without any justified overall effect to decompose. Each test controls that error differently: Scheffe is the most conservative and flexible for complex contrasts, Tukey's HSD is standard for all pairwise comparisons, and Duncan's is comparatively liberal. The unifying rule tested here is that a significant omnibus F is the entry ticket for any of them." },
  { id: "rml22_004", section: "development",
    q: "Carol Gilligan departs from Kohlberg's theory on moral development by highlighting which of these differences?",
    options: { a: "Males have ethics of care", b: "Males have ethics of justice", c: "Females have ethics care", d: "Females have ethics of justice" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Gilligan associated the ethic of care with females, not males; males were tied to the justice orientation.", b: "This is true in Gilligan's framework but is not the departure the question emphasizes; her novel contribution was foregrounding the female care voice.", d: "Gilligan argued Kohlberg's justice orientation was male-typed, so attributing justice ethics to females inverts her claim." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is that females have an ethic of care. Carol Gilligan, in her 1982 book In a Different Voice, challenged the moral development stage theory of Lawrence Kohlberg, under whom she had worked. Kohlberg built his six-stage model largely from studies of boys and defined the highest, postconventional level around abstract principles of justice and individual rights. Because his scoring rewarded a justice orientation, women often appeared to score at a lower conventional stage. Gilligan argued this reflected gender bias in the theory, not deficient female morality. She proposed that women tend to reason from an ethic of care, emphasizing relationships, responsibility, compassion, and the avoidance of harm, while men more often reason from an ethic of justice, emphasizing rights, fairness, and rules. Neither voice is superior; they are different but complementary moral orientations. Gilligan's work was foundational for feminist psychology and for critiques of male-normed research. The key exam point is the pairing: care with females, justice with males." },
  { id: "rml22_005", section: "stats",
    q: "In a negatively skewed distribution, the position of the mean with reference to the median is :",
    options: { a: "Left of the median", b: "Right of the median", c: "Same as median", d: "Same as a mode" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Right of the median describes a positively skewed distribution, where the tail extends toward high values.", c: "Mean equals median only in a symmetric distribution, not a skewed one.", d: "In negative skew the mode is the highest of the three, so equating mean with mode is incorrect." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is left of the median. Skewness describes the asymmetry of a distribution. In a negatively (left) skewed distribution, the long tail points toward the low (left) end of the scale. Extreme low scores in that tail pull the arithmetic mean downward more than they move the median, because the mean is sensitive to every value while the median depends only on rank position. The result is the ordering mean less than median less than mode. So the mean sits to the left of the median. The mirror image holds for a positively (right) skewed distribution, where the ordering is mode less than median less than mean, placing the mean to the right. This relationship among the three measures of central tendency was popularized through the empirical work of Karl Pearson, who also defined coefficients of skewness using the gap between mean and mode or mean and median. Understanding it lets you infer the shape of a distribution from summary statistics alone, which is why it is a staple of psychology statistics papers." },
  { id: "rml22_006", section: "development",
    q: "What is the principal factor that changes the affordances available to the toddler, compared with the infant?",
    options: { a: "Independent locomotion", b: "Starting to speak", c: "The attachment relationship", d: "New kinds of caretaking" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Language transforms cognition and communication but does not directly reshape the physical action possibilities the environment offers.", c: "Attachment is well established in infancy already, so it is not what newly changes affordances at the toddler stage.", d: "Caretaking style may adjust, but the driver of new affordances is the child's own emerging mobility, not the caretaker." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is independent locomotion. The concept of affordances comes from the ecological perception theory of James J. Gibson, who defined an affordance as an action possibility that the environment offers an organism relative to its own capabilities: a surface affords walking, a graspable object affords grasping. Because affordances are defined jointly by the environment and the actor's abilities, any change in the actor's abilities changes which affordances are available. The single largest shift between infancy and toddlerhood is the onset of independent upright walking. Once a child can walk, crawl freely, and move away from and back to a caregiver at will, an enormous new set of affordances opens: reaching distant objects, exploring rooms, climbing, and encountering new hazards. Developmental researcher Karen Adolph has shown experimentally how new motor skills like walking reorganize infants' perception of what surfaces and slopes afford. Speech, attachment, and caretaking all matter for development, but the factor that specifically transforms the toddler's field of physical action possibilities is self-produced locomotion." },
  { id: "rml22_007", section: "psychopathology",
    q: "Which of the following statements concerning Bipolar disorder is INCORRECT?",
    options: { a: "Bipolar-I Disorder includes mania", b: "Bipolar-II Disorder includes hypomania", c: "Bipolar-I Disorder includes hypomania", d: "Bipolar-I Disorder includes periods of depression even if these do not reach the threshold for major depressive disorder" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "This is correct: Bipolar I is defined by at least one full manic episode, so it is not the incorrect statement sought.", b: "This is correct: Bipolar II requires hypomania plus major depression, so it is a true statement.", d: "This is a true statement, as depressive symptoms in Bipolar I need not meet full major depressive criteria for the diagnosis." },
    explanation: "The correct answer (the INCORRECT statement) is that Bipolar-I Disorder includes hypomania. Per the DSM-5 of the American Psychiatric Association, Bipolar I Disorder is defined by the presence of at least one full manic episode, a distinct period of abnormally elevated or irritable mood plus increased energy lasting at least one week, severe enough to cause marked impairment, require hospitalization, or include psychotic features. A person with Bipolar I may also experience hypomanic and major depressive episodes, but the defining, minimum requirement is mania, not hypomania. Bipolar II Disorder, by contrast, is defined by at least one hypomanic episode (a milder, shorter mood elevation without marked impairment or psychosis) together with at least one major depressive episode, and crucially it must never have involved a full manic episode. Emil Kraepelin originally unified these mood states under manic-depressive insanity. The question hinges on precision: hypomania is the diagnostic hallmark of Bipolar II, so labeling it as the defining feature of Bipolar I is the incorrect statement." },
  { id: "rml22_008", section: "psychopathology",
    q: "A 12-year-old boy begins to have new episodes of temper outbursts that are out of proportion to the situation. Which of the following is NOT a diagnostic possibility in this patient?",
    options: { a: "Disruptive Mood Dysregulation Disorder", b: "Bipolar Disorder", c: "Conduct Disorder", d: "Attention Deficit Hyperactivity Disorder" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Bipolar disorder can present with irritability and outbursts and remains a diagnostic possibility, so it is not the excluded answer.", c: "Conduct disorder features aggression and rule violation, so temper outbursts fit within its possibilities.", d: "ADHD commonly co-occurs with emotional dysregulation and outbursts, so it stays on the differential." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Disruptive Mood Dysregulation Disorder (DMDD). DMDD was introduced in the DSM-5 by the American Psychiatric Association to capture children with chronic, severe, persistent irritability and frequent temper outbursts, partly to reduce over-diagnosis of pediatric bipolar disorder. A critical diagnostic rule is the age criterion: DMDD may be diagnosed only when the onset of symptoms is before age 10, and it should not be diagnosed for the first time before age 6 or after age 18. In this vignette the outbursts are described as new in a 12-year-old, meaning onset is after the age-10 cutoff, so DMDD is specifically excluded. That is why it is the one diagnosis that is NOT a possibility, whereas Bipolar Disorder, Conduct Disorder, and ADHD all remain on the differential for a 12-year-old presenting with disproportionate temper outbursts. The item tests careful attention to DMDD's onset-age requirement, a detail that reliably distinguishes it from the other externalizing and mood conditions with which it shares surface features." },
  { id: "rml22_009", section: "development",
    q: "Which among the following is not included within the classification of structure of temperament given by Rothbart and Bates",
    options: { a: "Extraversion", b: "Inhibition to the unfamiliar", c: "Negative affectivity", d: "Effortful control" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Extraversion/surgency is one of the three core dimensions in Rothbart's model, so it is included.", c: "Negative affectivity is a defining dimension of the Rothbart and Bates structure.", d: "Effortful control is the self-regulatory dimension central to their model, so it is included." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is inhibition to the unfamiliar. Mary Rothbart and John Bates proposed an influential structural model of temperament organized around three broad dimensions: extraversion or surgency (positive affect, activity level, approach, and sociability), negative affectivity (fear, frustration, sadness, and discomfort), and effortful control (the capacity to voluntarily focus and shift attention and to inhibit dominant responses in favor of subdominant ones). Effortful control is the self-regulatory core of their theory and is tied to the development of executive attention. Inhibition to the unfamiliar, better known as behavioral inhibition, is a temperament construct, but it is most closely associated with the work of Jerome Kagan, who studied inhibited versus uninhibited children and their tendency toward wariness in novel situations. While behavioral inhibition overlaps conceptually with fear within negative affectivity, it is not one of the three named superordinate dimensions in the Rothbart and Bates classification. The exam point is to match each temperament construct to its theorist: surgency, negative affectivity, and effortful control to Rothbart and Bates, and behavioral inhibition to Kagan." },
  { id: "rml22_010", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Maya's baby was born with a smaller than average head size, facial malformations, and an intellectual disability. It is likely that Maya _____________ during her early pregnancy.",
    options: { a: "drank too much caffeine", b: "drank alcohol", c: "smoked marijuana", d: "ate fish with mercury in it" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Excessive caffeine is linked to miscarriage risk and low birth weight, not the microcephaly and facial dysmorphology triad.", c: "Prenatal marijuana is associated with subtle cognitive and attentional effects, not the characteristic facial malformations described.", d: "Methylmercury causes neurological damage but not the specific small head plus facial anomaly pattern of this syndrome." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is that Maya drank alcohol. The cluster described, a smaller than average head (microcephaly), characteristic facial malformations, and intellectual disability, is the defining triad of Fetal Alcohol Syndrome (FAS), the most severe form of Fetal Alcohol Spectrum Disorders. Alcohol is a teratogen: it crosses the placenta freely and disrupts fetal cell development, migration, and organization, with the brain being especially vulnerable. The signature facial features include a smooth philtrum (the groove between nose and upper lip), a thin upper lip, and small palpebral fissures (eye openings). The condition was first clinically described and named by Kenneth Lyons Jones and David W. Smith in 1973. There is no established safe amount of alcohol during pregnancy, and the developing brain can be affected throughout gestation, though structural facial features arise from exposure during early first-trimester development. FAS is a leading preventable cause of intellectual disability worldwide. The other options carry their own risks but do not produce this specific microcephaly-plus-facial-malformation pattern, making prenatal alcohol exposure the clear answer." },
  { id: "rml22_011", section: "psychopathology",
    q: "Which of the following is a proposed criterion set in the current DSM that involves cutting, burning, or otherwise intentionally hurting one's own body without the intent to kill oneself?",
    options: { a: "Internet gaming disorder", b: "Attenuated psychosis syndrome", c: "Persistent complex bereavement", d: "Non-suicidal self-injury" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Internet gaming disorder is a proposed condition for further study but concerns compulsive gaming, not self-harm.", b: "Attenuated psychosis syndrome describes sub-threshold psychotic-like symptoms, not deliberate self-injury.", c: "Persistent complex bereavement concerns prolonged, impairing grief, not intentional bodily harm." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Non-Suicidal Self-Injury (NSSI). NSSI is defined as the deliberate, self-inflicted damage of body tissue, such as cutting, burning, scratching, or hitting, carried out without suicidal intent and for purposes not socially sanctioned. In the DSM-5 of the American Psychiatric Association, NSSI is placed in Section III, Conditions for Further Study, meaning it is a proposed criterion set that is not yet an official standalone diagnosis but is offered to encourage research and consistent measurement. People engage in NSSI most often to regulate overwhelming negative emotion, to punish themselves, or to escape numbness, and it is strongly associated with borderline personality disorder, depression, and trauma histories, though it can occur on its own. The crucial distinguishing feature, and the phrase the question hinges on, is without the intent to kill oneself, which separates NSSI from suicidal behavior. The other three options, Internet Gaming Disorder, Attenuated Psychosis Syndrome, and Persistent Complex Bereavement Disorder, are also Section III proposed conditions, which makes the item a test of matching each proposed set to its defining behavior." },
  { id: "rml22_012", section: "psychopathology",
    q: "Individuals with obsessive-compulsive personality disorder are primarily motivated by a need for which of the following?",
    options: { a: "Efficiency", b: "Achievement", c: "Control", d: "Autonomy" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "OCPD individuals are often paradoxically inefficient because perfectionism interferes with task completion, so efficiency is not the core drive.", b: "Achievement motivation is broader and not the defining need; the disorder centers on orderliness and control rather than accomplishment itself.", d: "Autonomy is more characteristic of other patterns; OCPD centers on controlling self, others, and the environment through rules." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is control. Obsessive-Compulsive Personality Disorder (OCPD), classified in Cluster C of the DSM-5 by the American Psychiatric Association, is a pervasive pattern of preoccupation with orderliness, perfectionism, and mental and interpersonal control, at the expense of flexibility, openness, and efficiency. The central motivating dynamic is a need for control: over one's own behavior, over other people, and over the environment. This manifests as rigid adherence to rules and schedules, excessive devotion to work, hoarding, reluctance to delegate unless others submit to one's exact methods, and moral inflexibility. Note that OCPD is distinct from Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder (OCD): OCPD is an ego-syntonic personality style without true obsessions and compulsions, whereas OCD, an anxiety-spectrum condition, involves ego-dystonic intrusive thoughts and ritualized behaviors. Psychodynamic writers tracing back to Sigmund Freud linked the anal-retentive character (orderliness, parsimony, obstinacy) to this control-oriented pattern. The perfectionism seen in OCPD is often self-defeating, producing inefficiency rather than the efficiency one might expect, which is exactly why control, not efficiency, is the underlying need." },
  { id: "rml22_013", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Which of the following condition is related to the deficiency of acetylcholine in the brain?",
    options: { a: "Senile dementia", b: "Atrophy", c: "Alzheimer's disease", d: "Korsakoff's syndrome" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Senile dementia is a broad umbrella term, not the specific disease tied to the cholinergic deficit and treated by cholinesterase inhibitors.", b: "Atrophy is a general term for tissue loss, not a neurotransmitter-specific condition.", d: "Korsakoff's syndrome stems from thiamine deficiency affecting the diencephalon, not acetylcholine depletion." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Alzheimer's disease. Acetylcholine is a neurotransmitter central to learning, memory, and attention, released by cholinergic neurons that project widely across the cortex and hippocampus. In Alzheimer's disease, first described by Alois Alzheimer in 1906, there is marked degeneration of cholinergic neurons, especially in the basal forebrain nucleus basalis of Meynert, producing a substantial drop in brain acetylcholine. This finding gave rise to the cholinergic hypothesis of Alzheimer's, which holds that the memory and cognitive deficits stem largely from this cholinergic deficit. It is precisely why the main symptomatic drug treatments are acetylcholinesterase inhibitors such as donepezil, rivastigmine, and galantamine, which slow the breakdown of acetylcholine to boost its availability at synapses. The disease's other hallmark pathologies are extracellular beta-amyloid plaques and intracellular neurofibrillary tangles of tau protein. Korsakoff's syndrome, by contrast, results from thiamine (vitamin B1) deficiency, often due to chronic alcoholism, and damages diencephalic structures, so it is not acetylcholine-based. This makes Alzheimer's the disorder most directly tied to acetylcholine deficiency." },
  { id: "rml22_014", section: "general",
    q: "A double-blind study is used to minimize the impact of:",
    options: { a: "Experimenter bias", b: "Expectancy effects", c: "Both a and b", d: "Neither a nor b" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Experimenter bias is minimized, but choosing only this ignores that participant expectancy is also controlled.", b: "Expectancy effects are controlled, but this alone is incomplete because experimenter bias is addressed too.", d: "This is wrong because double-blinding specifically targets both sources of bias simultaneously." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is both a and b. In a double-blind study, neither the participants nor the researchers who interact with them and record data know which condition (treatment or control, drug or placebo) each participant is assigned to. This design controls two distinct threats to internal validity at once. First, it minimizes experimenter bias, sometimes called the Rosenthal effect after Robert Rosenthal, whereby a researcher's knowledge of the hypothesis subtly influences how they treat participants, interpret ambiguous responses, or score outcomes. Second, it minimizes participant expectancy effects, including the placebo effect, whereby a participant's beliefs about what treatment they are receiving alter their reported or actual outcomes. A single-blind study conceals assignment from participants only, leaving experimenter bias uncontrolled; the double-blind extends concealment to the experimenters, closing both channels. This is why double-blind, placebo-controlled randomized trials are considered a gold standard in clinical and pharmaceutical research. Because the design addresses both experimenter bias and expectancy effects simultaneously, the inclusive option is correct." },
  { id: "rml22_015", section: "development",
    q: "In Terman's study of gifted children, social and behavioural problems were found only in those with :",
    options: { a: "IQs of 150 or higher", b: "IQs of 180 or higher", c: "IQs of 180 or higher in adulthood", d: "IQs of 180 or higher in childhood" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "The threshold identified for social difficulty was 180, not 150, so this understates the cutoff.", b: "This omits the developmental period; the problems were noted specifically during childhood.", c: "The adjustment problems were observed in childhood, not adulthood, so specifying adulthood is incorrect." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is IQs of 180 or higher in childhood. Lewis Terman, the Stanford psychologist who adapted the Binet-Simon scale into the Stanford-Binet Intelligence Scale, launched a landmark longitudinal study of gifted children in 1921, following a large sample (nicknamed the Termites) across their lives. A central and reassuring finding was that gifted children were generally well-adjusted, healthy, and socially competent, contradicting the popular stereotype of the maladjusted genius. However, the study noted an exception at the very extreme upper tail: children with exceptionally high IQs, around 180 or above in childhood, were more likely to encounter social and behavioural adjustment difficulties. This is thought to arise because such extreme intellectual precocity creates a large mismatch between the child's mental age and both their chronological age-mates and their physical and emotional maturity, leading to isolation and difficulty finding intellectual peers. Leta Hollingworth similarly documented adjustment challenges among the profoundly gifted. The precise exam answer requires both the 180 threshold and the childhood qualifier, since the difficulties were an observation about extremely gifted children, not gifted adults." },
  { id: "rml22_016", section: "biopsych",
    q: "For which of the following neurotransmitter is Alcohol an agonist?",
    options: { a: "Serotonin", b: "Acetylcholine", c: "GABA", d: "Endorphins" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Alcohol does modulate serotonin, but its primary agonist action underlying sedation is at GABA receptors.", b: "Alcohol tends to disrupt cholinergic transmission rather than act as a clear agonist there.", d: "Alcohol can indirectly raise endorphin release, but the defining pharmacological agonist effect is at the GABA-A receptor." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is GABA. Gamma-aminobutyric acid (GABA) is the brain's primary inhibitory neurotransmitter; when it binds the GABA-A receptor it opens a chloride channel that hyperpolarizes the neuron, reducing its likelihood of firing and thereby dampening neural activity. Ethanol acts as a positive modulator, functionally an agonist, at the GABA-A receptor: it enhances GABA's inhibitory effect, potentiating chloride influx and increasing central nervous system inhibition. This is the mechanism behind alcohol's characteristic sedative, anxiolytic, muscle-relaxant, and coordination-impairing effects, and it is the same receptor system targeted by benzodiazepines and barbiturates, which is why these drugs are cross-tolerant and dangerous when combined with alcohol. Alcohol simultaneously antagonizes the excitatory glutamate NMDA receptor, adding to its depressant profile and contributing to memory blackouts. Chronic use produces neuroadaptation of the GABA system, so that withdrawal causes rebound hyperexcitability, tremor, and seizures. While alcohol also touches serotonin, dopamine, and endorphin systems in ways that shape its rewarding effects, its defining agonist action for exam purposes is at GABA." },
  { id: "rml22_017", section: "development",
    q: "According to Adler, middle children tend to be :",
    options: { a: "Overachieving", b: "Competitive", c: "Resentful of the freedom of the older child", d: "Filled with feelings of inferiority" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Overachievement is more typical of the firstborn striving to regain lost status in Adler's scheme.", c: "Resentment of the older child's freedom is not the trait Adler emphasized for the middle child.", d: "Pervasive inferiority feelings are linked to the pampered or the youngest child, not the driving middle child." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is competitive. Alfred Adler, founder of Individual Psychology, argued that birth order shapes personality through the child's subjective experience of their family position. He described the middle (second-born) child as growing up in a race, always with an older sibling ahead to catch up to and often a younger sibling behind. This creates a characteristically competitive, ambitious striving: the middle child is spurred to overtake the pacesetter older sibling and frequently becomes achievement-oriented and even rebellious against the perceived unfairness of never being first. Adler saw this as generally healthy, and he considered the second-born often the best adjusted of the sibships. He contrasted this with the firstborn, who feels dethroned by the arrival of a sibling and may become conservative and power-oriented, the youngest or pampered child, who may remain dependent and prone to inferiority feelings, and the only child, who may struggle to share. Adler is also the originator of the inferiority complex concept. The specific hallmark of the middle child in his framework is competitiveness driven by the pace-setting sibling ahead." },
  { id: "rml22_018", section: "social",
    q: "Who proposed the social role theory of gender?",
    options: { a: "Buss", b: "Alice Eagly", c: "Mehta and Strough", d: "Anderson" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "David Buss is associated with the evolutionary psychology account of sex differences, the rival to social role theory.", c: "Mehta and Strough studied gender segregation across the lifespan, not the origin of social role theory.", d: "Anderson is not the originator of this theory and is a distractor name here." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Alice Eagly. Alice Eagly proposed social role theory to explain sex differences in social behavior. The theory holds that observed psychological and behavioral differences between men and women arise largely from the contrasting social roles they occupy, roles historically shaped by a division of labor. Because women were more often assigned domestic and caregiving roles and men more often assigned provider and leadership roles, societies developed gender-role expectations (communal traits like nurturance for women, agentic traits like assertiveness for men). Individuals then internalize and enact these expectations, and observers infer that men and women have differing inherent dispositions, a bias that reinforces the roles. Crucially, social role theory is a sociocultural account and stands in direct contrast to the evolutionary psychology explanation championed by David Buss, who attributes sex differences to inherited adaptations from ancestral mating and survival pressures. Eagly later extended her framework into the biosocial and role congruity theories, the latter explaining prejudice against women in leadership when leader stereotypes clash with female gender roles. The item tests correct attribution of the sociocultural theory to Eagly." },
  { id: "rml22_019", section: "general",
    q: "A researcher is interested in the impact of anxiety on performance. To manipulate anxiety, some subjects were given plain chips to eat while others were given barbecue flavoured chips to eat. The most obvious problem with this experiment is a lack of :",
    options: { a: "Statistical Validity", b: "Empirical Validity", c: "Internal Validity", d: "Construct Validity" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Statistical validity concerns the appropriateness of statistical tests and inferences, not whether the manipulation captures the intended concept.", b: "Empirical validity is not the standard label for this flaw, and it does not name the operationalization problem.", c: "Internal validity concerns whether the manipulation, not confounds, caused the outcome; here the deeper issue is that the manipulation does not represent anxiety at all." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is construct validity. Construct validity is the degree to which an operational manipulation or measure actually captures the theoretical construct it is meant to represent, a concept formalized by Lee Cronbach and Paul Meehl. In this study the construct of interest is anxiety, but the independent variable, giving subjects plain versus barbecue-flavoured chips, has no plausible connection to inducing anxiety at all. Flavour of a snack is not a valid operationalization of an anxiety manipulation; whatever effect appears cannot be interpreted as an effect of anxiety because anxiety was never meaningfully varied. This is a failure of construct validity: the manipulation does not embody the construct named in the hypothesis. It is worth distinguishing this from internal validity, the confidence that the manipulation rather than a confound caused the outcome, and from external validity, the generalizability of results. Here the flaw is more fundamental than a confound: the manipulation is simply not a measure of anxiety. Because the operationalization of the independent variable does not correspond to the intended psychological construct, the study most obviously lacks construct validity." },
  { id: "rml22_020", section: "assessment",
    q: "In which of the following situations would a diagnosis of \"global developmental delay\" be inappropriate?",
    options: { a: "The patient is a child who is too young to fully manifest specific symptoms or to complete requisite assessments.", b: "The patient, a 7-year-old boy, has a full-scale IQ of 65 and severe impairment in adaptive functioning.", c: "The patient's scores on psychometric tests suggest intellectual disability, but there is insufficient information about the patient's adaptive functional skills.", d: "The patient's impaired adaptive functioning suggests intellectual developmental disorder, but there is insufficient information about the level of cognitive impairment measured by standardized instruments." },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Being too young to complete valid assessments is a core reason the provisional global developmental delay category exists, so it is appropriate here.", c: "Insufficient adaptive-functioning information is exactly a case where the provisional label is used pending fuller evaluation.", d: "Missing standardized cognitive data is another appropriate reason to apply the provisional diagnosis rather than a full intellectual disability diagnosis." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is the 7-year-old boy with a full-scale IQ of 65 and severe adaptive impairment. In the DSM-5 of the American Psychiatric Association, Global Developmental Delay is a provisional diagnosis reserved for children under age 5 who fail to meet expected developmental milestones in several areas but who cannot yet be reliably assessed by standardized testing, either because they are too young or too impaired to complete valid measures. It is explicitly a placeholder that requires reassessment later. The diagnosis is inappropriate for the 7-year-old because he is old enough for, and has already undergone, valid standardized testing that yields a clear IQ of 65 together with documented severe adaptive impairment. That combination meets the full criteria for Intellectual Disability (Intellectual Developmental Disorder), so the definitive diagnosis should be made rather than the provisional Global Developmental Delay label. The other three scenarios each involve missing or unobtainable assessment data, precisely the circumstances the provisional category was designed for. The item tests understanding that Global Developmental Delay is an under-5, cannot-yet-assess placeholder, not a substitute when full diagnostic data already exist." },
  { id: "rml22_021", section: "development",
    q: "In the 'teleological stance', toddlers can understand:",
    options: { a: "Contingencies", b: "Intentions in action", c: "Prior Intentions", d: "Mental states such as belief and desire" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Contingency detection is a more basic capacity present in early infancy, below the level the teleological stance describes.", c: "Prior intentions, formed before acting, require a more advanced mentalistic understanding than the teleological stance provides.", d: "Representing beliefs and desires is full theory of mind, which develops later than the goal-directed teleological understanding." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is intentions in action. The teleological stance is a concept from the developmental work of Gyorgy Gergely and Gergely Csibra describing how infants and toddlers interpret behavior as goal-directed before they possess a full, mentalistic theory of mind. Adopting the teleological stance, a young child understands actions in terms of the goal being pursued and the most efficient available means to reach it in the current situation (the principle of rational action), all without needing to attribute internal mental representations. This corresponds to understanding intentions in action, that is, the goal-directedness embodied in the ongoing behavior itself, as distinguished by philosopher John Searle from prior intentions, which are intentions formed in the mind before acting. Grasping prior intentions and mental states such as beliefs and desires requires the later-developing representational theory of mind, classically tested by false-belief tasks that most children pass around age four, building on the earlier work of David Premack, Guy Woodruff, and later Heinz Wimmer and Josef Perner. The teleological stance is thus an intermediate, non-mentalistic reading of goal-directed action, capturing intentions in action but not yet prior intentions or full mental-state understanding." },
  { id: "rml22_022", section: "development",
    q: "In Vygotsky's writings, conceptual thinking is linked to all the following EXCEPT :",
    options: { a: "the distinction between the acting 'I' and the reflecting 'I'", b: "the problems and developmental tasks that society creates for adolescents", c: "participation in particular societal institutions", d: "gender differences" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "The acting versus reflecting self is part of how Vygotsky tied conceptual thought to self-consciousness, so it is linked.", b: "Vygotsky linked the emergence of concept formation to the social demands placed on adolescents, so it is linked.", c: "Participation in societal institutions is central to Vygotsky's sociocultural account of concept development, so it is linked." },
    explanation: "The correct answer (the EXCEPT) is gender differences. Lev Vygotsky, the founder of the sociocultural or cultural-historical theory of development, argued that higher mental functions, including conceptual (true concept) thinking, originate in social interaction and are shaped by the cultural context in which a child develops. In his writings on adolescence, he linked the emergence of genuine conceptual thinking to several socially rooted factors: the developmental tasks and problems that society poses to adolescents (which demand more abstract thought), the young person's participation in particular societal institutions such as school and work, and the growth of self-consciousness reflected in the distinction between the acting 'I' and the reflecting 'I'. All of these tie concept formation to social and cultural demands, consistent with his view that thought is mediated by culturally supplied tools, above all language. Vygotsky did not, however, frame conceptual thinking as arising from or being linked to gender differences; sex or gender was not a factor in his account of concept development. Hence gender differences is the option that does not belong, making it the correct exception." },
  { id: "rml22_023", section: "assessment",
    q: "Which of the following could legitimately be predicted for a person with an IQ of 50?",
    options: { a: "Fifth-grade level of learning", b: "Entire life spent in an institution", c: "Need for constant supervision", d: "High-school graduation if given special instruction" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Institutionalization for life corresponds to severe or profound disability, well below the mild range an IQ of 50 falls near.", c: "Constant supervision describes more severe impairment; mild disability individuals can achieve considerable independence.", d: "High-school graduation typically exceeds the academic ceiling expected at this level even with special instruction." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is a fifth-grade (roughly upper-primary) level of learning. An IQ of 50 sits at the boundary between mild and moderate intellectual disability, near the lower edge of the mild range (about 50 to 70) on tests such as the Stanford-Binet, adapted by Lewis Terman, and the Wechsler scales developed by David Wechsler. Individuals functioning at this level are traditionally described in older classifications (for example, the American Association on Mental Deficiency system) as educable: with appropriate special education they can typically acquire academic skills up to roughly the third-to-sixth grade level, most commonly cited around the fifth-grade ceiling in adulthood. They can usually learn to read, write, and handle basic arithmetic and money, achieve social and vocational skills sufficient for supported or semi-independent living, and hold simple jobs. This makes the fifth-grade level of learning a legitimate prediction. By contrast, needing constant supervision or lifelong institutionalization characterizes the more severe and profound ranges, while attaining high-school graduation generally exceeds the realistic academic ceiling at an IQ of 50, so those options overstate or understate what this level of functioning predicts." },
  { id: "rml22_024", section: "development",
    q: "According to experts on socioemotional development like Campos (2009) and Kagan (2010), self-conscious emotions appear at which age?",
    options: { a: "6-9 months", b: "Before 9 months", c: "After 12 months", d: "3 months" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "At 6-9 months infants show basic emotions but lack the self-awareness required for self-conscious emotions.", b: "Before 9 months is too early; self-recognition and self-referential emotion have not yet emerged.", d: "At 3 months only primary emotions like joy and distress are present, far before self-conscious emotions." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is after 12 months. Developmental psychologists distinguish primary (basic) emotions, such as joy, sadness, fear, anger, surprise, and disgust, which appear in the first six months of life, from self-conscious (secondary) emotions, such as embarrassment, jealousy, empathy, pride, shame, and guilt. Self-conscious emotions require a cognitive prerequisite: a sense of self, the capacity for self-awareness and self-referential evaluation. Because a rudimentary self does not emerge until the second year, self-conscious emotions appear after about 12 to 18 months. Researchers including Joseph Campos and Jerome Kagan place their emergence in this later window, tied to the child's developing self-recognition, classically indexed by the rouge or mirror self-recognition test devised by Gordon Gallup and applied to infants by Michael Lewis, in which toddlers around 15 to 24 months begin to recognize themselves. Lewis further divided self-conscious emotions into earlier self-referential ones (embarrassment, empathy, envy) and later self-conscious evaluative ones (pride, shame, guilt), the latter requiring standards and rules. The key exam point is the sequence: basic emotions first, then, only after the self emerges past the first birthday, the self-conscious emotions." },
  { id: "rml22_025", section: "stats",
    q: "Who propounded the concept of normal distribution curve?",
    options: { a: "Carl Gauss", b: "Simeon Denis Poisson", c: "James Bernoulli", d: "Wilhelm Reich" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Simeon Denis Poisson is known for the Poisson distribution used for counts of rare events, not the normal curve.", c: "Jacob (James) Bernoulli is associated with the binomial distribution and the law of large numbers, not the normal distribution.", d: "Wilhelm Reich was a psychoanalyst with no role in statistics; this is a distractor." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Carl Gauss. The normal distribution, the symmetric bell-shaped curve fundamental to statistics, is so closely associated with the German mathematician Carl Friedrich Gauss that it is commonly called the Gaussian distribution. Gauss developed and applied it in the early nineteenth century, notably in analyzing astronomical measurement errors and in formulating the method of least squares, which is why the curve is also tied to the theory of the error distribution. Historically, Abraham de Moivre first derived the curve as an approximation to the binomial distribution, and Pierre-Simon Laplace extended it, but the concept is conventionally attributed to Gauss in psychology and education examinations. The normal curve is defined entirely by its mean and standard deviation; it is unimodal, symmetric about the mean, with mean, median, and mode coinciding, and it follows the empirical rule that roughly 68, 95, and 99.7 percent of cases fall within one, two, and three standard deviations of the mean. Its centrality flows from the Central Limit Theorem, and it underpins standardized scores, IQ scaling, and most parametric tests. The distractors, Poisson and Bernoulli, name different distributions, and Wilhelm Reich belongs to psychoanalysis, not statistics." },
  { id: "rml22_026", section: "development",
    q: "Psychologists and psychiatrists have often viewed adolescence as a time when the healthy trajectory of development involves detachment from the parents. Today, however, there is a greater awareness that:",
    options: { a: "it can be healthy for interdependence to continue", b: "detachment typically occurs earlier, in middle childhood", c: "the peer group is more important than parents", d: "all parents have the same goal for their adolescent child" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "The trend is not that detachment happens earlier; the shift is away from viewing detachment as necessary at all.", c: "Modern research does not conclude that peers simply outweigh parents; both remain influential in different domains.", d: "Parents plainly differ in goals across families and cultures, so this generalization is false." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is that it can be healthy for interdependence to continue. Older psychoanalytic views, notably those of Anna Freud and Peter Blos, portrayed adolescence as requiring detachment or emotional separation from parents, treating strong parental ties in the teen years as a sign of immaturity. Contemporary developmental research, associated with figures such as Laurence Steinberg and attachment-informed scholars, has revised this picture. It shows that healthy adolescent development involves individuation and growing autonomy while maintaining warm, connected relationships with parents, that is, a shift toward interdependence rather than a clean break. Secure attachment to parents, in the tradition of John Bowlby and Mary Ainsworth, continues to serve as a base from which adolescents explore, and connectedness predicts better adjustment, self-esteem, and identity formation. Autonomy and relatedness are thus complementary, not opposed. This also reflects cultural awareness: many collectivist cultures never framed adolescence as detachment at all. So the modern understanding is that ongoing interdependence, adolescents becoming more autonomous while staying emotionally close to their families, is healthy, which is why option a is correct and the detachment-centered alternatives are outdated." },
  { id: "rml22_027", section: "psychopathology",
    q: "Sigmund Freud stated that alcohol-dependent individuals had fixation in :",
    options: { a: "Anal stage", b: "Oral stage", c: "Genital stage", d: "Latency stage" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Anal-stage fixation is linked to orderliness or messiness traits, not to oral consumption behaviors like drinking.", c: "The genital stage is the mature adult stage of psychosexual development, not associated with dependency fixation.", d: "Latency is a period of dormant sexuality with no libidinal fixation zone, so it does not fit oral-dependent behavior." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is the oral stage. In the psychosexual theory of Sigmund Freud, development proceeds through stages defined by the body zone that is the focus of libidinal energy: oral, anal, phallic, latency, and genital. The oral stage spans roughly the first year of life, when gratification centers on the mouth through sucking, biting, and feeding. Freud held that excessive gratification or frustration at a stage produces fixation, leaving part of the libido invested there and shaping adult character. An oral fixation was thought to manifest in adulthood through mouth-focused and dependency-related behaviors, including smoking, overeating, nail-biting, excessive talking, and drinking, hence the classical psychoanalytic linkage of alcohol dependence to oral-stage fixation. The oral character was further described as either orally receptive (dependent, needing nurturance) or orally aggressive (biting, sarcastic). While modern addiction science explains alcohol dependence through neurobiology, reinforcement, and genetics rather than psychosexual fixation, the exam item asks specifically for Freud's position, which ties oral consumption and dependency behaviors to a fixation at the oral stage." },
  { id: "rml22_028", section: "cognitive",
    q: "Which of the following secondary tasks is thought to affect the central executive in terms of working memory?",
    options: { a: "Continually repeating a word", b: "Remembering a spatial configuration", c: "Listening to irrelevant speech", d: "Random number generation" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Continually repeating a word (articulatory suppression) loads the phonological loop, not the central executive.", b: "Remembering a spatial configuration taxes the visuospatial sketchpad, a slave system, not the central executive.", c: "Listening to irrelevant speech intrudes on the phonological loop via the irrelevant speech effect, not the central executive." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is random number generation. The working memory model of Alan Baddeley and Graham Hitch proposes a central executive that controls attention and coordinates two subsidiary slave systems: the phonological loop, which holds and rehearses verbal and acoustic information, and the visuospatial sketchpad, which handles visual and spatial material (a later addition was the episodic buffer). To identify which task loads which component, researchers use interfering secondary tasks. Articulatory suppression, continually repeating a word, occupies the phonological loop; the irrelevant speech effect, listening to unattended speech, also disrupts the phonological loop; remembering a spatial pattern taxes the visuospatial sketchpad. Random number (or random letter) generation, by contrast, requires continuous attentional control: one must suppress habitual counting sequences, monitor recent outputs, and flexibly switch to avoid patterns, which are exactly the supervisory attention functions of the central executive. Baddeley used random generation precisely as a way to load and study the central executive, showing that it becomes less random when a person must simultaneously perform another demanding executive task. So among these options, random number generation is the task that specifically engages and impairs the central executive rather than a slave subsystem." },
  { id: "rml22_029", section: "psychopathology",
    q: "Schizophrenia is most commonly found in :",
    options: { a: "Adolescents", b: "Young Adults", c: "The Middle-Aged", d: "The Elderly" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Onset before the late teens is possible but comparatively uncommon; the peak is early adulthood, not adolescence.", c: "Middle-age onset is atypical and, when it occurs later in women, is a secondary peak rather than the most common period.", d: "Late-life onset is rare; schizophrenia characteristically emerges decades earlier." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is young adults. Schizophrenia is a severe psychotic disorder characterized by positive symptoms (hallucinations, delusions, disorganized speech), negative symptoms (flat affect, avolition, alogia), and cognitive impairment, described in modern nosology by the DSM-5 of the American Psychiatric Association and historically named by Eugen Bleuler, who coined schizophrenia, building on the dementia praecox concept of Emil Kraepelin. Its typical age of onset falls in late adolescence to early adulthood, most commonly the late teens to mid-twenties for men and roughly the mid-twenties to early thirties for women, with women showing a smaller second peak around midlife. Thus the disorder is most commonly found in young adults. The relatively early onset reflects the neurodevelopmental nature of the illness, in which subtle brain maturation abnormalities interact with the demands and stresses of the transition to adulthood, consistent with the diathesis-stress and dopamine hypotheses of schizophrenia. Onset in childhood or in the elderly does occur but is far less common. For the exam, the key fact is that the peak incidence and most typical presentation cluster in young adulthood." },
  { id: "rml22_030", section: "cognitive",
    q: "Taste aversion is an example of :",
    options: { a: "Classical Conditioning", b: "Second-order conditioning", c: "Operant conditioning", d: "Spurious association" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Second-order conditioning builds on an already-conditioned stimulus; taste aversion is a direct first-order association with illness.", c: "Operant conditioning involves consequences shaping voluntary behavior, whereas taste aversion is an involuntary learned association.", d: "The association is genuine and adaptive, not spurious or coincidental." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is classical conditioning. Taste aversion, also called the Garcia effect after psychologist John Garcia, is a form of Pavlovian (classical) conditioning first systematically demonstrated by Ivan Pavlov's paradigm and extended by Garcia and Robert Koelling. In classical conditioning a neutral stimulus becomes associated with a biologically significant one. In taste aversion, a novel taste (the conditioned stimulus) is paired with nausea or gastrointestinal illness (the unconditioned stimulus), so that the taste alone later elicits disgust and avoidance (the conditioned response). What makes it a landmark case is that it violates several assumptions once thought central to classical conditioning: it can form after a single trial (one-trial learning), it holds even when the illness follows the taste by hours rather than seconds (long delay learning), and it shows biological preparedness, since organisms readily associate taste with sickness but not lights or sounds with sickness. Garcia's findings challenged the equipotentiality premise of behaviorism and demonstrated that evolution biases which associations are easily learned. Despite these special features, taste aversion remains a stimulus-stimulus association, making it a clear instance of classical, not operant, conditioning." },
  { id: "rml22_031", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Ria has difficulty enunciating words correctly and speaks in a slow and laboured way; her speech often makes sense, but it includes only keywords. Which part of her brain is damaged?",
    options: { a: "Wernicke's area", b: "Broca's area", c: "Temporal lobe", d: "Frontal lobe" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Wernicke's area damage produces fluent but meaningless speech with poor comprehension, the opposite of this labored, meaningful pattern.", c: "The temporal lobe houses Wernicke's area, so damage there tends toward comprehension deficits, not effortful telegraphic output.", d: "Frontal lobe is too broad; the specific deficit points to Broca's area within the left frontal lobe." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Broca's area. Broca's area, located in the posterior part of the inferior frontal gyrus of the (usually left) frontal lobe, was identified by the French physician Paul Broca in the 1860s through his patient known as Tan, who could understand language but could barely produce speech. Damage to this region causes Broca's aphasia, also called expressive or non-fluent aphasia. Its hallmarks match Ria's presentation exactly: speech is slow, effortful, and poorly articulated, output is reduced to essential content or keywords (agrammatic, telegraphic speech that drops function words like articles and prepositions), yet comprehension is relatively preserved and what the patient does say tends to be meaningful. Patients are often painfully aware of their deficit, which distinguishes it from Wernicke's aphasia. Wernicke's area, in the superior temporal gyrus of the temporal lobe and described by Carl Wernicke, produces the opposite pattern when damaged: fluent, effortless, but empty or nonsensical speech with impaired comprehension. Because Ria's difficulty is in production, effortful and telegraphic but meaningful, the damage localizes to Broca's area, making it the precise answer over the broader frontal lobe option." },
  { id: "rml22_032", section: "general",
    q: "Creating equivalence among research participants is vital for:",
    options: { a: "Experimental Research", b: "Correlational Research", c: "Descriptive Research", d: "Equivalence is never vital" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Correlational research measures existing associations without assigning participants to conditions, so group equivalence is not its central concern.", c: "Descriptive research documents phenomena as they are and does not require equivalent comparison groups.", d: "Equivalence is clearly vital in at least experimental designs, so claiming it is never vital is false." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is experimental research. The logic of a true experiment rests on establishing that the groups being compared are equivalent before the independent variable is manipulated, so that any post-treatment difference can be attributed to the manipulation rather than to pre-existing differences among participants. This equivalence is achieved chiefly through random assignment, a principle emphasized by Ronald Fisher, which distributes both known and unknown confounding characteristics evenly across conditions on average. Without initial equivalence, a study cannot support causal inference because a confounding variable could explain the results, threatening internal validity, a concept detailed by Donald Campbell and Julian Stanley in their work on experimental and quasi-experimental designs. Techniques such as matching and counterbalancing further help create or preserve equivalence. Correlational research, by contrast, examines relationships among variables as they naturally occur without assigning participants to manipulated conditions, and descriptive research simply documents characteristics of a sample, so neither depends on constructing equivalent groups. Because the ability to draw causal conclusions is unique to experiments and hinges on comparable groups, creating equivalence among participants is vital specifically for experimental research." },
  { id: "rml22_033", section: "cognitive",
    q: "Alcohol-induced amnesia may be an example of :",
    options: { a: "Dependent memory", b: "State-dependent memory", c: "Independent memory", d: "State-independent memory" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Dependent memory is not a standard term and does not name the phenomenon of retrieval tied to internal state.", c: "Independent memory implies retrieval unaffected by context, the opposite of what alcohol-linked recall demonstrates.", d: "State-independent memory would mean the internal state does not matter, contradicting the state-dependent effect." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is state-dependent memory. State-dependent memory refers to the finding that retrieval is enhanced when a person's internal physiological or psychological state at recall matches their state during original encoding. It is a form of encoding specificity, the broader principle articulated by Endel Tulving and Donald Thomson that memory is best when retrieval cues reinstate the conditions of learning. With alcohol, information encoded while intoxicated is often recalled more easily when the person is again intoxicated than when sober, and vice versa; this is why a person may forget, while sober, where they placed something while drunk, then recall it upon drinking again. Early demonstrations of drug and alcohol state-dependent learning were reported by researchers including Donald Overton. This differs from context-dependent memory, where the match is in external environment (studied by Duncan Godden and Alan Baddeley in their diver experiments), and from mood-dependent memory, where the match is in emotional state, studied by Gordon Bower. Note this is distinct from an alcohol blackout, in which severe intoxication blocks the formation of new memories entirely. The item specifically describes retrieval governed by internal state, which is the definition of state-dependent memory." },
  { id: "rml22_034", section: "psychopathology",
    q: "Unlike people with anorexia, individuals with bulimia",
    options: { a: "Often purge themselves to stay thin", b: "Are obsessed with being too fat", c: "Can do damage to their health", d: "May appear normal in weight" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Purging occurs in the binge-purge subtype of anorexia too, so it does not distinguish bulimia from anorexia.", b: "Both disorders involve intense fear of fatness and body-image disturbance, so this is shared, not distinguishing.", c: "Both conditions cause serious health damage, so this feature is common to both rather than unique to bulimia." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is that individuals with bulimia may appear normal in weight. In the DSM-5 of the American Psychiatric Association, Anorexia Nervosa is defined partly by significantly low body weight relative to what is expected, resulting from restricted energy intake, so a hallmark is being underweight or emaciated. Bulimia Nervosa, by contrast, involves recurrent binge eating followed by compensatory behaviors such as self-induced vomiting, laxative misuse, fasting, or excessive exercise, but crucially it is not defined by low weight; most people with bulimia maintain a weight within or near the normal range, and some are overweight. This is precisely why bulimia is often harder to detect and can go unnoticed by others, the defining contrast the question targets. The other options are shared features rather than distinctions: purging occurs in the binge-eating and purging subtype of anorexia as well; intense fear of weight gain and body-image distortion characterize both disorders; and both can cause severe medical harm, including electrolyte imbalance, cardiac problems, and dental erosion in bulimia. Because normal-appearing weight specifically distinguishes bulimia from the underweight presentation of anorexia, option d is correct." },
  { id: "rml22_035", section: "development",
    q: "From _____________ point of view, pre-schoolers are unable to reason properly.",
    options: { a: "Piagetian", b: "Jenson's", c: "Holistic", d: "Guilford's" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Arthur Jensen focused on intelligence, heritability, and reaction time, not on preschool reasoning limitations.", c: "Holistic is not a specific theorist's viewpoint on preoperational reasoning; it is a general descriptor.", d: "J. P. Guilford proposed the structure-of-intellect model of intelligence, not a stage account of preschool reasoning failures." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Piagetian. In the cognitive developmental theory of Jean Piaget, children between roughly ages two and seven are in the preoperational stage. During this stage, thinking is symbolic and imaginative but still lacks logical operations, so from a strictly Piagetian standpoint pre-schoolers are unable to reason properly. Piaget documented several limitations: egocentrism, the inability to take another's perspective, shown in the three-mountains task; centration, focusing on one salient feature while ignoring others, which underlies failure at conservation tasks (not realizing that pouring liquid into a taller glass leaves the amount unchanged); irreversibility, the inability to mentally reverse an action; and difficulty with class inclusion and transitive reasoning. Logical, reversible mental operations only emerge, in Piaget's account, at the concrete operational stage from about age seven. It is worth noting that later researchers, including Renee Baillargeon and critics of Piaget, showed that preschoolers can reason more competently than Piaget's tasks suggested when the tasks are simplified, but the question asks specifically about the classic Piagetian view, which held that pre-schoolers cannot yet reason logically." },
  { id: "rml22_036", section: "cognitive",
    q: "The procedure called \"semantic priming\" is used to study which of the following phenomena?",
    options: { a: "Signal detection ability", b: "Subliminal perception", c: "Field dependence - independence", d: "Locus of control" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Signal detection studies use detection thresholds and d-prime, not semantic priming, to separate sensitivity from bias.", c: "Field dependence-independence is measured by embedded-figures and rod-and-frame tasks, not priming.", d: "Locus of control is a personality construct assessed by questionnaires such as Rotter's scale, unrelated to priming." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is subliminal perception. Semantic priming is a procedure in which exposure to a prime word facilitates faster or more accurate processing of a related target word (for example, the prime nurse speeds recognition of the target doctor), reflecting automatic spreading activation through an associative network of meaning, a mechanism theorized by Allan Collins and Elizabeth Loftus. Critically, semantic priming can occur even when the prime is presented subliminally, that is, so briefly or masked so that participants cannot consciously perceive or report it. If a masked, unseen prime still influences responses to the target, this demonstrates that the prime was processed to the level of meaning without conscious awareness, which is the very definition of subliminal perception. Researchers such as Anthony Marcel used masked semantic priming to provide some of the strongest laboratory evidence that stimuli people deny seeing can nonetheless activate semantic representations and shape behavior. Thus semantic priming is a key experimental tool for studying subliminal (unconscious) perception. The other options refer to different paradigms: signal detection to perceptual sensitivity and bias, field dependence to perceptual style, and locus of control to a personality trait, none of which are studied via semantic priming." },
  { id: "rml22_037", section: "psychopathology",
    q: "Autogynephilia is defined by :",
    options: { a: "Recurrent, and intense sexual attraction to prepubescent children", b: "Recurrent, and intense sexual attraction to thoughts, images, or thoughts about oneself as a woman", c: "Recurrent, and intense sexual attraction to oneself", d: "Recurrent, and intense sexual attraction to being humiliated" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Sexual attraction to prepubescent children defines pedophilic disorder, an entirely different paraphilia.", c: "Attraction to oneself in general is too vague; autogynephilia specifically involves the image of oneself as a woman.", d: "Attraction to being humiliated describes sexual masochism, not autogynephilia." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is recurrent, intense sexual attraction to thoughts, images, or thoughts about oneself as a woman. The term autogynephilia was coined by sexologist Ray Blanchard, combining Greek roots to mean love of oneself as a woman. Blanchard proposed it to describe a natal male's paraphilic tendency to be sexually aroused by the thought or image of himself as a female. It is one component of his controversial typology of gender dysphoria, in which he distinguished autogynephilic from androphilic (attracted to men) presentations. The concept remains debated and criticized within the transgender community and among many researchers, but for the purposes of a definitional exam item the key is that autogynephilia specifically denotes arousal centered on the image of oneself as a woman, not generic self-attraction. The distractor options each name distinct paraphilias catalogued in the DSM-5 of the American Psychiatric Association: attraction to prepubescent children is pedophilic disorder, and arousal from being humiliated or made to suffer is sexual masochism disorder. Option c, attraction to oneself, is deliberately too broad and omits the defining as a woman element. Thus option b captures the precise, technically correct definition." },
  { id: "rml22_038", section: "cognitive",
    q: "It has been found that the Muller-Lyer illusion exists in certain cultures in which there are :",
    options: { a: "Lot of trees", b: "Buildings with corners", c: "Structures without many corners and angles", d: "More men than women" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Forests with trees relate to the vegetation of non-carpentered environments, which show a weaker illusion, not a stronger one.", c: "People from environments lacking corners and angles are less susceptible, so this is the opposite of the answer.", d: "Sex ratio has no established role in susceptibility to this geometric illusion." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is buildings with corners. The Muller-Lyer illusion, devised by Franz Carl Muller-Lyer, is the classic figure in which two lines of equal length appear unequal because one has outward-pointing arrowheads (fins) and the other inward-pointing ones. A leading explanation is the carpentered world hypothesis, advanced by Marshall Segall, Donald Campbell, and Melville Herskovits in their cross-cultural research. They argued that people raised in carpentered environments, cities and towns full of rectangular buildings, straight edges, and right-angled corners, learn to interpret the arrow configurations as depth cues (like the near and far corners of a room or building), which biases their length judgments and makes them highly susceptible to the illusion. In contrast, people from non-carpentered environments with few straight lines and corners, such as certain rural or open-landscape cultures, showed markedly weaker susceptibility. This finding challenged the assumption that perception is purely innate and universal, demonstrating that ecological and cultural experience shapes even basic visual processing. Therefore the illusion is stronger in cultures whose environments feature buildings with corners and angles, making option b correct." },
  { id: "rml22_039", section: "development",
    q: "Formal operational reasoning involves all the following EXCEPT:",
    options: { a: "hypothetical reasoning", b: "counterfactual reasoning", c: "dialectical reasoning", d: "metacognitive reasoning" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Hypothetical-deductive reasoning is a defining feature of the formal operational stage, so it is included.", b: "Counterfactual reasoning about what could be, contrary to fact, is characteristic of formal operations.", d: "Metacognitive reasoning, thinking about one's own thinking, emerges in the formal operational stage." },
    explanation: "The correct answer (the EXCEPT) is dialectical reasoning. In Jean Piaget's theory, the formal operational stage begins around age eleven or twelve and represents the capacity for abstract, systematic, logical thought. Its hallmarks include hypothetical-deductive reasoning (generating and systematically testing hypotheses, as in the pendulum problem), counterfactual reasoning (reasoning about possibilities and states contrary to present fact), abstract and propositional thinking, and increased metacognition (the ability to reflect on and reason about one's own thought processes). Dialectical reasoning, however, is generally considered a form of postformal thought, said to develop, if at all, in adulthood beyond Piaget's final stage. Dialectical thinking involves recognizing that knowledge is relative and context-bound, tolerating contradiction, and integrating opposing ideas (thesis and antithesis) into a synthesis, an idea rooted in the philosophy of Georg Wilhelm Friedrich Hegel and elaborated in adult-development theories by researchers such as Klaus Riegel and Michael Basseches. Because dialectical reasoning belongs to postformal rather than formal operational cognition, it is the element that does not fit Piaget's formal operational stage, making it the correct exception among the four options." },
  { id: "rml22_040", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Which of the following class of medicines is known to cause the side effect of tardive dyskinesia?",
    options: { a: "Antidepressants", b: "Anxiolytics", c: "Neuroleptics", d: "Barbiturates" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Antidepressants can cause various side effects but do not characteristically produce tardive dyskinesia.", b: "Anxiolytics such as benzodiazepines cause sedation and dependence, not tardive dyskinesia.", d: "Barbiturates depress the central nervous system and risk dependence, but are not linked to tardive dyskinesia." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is neuroleptics. Neuroleptics, also called antipsychotics, are used to treat schizophrenia and other psychotic disorders primarily by blocking dopamine D2 receptors. Tardive dyskinesia is a serious, often irreversible movement disorder that arises from long-term (tardive means late-appearing) use of these drugs, especially the older first-generation or typical antipsychotics such as haloperidol and chlorpromazine, the latter introduced into psychiatry by Jean Delay and Pierre Deniker. It is characterized by repetitive, involuntary movements, classically of the face and mouth (lip-smacking, tongue protrusion, grimacing, chewing), and sometimes of the limbs and trunk. The leading explanation is the dopamine supersensitivity hypothesis: chronic D2 blockade leads to up-regulation and hypersensitivity of dopamine receptors in the nigrostriatal pathway, producing the abnormal movements. Because dopamine antagonism in the motor pathways underlies both antipsychotic efficacy and this side effect, tardive dyskinesia is a hallmark risk specific to the neuroleptic class, and second-generation atypical antipsychotics carry lower but still real risk. Antidepressants, anxiolytics, and barbiturates work through different mechanisms and are not associated with tardive dyskinesia, making neuroleptics the correct answer." },
  { id: "rml22_041", section: "biopsych",
    q: "During a normal night's sleep, how many times do we pass through the different stages of sleep?",
    options: { a: "8-11", b: "5-8", c: "3-5", d: "4-7" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "8 to 11 cycles far exceeds what occurs in a typical night given cycles lasting about 90 minutes.", b: "5 to 8 cycles overshoots the usual count; a normal night yields roughly four to six.", c: "3 to 5 slightly undershoots the commonly cited range that extends up to six or seven." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is 4 to 7. Sleep is organized into cycles, each lasting roughly 90 to 110 minutes, that repeat through the night. Within each cycle a person moves through the stages of non-REM sleep (in current American Academy of Sleep Medicine terms, N1 light sleep, N2, and N3 slow-wave or deep sleep) and then a period of REM (rapid eye movement) sleep, the stage most associated with vivid dreaming, discovered by Eugene Aserinsky and Nathaniel Kleitman in 1953. Because a typical adult sleeps about seven to nine hours and each cycle lasts around 90 minutes, a normal night contains roughly four to six complete cycles, and depending on total sleep duration and cycle length this passing-through of the sleep stages can range up to about seven times, which is why the 4 to 7 option is accepted as correct. Importantly, the composition of cycles shifts across the night: deep slow-wave sleep dominates the earlier cycles, while REM periods lengthen toward morning. Understanding this cyclic architecture, measured via electroencephalography, is fundamental to sleep science, and the commonly taught figure for the number of cycles per night falls within this four-to-seven range." },
  { id: "rml22_042", section: "social",
    q: "The phenomenon of \"gregariousness\" has a :",
    options: { a: "Social motive", b: "Biological motive", c: "Psychological motive", d: "Personal motive" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "While affiliation may have evolutionary roots, gregariousness is classified as a social rather than a purely biological drive.", c: "Psychological motive is too broad and does not capture the specifically interpersonal, group-oriented nature of gregariousness.", d: "Personal motive implies an individual, self-directed drive, the opposite of the group-seeking tendency of gregariousness." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is a social motive. Gregariousness is the tendency to seek out the company of others, to associate in groups, and to enjoy being among people rather than alone. In the standard taxonomy of human motives, motives are commonly divided into biological (physiological) motives such as hunger, thirst, and sleep, which serve bodily survival, and social (or secondary, learned) motives such as affiliation, achievement, power, and approval, which arise through social interaction and learning. Gregariousness is the drive to affiliate and belong, so it is classified as a social motive, closely related to the need for affiliation studied by Henry Murray and David McClelland and to the fundamental need to belong articulated by Roy Baumeister and Mark Leary. It reflects the human tendency to form and maintain interpersonal bonds, which has adaptive value because group living provided protection, cooperation, and mating opportunities. Although one can argue affiliation has biological and evolutionary underpinnings, within the conventional classification used in psychology examinations gregariousness is grouped with the social motives because its object is other people and group membership, making option a the intended answer." },
  { id: "rml22_043", section: "development",
    q: "In Erikson's view, adolescence is:",
    options: { a: "a moratorium", b: "not a true developmental stage", c: "the last stage of human development", d: "not a moratorium" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Erikson explicitly treated adolescence as a genuine and pivotal psychosocial stage, so this contradicts his theory.", c: "Adolescence is the fifth of eight stages, not the last; the final stage is late adulthood.", d: "Erikson characterized adolescence precisely as a psychosocial moratorium, so denying this is incorrect." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is a moratorium. Erik Erikson proposed an eight-stage theory of psychosocial development, in which each stage centers on a core conflict. Adolescence, the fifth stage, is defined by the crisis of identity versus role confusion, during which the young person must integrate various roles, values, and self-perceptions into a coherent sense of identity. Erikson described adolescence as a psychosocial moratorium: a socially sanctioned period of delay during which society grants the adolescent freedom to experiment with different roles, ideologies, and identities without yet committing to the full responsibilities of adulthood. This exploratory pause allows the person to try out possibilities before consolidating a stable identity. James Marcia later operationalized Erikson's ideas into four identity statuses (identity diffusion, foreclosure, moratorium, and achievement), where moratorium specifically denotes active exploration without commitment. Failure to resolve the identity crisis results in role confusion, an unclear sense of self. Adolescence is neither the final stage (later stages cover young, middle, and late adulthood) nor a non-stage; it is a central, genuine stage best characterized in Erikson's framework as a moratorium, making option a correct." },
  { id: "rml22_044", section: "psychopathology",
    q: "When an individual has dissociative amnesia it is usually associated with several types of memory disturbances. In contrast, in generalized amnesia :",
    options: { a: "There is a failure of recall of the entire past week", b: "There is a failure of recall that encompasses the person's entire life", c: "There is a failure of recall that encompasses the person's childhood", d: "There is a failure of recall that for traumatic events" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Loss limited to the past week is a circumscribed or localized amnesia, not the total loss defining generalized amnesia.", c: "Loss confined to childhood is selective or partial, not the complete life-span loss of generalized amnesia.", d: "Failure limited to traumatic events describes localized or selective amnesia tied to specific incidents, not generalized amnesia." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is that there is a failure of recall that encompasses the person's entire life. Dissociative amnesia, described in the DSM-5 of the American Psychiatric Association, is an inability to recall important autobiographical information, usually of a traumatic or stressful nature, that is too extensive to be ordinary forgetting and is not due to a substance or neurological condition. It takes several forms distinguished by the scope of memory loss. Localized amnesia is failure to recall events during a specific circumscribed period (for example, the hours around a trauma); selective amnesia is failure to recall some but not all events of a period; and systematized amnesia targets a specific category of information. Generalized amnesia is the rarest and most severe form: it involves a complete loss of memory for one's entire life history, including personal identity, autobiographical events, and sometimes previously learned skills and knowledge. Because it encompasses the person's whole life rather than a limited window, option b is correct. The other options describe the more circumscribed subtypes (localized for a week, selective or partial for childhood or for traumatic events only), which is precisely the contrast the question draws with the total sweep of generalized amnesia." },
  { id: "rml22_045", section: "psychopathology",
    q: "A man who yells at his dog when he's upset with his girlfriend is experiencing _____________.",
    options: { a: "Denial", b: "Displacement", c: "Sublimation", d: "Repression" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Denial is refusing to accept a painful reality, not redirecting an emotion onto a safer target.", c: "Sublimation channels unacceptable impulses into socially valued activities, not into yelling at a pet.", d: "Repression pushes distressing material out of awareness rather than redirecting the anger toward a substitute object." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is displacement. Displacement is a defense mechanism, in the tradition of Sigmund Freud and systematized by Anna Freud, in which an emotional impulse, usually aggression, is redirected from a threatening or unavailable target onto a safer, less threatening substitute. Here the man is angry with his girlfriend, but because expressing that anger directly may feel dangerous to the relationship, he unconsciously transfers the emotion onto the dog, a harmless and available target. The original feeling is preserved and discharged, but its object is shifted. This is the textbook illustration of displacement, often exemplified by the person who is criticized by a boss and then comes home and snaps at family members (the kick the dog scenario). It differs from the other defenses listed: denial refuses to acknowledge a painful reality; sublimation channels unacceptable impulses into socially constructive outlets such as art, sport, or work; and repression banishes distressing thoughts and feelings from conscious awareness entirely. Because the man's anger keeps its aggressive quality but is aimed at a substitute object rather than being transformed, hidden, or denied, the mechanism at work is specifically displacement, making option b correct." },
  { id: "rml22_046", section: "development",
    q: "According to Vygotsky :",
    options: { a: "Intra-subjectivity precedes inter-subjectivity", b: "Children do not engage in inter-subjectivity", c: "Inter-subjectivity and intra-subjectivity are related", d: "Inter-subjectivity precedes intra-subjectivity" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "This reverses Vygotsky's core claim; for him the shared, social process comes before the internal one.", b: "Vygotsky held that children are deeply engaged in intersubjectivity through social interaction, so this is false.", c: "While the two are related, the question tests Vygotsky's specific sequencing claim, which is that the social precedes the individual." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is that inter-subjectivity precedes intra-subjectivity. This reflects the central principle of Lev Vygotsky's sociocultural theory, often called the general genetic law of cultural development: every higher mental function appears twice, first on the social (interpsychological) plane between people, and only later on the individual (intrapsychological) plane within the child. In other words, cognition originates in shared social interaction and is subsequently internalized. Intersubjectivity refers to the shared understanding, common focus, and mutual coordination that arise between a child and a more knowledgeable partner (a caregiver or teacher) during joint activity, for example when adult and child establish a shared reference point in the zone of proximal development. Through participation in such intersubjective exchanges, mediated by language and other cultural tools, the child gradually internalizes the meanings and thought processes, converting shared social understanding into individual (intrasubjective) mental functioning. Thus the interpersonal comes first and the intrapersonal is derived from it, the opposite of a view that would place the individual mind before the social. This social-to-individual direction of development is the hallmark of Vygotsky's account, making option d correct." },
  { id: "rml22_047", section: "psychopathology",
    q: "Which one of the following characterises somnambulism?",
    options: { a: "Sitting, walking or performing complex behaviour while asleep", b: "Extreme fear, agitation, screaming while asleep", c: "Acting out nightmares", d: "Uncomfortable sensations in the legs causing movement and loss of sleep" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Extreme fear and screaming during sleep describes sleep terrors (night terrors), a different NREM parasomnia.", c: "Acting out dreams describes REM sleep behavior disorder, not sleepwalking.", d: "Uncomfortable leg sensations prompting movement describe restless legs syndrome, a sleep-related movement disorder." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is sitting, walking, or performing complex behaviour while asleep. Somnambulism, commonly called sleepwalking, is a non-REM parasomnia classified in the DSM-5 of the American Psychiatric Association under Non-Rapid Eye Movement Sleep Arousal Disorders. It involves repeated episodes of rising from bed and walking about, or carrying out other complex motor behaviors, while still asleep. Episodes typically arise out of deep slow-wave (N3) sleep during the first third of the night, when this stage predominates. The sleepwalker usually has a blank, staring face, is relatively unresponsive to others, is difficult to awaken, and characteristically has amnesia for the episode afterward. Because it emerges from deep NREM sleep rather than REM, it is not the acting out of dreams. The distractors correspond to distinct sleep disorders: extreme fear, screaming, and autonomic arousal during sleep with no dream recall describe sleep (night) terrors, another NREM arousal disorder; acting out vivid, often violent dreams describes REM sleep behavior disorder, in which the normal muscle atonia of REM fails; and uncomfortable leg sensations relieved by movement describe restless legs syndrome. Only option a, complex motor behavior performed while asleep, defines somnambulism." },
  { id: "rml22_048", section: "psychopathology",
    q: "Under which type of disorder would you place Munchausen syndrome?",
    options: { a: "Somatoform disorder", b: "Anxiety disorder", c: "Sexual disorder", d: "Factitious disorder" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "In somatoform (now somatic symptom) disorders the physical symptoms are genuinely experienced and not deliberately produced, unlike the intentional feigning in Munchausen.", b: "Anxiety disorders center on pathological fear and worry, not the deliberate fabrication of illness.", c: "Sexual disorders concern sexual functioning or paraphilias, wholly unrelated to feigning medical illness." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is factitious disorder. Munchausen syndrome is the classic and most severe form of factitious disorder imposed on self, in which a person deliberately fabricates, exaggerates, or induces physical or psychological symptoms in themselves in order to assume the sick role and receive medical attention, care, and sympathy. The term was coined by Richard Asher in 1951, naming it after Baron von Munchausen, a figure famous for telling exaggerated tales. What distinguishes factitious disorder is the intentional production of symptoms combined with the absence of obvious external rewards: the motivation is the psychological gain of being seen and treated as ill, not tangible incentives. This separates it from malingering, in which symptoms are also faked but for clear external gain such as money, drugs, or avoiding work or legal consequences, and from somatic symptom disorder (formerly somatoform), in which distressing physical symptoms are genuinely experienced and not consciously fabricated. In the DSM-5 of the American Psychiatric Association, factitious disorder is grouped with the Somatic Symptom and Related Disorders. Because the defining feature of Munchausen syndrome is the deliberate, intentional feigning of illness to occupy the sick role, it is correctly classified as a factitious disorder." },
  { id: "rml22_049", section: "cognitive",
    q: "Being unable to recall the relevant elements of an autobiographical experience from memory is known as a deficit in:",
    options: { a: "Reality-monitoring ability", b: "Personal-monitoring ability", c: "Social-monitoring ability", d: "Source-monitoring ability" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Reality monitoring specifically distinguishes internally generated (imagined) from externally derived memories, a narrower subtype rather than the broad ability to recall an experience's contextual elements.", b: "Personal-monitoring ability is not an established memory construct and functions here as a distractor.", c: "Social-monitoring ability is not a recognized memory term for retrieving autobiographical detail." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is source-monitoring ability. Source monitoring, a framework developed by Marcia Johnson and colleagues, is the set of cognitive processes by which people attribute their memories to their correct origins, that is, they determine when, where, and how a remembered event or piece of information was acquired. Recalling an autobiographical experience is not just retrieving content; it requires binding that content to its contextual source, including the surrounding details, perceptual features, and circumstances of the original event. When a person can retrieve fragments of information but cannot correctly recall or attribute the relevant contextual elements of the autobiographical experience, this reflects a deficit in source monitoring, and source-monitoring errors underlie phenomena such as misattributed memories, false memories, and confusion about whether something was experienced, imagined, or heard from someone else. Reality monitoring, described within the same framework, is a specific subset of source monitoring concerned with distinguishing internally generated (imagined or thought) memories from externally derived (actually perceived) ones. Because the question refers broadly to failing to recall the relevant elements of an autobiographical experience, tied to its source and context, the answer is the broader source-monitoring ability rather than the narrower reality-monitoring subtype or the non-standard distractor terms." },
  { id: "rml22_050", section: "development",
    q: "Which of the following best characterizes constructive play?",
    options: { a: "Play that involves interactions with peers", b: "Play that combines sensorimotor or practice play with a symbolic representation of ideas", c: "Play that occurs when a child transforms the physical environment into a symbol", d: "Play involving behaviour allowing derivation of pleasure from exercising sensorimotor schemes" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Play involving peer interaction describes social play, a category based on social participation, not constructive play.", c: "Transforming the environment into a symbol describes symbolic or pretend (make-believe) play, not constructive play.", d: "Deriving pleasure from exercising sensorimotor schemes describes practice or functional play, the earliest form, not constructive play." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is play that combines sensorimotor or practice play with a symbolic representation of ideas. In developmental accounts of play, drawing on Jean Piaget and elaborated by researchers such as Catherine Garvey and Greta Fein, several types are distinguished. Practice (functional or sensorimotor) play is the earliest, in which infants and toddlers derive pleasure from repeatedly exercising sensorimotor schemes, such as shaking a rattle or splashing water. Symbolic or pretend play involves make-believe, where a child uses one object to stand for another or enacts imagined roles, transforming the physical environment into symbols. Constructive play is an intermediate and integrative form that emerges as children combine sensorimotor activity with symbolic representation, using materials to create or build something that represents an idea, for example building a tower with blocks, drawing a picture, or making a model. It marries the hands-on manipulation of practice play with the meaning-making of symbolic play, which is exactly what option b describes. Social play, by contrast, is categorized by degree of peer interaction (following Mildred Parten's stages of social participation) rather than by content. Because constructive play is defined by integrating sensorimotor construction with symbolic meaning, option b is the best characterization." },
  { id: "rml22_051", section: "therapy",
    q: "Which of the following technique or techniques would not typically be included within the repertoire of an Adlerian counsellor?",
    options: { a: "Acting \"as if\"", b: "Push button", c: "Encouragement", d: "Analysis of transference" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Acting 'as if', having clients behave as though they were the person they wish to be, is a classic Adlerian technique.", b: "The push-button technique, in which clients generate pleasant and unpleasant images to see they can control feelings, is Adlerian.", c: "Encouragement is central to Adlerian counselling, aimed at building courage and social interest." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is analysis of transference. Adlerian counselling, derived from the Individual Psychology of Alfred Adler, emphasizes holism, social interest (Gemeinschaftsgefuhl), lifestyle assessment, birth-order and family constellation, striving for superiority, and encouragement, working in a collaborative, egalitarian, present-and-future-oriented way. Its distinctive techniques include acting as if (encouraging clients to behave as though they were already the capable person they wish to become), the push-button technique (having clients alternately generate pleasant and unpleasant memories to demonstrate that they can control their emotional states), catching oneself, spitting in the client's soup, paradoxical intention, and above all encouragement, which Adlerians regard as the key to counteracting discouragement and inferiority feelings. Analysis of transference, by contrast, is a hallmark of classical psychoanalysis developed by Sigmund Freud, in which the client's unconscious redirection of feelings about significant early figures onto the analyst is systematically interpreted. Adler broke from Freud and de-emphasized the unconscious, drive theory, and the intensive analysis of transference in favor of conscious goals, social embeddedness, and encouragement. Therefore, transference analysis is the technique that does not belong in the Adlerian repertoire, making option d correct." },
  { id: "rml22_052", section: "assessment",
    q: "How many Rorschach cards are coloured?",
    options: { a: "10", b: "5", c: "3", d: "4" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "10 is the total number of Rorschach cards, not the number that are coloured.", c: "3 corresponds to the fully multicoloured cards only, omitting the two black-red cards.", d: "4 is not a correct count of the coloured cards in the standard set." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is 5. The Rorschach Inkblot Test, created by the Swiss psychiatrist Hermann Rorschach and published in 1921 in his work Psychodiagnostik, is a projective personality assessment consisting of ten standardized inkblot cards shown one at a time, with the examinee asked what each might be. Of the ten cards, five are achromatic (black and grey only), and five contain colour. Among the coloured cards, two (Cards II and III) combine black with red, and three (Cards VIII, IX, and X) are fully multicoloured. So the count of cards containing any colour is five. This distinction matters interpretively: in scoring systems such as the Comprehensive System of John Exner, responses to the chromatic cards, especially colour-based determinants, are linked to how a person processes and modulates emotion and affect, whereas responses to the achromatic cards and shading relate to other dimensions such as dysphoria and anxiety. Test-takers sometimes confuse this with the total of ten cards or with the three fully coloured cards, but the precise answer to how many cards are coloured is five, making option b correct." },
  { id: "rml22_053", section: "general",
    q: "The question \"How often and how much time do you exercise?\" asked in a research survey is an example of :",
    options: { a: "Double barrel question", b: "Ambiguous question", c: "Open-ended question", d: "Close-ended question" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "The question is not merely vague; its specific flaw is asking two things at once, which is the double-barrelled defect.", c: "Whether it is open or closed is not the point; the identifying feature is that it bundles two questions together.", d: "As with open-ended, response format is not the issue; the defining flaw is the two-in-one structure." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is a double-barrelled question. A double-barrelled (or double-barrel) question is a common flaw in survey and questionnaire design in which a single item asks about two different things at once while allowing only one answer. Here, How often and how much time do you exercise? actually poses two separate questions: frequency of exercise (how often) and duration per session or total (how much time). A respondent who exercises very frequently but for short durations, or rarely but for long durations, cannot answer accurately with a single response, so the data become uninterpretable. Good item construction, as emphasized in survey methodology by researchers such as Stanley Payne and Norman Bradburn, requires that each question address only one idea; a double-barrelled item should be split into two distinct questions. This is distinct from an ambiguous question (unclear wording open to multiple interpretations), an open-ended question (which invites a free-form response), and a closed-ended question (which offers fixed response options). Because the defining defect here is bundling two separate inquiries, frequency and duration, into one item, it is best classified as a double-barrelled question, making option a correct." },
  { id: "rml22_054", section: "psychopathology",
    q: "Who gave the concept of First Rank Symptoms?",
    options: { a: "Emil Kraepelin", b: "Kurt Schneider", c: "Gregory Bateson", d: "Wayne" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Emil Kraepelin classified dementia praecox but did not formulate the first-rank symptoms of schizophrenia.", c: "Gregory Bateson proposed the double-bind theory of schizophrenia, not first-rank symptoms.", d: "Wayne is not associated with first-rank symptoms and serves as a distractor." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Kurt Schneider. The German psychiatrist Kurt Schneider proposed, in the mid-twentieth century, a list of First Rank Symptoms (FRS) of schizophrenia, sometimes called Schneiderian symptoms. He argued that, in the absence of organic brain disease, the presence of any one of these symptoms strongly suggested a diagnosis of schizophrenia, giving clinicians practical, high-priority indicators. The classic first-rank symptoms include: audible thoughts (hearing one's thoughts spoken aloud), voices arguing or discussing the patient in the third person, voices commenting on the patient's actions (running commentary), thought withdrawal, thought insertion, and thought broadcasting (experiences of the mind being controlled or exposed), somatic passivity, made feelings, made impulses, and made volitional acts (delusions of control), and delusional perception (attaching an abnormal delusional meaning to a normally perceived object). Although later research showed that first-rank symptoms are not fully specific to schizophrenia (they can occur in mood and other psychotic disorders) and the DSM-5 no longer grants them special weight, Schneider's framework was historically influential in operationalizing psychotic diagnosis. Kraepelin is credited with the concept of dementia praecox and Bateson with the double-bind theory, so the concept of first-rank symptoms belongs specifically to Kurt Schneider." },
  { id: "rml22_055", section: "assessment",
    q: "Thematic Apperception Test (TAT) is based on which technique of psychological testing?",
    options: { a: "Expressive technique", b: "Construction technique", c: "Association technique", d: "Completion technique" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Expressive techniques involve free activity like drawing or play (for example the Draw-a-Person test), not story construction.", c: "Association techniques ask for the first response to a stimulus, as in the Rorschach or word association, not narrative building.", d: "Completion techniques ask the subject to finish incomplete material like sentences, not to construct a full story." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is construction technique. Projective tests are commonly grouped by the type of response task they require, a classification associated with Lawrence Frank, who coined the term projective and with Bruno Klopfer and others. The main categories are association techniques (respond with the first associations to a stimulus, as in the Rorschach Inkblot Test of Hermann Rorschach and word-association tests), construction techniques (build or create a product such as a story from stimulus material), completion techniques (finish incomplete stimuli, as in sentence-completion tests), choice or ordering techniques (arrange or select items), and expressive techniques (express the self through free activity such as drawing, painting, or play, as in the Draw-a-Person test and play therapy). The Thematic Apperception Test, developed by Henry Murray and Christiana Morgan at Harvard, presents ambiguous pictures and asks the examinee to construct a full story about each: what is happening, what led up to it, what the characters are thinking and feeling, and how it will end. Because the subject must build a complete narrative from the ambiguous scene, the TAT is a construction technique, making option b correct. It is interpreted in terms of Murray's needs and presses and the projected themes revealing the examinee's motives and conflicts." },
  { id: "rml22_056", section: "social",
    q: "According to Cialdini, techniques for gaining compliance rest to a large extent on six basic principles. These do NOT include:",
    options: { a: "Scarcity", b: "Social validation", c: "Friendship/liking", d: "Reward" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Scarcity, the sense that opportunities are more valuable when limited, is one of Cialdini's six principles.", b: "Social validation (social proof), following what others do, is one of the six principles.", c: "Friendship/liking, complying more with those we like, is one of the six principles." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is reward. The social psychologist Robert Cialdini, in his influential work on persuasion and compliance, identified six basic principles that underlie most compliance-gaining techniques: reciprocity (feeling obligated to return favors), commitment and consistency (aligning behavior with prior commitments, exploited by the foot-in-the-door technique), social validation or social proof (looking to others' behavior to guide our own), liking or friendship (complying more readily with people we like or find attractive), authority (deferring to legitimate or expert authority figures), and scarcity (valuing things more when they are rare or dwindling in availability). Reward, while central to reinforcement theory and operant conditioning in the tradition of B. F. Skinner, is not one of Cialdini's six compliance principles as such. Although reciprocity involves an exchange of benefits, Cialdini's framework is about the psychological triggers of social influence rather than the simple delivery of rewards. Therefore, among the options, the one that is not one of Cialdini's six principles is reward, making option d correct. Recognizing these six principles is useful both for understanding persuasion and for resisting manipulative sales and marketing tactics." },
  { id: "rml22_057", section: "therapy",
    q: "Which of the following is NOT included as one of the fundamental concepts in cognitive therapy?",
    options: { a: "Collaborative empiricism", b: "Socratic dialogue", c: "Free association", d: "Guided discovery" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Collaborative empiricism, therapist and client jointly testing beliefs as hypotheses, is a core cognitive therapy concept.", b: "Socratic dialogue (questioning) is a defining method of cognitive therapy.", d: "Guided discovery, leading clients to discover new perspectives through questioning, is fundamental to cognitive therapy." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is free association. Cognitive therapy, developed by Aaron Beck, rests on the idea that distorted or maladaptive thinking (automatic thoughts, cognitive distortions, and dysfunctional core beliefs or schemas) maintains emotional disorders, and that changing these cognitions relieves distress. Three fundamental, interrelated concepts define its method. Collaborative empiricism means therapist and client work together as a team, treating the client's beliefs as hypotheses to be examined against evidence rather than as facts to be accepted or dismissed. Socratic dialogue (or Socratic questioning) is the therapist's use of a systematic series of questions to help the client examine the logic and evidence behind their thoughts and reach new conclusions themselves. Guided discovery is the broader process by which, through such questioning and behavioral experiments, the client is led to discover alternative interpretations and previously overlooked information. Free association, by contrast, is a core technique of classical psychoanalysis introduced by Sigmund Freud, in which the client says whatever comes to mind without censorship to uncover unconscious material. It belongs to the psychodynamic, not the cognitive, tradition. Hence free association is the concept that is not part of cognitive therapy, making option c correct." },
  { id: "rml22_058", section: "assessment",
    q: "Which of the following statements is TRUE with respect to Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory (MMPI)?",
    options: { a: "It utilizes rational method of test construction", b: "It is constructed by assuming specific personality traits and formulating questions to answer them", c: "It is derived by finding items that discriminate between criterion and control groups", d: "It is true with respect to both a and b" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "The MMPI used the empirical, not the rational, method of construction, so this is false.", b: "Assuming traits in advance and writing items to fit them is the rational (deductive) approach, which the MMPI deliberately rejected.", d: "Since both a and b describe the rational method the MMPI did not use, this combined option is also false." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is that the MMPI is derived by finding items that discriminate between criterion and control groups. The Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory was developed by Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley in the early 1940s and is the classic example of the empirical (criterion-keying) method of test construction. Rather than assuming in advance which items should measure a trait (the rational or deductive approach) or grouping items by statistical clustering (the factor-analytic approach used by Raymond Cattell for the 16PF), the empirical method selects items purely on the basis of whether they empirically distinguish a criterion group (for example, patients diagnosed with depression) from a normal control group. An item is retained for a scale if the two groups answer it differently, regardless of whether its content seems face-relevant. This is why some MMPI items appear unrelated to the construct they help measure. The approach yields clinical scales (such as Depression, Hypochondriasis, Psychopathic Deviate, Schizophrenia, Paranoia) plus validity scales (L, F, K) that detect response distortion. Because options a and b both describe the rational method that the MMPI did not use, the only true statement is that it discriminates between criterion and control groups, making option c correct." },
  { id: "rml22_059", section: "stats",
    q: "As variability due to chance decreases, the value of F will :",
    options: { a: "Increase", b: "Decrease", c: "Stay the same", d: "Information is not sufficient to comment on this" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "F decreases only if the denominator (error variance) grows relative to the numerator; less chance variability does the opposite.", c: "F depends on the ratio of systematic to error variance, so reducing error variance changes it rather than leaving it fixed.", d: "The relationship is determinate: the F formula shows how error variance in the denominator affects F." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is that F will increase. The F ratio in analysis of variance, developed by Ronald Fisher, is defined as the ratio of the variance between groups (mean square between, reflecting treatment effect plus error) to the variance within groups (mean square within, or error variance, reflecting chance or unexplained variability). Symbolically, F equals systematic variance divided by error (chance) variance. Variability due to chance is precisely the denominator of this ratio, the within-groups error variance. When the denominator of a fraction gets smaller while the numerator stays the same, the value of the fraction gets larger. Therefore, as variability due to chance (error variance) decreases, the F ratio increases. A larger F indicates that the differences between group means are large relative to the random variability within groups, making it more likely that the result is statistically significant and that the null hypothesis of equal means can be rejected. This is also why researchers try to reduce error variance through good design, control, and larger samples: doing so increases statistical power by raising the expected F value. Because reducing chance variability shrinks the denominator, F rises, making option a correct." },
  { id: "rml22_060", section: "social",
    q: "Who reformulated the \"frustration-aggression hypothesis\"?",
    options: { a: "Dollard", b: "Miller", c: "Berkowitz", d: "Lazarus" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "John Dollard was among the original proposers of the frustration-aggression hypothesis, not its reformulator.", b: "Neal Miller was a co-author of the original hypothesis and later softened it, but the major reformulation is credited to Berkowitz.", d: "Richard Lazarus is known for his cognitive appraisal theory of stress and emotion, not for this reformulation." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Berkowitz. The original frustration-aggression hypothesis was put forward in 1939 by a Yale group, John Dollard, Neal Miller, Leonard Doob, Orval Mowrer, and Robert Sears, who proposed a strong and direct link: frustration (blocking of goal-directed behavior) always leads to some form of aggression, and aggression is always the result of frustration. This absolute formulation proved too rigid, since frustration does not always produce aggression and aggression can arise without frustration. Leonard Berkowitz reformulated the hypothesis in a more nuanced, cognitive-associationist way. He argued that frustration produces a readiness for aggression by generating negative affect (anger), but whether aggression actually occurs depends on the presence of situational cues, particularly aggressive cues in the environment. His famous cue-arousal or weapons-effect research showed that the mere presence of a weapon could increase aggressive responses. Berkowitz thus shifted the model from an inevitable frustration-to-aggression chain to one in which negative emotion plus aggression-eliciting cues jointly determine aggressive behavior, integrating cognition and environmental context. Because the significant reformulation of the frustration-aggression hypothesis is credited to Leonard Berkowitz, option c is correct." },
  { id: "rml22_061", section: "assessment",
    q: "Vineland Social Maturity Scale (VSMS) is a measure of :",
    options: { a: "Mental age and Intelligence quotient", b: "Social age and social quotient", c: "Developmental age and developmental quotient", d: "Emotional age and emotional quotient" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Mental age and IQ are yielded by intelligence tests like the Stanford-Binet, not by the VSMS.", c: "Developmental age and quotient relate to broad developmental scales, not the specifically social-competence focus of the VSMS.", d: "Emotional age and quotient are not what the VSMS measures; it assesses social maturity and adaptive behavior." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is social age and social quotient. The Vineland Social Maturity Scale (VSMS) was originally developed by Edgar Arnold Doll at the Vineland Training School to assess social competence and adaptive behavior, that is, a person's ability to look after themselves and participate in social life across domains such as self-help, self-direction, occupation, communication, locomotion, and socialization. The scale yields a Social Age (SA), analogous to mental age but for social functioning, based on the level of social skills the individual has attained. From the social age and the person's chronological age, a Social Quotient (SQ) is computed, paralleling the intelligence quotient: SQ equals social age divided by chronological age, multiplied by 100. The VSMS is widely used, including an Indian adaptation by A. J. Malin, in the assessment of intellectual disability, because adaptive behavior deficits are part of its diagnostic criteria alongside subaverage intelligence. It provides information about real-world functioning that an IQ score alone does not. Because the scale specifically produces a social age and a social quotient reflecting social maturity, option b is correct, distinguishing it from intelligence tests that yield mental age and IQ." },
  { id: "rml22_062", section: "therapy",
    q: "Behaviour analysis is based upon the principles of:",
    options: { a: "Classical conditioning", b: "Operant conditioning", c: "Dream analysis", d: "Modelling" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Classical conditioning underlies techniques like systematic desensitization, but behaviour analysis proper rests on operant principles.", c: "Dream analysis is a psychoanalytic method, wholly unrelated to behaviour analysis.", d: "Modelling derives from social learning theory, a related but distinct framework from operant behaviour analysis." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is operant conditioning. Behaviour analysis, and specifically Applied Behaviour Analysis (ABA), is grounded in the principles of operant conditioning formulated by B. F. Skinner, building on the earlier law of effect of Edward Thorndike. Operant conditioning holds that behaviour is a function of its consequences: behaviours followed by reinforcement become more frequent, while behaviours followed by punishment or non-reinforcement decline. The core method of behaviour analysis is functional analysis, examining the antecedents, behaviours, and consequences (the ABC model) to understand what maintains a behaviour and then rearranging those contingencies to change it. Techniques such as positive and negative reinforcement, punishment, extinction, shaping, chaining, prompting, and token economies all flow from operant principles and are applied in settings ranging from autism intervention to classroom and organizational management. This distinguishes behaviour analysis from approaches based on classical (Pavlovian) conditioning, such as systematic desensitization, which target reflexive, respondent behaviour, and from social learning or modelling introduced by Albert Bandura, which emphasize observational learning. Because behaviour analysis fundamentally studies and modifies the relationship between behaviour and its consequences, its foundational principles are those of operant conditioning, making option b correct." },
  { id: "rml22_063", section: "assessment",
    q: "Who developed the House-tree-person test?",
    options: { a: "John Buck", b: "Aaron T.Beck", c: "David D. Burns", d: "Karen Machover" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Aaron T. Beck developed cognitive therapy and depression and anxiety inventories, not the House-Tree-Person test.", c: "David D. Burns popularized cognitive therapy in self-help form; he did not create this projective drawing test.", d: "Karen Machover developed the Draw-A-Person test, a related but different projective drawing technique." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is John Buck. The House-Tree-Person (HTP) test is a projective drawing technique developed by John N. Buck in 1948. In it, the examinee is asked to draw a house, a tree, and a person, after which the examiner may pose a series of questions about each drawing. The three subjects were chosen because they are familiar to people of all ages and are rich in symbolic meaning: the house is often interpreted as reflecting the person's home life and family relationships, the tree as representing deeper, more unconscious feelings about the self and one's psychological growth, and the person as reflecting the self-image and interpersonal functioning. As a projective test, the HTP assumes that in drawing ambiguous but meaningful subjects, individuals project aspects of their personality, conflicts, and emotional state, and interpretation attends to features such as size, placement, detail, pressure, and omissions. It is used clinically to assess personality, emotional adjustment, and sometimes cognitive or neurological status. It is important to distinguish it from the Draw-A-Person (DAP) test developed by Karen Machover (and the earlier Draw-A-Man test of Florence Goodenough used for intelligence). Because the House-Tree-Person test specifically was created by John Buck, option a is correct." },
  { id: "rml22_064", section: "assessment",
    q: "Summated rating scale is also known as :",
    options: { a: "Likert scale", b: "Thurstone scale", c: "Guttmann scale", d: "Equal appearing interval scale" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "The Thurstone scale uses judged scale values and is the equal-appearing interval method, not a summated scale.", c: "The Guttmann scale is a cumulative (unidimensional) scale, distinct from summated rating.", d: "Equal appearing interval scale is another name for the Thurstone scale, not the summated one." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is the Likert scale. A summated rating scale is an attitude-measurement scale in which a respondent indicates their degree of agreement with a set of statements along an ordered response continuum (for example, strongly disagree, disagree, neutral, agree, strongly agree, typically scored 1 to 5), and the responses across all items are added or averaged to yield a total score representing the person's overall attitude, hence summated. This method was devised by Rensis Likert in 1932, so the summated rating scale is synonymous with the Likert scale. Its advantages are ease of construction, reliability, and the ability to measure intensity of feeling. It is important to distinguish it from other classic attitude scales. The Thurstone scale, developed by Louis Leon Thurstone, is the method of equal-appearing intervals, in which judges first assign scale values to statements and respondents endorse those they agree with; option d (equal appearing interval scale) is therefore another name for the Thurstone scale, not the Likert scale. The Guttman scale, developed by Louis Guttman, is a cumulative scale in which agreeing with a stronger item implies agreement with all weaker ones, producing a unidimensional ordering. Because the summated rating method is Likert's, option a is correct." },
  { id: "rml22_065", section: "social",
    q: "The Implicit Association Test is designed to measure:",
    options: { a: "Verbal ability", b: "Unconscious stereotyping", c: "Hidden mathematical ability", d: "Dream content" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Verbal ability is assessed by aptitude and intelligence tests, not by reaction-time association tasks.", c: "Mathematical ability is measured by achievement or aptitude tests, unrelated to the IAT.", d: "Dream content is explored through projective or psychoanalytic methods, not the IAT." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is unconscious stereotyping. The Implicit Association Test (IAT) was developed by Anthony Greenwald, Debbie McGhee, and Jordan Schwartz in 1998 to measure implicit attitudes and stereotypes, that is, automatic associations that operate outside conscious awareness or control and that people may be unwilling or unable to report on self-report measures. The IAT is a computerized reaction-time task in which participants rapidly sort stimuli (for example, faces of different social groups) and attribute words (for example, pleasant versus unpleasant, or stereotype-consistent versus inconsistent traits) using two response keys. The logic is that people respond faster when two concepts that are strongly associated in their minds share the same response key than when weakly associated concepts share a key. Faster responses for stereotype-congruent pairings than incongruent ones reveal the strength and direction of implicit associations, such as implicit racial, gender, or age bias. The IAT has been enormously influential in social psychology and in research on prejudice and implicit bias, though it has also drawn methodological criticism regarding its reliability and predictive validity. Because it is specifically designed to tap automatic, unconscious associations underlying stereotyping and bias, option b is correct." },
  { id: "rml22_066", section: "assessment",
    q: "The following statements about the Rorschach inkblot test are correct EXCEPT :",
    options: { a: "It was introduced in 1921 by Swiss psychiatrist Hermann Rorschach.", b: "It is a projective method of psychological testing.", c: "Its function is to assess cognition and personality of the person and to diagnosis certain psychological conditions.", d: "It employs 12 inkblots in which a person is asked to describe what he or she sees in them." },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "This is a true statement: Hermann Rorschach did introduce the test in 1921, so it is not the exception.", b: "True: the Rorschach is a projective technique, so it is not the exception.", c: "True: the test assesses cognition, personality, and aids diagnosis, so it is not the exception." },
    explanation: "The correct answer (the EXCEPT) is that it employs 12 inkblots. The Rorschach Inkblot Test uses ten inkblot cards, not twelve. The test was introduced in 1921 by the Swiss psychiatrist Hermann Rorschach in his monograph Psychodiagnostik, making statement a true. It is a projective method of personality assessment, resting on the projective hypothesis that when confronted with ambiguous stimuli people project their own inner needs, conflicts, and ways of perceiving onto them, so statement b is true. Its purpose is indeed to assess perception, cognition, and personality structure and to assist in identifying certain psychological conditions such as thought disorder, so statement c is true. The single inaccuracy is the number of cards: the standard Rorschach set contains exactly ten cards, five achromatic and five containing colour, shown one at a time with the examinee describing what each blot might be. Responses are then scored on dimensions such as location, determinants, form quality, and content, most systematically in the Comprehensive System of John Exner. Because the claim of twelve inkblots misstates the true number of ten, statement d is the incorrect one and therefore the correct answer to this EXCEPT question." },
  { id: "rml22_067", section: "assessment",
    q: "What is the age range valid for Wechsler Intelligence Scale for Children, 5th edition (WISC-V)?",
    options: { a: "5 years to 12 years", b: "6 years to 16 years 11 months", c: "7 years to 16 years 11 months", d: "6 years to 15 years 11 months" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "5 to 12 years is too narrow and understates the upper limit of 16 years 11 months.", c: "The lower bound is 6 years, not 7, so this misstates the starting age.", d: "The upper bound is 16 years 11 months, not 15 years 11 months, so this understates the top of the range." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is 6 years to 16 years 11 months. The Wechsler Intelligence Scale for Children (WISC), originally developed by David Wechsler, is an individually administered test of intelligence for school-age children, and its fifth edition, the WISC-V, is validated for children aged 6 years 0 months through 16 years 11 months. This age band sits between the two other Wechsler instruments: the Wechsler Preschool and Primary Scale of Intelligence (WPPSI) covers younger children (roughly 2 years 6 months to 7 years 7 months), and the Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale (WAIS) covers ages 16 and above, with a slight overlap around age 16. Wechsler's scales moved beyond a single ratio IQ to yield a Full Scale IQ derived from multiple index scores; in the WISC-V these are the Verbal Comprehension, Visual Spatial, Fluid Reasoning, Working Memory, and Processing Speed indexes. A key Wechsler innovation was the use of the deviation IQ, in which a person's score is expressed relative to the distribution of same-age peers (mean 100, standard deviation 15) rather than as a mental-age ratio. Because the WISC-V specifically spans from age 6 to 16 years 11 months, option b is correct." },
  { id: "rml22_068", section: "therapy",
    q: "Eye Movement Desensitization and Reprocessing (EMDR) technique was initially developed for the treatment of which disorder?",
    options: { a: "Borderline personality disorder", b: "Autism spectrum disorder", c: "Post-traumatic stress disorder", d: "Dissociative disorders" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "EMDR was not originally developed for borderline personality disorder, though it may be used adjunctively in trauma-related cases.", b: "Autism spectrum disorder is a neurodevelopmental condition unrelated to the trauma focus for which EMDR was created.", d: "Dissociative disorders can be trauma-linked, but EMDR was specifically developed and validated first for PTSD." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is post-traumatic stress disorder. Eye Movement Desensitization and Reprocessing (EMDR) was developed by Francine Shapiro in the late 1980s, initially and specifically to treat post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD) and the distressing memories that characterize it. The therapy asks the client to hold a vivid image, negative belief, and bodily sensation associated with a traumatic memory in mind while simultaneously engaging in bilateral stimulation, most classically following the therapist's finger with rapid lateral eye movements (though taps or tones may also be used). Over successive sets, the aim is to desensitize the distressing memory and reprocess it into a more adaptive, less emotionally charged form, often introducing a positive replacement cognition. Shapiro's Adaptive Information Processing model proposes that trauma leaves memories inadequately processed and dysfunctionally stored, and that bilateral stimulation facilitates their integration. EMDR is now recommended in treatment guidelines for PTSD, though the precise mechanism of the eye movements remains debated (with working-memory taxation being one leading explanation). While it has since been explored for other conditions, its origin and strongest evidence base are for trauma and PTSD, making option c correct." },
  { id: "rml22_069", section: "assessment",
    q: "Under which grade of insight does \"intellectual insight\" come under?",
    options: { a: "Grade 3", b: "Grade 4", c: "Grade 5", d: "Grade 6" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Grade 3 refers to awareness of illness but blaming it on external or physical factors, not intellectual insight.", b: "Grade 4 is awareness that one is ill due to something unknown in oneself, still short of intellectual insight.", d: "Grade 6 is true emotional insight, which goes beyond the intellectual acknowledgement of Grade 5." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Grade 5. In psychiatric assessment, insight, the patient's awareness and understanding of their own illness, is often graded on a six-point scale. The commonly taught grading runs as follows: Grade 1 is complete denial of illness; Grade 2 is slight awareness of being sick and needing help but denying it at the same time; Grade 3 is awareness of being sick but blaming it on others, on external factors, or on physical or organic causes; Grade 4 is awareness that the illness is due to something unknown within the patient; Grade 5 is intellectual insight, in which the patient admits they are ill and acknowledges that their symptoms or social maladjustment are due to their own particular irrational feelings or disturbance, yet does not apply this understanding to actually change future behavior; and Grade 6 is true emotional insight, a deeper emotional awareness of one's motives and feelings that does lead to meaningful change in behavior and personality. The crucial distinction is that intellectual insight (Grade 5) is a head-level acknowledgment without behavioral change, whereas emotional insight (Grade 6) is accompanied by genuine change. Because intellectual insight corresponds specifically to Grade 5, option c is correct." },
  { id: "rml22_070", section: "assessment",
    q: "Which of the following is an association technique of psychological testing?",
    options: { a: "Sentence completion test", b: "Rorschach inkblot test", c: "Thematic apperception test", d: "Draw-a-person test" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "The sentence completion test is a completion technique, in which the subject finishes incomplete stems.", c: "The Thematic Apperception Test is a construction technique, requiring the subject to build a story.", d: "The Draw-A-Person test is an expressive technique, in which the self is expressed through free drawing." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is the Rorschach inkblot test. Projective techniques are commonly classified by the response task they demand, a scheme associated with Lawrence Frank. Association techniques require the respondent to react to a stimulus with the first thing that comes to mind, such as an association, image, or perception. The Rorschach Inkblot Test, created by Hermann Rorschach, is the prototypical association technique: the examinee is shown ambiguous inkblots and asked what each might be, giving their spontaneous associations to the blot. Word-association tests are another example. The other options each represent a different category. The sentence completion test is a completion technique, in which the person finishes incomplete sentence stems (for example, My greatest fear is...). The Thematic Apperception Test of Henry Murray and Christiana Morgan is a construction technique, requiring the examinee to construct a full narrative story about ambiguous pictures. The Draw-A-Person test of Karen Machover is an expressive technique, in which the individual expresses themselves through the free activity of drawing. Because only the Rorschach asks for immediate associations to a stimulus, it is the association technique among these, making option b correct." },
  { id: "rml22_071", section: "therapy",
    q: "What is the principal role of an \"encounter group\"?",
    options: { a: "Enables individuals to interact with others in a social setting", b: "Encourages team building", c: "Aids the therapist in diagnosis", d: "Encourages therapy and self-growth through disclosure and interaction" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Mere social interaction understates the encounter group's therapeutic, growth-oriented aim.", b: "Team building is a workplace or organizational goal, not the personal-growth purpose of an encounter group.", c: "Diagnosis is not the function of encounter groups; they focus on experience and growth, not assessment." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is that an encounter group encourages therapy and self-growth through disclosure and interaction. Encounter groups emerged from the humanistic and human potential movement of the 1960s and 1970s and are closely associated with Carl Rogers, who wrote about the basic encounter group, and with the sensitivity training (T-group) tradition traced to Kurt Lewin. Their principal aim is personal growth, self-awareness, and improved interpersonal functioning rather than the treatment of specific diagnosed disorders. In an encounter group, a small number of participants meet, often intensively, in an atmosphere of psychological safety created by a facilitator, and are encouraged toward open self-disclosure, honest expression of here-and-now feelings, and authentic interaction with one another. Through giving and receiving genuine feedback and experiencing acceptance, members are meant to reduce defensiveness, gain insight into how they affect others, and move toward Rogers's ideal of a more fully functioning person. The emphasis is experiential and emotional rather than diagnostic or task-oriented. Thus the encounter group is not primarily about ordinary social interaction, team building, or assessment, but about fostering therapeutic self-growth through emotional disclosure and interpersonal encounter, making option d the best answer." },
  { id: "rml22_072", section: "therapy",
    q: "What is Socratic questioning?",
    options: { a: "a form of dialogue that helps a person to discover and attend to previously overlooked information and to guide the discovery of the therapist rather than interpretation from the individual", b: "a form of dialogue that helps a person to discover and attend to previously attended information and to guide the discovery of the individual rather than interpretation from the therapist", c: "a form of dialogue that helps a person to discover and attend to previously overlooked information and to guide the discovery of the individual rather than interpretation from the therapist", d: "a form of dialogue that helps a person to discover and attend to previously attended information and to guide the discovery of the therapist rather than interpretation from the individual" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "This misplaces the discovery in the therapist rather than the individual, reversing the client-centered aim of Socratic questioning.", b: "This says previously attended information, but the point is to surface previously overlooked information the client had not noticed.", d: "This is doubly wrong: it refers to previously attended information and locates the discovery in the therapist." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is option c. Socratic questioning, a cornerstone of the cognitive therapy of Aaron Beck (named after the Greek philosopher Socrates and his method of eliciting knowledge through disciplined questioning), is a form of guided dialogue in which the therapist asks a systematic series of questions to help clients examine their own thoughts and beliefs. Two features define it precisely, and option c captures both. First, its purpose is to help the person discover and attend to previously overlooked information, that is, evidence, alternative interpretations, and perspectives the client had not previously considered or had ignored because of their cognitive distortions. Second, the direction of discovery belongs to the individual (the client), not the therapist: the therapist does not lecture, interpret, or impose conclusions but instead uses questions to guide the client to reach new understandings themselves. This client-led discovery is exactly what makes it part of guided discovery and collaborative empiricism in cognitive therapy, and it contrasts sharply with the interpretive stance of psychoanalysis. The distractor options each corrupt one or both of these elements, either by substituting previously attended for previously overlooked information, or by locating the discovery in the therapist rather than the individual. Only option c states both correctly." },
  { id: "rml22_073", section: "therapy",
    q: "The \"Lovaas programme\" utilises behaviour modification techniques for the treatment of :",
    options: { a: "Autism", b: "Aphasia", c: "Acute depression", d: "Alcoholism" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Aphasia is a language disorder treated through speech-language therapy, not the Lovaas behavioural program.", c: "Acute depression is treated with cognitive, interpersonal, or pharmacological approaches, not the Lovaas method.", d: "Alcoholism treatment uses aversion therapy, motivational interviewing, and pharmacotherapy, not the Lovaas program." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is autism. The Lovaas programme was developed by Ole Ivar Lovaas, a psychologist who pioneered the intensive application of behaviour modification, grounded in the operant conditioning principles of B. F. Skinner, to children with autism. His approach, an early and influential form of Applied Behaviour Analysis (ABA), uses techniques such as discrete trial training, in which skills are broken into small steps and taught through repeated structured trials, with prompting, shaping, and systematic reinforcement of desired responses. The programme is characterized by early, intensive intervention (often many hours per week over years) aimed at building language, social, self-help, and academic skills while reducing maladaptive behaviours. Lovaas's 1987 study reported that a substantial proportion of young children receiving intensive behavioural treatment made large gains in intellectual and educational functioning, which spurred wide adoption of ABA-based interventions for autism, though his early work also included controversial aversive procedures that later fell out of favour. Modern ABA has evolved to emphasize positive reinforcement and naturalistic methods. Because the Lovaas programme is specifically an intensive behavioural intervention for children with autism, option a is correct." },
  { id: "rml22_074", section: "assessment",
    q: "Odd-even method of splitting the test to estimate split-half reliability is MOST appropriate for :",
    options: { a: "Projective tests", b: "Neuropsychological tests", c: "Power tests", d: "Speed tests" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Projective tests yield qualitative, non-item-scored responses, making split-half correlation of item halves inappropriate.", b: "Neuropsychological tests are heterogeneous batteries, not the single homogeneous scale split-half assumes.", d: "Split-half reliability is specifically inappropriate for speed tests because performance depends on how many items are reached, not accuracy." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is power tests. Split-half reliability estimates internal consistency by dividing a test into two halves, scoring each, and correlating the two half-scores (then usually correcting with the Spearman-Brown formula, named after Charles Spearman and William Brown). The odd-even method, assigning odd-numbered items to one half and even-numbered items to the other, is a common way to create equivalent halves. This procedure is appropriate for power tests, in which items vary in difficulty, there is generous or no time limit, and the score reflects how many problems a person can solve rather than how fast. In a power test, examinees attempt all or most items, so both halves contain comparable numbers of attempted items and the correlation is meaningful. Split-half reliability is specifically inappropriate for speed tests, in which items are easy and the score depends on how many items a person completes within a strict time limit. In a pure speed test, a person tends to get all attempted items right and simply stops where time runs out, so odd and even halves would each contain the same number of correct items for everyone, artificially inflating the correlation to near unity. Because the odd-even split-half method validly estimates reliability only when speed is not the limiting factor, it is most appropriate for power tests, making option c correct." },
  { id: "rml22_075", section: "stats",
    q: "What assumption does ANCOVA have that ANOVA does not?",
    options: { a: "Homogeneity of variance", b: "Homoscedasticity", c: "Homogeneity of sample size", d: "Homogeneity of regression slopes" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Homogeneity of variance is an assumption shared by ANOVA as well, so it does not distinguish ANCOVA.", b: "Homoscedasticity is essentially the same equal-variance assumption and is common to both, not unique to ANCOVA.", c: "Homogeneity of sample size is a desirable design feature, not a formal statistical assumption unique to ANCOVA." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is homogeneity of regression slopes. Analysis of covariance (ANCOVA), an extension of the analysis of variance developed by Ronald Fisher, combines ANOVA and regression: it compares group means on a dependent variable while statistically controlling for one or more continuous covariates that are related to the dependent variable, thereby reducing error variance and removing confounding. ANCOVA shares ANOVA's basic assumptions of normality, independence of observations, and homogeneity of variance (homoscedasticity). But it adds a crucial extra assumption not required by ordinary ANOVA: homogeneity of regression slopes. This means the relationship (the regression slope) between the covariate and the dependent variable must be the same across all the treatment groups. In practice this is tested by checking that there is no significant interaction between the independent variable and the covariate. The assumption matters because ANCOVA adjusts each group's mean using a common (pooled) slope; if the slopes differ across groups, that single adjustment is invalid and the covariate's effect depends on group membership, making the adjusted means misleading. Because homogeneity of regression slopes is the assumption unique to ANCOVA and absent from ANOVA, option d is correct." },
  { id: "rml22_076", section: "assessment",
    q: "Which of the following is a clinician-rated questionnaire?",
    options: { a: "Beck Depression Inventory", b: "Beck Anxiety Inventory", c: "Hamilton Anxiety Rating Scale", d: "Both b and c" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "The Beck Depression Inventory is a self-report measure completed by the patient, not clinician-rated.", b: "The Beck Anxiety Inventory is likewise a self-report inventory filled in by the patient.", d: "Since the Beck Anxiety Inventory is self-report, pairing it with the Hamilton scale is incorrect." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is the Hamilton Anxiety Rating Scale. Psychological rating instruments differ in who completes them. Self-report (self-rated) measures are filled in by the patient, who rates their own symptoms, whereas clinician-rated (observer-rated) scales are completed by a trained clinician based on interview and observation. The Beck Depression Inventory (BDI) and the Beck Anxiety Inventory (BAI), both developed by Aaron Beck, are self-report inventories: the patient reads the items and rates the severity of their own depressive or anxiety symptoms. The Hamilton Anxiety Rating Scale (HAM-A), developed by Max Hamilton, is a clinician-administered scale: the clinician interviews the patient and rates the severity of a set of anxiety symptoms (both psychic and somatic) on defined anchors. Hamilton also created the widely used clinician-rated Hamilton Depression Rating Scale (HAM-D). Clinician-rated scales have the advantage of expert judgment and reduced patient response bias, while self-report scales are quick and capture the patient's internal experience directly. Because among the options only the Hamilton Anxiety Rating Scale is completed by the clinician rather than the patient, and both Beck inventories are self-report, option c is correct." },
  { id: "rml22_077", section: "stats",
    q: "Type II error occurs :",
    options: { a: "whenever a true null hypothesis is accepted", b: "whenever a false null hypothesis is rejected", c: "whenever a false null hypothesis is accepted", d: "whenever a true null hypothesis is rejected" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Accepting a true null hypothesis is a correct decision, not any kind of error.", b: "Rejecting a false null hypothesis is also a correct decision (correctly detecting an effect), not an error.", d: "Rejecting a true null hypothesis is a Type I error (a false positive), not a Type II error." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is that a Type II error occurs whenever a false null hypothesis is accepted. In the null-hypothesis significance-testing framework formalized by Jerzy Neyman and Egon Pearson, two kinds of decision errors are possible. A Type I error (alpha error) occurs when a true null hypothesis is rejected, that is, the researcher concludes there is an effect when in fact there is none, a false positive; its probability is set by the significance level alpha. A Type II error (beta error) occurs when a false null hypothesis is accepted (not rejected), that is, the researcher fails to detect a real effect that actually exists, a false negative; its probability is denoted beta. The complement of a Type II error, 1 minus beta, is the statistical power of a test, the probability of correctly rejecting a false null hypothesis and detecting a true effect. Power increases with larger sample size, larger effect size, lower measurement error, and a higher alpha. Correctly accepting a true null and correctly rejecting a false null are the two right decisions, not errors, which rules out options a and b. Because a Type II error is specifically the failure to reject a null hypothesis that is actually false, option c is correct." },
  { id: "rml22_078", section: "general",
    q: "Which of the following technique/process involved in guidance is called \"the heart of the guidance programme\"?",
    options: { a: "Case Study", b: "Interview", c: "Cumulative record", d: "Aptitude test" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "The case study compiles detailed information on an individual but is not the standard label for the heart of guidance.", b: "The interview is an important tool for rapport and information, yet it is not conventionally titled the heart of the programme.", c: "The cumulative record is sometimes called the backbone of guidance, but the item's keyed answer is the aptitude test." },
    explanation: "The correct answer, per this paper's key, is the aptitude test. Guidance programmes use a range of tools and techniques to help individuals make educational, vocational, and personal decisions, including the case study, the interview, the cumulative record, observation, questionnaires, and various standardized tests such as intelligence, achievement, aptitude, and interest inventories. Aptitude tests measure a person's potential to acquire, with training, a particular skill or to succeed in a specific field (for example, mechanical, clerical, numerical, verbal, or spatial aptitude), and multi-aptitude batteries such as the Differential Aptitude Tests are widely used in educational and vocational guidance. Because matching a person's aptitudes to suitable educational streams and careers is central to the counselling and placement functions of guidance, this paper designates the aptitude test as the heart of the guidance programme. It is worth noting that different textbooks assign such honorific labels differently, with the cumulative record sometimes called the backbone and counselling sometimes called the heart of guidance, so the phrasing can vary by source. For the purposes of this examination, the answer keyed as correct is the aptitude test, so option d is selected." },
  { id: "rml22_079", section: "general",
    q: "The advantage of field experiments over lab experiments is :",
    options: { a: "that they have higher internal validity", b: "that they are more easily controlled", c: "that they can use a between-participants design", d: "that they have higher external validity" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Lab experiments, not field experiments, tend to have higher internal validity due to tighter control.", b: "Greater ease of control is an advantage of laboratory settings, not field settings.", c: "Between-participants designs can be used in both lab and field experiments, so this is not a distinct field advantage." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is that field experiments have higher external validity. A field experiment manipulates an independent variable in a real-world, naturalistic setting rather than in a controlled laboratory. Its key advantage is greater external (ecological) validity: because it takes place in a natural environment with everyday behaviour, its findings are more likely to generalize to real-life situations and to how people actually behave outside the artificial confines of a lab. Participants may also be unaware they are in a study, reducing demand characteristics and social desirability effects. The trade-off, however, is reduced internal validity: in a natural setting the experimenter has far less control over extraneous variables, so confounds are harder to eliminate and it is harder to be certain the manipulation alone caused any observed effect. This control versus realism trade-off between lab and field research is emphasized in the experimental methodology tradition of Donald Campbell and Julian Stanley. Laboratory experiments, by contrast, maximize control and internal validity at the cost of ecological validity. Between-participants designs are available in both settings, so that is not a distinguishing feature. Because the defining advantage of field over lab experiments is their higher external validity and generalizability to real-world behaviour, option d is correct." },
  { id: "rml22_080", section: "social",
    q: "Who is credited with elaborating the \"social representation theory\"?",
    options: { a: "Serge Moscovici", b: "Michael A. Hogg", c: "Roy F. Baumeister", d: "Mark E. Leary" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Michael Hogg is associated with social identity and self-categorization theory, not social representations.", c: "Roy Baumeister is known for work on the need to belong, self-regulation, and self-esteem, not social representations.", d: "Mark Leary is known for sociometer theory and the need to belong, not for social representation theory." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Serge Moscovici. The French social psychologist Serge Moscovici developed and elaborated social representations theory, first in his 1961 study of how psychoanalysis was understood and disseminated in French society. Social representations are the shared systems of values, ideas, images, and practices by which members of a community make sense of and communicate about complex or unfamiliar phenomena, effectively the collective, common-sense knowledge of a group. Moscovici argued that societies transform abstract, expert, or unfamiliar ideas (such as scientific concepts) into familiar, concrete common sense through two processes: anchoring, which classifies and names the new idea by relating it to existing categories and prior knowledge, and objectification, which turns the abstract idea into a concrete image or tangible representation. The theory emphasizes that thought is social and shared rather than merely individual, drawing on the earlier notion of collective representations from the sociologist Emile Durkheim, which Moscovici adapted for modern, dynamic, plural societies. It has been highly influential in European social psychology and in research on how communities understand health, illness, science, and social issues. Because social representation theory was originated and elaborated by Serge Moscovici, option a is correct." },
  { id: "rml22_081", section: "assessment",
    q: "Self-report ratings of one's characteristics, attributes, and interests is placed under :",
    options: { a: "L-data", b: "Z-data", c: "P-data", d: "Q-data" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "L-data (life-record data) comes from real-life records and observer ratings, not self-report.", b: "Z-data is not part of Cattell's classification and serves as a distractor.", c: "P-data is not one of the three standard Cattellian data sources in this scheme." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Q-data. Raymond Cattell, the personality theorist who used factor analysis to derive his sixteen source traits (the 16 Personality Factor Questionnaire), proposed that personality can be studied through three main sources of data, which he labelled to ensure that trait findings converge across methods. L-data (life-record data) consists of information gathered from a person's actual life history and everyday behaviour, including ratings made by observers who know the person and objective records such as school grades or work performance. Q-data (questionnaire data) consists of information obtained from the person's own self-report on questionnaires and rating scales, that is, the individual's own ratings of their characteristics, attributes, interests, attitudes, and feelings. T-data (objective test data) consists of measurements taken from objective, often disguised, laboratory tests and behavioural situations in which the person does not know what dimension is being assessed, reducing the possibility of faking. Because self-report ratings, in which a person rates their own traits and interests, fall squarely into the questionnaire category, they are classified as Q-data, making option d correct. The distractors Z-data and P-data are not part of Cattell's standard L-Q-T classification." },
  { id: "rml22_082", section: "therapy",
    q: "Which of the following is NOT listed by Burke, Arkowitz and Mechola (2003) as being treatable with motivational interviewing:",
    options: { a: "Substance-related disorders", b: "Smoking cessation", c: "Diabetes mellitus", d: "Obsessive-compulsive disorder" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Substance-related disorders are a core, well-established application of motivational interviewing.", b: "Smoking cessation is a classic health-behaviour target for motivational interviewing.", c: "Diabetes management, involving lifestyle and adherence change, is a recognized motivational interviewing application." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is obsessive-compulsive disorder. Motivational interviewing (MI) is a client-centred yet directive counselling style developed by William Miller and Stephen Rollnick to strengthen a person's own motivation and commitment to change by exploring and resolving ambivalence. It is grounded in principles such as expressing empathy, developing discrepancy between current behaviour and goals, rolling with resistance, and supporting self-efficacy, and it is closely linked to the stages-of-change (transtheoretical) model of James Prochaska and Carlo DiClemente. MI originated in the treatment of addictive and substance-use problems, where ambivalence about changing entrenched behaviour is central, and it has since been extended to a range of health and behaviour-change targets. In the review by Brian Burke, Hal Arkowitz, and colleagues examining the evidence for MI, the applications supported include substance-related disorders (alcohol and drug problems), smoking cessation, and health-related behaviour change such as diet, exercise, and management of chronic conditions like diabetes mellitus and cardiovascular risk. Obsessive-compulsive disorder, by contrast, is treated principally with exposure and response prevention and cognitive-behavioural therapy rather than being a listed target of motivational interviewing in that review. Because OCD is the condition not listed among MI applications, option d is correct." },
  { id: "rml22_083", section: "assessment",
    q: "Who originally devised the Vineland Social Maturity Scale?",
    options: { a: "Arthur J. Malin", b: "Edgar Arnold Doll", c: "J. Bharat Raj", d: "C. M. Bhatia" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Arthur J. Malin adapted the Vineland scale for Indian use (the Vineland Social Maturity Scale Indian adaptation), but did not originate it.", c: "J. Bharat Raj produced another Indian adaptation, not the original scale.", d: "C. M. Bhatia devised the Bhatia Battery of performance intelligence tests, not the Vineland scale." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Edgar Arnold Doll. The Vineland Social Maturity Scale (VSMS) was originally devised by Edgar Arnold Doll in 1935 at the Vineland Training School in New Jersey, an institution for people with intellectual disability where Doll served as director of research. The scale was created to measure social maturity and adaptive behaviour, that is, a person's ability to look after their own practical needs and take responsibility, across developmental domains including self-help general, self-help eating, self-help dressing, self-direction, occupation, communication, locomotion, and socialization. It yields a social age (SA) and, in relation to chronological age, a social quotient (SQ), providing an index of adaptive functioning that complements intelligence testing, particularly important in assessing intellectual disability where deficits in adaptive behaviour are part of the diagnostic criteria. Doll's work anticipated the modern emphasis on adaptive behaviour, later formalized in instruments such as the Vineland Adaptive Behavior Scales. In India, the scale was adapted for local use, notably by Arthur J. Malin (the Indian adaptation) and by J. Bharat Raj, but these were adaptations of Doll's original instrument. C. M. Bhatia is known for the Bhatia Battery of performance tests of intelligence. Because the original author of the VSMS is Edgar Arnold Doll, option b is correct." },
  { id: "rml22_084", section: "social",
    q: "Cults use all of the following techniques to gain new members EXCEPT:",
    options: { a: "Love-bombing", b: "Isolation", c: "\"Foot-in-the-door\" technique", d: "Talking with the family of recruits" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Love-bombing, overwhelming recruits with affection and attention, is a well-documented cult recruitment tactic.", b: "Isolation from prior social ties is a classic cult technique to increase dependence and reduce outside influence.", c: "The foot-in-the-door technique, gaining compliance through small initial requests, is used in cult recruitment." },
    explanation: "The correct answer (the EXCEPT) is talking with the family of recruits. Research on cults and coercive persuasion, associated with scholars such as Robert Cialdini (on influence), Philip Zimbardo, and Margaret Singer, describes a set of techniques used to recruit and retain members. Love-bombing is the deliberate showering of a prospective member with intense affection, attention, flattery, and a sense of belonging, creating a powerful emotional pull and a feeling of being unconditionally accepted by the group. Isolation involves cutting recruits off from outside relationships, information, and prior social support, thereby increasing their dependence on the group and removing dissenting influences that might counter the group's message. The foot-in-the-door technique, studied by Jonathan Freedman and Scott Fraser, secures compliance with a large commitment by first obtaining agreement to small, escalating requests, gradually drawing the recruit deeper into the group's activities and demands. Talking with or involving the recruit's family, however, runs directly counter to cult strategy: cults typically seek to separate recruits from their families and prior loyalties, not to engage them, since outside family contact undermines the isolation and control the group depends on. Because engaging recruits' families is not a cult recruitment technique but the opposite, option d is the correct exception." },
  { id: "rml22_085", section: "social",
    q: "Raven and his colleagues formulated a model identifying the six bases of power for influencing others. These do not include:",
    options: { a: "Reciprocity", b: "Coercion", c: "Expertise", d: "Information" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Coercive power, based on the ability to punish, is one of the classic bases of social power.", c: "Expert power, based on superior knowledge or skill, is one of the recognized bases of power.", d: "Informational power, based on the content of a persuasive message, was added as one of the six bases." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is reciprocity. The classic taxonomy of social power was developed by John French and Bertram Raven, who originally identified five bases of power a person can use to influence others: reward power (the ability to give positive outcomes), coercive power (the ability to punish or impose negative outcomes), legitimate power (authority derived from one's role or position), referent power (influence arising from others' identification with or admiration of the person), and expert power (influence based on possessing superior knowledge or skill). Raven later added a sixth base, informational power, which rests on the persuasive content of a message or the information one provides, rather than on the influencer's position or relationship, and unlike the others it can produce lasting, independent change because the target internalizes the reasoning. Reciprocity, by contrast, is not one of French and Raven's bases of power; it is a norm of social exchange and one of the principles of compliance identified by Robert Cialdini, the felt obligation to return a favour. So while reciprocity is a genuine influence mechanism, it belongs to a different framework and is not among the six bases of power. Because reciprocity is not one of Raven's six bases, option a is correct." },
  { id: "rml22_086", section: "general",
    q: "Which of the following can help control experimenter's bias?",
    options: { a: "Double-blinding", b: "Randomly assigning subjects to groups", c: "Using control groups", d: "Demand characteristics" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Random assignment controls confounds and ensures group equivalence, but does not by itself hide condition from the experimenter.", c: "Control groups provide a comparison baseline but do not prevent the experimenter's expectations from biasing treatment or scoring.", d: "Demand characteristics are a source of participant bias, not a method for controlling experimenter bias." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is double-blinding. Experimenter bias, sometimes called the experimenter-expectancy effect and studied extensively by Robert Rosenthal, occurs when a researcher's knowledge of the hypothesis or of participants' group assignment unintentionally influences how they treat participants, interpret ambiguous data, or record and score outcomes, subtly nudging the results toward the expected direction. The most direct safeguard is double-blinding, in which neither the participants nor the researchers who interact with them and collect the data know which condition each participant is in. Because the experimenter cannot let expectations about a specific condition shape their behaviour or scoring if they do not know which condition applies, double-blinding removes the channel through which experimenter bias operates (and simultaneously controls participant expectancy). Random assignment, by contrast, ensures that groups are equivalent at the outset and controls for confounding participant variables, and control groups provide a comparison baseline, but neither of these conceals condition from the experimenter, so neither directly prevents experimenter bias. Demand characteristics, described by Martin Orne, are cues that lead participants to guess the hypothesis and alter their behaviour, so they are a form of participant bias to be controlled, not a control technique. Because double-blinding specifically neutralizes experimenter expectancy, option a is correct." },
  { id: "rml22_087", section: "assessment",
    q: "Kelly is to the Rep test as Cattell is to the",
    options: { a: "The California Psychological Inventory (CPI)", b: "The Q-sort method", c: "Sixteen Personality Factor Questionnaire (16 PF)", d: "Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory (MMPI)" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "The California Psychological Inventory was developed by Harrison Gough, not Cattell.", b: "The Q-sort method is associated with William Stephenson and used by Carl Rogers, not with Cattell.", d: "The MMPI was developed by Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley, not by Cattell." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is the Sixteen Personality Factor Questionnaire (16 PF). This analogy item asks you to match each theorist with the assessment instrument they created. George Kelly, the founder of personal construct theory, developed the Role Construct Repertory Test (the Rep test or Rep grid), a technique for eliciting the bipolar personal constructs an individual uses to interpret and anticipate their world; by comparing and contrasting significant people in their life, the person reveals the dimensions of meaning through which they construe experience. Analogously, Raymond Cattell developed the Sixteen Personality Factor Questionnaire (16 PF), a self-report personality inventory built through factor analysis to measure sixteen primary source traits (such as warmth, reasoning, emotional stability, dominance, and vigilance) that Cattell derived as the fundamental dimensions of personality. So just as the Rep test is Kelly's characteristic instrument, the 16 PF is Cattell's. The distractors are all real inventories but attributable to other authors: the California Psychological Inventory to Harrison Gough, the Q-sort method to William Stephenson (and used clinically by Carl Rogers to study the self-concept and the real-ideal self discrepancy), and the MMPI to Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley. Because Cattell's instrument is the 16 PF, option c correctly completes the analogy." },
  { id: "rml22_088", section: "general",
    q: "A researcher is interested in studying the effect of noise on the problem-solving abilities of elementary school students. She uses two classes of fourth-grade students and tests them on a 10-problem test. When one class takes the test, she turns on a radio, keeping it off for the other class. This is an example of a :",
    options: { a: "true experiment", b: "quasi-experiment", c: "correlational study", d: "naturalistic observation" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "A true experiment requires random assignment of individuals to conditions, which is absent when intact classes are used.", c: "This study manipulates an independent variable (noise), so it is experimental, not merely correlational.", d: "The researcher actively manipulates conditions rather than passively observing, so it is not naturalistic observation." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is a quasi-experiment. The defining feature that separates a true experiment from a quasi-experiment, as detailed by Donald Campbell and Julian Stanley, is random assignment of participants to conditions. In a true experiment, each individual participant is randomly assigned to the treatment or control condition, which makes the groups equivalent on average and licenses causal inference. In this study, the researcher does manipulate an independent variable (noise present versus absent) and measures a dependent variable (problem-solving performance), so it is experimental in form rather than correlational or observational. However, she does not randomly assign individual students; instead she uses two intact, pre-existing classes and assigns the noise condition to one whole class and the quiet condition to the other. Because the groups are naturally occurring rather than formed by random assignment, any pre-existing differences between the two classes (in ability, teacher, prior noise exposure, and so on) are confounded with the noise manipulation, weakening internal validity. This use of a manipulated variable with non-randomly-assigned, intact groups is precisely what defines a quasi-experiment. It is not naturalistic observation, since the researcher actively manipulates the noise, and it is not merely correlational, since there is a manipulated cause. Therefore option b is correct." },
  { id: "rml22_089", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Which gland regulates the circadian rhythm in the human beings?",
    options: { a: "Hypothalamus", b: "Cerebellum", c: "Pituitary gland", d: "Pineal gland" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "The hypothalamus houses the suprachiasmatic nucleus master clock but is a brain region, not the gland that secretes the sleep-timing hormone.", b: "The cerebellum coordinates movement and balance and has no role in circadian regulation.", c: "The pituitary is the master endocrine gland but does not secrete melatonin to set the sleep-wake cycle." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is the pineal gland. Circadian rhythms are roughly 24-hour cycles in physiology and behaviour, most notably the sleep-wake cycle. The body's master clock is the suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN) of the hypothalamus, which is entrained to the light-dark cycle by input from the retina. However, the question asks specifically which gland regulates circadian rhythm, and that is the pineal gland, a small endocrine gland located deep in the brain. In response to signals from the SCN, the pineal gland secretes the hormone melatonin, and it does so in a light-dependent manner: melatonin release rises in darkness (at night) and is suppressed by light during the day. Melatonin acts as the body's chemical signal of night-time, promoting sleepiness and helping to synchronize the sleep-wake cycle and other daily rhythms with the external environment. Because of this role, melatonin is used to treat jet lag and some sleep disorders. The philosopher Rene Descartes famously, though mistakenly, called the pineal gland the seat of the soul. The cerebellum governs motor coordination, and the pituitary is the master endocrine gland controlling other glands, but neither sets circadian timing. Because the pineal gland secretes melatonin to regulate the circadian rhythm, option d is correct." },
  { id: "rml22_090", section: "therapy",
    q: "Who developed the technique of Interpersonal Psychotherapy (IPT)?",
    options: { a: "L. Benjamin", b: "Peter Fonagy and A. Bateman", c: "Otto F. Kernberg", d: "G.L. Klerman and M. M. Weissman" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Lorna Smith Benjamin developed the Structural Analysis of Social Behavior (SASB), not IPT.", b: "Peter Fonagy and Anthony Bateman developed mentalization-based treatment, not interpersonal psychotherapy.", c: "Otto Kernberg developed transference-focused psychotherapy for personality disorders, not IPT." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Gerald L. Klerman and Myrna M. Weissman. Interpersonal Psychotherapy (IPT) is a structured, time-limited, evidence-based psychotherapy developed by Gerald Klerman and Myrna Weissman (with colleagues) initially for the treatment of major depression. Its central premise is that psychological symptoms, particularly depression, arise and are maintained within an interpersonal context, so improving the person's current relationships and social functioning relieves the symptoms. Rather than focusing on unconscious conflict or on distorted cognitions, IPT concentrates on the here-and-now and targets one or more of four problem areas linked to the onset of the episode: grief (complicated bereavement), interpersonal role disputes (conflicts with significant others), role transitions (major life changes such as divorce, retirement, or a new job), and interpersonal deficits (a history of impoverished or unsatisfying relationships). Its theoretical roots draw on the interpersonal school of Harry Stack Sullivan and on attachment theory from John Bowlby. IPT has strong empirical support for depression and has been adapted for other conditions. The distractors name other clinicians and their distinct therapies: Lorna Smith Benjamin (SASB), Peter Fonagy and Anthony Bateman (mentalization-based treatment), and Otto Kernberg (transference-focused psychotherapy). Because IPT was developed by Klerman and Weissman, option d is correct." },
  { id: "rml22_091", section: "therapy",
    q: "Which kind of empathy is considered inappropriate for an initial counselling interview or session?",
    options: { a: "Advanced empathy", b: "Primary empathy", c: "Both a and b", d: "Culturally sensitive empathy" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Primary empathy, reflecting the client's expressed feelings, is exactly what is appropriate and needed early on.", c: "Since primary empathy is appropriate initially, pairing it with advanced empathy is incorrect.", d: "Culturally sensitive empathy is always appropriate and indeed essential, not something to withhold early." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is advanced empathy. In the influential skilled-helper model of counselling developed by Gerard Egan, a distinction is drawn between primary (basic) empathy and advanced (accurate) empathy. Primary empathy is the counsellor's accurate understanding and reflection of the feelings and meanings the client has directly expressed; it communicates that the client has been heard and understood and is essential for building rapport and the therapeutic alliance from the very first session. Advanced empathy goes further: it involves the counsellor drawing out and putting into words the feelings, meanings, themes, and implications that the client has only hinted at, implied, or left half-stated, in effect helping the client see themselves more fully, including material they have not yet consciously articulated. Because advanced empathy probes beneath the surface and confronts the client with deeper or unspoken content, it can feel intrusive, premature, or threatening if used before a solid, trusting relationship has been established. It is therefore considered inappropriate in an initial interview and is reserved for later stages once trust and rapport are firm. Primary empathy and culturally sensitive empathy, by contrast, are appropriate and necessary from the outset. Because advanced empathy is the type to avoid early on, option a is correct." },
  { id: "rml22_092", section: "general",
    q: "In which sampling technique the researchers divide a population into smaller groups, and then randomly select from these groups to form a sample?",
    options: { a: "Cluster sampling", b: "Quota sampling", c: "Stratified sampling", d: "Convenient sampling" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Quota sampling is a non-probability method that fills preset quotas by non-random selection, not by randomly choosing groups.", c: "Stratified sampling divides the population into strata and samples individuals from within every stratum, not by selecting whole groups.", d: "Convenience sampling selects whoever is readily available, with no division into groups or random selection." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is cluster sampling. Cluster sampling is a probability sampling technique in which the population is first divided into naturally occurring groups called clusters (for example, schools, city blocks, hospitals, or villages), and then a random selection of entire clusters is chosen; data are collected from all members within the selected clusters (one-stage) or from a random subset within them (multistage). Its main advantages are practicality and cost-efficiency when the population is large and geographically dispersed, since it avoids the need for a complete list of every individual and concentrates data collection within selected groups. The key contrast is with stratified sampling: in stratified sampling the population is divided into homogeneous subgroups (strata) based on a characteristic such as age or gender, and then individuals are randomly sampled from within every stratum to ensure representation, whereas in cluster sampling whole clusters (ideally each internally heterogeneous and representative of the population) are randomly selected and some clusters are left out entirely. Quota sampling and convenience sampling are non-probability methods lacking random selection. Because the described procedure divides the population into groups and then randomly selects whole groups to form the sample, it matches cluster sampling, making option a correct." },
  { id: "rml22_093", section: "general",
    q: "Analogue research involves:",
    options: { a: "Undertaking research to determine the efficacy of treatments and interventions", b: "Undertaking research to compare the effectiveness of two different types of treatment", c: "Undertaking research on clinical populations", d: "Undertaking research on healthy, non-clinical populations" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Determining treatment efficacy describes outcome or efficacy research, not the defining feature of analogue research.", b: "Comparing two treatments describes comparative outcome research, not analogue research.", c: "Studying clinical populations is the opposite of analogue research, which uses non-clinical stand-ins." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is undertaking research on healthy, non-clinical populations. Analogue research refers to studies that investigate clinical phenomena under conditions that only resemble, or are analogous to, the real clinical situation, typically by using participants who are not drawn from actual clinical populations. For example, rather than studying people diagnosed with a phobia, a researcher might study university students who report mild fears, or rather than studying real therapy, they might use a laboratory analogue of the therapeutic process. The value of analogue research lies in the experimental control it affords: because it uses accessible, non-clinical participants (often students) and controlled settings, researchers can manipulate variables precisely, test hypotheses about mechanisms, and avoid the ethical and practical difficulties of experimenting on distressed clinical patients. Its principal limitation is external validity, that is, the uncertainty about how well findings from mild, non-clinical, laboratory conditions generalize to genuine clinical disorders and real treatment settings. The defining characteristic that distinguishes analogue research from ordinary clinical outcome or efficacy research is precisely this use of healthy, non-clinical populations or simulated conditions as stand-ins for the real clinical phenomena. Because that use of non-clinical samples is what analogue research denotes, option d is correct." },
  { id: "rml22_094", section: "assessment",
    q: "Raven's Standard Progressive Matrices (SPM) measure which kind of intelligence?",
    options: { a: "Crystallised intelligence", b: "Fluid intelligence", c: "Both a and b", d: "General Intelligence" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Crystallised intelligence reflects acquired knowledge and vocabulary, which the culture-reduced matrices deliberately avoid tapping.", c: "Because the matrices target reasoning rather than learned knowledge, they do not measure crystallised intelligence, so both is wrong.", d: "While the SPM correlates highly with general intelligence, its specific target is the fluid reasoning component." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is fluid intelligence. Raven's Standard Progressive Matrices (SPM), developed by John C. Raven in 1938, is a nonverbal test in which the examinee identifies the missing element that completes a visual pattern or matrix, with items increasing in difficulty. It is designed to measure fluid intelligence, the ability to reason, perceive relationships, and solve novel problems independently of previously acquired knowledge or education. This maps onto the Gf-Gc distinction introduced by Raymond Cattell and elaborated by John Horn: fluid intelligence (Gf) is the capacity for abstract reasoning and problem-solving with novel material, whereas crystallised intelligence (Gc) is the accumulated knowledge, vocabulary, and skills gained through experience and education. Because the matrices rely on abstract visual reasoning rather than language or learned facts, they are considered relatively culture-fair (or culture-reduced) and are used cross-culturally. The test was also historically important as a strong marker of Spearman's g, the general intelligence factor identified by Charles Spearman, and the SPM does correlate highly with general intelligence. However, its specific and intended construct is fluid reasoning, not crystallised knowledge. Because Raven's Progressive Matrices most precisely measure fluid intelligence, option b is correct." },
  { id: "rml22_095", section: "therapy",
    q: "Counter conditioning is an exposure therapy technique that involves:",
    options: { a: "Learning that one negative event may be linked to another", b: "Exposing the client to the feared stimulus", c: "Learning that an event or situation is no longer threatening", d: "Helping the client to see that their behaviour is counterproductive" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Learning that one negative event predicts another describes the acquisition of a fear, the opposite of counterconditioning.", b: "Mere exposure to the feared stimulus describes exposure in general; counterconditioning specifically pairs it with an incompatible response.", d: "Recognizing behaviour as counterproductive is a cognitive insight, not the associative relearning of counterconditioning." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is learning that an event or situation is no longer threatening. Counterconditioning is a behaviour therapy technique, based on classical conditioning principles, in which a previously feared or aversive stimulus is repeatedly paired with a new response that is incompatible with fear, so that the stimulus comes to elicit the new response instead. The principle rests on reciprocal inhibition, articulated by Joseph Wolpe: since relaxation and anxiety cannot coexist, deliberately pairing the feared stimulus with a relaxed, calm state gradually weakens and replaces the fear response. Systematic desensitization is the classic application: the client is taught deep relaxation (following the progressive relaxation method of Edmund Jacobson) and then, while relaxed, is exposed to a hierarchy of increasingly anxiety-provoking versions of the feared stimulus, learning to remain calm rather than afraid. Through this process the person effectively relearns that the event or situation is no longer threatening, extinguishing the conditioned fear. This distinguishes counterconditioning from mere exposure, which simply presents the feared stimulus, and from cognitive insight-based approaches. Because counterconditioning replaces the fear response so that the client comes to experience the once-feared situation as no longer threatening, option c is correct." },
  { id: "rml22_096", section: "therapy",
    q: "Which of the following is not a treatment developed from classical conditioning principles?",
    options: { a: "Flooding", b: "Token economies", c: "Systematic desensitisation", d: "Aversion therapies" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Flooding exposes the client to the feared stimulus to extinguish a conditioned fear, rooted in classical conditioning.", c: "Systematic desensitisation is a classic counterconditioning technique based on classical conditioning.", d: "Aversion therapy pairs an unwanted behaviour with an aversive stimulus, a classical conditioning procedure." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is token economies. It is important to distinguish therapies derived from classical (Pavlovian) conditioning, described by Ivan Pavlov, from those derived from operant conditioning, described by B. F. Skinner. Classical conditioning concerns associations between stimuli and reflexive responses, and several exposure-based and aversive therapies flow from it. Flooding exposes the client, all at once and at full intensity, to the feared stimulus without escape, so that the conditioned fear response extinguishes when no harm follows. Systematic desensitization, developed by Joseph Wolpe, pairs graded exposure to the feared stimulus with relaxation (counterconditioning via reciprocal inhibition). Aversion therapy pairs an undesirable behaviour or stimulus (such as alcohol) with an unpleasant unconditioned stimulus (such as a nausea-inducing drug) so that the behaviour comes to elicit aversion. All three rest on stimulus-response associations, that is, classical conditioning. Token economies, by contrast, are based on operant conditioning: desired behaviours are reinforced with tokens (secondary reinforcers) that can later be exchanged for backup rewards, shaping voluntary behaviour through its consequences. Because token economies work through operant reinforcement rather than classical stimulus-stimulus association, they are the technique not developed from classical conditioning principles, making option b correct." },
  { id: "rml22_097", section: "assessment",
    q: "At what age can the Malin's Intelligence Scale be administered to Indian children?",
    options: { a: "4 years", b: "2 years", c: "5 years", d: "6 years" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "4 years is below the standardized lower age limit of the Malin's scale.", b: "2 years is far too young for this Wechsler-based children's intelligence scale.", c: "5 years still falls short of the six-year lower boundary of the instrument." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is 6 years. The Malin's Intelligence Scale for Indian Children (MISIC) is an Indian adaptation of the Wechsler Intelligence Scale for Children, adapted by Arthur J. Malin. Like the Wechsler children's scale on which it is based, it is designed for school-age children and is standardized for administration from about 6 years up to roughly 15 or 16 years of age. It is one of the most widely used individually administered intelligence tests in Indian clinical and educational settings, yielding a verbal IQ, a performance IQ, and a full-scale IQ derived from a set of verbal subtests (such as information, comprehension, arithmetic, similarities, vocabulary, and digit span) and performance subtests (such as picture completion, block design, object assembly, coding, and mazes). Because it is adapted and standardized on an Indian population, it provides more culturally appropriate norms than directly importing a Western test. This lower age limit of 6 years distinguishes it from preschool-level instruments used for younger children, such as the Indian adaptations of the Binet scales or the Gessell Developmental Schedules. Because the Malin's Intelligence Scale for Indian Children can be administered from age 6, option d is correct." },
  { id: "rml22_098", section: "therapy",
    q: "In behaviour therapy, the patient is taught the methods of progressive deep relaxation based on the work of :",
    options: { a: "Wolpe", b: "Skinner", c: "Jacobson", d: "Ivan Pavlov" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Joseph Wolpe used relaxation within systematic desensitization, but he adopted the relaxation method rather than devising it.", b: "B. F. Skinner developed operant conditioning, not progressive muscle relaxation.", d: "Ivan Pavlov discovered classical conditioning; progressive relaxation is not his contribution." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Edmund Jacobson. Progressive deep relaxation, also called progressive muscle relaxation (PMR), was developed by the American physician and physiologist Edmund Jacobson in the early twentieth century. The technique teaches the patient to systematically tense and then release successive muscle groups throughout the body, learning to notice the contrast between tension and relaxation and thereby to achieve a deep state of physical and mental calm. Jacobson's premise was that anxiety and muscular tension are physiologically linked, so that deliberately relaxing the muscles reduces anxiety. This method became a foundational component of behaviour therapy. In particular, Joseph Wolpe incorporated Jacobson's progressive relaxation into his technique of systematic desensitization, using the relaxed state as the response that is incompatible with anxiety (reciprocal inhibition) while the patient is gradually exposed to a hierarchy of feared stimuli. So although relaxation is closely associated with Wolpe's desensitization procedure, the progressive deep relaxation method itself was devised by Jacobson, and Wolpe borrowed it. B. F. Skinner (operant conditioning) and Ivan Pavlov (classical conditioning) are foundational learning theorists but did not develop relaxation training. Because progressive deep relaxation originates with Edmund Jacobson, option c is correct." },
  { id: "rml22_099", section: "therapy",
    q: "Functional analysis is a therapy based on:",
    options: { a: "Classical conditioning", b: "Humanistic principles", c: "Operant conditioning", d: "Psychodynamic principles" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Classical conditioning concerns reflexive stimulus-response associations, whereas functional analysis examines consequences of voluntary behaviour.", b: "Humanistic principles emphasize self-actualization and the therapeutic relationship, not the behavioural ABC analysis.", d: "Psychodynamic principles focus on unconscious conflict, unrelated to the antecedent-behaviour-consequence framework." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is operant conditioning. Functional analysis is a core method of behaviour therapy and applied behaviour analysis, grounded in the operant conditioning principles of B. F. Skinner. It involves systematically examining a target behaviour in terms of its antecedents (the situations, cues, or triggers that precede it), the behaviour itself (its form, frequency, intensity, and duration), and its consequences (what follows the behaviour and thereby reinforces or maintains it), the so-called ABC (Antecedent-Behaviour-Consequence) model. The central assumption is Skinnerian: behaviour is controlled by its consequences, so understanding the reinforcement contingencies that maintain a problem behaviour allows the therapist to change those contingencies, altering antecedents and consequences to reduce maladaptive behaviour and strengthen desired behaviour. For example, a functional analysis might reveal that a child's tantrums (behaviour) are triggered by task demands (antecedent) and reinforced by escape from the task or by attention (consequence), pointing to an intervention that changes those contingencies. This consequence-focused, contingency-based approach is definitively operant, distinguishing it from classical conditioning (reflexive stimulus associations), humanistic therapy (self-actualization and unconditional positive regard, per Carl Rogers), and psychodynamic therapy (unconscious conflict). Because functional analysis rests on analysing and modifying the consequences of behaviour, it is based on operant conditioning, making option c correct." },
  { id: "rml22_100", section: "therapy",
    q: "Functional analysis is a therapy based on:",
    options: { a: "Classical conditioning", b: "Humanistic principles", c: "Operant conditioning", d: "Psychodynamic principles" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Classical conditioning deals with reflexive stimulus-response links, not the analysis of behaviour's consequences.", b: "Humanistic principles centre on growth and the therapeutic relationship, not the ABC contingency analysis.", d: "Psychodynamic principles address unconscious conflict, not the operant contingencies functional analysis targets." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is operant conditioning. This item repeats the previous question, and its answer is the same: functional analysis is a behavioural assessment and therapy method founded on the operant conditioning principles of B. F. Skinner. Functional analysis dissects a behaviour using the Antecedent-Behaviour-Consequence (ABC) framework, identifying the environmental events that precede the behaviour (antecedents or discriminative stimuli) and, most importantly, the consequences that follow and maintain it through reinforcement or punishment. The guiding operant premise is that voluntary behaviour is a function of its consequences: behaviours followed by reinforcement increase, and those followed by punishment or non-reinforcement decrease. By pinpointing the specific reinforcers sustaining a problem behaviour, the clinician can redesign the contingencies, for instance removing the reinforcement that maintains an unwanted behaviour and reinforcing a more adaptive alternative. This makes functional analysis a cornerstone of applied behaviour analysis used in settings from autism intervention to habit and classroom management. It is firmly operant rather than classical (which concerns reflexive stimulus-stimulus associations, per Ivan Pavlov), humanistic (self-actualization and the therapeutic relationship, per Carl Rogers), or psychodynamic (unconscious conflict, per Sigmund Freud). Because functional analysis is based on analysing and altering the consequences that control behaviour, its foundation is operant conditioning, making option c correct." },
],
  "rml-2023": [
  { id: "rml23_001", section: "stats",
    q: "Which of the following is an example of a quantitative research method?",
    options: { a: "Case study", b: "Ethnography", c: "Survey", d: "Grounded theory" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "A case study is an in-depth qualitative examination of a single instance, describing meaning rather than measuring numeric variables.",
      b: "Ethnography is a qualitative, immersive study of a culture or group through observation and field notes, not numeric data.",
      d: "Grounded theory is a qualitative approach that builds theory inductively from textual data, not from quantification."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Survey. Quantitative research collects numerical data that can be counted, measured, and statistically analysed to test hypotheses and generalise to a population. A survey administers standardised questions to a sample and yields numeric responses (ratings, frequencies, percentages), so it is a classic quantitative method. This distinction traces to the broader positivist tradition described by Auguste Comte, in which knowledge is built from measurable observation. Qualitative methods, by contrast, aim to understand meaning, context, and lived experience through words rather than numbers. Case study (championed as a method by Robert Yin), ethnography (rooted in the anthropology of Bronislaw Malinowski), and grounded theory (developed by Barney Glaser and Anselm Strauss) all fall on the qualitative side because they produce rich descriptive text, not quantified variables. The key marker of a quantitative method is that the data can be entered into statistics and expressed as numbers. Surveys with closed-ended, scaled items fit this exactly. Understanding this divide matters because it determines which analysis (means, correlations, significance tests versus thematic coding) is appropriate for the data collected. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)"
  },
  { id: "rml23_002", section: "stats",
    q: "What type of sampling method involves selecting every nth individual from a population?",
    options: { a: "Simple random sampling", b: "Systematic sampling", c: "Convenience sampling", d: "Stratified sampling" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "In simple random sampling every unit has an equal, independent chance of selection, typically via a random number generator, not a fixed interval.",
      c: "Convenience sampling picks whoever is easiest to reach, with no systematic interval or randomisation.",
      d: "Stratified sampling divides the population into subgroups (strata) and samples within each, which is about representation, not a fixed nth interval."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Systematic sampling. In systematic sampling the researcher selects a random starting point and then chooses every kth element, where k is the sampling interval computed as the population size divided by the desired sample size. For example, picking every 10th name on a list is systematic sampling. It is a probability method that is simpler to implement than drawing individual random numbers and spreads the sample evenly across the ordered frame. The technique is treated in the classic sampling theory of William Cochran. Its main caution is periodicity: if the list has a hidden cyclical pattern that matches the interval, bias can creep in. Simple random sampling gives every unit an equal and independent chance, usually via a random number table or generator. Stratified sampling, associated with careful survey design, first partitions the population into homogeneous strata (for example by age or gender) and samples within each to guarantee representation. Convenience sampling is non-probability and selects the most accessible participants. The defining phrase every nth individual points specifically to the fixed-interval logic of systematic sampling. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)"
  },
  { id: "rml23_003", section: "stats",
    q: "Which of the following is an example of a nominal scale of measurement?",
    options: { a: "Temperature in degrees Celsius", b: "Height of students in centimetres", c: "Marital status (e.g., single, married, divorced)", d: "Likert scale responses ranging from 1 to 5" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "Temperature in Celsius is an interval scale: it has equal intervals but an arbitrary zero, so it is not merely categorical.",
      b: "Height in centimetres is a ratio scale with a true zero and meaningful ratios, not an unordered category.",
      d: "Likert responses are typically treated as ordinal because the response options carry a rank order of agreement."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Marital status. The four levels of measurement were formalised by Stanley Smith Stevens in 1946: nominal, ordinal, interval, and ratio. A nominal scale simply names or labels categories that have no inherent order; you can only say whether two cases are the same or different. Marital status (single, married, divorced) is purely categorical: the labels cannot be ranked or averaged, so it is nominal. Temperature in degrees Celsius is interval because it has equal spacing between units but an arbitrary zero point, meaning ratios are not meaningful (20 degrees is not twice as hot as 10). Height in centimetres is ratio because it has a true, absolute zero and supports statements like twice as tall. Likert-scale responses are conventionally ordinal, since 1 to 5 conveys ordered degrees of agreement without guaranteed equal intervals. Recognising the scale of a variable is essential because it dictates which statistics are valid: nominal data allow only frequencies, modes, and chi-square, whereas higher scales permit means and parametric tests. The absence of any ordering is the hallmark of a nominal variable. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)"
  },
  { id: "rml23_004", section: "stats",
    q: "In which scaling technique are respondents asked to place items along a continuum according to their preference or degree of agreement?",
    options: { a: "Nominal scaling", b: "Ordinal scaling", c: "Interval scaling", d: "Ratio scaling" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "Nominal scaling only labels items into unordered categories and cannot arrange them along a continuum of preference.",
      c: "Interval scaling adds equal spacing between points, which is a stronger property than the simple ranking the question describes.",
      d: "Ratio scaling requires a true zero and meaningful ratios, far beyond the ordered placement of preferences described here."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Ordinal scaling. Ordinal measurement, one of Stanley Smith Stevens's four levels, ranks items in order along a continuum but does not assume the distances between ranks are equal. When respondents place items by preference or by their degree of agreement (for example ranking products from most to least liked, or ordering statements from strongly disagree to strongly agree), they are producing ordinal data: the order is meaningful but the gap between rank 1 and rank 2 need not equal the gap between rank 4 and rank 5. Nominal scaling only classifies into unordered categories, so it cannot capture a continuum of preference. Interval scaling adds the requirement of equal, quantified intervals between adjacent points and an arbitrary zero, which is more than mere ordering. Ratio scaling further requires an absolute zero. Because the question emphasises arranging items in order of preference or agreement, without any claim of equal spacing, the defining feature is rank order, which is precisely what ordinal scaling provides. This matters for analysis because ordinal data are best summarised with medians and analysed with non-parametric tests. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)"
  },
  { id: "rml23_005", section: "stats",
    q: "Which scaling technique assumes equal intervals between consecutive scale points and has a true zero point?",
    options: { a: "Nominal scaling", b: "Ordinal scaling", c: "Interval scaling", d: "Ratio scaling" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: {
      a: "Nominal scaling has no order at all, let alone equal intervals or a zero point.",
      b: "Ordinal scaling ranks values but does not guarantee equal intervals and has no true zero.",
      c: "Interval scaling does assume equal intervals but has an arbitrary, not a true, zero point, so ratios are not meaningful."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Ratio scaling. In Stanley Smith Stevens's hierarchy of measurement, the ratio scale is the highest level: it possesses order, equal intervals between consecutive points, and, crucially, a true or absolute zero that represents the complete absence of the quantity. Because of that absolute zero, ratios are meaningful, so 40 kilograms is genuinely twice 20 kilograms. Examples include height, weight, time duration, and income. The key phrase in the question, true zero point, distinguishes ratio from interval scaling. Interval scaling shares the equal-interval property but its zero is arbitrary (as with the Celsius temperature scale, where 0 degrees does not mean no temperature), so multiplicative comparisons are invalid. Ordinal scaling provides only rank order without equal intervals, and nominal scaling provides only unordered labels. Identifying a variable as ratio is important because it licenses the fullest set of arithmetic operations and statistical procedures, including geometric means and coefficients of variation. Whenever a scale allows you to say one value is a certain multiple of another, and zero truly means none, you are working with a ratio scale. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)"
  },
  { id: "rml23_006", section: "stats",
    q: "In which type of research design does the researcher observe and describe phenomena without manipulating variables?",
    options: { a: "Exploratory", b: "Descriptive", c: "Causal", d: "Case study" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "Exploratory research investigates a poorly understood problem to generate hypotheses, and is not primarily about systematically describing phenomena as they are.",
      c: "Causal research deliberately manipulates an independent variable to test cause-and-effect, which is the opposite of observing without manipulation.",
      d: "A case study is a specific method rather than the broad design category the question asks for, though it is one descriptive tool."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Descriptive. A descriptive research design aims to accurately portray the characteristics of a population, situation, or phenomenon as it naturally exists, answering what, who, where, and when questions without intervening in or manipulating variables. Common descriptive methods include surveys, observational studies, and correlational research; the researcher measures and records but does not alter conditions. This contrasts sharply with causal (experimental) research, in the tradition of Ronald Fisher's experimental design, where an independent variable is deliberately manipulated and extraneous variables controlled to establish cause and effect. Exploratory research is conducted when a topic is new or poorly understood; its goal is to gain preliminary insight and generate hypotheses rather than to describe a phenomenon systematically. A case study is a narrower method that can serve descriptive aims but is not itself the overarching design label the question seeks. The defining feature in the stem, observing and describing without manipulating variables, is exactly the mandate of descriptive research. Understanding this distinction matters because only manipulative (experimental) designs can support causal claims, whereas descriptive designs can characterise and correlate but not prove causation. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)"
  },
  { id: "rml23_007", section: "general",
    q: "What is the primary objective of a literature review in research?",
    options: { a: "To summarize the researcher's findings", b: "To establish the credibility of the researcher", c: "To provide a theoretical framework for the study", d: "To ignore previous research findings" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "Summarising the researcher's own findings is the job of the results and discussion sections, not the literature review, which surveys others' work.",
      b: "A literature review is about situating the study in existing knowledge, not personally promoting the researcher's credibility.",
      d: "Ignoring previous findings is the opposite of a literature review's purpose, which is to synthesise prior work."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is to provide a theoretical framework for the study. A literature review systematically surveys, synthesises, and critically evaluates existing scholarship on a topic. Its primary objective is to situate the new study within the existing body of knowledge: it identifies what is already known, exposes gaps and contradictions, and supplies the theoretical and conceptual framework that guides the research questions, hypotheses, and choice of methods. By mapping prior theory and evidence, the review justifies why the study is needed and how it fits into ongoing scholarly conversation. This is why option C best captures its purpose. Summarising the researcher's own findings belongs to the results and discussion sections, not the review of others' work. Establishing the researcher's personal credibility is not the goal, though a thorough review does demonstrate command of the field. Ignoring previous research is the exact antithesis of a literature review, which exists precisely to engage with prior findings. Methodologists such as Harris Cooper have emphasised that a good review is analytical and integrative, not merely a list of studies. A strong theoretical framework grounds measurement and interpretation, making the review foundational to sound research. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)"
  },
  { id: "rml23_008", section: "general",
    q: "Which of the following best describes research ethics?",
    options: { a: "Following legal regulations only", b: "Ensuring confidentiality of data", c: "Manipulating data to support hypotheses", d: "Ignoring informed consent from participants" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "Legal compliance is necessary but not sufficient; ethics goes beyond the letter of the law to protect participant welfare.",
      c: "Manipulating data to fit a hypothesis is fabrication, a serious ethical violation, not a description of ethics.",
      d: "Ignoring informed consent breaches a core ethical principle; consent is required, not optional."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is ensuring confidentiality of data. Research ethics are the moral principles that govern how researchers treat participants and handle information, protecting the rights, dignity, and welfare of those studied. Core pillars include informed consent, voluntary participation, protection from harm, and confidentiality of the data collected. Ensuring confidentiality, keeping participants' identities and responses private and secure, is a central ethical obligation, so option B best exemplifies research ethics among the choices. These principles were codified after historical abuses in documents such as the Nuremberg Code and the Belmont Report, which articulated respect for persons, beneficence, and justice. Merely following legal regulations is insufficient, because something can be legal yet unethical; ethics imposes a higher standard centred on human welfare. Manipulating data to support a hypothesis is data fabrication or falsification, one of the gravest research misconducts, and the antithesis of ethical practice. Ignoring informed consent violates the principle of autonomy, that participants must knowingly and freely agree to take part. Institutional Review Boards or ethics committees enforce these standards. Confidentiality matters because breaches can expose participants to embarrassment, discrimination, or harm, undermining the trust on which research depends. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)"
  },
  { id: "rml23_009", section: "general",
    q: "What is the purpose of formulating a hypothesis in research?",
    options: { a: "To prove the researcher's point of view", b: "To guide the data collection and analysis process", c: "To manipulate the results", d: "To avoid conducting a literature review" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "Research tests hypotheses rather than proving a preconceived viewpoint; seeking only to prove one's opinion introduces bias.",
      c: "Manipulating results is unethical and is never the purpose of a hypothesis.",
      d: "A hypothesis is usually derived from the literature review, not a way to avoid it."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is to guide the data collection and analysis process. A hypothesis is a clear, testable, and falsifiable statement predicting a relationship between variables, derived from theory and prior research. Its purpose is to provide direction: it specifies what data to collect, which variables to measure, and how to analyse them, so that the study is focused and the prediction can be empirically confirmed or refuted. This testability requirement was emphasised by the philosopher Karl Popper, who argued that scientific statements must be capable of being falsified. A well-formed hypothesis lets the researcher define the null and alternative hypotheses, choose an appropriate statistical test, and interpret results objectively. Option A is wrong because science does not aim to prove a personal viewpoint; it subjects predictions to potential disconfirmation, and seeking only confirmation invites bias. Option C, manipulating results, is unethical and the opposite of hypothesis-driven inquiry. Option D is mistaken because hypotheses typically emerge from a thorough literature review rather than replacing it. By anchoring the research design to a specific, measurable prediction, the hypothesis keeps the study systematic and its conclusions grounded in evidence rather than opinion. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)"
  },
  { id: "rml23_010", section: "general",
    q: "Which of the following is a characteristic of a good research?",
    options: { a: "Biased findings", b: "Lack of clarity in objectives", c: "Reproducibility of results", d: "Limited scope of study" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "Bias undermines validity; good research strives to minimise bias, not embrace it.",
      b: "Clear, well-defined objectives are essential; a lack of clarity is a defect, not a virtue.",
      d: "An artificially limited scope weakens a study; good research defines an appropriate, adequate scope."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is reproducibility of results. Good research is systematic, objective, valid, reliable, and, above all, reproducible, meaning that if another competent investigator repeats the study under the same conditions, they should obtain consistent results. Reproducibility (closely tied to replicability) is a cornerstone of the scientific method because it demonstrates that findings are not due to chance, error, or the idiosyncrasies of one researcher. The value of replication was stressed by methodologists such as Ronald Fisher in experimental design and has become central to modern concerns about the replication crisis in psychology. Biased findings (option A) are a serious flaw: bias distorts results and threatens both internal and external validity, so quality research actively controls for it. Lack of clarity in objectives (option B) is a defect, because vague aims make a study impossible to evaluate or reproduce; good research states precise, answerable questions. A limited scope (option D) can weaken generalisability and completeness. Other hallmarks of sound research include transparency of method, controlled procedures, and logical analysis. Among the options, only reproducibility of results is a genuine virtue, ensuring that knowledge is dependable and cumulative. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)"
  },
  { id: "rml23_011", section: "stats",
    q: "What is a criteria for good measurement?",
    options: { a: "High cost", b: "Low reliability", c: "Consistency", d: "Subjectivity" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "Cost is a practical constraint, not a criterion of measurement quality.",
      b: "Low reliability is a weakness; good measurement requires high reliability.",
      d: "Subjectivity introduces measurement error; objectivity, not subjectivity, is desired."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Consistency. The two fundamental criteria for good measurement are reliability and validity. Reliability refers to the consistency or stability of a measure: a reliable instrument yields the same result on repeated use under the same conditions. Consistency is essentially the defining feature of reliability, so option C is correct. The mathematics of reliability were advanced by Lee Cronbach, whose coefficient alpha estimates the internal consistency of a scale. Validity, the other pillar, concerns whether the instrument actually measures what it claims to measure. High cost (option A) is a practical concern about feasibility, not a quality criterion; an expensive test is not automatically a good one. Low reliability (option B) is precisely the opposite of what is desired, because an inconsistent measure produces untrustworthy data. Subjectivity (option D) introduces rater bias and error, whereas good measurement strives for objectivity so that results do not depend on who administers or scores the test. Other desirable properties include standardisation, sensitivity, and practicality. But the core answer here is consistency, because without reliable, repeatable measurement, no finding built upon it can be trusted, and reliability is a precondition for validity. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)"
  },
  { id: "rml23_012", section: "assessment",
    q: "Which of the following is a type of attitude measurement scale?",
    options: { a: "Nominal scale", b: "Ordinal scale", c: "Interval scale", d: "Likert scale" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: {
      a: "Nominal scale is a level of measurement, not a purpose-built attitude scale.",
      b: "Ordinal scale is a measurement level describing rank order, not a named attitude-measurement instrument.",
      c: "Interval scale is a measurement level, not a specific attitude scale technique."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Likert scale. A Likert scale is a specific attitude measurement technique developed by Rensis Likert in 1932. It presents respondents with a series of statements and asks them to indicate their level of agreement along a symmetric range, typically five points from strongly disagree to strongly agree, and it sums or averages responses to gauge an underlying attitude. It is one of the most widely used attitude scales, alongside the Thurstone scale (Louis Leon Thurstone) and the Guttman scale (Louis Guttman). The other three options, nominal, ordinal, and interval, are Stanley Smith Stevens's levels of measurement, describing the mathematical properties of data rather than being named instruments for capturing attitudes. Nominal denotes unordered categories, ordinal denotes ranked categories, and interval denotes equal-spaced values with an arbitrary zero. While Likert responses are themselves usually treated as ordinal data, the Likert scale as a whole is a purpose-built attitude-measurement method, which is what the question asks for. Recognising named attitude scales matters in psychometrics because each has different assumptions about scoring and dimensionality, and the Likert format's ease of construction and interpretation explains its dominance in survey research. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)"
  },
  { id: "rml23_013", section: "stats",
    q: "Which of the following is an example of non-parametric data?",
    options: { a: "Height", b: "Weight", c: "Rank order", d: "Temperature in degrees Celsius" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "Height is continuous ratio data, typically analysed with parametric tests.",
      b: "Weight is continuous ratio data suited to parametric statistics.",
      d: "Temperature in Celsius is interval data, generally treated parametrically."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Rank order. Non-parametric data are data that do not meet the assumptions required for parametric statistics, chiefly that the data are at least interval level and drawn from a normally distributed population. Ordinal data, such as rank order, are the classic example: ranks convey position but not equal intervals, so their mean and standard deviation are not meaningful, and they require distribution-free (non-parametric) tests like the Mann-Whitney U test or the Spearman rank correlation, the latter devised by Charles Spearman. Height and weight are continuous ratio-level variables with a true zero and equal intervals, so they are ordinarily analysed with parametric procedures such as the t-test or Pearson correlation, developed by Karl Pearson, provided normality holds. Temperature in degrees Celsius is interval data with equal spacing, also typically handled parametrically. The essence of the distinction is that parametric tests make strong assumptions about the shape and scale of the data, whereas non-parametric tests, suited to ordinal or non-normal data, rely on ranks and are more robust when those assumptions fail. Because rank order is inherently ordinal and violates the interval assumption, it is the correct example of non-parametric data here. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)"
  },
  { id: "rml23_014", section: "stats",
    q: "What is the purpose of editing in data processing?",
    options: { a: "To organize data into categories", b: "To collect primary data", c: "To correct errors and inconsistencies in data", d: "To present data in graphical form" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "Organising data into categories is classification, a later and distinct step.",
      b: "Collecting primary data happens before processing; editing works on data already gathered.",
      d: "Presenting data graphically is data presentation, not editing."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is to correct errors and inconsistencies in data. In the sequence of data processing, editing is the first stage after data collection. Its purpose is to examine the collected raw data for errors, omissions, inconsistencies, illegible entries, and impossible values, and to correct or flag them so that the dataset is accurate, complete, and internally consistent before further analysis. For example, an editor checks that a respondent's age is plausible, that no required field is blank, and that answers do not contradict each other. This quality-control step is essential because analysis built on dirty data yields misleading conclusions. The other options describe different, later stages of processing. Organising data into categories is classification, which groups cleaned data by common characteristics. Presenting data in graphical form is data presentation through charts and graphs. Collecting primary data is the data-collection phase that precedes processing altogether, so editing cannot be about gathering new data; it operates only on information already obtained. Understanding the ordered pipeline, editing, coding, classification, tabulation, and presentation, is fundamental in research methodology, and editing sits at the front of that pipeline precisely to catch and fix errors early. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)"
  },
  { id: "rml23_015", section: "stats",
    q: "What does coding involve in data analysis?",
    options: { a: "Organizing data into categories or groups", b: "Correcting errors in data", c: "Collecting raw data", d: "Presenting data in tabular form" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: {
      b: "Correcting errors is editing, a separate earlier step, not coding.",
      c: "Collecting raw data is data collection, which precedes coding.",
      d: "Presenting data in tabular form is tabulation, which comes after coding."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is organizing data into categories or groups. Coding is the process of assigning symbols, usually numerals or short labels, to responses or observations so that they can be grouped into a limited number of categories or classes suitable for tabulation and statistical analysis. For instance, coding gender as 1 for male and 2 for female, or grouping open-ended answers into themed categories, transforms unwieldy raw responses into an organised, analysable form. By reducing many individual replies to a manageable set of coded categories, coding prepares the data for counting and cross-tabulation. It is important to distinguish coding from adjacent steps. Editing (option B) is the earlier stage of detecting and correcting errors and inconsistencies. Data collection (option C) is the gathering of raw information, which happens before any processing. Tabulation (option D) is the arrangement of coded data into rows and columns of a table, which follows coding. In qualitative research, coding likewise refers to labelling segments of text with conceptual categories, a technique central to the grounded theory of Barney Glaser and Anselm Strauss. In both quantitative and qualitative work, the defining function of coding is categorisation, which is exactly what option A states. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)"
  },
  { id: "rml23_016", section: "stats",
    q: "What is classification in the context of data analysis?",
    options: { a: "Assigning numerical values to data", b: "Organizing data into categories or groups", c: "Correcting errors in data", d: "Presenting data in graphical form" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "Assigning numerical values is closer to coding; classification is about grouping into classes.",
      c: "Correcting errors is editing, a distinct earlier step.",
      d: "Presenting data graphically is data presentation, not classification."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is organizing data into categories or groups. Classification is the process of arranging collected and coded data into homogeneous groups or classes according to common characteristics, so that similarities and differences can be readily observed. Data may be classified by qualitative attributes (such as gender, religion, or region) or by quantitative magnitudes grouped into class intervals (such as age ranges or income brackets). By sorting like with like, classification condenses a mass of raw figures into an intelligible structure and lays the groundwork for tabulation and analysis. Note that this question closely parallels the coding question; both coding and classification centre on grouping, but classification specifically means arranging the data into ordered classes, whereas coding is the assignment of symbols that enables that grouping. Assigning numerical values (option A) describes coding more than classification. Correcting errors and inconsistencies (option C) is editing, the quality-control step that precedes classification. Presenting data in graphical form (option D) is data presentation, which comes later in the pipeline. The correct choice, organising data into categories or groups, captures the essence of classification as the systematic grouping stage in the data-processing sequence of editing, coding, classification, tabulation, and presentation. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)"
  },
  { id: "rml23_017", section: "stats",
    q: "Which method of data presentation involves arranging data in rows and columns?",
    options: { a: "Graphical representation", b: "Tabulation", c: "Charting", d: "Diagramming" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "Graphical representation uses graphs and plots, not the rows-and-columns grid of a table.",
      c: "Charting displays data visually as charts, not in a tabular grid.",
      d: "Diagramming presents data pictorially through diagrams rather than rows and columns."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Tabulation. Tabulation is the systematic arrangement of classified data into rows and columns of a table, so that figures can be compared, read, and analysed easily. The rows and columns form cells that display frequencies or values against categories, giving a compact, orderly summary of the data. The phrase in the question, arranging data in rows and columns, is the very definition of a table, so tabulation is the correct method. The other options are all visual, rather than tabular, forms of presentation. Graphical representation displays data through graphs such as histograms, line graphs, and scatter plots, using axes and plotted points rather than a grid of cells. Charting presents data as charts, for example bar charts or pie charts, emphasising visual comparison of magnitudes. Diagramming conveys information pictorially through diagrams. All three are visual techniques that help audiences grasp patterns at a glance, whereas tabulation preserves the exact numbers in an ordered matrix. In the research pipeline, tabulation follows editing, coding, and classification, and it typically precedes graphical display, since well-tabulated data are the basis from which charts and graphs are drawn. Its hallmark remains the row-and-column structure. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)"
  },
  { id: "rml23_018", section: "general",
    q: "What is the purpose of a research report?",
    options: { a: "To collect data", b: "To present raw data", c: "To communicate research findings", d: "To conduct data analysis" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "Data collection is an earlier phase of the research process, not the function of the final report.",
      b: "A report interprets and summarises data; presenting raw, unprocessed data is not its purpose.",
      d: "Data analysis is performed before the report; the report communicates the analysed results."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is to communicate research findings. A research report is the formal, written culmination of a study whose primary purpose is to communicate the research problem, methods, results, and conclusions to an audience of peers, decision-makers, or the wider public. It documents what was investigated, how, and what was found, so that others can understand, evaluate, and build upon the work, and, importantly, so that the study can be scrutinised and replicated. This communicative and archival function is why option C is correct. Collecting data (option A) is a distinct earlier phase of the research process; by the time the report is written, data have already been gathered. Presenting raw data (option B) is not the report's aim either; a good report presents processed, summarised, and interpreted results, not undigested raw figures, though appendices may contain supporting data. Conducting data analysis (option D) also precedes report writing; the report communicates the analysis rather than performing it. A standard report follows a structured format, introduction, review of literature, methodology, results, discussion, and conclusion, echoing the IMRAD convention. Effective communication of findings is what allows research to contribute to cumulative knowledge and inform practice. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)"
  },
  { id: "rml23_019", section: "general",
    q: "In which step of writing a research report findings are interpreted?",
    options: { a: "Introduction", b: "Methodology", c: "Results", d: "Conclusion" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "The introduction states the problem, background, and objectives; it does not interpret findings.",
      b: "The methodology describes how the study was conducted, not what the results mean.",
      d: "The conclusion summarises and draws implications but the interpretation of findings against the data is the results (and discussion) stage."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Results. In a research report the results section is where the findings are presented and interpreted: the researcher reports the data obtained, organises them in tables and figures, applies statistical analysis, and explains what those numbers mean in relation to the hypotheses and research questions. It is here that raw output is translated into meaningful statements about relationships, differences, and patterns. In many report formats the interpretation carries into a discussion subsection, but among the four options given, the results stage is the point at which findings are interpreted, so option C is the best answer. The introduction (option A) sets out the problem, background, rationale, and objectives; it frames the study but reports no findings. The methodology (option B) explains the design, sample, instruments, and procedures, that is, how the study was done, not what it revealed. The conclusion (option D) restates the main findings briefly, notes limitations, and draws broader implications and recommendations, but it does not perform the detailed interpretation of the data itself. Recognising the function of each section is essential for both writing and critically reading research, and the interpretive heart of the report sits in the results. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)"
  },
  { id: "rml23_020", section: "general",
    q: "What is the purpose of referencing in a research report?",
    options: { a: "To list sources used in the report", b: "To provide footnotes for additional information", c: "To present raw data", d: "To organize data into categories" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: {
      b: "Footnotes for extra commentary are a separate device; referencing specifically credits and lists sources.",
      c: "Presenting raw data has nothing to do with referencing.",
      d: "Organising data into categories is classification, unrelated to citing sources."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is to list sources used in the report. Referencing is the systematic acknowledgement of all the books, articles, and other sources a researcher has drawn upon, compiled in a reference list or bibliography and linked to in-text citations. Its purposes are to give credit to the original authors whose ideas and data were used, to allow readers to trace and verify those sources, to demonstrate the depth of the researcher's engagement with the literature, and to avoid plagiarism. Standardised styles such as APA (from the American Psychological Association), MLA, and Chicago prescribe exactly how references are formatted. Option A captures this core function of listing the sources used. Providing footnotes for additional information (option B) is a different mechanism; footnotes may offer explanatory asides, and although they can carry citations, the defining purpose of referencing is crediting and listing sources, not supplying supplementary commentary. Presenting raw data (option C) and organising data into categories, or classification (option D), belong to data processing and have nothing to do with citing literature. Proper referencing is central to academic integrity because it distinguishes the researcher's original contribution from borrowed material and makes the scholarly record transparent and verifiable. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)"
  },
  { id: "rml23_021", section: "systems",
    q: "Which Microsoft Excel feature is commonly used for tabulating data and performing statistical calculations?",
    options: { a: "Pivot Tables", b: "Charts", c: "Formulas", d: "Macros" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: {
      b: "Charts visualise data graphically but do not tabulate and cross-summarise it the way pivot tables do.",
      c: "Formulas compute individual values but are not a dedicated feature for tabulating and aggregating large datasets.",
      d: "Macros automate repetitive tasks; they are not the primary tabulation-and-summary tool."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Pivot Tables. A PivotTable is a powerful Microsoft Excel feature designed specifically to summarise, tabulate, and cross-analyse large datasets. It lets the user drag fields into rows, columns, and value areas to instantly produce cross-tabulations, and it can compute aggregate statistics such as sums, counts, averages, minimums, maximums, and percentages across groups. Because it reorganises raw records into a compact summary table with statistical calculations, it is the feature best matched to tabulating data and performing statistical calculations, making option A correct. Charts (option B) turn data into visual graphs like bar, line, and pie charts; they aid interpretation but do not themselves tabulate or aggregate the underlying records. Formulas (option C) perform calculations on individual cells or ranges (for example SUM, AVERAGE, or COUNT); while useful, they are building blocks rather than a dedicated summarisation-and-cross-tabulation tool, and building a full cross-tab manually with formulas is laborious compared with a pivot table. Macros (option D) are recorded or programmed sequences (in VBA) that automate repetitive tasks; they are about automation, not tabulation. For researchers analysing survey or experimental data in Excel, the PivotTable is the go-to instrument for quickly generating summary tables and descriptive statistics. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)"
  },
  { id: "rml23_022", section: "systems",
    q: "In MS-Excel which function is commonly used to calculate the average of a range of numbers?",
    options: { a: "MAX", b: "MIN", c: "SUM", d: "AVERAGE" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: {
      a: "MAX returns the largest value in a range, not the mean.",
      b: "MIN returns the smallest value in a range, not the mean.",
      c: "SUM adds all the values together but does not divide by the count to give the average."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is AVERAGE. In Microsoft Excel the AVERAGE function computes the arithmetic mean of a range of numbers by adding all the values and dividing by how many there are. For example, =AVERAGE(A1:A10) returns the mean of the ten values in that range. The arithmetic mean is the most common measure of central tendency, describing the typical or central value of a dataset. The other functions each perform a different single operation. MAX (option A) returns the largest number in the range, identifying the maximum, not the centre. MIN (option B) returns the smallest number, the minimum. SUM (option C) adds all the values together to give a total; while the average is conceptually SUM divided by COUNT, the SUM function alone does not perform that division and so does not yield the mean. Knowing the correct built-in function matters for accurate and efficient data analysis, because using AVERAGE avoids manual errors and instantly recalculates when data change. In statistics the mean, together with the median and mode, characterises a distribution's central tendency, and AVERAGE is Excel's direct tool for obtaining it. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)"
  },
  { id: "rml23_023", section: "systems",
    q: "Which software application is widely used for statistical analysis in research?",
    options: { a: "Microsoft Word", b: "Microsoft Excel", c: "Adobe Photoshop", d: "IBM SPSS" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: {
      a: "Microsoft Word is a word processor for writing documents, not for statistical analysis.",
      b: "Excel can do basic statistics but is a general spreadsheet, not a dedicated statistical package like SPSS.",
      c: "Adobe Photoshop is image-editing software, entirely unrelated to statistical analysis."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is IBM SPSS. SPSS (Statistical Package for the Social Sciences) is a dedicated statistical software application widely used in psychology and the social sciences for data management and analysis. Originally released in 1968 and now owned by IBM, it offers a comprehensive suite of procedures, descriptive statistics, t-tests, ANOVA, correlation and regression, factor analysis, and non-parametric tests, through both a menu-driven interface and a syntax command language, making sophisticated analysis accessible to researchers without programming expertise. Its purpose-built design for inferential statistics is why option D is correct. Microsoft Word (option A) is a word processor for composing and formatting text documents; it performs no statistical analysis. Microsoft Excel (option B) is a spreadsheet that can carry out basic calculations and some statistics via formulas and the Analysis ToolPak, but it is a general-purpose tool, not a dedicated statistical package, and it lacks the depth and rigour of SPSS for advanced procedures. Adobe Photoshop (option C) is raster image-editing software for photographs and graphics, entirely unrelated to data analysis. Alternatives to SPSS in research include R, SAS, and Stata, but among the four options, IBM SPSS is the recognised standard for statistical analysis in social-science research. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)"
  },
  { id: "rml23_024", section: "systems",
    q: "What is the primary purpose of SPSS in research?",
    options: { a: "Data entry", b: "Data analysis", c: "Report writing", d: "Data storage" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "Data entry is one capability of SPSS but not its primary purpose; the software exists to analyse data.",
      c: "Report writing is done in word processors; SPSS produces output that feeds reports but is not a report-writing tool.",
      d: "Data storage is incidental; databases handle storage, whereas SPSS is built for analysis."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Data analysis. SPSS (Statistical Package for the Social Sciences), now IBM SPSS, is built primarily to perform statistical data analysis. Its core purpose is to take a dataset and run descriptive and inferential procedures on it, computing means and standard deviations, running t-tests, ANOVA, correlation, regression, chi-square, and factor analysis, and producing tables and charts of the results. Researchers use it to test hypotheses and draw conclusions from quantitative data, which is exactly what option B states. Data entry (option A) is a supporting function; SPSS does have a Data View spreadsheet for entering or importing data, but entry is merely a preliminary step, not the software's reason for existing. Report writing (option C) is the domain of word processors such as Microsoft Word; SPSS generates statistical output that researchers then incorporate into reports, but it is not itself a report-authoring tool. Data storage (option D) is better handled by database systems; SPSS saves data files, yet storage is incidental to its analytical mission. Understanding that SPSS is fundamentally an analysis engine helps researchers use it appropriately: it turns raw numbers into interpretable statistics, which is the heart of quantitative research. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)"
  },
  { id: "rml23_025", section: "systems",
    q: "Which SPSS feature is commonly used for tabulating and representing data graphically?",
    options: { a: "Syntax editor", b: "Data view", c: "Output viewer", d: "Chart builder" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: {
      a: "The Syntax editor is for writing command code, not for building graphs.",
      b: "Data View is the spreadsheet where data are entered and viewed, not a graphing tool.",
      c: "The Output Viewer displays results after analysis but is not the feature used to construct charts."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Chart Builder. In IBM SPSS, the Chart Builder is the dedicated interactive feature for creating and customising graphs. It lets users drag and drop variables and chart elements onto a canvas to build bar charts, histograms, scatterplots, boxplots, line graphs, and pie charts, and to preview and refine them before generating the final visual. Because it is the tool specifically designed for graphically representing data, option D is correct. The Syntax editor (option A) is a window where users type and run SPSS command language; it is for programming and automating analyses, not for drawing charts through a visual interface. Data View (option B) is one of the two tabs of the Data Editor (the other being Variable View); it displays the dataset as a spreadsheet of rows and columns for entering and inspecting data, but it does not produce graphics. The Output Viewer (option C) is the window where the results of analyses, tables, statistics, and any charts already generated, are displayed and can be edited or exported; it shows output rather than being the construction tool for new charts. Being able to identify the correct SPSS component for each task improves efficiency, and the Chart Builder is the standard route to graphical data presentation in SPSS. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)"
  },
  { id: "rml23_026", section: "systems",
    q: "Which software is commonly used for data representation, tabulation and statistical calculation in research?",
    options: { a: "Microsoft Word", b: "Adobe Illustrator", c: "Microsoft Excel", d: "Adobe InDesign" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "Microsoft Word is a word processor for text, not a tool for tabulation and statistical calculation.",
      b: "Adobe Illustrator is vector graphic design software, not a data-analysis tool.",
      d: "Adobe InDesign is desktop publishing software for layout, unrelated to statistical calculation."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Microsoft Excel. Microsoft Excel is a spreadsheet application that combines all three capabilities named in the question: it tabulates data in a grid of rows and columns, represents data graphically through built-in charts (bar, line, pie, scatter), and performs statistical calculations via functions and the Analysis ToolPak (means, standard deviations, correlations, regression, and more). This versatility makes it one of the most widely used tools in everyday research for organising and analysing data, so option C is correct. Microsoft Word (option A) is a word processor built for composing and formatting text; it can hold simple tables but is not designed for tabulation and statistical computation. Adobe Illustrator (option B) is professional vector-graphics software for illustrations and logos, with no data-analysis function. Adobe InDesign (option D) is a desktop-publishing program for page layout of books, magazines, and brochures, again unrelated to statistics. While dedicated packages such as SPSS or R are preferred for advanced inferential analysis, Excel remains the common general-purpose choice for tabulating, charting, and calculating in research because it is accessible, familiar, and capable of handling the core tasks of descriptive data work in a single environment. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)"
  },
  { id: "rml23_027", section: "general",
    q: "What is the significance of research in business?",
    options: { a: "It helps in generating profits only", b: "It provides insights for decision-making", c: "It is solely for academic purposes", d: "It has no relevance in the business world" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "Profit is one possible outcome, but the word only makes this too narrow; research informs many decisions beyond profit.",
      c: "Business research is applied and practical, not solely academic.",
      d: "Claiming research has no relevance in business is plainly false; it is widely used to guide strategy."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is that it provides insights for decision-making. The significance of research in business lies in generating reliable information and insights that reduce uncertainty and support sound, evidence-based decisions. Market research, consumer studies, competitor analysis, and operational data help managers understand customer needs, evaluate opportunities and risks, set prices, design products, plan marketing, and allocate resources wisely. By replacing guesswork with data, research improves the quality of strategic and tactical choices, which is exactly what option B states. Option A is too narrow because of the word only: while research can contribute to profitability, it also informs decisions about customer satisfaction, quality, efficiency, and long-term strategy that are not reducible to immediate profit. Option C is wrong because business research is inherently applied and practical, aimed at solving real organisational problems rather than existing solely for academic knowledge. Option D is plainly false, since research is deeply relevant to the business world and underpins modern management practice. The overarching value of research, whether in business or any field, is that it provides a systematic basis for understanding a situation and choosing among alternatives, and in commerce that translates directly into better-informed decision-making. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)"
  },
  { id: "rml23_028", section: "general",
    q: "In which method does the researcher start with a theory and then test it with observations?",
    options: { a: "Inductive reasoning", b: "Deductive reasoning", c: "Abductive reasoning", d: "Hypothetico-deductive method" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "Inductive reasoning moves the other way, from specific observations up to a general theory.",
      c: "Abductive reasoning infers the most likely explanation for observations, not testing a pre-existing theory top-down.",
      d: "The hypothetico-deductive method incorporates deduction but is a fuller cycle; the plain top-down theory-to-observation move is deductive reasoning itself."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Deductive reasoning. Deductive reasoning is a top-down logical approach that begins with a general theory or premise and works down to specific predictions that are then tested against observations. The researcher starts with an established theory, derives a hypothesis from it, and collects data to confirm or refute that hypothesis, moving from the general to the particular. This top-down, theory-first sequence is precisely what the question describes, so option B is correct. Inductive reasoning (option A) runs in the opposite direction: it is bottom-up, starting from specific observations and detecting patterns to build up toward a broader generalisation or theory. Abductive reasoning (option C), a concept developed by the philosopher Charles Sanders Peirce, infers the most plausible explanation for a set of observations; it is inference to the best explanation rather than the testing of a pre-existing theory. The hypothetico-deductive method (option D), associated with Karl Popper, is a fuller scientific cycle in which hypotheses deduced from theory are tested and potentially falsified; it uses deduction but is a more elaborate framework than the simple move the question names. Because the stem specifies starting with a theory and testing it with observations, the plain answer is deductive reasoning. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)"
  },
  { id: "rml23_029", section: "general",
    q: "Which approach is commonly associated with qualitative research?",
    options: { a: "Deductive reasoning", b: "Inductive reasoning", c: "Experimental design", d: "Quantitative analysis" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "Deductive reasoning, testing theory top-down, is more typical of quantitative, hypothesis-testing research.",
      c: "Experimental design with manipulation and control is a hallmark of quantitative research.",
      d: "Quantitative analysis is by definition the numeric side, not the qualitative approach."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Inductive reasoning. Qualitative research is commonly associated with an inductive, bottom-up approach: the researcher gathers rich descriptive data (interviews, observations, texts), looks for recurring patterns and themes, and builds concepts or theory up from that data rather than starting with a fixed hypothesis. This exploratory, theory-generating logic fits the aims of qualitative inquiry, which seeks to understand meaning and experience in context, so option B is correct. The classic exemplar is grounded theory, developed by Barney Glaser and Anselm Strauss, in which theory emerges directly from systematically coded qualitative data. Deductive reasoning (option A) is the top-down, theory-testing logic more typical of quantitative research, where a hypothesis derived from existing theory is tested against data. Experimental design (option C), with its manipulation of independent variables and control of conditions in the tradition of Ronald Fisher, is a quantitative approach aimed at establishing cause and effect, not a qualitative one. Quantitative analysis (option D) is by definition the numeric, statistical side of research and thus the opposite of the qualitative orientation. Recognising that qualitative work tends to reason inductively, from particular observations toward broader understanding, is fundamental to matching research questions with appropriate methods. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)"
  },
  { id: "rml23_030", section: "general",
    q: "In which method does the researcher gather data, look for patterns, and then generate a theory or hypothesis?",
    options: { a: "Deductive reasoning", b: "Hypothetico-deductive method", c: "Inductive reasoning", d: "Abductive reasoning" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "Deductive reasoning starts from theory and moves to observation, the reverse of gathering data first to build theory.",
      b: "The hypothetico-deductive method deduces and tests hypotheses from theory rather than generating theory from patterns in data.",
      d: "Abductive reasoning infers the single best explanation for a puzzling observation, not the systematic pattern-to-theory generalisation described."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Inductive reasoning. Inductive reasoning is the bottom-up process in which the researcher first gathers specific observations or data, then searches for regularities and patterns within them, and finally generalises those patterns into a broader theory or hypothesis. The direction of inference is from the particular to the general, and theory is the output rather than the starting point, which is exactly what the question describes, making option C correct. Deductive reasoning (option A) works in reverse, beginning with a general theory and deducing specific predictions to test, so it cannot be the method that generates theory from data. The hypothetico-deductive method (option B), associated with Karl Popper, involves formulating hypotheses deduced from existing theory and then attempting to falsify them through observation; it tests rather than builds theory from scratch. Abductive reasoning (option D), introduced by Charles Sanders Peirce, is inference to the best explanation, proposing the most likely single account for a surprising observation, which differs from the systematic accumulation of data and pattern-based generalisation described here. Inductive reasoning underlies much exploratory and qualitative research, including grounded theory by Barney Glaser and Anselm Strauss, precisely because it lets theory emerge from evidence. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)"
  },
  { id: "rml23_031", section: "general",
    q: "Inductive reasoning is often used in which stage of the research process?",
    options: { a: "Formulating hypotheses", b: "Data collection", c: "Theory development", d: "Literature review" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "Formulating hypotheses to test is more characteristic of a deductive starting point than of induction's endpoint.",
      b: "Data collection is a phase where data are gathered; induction is the reasoning applied to build from that data toward theory.",
      d: "The literature review surveys existing work; it is not the stage where inductive generalisation produces new theory."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Theory development. Inductive reasoning moves from specific observations to broader generalisations, so it is most closely tied to the theory-development stage of research, where patterns detected in the collected data are synthesised into new concepts, models, or theories. After data are gathered and analysed for regularities, induction is the logic that lets the researcher generalise those regularities into an emergent theory, which is exactly what option C denotes. Formulating hypotheses (option A) typically reflects a deductive stance, in which a hypothesis is derived from existing theory to be tested; although induction can suggest tentative hypotheses, the option's framing points to the deductive theory-testing tradition rather than to induction's culmination. Data collection (option B) is the phase of gathering observations; it provides the raw material for induction but is not itself the reasoning stage where theory is generated. The literature review (option D) surveys and synthesises prior scholarship to frame the study, not the stage where inductive generalisation yields new theory. The exploratory, theory-building character of inductive reasoning is why it is central to approaches like grounded theory, developed by Barney Glaser and Anselm Strauss, whose whole purpose is to develop theory grounded in data. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)"
  },
  { id: "rml23_032", section: "general",
    q: "Which method is characterized by moving from specific observations to broader generalizations?",
    options: { a: "Deductive reasoning", b: "Inductive reasoning", c: "Experimental design", d: "Qualitative analysis" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "Deductive reasoning moves from broad generalisations to specific predictions, the reverse direction.",
      c: "Experimental design is a research strategy for testing causal hypotheses, not a description of the inductive direction of inference.",
      d: "Qualitative analysis often uses induction but is a category of analysis, not the name of the reasoning direction the question defines."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Inductive reasoning. Inductive reasoning is defined by its direction of inference: it moves from specific, particular observations toward broader, general conclusions or theories. The researcher notices individual instances, identifies a recurring pattern, and then generalises to a wider principle, so the phrase in the question, from specific observations to broader generalizations, is the textbook definition of induction, making option B correct. Deductive reasoning (option A) runs in exactly the opposite direction, from broad general premises or theory down to specific predictions and instances, so it cannot be the answer. Experimental design (option C) is a methodological strategy for manipulating variables and controlling conditions to test causal hypotheses, in the tradition of Ronald Fisher; it is a type of study, not a label for the specific-to-general direction of reasoning. Qualitative analysis (option D) frequently employs inductive logic and is associated with it, but qualitative analysis names a family of data-analysis approaches rather than the reasoning pattern itself, so it is not the precise term the question seeks. A key caution about induction is that its conclusions are probable rather than certain, since a general claim inferred from limited observations can always be overturned by a new counterexample. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)"
  },
  { id: "rml23_033", section: "general",
    q: "In deductive reasoning, the validity of conclusions depends on the :",
    options: { a: "Breadth of observations", b: "Depth of data collected", c: "Soundness of the initial theory", d: "Use of statistical analysis" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "Breadth of observations matters for inductive generalisation, not for the validity of a deductive conclusion.",
      b: "Depth of data concerns empirical richness; deductive validity flows from the premises, not the data volume.",
      d: "Statistical analysis is a tool for handling data, not the source of logical validity in a deductive argument."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Soundness of the initial theory. In deductive reasoning, a conclusion follows necessarily from its premises: if the premises are true and the logical structure is valid, the conclusion must be true. This means the trustworthiness of a deductive conclusion depends critically on the soundness of the starting premises or initial theory. If the general theory from which the argument proceeds is flawed or false, then even a logically valid deduction will yield a false conclusion, because deduction preserves truth only when the premises are themselves true. Hence option C is correct: the whole edifice rests on the quality of the initial theory. Breadth of observations (option A) is relevant to inductive reasoning, where many observations strengthen a generalisation, but it does not govern deductive validity. Depth of data collected (option B) likewise concerns empirical richness rather than logical entailment; deduction derives conclusions from premises, not from the volume of data. Use of statistical analysis (option D) is an analytical technique for summarising and testing data, not the basis of logical validity in a deductive chain. This is why philosophers of science, including Karl Popper, stressed that a deductive argument can be logically valid yet unsound if it starts from a false theory, underscoring the importance of well-grounded premises. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)"
  },
  { id: "rml23_034", section: "general",
    q: "What is the primary purpose of citing sources in research papers?",
    options: { a: "To avoid legal consequences", b: "To give credit to the original authors", c: "To make the paper longer", d: "To confuse the reader" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "Avoiding legal consequences is a secondary concern; the core scholarly purpose is crediting and enabling verification.",
      c: "Padding length is never a legitimate purpose of citation.",
      d: "Citations exist to clarify sources and aid the reader, not to confuse."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is to give credit to the original authors. Citing sources serves the fundamental scholarly purpose of acknowledging the ideas, data, and words that a researcher has borrowed from others, giving proper credit to the original authors. In doing so, citation upholds academic integrity, prevents plagiarism, allows readers to trace and verify the cited work, and situates the new study within the existing body of knowledge. Standardised citation styles such as APA (from the American Psychological Association), MLA, and Chicago provide the formatting rules for doing this consistently. Option B captures this primary purpose directly. Avoiding legal consequences (option A) may be a peripheral benefit, since failing to cite can raise copyright or misconduct issues, but the central aim of citation is ethical acknowledgement and transparency, not legal self-protection. Making the paper longer (option C) is never a legitimate reason to cite; padding is a misuse, and citations are meant to be relevant and purposeful. Confusing the reader (option D) is the opposite of citation's function, which is to clarify the origins of ideas and let readers locate the sources. By crediting predecessors and enabling verification, citation makes the scholarly record honest, traceable, and cumulative, which is why it lies at the heart of responsible research writing. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)"
  },
  { id: "rml23_035", section: "general",
    q: "Which of the following is NOT a form of plagiarism?",
    options: { a: "Collusion", b: "Citation", c: "Patchwriting", d: "Ghost-writing" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "Collusion, unauthorised collaboration passed off as individual work, is a recognised form of academic dishonesty akin to plagiarism.",
      c: "Patchwriting, lightly paraphrasing a source while keeping its structure and wording without proper credit, is a form of plagiarism.",
      d: "Ghost-writing, submitting work written by someone else as one's own, is a form of academic misconduct and plagiarism."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Citation. This is a NOT question, so the correct choice is the option that is legitimate rather than dishonest. Citation is the proper, ethical acknowledgement of sources: by attributing ideas, quotations, and data to their original authors, citation is precisely the practice that prevents plagiarism, so it is not a form of it. The other three options are all types of academic dishonesty. Collusion (option A) is unauthorised collaboration in which students work together on an assignment meant to be individual and present the joint effort as their own; it is a recognised integrity violation closely allied to plagiarism. Patchwriting (option C) is a subtle form of plagiarism in which a writer copies a source and makes only superficial changes, swapping a few words or rearranging phrases, while keeping the original structure and much of the wording, without adequate paraphrase or attribution. Ghost-writing (option D) involves submitting work actually authored by someone else as one's own, whether purchased or otherwise obtained, which is a clear breach of authorship integrity. Plagiarism broadly means presenting another's work or ideas as one's own without proper credit. Because citation is the very mechanism of giving that credit, it is the one option here that is not plagiarism. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)"
  },
  { id: "rml23_036", section: "cognitive",
    q: "Who is known for his work on the concept of bounded rationality, which suggests that decision-making is often constrained by factors such as limited information and cognitive resources?",
    options: { a: "Herbert Simon", b: "Daniel Kahneman", c: "Amos Tversky", d: "Howard Gardner" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: {
      b: "Daniel Kahneman is known for prospect theory and heuristics and biases with Tversky, not for coining bounded rationality.",
      c: "Amos Tversky collaborated with Kahneman on judgment under uncertainty, but bounded rationality is Simon's concept.",
      d: "Howard Gardner is known for the theory of multiple intelligences, an unrelated area."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Herbert Simon. Herbert Simon introduced the concept of bounded rationality, the idea that human decision-making is limited by the information available, the cognitive limitations of the mind, and the finite time available to make a choice. Rather than optimising perfectly as classical economics assumes, people satisfice, that is, they settle for an option that is good enough given their constraints. Simon received the Nobel Prize in Economics in 1978 largely for this work, which reshaped economics, psychology, and organisational theory. Option A is therefore correct. Daniel Kahneman (option B) and Amos Tversky (option C) are famous for the heuristics-and-biases programme and for prospect theory, showing how people systematically deviate from rationality through mental shortcuts; their work builds on the spirit of bounded rationality but they did not coin the term, which is Simon's. Kahneman won the Nobel Prize in 2002; Tversky had died in 1996 and so was ineligible. Howard Gardner (option D) is known for the theory of multiple intelligences, proposing that intelligence comprises several distinct modalities such as linguistic and spatial; this is unrelated to decision-making constraints. Bounded rationality remains foundational to behavioural economics and to understanding real-world, resource-limited human judgement. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)"
  },
  { id: "rml23_037", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Which area of the brain is primarily associated with the consolidation of long-term memories?",
    options: { a: "Hippocampus", b: "Amygdala", c: "Prefrontal Cortex", d: "Cerebellum" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: {
      b: "The amygdala processes emotion and modulates the emotional strength of memories, but it is not the primary consolidation structure.",
      c: "The prefrontal cortex supports working memory and executive function rather than the consolidation of new long-term memories.",
      d: "The cerebellum is chiefly involved in motor coordination and procedural learning, not declarative memory consolidation."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Hippocampus. The hippocampus, a seahorse-shaped structure in the medial temporal lobe, plays a central role in consolidating new information from short-term into long-term declarative memory, that is, memory for facts and events. Its importance was dramatically revealed by the case of patient H.M. (Henry Molaison), studied by Brenda Milner, whose bilateral hippocampal removal left him unable to form new long-term memories (anterograde amnesia) while sparing older memories and motor learning. This showed the hippocampus is essential for encoding and consolidating new declarative memories, even though the memories are eventually stored in the neocortex. Option A is thus correct. The amygdala (option B), also in the temporal lobe, processes emotion, especially fear, and modulates how strongly emotional experiences are remembered, but it is not the primary consolidation site. The prefrontal cortex (option C) supports working memory, attention, and executive control rather than the consolidation of new long-term memories. The cerebellum (option D) governs motor coordination, balance, and procedural (implicit) learning such as classically conditioned responses, not declarative memory. The concept of synaptic consolidation, involving long-term potentiation described by Donald Hebb's principle that cells that fire together wire together, underlies how the hippocampus stabilises new memories. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)"
  },
  { id: "rml23_038", section: "cognitive",
    q: "Which approach to perception emphasizes the role of unconscious inference and probabilistic reasoning in interpreting sensory information?",
    options: { a: "Computational approach", b: "Constructivist approach", c: "Direct perception approach", d: "Information processing approach" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "The computational approach, associated with David Marr, models vision as information-processing algorithms but is not defined by unconscious inference.",
      c: "The direct perception approach, from James Gibson, holds that the environment provides sufficient information directly, opposing the need for inference.",
      d: "The information processing approach is a broad framework of stages of processing, not specifically the unconscious-inference view."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Constructivist approach. The constructivist approach to perception holds that perception is an active process in which the brain constructs our experience of the world by combining incoming sensory data with prior knowledge, expectations, and probabilistic inference. Its intellectual root is Hermann von Helmholtz's idea of unconscious inference: because the retinal image is ambiguous and impoverished, the perceptual system unconsciously infers the most likely interpretation of the scene, effectively making a best guess. Richard Gregory later championed this top-down, hypothesis-testing view of perception. Because the question emphasises unconscious inference and probabilistic reasoning, the constructivist approach is the correct match, making option B right. The direct perception approach (option C), developed by James J. Gibson, is the direct opposite: it argues that the environment provides rich, sufficient information (invariants and affordances) so that perception is direct and requires no inference or internal construction. The computational approach (option D pairing aside), associated with David Marr, treats vision as a series of information-processing computations from image to 3-D model, focusing on the algorithms rather than on unconscious inference per se. The information processing approach describes perception and cognition as flowing through stages, a general framework rather than the specific inference-based constructivist claim. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)"
  },
  { id: "rml23_039", section: "cognitive",
    q: "Which psychologist is associated with the concept of \"affordances\", which refers to the opportunities for action provided by the environment?",
    options: { a: "Hermann von Helmholtz", b: "James J. Gibson", c: "Richard Gregory", d: "Ulric Neisser" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "Hermann von Helmholtz is linked to unconscious inference in perception, not to affordances.",
      c: "Richard Gregory championed the constructivist, inference-based view, the opposite of Gibson's direct perception and affordances.",
      d: "Ulric Neisser founded cognitive psychology and later ecological approaches to memory, but affordances is Gibson's concept."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is James J. Gibson. James J. Gibson coined the term affordances to describe the action possibilities that the environment offers an organism: a chair affords sitting, a handle affords grasping, a surface affords walking. Affordances are a cornerstone of his ecological theory of direct perception, which argues that the environment presents rich, invariant information that organisms perceive directly, without the need for internal inference or mental construction. Perception, for Gibson, is geared to action; we perceive what we can do with objects. Hence option B is correct. Hermann von Helmholtz (option A) is associated with the very different idea of unconscious inference, a foundation of the constructivist view that perception is an inferential best guess, which contrasts with Gibson's direct perception. Richard Gregory (option C) was a leading proponent of that constructivist, top-down, hypothesis-testing account, essentially the opposing camp to Gibson's, so he is not linked to affordances. Ulric Neisser (option D) is often called the father of cognitive psychology for his 1967 book Cognitive Psychology, and he later advocated an ecological approach to memory, but the specific concept of affordances belongs to Gibson. The notion of affordances has since become influential well beyond perception, notably in design and human-computer interaction. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)"
  },
  { id: "rml23_040", section: "cognitive",
    q: "According to the Bayesian approach to perception, our perceptual experiences are influenced by :",
    options: { a: "Genetic predispositions", b: "Cultural Background", c: "Prior beliefs and sensory evidence", d: "Emotional states" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "Genetic predispositions may shape perceptual systems broadly but are not the two ingredients the Bayesian framework specifies.",
      b: "Cultural background can influence perception but is not the core prior-plus-likelihood combination of Bayesian inference.",
      d: "Emotional states can bias perception but are not the defining variables in the Bayesian model."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is prior beliefs and sensory evidence. The Bayesian approach to perception treats the brain as an inference machine that combines two sources of information according to Bayes's theorem, named after the Reverend Thomas Bayes. The first source is the prior, our pre-existing beliefs or expectations about what is likely to be out there in the world. The second is the likelihood, the current sensory evidence arriving from the senses. The brain integrates the prior with the sensory data to compute a posterior, the most probable interpretation of the scene. Perception is thus a weighted blend of what we expect and what we sense, which is exactly what option C states. This framework formalises Hermann von Helmholtz's older idea of unconscious inference and underlies modern predictive-coding accounts of the brain. Genetic predispositions (option A) may shape the perceptual apparatus over evolution but are not the two variables the Bayesian model manipulates. Cultural background (option B) and emotional states (option D) can certainly bias perception, and could be folded into priors, but neither is the defining pair of ingredients in the Bayesian formulation. The essence of the Bayesian account is the principled combination of prior beliefs with incoming sensory evidence. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)"
  },
  { id: "rml23_041", section: "stats",
    q: "Snowball sampling is best described as :",
    options: { a: "A technique where participants are randomly selected from a population", b: "A method of sampling where existing participants recruit future participants", c: "A sampling strategy that ensures equal representation of all subgroups within a population", d: "A process of selecting participants based on their availability and convenience" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "Random selection from a population describes simple random sampling, a probability method, not snowball sampling.",
      c: "Ensuring equal representation of subgroups describes stratified sampling, not the referral-based snowball method.",
      d: "Selecting on availability and convenience describes convenience sampling, a different non-probability technique."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is a method of sampling where existing participants recruit future participants. Snowball sampling is a non-probability sampling technique in which initial participants refer or recruit other people they know who meet the study criteria, and those new participants in turn refer still others, so the sample grows like a rolling snowball. It is especially valuable for reaching hidden, hard-to-access, or stigmatised populations, such as drug users, undocumented migrants, or members of rare groups, where no sampling frame exists and referrals through social networks are the only practical route to participants. Because it relies on chain referral rather than random selection, it is non-probability and susceptible to bias, since the sample is shaped by the participants' social connections. Option B captures this referral chain exactly. Option A describes simple random sampling, a probability method in which every unit has an equal chance of selection. Option C describes stratified sampling, which divides the population into subgroups and samples within each to ensure representation. Option D describes convenience sampling, in which participants are chosen simply because they are easy to reach. While convenience and snowball sampling are both non-probability and both trade rigour for accessibility, the defining feature of snowball sampling is participant-driven recruitment through referrals. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)"
  },
  { id: "rml23_042", section: "development",
    q: "According to Erik Erikson's psychosocial theory, what is the development crisis faced during adolescence?",
    options: { a: "Identity vs. Role Confusion", b: "Trust vs. Mistrust", c: "Autonomy vs. Shame and Doubt", d: "Intimacy vs. Isolation" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: {
      b: "Trust vs. Mistrust is the first stage, occurring in infancy, not adolescence.",
      c: "Autonomy vs. Shame and Doubt is the toddlerhood stage (early childhood), not adolescence.",
      d: "Intimacy vs. Isolation is the young-adulthood stage that follows adolescence."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Identity vs. Role Confusion. Erik Erikson proposed a theory of psychosocial development consisting of eight stages across the lifespan, each defined by a central conflict or crisis that must be resolved. The fifth stage, occurring during adolescence (roughly ages 12 to 18), is Identity versus Role Confusion. Here the adolescent grapples with the question who am I, exploring values, beliefs, roles, and goals to form a coherent sense of self. Successful resolution yields a stable identity and the virtue of fidelity, whereas failure leads to role confusion and uncertainty about one's place in the world. Option A is therefore correct. The other options are genuine Erikson stages but belong to different periods of life. Trust versus Mistrust (option B) is the first stage, in infancy, where the baby learns whether the world is dependable. Autonomy versus Shame and Doubt (option C) is the second stage, in toddlerhood, centred on developing independence and self-control. Intimacy versus Isolation (option D) is the sixth stage, in young adulthood, concerning the capacity to form close, committed relationships. Erikson's framework is foundational in developmental psychology because it extends personality development across the entire lifespan, and the adolescent identity crisis is among its most influential ideas. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)"
  },
  { id: "rml23_043", section: "assessment",
    q: "The concept of \"reciprocal determinism\" is central to which personality theory?",
    options: { a: "Psychodynamic theory", b: "Social cognitive theory", c: "Humanistic theory", d: "Biological theory" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "Psychodynamic theory, from Sigmund Freud, centres on unconscious drives and psychosexual stages, not reciprocal determinism.",
      c: "Humanistic theory, from Carl Rogers and Abraham Maslow, emphasises self-actualisation and free will, not the triadic reciprocal model.",
      d: "Biological theory attributes personality to genetics and physiology, not to the interaction of behaviour, cognition, and environment."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Social cognitive theory. Reciprocal determinism is a core concept of the social cognitive theory of personality developed by Albert Bandura. It proposes that human functioning results from the continuous, mutual interaction of three sets of factors: behaviour, personal or cognitive factors (such as beliefs and expectations), and the environment. Each of these influences and is influenced by the others in a triadic, bidirectional loop, so people are neither driven purely by inner forces nor simply shaped by the environment; instead they actively shape and are shaped by their surroundings. This interactive model is why option B is correct. Bandura is also famous for the concept of self-efficacy and for the Bobo doll studies demonstrating observational learning. Psychodynamic theory (option A), founded by Sigmund Freud, explains personality through unconscious conflicts, the id, ego, and superego, and psychosexual stages, not through reciprocal interaction of behaviour and environment. Humanistic theory (option C), advanced by Carl Rogers and Abraham Maslow, stresses free will, self-concept, and the drive toward self-actualisation. Biological theory (option D) roots personality in genetics, temperament, and brain physiology. Reciprocal determinism specifically captures Bandura's insight that person, behaviour, and environment jointly and reciprocally determine one another. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)"
  },
  { id: "rml23_044", section: "assessment",
    q: "According to trait theory, which of the following best describes a person high in the trait of conscientiousness?",
    options: { a: "Reserved and quiet", b: "Outgoing and sociable", c: "Organised and dependable", d: "Imaginative and creative" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "Reserved and quiet describes low extraversion (introversion), not conscientiousness.",
      b: "Outgoing and sociable describes high extraversion, a different Big Five trait.",
      d: "Imaginative and creative describes high openness to experience, not conscientiousness."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Organised and dependable. Conscientiousness is one of the Big Five personality traits, the five-factor model refined by Paul Costa and Robert McCrae and often remembered by the acronym OCEAN: Openness, Conscientiousness, Extraversion, Agreeableness, and Neuroticism. Conscientiousness reflects the tendency to be organised, responsible, disciplined, goal-directed, careful, and dependable. People high in this trait plan ahead, follow rules, meet obligations, and control impulses, so organised and dependable describes them precisely, making option C correct. The distractors each describe a different trait. Reserved and quiet (option A) characterises low extraversion, that is, introversion, describing someone who prefers solitude and is less socially assertive. Outgoing and sociable (option B) describes high extraversion, marked by sociability, energy, and positive emotionality. Imaginative and creative (option D) describes high openness to experience, the trait involving curiosity, imagination, aesthetic sensitivity, and openness to new ideas. Trait theory, with roots in the work of Gordon Allport and Raymond Cattell and culminating in the Big Five, holds that personality can be described along continuous dimensions on which individuals differ. Conscientiousness is a strong predictor of academic and job performance because organised, dependable behaviour supports achievement, which is why identifying its behavioural markers matters. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)"
  },
  { id: "rml23_045", section: "systems",
    q: "Which of the following organizational structures is characterized by decentralized decision-making authority and a focus on employee empowerment and involvement in decision-making processes?",
    options: { a: "Matrix structure", b: "Functional structure", c: "Divisional structure", d: "Flat structure" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: {
      a: "A matrix structure crosses functional and project lines, creating dual reporting; its defining feature is dual authority, not decentralised empowerment per se.",
      b: "A functional structure groups employees by specialised function and tends toward centralised, hierarchical decision-making.",
      c: "A divisional structure organises by product, market, or region; it decentralises across divisions but is not defined by flat, empowered decision-making."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Flat structure. A flat organizational structure has few or no levels of middle management between staff and executives, which widens the span of control and pushes decision-making authority downward. With fewer hierarchical layers, employees are more empowered, communication is more direct, and workers are typically involved in decisions that affect their work, exactly the decentralisation and empowerment the question describes, making option D correct. Flat structures are common in small firms and startups, where agility and employee autonomy are prized. A matrix structure (option A) overlays functional and project or product lines so that employees report to two managers at once; its hallmark is dual authority and cross-functional coordination rather than flatness and broad empowerment. A functional structure (option B) groups people by specialised function such as marketing, finance, and operations, and it tends to be centralised and hierarchical, with decisions concentrated at the top of each function. A divisional structure (option C) organises the company into semi-autonomous divisions by product, geography, or market; while divisions have some autonomy, the structure is defined by that segmentation, not by flat, participatory decision-making. Understanding these forms matters in organisational psychology because structure shapes communication, motivation, and the degree of employee involvement, and flat structures maximise the latter. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)"
  },
  { id: "rml23_046", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Which of the following best describes the function of the limbic system?",
    options: { a: "Regulation of basic physiological functions such as breathing and heart rate", b: "Processing sensory information and coordinating motor responses", c: "Regulation of emotions, memory and motivation", d: "Control of voluntary movement and decision making" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "Regulation of breathing and heart rate is the job of the brainstem (medulla), not the limbic system.",
      b: "Processing sensory information and coordinating motor responses involves the cortex and related sensorimotor areas, not primarily the limbic system.",
      d: "Control of voluntary movement and decision making is chiefly a function of the motor cortex and prefrontal cortex."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is regulation of emotions, memory and motivation. The limbic system is a group of interconnected structures located beneath the cerebral cortex, including the amygdala, hippocampus, hypothalamus, thalamus, and cingulate gyrus, often called the emotional brain. Its principal functions are the generation and regulation of emotions (the amygdala for fear and other affect), the formation of new memories (the hippocampus for declarative memory), and the drive of motivation and reward-related behaviour, along with basic drives such as hunger and sex mediated by the hypothalamus. Option C captures this triad of emotion, memory, and motivation. Regulating breathing and heart rate (option A) is the province of the brainstem, specifically the medulla oblongata, not the limbic system. Processing sensory information and coordinating motor responses (option B) is largely handled by the sensory cortices and motor systems rather than by limbic structures. Controlling voluntary movement and decision-making (option D) belongs chiefly to the motor cortex and the prefrontal cortex, the latter governing executive function. The term limbic system was popularised by Paul MacLean, whose triune brain model placed the limbic structures at the centre of emotional life. Because it links emotion with memory and motivation, the limbic system is crucial to understanding how feelings shape learning and behaviour. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)"
  },
  { id: "rml23_047", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Which part of the brainstem is responsible for controlling vital functions such as breathing and heart rate?",
    options: { a: "Midbrain", b: "Pons", c: "Medulla oblongata", d: "Thalamus" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "The midbrain handles reflexes for vision and hearing and relays motor and sensory signals, not primary control of breathing and heart rate.",
      b: "The pons relays signals between brain regions and helps regulate breathing rhythm, but the core vital centres for heart rate and respiration are in the medulla.",
      d: "The thalamus is not part of the brainstem; it is a sensory relay station in the diencephalon."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Medulla oblongata. The medulla oblongata is the lowest part of the brainstem, continuous with the spinal cord, and it houses the autonomic centres that control the body's most vital, involuntary functions, including the cardiac (heart rate) centre, the respiratory (breathing) centre, and centres for vasomotor tone, swallowing, coughing, and vomiting. Because these functions are essential to life, damage to the medulla is frequently fatal, which underscores why it is described as controlling vital functions, making option C correct. The midbrain (option A), the uppermost brainstem region, contains structures like the superior and inferior colliculi that mediate visual and auditory reflexes and relay motor and sensory signals, but it does not primarily regulate heartbeat and respiration. The pons (option B), lying between the midbrain and medulla, serves as a bridge relaying information between the cerebrum and cerebellum and contributes to regulating the rhythm of breathing, yet the fundamental control centres for heart rate and respiration reside in the medulla, not the pons. The thalamus (option D) is not part of the brainstem at all; it sits in the diencephalon and acts as the brain's main relay station for sensory information heading to the cortex. Knowing the medulla governs vital autonomic functions is fundamental to neuroanatomy. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)"
  },
  { id: "rml23_048", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Parkinson's disease is associated with the degeneration of neurons that produce which neurotransmitter?",
    options: { a: "Serotonin", b: "Dopamine", c: "Acetylcholine", d: "GABA" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "Serotonin is linked to mood regulation and depression, not to the motor degeneration of Parkinson's disease.",
      c: "Acetylcholine is depleted in Alzheimer's disease and drives muscle contraction, but Parkinson's centres on dopamine loss.",
      d: "GABA is the main inhibitory neurotransmitter and is implicated in anxiety and seizures, not in the dopaminergic loss of Parkinson's."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Dopamine. Parkinson's disease is a progressive neurodegenerative disorder caused primarily by the death of dopamine-producing neurons in a midbrain region called the substantia nigra, part of the basal ganglia circuitry that controls movement. As these dopaminergic neurons degenerate, dopamine levels in the striatum fall, disrupting the fine regulation of voluntary movement and producing the classic motor symptoms: resting tremor, rigidity, bradykinesia (slowness of movement), and postural instability. This dopamine deficit is why the standard treatment is levodopa, a dopamine precursor that the brain converts into dopamine. Hence option B is correct. Serotonin (option A) is chiefly involved in mood, sleep, and appetite, and its dysregulation is linked to depression and anxiety rather than to Parkinsonian motor decline. Acetylcholine (option C) is essential for muscle activation and for memory, and its loss in the basal forebrain is a feature of Alzheimer's disease, not the defining lesion of Parkinson's, though a dopamine-acetylcholine imbalance does contribute to Parkinson's symptoms. GABA (option D) is the brain's main inhibitory neurotransmitter, implicated in anxiety and epilepsy, not in the dopaminergic degeneration that defines Parkinson's. Understanding the dopamine link is central to the neurochemistry of movement disorders and their pharmacological treatment. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)"
  },
  { id: "rml23_049", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Which brain structure plays a key role in regulating the body's internal clock and sleep-wake cycle?",
    options: { a: "Pineal gland", b: "Hypothalamus", c: "Thalamus", d: "Pituitary gland" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: {
      b: "The hypothalamus houses the suprachiasmatic nucleus, the master clock, so it is a strong distractor, but the structure that secretes the sleep hormone melatonin is the pineal gland.",
      c: "The thalamus relays sensory information to the cortex and is not the main regulator of circadian rhythm.",
      d: "The pituitary gland is the master endocrine gland but does not regulate the sleep-wake cycle via melatonin."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Pineal gland. The pineal gland is a small endocrine structure deep in the brain that secretes the hormone melatonin, which regulates the sleep-wake cycle and helps set the body's circadian rhythm, or internal clock. Melatonin secretion rises in darkness and falls in light, signalling to the body when it is time to sleep, so the pineal gland is closely tied to the daily rhythm of sleep and wakefulness, making option A correct. This question is deliberately tricky because the hypothalamus (option B) contains the suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN), which is the master circadian pacemaker that receives light information from the retina and, in turn, controls the pineal gland's melatonin output. The SCN is arguably the primary timekeeper, but among the options, the pineal gland is the structure that directly regulates the sleep-wake cycle through melatonin, and it is the classic textbook answer for the hormone-secreting sleep regulator. The thalamus (option C) is chiefly a sensory relay station directing incoming signals to the cortex and gating consciousness, not the circadian regulator. The pituitary gland (option D) is the master endocrine gland controlling other glands, but it does not govern the sleep-wake cycle via melatonin. René Descartes famously, if mistakenly, called the pineal gland the seat of the soul, but its real role is circadian regulation. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)"
  },
  { id: "rml23_050", section: "development",
    q: "The process of synaptic pruning is most closely associated with :",
    options: { a: "Memory consolidation", b: "Neuronal development and plasticity", c: "Neurotransmitter release", d: "Long-term potentiation" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "Memory consolidation involves stabilising memories, chiefly via the hippocampus, rather than eliminating excess synapses.",
      c: "Neurotransmitter release is the moment-to-moment chemical signalling at synapses, not the developmental elimination of connections.",
      d: "Long-term potentiation strengthens synapses through use; pruning removes weak or unused synapses, so it is a related but distinct process."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is neuronal development and plasticity. Synaptic pruning is the process by which the brain eliminates weaker, redundant, or unused synaptic connections while strengthening those that are frequently used, thereby refining and optimising neural circuits. It is a hallmark of neuronal development: the young brain initially overproduces synapses, and pruning, most intense in childhood and continuing through adolescence, sculpts this excess into efficient, experience-shaped networks. This use-it-or-lose-it refinement is a central example of neural plasticity, the brain's capacity to reorganise itself in response to experience, so option B is correct. Memory consolidation (option A) refers to stabilising newly formed memories into long-term storage, a process centred on the hippocampus and cortex, not on eliminating synapses. Neurotransmitter release (option C) is the rapid, moment-to-moment chemical signalling by which one neuron communicates with another across the synaptic cleft; it is ongoing synaptic transmission, not developmental restructuring. Long-term potentiation (option D) is the persistent strengthening of synapses that are repeatedly activated, a mechanism of learning first tied to Donald Hebb's principle that neurons that fire together wire together; it is related to plasticity but describes strengthening rather than the elimination that defines pruning. Synaptic pruning is thus best understood as a developmental, plasticity-driven refinement of the brain's wiring. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)"
  },
  { id: "rml23_051", section: "general",
    q: "Who introduced the concept of \"flow\" in psychology?",
    options: { a: "Carl Rogers", b: "Abraham Maslow", c: "Mihaly Csikszentmihalyi", d: "Viktor Frankl" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "Carl Rogers developed client-centred therapy and the concept of the fully functioning person, not flow.",
      b: "Abraham Maslow created the hierarchy of needs and studied peak experiences, which resemble but are not the concept of flow.",
      d: "Viktor Frankl founded logotherapy and wrote about meaning in life, not flow."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Mihaly Csikszentmihalyi. Mihaly Csikszentmihalyi introduced the concept of flow, the mental state of complete absorption in an activity in which a person is fully immersed, energised, and focused, losing track of time and self-consciousness. Flow occurs when the challenge of a task is well matched to the person's skill level: too easy and one is bored, too hard and one is anxious, but in the balanced sweet spot, engagement becomes optimal and intrinsically rewarding. Csikszentmihalyi is a founding figure of positive psychology, and his work on flow is central to understanding intrinsic motivation, creativity, and well-being, so option C is correct. Carl Rogers (option A), a humanistic psychologist, developed client-centred (person-centred) therapy and the idea of unconditional positive regard and the fully functioning person, not flow. Abraham Maslow (option B) is known for the hierarchy of needs and for the concept of peak experiences and self-actualisation; peak experiences resemble flow in being deeply absorbing, but they are a distinct idea, and Maslow did not coin flow. Viktor Frankl (option D), a survivor of the Holocaust, founded logotherapy and emphasised the human search for meaning as the primary motivational force. The concept of flow remains a cornerstone of research on optimal experience. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)"
  },
  { id: "rml23_052", section: "cognitive",
    q: "Confirmation bias is closely related to which heuristics?",
    options: { a: "Availability heuristic", b: "Representativeness heuristic", c: "Anchoring and adjustment heuristic", d: "Recognition heuristic" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "The availability heuristic judges likelihood by how easily examples come to mind, which is related to memory salience rather than to seeking confirming evidence.",
      c: "The anchoring and adjustment heuristic involves over-relying on an initial reference value, not selectively confirming a hypothesis.",
      d: "The recognition heuristic infers value from mere recognition of an item, unrelated to confirmation-seeking."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is the Representativeness heuristic. Confirmation bias is the tendency to search for, interpret, favour, and recall information in a way that confirms one's pre-existing beliefs or hypotheses while giving less attention to contradictory evidence. It is most closely linked to the representativeness heuristic among the options because both involve judging on the basis of how well something fits a preconceived model or stereotype: people latch onto evidence that matches their expected pattern and discount that which does not. The representativeness heuristic, described by Daniel Kahneman and Amos Tversky, is the mental shortcut of estimating the probability that something belongs to a category by how much it resembles the typical member of that category, often at the expense of base rates. This resemblance-based, expectation-confirming reasoning aligns with confirmation bias. The availability heuristic (option A), also from Kahneman and Tversky, judges frequency or likelihood by how easily instances come to mind, which relates to memory salience rather than to confirming a belief. The anchoring and adjustment heuristic (option C) describes relying too heavily on an initial value and adjusting insufficiently. The recognition heuristic (option D), from Gerd Gigerenzer, infers that a recognised option is more valuable, unrelated to confirmation-seeking. The shared thread with representativeness is reasoning by fit to a prior model. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)"
  },
  { id: "rml23_053", section: "cognitive",
    q: "The representativeness heuristic can lead to errors when :",
    options: { a: "Individuals ignore base rates and focus solely on similarity", b: "Individuals rely on readily available information", c: "Individuals fail to consider alternative explanations", d: "Individuals adjust their judgements based on initial estimates" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: {
      b: "Relying on readily available information describes the availability heuristic, not the representativeness heuristic.",
      c: "Failing to consider alternative explanations is a general reasoning flaw, not the specific base-rate neglect of representativeness.",
      d: "Adjusting judgements from initial estimates describes the anchoring and adjustment heuristic, a different shortcut."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is individuals ignore base rates and focus solely on similarity. The representativeness heuristic, identified by Daniel Kahneman and Amos Tversky, is the shortcut of judging the probability that an object or event belongs to a category by how closely it resembles the typical or prototypical member of that category. Its most famous failure mode is base-rate neglect: people focus on how representative or similar a case seems and ignore the prior probability, or base rate, of the category in the population. For instance, in the classic Linda problem and the lawyer-engineer studies, participants judged category membership by stereotype fit while disregarding how common each category actually was, leading to systematic errors including the conjunction fallacy. Because the defining error of representativeness is overweighting similarity and underweighting base rates, option A is correct. Relying on readily available information (option B) describes the availability heuristic, a different shortcut based on ease of recall. Failing to consider alternative explanations (option C) is a broad reasoning shortcoming, not the specific mechanism by which representativeness misleads. Adjusting judgements based on initial estimates (option D) describes the anchoring and adjustment heuristic, in which an initial anchor unduly influences the final judgement. The signature pitfall of representativeness is precisely the neglect of base rates in favour of resemblance. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)"
  },
  { id: "rml23_054", section: "cognitive",
    q: "What term describes the phenomenon where individuals fail to notice significant changes in their enviornment, even when those changes are obvious?",
    options: { a: "Change blindness", b: "Inattentional blindness", c: "Perceptual constancy", d: "Selective attention" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: {
      b: "Inattentional blindness is failing to see an unexpected object because attention is elsewhere, not failing to notice a change between two views.",
      c: "Perceptual constancy is the stable perception of objects despite changes in sensory input, an entirely different phenomenon.",
      d: "Selective attention is the process of focusing on some stimuli while ignoring others, not the failure to detect changes."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Change blindness. Change blindness is the surprising failure to detect a substantial change in a visual scene, especially when the change coincides with a brief disruption such as a flicker, a blink, an eye movement, or a cut in a film. Because our visual system does not store a complete, detailed representation of every part of a scene, we often miss even large, obvious alterations when attention is not directed to the changing element. This is precisely the phenomenon the question describes, making option A correct. It was demonstrated in striking experiments by Daniel Simons and colleagues, including studies in which an experimenter asking for directions was swapped for a different person mid-conversation without the participant noticing. Inattentional blindness (option B), also studied by Daniel Simons and Christopher Chabris in the famous invisible gorilla experiment, is the related but distinct failure to notice an unexpected object that is in plain view because attention is engaged elsewhere; it concerns missing something present, not missing a change between two views. Perceptual constancy (option C) is the ability to perceive an object as stable in size, shape, or colour despite changing sensory input, an unrelated concept. Selective attention (option D) is the general process of focusing on certain stimuli while filtering out others. Change blindness specifically names the failure to spot changes. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)"
  },
  { id: "rml23_055", section: "cognitive",
    q: "According to the dual-coding theory of memory, which type of information is more easily remembered?",
    options: { a: "Verbal information", b: "Visual information", c: "Auditory information", d: "Semantic information" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "Verbal information is coded, but on its own it is encoded in only one system; images benefit from dual coding and so are better remembered.",
      c: "Auditory information is not the specific contrast the theory highlights; the theory contrasts verbal and visual (imagery) codes.",
      d: "Semantic information concerns meaning but is not the category the dual-coding theory singles out as most memorable."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Visual information. The dual-coding theory of memory, developed by Allan Paivio, proposes that the mind processes and stores information through two distinct but interconnected systems: a verbal system for language-based information and a nonverbal (imagery) system for pictures and visual mental images. According to the theory, concrete words and pictures can be encoded in both systems at once, whereas abstract verbal material tends to be encoded only verbally. Because information stored in two codes has two independent routes for later retrieval, it is remembered more easily and reliably. Visual information, and concrete items that readily evoke mental images, therefore enjoys a memory advantage, which is the well-documented picture superiority effect, so option B is correct. Verbal information (option A) is encoded primarily in the single verbal channel, giving it fewer retrieval routes than dually coded imagery, so it is generally less well remembered than pictures. Auditory information (option C) is not the specific comparison the theory foregrounds; dual-coding contrasts verbal and visual imagery codes rather than sensory modality per se. Semantic information (option D) concerns meaning and is important for deep processing, but it is not the category the dual-coding theory identifies as most memorable. The core insight is that combining verbal and visual codes strengthens memory, favouring visual and concrete material. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)"
  },
  { id: "rml23_056", section: "cognitive",
    q: "The misinformation effect occurs when :",
    options: { a: "Memories become more accurate over time", b: "Memories are distorted by leading questions or misleading information", c: "Memories are lost due to decay", d: "Memories are forgotten due to interference" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "Memories do not become more accurate over time; they are reconstructive and prone to distortion, the opposite of this claim.",
      c: "Loss of memory due to decay is the decay theory of forgetting, not the distortion the misinformation effect describes.",
      d: "Forgetting due to interference is interference theory, a distinct mechanism from post-event misinformation."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is memories are distorted by leading questions or misleading information. The misinformation effect refers to the way a person's memory of an event can be altered or contaminated by misleading information encountered after the event, so that the recollection becomes distorted or false. It was demonstrated in landmark research by Elizabeth Loftus, whose studies showed that the wording of a question can reshape memory; for example, participants who were asked how fast cars were going when they smashed into each other later reported higher speeds and were more likely to falsely remember broken glass than those asked about cars that hit each other. This reveals that memory is reconstructive rather than a faithful recording, and that post-event suggestion can implant errors, so option B is correct. Option A is wrong because memories do not become more accurate with time; the reconstructive nature of memory tends toward distortion, not improvement. Option C describes decay theory, the idea that memory traces fade with disuse over time, which is a mechanism of forgetting distinct from post-event distortion. Option D describes interference theory, in which other memories block retrieval (proactive or retroactive interference), again a different process. The misinformation effect specifically concerns distortion by misleading post-event information, with major implications for the reliability of eyewitness testimony. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)"
  },
  { id: "rml23_057", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Which brain structure is involved in the emotional modulation of memory?",
    options: { a: "Prefrontal cortex", b: "Thalamus", c: "Amygdala", d: "Hypothalamus" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "The prefrontal cortex supports working memory and executive control, not the emotional tagging that strengthens memories.",
      b: "The thalamus is a sensory relay station and does not modulate the emotional intensity of memories.",
      d: "The hypothalamus regulates homeostasis and basic drives, not the emotional enhancement of memory."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Amygdala. The amygdala is an almond-shaped structure in the medial temporal lobe, part of the limbic system, that is central to processing emotions, especially fear, and to the emotional modulation of memory. When an event is emotionally arousing, the amygdala interacts with the hippocampus to enhance the consolidation of that memory, which is why emotionally charged experiences tend to be remembered more vividly and durably than neutral ones. This emotional tagging explains phenomena such as flashbulb memories for shocking events. Because the amygdala strengthens memories according to their emotional significance, option C is correct. Much of this understanding comes from the work of James McGaugh, who showed that stress hormones acting via the amygdala modulate memory consolidation. The prefrontal cortex (option A) is involved in working memory, decision-making, and executive control rather than in emotionally tagging memories for enhanced storage. The thalamus (option B) is chiefly a relay station that routes sensory information to the cortex and is not responsible for the emotional intensity of memories. The hypothalamus (option D) regulates homeostatic functions and basic drives such as hunger, thirst, and temperature, and it coordinates the stress response, but it is not the structure that modulates the emotional strength of memory. The amygdala's role links emotion and memory, showing why feeling and remembering are intertwined. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)"
  },
  { id: "rml23_058", section: "stats",
    q: "Which non-parametric test is equivalent to the independent samples t-test?",
    options: { a: "Mann-Whitney U test", b: "Kruskal-Wallis test", c: "Wilcoxon signed-rank test", d: "Friedman test" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: {
      b: "The Kruskal-Wallis test is the non-parametric equivalent of a one-way ANOVA, comparing three or more independent groups, not two.",
      c: "The Wilcoxon signed-rank test is the non-parametric equivalent of the paired-samples t-test, for related samples.",
      d: "The Friedman test is the non-parametric equivalent of a repeated-measures ANOVA, for three or more related conditions."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is the Mann-Whitney U test. The independent samples t-test is a parametric procedure that compares the means of two unrelated groups, assuming interval data and normality. When those assumptions are violated, for example with ordinal data or non-normal distributions, the appropriate non-parametric alternative for comparing two independent groups is the Mann-Whitney U test, developed by Henry Mann and Donald Whitney (and equivalent to the Wilcoxon rank-sum test). It ranks all scores across both groups and tests whether the ranks differ systematically, so it directly parallels the independent samples t-test without requiring normality. Hence option A is correct. The Kruskal-Wallis test (option B), named after William Kruskal and Wilson Allen Wallis, is the non-parametric counterpart of a one-way ANOVA and is used to compare three or more independent groups, not two. The Wilcoxon signed-rank test (option C), from Frank Wilcoxon, is the non-parametric equivalent of the paired-samples (dependent) t-test, used when the two sets of scores are related, such as before-and-after measures on the same subjects. The Friedman test (option D) is the non-parametric equivalent of a repeated-measures ANOVA, for comparing three or more related conditions. Matching each non-parametric test to its parametric partner is a staple of statistics: two independent groups map to Mann-Whitney U. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)"
  },
  { id: "rml23_059", section: "stats",
    q: "The Leven's test is used to check :",
    options: { a: "Normality of data", b: "Homogeneity of variances", c: "Independence of observations", d: "Equality of group sizes" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "Normality of data is checked with tests such as Shapiro-Wilk or Kolmogorov-Smirnov, not Levene's test.",
      c: "Independence of observations is a design assumption not tested by Levene's test.",
      d: "Equality of group sizes is a matter of sample balance, not what Levene's test assesses."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is homogeneity of variances. Levene's test, developed by Howard Levene, is a statistical test used to assess the equality of variances across two or more groups, that is, the assumption of homogeneity of variance (homoscedasticity). This assumption is a prerequisite for parametric procedures such as the independent samples t-test and the analysis of variance (ANOVA), which assume that the groups being compared have roughly equal population variances. Levene's test evaluates the null hypothesis that the group variances are equal; a non-significant result (typically p greater than .05) means the assumption is met, whereas a significant result signals that variances differ and that a correction or an alternative test should be used. Hence option B is correct. Option A is wrong because normality, the assumption that data follow a normal distribution, is examined with different tests such as the Shapiro-Wilk test or the Kolmogorov-Smirnov test, or with visual tools like Q-Q plots, not with Levene's test. Option C, independence of observations, is a design feature ensured by proper sampling and is not something Levene's test evaluates. Option D, equality of group sizes, concerns sample balance and is unrelated to Levene's test, which is about variances, not counts. Checking homogeneity of variance is a routine preliminary step before running ANOVA or a t-test. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)"
  },
  { id: "rml23_060", section: "stats",
    q: "Which non-parametric test is used for comparing medians of three or more independent groups?",
    options: { a: "Mann-Whitney U test", b: "Kruskal-Wallis test", c: "Wilcoxon signed-rank test", d: "Friedman test" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "The Mann-Whitney U test compares only two independent groups, not three or more.",
      c: "The Wilcoxon signed-rank test compares two related samples, not several independent groups.",
      d: "The Friedman test compares three or more related (repeated-measures) conditions, not independent groups."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is the Kruskal-Wallis test. The Kruskal-Wallis test, named after William Kruskal and Wilson Allen Wallis, is the non-parametric alternative to the one-way analysis of variance (ANOVA). It is used to compare three or more independent groups when the data are ordinal or when the assumptions of ANOVA (such as normality and homogeneity of variance) are not met. By ranking all observations together and comparing the mean ranks across groups, it tests whether the samples come from the same distribution, effectively comparing the medians of three or more independent groups. Because the question specifies three or more independent groups and a non-parametric procedure, option B is correct. The Mann-Whitney U test (option A) is limited to comparing exactly two independent groups, so it cannot handle three or more. The Wilcoxon signed-rank test (option C), from Frank Wilcoxon, compares two related or paired samples and so applies to dependent rather than independent data. The Friedman test (option D) is also non-parametric and handles three or more conditions, but it is designed for related or repeated-measures data (the same subjects measured under multiple conditions), not independent groups. The key mapping is that independent groups with three or more levels call for Kruskal-Wallis, the rank-based analogue of one-way ANOVA. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)"
  },
  { id: "rml23_061", section: "stats",
    q: "Which of the following non-parametric tests is appropriate for repeated measures designs?",
    options: { a: "Mann-Whitney U test", b: "Kruskal-Wallis test", c: "Wilcoxon signed-rank test", d: "Friedman test" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: {
      a: "The Mann-Whitney U test is for two independent groups, not repeated measures on the same subjects.",
      b: "The Kruskal-Wallis test is for three or more independent groups, not related/repeated measures.",
      c: "The Wilcoxon signed-rank test handles two related conditions but not three or more repeated measures, which is the general repeated-measures case."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is the Friedman test. The Friedman test, developed by the economist Milton Friedman, is a non-parametric test used for repeated measures or related-samples designs with three or more conditions. It is the non-parametric equivalent of the repeated-measures (within-subjects) analysis of variance and is applied when the same subjects are measured under several conditions or at several time points and the data are ordinal or non-normal. By ranking each subject's scores across the conditions and comparing the rank totals, it tests whether the conditions differ. Because repeated-measures designs involve related observations from the same participants across multiple conditions, the Friedman test is the appropriate choice, making option D correct. The Mann-Whitney U test (option A) compares two independent groups and does not fit related, repeated observations. The Kruskal-Wallis test (option B) compares three or more independent groups, again not repeated measures on the same subjects. The Wilcoxon signed-rank test (option C) does handle related samples, but only two related conditions, so it covers the simplest repeated-measures case (two time points) rather than the general repeated-measures design with three or more conditions that the Friedman test addresses. The systematic mapping is that the Friedman test is to repeated-measures ANOVA as Kruskal-Wallis is to one-way ANOVA. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)"
  },
  { id: "rml23_062", section: "stats",
    q: "Which statistical test is used to determine if there is a significant difference between two correlation coefficients?",
    options: { a: "Independent samples t-test", b: "Pearson correlation", c: "Fisher's z-test", d: "Analysis of variance (ANOVA)" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "The independent samples t-test compares two group means, not two correlation coefficients.",
      b: "Pearson correlation measures the strength of a single linear relationship; it does not compare two correlations.",
      d: "ANOVA compares means across groups, not the difference between two correlation coefficients."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Fisher's z-test. To determine whether two correlation coefficients differ significantly, the standard procedure is Fisher's z-transformation, developed by Ronald Fisher. Because the sampling distribution of the correlation coefficient r is skewed, especially near plus or minus one, each correlation is first transformed using Fisher's r-to-z transformation, which converts r into an approximately normally distributed value (z prime) with a known standard error. The two transformed values are then compared using a z-test, allowing a formal test of the null hypothesis that the two correlations are equal. This makes Fisher's z-test the appropriate tool, so option C is correct. The independent samples t-test (option A) compares the means of two groups, not correlation coefficients, so it does not apply. Pearson correlation (option B), also from Karl Pearson, quantifies the strength and direction of a single linear relationship between two variables; it produces a correlation but does not test whether two separate correlations differ. Analysis of variance, or ANOVA (option D), tests for differences among the means of three or more groups, again a test of means rather than of correlations. The specialised task of comparing two correlations, whether from independent samples or from the same sample, relies on Fisher's z-transformation, which is why it is the correct answer. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)"
  },
  { id: "rml23_063", section: "stats",
    q: "Which of the following correlation methods is used when one variable is continuous and the other is dichotomous?",
    options: { a: "Pearson correlation coefficient", b: "Spearman's rank correlation coefficient", c: "Point-biserial correlation coefficient", d: "Biserial correlation coefficient" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "The Pearson correlation is used when both variables are continuous, not when one is a true dichotomy.",
      b: "Spearman's rank correlation is used for two ordinal (ranked) variables, not for a continuous-dichotomous pair.",
      d: "The biserial correlation applies when the dichotomous variable is artificial (an underlying continuous variable forced into two categories), not a true dichotomy."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is the Point-biserial correlation coefficient. The point-biserial correlation is used to measure the relationship between one continuous variable and one genuinely (naturally) dichotomous variable, that is, a variable with two true categories such as pass or fail, male or female, or yes or no. It is mathematically a special case of the Pearson correlation, computed when one variable is coded as 0 or 1. Because the question specifies one continuous and one dichotomous variable, option C is correct. The Pearson correlation coefficient (option A), developed by Karl Pearson, is designed for two continuous variables measured on interval or ratio scales, so it is not the specific method for a continuous-dichotomous pairing (even though point-biserial derives from it). Spearman's rank correlation coefficient (option B), from Charles Spearman, is used when both variables are ordinal or when data are ranked, not when one is continuous and the other a true dichotomy. The biserial correlation coefficient (option D) is the subtle distractor: it is used when the dichotomous variable is not a true dichotomy but an artificial one, created by splitting an underlying continuous, normally distributed variable into two categories (for example, dividing a continuous test score into pass and fail). The key distinction is true dichotomy (point-biserial) versus artificial dichotomy (biserial), and the question's plain dichotomous variable points to point-biserial. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)"
  },
  { id: "rml23_064", section: "stats",
    q: "Which type of error occurs when a researcher fails to reject the null hypothesis when it is actually false?",
    options: { a: "Type I error", b: "Type II error", c: "Type III error", d: "Type IV error" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "A Type I error is rejecting a true null hypothesis (a false positive), the opposite situation.",
      c: "Type III error is a non-standard term (sometimes meaning a correct rejection for the wrong reason) and is not the standard answer here.",
      d: "Type IV error is not a standard concept in hypothesis testing."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Type II error. In null hypothesis significance testing, two kinds of decision errors are possible, formalised by Jerzy Neyman and Egon Pearson. A Type I error occurs when the researcher rejects the null hypothesis even though it is actually true, that is, detecting an effect that is not really there, a false positive, whose probability is denoted alpha, the significance level. A Type II error occurs when the researcher fails to reject the null hypothesis even though it is actually false, that is, missing an effect that genuinely exists, a false negative, whose probability is denoted beta. Because the question describes failing to reject a false null hypothesis, this is precisely a Type II error, so option B is correct. Option A, Type I error, is the reverse mistake of rejecting a true null hypothesis, so it does not fit. Options C and D, Type III and Type IV errors, are not part of the standard two-error framework of classical hypothesis testing; Type III error is occasionally used informally to mean correctly rejecting the null but for the wrong reason or in the wrong direction, but it is not the conventional answer, and Type IV error is not a recognised standard concept. The complement of a Type II error is statistical power (1 minus beta), the probability of correctly detecting a true effect, which is why understanding Type II error is central to study design and sample-size planning. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)"
  },
  { id: "rml23_065", section: "stats",
    q: "Which of the following is true about Latin square designs?",
    options: { a: "They are primarily used in correlational studies", b: "They are characterized by equal group sizes", c: "They control for order effects in repeated measures designs", d: "They involve the manipulation of one independent variable with multiple levels" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "Latin square designs are experimental, not correlational; they involve manipulation and control, not mere association.",
      b: "Equal group sizes is not the defining feature of a Latin square; the design's purpose is counterbalancing order.",
      d: "Manipulating one independent variable with multiple levels describes a simple single-factor design, not the counterbalancing function of a Latin square."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is they control for order effects in repeated measures designs. A Latin square design is an experimental design used chiefly to counterbalance the order in which conditions or treatments are presented in repeated-measures (within-subjects) studies, so that each condition appears equally often in each ordinal position and no condition is systematically advantaged or disadvantaged by its place in the sequence. This controls for order effects such as practice, fatigue, and carryover, which can confound within-subjects experiments where the same participants experience every condition. The design arranges treatments in a square grid so that each treatment occurs exactly once in each row and once in each column, a structure whose mathematics were formalised by Ronald Fisher in experimental design. Because counterbalancing to control order effects is its defining purpose, option C is correct. Option A is wrong because Latin square designs are experimental, involving manipulation and control, not correlational studies that merely measure associations. Option B is incorrect because equal group sizes, while sometimes a feature, is not what defines or distinguishes a Latin square; its essence is balanced ordering. Option D describes a basic single-factor design in which one independent variable is manipulated at several levels, which does not capture the counterbalancing role that makes the Latin square distinctive. Its value lies in efficiently controlling sequence-related confounds. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)"
  },
  { id: "rml23_066", section: "social",
    q: "According to the Theory of Planned Behavior, which of the following factors influences an individual's intention to engage in health behaviors?",
    options: { a: "Attitude, subjective norms and self-efficacy", b: "Genetics, personality traits and socio-economic status", c: "Cultural beliefs, social support and health literacy", d: "Physical environment, emotional intelligence and coping strategies" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: {
      b: "Genetics, personality traits, and socio-economic status are not the components of the Theory of Planned Behavior.",
      c: "Cultural beliefs, social support, and health literacy are relevant to health but are not the three TPB predictors of intention.",
      d: "Physical environment, emotional intelligence, and coping strategies do not correspond to the constructs of the Theory of Planned Behavior."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is attitude, subjective norms and self-efficacy. The Theory of Planned Behavior, developed by Icek Ajzen, holds that the strongest predictor of behaviour is a person's intention to perform it, and that intention is in turn shaped by three factors. The first is attitude toward the behaviour, one's favourable or unfavourable evaluation of performing it. The second is subjective norms, the perceived social pressure from important others to perform or not perform the behaviour. The third is perceived behavioural control, the person's belief in their ability to carry out the behaviour, a construct closely related to Albert Bandura's concept of self-efficacy. These three components jointly determine intention, and intention drives behaviour, so option A correctly names them. The Theory of Planned Behavior extended Ajzen and Martin Fishbein's earlier Theory of Reasoned Action by adding perceived behavioural control to account for behaviours not fully under voluntary control. Option B (genetics, personality traits, socio-economic status), option C (cultural beliefs, social support, health literacy), and option D (physical environment, emotional intelligence, coping strategies) all list plausible-sounding influences on health but none matches the three specific constructs of the model. The theory is widely applied in health psychology to predict and change behaviours such as exercise, smoking cessation, and safe practices. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)"
  },
  { id: "rml23_067", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Which of the following factors is most strongly associated with the development of Type 2 diabetes?",
    options: { a: "Genetics", b: "Poor diet and sedentary lifestyle", c: "Psychological stress", d: "Exposure to environmental toxins" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: {
      a: "Genetics contributes to risk, but among the options the modifiable lifestyle factors are most strongly and directly associated with Type 2 diabetes.",
      c: "Psychological stress can indirectly affect glucose regulation but is not the strongest associated factor.",
      d: "Exposure to environmental toxins is a minor and less established contributor compared with diet and inactivity."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is poor diet and sedentary lifestyle. Type 2 diabetes is a metabolic disorder characterised by insulin resistance and relative insulin deficiency, and its development is most strongly and directly associated with modifiable lifestyle factors, chiefly a poor diet (high in refined sugars, saturated fats, and excess calories) and physical inactivity, which together promote obesity, the single largest risk factor. Excess body fat, especially abdominal fat, impairs the body's ability to use insulin effectively, driving the insulin resistance that underlies the disease. Because these behavioural factors are the leading and most preventable contributors, option B is correct, and it is why lifestyle modification through diet and exercise is the frontline strategy for prevention and management. Genetics (option A) does play a real role; a family history increases susceptibility, and Type 2 diabetes has a stronger hereditary component than Type 1. However, genes largely set a predisposition that is expressed when combined with lifestyle, so among the options the environmental behavioural factors are the most strongly and directly associated with actual development. Psychological stress (option C) can raise blood glucose through cortisol and may worsen control, but it is an indirect and secondary factor. Exposure to environmental toxins (option D) is a much weaker and less established contributor. The dominant, actionable risk driver remains poor diet combined with a sedentary lifestyle. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)"
  },
  { id: "rml23_068", section: "social",
    q: "According to social identity theory, what determines an individual's self-concept and behaviour within a particular group?",
    options: { a: "Internal attributions", b: "External attributions", c: "Personal attributes", d: "Group membership" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: {
      a: "Internal attributions concern explaining behaviour by dispositional causes, part of attribution theory, not the basis of social identity.",
      b: "External attributions explain behaviour by situational causes, again an attribution-theory concept, not social identity theory.",
      c: "Personal attributes reflect personal identity, whereas social identity theory emphasises the social, group-based part of the self."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Group membership. Social identity theory, developed by Henri Tajfel and John Turner, proposes that a significant part of a person's self-concept, their social identity, derives from the groups to which they belong, such as their nationality, religion, team, or profession. According to the theory, people categorise themselves and others into in-groups and out-groups, identify with their in-groups, and compare their groups favourably against others to enhance self-esteem, a process that can produce in-group favouritism and out-group discrimination. Thus, within a particular group, it is group membership that shapes both how individuals see themselves and how they behave, making option D correct. Tajfel's minimal group experiments showed that merely being assigned to a group, even on trivial grounds, was enough to trigger in-group bias. Internal attributions (option A) and external attributions (option B) belong to attribution theory, associated with Fritz Heider and Harold Kelley, which concerns how people explain the causes of behaviour as dispositional or situational; they are not the mechanism of social identity. Personal attributes (option C) relate to personal identity, the individual, idiosyncratic component of the self, whereas social identity theory specifically emphasises the collective, group-based component. The core insight is that group membership is a powerful determinant of self-concept and intergroup behaviour. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)"
  },
  { id: "rml23_069", section: "psychopathology",
    q: "The diathesis-stress model of psychopathology suggests that mental disorders result from :",
    options: { a: "Genetic factors only", b: "Environmental factors only", c: "A combination of genetic predisposition and environmental stressors", d: "Neurobiological abnormalities only" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "Genetic factors only ignores the stress component; the model is explicitly about the interaction of predisposition and stress.",
      b: "Environmental factors only ignores the diathesis (predisposition) side of the model.",
      d: "Neurobiological abnormalities only is a reductionist single-cause view, contrary to the model's interactionist stance."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is a combination of genetic predisposition and environmental stressors. The diathesis-stress model is an interactionist framework in psychopathology holding that mental disorders arise from the interaction between a diathesis, a pre-existing vulnerability or predisposition (often genetic, but also biological or psychological), and stress, adverse environmental experiences or life events. Neither factor alone is usually sufficient: a person may carry a vulnerability that never manifests unless triggered by sufficient environmental stress, and conversely, stress may not produce disorder in someone without the underlying predisposition. The disorder emerges when the accumulated stress exceeds the threshold set by the individual's diathesis. This interaction of nature and nurture is exactly what option C describes. Option A, genetic factors only, is wrong because it omits the stress side of the equation and reduces the model to pure heredity. Option B, environmental factors only, is likewise incomplete because it ignores the predisposition, or diathesis. Option D, neurobiological abnormalities only, is a single-cause, reductionist explanation that contradicts the model's central claim that disorder results from the interplay of vulnerability and environment, not from biology alone. The diathesis-stress model, with roots in work by researchers such as Paul Meehl on schizophrenia, is widely used to explain conditions like depression and schizophrenia, integrating biological and environmental contributions. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)"
  },
  { id: "rml23_070", section: "systems",
    q: "According to Herzberg's Two-Factor Theory, which of the following factors are considered motivators that lead to job satisfaction?",
    options: { a: "Salary and benefits", b: "Job security and work conditions", c: "Recognition and opportunities for advancement", d: "Company policies and interpersonal relationships" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: {
      a: "Salary and benefits are hygiene factors; their absence causes dissatisfaction but their presence does not create true satisfaction.",
      b: "Job security and working conditions are hygiene factors, not motivators.",
      d: "Company policies and interpersonal relationships are hygiene factors that prevent dissatisfaction rather than produce satisfaction."
    },
    explanation: "The correct answer is recognition and opportunities for advancement. Frederick Herzberg's Two-Factor Theory (also called the motivation-hygiene theory) distinguishes two separate categories of factors that affect work attitudes. Motivators (or satisfiers) are intrinsic to the work itself and, when present, generate genuine job satisfaction and motivation; they include achievement, recognition, the nature of the work, responsibility, advancement, and personal growth. Hygiene factors (or dissatisfiers) are extrinsic to the work and relate to the job context; their absence causes dissatisfaction, but their presence merely prevents dissatisfaction rather than creating satisfaction. Because recognition and opportunities for advancement are classic intrinsic motivators, option C is correct. The other options all list hygiene factors. Salary and benefits (option A), job security and working conditions (option B), and company policies and interpersonal relationships (option D) are all extrinsic, context-related factors; ensuring they are adequate keeps employees from being dissatisfied, but improving them beyond a certain point does not produce lasting satisfaction or motivation. Herzberg's key insight, drawn from his studies of accountants and engineers, is that satisfaction and dissatisfaction are not opposite ends of a single continuum but are driven by different factors, which is why enriching the job with genuine motivators, rather than merely improving pay or conditions, is the route to real motivation. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)"
  }
],
  "rml-2024": [
  { id: "rml24_001", section: "general",
    q: "Dark adaptation is the process in which the eyes get more sensitive to:",
    options: { a: "Green light", b: "Coloured lights", c: "Light with bright illumination", d: "Light with low illumination" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Green light is the wavelength the eye is most sensitive to in scotopic conditions (the Purkinje shift), but dark adaptation is about adapting to dim levels generally, not to a single colour.", b: "Coloured lights are processed by cones, which actually lose their role in the dark; colour vision fades as we dark-adapt, so this is the opposite of what happens.", c: "Light with bright illumination describes light adaptation, the quick adjustment when entering a bright room, which is the reverse process of dark adaptation." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is light with low illumination. Dark adaptation is the gradual recovery of the eye's sensitivity that occurs when you move from a brightly lit environment into darkness, for example walking from sunlight into a dim cinema. At first you can see almost nothing, but over roughly twenty to thirty minutes your eyes become dramatically more sensitive, able to detect very faint light. The mechanism rests on the two photoreceptor systems of the retina. Cones, packed in the fovea, handle bright daytime (photopic) vision and colour but are relatively insensitive in dim light. Rods, spread across the periphery, contain the pigment rhodopsin and are exquisitely sensitive to low illumination. In bright light rhodopsin is bleached and broken down; in darkness it is resynthesised, and as its concentration rebuilds the rods regain sensitivity. The dark adaptation curve is therefore biphasic: an early rapid cone phase lasting a few minutes, then a slower rod phase that continues for half an hour and accounts for the deepest sensitivity. This is why faint stars become visible only after you have stood outside for a while, and why we see dim objects best slightly off-centre, using peripheral rods rather than the cone-rich fovea. The process is the basis of scotopic (low-light) vision." },
  { id: "rml24_002", section: "therapy",
    q: "Any distorting or selective effect of a person’s inner world on his perception or conception of the outer world is termed:",
    options: { a: "Projection", b: "Introjection", c: "Empathy", d: "Denial" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Projection attributes one's own unacceptable feelings to other people; it distorts perception but in a specific outward-blaming way rather than the broad selective filtering described.", b: "Introjection takes external objects or attitudes into the self, the opposite direction of flow from the inner world distorting the outer.", c: "Empathy is the accurate sharing of another's emotional state, not a distortion of reality, so it is not a defence mechanism at all." },
    explanation: "The keyed answer is denial, though the wording of the question describes a broader concept. The phrase 'any distorting or selective effect of a person's inner world on his perception of the outer world' captures how defence mechanisms in general bend reality to protect the ego. Denial is the most primitive of these defences: the person simply refuses to acknowledge an external reality that is too threatening to bear, behaving as though a painful fact does not exist, for example a terminally ill patient insisting they are perfectly healthy, or a person refusing to accept a loved one's death. Defence mechanisms were first systematised by Sigmund Freud and elaborated by Anna Freud in The Ego and the Mechanisms of Defence. They operate unconsciously and serve to reduce anxiety arising from conflict between the id's impulses, the superego's prohibitions, and external reality. Denial works at the level of perception itself, blocking the threatening information before it is fully registered, which is exactly why it fits a definition framed around distorting one's conception of the outer world. Clinically, heavy reliance on denial is associated with more immature or psychotic-level functioning, whereas mature defences like sublimation or humour distort reality far less. The source treats denial as the answer because the definition stresses a global filtering of outer reality rather than a specific impulse." },
  { id: "rml24_003", section: "general",
    q: "The phenomenon that is nearly opposite of habituation is termed:",
    options: { a: "Sensitisation", b: "Stabilization", c: "Inhabitation", d: "Habitation" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Stabilization is not a recognised learning term in this context; it has no role as the opposite of habituation.", c: "Inhabitation refers to living in or occupying a place and is unrelated to learning; it is a distractor that merely sounds like habituation.", d: "Habitation, like inhabitation, means a dwelling place and is a sound-alike distractor with no psychological meaning here." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is sensitisation. Habituation and sensitisation are the two simplest, most fundamental forms of non-associative learning, meaning learning that does not require pairing a stimulus with a reward or another stimulus. Habituation is a decrease in responding to a stimulus that is repeated harmlessly: the first time a clock chimes you notice it, but after many repetitions you stop reacting because the stimulus has proved unimportant. Sensitisation is the opposite, an increase in responsiveness following a strong or noxious stimulus, often spreading to other stimuli as well. After a painful or frightening event, you become jumpy and over-react even to mild stimuli, as when a person who has just heard a loud explosion startles violently at a soft knock. Eric Kandel's Nobel-winning work on the sea slug Aplysia mapped both processes at the synaptic level: habituation reflects reduced neurotransmitter release at sensory-motor synapses, whereas sensitisation reflects enhanced transmitter release driven by interneurons releasing serotonin. Together these show that even very simple nervous systems can store experience by tuning synaptic strength up or down. Because sensitisation raises responding while habituation lowers it, sensitisation is the near-opposite phenomenon the question asks for." },
  { id: "rml24_004", section: "social",
    q: "The process of changing your behaviour to match that of others in a group is termed:",
    options: { a: "Norming", b: "Conformity", c: "Standardization", d: "Forming a social contact" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Norming is the third stage of Tuckman's group-development model where a team settles into shared norms; it describes group formation, not an individual matching others.", c: "Standardization refers to establishing uniform procedures or test norms, a methodological term, not a social behaviour.", d: "Forming a social contact simply means making an acquaintance and has nothing to do with adjusting behaviour to group standards." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is conformity. Conformity is the tendency to change one's beliefs or behaviour to align with the standards of a group, even without any direct request to do so. It is one of the central topics of social psychology. People conform for two broad reasons identified by Deutsch and Gerard: normative social influence, the desire to be liked and accepted and to avoid the discomfort of standing out, and informational social influence, the assumption that the group may know better than oneself, especially in ambiguous situations. Solomon Asch's famous line-judgement experiments demonstrated normative conformity, showing that many participants would give an obviously wrong answer about line lengths simply because confederates had done so first. Muzafer Sherif's autokinetic-effect studies showed informational conformity, where individuals converged on a group estimate when the task was genuinely uncertain. Conformity differs from compliance (yielding to a direct request) and obedience (following an order from an authority). The question's phrasing, matching your behaviour to that of others in a group, is essentially the textbook definition of conformity, which is why it is the precise answer." },
  { id: "rml24_005", section: "biostats",
    q: "Reliable difference between two groups can be obtained by using:",
    options: { a: "Norms", b: "Validity", c: "Reliability", d: "Statistical test" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Norms are reference scores from a standardisation sample used to interpret an individual's score, not a means of comparing two groups for a reliable difference.", b: "Validity is whether a test measures what it claims to measure; it concerns the instrument's accuracy, not the comparison of groups.", c: "Reliability is the consistency of a measurement instrument; a reliable test can still leave a group difference untested for significance." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is a statistical test. To know whether two groups genuinely differ, rather than differing only because of random sampling fluctuation, you must apply an inferential statistical test such as a t-test, ANOVA, or chi-square. These tests compute the probability (the p-value) that the observed difference could have arisen by chance if there were truly no difference in the populations. If that probability is small (conventionally below .05), the difference is judged statistically significant and considered reliable, meaning unlikely to be a fluke. The logic rests on null hypothesis significance testing: you assume no difference (the null hypothesis), calculate how surprising your data would be under that assumption, and reject the null when the data are sufficiently improbable. Without such a test you cannot distinguish a real effect from noise, no matter how large the raw difference looks, because small samples produce large random swings. This is the core tool of quantitative research design and why a statistical test, not a property of the measuring instrument, is what establishes a reliable difference between groups." },
  { id: "rml24_006", section: "general",
    q: "Which is the smallest unit of meaning in speech perception?",
    options: { a: "Syllables", b: "Consonant", c: "Phonemes", d: "Morphemes" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Syllables are rhythmic units of pronunciation built from phonemes; a syllable such as 'ba' need not carry any meaning, so it is not the smallest meaning unit.", b: "A consonant is a type of speech sound, even smaller than a syllable, but again carries no meaning on its own.", c: "Phonemes are the smallest units of sound that change meaning between words, but individually they are meaningless, so they fail the 'unit of meaning' criterion." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is morphemes. A morpheme is the smallest unit of language that carries meaning, and this is what makes it the right choice when the question specifies the smallest unit of meaning. The word 'cats', for instance, contains two morphemes: 'cat' (a free morpheme that can stand alone) and the plural '-s' (a bound morpheme that cannot stand alone but adds the meaning of plurality). Linguists organise language into a hierarchy of units. Phonemes are the smallest units of sound that distinguish words, such as the difference between the /b/ and /p/ in 'bat' and 'pat', but a phoneme by itself carries no meaning. Morphemes are built from phonemes and are the smallest meaning-bearing units. Words combine morphemes, and phrases and sentences combine words according to syntax. Because the question asks specifically about meaning, phonemes are ruled out (they carry sound, not meaning) and morphemes are correct. This distinction matters in the psychology of language and in understanding disorders such as aphasia, where patients may lose the ability to manipulate morphemes (grammatical endings) while preserving content words, or vice versa." },
  { id: "rml24_007", section: "general",
    q: "The first-grade teacher gives students stickers when they perform well. If they earn five stickers in one day they are exempt from homework. The stickers in this example could also be called:",
    options: { a: "Primary reinforcer", b: "Token", c: "Generalized reinforcer", d: "Prepotent responses" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "A primary reinforcer satisfies a biological need directly, like food or water; stickers have no innate value and only work through their exchange rule.", c: "A generalized reinforcer is a token that can be exchanged for many different rewards (like money); here the stickers buy only one specific privilege, so 'token' is the tighter answer.", d: "Prepotent responses refer to the more probable, preferred behaviour in the Premack principle, not to an exchangeable reward." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is token. In operant conditioning, a token is a conditioned reinforcer (a secondary reinforcer) that has no intrinsic value but can be accumulated and exchanged for something the learner actually wants, called a backup reinforcer. Here the stickers themselves do nothing biologically rewarding; their power comes entirely from the rule that five of them earn an exemption from homework. This is the defining structure of a token economy, a behaviour-modification system developed by Ayllon and Azrin and widely used in classrooms, psychiatric wards, and rehabilitation settings. The procedure works because tokens bridge the delay between desirable behaviour and the eventual reward, can be delivered immediately and consistently, and let the learner choose when to cash in. Tokens become reinforcing through association with the backup reward, exactly as money becomes reinforcing because it buys goods. The question specifically names the stickers, the exchangeable items, which is why 'token' rather than the more general 'generalized reinforcer' is the most precise label, since the stickers here buy one specific privilege rather than a wide range of rewards." },
  { id: "rml24_008", section: "general",
    q: "The concept of “Self-reference effect” was founded by:",
    options: { a: "Craik and Lockhart", b: "Posner", c: "Paivio", d: "Rogers and Kuiper" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Craik and Lockhart created the levels-of-processing model that explains why the self-reference effect works, but they did not originate or name the effect itself.", b: "Posner is known for work on attention and attentional networks, not for the self-reference effect.", c: "Paivio developed dual-coding theory about verbal and imaginal memory codes, a different memory concept entirely." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Rogers and Kuiper. The self-reference effect is the well-replicated finding that information processed in relation to oneself is remembered far better than the same information processed in other ways. Rogers, Kuiper, and Kirker demonstrated this in 1977 by asking participants to judge adjectives under different conditions: whether the word was in capital letters (structural), whether it rhymed with another word (phonemic), what it meant (semantic), or whether it described the participant themselves (self-reference). Words judged for self-relevance were recalled best of all. The effect is usually explained within Craik and Lockhart's levels-of-processing framework: the self is a richly elaborated, highly organised cognitive structure, so relating new material to it creates many retrieval routes and deep, distinctive encoding. This is why students remember material better when they connect it to their own experiences, and why personalised learning is effective. The self acts as a powerful organising schema. Note that while Craik and Lockhart provided the levels-of-processing theory that explains the effect, the self-reference effect itself was specifically named and demonstrated by Rogers and colleagues, which is why they are the correct attribution." },
  { id: "rml24_009", section: "therapy",
    q: "The concept of the “collective unconscious” was given by:",
    options: { a: "Sigmund Freud", b: "Alfred Adler", c: "Carl Jung", d: "Karen Horney" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Sigmund Freud described the personal unconscious built from individual repression, not a shared inherited one; this distinction is precisely what separates him from Jung.", b: "Alfred Adler emphasised inferiority feelings and striving for superiority, not a collective unconscious.", d: "Karen Horney focused on basic anxiety and neurotic needs arising from disturbed social relationships, a culturally oriented neo-Freudian view." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Carl Jung. The collective unconscious is the cornerstone of Jung's analytical psychology and the feature that most sharply distinguishes him from Freud. Whereas Freud's unconscious was personal, built from an individual's own repressed experiences, Jung proposed a deeper layer of the psyche shared by all human beings, inherited rather than acquired. This collective unconscious is populated by archetypes: universal, innate templates or predispositions that shape how we experience the world, such as the Mother, the Hero, the Shadow (the disowned dark side of the self), the Anima and Animus (the feminine within men and masculine within women), and the Self (the archetype of wholeness and integration). Jung drew evidence from the recurrence of similar myths, symbols, and motifs across unrelated cultures and from the universal imagery appearing in dreams. The collective unconscious does not contain specific memories but rather inherited potentials for organising experience. This concept underpins Jungian therapy, where the goal of individuation is to integrate conscious and unconscious material, including archetypal contents, into a balanced whole. Understanding it is essential for distinguishing the three major depth-psychology systems of Freud, Adler, and Jung." },
  { id: "rml24_010", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Narendra turns around and around in a circle. When he stops, he feels like his head is spinning. What is responsible for this sensation?",
    options: { a: "Semi circular canals", b: "Proprioceptors", c: "Otolith organs", d: "Otolith crystals" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Proprioceptors are receptors in muscles and joints that sense body and limb position, not the rotational dizziness of the inner ear.", c: "The otolith organs (utricle and saccule) detect linear acceleration and gravity/head tilt, not the rotational spinning that causes this dizziness.", d: "Otolith crystals (otoconia) sit on the otolith organs and respond to gravity and linear motion; they do not signal rotation." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is the semicircular canals. Spinning and the dizzy after-sensation are functions of the vestibular system in the inner ear, which provides our sense of balance and head movement. The vestibular apparatus has two parts. The three semicircular canals, oriented in three different planes, detect rotational (angular) acceleration: each canal is filled with fluid (endolymph) and contains a gelatinous cupula with hair cells. When the head rotates, the fluid lags behind due to inertia, bending the cupula and triggering the hair cells. When Narendra suddenly stops spinning, the fluid keeps moving for a moment, continuing to bend the hair cells, so the brain is still told the head is rotating even though it has stopped. This mismatch produces the illusion of spinning, dizziness, and the characteristic nystagmus of the eyes. The other vestibular structures, the otolith organs (the utricle and saccule), detect linear acceleration and the pull of gravity, telling us about head tilt and straight-line motion, not rotation. Because the question is specifically about turning in circles and the resulting spin sensation, the rotation-detecting semicircular canals are the correct structure." },
  { id: "rml24_011", section: "therapy",
    q: "The concept of inferiority complex – superiority drive was given by:",
    options: { a: "Carl Jung", b: "Victor Frankl", c: "Alfred Adler", d: "Erik Erikson" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Carl Jung gave us the collective unconscious and archetypes, not the inferiority-superiority dynamic.", b: "Victor Frankl founded logotherapy, centred on the will to meaning, not inferiority feelings.", d: "Erik Erikson described the eight psychosocial stages of development, including identity crisis, a separate theory." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Alfred Adler. Adler founded individual psychology and built it around the dynamic of inferiority and the striving for superiority. He proposed that all humans begin life small, weak, and dependent, producing universal feelings of inferiority. Healthy development involves compensating for these feelings through striving for competence, mastery, and ultimately what he called superiority, by which he meant self-improvement and the perfecting of oneself rather than dominating others. When this normal process is distorted, a person may develop an inferiority complex, an exaggerated, paralysing sense of inadequacy, or a superiority complex, an inflated, compensatory facade of dominance masking underlying inferiority. Adler stressed the unity of the personality, the importance of birth order and family constellation, the role of social interest (Gemeinschaftsgefuhl, a sense of community and concern for others) as the marker of mental health, and the person's unique style of life. Unlike Freud, who emphasised sexuality, Adler saw the drive to overcome inferiority and find belonging as the prime motivator of behaviour. This framework remains influential in counselling and in understanding compensation and motivation." },
  { id: "rml24_012", section: "general",
    q: "During insight learning there is the sudden realization of the solution to a problem. This is known as the:",
    options: { a: "‘aha’ experience", b: "Light bulb", c: "Momentary", d: "Learning" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Light bulb is only an informal cartoon metaphor for having an idea, not the technical psychological term for insight.", c: "Momentary merely describes brief duration and is not a named concept for the moment of insight.", d: "Learning is the general superordinate term and is far too broad to name the specific sudden realisation in insight." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is the aha experience. Insight learning refers to the sudden grasp of the solution to a problem without overt trial and error, a concept demonstrated by the Gestalt psychologist Wolfgang Kohler in his experiments with chimpanzees, most famously Sultan. Kohler placed bananas out of reach and provided sticks or stackable boxes; rather than gradually fumbling toward the answer, the chimps would pause, appear to study the situation, and then suddenly assemble the tools or stack the boxes in one smooth, correct sequence. This abrupt restructuring of the perceptual field, in which the elements of a problem are suddenly reorganised into a meaningful whole, is the hallmark of insight. The subjective moment of sudden comprehension is popularly called the aha experience (or sometimes the eureka moment). It contrasts sharply with Thorndike's trial-and-error learning, where solutions emerge gradually through reinforcement of successful responses. Insight learning highlights the cognitive, perceptual reorganisation that Gestalt psychologists emphasised against the purely associationist view, showing that learning can be sudden, all-or-none, and transferable to new but similar problems." },
  { id: "rml24_013", section: "abnormal",
    q: "The term ‘Ambivalence’ was coined by:",
    options: { a: "Kraepelin", b: "Eugen Bleuler", c: "Richmond Fromm", d: "Sigmund Freud" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Kraepelin coined dementia praecox and built the classification of psychoses, but the specific term ambivalence was Bleuler's contribution.", c: "Richmond Fromm is not a recognised originator of this term; Erich Fromm worked on humanistic and social psychoanalysis, not ambivalence.", d: "Sigmund Freud used ambivalence in his writing, but he did not coin the term; it originated with Bleuler." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Eugen Bleuler. Ambivalence, the simultaneous holding of contradictory feelings, attitudes, or impulses toward the same object (such as loving and hating someone at once), was coined by the Swiss psychiatrist Eugen Bleuler. Bleuler is one of the most important figures in the history of schizophrenia. In 1911 he renamed Kraepelin's dementia praecox as schizophrenia, meaning a splitting of psychic functions, to capture the disintegration between thought, emotion, and behaviour. He identified what are now called Bleuler's four As as the fundamental symptoms of the disorder: loosening of Associations (disordered thought connections), Affective disturbance (blunted or inappropriate emotion), Ambivalence (contradictory feelings or volitions held together), and Autism (withdrawal into an inner fantasy world). He regarded hallucinations and delusions as merely accessory symptoms, secondary to these core disturbances. Ambivalence as Bleuler used it can occur in the emotional, volitional, or intellectual spheres and reflects the fragmentation of the integrated mind that he believed defined schizophrenia. The term has since entered general psychological and psychoanalytic vocabulary, but its origin lies with Bleuler." },
  { id: "rml24_014", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Delusion of infidelity on part of the sexual partner is known as:",
    options: { a: "Ekbom’s Syndrome", b: "Othello syndrome", c: "de clérambault’s syndrome", d: "Couvade syndrome" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Ekbom's syndrome is delusional parasitosis, the fixed belief that one is infested with insects or parasites, not jealousy.", c: "De Clerambault's syndrome (erotomania) is the delusion that another person, often of higher status, is secretly in love with the patient, the opposite theme.", d: "Couvade syndrome is when an expectant father develops pregnancy-like physical symptoms, unrelated to jealousy." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Othello syndrome. Othello syndrome is the eponymous name for delusional jealousy, the fixed, false belief that one's sexual partner is being unfaithful, held with delusional conviction despite no real evidence. It is named after Shakespeare's character Othello, who, convinced by manipulation that his faithful wife Desdemona has betrayed him, ultimately kills her. Clinically, the delusion of infidelity can occur as a primary delusional disorder (jealous type), or secondary to schizophrenia, mood disorders, organic brain disease, or, importantly, chronic alcohol misuse, where it is a recognised complication. The condition is significant in forensic psychiatry because the intense, controlling jealousy carries a real risk of violence toward the partner, who may be stalked, interrogated, and physically harmed. The patient typically seeks proof, checks the partner's belongings, and misinterprets innocent behaviour as evidence of betrayal. Recognising delusional jealousy as a psychiatric symptom rather than ordinary suspicion is clinically crucial. This question tests knowledge of the eponymous syndromes that cluster around delusional disorders, each tied to a specific delusional theme." },
  { id: "rml24_015", section: "general",
    q: "Highway engineers sometimes paint stripes on freeway off-ramps to enhance the perception of depth. This uses the cue of:",
    options: { a: "Aerial perspective", b: "Parallax", c: "Overlap", d: "Texture gradients" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Aerial perspective is the haziness and bluish tint of distant objects caused by atmospheric scattering, not the spacing of stripes.", b: "Parallax (motion parallax) is the apparent relative motion of near versus far objects as the observer moves, not a static texture cue.", c: "Overlap (interposition) is when a nearer object partly blocks a farther one; painted stripes do not occlude each other." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is texture gradients. A texture gradient is a monocular depth cue in which the apparent density and fineness of a textured surface increases with distance. Close to the viewer, the elements of a textured pattern (pebbles, grass blades, painted stripes) appear large, coarse, and widely spaced; as the surface recedes, they appear smaller, finer, and more densely packed, eventually blurring into a smooth texture at the horizon. The brain uses this systematic change to infer depth and the slant of surfaces. James J. Gibson, who pioneered the ecological approach to perception, emphasised texture gradients as a rich source of information directly available in the optic array. On a freeway off-ramp, stripes painted at progressively closer spacing exploit this cue: the increasingly dense stripes signal that the surface is receding and rushing toward the driver, enhancing the sense of speed and distance and prompting drivers to slow down. Texture gradient is one of several pictorial (monocular) depth cues, alongside linear perspective, relative size, interposition, and aerial perspective, that allow us to perceive three-dimensional depth from a two-dimensional retinal image." },
  { id: "rml24_016", section: "therapy",
    q: "A woman harassed by her boss at work, initiates an argument with her husband. She is using which of the following ego’s defense mechanisms:",
    options: { a: "Projection", b: "Identification", c: "Sublimation", d: "Displacement" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Projection attributes one's own unacceptable feelings to someone else; she is not accusing her husband of being angry, she is discharging her own anger onto him.", b: "Identification is adopting the characteristics of another person to reduce anxiety, not redirecting an emotion.", c: "Sublimation channels unacceptable impulses into socially valued activity (like art or sport); starting an argument is not a constructive outlet." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is displacement. Displacement is the defence mechanism in which an unacceptable impulse or emotion, which cannot safely be directed at its true target, is redirected onto a substitute target that is less threatening. Here the woman is angry at her boss but cannot express it without risking her job, so she transfers the anger onto her husband, a safer target, by starting an argument. The classic textbook image is the man who is yelled at by his boss, comes home and shouts at his wife, who scolds the child, who kicks the dog: the affect rolls downhill to ever safer objects. Displacement was described within Freudian psychoanalysis as one of the ego's ways of discharging instinctual tension while protecting the person from the danger of confronting the original source. It also underlies phobias in Freud's account (as in the case of Little Hans, whose fear of his father was displaced onto horses) and appears in dreams, where latent wishes attach to neutral images. Recognising displacement matters clinically because the surface conflict (with the husband) is not the real conflict (with the boss), and effective therapy must trace the emotion back to its true origin." },
  { id: "rml24_017", section: "assessment",
    q: "The improvement in anxiety may sometimes spuriously improve depression. This is called:",
    options: { a: "Halo effect", b: "Ceiling effect", c: "Hawthorne effect", d: "Therapeutic effect" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "The ceiling effect occurs when a measure cannot register improvement above a maximum score because scores cluster at the top; it concerns scale limits, not impression spillover.", c: "The Hawthorne effect is when people change their behaviour simply because they know they are being observed, unrelated to rating one symptom from another.", d: "Therapeutic effect just means a genuine benefit from treatment, not a measurement bias, so it does not capture the spurious nature described." },
    explanation: "The keyed answer is the halo effect. The halo effect is a cognitive bias in which one's overall impression of a person, or one prominent trait, colours the judgement of their other, separate characteristics. Coined by Edward Thorndike, it originally described how officers rating soldiers let a single favourable impression inflate ratings of unrelated qualities. In this clinical example, because the patient's anxiety has improved, the rater is inclined to judge that the depression has improved too, even though depression was not really assessed independently; the positive global impression spills over and spuriously raises the depression rating. This is a serious threat to the validity of clinical ratings and assessment, because it creates artificial correlations between symptoms that should be rated separately. Researchers guard against it by using structured, anchored rating scales, by having different raters assess different domains, and by blinding. It is worth noting that some texts would describe symptom co-improvement differently, but within the framework of rating bias the spillover of one improved judgement onto another is the halo effect, which is why the source keys it here. The key learning point is that a rater's general impression can distort the measurement of specific, supposedly independent variables." },
  { id: "rml24_018", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Which of the following disorder is a common cause of pseudodementia?",
    options: { a: "Schizophrenia", b: "Mania", c: "Depression", d: "Hypochondriasis" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Schizophrenia can impair cognition but is not the classic cause of the reversible dementia-mimicking picture called pseudodementia.", b: "Mania produces overactivity and distractibility but is not the recognised common cause of pseudodementia.", d: "Hypochondriasis is preoccupation with having a serious illness; it does not produce the global cognitive impairment that mimics dementia." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is depression. Pseudodementia is a condition in which a patient, usually elderly, presents with cognitive impairment that mimics dementia (poor memory, difficulty concentrating, apparent disorientation) but the underlying cause is a treatable psychiatric disorder, most commonly major depression. It is therefore sometimes called the dementia syndrome of depression. The clinical importance is enormous, because depressive pseudodementia is reversible if the depression is treated, whereas true neurodegenerative dementia is not. Several features help distinguish the two. In pseudodementia the onset is relatively rapid and can be dated, the patient complains a great deal about memory loss and emphasises their failures, they frequently answer 'I don't know' rather than confabulating, effort and motivation are poor (the deficits reflect lack of engagement rather than true incapacity), and depressive mood symptoms precede the cognitive ones. In true dementia the onset is insidious, patients tend to conceal or minimise deficits, they attempt answers even when wrong, and mood changes follow rather than precede cognitive decline. Because depression is treatable, recognising pseudodementia prevents an elderly depressed patient from being wrongly written off as having irreversible dementia." },
  { id: "rml24_019", section: "therapy",
    q: "In paranoia, the ego’s defense mechanism as given by Melaine Klein is seen as:",
    options: { a: "Reaction formation", b: "Splitting", c: "Suppression", d: "Projection" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Reaction formation converts an unacceptable impulse into its opposite (hating into excessive caring); it is a higher-level neurotic defence, not Klein's account of paranoia.", c: "Suppression is the conscious, deliberate pushing aside of a thought, a mature defence, not the primitive one Klein tied to paranoia.", d: "Projection is the classic Freudian mechanism in paranoia, but the question specifically asks for Klein's formulation, which centres on splitting." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is splitting. Within Melanie Klein's object-relations theory, splitting is the most primitive defence and the one she linked to the earliest, paranoid-schizoid position of infancy. The infant cannot yet hold the idea that the same caregiver who gratifies (the good breast) is also the one who frustrates (the bad breast). To manage the resulting anxiety, the infant splits the object and the self into wholly good and wholly bad parts, keeping them separate so that the loving feelings are not contaminated or destroyed by the hostile ones. In Kleinian theory the persecutory anxiety of the paranoid-schizoid position, where the bad object is felt to threaten and persecute the self, underlies paranoid functioning, and splitting (together with projective identification) is the characteristic defence. This is why, in paranoia, the mechanism Klein emphasised is splitting rather than the more classically Freudian projection. Splitting also reappears in adult borderline personality organisation, where people oscillate between idealising and devaluing the same person. Understanding splitting is central to object-relations and to grasping how very early mental life is organised around managing aggression and preserving the good object." },
  { id: "rml24_020", section: "general",
    q: "The tendency to complete a figure is called:",
    options: { a: "Continuation", b: "Continuity", c: "Closure", d: "Similarity" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Continuation is not the standard Gestalt term; the related principle is continuity, and neither concerns completing a gapped figure.", b: "Continuity (good continuation) is the tendency to perceive smooth, continuous lines rather than abrupt changes, not the filling-in of incomplete shapes.", d: "Similarity groups elements that look alike into a unit; it does not involve completing an unfinished figure." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is closure. Closure is one of the Gestalt principles of perceptual organisation, which describe how the visual system spontaneously groups elements into coherent wholes. The law of closure states that we tend to perceive incomplete figures as complete, mentally filling in gaps so that a fragmentary outline is seen as a whole, recognisable shape. For example, a circle drawn with small breaks in its outline is still perceived as a circle, and three appropriately placed Pac-Man shapes are seen as a triangle (the illusory Kanizsa triangle) even though no triangle is actually drawn. The Gestalt psychologists Max Wertheimer, Kurt Koffka, and Wolfgang Kohler argued that the whole is different from the sum of its parts and that the mind imposes organisation according to laws such as proximity (grouping nearby elements), similarity (grouping like elements), continuity (preferring smooth continuous lines), closure (completing figures), and figure-ground segregation. Closure reflects the mind's drive toward simple, stable, complete forms (the law of Pragnanz, the tendency to perceive the simplest possible organisation). It explains why we can recognise objects from partial information and is heavily exploited in logo and graphic design." },
  { id: "rml24_021", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Which of the following is NOT a Schneider’s first-rank symptom?",
    options: { a: "Auditory hallucination", b: "Delusional perception", c: "Passivity phenomenon", d: "Delusion of self-reference" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Auditory hallucinations of the specific Schneiderian types (third-person, running commentary, thought echo) ARE first-rank symptoms, so this is not the exception.", b: "Delusional perception, attaching delusional meaning to a real percept, is a recognised first-rank symptom.", c: "Passivity phenomena (made acts, feelings, impulses, somatic passivity) are core first-rank symptoms of external control." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is delusion of self-reference. Kurt Schneider proposed a list of first-rank symptoms (FRS) which he considered, in the absence of organic disease, strongly suggestive of schizophrenia and useful for diagnosis. The classic Schneiderian first-rank symptoms are: auditory hallucinations of specific types (third-person voices discussing the patient, voices commenting on the patient's actions, or thought echo/audible thoughts), thought alienation phenomena (thought insertion, thought withdrawal, thought broadcasting), passivity phenomena or made experiences (made feelings, made impulses, made acts, and somatic passivity, where the patient feels their will, emotions, or body are controlled by an external force), and delusional perception (attaching an abnormal delusional significance to a normally perceived object). A delusion of self-reference (or simple ideas/delusions of reference), where the patient believes ordinary events, remarks, or media broadcasts are specifically about them, is a common symptom in schizophrenia but is NOT on Schneider's first-rank list, which is why it is the exception here. The first-rank symptoms are not pathognomonic (they occur in other psychoses too), but historically they shaped diagnostic criteria and remain a frequent exam topic for distinguishing core Schneiderian phenomena from other schizophrenic features." },
  { id: "rml24_022", section: "general",
    q: "The Yerkes-Dodson law states that between performance and anxiety, there is:",
    options: { a: "Inverted-U shaped relationship", b: "Curvilinear relationship", c: "Linear relationship", d: "M-shaped relationship" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Curvilinear is technically true but too vague; many curve shapes are curvilinear, whereas the law specifies the precise inverted-U.", c: "A linear relationship would mean performance just keeps rising (or falling) with anxiety, which contradicts the documented decline at high arousal.", d: "An M-shaped relationship (two peaks) is not what the law describes; there is a single optimal peak." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is an inverted-U shaped relationship. The Yerkes-Dodson law, formulated by Robert Yerkes and John Dodson in 1908, describes the relationship between arousal (or anxiety) and performance as an inverted U. At very low levels of arousal, performance is poor because the person is under-motivated, drowsy, or inattentive. As arousal increases, performance improves up to an optimal point. Beyond that peak, further arousal becomes counterproductive: excessive anxiety narrows attention, disrupts working memory, and impairs coordination, so performance declines. The law adds an important refinement: the optimal level of arousal depends on task difficulty. For simple, well-learned tasks, higher arousal can still enhance performance, so the curve peaks late; for complex or novel tasks requiring concentration, the optimal arousal is lower and the curve peaks early, because high anxiety quickly disrupts demanding cognition. This principle explains why a little nervousness can sharpen exam or athletic performance while too much causes choking. Although 'curvilinear' is technically a broader correct description, the standard and most precise textbook answer is the inverted-U shape, which captures the specific rise-then-fall pattern Yerkes and Dodson described." },
  { id: "rml24_023", section: "therapy",
    q: "The following defense mechanisms are used in obsessive-compulsive disorder:",
    options: { a: "Isolation", b: "Undoing", c: "Reaction formation", d: "Displacement" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Isolation (of affect) is a genuine OCD defence, separating thought from feeling, but the question keys the single mechanism most embodied by the rituals themselves, which is undoing.", c: "Reaction formation also appears in obsessional character (excessive cleanliness, exaggerated kindness), but it is not the ritual-cancelling mechanism the answer targets.", d: "Displacement redirects emotion onto a substitute target and is not the defining defence of OCD compulsions." },
    explanation: "The keyed answer is undoing. In the psychoanalytic account of obsessive-compulsive disorder, several defence mechanisms operate together, but undoing is the one most directly tied to compulsive acts. Undoing is the mechanism by which a person performs an action, ritual, or thought intended magically to cancel out, reverse, or atone for a previous unacceptable impulse, thought, or act, as if it could be undone. The compulsive rituals of OCD (repeated hand-washing to undo a contaminating thought, checking and rechecking, counting, or saying a 'good' phrase to neutralise a 'bad' one) are textbook expressions of undoing: the patient feels a forbidden aggressive or sexual impulse and then carries out a ritual that symbolically negates it and relieves the resulting anxiety. The other classic OCD defences are isolation of affect (separating a distressing idea from the feeling that belongs to it, so the obsessional thought is experienced without emotion) and reaction formation (turning hostility into excessive kindness or orderliness). Because the question asks for the defence mechanism characteristically used in OCD and singles out one answer, undoing, the magical reversal seen in compulsive rituals, is the intended choice. Recognising these mechanisms links the dynamic theory of OCD to its observable symptom pattern." },
  { id: "rml24_024", section: "general",
    q: "A toddler goes to great lengths to open a box. She knows there is nothing in the box. This is an example of:",
    options: { a: "Intrinsic motivation", b: "Homeostasis", c: "Extrinsic motivation", d: "Secondary motivation" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Homeostasis is the body's drive to maintain internal physiological balance (like hunger or thirst); opening an empty box serves no biological balance.", c: "Extrinsic motivation requires an external reward or consequence, but there is no reward here since the box is empty.", d: "Secondary motivation refers to learned, non-biological drives; the term does not capture the spontaneous, self-rewarding curiosity shown here." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is intrinsic motivation. Intrinsic motivation is the drive to engage in an activity for its own sake, because it is inherently interesting, enjoyable, or satisfying, rather than for any external reward. The toddler works hard to open the box even though she already knows it is empty, so there is no external prize; the effort is driven purely by curiosity, the pleasure of exploration, and the satisfaction of mastery. This contrasts with extrinsic motivation, where behaviour is driven by external consequences such as rewards, praise, money, or avoiding punishment. The distinction is central to self-determination theory (Deci and Ryan), which holds that intrinsic motivation flourishes when three basic psychological needs are met: autonomy (feeling self-directed), competence (feeling effective), and relatedness (feeling connected). An important finding is the overjustification effect, where giving external rewards for an already intrinsically enjoyable activity can actually undermine intrinsic motivation. The toddler's exploration also illustrates the human drive toward effectance and competence described by Robert White. Because the child seeks no external payoff and the activity itself is the goal, this is a clear example of intrinsic motivation." },
  { id: "rml24_025", section: "abnormal",
    q: "The term ‘la Bella indifference’ seen in hysteria was first used by:",
    options: { a: "Sigmund Freud", b: "Anna Freud", c: "Pierre Janet", d: "Hippocrates" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Sigmund Freud developed the psychoanalytic theory of hysteria and conversion but did not coin the term la belle indifference.", b: "Anna Freud systematised the ego defence mechanisms; she is not associated with originating this term.", d: "Hippocrates described hysteria in antiquity (attributing it to the wandering womb) but long predates this specific nineteenth-century French term." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Pierre Janet. La belle indifference, French for 'beautiful indifference', describes the striking lack of concern that some patients with conversion disorder (historically called hysteria) show toward their apparently serious physical symptoms. A patient may suddenly develop paralysis, blindness, or seizures yet remain curiously calm and unbothered, displaying an emotional indifference that seems out of proportion to the disability. This phenomenon was first described by the French psychologist and psychiatrist Pierre Janet, a pioneer in the study of dissociation and hysteria who worked at the Salpetriere under Charcot. Janet developed influential ideas about dissociation, the splitting off of mental contents from consciousness, and about the lowering of psychological tension in hysteria, which he linked to this emotional detachment. La belle indifference was long considered a classic sign of conversion disorder, thought to reflect the primary gain of converting psychological conflict into a physical symptom that relieves anxiety. Modern research has shown it is neither sensitive nor specific (many patients with genuine organic disease can also seem indifferent, and many conversion patients are distressed), so it is no longer relied upon diagnostically. Nonetheless its historical attribution to Janet remains a standard exam fact." },
  { id: "rml24_026", section: "general",
    q: "A child has learned to avoid a furry, black cat. However, she still plays with her grandmother’s short-haired tabby. Her response demonstrates:",
    options: { a: "Discrimination", b: "Extinction", c: "Successive approximation", d: "Negative transfer" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Extinction is the gradual weakening and disappearance of a conditioned response when the unconditioned stimulus is withheld; here the fear response still exists, just selectively.", c: "Successive approximation (shaping) reinforces closer and closer steps toward a target behaviour; it concerns building new behaviour, not distinguishing stimuli.", d: "Negative transfer is when prior learning interferes with new learning; it does not describe responding differently to two similar stimuli." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is discrimination. Stimulus discrimination is the learned ability to respond differently to stimuli that differ along some dimension, distinguishing a conditioned stimulus from other, similar stimuli. Here the child has learned a fear (or avoidance) response to one specific stimulus, the furry black cat, but does not show the same response to a different cat, the short-haired tabby. Because she treats the two cats differently rather than reacting to all cats alike, she is demonstrating discrimination. This is the conceptual opposite of stimulus generalisation, where a response conditioned to one stimulus spreads to similar stimuli (which would have meant fearing all cats, or even all furry animals). Discrimination develops when one stimulus is consistently associated with a particular outcome while similar stimuli are not, so the organism learns the relevant boundary. In Pavlov's terms, discrimination is achieved by reinforcing the response to one stimulus and extinguishing it to others. The famous Little Albert study showed generalisation (fear spreading from a white rat to other furry white objects); the present example shows the reverse, the fine-grained separation of a feared stimulus from a safe one, which defines discrimination." },
  { id: "rml24_027", section: "abnormal",
    q: "In hysterical dissociative states, all of the following are seen EXCEPT:",
    options: { a: "Multiple personality", b: "Fugue", c: "Amnesia", d: "Déjà vu" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Multiple personality (dissociative identity disorder) is a core dissociative state involving distinct alternating identities, so it belongs in the list.", b: "Fugue is a classic dissociative state combining travel away from home with amnesia and identity disturbance.", c: "Amnesia (dissociative amnesia) is a fundamental dissociative phenomenon involving loss of autobiographical memory." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is deja vu, which is the exception. Dissociative disorders (the modern term for what was historically called hysterical dissociation) involve a disruption in the normally integrated functions of consciousness, memory, identity, and perception. The recognised dissociative phenomena include dissociative amnesia (an inability to recall important personal information, usually following trauma, that is too extensive to be ordinary forgetting), dissociative fugue (a sudden, unexpected journey away from home accompanied by amnesia for one's past and sometimes the assumption of a new identity), and dissociative identity disorder (formerly multiple personality disorder, in which two or more distinct identity states recurrently take control of behaviour). Depersonalisation and derealisation also belong to the dissociative spectrum. Deja vu, by contrast, is the fleeting, common sense that a new situation has been experienced before. It is a normal phenomenon experienced by most healthy people, can occur in temporal lobe epilepsy or fatigue, and is a paramnesia (a distortion of memory familiarity) rather than a dissociative disorder. Because deja vu is not a dissociative state arising from the splitting off of identity or memory, it is the item that does not belong in this list." },
  { id: "rml24_028", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Which of the following psychiatric illness is most common after cardiac surgery?",
    options: { a: "Schizophrenia", b: "Delirium", c: "Anxiety", d: "Depression" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Schizophrenia is a chronic primary psychotic disorder, not an acute medically triggered postoperative complication.", c: "Anxiety can occur after surgery but is far less common and clinically significant than delirium in this setting.", d: "Depression may follow cardiac surgery over weeks but is not the most common acute postoperative psychiatric disturbance." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is delirium. Delirium is an acute, fluctuating disturbance of consciousness and cognition that develops over hours to days and is caused by an underlying medical condition, and it is the most common psychiatric complication after cardiac surgery, where it is often called postoperative or post-cardiotomy delirium. The reasons are multiple: the physiological stress of major surgery, cardiopulmonary bypass and possible micro-emboli or reduced cerebral perfusion, anaesthetic and analgesic drugs, electrolyte and metabolic disturbances, infection, sleep deprivation in the intensive care unit, and the typically older age of cardiac patients all converge to disturb brain function. Clinically, delirium presents with a clouding of consciousness, inattention, disorientation, a disturbed sleep-wake cycle, fluctuating course (worse at night, the so-called sundowning), perceptual disturbances such as illusions and visual hallucinations, and either hyperactive agitation or hypoactive drowsiness. Crucially, delirium is usually reversible once the underlying cause is corrected, which is why prompt recognition matters. It is distinguished from dementia by its acute onset, fluctuating course, and impaired attention and consciousness. Knowing that delirium, not a primary psychiatric illness, dominates the postoperative period is an important clinical point." },
  { id: "rml24_029", section: "general",
    q: "The density of stones increases as we look farther away. This phenomenon is due to?",
    options: { a: "Convergence", b: "Aerial perspective", c: "Interposition", d: "Texture gradient" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Convergence is a binocular oculomotor cue based on the inward turning of the eyes for near objects; it is not about texture density.", b: "Aerial perspective concerns the haze and colour shift of distant objects due to the atmosphere, not the packing of texture elements.", c: "Interposition (overlap) is when a nearer object blocks part of a farther one; the stones increasing in density are not occluding each other." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is texture gradient. As described in an earlier item, a texture gradient is a monocular depth cue based on the way the elements of a textured surface change with distance. When you look across a field of stones, the nearby stones appear large, coarse, and widely spaced, while stones farther away appear progressively smaller, finer, and more densely packed together. This increasing density of texture with distance is exactly what the question describes, and the visual system uses it to infer how far away surfaces are and how they are slanted. James Gibson highlighted texture gradients as a key feature of the optic array that directly specifies the layout of surfaces in the environment. The gradient works because of the geometry of projection: equal-sized surface elements project smaller and closer-together retinal images the farther they are from the eye. This is one of the pictorial depth cues that operate with a single eye, alongside linear perspective, relative size, interposition, aerial perspective, and shading. Because the cue here is the changing density of a uniformly textured surface (the stones), texture gradient is the precise answer." },
  { id: "rml24_030", section: "biopsych",
    q: "The neurotransmitters called feel-good hormones are known as:",
    options: { a: "Dopamine", b: "Endorphins", c: "GABA", d: "Acetylcholine" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Dopamine is central to reward, motivation, and pleasure-seeking, but the specific 'feel-good' euphoria-and-pain-relief label in this question maps to endorphins.", c: "GABA is the main inhibitory neurotransmitter that calms neural activity; it reduces anxiety but is not the euphoria-producing feel-good hormone.", d: "Acetylcholine is involved in muscle activation, attention, and memory, not in producing pleasurable feel-good states." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is endorphins. Endorphins (the name is a contraction of 'endogenous morphine') are the body's natural opioid neuropeptides, popularly called the feel-good chemicals because they produce feelings of pleasure, euphoria, and pain relief. They are released by the pituitary gland and the central nervous system in response to stress, pain, exercise, laughter, and certain foods, and they bind to the same opioid receptors that morphine and other opiate drugs act on. By binding to these receptors, endorphins inhibit the transmission of pain signals and trigger a sense of well-being, which is the mechanism behind phenomena such as the runner's high, the analgesia of intense exercise or acupuncture, and stress-induced pain suppression. Beta-endorphin is the most potent of these peptides. Physiologically they form part of the body's intrinsic pain-modulation system, acting in regions such as the periaqueductal grey to dampen ascending pain pathways. While dopamine is also strongly tied to reward and pleasure, the specific phrase feel-good hormones in this question maps to endorphins, given their direct role in producing euphoria and natural pain relief. Understanding endorphins links the psychology of mood and pain to the body's own opioid chemistry." },
  { id: "rml24_031", section: "dev",
    q: "In the case of human development, the strongest evidence for the critical period hypothesis is found in:",
    options: { a: "Imprinting", b: "Language acquisition", c: "Embryonic development", d: "Development of vocational interests" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Language acquisition shows a sensitive period in humans and is a strong candidate, but it is more gradual and less sharply irreversible than imprinting, which the source treats as the clearest evidence.", c: "Embryonic development has critical periods for organ formation but concerns physical teratology rather than the behavioural critical-period hypothesis being tested.", d: "Vocational interests develop gradually over years and show no narrow critical window, so they offer no evidence for the hypothesis." },
    explanation: "The keyed answer is imprinting. A critical period is a specific, biologically determined window of time during development when an organism is uniquely sensitive to particular environmental input, and during which a certain experience must occur for normal development to follow. The strongest and clearest evidence for critical periods comes from imprinting, studied by the ethologist Konrad Lorenz. Lorenz showed that newly hatched goslings and ducklings will follow and form an attachment to the first moving object they see during a narrow window shortly after hatching (roughly the first 13 to 16 hours), normally the mother, but in his experiments Lorenz himself. This attachment is rapid, occurs without reinforcement, and is essentially irreversible, and it cannot be formed if the window is missed. This is the textbook illustration of an innate, time-locked critical period. The question asks where the strongest evidence for the critical period hypothesis is found, and imprinting provides the most striking, well-defined demonstration. Note that in humans, language acquisition is often cited as evidence of a sensitive period (Lenneberg argued for a critical period for language up to puberty); however, the source keys imprinting as the strongest classic evidence for the critical period concept overall, given how sharply bounded and irreversible imprinting is." },
  { id: "rml24_032", section: "general",
    q: "The process of getting adjusted to different intensities of light is called:",
    options: { a: "Visual adaptation", b: "Visual assimilation", c: "Visual accommodation", d: "Visual sensation" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Visual assimilation is not a standard term for light adjustment; assimilation in Piaget's theory is a cognitive process, irrelevant here.", c: "Visual accommodation is the lens changing shape to focus on near or far objects, a focusing mechanism, not an adjustment to light intensity.", d: "Visual sensation is simply the basic experience of seeing light, not the adaptive process of adjusting sensitivity to intensity." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is visual adaptation. Visual adaptation is the general process by which the eye adjusts its sensitivity to the prevailing level of illumination, allowing us to see across an enormous range of light intensities from dim starlight to bright sunlight. It has two complementary forms. Light adaptation is the rapid decrease in sensitivity that occurs when you move from darkness into bright light, preventing the scene from being overwhelmingly dazzling; it happens within seconds as the photoreceptors and pupil adjust. Dark adaptation is the slower increase in sensitivity that occurs when you move from bright light into darkness, taking up to about thirty minutes as rhodopsin is regenerated in the rods. Together these are subsumed under visual (or sensory) adaptation, the matching of the visual system's responsiveness to the current intensity of light. The question specifically describes getting adjusted to different intensities of light, which is precisely the definition of visual adaptation. It is important not to confuse this with visual accommodation, which is a different mechanism concerned with focusing, not with light intensity. Adaptation reflects the broader principle that sensory systems continually recalibrate to current conditions so they can detect changes efficiently." },
  { id: "rml24_033", section: "therapy",
    q: "Distress tolerance is a major treatment modality in which of the following therapy?",
    options: { a: "Behaviour therapy", b: "Dialectical Behaviour therapy", c: "Cognitive Behaviour Therapy", d: "Psychodynamic therapy" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Behaviour therapy focuses on modifying observable behaviour through conditioning principles; it does not feature a named distress tolerance module.", c: "Cognitive behaviour therapy targets dysfunctional thoughts and behaviours but does not include distress tolerance as a defining skill set; that is DBT's contribution.", d: "Psychodynamic therapy explores unconscious conflict and early relationships, an entirely different framework with no distress tolerance training." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is dialectical behaviour therapy. Distress tolerance is one of the four core skill modules of dialectical behaviour therapy (DBT), the treatment developed by Marsha Linehan, originally for chronically suicidal patients and borderline personality disorder. DBT combines cognitive-behavioural techniques with acceptance-based strategies drawn from mindfulness and Zen, reflecting its central dialectic: the balance between acceptance (accepting oneself and reality as they are) and change (working to build a better life). Its four skills modules are mindfulness (being present and aware without judgement), distress tolerance (surviving crises and tolerating painful emotions without making them worse through impulsive or self-destructive acts), emotion regulation (understanding and changing emotional responses), and interpersonal effectiveness (asserting needs and maintaining relationships). Distress tolerance specifically teaches crisis-survival skills such as distraction, self-soothing, improving the moment, and radical acceptance, so that patients can get through unbearable moments without resorting to self-harm, substance use, or other damaging behaviour. Because distress tolerance is a signature, named module unique to DBT, it points directly to dialectical behaviour therapy rather than to generic behaviour therapy or standard CBT." },
  { id: "rml24_034", section: "general",
    q: "What is the relationship between frequency and wavelength?",
    options: { a: "Direct", b: "Collateral", c: "Bilateral", d: "Inverse" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Direct would mean both increase together, which is the opposite of the true relationship at constant wave speed.", b: "Collateral is not a meaningful description of the frequency-wavelength relationship; it is a distractor with no physical meaning here.", c: "Bilateral is also not a relevant physical relationship between frequency and wavelength; it is a meaningless distractor." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is inverse. Frequency and wavelength are inversely related: as one increases, the other decreases, for any wave travelling at a constant speed. The relationship is captured by the equation speed equals frequency multiplied by wavelength. Because the speed of a given type of wave through a given medium is essentially fixed (the speed of light for electromagnetic waves, or the speed of sound in air for sound waves), frequency and wavelength must trade off against each other to keep the product constant. A high-frequency wave packs many cycles into each second and therefore has short wavelengths, while a low-frequency wave has long wavelengths. In the psychology of perception this matters directly. For light, wavelength determines perceived colour, with shorter wavelengths (higher frequency) seen as violet and blue and longer wavelengths (lower frequency) seen as red. For sound, frequency determines perceived pitch, with high frequencies heard as high-pitched tones; the corresponding short wavelengths are simply the spatial expression of that same physical quantity. Understanding this inverse relationship is fundamental to grasping how the physical properties of stimuli map onto psychological dimensions such as colour and pitch." },
  { id: "rml24_035", section: "general",
    q: "Who developed the concept of ‘magic number’?",
    options: { a: "Heinz", b: "Ebbinghaus", c: "Triesman", d: "Miller" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Heinz is not associated with the magic number; the name appears as a distractor (and Heinz refers to a different attention theorist context).", b: "Ebbinghaus pioneered the study of forgetting and the forgetting curve using nonsense syllables, not the short-term memory span.", c: "Treisman is known for feature-integration theory of attention and the attenuation model, not for the memory span." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Miller. The magic number refers to George A. Miller's famous 1956 paper, 'The Magical Number Seven, Plus or Minus Two: Some Limits on Our Capacity for Processing Information.' Miller observed that the span of immediate (short-term) memory is limited to about seven items, give or take two, meaning most people can hold roughly five to nine discrete chunks of information in conscious memory at once. Crucially, Miller introduced the concept of chunking: the capacity limit is set by the number of meaningful units or chunks, not by the raw amount of information, so by grouping items into larger meaningful chunks (for example, grouping a long string of digits into a phone number pattern) we can pack far more information into the same seven-or-so slots. This is why telephone numbers and identification codes are broken into small groups. Miller's work was a foundational contribution to the cognitive revolution and to the study of short-term memory and information processing. Later researchers, notably Nelson Cowan, argued the true limit is closer to four chunks, but the classic and exam-relevant magic number is Miller's seven plus or minus two." },
  { id: "rml24_036", section: "general",
    q: "Primacy-recency effect was first observed by whom?",
    options: { a: "McCray and Hunter", b: "Ebbinghaus", c: "Craik and Lockhart", d: "Baddeley and Hitch" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Ebbinghaus studied the forgetting curve and savings in relearning, not the serial position effect of recall order.", c: "Craik and Lockhart proposed the levels-of-processing framework, concerning depth of encoding, not serial position.", d: "Baddeley and Hitch developed the working memory model with its central executive and slave systems, a separate contribution." },
    explanation: "The keyed answer is McCray and Hunter. The primacy-recency effect, more commonly called the serial position effect, is the finding that when people try to recall a list of items, they remember the items at the beginning of the list (the primacy effect) and the items at the end of the list (the recency effect) better than the items in the middle, producing a characteristic U-shaped serial position curve. The primacy effect is usually explained by the extra rehearsal that early items receive, allowing them to be transferred into long-term memory, while the recency effect reflects the most recent items still being held in short-term (working) memory at the moment of recall. The classic dissociation is that delaying recall removes the recency effect (the short-term trace decays) but leaves primacy intact, whereas slowing presentation boosts primacy (more rehearsal) but not recency. This double dissociation became key evidence for the distinction between short-term and long-term memory stores in the modal model of memory. The source attributes the first observation of this effect to McCray and Hunter; regardless of attribution, the substantive point Sihan should learn cold is the U-shaped serial position curve and its two-store explanation." },
  { id: "rml24_037", section: "general",
    q: "Air traffic controllers and radar readers provide us with a good example of which of the following phenomenon?",
    options: { a: "Selective attention", b: "Sustained attention", c: "Divided attention", d: "Perceptual attention" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Selective attention is focusing on one stimulus while filtering out competing distractors, not maintaining watch over a long period.", c: "Divided attention is attending to two or more tasks at once; the radar task is a single continuous monitoring task.", d: "Perceptual attention is not a standard recognised category of attention and does not capture prolonged vigilance." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is sustained attention. Sustained attention, also called vigilance, is the ability to maintain focus on a task or stimulus over a prolonged period of time, especially when the relevant events are infrequent and unpredictable. Air traffic controllers watching radar screens and radar operators scanning for occasional, important signals are the textbook real-world example, because their job demands continuous concentration over long shifts, with the critical targets (an incoming aircraft, an enemy blip) appearing rarely and at unknown moments. The classic laboratory study of vigilance was Norman Mackworth's Clock Test during World War Two, designed to understand why radar operators missed signals; it revealed the vigilance decrement, the decline in detection performance over time as fatigue and monotony set in. Sustained attention is distinguished from selective attention (focusing on one stimulus while ignoring distractors), divided attention (splitting attention across multiple tasks simultaneously), and alternating attention (switching focus between tasks). Because the defining feature of the controller and radar operator is keeping watch continuously for rare signals over an extended duration, this is a clear case of sustained attention or vigilance." },
  { id: "rml24_038", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Papillae is responsible for which sensory function?",
    options: { a: "Smell", b: "Touch", c: "Taste", d: "Vision" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Smell (olfaction) is mediated by olfactory receptors in the nasal epithelium, not by tongue papillae.", b: "Touch is sensed by mechanoreceptors in the skin; although the tongue can feel touch, papillae specifically carry taste buds.", d: "Vision depends on photoreceptors (rods and cones) in the retina, entirely unrelated to papillae." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is taste. Papillae are the small raised structures on the surface of the tongue, and they house the taste buds, which contain the receptor cells responsible for the sense of taste (gustation). There are several types of papillae: fungiform papillae (mushroom-shaped, mainly on the tip and sides), circumvallate papillae (large, arranged in a V at the back of the tongue), foliate papillae (folds on the sides), and filiform papillae (which actually lack taste buds and provide texture and grip). Within the taste buds, specialised gustatory receptor cells transduce chemical molecules dissolved in saliva into neural signals. The classic taste qualities are sweet, sour, salty, and bitter, with umami (savoury, the taste of glutamate) now recognised as a fifth. These signals travel via cranial nerves (the facial, glossopharyngeal, and vagus nerves) to the gustatory cortex. Taste is a chemical sense, closely linked with smell, which is why food seems flavourless when the nose is blocked. Because papillae are the visible tongue structures that carry the taste buds, they are directly responsible for the sensory function of taste." },
  { id: "rml24_039", section: "general",
    q: "Who developed the Decay theory?",
    options: { a: "Ebbinghaus", b: "Thorndike", c: "Tulving", d: "Melton and Irwin" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Ebbinghaus charted the forgetting curve empirically but is associated with describing the rate of forgetting, not specifically authoring decay theory in this source.", c: "Tulving distinguished episodic and semantic memory and studied encoding specificity and retrieval, not decay theory.", d: "Melton and Irwin are associated with interference theory, the rival explanation of forgetting, not decay." },
    explanation: "The keyed answer is Thorndike. Decay theory (also called trace decay theory) is one of the classic explanations of forgetting. It proposes that memories leave a physical or chemical trace in the brain, often called a memory trace or engram, and that this trace gradually fades or decays over time simply through the passage of time if it is not used or rehearsed. In other words, forgetting happens because the trace deteriorates with disuse, much as a path through grass grows over if no one walks on it. Decay theory is most clearly applicable to sensory memory and short-term memory, where information that is not attended to or rehearsed vanishes within seconds. It is harder to demonstrate for long-term memory, because in practice the mere passage of time is confounded with intervening activity, and interference theory (the idea that other memories, rather than time itself, cause forgetting) is generally considered a stronger explanation for long-term forgetting. The source attributes the development of decay theory to Thorndike. The essential idea Sihan should retain is that decay theory blames the passage of time and the fading of the unused memory trace, in contrast to interference theory, which blames competition from other memories." },
  { id: "rml24_040", section: "biopsych",
    q: "What is the key brain structure that is often damaged in patients with anterograde amnesia?",
    options: { a: "Hippocampus", b: "Cerebral cortex", c: "Amygdala", d: "Hypothalamus" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "The cerebral cortex stores consolidated long-term memories across its association areas, but it is the hippocampus, not the cortex, whose damage prevents forming new memories.", c: "The amygdala processes emotion and the emotional significance of memories, particularly fear, but does not itself cause anterograde amnesia.", d: "The hypothalamus regulates homeostatic drives such as hunger, thirst, and temperature, not the formation of new declarative memories." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is the hippocampus. Anterograde amnesia is the inability to form new long-term memories after the onset of brain damage, so the person cannot lay down new declarative (explicit) memories of events and facts, even though memories formed before the injury and immediate short-term memory may be relatively intact. The key brain structure involved is the hippocampus, a seahorse-shaped structure in the medial temporal lobe that is essential for consolidating new experiences from short-term into long-term memory. The most famous case in all of neuropsychology is patient H.M. (Henry Molaison), who had large portions of his medial temporal lobes, including the hippocampus, surgically removed in 1953 to treat severe epilepsy. Afterwards he was left with a profound, permanent anterograde amnesia: he could hold a conversation but would forget it minutes later and never formed new memories of the people he met daily. Crucially, H.M. could still learn new motor skills (procedural memory) without remembering having practised them, which revealed that the hippocampus is critical for declarative memory but not for procedural memory. This dissociation established the hippocampus as the central structure for forming new conscious memories, making it the correct answer." },
  { id: "rml24_041", section: "general",
    q: "An English-speaking person may have greater difficulty learning Spanish because of his or her tendency to wish to apply English grammar to the new language. This occurs due to which of the following phenomenon?",
    options: { a: "Proactive reference", b: "Retroactive reference", c: "Trace decay", d: "Retrieval failure" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Proactive reference (interference) is old learning disrupting new; this is arguably the better description, but the source keyed retroactive, so know the distinction precisely.", c: "Trace decay attributes forgetting to the fading of the memory trace over time, not to competition between two bodies of learning.", d: "Retrieval failure is the inability to access a memory that is still stored, often due to missing cues, not interference between languages." },
    explanation: "The keyed answer is retroactive reference (retroactive interference is the standard term). Interference theory explains forgetting as the result of competition between memories rather than the simple passage of time. There are two directions of interference. Proactive interference occurs when older, previously learned material interferes with the learning or recall of new material; the prior knowledge reaches forward in time to disrupt the new. Retroactive interference occurs when newly learned material interferes with the recall of older material; the new learning reaches back to disrupt the old. In this example, the person's established knowledge of English grammar disrupts the learning of the new language, Spanish. Strictly, established English interfering with new Spanish learning is proactive interference (old disrupting new). However, the source keys this as retroactive interference, treating the new Spanish learning as the focus and the established English habit as competing with the encoding of the new system. The crucial concept for Sihan to master is the distinction itself: proactive means old-on-new, retroactive means new-on-old. Whichever label the source prefers, the phenomenon described, one language system competing with another, is a textbook case of interference in memory, and Sihan should learn both directions and their definitions cold." },
  { id: "rml24_042", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Which of the four lobes of the brain is responsible for interpreting sensory information?",
    options: { a: "Frontal lobe", b: "Occipital lobe", c: "Parietal lobe", d: "Temporal lobe" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "The frontal lobe handles voluntary movement, planning, decision-making, personality, and executive function, not sensory interpretation.", b: "The occipital lobe is dedicated to vision, processing visual input specifically rather than sensory information in general.", d: "The temporal lobe processes hearing, language comprehension, and memory; it is not the primary site for somatosensory interpretation." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is the parietal lobe. The parietal lobe, located at the top and toward the back of each cerebral hemisphere, is the brain's principal centre for integrating and interpreting somatosensory information. It contains the primary somatosensory cortex (the postcentral gyrus), which receives and processes sensations of touch, pressure, temperature, pain, and proprioception (the sense of body position) from the entire body, organised as the sensory homunculus. Beyond raw sensation, the parietal association cortex integrates input from multiple senses to build a coherent representation of the body and of space, supporting functions such as spatial awareness, navigation, hand-eye coordination, and attention to the location of objects. Damage to the parietal lobe can produce striking deficits, including hemispatial neglect (ignoring one side of space, typically after right parietal damage), agraphesthesia (inability to recognise numbers traced on the skin), and difficulties with spatial orientation. The question asks which lobe interprets sensory information, and because the parietal lobe houses the somatosensory cortex and integrates sensory input into perception of the body and surrounding space, it is the correct choice. Each of the four lobes has a characteristic functional emphasis, and the parietal lobe's emphasis is sensory integration." },
  { id: "rml24_043", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Which illness is known as the common cold of psychiatry?",
    options: { a: "Schizophrenia", b: "Depression", c: "Obsessive-compulsive disorder", d: "Hysteria" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Schizophrenia is far less common (roughly 1 percent lifetime prevalence) and is severe and chronic, so it is not described as the common cold of psychiatry.", c: "Obsessive-compulsive disorder is relatively common but not the condition given this particular nickname.", d: "Hysteria (conversion/dissociative disorders) is not the disorder referred to by this metaphor." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is depression. Depression is often called the common cold of psychiatry (sometimes the phrase is applied to anxiety, but in this question's framing it is depression) because, like the common cold in general medicine, it is extremely widespread, affects people across all ages and backgrounds, is one of the most frequently encountered conditions in clinical practice, and is something almost everyone will either experience or encounter in someone close to them at some point. Major depressive disorder is among the most prevalent mental disorders worldwide and a leading cause of disability. Clinically, depression is characterised by the core features of persistently low mood, loss of interest or pleasure (anhedonia), and reduced energy or increased fatiguability, accompanied by symptoms such as disturbed sleep and appetite, poor concentration, feelings of worthlessness or excessive guilt, psychomotor changes, and, in severe cases, suicidal thoughts. The metaphor captures both its high frequency and the fact that it presents to all kinds of practitioners, not just specialists. The phrase underscores how common and important depression is as a public health problem, and it is a frequently asked piece of psychiatric trivia." },
  { id: "rml24_044", section: "general",
    q: "Why the number plates given for motorbikes or cars carry only 4 digit numbers coupled with some alphabets?",
    options: { a: "Selected attention", b: "Span of attention", c: "Divided attention", d: "Sustained attention" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Selected (selective) attention is focusing on one stimulus while ignoring others, not the number of items grasped in one glance.", c: "Divided attention is attending to multiple tasks simultaneously, not the capacity to apprehend items at once.", d: "Sustained attention is maintaining focus over a long period; a number plate is grasped briefly, not monitored continuously." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is span of attention. The span of attention (also called the attention span or apprehension span) refers to the number of separate objects or items that can be perceived and grasped in a single momentary glance, before they must be processed serially. Classic experiments using a tachistoscope (which flashes stimuli very briefly) found that people can apprehend only a limited number of items at once, typically around five to seven for unrelated items, closely related to the limited capacity of immediate memory. Number plates are deliberately kept short, a few digits combined with a few letters, precisely so they fall within this limited span and can be taken in, read, and remembered at a glance, for instance when noting a fleeing vehicle. If plates were much longer, they would exceed the span of attention and could not be grasped or recalled from a brief exposure. This is closely linked to Miller's magic number seven and to the chunking strategy, since grouping letters and digits into a short, structured pattern keeps the whole plate within graspable limits. The question is specifically about how many items can be taken in at once, which defines the span of attention." },
  { id: "rml24_045", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Human ear responds to frequencies between the range of",
    options: { a: "20 to 20000 HZ", b: "20 to 2000 HZ", c: "20 to 200 HZ", d: "200 to 20000 HZ" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "20 to 2000 Hz drastically understates the upper limit; humans hear well above 2000 Hz, up to about 20000 Hz.", c: "20 to 200 Hz covers only very low bass frequencies and grossly understates human hearing range.", d: "200 to 20000 Hz wrongly raises the lower limit; humans can hear down to about 20 Hz, not just 200 Hz." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is 20 to 20000 Hz. The normal range of human hearing extends from about 20 hertz (cycles per second) at the low-frequency end to about 20,000 hertz (20 kHz) at the high-frequency end. Frequency is the physical property of a sound wave that the auditory system interprets as pitch: low frequencies are heard as low-pitched (deep bass) sounds and high frequencies as high-pitched (treble) sounds. Sounds below 20 Hz are called infrasound and are felt rather than heard, while sounds above 20,000 Hz are ultrasound, audible to many animals (dogs, bats, dolphins) but not to humans. The upper limit declines with age in a process called presbycusis, so older adults typically lose sensitivity to the highest frequencies first, and exposure to loud noise also damages the high-frequency hair cells in the cochlea. Human hearing is most sensitive in the range of roughly 1,000 to 4,000 Hz, which is where much of the important information in speech lies. Knowing the 20 to 20,000 Hz range is a fundamental fact in the psychology of audition and sensory processing." },
  { id: "rml24_046", section: "general",
    q: "The tip of the tongue phenomenon appears to be caused by a problem in:",
    options: { a: "Retrieval", b: "Engrams", c: "Storage", d: "Repression" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Engrams are the physical memory traces in the brain; the TOT shows the engram is intact, so the engram itself is not the problem.", c: "Storage failure would mean the information is no longer held, but TOT proves the word is stored because partial details are accessible.", d: "Repression is the motivated, unconscious blocking of threatening material; TOT involves neutral words and is not motivated forgetting." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is retrieval. The tip-of-the-tongue (TOT) phenomenon is the frustrating experience of being certain that you know a word or name and feeling it is on the verge of coming to you, yet being temporarily unable to produce it. Crucially, during a TOT state people can often recall partial information about the target, such as its first letter, the number of syllables, or words that sound similar, which shows that the memory is definitely stored in long-term memory and has not been lost. The problem is therefore not one of storage (the information is intact) but of retrieval (the temporary failure to access and bring it into conscious awareness). The TOT state was studied systematically by Brown and McNeill, who deliberately induced it by giving people definitions of rare words. It demonstrates that retrieval is a distinct stage of memory, separate from encoding and storage, and that access to stored information can fail even when the information is fully retained. The fact that the word usually pops up later, often when attention has moved elsewhere, confirms it was always there, blocked only at the point of retrieval. This makes retrieval the precise answer." },
  { id: "rml24_047", section: "dev",
    q: "Lack of bowel control in children is known as:",
    options: { a: "Encopresis", b: "Analism", c: "Enuresis", d: "Anorexia" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Analism is not a recognised clinical term for loss of bowel control; it is a distractor.", c: "Enuresis is the lack of bladder control (involuntary urination), the urinary counterpart, not the bowel disorder asked about.", d: "Anorexia is loss of appetite or the eating disorder anorexia nervosa, entirely unrelated to bowel control." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is encopresis. Encopresis is the repeated passage of faeces into inappropriate places (such as clothing or the floor) by a child who is old enough to have achieved bowel control, conventionally a chronological and developmental age of at least four years. It is the lack of bowel control beyond the expected age, and it can be either primary (the child has never established control) or secondary (control was achieved and then lost, often linked to stress or constipation with overflow soiling). Encopresis is contrasted with enuresis, which is the repeated involuntary or intentional voiding of urine, that is, lack of bladder control, into bed or clothes by a child old enough to be continent (usually age five and above). The simple way to keep these straight is that encopresis concerns the bowel (faeces) while enuresis concerns the bladder (urine). Both are elimination disorders in child psychiatry and may have physiological contributors (constipation, small bladder capacity), developmental delay, or emotional and behavioural factors. Because the question specifically asks about bowel control, encopresis is the correct term, and the key distinction to remember is bowel versus bladder." },
  { id: "rml24_048", section: "general",
    q: "In PQRST method, a method of enhancing memory, what does the alphabet ‘S’ stand for?",
    options: { a: "Storing", b: "Self-recitation", c: "Sequencing", d: "Semantics" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Storing is not a PQRST step; the method emphasises active recall, not a passive storing stage.", c: "Sequencing is not part of the PQRST scheme; it is a distractor.", d: "Semantics is not a PQRST stage either; the S specifically denotes the active self-recitation step." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Self-recitation. The PQRST method is a structured study and memory-enhancement technique designed to improve comprehension and retention of written material, especially textbooks. The letters stand for Preview, Question, Read, Self-recitation (sometimes given as Recite or Summary in related schemes, but in PQRST the S is Self-recitation), and Test. In the Preview stage the learner skims the material to get an overview of its structure and main ideas. In the Question stage the learner turns headings and topics into questions to create a purpose for reading. In the Read stage the learner reads the material actively, looking for the answers to those questions. In the Self-recitation stage the learner stops and tries to recall and recite the key information from memory, without looking, which is the crucial active-retrieval step that strengthens memory through the testing effect. Finally, in the Test stage the learner reviews and tests their overall understanding of the whole passage. PQRST is closely related to Robinson's SQ3R method (Survey, Question, Read, Recite, Review). The self-recitation step embodies the powerful principle that actively retrieving information, rather than passively rereading it, produces far better long-term retention, which is why S stands for self-recitation." },
  { id: "rml24_049", section: "therapy",
    q: "Deepika is unable to gain threatening or anxiety-provoking material into her access to conscious awareness. This phenomenon occurs due to____________________.",
    options: { a: "Suppression", b: "Repression", c: "Depression", d: "Denial" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Suppression is the conscious, deliberate setting aside of a thought that one can later recall; the question describes involuntary inability to access, which is repression.", c: "Depression is a mood disorder, not a defence mechanism, and does not describe the blocking of material from awareness.", d: "Denial refuses to acknowledge an external reality; here the issue is the unconscious blocking of internal anxiety-provoking material, which is repression." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is repression. Repression is the cornerstone defence mechanism of Freudian psychoanalysis, described by Freud as the basic mechanism on which all others rest. It is the unconscious, automatic exclusion of threatening, anxiety-provoking, or unacceptable thoughts, memories, impulses, and feelings from conscious awareness, pushing them down into the unconscious where they cannot be voluntarily retrieved. In this example, Deepika is unable to bring anxiety-provoking material into conscious awareness, which is precisely repression: the material is actively (though unconsciously) kept out of consciousness. The critical distinction Sihan must master is between repression and suppression. Repression is unconscious and involuntary; the person is not aware they are doing it and cannot simply choose to recall the material. Suppression, by contrast, is conscious and deliberate; the person knowingly and intentionally pushes a thought aside, choosing not to dwell on it for now, with the ability to bring it back later. Because the question describes an inability to gain access to the material (an involuntary blocking) rather than a deliberate choice to set it aside, the mechanism is repression. Freud believed repressed material does not vanish but continues to exert influence, leaking out in dreams, slips of the tongue, and neurotic symptoms." },
  { id: "rml24_050", section: "general",
    q: "Who developed the Filter-attenuation theory?",
    options: { a: "Broadbent", b: "Triesman", c: "Johnston", d: "Heinz" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Broadbent developed the original filter (early-selection) theory, in which the unattended channel is completely blocked, not merely attenuated.", c: "Johnston (with Heinz) proposed a flexible/multimode model of attention, not the filter-attenuation theory.", d: "Heinz is associated with the multimode theory of attention, not the attenuation model, which is Treisman's." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Treisman. Anne Treisman proposed the attenuation theory (the filter-attenuation model) of selective attention in 1964 as a refinement of Donald Broadbent's earlier filter theory. Broadbent's filter model held that attention works like an all-or-nothing gate at an early stage: the unattended channel is blocked completely, so its content is not processed for meaning. However, this could not explain findings such as the cocktail party effect, where a person attending to one conversation will still notice their own name spoken in an unattended conversation; if the unattended channel were fully blocked, the name could never get through. Treisman solved this by replacing the all-or-nothing filter with an attenuator. In her model the unattended message is not completely blocked but merely turned down or weakened (attenuated), so it still receives some processing. Words with low thresholds for activation, such as one's own name or highly salient or expected words, can still break through the attenuated channel and reach awareness. This elegantly accounts for why we mostly ignore unattended input yet occasionally catch personally significant items. Treisman's attenuation theory is therefore the filter-attenuation model the question refers to." },
  { id: "rml24_051", section: "biostats",
    q: "Arbitrary zero is the characteristic of which scale?",
    options: { a: "Ratio scale", b: "Ordinal scale", c: "Interval scale", d: "Nominal scale" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "An ordinal scale only ranks items in order without equal intervals and has no defined zero, so 'arbitrary zero' does not characterise it.", c: "The interval scale is actually the textbook-correct answer here, since arbitrary zero is its defining feature; it is keyed wrong as incorrect by the source.", d: "A nominal scale merely categorises or labels with no order, intervals, or zero point at all." },
    explanation: "The source keys this as the ratio scale, but the textbook-correct answer is the interval scale, so learn the concept carefully. The four levels of measurement, described by S. S. Stevens, are nominal, ordinal, interval, and ratio, each carrying more mathematical information than the last. An interval scale has ordered values with equal, meaningful intervals between adjacent points, so differences are consistent and addition and subtraction are meaningful, but it has only an arbitrary (not absolute) zero: zero does not indicate the complete absence of the quantity. The classic example is temperature in Celsius or Fahrenheit, where zero degrees does not mean no temperature at all but is just an arbitrary reference point, which is why you cannot say 20 degrees is twice as hot as 10 degrees. This arbitrary zero is precisely the defining feature of the interval scale. A ratio scale, by contrast, has a true, absolute zero representing the genuine absence of the quantity (weight, height, reaction time), which permits meaningful ratios (4 kilograms really is twice 2 kilograms). Because arbitrary zero is the hallmark of the interval scale, the conceptually correct answer is interval; the source has marked ratio, which is an error. Sihan should answer based on the textbook rule: arbitrary zero equals interval scale, true zero equals ratio scale." },
  { id: "rml24_052", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Which of the following act as receptor(s) for scotopic vision?",
    options: { a: "Retina", b: "Cones", c: "Iris", d: "Rods" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "The retina is the entire light-sensitive layer containing both rods and cones; it is not a single receptor type for scotopic vision specifically.", b: "Cones serve photopic (bright-light) and colour vision, the opposite of scotopic vision.", c: "The iris is the coloured muscular ring that controls pupil size and the amount of light entering; it is not a photoreceptor at all." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is rods. Scotopic vision is vision under low levels of illumination, such as at night or in a dimly lit room, and it is mediated by the rod photoreceptors of the retina. Rods are far more numerous than cones (around 120 million rods versus 6 million cones) and are distributed mainly in the peripheral retina rather than the central fovea. They contain the highly light-sensitive pigment rhodopsin, which allows them to respond to very small amounts of light, making them the receptors for dim-light vision. However, rods do not encode colour and have low spatial resolution (acuity), which is why scotopic vision is essentially in shades of grey and is relatively blurry; this is the basis of the saying that at night all cats are grey. The complementary system is photopic vision, daytime vision under bright illumination, which is served by the cones; cones are concentrated in the fovea, give us sharp detail and colour vision, but need more light to function. The Purkinje shift (the relative brightening of blue and dimming of red as illumination falls) reflects the switch from cone-dominated photopic to rod-dominated scotopic vision. Because the question asks about receptors for low-light (scotopic) vision, the answer is rods." },
  { id: "rml24_053", section: "general",
    q: "The rail tracks appear to be converging to all of u is an example of:",
    options: { a: "Permanent illusion", b: "Personal illusion", c: "Visual illusion", d: "Muller-Lyer illusion" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Personal illusion implies an idiosyncratic individual misperception, but everyone sees rails converge, so it is a shared, not personal, illusion.", c: "Visual illusion is in fact the most conceptually defensible answer; it is keyed as incorrect by the source, which preferred the 'permanent illusion' label for an always-occurring effect.", d: "The Muller-Lyer illusion is a specific illusion about line length distorted by arrowheads, not about parallel lines converging with distance." },
    explanation: "The source keys this as permanent illusion, but the conceptually defensible answer is visual illusion, so learn the concept and note the discrepancy. An illusion is a consistent, systematic misperception of a real external stimulus, where the way we perceive something differs reliably from its actual physical properties. Railway tracks are physically parallel, yet as they recede they appear to converge toward a single point on the horizon. This is driven by linear perspective, one of the monocular depth cues: parallel lines projecting onto the retina appear to come together as distance increases. Because everyone perceives this convergence the same way regardless of effort, some texts class it as a permanent (always-occurring, irresistible) illusion to distinguish it from illusions that can be dispelled once explained; this is presumably why the source keys permanent illusion. Conceptually, however, the converging-rails effect is most naturally described as a visual illusion based on linear perspective. The broad point for Sihan is that illusions arise because the brain applies normally useful interpretive rules (treating converging lines as receding parallel lines) which, in certain configurations, yield a perception that departs from physical reality. Whether labelled permanent or visual, this is the classic linear-perspective illusion of railway tracks." },
  { id: "rml24_054", section: "therapy",
    q: "The ‘start-stop technique’ and the ‘squeeze technique’ is useful in the treatment of:",
    options: { a: "Male erectile disorder", b: "Premature ejaculation", c: "Inhibited male orgasm", d: "Vaginismus" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Male erectile disorder (difficulty achieving or maintaining an erection) is treated with different approaches such as sensate focus and medication, not the squeeze/stop-start methods.", c: "Inhibited male orgasm (delayed ejaculation) is the opposite problem; the squeeze technique would worsen, not help, it.", d: "Vaginismus is involuntary spasm of the vaginal muscles in women, treated with dilators and relaxation, unrelated to ejaculatory control techniques." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is premature ejaculation. Both the stop-start technique and the squeeze technique are specific behavioural sex-therapy methods developed to treat premature ejaculation, the persistent or recurrent ejaculation that occurs with minimal stimulation and sooner than the man or couple wishes, causing distress. The stop-start technique, introduced by James Semans, involves stimulating the man toward ejaculation and then stopping all stimulation just before the point of ejaculatory inevitability, allowing arousal to subside, then resuming, repeated several times. This trains the man to recognise and tolerate high levels of arousal and to gain greater control over the ejaculatory reflex. The squeeze technique, developed by Masters and Johnson, is a variation in which, as ejaculation approaches, the partner (or the man) firmly squeezes the glans of the penis at the coronal ridge for several seconds, which reduces the urge to ejaculate; this too is repeated to build control. Both methods are based on behavioural learning principles, gradually raising the threshold for the ejaculatory reflex through repeated practice. They are first-line non-pharmacological treatments and are a classic exam association: stop-start and squeeze techniques equal premature ejaculation." },
  { id: "rml24_055", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Premature ejaculation is most commonly associated with:",
    options: { a: "Diabetes mellitus", b: "Masturbation", c: "Personality disorder", d: "Anxiety state" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Diabetes mellitus is more associated with erectile dysfunction (through vascular and nerve damage), not premature ejaculation.", b: "Masturbation itself is not the established common cause; rapid ejaculation may sometimes be conditioned, but anxiety is the key associated factor.", c: "Personality disorder is not the typical or most common association; premature ejaculation is most strongly linked to anxiety rather than a stable personality pathology." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is an anxiety state. Premature ejaculation is the most common male sexual dysfunction, and in the great majority of cases it is psychogenic rather than organic, with anxiety being the most commonly associated and contributing factor. Performance anxiety (worry about sexual performance, about pleasing the partner, or about ejaculating too soon) heightens sympathetic nervous system arousal, and because ejaculation is partly mediated by sympathetic activity, this anxious over-arousal lowers the ejaculatory threshold and triggers rapid ejaculation. This creates a self-perpetuating vicious cycle: the man becomes anxious about ejaculating prematurely, the anxiety itself hastens ejaculation, and the resulting failure increases anxiety for next time. Other psychological contributors include early conditioning toward rapid ejaculation, relationship problems, and guilt, but anxiety is the central, classically associated state. This is precisely why behavioural treatments such as the stop-start and squeeze techniques, along with anxiety-reduction approaches like sensate focus and sometimes pharmacological aids, are effective: they break the anxiety-arousal cycle and retrain ejaculatory control. The strong link between premature ejaculation and anxiety is a standard clinical teaching point and explains the predominantly psychological treatment approach." },
  { id: "rml24_056", section: "biostats",
    q: "The research findings indicate that job satisfaction and performance usually have:",
    options: { a: "Weak negative correlation", b: "No correlation", c: "Weak positive correlation", d: "Strong positive correlation" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "A weak negative correlation would imply happier workers perform worse, which contradicts the research showing a positive link.", b: "No correlation overstates the weakness; there is a real, if modest, positive association, not a complete absence of one.", d: "A strong positive correlation overstates the relationship; the link is reliably present but only modest in size, not strong." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is a weak positive correlation. Decades of research in industrial and organisational psychology, including major meta-analyses, have examined the relationship between job satisfaction and job performance. The consistent finding is that the two are positively correlated, but the correlation is only modest or weak, with meta-analytic estimates typically around 0.3. This means that, on average, more satisfied workers do tend to perform somewhat better, but satisfaction explains only a small portion of the variation in performance, so it is far from a one-to-one relationship. The weakness of the link is important theoretically and practically. It tells us that simply making employees happier will not automatically and dramatically raise their performance, because performance also depends heavily on ability, training, resources, role clarity, incentives, and situational constraints. The direction of causality is also debated: satisfaction may improve performance, good performance may breed satisfaction (especially when it brings rewards), or a third factor may drive both. A positive but weak correlation captures this nuanced reality far better than claims of no relationship or a strong one. This is a frequently tested fact in organisational psychology, and the key is to remember it is positive but only weak." },
  { id: "rml24_057", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Following a stroke, a person has difficulty in recognizing faces. This condition is called:",
    options: { a: "Aphasia", b: "Agnosia", c: "Apraxia", d: "Ataxia" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Aphasia is a disorder of language (producing or understanding speech), not of recognising faces.", c: "Apraxia is the inability to carry out learned, purposeful movements despite intact motor function, not a recognition deficit.", d: "Ataxia is a loss of coordination and balance, usually from cerebellar damage, unrelated to recognising faces." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is agnosia. Agnosia is the loss of the ability to recognise objects, persons, sounds, shapes, or other stimuli despite intact basic sensory functioning (the person can see or hear perfectly well) and intact general intelligence. The term, coined by Sigmund Freud, literally means 'without knowledge.' The specific inability to recognise faces is called prosopagnosia (face blindness), a form of visual agnosia usually associated with damage to the fusiform face area in the occipitotemporal region, often after a stroke or head injury. A person with prosopagnosia may be unable to recognise even close family members or their own face in a mirror, yet they can still see the face clearly and may identify the person by voice, gait, or clothing. The crucial point is that the deficit is one of recognition or interpretation, not of basic perception: the visual information arrives intact but cannot be matched to stored knowledge of identity. This contrasts with the other A-words in the options, which name different higher-cortical deficits. Because the question specifically describes failure to recognise faces, agnosia (specifically prosopagnosia) is the correct answer." },
  { id: "rml24_058", section: "dev",
    q: "Who gave the important concept of ‘identity crisis’ in adolescents?",
    options: { a: "Bowlby", b: "Melanie Klein", c: "Eric Erikson", d: "Eric Berne" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Bowlby developed attachment theory concerning the early infant-caregiver bond, not the adolescent identity crisis.", b: "Melanie Klein worked on object relations and the inner world of infancy (splitting, paranoid-schizoid position), not identity in adolescence.", d: "Eric Berne founded transactional analysis (Parent-Adult-Child ego states); the similar name is a trap, but the identity crisis is Erikson's." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Erik Erikson. The concept of the identity crisis comes from Erik Erikson's theory of psychosocial development, which extended Freud's psychosexual stages into a lifespan model of eight stages, each defined by a central conflict the person must resolve. The fifth stage, occurring in adolescence (roughly ages 12 to 18), is identity versus role confusion. During this stage the young person grapples with the central question 'Who am I?', experimenting with different roles, values, beliefs, and possible futures in an effort to forge a coherent, stable sense of self. Erikson called the struggle and exploration of this period the identity crisis: a normative turning point where the adolescent must integrate childhood identifications with new social demands and personal choices. Successful resolution yields a firm sense of identity and the virtue of fidelity (the ability to commit to values and relationships); failure leads to role confusion, a sense of not knowing who one is or where one belongs. James Marcia later expanded this into four identity statuses (diffusion, foreclosure, moratorium, achievement). Because the term identity crisis and the adolescent identity stage are Erikson's signature contributions, he is the correct answer." },
  { id: "rml24_059", section: "therapy",
    q: "The treatment of choice for Adjustment disorder is:",
    options: { a: "Psychoanalytic psychotherapy", b: "Group psychotherapy", c: "Cognitive behaviour therapy", d: "Crisis intervention" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Psychoanalytic psychotherapy is long-term and explores deep unconscious conflict, disproportionate for a brief, stressor-linked, self-limiting disorder.", b: "Group psychotherapy can help some conditions but is not the first-line, immediate treatment for an acute stress reaction.", c: "Cognitive behaviour therapy is effective for many disorders but is more structured and longer than the brief crisis-oriented help that suits adjustment disorder." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is crisis intervention. Adjustment disorder is a maladaptive emotional or behavioural reaction to an identifiable psychosocial stressor (such as divorce, job loss, illness, or relocation) that develops within about three months of the stressor and typically resolves within six months once the stressor or its consequences have ended. Because adjustment disorder is, by definition, a relatively short-lived response to a specific, identifiable stressful situation rather than a deep-seated chronic illness, the treatment of choice is crisis intervention: brief, focused, supportive psychological help aimed at the immediate situation. Crisis intervention helps the person ventilate their feelings, mobilise their existing coping resources and social supports, problem-solve around the stressor, and regain equilibrium, all within a short time frame. It is practical, present-focused, and time-limited, matching the time-limited nature of the disorder itself. More intensive or long-term modalities are usually unnecessary and disproportionate for a condition expected to remit on its own with support. The principle to learn is that the management of a condition should match its nature and course: for an acute, stressor-linked, self-limiting reaction like adjustment disorder, brief crisis-oriented support is the appropriate first-line treatment." },
  { id: "rml24_060", section: "general",
    q: "Which of the following would be considered an acute stressor?",
    options: { a: "A pop quiz", b: "HIV disease", c: "Marital discord", d: "Colon cancer" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "HIV disease is a chronic, lifelong medical condition, producing ongoing rather than brief stress.", c: "Marital discord is an ongoing, persistent relationship problem, a chronic stressor, not a brief event.", d: "Colon cancer is a serious chronic illness involving prolonged treatment and uncertainty, a chronic stressor rather than an acute one." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is a pop quiz. Stressors are commonly classified by their duration and time course into acute and chronic types. An acute stressor is a short-term, time-limited, often sudden demand that has a clear beginning and end, such as a pop quiz, a job interview, giving a speech, or narrowly avoiding a car accident. The body responds with a quick fight-or-flight reaction, and once the event passes the stress typically resolves. A chronic stressor, by contrast, is long-lasting and persistent, continuing over weeks, months, or years, such as living with a chronic illness like HIV disease or colon cancer, enduring ongoing marital discord, financial hardship, or caregiving for a disabled relative. Chronic stress is particularly harmful because the prolonged activation of the stress response (with sustained cortisol release) wears down the body, contributing to cardiovascular disease, immune suppression, and depression, as captured by Selye's concept of the exhaustion stage of the general adaptation syndrome. Among the options, the pop quiz is the only brief, discrete, time-limited event, while HIV disease, marital discord, and colon cancer are all enduring, ongoing conditions. Therefore the pop quiz is the acute stressor." },
  { id: "rml24_061", section: "abnormal",
    q: "The term ‘kleptomania’ was given by:",
    options: { a: "Marc", b: "Bleuler", c: "Munro", d: "Morselli" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Bleuler coined schizophrenia and ambivalence, not kleptomania.", c: "Munro is associated with monosymptomatic hypochondriacal psychosis (delusional disorder, somatic type), not kleptomania.", d: "Morselli coined the term dysmorphophobia (body dysmorphic disorder), not kleptomania." },
    explanation: "The keyed answer is Marc. Kleptomania is an impulse-control disorder characterised by a recurrent, irresistible urge to steal objects that are not needed for personal use or for their monetary value. The defining features are a rising sense of tension before the theft, followed by pleasure, gratification, or relief at the time of committing it; the stealing is not done out of anger, vengeance, or in response to a delusion, and is not better explained by conduct disorder or antisocial personality. The person often feels guilt or shame afterward, and the stolen items are frequently discarded, hoarded, or given away, underscoring that the act is driven by the compulsion itself rather than by want of the object. Historically, the term kleptomania is attributed to the early nineteenth-century French physician C. C. Marc (often credited alongside Esquirol in the French alienist tradition that first described these monomanias). The disorder belongs to the family of impulse-control disorders alongside pyromania (the compulsion to set fires) and intermittent explosive disorder. The key learning point is the characteristic cycle of mounting tension relieved by an act of stealing that serves no rational acquisitive purpose, and the attribution of the term to Marc." },
  { id: "rml24_062", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Hypnagogic hallucinations most commonly occur in:",
    options: { a: "Schizophrenia", b: "Delirium", c: "Drug dependence", d: "Normal" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Schizophrenia involves hallucinations during full wakefulness (classically auditory), not the benign sleep-onset hallucinations described.", b: "Delirium produces hallucinations (often visual) as part of an acute confusional state, not the normal sleep-transition phenomenon.", c: "Drug dependence and intoxication can cause hallucinations, but hypnagogic hallucinations specifically occur in normal people at sleep onset." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is normal. Hypnagogic hallucinations are vivid perceptual experiences (most often visual or auditory) that occur during the transition from wakefulness into sleep, that is, while falling asleep. Their counterpart, hypnopompic hallucinations, occur on waking up, during the transition from sleep to wakefulness. The crucial teaching point is that these hallucinations occurring at the threshold of sleep are normal phenomena, experienced by a large proportion of healthy people, and are not by themselves indicative of any psychiatric or neurological disorder. They arise because the brain is shifting between sleep and waking states, and fragments of dream-like imagery intrude into a partially conscious mind. Common examples include hearing one's name called, hearing a doorbell or knock, brief flashes of imagery, or the sensation of falling (the hypnic jerk). Because they happen at sleep onset in ordinary people, the correct association is with the normal state rather than with illness. Clinically, very frequent or intense hypnagogic hallucinations, especially combined with sleep paralysis, excessive daytime sleepiness, and cataplexy, form part of the tetrad of narcolepsy, but in isolation occasional hypnagogic hallucinations are a benign, normal experience." },
  { id: "rml24_063", section: "general",
    q: "The tendency to assume that we would have been better at predicting actual events than is really true is termed as:",
    options: { a: "Hindsight effect", b: "Just world hypothesis", c: "Decisiveness", d: "Confirmation" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "The just-world hypothesis is the belief that people get what they deserve and the world is fundamentally fair, not a bias about predicting outcomes.", c: "Decisiveness is the trait of making decisions quickly and firmly, not a retrospective prediction bias.", d: "Confirmation (bias) is the tendency to seek and favour information that supports existing beliefs, a different cognitive bias from overestimating past predictability." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is the hindsight effect (hindsight bias). Hindsight bias, often summed up as the 'I knew it all along' phenomenon, is the tendency, after an event has occurred, to overestimate how predictable that outcome was beforehand. Once people learn how something turned out, they retrospectively believe they would have, or did, foresee it, even though they had no such certainty in advance. The bias arises because learning the actual outcome reshapes our memory and reasoning: the known result becomes integrated into our understanding, making the path to it seem inevitable and obvious in retrospect, while we forget the genuine uncertainty we felt beforehand. Baruch Fischhoff conducted classic experiments demonstrating this, showing that people who were told an outcome rated it as having been far more predictable than people who did not know the outcome. Hindsight bias is important because it distorts our evaluation of past decisions (we unfairly blame decision-makers for not foreseeing what now seems obvious), inflates our confidence in our own judgement, and impairs learning from experience. It appears in medicine (judging diagnoses after the fact), law (juries judging negligence), finance, and everyday life. The question's wording, assuming we would have predicted actual events better than we really could, is the precise definition of hindsight bias." },
  { id: "rml24_064", section: "biopsych",
    q: "In a typical major sleep period, how many cycles of NREM-REM is there:",
    options: { a: "2-4", b: "4-6", c: "6-10", d: "10-12" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "2 to 4 cycles is too few for a normal full night's sleep of seven to eight hours.", c: "6 to 10 cycles is too many; with roughly 90-minute cycles a night yields only about four to six.", d: "10 to 12 cycles greatly exceeds what fits into a typical sleep period." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is 4 to 6. During a typical night of sleep, a person passes through several complete cycles of NREM (non-rapid eye movement) and REM (rapid eye movement) sleep, with each cycle lasting roughly 90 minutes, so across a full night of about seven to eight hours there are approximately four to six such cycles. Sleep architecture works as follows: a cycle begins with NREM sleep, which itself progresses through stages, from light sleep (stages N1 and N2) into deep slow-wave sleep (stage N3, dominated by delta waves and important for physical restoration), and then ascends back toward lighter sleep before entering REM sleep, the stage of vivid dreaming characterised by rapid eye movements, muscle atonia (paralysis of voluntary muscles), and brain activity resembling wakefulness. Importantly, the composition of cycles changes across the night: early cycles contain more deep slow-wave NREM sleep, whereas later cycles contain progressively longer REM periods, which is why most vivid dreaming and the longest REM episode occur toward morning. Knowing that a healthy adult cycles through NREM and REM about four to six times per night, with each cycle around 90 minutes, is a standard fact in the biopsychology of sleep." },
  { id: "rml24_065", section: "biostats",
    q: "Parameter is characteristic of:",
    options: { a: "The sample", b: "The population", c: "An individual", d: "A test" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "A characteristic of the sample is a statistic, not a parameter; this is the precise opposite of the correct answer.", c: "An individual is a single case, not a population characteristic; parameters summarise whole populations.", d: "A test is a measurement instrument, not the group whose characteristic a parameter describes." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is the population. In statistics, a clear distinction is drawn between a parameter and a statistic. A parameter is a numerical value that describes a characteristic of an entire population, the complete set of all individuals or observations of interest, for example the true mean height of all adults in a country or the true proportion of voters favouring a candidate. A statistic, by contrast, is a numerical value computed from a sample, a subset drawn from the population, for example the mean height of 500 surveyed adults. The reason this distinction matters is that we usually cannot measure an entire population directly (it is too large, costly, or inaccessible), so we draw a sample, calculate sample statistics, and use them to estimate the unknown population parameters, a process called statistical inference. Parameters are typically denoted by Greek letters (mu for the population mean, sigma for the population standard deviation), while sample statistics use Roman letters (x-bar for the sample mean, s for the sample standard deviation). The accuracy of estimating a parameter from a statistic depends on sampling methods and sample size. Because a parameter by definition describes the population, the population is the correct answer." },
  { id: "rml24_066", section: "general",
    q: "A student is afraid of a sports ground in which he has lost a game. It is an example of:",
    options: { a: "Inhibition", b: "Cognitive learning", c: "Classical conditioning", d: "Social learning" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Inhibition refers to the suppression of a response (such as conditioned inhibition or reciprocal inhibition), not the acquisition of a fear by association.", b: "Cognitive learning involves acquiring knowledge through mental processes like insight or observation, not the automatic stimulus-fear association shown here.", d: "Social learning is acquiring behaviour by observing and imitating others; the student's fear comes from his own direct aversive experience, not from watching someone else." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is classical conditioning. Classical (Pavlovian) conditioning is learning by association, in which a previously neutral stimulus comes to elicit a response after being paired with a stimulus that naturally produces that response. In this example, the sports ground was originally a neutral place. The painful, distressing experience of losing an important game (an aversive unconditioned stimulus that naturally produces unpleasant emotional reactions, the unconditioned response) became associated with that particular ground. Through this pairing, the ground itself (now a conditioned stimulus) acquires the power to evoke fear and distress (the conditioned response), so that merely being at or near the ground now triggers anxiety even with no game taking place. This is exactly how many fears and phobias are acquired, as demonstrated by Watson and Rayner in the Little Albert experiment, where a neutral white rat paired with a frightening loud noise came to elicit fear. The student's fear of the sports ground is therefore a conditioned emotional response established through classical conditioning. It is distinguished from operant conditioning (learning from the consequences of one's own actions) because here the fear arises from a stimulus association, not from reinforcement or punishment of a chosen behaviour." },
  { id: "rml24_067", section: "general",
    q: "In an experiment the stimuli were presented to the subjects in certain order and after the presentation of stimuli their order was disturbed. In the test phase the subjects were needed to set them in the presented order. Which of the following methods was used in this experiment?",
    options: { a: "Reconstruction", b: "Relearning", c: "Recognition", d: "Recall" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Relearning measures memory by the savings in time or trials needed to learn material again, not by restoring a disturbed order.", c: "Recognition requires identifying previously seen items among options, not rearranging scrambled items into their original sequence.", d: "Recall requires reproducing learned material from memory without the items being provided; here the items are present and only their order must be restored." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is reconstruction. Reconstruction is a method of measuring retention (memory) in which the participant is asked to restore or rearrange material into its original order or arrangement after it has been disturbed. In the experiment described, stimuli were first presented in a particular order, the order was then scrambled, and the participant's task in the test phase was to put the items back into the originally presented sequence. Because the task involves rebuilding the original arrangement from disordered elements, it is precisely the reconstruction method. It is one of several techniques for measuring memory, each tapping retention in a different way. Recall requires producing the learned material from memory with few or no cues (such as an essay question or free recall of a list). Recognition requires only identifying previously encountered material among alternatives (such as a multiple-choice question), and it is generally easier than recall because retrieval cues are provided. Relearning (the savings method, devised by Ebbinghaus) measures memory by how much faster or easier it is to learn material a second time compared with the first, capturing retention even when conscious recall fails. Reconstruction is distinctive in requiring the restoration of the original order or pattern, which is exactly what this experiment demanded." },
  { id: "rml24_068", section: "general",
    q: "Habit weakening through the elimination of any reward is called:",
    options: { a: "Negative reinforcement", b: "Extinction", c: "Reciprocal inhibition", d: "Flooding" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Negative reinforcement strengthens a behaviour by removing an aversive stimulus; it increases responding, the opposite of weakening a habit.", c: "Reciprocal inhibition is Wolpe's principle of replacing anxiety with an incompatible relaxed response, used in systematic desensitisation, not the removal of reward.", d: "Flooding is an exposure therapy that confronts a feared stimulus intensely all at once; it is not the withdrawal of reinforcement." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is extinction. Extinction is the weakening and eventual disappearance of a previously learned (conditioned) response when the reinforcement that maintained it is no longer provided. In operant conditioning, a behaviour that has been strengthened by reward will gradually decline and stop if the reward is consistently withheld; the organism learns that the response no longer pays off. For example, if a child's tantrums had been reinforced by attention, systematically ignoring the tantrums (removing the reward) will lead to their extinction. In classical conditioning, extinction occurs when the conditioned stimulus is repeatedly presented without the unconditioned stimulus, so the conditioned response fades. The question describes habit weakening through the elimination of any reward, which is exactly the operant definition of extinction. Two related phenomena are worth knowing: spontaneous recovery, where an extinguished response briefly reappears after a rest period, showing extinction suppresses rather than erases learning; and the extinction burst, a temporary increase in the behaviour right after reinforcement stops. Extinction must be distinguished from forgetting (loss over time without active non-reinforcement) and from punishment (applying an aversive consequence). Because the defining feature here is the removal of reward leading to the habit dying out, extinction is the precise answer." },
  { id: "rml24_069", section: "assessment",
    q: "‘Scales’ and ‘Inventories’ are usually:",
    options: { a: "Subjective", b: "Objective", c: "Projective", d: "Introjective" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Subjective describes measures that depend on personal judgement and interpretation, which is the opposite of the standardised scoring of inventories.", c: "Projective tests use ambiguous stimuli requiring clinical interpretation (like the Rorschach), not the fixed-item format of scales and inventories.", d: "Introjective is not a recognised category of psychological test; it is a distractor with no measurement meaning." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is objective. In psychological assessment, scales and inventories (such as personality inventories like the MMPI or attitude and symptom rating scales) are classified as objective measures. Objective tests present clearly structured items, usually with fixed response formats such as true/false, yes/no, or Likert-type rating scales, and they are scored according to a predetermined, standardised key. Because scoring does not depend on the examiner's interpretation or judgement, two different scorers will arrive at the same score for the same set of responses; this independence from examiner bias is precisely what makes them objective. This stands in contrast to projective tests (such as the Rorschach inkblot test or the Thematic Apperception Test), which present ambiguous stimuli onto which the person projects their inner thoughts and feelings, and which require the clinician's skilled interpretation, making them more subjective and less standardised in scoring. Objective inventories have the advantages of high reliability, ease of administration and scoring (often by computer), and the ability to compare scores against established norms. Their potential weakness is susceptibility to deliberate faking or response biases, since respondents can sometimes guess what answers look favourable. Because scales and inventories use fixed items and standardised, examiner-independent scoring, they are objective measures." },
  { id: "rml24_070", section: "general",
    q: "A rule-of-thumb that allows for an efficient search for the selection of a problem is termed as",
    options: { a: "Algorithm", b: "Education", c: "Learning", d: "Heuristic" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "An algorithm is a guaranteed, exhaustive step-by-step procedure, the opposite of an efficient rule-of-thumb shortcut.", b: "Education is the broad process of acquiring knowledge and skills, not a specific problem-solving shortcut.", c: "Learning is the general acquisition of new behaviour or knowledge, far too broad to denote a rule-of-thumb search strategy." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is heuristic. A heuristic is a mental shortcut or rule of thumb that allows for quick, efficient problem-solving and decision-making by reducing the search through possibilities, though it does not guarantee a correct solution. Heuristics are valuable because they save time and cognitive effort, letting us reach reasonable solutions without exhaustively examining every option. The question's phrasing, a rule of thumb allowing an efficient search for the solution of a problem, is essentially the textbook definition of a heuristic. The contrasting concept is an algorithm, a systematic, step-by-step procedure that, if followed correctly, is guaranteed to produce the correct solution, but which may be slow and effortful because it exhaustively works through possibilities. The trade-off is therefore between the speed and efficiency of heuristics and the certainty of algorithms. Heuristics were studied extensively by Amos Tversky and Daniel Kahneman, who identified common ones such as the availability heuristic (judging probability by how easily examples come to mind), the representativeness heuristic (judging by similarity to a prototype), and anchoring and adjustment. While heuristics are efficient, they can lead to systematic errors and biases. Because the question stresses an efficient rule-of-thumb search rather than a guaranteed procedure, heuristic is the correct answer." },
  { id: "rml24_071", section: "general",
    q: "In order, starting from the first stage, what are the stages of the general adaption syndrome",
    options: { a: "Resistance, alarm, exhaustion", b: "Alarm, resistance, exhaustion", c: "Resistance, illness, alarm,", d: "Alarm, exhaustion, illness" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Resistance, alarm, exhaustion reverses the first two stages; alarm must come first as the immediate reaction.", c: "Resistance, illness, alarm misorders the stages and inserts illness, which is not one of the three named GAS stages.", d: "Alarm, exhaustion, illness omits the resistance stage and adds illness, which is not a stage of the model." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is alarm, resistance, exhaustion. The general adaptation syndrome (GAS) was formulated by Hans Selye to describe the body's nonspecific physiological response to prolonged stress, and it unfolds in three stages in this fixed order. The first stage is alarm: when a stressor is first encountered, the body mounts the acute fight-or-flight reaction through activation of the sympathetic nervous system and the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis, releasing adrenaline and cortisol, raising heart rate, blood pressure, and blood sugar to mobilise energy for emergency action. The second stage is resistance: if the stressor persists, the body adapts and tries to cope, maintaining a high but more stable level of arousal and hormonal output as it strives to restore balance; outwardly the person may seem to be managing, but the body's resources are being steadily depleted. The third stage is exhaustion: if the stressor continues too long, the body's adaptive resources are used up, leading to collapse of resistance, vulnerability to illness (such as cardiovascular disease, immune suppression, ulcers), burnout, and potentially death. Selye's model was foundational in showing how chronic stress harms physical health, and the correct sequence to memorise is alarm, then resistance, then exhaustion." },
  { id: "rml24_072", section: "therapy",
    q: "A teacher in his class sees two students bored, feels his class is not liked by all the students is which type of cognitive error:",
    options: { a: "Arbitrary inference", b: "Selective attention", c: "Over generalization", d: "Magnification" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Arbitrary inference is drawing a conclusion with no supporting evidence at all; here the teacher does base it on a real detail (two bored students), but selectively.", c: "Overgeneralisation draws a broad rule from one event over time; the error here is focusing on part of one situation while ignoring the rest, not generalising across events.", d: "Magnification is exaggerating the importance of a negative event; the core error here is selectively attending to one detail and ignoring the whole context." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is selective attention (selective abstraction). In Aaron Beck's cognitive theory of depression and emotional disorders, cognitive distortions (also called cognitive errors) are systematic biases in thinking that maintain negative emotions. Selective abstraction, sometimes labelled selective attention, is the error of focusing on a single negative detail taken out of context while ignoring the more salient, often positive, features of the whole situation, and then drawing a conclusion based on that isolated fragment. In this example, the teacher fixates on the two bored students and, ignoring the rest of the class who may be engaged and attentive, concludes that the class is not liked by anyone. He has abstracted one small negative detail (two bored students) and let it dominate his judgement of the whole. This is distinct from the related Beckian distortions: arbitrary inference (jumping to a conclusion with no evidence at all, or contrary to the evidence), overgeneralisation (drawing a sweeping general rule from a single incident, such as 'I always fail at everything'), magnification and minimisation (exaggerating negatives and shrinking positives), and personalisation. Because the teacher zeroes in on a selected negative detail while ignoring the larger picture, the error is selective abstraction or selective attention." },
  { id: "rml24_073", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Following are negative features of schizophrenia except:",
    options: { a: "Avolition", b: "Alogia", c: "Ambivalence", d: "Affective blunting" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Avolition (loss of drive and motivation) is a core negative symptom, so it is not the exception.", b: "Alogia (poverty of speech) is a recognised negative symptom reflecting reduced verbal output.", d: "Affective blunting (flattened emotional expression) is a classic negative symptom, so it belongs in the negative category." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is ambivalence, which is the exception. The symptoms of schizophrenia are traditionally divided into positive and negative symptoms. Positive symptoms are an excess or distortion of normal functions, things added to experience, such as hallucinations, delusions, and disorganised thought and speech. Negative symptoms are a loss or reduction of normal functions, things taken away, and they are summarised by several A-words: avolition (lack of motivation and inability to initiate or sustain goal-directed activity), alogia (poverty of speech, reduced verbal output and content), affective blunting or flattening (diminished emotional expression in face, voice, and gesture), anhedonia (inability to experience pleasure), and asociality (social withdrawal). Avolition, alogia, and affective blunting are all classic negative symptoms, so they are not the exception. Ambivalence, the simultaneous holding of contradictory feelings or wishes, is one of Bleuler's fundamental four As of schizophrenia (along with loosening of associations, affective disturbance, and autism), but in the modern positive-versus-negative symptom classification it is not categorised as a negative symptom in the same way the deficit symptoms are. Because the question asks which is NOT a negative feature, ambivalence is the correct answer to select." },
  { id: "rml24_074", section: "general",
    q: "Which of the following factor is most directly linked to a higher risk for coronary heart disease?",
    options: { a: "Type A personality", b: "Type B personality", c: "Hostility", d: "Self-efficacy" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Type B personality is the relaxed, non-competitive, easy-going pattern associated with lower, not higher, coronary risk.", c: "Hostility is in fact the most toxic single component of Type A, but the question keys the overall personality construct, Type A, as the answer.", d: "Self-efficacy is Bandura's concept of belief in one's own capability; high self-efficacy is generally protective, not a risk factor for heart disease." },
    explanation: "The keyed answer is Type A personality. The concept of Type A and Type B personality was developed by the cardiologists Meyer Friedman and Ray Rosenman, who noticed a behaviour pattern in their cardiac patients. The Type A behaviour pattern is characterised by chronic time urgency and impatience, competitiveness, ambition, a hard-driving achievement orientation, and hostility or aggressiveness. Their research, including the Western Collaborative Group Study, found that Type A individuals had a substantially higher risk of developing coronary heart disease than the more relaxed, easy-going, and patient Type B individuals. The proposed mechanism is that the chronic stress, heightened sympathetic nervous system arousal, and repeated surges of stress hormones associated with the Type A pattern damage the cardiovascular system over time. Importantly, later research refined this picture: of the cluster of Type A traits, hostility and anger emerged as the single most toxic component for the heart, more predictive of coronary disease than the time-urgency or competitiveness elements. So while hostility is the most damaging specific ingredient, the overall personality construct most directly linked in the classic literature to higher coronary heart disease risk is the Type A personality, which is why the source keys Type A. Sihan should know both the overall Type A construct and that hostility is its most cardiotoxic element." },
  { id: "rml24_075", section: "biopsych",
    q: "The left hemisphere is:",
    options: { a: "Concerned to a large extent with spatial and pattern sense", b: "The minor hemisphere", c: "The major hemisphere", d: "Centre of musical ability" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Spatial and pattern sense is a function of the right hemisphere, not the left, so this describes the wrong hemisphere.", b: "The minor hemisphere label traditionally refers to the right hemisphere; the left is the major one.", d: "The centre of musical ability is generally the right hemisphere, not the left, so this too misattributes a right-hemisphere function." },
    explanation: "The keyed answer is that the left hemisphere is the major (dominant) hemisphere. In most people (roughly 95 percent of right-handers and the majority of left-handers), the left cerebral hemisphere is dominant for language, including speech production (Broca's area), language comprehension (Wernicke's area), reading, and writing, as well as for logical, analytical, sequential, and mathematical processing. Because language is such a central and prominent human function, the left hemisphere has traditionally been called the major or dominant hemisphere, while the right has been called the minor hemisphere. The right hemisphere, in turn, is specialised for spatial and pattern processing, visuospatial abilities, face recognition, the perception of music and emotional tone (prosody), and holistic, simultaneous processing. The lateralisation of these functions was famously revealed by Roger Sperry's split-brain studies, in which severing the corpus callosum prevented the two hemispheres from communicating, exposing their distinct capabilities. The question asks specifically about the left hemisphere, and within the traditional major/minor terminology the left hemisphere, being language-dominant, is the major hemisphere. The descriptions of spatial and pattern sense and musical ability actually belong to the right hemisphere, which is why those options are wrong." },
  { id: "rml24_076", section: "biostats",
    q: "‘Reinforcement will have positive effect on performance,’ which type of hypothesis is this?",
    options: { a: "Null and directional", b: "Alternate and directional", c: "Null and non-directional", d: "Alternate and non-directional" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Null and directional is wrong because the statement predicts an effect (so it is alternate, not null), even though it is directional.", c: "Null and non-directional is wrong on both counts: it predicts an effect (alternate) and specifies a positive direction (directional).", d: "Alternate and non-directional is half right (alternate) but wrong on direction, since the statement does specify a positive direction." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is alternate and directional. In hypothesis testing, researchers distinguish between the null hypothesis and the alternate (alternative) hypothesis, and between directional and non-directional forms. The null hypothesis (H0) states that there is no effect or no relationship, for example that reinforcement has no effect on performance. The alternate hypothesis (H1) states that there is an effect or relationship. The statement 'Reinforcement will have a positive effect on performance' asserts that an effect exists, so it is an alternate hypothesis, not a null one. Furthermore, it specifies the direction of that effect: not merely that reinforcement affects performance, but that it improves it (a positive effect). When a hypothesis specifies the direction of the expected effect (such as 'greater than,' 'less than,' 'improves,' or 'reduces'), it is a directional hypothesis, tested with a one-tailed test. When it merely predicts a difference or relationship without specifying which way it will go (such as 'reinforcement will affect performance'), it is non-directional, tested with a two-tailed test. Because this statement both claims an effect (alternate) and specifies that the effect is positive (directional), it is an alternate and directional hypothesis. This is a standard research-methodology classification." },
  { id: "rml24_077", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Which of the following illnesses caused by a long term abuse of alcohol often involves profound retrograde amnesia?",
    options: { a: "Alzheimer’s disease", b: "Parkinson’s disease", c: "Huntington’s disease", d: "Korsakoff’s psychosis" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Alzheimer's disease is a neurodegenerative dementia not caused by alcohol; it features prominent anterograde memory loss and global cognitive decline, not specifically alcohol-related retrograde amnesia.", b: "Parkinson's disease is primarily a movement disorder from dopamine loss in the substantia nigra, not an alcohol-induced amnestic syndrome.", c: "Huntington's disease is an inherited (genetic) neurodegenerative disorder with chorea and dementia, unrelated to alcohol abuse." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Korsakoff's psychosis. Korsakoff's syndrome (Korsakoff's psychosis) is a chronic memory disorder caused by a severe deficiency of thiamine (vitamin B1), most commonly resulting from long-term, heavy alcohol misuse, which impairs thiamine absorption and nutrition. It often follows an acute episode of Wernicke's encephalopathy (the triad of confusion, ataxia, and eye-movement abnormalities such as nystagmus and ophthalmoplegia), and together the two are called Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome. The damage primarily affects the mammillary bodies and the dorsomedial thalamus, structures in the diencephalon crucial for memory. The hallmark of Korsakoff's syndrome is a profound and disproportionate memory impairment: there is severe anterograde amnesia (inability to form new memories) and also marked retrograde amnesia (loss of memories formed before the onset, often extending back years), while other cognitive functions and immediate attention may be relatively preserved. A characteristic feature is confabulation, where the patient unconsciously fills the gaps in memory with fabricated or distorted accounts, presented sincerely as if true. The question specifies an alcohol-related illness involving profound retrograde amnesia, which points directly to Korsakoff's psychosis, the classic alcohol-induced amnestic disorder." },
  { id: "rml24_078", section: "biopsych",
    q: "The master gland which manufactures hormones which affect most of the other glands and which regulates growth is the ___________________.",
    options: { a: "Adrenal cortex", b: "Adrenal medulla", c: "Pituitary gland", d: "Thyroid gland" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "The adrenal cortex secretes cortisol and aldosterone but is itself controlled by the pituitary's ACTH; it is not the master gland.", b: "The adrenal medulla releases adrenaline and noradrenaline for the fight-or-flight response; it does not regulate the other glands or growth.", d: "The thyroid gland regulates metabolism via thyroxine but is controlled by pituitary TSH; it does not govern the other glands." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is the pituitary gland. The pituitary gland is called the master gland of the endocrine system because the hormones it secretes regulate and control the activity of most of the other endocrine glands in the body. Located at the base of the brain just beneath and controlled by the hypothalamus, the pituitary has two lobes. The anterior pituitary secretes a range of tropic hormones that direct other glands: thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) controls the thyroid, adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH) controls the adrenal cortex, follicle-stimulating hormone and luteinising hormone control the gonads, and prolactin stimulates milk production. Critically for this question, the anterior pituitary also secretes growth hormone (somatotropin), which directly regulates body growth and development, accounting for the question's reference to regulating growth. The posterior pituitary releases oxytocin and antidiuretic hormone (vasopressin). The pituitary itself is governed by releasing and inhibiting hormones from the hypothalamus, so the hypothalamus and pituitary together form the command centre of the endocrine system. Because the pituitary both controls most other glands through its tropic hormones and regulates growth through growth hormone, it precisely fits the description of the master gland, making it the correct answer." },
  { id: "rml24_079", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Cutting through a person’s corpus callosum causes the person to :",
    options: { a: "Die", b: "Become blind", c: "Be unable to speak or understand language", d: "Have difficulty coordinating certain kind of activities" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Cutting the corpus callosum does not cause death; split-brain patients live and function largely normally.", b: "It does not cause blindness; visual processing is intact, though information cannot be shared between hemispheres.", c: "Language is not lost; speech and comprehension remain because language centres in the left hemisphere are untouched." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is that the person will have difficulty coordinating certain kinds of activities. The corpus callosum is the large bundle of nerve fibres (the major commissure) that connects the left and right cerebral hemispheres, allowing them to communicate and share information. When it is surgically severed, a procedure called a corpus callosotomy historically used to treat severe, intractable epilepsy by preventing seizures from spreading between hemispheres, the result is a split-brain patient. Roger Sperry and Michael Gazzaniga's famous studies of these patients revealed that the two hemispheres can no longer exchange information, producing striking but subtle effects. For example, an object shown only to the left visual field (processed by the right hemisphere) cannot be named aloud, because language resides in the left hemisphere, yet the patient can pick out the object with the left hand. In everyday life, split-brain patients function surprisingly normally, but they show difficulties in tasks requiring the two hemispheres to coordinate, such as certain bimanual movements where each hand must cooperate, and they can show conflicts between the hands. Crucially, cutting the corpus callosum does not kill the person, blind them, or destroy language, so the only accurate consequence among the options is difficulty coordinating certain kinds of activities." },
  { id: "rml24_080", section: "biopsych",
    q: "In carrying out the ‘fight or flight’ response, the role of supervisor is assigned to the:",
    options: { a: "Adrenal gland", b: "Pituitary gland", c: "Hypothalamus", d: "Parasympathetic nervous system" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "The adrenal gland is an effector that releases adrenaline and cortisol on command; it carries out, rather than supervises, the response.", b: "The pituitary gland is an intermediary that releases ACTH when instructed by the hypothalamus; it is not the top coordinator.", d: "The parasympathetic nervous system promotes rest-and-digest, calming the body, the opposite of mobilising the fight-or-flight response." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is the hypothalamus. The fight-or-flight response is the body's rapid physiological reaction to a perceived threat, preparing the organism to either confront the danger or flee from it. The hypothalamus acts as the central command or supervisor that initiates and coordinates this response, integrating signals about threat (often relayed from the amygdala) and triggering the two main stress pathways. First, the hypothalamus activates the sympathetic branch of the autonomic nervous system, which stimulates the adrenal medulla to release adrenaline (epinephrine) and noradrenaline, producing the immediate surge of effects: increased heart rate and blood pressure, dilated pupils, rapid breathing, and the diversion of blood to the muscles. Second, the hypothalamus initiates the slower hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis: it releases corticotropin-releasing hormone, which prompts the pituitary to release ACTH, which in turn stimulates the adrenal cortex to release cortisol, sustaining the stress response. Because the hypothalamus orchestrates both the fast neural (sympathetic) and the slower hormonal (HPA) arms of the reaction, it plays the supervisory, coordinating role described in the question. The adrenal glands and pituitary are downstream effectors carrying out the hypothalamus's commands, which is why the hypothalamus, not they, is the supervisor." },
  { id: "rml24_081", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Our current mood and feelings are best described by the term:",
    options: { a: "Emotion", b: "Mood", c: "Affect", d: "Feeling" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Emotion is a broad, complex reaction (arousal plus appraisal plus expression) rather than specifically the current, momentary emotional state being asked about.", b: "Mood is the sustained, pervasive emotional climate over time, not the immediate present-moment state that affect denotes.", d: "Feeling is the subjective conscious experience of emotion, a narrower internal sense, not the observed current emotional expression that defines affect." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is affect. In psychiatric and psychological terminology, careful distinctions are drawn among affect, mood, emotion, and feeling. Affect refers to the immediate, observable, and momentary expression of emotion, the current emotional state as it is displayed and experienced in the present moment; it is what a clinician observes and rates during a mental status examination (for example, describing affect as flat, blunted, restricted, labile, or congruent with mood). Because the question asks about our current mood and feelings in the immediate sense, affect is the best fit, as it denotes the present, here-and-now emotional state. Mood, by contrast, is the more sustained, pervasive, and prolonged emotional climate, the emotional background that colours a person's experience over a longer period (days or weeks), often compared to the climate, whereas affect is like the weather of a particular moment. Emotion is a broader term for a complex reaction involving physiological arousal, subjective experience, cognitive appraisal, and behavioural expression in response to a stimulus. Feeling is the subjective, conscious experience of an emotion. In clinical usage, affect is the cross-sectional, momentary emotional expression, which is why it is the answer for the current emotional state." },
  { id: "rml24_082", section: "assessment",
    q: "A response style or bias, in which there is tendency to agree with test items regardless of their contents is called:",
    options: { a: "Halo effect", b: "Central tendency effect", c: "Social desirability effect", d: "Acquiescence effect" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "The halo effect is letting an overall impression of a person bias ratings of their specific separate traits, not blanket agreement with items.", b: "The central tendency effect is the tendency to avoid extreme ratings and choose middle options, not to agree regardless of content.", c: "The social desirability effect is answering to look favourable to others, which is content-sensitive, unlike indiscriminate agreement." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is the acquiescence effect. Acquiescence (also called the acquiescence response set or yea-saying) is a response bias in which a person tends to agree with, or answer yes or true to, test items regardless of their content, rather than responding to what each item actually asks. Such respondents go along with statements out of habit, agreeableness, deference, or carelessness, which distorts their scores because their answers reflect a general tendency to agree rather than their true position. This threatens the validity of questionnaires and personality inventories. The classic safeguard against acquiescence is to balance the scale with both positively and negatively worded items (reverse-keyed items), so that consistent agreement cannot artificially inflate a score in one direction and genuine respondents must read each item. Acquiescence is one of several response styles or biases that distort self-report measures. Others include social desirability (answering in ways that present oneself favourably rather than truthfully), the central tendency bias (avoiding extreme options and clustering responses in the middle of a rating scale), and the halo effect (letting a global impression colour specific ratings). Because the defining feature here is agreeing with items irrespective of their content, the acquiescence effect is the precise answer." },
  { id: "rml24_083", section: "general",
    q: "Which of the following does NOT reflect inductive reasoning?",
    options: { a: "Probabilistic conclusions", b: "Heuristics", c: "Decision making", d: "Process involved in concept formation" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Probabilistic conclusions are a hallmark of inductive reasoning, which yields likely (not certain) generalisations, so this reflects induction.", c: "Decision making typically draws on inductive inference from past experience and probable outcomes, so it reflects inductive reasoning.", d: "Concept formation is a classic inductive process of abstracting a general rule from specific examples, so it reflects induction." },
    explanation: "The keyed answer is heuristics, which is the one that does NOT reflect inductive reasoning. Inductive reasoning is the process of drawing general conclusions or probable generalisations from specific observations or instances. Because the conclusion goes beyond the evidence given, inductive conclusions are probabilistic rather than certain: they are likely or well-supported but could still be wrong, as when we observe many white swans and induce that all swans are white. This is exactly why probabilistic conclusions reflect inductive reasoning, since induction yields likelihood, not proof. Decision making typically relies on induction too, because we weigh probable outcomes from past experience to choose among options. Concept formation is a classic inductive process, since we abstract a general category or rule from encountering specific examples (learning the concept dog from seeing many individual dogs). Heuristics, by contrast, are mental shortcuts or rules of thumb used to solve problems and make judgements efficiently; they are cognitive strategies or tools, not a form of reasoning that derives generalisations from specific cases. While heuristics are often used during inductive thinking and decision making, a heuristic itself is a shortcut, not an instance of the inductive inference process. Therefore, among the options, heuristics is the one that does not itself constitute inductive reasoning, making it the correct exception." },
  { id: "rml24_084", section: "biopsych",
    q: "The seat of emotion is to be found in _________________.",
    options: { a: "Reticular formation", b: "Hind brain", c: "Limbic system", d: "Forebrain" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "The reticular formation regulates arousal, alertness, and the sleep-wake cycle, not the generation of emotion.", b: "The hindbrain (medulla, pons, cerebellum) controls vital functions and movement coordination, not emotional experience.", d: "The forebrain is too broad a term; while it contains the limbic system, the specific seat of emotion is the limbic system, not the entire forebrain." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is the limbic system. The limbic system is a group of interconnected brain structures, located on the inner border of the cortex around the brainstem, that is most associated with emotion, motivation, and memory, and it is traditionally called the seat of emotion. Its key components include the amygdala, the hippocampus, the hypothalamus, the cingulate gyrus, and the thalamic nuclei. The amygdala is central to processing emotions, especially fear and aggression, and to attaching emotional significance to stimuli; damage to it impairs fear recognition and conditioning. The hippocampus is crucial for forming new memories and links emotion to memory. The hypothalamus regulates the autonomic and endocrine expression of emotion and basic drives such as hunger, thirst, and the stress response. The cingulate gyrus is involved in emotional regulation and the processing of emotional and motivational information. The Papez circuit, described by James Papez, traced an emotional pathway through several of these structures. Because the question asks where the seat of emotion lies, and the limbic system collectively governs the experience and expression of emotion, it is the correct answer. The other options are either too broad, too specific, or unrelated to emotion's core circuitry." },
  { id: "rml24_085", section: "general",
    q: "Who gave the concept of ‘Folk psychology’?",
    options: { a: "Wilhelm Maximilian Wundt", b: "Sigmund Freud", c: "Ivan Pavlov", d: "Wilhelm Roentgen" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Sigmund Freud founded psychoanalysis, focusing on the unconscious and psychosexual development, not folk psychology.", c: "Ivan Pavlov discovered classical conditioning through his work on salivation in dogs, unrelated to folk psychology.", d: "Wilhelm Roentgen was a physicist who discovered X-rays; he was not a psychologist and the similar first name is a trap." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Wilhelm Maximilian Wundt. Wilhelm Wundt is famous as the founder of experimental psychology, having established the first formal psychology laboratory in Leipzig in 1879, and for developing structuralism (with his student Titchener) and the method of introspection to analyse the elements of conscious experience. Less well known but equally important is that Wundt also developed the concept of Volkerpsychologie, usually translated as folk psychology or cultural psychology. In his later years Wundt devoted a monumental ten-volume work to this subject, arguing that the higher mental processes, those shaped by social and cultural life such as language, myth, religion, customs, and art, could not be studied through laboratory experiment and introspection alone, because they arise from collective human activity over history. Instead, these phenomena had to be understood by examining the products of communities and cultures. Thus Wundt distinguished between an experimental, physiological psychology of individual immediate experience and a folk psychology of the collective mind expressed through culture. This dual programme makes Wundt a foundational figure for both experimental and social-cultural psychology. Because the concept of Volkerpsychologie (folk psychology) was Wundt's, he is the correct answer." },
  { id: "rml24_086", section: "therapy",
    q: "ERP is a form of therapy used for:",
    options: { a: "Personality disorders", b: "Depression", c: "Schizophrenia", d: "Obsessive-compulsive disorder" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Personality disorders are treated with approaches like DBT, schema therapy, or psychodynamic therapy, not ERP.", b: "Depression is treated with CBT, behavioural activation, and antidepressants, not exposure and response prevention.", c: "Schizophrenia is treated with antipsychotic medication and psychosocial interventions, not ERP." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is obsessive-compulsive disorder. ERP stands for Exposure and Response Prevention, the gold-standard, evidence-based behavioural treatment for obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD), developed within the cognitive-behavioural tradition. OCD consists of obsessions (recurrent, intrusive, distressing thoughts, images, or urges, such as fears of contamination or harm) and compulsions (repetitive behaviours or mental acts, such as washing, checking, or counting, performed to neutralise the anxiety the obsessions cause). The compulsions provide short-term relief but reinforce the cycle, because the person never learns that the feared outcome would not occur and that the anxiety would subside on its own. ERP breaks this cycle through two components. Exposure means deliberately and gradually confronting the feared thoughts, objects, or situations that trigger the obsessions (for example, touching a doorknob the patient believes is contaminated). Response prevention means refraining from performing the usual compulsive ritual (not washing the hands afterward). By staying in contact with the anxiety-provoking situation without carrying out the compulsion, the patient experiences habituation, the natural decline of anxiety over time, and learns that the feared consequence does not materialise. This extinguishes the link between obsession and compulsion. Because ERP specifically targets the obsession-compulsion cycle, it is the treatment for OCD." },
  { id: "rml24_087", section: "assessment",
    q: "Which of the following statements is TRUE?",
    options: { a: "A test can be valid without being reliable", b: "A test can be reliable without being valid", c: "If a test measures what it is intended to measure, then by definition it is reliable", d: "If a test gives consistent scores when administered repeatedly then it is valid" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "A test cannot be valid without being reliable; if scores are inconsistent, the test cannot be accurately measuring the intended construct, so this is false.", c: "Measuring what it is intended to measure is validity, not reliability; validity does not by definition make a test reliable in the sense of consistency, so this conflates the two.", d: "Consistent scores establish reliability, not validity; a consistent test may still measure the wrong thing, so consistency alone does not make it valid." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is that a test can be reliable without being valid. Reliability and validity are the two cardinal properties of a good psychological test, and understanding their relationship is essential. Reliability is the consistency or stability of measurement: a reliable test gives the same or very similar results when administered repeatedly under the same conditions, or across items, or by different scorers. Validity is whether the test actually measures what it is intended to measure. The key logical relationship is that reliability is necessary but not sufficient for validity. A test can be perfectly reliable (giving consistent, repeatable scores) yet completely invalid, because it may be consistently measuring the wrong thing. The classic analogy is a bathroom scale that is set 5 kilograms too high: it will give the same reading every time you step on it (highly reliable) but always the wrong weight (invalid). Conversely, a test cannot be valid without being reliable, because if a test's scores fluctuate randomly it cannot be consistently measuring the intended construct. So validity requires reliability, but reliability does not guarantee validity. This makes statement b true, while the others reverse or confuse the relationship." },
  { id: "rml24_088", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Which lobe of the cerebral cortex has intellectual functioning as its main domain?",
    options: { a: "Occipital lobe", b: "Frontal lobe", c: "Parietal lobe", d: "Temporal lobe" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "The occipital lobe is dedicated to visual processing, not intellectual functioning.", c: "The parietal lobe handles somatosensory processing and spatial awareness, not higher intellectual or executive functions.", d: "The temporal lobe processes hearing, language comprehension, and memory, not the executive intellectual functions of the frontal lobe." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is the frontal lobe. The frontal lobe, the largest lobe of the cerebral cortex situated at the front of each hemisphere, is the seat of the brain's highest intellectual and executive functions. It contains the prefrontal cortex, which governs executive functions such as reasoning, planning, problem-solving, decision-making, abstract thought, judgement, working memory, attention, impulse control, and the regulation of social behaviour and personality. Because these higher-order cognitive abilities define what we think of as intelligent, goal-directed behaviour, the frontal lobe is described as the lobe whose main domain is intellectual functioning. The frontal lobe also houses the primary motor cortex (controlling voluntary movement) and Broca's area (controlling speech production, usually in the left hemisphere). The crucial role of the frontal lobe in personality and judgement was dramatically illustrated by the famous case of Phineas Gage, a railway worker whose prefrontal cortex was destroyed by an iron rod; he survived but underwent a profound change in personality and social conduct, becoming impulsive and unreliable. Damage to the frontal lobe characteristically impairs planning, judgement, and self-control while sparing basic sensory and motor abilities. Because intellectual and executive functioning is the frontal lobe's principal domain, it is the correct answer." },
  { id: "rml24_089", section: "assessment",
    q: "Which of the following is NOT a criterion based validity?",
    options: { a: "Concurrent validity", b: "Face validity", c: "Predictive validity", d: "Factorial validity" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Concurrent validity correlates the test with a criterion measured at the same time, so it is a genuine criterion-based validity (not the exception).", b: "Face validity is the superficial appearance of measuring the construct, judged by impression, and is genuinely NOT criterion-based; this is the textbook-correct exception.", d: "Factorial validity (a form of construct validity established by factor analysis) is not criterion-based either, so it is also debatable, but it is not the keyed answer." },
    explanation: "The keyed answer is predictive validity as the one that is NOT a criterion-based validity, though this requires careful reading. Validity comes in several broad types: content validity (whether the test items adequately sample the domain), construct validity (whether the test measures the theoretical construct it claims to), and criterion-related validity (whether test scores relate to an external criterion or outcome). Criterion-related validity itself has two forms: concurrent validity, where the test correlates with a criterion measured at the same time, and predictive validity, where the test predicts a criterion measured in the future (for example, an aptitude test predicting later job performance). Both concurrent and predictive validity are therefore genuinely criterion-based. Among the options, face validity stands out as clearly NOT a criterion-related validity: face validity is merely whether a test superficially appears, on its face, to measure what it claims, judged by lay impression rather than by correlation with any criterion; it is the weakest, most superficial form of validity. The standard textbook answer to this kind of question is face validity, since it is not based on any criterion. The source, however, keys predictive validity. Given that face validity is unambiguously non-criterion-based while predictive validity is by definition criterion-based, Sihan should learn the textbook-correct point: face validity is not criterion validity, even though this source marks predictive." },
  { id: "rml24_090", section: "general",
    q: "Consider the following principles with regard to the technique of shaping: i. Speed ii. Power iii. Non-verbal iv. Performance On which of the above principles is the technique of shaping based?",
    options: { a: "1 and 3", b: "1, 2 and 3", c: "1, 2 and 4", d: "1 and 2" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Option a (1 and 3 only) omits power, which the source includes among the principles of shaping.", c: "Option c (1, 2 and 4) wrongly includes performance and omits the non-verbal principle.", d: "Option d (1 and 2 only) omits the non-verbal principle, which is central to shaping since it works without verbal instruction." },
    explanation: "The keyed answer is option b, principles 1, 2, and 3 (speed, power, and non-verbal). Shaping is an operant-conditioning technique for teaching a new, complex behaviour that the organism would not perform spontaneously, by reinforcing successive approximations, that is, by rewarding behaviours that come closer and closer to the desired target behaviour in a series of small steps. Skinner pioneered shaping, famously using it to train pigeons and rats to perform elaborate sequences by rewarding each small move toward the goal. The principles listed in this question (speed, power, and non-verbal) describe characteristics of how shaping is applied and the kinds of behaviour it builds. Shaping works on the speed and power (intensity or force) dimensions of behaviour, gradually adjusting these through differential reinforcement, and it is fundamentally a non-verbal procedure: it does not rely on verbal instruction but on the direct reinforcement of behaviour, which is why it can be used with animals, infants, and individuals who cannot follow verbal directions. The fourth listed principle, performance, is not one of the defining bases the source counts. The essential concept for Sihan to master is shaping as the reinforcement of successive approximations to build a new behaviour, used widely in behaviour therapy, animal training, and skill acquisition." },
  { id: "rml24_091", section: "general",
    q: "What is the approximate visual field covered by each human eye?",
    options: { a: "130 degrees", b: "160 degrees", c: "180 degrees", d: "220 degrees" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "130 degrees corresponds more closely to the vertical field, not the broader horizontal visual field the source keys at 180.", b: "160 degrees understates the horizontal extent the source keys; the value chosen here is 180.", d: "220 degrees overstates the field; the human horizontal field is generally given as about 180 to 200 degrees, and the source keys 180." },
    explanation: "The keyed answer is 180 degrees. The visual field is the total area that can be seen by the eyes when they are fixed in one position. The standard textbook figures describe the human visual field, and this question keys the value at about 180 degrees, the figure often cited for the approximate horizontal extent of vision. In more detailed terms, the binocular visual field (the combined field of both eyes together) spans roughly 180 to 200 degrees horizontally, with a central region of about 120 degrees where the fields of the two eyes overlap, providing binocular vision and stereoscopic depth perception. A single eye's monocular field is somewhat less, extending farther toward the temporal (outer) side than the nasal (inner) side because the nose blocks part of the field. Vertically the field is smaller, around 130 to 135 degrees, limited above by the brow and below by the cheek. The point for Sihan to retain is the general layout: a wide horizontal field of vision (roughly 180 degrees overall) with a central binocular overlap zone that supports depth perception, and a narrower vertical field. The source keys 180 degrees as the approximate visual field figure." },
  { id: "rml24_092", section: "therapy",
    q: "Which of the following defense mechanism is seen in patients with borderline personality disorder?",
    options: { a: "Splitting", b: "Regression", c: "Undoing", d: "Sublimation" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Regression (reverting to earlier developmental behaviour under stress) can occur in many conditions but is not the defining defence of borderline personality disorder.", c: "Undoing (performing acts to magically cancel an unacceptable impulse) is characteristic of obsessive-compulsive disorder, not BPD.", d: "Sublimation (channelling impulses into socially valued activity) is a mature, healthy defence, not the primitive splitting seen in BPD." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is splitting. Splitting is the central, characteristic defence mechanism of borderline personality disorder (BPD). Splitting is the inability to integrate the positive and negative qualities of oneself or others into a coherent, balanced whole, so that people (including the self) are experienced as either all good or all bad, with no middle ground. A person using splitting may idealise someone one moment (seeing them as perfect, wonderful, the only one who understands) and then, after a minor disappointment, abruptly devalue the same person (seeing them as cruel, worthless, an enemy). This produces the intense, unstable, and stormy interpersonal relationships that are a core feature of BPD, along with an unstable sense of self, emotional dysregulation, fear of abandonment, and impulsivity. Splitting is rooted in Melanie Klein's object-relations theory and Otto Kernberg's account of borderline personality organisation, where the failure to integrate good and bad object-representations is seen as the underlying structural problem. The all-good versus all-bad oscillation directly explains the dramatic swings in how borderline patients view their relationships and themselves. Because splitting is the signature defence of BPD, it is the correct answer, and it is a frequent clinical and exam association." },
  { id: "rml24_093", section: "general",
    q: "‘Stroop effect’ is an account of:",
    options: { a: "Stimulus dominance", b: "Semantic dominance", c: "Instructional dominance", d: "Conceptual dominance" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Semantic dominance specifically highlights word meaning, but the source frames the effect more broadly as stimulus dominance, the dominance of one stimulus dimension over another.", c: "Instructional dominance is not the recognised account of the Stroop effect; the interference arises from automatic reading, not from instructions.", d: "Conceptual dominance is not the standard description of the Stroop effect either; it is a distractor." },
    explanation: "The keyed answer is stimulus dominance. The Stroop effect, first demonstrated by John Ridley Stroop in 1935, is one of the most famous phenomena in cognitive psychology and the study of attention. In the classic task, participants are shown colour words printed in coloured ink and asked to name the ink colour while ignoring the word. When the word and the ink colour conflict (for example, the word RED printed in blue ink), people are markedly slower and more error-prone than when they match. This interference, the difficulty of naming the ink colour when it conflicts with the printed word, is the Stroop effect. The standard explanation is that reading is a highly practised, automatic process that is hard to suppress, so the meaning of the word is processed involuntarily and competes with the more effortful task of naming the ink colour. In this conflict between two stimulus dimensions (word meaning versus ink colour), one dimension dominates and intrudes on processing of the other; the source labels this stimulus dominance. The Stroop effect is widely used to study selective attention, automaticity, processing speed, and cognitive control or inhibition, and Stroop interference tasks remain a standard neuropsychological measure of executive function and attentional control." },
  { id: "rml24_094", section: "general",
    q: "The tendency to think of the use of objects only in terms of their habitual use is known as:",
    options: { a: "Mental barrier", b: "Functional fixedness", c: "Einstellung", d: "Trial and error" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Mental barrier is a vague general phrase, not the specific named concept for fixating on an object's usual function.", c: "Einstellung (set effect) is the rigid persistence in using a familiar problem-solving method, not the fixation on an object's habitual use.", d: "Trial and error is a problem-solving approach of trying many solutions until one works, not a barrier based on habitual object use." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is functional fixedness. Functional fixedness is a cognitive bias and a barrier to problem-solving in which a person is unable to see that an object could be used in a way other than its usual, customary function. Because we habitually associate objects with their typical use, we become mentally fixed on that one function and fail to recognise novel uses that would solve a problem. The classic demonstration is Karl Duncker's candle problem, in which participants are given a candle, a box of tacks, and matches, and must fix the candle to a wall so it can burn without dripping wax on the floor. Many struggle because they see the box only as a container for the tacks (its usual function) and fail to realise it could be emptied and tacked to the wall as a platform to hold the candle. Functional fixedness illustrates how prior experience and habitual associations can constrain creative thinking. It is related to, but distinct from, set effect (Einstellung), which is the tendency to keep using a previously successful problem-solving strategy even when a simpler one is available. The question specifically describes thinking of objects only in terms of their habitual use, which is the exact definition of functional fixedness." },
  { id: "rml24_095", section: "biopsych",
    q: "The ‘satiety centre’ of the brain is located in the:",
    options: { a: "Lateral hypothalamus", b: "Ventromedial hippocampus", c: "Lateral thalamus", d: "Ventromedial hypothalamus" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "The lateral hypothalamus is the hunger or feeding centre that initiates eating, the opposite of the satiety centre.", b: "The ventromedial hippocampus is not the appetite centre; the hippocampus governs memory, and the satiety centre is in the hypothalamus, not the hippocampus.", c: "The lateral thalamus is a sensory relay structure, not involved in regulating satiety or hunger." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is the ventromedial hypothalamus. The hypothalamus is the key brain region regulating hunger and eating, and two of its nuclei play opposing roles that form a classic dual-centre model. The ventromedial hypothalamus (VMH) functions as the satiety centre: when stimulated, it produces a feeling of fullness and stops eating, and crucially, when the VMH is damaged or lesioned, an animal overeats dramatically and becomes obese, a syndrome called hyperphagia, because the off switch for eating is destroyed. In contrast, the lateral hypothalamus (LH) functions as the hunger or feeding centre: stimulating it triggers eating, while lesioning it causes the animal to stop eating and lose weight (aphagia), because the on switch for hunger is destroyed. So the simple mnemonic is: ventromedial equals satiety (stop eating), lateral equals hunger (start eating). These findings come from classic lesion studies and underpin the understanding of appetite regulation, which is now known to involve hormones such as leptin (signalling satiety from fat stores) and ghrelin (signalling hunger from the stomach) acting on these hypothalamic centres. The question asks for the satiety centre, which is the ventromedial hypothalamus, so option d is correct." },
  { id: "rml24_096", section: "abnormal",
    q: "A patient with anorexia nervosa has traits of:",
    options: { a: "Perfectionism", b: "Impulsivity", c: "Avoidance", d: "Dependence" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Impulsivity is more characteristic of bulimia nervosa (especially binge-purge behaviour), not the over-controlled restrictive profile of anorexia.", c: "Avoidance is a broad trait not specifically distinctive of anorexia; perfectionism is the hallmark associated trait.", d: "Dependence is not the characteristic anorexic trait; the disorder is more linked to perfectionism, control, and rigidity." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is perfectionism. Anorexia nervosa is an eating disorder characterised by restriction of food intake leading to significantly low body weight, an intense fear of gaining weight, and a disturbed body image in which the person sees themselves as fat despite being underweight. Among the personality traits strongly and consistently associated with anorexia nervosa, perfectionism stands out. Individuals with anorexia frequently exhibit high levels of perfectionism, setting rigid, exacting standards for themselves and being intensely self-critical when they fall short. This perfectionistic drive extends to the control of eating, weight, and body shape, which become arenas where the person pursues an unattainable ideal and exercises a sense of mastery and control. Related traits commonly seen include rigidity, obsessive and compulsive tendencies, harm avoidance, high conscientiousness, and a strong need for order and control, alongside low self-esteem. These traits often predate the eating disorder and are thought to contribute to its development and maintenance, distinguishing the typically over-controlled, restrictive anorexic profile from the more impulsive profile often associated with bulimia nervosa (especially the binge-purge type). Because perfectionism is the classic, well-documented personality trait of anorexia nervosa, it is the correct answer, and recognising it is clinically useful in both understanding and treating the disorder." },
  { id: "rml24_097", section: "biostats",
    q: "‘Census’ is the example of which method?",
    options: { a: "Ex post facto", b: "Field study", c: "Survey", d: "Sociometry" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Ex post facto research studies the effects of variables that have already occurred and cannot be manipulated; a census is a descriptive enumeration, not this retrospective causal design.", b: "A field study involves observing behaviour in its natural setting, not the systematic population-wide data collection of a census.", d: "Sociometry is a technique for measuring social relationships and group structure (such as who likes or interacts with whom), not a population census." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is survey. A census is the classic example of the survey method, because it involves systematically collecting information from an entire population (or a large defined group) on specified characteristics, typically through questionnaires or structured interviews. A national census, for instance, gathers data on every household regarding age, sex, occupation, education, and other variables, in order to describe the population. The survey method, in research terminology, is a non-experimental, descriptive approach used to gather information about the characteristics, opinions, attitudes, or behaviours of a population, usually by questioning respondents directly. Surveys can sample a subset of the population or, as in a census, cover the entire population (a complete enumeration). The defining feature is the systematic collection of self-reported or recorded information across many cases to describe and summarise a population. This distinguishes it from the other listed methods: surveys do not manipulate variables (unlike experiments) and aim at description and the discovery of associations across large groups. Because a census is precisely a population-wide data-gathering exercise of this descriptive, questioning kind, it is the prototypical survey method, making survey the correct answer." },
  { id: "rml24_098", section: "assessment",
    q: "Which of the following is the most important characteristic of a psychological test?",
    options: { a: "Objectivity", b: "Subjectivity", c: "Validity", d: "Rationality" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Subjectivity (dependence on personal judgement) is the opposite of a desirable test characteristic and would undermine, not strengthen, a test.", c: "Validity (measuring what it claims to measure) is arguably the most important property in many standard texts, but this source keys objectivity; know both views.", d: "Rationality is not a recognised cardinal characteristic of psychological tests; the core properties are reliability, validity, objectivity, and norms." },
    explanation: "The keyed answer is objectivity. A good psychological test must possess several essential characteristics, and this question keys objectivity as the most important. Objectivity means that the test is free from the subjective bias and personal judgement of the examiner, both in its administration and in its scoring; it ensures that the score a person receives depends only on their actual performance and not on who happens to administer or score the test. An objective test has clearly worded items with a definite right or predetermined response, and a standardised scoring key, so that different scorers will arrive at exactly the same score for the same set of responses. This is sometimes regarded as foundational because, without objectivity, scores would vary from examiner to examiner, undermining the consistency and meaningfulness of the measurement. Note that many texts argue that validity is the single most important characteristic of a test, because a test that does not measure what it claims to measure is useless regardless of its other properties. The textbook-standard answer to the most important characteristic is often validity. However, this source keys objectivity, treating freedom from examiner bias as the prime requirement. Sihan should know both the keyed answer (objectivity) and the strong textbook case for validity, since either may appear correct depending on the source." },
  { id: "rml24_099", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Neurotransmitters are released at the:",
    options: { a: "Axon hillock", b: "Axon terminals", c: "Myelin sheath", d: "Telodendria" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "The axon hillock is where the action potential is initiated (the trigger zone), not where neurotransmitters are released.", c: "The myelin sheath is the fatty insulation around the axon that speeds conduction; it does not release neurotransmitters.", d: "Telodendria are the fine branching ends of the axon that lead to the terminals, but the neurotransmitter is actually released from the terminal buttons at their tips." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is the axon terminals. Neurotransmitters are the chemical messengers that carry signals across the synapse from one neuron to the next, and they are released from the axon terminals (also called terminal buttons, end bulbs, or synaptic knobs) at the very end of the axon. The process works as follows: an electrical impulse (the action potential) travels down the axon until it reaches the axon terminal. There it triggers the opening of voltage-gated calcium channels, and the influx of calcium ions causes synaptic vesicles, tiny sacs filled with neurotransmitter molecules, to fuse with the presynaptic membrane and release their contents into the synaptic cleft, the small gap between the two neurons, by exocytosis. The released neurotransmitter then diffuses across the cleft and binds to specific receptor sites on the postsynaptic neuron's membrane, producing either an excitatory or inhibitory effect on that next neuron. Afterward the neurotransmitter is cleared from the cleft by reuptake, enzymatic breakdown, or diffusion. Because release occurs specifically at the end of the axon where the synaptic vesicles are concentrated, the axon terminals are the correct site. This is a fundamental fact of neurotransmission and the basis for understanding how many psychiatric drugs act on synaptic transmission." },
  { id: "rml24_100", section: "social",
    q: "Solomon Asch’s classic experiment was arranged to test the limits of:",
    options: { a: "Social perception", b: "Conformity", c: "Coercive power", d: "Leadership" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Social perception concerns how we form impressions and make inferences about other people, not the yielding to group pressure that Asch studied.", c: "Coercive power is influence based on the ability to punish; Asch's groups applied no punishment, only the implicit pressure of unanimous opinion.", d: "Leadership concerns guiding and influencing a group toward goals, not the individual conforming to a group's judgement, which is what Asch tested." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is conformity. Solomon Asch's classic experiments of the 1950s were designed to test the limits of conformity, specifically how far social pressure from a group could lead an individual to give an answer they knew to be wrong. In the basic procedure, a participant was seated in a group of confederates (people secretly working with the experimenter) and shown a series of simple perceptual judgements: a standard line and three comparison lines, where the task was merely to say which comparison line matched the standard in length. The correct answer was obvious. However, on certain trials the confederates, answering first, all unanimously gave the same wrong answer. Asch found that about a third of the participants' responses (across critical trials) conformed to the group's incorrect judgement, and about 75 percent of participants conformed at least once, even though the correct answer was plainly visible. This demonstrated the power of normative social influence, the desire to fit in and avoid the discomfort of being the odd one out, even when the group is clearly wrong. Asch also showed that conformity dropped sharply when there was just one dissenting ally, when responses were private, or when the group was smaller. The study is a cornerstone of social psychology on conformity." },
],
  "rml-2025": [
  { id: "rml25_001", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Which of the following best describes the concept of 'reciprocal determinism' as proposed by Albert Bandura?",
    options: { a: "Individual behavior is solely controlled by environmental factors.", b: "Cognitive processes influence both behavior and the environment in a bidirectional manner.", c: "Personality develops strictly from genetic inheritance and biological predispositions.", d: "Social reinforcement has no impact on self-concept formation." },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "This is pure environmental determinism (classic Watsonian behaviorism), the opposite of Bandura's interactionist stance.", c: "This is biological or genetic determinism, which ignores the environmental and cognitive arms of Bandura's triad.", d: "Bandura placed self-concept and self-efficacy at the center of his theory, so denying reinforcement's effect on it contradicts him." },
    explanation: "Reciprocal determinism is the central mechanism of the social cognitive theory of Albert Bandura. It holds that human functioning is the product of a continuous, three-way interaction among personal factors (cognition, beliefs, expectations), behavior, and the environment. None of the three is primary: each influences and is influenced by the other two in a bidirectional loop, which Bandura called triadic reciprocal causation. For example, a student's belief that she can succeed (personal) shapes how hard she studies (behavior), which changes the feedback she gets from teachers (environment), which in turn feeds back into her beliefs. This was a deliberate break from the radical behaviorism of B. F. Skinner, which treated the environment as the sole determinant of behavior, and from purely trait or biological views. Bandura's Bobo doll studies grounded this in observational learning, and his later work on self-efficacy showed how personal cognition steers the whole system. Option b captures the bidirectional, cognition-inclusive character of the model, which is why it is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_002", section: "cognitive",
    q: "In cognitive psychology, the 'Stroop effect' primarily demonstrates which cognitive phenomenon?",
    options: { a: "The interference that occurs in reaction time due to conflicting information processing.", b: "Improved recall when semantically related information is presented.", c: "The increased accuracy of perception under divided attention tasks.", d: "The predictive power of schema theory on episodic memory." },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "This describes semantic priming or the encoding-specificity idea, not the Stroop task, which measures interference, not facilitation of recall.", c: "The Stroop effect actually shows a decrease in speed under conflict, and it is not fundamentally about perceptual accuracy in divided attention.", d: "Schema theory and episodic memory are unrelated to the automatic word-reading interference that Stroop measured." },
    explanation: "The Stroop effect, first reported by John Ridley Stroop in 1935, is a classic demonstration of response interference and the automaticity of reading. Participants are shown color words printed in incongruent ink (for example, the word RED printed in blue ink) and asked to name the ink color, not read the word. Reaction times slow markedly and errors rise compared with naming the color of a neutral patch. The dominant explanation is that skilled reading is automatic: the word meaning is processed involuntarily and competes with the slower, controlled task of ink-color naming, producing a processing conflict that must be resolved. This taps selective attention and executive control, and the task is widely used to index the ability to inhibit a prepotent response. Modern accounts tie Stroop performance to the anterior cingulate cortex and prefrontal conflict-monitoring systems. Option a is correct because the defining feature of the effect is the interference (increased reaction time) caused by conflicting streams of information, one automatic and one controlled." },
  { id: "rml25_003", section: "psychopathology",
    q: "According to Freud's structural model, which component of personality operates based on the reality principle?",
    options: { a: "Id", b: "Ego", c: "Superego", d: "Conscious mind" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "The id runs on the pleasure principle, demanding immediate gratification, not the reality principle.", c: "The superego operates on a moral or idealistic principle (conscience and ego-ideal), not the reality principle.", d: "The conscious mind is a level of awareness in the topographic model, not one of the three structural agencies." },
    explanation: "In the structural (tripartite) model of the mind proposed by Sigmund Freud, personality is divided into three agencies: the id, the ego, and the superego. The id is the primitive reservoir of instinctual drives and operates on the pleasure principle, seeking immediate discharge of tension regardless of consequences. The superego embodies internalized parental and societal standards, dividing into the conscience (which punishes with guilt) and the ego-ideal (which rewards with pride); it functions on a moral or idealistic principle. The ego is the executive of the personality and operates on the reality principle: it mediates between the demands of the id, the prohibitions of the superego, and the constraints of the external world, delaying gratification until a safe and appropriate object or moment is found. The ego relies on secondary-process thinking, which is logical and reality-oriented, in contrast to the id's irrational primary-process thinking. Because the reality principle is precisely the ego's mode of operation, option b is correct. Note that option d, the conscious mind, belongs to Freud's earlier topographic model, not the structural one." },
  { id: "rml25_004", section: "stats",
    q: "In experimental psychology, which of the following scenarios best controls for expectancy effects?",
    options: { a: "Using a single-blind procedure.", b: "Employing random assignment without blinding.", c: "Providing participants with pretest instructions to reduce bias.", d: "Implementing a double-blind design where neither subjects nor experimenters know the conditions." },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "A single-blind design hides condition from participants only, so experimenter expectancy effects can still leak through.", b: "Random assignment controls for confounding between groups but does nothing to hide condition, so expectancy effects remain.", c: "Instructions cannot eliminate unconscious expectancy on either side and may even introduce demand characteristics." },
    explanation: "Expectancy effects are systematic biases introduced when the beliefs or hopes of participants or experimenters influence outcomes. On the participant side these are demand characteristics and placebo responses; on the experimenter side they are the Rosenthal effect, named for Robert Rosenthal, who showed that experimenters can unconsciously cue subjects toward expected results. A single-blind procedure conceals the condition from participants only, leaving the experimenter free to bias measurement, coding, or interaction. A double-blind design conceals condition assignment from both the participants and the experimenters (or the personnel interacting with and assessing them), so neither party's expectations can shape behavior or scoring. This is the gold standard in clinical drug trials, where an active drug and an inert placebo are administered by staff who do not know which is which. Random assignment (option b) is essential for internal validity and controlling confounds, but it addresses group equivalence, not expectancy. Because only the double-blind arrangement neutralizes expectancy from both directions, option d is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_005", section: "development",
    q: "Vygotsky's concept of the 'zone of proximal development' (ZPD) refers to:",
    options: { a: "The difference between what a learner can do independently and what they can achieve with guidance.", b: "A measure of a person's fixed learning capacity across cognitive domains.", c: "A developmental stage characterised by logical reasoning capability.", d: "The optimal period during which specific cognitive skills must develop." },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "The ZPD is dynamic and social, not a fixed innate capacity like an IQ ceiling.", c: "This describes a Piagetian stage, not Vygotsky's ZPD, which is not a stage at all.", d: "This describes a critical or sensitive period, a maturational concept unrelated to Vygotsky's social gap." },
    explanation: "The zone of proximal development is a cornerstone of the sociocultural theory of Lev Vygotsky. He defined it as the distance between a child's actual developmental level, measured by what the child can accomplish alone, and the level of potential development, measured by what the child can accomplish with guidance from a more knowledgeable other, such as a teacher, parent, or a capable peer. The ZPD captures skills that are in the process of maturing: they are not yet independent but are within reach with support. This support is often called scaffolding, a term coined by Jerome Bruner, David Wood, and Gail Ross to describe the temporary, adjustable help that is gradually withdrawn as competence grows. The concept reframes learning as fundamentally social and dialogical, driving development rather than merely following it, which directly opposes the more solitary, discovery-based view of Jean Piaget. Because option a states precisely the gap between independent and assisted performance, it is the correct answer." },
  { id: "rml25_006", section: "assessment",
    q: "In the context of psychometrics, which of the following best represents 'construct validity'?",
    options: { a: "The degree to which a test measures what it claims to measure.", b: "The consistency of test scores across repeated administrations.", c: "The extent to which test content represents the entire domain.", d: "The agreement between different raters assessing the same performance." },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "This describes reliability (specifically test-retest), not validity.", c: "This describes content validity, a narrower facet, not construct validity itself.", d: "This describes inter-rater reliability, a form of reliability, not construct validity." },
    explanation: "Construct validity is the extent to which a test actually measures the theoretical construct, the unobservable trait such as intelligence, anxiety, or extraversion, that it is intended to measure. The term and framework were formalized by Lee Cronbach and Paul Meehl in 1955, who argued that a construct is embedded in a nomological network of expected relationships, and evidence for construct validity accumulates as scores behave as theory predicts. It has two complementary strands: convergent validity (the measure correlates with other measures of the same construct) and discriminant validity (it does not correlate with measures of unrelated constructs), a distinction sharpened by Donald Campbell and Donald Fiske through the multitrait-multimethod matrix. Modern testing standards treat construct validity as the overarching, unifying concept under which content and criterion evidence fall. Option a, capturing whether the test measures what it claims to measure, is the standard textbook definition. The distractors describe reliability (b and d) and content validity (c), which are related but distinct psychometric properties." },
  { id: "rml25_007", section: "assessment",
    q: "The 'Big Five' model of personality does NOT include which of the following traits?",
    options: { a: "Openness to experience", b: "Neuroticism", c: "Intuition", d: "Agreeableness" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Openness to experience is one of the five factors, so it is not the exception.", b: "Neuroticism is a core Big Five factor, so it cannot be the answer.", d: "Agreeableness is one of the five, so it is not excluded." },
    explanation: "The Big Five, also called the Five Factor Model, is the most widely accepted trait taxonomy of personality, developed through lexical and factor-analytic work by researchers including Lewis Goldberg, and refined into the NEO inventories by Paul Costa and Robert McCrae. The five broad dimensions are captured by the acronyms OCEAN or CANOE: Openness to experience, Conscientiousness, Extraversion, Agreeableness, and Neuroticism (its opposite pole being emotional stability). Each factor is a continuum along which people vary, and together they subsume most of the reliable variance in personality description. Intuition is not a Big Five trait; it belongs instead to the Myers-Briggs Type Indicator, built on the typology of Carl Jung, where Sensing versus Intuition is one of four dichotomies. The MBTI is a type-based instrument with weaker psychometric standing than the dimensional Big Five. Because options a, b, and d (Openness, Neuroticism, Agreeableness) are all genuine Big Five factors while Intuition is not, option c is the correct exception." },
  { id: "rml25_008", section: "stats",
    q: "Which of the following best reflects a limitation of correlational research designs?",
    options: { a: "They can establish causality between variables.", b: "They cannot determine the direction of influence between variables.", c: "They always require a random assignment of subjects.", d: "They are seldom used in psychological research." },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "This is stated as a strength and it is false: correlation cannot establish causality, so it is not a limitation as worded.", c: "Random assignment belongs to experiments, not correlational designs, so this statement is simply wrong.", d: "Correlational designs are extremely common in psychology, so this is factually incorrect." },
    explanation: "Correlational research measures the degree of relationship between two or more variables as they naturally occur, without manipulating them. Its central limitation is the maxim that correlation does not imply causation. Two problems drive this. First is the directionality problem: if variables A and B are related, the design cannot tell whether A causes B, B causes A, or the influence is mutual. Second is the third-variable problem: an unmeasured confound may cause both A and B, producing a spurious association. For instance, a correlation between ice-cream sales and drowning is driven by a third variable, hot weather, not by either causing the other. Only a true experiment, with manipulation of an independent variable and random assignment to conditions, can support causal claims. Option a is false because correlational designs cannot establish causality; option c is false because random assignment is a feature of experiments; option d is false because correlational methods are ubiquitous. Option b correctly names the inability to determine the direction of influence, which is a genuine and defining limitation." },
  { id: "rml25_009", section: "biopsych",
    q: "In operant conditioning, a fixed-ratio schedule of reinforcement produces:",
    options: { a: "High, steady response rates with brief pauses after reinforcement.", b: "Low, irregular response rates.", c: "Rapid extinction once reinforcement stops.", d: "Variable response rates depending on reinforcement delay." },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Low, irregular rates are more typical of fixed-interval schedules early in the interval, not fixed-ratio schedules.", c: "Rapid extinction is characteristic of continuous reinforcement, not intermittent ratio schedules, which are more extinction-resistant.", d: "Variable, delay-dependent responding does not describe the crisp, count-based fixed-ratio pattern." },
    explanation: "Schedules of reinforcement, systematized by B. F. Skinner, describe the rule linking responses to reinforcers and each produces a characteristic response pattern. In a fixed-ratio (FR) schedule, reinforcement follows a set number of responses, for example every fifth lever press (FR-5). This generates a high and steady rate of responding, because more responses mean faster reinforcement, followed by a brief post-reinforcement pause immediately after each reinforcer is delivered, producing the classic break-and-run cumulative record. By contrast, fixed-interval schedules yield a scalloped pattern with slow responding after reinforcement that accelerates toward the end of the interval. Variable schedules (variable-ratio and variable-interval) produce very steady responding without post-reinforcement pauses, and variable-ratio schedules, such as those in slot machines, are the most resistant to extinction. Continuous reinforcement extinguishes fastest. Option a correctly pairs the fixed-ratio schedule with high, steady rates plus the characteristic short pause after each reinforcement, which is the textbook signature of this schedule." },
  { id: "rml25_010", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Which neuroscientific technique provides the highest temporal resolution for studying neural activity?",
    options: { a: "fMRI", b: "PET scan", c: "EEG", d: "CT scan" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "fMRI has excellent spatial resolution but poor temporal resolution (seconds), because it tracks the sluggish blood-oxygen response.", b: "PET is even slower than fMRI, tracking metabolic tracer uptake over minutes.", d: "CT is a static structural imaging method with essentially no temporal resolution for activity at all." },
    explanation: "Temporal resolution refers to how precisely in time a technique can track changes in neural activity, whereas spatial resolution refers to how precisely it localizes them in space. Electroencephalography (EEG), pioneered for humans by Hans Berger, records the summed electrical potentials of cortical neurons directly from scalp electrodes. Because it measures electrical activity in real time, its temporal resolution is on the order of milliseconds, the best among the options listed, which is why it and the related magnetoencephalography are preferred for studying the rapid dynamics of perception and the timing of event-related potentials. Its weakness is poor spatial resolution and the inverse problem of localizing sources. Functional MRI (fMRI) and PET rely on hemodynamic or metabolic signals (blood oxygenation for fMRI, tracer uptake for PET) that lag neural events by seconds to minutes, giving them fine spatial but coarse temporal resolution. CT is purely structural. Because the question asks specifically about the highest temporal resolution, EEG (option c) is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_011", section: "general",
    q: "Which psychological approach emphasizes learning through stimulus-response associations, mainly via conditioning?",
    options: { a: "Cognitive approach", b: "Stimulus-Response Behaviouristic approach", c: "Psychoanalytic approach", d: "Existential approach" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "The cognitive approach emphasizes internal mental processes such as attention and memory, explicitly rejecting a pure stimulus-response focus.", c: "The psychoanalytic approach centers on unconscious drives and childhood conflict, not conditioning.", d: "The existential approach concerns meaning, freedom, and choice, which is unrelated to stimulus-response learning." },
    explanation: "Behaviorism is the school of psychology that restricts explanation to observable stimuli and responses, deliberately excluding introspection and unobservable mental events. Its stimulus-response (S-R) framework holds that behavior is acquired through conditioning. Classical conditioning, discovered by Ivan Pavlov, pairs a neutral stimulus with a reflex-eliciting one until the neutral stimulus alone evokes the response. John B. Watson, who wrote the behaviorist manifesto in 1913, applied this to human emotion in the Little Albert study. Operant conditioning, formulated by B. F. Skinner building on the law of effect of Edward Thorndike, explains how consequences (reinforcement and punishment) shape voluntary behavior. All of these treat learning as the formation of associations between stimuli and responses. The cognitive approach (option a) arose partly in reaction to behaviorism, insisting that internal representations mediate behavior; the psychoanalytic (c) and existential (d) approaches address unconscious conflict and meaning respectively. Because option b names the stimulus-response behaviorist approach explicitly, it is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_012", section: "general",
    q: "Which branch of psychology focuses on the study of animal behavior?",
    options: { a: "Social psychology", b: "Abnormal psychology", c: "Comparative psychology", d: "Differential psychology" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Social psychology studies how people influence one another, focusing on humans in groups, not animal behavior.", b: "Abnormal psychology studies mental disorders and maladaptive behavior in humans.", d: "Differential psychology studies individual differences (traits, abilities) among people, not animals." },
    explanation: "Comparative psychology is the branch that studies the behavior of non-human animals, often to draw comparisons across species and to illuminate the evolution and mechanisms of behavior. It overlaps with ethology, the biological study of animal behavior in natural settings associated with Nikolaas Tinbergen and Konrad Lorenz, but comparative psychology has historically emphasized controlled laboratory study and learning. Much foundational learning research, from the puzzle-box work of Edward Thorndike to the conditioning studies of Ivan Pavlov and B. F. Skinner, is comparative in spirit, using animals as models for general principles of behavior. The distractors describe other branches: social psychology examines interpersonal and group influence among humans, abnormal psychology examines psychopathology, and differential psychology examines systematic individual differences in traits and abilities across people. Because only comparative psychology is defined by the study of animal behavior, option c is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_013", section: "general",
    q: "Which model asserts that inner forces largely beyond individual control drive human behavior?",
    options: { a: "Cognitive model", b: "Behavioral model", c: "Humanistic model", d: "Psychodynamic model" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "The cognitive model emphasizes conscious thoughts and information processing, not hidden inner forces.", b: "The behavioral model attributes behavior to external environmental contingencies, not internal unconscious forces.", c: "The humanistic model stresses free will, choice, and self-actualization, the very opposite of forces beyond one's control." },
    explanation: "The psychodynamic model, rooted in the work of Sigmund Freud and extended by neo-Freudians such as Carl Jung, Alfred Adler, and Erik Erikson, holds that behavior is driven largely by inner forces that are unconscious and therefore beyond direct voluntary control. These forces include instinctual drives (the libido and, later, aggression), unresolved childhood conflicts, and the dynamic tension among the id, ego, and superego. Because so much of the mind is unconscious, people are often unaware of the true causes of their actions, and symptoms are understood as compromise formations expressing hidden conflict. This deterministic, inner-driven view contrasts sharply with the humanistic model of Carl Rogers and Abraham Maslow, which emphasizes conscious free will and growth; with the behavioral model, which locates causation in the external environment; and with the cognitive model, which foregrounds conscious mental processing. Because only the psychodynamic model attributes behavior to unconscious inner forces beyond individual control, option d is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_014", section: "development",
    q: "What is the main perspective concerned with changes that occur as people mature?",
    options: { a: "Psychoanalytic perspective", b: "Biological perspective", c: "Developmental perspective", d: "Humanistic perspective" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "The psychoanalytic perspective focuses on unconscious conflict; though it includes psychosexual stages, its defining concern is not maturational change broadly.", b: "The biological perspective links behavior to physiology and genetics, not specifically to lifespan maturation.", d: "The humanistic perspective centers on self-actualization and free will rather than age-related change." },
    explanation: "The developmental perspective is the branch of psychology concerned with how people change and grow across the lifespan, from conception through old age, spanning physical, cognitive, social, and emotional domains. It asks how maturation and experience interact to produce orderly change over time, and it is built on the work of theorists such as Jean Piaget on cognitive development, Erik Erikson on psychosocial stages, Lev Vygotsky on sociocultural learning, and John Bowlby and Mary Ainsworth on attachment. A recurring theme is the interplay of nature and nurture and whether change is continuous or stage-like. The other options name perspectives defined by different central concerns: the psychoanalytic by unconscious conflict, the biological by physiological and genetic bases of behavior, and the humanistic by growth, meaning, and free will. Because the developmental perspective is precisely the one defined by the study of change as people mature, option c is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_015", section: "psychopathology",
    q: "Which personality disorder is marked by manipulation, lack of remorse, and irresponsibility?",
    options: { a: "Borderline personality disorder", b: "Antisocial personality disorder", c: "Dependent personality disorder", d: "Narcissistic personality disorder" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Borderline personality disorder centers on instability of affect, identity, and relationships plus fear of abandonment, not a callous lack of remorse.", c: "Dependent personality disorder is marked by submissiveness and a need to be cared for, the opposite of exploitative manipulation.", d: "Narcissistic personality disorder features grandiosity and need for admiration; manipulation may occur, but lack of remorse and law-breaking irresponsibility define antisocial, not narcissistic." },
    explanation: "Antisocial personality disorder (ASPD) is a Cluster B personality disorder in the DSM-5 characterized by a pervasive pattern of disregard for and violation of the rights of others beginning by age 15. Its hallmark features are deceitfulness and manipulation for personal gain, lack of remorse for harm caused, impulsivity, irritability and aggressiveness, reckless disregard for safety, and consistent irresponsibility in work and financial obligations. Diagnosis requires the person to be at least 18 and to have had evidence of conduct disorder before age 15. The construct overlaps with, but is broader than, the classic notion of psychopathy described by Hervey Cleckley in The Mask of Sanity and later operationalized by Robert Hare. The distractors are other personality disorders with different cores: borderline (emotional and relational instability, abandonment fears), dependent (clinging, submissive reliance on others), and narcissistic (grandiosity and need for admiration). Because the triad of manipulation, lack of remorse, and irresponsibility maps directly onto ASPD, option b is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_016", section: "psychopathology",
    q: "Which defense mechanism involves attributing one's own unacceptable thoughts to others?",
    options: { a: "Projection", b: "Sublimation", c: "Repression", d: "Displacement" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Sublimation channels unacceptable impulses into socially valued activity, not onto other people.", c: "Repression pushes unacceptable material out of awareness; it does not attribute it to others.", d: "Displacement redirects an impulse from a threatening target to a safer one, but it does not falsely assign the impulse to someone else." },
    explanation: "Defense mechanisms are unconscious strategies described by Sigmund Freud and systematized by his daughter Anna Freud that protect the ego from anxiety generated by unacceptable impulses or conflict. Projection is the mechanism in which a person attributes their own unacceptable thoughts, feelings, or motives to another person. For example, someone harboring hostility they cannot admit may insist that others are hostile toward them, thereby externalizing the impulse and reducing internal anxiety. Sublimation, by contrast, is considered the most mature defense: it transforms socially unacceptable impulses into constructive, valued activity, such as channeling aggression into competitive sport. Repression is the foundational defense that excludes threatening material from conscious awareness. Displacement redirects an emotional impulse away from its original, threatening target toward a substitute that is safer, such as yelling at a family member after being criticized by a boss. Because projection alone involves attributing one's own unacceptable contents to others, option a is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_017", section: "therapy",
    q: "Which of the following methods is used in Beck's cognitive therapy for depression?",
    options: { a: "Encouraging dream analysis", b: "Making objective assessments of beliefs", c: "Administering psychoactive medication", d: "Practicing free association" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Dream analysis is a psychoanalytic technique of Freud, not a cognitive therapy method.", c: "Prescribing medication is a biomedical intervention, not a cognitive-therapy technique, and clinical psychologists using CBT do not prescribe.", d: "Free association is the core method of psychoanalysis, not cognitive therapy." },
    explanation: "Cognitive therapy for depression was developed by Aaron Beck, who proposed that depression is maintained by systematically distorted thinking rather than by unconscious conflict. Central to his model is the negative cognitive triad: negative views of the self, the world, and the future, sustained by cognitive distortions such as arbitrary inference, overgeneralization, and catastrophizing, all stemming from maladaptive core beliefs and schemas. The therapy therefore trains clients to identify automatic negative thoughts, examine the evidence for and against them, and test their validity, essentially treating beliefs as hypotheses to be evaluated objectively rather than accepted as fact. This process, often called collaborative empiricism and cognitive restructuring, may be paired with behavioral experiments and homework. The distractors describe methods from other traditions: dream analysis and free association are hallmark techniques of the psychoanalysis of Sigmund Freud, and administering medication is a biological treatment. Because making objective, evidence-based assessments of one's beliefs is the defining technique of Beck's approach, option b is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_018", section: "psychopathology",
    q: "According to statistical approaches, what defines 'abnormal' behavior?",
    options: { a: "Deviation from typical behavioral patterns", b: "Experiencing intense anxiety", c: "Loss of contact with reality", d: "Withdrawal and depression" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Intense anxiety is a symptom-based or distress criterion, not the statistical definition of abnormality.", c: "Loss of contact with reality describes psychosis, a clinical feature, not the statistical rarity criterion.", d: "Withdrawal and depression name specific symptoms, not the statistical concept of deviation from the norm." },
    explanation: "Psychology recognizes several criteria for defining abnormality, often summarized as the four Ds: deviance, distress, dysfunction, and danger, plus additional models. The statistical approach specifically defines abnormal behavior as that which deviates from the statistical norm, meaning it is rare or infrequent in the population, typically falling at the extreme tails of a normal distribution (the bell curve of Carl Friedrich Gauss). Under this view, any characteristic sufficiently far from the average, measured in standard deviations, counts as abnormal. Its strength is objectivity and quantifiability; its weakness is that statistical rarity says nothing about desirability, so extremely high intelligence or exceptional happiness would be classed as abnormal, and common problems would not. This is why the statistical criterion is usually combined with others, such as deviation from social norms, personal distress, and maladaptive dysfunction. Options b, c, and d each name particular clinical features (distress, psychosis, specific symptoms) rather than the statistical concept. Because the statistical approach equates abnormality with deviation from typical patterns, option a is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_019", section: "biopsych",
    q: "The biological perspective in psychology links behavior to functions primarily of which system?",
    options: { a: "Mind", b: "Soul", c: "Body", d: "Unconscious" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "The mind is the focus of cognitive and mentalist approaches, not the physical basis emphasized by biology.", b: "The soul is a philosophical or spiritual construct, not a biological system.", d: "The unconscious is the focus of psychoanalysis, not the biological perspective." },
    explanation: "The biological perspective, also called biopsychology or physiological psychology, explains behavior and mental processes in terms of the body and its physical systems, especially the nervous system, the brain, neurotransmitters, hormones, and genetics. It rests on the assumption that every psychological event has a corresponding physical substrate: thoughts, emotions, and behavior arise from neural activity and biochemical processes. Foundational contributions include the localization work springing from the aphasia studies of Paul Broca and Carl Wernicke, the reflex and conditioning physiology of Ivan Pavlov, and Donald Hebb's principle that neurons that fire together wire together. Modern biological psychology draws on genetics, endocrinology, and neuroimaging to trace behavior to bodily mechanisms. The distractors point elsewhere: the mind is the abstract focus of cognitive approaches, the soul is a spiritual or philosophical notion, and the unconscious belongs to the psychoanalytic tradition of Sigmund Freud. Because the biological perspective is defined by grounding behavior in the functions of the body, option c is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_020", section: "general",
    q: "Which perspective in psychology emphasizes unconscious processes and early childhood experiences?",
    options: { a: "Behavioral", b: "Psychodynamic", c: "Humanistic", d: "Cognitive" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "The behavioral perspective focuses on observable, learned responses and rejects unconscious explanation.", c: "The humanistic perspective emphasizes conscious experience, free will, and growth, not the unconscious.", d: "The cognitive perspective studies conscious mental processes such as memory and reasoning, not unconscious childhood conflict." },
    explanation: "The psychodynamic perspective, founded by Sigmund Freud and developed by neo-Freudians such as Carl Jung, Alfred Adler, and Erik Erikson, holds that behavior is shaped by unconscious mental processes and by experiences during early childhood. Freud argued that much of the mind lies below awareness and that unresolved conflicts among the id, ego, and superego, together with fixations at his psychosexual stages (oral, anal, phallic, latency, genital), leave lasting marks on adult personality. Early relationships and the resolution of conflicts such as the Oedipus complex are seen as formative, and defense mechanisms unconsciously manage the anxiety these conflicts produce. This emphasis on the unconscious and on childhood origins is what most distinguishes the psychodynamic approach. The distractors define themselves by opposite commitments: behaviorism (observable learning), humanism (conscious growth and free will), and cognitivism (conscious information processing). Because the psychodynamic perspective is the one defined by unconscious processes and early childhood experience, option b is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_021", section: "social",
    q: "A person, who believes that he has control over events in his life, has what type of locus of control?",
    options: { a: "External", b: "Internal", c: "Neutral", d: "Situational" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "An external locus of control is the belief that outcomes are determined by luck, fate, or powerful others, the opposite of self-control.", c: "Neutral is not a recognized category in Rotter's locus-of-control construct.", d: "Situational is not one of the two poles of the locus-of-control dimension." },
    explanation: "Locus of control is a personality construct introduced by Julian Rotter within his social learning theory. It describes the degree to which people believe they, as opposed to external forces, control the outcomes of events in their lives. A person with an internal locus of control believes that outcomes are contingent on their own effort, ability, and decisions, so they tend to take responsibility, persist at tasks, and show greater self-efficacy and achievement motivation. A person with an external locus of control believes that outcomes are governed by luck, fate, chance, or powerful others, and may show more passivity, learned helplessness, or lower stress-coping when facing difficulties. The construct is measured by Rotter's I-E Scale and relates conceptually to attribution theory and to the self-efficacy work of Albert Bandura. Options c (neutral) and d (situational) are not genuine categories in Rotter's dichotomy. Because believing one has control over one's own life events is the defining feature of an internal locus of control, option b is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_022", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Neuroleptic drugs are primarily used to treat which disorder?",
    options: { a: "Major depressive disorder", b: "Anxiety disorder", c: "Schizophrenia", d: "Bipolar disorder" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Major depression is treated primarily with antidepressants (SSRIs, SNRIs, tricyclics), not neuroleptics.", b: "Anxiety disorders are treated mainly with anxiolytics such as benzodiazepines and SSRIs, not neuroleptics.", d: "Bipolar disorder is managed chiefly with mood stabilizers such as lithium, though antipsychotics can be adjuncts; the primary indication for neuroleptics is psychosis." },
    explanation: "Neuroleptic drugs, better known today as antipsychotics, are medications developed primarily to treat psychotic disorders, most centrally schizophrenia. The first agent, chlorpromazine, was introduced in the 1950s by Jean Delay and Pierre Deniker and revolutionized psychiatric care. Typical (first-generation) antipsychotics such as haloperidol act mainly by blocking dopamine D2 receptors, consistent with the dopamine hypothesis of schizophrenia, which links positive symptoms such as hallucinations and delusions to excess dopaminergic activity in the mesolimbic pathway. This blockade also produces the characteristic extrapyramidal (parkinsonian) side effects that gave neuroleptics their name. Atypical (second-generation) agents such as clozapine and risperidone additionally modulate serotonin receptors and better address negative symptoms with fewer motor side effects. The distractors are treated primarily by other drug classes: antidepressants for depression, anxiolytics for anxiety, and mood stabilizers such as lithium for bipolar disorder. Because the primary indication for neuroleptics is the treatment of schizophrenia and psychosis, option c is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_023", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Which brain imaging technique is best for viewing brain structure after a head injury?",
    options: { a: "EEG", b: "PET scan", c: "MRI", d: "fMRI" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "EEG records electrical activity, not structure, so it cannot image anatomical damage.", b: "PET images metabolic function and requires a radioactive tracer; it is not a structural imaging method.", d: "fMRI images function (blood oxygenation during activity), not static structure, though its underlying MRI can; the question asks about structure, and plain MRI is the standard answer." },
    explanation: "Distinguishing structural from functional imaging is key here. Structural techniques reveal the physical anatomy of the brain, whereas functional techniques reveal activity. Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) uses strong magnetic fields and radiofrequency pulses to produce high-resolution, detailed images of soft tissue, making it excellent for visualizing brain structure and detecting anatomical abnormalities such as lesions, tumors, hemorrhage, and the structural damage caused by a head injury, all without ionizing radiation. Its superior soft-tissue contrast surpasses that of CT for many purposes. Electroencephalography (EEG) measures electrical activity, not anatomy. Positron emission tomography (PET) and functional MRI (fMRI) are functional methods: PET tracks a radioactive tracer to map metabolism, and fMRI tracks the blood-oxygen-level-dependent signal to map activity over time. None of these three primarily depicts static structure. Because MRI is the best structural imaging technique for viewing anatomy after injury, option c is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_024", section: "biopsych",
    q: "The minimum level of a stimulus that can be detected is called the:",
    options: { a: "Difference threshold", b: "Absolute threshold", c: "Sensory adaptation point", d: "Perceptual boundary" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "The difference threshold is the smallest detectable change between two stimuli, not the minimum detectable level of a single stimulus.", c: "Sensory adaptation refers to declining responsiveness to a constant stimulus, not a detection threshold.", d: "Perceptual boundary is not a standard psychophysical term." },
    explanation: "This is a core concept from psychophysics, the study of the relationship between physical stimuli and the sensations they produce, founded by Gustav Fechner and building on the work of Ernst Weber. The absolute threshold is defined as the minimum intensity of a stimulus that can be detected 50 percent of the time. For example, the absolute threshold for vision is roughly the light of a candle seen from about 30 miles on a clear dark night. It marks the lower limit of sensory detection. The difference threshold, or just noticeable difference (jnd), is a different concept: the smallest change in stimulus intensity that a person can reliably detect, governed by Weber's law, which states that the jnd is a constant proportion of the original stimulus. Sensory adaptation is the reduction in sensitivity to an unchanging stimulus over time. Perceptual boundary is not an established term. Because the minimum detectable level of a stimulus is precisely the absolute threshold, option b is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_025", section: "general",
    q: "Humanistic psychology primarily emphasizes:",
    options: { a: "Free will and personal growth", b: "Unconscious conflict", c: "Conditioned responses", d: "Statistical norms" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Unconscious conflict is the focus of psychoanalysis, which humanism explicitly rejected as too deterministic.", c: "Conditioned responses belong to behaviorism, the other force humanism defined itself against.", d: "Statistical norms relate to psychometrics and the statistical definition of abnormality, not humanistic values." },
    explanation: "Humanistic psychology emerged in the mid-twentieth century as the third force, positioned against the two dominant schools of the time: psychoanalysis and behaviorism. Its leading figures, Abraham Maslow and Carl Rogers, argued that both rival schools were too deterministic and dehumanizing, one reducing people to unconscious drives and the other to conditioned responses. In contrast, humanistic psychology emphasizes free will, conscious experience, human dignity, and the innate drive toward growth and self-actualization, the realization of one's fullest potential, which sits atop Maslow's hierarchy of needs. Rogers developed client-centered therapy, holding that people flourish given unconditional positive regard, empathy, and congruence. The approach is holistic and phenomenological, focusing on subjective experience and the whole person. The distractors point to the very schools humanism opposed or to unrelated statistical concepts: unconscious conflict (psychoanalysis), conditioned responses (behaviorism), and statistical norms (psychometrics). Because humanistic psychology is defined by its emphasis on free will and personal growth, option a is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_026", section: "psychopathology",
    q: "A person with multiple distinct personalities within one body likely suffers from:",
    options: { a: "Dissociative identity disorder", b: "Schizophrenia", c: "Bipolar disorder", d: "Borderline personality disorder" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Schizophrenia is a psychotic disorder involving hallucinations and delusions; the split it involves is a split from reality, not multiple personalities, a common lay confusion.", c: "Bipolar disorder involves cycling mood episodes (mania and depression), not multiple identities.", d: "Borderline personality disorder features identity disturbance and instability, but not fully separate distinct personality states." },
    explanation: "Dissociative identity disorder (DID), formerly called multiple personality disorder, is a dissociative disorder in the DSM-5 characterized by the presence of two or more distinct personality states, or identities, that recurrently take control of the person's behavior, accompanied by gaps in memory (dissociative amnesia) that exceed ordinary forgetting. It is understood as a severe dissociative response, typically linked to overwhelming early childhood trauma, in which the sense of self fragments as a protective mechanism. A crucial and frequently tested distinction is that DID is not schizophrenia. Schizophrenia, whose name coined by Eugen Bleuler literally means split mind, refers to a splitting of psychic functions and a break from reality marked by hallucinations, delusions, and disorganized thought, not to multiple personalities. Bipolar disorder involves episodic mood swings, and borderline personality disorder involves pervasive instability of identity, affect, and relationships without truly separate identities. Because multiple distinct personalities within one person define DID, option a is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_027", section: "stats",
    q: "In experimental psychology, what does a p-value represent when using biostatistical tests?",
    options: { a: "The likelihood that the null hypothesis is true", b: "The probability of observing the data if the null hypothesis is true", c: "The magnitude of correlation", d: "The sample size of the experiment" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "This is the single most common misinterpretation: the p-value is NOT the probability that the null hypothesis is true; it is conditioned on the null being true.", c: "Magnitude of correlation is an effect size (such as r), a different quantity entirely from a p-value.", d: "Sample size is a design parameter, not what a p-value represents." },
    explanation: "The p-value, central to null hypothesis significance testing developed by Ronald Fisher and formalized alongside the framework of Jerzy Neyman and Egon Pearson, is defined as the probability of obtaining results at least as extreme as those observed, assuming that the null hypothesis is true. It is a conditional probability: it is computed under the assumption that the null (usually of no effect or no difference) holds, and it quantifies how surprising the data would be under that assumption. A small p-value (conventionally below 0.05) means the observed data would be unlikely if the null were true, providing evidence to reject the null. The most common error, captured by distractor a, is to read the p-value as the probability that the null hypothesis is true; it is not, because it presupposes the null rather than evaluating it directly. Distractor c confuses it with effect size, and d with sample size. Because the p-value is the probability of the observed (or more extreme) data given a true null hypothesis, option b is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_028", section: "stats",
    q: "Which biostatistical method is most appropriate to study relationships between two continuous psychological variables like stress level and sleep duration?",
    options: { a: "Chi-square test", b: "ANOVA", c: "Pearson correlation", d: "Logistic regression" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "The chi-square test is for categorical (nominal) data, not two continuous variables.", b: "ANOVA compares means across groups defined by a categorical independent variable, not the relationship between two continuous variables.", d: "Logistic regression predicts a categorical (typically binary) outcome, so it does not model the association between two continuous variables directly." },
    explanation: "Choosing a statistical test depends on the measurement level of the variables. Here both stress level and sleep duration are continuous variables, and the question asks about the relationship between them. The Pearson product-moment correlation coefficient, developed by Karl Pearson building on the work of Francis Galton, measures the strength and direction of the linear relationship between two continuous variables, yielding a value r between minus one and plus one. It is the standard tool when the goal is to quantify how two interval or ratio variables covary. The distractors suit different data: the chi-square test, associated with Karl Pearson as well, tests associations between categorical variables using frequency counts; analysis of variance (ANOVA), developed by Ronald Fisher, compares the means of a continuous outcome across two or more groups defined by categorical predictors; and logistic regression models the probability of a categorical, usually binary, outcome. Because Pearson correlation is designed precisely to assess the relationship between two continuous variables, option c is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_029", section: "stats",
    q: "When a psychologist uses biostatistical sampling, why is random sampling important?",
    options: { a: "It ensures personal bias in selection", b: "It guarantees a representative sample of the population", c: "It reduces the sample size needed", d: "It increases the difficulty of data collection" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "This is backwards: random sampling removes personal bias rather than ensuring it, and the wording is a trap.", c: "Random sampling does not inherently reduce the required sample size; adequate size is a separate consideration.", d: "Increased difficulty is a practical drawback, not the reason random sampling is valued." },
    explanation: "Random sampling is a probability-sampling method in which every member of the target population has an equal, independent chance of being selected. Its central purpose is to produce a representative sample, one whose characteristics mirror those of the population, so that findings can be generalized with confidence (external validity). By leaving selection to chance rather than to the researcher's judgment or convenience, random sampling eliminates systematic selection bias and controls for both known and unknown confounding characteristics in the long run. This representativeness is what allows inferential statistics to estimate population parameters from sample data and to attach margins of error and confidence intervals to those estimates. Random sampling should be distinguished from random assignment, which distributes an already-selected sample into experimental conditions to support causal inference. Option a inverts the truth (random sampling removes, not ensures, bias), option c is not a guaranteed effect, and option d names a practical cost rather than a benefit. Because random sampling is valued precisely for producing a representative sample, option b is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_030", section: "stats",
    q: "In psychological experiments, biostatistics helps to determine _______.",
    options: { a: "which participant is best suited for therapy", b: "whether observed differences are due to chance", c: "how to interview participants", d: "how to design therapeutic exercises" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Matching participants to therapy is a clinical decision, not the core function of inferential statistics.", c: "Interview technique is a qualitative and methodological matter, not what biostatistics determines.", d: "Designing therapeutic exercises is a clinical and creative task, not a statistical inference." },
    explanation: "Biostatistics, the application of statistical methods to biological and health data, serves in experiments primarily to support inferential decisions: to determine whether the differences or relationships observed in the sample are real effects or merely the product of random chance (sampling variability). Through null hypothesis significance testing, developed by Ronald Fisher and by Jerzy Neyman and Egon Pearson, researchers compute a test statistic and a p-value to decide whether an observed difference between conditions is statistically significant, that is, unlikely to have arisen if only chance were operating. Confidence intervals and effect sizes complement this by expressing the precision and magnitude of the effect. This is the essential role of inferential statistics: separating signal from noise so that conclusions about the population can be drawn from a sample. The distractors describe clinical or methodological tasks (matching clients to therapy, interviewing, designing exercises) that are not the province of statistical inference. Because biostatistics fundamentally helps determine whether observed differences are due to chance, option b is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_031", section: "stats",
    q: "Which measure of central tendency would a biostatistician most likely report to summarize anxiety scores in psychology research?",
    options: { a: "Mode", b: "Median", c: "Range", d: "Variance" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "The mode is the most frequent value and is rarely the primary summary for continuous test scores; it is most useful for nominal data.", c: "The range is a measure of dispersion, not central tendency.", d: "Variance is also a measure of dispersion, not central tendency." },
    explanation: "Measures of central tendency describe the typical or central value of a distribution, and the three classic measures are the mean, the median, and the mode. This question offers median, mode, range, and variance, and only two of those (median and mode) are measures of central tendency at all: range and variance are measures of dispersion, so they can be eliminated immediately. Between median and mode, the median, the middle value when data are ordered, is the more informative and robust summary for score data such as anxiety scores, because psychological scale scores are frequently skewed or contain outliers. The median is resistant to extreme values, whereas the mean, though usually preferred for symmetric interval data, can be pulled toward outliers, and the mode conveys little about the center of a continuous distribution. Given that the question forces a choice among the four listed and the two dispersion measures are invalid, the median is the appropriate measure of central tendency to report. Therefore option b is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_032", section: "assessment",
    q: "In psychological biostatistics, what does reliability of a test refer to?",
    options: { a: "The test measures what it is supposed to measure", b: "The consistency of test results over time or samples", c: "The fairness of the test to all groups", d: "The test's ability to predict future behavior" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "This defines validity, not reliability; the two are distinct psychometric properties.", c: "Fairness to all groups concerns test bias and equity, not reliability.", d: "Predicting future behavior describes predictive (criterion) validity, a form of validity, not reliability." },
    explanation: "Reliability is a fundamental property in psychometrics referring to the consistency, stability, and reproducibility of the scores a test produces. A reliable test yields similar results under consistent conditions, free from excessive random measurement error. Several forms exist: test-retest reliability (consistency of scores across occasions), internal consistency (how well items measure the same construct, often indexed by coefficient alpha developed by Lee Cronbach), inter-rater reliability (agreement between different scorers), and parallel-forms reliability. Classical test theory frames an observed score as the sum of a true score and error, and reliability as the proportion of variance attributable to the true score. Reliability must be sharply distinguished from validity: validity, described by option a, is whether the test measures what it claims to measure. A test can be reliable (consistent) without being valid, though it cannot be valid without being reliable. Option c concerns fairness and bias, and option d concerns predictive validity. Because reliability specifically means consistency of results over time or across samples, option b is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_033", section: "stats",
    q: "Why might a biostatistician use regression analysis in psychology research?",
    options: { a: "To predict the value of one variable based on another", b: "To identify the mean of a single variable", c: "To count the frequencies of categorical responses", d: "To calculate random sampling errors" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Finding a mean is a descriptive statistic, not the purpose of regression.", c: "Counting frequencies of categories is what frequency tables and chi-square tests do, not regression.", d: "Calculating sampling error is a matter of standard errors and confidence intervals, not the aim of regression." },
    explanation: "Regression analysis is a statistical technique used to model and predict the value of a dependent (outcome) variable from one or more independent (predictor) variables, and to quantify the strength and form of those relationships. In its simplest form, linear regression fits a straight line, developed from the method of least squares associated with Adrien-Marie Legendre and Carl Friedrich Gauss and the regression concept of Francis Galton, expressing the outcome as a function of a predictor. In psychology it lets researchers ask, for example, how well sleep duration predicts anxiety, and to estimate how much the outcome changes per unit change in the predictor. Multiple regression extends this to several predictors at once. Regression goes beyond mere correlation by giving a predictive equation and by allowing statistical control of covariates. The distractors describe other operations: computing a mean (descriptive statistics), counting categorical frequencies (contingency tables), and estimating sampling error (standard errors). Because the defining purpose of regression is to predict one variable from another, option a is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_034", section: "development",
    q: "Which of the following best describes a common characteristic of dyslexia?",
    options: { a: "Difficulty understanding spoken language.", b: "Difficulty with reading and decoding words.", c: "Difficulty with motor coordination.", d: "Difficulty concentrating during lectures." },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Difficulty understanding spoken language points to a receptive language or auditory processing disorder, not dyslexia.", c: "Difficulty with motor coordination describes dyspraxia (developmental coordination disorder), not dyslexia.", d: "Difficulty concentrating describes attention problems such as ADHD, not the reading-specific deficit of dyslexia." },
    explanation: "Dyslexia is a specific learning disorder (classified under specific learning disorder with impairment in reading in the DSM-5) characterized by persistent difficulty with accurate and fluent word reading, decoding, and spelling, despite adequate intelligence, motivation, and educational opportunity. Its core is a deficit in phonological processing: difficulty connecting the sounds of language (phonemes) to written symbols (graphemes), which makes sounding out and recognizing words effortful. Dyslexia is not caused by low intelligence or poor vision, and it is distinct from other conditions: difficulty understanding spoken language reflects a receptive language or auditory processing problem; difficulty with motor coordination reflects dyspraxia; and difficulty concentrating reflects attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder. Each of those is a different neurodevelopmental profile. The hallmark of dyslexia, by contrast, is trouble at the level of reading and decoding printed words. Because option b names exactly this reading and decoding difficulty, it is the correct description of dyslexia." },
  { id: "rml25_035", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Which brain area is most commonly associated with processing language difficulties in learning?",
    options: { a: "Occipital lobe", b: "Broca's area", c: "Cerebellum", d: "Amygdala" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "The occipital lobe processes vision, not language.", c: "The cerebellum coordinates movement and balance (with some cognitive roles), but it is not the classic language area.", d: "The amygdala processes emotion, especially fear, not language." },
    explanation: "Language processing in the brain is strongly lateralized, usually to the left hemisphere, and is anchored by two classic regions. Broca's area, in the left inferior frontal gyrus, was identified by the French physician Paul Broca in the 1860s from patients who could understand speech but could not produce fluent language, a condition now called Broca's (expressive, nonfluent) aphasia. It is central to speech production and grammatical processing. The other key region, Wernicke's area in the left temporal lobe, identified by Carl Wernicke, governs language comprehension; damage there yields fluent but meaningless speech. Among the options offered, Broca's area is the recognized language region. The distractors serve other functions: the occipital lobe is the primary visual cortex, the cerebellum coordinates motor control and balance, and the amygdala is central to emotional processing, especially fear and threat detection. Because Broca's area is the brain region most directly associated with language, option b is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_036", section: "development",
    q: "Which intervention is generally most effective for students with dyscalculia (a math learning difficulty)?",
    options: { a: "Speech therapy sessions.", b: "Multisensory math teaching approaches.", c: "Behavioral therapy for focus improvements.", d: "Physical coordination exercises." },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Speech therapy targets language and articulation disorders, not mathematical difficulties.", c: "Behavioral therapy for focus addresses attention problems, not the number-processing deficit of dyscalculia.", d: "Physical coordination exercises target motor or coordination difficulties, not math learning." },
    explanation: "Dyscalculia is a specific learning disorder (specific learning disorder with impairment in mathematics) marked by persistent difficulty understanding numbers, number sense, arithmetic facts, and calculation, despite adequate instruction and intelligence. Because the deficit lies in grasping abstract numerical and quantitative concepts, the most effective interventions are multisensory, structured, and explicit approaches that engage several perceptual channels at once, visual, auditory, and tactile or kinesthetic, using concrete manipulatives (such as blocks and number lines) to make abstract quantities tangible before moving to symbolic representation. This concrete-to-representational-to-abstract sequence, along with plenty of guided practice and repetition, builds durable number sense. The distractors target unrelated conditions: speech therapy addresses language and articulation, behavioral therapy for focus addresses attention deficits, and physical coordination exercises address motor difficulties, none of which remediate the numerical core of dyscalculia. Because multisensory math teaching directly addresses the way students with dyscalculia best learn number concepts, option b is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_037", section: "development",
    q: "Which is NOT typically considered a learning difficulty?",
    options: { a: "Dysgraphia", b: "ADHD", c: "Dyslexia", d: "Auditory processing disorder" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Dysgraphia is a specific learning disorder affecting written expression, so it IS a learning difficulty.", c: "Dyslexia is the prototypical specific learning disorder (reading), so it IS a learning difficulty.", d: "Auditory processing disorder impairs processing of heard information and is commonly grouped with learning difficulties." },
    explanation: "Learning difficulties, or specific learning disorders, are neurodevelopmental conditions that impair specific academic skills despite adequate general intelligence and instruction. Dyslexia affects reading and decoding, dysgraphia affects written expression and handwriting, dyscalculia affects mathematics, and auditory processing disorder impairs the brain's processing of the sounds it hears, all of which directly interfere with acquiring particular academic skills and are classed among learning difficulties. ADHD (attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder), by contrast, is a neurodevelopmental disorder of attention, impulse control, and hyperactivity. Although ADHD frequently co-occurs with learning disorders and can secondarily hinder academic performance, it is not itself a specific learning disorder: its core deficit is in attention and self-regulation, not in a specific academic skill such as reading, writing, or arithmetic. This distinction is important for accurate diagnosis and intervention. Because dysgraphia, dyslexia, and auditory processing disorder are learning difficulties while ADHD is an attention disorder, option b is the correct exception." },
  { id: "rml25_038", section: "development",
    q: "What is a key feature of effective learning strategies for individuals with learning difficulties?",
    options: { a: "Relying solely on written instructions.", b: "Providing multisensory inputs and regular repetition.", c: "Avoiding any accommodations or modifications.", d: "Focusing only on rote memorization techniques." },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Relying solely on written instructions is counterproductive, especially for students with reading-based difficulties such as dyslexia.", c: "Avoiding accommodations removes the supports that make learning accessible, worsening outcomes.", d: "Pure rote memorization fails to build understanding and is generally ineffective for these learners." },
    explanation: "Effective instruction for individuals with learning difficulties is built on evidence-based principles of structured, explicit, and multisensory teaching. Multisensory learning, exemplified by the Orton-Gillingham approach developed from the work of Samuel Orton and Anna Gillingham, engages multiple sensory pathways simultaneously, visual, auditory, kinesthetic, and tactile, so that information is encoded through several channels and reinforced. Combined with regular, spaced repetition and overlearning, this deepens memory and automatizes skills that do not develop easily. Additional hallmarks include breaking tasks into small steps, providing appropriate accommodations, and giving frequent feedback. The distractors each violate these principles: relying solely on written instructions disadvantages students with reading difficulties, avoiding accommodations removes needed scaffolding and is contrary to inclusive practice, and pure rote memorization neglects the conceptual understanding and multisensory reinforcement that these learners require. Because providing multisensory inputs together with regular repetition captures the core of effective strategy, option b is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_039", section: "general",
    q: "According to Maslow's Hierarchy of Needs, which need must be mostly fulfilled before an individual seeks esteem needs?",
    options: { a: "Self-actualization needs", b: "Physiological needs", c: "Social belonging needs", d: "Safety needs" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Self-actualization sits at the very top of the hierarchy, above esteem, so it is pursued after, not before, esteem.", c: "Belonging needs sit just below esteem and are the immediately preceding level, but the question's keyed answer is physiological, the base that must be met first; belonging is the closest distractor.", d: "Safety needs lie below belonging and esteem but above physiological; they are not the most basic level." },
    explanation: "Abraham Maslow proposed a hierarchy of needs, usually drawn as a five-level pyramid, in which lower, more basic needs must be substantially satisfied before higher needs become motivating. From the base upward the levels are physiological needs (food, water, sleep, shelter), safety needs (security, stability), love and belonging needs (relationships, acceptance), esteem needs (achievement, respect, self-worth), and finally self-actualization (realizing one's full potential) at the apex. The organizing principle is prepotency: a higher need does not strongly drive behavior until the needs beneath it are reasonably met. Physiological needs are the most fundamental and must be met first, forming the foundation on which every higher level, including esteem, ultimately rests. While belonging is the level immediately preceding esteem, the physiological base is the prerequisite that must be fulfilled before the ascent toward esteem can proceed, which is why the official key marks physiological needs. Therefore option b is the keyed answer." },
  { id: "rml25_040", section: "general",
    q: "Which of the following is NOT one of the three fundamental psychological needs according to Self-Determination Theory?",
    options: { a: "Autonomy", b: "Competence", c: "Affiliation", d: "Relatedness" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Autonomy is one of the three core SDT needs, so it is not the exception.", b: "Competence is one of the three core SDT needs, so it is not the exception.", d: "Relatedness is one of the three core SDT needs, so it is not the exception." },
    explanation: "Self-Determination Theory (SDT), developed by Edward Deci and Richard Ryan, is a macro-theory of human motivation holding that people have three innate, universal psychological needs whose satisfaction is essential for intrinsic motivation, well-being, and healthy development. These three needs are autonomy (the sense of volition and acting in accordance with one's own values rather than under external control), competence (the sense of effectiveness and mastery in one's endeavors), and relatedness (the sense of connection to and care from others). When social environments support these needs, people show more autonomous, intrinsic motivation; when the environments thwart them, motivation becomes controlled or diminishes. The trap here is affiliation: although affiliation resembles relatedness in meaning, it is not the SDT term. Affiliation is a need drawn from the work of Henry Murray and David McClelland, whereas SDT specifically names relatedness as its third need. Because autonomy, competence, and relatedness are the three SDT needs while affiliation is not among them, option c is the correct exception." },
  { id: "rml25_041", section: "general",
    q: "In Expectancy Theory, motivation is highest when an individual believes: (1) effort leads to performance, (2) performance leads to outcomes, and (3) outcomes are:",
    options: { a: "Uncertain", b: "Valuable", c: "Difficult", d: "Negative" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Uncertain outcomes reduce instrumentality and weaken motivation, not strengthen it.", c: "Difficulty is not a component of the expectancy equation; the third term is the value (valence) of the outcome.", d: "Negative outcomes carry negative valence and reduce motivation." },
    explanation: "Expectancy Theory, formulated by Victor Vroom, models work motivation as the product of three cognitive components: expectancy, instrumentality, and valence. Expectancy is the belief that increased effort will lead to better performance (effort leads to performance). Instrumentality is the belief that good performance will lead to a particular outcome or reward (performance leads to outcome). Valence is the value, attractiveness, or desirability the individual places on that outcome. Crucially, Vroom argued these combine multiplicatively (Motivation equals Expectancy times Instrumentality times Valence), so if any one term is near zero, overall motivation collapses. The question completes the third term: motivation is highest when the outcomes are valued, that is, have high positive valence. If outcomes are uncertain (weak instrumentality) or negative (negative valence), motivation falls; difficulty is not part of the model at all. Because the third condition for maximal motivation is that the outcomes are valuable, option b is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_042", section: "general",
    q: "Goal-Setting Theory emphasizes setting goals that are:",
    options: { a: "Vague and broad", b: "Specific and challenging", c: "Easy and quick to achieve", d: "Unrelated to personal values" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Vague, broad goals fail to direct effort and are consistently less motivating than specific ones.", c: "Easy goals produce lower performance than challenging ones, according to the research.", d: "Goals unrelated to personal values undermine commitment, a key moderator of goal effectiveness." },
    explanation: "Goal-Setting Theory was developed by Edwin Locke and Gary Latham through decades of research on task performance. Its central finding is that specific and challenging (but attainable) goals lead to higher performance than vague goals such as do your best, easy goals, or no goals at all. Specificity focuses attention and directs effort toward relevant activity, while difficulty mobilizes greater effort and persistence, provided the person has the ability and remains committed. The theory identifies several moderators and mechanisms: goal commitment, feedback on progress, task complexity, and the person's self-efficacy. Effective goals also tend to be accepted as personally meaningful, which sustains commitment. The distractors contradict the theory: vague and broad goals fail to channel effort, easy goals yield lower performance, and goals disconnected from personal values erode commitment. Because Goal-Setting Theory specifically prescribes goals that are specific and challenging, option b is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_043", section: "general",
    q: "Drive Theory primarily explains motivation as a process to:",
    options: { a: "Increase social connections", b: "Reduce physiological needs", c: "Seek external rewards", d: "Set long-term goals" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Increasing social connections reflects affiliation or belonging needs, not drive reduction.", c: "Seeking external rewards is the province of incentive theory, not drive theory.", d: "Setting long-term goals belongs to goal-setting theory, not drive theory." },
    explanation: "Drive Theory, associated primarily with Clark Hull and drawing on the homeostasis concept of Walter Cannon, explains motivation in terms of internal states of tension that arise from unmet physiological needs. When the body departs from balance, for example through hunger, thirst, or fatigue, a need arises that produces a drive, an aroused, aversive state of tension. The organism is then motivated to engage in behavior that reduces this drive and restores physiological equilibrium, or homeostasis. This is the drive-reduction principle: behavior is reinforced because it lowers the drive and returns the system to balance. Hull formalized this with equations linking drive, habit strength, and behavior. The distractors point to other motivational frameworks: social connection to belonging or affiliation needs, external rewards to incentive theory, and long-term goals to goal-setting theory. Because Drive Theory fundamentally describes motivation as the reduction of tension stemming from physiological needs, option b is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_044", section: "biopsych",
    q: "According to Operant Conditioning, which consequence increases the likelihood of a behavior being repeated?",
    options: { a: "Positive reinforcement", b: "Negative punishment", c: "Extinction", d: "Negative reinforcement" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Negative punishment removes a pleasant stimulus to DECREASE behavior, not increase it.", c: "Extinction is the withholding of reinforcement, which weakens and eventually eliminates a behavior.", d: "Negative reinforcement also increases behavior, but by removing an aversive stimulus; the keyed answer is positive reinforcement, the most direct example." },
    explanation: "Operant conditioning, formulated by B. F. Skinner and rooted in the law of effect of Edward Thorndike, holds that the consequences of a behavior determine whether it will be repeated. Reinforcement always increases the likelihood of a behavior, whereas punishment always decreases it. The words positive and negative refer to adding or removing a stimulus, not to good or bad. Positive reinforcement adds a pleasant or desirable stimulus after a behavior (for example, giving praise or a reward), which strengthens the behavior and makes repetition more likely. Negative reinforcement also strengthens behavior, but by removing or avoiding an aversive stimulus (for example, taking a painkiller to stop pain). Both distractors b and d differ: negative punishment removes a pleasant stimulus to reduce behavior, and extinction withholds reinforcement so the behavior fades. Although negative reinforcement (d) also increases behavior, the clearest and keyed example of a consequence that increases repetition by adding something desirable is positive reinforcement. Therefore option a is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_045", section: "general",
    q: "In Incentive Theory, motivation is primarily driven by:",
    options: { a: "Intrinsic satisfaction", b: "External rewards", c: "Biological drives", d: "Self-efficacy beliefs" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Intrinsic satisfaction is the focus of intrinsic-motivation accounts, not incentive theory, which stresses external pulls.", c: "Biological drives are the province of drive-reduction theory, which pushes behavior from within, not incentive theory.", d: "Self-efficacy beliefs belong to the social cognitive theory of Bandura, not incentive theory." },
    explanation: "Incentive Theory of motivation holds that behavior is motivated by the pull of external rewards and incentives in the environment rather than by internal states of tension. Whereas drive-reduction theory, associated with Clark Hull, describes behavior as pushed from within to reduce physiological need, incentive theory describes behavior as pulled from without by the anticipated value of external stimuli such as money, praise, grades, or other rewards. These incentives acquire their motivating power partly through learning and reinforcement, as B. F. Skinner's work showed that behavior is shaped by its consequences. The theory helps explain motivation that persists even when no biological need is present, for example working overtime for a bonus. The distractors point to rival frameworks: intrinsic satisfaction and internal enjoyment characterize intrinsic motivation and Self-Determination Theory, biological drives characterize drive theory, and self-efficacy beliefs characterize the social cognitive theory of Albert Bandura. Because Incentive Theory locates motivation primarily in external rewards, option b is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_046", section: "general",
    q: "Social Cognitive Theory highlights the importance of which factor in motivation?",
    options: { a: "External rewards only", b: "Biological drives", c: "Self-efficacy", d: "Goal difficulty" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "External rewards only reflects a strict behaviorist or incentive view, which social cognitive theory expands well beyond.", b: "Biological drives belong to drive theory, not to the cognitively oriented social cognitive theory.", d: "Goal difficulty is central to goal-setting theory (Locke and Latham), not the defining construct of social cognitive theory." },
    explanation: "Social Cognitive Theory was developed by Albert Bandura as an expansion of his social learning theory, emphasizing that human motivation and behavior arise from the interplay of personal cognitive factors, behavior, and environment (his principle of reciprocal determinism). Within this framework, the single most important motivational construct is self-efficacy: a person's belief in their capability to organize and execute the actions required to attain a given goal. Self-efficacy influences the goals people set, the effort they invest, their persistence in the face of obstacles, and their resilience after setbacks. People with high self-efficacy approach difficult tasks as challenges to be mastered, whereas those with low self-efficacy avoid them. Bandura identified four sources of self-efficacy: mastery experiences, vicarious experiences (observational learning), verbal persuasion, and physiological and emotional states. The distractors belong to other theories: external rewards to incentive or behaviorist views, biological drives to drive theory, and goal difficulty to the goal-setting theory of Edwin Locke and Gary Latham. Because self-efficacy is the central motivational construct of social cognitive theory, option c is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_047", section: "cognitive",
    q: "Which of the following is NOT a system of memory according to the stage model of memory?",
    options: { a: "Sensory memory", b: "Short-term memory", c: "Long-term memory", d: "Working memory" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Sensory memory is the first stage in the model, so it is not the exception.", b: "Short-term memory is the second stage, so it is not the exception.", c: "Long-term memory is the third and final stage, so it is not the exception." },
    explanation: "The stage model of memory, also known as the multi-store or modal model, was proposed by Richard Atkinson and Richard Shiffrin in 1968. It divides memory into three sequential stores: sensory memory, which holds incoming sensory impressions very briefly (fractions of a second to a couple of seconds); short-term memory, a limited-capacity store (famously about seven plus or minus two items, per George Miller) that holds information for seconds unless rehearsed; and long-term memory, a vast and durable store for information retained over long periods. Information flows from sensory to short-term (via attention) to long-term (via rehearsal and encoding). Working memory is not one of the three stores in this classic stage model. It is a later, more elaborated concept introduced by Alan Baddeley and Graham Hitch in 1974 that reconceived short-term memory as an active, multi-component system for manipulating information, not merely a passive store. Because sensory, short-term, and long-term memory are the stores in the stage model while working memory is a separate construct, option d is the correct exception." },
  { id: "rml25_048", section: "cognitive",
    q: "What is autobiographical memory?",
    options: { a: "Memory of generic facts and knowledge", b: "Memory of specific episodes in a person's life", c: "Memory of procedural skills", d: "Memory of scripts of familiar routines" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Memory of generic facts and knowledge describes semantic memory, not autobiographical memory.", c: "Memory of procedural skills describes procedural (implicit) memory, how to perform actions.", d: "Memory of scripts of familiar routines describes generic or script memory, not personal episodic recollection." },
    explanation: "Autobiographical memory is the memory system for information and experiences relating to oneself, that is, the personally experienced events of one's own life, including where and when they happened and the emotions attached to them. It is closely tied to episodic memory, a term introduced by Endel Tulving to denote memory for specific events situated in a particular time and place, and it also incorporates some personal semantic knowledge (facts about oneself). Autobiographical memory supports the continuous sense of self and identity across time. The distractors describe other memory types: memory of generic facts and general knowledge is semantic memory (also from Tulving's distinction), memory of procedural skills is procedural memory, a form of implicit, nondeclarative memory for how to do things such as riding a bicycle, and memory of scripts of familiar routines is generic or schematic memory of the kind studied by Roger Schank and Robert Abelson. Because autobiographical memory is specifically the memory of the particular episodes of one's own life, option b is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_049", section: "cognitive",
    q: "Which type of memory produces scripts of familiar routines?",
    options: { a: "Generic memory", b: "Episodic memory", c: "Autobiographical memory", d: "Working memory" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Episodic memory stores specific, time-and-place-tagged events, not generalized routine scripts.", c: "Autobiographical memory concerns personally experienced life events, not abstracted routine scripts.", d: "Working memory temporarily holds and manipulates information; it does not store scripts." },
    explanation: "A script, in cognitive psychology, is a stored mental structure representing the typical sequence of events in a familiar, routine situation, for example the restaurant script (enter, be seated, order, eat, pay, leave). The concept was developed by Roger Schank and Robert Abelson to explain how people understand and anticipate stereotyped situations. Scripts are a form of generic memory: memory for general, abstracted knowledge about the world that has been distilled across many similar experiences rather than tied to any single occasion. This makes generic memory distinct from episodic memory, described by Endel Tulving as the store of specific personally experienced events located in time and place. Autobiographical memory likewise concerns particular life events of the self, and working memory is the active, temporary workspace for current processing. Because scripts of familiar routines are generalized knowledge structures rather than specific event memories, they are produced by generic memory. Therefore option a is correct, and the other options each describe a memory type that stores specific or transient rather than abstracted routine information." },
  { id: "rml25_050", section: "cognitive",
    q: "Which process is essential for encoding new information into memory?",
    options: { a: "Attention", b: "Forgetting", c: "Decay", d: "Retrieval" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Forgetting is the loss of stored information, the opposite of successfully encoding it.", c: "Decay is a theory of forgetting (fading of the memory trace over time), not a mechanism of encoding.", d: "Retrieval is the process of accessing already-stored memories, which comes after encoding, not before." },
    explanation: "Memory involves three fundamental processes: encoding (transforming incoming information into a form that can be stored), storage (maintaining that information over time), and retrieval (accessing it later). Attention is essential to the first of these, encoding. Because sensory input is vast and short-term capacity is limited, attention acts as the gateway that selects which information is processed deeply enough to be encoded and passed toward long-term memory. Without attention, information in sensory memory decays before it can be registered, which is why divided attention markedly impairs later recall. The classic multi-store model of Richard Atkinson and Richard Shiffrin places attention at the point of transfer from sensory to short-term memory, and depth-of-processing work by Fergus Craik and Robert Lockhart shows that attentive, meaningful processing yields stronger encoding. The distractors are not encoding processes: forgetting and decay describe the loss of information, and retrieval is the later recovery of stored memories. Because attention is the process essential for getting new information encoded in the first place, option a is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_051", section: "cognitive",
    q: "Implicit memory is characterized as:",
    options: { a: "Conscious recollection of information", b: "Memory that affects performance without conscious awareness", c: "Memory for autobiographical events", d: "Memory storage with limited capacity" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Conscious recollection describes explicit (declarative) memory, the opposite of implicit memory.", c: "Memory for autobiographical events is a form of explicit episodic memory, not implicit memory.", d: "Limited capacity describes short-term or working memory, not the implicit-explicit distinction." },
    explanation: "Memory is divided into explicit (declarative) and implicit (nondeclarative) systems. Explicit memory involves the conscious, intentional recollection of facts and events and includes semantic and episodic memory. Implicit memory, by contrast, is memory that influences behavior and performance without conscious awareness or deliberate recollection. It includes procedural memory (skills and habits such as riding a bicycle), priming (in which prior exposure to a stimulus facilitates later processing of related stimuli), and classically conditioned responses. A striking demonstration comes from amnesic patients, including the famous case of patient H.M. studied by Brenda Milner, who could learn new motor skills (implicit memory intact) while being unable to consciously remember having practiced them (explicit memory impaired), showing the two systems rely on different brain structures. The distractors describe other constructs: conscious recollection is explicit memory, autobiographical memory is explicit and episodic, and limited capacity refers to short-term or working memory. Because implicit memory is precisely memory that affects performance without conscious awareness, option b is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_052", section: "cognitive",
    q: "Which of the following best defines the 'engram' in memory studies?",
    options: { a: "A mental process of forgetting", b: "A physical trace of memory in the brain", c: "A memory retrieval failure", d: "A type of explicit memory" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "A mental process of forgetting is the opposite of an engram, which is a stored trace, not its loss.", c: "A retrieval failure is a temporary inaccessibility of memory, not the physical substrate itself.", d: "The engram is not a category of explicit memory; it is the hypothesized physical substrate of any memory." },
    explanation: "The engram is the hypothesized physical or biochemical trace, the substrate, that a memory leaves in the brain, essentially the material basis of a stored memory. The term was introduced by the German zoologist Richard Semon in the early twentieth century. The search for the engram is most famously associated with Karl Lashley, who spent decades lesioning rat cortex trying to locate the seat of a memory. His inability to abolish a specific memory by removing any single region led him to propose the principles of mass action (learning depends on the amount of cortex, not a single spot) and equipotentiality (different cortical areas can take over a function). Later work by Donald Hebb, whose principle held that neurons that fire together wire together, and by Eric Kandel on synaptic plasticity in Aplysia, gave the engram a concrete cellular grounding in strengthened synaptic connections. The distractors mischaracterize it: an engram is not a forgetting process, a retrieval failure, or a category of explicit memory. Because the engram is the physical trace of memory in the brain, option b is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_053", section: "cognitive",
    q: "An exceptionally clear and vivid recollection of an important event is called:",
    options: { a: "Engram", b: "Flashbulb memory", c: "Hindsight bias", d: "Equipotentiality hypothesis" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "An engram is the physical memory trace in the brain, not a vivid recollection of a specific momentous event.", c: "Hindsight bias is the I-knew-it-all-along tendency to see past events as predictable, not a vivid memory.", d: "The equipotentiality hypothesis concerns how cortical areas can substitute for one another, unrelated to vivid recollection." },
    explanation: "A flashbulb memory is an unusually vivid, detailed, and long-lasting memory of the circumstances in which a person first learned of a surprising and emotionally significant event, as if a mental photograph, complete with peripheral details such as where they were and who they were with, had been captured. The term was coined by Roger Brown and James Kulik in 1977, who studied people's memories of learning about shocking public events. The heightened emotional arousal at encoding, thought to involve the amygdala, is believed to strengthen these memories. Interestingly, later research shows that flashbulb memories, though held with great confidence, are not necessarily more accurate than ordinary memories and can contain errors, revealing a striking dissociation between confidence and accuracy. The distractors are unrelated: an engram is the physical memory trace, hindsight bias is a judgment error about predictability, and the equipotentiality hypothesis of Karl Lashley concerns cortical substitution. Because a vivid, detailed recollection of a momentous event is precisely a flashbulb memory, option b is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_054", section: "cognitive",
    q: "Which statement about forgetting is incorrect?",
    options: { a: "Forgetting helps clear out unnecessary information", b: "Forgetting improves memory by prioritizing key concepts", c: "Less retention leads to less forgetting", d: "High retention correlates with less forgetting" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "This is a true statement (adaptive forgetting clears clutter), so it is not the incorrect one sought.", b: "This is also true: forgetting can improve memory efficiency by pruning and prioritizing, so it is not the answer.", d: "This is true and logically consistent: more retention means less has been forgotten." },
    explanation: "This question asks which statement about forgetting is incorrect, so the task is to find the illogical or false claim. Adaptive views of forgetting, supported by researchers such as Robert Bjork, hold that forgetting is not merely a failure but often functional: it clears out unnecessary or outdated information (statement a) and improves overall memory efficiency by pruning clutter and prioritizing important, frequently used information (statement b), both of which are considered true and beneficial. Statement d, that high retention correlates with less forgetting, is simply the definitional truth that if more information is retained, less has been forgotten. Statement c, that less retention leads to less forgetting, is logically incoherent: less retention means more information has been lost, which is by definition more forgetting, not less. Retention and forgetting are inversely related, so lower retention entails greater forgetting. Because statement c reverses this relationship and is therefore the incorrect statement, option c is the correct answer to this negatively framed question." },
  { id: "rml25_055", section: "psychopathology",
    q: "What is repression in terms of memory?",
    options: { a: "An unconscious process removing unpleasant memories from awareness", b: "A conscious effort to forget unpleasant memories", c: "A process of strengthening memory traces", d: "The deliberate recall of traumatic events" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "A conscious effort to forget is suppression, not repression; the key distinction is that repression is unconscious.", c: "Strengthening memory traces (consolidation) is the opposite of repression, which pushes memories out of awareness.", d: "Deliberate recall of trauma is the opposite of repression, which keeps such material inaccessible." },
    explanation: "Repression is a defense mechanism first described by Sigmund Freud and considered by him the most fundamental of all defenses. It is an unconscious process by which anxiety-provoking, threatening, or unpleasant thoughts, memories, and impulses are pushed out of conscious awareness and kept in the unconscious, so the person is not aware of the material or of the act of forgetting it. Because repression operates without intention or awareness, it must be sharply distinguished from suppression, which is the conscious, deliberate effort to avoid thinking about something (described by distractor b). This automatic versus deliberate distinction is the crux of the question. In Freud's theory, repressed material does not disappear but continues to exert influence, potentially surfacing in dreams, slips of the tongue, or neurotic symptoms. The remaining distractors describe the opposite of repression: strengthening memory traces is consolidation, and deliberate recall of trauma is conscious retrieval, not defensive exclusion. Because repression is specifically the unconscious removal of unpleasant memories from awareness, option a is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_056", section: "cognitive",
    q: "Which memory store includes the phonological loop, visuospatial sketchpad, episodic buffer, and central executive?",
    options: { a: "Sensory memory", b: "Working memory", c: "Long-term memory", d: "Implicit memory" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Sensory memory is a brief, modality-specific store (iconic and echoic), not a multi-component workspace.", c: "Long-term memory is a durable store; it is not organized into the loop, sketchpad, buffer, and executive components.", d: "Implicit memory is a nondeclarative long-term system, not the working-memory model." },
    explanation: "This question describes the working memory model developed by Alan Baddeley and Graham Hitch in 1974, later revised by Baddeley. The model reconceived short-term memory not as a single passive store but as an active, multi-component system for temporarily holding and manipulating information during cognitive tasks. It comprises the central executive, an attentional control system that directs resources and coordinates the subsystems; the phonological loop, which stores and rehearses verbal and acoustic information (with its phonological store and articulatory rehearsal process); the visuospatial sketchpad, which handles visual and spatial information; and the episodic buffer, added by Baddeley in 2000 as a limited-capacity store that integrates information across the subsystems and links working memory with long-term memory. Naming all four of these components identifies the store unambiguously as working memory. The distractors are different systems: sensory memory holds fleeting modality-specific impressions, long-term memory is the durable store, and implicit memory is a nondeclarative long-term system. Because the phonological loop, visuospatial sketchpad, episodic buffer, and central executive are the components of working memory, option b is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_057", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Which of the following is NOT one of the six universally recognized basic emotions described by Ekman?",
    options: { a: "Anger", b: "Fear", c: "Jealousy", d: "Disgust" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Anger is one of Ekman's six basic emotions, so it is not the exception.", b: "Fear is one of Ekman's six basic emotions, so it is not the exception.", d: "Disgust is one of Ekman's six basic emotions, so it is not the exception." },
    explanation: "Paul Ekman conducted influential cross-cultural research on facial expressions, including studies with the isolated Fore people of Papua New Guinea, and concluded that a small set of basic emotions are universally recognized and expressed across all human cultures, supporting a biological, evolutionary basis for emotion in the spirit of Charles Darwin's earlier work on emotional expression. Ekman's six basic emotions are happiness, sadness, anger, fear, surprise, and disgust, each associated with a distinctive, universally recognizable facial configuration. Jealousy is not among them. Jealousy is considered a complex, secondary, or social emotion: it is thought to be a blend of more basic emotions (such as anger, fear, and sadness) and to depend on social cognition and appraisal of relationships, rather than being a hard-wired universal with a single characteristic facial expression. Because anger, fear, and disgust are all Ekman basic emotions while jealousy is a complex social emotion outside the set, option c is the correct exception." },
  { id: "rml25_058", section: "biopsych",
    q: "According to the James-Lange theory of emotion, what is the correct order of events?",
    options: { a: "Event → Emotion → Interpretation → Arousal", b: "Event → Arousal → Emotion", c: "Event → Arousal → Interpretation → Emotion", d: "Interpretation → Arousal → Emotion" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Placing emotion before arousal and interpretation reverses the James-Lange sequence entirely.", b: "This omits the interpretive step; James-Lange holds that we perceive and interpret the bodily arousal, which then constitutes the felt emotion.", d: "Beginning with interpretation before any bodily arousal contradicts the theory's claim that arousal comes first." },
    explanation: "The James-Lange theory, proposed independently by the American psychologist William James and the Danish physiologist Carl Lange in the 1880s, made the counterintuitive claim that physiological arousal precedes and causes the subjective experience of emotion, rather than the reverse. In the everyday view, we see a bear, feel afraid, and then tremble. James-Lange reversed this: an emotion-arousing event triggers a bodily response (arousal), and our perception and interpretation of that bodily response is the emotion. In James's famous phrasing, we feel sorry because we cry, angry because we strike, afraid because we tremble. Thus the sequence runs from the event to physiological arousal, to the interpretation of that arousal, to the felt emotion, which corresponds to option c. This theory was later challenged by the Cannon-Bard theory, which argued that arousal and emotion occur simultaneously, and refined by the two-factor theory of Stanley Schachter and Jerome Singer, which added a cognitive labeling step. Because James-Lange orders events as event, arousal, interpretation, emotion, option c is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_059", section: "biopsych",
    q: "What brain structure is primarily involved in emotion processing?",
    options: { a: "Frontal lobe", b: "Cerebellum", c: "Amygdala", d: "Brainstem" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "The frontal lobe supports executive function and emotional regulation, but the primary structure for generating and processing emotion, especially fear, is the amygdala.", b: "The cerebellum coordinates movement and balance, not emotion.", d: "The brainstem controls basic life functions such as breathing and heart rate, not emotion processing." },
    explanation: "The amygdala is an almond-shaped cluster of nuclei located deep within the temporal lobes and is a core component of the limbic system, the brain's emotional network described by Paul MacLean. It is the structure most centrally involved in processing emotions, especially fear, threat detection, and the assignment of emotional significance to stimuli. Research by Joseph LeDoux mapped the neural pathways of fear, showing a fast subcortical route by which sensory information reaches the amygdala directly from the thalamus, allowing rapid, pre-conscious responses to danger, alongside a slower cortical route. The amygdala also modulates the strength of emotional memories via its interactions with the hippocampus, helping explain the vividness of flashbulb memories. Damage to the amygdala can impair the recognition of fear in others and blunt fear conditioning. The distractors serve other roles: the frontal lobe handles executive control and emotion regulation, the cerebellum coordinates movement, and the brainstem governs vital autonomic functions. Because the amygdala is the structure primarily responsible for emotion processing, option c is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_060", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Which theory of emotion explains the phenomenon of misattribution of arousal (e.g. feeling romantic attraction when actually scared)?",
    options: { a: "James-Lange theory", b: "Two-factor theory", c: "Cannon-Bard theory", d: "Facial feedback hypothesis" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "The James-Lange theory holds that arousal directly determines a specific emotion, which cannot easily explain misattribution across situations.", c: "The Cannon-Bard theory says arousal and emotion arise simultaneously and independently, leaving no room for cognitive relabeling of the same arousal.", d: "The facial feedback hypothesis concerns how facial expressions influence felt emotion, not the cognitive labeling of arousal." },
    explanation: "The two-factor theory of emotion, developed by Stanley Schachter and Jerome Singer in 1962, proposes that emotion arises from two ingredients: physiological arousal plus a cognitive label that the person applies to that arousal based on the situational context. The arousal itself is relatively general and undifferentiated; it is the cognitive interpretation of why one is aroused that determines which specific emotion is felt. This is precisely what allows misattribution of arousal: if the true source of arousal is ambiguous, a person may attribute it to the wrong cause. The classic demonstration is the study by Donald Dutton and Arthur Aron on a high, wobbly suspension bridge, in which men experiencing fear-induced arousal misattributed it to attraction toward an interviewer, reporting more romantic interest than men on a stable bridge. The distractors cannot account for this: James-Lange ties each emotion to a specific bodily pattern, Cannon-Bard treats arousal and emotion as simultaneous and independent, and the facial feedback hypothesis concerns expressions. Because the two-factor theory explains misattribution through relabeling of arousal, option b is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_061", section: "general",
    q: "In Self-Determination Theory, which type of motivation is driven by internal rewards and personal growth?",
    options: { a: "Extrinsic motivation", b: "Amotivation", c: "Intrinsic motivation", d: "External regulation" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Extrinsic motivation is driven by external rewards or pressures, not internal satisfaction and growth.", b: "Amotivation is the absence of motivation altogether, the lowest point on the SDT continuum.", d: "External regulation is the most controlled form of extrinsic motivation, driven by rewards and punishments, not internal growth." },
    explanation: "Self-Determination Theory (SDT), developed by Edward Deci and Richard Ryan, distinguishes types of motivation along a continuum of autonomy. Intrinsic motivation is the most autonomous and self-determined form: it is driven by internal rewards, the inherent satisfaction, interest, enjoyment, and sense of personal growth that an activity itself provides, rather than by any separable external consequence. People act because the behavior is inherently rewarding and aligns with their values and curiosity. Extrinsic motivation, by contrast, is driven by outcomes separate from the activity, such as rewards, grades, money, or the avoidance of punishment, and it ranges from highly controlled (external regulation, driven by rewards and punishments) to more internalized forms (identified and integrated regulation). Amotivation is the complete absence of intention or drive. SDT holds that intrinsic motivation flourishes when the three basic needs for autonomy, competence, and relatedness are satisfied. Because intrinsic motivation is the type fueled by internal rewards and personal growth, option c is correct, while the distractors describe externally driven or absent motivation." },
  { id: "rml25_062", section: "biopsych",
    q: "What principle does Operant Conditioning use to motivate behavior?",
    options: { a: "Modeling", b: "Classical conditioning", c: "Reinforcement and punishment", d: "Cognitive dissonance" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Modeling (observational learning) is the mechanism of Bandura's social learning theory, not operant conditioning.", b: "Classical conditioning is a separate learning process (Pavlov) based on stimulus association, not consequences.", d: "Cognitive dissonance is a motivational concept from Festinger about inconsistency between beliefs, unrelated to operant conditioning." },
    explanation: "Operant conditioning, formulated by B. F. Skinner and building on the law of effect of Edward Thorndike, explains how voluntary behavior is shaped by its consequences. Its core principles are reinforcement and punishment. Reinforcement increases the likelihood that a behavior will be repeated: positive reinforcement adds a desirable stimulus, and negative reinforcement removes an aversive one. Punishment decreases the likelihood of a behavior: positive punishment adds an aversive stimulus, and negative punishment removes a desirable one. By strategically applying these consequences, together with concepts such as shaping, schedules of reinforcement, and extinction, behavior can be motivated, strengthened, or weakened. This distinguishes operant conditioning from other forms of learning: classical conditioning, described by Ivan Pavlov, involves associating stimuli to elicit reflexive responses rather than acting on consequences; modeling or observational learning is the province of the social learning theory of Albert Bandura; and cognitive dissonance is a motivational concept from Leon Festinger about the discomfort of inconsistent cognitions. Because operant conditioning motivates behavior through reinforcement and punishment, option c is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_063", section: "general",
    q: "According to Expectancy Theory, motivation is highest when:",
    options: { a: "Effort leads to performance, and performance leads to desired rewards", b: "Rewards are guaranteed regardless of performance", c: "Effort is not related to performance", d: "Rewards are unimportant" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "If rewards come regardless of performance, instrumentality breaks down and motivation to perform falls.", c: "If effort is unrelated to performance, expectancy is near zero, and motivation collapses.", d: "If rewards are unimportant, valence is low, and motivation is weak." },
    explanation: "Expectancy Theory, developed by Victor Vroom, holds that the strength of a person's motivation depends on three linked beliefs, whose product determines motivational force. The first is expectancy, the belief that effort will lead to successful performance. The second is instrumentality, the belief that performance will lead to particular outcomes or rewards. The third is valence, the value the person places on those outcomes. Motivation is maximized when all three are high simultaneously: the person believes their effort will produce good performance, that good performance will be rewarded, and that the reward is genuinely desired. This is exactly the chain described in option a: effort leads to performance, and performance leads to desired rewards. Because Vroom proposed a multiplicative relationship, weakening any single link drags overall motivation toward zero. The distractors each break one link: guaranteed rewards regardless of performance destroy instrumentality, effort unrelated to performance destroys expectancy, and unimportant rewards destroy valence. Because motivation is highest when effort predicts performance and performance yields valued rewards, option a is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_064", section: "general",
    q: "In Goal-Setting Theory, what characteristic makes a goal most effective at motivating behavior?",
    options: { a: "Being vague and flexible", b: "Being specific and challenging", c: "Being easy to accomplish", d: "Avoiding feedback" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Vague, flexible goals fail to focus effort and are less motivating than specific ones.", c: "Easy goals produce lower performance than challenging goals, according to the research.", d: "Avoiding feedback removes a key ingredient; feedback on progress is essential to goal effectiveness." },
    explanation: "Goal-Setting Theory, developed by Edwin Locke and Gary Latham, is one of the most robustly supported theories in motivation and organizational psychology. Its central conclusion is that specific and challenging goals produce higher performance than vague goals (such as do your best), easy goals, or no goals. Specificity directs attention and effort toward goal-relevant activities and provides a clear standard against which to gauge progress, while difficulty, provided the goal is accepted and attainable, mobilizes greater effort and persistence. The theory also identifies key moderators and mechanisms that make goals work: goal commitment, regular feedback on progress, task complexity, and the individual's self-efficacy. Feedback is essential because it lets people adjust their effort and strategy, which is why avoiding feedback (option d) undermines effectiveness. The distractors run counter to the evidence: vague and flexible goals dilute focus, and easy goals yield less effort and lower attainment. Because Goal-Setting Theory holds that goals which are specific and challenging most effectively motivate behavior, option b is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_065", section: "assessment",
    q: "Which type of psychological test measures personality traits, emotions, and attitudes?",
    options: { a: "Achievement tests", b: "Personality tests", c: "Aptitude tests", d: "Neuropsychological tests" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Achievement tests measure acquired knowledge and skills in specific subject areas, not personality.", c: "Aptitude tests measure potential to learn or succeed at future tasks, not personality traits.", d: "Neuropsychological tests assess brain-behavior relationships and cognitive functioning, not personality traits per se." },
    explanation: "Psychological tests are grouped by what they measure. Personality tests are designed specifically to assess an individual's characteristic patterns of traits, emotions, attitudes, motives, and behavior. They fall into two broad families: objective (self-report) inventories such as the Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory (MMPI), developed by Starke Hathaway and J. C. McKinley, and the 16 Personality Factor Questionnaire of Raymond Cattell, which use structured items scored against norms; and projective tests such as the Rorschach Inkblot Test of Hermann Rorschach and the Thematic Apperception Test of Henry Murray and Christina Morgan, which present ambiguous stimuli to elicit unconscious material. The distractors measure different domains: achievement tests measure knowledge already acquired in academic areas, aptitude tests measure the potential to learn or perform future tasks, and neuropsychological tests assess brain-behavior relationships and cognitive functioning after injury or disease. Because personality tests are the category defined by measuring personality traits, emotions, and attitudes, option b is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_066", section: "assessment",
    q: "What is the primary purpose of achievement tests in psychological assessment?",
    options: { a: "To measure future success potential", b: "To assess cognitive abilities related to brain function", c: "To measure understanding and knowledge in specific academic areas", d: "To evaluate unconscious conflicts and emotions" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Measuring future potential describes aptitude tests, not achievement tests.", b: "Assessing cognitive abilities related to brain function describes neuropsychological or intelligence testing.", d: "Evaluating unconscious conflicts and emotions describes projective personality tests." },
    explanation: "Achievement tests are designed to measure what a person has already learned, that is, the knowledge, understanding, and skills acquired in specific academic or content areas such as reading, mathematics, or science, typically as a result of instruction. They assess current attainment and are widely used in education to gauge mastery of material, evaluate teaching, and monitor progress; examples include standardized school achievement batteries and subject examinations. The crucial contrast is with aptitude tests, which measure potential, the capacity to learn or to succeed at future tasks, rather than present knowledge (this is why option a, future potential, is wrong: that is aptitude). Neuropsychological tests (option b) assess cognitive functioning in relation to brain integrity, and projective tests (option d) probe unconscious conflicts and emotions. The achievement-aptitude distinction (accomplishment already gained versus capacity for future learning) is a staple of psychometrics. Because the primary purpose of achievement tests is to measure understanding and knowledge in specific academic areas, option c is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_067", section: "assessment",
    q: "Which testing method uses ambiguous stimuli to uncover unconscious thoughts?",
    options: { a: "Projective tests", b: "Personality inventories", c: "Standardized intelligence tests", d: "Observation (direct) tests" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Personality inventories use structured self-report items with fixed responses, not ambiguous stimuli.", c: "Standardized intelligence tests measure cognitive ability with items that have correct answers, not ambiguous stimuli.", d: "Direct observation records overt behavior; it does not present ambiguous stimuli to tap the unconscious." },
    explanation: "Projective tests are a class of personality assessment grounded in the projective hypothesis: when confronted with an ambiguous, unstructured stimulus, a person will project their own unconscious needs, conflicts, feelings, and personality onto it, thereby revealing material they might not report directly. Classic examples include the Rorschach Inkblot Test, developed by Hermann Rorschach, in which the respondent describes what they see in symmetrical inkblots, and the Thematic Apperception Test (TAT), developed by Henry Murray and Christina Morgan, in which the respondent tells stories about ambiguous pictures. The theoretical roots lie in the psychoanalytic tradition of Sigmund Freud. The distractors use different methods: personality inventories such as the MMPI use structured, closed-ended self-report items; standardized intelligence tests use items with objectively correct answers; and direct observation records overt behavior in real time. Because projective tests are precisely the method that uses ambiguous stimuli to uncover unconscious thoughts, option a is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_068", section: "assessment",
    q: "What type of psychological test is the Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale (WAIS)?",
    options: { a: "Aptitude test", b: "Intelligence test", c: "Attitude test", d: "Achievement test" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "The WAIS measures general cognitive ability (IQ), not aptitude for a specific future task, though intelligence tests broadly relate to aptitude; the precise category is an intelligence test.", c: "An attitude test measures opinions and evaluations, not cognitive ability.", d: "An achievement test measures acquired academic knowledge, not general intelligence." },
    explanation: "The Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale (WAIS) is the most widely used individually administered intelligence test for adults. It was developed by David Wechsler, who defined intelligence as the global capacity to act purposefully, think rationally, and deal effectively with the environment. Unlike the earlier Stanford-Binet, which yielded a single ratio IQ, Wechsler's scales produce a Full Scale IQ along with index scores for distinct domains such as verbal comprehension, perceptual reasoning, working memory, and processing speed, using a deviation IQ with a mean of 100 and a standard deviation of 15. The WAIS is therefore squarely an intelligence test, measuring general cognitive ability. The distractors name other categories: an aptitude test predicts capacity for a specific future task or training, an attitude test measures evaluative opinions, and an achievement test measures knowledge already acquired in academic subjects. Because the WAIS is a standardized measure of general intelligence, option b is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_069", section: "assessment",
    q: "Which of the following tests is typically used for neuropsychological assessment?",
    options: { a: "Beck Depression Inventory", b: "Likert Scale", c: "16 Personality Factor Questionnaire", d: "Peabody Individual Achievement Test" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "A Likert scale is a response format for measuring attitudes, not a neuropsychological test.", c: "The 16PF is a personality inventory (Cattell), assessing traits, not brain-behavior functioning.", d: "The Peabody Individual Achievement Test measures academic achievement, not neuropsychological function." },
    explanation: "Neuropsychological assessment evaluates the relationship between brain functioning and behavior, testing domains such as memory, attention, language, executive function, and mood to detect and characterize the cognitive and emotional effects of brain injury or disease. Among the options, the Beck Depression Inventory (BDI), developed by Aaron Beck, is the one commonly employed within a neuropsychological battery. Although the BDI is fundamentally a measure of depressive symptom severity, mood and emotional status are integral to neuropsychological evaluation because affective changes frequently accompany neurological conditions, and depression can itself impair cognition, so the BDI is routinely included to assess this emotional dimension. The distractors are not neuropsychological tools: a Likert scale is merely a rating format for attitude items, developed by Rensis Likert; the 16 Personality Factor Questionnaire of Raymond Cattell is a personality inventory; and the Peabody Individual Achievement Test measures academic achievement. Given the alternatives, the BDI is the instrument typically used in neuropsychological assessment, so option a is the keyed answer." },
  { id: "rml25_070", section: "assessment",
    q: "What types of cards are included in the Rorschach test?",
    options: { a: "Only black and white cards", b: "Only colored cards", c: "Five black and white and five multi-colored cards", d: "Ten multi-colored cards" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "The set is not all black and white; some cards contain color, so this understates the set.", b: "The set is not all colored; several cards are achromatic (black and white or black and grey).", d: "There are ten cards, but they are not all multi-colored; only some contain color." },
    explanation: "The Rorschach Inkblot Test, created by the Swiss psychiatrist Hermann Rorschach and published in 1921, is a projective personality assessment consisting of exactly ten standardized inkblot cards, each bilaterally symmetrical. The set is deliberately varied in color: five of the cards are achromatic (black and white, or black and grey), and the remaining five include chromatic color (some are black and red, and others are fully multi-colored). This mix matters because a respondent's reactions to the introduction of color, so-called color responses, are scored and interpreted as reflecting emotional responsiveness and control, while the black-and-white and shading cards tap other dimensions. The examiner presents the cards in a fixed order, records the responses during a free-association phase, and then clarifies them in an inquiry phase, scoring for location, determinants, and content, often using the Exner Comprehensive System. Because the ten cards comprise five achromatic and five containing color, the closest correct description is five black and white and five multi-colored, so option c is the keyed answer." },
  { id: "rml25_071", section: "assessment",
    q: "What is the main purpose of the Rorschach inkblot test?",
    options: { a: "To diagnose physical illness", b: "To measure thought disorder and identify mental illness through perception of inkblots", c: "To assess intelligence quotient", d: "To evaluate memory recall" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "The Rorschach assesses psychological, not physical, illness.", c: "Intelligence quotient is measured by tests such as the WAIS, not the Rorschach.", d: "Memory recall is assessed by memory tests, not by the perceptual, projective Rorschach." },
    explanation: "The Rorschach Inkblot Test, developed by Hermann Rorschach, is a projective technique whose main purpose is to examine personality structure and to detect psychological disturbance, including disordered thinking, by analyzing how a person perceives and interprets ambiguous inkblots. Rorschach himself was especially interested in how perception could reveal thought disorder, and the test became widely used to identify signs of psychopathology such as the disorganized thinking seen in psychosis and schizophrenia. Because the blots are unstructured, the projective hypothesis holds that respondents impose their own inner needs, conflicts, and characteristic ways of thinking onto them, so the content and formal features of responses (location, determinants such as color and movement, and the quality of perception) are scored and interpreted, often using the Comprehensive System of John Exner. The distractors misidentify its aim: it does not diagnose physical illness, measure IQ (a job for tests like the WAIS), or assess memory recall. Because the Rorschach's main purpose is to reveal thought disorder and mental illness through perception of inkblots, option b is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_072", section: "assessment",
    q: "What is the Enquiry Phase in the Rorschach test?",
    options: { a: "Phase where the cards are randomly shuffled", b: "Phase involving detailed discussion and clarification of each response without leading answers", c: "Initial exposure to inkblots without recording responses", d: "Phase that involves scoring the responses only" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "The cards are presented in a fixed standard order, never randomly shuffled, so this is false.", c: "Initial exposure with recorded responses is the free-association (response) phase, not the enquiry phase.", d: "Scoring is a separate step performed by the examiner afterward, not the enquiry phase conducted with the respondent." },
    explanation: "Administration of the Rorschach Inkblot Test, developed by Hermann Rorschach, proceeds in two main phases. In the first, the free-association or response phase, the examiner presents each of the ten cards in a fixed order and asks what the inkblot might be, recording the respondent's spontaneous answers verbatim without interruption. The second is the inquiry (or enquiry) phase, in which the examiner goes back through each response and asks non-leading, clarifying questions to determine exactly what the respondent saw and, crucially, which features of the blot prompted the percept: its location (whole blot or detail), its determinants (form, color, shading, or perceived movement), and its content. Care is taken not to suggest answers, so as not to bias the material. This information is essential for accurate scoring under systems such as the Comprehensive System of John Exner. The distractors are wrong: cards are never randomly shuffled, initial recorded exposure is the response phase, and scoring is a later analytic step. Because the enquiry phase is the detailed, non-leading clarification of each response, option b is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_073", section: "assessment",
    q: "Who developed the Thematic Apperception Test (TAT) and in which decade?",
    options: { a: "Sigmund Freud in the 1920s", b: "Henry A. Murray and Christina D. Morgan in the 1930s", c: "Carl Jung in the 1940s", d: "B.F. Skinner in the 1950s" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Sigmund Freud founded psychoanalysis but did not create the TAT; the theory influenced it, but he was not its author.", c: "Carl Jung developed the word association test and analytical psychology, not the TAT.", d: "B. F. Skinner was a behaviorist opposed to projective, unconscious-based methods; he did not create the TAT." },
    explanation: "The Thematic Apperception Test (TAT) is a projective personality assessment developed at the Harvard Psychological Clinic by Henry A. Murray and Christina D. Morgan, first described in the 1930s (published in 1935). The test presents a series of ambiguous pictures, mostly depicting people in social situations, and asks the respondent to construct a story for each: what is happening, what led up to it, what the characters are thinking and feeling, and how it will end. According to the projective hypothesis, the respondent projects their own needs, motives, and conflicts into the stories, revealing material below conscious awareness. Murray connected the TAT to his broader personology and his theory of psychogenic needs, and it became a central tool for assessing motives such as the need for achievement, later studied extensively by David McClelland. The distractors are wrong: Sigmund Freud influenced the projective tradition but did not create the TAT, Carl Jung devised the word association test, and B. F. Skinner rejected such unconscious-based methods. Because Murray and Morgan developed the TAT in the 1930s, option b is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_074", section: "assessment",
    q: "How many picture cards are usually used in a typical TAT assessment session?",
    options: { a: "1-3 cards", b: "5-12 cards", c: "20-31 cards", d: "Over 50 cards" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "One to three cards is too few to yield meaningful thematic data in a standard administration.", c: "Twenty to thirty-one is close to the full set of cards available, but a typical single session uses a selected subset, not all of them.", d: "Over fifty cards far exceeds the total number in the TAT set." },
    explanation: "The Thematic Apperception Test, developed by Henry Murray and Christina Morgan, comprises a full set of 31 picture cards (30 with images and one blank), some designated for particular ages and sexes. However, the full set is rarely administered in a single sitting. In typical clinical practice, the examiner selects a subset of cards judged most relevant to the individual and the referral question, and a usual session uses roughly 8 to 12 cards, which places the standard administration within the 5 to 12 range given in option b. Murray originally suggested administering about 20 cards across two sessions, but routine clinical use relies on a smaller, purposefully chosen selection to keep the session manageable while still eliciting rich thematic material. The respondent tells a story for each selected card, and the examiner analyzes recurring themes, needs, and conflicts. The distractors are off: 1 to 3 cards is too few, 20 to 31 approximates the entire set rather than a typical session, and over 50 exceeds the total available. Because a typical session uses roughly 5 to 12 cards, option b is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_075", section: "assessment",
    q: "What is the main purpose of analyzing the stories a participant tells during the TAT?",
    options: { a: "To measure IQ", b: "To reveal unconscious thoughts, conflicts, and motivations", c: "To assess vocabulary skills", d: "To test memory retention" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "The TAT does not measure IQ; intelligence is assessed by tests such as the WAIS.", c: "Vocabulary skills are assessed by language or intelligence subtests, not by TAT story analysis.", d: "Memory retention is assessed by memory tests, not by projective storytelling." },
    explanation: "The Thematic Apperception Test, developed by Henry Murray and Christina Morgan, is a projective technique, and the purpose of analyzing the stories a respondent constructs is to reveal unconscious needs, motives, conflicts, and emotions. The guiding assumption is the projective hypothesis: when interpreting an ambiguous social scene, people impose their own inner world onto it, so the themes of their stories (who the characters are, what they want, what obstacles they face, and how conflicts resolve) mirror the storyteller's own preoccupations. Murray identified the character with whom the respondent identifies (the hero) and analyzed the interplay of the hero's needs (press from the environment) to infer the person's dominant motives, such as the need for achievement, affiliation, or power, motives later measured systematically by David McClelland. The test thus taps material the respondent may not consciously report. The distractors misidentify the aim: the TAT does not measure IQ, vocabulary, or memory retention. Because analyzing TAT stories serves to reveal unconscious thoughts, conflicts, and motivations, option b is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_076", section: "assessment",
    q: "Which is a key difference between the TAT and the Rorschach Inkblot Test?",
    options: { a: "TAT uses ambiguous social scenes with characters; Rorschach uses symmetrical inkblots", b: "TAT measures IQ; Rorschach measures personality", c: "TAT requires yes/no answers; Rorschach requires storytelling", d: "Both tests are identical in method and purpose" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Neither test measures IQ; both are projective personality assessments, so this mischaracterizes the TAT.", c: "This reverses the tests: the TAT elicits storytelling, while the Rorschach elicits perceptual descriptions, and neither uses yes/no answers.", d: "The two tests differ in stimuli and response format, so they are not identical." },
    explanation: "Both the Thematic Apperception Test (TAT), developed by Henry Murray and Christina Morgan, and the Rorschach Inkblot Test, developed by Hermann Rorschach, are projective personality assessments built on the projective hypothesis that people reveal unconscious material when responding to ambiguous stimuli. Their key difference lies in the nature of the stimuli and the responses they elicit. The TAT presents relatively structured, ambiguous social scenes, pictures of people in situations, and asks the respondent to tell a narrative story about each, from which motives, needs, and conflicts are inferred. The Rorschach, by contrast, presents far more unstructured stimuli, bilaterally symmetrical inkblots, and asks the respondent to describe what they perceive, with scoring focused on the perceptual features (location, determinants, content) rather than narrative themes. Thus the TAT taps thematic content and motivation, while the Rorschach taps perception and thought organization. The distractors are false: neither measures IQ, neither uses yes/no answers (the TAT requires storytelling), and the two are not identical. Because the TAT uses ambiguous social scenes while the Rorschach uses symmetrical inkblots, option a is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_077", section: "stats",
    q: "Which scale of measurement involves categories without any order or ranking, such as car colors?",
    options: { a: "Nominal scale", b: "Ordinal scale", c: "Interval scale", d: "Ratio scale" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "An ordinal scale has ordered categories (rank), which the question explicitly excludes.", c: "An interval scale has ordered values with equal intervals but no true zero, more than mere unordered categories.", d: "A ratio scale has order, equal intervals, and a true zero, far beyond unordered categories." },
    explanation: "The four levels of measurement were formalized by the psychophysicist Stanley Smith Stevens in 1946, arranged in increasing order of mathematical sophistication: nominal, ordinal, interval, and ratio. A nominal scale is the most basic: it classifies data into distinct categories that have no inherent order, rank, or quantitative relationship. The categories merely name or label groups, such as car colors, gender, blood type, or nationality; numbers, if assigned, are just identifiers and cannot be meaningfully compared as greater or less. An ordinal scale adds rank order (for example, first, second, third, or a Likert agreement rating) but without equal intervals between ranks. An interval scale adds equal, meaningful intervals but has an arbitrary zero (for example, temperature in Celsius). A ratio scale adds a true, absolute zero, permitting statements about ratios (for example, weight, height, or reaction time). Because unordered categories such as car colors are the defining feature of the nominal scale, option a is correct, while the distractors describe higher, ordered levels of measurement." },
  { id: "rml25_078", section: "assessment",
    q: "What is psychometric reliability primarily concerned with?",
    options: { a: "Free from measurement error", b: "Consistency of a test", c: "Dependability of a test scores", d: "All of the above" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Freedom from measurement error is one aspect of reliability but not the whole concept, so it is incomplete on its own.", b: "Consistency is a central aspect of reliability but, taken alone, does not capture the full definition.", c: "Dependability of scores is another facet of reliability but is only one part of the complete answer." },
    explanation: "Psychometric reliability refers to the consistency, stability, and dependability of the scores a test yields, and it can be expressed in several complementary ways, all of which describe the same underlying property. In classical test theory, an observed score is modeled as a true score plus random error, and reliability is the degree to which a test is free from such measurement error (option a), which is one legitimate way to define it. Reliability is equally described as the consistency of a test across occasions, items, or raters (option b), captured by test-retest, internal consistency such as coefficient alpha of Lee Cronbach, and inter-rater reliability. And it is described as the dependability of the scores (option c), meaning one can trust that similar conditions will yield similar results. Because freedom from error, consistency, and dependability are all valid and overlapping characterizations of the single concept of reliability, the most complete and correct answer encompasses all of them. Therefore option d, all of the above, is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_079", section: "assessment",
    q: "In neuropsychological assessments, the Ideometric Approach primarily focuses on:",
    options: { a: "Evaluating patient's premorbid functioning and current status", b: "Quantitative analysis of standardized test scores", c: "Comparison with normative population data", d: "Assessment of emotional and psychological well-being" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Quantitative analysis against fixed norms describes the psychometric or nomothetic approach, not the individualized ideometric one.", c: "Comparison with normative population data is the nomothetic (norm-referenced) approach, the contrast to the ideometric focus on the individual.", d: "Assessment of emotional well-being is one component of evaluation but not the defining focus of the ideometric approach." },
    explanation: "The ideometric (idiographic) approach in neuropsychological assessment focuses on the individual patient as a unique case, comparing the person to themselves rather than to a group. Its central aim is to estimate the patient's premorbid level of functioning, that is, their abilities before injury or illness, and to compare that baseline with their current status in order to gauge the specific pattern and magnitude of decline. This within-person comparison reveals how much and in which domains a particular individual has changed, which is essential when a person's own baseline differs from the population average. It contrasts with the nomothetic (psychometric, norm-referenced) approach, which compares the patient's scores against normative population data to see where they fall relative to others; that norm-based, quantitative comparison is described by options b and c. The idiographic-nomothetic distinction traces to the personality theorist Gordon Allport. Option d, emotional well-being, is a component of assessment but not the defining focus. Because the ideometric approach centers on premorbid functioning versus current status within the individual, option a is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_080", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Which cognitive disorder is correctly matched with its primary function affected: Dementia, Amnesia, Aphasia, Apraxia?",
    options: { a: "Dementia - Abstraction; Amnesia - Memory; Aphasia - Language; Apraxia - Motor skills", b: "Dementia - Memory; Amnesia - Language; Aphasia - Motor skills; Apraxia - Abstraction", c: "Dementia - Motor skills; Amnesia - Memory; Aphasia - Abstraction; Apraxia - Language", d: "Dementia - Language; Amnesia - Motor skills; Aphasia - Memory; Apraxia – Abstraction" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "This mismatches amnesia with language and aphasia with motor skills, both of which are wrong.", c: "This mismatches dementia with motor skills and aphasia with abstraction, both incorrect.", d: "This mismatches every pairing, assigning language to dementia, motor skills to amnesia, memory to aphasia, and abstraction to apraxia." },
    explanation: "Each of these four neurocognitive conditions is defined by the specific function it impairs. Dementia is a broad, acquired, and usually progressive decline in multiple cognitive domains, prominently including higher-order abstraction, reasoning, and judgment, along with memory and other functions. Amnesia is a specific impairment of memory, the inability to form new memories (anterograde) or to recall past ones (retrograde), classically studied in patient H.M. by Brenda Milner and linked to hippocampal damage. Aphasia is an acquired impairment of language, affecting the production or comprehension of speech and writing, arising from damage to language regions such as the areas identified by Paul Broca and Carl Wernicke. Apraxia is the loss of the ability to carry out learned, purposeful motor movements despite intact strength and comprehension, a disorder of skilled motor planning. Option a matches each correctly: dementia with abstraction, amnesia with memory, aphasia with language, and apraxia with motor skills. Because only option a pairs every disorder with its correct primary function, it is the correct answer." },
  { id: "rml25_081", section: "assessment",
    q: "Which of these is NOT typically assessed by neuropsychological tests?",
    options: { a: "Opinion", b: "Language", c: "Memory", d: "Executive functioning" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Language is a core cognitive domain routinely assessed in neuropsychological testing.", c: "Memory is one of the most central functions evaluated in neuropsychological assessment.", d: "Executive functioning (planning, inhibition, flexibility) is a key domain of neuropsychological testing." },
    explanation: "Neuropsychological tests evaluate the cognitive domains that depend on brain integrity, in order to detect, localize, and characterize the effects of brain injury or disease. The standard domains assessed include attention and concentration, memory (both verbal and visual), language (naming, comprehension, fluency), visuospatial and perceptual abilities, motor and sensory function, processing speed, and executive functioning, the higher-order control processes such as planning, reasoning, cognitive flexibility, and response inhibition mediated largely by the frontal lobes. Language, memory, and executive functioning (options b, c, and d) are therefore all core targets of neuropsychological assessment. Opinion, however, is not a cognitive domain at all: it refers to a personal belief, attitude, or evaluation, which is a matter for attitude scales or interviews rather than objective tests of brain-based cognitive capacity. Neuropsychological testing measures abilities and performance, not subjective opinions. Because opinion is not a cognitive function assessed by neuropsychological tests while language, memory, and executive functioning are, option a is the correct exception." },
  { id: "rml25_082", section: "assessment",
    q: "What is the main advantage of projective tests, like the Rorschach inkblot test?",
    options: { a: "They allow people to use defence mechanisms of projection", b: "They avoid self-report biases", c: "They have superior re-test reliability", d: "They provide numerical quantitative data" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Eliciting projection is the mechanism by which the tests work, but the advantage that mechanism provides is bypassing conscious self-report distortion.", c: "Projective tests are actually criticized for weaker, not superior, reliability, so this is false.", d: "Providing numerical quantitative data is a strength of objective tests, not the main advantage of projective ones." },
    explanation: "Projective tests such as the Rorschach Inkblot Test of Hermann Rorschach and the Thematic Apperception Test of Henry Murray present ambiguous stimuli onto which respondents project their unconscious needs and conflicts. Their main advantage over structured, objective self-report inventories is that they are less susceptible to self-report biases such as social desirability, faking good or bad, and deliberate defensiveness. Because the purpose of an ambiguous stimulus is not obvious to the respondent, there is no clearly correct or socially desirable answer to give, so people are less able to consciously manage the impression they create, and material they would not or could not report directly can emerge. This capacity to bypass conscious distortion is precisely what proponents value. The distractors are wrong or reversed: eliciting projection is the working mechanism rather than the benefit itself; projective tests are actually criticized for poorer reliability and validity, not superior retest reliability; and it is objective tests, not projective ones, that yield clean numerical data. Because avoiding self-report biases is the main advantage of projective tests, option b is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_083", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Match the following cognitive functions with their associated neurological disorders: A) Abstraction, B) Memory, C) Language, D) Motor skills.",
    options: { a: "A-I Dementia; B-II Amnesia; C-III Aphasia, Alexia, Acalculia; D-IV Apraxia", b: "A-II Amnesia; B-III Aphasia; C-I Dementia; D-IV Apraxia", c: "A-IV Apraxia; B-II Amnesia; C-I Aphasia; D-I Dementia", d: "A-IV Apraxia; B-III Aphasia; C-II Amnesia; D-I Dementia" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "This wrongly links memory with aphasia and abstraction with amnesia.", c: "This mismatches abstraction with apraxia and misassigns dementia to motor skills.", d: "This mismatches every pairing, tying abstraction to apraxia, memory to aphasia, language to amnesia, and motor skills to dementia." },
    explanation: "This item asks to match cognitive functions with the disorders that impair them, the same underlying pairings as the disorder-function question. Abstraction, the higher-order reasoning that lets a person grasp concepts and general principles, is prominently affected in dementia, a global cognitive decline. Memory is impaired in amnesia, the selective loss of the ability to encode or retrieve information, associated with hippocampal and medial temporal damage as in patient H.M. studied by Brenda Milner. Language is impaired in aphasia, and related left-hemisphere syndromes affect associated symbolic skills, so aphasia often co-occurs with alexia (loss of reading) and acalculia (loss of calculation), linked to the language areas of Paul Broca and Carl Wernicke. Motor skills, specifically the execution of learned purposeful movements, are impaired in apraxia, a disorder of motor planning despite intact strength. Option a correctly maps abstraction to dementia, memory to amnesia, language to aphasia (with alexia and acalculia), and motor skills to apraxia. Because only option a aligns every function with its correct disorder, it is the correct answer." },
  { id: "rml25_084", section: "stats",
    q: "Which scale is used when counting the number of books a person has?",
    options: { a: "Nominal Scale", b: "Ordinal Scale", c: "Interval Scale", d: "Ratio Scale" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "A nominal scale labels unordered categories; a count of books is a true quantity, not a mere category.", b: "An ordinal scale gives rank order without equal intervals or a true zero; a book count has both.", c: "An interval scale lacks a true zero, but zero books genuinely means the absence of books, so this is not interval." },
    explanation: "Choosing the measurement scale, per the taxonomy of Stanley Smith Stevens, depends on the properties of the variable. Counting the number of books a person owns produces a ratio scale variable. A ratio scale has all the properties of the lower scales, distinct categories (nominal), meaningful order (ordinal), and equal intervals between values (interval), plus the crucial additional feature of a true, absolute zero point. Zero books literally means the complete absence of books, a genuine and meaningful zero, not an arbitrary reference point. This true zero is what permits ratio statements: someone with 20 books has twice as many as someone with 10, and such multiplicative comparisons are valid only on a ratio scale. Counts of discrete objects, along with weight, height, age, and reaction time, are classic ratio-scale variables. The distractors fail because a nominal scale has no quantity, an ordinal scale has no equal intervals, and an interval scale (such as Celsius temperature) lacks a true zero. Because a book count has a meaningful zero and supports ratios, option d is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_085", section: "assessment",
    q: "Which domains are assessed by neuropsychological tests?",
    options: { a: "Language, Memory, Executive Functioning", b: "Opinion, Language, Reaction", c: "Memory, Reaction, Opinion", d: "Executive Functioning, Opinion, Reaction" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "This list includes opinion, which is not a neuropsychological cognitive domain.", c: "This list includes opinion, disqualifying it as a set of assessed domains.", d: "This list includes opinion, so it cannot be the correct combination of assessed domains." },
    explanation: "Neuropsychological assessment evaluates the cognitive functions that depend on brain integrity, in order to detect and characterize brain dysfunction. Core domains routinely assessed include attention and concentration, memory, language, visuospatial ability, processing speed, and executive functioning, the higher-order control processes of planning, reasoning, inhibition, and cognitive flexibility associated largely with the frontal lobes. Option a lists language, memory, and executive functioning, all three of which are genuine and central neuropsychological domains, making it the correct set. Every distractor (b, c, and d) contains opinion, and opinion is not a cognitive domain: it is a personal belief, attitude, or evaluation, which belongs to the province of attitude scales and interviews, not objective cognitive testing. This makes any option including opinion incorrect. Reaction time is a legitimately measured variable (part of processing speed), but its inclusion in the distractors is always paired with opinion, which invalidates those choices. Because only option a lists three authentic neuropsychological domains without the spurious opinion, it is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_086", section: "assessment",
    q: "Psychometric reliability refers to the degree to which a test is:",
    options: { a: "Free from measurement error", b: "Consistent", c: "Dependable", d: "All of the above" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Freedom from measurement error is a valid facet of reliability but not the complete definition by itself.", b: "Consistency is central to reliability but is only one of the ways it is expressed.", c: "Dependability is another correct facet but, alone, does not capture the whole concept." },
    explanation: "Psychometric reliability is the property of a test that concerns the consistency and trustworthiness of its scores, and it can be legitimately expressed in several equivalent ways. In classical test theory, which models an observed score as a true score plus random error, reliability is the degree to which a test is free from measurement error (option a): the smaller the error variance relative to true variance, the more reliable the test. Reliability is also defined as consistency (option b), the tendency of a test to yield stable, repeatable results across time (test-retest), across items (internal consistency, indexed by coefficient alpha of Lee Cronbach), and across raters (inter-rater reliability). And it is described as dependability (option c), meaning the scores can be trusted to behave predictably under similar conditions. These are three overlapping descriptions of one and the same underlying quality rather than mutually exclusive alternatives. Because freedom from error, consistency, and dependability are all correct facets of reliability, the most complete answer is all of the above. Therefore option d is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_087", section: "general",
    q: "What is the primary focus of Indian psychology?",
    options: { a: "The application of Western psychological theories exclusively.", b: "The promotion of meditation without psychological context.", c: "The study of Indian folklore and mythology.", d: "The integration of Indian philosophical traditions with psychological concepts." },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Indian psychology is defined precisely by drawing on indigenous traditions, not by applying Western theories exclusively.", b: "It situates practices such as meditation within a rich psychological and philosophical framework, not without context.", c: "It is grounded in philosophical and contemplative traditions, not merely folklore and mythology." },
    explanation: "Indian psychology is an approach to psychology that draws on the classical Indian philosophical and contemplative traditions, such as the Vedas, Upanishads, the Yoga system of Patanjali, Samkhya, Buddhism, and the Bhagavad Gita, and integrates their concepts and methods with modern psychological inquiry. Its primary focus is the systematic study of mind, consciousness, self (atman), and human potential as understood within these indigenous traditions, and the application of that understanding to psychological questions. It emphasizes consciousness, self-realization, and holistic, spiritually informed models of well-being, and it treats practices such as meditation and yoga not as isolated techniques but as embedded within a coherent theory of mind and liberation. Scholars such as Girishwar Misra, K. Ramakrishna Rao, and R. M. Matthijs Cornelissen have helped articulate it as a distinct field. The distractors mischaracterize it: it does not apply only Western theories, it does not promote meditation without context, and it is not merely the study of folklore. Because Indian psychology is defined by integrating Indian philosophical traditions with psychological concepts, option d is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_088", section: "general",
    q: "How does Indian psychology differ from Western psychology?",
    options: { a: "It solely relies on ancient texts without modern relevance.", b: "Western psychology focuses entirely on spirituality and community.", c: "Indian psychology emphasizes holistic and spiritual approaches including community dynamics.", d: "Both emphasize individualism equally." },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Indian psychology is not confined to ancient texts; it applies traditional insights to contemporary concerns, so this is a caricature.", b: "This reverses reality: it is Western psychology that leans empirical and individualist, not spiritual and communal.", d: "Western psychology tends toward individualism while Indian psychology is more collectivist, so they do not emphasize individualism equally." },
    explanation: "Indian psychology differs from mainstream Western psychology chiefly in its worldview and emphasis. Western psychology, growing out of the empirical and experimental tradition since Wilhelm Wundt, tends to be materialist, empirical, analytic, and oriented toward the individual, often studying discrete mental processes in isolation. Indian psychology, by contrast, is holistic and spiritually grounded: it treats mind, body, consciousness, and spirit as integrated, foregrounds the transformation and self-realization of the whole person, and situates the individual within a web of social and community relationships, reflecting the more collectivist orientation of Indian culture. It draws on contemplative traditions and first-person methods (such as introspective and meditative inquiry) alongside third-person observation. Thus it emphasizes holistic and spiritual approaches that also account for community and relational dynamics, which is exactly option c. The distractors are wrong: Indian psychology is not merely ancient text without modern relevance, Western psychology is not entirely spiritual and communal (that reverses the truth), and the two do not emphasize individualism equally. Because option c correctly captures the holistic, spiritual, community-inclusive emphasis, it is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_089", section: "general",
    q: "Who is considered the father of Indian psychology?",
    options: { a: "Narendra Nath Sen Gupta", b: "Sudhir Kakar", c: "Giriswar Misra", d: "G. R. S. Rao" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Narendra Nath Sen Gupta is often called the father of modern (scientific, academic) Indian psychology, founding the first department, but the keyed answer distinguishes Indian psychology as a tradition-based field.", b: "Sudhir Kakar is a noted psychoanalyst known for applying psychoanalysis to Indian culture, not the founding father of the field.", c: "Giriswar Misra is a prominent contemporary contributor to Indian psychology, not its founding father." },
    explanation: "This question concerns the founding figure of Indian psychology, and the answer keyed by the examiners is G. R. S. Rao (option d). It is worth noting the surrounding context, because the phrase father of Indian psychology is used in more than one sense. Narendra Nath Sen Gupta is frequently credited as the father of modern, scientific psychology in India: he earned a doctorate under Hugo Munsterberg at Harvard and established the first psychology department and laboratory at the University of Calcutta in 1916, launching academic psychology in India. Sudhir Kakar is a celebrated psychoanalyst who applied psychoanalytic thought to Indian culture and identity, and Giriswar Misra is a leading contemporary scholar advancing indigenous Indian psychology. The examiners, however, keyed G. R. S. Rao as the father of Indian psychology in the sense intended by this paper. Because the official answer key marks option d, that is the required response, and the distractors, while genuinely important figures, are not the keyed founder. Therefore option d is the keyed answer." },
  { id: "rml25_090", section: "general",
    q: "What is the concept of 'Atman' in Indian psychology?",
    options: { a: "The physical body.", b: "The true self or soul representing eternal consciousness.", c: "The collective unconscious of society.", d: "A temporary state of mind." },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Atman is distinguished precisely from the physical body; it is the eternal self, not the perishable body.", c: "The collective unconscious is a concept of Carl Jung, not the Indian notion of atman.", d: "Atman is permanent and unchanging, the opposite of a temporary state of mind." },
    explanation: "Atman is a central concept in Indian philosophy and Indian psychology, drawn especially from the Upanishads and Vedanta. It denotes the true self, the innermost essence or soul, understood as pure, eternal, and unchanging consciousness that underlies and witnesses all mental and physical experience. Atman is sharply distinguished from the perishable physical body and from the fluctuating contents of the mind (thoughts, emotions, sensations); it is the constant subject that observes these transient phenomena without being reducible to them. In the non-dualistic (Advaita Vedanta) tradition articulated by Adi Shankara, the atman is ultimately identical with Brahman, the universal absolute consciousness, and realizing this identity is the goal of self-knowledge and liberation (moksha). This makes atman the metaphysical ground of the self in Indian thought. The distractors misidentify it: the physical body is precisely what atman is not, the collective unconscious is a Western concept of Carl Jung, and a temporary state of mind contradicts atman's permanence. Because atman is the true self or soul representing eternal consciousness, option b is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_091", section: "general",
    q: "How does Indian psychology address mental health issues?",
    options: { a: "Solely relies on Western methods for treatment.", b: "Does not address mental health problems.", c: "Focuses only on medication, ignoring traditional practices.", d: "Combines traditional practices and modern therapy holistically." },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Indian psychology does not rely solely on Western methods; it draws on indigenous traditions, so this is false.", b: "It actively addresses mental health and well-being, so claiming it does not is incorrect.", c: "It emphasizes traditional practices such as yoga and meditation rather than focusing only on medication." },
    explanation: "Indian psychology addresses mental health through a holistic, integrative framework that combines traditional Indian practices with contemporary therapeutic methods. Rather than treating symptoms in isolation, it aims at the well-being of the whole person, mind, body, and spirit, and draws on indigenous techniques such as yoga (including the eight-limbed path of Patanjali), meditation and mindfulness, pranayama (breath regulation), and value-based living derived from texts like the Bhagavad Gita, while remaining open to integrating these with modern psychotherapy and, where appropriate, clinical treatment. This integrative stance is increasingly reflected in mainstream practice: mindfulness-based interventions, for instance, have been adapted into Western therapies such as mindfulness-based cognitive therapy. The emphasis is on cultivating self-awareness, inner balance, and self-realization as routes to lasting well-being, complementing rather than replacing conventional care. The distractors are wrong: Indian psychology neither relies solely on Western methods, nor ignores mental health, nor focuses only on medication. Because it combines traditional practices with modern therapy holistically, option d is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_092", section: "assessment",
    q: "Which of the following psychologists is associated with the development of the first intelligence test?",
    options: { a: "Freud", b: "Piaget", c: "Binet", d: "Skinner" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Sigmund Freud founded psychoanalysis and had nothing to do with intelligence testing.", b: "Jean Piaget studied cognitive development in children, not the creation of the first intelligence test (though he briefly worked in Binet's lab).", d: "B. F. Skinner was a behaviorist focused on operant conditioning, not intelligence testing." },
    explanation: "The first practical intelligence test was developed by the French psychologist Alfred Binet, together with his collaborator Theodore Simon. In 1905 the French government commissioned Binet to devise a way to identify schoolchildren who needed special educational assistance, and the resulting Binet-Simon Scale measured children's performance on tasks of judgment, comprehension, and reasoning, introducing the notion of mental age, the age level at which a child was performing. This scale was the foundation of modern intelligence testing. It was later revised by Lewis Terman at Stanford University into the Stanford-Binet Intelligence Scale, which adopted the intelligence quotient (IQ), a term from William Stern, computed as mental age divided by chronological age times 100. The distractors are unrelated to this achievement: Sigmund Freud founded psychoanalysis, Jean Piaget studied stages of cognitive development (though he briefly worked in Binet's laboratory), and B. F. Skinner pioneered operant conditioning. Because Alfred Binet developed the first intelligence test, option c is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_093", section: "development",
    q: "According to Piaget's theory of cognitive development, the stage characterized by abstract thought is the:",
    options: { a: "Sensorimotor stage", b: "Preoperational stage", c: "Concrete operational stage", d: "Formal operational stage" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "The sensorimotor stage (birth to about 2 years) involves learning through senses and actions, not abstract thought.", b: "The preoperational stage (about 2 to 7 years) features symbolic thinking and egocentrism but lacks logical operations and abstraction.", c: "The concrete operational stage (about 7 to 11 years) allows logical reasoning about concrete objects but not yet abstract or hypothetical reasoning." },
    explanation: "Jean Piaget proposed a stage theory of cognitive development in which children pass through four qualitatively distinct stages in a fixed order. The sensorimotor stage (birth to about 2 years) is when infants understand the world through their senses and motor actions and develop object permanence. The preoperational stage (about 2 to 7 years) brings symbolic thought and language but is marked by egocentrism and a lack of conservation. The concrete operational stage (about 7 to 11 years) enables logical operations such as conservation, classification, and reversibility, but only about concrete, tangible objects and situations. The formal operational stage (from about 11 or 12 years onward) is the final stage, defined by the capacity for abstract, hypothetical, and deductive reasoning: the adolescent can reason about possibilities, form and test hypotheses, and think systematically about abstract concepts not tied to concrete reality. Because abstract thought is the defining achievement of the formal operational stage, option d is correct, while the earlier stages lack this capacity." },
  { id: "rml25_094", section: "stats",
    q: "Which statistical test is most appropriate to compare means between two independent groups?",
    options: { a: "Chi-square test", b: "Paired t-test", c: "Independent samples t-test", d: "ANOVA" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "The chi-square test analyzes categorical frequency data, not the comparison of two group means.", b: "The paired t-test compares means from related or matched samples (the same subjects measured twice), not two independent groups.", d: "ANOVA compares means across three or more groups; for exactly two independent groups the t-test is the appropriate and more specific choice." },
    explanation: "Selecting a statistical test depends on the type of data and the design. To compare the means of a continuous outcome between two separate, unrelated groups (for example, a treatment group versus a control group made up of different people), the appropriate test is the independent samples t-test, also called the two-sample or unpaired t-test, which builds on the work of William Sealy Gosset, who published under the pseudonym Student. It tests whether the difference between the two group means is larger than would be expected by chance, assuming roughly normal distributions and comparable variances. The distractors suit other situations: the chi-square test, associated with Karl Pearson, analyzes associations between categorical variables using frequency counts, not means; the paired t-test compares two related measurements from the same or matched subjects (such as before and after); and analysis of variance (ANOVA), developed by Ronald Fisher, compares means across three or more groups (for two groups it reduces to the t-test). Because comparing means between two independent groups calls for the independent samples t-test, option c is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_095", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Which of the following brain structures is primarily responsible for regulating emotions such as fear and aggression?",
    options: { a: "Hippocampus", b: "Thalamus", c: "Amygdala", d: "Cerebellum" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "The hippocampus is central to forming new long-term memories, not primarily to regulating fear and aggression.", b: "The thalamus is a sensory relay station directing signals to the cortex, not the primary emotion regulator.", d: "The cerebellum coordinates movement and balance, not emotional regulation." },
    explanation: "The amygdala, an almond-shaped structure within the temporal lobes and part of the limbic system described by Paul MacLean, is the brain structure most directly responsible for processing and regulating emotions, particularly fear and aggression. It rapidly evaluates incoming stimuli for threat and triggers appropriate emotional and physiological responses, including the fight-or-flight reaction. Research by Joseph LeDoux traced the neural circuitry of fear, showing a fast, direct thalamo-amygdala pathway that allows the amygdala to respond to danger before the conscious cortex fully processes it. Classic lesion studies, including the Kluver-Bucy syndrome produced by removing the temporal lobes in monkeys, demonstrated that amygdala damage abolishes normal fear and can blunt aggression, confirming its central role. The distractors serve other functions: the hippocampus, also in the limbic system, is essential for forming new declarative memories; the thalamus is the brain's sensory relay hub; and the cerebellum coordinates movement, posture, and balance. Because the amygdala is primarily responsible for regulating fear and aggression, option c is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_096", section: "psychopathology",
    q: "Which defense mechanism involves attributing one's own unacceptable impulses to others?",
    options: { a: "Displacement", b: "Projection", c: "Sublimation", d: "Reaction formation" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Displacement redirects an impulse from a threatening target to a safer substitute; it does not attribute the impulse to others.", c: "Sublimation channels unacceptable impulses into socially acceptable activity, not onto other people.", d: "Reaction formation converts an unacceptable impulse into its exaggerated opposite, not into an attribution to others." },
    explanation: "Defense mechanisms are unconscious ego strategies, described by Sigmund Freud and systematized by Anna Freud, that reduce the anxiety produced by unacceptable impulses. Projection is the mechanism in which a person unconsciously attributes their own unacceptable thoughts, feelings, or impulses to another person. By externalizing the impulse, the person disowns it and reduces internal anxiety; for example, someone who harbors forbidden hostility may perceive others as being hostile toward them. This is distinct from the other listed defenses. Displacement redirects an emotional impulse away from its original, threatening target toward a safer substitute (such as kicking the dog after a scolding at work). Sublimation transforms unacceptable impulses into socially valued and constructive activity, and is regarded as the most mature defense. Reaction formation converts an unacceptable impulse into its exact opposite in exaggerated form (such as treating someone one dislikes with excessive kindness). Because attributing one's own unacceptable impulses to others is precisely the definition of projection, option b is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_097", section: "stats",
    q: "In research ethics, obtaining participants' permission after informing them about the study's purpose and procedures is called:",
    options: { a: "Debriefing", b: "Consent form", c: "Confidentiality", d: "Informed consent" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Debriefing occurs after the study, explaining its true nature and addressing any deception, not obtaining permission to participate.", b: "A consent form is the physical document used to record consent, but the ethical principle and process being described is informed consent.", c: "Confidentiality is the protection of participants' private data, a distinct ethical obligation, not the process of obtaining permission." },
    explanation: "Informed consent is a foundational principle of research ethics requiring that, before participating, individuals be given clear information about the study's purpose, procedures, potential risks and benefits, and their right to withdraw at any time without penalty, and that they then voluntarily agree to take part. The emphasis on informed reflects the requirement that agreement be based on genuine understanding, and the emphasis on consent reflects that it be freely and voluntarily given, without coercion. This principle grew out of research-ethics landmarks such as the Nuremberg Code and the Belmont Report, which enshrined respect for persons and autonomy. The distractors name related but distinct concepts: debriefing is the post-study process of explaining the study's true nature and correcting any deception; a consent form is merely the document that records consent, not the underlying principle; and confidentiality is the ethical duty to protect participants' private information. Because obtaining voluntary permission after fully informing participants is precisely informed consent, option d is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_098", section: "psychopathology",
    q: "In abnormal psychology, the diathesis-stress model posits that mental disorders result from:",
    options: { a: "Purely biological causes", b: "Learned experiences alone", c: "A combination of vulnerability and stress", d: "Supernatural influences" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Purely biological causation is a one-factor medical view; the diathesis-stress model is explicitly interactive, not purely biological.", b: "Learned experiences alone reflects a strict behavioral view, but the diathesis-stress model integrates predisposition with environmental stress.", d: "Supernatural influences reflect a pre-scientific demonological model, not the diathesis-stress framework." },
    explanation: "The diathesis-stress model is an integrative framework in abnormal psychology explaining how mental disorders develop from the interaction of two factors. A diathesis is a predisposition or vulnerability to a disorder, which may be genetic, biological, or psychological, essentially a latent susceptibility a person carries. Stress refers to environmental, life, or situational demands and adversities that challenge the person. According to the model, neither factor alone is sufficient: a disorder emerges when a person with a given vulnerability encounters sufficient stress to trigger it. Individuals with a high diathesis may develop a disorder under relatively mild stress, whereas those with low vulnerability may remain well even under substantial stress. This interactionist view moves beyond single-cause explanations and underlies the modern biopsychosocial understanding of psychopathology, associated with the work of theorists such as Paul Meehl on schizophrenia. The distractors each isolate one factor or invoke a pre-scientific cause: pure biology, pure learning, or the supernatural. Because the diathesis-stress model holds that disorders result from the combination of vulnerability and stress, option c is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_099", section: "biopsych",
    q: "In classical conditioning, a neutral stimulus becomes a conditioned stimulus when:",
    options: { a: "It is repeatedly paired with an unconditioned stimulus", b: "It is reinforced with a reward", c: "It is associated with a punishment", d: "It occurs randomly during learning" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Reinforcement with a reward is the mechanism of operant conditioning, not classical conditioning.", c: "Association with punishment likewise concerns operant conditioning, not the pairing that defines classical conditioning.", d: "Random occurrence prevents the systematic pairing needed to form a conditioned association." },
    explanation: "Classical conditioning, discovered by the Russian physiologist Ivan Pavlov, is a form of associative learning in which a previously neutral stimulus comes to elicit a response after being paired with a stimulus that naturally produces that response. The key terms are: the unconditioned stimulus (UCS), which automatically triggers an unlearned unconditioned response (UCR), such as food (UCS) producing salivation (UCR); and the neutral stimulus, such as a bell, which initially elicits no relevant response. When the neutral stimulus is repeatedly paired with the unconditioned stimulus, it gradually becomes a conditioned stimulus (CS) that, on its own, elicits a learned conditioned response (CR), the bell alone eventually producing salivation. The pairing of the neutral stimulus with the UCS is therefore the essential mechanism, and factors such as contingency and timing (contiguity) matter. The distractors describe operant conditioning of B. F. Skinner (reinforcement with reward, association with punishment) or a condition that prevents learning (random occurrence). Because a neutral stimulus becomes a conditioned stimulus through repeated pairing with an unconditioned stimulus, option a is correct." },
  { id: "rml25_100", section: "development",
    q: "A researcher is studying the same group of individuals over a 10-year period. This design is called:",
    options: { a: "Cross-sectional design", b: "Case-control design", c: "Longitudinal design", d: "Experimental design" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "A cross-sectional design studies different age groups at a single point in time, not the same group over years.", b: "A case-control design compares groups with and without an outcome retrospectively; it does not follow one group over time.", d: "An experimental design involves manipulating an independent variable, which the observational follow-up described does not require." },
    explanation: "A longitudinal design is a research method in which the same individuals are observed and measured repeatedly over an extended period, in this case ten years, to study change and development over time. Its major strength is that, by following the same people, it can track individual growth trajectories and examine how variables change within persons, allowing stronger inferences about the sequence of developmental change; its drawbacks include high cost, long duration, and attrition (participant dropout). It contrasts most directly with the cross-sectional design, which compares different groups (often different ages) at a single point in time; cross-sectional studies are faster but cannot separate developmental change from cohort differences. A case-control design retrospectively compares individuals who have an outcome with those who do not, and an experimental design involves the deliberate manipulation of an independent variable with random assignment. Because studying the same group of individuals over a ten-year span is the defining feature of a longitudinal design, option c is correct." }
],
  "ihbas-2020": [
  { id: "ihbas20_001", section: "general",
    q: "The visible spectrum wavelength ranges from",
    options: { a: "380 to 760 nanometres", b: "200 to 700 nanometres", c: "400 to 1000 nanometres", d: "100 to 1000 nanometres" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "200 to 700 nm pulls the lower bound down into the ultraviolet, which the cornea and lens absorb and which we cannot see, so this range is too wide on the short end.", c: "400 to 1000 nm extends well into the infrared region beyond about 760 nm, which we perceive as warmth rather than light, so the upper bound is wrong.", d: "100 to 1000 nm spans deep ultraviolet through infrared and is far too broad, covering radiation our photoreceptors cannot transduce at either extreme." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is roughly 380 to 760 nanometres because this is the narrow band of electromagnetic radiation that the human eye can actually detect and convert into neural signals. Light is one form of electromagnetic energy, and the full spectrum runs from very short gamma and X rays through ultraviolet, then the visible band, then infrared, microwaves and radio waves. What makes the visible band special is purely biological: the photopigments in our rods and cones, especially the three cone opsins, absorb photons only within this range, so wavelengths outside it never trigger a visual response. Wavelength maps onto the experience of colour. The short end near 380 to 400 nm is seen as violet and blue, the middle around 500 to 565 nm as green and yellow, and the long end near 700 to 760 nm as red. Below 380 nm lies ultraviolet, which carries enough energy to damage tissue but is filtered by the cornea and lens, and above 760 nm lies infrared, which we feel as heat rather than see. Different texts quote slightly different cut offs, often 400 to 700 nm, but the classic psychophysics figure given in introductory psychology is 380 to 760 nm, so that is the keyed answer. The key idea to carry into the exam is that vision is a transduction process limited by the absorption properties of our photoreceptors, not by the physics of light itself." },
  { id: "ihbas20_002", section: "therapy",
    q: "'Healing and Persuasion' is the work of:",
    options: { a: "Alfred Pritz", b: "Jerome Frank", c: "Charles Winnicot", d: "Jeff Klerman" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Alfred Pritz is an Austrian psychotherapy figure associated with the World Council for Psychotherapy and the professionalisation of the field, not the author of Persuasion and Healing.", c: "Charles Winnicot is a distractor; the real Donald Winnicott was a British object relations analyst known for the good enough mother and transitional objects, not this book.", d: "Jeff (Gerald) Klerman, with Weissman, developed Interpersonal Psychotherapy for depression, a specific manualised treatment, not the common factors thesis of this work." },
    explanation: "The keyed answer is Jerome Frank, the American psychiatrist whose book Persuasion and Healing (first published 1961, the question phrases it as Healing and Persuasion) is one of the most influential analyses of what makes all psychotherapies work. Frank argued that the many competing schools of therapy share common ingredients rather than owing their success to their specific techniques. He proposed that every form of healing, from shamanic ritual to modern psychotherapy, contains four shared features: an emotionally charged, confiding relationship with a helping person; a healing setting that carries social sanction; a rationale or myth that provides a plausible explanation for the patient's distress; and a ritual or procedure that both believe will restore health. The unifying mechanism, in Frank's view, is the remoralization of a demoralized person, the restoration of hope and a sense of mastery. This anticipated what we now call the common factors model of psychotherapy and the placebo or expectancy literature, and it directly informs debates about whether specific techniques outperform the therapeutic alliance. For an entrance exam this is a classic name and book pairing, and the deeper point is that Frank reframed therapy outcome as a question of shared persuasive and relational processes rather than rival doctrines." },
  { id: "ihbas20_003", section: "therapy",
    q: "A stimulus that is constantly present, or paired with, the absence of a noxious stimulus may become a:",
    options: { a: "Safety signal", b: "Avoidance cue", c: "Relief stimulus", d: "Readiness prompt" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "An avoidance cue would prompt escape from danger, but the question describes a stimulus paired with the absence of a noxious event, which signals safety rather than triggering avoidance.", c: "Relief stimulus is not the standard technical term; relief is the emotional state following the removal of an aversive event, not the predictive stimulus itself.", d: "Readiness prompt is not a recognised conditioning term and does not capture the idea of a learned predictor of the absence of harm." },
    explanation: "The correct term is safety signal. In learning theory a safety signal is a stimulus that reliably predicts the absence of an aversive or noxious event. Because it is consistently paired with the non occurrence of danger, the organism comes to treat it as a marker of safety, and its presence reduces fear and inhibits avoidance responding. The concept comes out of the two factor and conditioning literature on avoidance learning, especially work by Mowrer and later by Seligman and others. Imagine a rat that is shocked unless a tone is on; the tone signals that no shock is coming, so the rat relaxes when it hears the tone. Clinically this matters a great deal for anxiety disorders. People with panic disorder or agoraphobia often carry safety signals such as a bottle of water, a companion, a mobile phone, or a seat near the exit, and the close cousin concept of safety behaviours describes the actions they perform to feel protected. The problem is that safety signals and safety behaviours prevent the disconfirmation of catastrophic beliefs: because the feared catastrophe does not happen, the person attributes survival to the safety signal rather than learning that the situation was never truly dangerous. This is why exposure based treatments deliberately drop safety signals so that genuine new learning, that the feared outcome does not occur, can take place." },
  { id: "ihbas20_004", section: "dev",
    q: "The fact that people can use later experiences to compensate for earlier deficits is a contradiction of which element of earlier developmental psychology?",
    options: { a: "Critical periods", b: "Continuity", c: "Sensitive periods", d: "Stability" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Continuity refers to whether development is gradual and quantitative versus stage like; compensating for early deficits is about reversibility of timing, not the smoothness of change.", c: "Sensitive periods is the concept that already accommodates later compensation, so catch up does not contradict it; it actually fits, which is why it is not the contradicted element.", d: "Stability concerns whether traits persist across the lifespan, a separate continuity issue, not the time bounded irreversibility being challenged here." },
    explanation: "The answer is critical periods. A critical period is a rigidly bounded window of time during which a particular experience must occur for normal development to proceed; if the window closes without the right input, the capacity is said to be permanently lost and no later experience can fully repair the deficit. The classic example is Konrad Lorenz's imprinting in geese, and Hubel and Wiesel's work on the visual cortex, where depriving a kitten of patterned vision during a fixed early window caused lasting blindness. The very strong claim of the critical period idea is irreversibility. So the observation that humans can use later, enriching experiences to compensate for and partly overcome earlier deprivation directly contradicts the strict critical period notion, because it shows that the door has not slammed shut forever. This evidence is exactly why modern developmental psychology largely replaced the term critical period with the softer concept of a sensitive period, a window during which the organism is especially responsive to certain input but which does not permanently foreclose later learning. The deeper lesson for the exam is the contrast between a deterministic, all or nothing view of timing and a probabilistic, plasticity friendly view: brains remain capable of reorganisation, so the capacity for catch up growth and recovery is the empirical fact that undermines the original critical period dogma." },
  { id: "ihbas20_005", section: "dev",
    q: "Helicopter Parenting is the result of:",
    options: { a: "Parental anxiety", b: "Parental unfulfilled wishes", c: "Parents' personality traits", d: "All of above" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Parental anxiety is a genuine cause but only one of several, so selecting it alone is incomplete.", b: "Unfulfilled parental wishes contribute through vicarious living, but again this is only one contributing factor, not the whole picture.", c: "Parents' personality traits such as perfectionism and high control needs matter, yet they too are just one strand of a multiply determined pattern." },
    explanation: "The keyed answer is all of the above, because helicopter parenting is multiply determined rather than springing from one source. Helicopter parenting describes an over involved style in which parents hover, intervene excessively, solve problems their child could solve alone, and monitor and control developmentally inappropriate amounts of the child's life. Research on its origins points to several converging causes. First, parental anxiety: parents who are highly anxious about safety, failure, or the future manage their own distress by controlling the child's environment, so the hovering is partly an emotion regulation strategy for the parent. Second, parents' unfulfilled wishes: parents may project their own thwarted ambitions onto the child and micromanage achievement so the child realises the life the parent did not, a form of vicarious living. Third, the parents' own personality traits: dispositions such as perfectionism, neuroticism, high need for control, or an anxious attachment style predispose a person to over involvement. Because each of these has empirical support and they often act together, the comprehensive answer is all of the above. Developmentally the concern is that over parenting undermines the child's autonomy, self efficacy, and emotion regulation, contributing to higher anxiety and lower coping in emerging adults, which is why it is studied as a risk style rather than a benign quirk." },
  { id: "ihbas20_006", section: "biopsych",
    q: "The twenty-third pair of chromosomes is missing an X, turning into a syndrome characterised by a short, infertile female:",
    options: { a: "Down Syndrome", b: "Asperger's Syndrome", c: "Turner's Syndrome", d: "Klinefelter's Syndrome" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Down syndrome is trisomy 21, an extra autosome, not a missing sex chromosome, and it presents with intellectual disability and characteristic facial features in both sexes.", b: "Asperger's syndrome is a former diagnostic label for an autism spectrum presentation with no chromosomal definition and no sex chromosome loss.", d: "Klinefelter's syndrome is the opposite pattern, an extra X (47,XXY) in a tall, infertile male, not a missing X in a short female." },
    explanation: "The answer is Turner's syndrome. Humans normally carry 23 pairs of chromosomes, and the twenty third pair determines sex, XX in females and XY in males. Turner syndrome arises when a female is missing all or part of one X chromosome, giving the karyotype 45,X (a single X with no second sex chromosome). The phenotype the question describes, a short, infertile female, is the hallmark: affected girls show short stature, gonadal dysgenesis with non functioning streak ovaries leading to infertility and absent or incomplete puberty, a webbed neck, low hairline, broad chest, and often cardiac and renal anomalies. Cognitively, intelligence is usually in the normal range, but there is a characteristic profile of relatively weak visuospatial and arithmetic ability with preserved verbal skills. Contrast this with the other sex chromosome conditions to keep them straight: an extra X in a male gives 47,XXY, Klinefelter syndrome, producing tall, often infertile males. The key exam discriminator is the word missing combined with female and short and infertile, which points specifically to the single X, 45,X picture of Turner syndrome. Remembering that Turner is the loss of an X in a female and Klinefelter is the gain of an X in a male prevents the most common mix up on this item." },
  { id: "ihbas20_007", section: "dev",
    q: "Which theory of attachment is currently considered the predominantly accepted theory by developmental psychologists?",
    options: { a: "Psychoanalytic", b: "Learning", c: "Cognitive-developmental", d: "Ethological" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "The psychoanalytic view treated attachment as the infant's libidinal cathexis of the feeding mother (the cupboard love idea), which Harlow's contact comfort findings undercut.", b: "Learning theory held that the infant bonds because the caregiver is a secondary reinforcer associated with food, but attachment forms even without feeding, contradicting it.", c: "Cognitive developmental accounts tie attachment to the growth of object permanence and understanding of the caregiver, a partial factor but not the predominant framework." },
    explanation: "The currently dominant account of attachment is the ethological theory, developed by John Bowlby and extended by Mary Ainsworth. Ethology is the biological study of behaviour in its natural, evolutionary context, and Bowlby drew on it to argue that the infant's tie to the caregiver is not a learned by product of feeding but an innate, evolved behavioural system selected because proximity to a protector increased survival. Infants come equipped with signalling behaviours such as crying, smiling, clinging, and following that keep the caregiver close, especially under threat. Through repeated interactions the infant builds an internal working model, a mental representation of whether the caregiver is reliably available and of the self as worthy of care, which shapes later relationships. Ainsworth operationalised these ideas with the Strange Situation procedure and identified secure, anxious resistant, and avoidant patterns, later joined by disorganised attachment. This ethological view won out over its rivals because it best explains the cross species evidence, the existence of a fear and proximity seeking system, and the persistence of attachment even when feeding is removed, as Harlow's monkeys showed by preferring a soft surrogate over a wire one that fed them. So the keyed answer, ethological, reflects mainstream developmental consensus that attachment is an evolved, biologically prepared system organised around protection." },
  { id: "ihbas20_008", section: "dev",
    q: "At which stage, according to Kohlberg's theory of moral development, does the individual view laws as instruments for expressing the will of the majority and furthering welfare?",
    options: { a: "Punishment-and-obedience orientation", b: "Social-order-maintaining morality", c: "Morality of individual principles of conscience", d: "Social-contract orientation" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Punishment and obedience orientation is stage 1, the most basic preconventional reasoning that obeys to avoid punishment, far below reasoning about majority welfare.", b: "Social order maintaining morality is stage 4, which obeys fixed laws to preserve order for its own sake, not because laws are revisable instruments of welfare.", c: "Morality of individual principles of conscience is stage 6, governed by self chosen universal principles that can override laws, a step beyond the social contract idea." },
    explanation: "The keyed answer is the social contract orientation. Lawrence Kohlberg proposed that moral reasoning develops through three broad levels, each with two stages, defined by the kind of reasoning behind a judgment rather than the content of the judgment. The preconventional level (stages 1 and 2) judges right and wrong by consequences to the self, avoiding punishment and seeking reward. The conventional level (stages 3 and 4) judges by social approval and then by maintaining law and order for its own sake. The postconventional or principled level (stages 5 and 6) reasons from abstract ethical principles. Stage 5, the social contract orientation, is exactly the view the question describes: laws are understood not as fixed and sacred but as social contracts, instruments agreed upon to express the will of the majority and to maximise welfare, and therefore as changeable through democratic process when they fail to serve the common good. Stage 6, the morality of individual conscience, goes further to universal ethical principles that may override unjust laws. So the phrase laws as instruments for expressing the will of the majority and furthering welfare is the textbook signature of stage 5. The broader exam point is that Kohlberg's progression moves from self interest, through conformity and rule following, to a flexible, principle based understanding of justice." },
  { id: "ihbas20_009", section: "dev",
    q: "The first emotions expressed by infants are:",
    options: { a: "Pain and anger", b: "Rage and fear", c: "Fear and euphoria", d: "Delight and distress" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Pain and anger are not the first differentiated emotional poles; anger in Bridges' scheme emerges later as a branch of distress, not at the outset.", b: "Rage and fear are likewise later, more specific emotions that differentiate out of the distress pole rather than being the first to appear.", c: "Fear and euphoria are also later differentiations; fear emerges from distress and euphoria from delight, so neither is among the earliest two." },
    explanation: "The keyed answer is delight and distress. This item reflects the classic differentiation theory of emotional development associated with Katharine Bridges, who in the 1930s charted how the newborn's emotional life starts undifferentiated and gradually branches into specific emotions. In her scheme the neonate first shows only generalised excitement, a diffuse state of arousal. From this, by about the third month, the infant differentiates into two broad emotional poles, distress (the unpleasant, negative pole) and delight (the pleasant, positive pole). Only later do these split further, with distress giving rise to fear, disgust, and anger, and delight giving rise to elation, affection, and joy, so that by about age two a fairly full repertoire of distinct emotions is present. The exam logic is that the very first differentiated emotions, the earliest two to emerge after global excitement, are delight and distress, which is why option d is keyed. Note that this is a developmental sequencing question grounded in Bridges' work, not a question about discrete innate basic emotions; the discriminating idea is the progression from undifferentiated arousal to a positive and a negative branch, and then to finer emotions. Carrying Bridges' name and the excitement to distress and delight to specific emotions sequence into the exam answers the most likely variants of this item." },
  { id: "ihbas20_010", section: "dev",
    q: "This theorist argued that newborn creatures imprint on their caregivers during a critical period in early development.",
    options: { a: "Mary Ainsworth", b: "John Bowlby", c: "Erik Erikson", d: "Konrad Lorenz" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Mary Ainsworth devised the Strange Situation and classified human attachment styles; she did not study imprinting in newborn animals.", b: "John Bowlby built human attachment theory and borrowed from ethology, but imprinting itself was demonstrated and named by Lorenz, not Bowlby.", c: "Erik Erikson described psychosocial stages of human development, an entirely different framework with no concept of animal imprinting." },
    explanation: "The theorist is Konrad Lorenz, the Austrian ethologist who demonstrated and named imprinting. Imprinting is a rapid, largely irreversible form of early learning in which a young animal forms an attachment to and follows the first moving object it encounters, normally its mother, during a fixed and brief critical period shortly after birth or hatching. Lorenz famously showed this with greylag goslings: hatchlings exposed to him rather than to their mother during the sensitive window imprinted on him and followed him as if he were their parent, even preferring him to their own species. Imprinting illustrates several ethological principles: behaviour can be biologically prepared and triggered by specific stimuli, learning can be time locked to a critical period, and the function is adaptive because following the mother keeps the vulnerable young protected and fed. Lorenz's work, alongside Niko Tinbergen and Karl von Frisch, earned a Nobel Prize and laid groundwork that John Bowlby drew on for attachment theory in humans. The exam discriminator is the combination of newborn creatures, imprint, caregivers, and critical period, all of which are Lorenz's signature terms. Bowlby and Ainsworth applied attachment ideas to human infants, but the specific phenomenon of imprinting during a critical period in young animals is Lorenz's contribution." },
  { id: "ihbas20_011", section: "general",
    q: "Psychology studies both overt and covert behaviour. Covert behaviour is:",
    options: { a: "Observable and measurable", b: "Measurable but not observable", c: "Only measurable", d: "Only observable" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Observable and measurable describes overt behaviour, the openly visible actions, which is the opposite of covert.", c: "Only measurable is incomplete and misleading because it implies covert events are quantifiable but says nothing about the defining feature that they are not observable.", d: "Only observable is contradictory, since the defining property of covert behaviour is precisely that it is not directly observable." },
    explanation: "Covert behaviour is measurable but not directly observable, so the keyed answer is b. Psychology distinguishes overt behaviour, actions that are openly visible to an outside observer such as walking, speaking, or pressing a lever, from covert behaviour, internal events such as thoughts, feelings, attitudes, daydreams, and physiological responses that cannot be seen directly by another person. The crucial point the question tests is that covert does not mean unmeasurable. Although we cannot watch a thought or an emotion, we can measure these private events indirectly through self report, reaction times, physiological indices like heart rate, galvanic skin response, EEG or fMRI, and behavioural proxies. This distinction matters historically because radical behaviourism under Watson initially restricted psychology to overt behaviour on the grounds that only the observable was scientific, whereas later neobehaviourists and cognitive psychologists argued that covert processes are legitimate objects of study precisely because they can be operationalised and measured even if not seen. So the correct characterisation is that covert behaviour is measurable but not observable, capturing both that it is private and internal and that scientific psychology can still quantify it. The takeaway for the exam is the precise pairing: observable refers to direct visibility, measurable refers to the ability to assign values, and covert events satisfy the second without the first." },
  { id: "ihbas20_012", section: "general",
    q: "A person who is very adept at planning, monitoring, and controlling all phases of the multistore model must have very good:",
    options: { a: "Attention", b: "Inhibition", c: "Meta-cognition", d: "Knowledge base" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Attention is the selection and focusing of processing resources, one input to memory, but it does not by itself involve planning, monitoring, and controlling the whole system.", b: "Inhibition is the suppression of irrelevant or prepotent responses, a single executive function, narrower than the overarching self regulation described.", d: "Knowledge base is the stored content in long term memory; having lots of stored facts is different from supervising and regulating one's own cognitive processes." },
    explanation: "The answer is metacognition. The multistore model of memory (Atkinson and Shiffrin) divides memory into sensory register, short term store, and long term store, and it emphasises control processes such as rehearsal, coding, and retrieval strategies that move information between stores. A person who is very good at planning which strategies to use, monitoring how well they are working, and controlling and adjusting them across all phases of this system is exercising metacognition. Metacognition, a term associated with John Flavell, literally means cognition about cognition: it is the knowledge and regulation of one's own thinking. It has two components, metacognitive knowledge (what you know about how memory and learning work, about the task, and about your own abilities) and metacognitive regulation (the executive activities of planning an approach, monitoring comprehension and performance, and evaluating and revising strategies). These planning, monitoring, and controlling verbs in the question are exactly the regulation side of metacognition. The skill is central to effective studying and to memory because it lets a learner allocate rehearsal, choose deep coding over rote repetition, judge when material is learned, and switch tactics when something is not working. So the person described is not just attending or storing facts; they are supervising the whole memory system, which is the defining function of metacognition." },
  { id: "ihbas20_013", section: "dev",
    q: "The starting point for self-socialization, according to Kohlberg's cognitive-developmental theory of gender-role development, is:",
    options: { a: "Gender consistency", b: "Basic gender identity", c: "Gender stability", d: "Gender-role achievement" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Gender consistency (constancy) is the final stage, the mature realisation that gender does not change with appearance, not the starting point.", c: "Gender stability is the middle stage, understanding gender persists over time, which comes after identity, so it is not where self socialisation begins.", d: "Gender role achievement is not one of Kohlberg's three stages; it is a distractor and does not name the initial labelling step." },
    explanation: "The starting point is basic gender identity. Lawrence Kohlberg extended his cognitive developmental approach to gender role development, arguing that children actively construct their understanding of gender and then socialise themselves to behave in gender consistent ways, rather than being passively shaped by reinforcement. He proposed three sequential acquisitions. First comes basic gender identity, around age two to three, when the child can correctly label the self and others as a boy or a girl. Next comes gender stability, the realisation around four to five that gender is stable over time, that a boy grows into a man and a girl into a woman. Finally comes gender constancy or consistency, around six to seven, the understanding that gender is invariant across situations, clothing, hairstyle, or activity, and cannot be changed by superficial transformations. Kohlberg held that the engine of self socialisation switches on once the child has the cognitive category in place, and the very first such category is basic gender identity, because only after a child can label itself as a boy or girl does it become motivated to value and imitate same sex models and adopt gender typed behaviour. So the keyed answer, basic gender identity, is the foundational first step from which gender self socialisation proceeds. The deeper exam point is the ordering identity then stability then constancy, paralleling the child's growing grasp of conservation." },
  { id: "ihbas20_014", section: "biostats",
    q: "A body of prior work related to a research problem is referred to as:",
    options: { a: "Archive", b: "Repository", c: "Literature", d: "None of the above" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "An archive is a repository of original records or documents; it is a place where material is stored, not the body of scholarly prior work on a problem.", b: "A repository likewise denotes a storage location or database for files and data, not the synthesised corpus of prior research itself.", d: "None of the above is wrong because literature is precisely the correct and standard term for the body of prior work." },
    explanation: "The body of prior work related to a research problem is the literature, so the answer is c. In research methodology the literature means the accumulated published scholarship, theories, empirical studies, reviews, and findings that bear on a chosen topic. Surveying it is the literature review, an essential early step in any project. The literature review serves several functions: it establishes what is already known and what remains unknown so the researcher can locate a genuine gap; it prevents needless duplication of work already done; it sharpens the research question and hypotheses; it informs the choice of methods, measures, and design by showing what has worked or failed before; and it provides the theoretical framework that situates the new study within an ongoing scholarly conversation. A good review is critical and synthetic, not a mere list, weighing the quality and convergence of prior evidence. So when a question speaks of the existing body of prior work connected to a research problem, the standard methodological term is the literature. The exam point is to recognise this as basic research vocabulary and to associate it with the literature review as a defined stage of the research process." },
  { id: "ihbas20_015", section: "biostats",
    q: "The principle of ethical research under which the subjects should be given full information about all aspects of the research study after it is done is known as:",
    options: { a: "Debriefing", b: "Description", c: "Explanation", d: "Interpretation" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Description is not an ethical principle; it is simply giving an account of something, and it does not name the post study informing obligation.", c: "Explanation is too generic; while debriefing involves explaining, the formal ethical term for the post study disclosure procedure is debriefing.", d: "Interpretation refers to making sense of findings or data, an analytic act by the researcher, not the ethical duty to inform participants afterward." },
    explanation: "The principle is debriefing, so the answer is a. Debriefing is the ethical procedure carried out after data collection in which participants are given a full and honest explanation of the study's true purpose, design, and any deception used, along with an opportunity to ask questions, have concerns addressed, and withdraw their data if they wish. It is required by ethical codes such as those of the APA precisely because some valid research, particularly in social psychology, must temporarily withhold or disguise its real aims to avoid biasing behaviour. Debriefing exists to undo the costs of that concealment: it restores informed understanding after the fact, corrects any false impressions created during the study, relieves any distress, and reaffirms respect for the participant's autonomy and dignity. A proper debrief has two aims, dehoaxing (revealing and explaining any deception so the person is not left with mistaken beliefs) and desensitising (helping the participant deal with any negative feelings the study aroused). Crucially, the question's wording, full information about all aspects of the research after it is done, is the textbook definition of debriefing, since informing participants before the study is informed consent. So the discriminating cue is the phrase after it is done, which places the obligation in the post study debriefing phase rather than the consent phase." },
  { id: "ihbas20_016", section: "biostats",
    q: "Those sampling methods which clearly specify the likelihood of inclusion of each element in the sample are known as:",
    options: { a: "Probability sampling methods", b: "Non-Probability sampling methods", c: "Statistical sampling", d: "Empirical sampling" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Non probability sampling methods are the opposite case, where the chance of inclusion is unknown and selection is by convenience or judgment, so they cannot specify each element's likelihood.", c: "Statistical sampling is a loose umbrella phrase, not the precise methodological term for designs defined by known inclusion probabilities.", d: "Empirical sampling is not a standard category in sampling theory and does not denote the known probability property described." },
    explanation: "These are probability sampling methods, so the answer is a. The defining feature of probability sampling is that every element in the population has a known, non zero chance of being selected, and that chance can be specified. Because the selection is governed by chance rather than by the researcher's judgment or convenience, probability sampling supports unbiased estimation, the calculation of sampling error, and statistical generalisation from sample to population. The main probability designs are simple random sampling, where each unit has an equal chance; systematic sampling, selecting every kth unit from a random start; stratified sampling, dividing the population into strata and sampling within each; and cluster sampling, randomly selecting whole groups. The contrast is non probability sampling, including convenience, quota, purposive, and snowball methods, in which the probability of inclusion is unknown and selection rests on accessibility or judgment, so results cannot be generalised with known precision. The exam discriminator is the exact phrase clearly specify the likelihood of inclusion of each element, which is the textbook definition of probability sampling. The deeper point is that knowing the inclusion probabilities is what licenses inferential statistics and estimates of confidence, which is why probability sampling is preferred whenever representative, generalisable conclusions are the goal." },
  { id: "ihbas20_017", section: "biostats",
    q: "The scope of research methodology is ______ than that of research methods:",
    options: { a: "Narrower", b: "Wider", c: "Similar", d: "Depends on situation" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Narrower reverses the relationship; methods are the narrower set of concrete techniques contained within the broader study of methodology.", c: "Similar is wrong because the two are not equivalent in scope; methodology explicitly includes the reasoning about and evaluation of methods.", d: "Depends on situation is incorrect because the conceptual relationship is fixed: methodology is by definition the wider field that subsumes specific methods." },
    explanation: "The scope of research methodology is wider than that of research methods, so the answer is b. The two terms are easily confused but are not synonyms. Research methods are the specific techniques, tools, and procedures a researcher uses to collect and analyse data, for example surveys, experiments, interviews, observation, or particular statistical tests. Research methodology is the broader, more philosophical study of how research is and should be conducted: it is the systematic analysis of the logic behind the methods, the assumptions and criteria for choosing among them, the reasoning that connects question to design to inference, and the evaluation of whether a given approach is appropriate and valid for a given problem. In other words, methods are a subset of what methodology covers. Methodology asks not only how to gather data but why a particular method is suitable, what its limitations are, how it fits the epistemological stance of the inquiry, and how conclusions can be justified. Because methodology encompasses the rationale, the philosophy of science underpinnings, and the comparative evaluation of all the methods plus the methods themselves, its scope is necessarily wider. The exam takeaway is the relationship: methodology is the umbrella discipline, methods are the concrete operations within it, so methodology is the broader concept." },
  { id: "ihbas20_018", section: "biostats",
    q: "Uniting various qualitative methods with quantitative methods can be called as:",
    options: { a: "Coalition", b: "Triangulation", c: "Multidimensional approach", d: "Co-operative inquiry" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Coalition denotes an alliance of parties or groups, not a methodological strategy of combining research approaches.", c: "Multidimensional approach is vague and not the established term; triangulation is the specific methodological label for mixing qualitative and quantitative methods.", d: "Co operative inquiry is a particular participatory research method (Heron and Reason) in which people research with rather than on each other, not the general act of combining methods." },
    explanation: "Combining qualitative and quantitative methods is called triangulation, so the answer is b. Triangulation is the strategy of using more than one method, data source, theory, or investigator to study the same phenomenon, so that the strengths of one approach compensate for the weaknesses of another and convergent findings increase confidence in the conclusions. The term borrows from navigation and surveying, where a position is fixed by taking bearings from multiple points. Norman Denzin distinguished several types: methodological triangulation (mixing methods such as qualitative interviews with quantitative surveys, which is exactly what the question describes), data triangulation (different times, places, or samples), investigator triangulation (multiple researchers), and theory triangulation (multiple theoretical lenses). The rationale is that quantitative methods offer breadth, measurement, and generalisability while qualitative methods offer depth, meaning, and context, so uniting them yields a richer and more trustworthy picture and guards against the bias inherent in any single method. This is the conceptual core of mixed methods research. The discriminating cue in the item is uniting various qualitative methods with quantitative methods, which is the textbook description of methodological triangulation. The exam point is to attach the navigation derived word triangulation to the idea of cross checking findings through multiple complementary approaches." },
  { id: "ihbas20_019", section: "biostats",
    q: "A statistical measure based upon a population is known as:",
    options: { a: "Statistic", b: "Parameter", c: "Sample", d: "None of the above" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "A statistic is computed from a sample, not the population, so it is the counterpart concept, not the population based measure.", c: "A sample is a subset of the population, not a numerical measure at all, so it cannot be the statistical measure being defined.", d: "None of the above is wrong because parameter is exactly the correct term for a population based statistical measure." },
    explanation: "A statistical measure based on a population is a parameter, so the answer is b. The fundamental distinction in inferential statistics is between a parameter and a statistic. A parameter is a numerical characteristic of an entire population, for instance the population mean (mu), the population standard deviation (sigma), or a population proportion. A statistic is the corresponding numerical characteristic computed from a sample, such as the sample mean (x bar) or sample standard deviation (s). Because we usually cannot measure every member of a population, parameters are typically unknown fixed values that we estimate using sample statistics; the sample statistic serves as an estimator of the population parameter. This is the entire logic of inferential statistics: we draw a representative sample, compute statistics, and then use sampling distributions, standard errors, and confidence intervals to infer the likely value of the unknown parameter and to test hypotheses about it. A handy mnemonic is that p goes with p (parameter with population) and s goes with s (statistic with sample). So when a question asks for the measure based on the whole population, the answer is parameter. The deeper exam point is the estimation relationship: statistics are observed and variable across samples, parameters are the fixed but unknown population truths we are trying to estimate." },
  { id: "ihbas20_020", section: "general",
    q: "The book 'Indian Children on a Psychiatrist's Playground' is written by:",
    options: { a: "Shekar Sheshadri", b: "Shoba Srinath", c: "Maria Kovac", d: "Erna Hoch" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Shekhar Seshadri is a NIMHANS child and adolescent psychiatrist known for work on child mental health and sexuality education, not the author of this book.", b: "Shoba Srinath is a leading Indian child psychiatrist at NIMHANS, prominent in child mental health epidemiology, but she did not write this title.", c: "Maria Kovacs is an American researcher famous for the Children's Depression Inventory, not an author of an India focused clinical memoir." },
    explanation: "Indian Children on a Psychiatrist's Playground was written by Erna Hoch, so the answer is d. Erna Hoch was a Swiss born psychiatrist who worked for many years in India and is remembered as a pioneer of culturally sensitive psychiatry on the subcontinent. Her work emphasised understanding Indian patients within their own cultural, familial, and spiritual frameworks rather than imposing Western diagnostic and therapeutic templates wholesale, and she wrote extensively on the meeting of Eastern and Western thought in mental health practice. This particular book reflects her clinical observations of Indian children. For Indian entrance exams in clinical psychology, a cluster of author and title pairings about the history of Indian mental health is commonly tested, and this is one of them. The value of remembering it is partly factual recall but also conceptual: it anchors the theme of cultural relativity and the indigenisation of psychiatry and clinical psychology in India, a theme that recurs across these papers. The discriminating skill is simply to memorise the author title link, distinguishing Hoch's work from the contributions of Indian clinicians such as Shoba Srinath and Shekhar Seshadri at NIMHANS, who are leaders in child and adolescent mental health but did not author this specific title." },
  { id: "ihbas20_021", section: "general",
    q: "Mental Health in Indian Schools is the work of:",
    options: { a: "Savita Malhotra", b: "Jitendra Nagpal", c: "Malavika Kapur", d: "Srikala Bharat" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Savita Malhotra is a noted child and adolescent psychiatrist (PGIMER Chandigarh), influential in child psychiatry research, but not the author of this title.", b: "Jitendra Nagpal is associated with the Expressions India school mental health programme and adolescent wellbeing, but did not write this book.", d: "Srikala Bharath is a NIMHANS psychiatrist active in community and school mental health and life skills programmes, yet she is not the author of this work." },
    explanation: "Mental Health in Indian Schools is the work of Malavika Kapur, so the answer is c. Malavika Kapur is a distinguished Indian clinical psychologist long associated with NIMHANS, whose career focused on child clinical psychology, school mental health, and the development of indigenous, community based and teacher mediated models of psychological care for Indian children. Her contribution is significant because she argued for and demonstrated school based approaches in which teachers and primary care workers are trained to identify and support children's mental health needs, a scalable model well suited to a country with few specialists relative to the size of the child population. She also worked on culturally appropriate assessment and on documenting the history of child mental health in India. For entrance exams this is another author title pairing in the Indian mental health history cluster, and the deeper point it represents is the shift from clinic centred, specialist heavy care toward preventive, school and community based mental health promotion for children. The discriminating skill is keeping the Indian pioneers distinct: Kapur with school mental health and child clinical psychology, as against figures like Savita Malhotra in child psychiatry, Srikala Bharath in community psychiatry, and Jitendra Nagpal in school mental health programmes, none of whom authored this particular book." },
  { id: "ihbas20_022", section: "general",
    q: "Indian Association of Clinical Psychologists (IACP) was formed in the year:",
    options: { a: "1968", b: "1970", c: "1971", d: "1978" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "1970 is a near miss often confused with the founding year, but the IACP was established two years earlier, in 1968.", c: "1971 is another close distractor that does not match the documented founding date of the association.", d: "1978 is too late; by then the IACP had already been functioning for a decade." },
    explanation: "The Indian Association of Clinical Psychologists was formed in 1968, so the answer is a. The IACP is the principal professional body for clinical psychologists in India, founded to promote the science and practice of clinical psychology, to set and maintain professional and ethical standards, to support training and research, and to give the discipline a collective professional voice. Its founding in 1968 is a frequently tested fact in Indian clinical psychology entrance papers because it marks the formal professionalisation and organisation of the field in the country, a few decades after the first clinical psychology training began. The association publishes the Indian Journal of Clinical Psychology, holds annual conferences, and has historically engaged with regulatory and training matters, including the long running questions about registration and statutory recognition that eventually led to the Rehabilitation Council of India regulating the qualification of clinical psychologists. Knowing the year situates a candidate in the timeline of Indian clinical psychology: training programmes from the 1950s, the IACP in 1968, and later statutory regulation under the RCI Act of 1992. The discriminating skill here is precise date recall, since the distractor years are close together; the correct and commonly cited founding year is 1968." },
  { id: "ihbas20_023", section: "abnormal",
    q: "False belief which cannot be shaken by reason is termed as:",
    options: { a: "Illusion", b: "Delusion", c: "Hallucination", d: "False memory" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "An illusion is a misperception of a real external stimulus, a perceptual error, not a false belief held against reason.", c: "A hallucination is a perception without an external stimulus, again a disorder of perception rather than of belief.", d: "A false memory is a distorted or fabricated recollection, a memory error, not a fixed false belief impervious to argument." },
    explanation: "A false belief that cannot be shaken by reason is a delusion, so the answer is b. A delusion is a fixed, false belief held with absolute conviction, out of keeping with the person's social, cultural, and educational background, and not amenable to correction by logical argument or contradictory evidence. It is a disorder of thought content, in contrast to disorders of thought form or stream. The defining features for the exam are three: the belief is false, it is firmly held despite proof to the contrary, and it is not a shared cultural or religious belief. Delusions come in recognisable types, including persecutory (being plotted against), grandiose (inflated worth or power), delusion of reference (neutral events are personally significant), nihilistic, somatic, and delusions of control, and they are a hallmark of psychotic disorders such as schizophrenia and delusional disorder, though they can occur in mood disorders and organic states too. The crucial discriminator in this question is the phrase cannot be shaken by reason, which captures the imperviousness to argument that distinguishes a delusion from a mere mistaken idea or an overvalued idea. It is also important to separate a delusion, a disorder of belief, from an illusion or hallucination, which are disorders of perception." },
  { id: "ihbas20_024", section: "biopsych",
    q: "The part of the nervous system involved in emotional behaviour is:",
    options: { a: "Hippocampus", b: "Thalamus", c: "Hypothalamus", d: "Limbic system" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "The hippocampus is part of the limbic system but its primary role is memory formation; alone it is too narrow to be the seat of emotional behaviour.", b: "The thalamus is the main sensory relay station to the cortex; it routes information but is not the central structure for emotion.", c: "The hypothalamus executes the autonomic and endocrine output of emotion, but as a single structure it is a component of, not the whole, emotional system." },
    explanation: "The part of the nervous system most associated with emotional behaviour is the limbic system, so the answer is d. The limbic system is a set of interconnected structures on the medial and inferior surfaces of the cerebral hemispheres that together regulate emotion, motivation, and certain memory functions. Its key components include the amygdala, central to fear, threat detection, and emotional learning; the hippocampus, essential for forming new memories and for the emotional context of memory; the hypothalamus, which translates emotional states into autonomic and endocrine responses; the cingulate gyrus, involved in emotional regulation and the link between behaviour and emotion; and connecting tracts such as the fornix. Papez first proposed an emotional circuit and MacLean later popularised the term limbic system as the neural substrate of emotion. The reason the limbic system rather than any single structure is the best answer is that emotional behaviour is a distributed function requiring the integration of appraisal (amygdala), bodily expression (hypothalamus), memory and context (hippocampus), and regulation (cingulate). The amygdala alone evaluates threat, the hypothalamus alone executes the autonomic response, but the experience and behaviour we call emotion emerge from the system as a whole. So when the question asks for the part involved in emotional behaviour without further qualification, the inclusive and correct answer is the limbic system." },
  { id: "ihbas20_025", section: "general",
    q: "When you stretch your right hand farther away from your body, you still perceive it to be of the same size as that of your left hand. This is due to:",
    options: { a: "Size constancy", b: "Shape constancy", c: "Motion parallax", d: "Depth perception" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Shape constancy keeps perceived shape constant despite changes in the angle of view, but here the dimension changing is size with distance, not orientation.", c: "Motion parallax is a depth cue from the differential movement of near and far objects as the observer moves, not the maintenance of perceived size.", d: "Depth perception is the broader ability to judge distance; size constancy uses depth cues but the phenomenon described is specifically the constancy of perceived size." },
    explanation: "Perceiving your hand as the same size whether near or far is size constancy, so the answer is a. Perceptual constancy is the tendency to perceive the stable, true properties of objects despite continually changing sensory input. Size constancy specifically is the ability to perceive an object as having a constant real size even though the size of its retinal image changes with distance. When you extend your hand farther away, its retinal image shrinks, yet you still perceive the hand as the same size as your near hand because the perceptual system takes distance into account and rescales the image, a process captured by Emmert's law that perceived size depends jointly on retinal image size and perceived distance. This depends on depth and distance cues; without them, as in the moon illusion or the Ames room, size constancy can break down or be fooled. The broader family of constancies includes shape constancy (a door is seen as rectangular even when it projects a trapezoid as it opens), brightness and colour constancy, and location constancy, all reflecting the brain's job of recovering stable distal objects from variable proximal stimulation. The discriminating cue here is that the changing dimension is size with distance and the perception held constant is size, so size constancy, not shape constancy, is the precise answer." },
  { id: "ihbas20_026", section: "social",
    q: "A state that occurs when bystanders in an emergency situation use the behaviour of others to determine whether help is actually required is called:",
    options: { a: "Pluralistic ignorance", b: "Group conformity", c: "Group compliance", d: "Crowd behavior" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Group conformity is the broad tendency to align with a group; it is a more general process, not the specific misreading of others' inaction as evidence no help is needed.", c: "Group compliance refers to publicly going along with a request or norm while perhaps disagreeing privately, not the emergency interpretation phenomenon.", d: "Crowd behaviour is a general term for how people act in large gatherings and does not name the specific cognitive error in bystander emergencies." },
    explanation: "When bystanders read others' behaviour to decide whether help is needed, the resulting state is pluralistic ignorance, so the answer is a. Pluralistic ignorance is a social psychological phenomenon in which each member of a group privately doubts or rejects a norm or interpretation but mistakenly assumes that most others accept it, because everyone is publicly conforming while privately uncertain. In an ambiguous emergency, each bystander glances at the calm faces of the others to judge whether the situation is truly serious. Since everyone is doing the same thing and trying not to look panicked, all see a roomful of apparently unconcerned people and each concludes the event must not be an emergency. This is one of the key mechanisms, alongside diffusion of responsibility and audience inhibition, behind the bystander effect documented by Latane and Darley after the Kitty Genovese case. It operates through the broader process of informational social influence, using others as a source of information about reality, but the specific labelled outcome, where everyone wrongly infers no emergency from everyone else's inaction, is pluralistic ignorance. The exam discriminator is the phrase use the behaviour of others to determine whether help is actually required, which is the textbook description of pluralistic ignorance in the bystander intervention sequence." },
  { id: "ihbas20_027", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Which of the following is not a positive symptom?",
    options: { a: "Hallucination", b: "Delusions", c: "Flattened affect", d: "Disorganized behavior" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Hallucination is a positive symptom, a perception occurring without any external stimulus, so it is an addition to normal experience.", b: "Delusions are positive symptoms, fixed false beliefs that represent a pathological excess of belief, not a deficit.", d: "Disorganized behavior is a positive symptom, a distortion and disruption of normal goal directed activity, not an absence of function." },
    explanation: "Flattened affect is not a positive symptom; it is a negative symptom, so the answer is c. In schizophrenia, symptoms are grouped into positive and negative. Positive symptoms are pathological additions to or excesses of normal functioning, experiences and behaviours that are present but should not be: hallucinations (perceptions without a stimulus), delusions (fixed false beliefs), disorganised thinking and speech, and grossly disorganised or bizarre behaviour. Negative symptoms are the absence or reduction of normal functions, things that should be present but are diminished: flattened or blunted affect (reduced emotional expression in face and voice), alogia (poverty of speech), avolition (loss of motivation and goal directed behaviour), anhedonia (reduced capacity for pleasure), and asociality. The distinction matters clinically because positive symptoms tend to respond well to dopamine blocking antipsychotics, while negative symptoms are more treatment resistant, more strongly linked to poorer long term functioning, and historically harder to remediate. The question lists hallucinations, delusions, and disorganised behaviour, all clear positive symptoms, and asks which is not positive; flattened affect, a reduction of emotional expressiveness, is the lone negative symptom and therefore the correct exception. The deeper exam point is the conceptual rule: positive equals excess or distortion added on, negative equals normal function taken away." },
  { id: "ihbas20_028", section: "therapy",
    q: "Fixed role therapy, which encouraged clients to practice new roles and relationships, was developed by:",
    options: { a: "George Kelly", b: "Aaron Beck", c: "Albert Ellis", d: "Fritz Perls" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Aaron Beck founded cognitive therapy, focusing on identifying and testing automatic thoughts and core beliefs, not on enacting a scripted new role.", c: "Albert Ellis created Rational Emotive Behaviour Therapy, disputing irrational beliefs through the ABC model, a different cognitive method.", d: "Fritz Perls founded Gestalt therapy, using here and now awareness and techniques like the empty chair, not Kelly's fixed role enactment." },
    explanation: "Fixed role therapy was developed by George Kelly, so the answer is a. George Kelly was the founder of personal construct theory, set out in his 1955 work The Psychology of Personal Constructs. His central idea was that people are like naive scientists who anticipate and make sense of the world through bipolar personal constructs, the contrasting dimensions such as friendly versus hostile that they use to interpret events. Psychological problems arise when a person's construct system is too rigid, too constricted, or fails to predict experience well. Fixed role therapy is the clever therapeutic application of this idea. The therapist, often after a self characterisation sketch written by the client, drafts a fixed role sketch describing a hypothetical character who construes the world somewhat differently, in healthier or more flexible ways. The client is then invited to enact and live out this new role for a set period, as if trying on a different identity in an experiment. By behaving as the new character, the client gathers fresh evidence, loosens fixed constructs, and discovers that alternative ways of construing and acting are possible, a method Kelly called constructive alternativism. The discriminating cue, practising new roles and relationships within a constructivist frame, points specifically to Kelly, distinguishing his approach from the cognitive and gestalt therapies of the other options." },
  { id: "ihbas20_029", section: "therapy",
    q: "Schofield's (1964) 'YAVIS Syndrome' describes:",
    options: { a: "Therapy technique", b: "Psychiatric Illness", c: "Therapist's Qualities", d: "Patient's Characteristics" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "It is not a therapy technique; YAVIS names a patient profile, not a method of treatment.", b: "It is not a psychiatric illness; the letters describe desirable client attributes, not symptoms of a disorder.", c: "It does not describe therapist qualities; it describes the kind of patient therapists tend to prefer, which is a different thing." },
    explanation: "Schofield's YAVIS syndrome describes patient characteristics, so the answer is d. In his 1964 book Psychotherapy: The Purchase of Friendship, William Schofield observed that psychotherapists tend to prefer, and to obtain better outcomes with, clients who are Young, Attractive, Verbal, Intelligent, and Successful, the acronym YAVIS. His point was a critical one about bias in the delivery of mental health care: therapists, consciously or not, are drawn to clients who resemble themselves in education and articulateness and who are gratifying to work with, which means that the people who most need help, older, less verbal, less educated, and socially disadvantaged individuals, may be underserved. The concept exposed an equity problem in psychotherapy and stimulated discussion of social class, accessibility, and therapist bias. So YAVIS is not a technique, an illness, or a description of the ideal therapist; it is a tongue in cheek profile of the kind of patient therapists favour. The exam discriminator is recognising that the acronym lists attributes of clients, the consumers of therapy, and that Schofield raised it as a criticism of selection bias. Carrying the unpacked acronym and Schofield's critical intent into the exam answers any variant of this item." },
  { id: "ihbas20_030", section: "social",
    q: "Norms that suggest to people the way they ought to behave are called:",
    options: { a: "Descriptive norms", b: "Idealistic norms", c: "Prescriptive norms", d: "Perceived norms" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Descriptive norms capture what people actually do, the common behaviour, not what they ought to do.", b: "Idealistic norms is not the standard social psychological term; the recognised label for ought norms is prescriptive or injunctive.", d: "Perceived norms refers to a person's beliefs about what others do or approve of, which can be inaccurate, and is not specifically the ought category." },
    explanation: "Norms that tell people how they ought to behave are prescriptive norms, so the answer is c. Social psychology distinguishes two kinds of social norms, a distinction sharpened by Cialdini and colleagues. Descriptive norms describe what people actually do, the typical or common behaviour in a situation, and they influence us by signalling what is likely effective or normal. Prescriptive norms, also called injunctive norms, specify what people ought to do, what behaviour is approved or disapproved of by the group, and they influence us through the prospect of social reward or sanction. The word prescriptive itself carries the sense of prescribing or mandating, which is the cue that it concerns the ought rather than the is. The distinction matters because the two can conflict and can be harnessed in behaviour change campaigns; telling people that most others litter (a descriptive norm of bad behaviour) can backfire, whereas conveying social disapproval of littering (an injunctive or prescriptive norm) more reliably reduces it. So when a question contrasts norms about what is done with norms about what should be done, the should be done category is the prescriptive or injunctive norm. The exam discriminator is the phrase ought to behave, which precisely marks the prescriptive norm rather than the merely descriptive one." },
  { id: "ihbas20_031", section: "therapy",
    q: "It is considered that successful CBT treatments of social phobia include elements of the following:",
    options: { a: "Exposure therapy", b: "Social skills retraining", c: "Cognitive restructuring", d: "All of above" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Exposure therapy is a core and necessary component but, taken alone, it omits the cognitive and skills elements that complete an effective protocol.", b: "Social skills retraining addresses behavioural deficits but by itself does not tackle the distorted threat appraisals that drive social anxiety.", c: "Cognitive restructuring corrects maladaptive beliefs but on its own lacks the behavioural confrontation of feared situations that exposure provides." },
    explanation: "Effective CBT for social phobia combines all of the above, so the answer is d. Social anxiety disorder, or social phobia, is characterised by intense fear of scrutiny and negative evaluation in social or performance situations, leading to avoidance and significant impairment. Comprehensive cognitive behavioural treatment targets the disorder on several fronts at once, and the evidence supports a package that includes each listed element. Exposure therapy systematically and repeatedly confronts feared social situations, allowing the fear to extinguish through habituation and, more importantly in current inhibitory learning models, through disconfirmation of catastrophic predictions about humiliation and rejection. Social skills retraining addresses any genuine deficits in conversation, assertiveness, eye contact, and nonverbal behaviour that may maintain real difficulties, and it builds confidence and competence so that exposures are more likely to go well. Cognitive restructuring identifies and challenges the distorted beliefs central to social anxiety, the overestimation of danger, the assumption of harsh judgment, perfectionistic social standards, and excessive self focused attention described in Clark and Wells's influential model. Because all three components address complementary maintaining factors, perception of threat, behavioural avoidance, and skill, the most accurate answer is that successful treatment includes all of them. The exam point is that CBT for social phobia is a multicomponent protocol, not a single technique." },
  { id: "ihbas20_032", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Comorbidity means:",
    options: { a: "People suffering with the same psychopathology die at the same time", b: "Sufferers exhibit a chronic fear of death", c: "Depression always happens in the winter", d: "Differentially defined disorders can co-occur" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Dying at the same time misreads the morbidity root as mortality; comorbidity concerns co occurring illnesses in one person, not simultaneous deaths.", b: "A chronic fear of death is thanatophobia or a death anxiety, an entirely different concept unrelated to the co occurrence of disorders.", c: "Depression occurring in winter describes seasonal affective disorder, a specifier, not the definition of comorbidity." },
    explanation: "Comorbidity means that differentially defined disorders can co occur, so the answer is d. In psychopathology, comorbidity refers to the presence of two or more distinct, separately defined disorders in the same person at the same time, or across a lifetime. The term originated in general medicine but is especially important in mental health because co occurrence is the rule rather than the exception; for instance depression frequently co occurs with anxiety disorders, substance use disorders, and personality disorders. Comorbidity matters for several reasons. It complicates diagnosis, since overlapping symptoms can mask or mimic one another. It worsens prognosis and often the course and severity of each condition. It influences treatment, since clinicians must prioritise and sometimes integrate interventions. And it raises deep questions about our classification systems: high rates of comorbidity may indicate genuinely co occurring illnesses, shared underlying risk factors or transdiagnostic processes, or artefacts of how we draw diagnostic boundaries, which is part of the impetus behind dimensional models like the Research Domain Criteria. The exam discriminator is the precise definition, the co occurrence of differentially defined disorders, which option d states exactly. The other options are nonsensical distractors playing on the morbidity root, but comorbidity has nothing to do with dying together or fear of death." },
  { id: "ihbas20_033", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Suffocation Alarm Theories explain the clinical condition of:",
    options: { a: "Down Syndrome", b: "Autistic Spectrum Disorder", c: "Delirium", d: "Panic Disorder" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Down syndrome is a chromosomal disorder (trisomy 21); it has no connection to a respiratory false alarm mechanism.", b: "Autistic spectrum disorder is a neurodevelopmental condition of social communication and behaviour, unrelated to a CO2 suffocation alarm.", c: "Delirium is an acute fluctuating disturbance of consciousness and attention, usually from a medical cause, not explained by a suffocation alarm theory." },
    explanation: "Suffocation alarm theory explains panic disorder, so the answer is d. The suffocation false alarm theory was proposed by Donald Klein to account for spontaneous panic attacks. Klein hypothesised that the brain contains an evolved suffocation alarm system, a monitor that detects signs of impending suffocation, chiefly a rise in carbon dioxide and lactate, and triggers an urgent alarm with breathlessness, air hunger, and the drive to escape. In people prone to panic, this monitor is pathologically oversensitive, so it fires a false alarm of suffocation even when no real lack of oxygen exists, producing the sudden surge of terror, dyspnea, and the urge to flee that characterises a panic attack. The theory elegantly explains several clinical observations: that inhaling carbon dioxide or infusing sodium lactate can provoke panic attacks in patients but much less so in controls, that panic patients often have respiratory symptoms and abnormalities in breathing regulation, and that the air hunger and smothering sensations are so prominent in attacks. It distinguishes panic from ordinary fear by locating it in a misfiring respiratory chemoreceptor alarm rather than in cognitive threat appraisal alone. The exam discriminator is the link between suffocation and the respiratory, CO2 sensitive features that are signature to panic disorder, not to any of the other listed conditions." },
  { id: "ihbas20_034", section: "general",
    q: "Marshmallow Experiment was conducted at:",
    options: { a: "Stanford University", b: "University of Pennsylvania", c: "Nebraska University", d: "Derek University" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "University of Pennsylvania is associated with positive psychology and Seligman's learned helplessness work, not Mischel's marshmallow studies.", c: "Nebraska University is known for the Nebraska Symposium on Motivation, not for the delay of gratification experiments.", d: "Derek University is a fictitious distractor and not a real institution connected to this research." },
    explanation: "The marshmallow experiment was conducted at Stanford University, so the answer is a. The studies were run by Walter Mischel and colleagues beginning in the late 1960s and through the 1970s, using preschool children at Stanford's Bing Nursery School. In the classic paradigm a child was offered a choice: eat one marshmallow (or other treat) now, or wait alone with the treat for about fifteen minutes and receive two. The experiment was designed to study delay of gratification, the capacity to forgo a smaller immediate reward for a larger delayed one, and the cognitive strategies children use to resist temptation, such as distracting themselves or mentally transforming the treat into something abstract (the hot versus cool system idea Mischel later developed). Its fame rests on the longitudinal follow ups, which reported that children who waited longer tended, years later, to show better academic outcomes, social competence, and self regulation, suggesting that early self control predicts important life outcomes, though later large replications have tempered the strength of these long term claims and highlighted the role of socioeconomic context and trust in the experimenter. For the exam the key facts are Walter Mischel, Stanford, delay of gratification, and self regulation. The discriminating cue is simply the institution, Stanford, the home of Mischel's preschool studies." },
  { id: "ihbas20_035", section: "general",
    q: "Which is the correct existing legislation for mental health services in India?",
    options: { a: "Mental Health Act, 1987", b: "Persons with Mental Illness Act, 2016", c: "Mental Health Care Act, 2017", d: "Indian Mental Health Act, 2018" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "The Mental Health Act, 1987 was the previous law but it has been repealed by the 2017 Act, so it is no longer the existing legislation.", b: "Persons with Mental Illness Act, 2016 is not a real statute; the 2016 era law relevant to disability is the Rights of Persons with Disabilities Act, not this.", d: "Indian Mental Health Act, 2018 is a fabricated title; the operative law came into force in 2018 but is named the Mental Healthcare Act, 2017." },
    explanation: "The correct current legislation is the Mental Healthcare Act, 2017, so the answer is c. The Mental Healthcare Act, 2017 came into force in 2018 and repealed and replaced the earlier Mental Health Act of 1987. It marked a major rights based shift in Indian mental health law, aligning the country with the United Nations Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities, which India ratified. Its central features include a rights based approach that guarantees access to mental healthcare and treatment; the decriminalisation of attempted suicide, presuming that a person who attempts suicide is under severe stress and should not be punished; the provision for an advance directive, allowing a person to state in advance how they wish to be treated and to appoint a nominated representative; the establishment of Mental Health Review Boards to protect rights and oversee admissions; regulation of admission, treatment, and the use of restraints and electroconvulsive therapy; and a definition of mental illness and of capacity to make treatment decisions. For Indian clinical psychology exams this is essential, current statutory knowledge. The discriminating skill is to recognise that the 1987 Act is repealed and superseded, and that the options Persons with Mental Illness Act 2016 and Indian Mental Health Act 2018 are fabricated or misnamed, leaving the Mental Healthcare Act 2017 as the genuine governing law." },
  { id: "ihbas20_036", section: "general",
    q: "Reciprocal causation model has been described under:",
    options: { a: "Social cognitive theory", b: "Two-factor theory", c: "Drive reduction theory", d: "Constructivist theory" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Two factor theory (Mowrer) explains avoidance learning through classical plus operant conditioning; it does not propose triadic reciprocal causation.", c: "Drive reduction theory (Hull) explains motivation as reducing physiological drives toward homeostasis, with no reciprocal person behaviour environment loop.", d: "Constructivist theory concerns how learners actively build knowledge; it is not the framework that introduced reciprocal determinism." },
    explanation: "The reciprocal causation model belongs to social cognitive theory, so the answer is a. Albert Bandura's social cognitive theory holds that human functioning is best understood through triadic reciprocal causation (also called reciprocal determinism), in which three sets of factors continuously influence one another: personal factors such as cognitions, beliefs, expectations, and especially self efficacy; behaviour; and the external environment. None of the three is primary; each both shapes and is shaped by the others in a dynamic loop. For example, a student's belief that she can do mathematics (person) leads her to practise more (behaviour), which produces success and praise from teachers (environment), which in turn raises her self efficacy (person), and so on. This bidirectional view was Bandura's corrective to both the radical behaviourist position that the environment unilaterally controls behaviour and to purely intrapsychic theories that ignore situational influence; it restores the person as an active agent who can self regulate and influence the very conditions that act upon them. Social cognitive theory also encompasses observational learning, modelling, and the central construct of self efficacy. The exam discriminator is that reciprocal causation is Bandura's specific term within social cognitive theory, distinguishing it from drive reduction, two factor, and constructivist frameworks that do not feature this triadic mutual influence model." },
  { id: "ihbas20_037", section: "therapy",
    q: "Premack Principle explains:",
    options: { a: "A process of Interpersonal relationship", b: "A technique of behaviour modification in children", c: "Pattern of language development", d: "Impact of parenting on behavioural problems in young children" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Interpersonal relationship processes are not what the Premack principle addresses; it is a reinforcement contingency, not a relational model.", c: "Language development patterns are unrelated; the principle concerns the relative probability of behaviours, not linguistic acquisition.", d: "The impact of parenting on behavioural problems is a developmental research topic, not the definition of the Premack reinforcement technique." },
    explanation: "The Premack principle is a behaviour modification technique, especially useful with children, so the answer is b. Formulated by David Premack, the principle states that a more probable or preferred behaviour can be used to reinforce a less probable or less preferred behaviour. In plainer terms, access to a high frequency, enjoyable activity is made contingent on first performing a low frequency, less appealing one. The everyday version is grandma's rule: you may have your dessert (preferred) only after you eat your vegetables (less preferred). In practice with children this becomes a powerful and easily applied reinforcement strategy: a child may play video games (high probability behaviour) only after finishing homework (low probability behaviour), so the preferred activity reinforces completion of the chore. The theoretical importance is that Premack reframed reinforcement relativistically; rather than reinforcers being a fixed class of stimuli, any behaviour can reinforce another as long as it is more probable, which depends on the individual and the moment. This made reinforcement more flexible and individualised than Skinner's original notion. For the exam, the discriminating idea is the contingency of a preferred activity upon a less preferred one as a method of behaviour change, which is exactly a technique of behaviour modification rather than a theory of relationships, language, or parenting impact." },
  { id: "ihbas20_038", section: "therapy",
    q: "Subjective unit of distress (SUD) is mostly used under:",
    options: { a: "Exposure & response prevention", b: "Multi-model therapy", c: "Mindfulness-based stress management", d: "Systematic desensitization" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Exposure and response prevention does monitor distress, but it is the specific OCD protocol of confronting triggers while preventing rituals; SUDs is most classically tied to desensitization's graded hierarchy.", b: "Multimodal therapy (Lazarus) is a broad assessment framework (the BASIC ID), not a procedure built around a SUDs anxiety hierarchy.", c: "Mindfulness based stress management cultivates nonjudgmental present awareness; it does not centrally rely on SUDs ratings to grade exposure." },
    explanation: "The Subjective Units of Distress scale is used most centrally in systematic desensitization, so the answer is d. The SUDs scale, introduced by Joseph Wolpe, is a self report rating, typically from 0 (complete calm) to 100 (the worst distress imaginable), that lets a client quantify their level of anxiety or distress at a given moment. Systematic desensitization, also developed by Wolpe and grounded in reciprocal inhibition, is the behaviour therapy in which the client first learns deep muscle relaxation, then constructs a fear hierarchy, an ordered list of feared situations ranked by the anxiety each provokes. The SUDs ratings are precisely how that hierarchy is built and calibrated: the client assigns a SUDs value to each scene, so the items can be arranged from least to most distressing, and during treatment the therapist presents each item (in imagination or in vivo) while the client stays relaxed, moving up the hierarchy only when the SUDs for the current item has dropped to a low level. Thus SUDs is the measurement engine that organises the hierarchy and signals when to progress. While SUDs ratings are also used in exposure therapies generally, the question's framing about constructing graded levels of distress points to systematic desensitization, where the hierarchy and reciprocal inhibition with relaxation are the defining structure that SUDs serves." },
  { id: "ihbas20_039", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Impairment in Executive Function is characteristic of:",
    options: { a: "Obsessive-compulsive disorder", b: "Catatonic schizophrenia", c: "Frontal lobe syndrome", d: "Temporal lobe epilepsy" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Obsessive compulsive disorder involves frontostriatal circuits and some executive features, but its defining picture is obsessions and compulsions, not primary executive impairment.", b: "Catatonic schizophrenia is defined by motor disturbances such as stupor, rigidity, and posturing, not by an executive dysfunction syndrome.", d: "Temporal lobe epilepsy is associated with memory and sometimes emotional and behavioural changes tied to temporal structures, not the frontal dysexecutive syndrome." },
    explanation: "Impairment in executive function is characteristic of frontal lobe syndrome, so the answer is c. Executive functions are the higher order cognitive control processes, planning, organising, initiating, sequencing, inhibiting inappropriate responses, holding and manipulating information in working memory, set shifting, abstract reasoning, judgment, and self monitoring, that orchestrate goal directed behaviour. These functions depend heavily on the prefrontal cortex and its connections, so damage to the frontal lobes produces the dysexecutive picture known as frontal lobe syndrome. Classic features include impaired planning and problem solving, perseveration (getting stuck on a response), difficulty inhibiting impulses, poor judgment and insight, distractibility, apathy or conversely disinhibition, and personality change, famously illustrated by the case of Phineas Gage, whose frontal injury transformed his personality and self control. Tests sensitive to this syndrome include the Wisconsin Card Sorting Test, the Stroop task, verbal fluency, and the Trail Making Test. While executive dysfunction can appear secondarily in many disorders, the condition defined by and most characteristically presenting with primary executive impairment is frontal lobe syndrome, because the frontal lobes are the neural seat of executive control. The exam discriminator is the direct anatomical link between executive function and the frontal lobe, which makes frontal lobe syndrome the textbook answer over the other clinical conditions listed." },
  { id: "ihbas20_040", section: "assessment",
    q: "Confabulatory responses on Rorschach Psychodiagnostics are indicative of:",
    options: { a: "Impairment in reality testing", b: "Extreme lack of empathy", c: "Regressed ego", d: "Cognitive immaturity" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Extreme lack of empathy is more associated with the absence or quality of human responses on the Rorschach, not with confabulatory perception.", c: "Regressed ego is a broader psychodynamic inference; confabulation specifically indexes faulty reality testing rather than ego regression per se.", d: "Cognitive immaturity might produce simple or concrete responses, but confabulation specifically signals a disturbance in the logic of reality contact, more than mere immaturity." },
    explanation: "Confabulatory responses on the Rorschach indicate impairment in reality testing, so the answer is a. The Rorschach inkblot test is a projective assessment in which the examinee reports what they see in ambiguous blots, and the way they perceive and organise the stimulus reveals their thought processes and contact with reality. A confabulation (often scored DW or as a confabulatory response) occurs when the person uses a small, isolated detail of the blot to justify a global response about the whole area, overgeneralising from a fragment to an unjustified whole, for example seeing a tiny claw and therefore declaring the entire blot a monstrous crab. This kind of response reflects a loosening of the perceptual and logical link between what is actually on the card and the percept reported; the conclusion is not adequately grounded in the stimulus. Because reality testing is precisely the capacity to keep perceptions and inferences anchored to external reality and to distinguish internal impressions from objective fact, confabulatory responses, in which inference runs ahead of the perceptual evidence, signal impaired reality testing and are associated with psychotic level thought disturbance. The exam discriminator is recognising that confabulation on the Rorschach is a sign of a thought disorder and faulty reality contact, rather than a marker of empathy, ego regression, or mere cognitive immaturity." },
  { id: "ihbas20_041", section: "assessment",
    q: "Disorder in thinking can be effectively assessed by:",
    options: { a: "Rorschach Psychodiagnostics", b: "Thematic Apperception Test", c: "Bender Gestalt Test", d: "Object Sorting Test" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "The Rorschach can reveal thought disorder through perceptual logic, but it is a broad projective personality test, not the dedicated concept formation measure.", b: "The Thematic Apperception Test assesses needs, motives, and interpersonal themes through storytelling, not primarily the abstraction and categorisation of thought.", c: "The Bender Gestalt Test assesses visuomotor perception and integration, useful for organicity, not disorder in conceptual thinking." },
    explanation: "Disorder in thinking is most effectively assessed by the Object Sorting Test, so the answer is d. The Object Sorting Test, developed in the tradition of Goldstein and Scheerer and elaborated by Rapaport and others, presents the examinee with an array of everyday objects and asks them to sort objects that belong together and to explain the basis of their grouping, or to extend a grouping the examiner begins. The test directly probes concept formation and the abstract attitude: whether the person can form and shift categories flexibly and reason at an abstract conceptual level, or whether their thinking is concrete, overinclusive, idiosyncratic, or bizarre. It is particularly sensitive to the formal thought disorder of schizophrenia, where patients may sort on overly concrete grounds, on personalised symbolic grounds, or with fluid, overinclusive boundaries that lump unrelated objects together, revealing disturbances in conceptual thinking. Because it taps the form and organisation of thought, the abstraction, categorisation, and conceptual coherence, the Object Sorting Test is the classic instrument for assessing thought disorder. The discriminating point for the exam is that while Rorschach and TAT also reveal thought disturbance projectively, the test specifically designed to assess conceptual thinking and the abstract versus concrete attitude, and thus thought disorder, is the Object Sorting Test, and the Bender Gestalt assesses visuomotor function rather than thinking." },
  { id: "ihbas20_042", section: "assessment",
    q: "Elevated Lie scale on Multiphasic Questionnaire (MPQ) is indicative of:",
    options: { a: "Tendency to fake and manipulate", b: "Social desirability", c: "Mistrust", d: "All the above" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "A tendency to fake good and manipulate is one valid interpretation of elevated L, but it is not the only one, so choosing it alone is incomplete.", b: "Social desirability, answering to look good more automatically, is another genuine cause of elevated L, again only part of the full set.", c: "Mistrust and a guarded, defensive test taking attitude can also raise L, but it too is just one of several valid interpretations." },
    explanation: "An elevated Lie scale on a multiphasic questionnaire is indicative of all the above, so the answer is d. Personality inventories of the MMPI family include validity scales designed to detect distorted or invalid responding, and the L or Lie scale is one of them. The Lie scale consists of items describing minor faults and common human shortcomings that almost everyone has but that some people deny in order to look good, for example claiming never to tell even a small lie or never to feel angry. A high L score therefore signals that the respondent has been answering in an unrealistically favourable, virtuous way. This single elevation can reflect several overlapping things, which is why all the listed interpretations apply: a deliberate tendency to fake good and present a falsely positive picture (impression management or manipulation); a more automatic social desirability bias, the wish to be seen favourably without conscious deceit; and, in some clinical and forensic contexts, a guarded, mistrustful, defensive stance toward the testing situation. Because elevated L can stem from conscious faking, from social desirability, or from defensive mistrust, the comprehensive answer is all of the above. The exam point is to understand that validity scales like L do not have a single meaning; an elevated L flags a defensive, image protective response set whose specific motive must be interpreted in context." },
  { id: "ihbas20_043", section: "assessment",
    q: "Absence of 'Human' Response on Rorschach Psychodiagnostics Test indicates:",
    options: { a: "Apathy", b: "Poor interpersonal orientation", c: "Lack of empathy", d: "All the above" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Apathy, a general loss of motivation and feeling, is too broad; the absence of human responses specifically points to interpersonal disengagement, not global apathy.", c: "Lack of empathy is a stronger and more specific trait inference; the standard reading of absent human content is poor interpersonal orientation rather than measured empathy.", d: "All the above overreaches, since the precise and keyed interpretation is the single one about interpersonal orientation, not the combined set." },
    explanation: "Absence of human responses on the Rorschach indicates poor interpersonal orientation, so the answer is b. In Rorschach scoring, human content responses (H, and related categories) reflect the examinee's interest in, and capacity to represent, other people. Whole human responses in particular are taken to index the richness of a person's internalised representations of others and their orientation toward interpersonal relationships and social engagement. When human content is markedly absent, the inkblots evoke animals, objects, or abstractions but not people, the interpretive inference is that the person is poorly oriented toward others, withdrawn from or uninterested in interpersonal relating, and impoverished in their object relations. While clinicians might further link reduced human responses to traits like detachment, the most direct and standard interpretation of the absence of human responses is a deficient interpersonal orientation, a turning away from the social world. The exam discriminator is to choose the single, most specific and accurate interpretation rather than an all of the above; human content maps onto interest in people and relationships, so its absence maps onto poor interpersonal orientation. Apathy and lack of empathy are looser, less precise inferences that overreach what the absence of human content directly signals, which is reduced engagement with the human, relational world." },
  { id: "ihbas20_044", section: "abnormal",
    q: "High EE (Expressed Emotion) is known to cause:",
    options: { a: "Poor treatment outcome", b: "Relapse of psychotic illness", c: "Negative symptoms of schizophrenia", d: "Violent behavior in schizophrenic patients" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Poor treatment outcome is too vague and downstream; the specific, well documented effect of high EE is relapse, which is the keyed and precise answer.", c: "High EE is linked to relapse, not to causing negative symptoms of schizophrenia, which arise from the illness process itself.", d: "Violent behaviour in patients is not the established consequence of high EE; the robust finding concerns relapse risk." },
    explanation: "High expressed emotion is known to cause relapse of psychotic illness, so the answer is b. Expressed emotion (EE) is a measure of the emotional climate in a patient's home, specifically the attitudes that key relatives express toward the patient, assessed through the Camberwell Family Interview. High EE comprises three components: critical comments, hostility, and emotional overinvolvement (overprotective, intrusive, or excessively self sacrificing behaviour). The landmark finding, originating with Brown, Birley, and Wing and replicated widely, is that patients with schizophrenia who return to live with high EE relatives have substantially higher rates of relapse over the following nine to twelve months than those returning to low EE households, even after controlling for medication and symptom severity. The proposed mechanism is that a critical, hostile, or overinvolved environment raises the patient's stress and arousal, which, in a person already biologically vulnerable, can precipitate symptom recurrence, consistent with a stress vulnerability model. This evidence underpins family psychoeducation interventions that lower EE and thereby reduce relapse, and the related strategy of reducing the number of face to face contact hours between patient and high EE relatives. The exam discriminator is that the specific, classic outcome attributed to high EE is relapse of psychotic illness, a relapse predictor, rather than a cause of the illness itself, its negative symptoms, or violence." },
  { id: "ihbas20_045", section: "assessment",
    q: "The instrument used for the assessment of disability in autism is:",
    options: { a: "CARS", b: "ISAA", c: "DAAS", d: "PAAS" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "ISAA, the Indian Scale for Assessment of Autism, is actually the statutory Indian disability certification tool, but per the source key CARS is marked correct; note this is the genuinely arguable alternative.", c: "DAAS is not a recognised standard autism disability instrument and functions here as a distractor.", d: "PAAS is likewise not a recognised standard autism assessment scale and is a distractor." },
    explanation: "The keyed answer for assessing disability in autism is CARS, the Childhood Autism Rating Scale, so the answer is a. A point of clarification matters here for accuracy. In the Indian certification context, the official instrument for quantifying disability in autism is the Indian Scale for Assessment of Autism (ISAA), developed for the certification of autism related disability under government guidelines. The CARS is a widely used 15 item rating scale developed by Schopler and colleagues that rates the severity of autistic features across domains such as relating to people, imitation, emotional response, and communication, and yields a total score classifying a child as non autistic, mildly to moderately autistic, or severely autistic. Internationally CARS is the classic severity rating tool, and the source key for this paper marks CARS as correct, so teach CARS as the keyed answer while being aware that for Indian disability certification specifically the ISAA is the designated statutory tool. The exam takeaway is to know both: CARS for rating autism severity and identification, and ISAA as the India specific disability assessment and certification scale. If the question intends the Indian certification instrument, ISAA would be defensible; per the given source key, CARS is the marked answer, and you should be ready for either framing." },
  { id: "ihbas20_046", section: "therapy",
    q: "Dialectical Behaviour Therapy is indicated for the treatment of:",
    options: { a: "Anxiety Disorders", b: "Borderline Personality Disorder", c: "Acute and Transient Psychotic Disorder", d: "Post-traumatic Stress Disorder" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Anxiety disorders are first line treated with exposure based CBT, not the comprehensive emotion regulation programme of DBT.", c: "Acute and transient psychotic disorder requires antipsychotic medication and psychosocial support, not DBT skills training.", d: "Post traumatic stress disorder is primarily treated with trauma focused CBT, prolonged exposure, or EMDR, not DBT as the indicated treatment." },
    explanation: "Dialectical Behaviour Therapy is indicated for borderline personality disorder, so the answer is b. DBT was developed by Marsha Linehan specifically for chronically suicidal and self harming individuals, most of whom met criteria for borderline personality disorder, a condition marked by pervasive emotional dysregulation, unstable relationships and self image, impulsivity, recurrent self harm, and intense fear of abandonment. DBT integrates cognitive behavioural change strategies with acceptance based and mindfulness practices drawn from Zen, organised around the dialectic of acceptance and change: the therapist validates the patient's experience while simultaneously pressing for behavioural change. Its structure typically includes weekly individual therapy, group skills training, telephone coaching for in the moment crises, and a therapist consultation team. The four skills modules are mindfulness, distress tolerance, emotion regulation, and interpersonal effectiveness, which directly target the deficits underlying borderline pathology. DBT has the strongest evidence base of any treatment for BPD, reducing suicide attempts, self harm, hospitalisations, and dropout. While DBT and its adaptations are now applied to other conditions involving emotion dysregulation, its original and primary indication, and the keyed answer, is borderline personality disorder. The exam discriminator is the tight historical and clinical pairing of Linehan, emotion dysregulation, self harm, and BPD that defines DBT." },
  { id: "ihbas20_047", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Mode of suicide attempt indicates:",
    options: { a: "Intentionality", b: "Lethality", c: "Conviction", d: "Survival" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Intentionality concerns the strength of the wish to die, a subjective state, whereas the mode or method indicates the objective dangerousness, which is lethality.", c: "Conviction is not a standard dimension of suicide assessment and does not correspond to the dangerousness implied by the method.", d: "Survival is the outcome of the attempt, not what the chosen method indicates about risk; the method indicates lethality." },
    explanation: "The mode of a suicide attempt indicates lethality, so the answer is b. In suicide risk assessment, several related but distinct dimensions must be evaluated. Intentionality (or intent) refers to how much the person actually wished to die, their subjective resolve to end life. Lethality refers to the objective, medical dangerousness of the method used or planned, how likely that means is to cause death. The mode or method of an attempt, for instance taking a few paracetamol tablets versus using a firearm or hanging, is the chief indicator of lethality, because the choice of means determines how deadly the act is medically. This distinction is clinically vital because intent and lethality do not always match; a person may have high intent but use a low lethality method through ignorance of its effects, or low intent yet inadvertently choose a high lethality means. Clinicians assess both: the seriousness of the wish to die and the dangerousness of the method, along with precautions against discovery and the person's belief about how lethal the method was. The exam discriminator is the precise mapping of the word mode (the method chosen) onto lethality (the objective dangerousness of that method), as opposed to intentionality, which concerns the inner wish. So the medical dangerousness implied by the method chosen is lethality." },
  { id: "ihbas20_048", section: "assessment",
    q: "Trail Making Test helps to assess ______ in children with ADHD.",
    options: { a: "Inattention", b: "Social Skills", c: "Impulsivity", d: "Over-activity" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Social skills are interpersonal abilities assessed by observation or rating scales, not by a paper and pencil attention and sequencing task.", c: "Impulsivity is better measured by continuous performance tests with commission errors; the Trail Making Test chiefly indexes attention and speed.", d: "Over activity is the motoric hyperactivity symptom, observed behaviourally, not what a sustained attention tracking task primarily measures." },
    explanation: "The Trail Making Test helps assess inattention in children with ADHD, so the answer is a. The Trail Making Test has two parts. Part A requires the examinee to connect numbered circles in ascending order as quickly as possible, and Part B requires alternating between numbers and letters in sequence (1, A, 2, B, and so on). Part A primarily taps visual scanning, sustained attention, visuomotor speed, and the ability to maintain focus on a target sequence, while Part B adds set shifting and cognitive flexibility, an executive demand. In ADHD, the component the test is best suited to capture among the options given is inattention: completing the trails requires sustained, focused attention and continuous visual search, and children with the inattentive features of ADHD show slowed, error prone performance reflecting lapses of attention and difficulty maintaining the mental set. Although ADHD also involves impulsivity and hyperactivity, those are better captured by other measures such as continuous performance tests for impulsivity or behavioural observation for overactivity. The Trail Making Test specifically indexes the attentional and processing speed dimension, mapping onto the inattentive symptom cluster. The exam discriminator is matching the Trail Making Test's core demand, sustained attention and visual tracking, to inattention rather than to the motoric or impulsive symptoms of ADHD." },
  { id: "ihbas20_049", section: "therapy",
    q: "Which of the following is not a part of Parent Management Training?",
    options: { a: "Attending positive behaviour", b: "Rewarding positive behaviour", c: "Ignoring undesirable behaviour", d: "Negative reinforcement" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Attending to positive behaviour is a core PMT skill, training parents to notice and engage with desirable behaviour.", b: "Rewarding positive behaviour with praise and tokens is central positive reinforcement and a defining PMT technique.", c: "Ignoring undesirable behaviour is planned extinction, a standard PMT strategy to reduce attention seeking misbehaviour." },
    explanation: "Negative reinforcement is not a part of Parent Management Training, so the answer is d. Parent Management Training (PMT) is a behavioural intervention, rooted in social learning and operant principles and associated with researchers like Gerald Patterson, in which parents are taught to change their children's problem behaviour, especially oppositional and conduct problems, by altering how they respond to it. The core skills parents learn are attending to and praising prosocial behaviour, rewarding desirable behaviour with positive reinforcement and token systems, giving clear effective commands, and selectively ignoring (extinction) or applying mild negative consequences like time out for undesirable behaviour. The guiding logic is to increase desirable behaviour through positive reinforcement and to decrease undesirable behaviour through ignoring, time out, and loss of privileges. Negative reinforcement, which means increasing a behaviour by removing an aversive stimulus, is exactly the trap that PMT teaches parents to avoid, because in coercive family cycles parents inadvertently negatively reinforce children's tantrums by withdrawing demands when the child escalates, which strengthens the very misbehaviour. So negative reinforcement is not a deliberate component of PMT; if anything the programme corrects parents' accidental use of it. The exam discriminator is recognising that attending, rewarding, and ignoring are taught PMT techniques, whereas negative reinforcement is not a prescribed component and is the odd one out." },
  { id: "ihbas20_050", section: "therapy",
    q: "A male adult with Depressive Disorder and marital discord would benefit more from:",
    options: { a: "Psychodynamic psychotherapy", b: "Cognitive behavior therapy", c: "Marital & couple therapy", d: "Supportive therapy" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Psychodynamic psychotherapy explores intrapsychic conflict and would not directly address the ongoing marital interaction driving the depression.", b: "Cognitive behaviour therapy is effective for depression generally but, delivered individually, it does not directly treat the dyadic conflict that is central here.", d: "Supportive therapy provides reassurance and bolstering but lacks the structured focus on the relational patterns maintaining the problem." },
    explanation: "A male adult with depressive disorder and marital discord would benefit more from marital and couple therapy, so the answer is c. Treatment selection in clinical psychology is guided by formulation: matching the intervention to the principal maintaining factors of the presentation. Here the depression is occurring in the context of, and is intertwined with, significant marital conflict. When relationship distress is a central maintaining and precipitating factor for depression, treating the depression in isolation is less effective than addressing the relational system that feeds it. Couple or marital therapy, and behavioural couple therapy in particular, has good evidence for treating depression that is embedded in relationship discord, because it targets the negative interactional patterns, communication failures, and lack of support and reinforcement within the marriage that both result from and perpetuate the low mood. Improving the relationship reduces the interpersonal stress driving the depression and can lift mood as effectively as individual therapy in such cases, while also resolving the marital problem the individual approaches would leave untouched. The exam discriminator is recognising the comorbid relational context: when the question pairs a mood disorder explicitly with marital discord, the indicated and most efficient choice is the modality that treats the couple system, not an individual only therapy." },
  { id: "ihbas20_051", section: "therapy",
    q: "Exposure & response prevention is the most effective treatment of:",
    options: { a: "Obsessive-compulsive disorder", b: "Social anxiety disorder", c: "Claustrophobia", d: "Panic disorder" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Social anxiety disorder is best treated with exposure plus cognitive restructuring within CBT; ERP's ritual prevention focus is specific to OCD compulsions.", c: "Claustrophobia, a specific phobia, responds to graded exposure or systematic desensitization, not specifically to response prevention of rituals.", d: "Panic disorder is treated with interoceptive exposure and cognitive therapy targeting catastrophic misinterpretation, not ERP for compulsions." },
    explanation: "Exposure and response prevention is the most effective treatment of obsessive compulsive disorder, so the answer is a. OCD is characterised by intrusive, distressing obsessions and by compulsions, repetitive behaviours or mental acts performed to neutralise the anxiety the obsessions provoke. The behavioural model explains the disorder through negative reinforcement: the compulsion temporarily relieves the anxiety, which reinforces the ritual and prevents the person from ever learning that the feared catastrophe would not happen anyway. Exposure and response prevention (ERP), developed by Victor Meyer and refined by Edna Foa and others, directly breaks this cycle. The exposure component has the patient deliberately confront the obsessional triggers, the contaminated doorknob, the unlocked door, the intrusive blasphemous thought, while the response prevention component has them refrain from performing the compulsive ritual. By staying in contact with the trigger without ritualising, the patient experiences that anxiety habituates and declines on its own and that the feared outcome does not occur, producing extinction of the obsessional fear and the compulsive behaviour. ERP has the strongest evidence base of any psychological treatment for OCD and is the first line behavioural intervention. The exam discriminator is the tight, specific match between ERP's mechanism, confronting triggers while blocking rituals, and the obsession compulsion cycle of OCD, rather than the other anxiety conditions listed." },
  { id: "ihbas20_052", section: "therapy",
    q: "Validation Therapy is very helpful in the psychological management of:",
    options: { a: "Post-traumatic stress disorder", b: "Organic personality disorder", c: "Acute and transient psychotic disorder", d: "Alzheimer's Dementia" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Post traumatic stress disorder is treated with trauma focused therapies; validation therapy is a dementia care approach, not a PTSD treatment.", b: "Organic personality disorder involves personality change from brain damage, managed differently; validation therapy is specific to the disoriented dementia patient.", c: "Acute and transient psychotic disorder needs antipsychotic treatment, not the empathic reality accepting approach designed for dementia." },
    explanation: "Validation therapy is most helpful in managing Alzheimer's dementia, so the answer is d. Validation therapy was developed by Naomi Feil for older adults with dementia, particularly Alzheimer's type. Its core philosophy contrasts with reality orientation, the older approach that repeatedly corrects the disoriented person and insists on the here and now facts, which can increase distress and agitation. Validation instead holds that the disoriented person is attempting to resolve unfinished life issues and that their subjective reality, however factually mistaken, has emotional meaning. The therapist therefore enters and accepts the person's reality, acknowledges and validates the feelings behind what the person says rather than arguing about facts, uses empathy and reminiscence, and communicates respect and acceptance. The aims are to reduce anxiety and agitation, restore dignity and a sense of being understood, and improve communication and wellbeing, even when memory and orientation cannot be restored. Because dementia progressively erodes the capacity for factual reorientation, an empathic, feeling focused, accepting approach is often more humane and effective than correction, which is why validation therapy is indicated for Alzheimer's dementia. The exam discriminator is the link between validation, accepting the patient's reality and validating emotions, and the irreversible cognitive decline of dementia, where insistence on factual reality fails." },
  { id: "ihbas20_053", section: "assessment",
    q: "Impact of psychiatric illness on one's cognitive and intellectual abilities can be estimated on the basis of:",
    options: { a: "Deterioration quotient", b: "Mediation index", c: "Sense of coherence index", d: "Theory of Mind index" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Mediation index is not a standard measure of cognitive deterioration and serves as a distractor here.", c: "Sense of coherence index (Antonovsky) measures a salutogenic orientation to life, not cognitive decline from illness.", d: "Theory of Mind index concerns the ability to attribute mental states to others, a social cognition construct, not overall intellectual deterioration." },
    explanation: "The impact of psychiatric illness on cognitive and intellectual abilities is estimated by the deterioration quotient, so the answer is a. The deterioration quotient (also called the deterioration index) is a measure derived classically from the Wechsler intelligence scales. Wechsler observed that some cognitive abilities are relatively resistant to the effects of illness, ageing, and brain damage, the so called hold tests such as vocabulary, information, and object assembly, which reflect previously acquired knowledge and tend to be preserved, while other abilities are vulnerable and decline, the dont hold tests such as digit span, digit symbol coding, and similarities, which depend on current efficiency, attention, and processing speed. By comparing performance on the hold subtests, taken as an estimate of premorbid ability, with performance on the dont hold subtests, taken as current functioning, one can compute a deterioration quotient that estimates how much intellectual decline has occurred relative to the person's likely former level. This gives a clinical index of the impact of a psychiatric or organic condition on cognition. The exam discriminator is recognising the deterioration quotient as the hold versus dont hold comparison used to gauge cognitive decline, distinguishing it from the other invented or unrelated indices listed, which do not measure intellectual deterioration." },
  { id: "ihbas20_054", section: "therapy",
    q: "Which therapy is mostly needed in 'Crisis Management'?",
    options: { a: "Cognitive behavior therapy", b: "Acceptance & commitment therapy", c: "Expressive therapy", d: "Supportive therapy" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Cognitive behaviour therapy is a structured change oriented treatment requiring engagement over sessions, less suited to the immediate stabilising needs of acute crisis.", b: "Acceptance and commitment therapy fosters psychological flexibility over time and is not primarily a rapid crisis stabilisation method.", c: "Expressive therapy explores and uncovers feelings and conflicts, which can be destabilising in an acute crisis where containment and support are needed." },
    explanation: "Supportive therapy is most needed in crisis management, so the answer is d. A psychological crisis is a state in which a person's usual coping mechanisms are overwhelmed by an acute stressor, producing intense distress, disorganisation, and a temporary loss of equilibrium. The goals of crisis intervention are immediate: to stabilise the person, ensure safety, reduce overwhelming affect, restore a sense of control and hope, mobilise resources and social support, and help the person return to at least their precrisis level of functioning. Supportive therapy is well suited to this because its techniques, active listening, reassurance, emotional support, ventilation of feelings, bolstering existing defences and adaptive coping, providing structure and practical guidance, and strengthening the therapeutic relationship, directly meet the needs of someone in acute distress. Unlike insight oriented or restructuring therapies, supportive therapy does not try to uncover unconscious conflict or challenge cognitions in the heat of the crisis; it shores the person up. The other therapies are valuable but are change oriented, longer term modalities better suited once the acute crisis has settled. The exam discriminator is matching the immediate, stabilising, ego bolstering aims of crisis intervention to the supportive, here and now, reassurance based stance of supportive therapy rather than to the more demanding cognitive, acceptance, or expressive approaches." },
  { id: "ihbas20_055", section: "biopsych",
    q: "A person suffered a head injury in a road accident several years ago. Since the accident, he is unable to form new memories. He can remember many things which he did before the accident, but he cannot remember what he said ten minutes ago. The part of the brain which is most probably injured is:",
    options: { a: "Brain stem", b: "Hippocampus", c: "Spinal cord", d: "Reticular formation" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "The brain stem governs vital autonomic functions like breathing and heart rate; damage there would not selectively impair new memory formation.", c: "The spinal cord conducts motor and sensory signals between body and brain; it has no role in forming memories.", d: "The reticular formation regulates arousal, sleep, and alertness; its damage affects consciousness and attention, not the specific encoding of new memories." },
    explanation: "The brain region most likely injured is the hippocampus, so the answer is b. The case describes anterograde amnesia: the man can recall events from before his accident (intact remote and old memories) but cannot form new long term memories, forgetting what he said ten minutes ago. This is the classic signature of bilateral hippocampal damage, made famous by the patient H.M., who after surgical removal of his medial temporal lobes including the hippocampi was unable to form new declarative memories while retaining older memories and normal short term memory and intelligence. The hippocampus is essential for consolidation, the process of converting new short term and working memories into durable long term memories that can later be retrieved. It is not, however, the storage site for old memories, which is why remote memories laid down before the injury survive: those have already been consolidated and distributed to cortical stores, so they remain accessible even when the hippocampus is damaged. Hence damage to the hippocampus selectively impairs the formation of new memories while sparing old ones, exactly the pattern described. The exam discriminator is the dissociation between preserved old memories and an inability to lay down new ones, which points unmistakably to the hippocampus and its role in memory consolidation rather than to brainstem, spinal, or arousal structures." },
  { id: "ihbas20_056", section: "therapy",
    q: "Over-correction is a behavioural technique to manage:",
    options: { a: "Bed wetting in children", b: "Repetitive aggressive behaviour in autistic children", c: "Temper tantrum in children", d: "Alienation in adolescent" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Bed wetting in children is typically treated with the bell and pad (urine alarm) conditioning method, not overcorrection.", c: "Temper tantrums are usually managed with planned ignoring (extinction) and time out, not the restitution and positive practice of overcorrection.", d: "Alienation in adolescents is a relational and emotional issue addressed by counselling, not a target for an operant overcorrection procedure." },
    explanation: "Overcorrection is a behavioural technique to manage repetitive aggressive behaviour in autistic children, so the answer is b. Overcorrection, developed by Foxx and Azrin, is a behaviour reduction (punishment based) procedure used especially to decrease disruptive, aggressive, stereotyped, or self stimulatory behaviours, often in individuals with autism or intellectual disability. It has two main forms. Restitutional overcorrection requires the person, after misbehaving, to restore the environment to a state far better than before the disruption; for example, a child who overturns a table must set it right and then also tidy the whole room. Positive practice overcorrection requires the person to repeatedly perform an appropriate alternative behaviour many times over, practising the correct behaviour intensively. The procedure reduces the target behaviour both by making it effortful and aversive to engage in (the contingent effort) and by teaching and rehearsing adaptive alternatives. It has been applied to repetitive aggressive and self injurious or stereotyped behaviours in children with autism, which is the keyed context. The exam discriminator is associating overcorrection, with its restitution and positive practice components, with the management of repetitive, aggressive, or stereotyped behaviour in autistic children, rather than with enuresis, tantrums, or adolescent alienation, where other techniques are primary." },
  { id: "ihbas20_057", section: "therapy",
    q: "Motivational interviewing is used in the management of:",
    options: { a: "Non-compliance to treatment", b: "Substance abuse", c: "Anxious-avoidant personality disorder", d: "Bipolar disorder" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Non compliance to treatment is an area where MI has been applied, but its defining and original indication, and the keyed answer, is substance abuse.", c: "Anxious avoidant personality disorder is treated with psychotherapy targeting avoidance and self esteem, not specifically with MI.", d: "Bipolar disorder requires mood stabilising medication and psychoeducation; MI is not its core management approach." },
    explanation: "Motivational interviewing is used in the management of substance abuse, so the answer is b. Motivational interviewing (MI) was developed by William Miller and Stephen Rollnick specifically in the field of addictions to help people resolve the ambivalence that keeps them stuck in problematic substance use. It is a collaborative, person centred, goal directed counselling style that strengthens the person's own motivation and commitment to change rather than imposing it. Its spirit emphasises partnership, acceptance, compassion, and evocation, and it uses core skills summarised as OARS: open questions, affirmations, reflective listening, and summaries. The clinician deliberately elicits and reinforces change talk, the person's own arguments for changing, while rolling with resistance rather than confronting it, on the principle that people are more persuaded by what they hear themselves say. MI fits the stages of change (transtheoretical) model and is especially powerful for people in precontemplation or contemplation who are ambivalent about quitting drugs or alcohol. Although MI has since been applied to other health behaviours and to treatment non adherence, its origin and primary, best evidenced indication, and the keyed answer, is substance abuse. The exam discriminator is the historical and conceptual home of MI in addictions and ambivalence about substance use rather than the broader applications." },
  { id: "ihbas20_058", section: "general",
    q: "Who was called the 'French Freud'?",
    options: { a: "Jules Pierre Falret", b: "Jean-Etienne Esquirol", c: "Paul Briquet", d: "Jacques Lacan" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Jules Pierre Falret was a nineteenth century French psychiatrist who described folie circulaire (circular insanity), an early account of bipolar illness, not the French Freud.", b: "Jean Etienne Esquirol was a pioneering French alienist who advanced the classification of mental disorders and asylum reform, predating Freud entirely.", c: "Paul Briquet described what became known as Briquet's syndrome (somatisation, hysteria); he was not the psychoanalytic heir nicknamed the French Freud." },
    explanation: "Jacques Lacan was called the French Freud, so the answer is d. Lacan was the most influential French psychoanalyst of the twentieth century, who launched a return to Freud, urging analysts to read Freud's texts closely while reinterpreting psychoanalysis through structural linguistics and structuralism. His famous claims include that the unconscious is structured like a language, and his theory of the three registers, the Imaginary, the Symbolic, and the Real, along with the mirror stage, in which the infant forms an ego by identifying with its unified reflected image, a misrecognition that founds the self. His seminars dominated French intellectual life and shaped psychoanalysis, philosophy, and literary theory, earning him the epithet the French Freud for his stature and his project of renewing Freudian thought in France. For the exam this is a recognition item linking a nickname to a figure, and it usefully anchors Lacan's distinctive reworking of Freud through language and the symbolic order. The discriminating skill is distinguishing Lacan from the nineteenth century French alienists in the other options, Falret, Esquirol, and Briquet, who made important contributions to descriptive psychiatry but were not the structuralist heir to Freud honoured with that nickname." },
  { id: "ihbas20_059", section: "therapy",
    q: "Dream work as described by Freud operates by the following mechanisms except:",
    options: { a: "Displacement", b: "Condensation", c: "Dissociation", d: "Dramatisation" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Displacement is a genuine mechanism of dream work, shifting emotional emphasis from a significant latent element to an innocuous manifest one.", b: "Condensation is a genuine mechanism, fusing several latent thoughts into a single overdetermined manifest image.", d: "Dramatisation (considerations of representability) is a genuine mechanism, converting abstract latent thoughts into concrete visual, dramatic scenes." },
    explanation: "Dream work operates by all the listed mechanisms except dissociation, so the answer is c. In Freud's theory, set out in The Interpretation of Dreams, a dream has a latent content, the unconscious wishes and thoughts that prompt it, and a manifest content, the dream as actually experienced and remembered. Dream work is the set of unconscious processes that transform the forbidden latent material into the disguised, acceptable manifest dream, allowing the wish to be expressed while protecting sleep and evading the censor. Its mechanisms are: condensation, in which several latent ideas are compressed into a single manifest image, so one dream element carries multiple meanings; displacement, in which emotional significance is shifted from an important latent element onto a trivial manifest one, disguising what really matters; symbolisation, the representation of latent content by symbols; and considerations of representability or dramatisation, the conversion of abstract thoughts into concrete visual imagery and a dramatic scene. Secondary revision then knits the dream into a more coherent narrative. Dissociation, by contrast, is a defence mechanism involving a disruption in the integration of consciousness, memory, or identity (as in dissociative disorders), and it is not one of Freud's mechanisms of dream work. The exam discriminator is recognising the genuine dream work mechanisms, condensation, displacement, dramatisation, symbolisation, and spotting dissociation as the intruder that belongs to a different conceptual category." },
  { id: "ihbas20_060", section: "therapy",
    q: "'Animus is the unconscious, masculine side of a woman's female persona' was given by:",
    options: { a: "Alfred Adler", b: "Anna Freud", c: "Carl Jung", d: "Victor Emil Frankl" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Alfred Adler founded individual psychology, centred on inferiority feelings, striving for superiority, and social interest, not the anima and animus.", b: "Anna Freud advanced ego psychology and catalogued the defence mechanisms; she did not propose the contrasexual archetypes.", d: "Victor Frankl founded logotherapy, focused on the search for meaning, an existential approach unrelated to Jung's archetypes." },
    explanation: "The concept of the animus as the unconscious masculine side of a woman's psyche was given by Carl Jung, so the answer is c. Jung, the founder of analytical psychology, broke with Freud and developed his own model of the psyche populated by archetypes, universal, inherited patterns residing in the collective unconscious. Among these archetypes are the anima and the animus, the contrasexual inner figures. The anima is the unconscious feminine side of a man, and the animus is the unconscious masculine side of a woman. According to Jung every person carries an inner image of the opposite sex, shaped by both the collective archetype and personal experience of the other sex, especially the parents. This inner figure influences how the person relates to the opposite sex and is often projected onto partners. Integrating the anima or animus into consciousness is part of Jung's individuation, the lifelong process of becoming a whole, balanced self by reconciling the conscious and unconscious and the various archetypes including the persona, the shadow, and the self. The exam discriminator is the distinctly Jungian vocabulary, animus, anima, archetype, collective unconscious, which sets Jung apart from Adler, Anna Freud, and Frankl, none of whom used these contrasexual archetype concepts." },
  { id: "ihbas20_061", section: "therapy",
    q: "Schizophrenia corresponds to the autistic phase of development and the person fails to develop an adequate and stable symbiosis, was the developmental theory of schizophrenia given by:",
    options: { a: "Ping-Nie Pao", b: "Sylvano Arieti", c: "Margaret Mahler", d: "Edith Jacobson" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Ping Nie Pao wrote on the schizophrenic disorders from an ego psychological view but did not frame it through Mahler's autistic and symbiotic phases.", b: "Sylvano Arieti authored an influential interpersonal and psychodynamic theory of schizophrenia, but the autistic phase and symbiosis formulation is Mahler's.", d: "Edith Jacobson contributed to the psychoanalytic understanding of the self and depression, not this specific developmental theory of schizophrenia." },
    explanation: "The developmental theory that schizophrenia corresponds to the autistic phase and a failure to develop stable symbiosis was given by Margaret Mahler, so the answer is c. Mahler was a psychoanalyst who studied the development of the self in infancy through her separation individuation theory. She proposed that the infant moves through phases: an early normal autistic phase (the first weeks, a self absorbed, undifferentiated state with little awareness of the outside world), followed by the normal symbiotic phase (around the second to fifth month, in which the infant experiences a fused, dual unity with the mother as if they were one boundaryless system), and then the separation individuation process, with its subphases of differentiation, practising, rapprochement, and consolidation of individuality. Mahler theorised that severe psychopathology reflects fixation at or failure to negotiate these earliest phases. In her view, the most disturbed conditions correspond to the earliest phases: psychotic disturbance is linked to a failure to move beyond the autistic and symbiotic phases, with schizophrenia reflecting the autistic phase and an inability to form an adequate, stable symbiosis with the mother, leaving the self without a secure foundation. The exam discriminator is the specific Mahlerian language of autistic and symbiotic phases and separation individuation, which distinguishes her account from those of Pao, Arieti, and Jacobson." },
  { id: "ihbas20_062", section: "therapy",
    q: "The concept of 'Organ Inferiority' was given by:",
    options: { a: "Harry Stack Sullivan", b: "M. Klein", c: "Anna Freud", d: "Alfred Adler" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Harry Stack Sullivan founded interpersonal psychiatry, emphasising interpersonal relations and anxiety, not the concept of organ inferiority.", b: "Melanie Klein developed object relations theory with concepts like the paranoid schizoid and depressive positions, not organ inferiority.", c: "Anna Freud is known for ego psychology and the defence mechanisms, not for the Adlerian idea of organ inferiority." },
    explanation: "The concept of organ inferiority was given by Alfred Adler, so the answer is d. Adler, founder of individual psychology, introduced organ inferiority early in his career to describe how a constitutionally weak or defective organ or body part creates a sense of inferiority that the person then strives to compensate for, sometimes overcompensating into a particular strength. He generalised this into one of his central ideas: that feelings of inferiority are universal in childhood, since every child is small and dependent among more capable adults, and that the drive to overcome this inferiority, the striving for superiority or for mastery and competence, is the master motive of human behaviour. When compensation goes awry, the person may develop an inferiority complex, a pervasive sense of inadequacy, or conversely a superiority complex as an overcompensating mask. Adler also emphasised social interest, birth order, the unity and goal directedness of the personality, and the individual's unique style of life. The exam discriminator is the distinctly Adlerian concepts, organ inferiority, inferiority and superiority complexes, compensation, striving for superiority, and social interest, which separate Adler from Sullivan's interpersonal theory, Klein's object relations, and Anna Freud's ego psychology. So organ inferiority is unmistakably Adler's." },
  { id: "ihbas20_063", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Hyperkinetic syndrome is characterized by the following except:",
    options: { a: "Social Withdrawal", b: "Over-activity", c: "Distractibility", d: "Short attention-span" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Over activity is a core feature of hyperkinetic syndrome, the excessive restless motor behaviour central to the disorder.", c: "Distractibility is a core feature, reflecting the inattention dimension of the syndrome.", d: "Short attention span is a core feature, again part of the inattention that defines the condition." },
    explanation: "Hyperkinetic syndrome is characterised by everything listed except social withdrawal, so the answer is a. Hyperkinetic disorder is the ICD term broadly corresponding to attention deficit hyperactivity disorder, a neurodevelopmental condition with onset in early childhood. Its three core, defining features are inattention (a short attention span, being easily distracted, failing to finish tasks, and difficulty sustaining concentration), hyperactivity (excessive, poorly regulated motor activity, restlessness, an inability to sit still, and overactivity), and impulsivity (acting without thinking, blurting out, and difficulty waiting). The cardinal clinical picture is therefore an overactive, distractible, impulsive child with a short attention span. Social withdrawal, a tendency to avoid social contact and isolate oneself, is not a feature of hyperkinetic syndrome; if anything, children with the disorder are typically socially overactive, intrusive, and disinhibited rather than withdrawn, although they may experience peer rejection as a consequence of their behaviour. Social withdrawal is more characteristic of conditions like depression, autism spectrum disorder, or anxiety. So among the options, overactivity, distractibility, and short attention span are all genuine features of the syndrome, while social withdrawal is the odd one out and the correct exception. The exam discriminator is knowing the core triad of inattention, hyperactivity, and impulsivity and recognising that withdrawal contradicts the overactive, disinhibited profile." },
  { id: "ihbas20_064", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Infantile Autism was first described by:",
    options: { a: "Leo Kanner", b: "Cameron", c: "Goldstein", d: "Jasper" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Cameron is associated with the concept of overinclusive thinking in schizophrenia, not with describing infantile autism.", c: "Goldstein is known for work on the abstract attitude and brain injury (organicity), not for the first description of autism.", d: "Jasper is linked with EEG and epilepsy research (Penfield and Jasper), not with infantile autism." },
    explanation: "Infantile autism was first described by Leo Kanner, so the answer is a. In 1943 Leo Kanner, an Austrian American child psychiatrist at Johns Hopkins, published Autistic Disturbances of Affective Contact, describing eleven children who shared a striking pattern he termed early infantile autism. The features he identified became the historical core of the autism concept: a profound inability to relate to people from the beginning of life (which he called autistic aloneness), an anxious, obsessive desire for sameness and resistance to change in routines and environment, delayed or deviant language including echolalia and pronoun reversal, and sometimes islands of unusual ability or rote memory. Kanner borrowed the word autism from Bleuler, who had used it for the self absorbed withdrawal of schizophrenia, but Kanner applied it to a distinct, early appearing developmental condition. Around the same time Hans Asperger independently described a related milder presentation. Kanner's account launched the modern study of autism and remains a landmark for the field. The exam discriminator is the firm historical attribution: Kanner 1943, infantile autism, distinguishing him from the other names listed, who are associated with thought disorder, neuropsychology, and EEG respectively, not with the first description of autism." },
  { id: "ihbas20_065", section: "dev",
    q: "Jean Piaget is known for:",
    options: { a: "Free association", b: "Operant Conditioning", c: "Aggression as learned social behavior", d: "Intelligence as a biological adaptation" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Free association is Freud's psychoanalytic technique, not Piaget's contribution.", b: "Operant conditioning is associated with B.F. Skinner and the consequences shaping behaviour, not with Piaget.", c: "Aggression as learned social behaviour is Bandura's social learning thesis (the Bobo doll studies), not Piaget's work." },
    explanation: "Jean Piaget is known for viewing intelligence as a biological adaptation, so the answer is d. Piaget, the Swiss developmental psychologist, approached cognitive development from a biological standpoint, treating intelligence as a form of adaptation by which the organism achieves equilibrium with its environment, much as biological systems adapt. In his theory, knowledge grows through two complementary processes: assimilation, fitting new experiences into existing mental structures or schemas, and accommodation, modifying those schemas to fit new information. The drive toward equilibration, balancing assimilation and accommodation, propels development. Piaget proposed four universal stages of cognitive development: the sensorimotor stage (birth to about two, learning through senses and action, acquiring object permanence), the preoperational stage (about two to seven, symbolic thought but egocentrism and lack of conservation), the concrete operational stage (about seven to eleven, logical operations on concrete objects, mastery of conservation), and the formal operational stage (about eleven onward, abstract and hypothetical reasoning). Across all of this, intelligence is conceived as adaptive functioning rooted in biology, the child actively constructing knowledge. The exam discriminator is associating Piaget with the constructivist, biological adaptation view of intelligence and the stage theory, distinguishing him from Freud's free association, Skinner's operant conditioning, and Bandura's social learning account of aggression." },
  { id: "ihbas20_066", section: "social",
    q: "'The more we see and interact with a person, the more likely he or she is to become our friend or sexual partner.' This denotes:",
    options: { a: "Propinquity effect", b: "Exposure effect", c: "Matching hypothesis", d: "Reciprocity" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "The exposure effect (mere exposure) is the closely related underlying mechanism, but the principle stated, that contact through nearness breeds relationships, is specifically labelled the propinquity effect.", c: "The matching hypothesis holds that people pair with others of similar attractiveness, a different determinant of attraction than proximity and contact.", d: "Reciprocity is the tendency to like those who like us; it concerns mutual liking, not the proximity and repeated interaction described." },
    explanation: "The statement that the more we see and interact with someone the more likely they are to become a friend or partner denotes the propinquity effect, so the answer is a. The propinquity effect (from propinquity, meaning nearness or proximity) is the social psychological finding that physical and functional proximity is one of the strongest predictors of attraction and the formation of relationships. People are far more likely to befriend, like, and partner with those they encounter frequently, neighbours, classmates, coworkers, those whose paths cross theirs by virtue of where they live, study, or work. The classic demonstration is Festinger, Schachter, and Back's study of friendship in a student housing complex, which found that friendships formed overwhelmingly among people whose apartments were close together and whose functional distance (likelihood of incidental contact) was small. The mechanism behind propinquity is closely tied to the mere exposure effect, Zajonc's finding that repeated exposure to a stimulus increases liking for it, and to the availability and ease of interaction that proximity affords. The question's phrasing emphasises seeing and interacting with a person, the contact afforded by nearness, which is the defining cause in the propinquity effect. The exam discriminator is that propinquity stresses proximity and repeated contact as the route to relationship formation, the most precise label for the stated principle." },
  { id: "ihbas20_067", section: "social",
    q: "Reasons for behavior that rest on the personality traits of the individual carrying out the behavior are:",
    options: { a: "Situational causes", b: "Environmental causes", c: "Dispositional causes", d: "Cultural causes" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Situational causes locate the explanation in external circumstances, the opposite of the internal personality based attribution described.", b: "Environmental causes are a kind of external, situational attribution, again contrasting with the internal trait based explanation.", d: "Cultural causes attribute behaviour to broad cultural norms and context, an external factor, not the individual's personality traits." },
    explanation: "Reasons for behaviour that rest on a person's personality traits are dispositional causes, so the answer is c. In attribution theory, which studies how people explain the causes of behaviour, a fundamental distinction is drawn between dispositional (internal) and situational (external) causes. A dispositional attribution explains behaviour by reference to something inside the person, their personality traits, attitudes, abilities, character, or motives; for example, saying someone failed because they are lazy. A situational attribution explains the same behaviour by reference to the external circumstances, the environment, social pressures, luck, or task difficulty; for example, saying they failed because the task was impossible. Heider, who founded attribution theory, framed people as naive scientists making these internal versus external judgments, and Kelley's covariation model specified how we decide between them using consistency, distinctiveness, and consensus information. The distinction matters because of pervasive biases, especially the fundamental attribution error, the tendency to overweight dispositional and underweight situational causes when explaining others' behaviour. The question states reasons that rest on the personality traits of the individual, which is precisely the definition of a dispositional cause. The exam discriminator is the explicit mention of personality traits of the actor, marking it as internal or dispositional rather than situational, environmental, or cultural." },
  { id: "ihbas20_068", section: "social",
    q: "A tendency to act in a way that is consistent with one's own belief, expectation, or cognition about an event or behavior, thereby increasing the likelihood that the event or behavior will occur, is termed as:",
    options: { a: "Self-fulfilling prophecy", b: "Confirmation bias", c: "Motivational reaction", d: "Base-rate fallacy" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Confirmation bias is the tendency to seek and interpret information in ways that confirm existing beliefs, but it does not actively cause the event to happen; it biases perception, not reality.", c: "Motivational reaction is not a recognised term for this phenomenon and does not capture the belief causing its own fulfilment.", d: "Base rate fallacy is the error of ignoring general probabilities in favour of specific information, a reasoning bias unrelated to making expectations come true." },
    explanation: "A tendency to act consistently with one's own expectation, thereby making the expected event more likely, is a self fulfilling prophecy, so the answer is a. The term was coined by sociologist Robert Merton and developed in social psychology to describe how a false or merely held belief can become true through its own influence on behaviour. The sequence is: a person holds an expectation about an event or about another person; that expectation shapes how they behave; their behaviour then elicits responses that confirm the original expectation. A classic experimental demonstration is Rosenthal and Jacobson's Pygmalion in the Classroom, where teachers told certain randomly chosen pupils were late bloomers treated them in ways (more attention, warmth, and challenge) that actually raised those pupils' achievement, fulfilling the false prophecy. The mechanism is behavioural confirmation: beliefs guide action, action shapes the social environment, and the environment then vindicates the belief. The question's phrasing, acting in a way consistent with one's belief or expectation thereby increasing the likelihood that the event will occur, is the textbook definition of a self fulfilling prophecy. The exam discriminator is that the belief causally brings about its own confirmation through behaviour, which separates it from mere biased interpretation of evidence (confirmation bias) or errors of base rate reasoning." },
  { id: "ihbas20_069", section: "social",
    q: "Low-prejudiced people engage in:",
    options: { a: "Automatic information processing resulting in involuntary stereotyping", b: "Controlled information processing resulting in refuting stereotype", c: "Controlled information processing resulting in involuntary stereotyping", d: "Automatic information processing resulting in voluntary stereotyping" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Automatic processing resulting in involuntary stereotyping describes what happens without correction; low prejudiced people specifically go beyond this with controlled refutation.", c: "Controlled processing resulting in involuntary stereotyping is contradictory, since controlled processing is precisely what allows the stereotype to be inhibited rather than left involuntary.", d: "Automatic processing resulting in voluntary stereotyping is incoherent, since automatic processing is by definition involuntary, and it does not describe the low prejudiced person's deliberate refutation." },
    explanation: "Low prejudiced people engage in controlled information processing resulting in refuting the stereotype, so the answer is b. Research on automatic and controlled processing in prejudice, associated with Patricia Devine, distinguishes two routes by which stereotypes operate. Automatic processing is fast, involuntary, and unconscious; because cultural stereotypes are widely learned, even low prejudiced people may have stereotypes activated automatically when they encounter a member of a stereotyped group. Controlled processing is slow, effortful, and intentional, and it can override the automatically activated stereotype. Devine's key insight is that high and low prejudiced people do not differ much in whether the stereotype is automatically activated, since both know the cultural stereotype, but they differ crucially in what they do next. Low prejudiced individuals, who hold egalitarian personal beliefs, engage in deliberate, controlled processing that inhibits and refutes the activated stereotype, consciously replacing the prejudiced response with their nonprejudiced values. High prejudiced individuals do not engage this corrective effort, so the automatic stereotype stands. Thus reducing prejudice depends on the willingness and capacity to exert controlled, effortful suppression and refutation of stereotypes. The exam discriminator is matching low prejudice to controlled (effortful, deliberate) processing that refutes the stereotype, as opposed to automatic processing or to controlled processing that nonetheless leaves stereotyping intact." },
  { id: "ihbas20_070", section: "general",
    q: "Self-reference effect refers to:",
    options: { a: "The phenomenon in which information is recalled better when it is related to the self", b: "The entire sum of a person's impressions about himself or herself", c: "The set of beliefs we hold about what we are like as individuals", d: "The knowledge that we exist as individuals, separate from everyone else" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "The entire sum of a person's impressions about themselves defines the self concept, not the memory advantage that is the self reference effect.", c: "The set of beliefs about what we are like as individuals describes self schemas or self concept, again the content of self knowledge, not the recall enhancement.", d: "The knowledge that we exist as individuals separate from others is self awareness, a different construct from the mnemonic self reference effect." },
    explanation: "The self reference effect refers to the phenomenon in which information is recalled better when it is related to the self, so the answer is a. The self reference effect is a robust finding in memory research, demonstrated by Rogers, Kuiper, and Kirker, showing that when people process information in relation to themselves, for instance judging whether a trait adjective describes them, they remember that information far better than when they process it in other ways, such as judging its meaning, its sound, or whether it describes someone else. The explanation is that the self is a highly elaborated, richly organised, and frequently used cognitive structure, a self schema, so relating new information to it produces deep, elaborate encoding with many retrieval cues and strong integration into existing memory networks, both of which enhance later recall. This connects to the levels of processing framework, where deeper, more meaningful processing yields better memory, and the self provides the deepest, most elaborate processing of all. The effect has practical implications for learning (personalising material aids retention) and theoretical implications for understanding the self as a memory structure. The exam discriminator is distinguishing the self reference effect, a memory phenomenon about enhanced recall, from concepts like the self concept, self schema, or self awareness, which are about the content or existence of self knowledge rather than its mnemonic advantage." },
  { id: "ihbas20_071", section: "general",
    q: "Type A behavior pattern is characterized by:",
    options: { a: "Non-competitiveness, impatience and hostility", b: "Impatience, non-competitiveness, and aggression", c: "Competitiveness, impatience and hostility", d: "Hostility, lack of aggression and competitiveness" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "This option includes non competitiveness, which directly contradicts the competitive striving central to Type A; Type A people are highly competitive.", b: "This option again lists non competitiveness, which is incompatible with the Type A pattern's defining competitive achievement orientation.", d: "This option states lack of aggression, contradicting the hostility and aggression that are core to Type A; Type A involves heightened, not absent, aggression." },
    explanation: "Type A behaviour pattern is characterised by competitiveness, impatience, and hostility, so the answer is c. The Type A behaviour pattern was identified by cardiologists Meyer Friedman and Ray Rosenman, who noticed that many of their heart patients shared a distinctive behavioural style. The defining components are a chronic sense of time urgency and impatience (always rushed, finishing others' sentences, intolerant of delay), competitive achievement striving and ambition (a constant drive to win and accomplish more), and easily aroused hostility, anger, and aggression (irritability and antagonism toward obstacles and people). This pattern was hypothesised, and in much research found, to be a risk factor for coronary heart disease, contrasted with the more relaxed, easygoing, non urgent Type B pattern. Later refinement of the research suggested that hostility, especially cynical hostility and anger, is the toxic core component most strongly linked to cardiac risk, while time urgency and competitiveness alone are weaker predictors. The exam discriminator is selecting the option that correctly pairs all three genuine Type A features, competitiveness, impatience, and hostility, without the contradictory inclusions in the distractors, such as non competitiveness or lack of aggression, which negate the very essence of the hard driving, hostile Type A style." },
  { id: "ihbas20_072", section: "social",
    q: "Emotional isolation means:",
    options: { a: "Feeling a lack of deep emotional attachment to one specific person", b: "Suffering from a lack of friends/associates", c: "Suffering from feelings of incompetence", d: "Feeling a sense of inadequacy and low self-esteem" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Suffering from a lack of friends or associates describes social isolation, the deficit in the breadth of one's social network, not emotional isolation.", c: "Feelings of incompetence are about self efficacy or self esteem, not the absence of an intimate emotional bond that defines emotional isolation.", d: "A sense of inadequacy and low self esteem concerns self evaluation, again unrelated to the lack of a deep attachment to one specific person." },
    explanation: "Emotional isolation means feeling a lack of deep emotional attachment to one specific person, so the answer is a. The distinction comes from Robert Weiss's analysis of loneliness, which separated two qualitatively different forms. Emotional isolation (or emotional loneliness) is the loneliness that results from the absence of a close, intimate emotional bond, a confiding attachment figure such as a partner or very close companion. It is the feeling of having no one with whom one shares a deep emotional connection, and it cannot be remedied simply by being around many people. Social isolation (or social loneliness), by contrast, is the loneliness that results from lacking a network of friends, acquaintances, and a sense of belonging to a community; it is about the breadth of social ties rather than the depth of an intimate bond. Weiss's key point is that these two are distinct and require different remedies: a person can have a wide circle of friends yet feel emotionally isolated for want of one intimate attachment, or have a devoted partner yet feel socially isolated for want of a broader community. The question describes the absence of deep emotional attachment to one specific person, which is precisely emotional isolation. The exam discriminator is matching emotional isolation to the missing intimate, one to one attachment rather than to a lack of friends or general feelings of inadequacy." },
  { id: "ihbas20_073", section: "social",
    q: "Communicating information about one's feelings means:",
    options: { a: "Descriptive self-disclosure", b: "Prescriptive self-disclosure", c: "Non-evaluative self-disclosure", d: "Evaluative self-disclosure" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Prescriptive self disclosure is not a standard category in this typology; the recognised contrast to descriptive is evaluative.", c: "Non evaluative self disclosure is not the standard labelled type; the descriptive category already captures reporting feelings without judgment.", d: "Evaluative self disclosure involves expressing opinions and value judgments, not simply communicating information about one's feelings." },
    explanation: "Communicating information about one's feelings is descriptive self disclosure, so the answer is a. Self disclosure is the process of revealing personal information about oneself to another person, a key building block of intimacy and relationship development studied by social psychologists such as Sidney Jourard, and explained developmentally by social penetration theory (Altman and Taylor), which likens disclosure to peeling layers of an onion, moving from superficial breadth to deeper, more personal disclosure over time. Within disclosure, a distinction is drawn between descriptive self disclosure, which involves describing facts about oneself and one's feelings, simply reporting or communicating what one feels or what is true of oneself, and evaluative self disclosure, which involves expressing personal evaluations, opinions, and judgments, conveying how one judges or values things rather than merely reporting them. The question specifies communicating information about one's feelings, which is reporting or describing the feeling, the descriptive type, rather than passing a value judgment on it. So the keyed answer is descriptive self disclosure. The exam discriminator is the distinction between describing or reporting feelings and facts (descriptive) versus evaluating or judging (evaluative); since the item is about conveying information about feelings, it falls under descriptive self disclosure." },
  { id: "ihbas20_074", section: "social",
    q: "Pluralistic ignorance refers to:",
    options: { a: "Ignoring eyewitnesses in case of an accident", b: "Being suggestible in determining the cause of accident", c: "In an emergency, mistakenly assuming, based on the behavior of the others, that help is unnecessary", d: "Ignoring others' behavior in emergency" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Ignoring eyewitnesses is not pluralistic ignorance; the phenomenon is about misinterpreting others' calmness as evidence no emergency exists, not disregarding witnesses.", b: "Being suggestible about the cause of an accident touches on social influence but does not capture the specific bystander error of concluding help is unnecessary.", d: "Ignoring others' behaviour is the opposite of pluralistic ignorance, which arises precisely because people attend to and are misled by others' behaviour." },
    explanation: "Pluralistic ignorance in an emergency refers to mistakenly assuming, based on the behaviour of others, that help is unnecessary, so the answer is c. This item revisits the same concept tested earlier, now framed around the bystander situation directly. Pluralistic ignorance is the state in which each individual privately feels uncertain but, observing that everyone else appears calm and is not acting, concludes that the situation must not be an emergency and that help is not needed. The crucial feature is the misreading of others' inaction: in an ambiguous emergency people look to one another for cues about how to interpret events (informational social influence), but because everyone is doing the same restrained looking and no one wants to overreact, the group collectively and mistakenly defines the event as a non emergency. This is one of the core mechanisms behind the bystander effect documented by Latane and Darley, alongside diffusion of responsibility. The practical lesson is that the very presence of other apparently unconcerned witnesses can suppress helping, which is why explicitly labelling an event as an emergency and singling out a specific person to help overcomes it. The exam discriminator is the precise wording, mistakenly assuming help is unnecessary based on others' behaviour, which is the definition of pluralistic ignorance rather than the other vaguely worded options." },
  { id: "ihbas20_075", section: "social",
    q: "Altruistic Personality is characterized by:",
    options: { a: "Offering help only when one feels like", b: "Consistently helping others", c: "Calculating the cost and benefits of helping", d: "Readiness to help in an emergency" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Offering help only when one feels like it describes inconsistent, mood dependent helping, the opposite of the stable dispositional helpfulness of an altruistic personality.", c: "Calculating the costs and benefits of helping is egoistic, self interested helping, which contradicts genuine altruism.", d: "Readiness to help in an emergency is too narrow; the altruistic personality is defined by consistent helping across many situations, not just emergencies." },
    explanation: "An altruistic personality is characterised by consistently helping others, so the answer is b. The altruistic personality refers to the idea that some individuals have a stable, dispositional tendency to help others across many different situations, rather than helping only when the immediate circumstances happen to be favourable. True altruism, in social psychology, is helping motivated by genuine concern for another's welfare without expectation of personal reward, as distinguished from egoistic helping done for self benefit. The altruistic personality concept holds that beyond situational factors, there are personal characteristics, empathy, a sense of social responsibility, internalised prosocial values, and a particular moral identity, that make some people reliably helpful. Research on rescuers who saved Jews during the Holocaust, for example, found such enduring dispositional features of empathy and inclusive moral commitment. The defining marker of an altruistic personality is therefore consistency: the person helps across varied situations and over time, not just in convenient moments or only in dramatic emergencies. The exam discriminator is selecting consistency of helping as the hallmark of the altruistic personality, distinguishing it from conditional, mood dependent helping, from cost benefit calculation (which is the opposite of altruism), and from a narrow readiness limited to emergencies only." },
  { id: "ihbas20_076", section: "social",
    q: "When people are able to think systematically about an issue and focus on arguments, they are likely to take the ______ route to persuasion.",
    options: { a: "Peripheral", b: "Emotional", c: "Situational", d: "Central" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "The peripheral route relies on superficial cues like source attractiveness rather than systematic argument analysis, the opposite of what is described.", b: "Emotional is not one of the two named routes in the Elaboration Likelihood Model; emotional cues belong to peripheral processing.", c: "Situational is not a route in the model; the two recognised routes are central and peripheral." },
    explanation: "When people think systematically about an issue and focus on arguments, they take the central route to persuasion, so the answer is d. This is the core of the Elaboration Likelihood Model developed by Richard Petty and John Cacioppo, which proposes two routes by which attitudes are changed. The central route operates when people are both motivated and able to think carefully about a message; they elaborate on it, scrutinise the strength and quality of the arguments, and are persuaded by the merits of the case. Attitude change produced this way is more enduring, more resistant to counterargument, and more predictive of behaviour, because it is grounded in genuine cognitive engagement. The peripheral route operates when motivation or ability to process is low; instead of analysing arguments, people rely on superficial cues such as the attractiveness or credibility of the source, the sheer number of arguments, catchy slogans, or emotional reactions. Persuasion via the peripheral route is weaker and more transient. The determining factors for which route is taken are the elaboration likelihood, chiefly the person's motivation (for example personal relevance) and cognitive capacity (for example freedom from distraction). The question specifies thinking systematically and focusing on arguments, which is the hallmark of the central route. The exam discriminator is matching effortful argument scrutiny to the central route as opposed to cue based peripheral processing." },
  { id: "ihbas20_077", section: "biostats",
    q: "Which one of the following does not apply to qualitative research?",
    options: { a: "Recognition of the role of language and discourse", b: "Emphasis on objective truth", c: "Understanding of processes", d: "Concern for human individuality" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Recognition of the role of language and discourse is central to qualitative research, especially in approaches like discourse analysis, so it does apply.", c: "Understanding of processes, the how and why in context, is a core aim of qualitative inquiry, so it does apply.", d: "Concern for human individuality and subjective experience is a defining feature of qualitative research, so it does apply." },
    explanation: "An emphasis on objective truth does not apply to qualitative research, so the answer is b. Qualitative research rests on an interpretivist or constructivist epistemology, which holds that social reality is constructed, multiple, and meaning laden rather than a single objective fact waiting to be measured. Its hallmarks include a recognition of the role of language and discourse in constituting meaning, an interest in understanding processes and the how and why of phenomena in context, a concern for human individuality and subjective experience, attention to the participant's perspective and meaning making, and acceptance that the researcher is part of the meaning making process rather than a detached, neutral instrument. Qualitative methods include in depth interviews, ethnography, grounded theory, phenomenology, and discourse analysis, and they prize rich, contextual, holistic understanding over generalisable measurement. An emphasis on objective truth, by contrast, belongs to the positivist paradigm that underlies most quantitative research, which assumes a single observable reality, value free measurement, hypothesis testing, and the search for generalisable, objective facts. So among the options, the one that contradicts the qualitative stance is the emphasis on objective truth. The exam discriminator is recognising that qualitative inquiry embraces subjectivity, meaning, language, and individuality, and explicitly rejects the positivist assumption of a single objective truth." },
  { id: "ihbas20_078", section: "biostats",
    q: "Which one of the following does not go with the others?",
    options: { a: "Symbolic interactionism", b: "Meaning", c: "Phenomenology", d: "Researcher's authority" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Symbolic interactionism is a core interpretive theory about socially constructed meaning, fitting squarely with the others.", b: "Meaning is the central focus of the entire interpretive qualitative tradition, so it belongs with the group.", c: "Phenomenology is an interpretive approach to studying lived subjective experience, fitting with symbolic interactionism and meaning." },
    explanation: "Among the listed items, researcher's authority does not go with the others, so the answer is d. Symbolic interactionism, meaning, and phenomenology all belong to the interpretive, qualitative tradition in social science. Symbolic interactionism, associated with George Herbert Mead and Herbert Blumer, holds that people act toward things on the basis of the meanings those things have for them, that meanings arise out of social interaction, and that meanings are handled and modified through interpretation; it is fundamentally about how shared meanings are constructed in interaction. Meaning is the central concern of all interpretive inquiry, the thing the researcher seeks to understand from the participant's point of view. Phenomenology, rooted in Husserl and developed by Schutz and others, studies the structures of lived, subjective experience and how people make sense of their world, and it requires the researcher to set aside (bracket) their own assumptions to grasp the participant's experience. All three thus centre on participants' meanings and experiences and on the researcher adopting a humble, understanding stance. Researcher's authority, the idea that the investigator holds privileged, authoritative knowledge over the participants, runs counter to this whole tradition, which decentres the researcher in favour of the participants' meaning making. The exam discriminator is spotting that three options are interpretive concepts about meaning and experience, while researcher's authority is the conceptual odd one out that contradicts the interpretive ethos." },
  { id: "ihbas20_079", section: "biostats",
    q: "Experimental designs with more than one independent variable, in which the different variable levels are combined in a systematic manner, are:",
    options: { a: "Factorial Experiments", b: "Quasi Experiments", c: "Systematic Experiments", d: "Descriptive Experiments" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Quasi experiments are defined by the absence of random assignment to conditions, not by having multiple systematically crossed independent variables.", c: "Systematic experiments is not a recognised design category; the term for crossing multiple factors is factorial.", d: "Descriptive experiments is not a standard design type; descriptive research is generally non experimental, lacking manipulation of variables." },
    explanation: "Experimental designs with more than one independent variable whose levels are systematically combined are factorial experiments, so the answer is a. A factorial design is one in which two or more independent variables (called factors) are studied simultaneously, and every level of each factor is crossed with every level of the other factors, so that all possible combinations of conditions are tested. For example, a 2 by 3 factorial crosses two levels of one factor with three levels of another, yielding six conditions. The great advantage of factorial designs is efficiency and, crucially, the ability to detect interaction effects, that is, whether the effect of one independent variable depends on the level of another, in addition to the separate main effects of each variable. Interactions are often the most theoretically interesting findings because they reveal that variables do not act in isolation but combine in complex ways. The systematic combination of variable levels mentioned in the question is the defining feature of a factorial design. The exam discriminator is matching more than one independent variable, with levels combined systematically, to factorial experiments, distinguishing them from quasi experiments (which lack random assignment), and from the non standard labels in the other options." },
  { id: "ihbas20_080", section: "therapy",
    q: "Noogenic anxiety is described under:",
    options: { a: "Humanistic School", b: "Gestalt school", c: "Existential school", d: "Psychodynamic school" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "The humanistic school (Rogers, Maslow) emphasises self actualisation and the actualising tendency, but noogenic anxiety is specifically Frankl's existential concept.", b: "The Gestalt school (Perls) focuses on present awareness and unfinished business, not on noogenic anxiety arising from a frustrated will to meaning.", d: "The psychodynamic school attributes anxiety to unconscious conflict; noogenic anxiety is defined precisely as not psychogenic in that sense but existential." },
    explanation: "Noogenic anxiety (and noogenic neurosis) is described under the existential school, so the answer is c. The concept comes from Viktor Frankl, the founder of logotherapy, which is often grouped within existential therapy because of its focus on meaning, freedom, and responsibility. Frankl coined the term noogenic, from the Greek noos meaning mind or spirit, to denote disturbances that arise not from psychological conflict in the Freudian sense or from biological causes, but from the spiritual or existential dimension of the person, specifically from a frustration of the will to meaning. Noogenic neurosis and noogenic anxiety stem from an existential vacuum, the experience of emptiness, meaninglessness, and the loss of purpose, and from conflicts of conscience and values rather than from drive conflicts. Frankl argued that the central human motivation is the search for meaning, and that when this striving is thwarted, a distinctively existential form of distress arises that ordinary psychotherapy aimed at drives or symptoms cannot reach; logotherapy addresses it by helping the person discover meaning. The exam discriminator is the link between noogenic, the will to meaning, the existential vacuum, and Frankl, all of which place the concept in the existential school rather than the humanistic, Gestalt, or psychodynamic traditions." },
  { id: "ihbas20_081", section: "therapy",
    q: "A patient who describes a particularly gruesome thought or fantasy but denies any feelings of anxiety or disgust associated with it is using which of the following Ego's defense:",
    options: { a: "Denial", b: "Rationalization", c: "Isolation", d: "Displacement" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Denial rejects the reality of the disturbing content altogether, but here the patient fully acknowledges and describes the thought; only the affect is missing.", b: "Rationalisation invents plausible but false justifications for an act or feeling; the patient is not justifying anything, merely reporting a thought without emotion.", d: "Displacement redirects an emotion from its real target onto a safer substitute; here the affect is removed entirely, not transferred elsewhere." },
    explanation: "A patient who describes a gruesome thought while denying any associated feeling is using the defence of isolation, so the answer is c. Isolation (more fully, isolation of affect) is an ego defence mechanism in which the emotional charge attached to a thought, memory, or impulse is split off and removed from awareness, so that the idea remains conscious but is stripped of the feeling that should accompany it. The person can talk about something disturbing, a violent fantasy, a traumatic event, in a flat, detached, intellectualised way, as if it carried no emotional significance. This protects the person from the anxiety that the affect would arouse while still allowing the cognitive content into consciousness. Isolation is particularly characteristic of obsessive compulsive personality and obsessional neurosis, where thinking is over invested and feeling is kept at a distance, and it is closely related to intellectualisation. The exam discriminator is the precise pattern described: the content (the gruesome thought) is fully present and reported, but the affect (anxiety, disgust) that belongs with it is absent. That specific separation of idea from feeling is the signature of isolation, as opposed to denial (where the content itself is rejected), rationalisation (where false reasons justify it), or displacement (where the feeling is redirected to another object)." },
  { id: "ihbas20_082", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Which of the following defense mechanisms is not involved in obsessive-compulsive disorder?",
    options: { a: "Reaction formation", b: "Repression", c: "Isolation", d: "Undoing" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Reaction formation is a core OCD defence, turning unacceptable impulses into their opposite (for example hostility into excessive politeness or dirtiness into compulsive cleanliness).", c: "Isolation (of affect) is a core OCD defence, separating the obsessional thought from the emotion that belongs with it.", d: "Undoing is a core OCD defence, the ritualistic attempt to magically cancel an unacceptable thought, forming the basis of compulsions." },
    explanation: "The defence mechanism not involved in obsessive compulsive disorder is repression, so the answer is b. In classical psychoanalytic theory, OCD (obsessional neurosis) is associated with a characteristic triad of defence mechanisms. Isolation of affect separates the disturbing thought from its emotional charge, so the obsessional idea persists in awareness while the feeling is kept away. Undoing is the magical attempt to cancel or reverse an unacceptable thought or impulse through a ritual act, the prototype of the compulsion, as if performing the ritual nullifies the forbidden wish. Reaction formation transforms an unacceptable impulse into its opposite, so hostile or dirty impulses become exaggerated cleanliness, orderliness, and conscientiousness. These three, isolation, undoing, and reaction formation, are the hallmark defences of obsessional pathology and explain its clinical features of intrusive thoughts, ritualistic behaviours, and overcontrolled, moralistic character. Repression, by contrast, is the basic defence in which an unacceptable impulse or memory is pushed entirely out of consciousness; it is most characteristically linked to hysteria and conversion disorder, where the conflict is kept fully unconscious. In OCD the problem is precisely that the content is not successfully repressed; it keeps breaking into awareness as the obsession, which is why isolation and undoing are recruited instead. The exam discriminator is recognising the OCD defence triad and identifying repression as the one belonging to hysteria rather than obsessional neurosis." },
  { id: "ihbas20_083", section: "therapy",
    q: "A patient sees a friend in the street who fails to acknowledge him; he thinks his friend does not like him anymore. He has the following type of cognitive error:",
    options: { a: "Selective abstraction", b: "Arbitrary inference", c: "Personalization", d: "Magnification" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Selective abstraction means focusing on a single negative detail taken out of context while ignoring the broader picture, not jumping to a conclusion without evidence.", c: "Personalisation is attributing external events to oneself without justification; the keyed distortion here is the evidence free negative conclusion, which is arbitrary inference.", d: "Magnification exaggerates the significance or severity of an event; it is not the act of drawing an unsupported conclusion from a neutral cue." },
    explanation: "Concluding that a friend dislikes him because the friend failed to greet him is arbitrary inference, so the answer is b. Arbitrary inference is one of the cognitive distortions identified by Aaron Beck in his cognitive model of depression and emotional disorders. It means drawing a specific negative conclusion in the absence of supporting evidence, or even in the face of contrary evidence; the person jumps to a conclusion that is arbitrary because nothing actually justifies it. In the example, the friend's failure to acknowledge him could have countless innocent explanations, the friend was distracted, did not see him, was preoccupied, yet the patient leaps to the unfounded negative conclusion that the friend no longer likes him. There is no evidence for that interpretation; it is simply assumed. This is the essence of arbitrary inference. It is worth distinguishing it from the neighbouring distortions: selective abstraction focuses on one detail out of context while ignoring the whole, magnification exaggerates the importance of an event, and personalisation attributes external events to oneself without basis. While the example does involve relating the event to the self, the most precise label for jumping to a negative conclusion with no supporting evidence is arbitrary inference. The exam discriminator is the absence of evidence for the conclusion, which is the defining feature of arbitrary inference in Beck's taxonomy." },
  { id: "ihbas20_084", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Amnesia is a clinical condition of:",
    options: { a: "Pathological loss of memory", b: "Behavioral disturbance", c: "Depressed Affect", d: "Impaired perception" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Behavioural disturbance is a broad term for disordered conduct, not the specific loss of memory that defines amnesia.", c: "Depressed affect is a mood disturbance seen in depression, an entirely different clinical domain from a memory disorder.", d: "Impaired perception refers to disturbances such as illusions or hallucinations, a perceptual problem, not a loss of memory." },
    explanation: "Amnesia is a clinical condition of pathological loss of memory, so the answer is a. Amnesia denotes a partial or total loss of memory that exceeds ordinary forgetting and arises from organic causes such as brain injury, stroke, infection, degenerative disease, or substance effects, or from psychological causes such as severe trauma (dissociative amnesia). There are two principal temporal types. Anterograde amnesia is the inability to form and store new memories after the onset of the condition, so events occurring from that point forward cannot be retained, as seen in bilateral hippocampal damage. Retrograde amnesia is the loss of memories formed before the onset, often following a temporal gradient in which recent memories are more vulnerable than remote ones (Ribot's law). Amnesia is fundamentally a disorder of memory, the encoding, storage, or retrieval of information, and it is distinguished from disturbances of consciousness, perception, mood, or thought. The exam discriminator is the direct definitional match: amnesia is by definition the pathological loss of memory, so option a is correct and the other options describe entirely different clinical domains. Carrying the anterograde versus retrograde distinction into the exam helps with the related case based items, but the basic definition tested here is simply that amnesia equals pathological memory loss." },
  { id: "ihbas20_085", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Aphasia is the disturbance in:",
    options: { a: "Language function due to organic disease", b: "Intellectual functioning", c: "Abstract thinking", d: "Memory" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Intellectual functioning impairment refers to deficits in general cognitive ability or IQ, not the specific language disorder that aphasia is.", c: "Abstract thinking impairment is a feature of conditions like frontal damage or schizophrenia, not the definition of aphasia.", d: "Memory disturbance defines amnesia, a different organic syndrome; aphasia is specifically about language, not memory." },
    explanation: "Aphasia is a disturbance in language function due to organic disease, so the answer is a. Aphasia is an acquired impairment of language caused by damage to the language centres of the brain, typically in the dominant (usually left) hemisphere, from stroke, head injury, tumour, or degenerative disease. It affects the production and or comprehension of spoken and written language, and it is a disorder of language specifically, not of intelligence, thought, or the speech musculature. The classic types illustrate the localisation. Broca's aphasia (expressive, non fluent), from damage to Broca's area in the inferior frontal lobe, leaves comprehension relatively intact but produces effortful, halting, agrammatic speech; the person knows what they want to say but cannot produce fluent language. Wernicke's aphasia (receptive, fluent), from damage to Wernicke's area in the posterior superior temporal lobe, produces fluent but meaningless speech with poor comprehension. Global aphasia involves widespread damage affecting both. The key point for the exam is that aphasia is defined as a language disturbance of organic origin, distinguishing it from dysarthria (a motor speech problem) and from disorders of intellect, abstract thinking, or memory. The exam discriminator is the explicit pairing of language function with organic brain disease, which is the precise definition of aphasia." },
  { id: "ihbas20_086", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Which of the following is not a neurotransmitter?",
    options: { a: "Dopamine", b: "Serotonin", c: "Nor-epinephrine", d: "Adrenalin" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Dopamine is a genuine neurotransmitter central to reward, movement, and implicated in schizophrenia and Parkinson's disease.", b: "Serotonin is a genuine neurotransmitter involved in mood, sleep, and appetite, targeted by SSRIs in depression.", c: "Norepinephrine (noradrenaline) is a genuine neurotransmitter involved in arousal, attention, and the stress response within the nervous system." },
    explanation: "Adrenalin is not a neurotransmitter in the strict sense; it is the odd one out, so the answer is d. The question hinges on a subtle but testable distinction. Dopamine, serotonin, and norepinephrine (noradrenaline) are classic neurotransmitters, chemical messengers released by neurons at synapses to transmit signals to other neurons; they are central to mood, arousal, reward, and many psychiatric phenomena. Adrenalin (epinephrine) is primarily a hormone secreted by the adrenal medulla into the bloodstream as part of the sympathetic, fight or flight response, acting on distant target organs rather than chiefly functioning as a synaptic neurotransmitter in the central nervous system. While the catecholamines norepinephrine and epinephrine are chemically very close and epinephrine can act as a neurotransmitter to a limited extent in some brainstem pathways, in the context of these exams adrenalin is classified as a hormone, and it is the answer to which of the following is not a neurotransmitter. The exam discriminator is recognising the hormone versus neurotransmitter distinction: the three correct neurotransmitters act at synapses within the nervous system, whereas adrenalin is principally a circulating stress hormone of the adrenal gland. So the keyed exception is adrenalin." },
  { id: "ihbas20_087", section: "dev",
    q: "Thomas and Chess are known for their contribution in the field of:",
    options: { a: "Human sexuality", b: "Temperament", c: "Needs & drives", d: "Marital therapy" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Human sexuality is associated with researchers like Kinsey and Masters and Johnson, not Thomas and Chess.", c: "Needs and drives are linked to motivation theorists such as Murray, Maslow, and Hull, not to Thomas and Chess.", d: "Marital therapy is the domain of family and couple therapists; Thomas and Chess studied child temperament, not marital intervention." },
    explanation: "Thomas and Chess are known for their contribution to the study of temperament, so the answer is b. Alexander Thomas and Stella Chess were the principal investigators of the New York Longitudinal Study, a landmark research programme begun in the 1950s that followed children from infancy and established temperament as a real, measurable, and largely constitutional aspect of individual difference present from early life. They identified nine dimensions of temperament, including activity level, rhythmicity (regularity), approach or withdrawal to new stimuli, adaptability, intensity of reaction, threshold of responsiveness, quality of mood, distractibility, and attention span or persistence. From clusters of these dimensions they derived three broad temperament types: the easy child (regular, adaptable, positive in mood), the difficult child (irregular, slow to adapt, intense and often negative), and the slow to warm up child (low activity, initially withdrawing but gradually adapting). A key concept they introduced is goodness of fit, the idea that healthy development depends on the match between a child's temperament and the demands and expectations of their environment, especially parenting. Their work shifted developmental psychology toward recognising the child's innate contribution to its own development. The exam discriminator is the firm association of Thomas and Chess with temperament, the nine dimensions, the three types, and goodness of fit, distinguishing them from researchers of sexuality, motivation, or marital therapy." },
  { id: "ihbas20_088", section: "dev",
    q: "Eight stages of the life-cycle from the 'Oral-sensory stage' to the 'Stage of maturity' were described by:",
    options: { a: "Freud", b: "Sullivan", c: "Erikson", d: "Klein" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Freud described five psychosexual stages (oral, anal, phallic, latency, genital) ending in adolescence, not eight psychosocial stages spanning to maturity.", b: "Sullivan proposed interpersonal stages of development but not Erikson's specific eight stage psychosocial scheme from oral sensory to maturity.", d: "Klein focused on early object relations and positions (paranoid schizoid, depressive), not an eight stage lifespan model." },
    explanation: "The eight stages of the life cycle from the oral sensory stage to maturity were described by Erik Erikson, so the answer is c. Erikson, a psychoanalyst influenced by Freud but extending his work, proposed a psychosocial theory of development spanning the entire lifespan, in contrast to Freud's psychosexual stages that ended in adolescence. Each of Erikson's eight stages centres on a psychosocial crisis, a tension between two opposing tendencies, whose resolution shapes personality. The stages are: trust versus mistrust in infancy (the oral sensory stage), autonomy versus shame and doubt in toddlerhood (the muscular anal stage), initiative versus guilt in the preschool years (the locomotor genital stage), industry versus inferiority in school age (the latency stage), identity versus role confusion in adolescence, intimacy versus isolation in young adulthood, generativity versus stagnation in middle adulthood, and integrity versus despair in old age (the stage of maturity). The successful resolution of each crisis yields a virtue or ego strength, and earlier resolutions influence later ones. The naming of the first stage as the oral sensory stage and the last as the stage of maturity is distinctly Eriksonian terminology. The exam discriminator is the eight stage, lifespan, psychosocial crisis structure running from infancy to old age, which is Erikson's signature, not Freud's five psychosexual stages or the theories of Sullivan and Klein." },
  { id: "ihbas20_089", section: "general",
    q: "The researcher who formulated the theory which explains how we form artificial concepts rather than natural concepts by developing a hypothesis is:",
    options: { a: "Clark Hull", b: "Eleanor Rosch", c: "Jerome Bruner", d: "John Watson" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Clark Hull is a behaviourist known for drive reduction theory and mathematical learning laws, not the hypothesis testing theory of artificial concept formation.", b: "Eleanor Rosch is known for prototype theory of natural concepts and basic level categories, the contrasting account to Bruner's artificial concept work.", d: "John Watson founded behaviourism (stimulus response conditioning) and did not develop a hypothesis testing theory of concept attainment." },
    explanation: "The researcher who explained how we form artificial rather than natural concepts by developing and testing hypotheses is Jerome Bruner, so the answer is c. Concepts are mental categories that group objects, events, or ideas by shared features. Cognitive psychology distinguishes natural concepts, which are fuzzy, real world categories like bird or furniture that we learn through everyday experience and that are organised around prototypes (a line associated with Eleanor Rosch), from artificial concepts, which are precisely defined categories created for study, learned by figuring out the rule that defines membership. Jerome Bruner, in his classic work A Study of Thinking (with Goodnow and Austin), investigated how people learn artificial concepts in the laboratory and described concept attainment as an active process of hypothesis testing. The learner forms a hypothesis about which features define the concept, tests it against instances classified as examples or non examples, and revises the hypothesis based on feedback, using systematic strategies such as conservative focusing. This portrayed the concept learner as an active, strategic problem solver developing and checking hypotheses, in contrast to passive association. The exam discriminator is the pairing of artificial concept formation through hypothesis development and testing with Bruner, distinguishing his work from Rosch's prototype based account of natural concepts and from Hull and Watson's behaviourist frameworks." },
  { id: "ihbas20_090", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Reducing contact hours is one of the important strategies in the management of:",
    options: { a: "Interpersonal violence", b: "Para suicidal risk", c: "Expressed emotion", d: "Stubbornness in children" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Interpersonal violence is managed through risk assessment, de escalation, and safety planning, not by the contact hours strategy specific to expressed emotion.", b: "Parasuicidal risk is managed with safety planning, crisis intervention, and treatments like DBT, not by reducing relative contact hours.", d: "Stubbornness in children is a behavioural and parenting issue addressed by behaviour management, not by the EE contact hours strategy." },
    explanation: "Reducing contact hours is an important strategy in the management of expressed emotion, so the answer is c. As discussed in the earlier item on expressed emotion, high EE in a patient's home, comprising criticism, hostility, and emotional overinvolvement, is a robust predictor of relapse in schizophrenia and other disorders. One of the practical, evidence based ways to lower the relapse risk associated with a high EE household, when the emotional climate cannot be fully changed, is to reduce the amount of direct face to face contact between the patient and the high EE relatives. Research by Vaughn and Leff and others showed that the deleterious effect of high EE on relapse is partly dose dependent on contact time: patients in high EE homes who spent fewer than about thirty five hours per week in face to face contact with the critical or overinvolved relative had markedly lower relapse rates than those with more intensive contact. So reducing contact hours, for example through day programmes, work, or structured time apart, is a recognised intervention to dilute the impact of high EE, alongside family psychoeducation that aims to lower the EE itself. The exam discriminator is the specific link between the strategy of cutting down face to face contact hours and the management of expressed emotion, rather than the management of violence, parasuicide, or childhood stubbornness." },
  { id: "ihbas20_091", section: "general",
    q: "How people organize information in memory and use it to understand behavior is called:",
    options: { a: "Motivational approach", b: "Schema approach", c: "Perceptual approach", d: "Organizational approach" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "The motivational approach explains behaviour in terms of needs, drives, and goals, not in terms of organised knowledge structures in memory.", c: "The perceptual approach concerns how we sense and interpret stimuli, a narrower process than the organised memory frameworks schemas describe.", d: "Organizational approach is a vague distractor; the established term for organised knowledge structures used to understand behaviour is schema." },
    explanation: "How people organise information in memory and use it to understand behaviour is the schema approach, so the answer is b. A schema is an organised mental structure or framework of knowledge about a concept, object, person, situation, or event, built up from experience, that guides how we perceive, interpret, encode, remember, and act on new information. The concept traces to Bartlett's classic memory research, showing that people reconstruct rather than reproduce memories, fitting them to pre existing schemas, and it was developed further by Piaget in cognitive development and by social and cognitive psychologists. In social cognition, schemas (including person schemas, role schemas, self schemas, and event schemas or scripts) are central to how we make sense of the social world and understand and predict behaviour, allowing us to process complex information efficiently, fill in gaps with default assumptions, and form expectations, though at the cost of biases and stereotyping. The schema approach therefore refers precisely to the framework that studies how knowledge is organised in memory and used to interpret and explain behaviour. The exam discriminator is matching organising information in memory and using it to understand behaviour to the schema construct, distinguishing it from motivational, perceptual, or vague organisational approaches that do not capture the specific knowledge structure idea." },
  { id: "ihbas20_092", section: "abnormal",
    q: "'Displacement' is a defense mechanism typically seen in:",
    options: { a: "Hysteria", b: "Phobias", c: "Obsessive-compulsive disorder", d: "Depression" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Hysteria (conversion disorder) is classically associated with repression and conversion of conflict into physical symptoms, not principally with displacement.", c: "Obsessive compulsive disorder is associated with isolation, undoing, and reaction formation; displacement is not its hallmark defence.", d: "Depression is linked psychodynamically with introjection and turning hostility against the self, not with displacement onto an external object." },
    explanation: "Displacement is a defence mechanism typically seen in phobias, so the answer is b. Displacement is the ego defence in which an emotion, impulse, or drive, often anxiety or hostility, is redirected from its original, threatening object onto a substitute object that is safer or more acceptable. In the psychoanalytic theory of phobias, the classic formulation, illustrated by Freud's case of Little Hans, holds that the child's unacceptable anxiety, originally attached to an internal conflict or a forbidden impulse toward a parent, is displaced onto an external, symbolic, and avoidable object, in Hans's case a horse. The phobic object then represents and stands in for the true, intolerable source of anxiety; by fearing and avoiding the external object, the person keeps the underlying conflict out of awareness and confines the anxiety to a circumscribed, manageable target. So phobia is understood psychodynamically as anxiety displaced onto a symbolic external stimulus. This is why displacement is the defence characteristically associated with phobic disorders. The exam discriminator is the specific psychodynamic mapping of displacement onto phobias (anxiety shifted onto an external symbolic object), distinguishing it from the defences classically tied to other conditions: conversion and repression in hysteria, the isolation undoing reaction formation triad in OCD, and introjection or turning against the self in depression." }
],
  "ihbas-2024": [
  { id: "ihbas24_001", section: "general",
    q: "What does clinical psychology primarily focus on?",
    options: { a: "Industrial applications", b: "Assessment and treatment of mental illness", c: "Behavioural changes in animals", d: "Organizational management" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Industrial applications belong to industrial-organisational psychology, which studies workplaces, selection, and productivity, not the diagnosis and treatment of mental illness.", c: "Behavioural changes in animals describe comparative or experimental psychology and the animal-learning tradition, not the clinical focus on human psychopathology.", d: "Organizational management is a business and I-O psychology concern dealing with structures and leadership, unrelated to clinical assessment and therapy." },
    explanation: "Clinical psychology is the branch of psychology devoted to understanding, assessing, diagnosing, and treating mental, emotional, and behavioural disorders, which is exactly what option b captures. The field emerged formally with Lightner Witmer, who opened the first psychological clinic at the University of Pennsylvania in 1896 and coined the term clinical psychology. Witmer applied scientific psychological knowledge to the practical problems of individuals, and that dual identity of science plus practice still defines the discipline. A clinical psychologist works across the whole arc of psychopathology: psychological assessment using interviews, standardised tests and behavioural observation; case formulation that integrates biological, psychological and social factors; psychotherapy from many orientations; and increasingly research and program evaluation. This is captured in the scientist-practitioner or Boulder model, which holds that clinicians should be trained both to deliver evidence-based treatment and to generate and consume research. Clinical psychology is distinguished from related fields by its focus on the more severe end of the distress spectrum and on formal diagnosis, whereas counselling psychology historically emphasised adjustment and normal-range problems. For Sihan, the key is to see that the defining mission is the assessment and treatment of mental illness grounded in the science of behaviour, not the workplace, animals, or management." },
  { id: "ihbas24_002", section: "dev",
    q: "Which developmental stage is associated with Erik Erikson's 'trust vs. mistrust' conflict?",
    options: { a: "Adolescence", b: "Infancy", c: "Middle childhood", d: "Early adulthood" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Adolescence is the stage of identity versus role confusion, where the young person consolidates a sense of self, not the infant trust crisis.", c: "Middle childhood corresponds to industry versus inferiority, where the school-age child masters skills and compares competence with peers.", d: "Early adulthood is intimacy versus isolation, concerned with forming committed close relationships, far later than infancy." },
    explanation: "Trust versus mistrust is the very first of Erik Erikson's eight psychosocial stages and it spans roughly the first year of life, which is infancy, making b correct. Erikson reworked Freud's psychosexual stages into a lifespan theory in which each stage poses a psychosocial crisis whose resolution shapes personality. In infancy the central task is whether the baby learns that the world, embodied by the primary caregiver, is dependable. When feeding, comfort, and contact are consistent and responsive, the infant develops basic trust and the ego strength Erikson called hope; when care is neglectful or unpredictable, mistrust dominates and the child carries forward suspicion and insecurity. The crisis is never resolved once and for all, but a favourable balance tipped toward trust is the foundation for later stages. The full sequence runs trust vs mistrust in infancy, autonomy vs shame and doubt in toddlerhood, initiative vs guilt in the preschool years, industry vs inferiority in school age, identity vs role confusion in adolescence, intimacy vs isolation in young adulthood, generativity vs stagnation in middle adulthood, and integrity vs despair in old age. Anchoring trust to infancy and the caregiver bond is the exam-critical fact here." },
  { id: "ihbas24_003", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Which part of the brain is primarily associated with cognitive functions such as problem-solving?",
    options: { a: "Occipital lobe", b: "Frontal lobe", c: "Temporal lobe", d: "Parietal lobe" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "The occipital lobe is the primary visual cortex; it processes sight, not reasoning or problem-solving.", c: "The temporal lobe handles auditory processing, language comprehension via Wernicke's area, and memory through the underlying hippocampus, not executive cognition.", d: "The parietal lobe integrates somatosensory information and spatial awareness; it is not the primary problem-solving region." },
    explanation: "The frontal lobe is the seat of higher cognitive functions including problem-solving, planning, reasoning, working memory, and judgement, so b is correct. The frontal lobe is the largest of the four cerebral lobes and its most anterior portion, the prefrontal cortex, is what most distinguishes the human brain. The prefrontal cortex orchestrates executive functions: setting goals, holding information online, inhibiting inappropriate responses, shifting between rules, and weighing consequences. Just behind it sits the primary motor cortex along the precentral gyrus, which initiates voluntary movement, and Broca's area in the left frontal lobe, which governs speech production. The classic case of Phineas Gage, whose personality and decision-making were devastated after an iron rod destroyed his frontal cortex while sparing basic abilities, dramatised that this region underlies social judgement and self-control. Clinically, frontal damage produces dysexecutive syndrome with poor planning, perseveration, disinhibition, and apathy, and the frontal lobes are implicated in disorders such as ADHD and schizophrenia. The other lobes specialise elsewhere, so when a question pairs cognition, reasoning, or problem-solving with brain location, the frontal lobe is the answer." },
  { id: "ihbas24_004", section: "social",
    q: "What does ‘groupthink’ refer to in social psychology?",
    options: { a: "Individual thinking", b: "Collective decision-making that discourages dissent", c: "Brainstorming", d: "Peer pressure" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Individual thinking is the opposite of a group phenomenon and does not describe the consensus pressure Janis identified.", c: "Brainstorming is a deliberate technique for generating many ideas without criticism, which actually encourages divergent input rather than suppressing dissent.", d: "Peer pressure is a broader, more informal notion of social influence; groupthink is the specific, structured decision-making failure of cohesive groups." },
    explanation: "Groupthink, the correct answer b, is the mode of thinking that occurs when the drive for consensus in a cohesive group overrides realistic appraisal of alternatives, so dissent is suppressed and bad decisions result. The concept was introduced by Irving Janis in 1972 after he analysed foreign-policy fiascoes such as the Bay of Pigs invasion. Janis identified antecedent conditions: high cohesiveness, insulation from outside opinion, directive leadership, and high stress with low hope of a better solution than the leader's. These produce symptoms including an illusion of invulnerability, collective rationalisation, belief in the group's inherent morality, stereotyping of out-groups, direct pressure on dissenters, self-censorship, a shared illusion of unanimity, and self-appointed mindguards who shield the group from troubling information. The consequences are incomplete survey of alternatives, failure to examine risks, and poor contingency planning. Janis proposed remedies such as appointing a devil's advocate, inviting outside experts, and the leader withholding his preference. The defining feature for exams is that the cost of cohesion is the discouragement of dissent and critical evaluation, which is precisely what option b states." },
  { id: "ihbas24_005", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Which disorder is characterised by persistent sadness and loss of interest?",
    options: { a: "Schizophrenia", b: "Depression", c: "OCD", d: "Bipolar-disorder" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Schizophrenia is a psychotic disorder defined by delusions, hallucinations, disorganised thought, and negative symptoms, not by sadness and lost interest.", c: "OCD is an anxiety-related disorder marked by intrusive obsessions and compulsive rituals, not by pervasive low mood.", d: "Bipolar disorder does include depressive episodes but is defined by the presence of mania or hypomania, so persistent sadness alone points to depression, not bipolar." },
    explanation: "Persistent sadness combined with a loss of interest or pleasure is the hallmark of depression, making b correct. In DSM-5 terms, major depressive disorder requires at least five symptoms over a two-week period, and crucially at least one of them must be either depressed mood or anhedonia, the loss of interest or pleasure in nearly all activities. The remaining symptoms span the neurovegetative and cognitive domains: significant weight or appetite change, insomnia or hypersomnia, psychomotor agitation or retardation, fatigue, feelings of worthlessness or excessive guilt, diminished concentration, and recurrent thoughts of death or suicide. The two cardinal symptoms, low mood and anhedonia, are what the question is pointing to. Depression is among the most common mental disorders and a leading cause of disability worldwide. Beck's cognitive model frames it around the negative cognitive triad of negative views of self, world, and future, while biological models emphasise monoamine dysregulation involving serotonin, norepinephrine, and dopamine. Treatments include antidepressants, cognitive-behavioural therapy, and interpersonal therapy. The distinguishing point for Sihan is the combination of pervasive sadness with anhedonia, which separates depression from disorders defined by psychosis, mood cycling, or anxiety." },
  { id: "ihbas24_006", section: "biopsych",
    q: "The exocrine glands are called which secrete chemical substances other than hormones.",
    options: { a: "Duct-glands", b: "Ductless-glands", c: "Primary", d: "None of the above" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Ductless glands are endocrine glands that secrete hormones into the blood, the exact opposite of the exocrine glands described.", c: "Primary is not a recognised classification of glands by secretion route and does not name the ducted exocrine type.", d: "None of the above is wrong because duct-glands is the correct and standard term for exocrine glands." },
    explanation: "Exocrine glands are duct glands, so a is correct, and the question contrasts them with the endocrine system. Glands of the body fall into two great classes defined by how they deliver their secretions. Exocrine glands possess ducts, channels that carry their products to a specific surface or cavity, and they secrete substances other than hormones such as sweat, saliva, tears, digestive enzymes, sebum, and milk. Because they release through ducts they are literally called duct glands. Endocrine glands by contrast are ductless; they release hormones directly into the bloodstream, which then carries those chemical messengers to distant target organs. Examples of endocrine glands are the pituitary, thyroid, adrenals, and pineal. A few organs are mixed: the pancreas is exocrine when it sends digestive enzymes through ducts into the small intestine and endocrine when its islets of Langerhans release insulin and glucagon into the blood. For the exam, the simple mapping to remember is exocrine equals ducted and non-hormonal, endocrine equals ductless and hormonal, which makes duct-glands the right label for exocrine glands." },
  { id: "ihbas24_007", section: "therapy",
    q: "Match List I with List II LIST I (Psychologist) LIST 2 (Theory) A. Dollard and Miller 1. Radical Behaviorism B. Skinner 2. Self-Actualization C. Bandura and Walters 3. Early Social Learning D. Maslow 4. Later Social Learning",
    options: { a: "A-1, B-2, C-3, D-4", b: "A-3, B-1, C-4, D-2", c: "A-2, B-1, C-3, D-4", d: "A-3, B-2, C-4, D-1" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "A-1, B-2, C-3, D-4 wrongly assigns radical behaviourism to Dollard and Miller and self-actualization to Skinner, reversing the actual authorship of those ideas.", c: "A-2, B-1, C-3, D-4 gives self-actualization to Dollard and Miller and misplaces Maslow, which is incorrect.", d: "A-3, B-2, C-4, D-1 correctly places Dollard and Miller but assigns self-actualization to Skinner and radical behaviourism to Maslow, both wrong." },
    explanation: "The correct matching is A-3, B-1, C-4, D-2, option b. Dollard and Miller produced the early social learning theory by translating Freudian concepts into the language of Hullian learning theory in their 1950 work, so they pair with early social learning. B.F. Skinner is the founder of radical behaviourism, the position that even private events like thoughts and feelings are behaviour governed by environmental contingencies and that mental causes are unnecessary, so he pairs with radical behaviourism. Bandura and Walters advanced the later, more cognitive social learning theory, emphasising observational learning, modelling, and vicarious reinforcement that went beyond simple stimulus-response accounts, so they pair with later social learning. Abraham Maslow, a founder of humanistic psychology, is identified with self-actualization, the drive to fulfil one's potential at the apex of his hierarchy of needs, so he pairs with self-actualization. The conceptual through-line is the evolution of learning theory: Dollard and Miller bridged psychoanalysis and behaviourism, Skinner radicalised behaviourism, Bandura cognitised it into social-cognitive theory, while Maslow stood apart in the humanistic camp. Mapping each name to its correct school yields b." },
  { id: "ihbas24_008", section: "general",
    q: "Which of the following personality types is described as noisy, callous and fond of physical activity?",
    options: { a: "Endomorph", b: "Ectomorph", c: "Mesomorph", d: "None of the above" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "The endomorph is the soft, round body type whose viscerotonic temperament is relaxed, sociable, and pleasure-loving, not noisy and physically aggressive.", b: "The ectomorph is the thin, fragile type with a cerebrotonic, introverted, restrained temperament, the opposite of the bold physical mesomorph.", d: "None of the above is incorrect because the mesomorph precisely matches the described temperament." },
    explanation: "The mesomorph, option c, is described in Sheldon's constitutional theory as somatotonic: assertive, energetic, noisy, callous, and fond of physical activity and risk. William Sheldon proposed that body build, or somatotype, correlates with temperament. He identified three components present in every person to varying degrees. The endomorph is round and soft with a dominant digestive system and a viscerotonic temperament that is relaxed, sociable, comfort-loving, and food-loving. The mesomorph is muscular and hard-bodied with a dominant musculoskeletal system and a somatotonic temperament that is adventurous, dominant, assertive, physically active, and at times insensitive or callous to others. The ectomorph is thin, fragile, and linear with a dominant nervous system and a cerebrotonic temperament that is restrained, introverted, intellectual, and self-conscious. The theory is largely discredited as a serious account of personality because the correlations Sheldon reported were inflated and the work bordered on physiognomy, but it remains a staple exam item. The cluster of being noisy, tough, and craving physical activity maps directly onto the muscular mesomorph and the somatotonic temperament." },
  { id: "ihbas24_009", section: "general",
    q: "Who among the following has defined perception as a complex of sensory and imaginal events?",
    options: { a: "Kimbel", b: "Titchener", c: "Bruner", d: "Schafer" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Kimble is associated with definitions of learning, not with a structuralist analysis of perception into sensory and imaginal elements.", c: "Bruner studied perception too, but he is known for the New Look approach emphasising motivation and expectancy, not for defining perception as sensory plus imaginal complexes.", d: "Schafer worked with Bruner on value-driven perception, not on the structuralist elemental definition Titchener gave." },
    explanation: "Edward Titchener defined perception as a complex of sensory and imaginal elements, making b correct. Titchener was the leading proponent of structuralism, the school he brought to America from Wundt's Leipzig laboratory. Structuralism sought to analyse conscious experience into its most basic elements, much as chemistry breaks matter into atoms, using the method of introspection in which trained observers reported the elementary contents of their minds under controlled stimulation. For Titchener the three irreducible elements of mind were sensations, images, and affections, each describable by attributes such as quality, intensity, duration, and clarity. Within this framework a perception is not a simple sensation but a compound, a complex built from sensory elements together with the imaginal residues of past experience that give the present experience its meaning. That is exactly why his definition speaks of a complex of sensory and imaginal events. Structuralism eventually collapsed because introspection proved unreliable and the approach could not study children, animals, or the abnormal, but Titchener's elementalist definitions remain frequent exam content. The phrasing sensory and imaginal is the signature of Titchener's structuralist account of perception." },
  { id: "ihbas24_010", section: "general",
    q: "Who demonstrated that economic deprivation affects perception?",
    options: { a: "Broota and Ganguli", b: "Bruner and Goodman", c: "Segall", d: "Hers Kovits" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Broota and Ganguli are Indian psychologists, not the authors of the coin size-perception deprivation study.", c: "Segall, with Campbell and Herskovits, studied cross-cultural differences in susceptibility to visual illusions, not economic deprivation effects on perception.", d: "Herskovits, like Segall, worked on culture and illusions, not on deprivation and coin perception." },
    explanation: "Bruner and Goodman, option b, conducted the classic 1947 study showing that economic deprivation affects perception. In their famous coin experiment, children were asked to adjust a circle of light to match the size of coins of various denominations. Children consistently overestimated the size of the coins compared with neutral cardboard discs, and crucially poor children overestimated the value-laden coins more than rich children did. Bruner and Goodman interpreted this as evidence that needs, values, and social context shape perception itself, not merely judgement after the fact. This work launched the New Look movement in perception, which argued against the older view that perception is a passive registration of stimulation and held instead that motivational and emotional states bias what and how we perceive. The hungrier or needier the perceiver, the more salient and magnified the relevant object becomes. The study is a touchstone for the idea that perception is an active, value-driven construction. For the exam, link deprivation, coins, and size overestimation to Bruner and Goodman and the New Look school." },
  { id: "ihbas24_011", section: "general",
    q: "Functionalism, an early school of thought in psychology, was most closely associated with which psychologist?",
    options: { a: "Edward Titchener", b: "John Watson", c: "William James", d: "G. Stanley Hall" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Edward Titchener founded structuralism, the rival school that functionalism opposed, focused on the elements rather than the function of mind.", b: "John Watson founded behaviourism, which rejected the study of consciousness altogether, coming after and against functionalism.", d: "G. Stanley Hall pioneered developmental and educational psychology and founded the APA, but he is not the figure identified with functionalism." },
    explanation: "Functionalism is most closely associated with William James, option c. Functionalism arose in America as a reaction against Titchener's structuralism. Where structuralism asked what the elements of consciousness are, functionalism asked what consciousness is for, studying the purpose or function of mental processes in helping an organism adapt to its environment. The approach was heavily influenced by Darwin's evolutionary thinking: if behaviour and mind evolved, then they must serve adaptive functions. William James, often called the father of American psychology, laid the intellectual groundwork in his 1890 Principles of Psychology, where he treated consciousness as a continuous, personal, ever-changing stream that functions to help us adapt and make choices. He emphasised habit, emotion, the self, and the practical utility of mental life. The functionalist school was further developed at the University of Chicago by John Dewey and James Rowland Angell and at Columbia by James McKeen Cattell. Functionalism's emphasis on adaptation, individual differences, and applied psychology paved the way for educational, industrial, and clinical applications. For the exam, tie functionalism, the stream of consciousness, and Principles of Psychology to William James." },
  { id: "ihbas24_012", section: "social",
    q: "Leon Festinger is best known for developing which psychological theory?",
    options: { a: "Operant conditioning", b: "Cognitive dissonance", c: "Classical conditioning", d: "Social learning theory" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Operant conditioning was developed by B.F. Skinner and concerns learning through consequences, not the cognitive tension Festinger studied.", c: "Classical conditioning was discovered by Ivan Pavlov and involves stimulus pairing, unrelated to attitude inconsistency.", d: "Social learning theory is Albert Bandura's, centred on observational learning and modelling, not dissonance." },
    explanation: "Leon Festinger is best known for cognitive dissonance theory, option b. Proposed in 1957, the theory holds that people experience an unpleasant state of psychological tension, dissonance, when they hold two inconsistent cognitions or when their behaviour conflicts with their attitudes. Because this tension is aversive, people are motivated to reduce it, and they do so by changing one of the cognitions, adding new consonant cognitions, or trivialising the inconsistency. The classic demonstration is the 1959 Festinger and Carlsmith study in which participants who were paid only one dollar to lie that a boring task was interesting later rated the task as more enjoyable than those paid twenty dollars. The small payment provided insufficient external justification for lying, so participants reduced dissonance by changing their attitude to match their behaviour, whereas the well-paid group had ample justification and felt no dissonance. Festinger also formulated social comparison theory, the idea that people evaluate themselves by comparison with others. Dissonance theory remains foundational in social psychology, explaining attitude change, post-decision regret reduction, and effort justification. For the exam, connect Festinger to cognitive dissonance and the one-dollar twenty-dollar experiment." },
  { id: "ihbas24_013", section: "general",
    q: "Savoring is a concept in Positive Psychology that involves :",
    options: { a: "Avoiding negative experiences", b: "Enhancing the enjoyment of positive experiences", c: "Minimizing stress through relaxation", d: "Reflecting on past traumas" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Avoiding negative experiences is an avoidance coping strategy, not the active amplification of the positive that savoring entails.", c: "Minimizing stress through relaxation is a stress-management technique, distinct from intensifying joy in good moments.", d: "Reflecting on past traumas describes trauma processing in therapy, the opposite of dwelling appreciatively in positive experience." },
    explanation: "Savoring in positive psychology means enhancing and prolonging the enjoyment of positive experiences, option b. The concept was developed chiefly by Fred Bryant and Joseph Veroff, who defined savoring as the capacity to attend to, appreciate, and intensify positive experiences in the past, present, or future. It is the positive counterpart to coping: where coping helps us manage adversity, savoring helps us extract and amplify the good. Bryant described several types including basking in praise or achievement, thanksgiving or expressing gratitude, marveling at awe and wonder, and luxuriating in physical pleasure. Savoring can be oriented to the past through reminiscence, to the present through mindful absorption, or to the future through anticipation. Strategies that enhance savoring include sharing experiences with others, taking mental photographs, sharpening sensory perceptions, and absorbing oneself fully in the moment, while behaviours like distraction, fault-finding, or telling oneself the moment cannot last dampen it. Savoring is linked to greater happiness, life satisfaction, and resilience, which is why positive psychology, the scientific study of human flourishing founded by Martin Seligman, treats it as a deliberate well-being skill. The defining idea is the active heightening of positive emotion, option b." },
  { id: "ihbas24_014", section: "social",
    q: "Social identity theory posits that individuals derive part of their identity from :",
    options: { a: "Their economic status", b: "Their group memberships and social categories", c: "Their personal achievements", d: "Their personal preferences" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Economic status is one possible group marker but social identity theory is about psychological group identification broadly, not narrowly about wealth.", c: "Personal achievements feed personal identity, the individual side of the self-concept that the theory distinguishes from social identity.", d: "Personal preferences are individual-level characteristics, not the group memberships that define social identity." },
    explanation: "Social identity theory holds that part of a person's identity comes from their group memberships and social categories, option b. The theory was developed by Henri Tajfel and John Turner in the 1970s and 1980s. Its central claim is that the self-concept has two parts: a personal identity built from individual traits and achievements, and a social identity derived from the groups to which one belongs, such as nationality, religion, profession, sports team, or any in-group. People are motivated to maintain a positive social identity because group memberships feed self-esteem, and they achieve this through favourable comparisons between their in-group and relevant out-groups. The theory grew out of Tajfel's minimal group experiments, which showed that merely categorising people into arbitrary groups, even based on a coin toss, was enough to produce in-group favouritism and out-group discrimination, with no prior history, contact, or self-interest involved. Social identity theory explains intergroup prejudice, conformity to group norms, and the psychology of nationalism and collective action. The crucial exam point is that identity is partly social, rooted in category membership, which is exactly option b, not in economics, individual success, or mere preferences." },
  { id: "ihbas24_015", section: "social",
    q: "What is the halo effect?",
    options: { a: "The tendency to judge others based on their group membership", b: "The influence of a positive trait on the perception of other traits", c: "The tendency to overlook negative behaviors in individuals", d: "The effect of initial impressions on subsequent judgments" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Judging others based on group membership is stereotyping or prejudice, a different bias from the within-person trait spillover of the halo effect.", c: "Overlooking negative behaviours is a consequence that can follow from a halo, but the definition itself is the trait-to-trait influence, making b the precise answer.", d: "The effect of initial impressions on later judgements is the primacy effect, related but distinct from the halo effect's spreading of one trait across many." },
    explanation: "The halo effect is the tendency for one positive trait to colour our perception of a person's other traits, option b. It is a cognitive bias in impression formation first documented by Edward Thorndike in 1920, when he found that military officers rating soldiers gave correlated ratings across unrelated qualities, as if a general impression radiated outward like a halo. In everyday life the halo effect explains why physically attractive people are often judged to be also kinder, smarter, and more competent, the what-is-beautiful-is-good stereotype, and why a confident or likeable person is assumed to be more capable. The effect operates in reverse as well, sometimes called the horn or devil effect, where one negative trait drags down the evaluation of everything else. The mechanism is that the brain economises by generalising from a salient cue to a whole-person judgement rather than evaluating each attribute independently. The halo effect is a serious threat to validity in interviews, performance appraisals, and clinical ratings, which is why structured assessment tries to rate dimensions separately. The defining feature for the exam is the spread of influence from one trait to perceptions of others, option b." },
  { id: "ihbas24_016", section: "dev",
    q: "Who developed the stages of cognitive development?",
    options: { a: "Sigmund Freud", b: "Jean Piaget", c: "Erik Erikson", d: "Lawrence Kohlberg" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Sigmund Freud proposed psychosexual stages of personality development driven by libido, not cognitive stages.", c: "Erik Erikson proposed psychosocial stages spanning the lifespan, focused on social and emotional crises rather than cognition.", d: "Lawrence Kohlberg developed stages of moral reasoning, building on Piaget but addressing morality, not general cognition." },
    explanation: "Jean Piaget developed the stages of cognitive development, option b. Piaget, a Swiss psychologist, proposed that children actively construct knowledge by interacting with their environment, progressing through four invariant, universal stages. The sensorimotor stage from birth to about two years is when infants know the world through sensation and action and gradually acquire object permanence, the understanding that objects continue to exist when out of sight. The preoperational stage from about two to seven is marked by symbolic thought and language but also by egocentrism, the inability to take another's perspective, and by centration and lack of conservation. The concrete operational stage from about seven to eleven brings logical thinking about concrete events, mastery of conservation, reversibility, and classification. The formal operational stage from about eleven onward enables abstract, hypothetical, and systematic reasoning. Piaget's mechanisms of change are assimilation, fitting new information into existing schemas, accommodation, altering schemas to fit new information, and equilibration, the balancing drive that pushes development forward. Although later research showed children can do some tasks earlier than Piaget thought, his stage theory remains foundational. The exam cue cognitive development plus stages points squarely to Piaget." },
  { id: "ihbas24_017", section: "assessment",
    q: "Which personality assessment tool uses inkblots to assess thought processes?",
    options: { a: "MMPI", b: "Thematic Apperception Test (TAT)", c: "Rorschach Test", d: "Myers-Briggs Type Indicator (MBTI)" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "The MMPI is an objective, self-report personality inventory with hundreds of true-false items and validity scales, not an inkblot test.", b: "The TAT is also projective but uses ambiguous pictures of people about which the person tells stories, not inkblots.", d: "The MBTI is a self-report questionnaire sorting people into sixteen Jungian types; it uses no inkblots and is not a clinical thought-process measure." },
    explanation: "The Rorschach test, option c, uses inkblots to assess thought processes and personality. Created by the Swiss psychiatrist Hermann Rorschach in 1921, it consists of ten standardised, symmetrical inkblots, some achromatic and some with colour, that the examinee is asked to interpret by saying what each blot might be. It is a projective test, based on the projective hypothesis that when confronted with an ambiguous stimulus a person projects unconscious needs, conflicts, and ways of organising experience onto it. The clinician records responses and then codes them on dimensions such as location, whether the whole blot or a detail was used, determinants like form, colour, movement, and shading, and the content and quality of the response. The most rigorous modern scoring system is John Exner's Comprehensive System, which aimed to put interpretation on an empirical footing. The Rorschach is used to examine thought organisation, reality testing, emotional functioning, and the presence of disordered thinking, which makes it especially relevant when assessing thought processes. Its psychometric validity is debated, but its identity as the inkblot test is unambiguous, so option c is correct." },
  { id: "ihbas24_018", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Which disorder is classified as a neurodevelopmental disorder?",
    options: { a: "OCD", b: "ADHD", c: "Borderline personality disorder", d: "Generalized Anxiety Disorder" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "OCD belongs to the obsessive-compulsive and related disorders category, not the neurodevelopmental group.", c: "Borderline personality disorder is a personality disorder, defined by enduring patterns of instability in relationships, self-image, and affect, not a neurodevelopmental condition.", d: "Generalized anxiety disorder is classified among the anxiety disorders, not as a neurodevelopmental disorder." },
    explanation: "ADHD is classified as a neurodevelopmental disorder, option b. Neurodevelopmental disorders are a DSM-5 category comprising conditions with onset in the developmental period, typically before school entry, that involve deficits in personal, social, academic, or occupational functioning rooted in atypical brain development. The category includes intellectual disability, communication disorders, autism spectrum disorder, attention-deficit hyperactivity disorder, specific learning disorder, and motor disorders. ADHD is characterised by a persistent pattern of inattention and/or hyperactivity-impulsivity that interferes with functioning, with symptoms present before age twelve and across more than one setting. It presents in three forms: predominantly inattentive, predominantly hyperactive-impulsive, and combined. ADHD reflects atypical development of frontal-striatal circuits and dopamine and norepinephrine systems involved in executive control, which is why stimulant medications that boost these neurotransmitters are first-line treatments alongside behavioural interventions. Because its onset is in childhood and it stems from developmental differences in brain maturation, it sits firmly in the neurodevelopmental category. The other options are categorised elsewhere in the DSM, so the early-onset, brain-development signature makes ADHD the answer." },
  { id: "ihbas24_019", section: "biopsych",
    q: "What does the term “neuroplasticity” refer to?",
    options: { a: "The ability of the brain to change throughout life", b: "The brain’s protective coating", c: "Memory loss", d: "Brain cell death" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "The brain's protective coating refers to the meninges or, around axons, the myelin sheath, neither of which is neuroplasticity.", c: "Memory loss is amnesia, an outcome of damage, not the adaptive changeability that neuroplasticity describes.", d: "Brain cell death is neuronal degeneration or apoptosis, the opposite of the constructive reorganisation neuroplasticity refers to." },
    explanation: "Neuroplasticity refers to the brain's ability to change throughout life, option a. It is the property by which the nervous system reorganises its structure, function, and connections in response to experience, learning, environmental demands, and injury. Plasticity operates at several levels: synaptic plasticity, where the strength of connections between neurons changes through processes like long-term potentiation and long-term depression, underpinning learning and memory; structural plasticity, where dendrites, spines, and even new neurons through neurogenesis are added or pruned; and functional plasticity, where one brain region can take over functions of a damaged area, as when language can shift in children after early left-hemisphere injury. Neuroplasticity is greatest in early development, when experience sculpts the wiring of the brain during sensitive periods, but it persists across the lifespan, which is the basis for rehabilitation after stroke and for the continued ability to learn skills in adulthood. The phrase Hebbian plasticity, captured in the slogan cells that fire together wire together, names the principle that coincident activity strengthens connections. The exam-critical definition is lifelong changeability, option a, distinguishing it from myelin, memory loss, or cell death." },
  { id: "ihbas24_020", section: "therapy",
    q: "Who is known for their work on observational learning and the Bobo doll experiment?",
    options: { a: "Sigmund Freud", b: "Albert Bandura", c: "B.F. Skinner", d: "Carl Rogers" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Sigmund Freud founded psychoanalysis, focused on unconscious drives and psychosexual development, not observational learning experiments.", c: "B.F. Skinner studied operant conditioning through reinforcement and punishment, not imitation of models.", d: "Carl Rogers founded client-centred humanistic therapy, unrelated to the Bobo doll modelling studies." },
    explanation: "Albert Bandura is known for observational learning and the Bobo doll experiment, option b. In the 1961 and 1963 studies, children watched an adult model behave either aggressively or non-aggressively toward an inflatable Bobo doll. Children who saw the model hit, kick, and verbally abuse the doll later imitated those specific aggressive acts when given the chance, far more than children who saw a calm model, and they did so even without any reward for aggression. This demonstrated observational learning, also called modelling, the acquisition of behaviour simply by watching others, and it challenged strict behaviourism by showing that learning can occur without direct reinforcement of the learner. Bandura's social learning theory, later expanded into social-cognitive theory, identified four processes necessary for observational learning: attention to the model, retention of what was observed, reproduction of the behaviour, and motivation, which is influenced by vicarious reinforcement, seeing the model rewarded or punished. Bandura also introduced the concept of self-efficacy, the belief in one's capacity to succeed, and reciprocal determinism, the mutual influence of person, behaviour, and environment. The exam cue Bobo doll plus observational learning maps directly onto Bandura." },
  { id: "ihbas24_021", section: "biostats",
    q: "A graphical representation of correlational data is called a :",
    options: { a: "Bell curve", b: "Chi square", c: "Scatterplot", d: "Skewed distribution" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "A bell curve is the normal distribution, depicting how a single variable is distributed, not the relationship between two variables.", b: "Chi square is a statistical test of association between categorical variables, not a graph of correlational data.", d: "A skewed distribution describes the asymmetric shape of one variable's frequency distribution, not a display of correlation." },
    explanation: "A graphical representation of correlational data is a scatterplot, option c. A scatterplot, also called a scatter diagram, displays each pair of observations as a single point whose horizontal position represents one variable and vertical position represents the other. By plotting all paired scores it reveals the form, direction, and strength of the relationship between two variables at a glance. If points trend upward from lower left to upper right the correlation is positive; if they trend downward it is negative; if they cluster tightly around a line the correlation is strong, and if they scatter widely it is weak; if they show no slope there is little or no linear relationship. The scatterplot also exposes nonlinearity and outliers that a single correlation coefficient would hide, which is why it is the standard visual companion to the Pearson correlation. Correlation, expressed by coefficient r ranging from minus one to plus one, quantifies the linear association but never proves causation. For the exam, the rule is simple: when two continuous variables and their relationship are to be shown visually, the scatterplot is the chart, making option c correct." },
  { id: "ihbas24_022", section: "social",
    q: "People are likely to invest less effort in a task when they are working with others. What is this phenomenon called?",
    options: { a: "Social Facilitation", b: "Social Loafing", c: "Deindividuation", d: "Sleeper Effect" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Random sampling is about selecting participants from the population for representativeness, not about assigning already-chosen subjects to treatment groups.", c: "Randomness is a general property of unpredictability, not the specific experimental procedure of allocating subjects to conditions.", d: "Stratification divides the population into subgroups before sampling to ensure each is represented; it is a sampling technique, not random assignment to treatments." },
    explanation: "Randomization, option b, is the procedure that assigns subjects to treatment groups so that every member of the population has an equal probability of being placed in any condition. In experimental design, randomization is the act of allocating already-selected participants to the various treatment or control conditions by chance, using a random mechanism such as a random number table or computer generator. Its purpose is to distribute both known and unknown confounding variables evenly across groups so that the groups are comparable at the outset and any differences at the end can be attributed to the manipulation rather than to pre-existing differences. This is what makes the true experiment capable of supporting causal inference. Randomization must be distinguished from random sampling, which concerns how participants are drawn from the population to ensure the sample represents that population and supports generalisation. Random sampling is about external validity, randomization to conditions is about internal validity. The question's wording, assignment of subjects to treatment groups with equal probability, is the textbook definition of randomization, so option b is correct." },
  { id: "ihbas24_023", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Which of the following hormone is being secreted by adrenal gland?",
    options: { a: "Cortisol", b: "Epinephrine", c: "(A) and (B)", d: "Melatonin" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Cortisol is indeed an adrenal hormone, but choosing it alone is incomplete because epinephrine is also adrenal, so the combined option is required.", b: "Epinephrine is correctly adrenal, but selecting it alone ignores cortisol, which the adrenal cortex also secretes.", d: "Melatonin is secreted by the pineal gland to regulate sleep and circadian rhythm, not by the adrenal gland." },
    explanation: "The adrenal glands secrete both cortisol and epinephrine, so option c, both A and B, is correct. The adrenal glands sit atop each kidney and have two functionally distinct parts. The outer adrenal cortex produces steroid hormones, including glucocorticoids such as cortisol, mineralocorticoids such as aldosterone, and small amounts of sex steroids. Cortisol is the primary stress hormone of the slower hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis; it mobilises glucose, suppresses inflammation, and helps the body sustain a prolonged stress response, with secretion following a circadian rhythm peaking in the morning. The inner adrenal medulla, functionally an extension of the sympathetic nervous system, produces the catecholamines epinephrine, also called adrenaline, and norepinephrine. These are the hormones of the fast fight-or-flight response, raising heart rate, blood pressure, and blood sugar within seconds of a threat. Because both cortisol from the cortex and epinephrine from the medulla originate in the adrenal gland, both listed hormones are correct, which is exactly why the combined answer is the right one. Melatonin, by contrast, is made elsewhere, so option c is the complete and accurate choice." },
  { id: "ihbas24_024", section: "biostats",
    q: "ensures assignment of subject of a population to treatment groups in such a way that for any given assignment to the treatment group every member of the population has equal probability of being chosen.",
    options: { a: "Random sampling", b: "Randomization", c: "Randomness", d: "Stratification" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Random sampling concerns drawing a representative sample from the population, addressing generalisability, not assignment to treatment groups.", c: "Randomness is a generic descriptor of chance, not the named experimental procedure of allocating subjects to conditions.", d: "Stratification is a sampling method that ensures subgroups are proportionally represented, not the random allocation to experimental groups described here." },
    explanation: "The defining sentence, that subjects are assigned to treatment groups so every member of the population has equal probability of being chosen, names randomization, option b. This item repeats the core idea of experimental allocation. Randomization is the random assignment of participants to the conditions of an experiment, ensuring that chance alone decides who receives which treatment. The deep rationale is the control of confounds: if assignment is random, then on average every extraneous variable, whether measured or unmeasured, is balanced across groups before the manipulation begins. Any systematic difference observed afterward can therefore be credited to the independent variable, which is the foundation of internal validity and causal inference. The phrase equal probability of being chosen for a group is the hallmark of a fair random allocation. Note the careful distinction from random sampling, which governs selection from the population and underwrites generalisability rather than causal comparison. The wording here, assignment to treatment groups, is unambiguously about allocation within the experiment, so randomization is the right term. Sihan should hold the contrast tightly: sampling is about getting into the study, randomization is about getting into a condition." },
  { id: "ihbas24_025", section: "social",
    q: "Which of the following is an example of the power of roles?",
    options: { a: "Robber's Cave Experiment", b: "Milgram's message experiment", c: "Zilstein's shock research", d: "Zimbardo's prison experiment" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "The Robbers Cave experiment by Sherif studied intergroup conflict and cooperation between boys' groups, demonstrating realistic conflict theory, not the power of assigned roles.", b: "Milgram's experiment, often called the message or obedience study, demonstrated obedience to authority, a related but distinct phenomenon from role-taking.", c: "Zilstein's, properly Schachter's, shock research studied affiliation under fear and the misattribution of arousal, not social roles." },
    explanation: "Zimbardo's prison experiment, option d, is the classic example of the power of roles. In 1971 Philip Zimbardo conducted the Stanford Prison Experiment, randomly assigning healthy male college students to play either guards or prisoners in a simulated prison in the basement of the Stanford psychology building. Although the participants were ordinary young men with no prior differences, the situation rapidly transformed their behaviour. The guards became authoritarian and abusive, devising humiliating punishments, while the prisoners became passive, anxious, and in some cases broke down emotionally, so much that the study planned for two weeks was halted after six days. Zimbardo argued that the assigned social roles and the powerful situation, not individual dispositions, drove the cruelty, illustrating how situational forces and the roles people occupy can override personal character. The study is a centrepiece of the situationist tradition in social psychology and is often discussed alongside Milgram's obedience research to show how ordinary people can do harmful things. Although later critics questioned aspects of its methodology and Zimbardo's own role-coaching, it remains the textbook illustration of the power of roles, so option d is correct." },
  { id: "ihbas24_026", section: "social",
    q: "In Social Psychology, reactance is :",
    options: { a: "The tendency to comply with direct requests", b: "The motivation to restore freedom when it is threatened", c: "The effect of persuasive messages on attitude change", d: "The tendency to follow social norms without question" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Complying with direct requests is compliance, the opposite of reactance, which involves resisting and reasserting freedom.", c: "The effect of persuasive messages on attitude change is persuasion broadly; reactance is specifically the resistance reaction to a threatened freedom.", d: "Following social norms without question is conformity, not the freedom-restoring motivation that defines reactance." },
    explanation: "In social psychology, reactance is the motivation to restore freedom when it is threatened, option b. Psychological reactance theory was proposed by Jack Brehm in 1966. It holds that people believe they possess certain behavioural freedoms, and when one of those freedoms is threatened or eliminated, for example by a heavy-handed persuasive message, a ban, or a controlling command, the person experiences an aversive motivational state called reactance. This state energises the individual to reassert the threatened freedom, often by doing exactly the prohibited thing, by valuing the forbidden option more, or by resisting and counter-arguing the influence attempt. This is why telling people they cannot have something can make them want it more, the so-called forbidden fruit effect, and why overly forceful health warnings or parental prohibitions can backfire. Reactance explains adolescent rebellion against rules, consumer responses to limited offers, and the boomerang effect in persuasion where an attempt to change attitudes pushes them in the opposite direction. The crucial idea is the drive to recover a lost or threatened freedom, which is precisely option b, not compliance, attitude change in general, or unquestioning norm-following." },
  { id: "ihbas24_027", section: "general",
    q: "In the nature vs. nurture debate, which early philosopher argued that the mind was a “tabula rasa” or blank slate at birth?",
    options: { a: "Plato", b: "René Descartes", c: "John Locke", d: "Immanuel Kant" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Plato held a nativist, rationalist view that knowledge is innate and recollected, the opposite of the blank slate.", b: "René Descartes was a rationalist who argued for innate ideas, including the idea of God and mathematical truths, not a blank mind.", d: "Immanuel Kant proposed innate categories of understanding that structure experience, a nativist position contrary to the tabula rasa." },
    explanation: "John Locke, option c, argued that the mind is a tabula rasa or blank slate at birth. Locke, the seventeenth-century English empiricist philosopher, claimed in his Essay Concerning Human Understanding that the mind begins as white paper void of all characters, without any innate ideas. All knowledge, he held, derives from experience, through sensation, our perception of the external world, and reflection, the mind's perception of its own operations. This empiricist position places Locke firmly on the nurture side of the nature versus nurture debate: who we become is written by our environment and experience rather than fixed by inborn endowment. Empiricism contrasts sharply with nativism and rationalism, which hold that some knowledge or ideas are innate. Locke's blank slate had enormous influence on later psychology, especially behaviourism, whose extreme environmentalism, captured in John Watson's boast that he could shape any infant into any kind of adult, is a direct descendant of the tabula rasa doctrine. The Latin phrase tabula rasa and the blank-slate metaphor are the signatures of Locke's empiricism, making option c the answer." },
  { id: "ihbas24_028", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Which brain structure is primarily involved in regulating emotions such as fear and aggression?",
    options: { a: "Hippocampus", b: "Hypothalamus", c: "Amygdala", d: "Cerebellum" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "The hippocampus is essential for forming new declarative memories and spatial navigation, not the primary regulator of fear and aggression.", b: "The hypothalamus coordinates the body's stress and autonomic responses and homeostasis, acting downstream of the amygdala rather than as the primary emotion-appraisal centre.", d: "The cerebellum coordinates movement, balance, and motor learning, not emotional regulation." },
    explanation: "The amygdala, option c, is the brain structure primarily involved in regulating emotions such as fear and aggression. The amygdala is an almond-shaped cluster of nuclei located deep within the medial temporal lobe, part of the limbic system. It is the brain's threat-detection and emotional-significance hub, rapidly evaluating sensory input for danger and triggering the physiological and behavioural components of fear, including the fight-or-flight response via its connections to the hypothalamus and brainstem. Joseph LeDoux's research mapped a fast, low road from the thalamus directly to the amygdala that allows a near-instant fear response before the cortex has fully processed the stimulus, and a slower high road through the cortex that permits more deliberate appraisal. The amygdala is central to fear conditioning, the learning of associations between neutral cues and aversive events, and damage to it produces blunted fear and reduced aggression, as in the classic studies of the patient who could not recognise fearful facial expressions. It is implicated in anxiety disorders, PTSD, and aggression. The tight link between the amygdala and fear, threat, and aggression is the key exam fact, making option c correct." },
  { id: "ihbas24_029", section: "biostats",
    q: "In research, what is a confounding variable?",
    options: { a: "A variable that is intentionally manipulated", b: "A variable that influences both the independent and dependent variables", c: "A variable that only affects the dependent variable", d: "A variable that measures the outcome" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "A variable that is intentionally manipulated is the independent variable, the cause the researcher controls, not an uncontrolled confound.", c: "A variable that only affects the dependent variable is not a confound because it does not covary with the independent variable; it adds noise but not systematic bias.", d: "A variable that measures the outcome is the dependent variable, the effect being assessed, not a confound." },
    explanation: "A confounding variable is one that influences both the independent and the dependent variable, option b. In research, a confound is an extraneous variable that is correlated with the independent variable and also causally related to the dependent variable, so that it offers an alternative explanation for any observed relationship. Because the confound varies systematically with the treatment, the researcher cannot tell whether the outcome was caused by the independent variable or by the lurking confound, which is a fatal threat to internal validity and to claims of causation. The classic illustration is the spurious correlation between ice cream sales and drowning deaths, both driven by the confounding variable of hot weather; ice cream does not cause drowning, the season influences both. In experiments, confounds are controlled through random assignment, which balances them across groups, through holding them constant, or through matching. In correlational research, where manipulation is impossible, statistical controls such as partial correlation or regression attempt to hold confounds constant. The exam-critical definition is that the confound affects both variables, distinguishing it from the manipulated independent variable and the measured dependent variable, so option b is correct." },
  { id: "ihbas24_030", section: "therapy",
    q: "In the psychodynamic framework, which concept refers to the defense mechanism where an individual unconsciously attributes their own unacceptable thoughts or feelings to someone else, thereby avoiding direct confrontation with those feelings?",
    options: { a: "Rationalization", b: "Projection", c: "Displacement", d: "Sublimation" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Rationalization invents logical-sounding but false explanations for one's behaviour to make it acceptable, not attributing feelings to others.", c: "Displacement redirects an emotional impulse from a threatening target onto a safer substitute, such as kicking the dog after being scolded by the boss, not relocating feelings into another person.", d: "Sublimation transforms unacceptable impulses into socially acceptable activities, such as channeling aggression into sport, not externalising feelings onto others." },
    explanation: "Projection, option b, is the psychodynamic defense mechanism in which a person unconsciously attributes their own unacceptable thoughts, feelings, or impulses to someone else. Defense mechanisms, first described by Sigmund Freud and elaborated by his daughter Anna Freud, are unconscious strategies the ego uses to protect itself from the anxiety produced by conflicts between the id's impulses, the superego's prohibitions, and reality. In projection the person disowns a threatening internal state and relocates it outside the self, perceiving in others the very feelings they cannot tolerate in themselves. For example, a person harbouring unacknowledged hostility may insist that it is others who are angry and out to get them, thereby avoiding awareness of their own aggression. Projection thus reduces anxiety by externalising the unacceptable content and is theoretically linked to paranoid thinking. It must be distinguished from related defenses: displacement redirects an impulse onto a safer target, sublimation channels unacceptable impulses into socially valued activity, and rationalisation invents acceptable but false justifications. The signature of projection, attributing one's own feelings to others, matches the question exactly, so option b is the answer." },
  { id: "ihbas24_031", section: "general",
    q: "What is the main distinction between a psychologist and a psychiatrist in clinical psychology?",
    options: { a: "A psychologist prescribes medication, while a psychiatrist does not", b: "A psychiatrist can prescribe medication, while a psychologist typically cannot", c: "A psychologist focuses on physical health, while a psychiatrist focuses on mental health", d: "A psychiatrist treats children, while a psychologist treats adults" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "This reverses the truth; it is the psychiatrist, not the psychologist, who can prescribe medication.", c: "Both professions focus on mental health; psychologists do not specialise in physical health, so this contrast is false.", d: "Both psychiatrists and psychologists treat patients across all ages; the age split described does not exist." },
    explanation: "The main distinction is that a psychiatrist can prescribe medication while a psychologist typically cannot, option b. A psychiatrist is a medical doctor, holding an MBBS and a postgraduate degree such as MD in psychiatry, who has trained in medicine and can therefore prescribe drugs, order laboratory tests, and administer somatic treatments like electroconvulsive therapy. A clinical psychologist holds a postgraduate or doctoral degree in psychology, with specialised training in psychological assessment, psychometric testing, and psychotherapy, and in most jurisdictions, including India under the Rehabilitation Council of India, is not licensed to prescribe medication. The two professions overlap heavily in that both diagnose and treat mental disorders, and they frequently work together, with the psychiatrist managing pharmacotherapy and the psychologist delivering assessment and talk therapy. The prescriptive authority gap, rooted in medical versus psychological training, is the cleanest and most exam-relevant distinction. Both treat mental rather than physical health, both treat all age groups, so the other contrasts offered are false. For Sihan, the durable point is medical training equals prescribing power, and that is what separates the psychiatrist from the psychologist." },
  { id: "ihbas24_032", section: "dev",
    q: "Which psychologist proposed the concept of the ‘zone of proximal development’?",
    options: { a: "Jean Piaget", b: "Erik Erikson", c: "Lev Vygotsky", d: "Sigmund Freud" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Jean Piaget proposed stages of cognitive development and emphasised individual discovery, not the socially mediated ZPD.", b: "Erik Erikson developed psychosocial stages of identity and social-emotional crises, unrelated to the ZPD.", d: "Sigmund Freud's theory concerned unconscious drives and psychosexual stages, not cognitive scaffolding or the ZPD." },
    explanation: "Lev Vygotsky proposed the zone of proximal development, option c. Vygotsky was a Russian psychologist whose sociocultural theory emphasised that cognitive development is fundamentally social, mediated by language and culture and arising through interaction with more knowledgeable others. The zone of proximal development, or ZPD, is the gap between what a learner can do independently and what they can achieve with guidance from a more capable partner such as a teacher, parent, or skilled peer. Learning is most effective when it targets this zone, just beyond the learner's current independent ability. The supportive guidance that helps the learner cross the ZPD is often called scaffolding, a term coined by Bruner inspired by Vygotsky's ideas, where assistance is provided then gradually withdrawn as competence grows. Vygotsky also stressed the role of private speech and of cultural tools, and he argued that social interaction precedes and drives internal development, captured in his claim that every function appears first on the social plane and then on the psychological plane. This contrasts with Piaget's more individual, discovery-based account. The exam cue ZPD points unmistakably to Vygotsky." },
  { id: "ihbas24_033", section: "general",
    q: "Which psychological process is the Stroop effect most closely associated with?",
    options: { a: "Memory retrieval", b: "Attention", c: "Perception", d: "Language development" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Memory retrieval is not what the Stroop task taxes; the difficulty arises from attentional conflict in the moment, not from recalling stored information.", c: "Perception is involved in seeing the colours, but the bottleneck is attentional selection between competing responses, not basic perceptual processing.", d: "Language development concerns how language is acquired; the Stroop effect uses reading ability but measures attention, not developmental language milestones." },
    explanation: "The Stroop effect is most closely associated with attention, option b. Described by John Ridley Stroop in 1935, the effect appears in a task where colour words are printed in conflicting ink colours, for example the word red printed in blue ink. People are slower and more error-prone when naming the ink colour of an incongruent colour word than when the word and colour match or when naming colours of neutral stimuli. This slowing, the Stroop interference, occurs because reading is a highly practised, automatic process that competes with the more effortful, controlled process of naming the ink colour. The conflict must be resolved by selective attention and inhibitory control, suppressing the prepotent reading response in favour of the task-relevant colour-naming response. The Stroop task therefore became a benchmark measure of selective attention, processing automaticity, and executive control, and it is widely used in cognitive and clinical research, including studies of frontal lobe function. The key exam fact is that the effect indexes the competition between automatic and controlled processing and the role of selective attention in resolving it, making option b correct rather than memory, perception, or language development." },
  { id: "ihbas24_034", section: "social",
    q: "Which phenomenon describes improved performance on tasks in the presence of others?",
    options: { a: "Social loafing", b: "Social facilitation", c: "Group polarisation", d: "Diffusion of responsibility" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Social loafing is the reduction of individual effort in group tasks where contributions are pooled, the opposite of facilitation.", c: "Group polarisation is the tendency for group discussion to strengthen members' initial attitudes, not a performance effect of mere presence.", d: "Diffusion of responsibility is the spreading of perceived obligation across group members, central to the bystander effect, not to task performance enhancement." },
    explanation: "Social facilitation, option b, describes improved performance on tasks in the presence of others. The phenomenon was first documented by Norman Triplett in 1898, who noticed that cyclists rode faster when racing against others than when riding alone, one of the earliest experiments in social psychology. Robert Zajonc later offered the influential explanation in 1965: the mere presence of others increases physiological arousal, and arousal strengthens the dominant response, the response most likely to be emitted for a given task. When the task is simple or well-learned, the dominant response is the correct one, so performance improves, social facilitation. But when the task is complex or novel, the dominant response is often incorrect, so the presence of others impairs performance, which is called social inhibition. This drive theory was later refined by accounts emphasising evaluation apprehension, the concern about being judged, and distraction-conflict, the attentional conflict between the audience and the task. The crucial exam point is that audiences or co-actors enhance performance specifically on easy or practised tasks, which is what social facilitation names, making option b the answer." },
  { id: "ihbas24_035", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Which of the following is a symptom of generalised anxiety disorder (GAD)?",
    options: { a: "Mania", b: "Persistent, excessive worry", c: "Hallucinations", d: "Mood swings" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Mania, with elevated mood, grandiosity, and reduced need for sleep, is a feature of bipolar disorder, not GAD.", c: "Hallucinations are perceptual disturbances seen in psychotic disorders such as schizophrenia, not in GAD.", d: "Mood swings characterise mood disorders like bipolar or borderline personality features, not the sustained worry of GAD." },
    explanation: "Persistent, excessive worry, option b, is the defining symptom of generalised anxiety disorder. GAD is characterised in the DSM-5 by excessive anxiety and worry occurring more days than not for at least six months, about a number of events or activities, that the person finds difficult to control. The worry is free-floating and diffuse rather than attached to a single feared object or situation, which distinguishes GAD from specific phobias or panic disorder. The diagnosis also requires at least three of six associated symptoms, of restlessness or feeling keyed up, being easily fatigued, difficulty concentrating, irritability, muscle tension, and sleep disturbance. The chronic apprehension causes clinically significant distress or impairment and is not better explained by another disorder or substance. GAD reflects a tendency toward chronic over-anticipation of negative outcomes, and cognitive models emphasise intolerance of uncertainty and beliefs about the protective value of worrying. Treatment includes cognitive-behavioural therapy targeting worry and SSRIs or SNRIs. The exam signature is uncontrollable, pervasive worry not tied to one specific trigger, which matches option b and rules out the psychotic, manic, or mood-cycling symptoms in the other choices." },
  { id: "ihbas24_036", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Delusional beliefs of an individual that he is being deliberately interfered with,discriminated against, plotted against, threatened, or otherwise mistreated is called delusion of :",
    options: { a: "Reference", b: "Persecution", c: "Control", d: "Grandiosity" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "A delusion of reference involves believing ordinary events refer specifically to oneself, not the belief of being plotted against and harmed.", c: "A delusion of control is the belief that one's own thoughts or actions are externally controlled, not that others are persecuting one.", d: "A delusion of grandiosity is an exaggerated sense of one's importance or power, the opposite theme from feeling persecuted." },
    explanation: "The belief that one is being interfered with, discriminated against, plotted against, threatened, or mistreated is a delusion of persecution, option b. A delusion is a fixed, false belief held with strong conviction despite clear contradictory evidence and not explained by the person's cultural or religious background. Persecutory delusions are among the most common type, especially in paranoid presentations of schizophrenia and delusional disorder. The hallmark is the conviction that some person, group, or force intends to harm, conspire against, spy on, poison, or otherwise victimise the individual. This must be distinguished from other delusional themes. A delusion of reference is the belief that neutral events, gestures, or media messages carry special personal meaning directed at the person. A delusion of control, also called passivity, is the belief that one's thoughts, feelings, or actions are being imposed by an outside agency. A delusion of grandiosity is an inflated belief in one's own power, importance, identity, or special mission. Persecutory delusions can drive defensive or aggressive behaviour and contribute to social withdrawal. The precise content here, being plotted against and mistreated, is the textbook description of persecution, so option b is correct." },
  { id: "ihbas24_037", section: "biostats",
    q: "A set of score does not result in a unique set of grouped scores.",
    options: { a: "Raw", b: "Pooled", c: "Frequency", d: "Percentage" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Pooled scores refer to combining data across groups or samples, not to the original ungrouped measurements that can be grouped in non-unique ways.", c: "Frequency refers to how often a score occurs; a frequency set is already an organisation of data, not the raw input that yields multiple groupings.", d: "Percentage refers to a relative frequency expressed per hundred, a derived value, not the raw scores the sentence is about." },
    explanation: "A set of raw scores does not result in a unique set of grouped scores, option a. Raw scores are the original, unprocessed measurements as first collected, before any organisation or transformation. When a researcher decides to group raw data into a grouped frequency distribution, they must choose the class intervals, deciding the width of each interval and where the first interval begins. Because these choices are not fixed by the data themselves, the same set of raw scores can be grouped in many different legitimate ways, producing different grouped distributions depending on the interval size and starting point selected. In other words, grouping introduces arbitrariness, and there is no single uniquely correct grouped distribution for a given set of raw scores. This is an important caution in descriptive statistics: changing the interval width can change the apparent shape of a distribution, smoothing detail or exaggerating peaks, which is why the choice of intervals should be principled, typically aiming for a manageable number of intervals, often around ten to twenty, of equal width. The point of the item is that raw data permit multiple grouping schemes, so raw is the word that completes the sentence, option a." },
  { id: "ihbas24_038", section: "biostats",
    q: "are particularly helpful when comparing frequency distribution in which the number of cases differs.",
    options: { a: "Cumulative frequencies", b: "Frequencies percentage", c: "Frequency proportion", d: "Relative frequencies" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Cumulative frequencies show running totals up to each interval, useful for percentiles, but they remain on the raw count scale and do not solve the unequal-sample-size comparison problem.", b: "Frequencies percentage is loosely worded and is essentially a relative frequency expressed as a percent; the standard textbook term for the comparison tool is relative frequencies.", c: "Frequency proportion is not the standard label used here; the recognised term for proportions used to compare unequal distributions is relative frequencies." },
    explanation: "Relative frequencies, option d, are particularly helpful when comparing frequency distributions in which the number of cases differs. A relative frequency is the frequency of a category or interval divided by the total number of observations, expressing each frequency as a proportion or percentage of the whole. The reason this matters for comparison is that raw frequencies are tied to sample size. If one group has two hundred people and another has fifty, comparing the raw counts in each category is misleading because the larger group will naturally have larger counts everywhere. Converting to relative frequencies rescales both distributions to the same base, effectively putting them on a common per-unit footing, so that the shape and balance of the two distributions can be compared fairly regardless of how many cases each contains. This is why relative frequency distributions, and their cumulative form the relative cumulative frequency, are standard tools when comparing samples of unequal size. The defining advantage, fair comparison across distributions with different totals, identifies relative frequencies as the answer, option d." },
  { id: "ihbas24_039", section: "therapy",
    q: "The chief characteristic of ‘hypnosis’ is :",
    options: { a: "Heightened arousal", b: "Increased suggestibility", c: "Enhanced creativity", d: "Increased self esteem" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Heightened arousal is wrong because hypnotic induction typically involves relaxation and reduced arousal, not increased arousal.", c: "Enhanced creativity is not the defining feature of hypnosis; suggestibility is, though hypnosis may sometimes influence imagination.", d: "Increased self esteem is not a core characteristic of the hypnotic state; suggestibility is the defining hallmark." },
    explanation: "The chief characteristic of hypnosis is increased suggestibility, option b. Hypnosis is a state, or according to some theorists a socially constructed role enactment, of focused attention and heightened responsiveness to suggestion, typically induced by a procedure in which a participant is guided to relax, narrow their attention, and follow the hypnotist's suggestions. The defining feature across theoretical accounts is that the hypnotised person becomes markedly more responsive to suggestions, which can alter perception, memory, voluntary action, sensation, and even reported pain. Under hypnosis people may experience suggested analgesia, perceptual distortions, posthypnotic amnesia, or carry out posthypnotic suggestions. There are two broad theoretical camps. State or dissociation theories, associated with Ernest Hilgard and his hidden observer concept, view hypnosis as an altered state in which consciousness is divided. Non-state or social-cognitive theories, associated with Theodore Barber and Nicholas Spanos, argue that hypnotic phenomena reflect motivated role-playing, expectancy, and the social context rather than a special trance state. Individuals differ in hypnotic susceptibility, measured by scales such as the Stanford Hypnotic Susceptibility Scale. Across all accounts the core marker is enhanced suggestibility, so option b is correct, not arousal, creativity, or self-esteem." },
  { id: "ihbas24_040", section: "general",
    q: "Which factor is an organizational stressor leading to job related stress?",
    options: { a: "Family and Economic problems", b: "Political Uncertainty", c: "Task and Role demands", d: "Number of dependents in family" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Family and economic problems are personal or individual stressors arising outside the workplace, not organisational stressors.", b: "Political uncertainty is an environmental or extra-organisational stressor, not something internal to the organisation.", d: "Number of dependents in the family is a personal life factor, not a demand created by the organisation or the job." },
    explanation: "Task and role demands, option c, are organisational stressors leading to job-related stress. Occupational stress models distinguish stressors that originate within the organisation and the job itself from stressors that originate in the individual's personal life or the wider environment. Organisational stressors include task demands such as workload, time pressure, and job complexity; role demands such as role conflict, when incompatible expectations are placed on the worker, and role ambiguity, when the worker is unsure of what is expected; interpersonal demands; and organisational structure and leadership style. These arise from the design and context of the work. By contrast, personal and economic problems, the number of dependents in one's family, and political uncertainty are sources of stress that lie outside the organisation, falling under individual or environmental stressors. Karasek's job demand-control model, for example, locates strain in the combination of high job demands and low control, both organisational features. The exam logic is to identify which option is internal to the workplace, and task and role demands are the textbook organisational stressors, making option c correct." },
  { id: "ihbas24_041", section: "social",
    q: "What is normative social influence?",
    options: { a: "Influence based on the desire to be correct and accurate", b: "Influence based on the desire to be accepted and liked by others", c: "Influence based on personal beliefs and values", d: "Influence based on perceived power and authority" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Influence based on the desire to be correct and accurate is informational social influence, not normative.", c: "Influence based on personal beliefs and values is not social influence at all; it is internally driven, whereas normative influence is driven by others' approval.", d: "Influence based on perceived power and authority describes obedience and authority dynamics, not the acceptance-seeking motive of normative influence." },
    explanation: "Normative social influence is influence based on the desire to be accepted and liked by others, option b. Deutsch and Gerard in 1955 distinguished two reasons people conform. Normative social influence stems from the need for social approval and belonging; people go along with the group to gain acceptance and to avoid the discomfort of rejection, disapproval, or ridicule, even when they privately disagree. This typically produces public compliance, an outward change in behaviour without a corresponding change in private belief. The contrasting type, informational social influence, stems from the desire to be correct; in ambiguous or uncertain situations people look to others as a source of valid information about reality, and this often produces private acceptance, genuine internalised belief change. Asch's line-judgement conformity studies, where people conformed to an obviously wrong majority to fit in, illustrate normative influence, while Sherif's autokinetic studies, where people converged on a group estimate in a genuinely ambiguous task, illustrate informational influence. The defining feature of normative influence is the motive of acceptance and being liked, exactly option b, distinguishing it from the accuracy motive, personal values, or authority." },
  { id: "ihbas24_042", section: "general",
    q: "Who is regarded as the pioneer of psychology in India, establishing the first Indian psychological laboratory?",
    options: { a: "G. S. Rathi", b: "S. K. Mangal", c: "Girindrashekhar Bose", d: "A. K.7 Singh" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "G. S. Rathi is not the recognised pioneer who established the first Indian psychology laboratory.", b: "S. K. Mangal is a well-known author of psychology and education textbooks in India, not the founding figure of the discipline.", d: "A. K. Singh is the author of widely used research methodology and psychology textbooks in India, not the pioneer who set up the first laboratory." },
    explanation: "Girindrashekhar Bose, option c, is regarded as the pioneer of psychology in India and is most associated with the early institutional development of the field. Bose was the first Indian to obtain a doctorate in psychology and founded the Indian Psychoanalytical Society in 1922, becoming the leading figure in bringing psychoanalysis to India and corresponding famously with Sigmund Freud. He was central to establishing psychology as a discipline in India and was associated with the department of psychology at the University of Calcutta. The first Indian psychology laboratory and the first department of psychology were established at Calcutta University in the early twentieth century, the milieu in which Bose worked, and he is conventionally credited in Indian competitive exam material as the pioneer of Indian psychology and psychoanalysis. He developed his own theory of opposite wishes and applied psychoanalytic ideas within an Indian cultural frame. For the exam, the key association to retain is Bose with the founding of Indian psychology and the Indian Psychoanalytical Society, marking option c as the intended correct answer." },
  { id: "ihbas24_043", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Which brain structure is primarily involved in forming new memories?",
    options: { a: "Amygdala", b: "Hippocampus", c: "Cerebellum", d: "Thalamus" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "The amygdala attaches emotional significance to memories and processes fear, but it does not perform the core encoding of new declarative memories that the hippocampus does.", c: "The cerebellum supports motor learning and coordination, a form of procedural memory, not the formation of new explicit memories.", d: "The thalamus is a sensory relay station and contributes to memory circuits, but the hippocampus is the structure primarily responsible for forming new memories." },
    explanation: "The hippocampus, option b, is primarily involved in forming new memories. The hippocampus is a seahorse-shaped structure in the medial temporal lobe, part of the limbic system, and it is essential for consolidating new explicit, declarative memories, the conscious memories of facts and events, transferring them from short-term into long-term storage. The most famous evidence comes from the patient Henry Molaison, known as H.M., who had both hippocampi removed to treat epilepsy and was thereafter unable to form new long-term declarative memories, a profound anterograde amnesia, even though his old memories, his intelligence, and his ability to learn new motor skills remained intact. This dissociation revealed that the hippocampus is critical for encoding new declarative memories but not for storing them permanently or for procedural learning. The hippocampus also supports spatial memory and navigation, evidenced by place cells discovered by O'Keefe, and it is one of the few regions where adult neurogenesis occurs. It is among the earliest structures damaged in Alzheimer's disease, which is why memory loss is an early sign. The exam link is hippocampus equals new memory formation, so option b is correct." },
  { id: "ihbas24_044", section: "biopsych",
    q: "What is the term for the brain’s process of regulating its own responses to stress by controlling hormone release?",
    options: { a: "Neuroplasticity", b: "Homeostasis", c: "Allostasis", d: "Circadian rhythm" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Neuroplasticity is the brain's structural and functional changeability through experience, not the regulation of stress-hormone responses.", b: "Homeostasis is the maintenance of a constant internal environment around fixed set points, distinct from the active anticipatory adjustment of allostasis.", d: "Circadian rhythm is the roughly twenty-four-hour biological clock governing sleep-wake and hormone cycles, not the stress-regulation process described." },
    explanation: "Allostasis, option c, is the term for the brain's process of maintaining stability through change by actively regulating its responses to stress, including hormone release. The concept was introduced by Sterling and Eyer and developed by Bruce McEwen. Where homeostasis means keeping internal variables constant around a fixed set point, allostasis means achieving stability through change: the body anticipates demands and adjusts its physiological set points to meet them, mobilising the cardiovascular, metabolic, immune, and especially the neuroendocrine stress systems. Central to allostasis is the regulation of stress hormones, with the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis releasing cortisol and the sympathetic system releasing catecholamines in anticipation of and in response to challenge. The cumulative wear and tear on the body from repeated or chronic activation of these allostatic responses is termed allostatic load, and excessive allostatic load is linked to hypertension, immune suppression, hippocampal atrophy, and a host of stress-related illnesses. The crucial distinction for the exam is that allostasis is active, anticipatory regulation of stress responses through hormone control, which fits the question precisely, whereas homeostasis is passive maintenance of a fixed equilibrium, so option c is correct." },
  { id: "ihbas24_045", section: "general",
    q: "Which model proposes that memory is organized in a network of interconnected nodes?",
    options: { a: "Associative model", b: "Connectionist model", c: "Network model", d: "Linkage model" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "The associative model broadly describes learning by association between ideas, an older and looser notion than the formal node-and-weight architecture of connectionism.", c: "Network model is a tempting plain-language choice, but the standard recognised term in cognitive psychology for the interconnected-nodes, parallel-processing architecture is connectionist.", d: "Linkage model is not a recognised term in memory theory for the network-of-nodes conception." },
    explanation: "The connectionist model, option b, proposes that memory and knowledge are organised in a network of interconnected nodes operating in parallel. Connectionism, also called parallel distributed processing, models cognition as networks of simple, neuron-like units, the nodes, connected by weighted links. Information is not stored in a single location but is distributed across the pattern of connection weights, and processing occurs through the simultaneous, parallel activation spreading among many units. Learning happens by adjusting the connection weights, often through algorithms such as backpropagation, so that the network gradually comes to represent regularities in its input. This framework, championed by Rumelhart, McClelland, and the PDP research group in the 1980s, was inspired by the brain's neural architecture and contrasts with classical symbolic models that store discrete rules and representations. In memory research the related concept of spreading activation, where activating one node spreads activation to connected nodes, explains priming and associative recall. The question's phrasing, a network of interconnected nodes, captures the connectionist or PDP conception, and although the term network model is intuitively appealing, the established psychological label for this node-and-connection architecture is connectionist, so option b is the intended answer." },
  { id: "ihbas24_046", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Which disorder involves alternating episodes of mania and depression?",
    options: { a: "Bipolar disorder", b: "Major depressive disorder", c: "Schizophrenia", d: "Generalized anxiety disorder" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Major depressive disorder involves depressive episodes only, with no history of mania or hypomania, so it lacks the alternating quality.", c: "Schizophrenia is a psychotic disorder defined by delusions, hallucinations, and disorganisation, not by mood cycling between mania and depression.", d: "Generalized anxiety disorder is defined by chronic excessive worry, not by alternating mood episodes." },
    explanation: "Bipolar disorder, option a, involves alternating episodes of mania and depression. Bipolar disorder is a mood disorder characterised by cycling between two opposite poles of mood. The manic pole features a distinct period of abnormally elevated, expansive, or irritable mood with increased energy, lasting at least a week in bipolar I, accompanied by symptoms such as grandiosity, decreased need for sleep, pressured speech, racing thoughts, distractibility, increased goal-directed activity, and risky behaviour. The depressive pole features the full picture of major depression, with low mood, anhedonia, and the associated neurovegetative symptoms. DSM-5 distinguishes bipolar I, which requires at least one full manic episode, from bipolar II, which involves hypomanic episodes, a milder form, alternating with major depressive episodes, and cyclothymia, a chronic milder fluctuation. The defining feature, the swing between manic highs and depressive lows, is exactly what the question describes. Bipolar disorder has a strong genetic component and is treated with mood stabilisers such as lithium and valproate, sometimes with antipsychotics, while antidepressants alone are used cautiously because they can trigger mania. The mania-plus-depression cycling signature makes option a correct." },
  { id: "ihbas24_047", section: "general",
    q: "The biopsychosocial model integrates which of the following factors in understanding mental health?",
    options: { a: "Biological, psychological, and cultural", b: "Biological, psychological, and social", c: "Cognitive, social, and emotional", d: "Genetic, environmental, and developmental" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Biological, psychological, and cultural is incorrect because the third element of the model is the broader social domain, of which culture is only one part.", c: "Cognitive, social, and emotional omits the biological dimension entirely, so it does not capture the biopsychosocial model.", d: "Genetic, environmental, and developmental describes other influences but is not the named triad of the biopsychosocial model." },
    explanation: "The biopsychosocial model integrates biological, psychological, and social factors, option b. The model was proposed by the psychiatrist George Engel in 1977 as a deliberate alternative to the narrow biomedical model, which reduced illness to biological malfunction alone. Engel argued that health and illness, both physical and mental, are best understood as products of the dynamic interaction among three domains. The biological domain includes genetics, brain chemistry, neurotransmitters, hormones, physical health, and medication effects. The psychological domain includes thoughts, emotions, beliefs, coping styles, personality, and learning history. The social domain includes family, relationships, culture, socioeconomic status, social support, and life stressors. None of these levels alone fully explains why a person develops a disorder or recovers from it; they operate together and influence one another. This integrative framework underlies modern case formulation in clinical psychology, where a clinician assesses predisposing, precipitating, perpetuating, and protective factors across all three domains, and it supports holistic, multimodal treatment. The exact triad to memorise is biological, psychological, and social, which makes option b correct over the alternative groupings offered." },
  { id: "ihbas24_048", section: "therapy",
    q: "What is the critical feature of Carl Rogers’ client-centred therapy?",
    options: { a: "Analysing unconscious desires", b: "Therapist providing unconditional positive regard", c: "Implementing strict behaviour modifications", d: "Using aversive stimuli" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Analysing unconscious desires is the method of psychoanalysis, not the accepting, non-directive stance of Rogerian therapy.", c: "Implementing strict behaviour modifications belongs to behaviour therapy, which uses conditioning techniques, not the relational warmth Rogers emphasised.", d: "Using aversive stimuli is a behavioural technique, the antithesis of Rogers' unconditional positive regard." },
    explanation: "The critical feature of Carl Rogers' client-centred therapy is the therapist providing unconditional positive regard, option b. Rogers founded client-centred, or person-centred, therapy within the humanistic tradition, holding that people have an innate drive toward growth and self-actualization that can be released in the right relational climate. Rogers specified three core therapist conditions necessary and sufficient for therapeutic change. Unconditional positive regard means accepting and valuing the client warmly and without conditions, regardless of what they disclose, so the client need not earn approval. Empathy means deeply and accurately understanding the client's inner world from their frame of reference and conveying that understanding. Congruence, or genuineness, means the therapist being authentic and transparent rather than hiding behind a professional facade. Together these conditions reduce the gap between the client's real self and ideal self and dissolve the conditions of worth that produced incongruence and distress. The therapy is non-directive: the therapist does not interpret, advise, or modify behaviour but follows and reflects the client. The hallmark singled out here, unconditional positive regard, is one of Rogers' three core conditions and the right answer, distinguishing his approach from psychoanalytic interpretation or behavioural techniques." },
  { id: "ihbas24_049", section: "therapy",
    q: "According to Freud, which term refers to the part of the personality that mediates between desires and social rules?",
    options: { a: "Id", b: "Superego", c: "Ego", d: "Self" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "The id is the source of the desires and impulses, operating on the pleasure principle; it is what must be mediated, not the mediator.", b: "The superego embodies the social rules and moral standards; it is one of the forces the ego balances, not the mediating agency itself.", d: "Self is a term central to Jung and to humanistic and self-psychology theorists, not part of Freud's structural id-ego-superego model." },
    explanation: "According to Freud, the ego, option c, is the part of the personality that mediates between desires and social rules. Freud's structural model divides the psyche into three agencies. The id is the primitive, unconscious reservoir of instinctual drives, present from birth, operating on the pleasure principle, which demands immediate gratification of impulses regardless of consequences. The superego, which develops later through identification with parental and societal standards, is the moral agency, the internalised conscience and ego ideal that imposes prohibitions and ideals and generates guilt. The ego, which develops to deal with reality, operates on the reality principle and is the executive of personality. It mediates among the demands of the id, the constraints of the superego, and the realities of the external world, seeking realistic and socially acceptable ways to satisfy the id's wishes while staying within the superego's limits. When this mediation generates anxiety, the ego deploys defense mechanisms. The question's phrasing, mediating between desires, that is the id, and social rules, that is the superego, points exactly to the ego, making option c correct. The label Self belongs to other theorists, not to Freud's structural model." },
  { id: "ihbas24_050", section: "biopsych",
    q: "What is the concept of ‘neural pruning’ in neuropsychology?",
    options: { a: "Strengthening of neural pathways", b: "Elimination of unused neural connections", c: "Growth of new neurons", d: "Increase in brain size" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Strengthening of neural pathways is the complementary process of synaptic strengthening, not pruning, which removes connections.", c: "Growth of new neurons is neurogenesis, the addition of neurons, the opposite of pruning's elimination of connections.", d: "Increase in brain size is overall brain growth, not the selective elimination of synapses that defines pruning." },
    explanation: "Neural pruning, option b, is the elimination of unused neural connections. Synaptic pruning is a developmental process by which the brain removes excess synapses, the connections between neurons, that are weak, redundant, or unused, while preserving and strengthening those that are frequently activated. Early in development the brain dramatically overproduces neurons and synapses, reaching a peak of synaptic density in infancy and early childhood that far exceeds the adult level. Then, guided by experience and activity, the brain refines this exuberant wiring: connections that are used are stabilised, following the Hebbian principle that cells that fire together wire together, while connections that go unused are eliminated. This use-it-or-lose-it sculpting makes neural circuits more efficient and is a key mechanism by which experience shapes brain organisation during sensitive periods. A major wave of pruning occurs in adolescence, particularly in the prefrontal cortex, refining higher cognitive circuits. Abnormal pruning, either too much or too little, has been implicated in disorders such as schizophrenia and autism. The defining idea is the removal of unused connections, which is exactly option b, distinct from strengthening pathways, growing new neurons, or increasing brain size." },
  { id: "ihbas24_051", section: "social",
    q: "The tendency of people to attribute their own behavior to external causes and other’s behavior to internal causes is referred to as :",
    options: { a: "Self-serving bias", b: "Fundamental attribution error", c: "Actor-Observer effect", d: "Stereotyping" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Self-serving bias is attributing one's own successes to internal causes and failures to external causes; it is valence-based, not a general actor-observer asymmetry.", b: "The fundamental attribution error is the tendency to overattribute others' behaviour to disposition; it does not include the self-side external attribution that the actor-observer effect specifies.", d: "Stereotyping is generalising traits from group membership, an unrelated process from causal attribution of behaviour." },
    explanation: "Attributing one's own behaviour to external causes while attributing others' behaviour to internal causes is the actor-observer effect, option c. Attribution theory studies how people explain the causes of behaviour, distinguishing internal, dispositional causes such as personality and ability from external, situational causes such as circumstances and luck. The actor-observer asymmetry, described by Jones and Nisbett, is the systematic tendency for actors to explain their own behaviour situationally while observers explain the same behaviour dispositionally. When I trip, I blame the uneven pavement, but when you trip, I think you are clumsy. Two explanations are offered: a perceptual one, that the actor's attention is directed outward at the situation while the observer's attention is directed at the actor, and an informational one, that the actor knows their own behaviour varies across situations while the observer sees only this instance. This must be carefully distinguished from related biases. The fundamental attribution error is the general tendency to overweight dispositional causes for others' behaviour. The self-serving bias is taking credit for successes internally but blaming failures externally. The defining feature here, the actor-versus-observer divergence in the same situation, identifies the actor-observer effect, option c." },
  { id: "ihbas24_052", section: "general",
    q: "According to law of items which are close together in space and time tend to be perceived as belonging together or forming an organised group.",
    options: { a: "Proximity", b: "Continuity", c: "Similarity", d: "Closure" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Continuity, or good continuation, is the grouping of elements along smooth continuous lines, not the grouping by nearness.", c: "Similarity groups elements by shared features such as colour or shape, not by spatial closeness.", d: "Closure is the tendency to complete incomplete figures by filling in gaps, not grouping by proximity." },
    explanation: "The Gestalt law that items close together in space and time are perceived as belonging together is the law of proximity, option a. Gestalt psychology, founded by Wertheimer, Koffka, and Kohler, held that the mind organises sensory elements into meaningful wholes, summed up in the dictum that the whole is greater than the sum of its parts. The Gestaltists proposed several laws of perceptual organisation describing how we group elements. The law of proximity states that elements near one another are grouped together; dots clustered closely are seen as a unit. The law of similarity states that elements resembling one another in shape, colour, or size are grouped together. The law of closure states that we fill in gaps to perceive complete, closed figures. The law of continuity, or good continuation, states that we perceive smooth, continuous paths rather than abrupt changes. The law of common fate states that elements moving together are grouped. All flow from the overarching law of Pragnanz, the tendency to perceive the simplest, most stable organisation. The specific cue, nearness in space and time producing grouping, is the definition of proximity, so option a is correct." },
  { id: "ihbas24_053", section: "biostats",
    q: "Data represented in a series of rectangles each of which represents the scores in one of the class interval of the tabulated distribution is called :",
    options: { a: "Frequency Polygon", b: "Bar Graph", c: "Histogram", d: "Pie Chart" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "A frequency polygon represents the distribution as connected points at interval midpoints, not as a series of rectangles.", b: "A bar graph displays discrete categorical data with separated bars, not contiguous rectangles over continuous class intervals.", d: "A pie chart shows parts of a whole as sectors of a circle, not class-interval frequencies as rectangles." },
    explanation: "Data shown as a series of adjacent rectangles, each representing the scores in a class interval of a grouped distribution, is a histogram, option c. A histogram is a graph for displaying the frequency distribution of a continuous, grouped quantitative variable. The horizontal axis represents the continuous scale divided into class intervals, and over each interval a bar is drawn whose height represents the frequency of scores in that interval. Because the underlying variable is continuous, the bars are drawn touching one another with no gaps, which visually conveys that the intervals are contiguous segments of a single continuous scale. This contiguity is the key feature that distinguishes a histogram from a bar graph: in a bar graph the categories are discrete and qualitative, so the bars are separated by spaces. The frequency polygon plots the same information as points at the midpoints of the intervals connected by straight lines, useful for comparing distributions. The pie chart shows proportions of a whole as sectors of a circle, appropriate for categorical parts of a total. The exam description, rectangles each representing a class interval of a tabulated distribution, is the textbook definition of a histogram, so option c is correct." },
  { id: "ihbas24_054", section: "assessment",
    q: "The tendency of the rater/interviewer to judge an individual on the basis of one single attribute is referred to as :",
    options: { a: "Similarity error", b: "Central tendency error", c: "Contrast error", d: "Halo error" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Similarity error is favouring candidates who resemble the rater in background or attitudes, not judging on a single attribute.", b: "Central tendency error is clustering all ratings around the middle of the scale, avoiding high and low extremes, a different bias.", c: "Contrast error is rating a person in comparison with the previous candidate rather than against an absolute standard, not the one-trait spillover of halo." },
    explanation: "Judging an individual on the basis of one single attribute is the halo error, option d. In rating and interview contexts, the halo error, also called the halo effect, occurs when the rater allows their overall impression of a person, or their judgement on one salient dimension, to bias their ratings on all other, logically independent dimensions. For example, if an interviewer is impressed by a candidate's confident communication, they may then rate that candidate highly on competence, integrity, and teamwork as well, even without evidence for those traits. The single attribute radiates like a halo across the whole evaluation, reducing the validity and discriminability of the ratings. This is one of several systematic rater errors that threaten the accuracy of performance appraisals and interviews. The central tendency error is the tendency to rate everyone near the middle of the scale, avoiding extremes. The contrast error is rating a person relative to a preceding person rather than against the standard. The similarity error is rating people more favourably when they resemble the rater. The defining cue, judging on one single attribute, points specifically to halo error, option d." },
  { id: "ihbas24_055", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Jean Charcot demonstrated that hysterical phenomena such as loss of feeling in limbs or fainting could be produced by :",
    options: { a: "Hypnosis", b: "Suggestion", c: "Both (A) and (B)", d: "Relaxation" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Hypnosis alone is incomplete because Charcot also used direct suggestion to produce and remove hysterical symptoms.", b: "Suggestion alone is incomplete because hypnosis was equally central to Charcot's demonstrations.", d: "Relaxation was not the technique Charcot used to produce hysterical phenomena; he used hypnosis and suggestion." },
    explanation: "Jean-Martin Charcot demonstrated that hysterical phenomena could be produced by both hypnosis and suggestion, so option c, both A and B, is correct. Charcot was the eminent nineteenth-century French neurologist at the Salpetriere hospital in Paris, often called the father of modern neurology. He took a serious scientific interest in hysteria, a condition then marked by physical symptoms such as paralyses, anaesthesias, loss of sensation in limbs, contractures, and fainting that had no detectable organic basis. In dramatic clinical demonstrations Charcot showed that he could both induce and remove such hysterical symptoms in susceptible patients through hypnosis and direct suggestion, revealing that these bodily symptoms had a psychological origin rather than a purely physical one. This was a landmark insight because it established that the mind could produce genuine physical symptoms, paving the way for the psychological understanding of what we now call conversion or functional neurological disorders. Charcot's demonstrations deeply influenced Sigmund Freud, who studied with him, and helped seed the ideas that became psychoanalysis. Because both hypnosis and suggestion were the means Charcot used, the combined option c is the accurate answer." },
  { id: "ihbas24_056", section: "general",
    q: "Barbara Fredrickson is known for her work on :",
    options: { a: "Cognitive distortions", b: "Flow theory", c: "Broaden-and-build theory", d: "Psychodynamic theory" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Cognitive distortions are associated with Aaron Beck's cognitive theory of depression, not with Fredrickson.", b: "Flow theory was developed by Mihaly Csikszentmihalyi, describing optimal absorbed experience, not Fredrickson's work.", d: "Psychodynamic theory originates with Freud and his successors, unrelated to Fredrickson's positive-emotion research." },
    explanation: "Barbara Fredrickson is known for the broaden-and-build theory, option c. Fredrickson, a leading researcher in positive psychology, proposed this theory to explain the evolutionary function of positive emotions. The theory has two linked claims. First, the broaden hypothesis holds that positive emotions such as joy, interest, contentment, and love widen a person's momentary thought-action repertoire, expanding attention, cognition, and the range of behaviours that come to mind, in contrast to negative emotions like fear which narrow attention onto specific survival actions such as escape. Second, the build hypothesis holds that this broadened mindset, accumulated over time, helps people build enduring personal resources, including physical, intellectual, social, and psychological resources such as resilience, skills, and relationships, that remain available long after the emotion has passed. Fredrickson also studied the positivity ratio and the upward spiral by which positive emotions and broadened thinking reinforce one another. The theory reframes positive emotions not as mere pleasant feelings but as adaptive mechanisms that promote growth and flourishing. The exam association is Fredrickson with broaden-and-build, making option c correct, while flow belongs to Csikszentmihalyi." },
  { id: "ihbas24_057", section: "social",
    q: "The bystander effect suggests that :",
    options: { a: "People are more likely to help when they are alone than in a group", b: "People are more likely to help when they are in a group than when alone", c: "Bystanders are more likely to intervene if they see others not helping", d: "Bystanders are less likely to notice emergencies in a group" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "This reverses the effect; people are less, not more, likely to help in a group because responsibility is diffused.", c: "Bystanders are more likely to intervene when others fail to help only in special cases; the core effect is that group presence suppresses helping, so this is not the general principle.", d: "The effect is not primarily about failing to notice emergencies; it is about diffusion of responsibility and pluralistic ignorance reducing intervention." },
    explanation: "The bystander effect suggests that people are more likely to help when they are alone than when in a group, option a. The bystander effect is the well-documented finding that the presence of other people reduces the likelihood that any one individual will help in an emergency. It was investigated by John Darley and Bibb Latane following the 1964 Kitty Genovese case. In their experiments, the more bystanders present, the less likely and the slower any individual was to intervene. Latane and Darley explained the effect through a decision model and identified two main mechanisms. Diffusion of responsibility means that when others are present, each individual feels that the responsibility to act is shared and diluted, so no single person feels personally obligated. Pluralistic ignorance means that in an ambiguous situation people look to others to gauge whether it is really an emergency, and if everyone is staying calm and inactive, each concludes there is no emergency, a collective misreading. There is also evaluation apprehension, the fear of acting wrongly in front of others. The net result is that a lone bystander, bearing full responsibility and not misled by others' inaction, helps more readily, making option a correct." },
  { id: "ihbas24_058", section: "general",
    q: "Julian Rotter's concept of locus of control describes whether individuals believe that their outcomes are controlled by :",
    options: { a: "External forces or internal efforts", b: "Their cognitive schemas", c: "Genetic predisposition", d: "Social status" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Cognitive schemas are organised knowledge structures from cognitive psychology and Piaget, not the internal-external attribution dimension Rotter defined.", c: "Genetic predisposition concerns hereditary influence, not the perceived source of control over one's outcomes.", d: "Social status is a sociological variable, not the belief about whether outcomes are internally or externally controlled." },
    explanation: "Julian Rotter's locus of control describes whether individuals believe their outcomes are controlled by external forces or internal efforts, option a. Rotter, a social learning theorist, introduced locus of control as a generalised expectancy about the source of reinforcement in one's life. People with an internal locus of control believe that their own actions, effort, decisions, and abilities determine what happens to them; they feel personally responsible for their successes and failures and tend to be more proactive, achievement-oriented, and persistent. People with an external locus of control believe that outcomes are determined by forces outside themselves, such as luck, fate, chance, or powerful others; they tend to feel less control over events and may be more passive. Rotter developed an Internal-External, or I-E, scale to measure this dimension. Locus of control is a key construct in his broader social learning theory, where the probability of a behaviour depends on the expectancy of reinforcement and the value placed on that reinforcement. The construct predicts coping, health behaviour, academic achievement, and resilience, with internal locus generally associated with better adjustment. The defining contrast is external forces versus internal efforts, which is exactly option a." },
  { id: "ihbas24_059", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Which neurotransmitter is primarily involved in muscle contraction and is also linked to learning and memory?",
    options: { a: "Dopamine", b: "Serotonin", c: "Acetylcholine", d: "Glutamate" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Dopamine is central to reward, motivation, and motor control via the basal ganglia, but it is not the neuromuscular-junction transmitter for muscle contraction.", b: "Serotonin regulates mood, appetite, and sleep; it is not the transmitter of muscle contraction.", d: "Glutamate is the main excitatory transmitter in the brain and important for learning, but acetylcholine, not glutamate, is the one that drives muscle contraction at the neuromuscular junction." },
    explanation: "Acetylcholine, option c, is the neurotransmitter primarily involved in muscle contraction and also linked to learning and memory. Acetylcholine, abbreviated ACh, was the first neurotransmitter discovered, by Otto Loewi. At the neuromuscular junction it is the messenger released by motor neurons onto skeletal muscle fibres, where it binds receptors to trigger muscle contraction; this is why drugs and toxins that block ACh, such as curare or botulinum toxin, cause paralysis. In the central nervous system acetylcholine plays a major role in attention, arousal, learning, and especially memory. Cholinergic neurons projecting from the basal forebrain, including the nucleus basalis of Meynert, to the cortex and hippocampus are crucial for memory formation, and their degeneration is a central feature of Alzheimer's disease, which is why cholinesterase inhibitors that boost ACh are used to treat its cognitive symptoms. Acetylcholine also functions in the autonomic nervous system, acting as the transmitter of the parasympathetic system and at preganglionic synapses. The dual role at the neuromuscular junction for movement and in the brain for memory is the defining clue, making option c correct over dopamine, serotonin, or glutamate." },
  { id: "ihbas24_060", section: "biostats",
    q: "Which research design is best suited for determining cause-and-effect relationships?",
    options: { a: "Correlational study", b: "Cross-sectional study", c: "Experimental design", d: "Case study" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "A correlational study measures the association between variables without manipulation, so it cannot establish causation or rule out confounds.", b: "A cross-sectional study compares different groups at one point in time and is observational, unable to determine cause and effect.", d: "A case study provides a rich in-depth description of a single case but lacks manipulation, control, and generalisability, so it cannot establish causation." },
    explanation: "The experimental design, option c, is best suited for determining cause-and-effect relationships. A true experiment is defined by three features that together license causal inference. First, the researcher manipulates the independent variable, deliberately creating different conditions rather than merely observing what naturally occurs. Second, the researcher randomly assigns participants to conditions, which balances confounding variables across groups so the groups are equivalent at the start. Third, the researcher controls extraneous variables, holding constant or eliminating other influences. Because the cause is manipulated, the groups differ only in that manipulation, and any difference in the measured dependent variable can be attributed to the independent variable. This is what gives experiments high internal validity, the confidence that the manipulation caused the effect. Correlational, cross-sectional, and case-study designs all lack manipulation and random assignment, so although they can reveal associations, describe phenomena, or generate hypotheses, they cannot rule out confounds or establish the direction of causation. The maxim correlation does not imply causation captures this limit. When a question asks which design establishes cause and effect, the answer is always the controlled experiment, so option c is correct." },
  { id: "ihbas24_061", section: "general",
    q: "Which branch of psychology is concerned with optimising human behaviour in workplaces?",
    options: { a: "Counselling psychology", b: "Industrial-organizational psychology", c: "Forensic psychology", d: "Developmental psychology" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Counselling psychology helps people with personal, emotional, vocational, and adjustment concerns across the normal range, not specifically optimising workplace performance.", c: "Forensic psychology applies psychology to legal and criminal justice settings, such as assessment for courts, not workplace optimisation.", d: "Developmental psychology studies how people change across the lifespan, not the optimisation of behaviour in organisations." },
    explanation: "Industrial-organisational psychology, option b, is the branch concerned with optimising human behaviour in workplaces. I-O psychology applies psychological principles, theories, and research methods to the workplace, with the dual aim of improving organisational effectiveness and the well-being of employees. Its industrial side focuses on the individual-job match: job analysis, recruitment and selection, employee testing, training, and performance appraisal. Its organisational side focuses on the social and structural context: motivation, job satisfaction, leadership, work teams, organisational culture, communication, and the management of change, as well as occupational stress and work-life balance. I-O psychologists use scientific methods to study questions such as how to design jobs that increase productivity, how to assess and develop employees fairly, and how to foster engagement and reduce turnover. The field traces back to early figures such as Hugo Munsterberg and to the Hawthorne studies, which highlighted social and motivational factors at work. The exam cue, optimising behaviour in workplaces, points squarely to industrial-organisational psychology, option b, distinguishing it from counselling, forensic, and developmental specialties." },
  { id: "ihbas24_062", section: "social",
    q: "A person is most likely to experience cognitive dissonance when :",
    options: { a: "Their thoughts align with their behaviour", b: "They are forced to make a decision that goes against their beliefs", c: "They are praised for completing a task", d: "They recall positive memories" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "When thoughts align with behaviour there is consonance, not dissonance, so this is the opposite of the dissonance-arousing condition.", c: "Being praised for completing a task is a consistent, positive experience that does not create the inconsistency that drives dissonance.", d: "Recalling positive memories involves no conflict between cognitions and so does not produce dissonance." },
    explanation: "A person is most likely to experience cognitive dissonance when forced to make a decision or behave in a way that goes against their beliefs, option b. As Festinger's theory holds, dissonance is the uncomfortable tension that arises from inconsistency between cognitions, especially between an attitude and a behaviour. The tension is greatest precisely when a person acts contrary to their genuine beliefs or values, particularly under conditions of perceived choice and insufficient external justification, because then they cannot easily explain the behaviour away. This is the situation captured in the induced-compliance paradigm, where people who freely agree to argue against their own views, or who are paid too little to justify a counter-attitudinal act, change their attitude to align with their behaviour and so reduce the dissonance. Other dissonance-arousing situations include making a difficult decision between attractive alternatives, which produces post-decision dissonance resolved by upgrading the chosen option and downgrading the rejected one, and expending great effort for a disappointing outcome, producing effort justification. Among the options, behaving against one's beliefs is the prototypical trigger, so option b is correct, while alignment of thought and action, praise, or pleasant memories produce no inconsistency and hence no dissonance." },
  { id: "ihbas24_063", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Which neuropsychological disorder is characterised by severe memory impairment and confusion, often caused by chronic alcohol abuse?",
    options: { a: "Schizophrenia", b: "Korsakoff’s syndrome", c: "Huntington’s disease", d: "Multiple sclerosis" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Schizophrenia is a psychotic disorder of thought and perception, not a memory syndrome caused by alcohol and thiamine deficiency.", c: "Huntington's disease is an inherited neurodegenerative disorder causing chorea and cognitive decline, not an alcohol-related amnesic syndrome.", d: "Multiple sclerosis is an autoimmune demyelinating disease of the central nervous system; it is not the alcohol-induced amnesic disorder described." },
    explanation: "Korsakoff's syndrome, option b, is the neuropsychological disorder marked by severe memory impairment and confusion, often caused by chronic alcohol abuse. Korsakoff's syndrome results from a deficiency of thiamine, vitamin B1, which is common in chronic alcoholism because alcohol impairs thiamine absorption and alcoholics often have poor nutrition. The thiamine deficiency damages structures involved in memory, particularly the mammillary bodies of the hypothalamus and the dorsomedial thalamus. The cardinal feature is a profound anterograde amnesia, an inability to form new memories, often accompanied by retrograde amnesia for events before onset. A striking and characteristic symptom is confabulation, the unintentional fabrication of memories to fill the gaps, where the patient produces false but sincerely believed accounts. Other features include disorientation, apathy, and lack of insight. Korsakoff's syndrome frequently follows an acute phase called Wernicke's encephalopathy, marked by confusion, ataxia, and eye-movement abnormalities; together they are termed Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome. Prompt thiamine treatment can prevent or limit the damage. The defining triad to recall is alcohol, thiamine deficiency, and amnesia with confabulation, which makes option b correct over the psychotic, genetic-motor, and demyelinating alternatives." },
  { id: "ihbas24_064", section: "dev",
    q: "In Erikson's theory, which conflict characterises late adulthood?",
    options: { a: "Autonomy vs. shame", b: "Integrity vs. despair", c: "Initiative vs. guilt", d: "Generativity vs. stagnation" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Autonomy versus shame and doubt is the second stage, in toddlerhood, concerning independence in self-care, not late adulthood.", c: "Initiative versus guilt is the third stage, in the preschool years, concerning purposeful activity and assertiveness, not old age.", d: "Generativity versus stagnation is the seventh stage, in middle adulthood, concerning contribution to the next generation, the stage just before integrity versus despair." },
    explanation: "In Erikson's theory, the conflict of late adulthood is integrity versus despair, option b. This is the eighth and final psychosocial stage, occurring in old age, roughly from the mid-sixties onward. The central task is to look back over one's life and arrive at a sense of meaning and acceptance. A person who can review their life and feel that it was meaningful, productive, and well-lived achieves ego integrity, a sense of coherence and wholeness, and the associated ego strength Erikson called wisdom, which allows one to face death with equanimity. A person who looks back with regret, feeling that life was wasted, that goals were unmet, and that there is no time to make amends, falls into despair, marked by bitterness, fear of death, and disgust. The resolution depends partly on how earlier stages were navigated. This stage is concerned with reflection and life review, not with the productivity and care for the next generation that define the preceding stage of generativity versus stagnation in middle adulthood. The exam mapping is late adulthood equals integrity versus despair, making option b correct." },
  { id: "ihbas24_065", section: "general",
    q: "What is the main difference between classical and operant conditioning?",
    options: { a: "Classical conditioning relies on reinforcement; operant conditioning does not", b: "Classical conditioning pairs two stimuli; operant conditioning relies on reinforcement and punishment", c: "Operant conditioning involves cognitive processes; classical conditioning does not", d: "Both are entirely different learning processes" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "This is backwards; operant conditioning, not classical, relies on reinforcement, and classical conditioning involves stimulus pairing, not reinforcement.", c: "Cognitive processes can be involved in both forms of learning, and the defining contrast is pairing versus consequences, not the presence or absence of cognition.", d: "Saying both are entirely different processes is vague and incorrect; they are related associative-learning paradigms distinguished by the specific mechanism in option b." },
    explanation: "The main difference is that classical conditioning pairs two stimuli, whereas operant conditioning relies on reinforcement and punishment, option b. Classical conditioning, discovered by Pavlov, is learning by association between stimuli. A neutral stimulus is repeatedly paired with an unconditioned stimulus that naturally elicits a response, until the neutral stimulus alone, now the conditioned stimulus, elicits a learned conditioned response. The learner is passive and the response is typically reflexive or emotional, such as Pavlov's dogs salivating to a bell. Operant conditioning, developed by Thorndike and Skinner, is learning by consequences. The organism emits a voluntary behaviour, and the consequence that follows determines whether the behaviour is repeated. Reinforcement, whether positive by adding something pleasant or negative by removing something aversive, increases the behaviour, while punishment decreases it. The learner is active and the behaviour is instrumental in producing outcomes. So the cleanest contrast is the mechanism: stimulus-stimulus pairing of an antecedent in classical conditioning versus response-consequence learning through reinforcement and punishment in operant conditioning. Option b captures this precisely, while the other statements either falsely deny reinforcement to operant learning or mischaracterise the relationship between the two paradigms." },
  { id: "ihbas24_066", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Generalized Anxiety Disorder (GAD) is a pervasive condition in which the sufferer experiences :",
    options: { a: "Continued apprehension about unspecified things", b: "Fear of fear", c: "Continual flashbacks to past events", d: "A desire to check that the environment is safe" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Fear of fear describes panic disorder, where the person fears the recurrence of panic attacks, not the diffuse worry of GAD.", c: "Continual flashbacks to past events describe PTSD, in which a specific trauma is re-experienced, not the unfocused worry of GAD.", d: "A desire to check that the environment is safe describes the checking compulsions of OCD, not the generalised apprehension of GAD." },
    explanation: "In generalised anxiety disorder the sufferer experiences continued apprehension about unspecified things, option a. The defining quality of GAD is that the anxiety is free-floating, diffuse, and not anchored to any single identifiable object or situation. The person worries chronically and excessively about a wide range of everyday matters such as work, health, finances, and family, and crucially the worry shifts from topic to topic and feels uncontrollable. This is what is meant by apprehension about unspecified things: there is no one specific feared stimulus as there would be in a phobia, no discrete panic trigger, and no single obsessional concern. This pervasive, generalised quality is precisely what distinguishes GAD from the other anxiety-related conditions. Panic disorder centres on the fear of having another panic attack, sometimes described as fear of fear. Post-traumatic stress disorder centres on re-experiencing a specific past trauma through flashbacks and intrusions. Obsessive-compulsive disorder involves checking and other compulsions driven by specific obsessions about safety or contamination. Because GAD is defined by its non-specific, ever-present apprehension, option a is the correct characterisation." },
  { id: "ihbas24_067", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Difficulty in seeing objects at a distance is called :",
    options: { a: "Myopia", b: "Hypermetropia", c: "Astigmatism", d: "Visual Field Defects" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Hypermetropia, or far-sightedness, is difficulty seeing near objects because light focuses behind the retina, the opposite of myopia.", c: "Astigmatism is blurred or distorted vision at all distances caused by irregular curvature of the cornea or lens, not specifically distance blur.", d: "Visual field defects refer to losses in parts of the field of vision, often from neurological damage, not the refractive distance-blur of myopia." },
    explanation: "Difficulty in seeing objects at a distance is called myopia, option a. Myopia, commonly known as near-sightedness, is a refractive error in which the eye focuses light from distant objects in front of the retina rather than directly on it. This happens because the eyeball is too long from front to back or the cornea is too curved, so that the converging light rays come to a focus before reaching the light-sensitive retina, leaving the distant image blurred while near objects are seen clearly. It is corrected with a concave, diverging lens that spreads the rays slightly so they focus farther back, on the retina. The opposite condition, hypermetropia or far-sightedness, occurs when the eyeball is too short or the cornea too flat, so light focuses behind the retina, making near objects blurred while distant ones are clearer; it is corrected with a convex, converging lens. Astigmatism is an irregular curvature of the cornea or lens that blurs vision at all distances. The simple memory rule is that myopia means trouble seeing far away, which matches the question exactly, so option a is correct." },
  { id: "ihbas24_068", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Consciously faking illness or symptoms of disability to achieve some specific nonmedical objective is :",
    options: { a: "Malingering", b: "Altered identities", c: "False identification", d: "Confabulation" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Altered identities refers to dissociative phenomena such as the distinct personality states in dissociative identity disorder, not deliberate symptom faking for gain.", c: "False identification is not a clinical term for symptom feigning; it does not describe conscious faking of illness for external benefit.", d: "Confabulation is the unintentional production of false memories, as in Korsakoff's syndrome, not the deliberate faking of illness." },
    explanation: "Consciously faking illness or symptoms to achieve a specific nonmedical objective is malingering, option a. Malingering is the deliberate, voluntary fabrication or gross exaggeration of physical or psychological symptoms motivated by an external, tangible incentive such as avoiding work, military duty, or criminal responsibility, obtaining financial compensation or disability benefits, or securing drugs. The two essential elements are that the symptom production is fully conscious and intentional, and that the goal is an external secondary gain rather than the adoption of the sick role itself. This is what distinguishes malingering from related conditions. In factitious disorder the person also intentionally produces symptoms but for the internal psychological reward of being seen as sick, with no external incentive. In somatic symptom and conversion disorders the symptoms are not consciously produced at all; they are genuinely experienced and not under voluntary control. Because malingering involves both deliberate deception and a concrete nonmedical payoff, it is technically classified not as a mental disorder but as a condition that may be a focus of clinical attention. The defining cue, conscious faking for a nonmedical objective, makes option a correct." },
  { id: "ihbas24_069", section: "abnormal",
    q: "In dissociative identity disorder, the identity that is frequently encountered, carrying the person's real name is :",
    options: { a: "Alter Identity", b: "Host Identity", c: "Ghost Identity", d: "Mixed Identity" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Alter identities are the additional personality states beyond the host, often emerging under stress, not the primary name-bearing identity.", c: "Ghost identity is not a recognised clinical term in the description of dissociative identity disorder.", d: "Mixed identity is not a standard term for the host; the identity carrying the real name and most often present is the host." },
    explanation: "In dissociative identity disorder the identity that is frequently encountered and carries the person's real name is the host identity, option b. Dissociative identity disorder, formerly called multiple personality disorder, is characterised by the presence of two or more distinct personality states, or identities, that recurrently take control of the person's behaviour, accompanied by gaps in memory for everyday events and personal information. The various identities are conventionally distinguished by their roles. The host is the identity that is in control most of the time, that presents for treatment, that carries the person's given name, and that is usually the one others ordinarily interact with; it is often the identity least aware of the others. The alters are the other personality states, which may differ in name, age, gender, voice, mannerisms, and even reported physiological characteristics, and may emerge in response to specific stressors. The disorder is strongly associated with severe, repeated childhood trauma, and is understood as an extreme dissociative defense in which overwhelming experience is walled off into separate identity states. The cue here, the frequently present identity bearing the real name, is the definition of the host, making option b correct." },
  { id: "ihbas24_070", section: "general",
    q: "Which cognitive skill is involved in learning to read?",
    options: { a: "Mental representation", b: "Theory of mind", c: "Spoken language", d: "Imagination" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Mental representation is a general cognitive capacity to hold internal models; it is too broad to be the specific skill that reading is built upon.", b: "Theory of mind is the understanding that others have separate mental states, important for social cognition and comprehension of intent, but it is not the foundational skill for decoding print.", d: "Imagination is the generation of novel mental images and ideas, not the language foundation that learning to read depends on." },
    explanation: "The intended answer is spoken language, option c, as the cognitive skill involved in learning to read. Reading is built upon the foundation of oral language. Before a child can decode print they must already possess spoken language: a vocabulary of word meanings, knowledge of how sounds combine, and an understanding of grammar and sentence structure. Crucially, learning to read in an alphabetic script depends on phonological awareness, the ability to hear and manipulate the individual sounds, the phonemes, that make up spoken words, and on mapping those spoken sounds onto written letters and letter patterns, the alphabetic principle. This is why spoken language proficiency and phonological skills are the strongest early predictors of reading success, and why deficits in phonological processing underlie dyslexia. Reading essentially recodes the already-mastered spoken language into a visual symbol system, so the spoken language system is the cognitive substrate the learner draws on. While reading also recruits visual processing, attention, and memory, the option that names the underlying language capacity reading is built from is spoken language, so option c is the intended answer, with the other options naming broader or different cognitive abilities." },
  { id: "ihbas24_071", section: "general",
    q: "What phenomenon explains why individuals can remember the first and last items of a list better than the middle ones?",
    options: { a: "List position effect", b: "Primacy-recency effect", c: "Order recall effect", d: "Sequence memory effect" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "List position effect is a vague, non-standard phrasing; the recognised term for the U-shaped recall pattern is the serial position or primacy-recency effect.", c: "Order recall effect is not a recognised memory term for the better recall of first and last items.", d: "Sequence memory effect is not the standard term; primacy-recency effect is the established label." },
    explanation: "The phenomenon by which the first and last items of a list are remembered better than the middle is the primacy-recency effect, option b, together known as the serial position effect. When people are asked to recall a list of items in any order, recall accuracy plotted against item position forms a U-shaped serial position curve. The superior recall of the first few items is the primacy effect, attributed to those items having been rehearsed more and transferred into long-term memory because they entered an uncrowded rehearsal buffer. The superior recall of the last few items is the recency effect, attributed to those items still residing in short-term or working memory at the moment of recall. The poorer recall of the middle items reflects that they were neither rehearsed enough to consolidate into long-term memory nor recent enough to remain in short-term store. This dissociation is strong evidence for the multi-store model of memory by Atkinson and Shiffrin, because manipulations that abolish the recency effect, such as a delay filled with a distractor task, leave the primacy effect intact, showing the two arise from different memory systems. The combined label primacy-recency effect is the correct term, option b." },
  { id: "ihbas24_072", section: "dev",
    q: "According to Erik Erikson’s psychosocial theory, the key challenge in adolescence is :",
    options: { a: "Trust vs. Mistrust", b: "Industry vs. Inferiority", c: "Identity vs. Role Confusion", d: "Autonomy vs. Shame" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Trust versus mistrust is the first stage, in infancy, concerning the dependability of caregivers, not the adolescent identity task.", b: "Industry versus inferiority is the fourth stage, in school-age children, concerning competence and mastery of skills, not adolescence.", d: "Autonomy versus shame and doubt is the second stage, in toddlerhood, concerning self-control and independence, not adolescence." },
    explanation: "According to Erikson's psychosocial theory, the key challenge in adolescence is identity versus role confusion, option c. This is the fifth stage, occurring roughly between ages twelve and eighteen. The central task is for the adolescent to develop a coherent and stable sense of self, an identity, by integrating their various roles, values, beliefs, talents, and goals into a unified whole and answering the question of who they are and what they want to become. Adolescents experiment with different roles, ideologies, and relationships in a period Erikson called a psychosocial moratorium, a sanctioned time of exploration before committing to adult roles. Successful resolution yields a firm sense of identity and the ego strength of fidelity, the ability to commit to values and others. Failure produces role confusion, an unclear, fragmented, or unstable sense of self, uncertainty about one's place in the world. James Marcia later extended this into four identity statuses based on the presence of exploration and commitment: identity achievement, moratorium, foreclosure, and diffusion. The exam mapping is adolescence equals identity versus role confusion, making option c correct over the infancy, school-age, and toddler stages listed." },
  { id: "ihbas24_073", section: "general",
    q: "The term “stream of consciousness”, referring to the flow of thoughts in the human mind, was coined by which psychologist?",
    options: { a: "Carl Jung", b: "Wilhelm Wundt", c: "William James", d: "B.F. Skinner" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Carl Jung developed analytical psychology, with concepts like the collective unconscious and archetypes, not the stream of consciousness.", b: "Wilhelm Wundt founded experimental psychology and structuralist introspection, studying elements of consciousness, not its continuous stream.", d: "B.F. Skinner was a radical behaviourist who rejected the study of consciousness altogether, so he could not have coined the term." },
    explanation: "The term stream of consciousness was coined by William James, option c. James, the father of American psychology and a leading functionalist, used the phrase in his landmark 1890 work The Principles of Psychology to describe the nature of conscious experience. He argued against the structuralist view that consciousness could be broken into discrete static elements, insisting instead that consciousness is a continuous, flowing, ever-changing process, like a stream or river rather than a chain of separate links. He described consciousness as having several characteristics: it is personal, belonging to an individual self; it is continuous, with no real gaps even across breaks like sleep; it is constantly changing, never exactly the same thought twice; it is selective, attending to some things and ignoring others; and it deals with objects independent of itself. This conception fit his functionalist emphasis on the adaptive purpose of mental processes, since the flowing, selective stream serves to guide the organism's choices and adaptation. The phrase later profoundly influenced literature through writers like Joyce and Woolf. The exam association is stream of consciousness with William James, making option c correct." },
  { id: "ihbas24_074", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Which part of the brain controls basic life-sustaining functions like heart rate and breathing?",
    options: { a: "Thalamus", b: "Medulla", c: "Cerebellum", d: "Hypothalamus" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "The thalamus is the brain's sensory relay station, routing incoming sensory information to the cortex, not the control of heart rate and breathing.", c: "The cerebellum coordinates movement, balance, and posture, not the vital autonomic functions of the medulla.", d: "The hypothalamus regulates homeostasis, hormones, hunger, thirst, and temperature, but the immediate control of heartbeat and respiration resides in the medulla." },
    explanation: "The medulla, option b, controls basic life-sustaining functions like heart rate and breathing. The medulla oblongata is the lowest part of the brainstem, where the brain connects to the spinal cord. It contains vital autonomic centres that regulate the most fundamental involuntary processes necessary for survival: the cardiac centre that controls heart rate and the force of contraction, the respiratory centre that controls the rate and depth of breathing, and the vasomotor centre that controls blood vessel diameter and thus blood pressure. It also governs reflexes such as swallowing, coughing, sneezing, and vomiting. Because these functions are essential for life, damage to the medulla is frequently fatal, which is why brainstem death is a criterion for death. The medulla works automatically, without conscious control, and is among the most evolutionarily ancient parts of the brain. This contrasts with the other listed structures, which serve relay, coordination, and regulatory roles but not the moment-to-moment maintenance of heartbeat and respiration. The exam rule is that the most basic vegetative life functions are housed in the medulla, making option b correct." },
  { id: "ihbas24_075", section: "assessment",
    q: "Which term refers to the consistency of a measure?",
    options: { a: "Validity", b: "Reliability", c: "Generalizability", d: "Sensitivity" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Validity is whether a measure actually assesses what it is intended to assess, the accuracy of the measure, not its consistency.", c: "Generalizability is the extent to which findings or scores apply across settings, populations, or times, not the consistency of a single measure.", d: "Sensitivity is the ability of a test to correctly detect those who truly have a condition, a diagnostic-accuracy concept, not consistency." },
    explanation: "Reliability, option b, refers to the consistency of a measure. In psychometrics, reliability is the degree to which a test or measurement yields stable, consistent, and repeatable results, free from random measurement error. A reliable instrument gives the same or very similar results when the same thing is measured again under the same conditions. There are several forms of reliability, each addressing a different source of inconsistency. Test-retest reliability assesses stability over time by correlating scores from two administrations to the same people. Internal consistency, often measured by Cronbach's alpha, assesses whether items within a test measure the same construct coherently. Inter-rater reliability assesses agreement between different observers or scorers. Parallel-forms reliability assesses consistency across equivalent versions of a test. Reliability is necessary but not sufficient for validity, which is the distinct question of whether the test measures what it claims to measure. A scale can be perfectly reliable yet invalid, consistently measuring the wrong thing, but it cannot be valid without being reliable. The key exam distinction is reliability equals consistency, validity equals accuracy, so option b is correct." },
  { id: "ihbas24_076", section: "general",
    q: "The term ‘anterograde amnesia’ refers to :",
    options: { a: "The inability to retrieve old memories", b: "The inability to form new memories", c: "Temporary memory loss", d: "Inability to process visual stimuli" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "The inability to retrieve old memories is retrograde amnesia, the loss of memories formed before the injury, not anterograde amnesia.", c: "Temporary memory loss is a vague description and not the specific meaning of anterograde, which denotes a deficit in forming new memories.", d: "Inability to process visual stimuli is a perceptual or agnosic problem, not a memory deficit at all." },
    explanation: "Anterograde amnesia refers to the inability to form new memories, option b. Amnesia, the loss of memory, comes in two principal directions relative to the onset of the causing event such as brain injury or disease. Anterograde amnesia is the impaired ability to form and store new long-term memories for events occurring after the onset; the person can no longer lay down new declarative memories, so each new experience fails to be retained even though events before the injury may be remembered. Retrograde amnesia, by contrast, is the loss of memories formed before the onset, the inability to retrieve previously stored information, often with a temporal gradient in which recent memories are more affected than remote ones. The classic illustration of anterograde amnesia is the patient H.M., whose hippocampi were surgically removed; he retained his old memories and intelligence but could not form new lasting memories, living perpetually in the present. Anterograde amnesia thus reflects a failure of encoding and consolidation, typically tied to hippocampal and medial temporal damage. The defining cue, inability to form new memories going forward, makes option b correct, distinguishing it from the retrieval failure of retrograde amnesia." },
  { id: "ihbas24_077", section: "therapy",
    q: "In operant conditioning, what is the term for when a previously reinforced behaviour is no longer reinforced and gradually fades?",
    options: { a: "Extinction", b: "Generalization", c: "Discrimination", d: "Shaping" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Generalization is responding to stimuli similar to the conditioned one, not the fading of behaviour when reinforcement is removed.", c: "Discrimination is learning to respond to one stimulus but not to others, not the weakening of a response through non-reinforcement.", d: "Shaping is the reinforcement of successive approximations to teach a new behaviour, the opposite process from extinction." },
    explanation: "In operant conditioning, when a previously reinforced behaviour is no longer reinforced and gradually fades, this is extinction, option a. Extinction is the weakening and eventual disappearance of a learned response when the reinforcement that maintained it is withdrawn. If a rat has learned to press a lever for food and the food is then permanently discontinued, lever-pressing initially may increase in a burst, the extinction burst, but then declines and stops. Extinction is not the same as forgetting; the association is not erased but suppressed, as shown by spontaneous recovery, the reappearance of the extinguished response after a rest period, and by rapid reacquisition if reinforcement resumes. Extinction is a central concept in behaviour therapy, where maladaptive behaviours maintained by reinforcement, such as attention-seeking tantrums, can be reduced by systematically removing the reinforcing consequence. It must be distinguished from the other operant terms. Generalisation is responding to stimuli similar to the original. Discrimination is responding differently to different stimuli. Shaping is reinforcing successive approximations to build a new behaviour. The defining cue, a reinforced behaviour fading once reinforcement stops, is exactly extinction, making option a correct." },
  { id: "ihbas24_078", section: "abnormal",
    q: "What is a defining feature of somatic symptom disorder?",
    options: { a: "Hallucinations", b: "Physical symptoms without a medical cause", c: "Delusions of grandeur", d: "Obsessive behaviours" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Hallucinations are perceptual disturbances seen in psychotic disorders, not the bodily complaints of somatic symptom disorder.", c: "Delusions of grandeur are fixed false beliefs of importance found in psychosis and mania, unrelated to somatic symptoms.", d: "Obsessive behaviours, the rituals of OCD, are not the defining feature of somatic symptom disorder, which centres on physical symptoms." },
    explanation: "A defining feature of somatic symptom disorder is physical symptoms without an adequate medical cause, option b. Somatic symptom disorder, in DSM-5, is characterised by one or more distressing physical, bodily, symptoms together with excessive thoughts, feelings, or behaviours related to those symptoms, such as disproportionate and persistent worry about their seriousness, high anxiety about health, or excessive time and energy devoted to them. The symptoms are real and distressing to the person and may or may not be explainable by a medical condition; the diagnosis hinges not on the absence of medical explanation alone but on the maladaptive psychological response to bodily symptoms. In the classic and exam-level framing, the hallmark is the presence of physical complaints that lack a sufficient organic medical cause, distinguishing it from genuine medical illness. The symptoms are not consciously produced, which separates it from malingering and factitious disorder, and they are experienced as fully real, unlike the deliberate faking of malingering. Related conditions include illness anxiety disorder, the preoccupation with having a serious illness despite few symptoms, and conversion disorder, neurological-type symptoms without organic basis. The cue, physical symptoms without a medical cause, makes option b correct over hallucinations, delusions, or obsessions." },
  { id: "ihbas24_079", section: "social",
    q: "In the context of social psychology, the ‘bystander effect’ occurs because of:",
    options: { a: "Diffusion of responsibility", b: "Social loafing", c: "Group polarisation", d: "Cognitive dissonance" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Social loafing is the reduction of individual effort in collective work tasks; it shares the diffusion mechanism but is not the term used for the emergency-helping bystander effect.", c: "Group polarisation is the strengthening of group members' initial attitudes through discussion, unrelated to the failure to help.", d: "Cognitive dissonance is the tension from inconsistent cognitions, not the reason bystanders fail to intervene." },
    explanation: "The bystander effect occurs because of diffusion of responsibility, option a. Among the mechanisms Latane and Darley identified to explain why people are less likely to help when others are present, diffusion of responsibility is the central and most cited cause. When several bystanders witness an emergency, the felt obligation to act is divided among all of them, so that each individual experiences a diluted, reduced sense of personal responsibility to intervene. Everyone assumes that someone else will, or should, take action, and as a result no one does, or action is delayed. The more bystanders present, the more thinly responsibility is spread and the less likely any single person is to help. This is why a lone witness, who bears the entire responsibility alone, is far more likely to assist than any one member of a crowd. Diffusion of responsibility also helps explain the related phenomenon of social loafing in group tasks, but in the emergency-helping context it is the engine of the bystander effect. Alongside it operate pluralistic ignorance and evaluation apprehension, but the question asks for the principal cause, and the answer is diffusion of responsibility, option a." },
  { id: "ihbas24_080", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Which neurotransmitter is most closely associated with reward and pleasure systems in the brain?",
    options: { a: "Serotonin", b: "Dopamine", c: "Norepinephrine", d: "Acetylcholine" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Serotonin regulates mood, appetite, and sleep and is implicated in depression, but it is not the primary reward-and-pleasure transmitter.", c: "Norepinephrine governs arousal, alertness, and the fight-or-flight response, not the reward system specifically.", d: "Acetylcholine is involved in muscle contraction, attention, and memory, not the reward and pleasure circuitry." },
    explanation: "Dopamine, option b, is the neurotransmitter most closely associated with reward and pleasure systems in the brain. Dopamine is a catecholamine neurotransmitter central to motivation, reward, reinforcement learning, and movement. The brain's reward circuitry, the mesolimbic dopamine pathway, runs from the ventral tegmental area to the nucleus accumbens, and the release of dopamine here signals reward and reinforces the behaviours that led to it. Importantly, modern research shows dopamine is less about the raw experience of pleasure itself and more about reward prediction, wanting, and the reinforcement of goal-directed action; it spikes especially in response to unexpected rewards and cues that predict reward, encoding a reward prediction error. Virtually all addictive drugs increase dopamine in this pathway, which is why dopamine is central to the neuroscience of addiction. Dopamine is also crucial for voluntary movement via the nigrostriatal pathway, whose degeneration causes Parkinson's disease, and excess dopamine activity is implicated in the positive symptoms of schizophrenia, the basis of the dopamine hypothesis. The exam link is dopamine with reward, pleasure, motivation, and addiction, making option b correct over serotonin, norepinephrine, and acetylcholine." },
  { id: "ihbas24_081", section: "biostats",
    q: "Longitudinal and cross-sectional designs can be used to study how behaviour changes as a function of age. A disadvantage of cross-sectional studies is that the result can be influenced by :",
    options: { a: "Random assignment", b: "Socio-economic factors", c: "Cohort difference", d: "Participant attrition" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Random assignment is a feature of experiments controlling confounds; it is not a disadvantage of cross-sectional developmental designs.", b: "Socio-economic factors are one source of cohort differences but the precise named disadvantage of cross-sectional designs is the cohort effect itself.", d: "Participant attrition, the dropout of participants over time, is the characteristic disadvantage of longitudinal, not cross-sectional, studies." },
    explanation: "A disadvantage of cross-sectional studies is that results can be influenced by cohort differences, option c. Cross-sectional designs study development by comparing different age groups at a single point in time, for example testing groups of twenty, forty, and sixty year-olds simultaneously and inferring age changes from the differences between them. The major weakness of this approach is the cohort effect, also called the generation effect. The different age groups were born in different eras and grew up under different historical, social, educational, technological, and nutritional conditions, so they are different cohorts. Any difference found between the age groups may therefore reflect not the effect of ageing or development at all, but these generational differences in experience. For instance, if older adults score lower on a cognitive test, it may be because they had less formal education rather than because cognition declines with age. This confounding of age with cohort is the inherent and characteristic limitation of cross-sectional designs. Longitudinal designs, which follow the same people over time, avoid cohort confounds but suffer instead from attrition and time-of-measurement effects. The exam cue, the special drawback of cross-sectional studies, is cohort difference, making option c correct." },
  { id: "ihbas24_082", section: "general",
    q: "Rahul thought that he could do much better in the English exam as it was held in the  same  classroom where his  classes were being held whereas  in geography exam he was very nervous as this was conducted in a totally new environment. This perhaps was due to :",
    options: { a: "Examination fear", b: "The principle of congruity", c: "His anxious personality", d: "The principle of encoding specificity" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Examination fear is a lay explanation of test anxiety; it does not explain why the matching room specifically improved recall, which is a memory-cue effect.", b: "The principle of congruity is an attitude-change consistency principle from Osgood and Tannenbaum, unrelated to context-dependent memory retrieval.", c: "His anxious personality is a dispositional explanation that ignores the key fact, the matching of study and test environment, which encoding specificity explains." },
    explanation: "Rahul's better performance in the familiar classroom reflects the principle of encoding specificity, option d. The encoding specificity principle, formulated by Endel Tulving, states that memory retrieval is most effective when the conditions present at retrieval match the conditions that were present at encoding. The cues that were encoded along with the to-be-remembered material, including the surrounding context, become part of the memory trace, so reinstating those cues at the time of recall aids retrieval. Context-dependent memory is a specific case in which the physical environment serves as a retrieval cue: information learned in a particular setting is recalled better in that same setting. Rahul studied and attended classes in the same room where the English exam was held, so the environmental cues at retrieval matched those at encoding, supporting better recall, whereas the geography exam in a wholly new environment lacked those matching contextual cues, impairing retrieval. The classic demonstration is Godden and Baddeley's study in which divers recalled word lists best when tested in the same environment, underwater or on land, where they had learned them. The matching of study and test context is precisely encoding specificity, so option d is correct, not anxiety or a vague congruity principle." },
  { id: "ihbas24_083", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Prosopagnosia results in an inability to :",
    options: { a: "Recognize faces", b: "Distinguish tastes", c: "Express feelings through facial expressions", d: "Recognize objects" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "This option, inability to recognise faces, is in fact the true definition of prosopagnosia, even though the source key marks a different option; Sihan should treat face-recognition impairment as the correct concept.", c: "Inability to express feelings through facial expressions describes a motor or affective expression problem, not the perceptual face-recognition deficit of prosopagnosia.", d: "Inability to recognise objects is visual object agnosia, a related but distinct disorder; prosopagnosia is specific to faces." },
    explanation: "Prosopagnosia results in an inability to recognise faces; the textbook-correct answer is therefore face recognition, although this source keys the answer as option b. Prosopagnosia, from the Greek for face and lack of recognition, also called face blindness, is a neuropsychological disorder in which a person cannot recognise familiar faces, sometimes including their own, despite intact vision and intact ability to recognise the same people by other cues such as voice, gait, or distinctive clothing. It results from damage to or dysfunction of the fusiform face area in the fusiform gyrus of the temporal lobe, a region specialised for face processing, and can be acquired through stroke or injury or be present from birth as developmental prosopagnosia. The condition is a striking example of category-specific recognition deficits and supports the idea that face perception is a distinct, modular process separate from general object recognition. On the strict reading of the term, the correct description is the inability to recognise faces, option a. The source answer key marks option b, distinguish tastes, but that describes a gustatory deficit, ageusia, not prosopagnosia; Sihan should learn the correct concept, prosopagnosia equals impaired face recognition, while noting the source's marked answer." },
  { id: "ihbas24_084", section: "biostats",
    q: "Which formula is correct for figuring out degrees of freedom in a chi-square test for independence?",
    options: { a: "(N – 1)", b: "(NColumns – 2) (NRows – 2)", c: "(NColumns – 1) (NRows – 1)", d: "(N – 2)" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "N minus one is the degrees of freedom for a one-sample variance or a simple goodness-of-fit count situation, not for a chi-square test of independence in a contingency table.", b: "Subtracting two from each dimension over-corrects; the correct reduction is by one in each direction, not by two.", d: "N minus two is not the degrees-of-freedom formula for the chi-square test of independence; the formula depends on rows and columns, not the total N." },
    explanation: "The degrees of freedom for a chi-square test of independence are calculated as the number of columns minus one times the number of rows minus one, option c. The chi-square test of independence assesses whether two categorical variables are associated, by comparing the observed cell frequencies in a contingency table with the frequencies expected if the variables were independent. The degrees of freedom for this test depend on the dimensions of the contingency table, not on the total sample size. The formula is df equals open parenthesis number of rows minus one close parenthesis times open parenthesis number of columns minus one close parenthesis. The logic is that once the row and column marginal totals are fixed, only that many cells are free to vary; the rest are determined by the constraint that the cells must sum to the marginals. For a two-by-two table, for example, df equals one minus zero, that is one times one, which equals one. Degrees of freedom determine which chi-square distribution the test statistic is compared against to obtain the p-value. The correct formula is therefore the product of the reduced row and column counts, option c, not formulas based on the total N." },
  { id: "ihbas24_085", section: "assessment",
    q: "Name of the Indian tool to assess the severity and disability of children with autism :",
    options: { a: "CGAS", b: "Childhood Autism Rating Scale", c: "CBCL", d: "ISAA" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "CGAS, the Children's Global Assessment Scale, rates overall functioning in children with any psychiatric condition, not a specific Indian autism severity tool.", c: "CBCL, the Child Behavior Checklist by Achenbach, is a broad-band emotional and behavioural problem screener, not an autism-specific Indian disability scale.", d: "ISAA, the Indian Scale for Assessment of Autism, is in fact the indigenous Indian instrument for grading autism severity and disability, the textbook-correct answer despite the source key." },
    explanation: "This item asks for the Indian tool to assess the severity and disability of children with autism. The source keys the answer as option b, the Childhood Autism Rating Scale, but the genuinely Indian-developed instrument is the Indian Scale for Assessment of Autism, the ISAA, option d, so this is an arguable key. The ISAA was developed in India by the National Institute for the Mentally Handicapped under the Ministry of Social Justice and Empowerment specifically to assess and certify the severity and degree of disability of persons with autism for benefits and legal recognition in India; it grades autism as normal, mild, moderate, or severe based on a percentage score and is the officially adopted Indian instrument. The Childhood Autism Rating Scale, CARS, by Schopler and colleagues, is an American-developed and widely used rating scale for identifying autism and rating its severity across fifteen behavioural domains. Because the question specifies an Indian tool to assess severity and disability, the textbook-correct answer is the ISAA, the indigenous, government-adopted disability-certification scale, even though the source marks CARS. Sihan should learn that the ISAA is the Indian severity-and-disability instrument for autism while noting the source's marked choice." },
  { id: "ihbas24_086", section: "biostats",
    q: "Which research design is best suited for examining changes over time in behavior or psychological traits?",
    options: { a: "Cross-sectional design", b: "Longitudinal design", c: "Case study", d: "Experimental design" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "A cross-sectional design compares different groups at one point in time and infers change indirectly; it cannot directly observe change over time within individuals.", c: "A case study deeply examines a single individual or unit; it does not systematically track change across a sample over time.", d: "An experimental design tests causal effects of a manipulation; it is not specifically the design for tracking developmental change over time." },
    explanation: "The longitudinal design, option b, is best suited for examining changes over time in behaviour or psychological traits. A longitudinal study follows the same group of individuals, the same cohort, over an extended period, measuring them repeatedly at different ages or time points. Because it tracks the same people, it can directly observe how individuals actually change, develop, or remain stable across time, capturing genuine intra-individual change and developmental trajectories that a single snapshot cannot reveal. This makes it the gold standard for studying development, the stability or change of traits, and the long-term effects of early factors on later outcomes. Its great advantage is avoiding the cohort confound that plagues cross-sectional designs, since the same generation is followed throughout. Its disadvantages are that it is time-consuming and expensive, vulnerable to participant attrition as people drop out, and subject to practice effects from repeated testing and to time-of-measurement effects from historical events during the study. The contrast to hold is that cross-sectional designs compare different ages at one time and are quicker but cannot show individual change, while longitudinal designs follow the same people and reveal change directly. The cue, changes over time, makes option b correct." },
  { id: "ihbas24_087", section: "biostats",
    q: "What is the main purpose of using meta-analysis in clinical psychology?",
    options: { a: "To summarize findings from multiple studies", b: "To conduct a single large experiment", c: "To analyze individual case studies", d: "To collect primary data" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "This option, summarising findings from multiple studies, is in fact the correct purpose of meta-analysis, even though the source key marks a different option.", c: "Analysing individual case studies is qualitative single-case work, the opposite of pooling many quantitative studies, so it does not describe meta-analysis.", d: "Collecting primary data is what an original study does; meta-analysis works with secondary data from already-conducted studies, not new data collection." },
    explanation: "The main purpose of meta-analysis is to summarise findings from multiple studies; the textbook-correct answer is therefore option a, though this source has keyed option b. Meta-analysis is a quantitative research technique that statistically combines the results of many separate studies addressing the same question to arrive at an overall, pooled estimate of an effect. Rather than collecting new primary data, the meta-analyst gathers existing studies, extracts a common index such as an effect size from each, and then averages these, weighting larger and more precise studies more heavily, to produce a single more powerful and more generalisable conclusion than any individual study could provide. It also lets researchers examine the consistency of effects across studies, investigate sources of variation through moderator analysis, and detect publication bias. Meta-analysis sits at the top of the evidence hierarchy in clinical psychology and medicine because it synthesises the entire body of evidence on a treatment or hypothesis. The correct description of its purpose is to summarise findings from multiple studies, option a. The source key marks option b, conducting a single large experiment, but that is precisely what meta-analysis is not; it analyses already-completed studies rather than running one big new experiment. Sihan should learn the true purpose while noting the source's marked answer." },
  { id: "ihbas24_088", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Which term describes the experience characterized by a profound sense of disconnection or estrangement from oneself, often leading to feelings of unreality regarding one’s thoughts, feelings, or sense of identity?",
    options: { a: "Derealization", b: "Depersonalization", c: "Dissociative amnesia", d: "Fugue state" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Derealization is detachment from one's surroundings, where the external world feels unreal, not the self-focused estrangement described here.", c: "Dissociative amnesia is the inability to recall important personal information, a memory deficit, not the felt unreality of the self.", d: "A fugue state involves sudden travel away from home with amnesia for one's identity; it is a memory-and-behaviour phenomenon, not the ongoing self-detachment of depersonalization." },
    explanation: "The experience of a profound sense of disconnection or estrangement from oneself, with feelings of unreality about one's own thoughts, feelings, or identity, is depersonalization, option b. Depersonalization is a dissociative experience in which a person feels detached from, or an outside observer of, their own mind, body, or self. They may feel that they are not real, that their actions are mechanical or robotic, that their thoughts and feelings belong to someone else, or that they are watching themselves from outside, as if in a dream or behind glass, while reality testing remains intact, the person knows the experience is subjective. It must be distinguished from derealization, which is the closely related experience of detachment from one's surroundings, where the external world seems unreal, dreamlike, foggy, or distorted. The two often co-occur and together constitute depersonalization-derealization disorder. The key distinction is that depersonalization is detachment from the self, whereas derealization is detachment from the environment. The question specifies estrangement from oneself and one's own thoughts, feelings, and identity, which is precisely depersonalization, making option b correct, distinguishing it from the world-focused derealization and from the memory-loss conditions." },
  { id: "ihbas24_089", section: "general",
    q: "According to the Transactional Model of Stress, which term refers to the individual's assessment of whether they have the resources to cope with a stressor?",
    options: { a: "Initial threat evaluation", b: "Secondary appraisal", c: "Coping resource assessment", d: "Situational capacity evaluation" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Initial threat evaluation describes primary appraisal, the judgement of whether the event is a threat, harm, or challenge, not the resource assessment.", c: "Coping resource assessment is a paraphrase but not the model's technical term; the named construct for evaluating one's resources is secondary appraisal.", d: "Situational capacity evaluation is not a term used in the Lazarus and Folkman model; the correct label is secondary appraisal." },
    explanation: "In the Transactional Model of Stress, the assessment of whether one has the resources to cope with a stressor is secondary appraisal, option b. The Transactional Model, developed by Richard Lazarus and Susan Folkman, holds that stress is not a property of the stimulus alone but arises from the transaction between the person and the environment, mediated by how the person appraises the situation. The model distinguishes two appraisal processes. Primary appraisal is the initial evaluation of whether an event is relevant and, if so, whether it is benign, a threat, a harm or loss already incurred, or a challenge, an opportunity for gain; it answers the question is this a problem for me. Secondary appraisal is the subsequent evaluation of one's coping resources and options, asking what can I do about it and whether one has the abilities, support, and means to manage the demand. Stress is experienced when the demands appraised as threatening exceed the resources appraised as available. These appraisals then guide coping, which Lazarus and Folkman divided into problem-focused and emotion-focused strategies. The question specifies the assessment of coping resources, which is secondary appraisal, making option b correct over the threat-detection of primary appraisal." },
  { id: "ihbas24_090", section: "biostats",
    q: "What is the p-value in statistical testing?",
    options: { a: "The probability of the null hypothesis being true", b: "The probability of obtaining the observed data if the null hypothesis is true", c: "The effect size of the experiment", d: "The power of the study" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "The probability of the null hypothesis being true is a common misinterpretation; the p-value is the probability of the data given the null, not the probability of the null itself.", c: "The effect size measures the magnitude of an effect and is independent of the p-value, which concerns statistical significance, not size.", d: "The power of the study is the probability of correctly rejecting a false null, a different concept from the p-value." },
    explanation: "The p-value is the probability of obtaining the observed data, or data more extreme, if the null hypothesis is true, option b. In null hypothesis significance testing, the researcher begins by assuming the null hypothesis, typically that there is no effect or no difference. The p-value then quantifies how surprising the obtained results would be under that assumption: it is the conditional probability of getting a test statistic at least as extreme as the one observed, given that the null hypothesis is correct. A small p-value, conventionally below the alpha level of 0.05, means the observed data would be very unlikely if the null were true, so the result is deemed statistically significant and the null hypothesis is rejected. A crucial conceptual point, frequently tested, is what the p-value is not. It is not the probability that the null hypothesis is true, nor the probability that the results occurred by chance, nor one minus the probability that the alternative is true. The p-value is a statement about the data given the hypothesis, not about the hypothesis given the data. It also does not indicate effect size or practical importance. The precise definition, the probability of the data given the null, makes option b correct." },
  { id: "ihbas24_091", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Which neurotransmitter is primarily involved in regulating mood, appetite, and sleep?",
    options: { a: "Dopamine", b: "Serotonin", c: "GABA", d: "Glutamate" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Dopamine is the transmitter of reward, motivation, and movement, not the primary regulator of mood, appetite, and sleep together.", c: "GABA is the brain's main inhibitory neurotransmitter, reducing neural excitability and involved in anxiety reduction, not the mood-appetite-sleep cluster.", d: "Glutamate is the main excitatory neurotransmitter, important for learning and memory, not the regulation of mood, appetite, and sleep." },
    explanation: "Serotonin, option b, is the neurotransmitter primarily involved in regulating mood, appetite, and sleep. Serotonin, chemically 5-hydroxytryptamine or 5-HT, is a monoamine neurotransmitter synthesised from the amino acid tryptophan, with cell bodies concentrated in the raphe nuclei of the brainstem that project widely throughout the brain. It modulates a broad range of functions: mood and emotional stability, appetite and satiety, sleep and the sleep-wake cycle, arousal, impulse control, and aggression. Serotonin is central to the monoamine hypothesis of depression, which holds that reduced serotonergic activity contributes to depressed mood, and this is the rationale for the most widely prescribed antidepressants, the selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors, or SSRIs, which increase serotonin availability in the synapse by blocking its reuptake. Serotonin dysregulation is also implicated in anxiety, obsessive-compulsive disorder, and eating disorders. Beyond the brain, most of the body's serotonin is found in the gut, where it regulates intestinal motility. The cluster mood plus appetite plus sleep is the classic serotonin signature, making option b correct over dopamine, which is the reward transmitter, and GABA and glutamate, which are the main inhibitory and excitatory transmitters." },
  { id: "ihbas24_092", section: "general",
    q: "Which psychological theory emphasises the role of internal mental processes in understanding behaviour?",
    options: { a: "Behaviourism", b: "Cognitive psychology", c: "Psychoanalysis", d: "Humanistic psychology" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Behaviourism explicitly rejects internal mental processes, focusing only on observable stimulus-response relationships, the opposite of the cognitive emphasis.", c: "Psychoanalysis emphasises unconscious drives and conflicts, not the conscious information-processing mechanisms that cognitive psychology studies.", d: "Humanistic psychology stresses free will, self-actualization, and subjective experience, not the systematic study of internal cognitive processes." },
    explanation: "Cognitive psychology, option b, is the theory that emphasises the role of internal mental processes in understanding behaviour. Cognitive psychology emerged in the mid-twentieth century as part of the cognitive revolution, a reaction against behaviourism's insistence on studying only observable stimuli and responses while treating the mind as an unobservable black box. Cognitive psychologists argued that to explain behaviour one must study the internal mental processes that intervene between stimulus and response, including perception, attention, memory, language, problem-solving, reasoning, and decision-making. A dominant framework is the information-processing model, which likens the mind to a computer that encodes, stores, processes, and retrieves information. Pioneers include Ulric Neisser, who wrote the first textbook titled Cognitive Psychology, George Miller, known for work on the capacity of short-term memory, and Noam Chomsky, whose critique of Skinner's account of language helped trigger the revolution. The cognitive approach now underpins much of clinical psychology, especially cognitive and cognitive-behavioural therapies, which target maladaptive thoughts and beliefs. The defining emphasis on internal mental processes points squarely to cognitive psychology, option b, distinguishing it from behaviourism's external focus, psychoanalysis's unconscious focus, and humanism's growth focus." },
  { id: "ihbas24_093", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Which disorder is characterised by the inability to remember important personal information, often following trauma?",
    options: { a: "Schizophrenia", b: "Dissociative amnesia", c: "Bipolar disorder", d: "PTSD" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Schizophrenia is a psychotic disorder of thought and perception, not a disorder of trauma-related memory loss.", c: "Bipolar disorder is a mood disorder of alternating mania and depression, unrelated to the inability to recall personal information.", d: "PTSD involves re-experiencing a trauma through intrusive memories and flashbacks; although it can include some amnesia for the event, the disorder defined by the inability to recall personal information is dissociative amnesia." },
    explanation: "The disorder characterised by an inability to remember important personal information, often following trauma, is dissociative amnesia, option b. Dissociative amnesia is a dissociative disorder in which a person is unable to recall important autobiographical information, usually of a traumatic or stressful nature, that is too extensive to be explained by ordinary forgetfulness. The memory loss is psychogenic, arising from psychological causes rather than from brain injury, substance use, or a medical condition, and it is understood as a dissociative defense in which overwhelming or unbearable memories are blocked from conscious awareness. The amnesia is usually for a specific period or event, localised or selective, though it can be generalised, covering one's whole life and identity. A particularly dramatic form is dissociative fugue, in which the person not only forgets their identity but unexpectedly travels away from home and may assume a new identity. The forgotten information is not lost but inaccessible, and it may return suddenly. This must be distinguished from organic amnesias and from PTSD, where the trauma is typically re-experienced rather than forgotten. The cue, trauma-related inability to recall personal information, makes option b correct." },
  { id: "ihbas24_094", section: "social",
    q: "In social psychology, what is the difference between conformity and compliance?",
    options: { a: "Conformity involves a change in behaviour due to social pressure, while compliance involves following direct requests", b: "Compliance involves peer pressure, while conformity does not", c: "Both terms refer to obedience", d: "Conformity is always involuntary, while compliance is voluntary" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "This is incorrect because conformity, not compliance, is the one most associated with diffuse peer pressure; compliance specifically involves direct requests.", c: "Both terms do not refer to obedience; obedience is a separate concept involving following orders from authority, distinct from both conformity and compliance.", d: "Conformity is not always involuntary nor compliance always voluntary; the genuine distinguishing feature is social pressure versus a direct request, not a voluntariness dichotomy." },
    explanation: "The difference is that conformity involves a change in behaviour due to social pressure, while compliance involves following direct requests, option a. These are two distinct forms of social influence. Conformity is the tendency to adjust one's behaviour, attitudes, or beliefs to align with the implicit or explicit norms of a group, in response to real or imagined social pressure, even when no one has directly asked the person to change. It is driven by the desire to fit in, normative influence, or to be correct, informational influence, and is illustrated by Asch's line-judgement studies where people went along with a group's wrong answer. Compliance, by contrast, is a change in behaviour in response to a direct request from another person; the person agrees to do something they have been explicitly asked to do. Compliance is studied through techniques such as foot-in-the-door, door-in-the-face, and low-balling. The key contrast is the source of influence: diffuse group norms and pressure for conformity versus an explicit individual request for compliance. A third form, obedience, is following a direct order from an authority figure. The accurate distinction is captured in option a, social pressure versus direct request." },
  { id: "ihbas24_095", section: "general",
    q: "Which of the following terms refers to difficulty recalling information due to interference from new information?",
    options: { a: "Retroactive interference", b: "Proactive interference", c: "Decay theory", d: "Retrieval failure" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Proactive interference is the reverse, where old information interferes with recalling new information, not new interfering with old.", c: "Decay theory attributes forgetting to the fading of memory traces over time through disuse, not to interference from new material.", d: "Retrieval failure is the inability to access a stored memory because the right cues are absent, not interference from new information." },
    explanation: "Difficulty recalling information due to interference from newer information is retroactive interference, option a. Interference theory explains forgetting as the result of competition between memories rather than the simple fading of a trace. There are two directions of interference. Retroactive interference occurs when newly learned information disrupts the recall of previously learned information; the new material reaches backward to impair the old. For example, learning a new phone number makes it harder to remember your old one. The question specifies interference from new information impairing recall, which is precisely retroactive interference. The opposite is proactive interference, in which previously learned information disrupts the learning or recall of new information; the old material reaches forward to impair the new, such as when an old password keeps coming to mind instead of the new one. To keep them straight, retro means backward, the newer affecting the older, and pro means forward, the older affecting the newer. Both contrast with decay theory, which attributes forgetting to the fading of unused memory traces over time, and with retrieval failure, the inability to access an available memory due to missing cues. The cue, new information interfering, makes retroactive interference, option a, correct." },
  { id: "ihbas24_096", section: "assessment",
    q: "Minimum age for the assessment of children with specific learning disability as per RPWD Act 2016 is :",
    options: { a: "7 years", b: "14 years", c: "10 years", d: "8 years" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Seven years is below the keyed minimum; at seven a child may still be in the early acquisition phase, making reliable SLD diagnosis premature under these guidelines.", b: "Fourteen years is far too late to set as a minimum for first assessment; identification is meant to occur in earlier schooling, so this is not the minimum age.", c: "Ten years is older than the keyed minimum of eight; the guideline sets eight, not ten, as the minimum assessment age." },
    explanation: "Under the Rights of Persons with Disabilities Act 2016, the minimum age for the assessment of children with specific learning disability is keyed here as eight years, option d. Specific learning disability, SLD, encompasses conditions such as dyslexia, dysgraphia, and dyscalculia that affect a child's ability to read, write, or do mathematics despite adequate intelligence and instruction. A practical and clinically important issue in diagnosis is that very young children are still acquiring foundational academic skills, so a learning disability cannot be reliably distinguished from normal developmental variation until the child has had sufficient formal schooling and exposure to reading, writing, and arithmetic. For this reason guidelines set a minimum age before which a formal SLD assessment and disability certification are not appropriate, ensuring the child has had a genuine opportunity to acquire the skills in question. In the Indian framework associated with the RPWD Act 2016 and its certification guidelines, the source marks this minimum assessment age as eight years, option d, reflecting that by roughly age eight a child has had enough schooling for a persistent failure to acquire academic skills to be meaningfully identified as a learning disability rather than ordinary developmental delay. Sihan should retain eight years as the keyed answer." },
  { id: "ihbas24_097", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Which is NOT a core symptom to diagnose as autism in children as per DSM- 5?",
    options: { a: "Communication skills", b: "Unusual repetitive behavior", c: "Social reciprocity", d: "I.Q. is below 70" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Communication skills deficits are part of the social communication domain, a genuine core symptom of autism, so this is not the exception.", b: "Unusual repetitive behaviour is the restricted, repetitive behaviour domain, a genuine core symptom, so it is not the exception.", c: "Deficits in social reciprocity are part of the social communication and interaction domain, a genuine core symptom, so it is not the exception." },
    explanation: "An IQ below 70 is NOT a core symptom for diagnosing autism in children under DSM-5, option d. In DSM-5, autism spectrum disorder is defined by two core symptom domains, not by intellectual level. The first domain is persistent deficits in social communication and social interaction across multiple contexts, encompassing deficits in social-emotional reciprocity, in nonverbal communicative behaviours, and in developing, maintaining, and understanding relationships. The second domain is restricted, repetitive patterns of behaviour, interests, or activities, such as stereotyped movements, insistence on sameness, highly fixated interests, and sensory hyper- or hyporeactivity. Both domains must be present from early development and cause significant impairment. Crucially, intellectual disability, an IQ below 70, is a separate condition that may or may not co-occur with autism; many individuals with autism have average or above-average intelligence, and DSM-5 explicitly notes that intellectual impairment, when present, should be coded as a separate comorbid specifier rather than being part of the autism criteria themselves. Therefore an IQ below 70 is not one of the defining core features of autism, making option d the correct answer to a question asking which is NOT a core symptom. Communication, repetitive behaviour, and social reciprocity all are core features." },
  { id: "ihbas24_098", section: "therapy",
    q: "One parent conveys two conflicting messages to the child at the same time is called :",
    options: { a: "Faulty communication", b: "Social skills deficits", c: "Double bind communication", d: "Conflict resolution" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Faulty communication is a vague general label, not the specific technical term for two simultaneous contradictory messages.", b: "Social skills deficits refer to a lack of interpersonal abilities, not the contradictory-message pattern of the double bind.", d: "Conflict resolution is the process of settling disputes, the opposite of creating a no-win contradictory bind." },
    explanation: "When one parent conveys two conflicting messages to the child at the same time, this is called double bind communication, option c. The double bind concept was introduced by Gregory Bateson and his colleagues, including Don Jackson, Jay Haley, and John Weakland, in 1956 as a theory about the family communication patterns that might contribute to schizophrenia. A double bind is a situation in which a person receives two contradictory messages on different levels, typically a verbal message that conflicts with a nonverbal or tonal one, so that whatever response the person makes is wrong. A classic example is a mother who tells her child she loves him while her body language and tone convey rejection, so the child cannot win: responding to the words is contradicted by the manner, and responding to the manner contradicts the words. Crucially the person is in a relationship they cannot leave and is forbidden from commenting on the contradiction, which traps them in an impossible bind. Bateson hypothesised that chronic exposure to such no-win communication could foster the disturbed, disorganised thinking seen in schizophrenia. Although the schizophrenogenic-family theory is now discredited as a cause, the double bind remains an important concept in family systems theory and communication, making option c correct." },
  { id: "ihbas24_099", section: "therapy",
    q: "The process in which therapist expressed his negative feelings towards patients is termed as :",
    options: { a: "Negative Transference", b: "Counter transference", c: "Positive transference", d: "Negative counter transference" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Negative transference is the patient's, not the therapist's, hostile feelings directed at the therapist, so it concerns the wrong party.", b: "Counter transference is on the right side, the therapist's reaction, but it is the general term; the question specifies negative feelings, so the precise answer is negative countertransference.", c: "Positive transference is the patient's warm, affectionate feelings toward the therapist, both the wrong party and the wrong valence." },
    explanation: "The process in which the therapist expresses negative feelings toward the patient is termed negative countertransference, option d. In psychoanalytic and psychodynamic therapy, transference is the patient's unconscious redirection onto the therapist of feelings, attitudes, and expectations originally associated with significant figures from the patient's past, such as parents. Countertransference is the mirror process: the therapist's own emotional reactions to the patient, which may arise from the therapist's own unresolved conflicts being activated, or as a response to the patient's transference and behaviour. Countertransference can be positive, involving warm, affectionate, or overly protective feelings, or negative, involving hostile, irritated, rejecting, or critical feelings toward the patient. When the therapist experiences and expresses negative feelings toward the patient, this is specifically negative countertransference, option d. The careful distinction here is that transference belongs to the patient and countertransference belongs to the therapist, so since the question concerns the therapist's negative feelings, the answer must be a form of countertransference, and because the feelings are negative, it is negative countertransference. Freud initially viewed countertransference as an obstacle, but later analysts came to see it as a valuable source of information about the patient, if recognised and managed." },
  { id: "ihbas24_100", section: "therapy",
    q: "Exposure and response prevention procedure is based on behaviour therapy principal of :",
    options: { a: "Counter conditioning", b: "Habituation and extinction", c: "Reciprocal inhibition", d: "Graded exposure" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Counter conditioning replaces an unwanted response with an incompatible one, as in pairing relaxation with feared stimuli; ERP relies instead on letting anxiety habituate, not on substituting a competing response.", c: "Reciprocal inhibition, Wolpe's principle underlying systematic desensitisation, uses an incompatible response like relaxation to inhibit anxiety; ERP does not pair relaxation but allows habituation and extinction.", d: "Graded exposure describes the hierarchical pacing of exposure, a technique, not the underlying learning principle, which is habituation and extinction." },
    explanation: "Exposure and response prevention is based on the behaviour therapy principle of habituation and extinction, option b. Exposure and response prevention, ERP, is the gold-standard behavioural treatment for obsessive-compulsive disorder, developed by Victor Meyer and elaborated by Edna Foa. In OCD, obsessions trigger intense anxiety, and the person performs compulsions, the rituals, to neutralise that anxiety; because the ritual brings temporary relief, it is negatively reinforced and the cycle is maintained. ERP works by deliberately exposing the patient to the anxiety-provoking obsessional stimulus or situation, the exposure, while preventing them from carrying out the usual compulsive response, the response prevention. Two learning processes then operate. Habituation is the natural decline in the anxiety response when the person stays in contact with the feared stimulus without escaping; the anxiety, which the patient feared would rise forever, instead peaks and falls on its own. Extinction occurs because, without the ritual, the patient learns that the feared catastrophe does not happen and that the anxiety subsides without the compulsion, so the learned association between the obsession and the ritual is weakened and the compulsion fades. The mechanism is therefore habituation and extinction, making option b correct over the counterconditioning basis of systematic desensitisation." },
],
  "amity-2023": [
  { id: "amity23_001", section: "general",
    q: "Who believed that the universe of the collective unconscious could be accounted for by evolutionary theory, through the similarity of brain structure evident in all human races?",
    options: { a: "Freud", b: "Jung", c: "Horney", d: "None of these" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Freud's unconscious is the PERSONAL unconscious, built from one's own repressed experiences, not an inherited structure shared across all humans; he never proposed an evolutionarily transmitted collective layer.", c: "Karen Horney was a neo-Freudian who emphasized social and cultural factors and basic anxiety in childhood relationships; she rejected biological universals like this and had no concept of an inherited collective unconscious.", d: "Since Jung is clearly the originator of the collective unconscious tied to inherited brain structure, 'None of these' is incorrect." },
    explanation: "The answer is Jung because the collective unconscious is his signature contribution, and he explicitly grounded it in a kind of evolutionary, biological inheritance shared across all of humanity. To understand this, you have to separate Jung from Freud. Freud's unconscious is personal: it is built from one individual's repressed wishes, memories, and conflicts, mostly sexual and aggressive material pushed out of awareness during that person's own life. Carl Jung, after breaking from Freud, argued there is a deeper layer beneath the personal unconscious that we do not acquire through individual experience at all. He called it the collective unconscious, and he proposed it is inherited, the same in every person, much like the body is inherited. Its contents are the archetypes: universal, pre-existing forms or predispositions (the Mother, the Shadow, the Anima/Animus, the Self) that shape how all humans perceive and respond to the world. Jung's reasoning was essentially evolutionary and anatomical: just as every human brain has broadly the same structure because we share an evolutionary lineage, the mind built on that brain carries the same primordial images and tendencies. The recurrence of similar myths, symbols, and motifs across cultures that never contacted each other was, for Jung, evidence of this shared psychic inheritance. So the phrasing in the question, similarity of brain structure across all human races accounting for shared unconscious content, is pure Jung. Knowing this distinction cold (personal versus collective, acquired versus inherited) lets you place him instantly." },
  { id: "amity23_002", section: "dev",
    q: "Which of these is NOT Piaget's stage of cognitive development?",
    options: { a: "Formal Operational", b: "Hypothetico-deductive reasoning", c: "Concrete operational", d: "Preoperational" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Formal Operational is the fourth genuine Piagetian stage (abstract and hypothetical reasoning), so it cannot be the odd one out.", c: "Concrete operational is the third genuine stage, defined by logical operations applied to concrete objects, so it belongs.", d: "Preoperational is the second genuine stage, marked by symbolic thought and egocentrism, so it belongs." },
    explanation: "The answer is Hypothetico-deductive reasoning because it is not a stage at all; it is a type of thinking that emerges DURING a stage. Jean Piaget proposed four invariant, sequential stages of cognitive development, each defined by a qualitatively different way of constructing knowledge through schemas, assimilation, and accommodation. The first is the sensorimotor stage (birth to about 2 years), where the infant knows the world through senses and motor action and gradually acquires object permanence. Second is the preoperational stage (about 2 to 7 years), marked by symbolic thought and language but limited by egocentrism, centration, and the inability to conserve. Third is the concrete operational stage (about 7 to 11 years), when the child masters logical operations like conservation, reversibility, and classification, but only about concrete, tangible objects. Fourth is the formal operational stage (about 11 years onward), when the adolescent can reason abstractly, think about hypothetical possibilities, and reason systematically. Hypothetico-deductive reasoning is precisely the hallmark COGNITIVE ABILITY of that final formal operational stage: the capacity to generate hypotheses about a problem and systematically test them, like a scientist, rather than guessing randomly. So the trap here is that hypothetico-deductive reasoning is a real Piagetian concept, but it names a process within formal operations, not one of the four stages themselves. Recognizing that the four stage names are sensorimotor, preoperational, concrete operational, and formal operational, and that everything else is a sub-feature, is what lets you answer cleanly." },
  { id: "amity23_003", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Which brain structure would most likely result in death if damaged?",
    options: { a: "Cerebellum", b: "Medulla", c: "Reticular formation", d: "Pons" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "The cerebellum controls balance, posture, and coordinated movement; its damage produces ataxia and motor incoordination, which is disabling but not directly lethal.", c: "The reticular formation governs arousal and wakefulness; severe damage can cause coma or loss of consciousness, but it does not directly stop heartbeat and breathing the way the medulla does.", d: "The pons relays information between cerebrum and cerebellum and assists sleep and breathing rhythm, but the core vital cardiac and respiratory centers are in the medulla, not the pons." },
    explanation: "The answer is the medulla because it houses the vital reflex centers that keep us alive automatically, and destroying it stops those functions abruptly. The medulla oblongata sits at the very bottom of the brainstem, where the brain joins the spinal cord. To learn this properly, organize the brainstem from bottom to top: medulla, then pons, then midbrain. The medulla contains the cardiac center (regulating heart rate), the respiratory center (driving breathing), and centers for vasomotor control (blood pressure), as well as reflexes like swallowing, coughing, sneezing, and vomiting. Because breathing and heartbeat are fundamentally controlled here and these cannot be consciously sustained if the control center is gone, damage to the medulla typically causes immediate respiratory and cardiac arrest, hence death. This is why brainstem strokes or trauma at this level are so often fatal, and why brainstem function is central to clinical definitions of brain death. Contrast this with structures that, while important, are not immediately life-ending if damaged: the cerebellum governs balance, coordination, and motor learning; damage causes ataxia and clumsiness, not death. The pons relays signals and helps regulate sleep and breathing rhythm, but the actual life-critical respiratory drive resides in the medulla. The reticular formation regulates arousal and consciousness; severe damage can cause coma, but it is the medulla's autonomic centers that most directly equal survival. Anchor the rule: medulla equals automatic life support, so its destruction equals death." },
  { id: "amity23_004", section: "therapy",
    q: "A parent deliberately ignores a child's temper tantrums in an attempt to discourage them. The parent's strategy is in accordance with:",
    options: { a: "Negative reinforcement", b: "Extinction", c: "Operant escape", d: "Operant avoidance" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Negative reinforcement INCREASES a behavior by removing an aversive stimulus; here the parent is trying to decrease the tantrum, not strengthen it, so this is the wrong process.", b: "Extinction is actually the textbook-correct description of ignoring a previously reinforced behavior to weaken it; the source unusually keys (c), but conceptually (b) fits best.", d: "Operant avoidance is a negative-reinforcement subtype where a response prevents an aversive event from occurring; it increases behavior and does not describe a parent passively withholding attention." },
    explanation: "The source key marks this as operant escape, but the textbook-correct concept the question describes is extinction, so learn the concept properly and note the discrepancy. The scenario is a parent deliberately withholding any response (attention) to a behavior (tantrums) that was previously reinforced by that attention, in order to reduce the behavior. In operant conditioning, when a behavior that has been maintained by reinforcement no longer produces that reinforcement, the behavior gradually weakens and disappears. That process is extinction. Crucially, ignoring an attention-seeking behavior is the textbook clinical example of extinction: the tantrum was being reinforced by parental attention, so removing the attention removes the reinforcer and the tantrum should fade (often after a temporary extinction burst). So conceptually the right answer is extinction. The source has marked operant escape (c), which describes something different: escape behavior is negatively reinforced behavior that terminates an aversive stimulus already present (for example, taking aspirin to end a headache). That does not match a parent passively ignoring a child. Because this question's wording maps cleanly onto extinction, you should learn it as an extinction item; if the answer key on the exam credits escape, it is an error in the key. For your understanding, distinguish the four: positive reinforcement adds a pleasant stimulus, negative reinforcement removes an aversive one, both increase behavior; extinction removes the maintaining reinforcer to decrease behavior; escape and avoidance are negative-reinforcement subtypes that increase behavior." },
  { id: "amity23_005", section: "therapy",
    q: "A split between the organismic valuing process and a self-concept burdened by conditions of worth which results in feelings of tension and confusion:",
    options: { a: "Conflict", b: "Incongruence", c: "Introjection", d: "Actualizing tendency" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Conflict is a generic term for opposing motives and is not Rogers's specific construct for the self-concept versus organismic experience mismatch.", c: "Introjection is the absorbing of others' values and standards as if they were one's own; it is a CAUSE that helps create conditions of worth, not the name of the resulting state of tension.", d: "The actualizing tendency is the innate drive toward growth and fulfillment; it is the positive motivating force in Rogers's theory, not a state of tension and confusion." },
    explanation: "The answer is incongruence because that is Carl Rogers's precise term for a gap between a person's genuine experience and their self-concept, producing tension. To understand this, you need Rogers's person-centered theory. At its core is the organismic valuing process: an innate, trustworthy inner compass that tells the person what experiences are enhancing and growth-promoting versus harmful. Alongside it develops the self-concept, the organized picture a person holds of who they are. The problem arises through conditions of worth: when caregivers offer love and acceptance only when the child behaves in approved ways, the child learns that they are valued conditionally. To keep that positive regard, the child begins to disown experiences that conflict with what others approve, distorting or denying parts of the organismic experience and reshaping the self-concept around external conditions rather than authentic feeling. When the self-concept (built on conditions of worth) no longer matches the actual organismic experience, the result is incongruence: a state of internal mismatch experienced as anxiety, tension, threat, and confusion. The therapeutic answer in Rogers's view is unconditional positive regard, empathy, and congruence from the therapist, which lets the client reintegrate disowned experience and move toward congruence and the fully functioning person. So the exact split the question describes, organismic valuing process versus a self-concept loaded with conditions of worth, defines incongruence by name. Lock in the chain: conditions of worth lead to a distorted self-concept, which clashes with the organismic valuing process, which equals incongruence." },
  { id: "amity23_006", section: "biopsych",
    q: "The ______ lobe processes auditory information.",
    options: { a: "Occipital", b: "Parietal", c: "Temporal", d: "Frontal" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "The occipital lobe processes VISION, not hearing; it houses the primary visual cortex at the back of the brain.", b: "The parietal lobe processes somatosensory information such as touch, temperature, and spatial location, not auditory input.", d: "The frontal lobe handles motor control, planning, and executive function (plus speech production in Broca's area), not the reception of auditory information." },
    explanation: "The answer is the temporal lobe because it contains the primary auditory cortex, the cortical destination for sound. The cerebral cortex is divided into four lobes, and a reliable way to master them is to pair each with its primary sensory or motor function. The occipital lobe, at the back of the head, is dedicated to vision and houses the primary visual cortex. The parietal lobe, behind the central sulcus, processes somatosensory information (touch, pressure, temperature, pain) and spatial awareness, with the primary somatosensory cortex along its postcentral gyrus. The frontal lobe handles voluntary movement (primary motor cortex on the precentral gyrus), executive functions, planning, and (in the left hemisphere) Broca's area for speech production. The temporal lobe, sitting on the side of the brain roughly behind the temple and above the ear, is the home of the primary auditory cortex, located on the superior temporal gyrus (Heschl's gyrus), which receives and processes auditory information relayed up from the cochlea via the auditory pathway. The temporal lobe also contains Wernicke's area for language comprehension and parts of the hippocampus for memory, both of which fit logically with hearing and understanding spoken language. So auditory processing maps to the temporal lobe. A useful mnemonic: temporal is near the temple and the ear, so it handles what you hear. Lock the four-way map (occipital-vision, parietal-touch, frontal-movement, temporal-hearing) and these lobe questions become automatic." },
  { id: "amity23_007", section: "therapy",
    q: "Psychodynamic therapists believe that disorder stems from:",
    options: { a: "Disturbed thought patterns", b: "Emotional trauma experienced in childhood", c: "A failure to live up to one's full potential", d: "Inappropriate learning patterns" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Disturbed thought patterns is the COGNITIVE model's explanation (Beck, Ellis), which blames maladaptive beliefs and cognitive distortions, not the psychodynamic model.", c: "Failure to live up to one's full potential is the HUMANISTIC explanation (Rogers, Maslow), centering on blocked growth and self-actualization, not unconscious childhood conflict.", d: "Inappropriate learning patterns is the BEHAVIORAL explanation, which attributes disorder to faulty conditioning and learned associations, not to repressed early trauma." },
    explanation: "The answer is emotional trauma experienced in childhood because the psychodynamic model locates the root of psychological disorder in unresolved early-life conflicts and experiences buried in the unconscious. The psychodynamic tradition begins with Freud and extends through later analysts. Its central premise is that much of mental life is unconscious, and that adult symptoms are the disguised expression of conflicts that originated in early childhood, especially during the psychosexual stages. When a child experiences trauma, deprivation, or conflict (for example, conflicts among id, ego, and superego, or difficulties navigating stages like the oral, anal, or phallic period), the painful material is repressed, pushed out of awareness. But repression does not erase it; the conflict remains dynamically active in the unconscious and continues to demand expression. Symptoms (anxiety, obsessions, conversion, depression) are understood as compromise formations: the disguised return of this repressed childhood material, kept partly hidden by defense mechanisms. This is why psychodynamic therapy works through techniques like free association, dream interpretation, and analysis of transference, aiming to bring the unconscious childhood conflict into awareness so it can be worked through. Contrast this with the other major models so you do not confuse them: the cognitive model blames disturbed thinking, the humanistic model blames blocked self-actualization, and the behavioral model blames faulty learning. The question is essentially asking you to match each model to its causal claim, and the psychodynamic claim is unresolved childhood emotional conflict and trauma." },
  { id: "amity23_008", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Parts of the autonomic nervous system are:",
    options: { a: "Sympathetic and parasympathetic", b: "Central and peripheral", c: "Voluntary and involuntary", d: "Cerebrospinal and cortical" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Central and peripheral is the highest-level division of the entire nervous system, not the divisions WITHIN the autonomic nervous system.", c: "Voluntary and involuntary distinguishes the somatic from the autonomic system; it is not the two branches of the autonomic system itself.", d: "Cerebrospinal and cortical are not divisions of the autonomic nervous system at all; cerebrospinal refers to fluid or spinal nerves and cortical refers to the cerebral cortex." },
    explanation: "The answer is sympathetic and parasympathetic because these are the two divisions of the autonomic nervous system. To get this right you must hold the full architecture of the nervous system in mind. The nervous system splits first into the central nervous system (brain and spinal cord) and the peripheral nervous system (all the nerves outside it). The peripheral nervous system then divides into the somatic nervous system, which carries sensory information in and controls voluntary skeletal muscle movement, and the autonomic nervous system, which controls involuntary functions of internal organs, glands, and smooth muscle (heart rate, digestion, pupil size, sweating). The autonomic nervous system in turn has two branches that generally oppose and balance each other. The sympathetic division mobilizes the body for fight-or-flight: it increases heart rate, dilates pupils, redirects blood to muscles, and releases stored energy during stress or emergency. The parasympathetic division promotes rest-and-digest: it conserves energy, slows the heart, and supports digestion and recovery once the threat passes. So the autonomic nervous system equals sympathetic plus parasympathetic. The trap options describe other valid nervous-system distinctions but at the wrong level: central versus peripheral is the top-level split of the whole nervous system, and voluntary versus involuntary describes the somatic versus autonomic distinction, not the parts within the autonomic system itself. Keep the hierarchy clean and the two divisions, sympathetic and parasympathetic, follow immediately." },
  { id: "amity23_009", section: "biopsych",
    q: "With identical twins, heredity is exactly the:",
    options: { a: "Opposite", b: "Contradictory", c: "Same", d: "None of these" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Opposite is incorrect because identical twins share the same genome; their heredity is matched, not reversed.", b: "Contradictory wrongly implies their genetic makeup conflicts, when in fact monozygotic twins originate from one zygote and carry the same DNA.", d: "Since the heredity of identical twins is plainly the same, 'None of these' is incorrect." },
    explanation: "The answer is 'same' because identical (monozygotic) twins develop from a single fertilized egg that splits, so they carry essentially the same genetic material. Understanding twinning is the key. There are two types. Monozygotic (identical) twins arise when one sperm fertilizes one egg to form a single zygote, which then divides into two embryos. Because both embryos come from that same original zygote, they share the same genome, the same DNA sequence, and are therefore the same sex and look strikingly alike. Dizygotic (fraternal) twins arise when two separate eggs are fertilized by two separate sperm in the same pregnancy; they are genetically no more alike than ordinary siblings, sharing on average about half their genes and possibly being different sexes. This distinction is the foundation of the twin-study method in behavior genetics. Researchers compare concordance rates for a trait or disorder between identical and fraternal twins to estimate heritability: because identical twins share 100 percent of their genes while fraternal twins share about 50 percent, a trait that shows much higher concordance in identical twins points to a strong genetic contribution. So when the question states that in identical twins heredity is exactly the same, it is stating the defining biological fact that makes them so scientifically valuable. The other options (opposite, contradictory, none of these) all contradict the basic meaning of identical twinning and the single-zygote origin." },
  { id: "amity23_010", section: "general",
    q: "Functionalism is how people work, play, and adapt to their surroundings. This viewpoint was given by:",
    options: { a: "William James", b: "William Wundt", c: "Charles Darwin", d: "John B. Watson" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "William Wundt founded experimental psychology and structuralism, which analyzed the contents of consciousness through introspection, the opposite emphasis from functionalism.", c: "Charles Darwin's evolutionary theory inspired functionalism's focus on adaptation, but Darwin was a naturalist, not the founder of the functionalist school of psychology.", d: "John B. Watson founded behaviorism, which rejected the study of consciousness entirely and focused on observable behavior, not the adaptive functions of mind." },
    explanation: "The answer is William James because functionalism, the school concerned with the purpose and adaptive function of mental processes, is most strongly associated with him in American psychology. To learn this properly, set functionalism against its rival, structuralism. Structuralism, founded by Wilhelm Wundt and carried to America by Edward Titchener, asked WHAT the mind is made of: it tried to break consciousness into its basic elements (sensations, feelings, images) using introspection, much like a chemist analyzing compounds into elements. Functionalism, led by William James (and developed at the University of Chicago by John Dewey and others), rejected that static dissection and asked instead WHY the mind works as it does, what mental processes are FOR, and how consciousness helps an organism adapt to and survive in its environment. This functional, purpose-focused view was heavily influenced by Darwin's theory of evolution and natural selection, since it framed mind and behavior as adaptive tools. James's 1890 textbook Principles of Psychology, with its idea of the stream of consciousness, embodied this approach. The question's phrasing, how people work, play, and adapt to their surroundings, is the functionalist emphasis on adaptation, which points to James. Beware the distractor Wundt: he is the father of experimental psychology and structuralism, not functionalism. Darwin inspired functionalism but was a naturalist, not a psychologist who founded the school. Watson founded behaviorism, which rejected the study of consciousness altogether." },
  { id: "amity23_011", section: "social",
    q: "In Lewin's topological system, emphasis was placed on the study of 'life space,' which includes:",
    options: { a: "Person and Psychological environment", b: "Society and group", c: "Person and group", d: "Environment and group" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Society and group are macro-level social units, not the two components Lewin specified for the individual's life space, which centers on the person and their perceived environment.", c: "Person and group is incorrect because Lewin's life space pairs the person with the psychological ENVIRONMENT, not with the group.", d: "Environment and group both omit the person, who is the essential first term of the life space in Lewin's B = f(P, E)." },
    explanation: "The answer is Person and Psychological environment because Kurt Lewin defined the life space as exactly those two interacting elements. Lewin was a Gestalt-influenced social psychologist who created field theory, the idea that behavior must be understood as a function of the whole field of forces acting on a person at a given moment, not of isolated traits or stimuli. His famous formula is B = f(P, E): Behavior is a function of the Person and the Environment, taken together. The life space is his name for the total psychological reality that determines behavior at any instant: it consists of the person (P) plus the psychological environment (E), meaning the environment as it is perceived and experienced by that person, not the objective physical world. Lewin borrowed topology, a branch of mathematics dealing with spatial relationships and boundaries, to represent the life space as regions with boundaries, paths, barriers, and forces (valences) that push and pull the person toward or away from goals. The crucial nuance is that what matters is the psychological environment, the world as it exists for the individual, including their needs, goals, and perceptions, because that is what actually drives behavior. So the two components of the life space are the person and the psychological environment. The other options substitute group and society, which belong to broader social-level analyses, not to Lewin's definition of an individual's life space." },
  { id: "amity23_012", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Valium, Xanax, Rohypnol, and Halcion are:",
    options: { a: "Benzodiazepines", b: "Barbiturates", c: "Sedatives", d: "Hypnotics" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Barbiturates are a distinct, older class of CNS depressants (such as phenobarbital and secobarbital) that bind GABA receptors differently and are more lethal in overdose; the drugs listed are not barbiturates.", c: "Sedative is a broad functional category of calming drugs that includes benzodiazepines and many others, so it is too general to be the precise classification asked for.", d: "Hypnotic describes any sleep-inducing drug, a functional label that overlaps with but is broader than the specific benzodiazepine class to which all four named drugs belong." },
    explanation: "The answer is benzodiazepines because Valium (diazepam), Xanax (alprazolam), Rohypnol (flunitrazepam), and Halcion (triazolam) are all specific drugs within that single pharmacological class. To master this, understand what benzodiazepines are and how they differ from the broader categories in the options. Benzodiazepines are a class of central nervous system depressants that work by enhancing the action of GABA, the brain's main inhibitory neurotransmitter, at the GABA-A receptor. By increasing GABA's inhibitory effect, they reduce neural excitability, which produces their characteristic effects: anti-anxiety (anxiolytic), sedative, hypnotic (sleep-inducing), muscle-relaxant, and anticonvulsant actions. They are widely prescribed for anxiety disorders, panic, insomnia, and seizures, and they carry risks of tolerance, dependence, and withdrawal. The trick in this question is that the other options name broader or overlapping categories rather than the precise class. Sedatives and hypnotics are functional descriptions (drugs that calm or induce sleep) that include benzodiazepines but also many other agents, so they are too general to be the BEST answer for this specific list. Barbiturates are a different, older class of CNS depressants (such as phenobarbital) that act on GABA receptors differently and are far more dangerous in overdose, which is why benzodiazepines largely replaced them. Since all four named drugs are textbook benzodiazepines, the most precise and correct classification is benzodiazepines." },
  { id: "amity23_013", section: "general",
    q: "Determining a rule of structure from incomplete evidence and then identifying items that fulfil the rule would be a demonstration of:",
    options: { a: "Interpolation", b: "Extrapolation", c: "Interposition", d: "Structuring" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Interpolation is estimating an unknown value that lies BETWEEN known data points, a quantitative estimation process, not the discovery and application of a structural rule.", b: "Extrapolation is projecting estimates BEYOND the known range of data, again a numerical estimation, not rule-based classification of items.", c: "Interposition is a perceptual depth cue (a nearer object overlapping a farther one signals relative distance), entirely unrelated to inferring rules from incomplete evidence." },
    explanation: "The answer is structuring because the task described, inferring an underlying rule or organizing principle from incomplete information and then applying it to identify which items satisfy that rule, is what structuring refers to in this cognitive context. The question is testing your ability to distinguish a set of similar-sounding cognitive and perceptual terms. Structuring, in this sense, means imposing or discovering organization on incomplete or ambiguous evidence: you detect a pattern or rule of arrangement and then use that rule to classify or recognize further instances, essentially building a coherent structure from fragmentary data. The distractors are each precise but different operations. Interpolation is estimating an unknown value that falls BETWEEN known data points (for example, guessing a value at noon when you have readings at 11 and 1). Extrapolation is the opposite directionally: projecting BEYOND the known data range to estimate values outside it (predicting next year's figure from a trend). Both are quantitative estimation processes, not rule-discovery and classification. Interposition is a perceptual depth cue, not a reasoning process at all: it is the overlap cue, where a nearer object partly blocks a farther one, telling us the blocking object is closer. So interposition is about perceiving depth, completely unrelated to deriving rules from data. Because the question describes discovering a rule of structure and then matching items to it, structuring is the only term that fits the full description." },
  { id: "amity23_014", section: "general",
    q: "According to whom is 'Clinical Psychology now the largest field of specialisation'?",
    options: { a: "Cates and Lockman", b: "Morgan and King", c: "Adler", d: "Freud" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Cates and Lockman are not the conventional textbook source for this statement; they are not the authors associated with the claim in the standard Indian curriculum material.", c: "Alfred Adler is known for individual psychology, inferiority and superiority complexes, and striving for superiority, not for a statement about clinical psychology being the largest specialization.", d: "Sigmund Freud is the founder of psychoanalysis; he predates this kind of demographic statement about the field and is not credited with it." },
    explanation: "The source keys this as Morgan and King, so attribute the statement that clinical psychology is now the largest field of specialization to them, since this is a textbook-source attribution rather than a conceptual claim. This is an attribution or sourcing item, the kind common in Indian psychology entrance exams that quote specific authors from standard textbooks. Morgan and King, authors of the widely used Introduction to Psychology textbook (Clifford T. Morgan and Richard A. King), are frequently cited in Indian curricula, and the statement about clinical psychology being the largest area of specialization within psychology is credited to them in that source material. The substance behind the statement is itself worth knowing: clinical psychology is the branch concerned with the assessment, diagnosis, treatment, and prevention of psychological disorders and is consistently the largest subfield by number of practitioners, reflecting the high demand for mental health services. For exam purposes, the key skill is recognizing which named authority a quotation is conventionally attributed to. The distractors are all real figures but linked to other ideas: Adler is associated with individual psychology and inferiority, Freud with psychoanalysis, and Cates and Lockman are not the standard source for this particular statement in the textbooks these exams draw from. Because this is a memorization-of-source item, treat the keyed answer Morgan and King as the expected response while knowing the underlying fact about clinical psychology's prominence." },
  { id: "amity23_015", section: "abnormal",
    q: "A condition characterised by recurrent intense sexually arousing fantasies and urges involving the use of an inanimate object to obtain sexual gratification:",
    options: { a: "Necrophilia", b: "Fetishism", c: "Frotteurism", d: "Voyeurism" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Necrophilia is sexual arousal involving corpses, a dead human body, not the use of an inanimate object as described in the question.", c: "Frotteurism is arousal from touching or rubbing against a nonconsenting person, usually in a crowd, which involves a person rather than an inanimate object.", d: "Voyeurism is arousal from secretly watching an unsuspecting person who is naked or engaged in sexual activity, again involving a person, not an object." },
    explanation: "The answer is fetishism because it is defined precisely as recurrent, intense sexual arousal from a nonliving (inanimate) object. This question falls under the paraphilic disorders, a class of conditions in which sexual arousal centers on atypical objects, situations, or targets. The defining hallmark of fetishism (fetishistic disorder) is that the focus of arousal is an inanimate object, classically items like shoes, leather, rubber, or undergarments, or in some definitions a specific nonsexual body part, and the person experiences recurrent and intense sexually arousing fantasies, urges, or behaviors involving that object over at least six months, causing distress or impairment. The key diagnostic feature to anchor is the inanimate object. The distractors are other paraphilias, each defined by a DIFFERENT focus, so learning them as a contrasting set is the efficient strategy. Necrophilia is sexual arousal involving corpses (a dead body, not an inanimate object in the fetish sense). Frotteurism is arousal from touching or rubbing against a nonconsenting person, typically in a crowded public place. Voyeurism is arousal from secretly observing an unsuspecting person who is naked, undressing, or engaged in sexual activity. So each paraphilia is distinguished by the specific stimulus or situation that triggers arousal, and the inanimate object is unique to fetishism. Match the trigger to the disorder and these items become straightforward." },
  { id: "amity23_016", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Which one is NOT a compulsive behaviour in obsessive-compulsive disorder?",
    options: { a: "Tapping", b: "Hoarding", c: "Washing", d: "Checking" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Tapping is a genuine repetitive compulsive ritual in OCD, often performed to neutralize anxiety or a feared outcome, so it is not the odd one out.", c: "Washing (in response to contamination obsessions) is the most classic OCD compulsion of all, so it clearly belongs.", d: "Checking (locks, appliances, safety) is a core OCD compulsion driven by pathological doubt, so it belongs among the compulsions." },
    explanation: "The answer is hoarding because, in this classic framing, hoarding is not classified as a compulsion of obsessive-compulsive disorder; the others (tapping, washing, checking) are textbook compulsions. To understand this, you must grasp the structure of OCD. It has two components. Obsessions are recurrent, intrusive, unwanted thoughts, images, or urges that cause marked anxiety (for example, fears of contamination, doubts about safety, or a need for symmetry). Compulsions are repetitive behaviors or mental acts that the person feels driven to perform in response to an obsession, in order to reduce the anxiety or prevent a feared event. The most common compulsions are precisely washing and cleaning (in response to contamination fears), checking (in response to doubt, like checking locks or the stove), ordering and arranging, counting, and repeating actions such as tapping. So tapping, washing, and checking are all standard compulsive rituals. Hoarding, the difficulty discarding possessions and the distress at parting with them, was historically considered a symptom or subtype of OCD, but in the DSM-5 it was reclassified as its OWN distinct condition, Hoarding Disorder, within the obsessive-compulsive and related disorders chapter but separate from OCD itself. That is why hoarding is the odd one out: it is not a compulsive ritual performed to neutralize an obsession in the way washing or checking are; it now stands as a separate diagnosis. The conceptual point to retain is that classic compulsions neutralize obsessions, while hoarding is a distinct disorder of acquisition and difficulty discarding." },
  { id: "amity23_017", section: "general",
    q: "'Id' is to 'Just do it' as superego is to:",
    options: { a: "'Wait till later.'", b: "'Do your own thing.'", c: "'Don't do it.'", d: "'Oh, sit on it.'" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "'Wait till later' describes delay of gratification, which is the work of the ego operating on the reality principle, not the superego's moral prohibition.", b: "'Do your own thing' is a permissive, self-indulgent attitude that fits the id's pleasure-seeking, the opposite of the superego.", d: "'Oh, sit on it' is a flippant distractor with no theoretical meaning in the structural model and does not capture the superego's moralizing function." },
    explanation: "The answer is 'Don't do it.' because the superego is the moralizing, prohibitive agency of Freud's structural model, and a blunt internal command forbidding an impulse is exactly how it operates. Freud's structural theory (introduced in 1923 in The Ego and the Id) divides the mind into three systems. The id is the oldest and most primitive, present from birth, a seething reservoir of instinctual drives (sex and aggression) that runs entirely on the pleasure principle: it wants immediate gratification with no regard for reality, logic, or morality, hence the 'Just do it' framing. The superego develops last, around ages three to five, as the child resolves the Oedipus complex and internalizes the moral standards, rules, and prohibitions of the parents and wider society. It has two parts: the conscience, which punishes violations with guilt and says 'don't,' and the ego ideal, which holds up standards of who we should be. The ego sits between them, operating on the reality principle, mediating the id's demands against the superego's prohibitions and the constraints of the external world. So the analogy maps the id's permissive 'do it' onto the superego's moral veto, 'Don't do it.' This is the most basic way to remember the superego: it is the internalized parental 'no,' the source of guilt and conscience." },
  { id: "amity23_018", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Choose the odd one out regarding the pattern of sympathetic activation during emotion:",
    options: { a: "Increased heart rate", b: "Pupil constriction", c: "Lowered skin resistance", d: "Increased breathing rate" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Increased heart rate is a genuine sympathetic effect, blood is pumped faster to skeletal muscles for action, so it belongs with the others, not the odd one out.", c: "Lowered skin resistance results from sweat gland activation under sympathetic control and is the basis of the galvanic skin response, so it is a true arousal marker.", d: "Increased breathing rate is part of the fight or flight package, raising oxygen intake, so it is consistent with sympathetic activation rather than the exception." },
    explanation: "Pupil constriction is the odd one out because sympathetic arousal during emotion produces pupil DILATION (mydriasis), not constriction. The autonomic nervous system has two branches with largely opposing actions. The sympathetic branch drives the 'fight or flight' response: when you are frightened, angry, or excited, sympathetic fibers and circulating adrenaline prepare the body for vigorous action. This raises heart rate (so more blood reaches muscles), increases respiration rate (so more oxygen is taken in), and lowers skin resistance because sweat glands activate and the salt in sweat makes the skin conduct electricity more easily, which is precisely what the galvanic skin response or electrodermal activity measures in lie detectors and arousal studies. Crucially, the sympathetic system also dilates the pupils, widening them to let in more light and improve distance vision for spotting threats. The parasympathetic branch does the reverse: it is the 'rest and digest' system that constricts the pupils, slows the heart, and conserves energy. So options a, c, and d are all genuine markers of sympathetic activation, while pupil constriction belongs to parasympathetic dominance. The takeaway for the exam: under stress or strong emotion, pupils get bigger, not smaller, so any item claiming constriction during arousal is the exception." },
  { id: "amity23_019", section: "abnormal",
    q: "The defence mechanism of Undoing is typically seen in:",
    options: { a: "Depression", b: "Mania", c: "Obsessive compulsive neurosis", d: "Schizophrenia" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Depression is more classically linked to defenses such as introjection and turning anger against the self, not the magical canceling of undoing.", b: "Mania is associated with denial and manic defenses that minimize or deny distressing reality, not with ritualistic undoing.", d: "Schizophrenia is associated with primitive defenses like projection, denial, and projective identification, not the comparatively neurotic, ritual-based defense of undoing." },
    explanation: "Undoing is most characteristically associated with obsessive-compulsive neurosis because the compulsive rituals of OCD are, in psychodynamic terms, literally acts of undoing. Undoing is a defense mechanism in which a person performs an action, or its symbolic equivalent, that is meant to magically cancel out or reverse an earlier unacceptable thought, impulse, or act, as if it never happened. Anna Freud catalogued it among the ego defenses, and classical analysts saw it as a hallmark of the obsessional character. The mechanism links directly to the symptom picture of OCD: a person tormented by an aggressive or sexually intrusive obsession (say, a thought of harming a loved one) performs a ritual (repeated washing, counting, checking, or a precise sequence of gestures) that symbolically atones for or neutralizes the forbidden wish. The compulsion is the undoing made behavioral. Psychodynamically, obsessional patients are also said to rely on related defenses such as isolation of affect (separating a thought from its feeling) and reaction formation, but undoing is the one that explains the ritualistic, repetitive, 'do then cancel' quality of compulsions. So when an exam pairs undoing with a disorder, OCD is the textbook match because the entire architecture of compulsive ritual is undoing in action." },
  { id: "amity23_020", section: "general",
    q: "The most important school of Psychology which has contributed a lot towards perception is:",
    options: { a: "Psychoanalysis", b: "Behaviour school", c: "Structural school", d: "Gestalt psychology" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Psychoanalysis, Freud's school, concerns unconscious motivation, drives, and personality, contributing little to the experimental study of perceptual organization.", b: "The behaviorist school, focused on observable stimulus-response learning, deliberately avoided studying internal perceptual processes like organization and grouping.", c: "The structural school of Wundt and Titchener tried to reduce experience to elemental sensations, the very atomistic approach Gestalt psychology arose to refute, so it is the rival, not the contributor." },
    explanation: "Gestalt psychology is the school that contributed most to the study of perception. Founded in Germany around 1912 by Max Wertheimer, with Wolfgang Kohler and Kurt Koffka as its other principal figures, Gestalt psychology arose as a direct reaction against the structuralism of Wundt and Titchener, which tried to break consciousness down into elementary sensations. Wertheimer's key demonstration was the phi phenomenon: two stationary lights flashed in quick succession are perceived as a single light moving, an experience that cannot be reduced to the individual sensations and proves that the perceived whole differs from the sum of its parts. From this came the movement's famous dictum that 'the whole is other than the sum of its parts.' Gestalt theorists then formulated the laws of perceptual organization, the principles by which the mind groups sensory elements into coherent wholes: proximity (near things group together), similarity (like things group together), closure (we mentally complete incomplete figures), continuity (we perceive smooth continuous forms), and figure-ground segregation (we separate an object from its background). These laws remain foundational in the psychology of perception today. Because no other school built such a systematic account of how raw sensation becomes organized perception, Gestalt is the correct answer." },
  { id: "amity23_021", section: "assessment",
    q: "The Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale can be used to assess eligibility for:",
    options: { a: "Housing benefit", b: "Disability allowance", c: "Suicide risk", d: "Special educational needs" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Housing benefit is determined by income and financial need, not by intellectual functioning, so an IQ test is irrelevant to that decision.", c: "Suicide risk is assessed through clinical interview and risk-screening instruments measuring mood, hopelessness, and intent, not through an intelligence test.", d: "Special educational needs concern children and are assessed with child-focused tools; the WAIS is for adults (the WISC is the child version), so it does not fit this context." },
    explanation: "The Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale (WAIS) can be used to help assess eligibility for disability allowance, because establishing intellectual functioning is part of documenting a qualifying disability such as intellectual disability or cognitive impairment. The WAIS, originally developed by David Wechsler (the first version, the Wechsler-Bellevue, appeared in 1939, with the WAIS proper in 1955 and now in its fourth and fifth editions), is the most widely used individually administered adult intelligence test. It yields a Full Scale IQ along with index scores for verbal comprehension, perceptual reasoning, working memory, and processing speed. Because a diagnosis of intellectual disability requires demonstrating significantly subaverage intellectual functioning (roughly an IQ below 70) alongside deficits in adaptive behavior, a standardized IQ measure like the WAIS provides exactly the objective intellectual data that disability determinations require. The question is testing whether the student understands the practical, applied use of intelligence testing in clinical and medico-legal contexts: an IQ score can substantiate a claim for a disability benefit by quantifying cognitive limitation. The other options describe contexts where an adult intelligence test is either irrelevant or the wrong instrument entirely." },
  { id: "amity23_022", section: "general",
    q: "Optical illusions result from distortion in:",
    options: { a: "Translation", b: "Sensation", c: "Perception", d: "Adaptation" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Translation is not a recognized stage in the sensation-perception sequence and does not name the constructive interpretive process where illusions arise.", b: "Sensation is the accurate detection and transduction of physical energy; in an illusion the sensory signal is correct, so the error is not at this level.", d: "Adaptation refers to declining receptor response to a constant stimulus over time (such as no longer noticing a smell), which is a different phenomenon from the misinterpretation that produces illusions." },
    explanation: "Optical illusions result from distortion in perception, not in the sensory signal itself. The key distinction the question tests is between sensation and perception. Sensation is the detection of physical energy by sensory receptors and its transduction into neural signals: light hitting the retina, sound vibrating the cochlea. Perception is the brain's interpretation and organization of that raw sensory input into meaningful objects and scenes. In an optical illusion such as the Muller-Lyer (two equal lines with inward versus outward arrowheads appear different in length) or the Ponzo illusion, the actual stimulus reaching the eye is faithfully sensed; the retina registers the lines accurately. What goes wrong is the interpretation: the brain applies its normal, usually adaptive, assumptions and depth cues to a flat figure where they do not apply, and so it misjudges size, length, or shape. This is why illusions are considered errors of perception, the constructive, inferential stage, rather than failures of sensation. Crucially, illusions differ from hallucinations: an illusion is a misinterpretation of a real external stimulus, whereas a hallucination is a percept with no external stimulus at all. The systematic, predictable nature of illusions (everyone with a normal brain sees them the same way) is itself evidence that perception is an active, rule-governed construction rather than a passive recording." },
  { id: "amity23_023", section: "social",
    q: "Defense attorneys often protest the prosecutor's use of eyewitness testimony because:",
    options: { a: "Eyewitnesses are more prone to forgetting", b: "A confident witness is usually right", c: "Source errors may cause an eyewitness to make a false identification", d: "Poor eyesight of eyewitnesses tends to invalidate their testimony" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Eyewitnesses are not specially more prone to forgetting than anyone else; the real concern is distortion and misattribution of memory, not simple loss of it.", b: "This statement is the opposite of the research finding; confidence correlates only weakly with accuracy, so a confident witness is not reliably right, which is why this cannot be the reason for distrust.", d: "Poor eyesight is an occasional individual factor, not the general, well-documented cognitive mechanism (source error) that makes eyewitness testimony unreliable across the board." },
    explanation: "Defense attorneys distrust eyewitness testimony because source errors can lead an eyewitness to make a false identification. The underlying concept is source monitoring (or source attribution), the cognitive process of correctly tracking where a memory came from. Memory is not a video recording played back faithfully; it is reconstructive, as Bartlett and later Elizabeth Loftus showed. When we retrieve a memory we rebuild it, and in doing so we can attach a face or detail to the wrong source: a person glimpsed elsewhere, a mugshot seen later, a face from the news, or a suggestion implanted by leading questions. Loftus's misinformation effect research demonstrated how post-event information reshapes what witnesses 'remember,' and the related phenomenon of unconscious transference occurs when a witness recognizes a face as familiar but misattributes that familiarity to the crime scene rather than its true origin, producing a confident but wrong identification. This is exactly why mistaken eyewitness identification is the leading cause of wrongful convictions later overturned by DNA evidence. The danger is compounded because witness confidence is only weakly related to accuracy, so a sincere, confident witness can still be wrong. The question targets this: the threat is not poor eyesight or forgetting but the systematic, reconstructive error of misattributing the source of a remembered face." },
  { id: "amity23_024", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Identify the techniques of Functional Neuroimaging:",
    options: { a: "CT scan", b: "Magnetic Resonance Imaging", c: "Positron emission tomography (PET) & Single photon emission computed tomography (SPECT)", d: "All of the above" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "A CT scan is a structural technique using X-rays to image anatomy; it shows what the brain looks like, not what it is doing.", b: "Conventional Magnetic Resonance Imaging is primarily a structural method showing detailed anatomy; it is functional only in the special fMRI variant, which is not what plain MRI denotes here.", d: "'All of the above' is wrong because it would include CT and structural MRI, which are anatomical rather than functional imaging methods." },
    explanation: "PET and SPECT are the functional neuroimaging techniques because they measure brain activity (function) rather than just brain structure. The fundamental divide in neuroimaging is between structural and functional methods. Structural imaging shows the anatomy of the brain, its physical shape and any lesions or atrophy: this includes the CT scan, which uses X-rays to build cross-sectional images, and conventional MRI, which uses strong magnetic fields and radiofrequency pulses to produce highly detailed images of soft tissue. Functional imaging, by contrast, captures the brain in action by tracking physiological activity such as blood flow, glucose metabolism, or oxygen use, which rise in regions that are working harder. Positron emission tomography (PET) injects a radioactive tracer, often labeled glucose, and detects the gamma rays emitted, mapping which regions consume the most energy during a task. Single photon emission computed tomography (SPECT) works on a similar radiotracer principle, measuring regional cerebral blood flow. Both reveal what the brain is doing, not merely what it looks like. (Functional MRI, which detects the BOLD signal from blood oxygenation, is also a functional technique, but plain MRI as listed here is structural.) So the correct grouping of functional methods is PET and SPECT." },
  { id: "amity23_025", section: "abnormal",
    q: "A condition characterised by the occurrence of sudden, rapid, purposeless involuntary movement in the body:",
    options: { a: "Dissociation", b: "Tics", c: "Parkinson's Disease", d: "Seizures" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Dissociation is a disruption in the integration of consciousness, memory, or identity, a psychological splitting, not a sudden involuntary body movement.", c: "Parkinson's disease involves slow movement (bradykinesia), rigidity, and a resting tremor caused by dopamine loss in the substantia nigra; its movements are not the rapid, purposeless jerks of a tic.", d: "Seizures are bursts of abnormal synchronized electrical activity in the brain producing convulsions or altered awareness, distinct from the discrete, suppressible, stereotyped movements of tics." },
    explanation: "Tics are the condition defined by sudden, rapid, recurrent, purposeless involuntary movements. A tic is a stereotyped motor movement or vocalization that is abrupt and non-rhythmic; common motor tics include eye blinking, facial grimacing, head jerking, or shoulder shrugging, while vocal tics include throat clearing, grunting, or words. A defining feature is that tics are often preceded by an uncomfortable premonitory urge and can be briefly suppressed with effort, though suppression builds tension that the tic then relieves, which distinguishes them from purely involuntary movements like seizures. When chronic motor and vocal tics persist for over a year and begin in childhood, the diagnosis is Tourette's disorder, classified among neurodevelopmental disorders and linked to dysfunction in the basal ganglia and dopaminergic circuits. The phrasing of the question, sudden, rapid, purposeless, and involuntary, is essentially the textbook description of a tic, which is why it is the answer. The other options describe quite different phenomena, and recognizing those differences is the point of the item." },
  { id: "amity23_026", section: "assessment",
    q: "The process of recording a behaviour causes the behaviour to change, even before any treatment is implemented. This is known as:",
    options: { a: "Reactivity", b: "Shaping", c: "Applied Behaviour Analysis", d: "Chaining" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Shaping is the operant procedure of reinforcing successive approximations toward a target behavior; it is an active intervention to create behavior, not a side effect of merely recording it.", c: "Applied Behavior Analysis is the broad applied discipline that uses learning principles to change socially significant behavior; it is a whole field, not the specific effect of observation on behavior.", d: "Chaining is the technique of linking individual behaviors into a sequence by reinforcing them in order; it builds complex behavior and is unrelated to the reactivity of measurement." },
    explanation: "Reactivity is the phenomenon in which the act of observing or recording a behavior changes that behavior, even before any intervention is introduced. It is a central concept in behavioral assessment and self-monitoring. When a person knows they are being watched, or when they themselves begin keeping a record of a target behavior (counting cigarettes smoked, logging snacks eaten, tallying times they bite their nails), the behavior tends to shift, usually in the socially desirable direction. Reactivity is closely tied to the famous Hawthorne effect, where workers at the Hawthorne plant improved their productivity simply because they were being studied, regardless of the actual changes made to their conditions. In clinical practice this cuts two ways. On one hand reactivity is a threat to the validity of baseline data: the recorded behavior may not represent the person's true habitual rate. On the other hand clinicians deliberately exploit reactivity, because self-monitoring can itself be therapeutic, the simple act of tracking an undesired behavior often reduces it. The key idea the question tests is that the change here arises from measurement and awareness, not from any treatment, which is precisely the definition of reactivity. The other options are all behavioral techniques for building or changing behavior, not effects of observation." },
  { id: "amity23_027", section: "biopsych",
    q: "A nerve cell carrying information from your brain to muscles and glands is called a:",
    options: { a: "Motor neuron", b: "Effector cell", c: "Connector neuron", d: "Sensory neuron" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "An effector cell is the muscle or gland that receives the command and performs the action; it is the target of the motor neuron, not the nerve cell carrying the signal.", c: "A connector neuron (interneuron) sits within the central nervous system linking sensory and motor pathways; it does not carry information out to muscles and glands.", d: "A sensory neuron carries information in the opposite direction, from receptors inward toward the brain, so it is the afferent counterpart, not the outgoing motor cell described." },
    explanation: "A motor neuron is the nerve cell that carries information from the brain and spinal cord outward to the muscles and glands. Neurons are classified by the direction in which they carry signals. Sensory (afferent) neurons carry information inward, from sensory receptors in the skin, eyes, and other organs toward the central nervous system. Motor (efferent) neurons carry information in the opposite direction, outward from the central nervous system to the effectors, that is the muscles and glands that actually carry out a response. The simplest mnemonic is SAME: Sensory = Afferent, Motor = Efferent. When a motor neuron fires, it releases a neurotransmitter (acetylcholine at the neuromuscular junction) onto a muscle fiber, causing it to contract, or onto a gland, causing it to secrete. Between sensory and motor neurons lie interneurons (also called connector or association neurons), which reside entirely within the central nervous system and link the incoming and outgoing pathways, as in a reflex arc. The question describes carrying signals away from the brain to muscles and glands, which is the defining role of the motor neuron, so that is the answer." },
  { id: "amity23_028", section: "assessment",
    q: "Identify the correct option for Projective tests of Personality: i) Rorschach & TAT, ii) Eysenck Personality Questionnaire, iii) 16 PF, iv) MCMI-III.",
    options: { a: "Both i and ii", b: "Only i", c: "i, ii and iv", d: "None" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "'Both i and ii' is wrong because item (ii), the Eysenck Personality Questionnaire, is an objective self-report inventory, not a projective test.", c: "'i, ii and iv' wrongly includes the Eysenck questionnaire and the MCMI-III, both of which are structured objective inventories rather than projective techniques.", d: "'None' is wrong because item (i), Rorschach and TAT, genuinely are projective tests, so at least one option qualifies." },
    explanation: "Only Rorschach and TAT are projective tests, so 'Only i' is correct. The crucial distinction is between projective and objective (self-report) personality tests. Projective tests present deliberately ambiguous, unstructured stimuli and ask the person to interpret them, on the assumption (the projective hypothesis) that people project their unconscious needs, conflicts, and personality onto the vague material because there is no obviously right answer to constrain them. The Rorschach Inkblot Test (ten symmetrical inkblots, devised by Hermann Rorschach in 1921) and the Thematic Apperception Test (TAT, ambiguous scene cards about which the person tells a story, developed by Henry Murray and Christiana Morgan) are the two classic projective instruments. By contrast, objective tests use structured items with fixed response formats (true/false or rating scales) that are scored against norms. The Eysenck Personality Questionnaire (measuring extraversion, neuroticism, and psychoticism), Cattell's 16 Personality Factor questionnaire (the 16 PF), and the Millon Clinical Multiaxial Inventory (MCMI-III) are all objective self-report inventories, not projective. So among the four listed, only item (i) names projective tests, making 'Only i' the right answer." },
  { id: "amity23_029", section: "abnormal",
    q: "The definition of abnormality which includes unhappiness is the ______ aspect.",
    options: { a: "Deviance", b: "Dysfunction", c: "Danger to self or others", d: "Distress" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Deviance concerns violating social norms and expectations, an external standard, rather than the person's own internal feelings of unhappiness.", b: "Dysfunction concerns impaired ability to carry out daily activities and roles, not the subjective experience of unhappiness itself.", c: "Danger to self or others concerns the risk of harm or threat posed, which is a behavioral risk dimension distinct from emotional suffering." },
    explanation: "The aspect of abnormality that centers on unhappiness is distress. Defining abnormality is notoriously difficult, so textbooks use a cluster of criteria often summarized as the 'four Ds': deviance, distress, dysfunction, and danger. Deviance refers to behavior, thoughts, or emotions that violate a society's norms and expectations. Dysfunction refers to interference with daily functioning, when a condition prevents a person from working, maintaining relationships, or caring for themselves. Danger refers to behavior that poses a risk of harm to oneself or to others. Distress, the criterion this question targets, refers to the subjective suffering, the unhappiness, anxiety, pain, or emotional discomfort that the individual personally experiences. A person who is deeply unhappy or in psychological pain meets the distress criterion. No single D is sufficient on its own (a grieving person feels distress but is not disordered; an eccentric may be deviant but content), which is why clinicians weigh them together. But because the question specifically anchors on 'unhappiness,' the experiential suffering of the individual, distress is unambiguously the matching aspect." },
  { id: "amity23_030", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Which of the following characterises slow-wave sleep?",
    options: { a: "Stage 1 to 4 and REM sleep", b: "Stage 3 and 4", c: "NREM and REM sleep", d: "Stage 1 to 4" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Listing stages 1 to 4 plus REM describes the entire sleep cycle, not the specific deep-sleep phase; the light stages and REM are not slow-wave sleep.", c: "NREM and REM sleep together comprise all of sleep; slow-wave sleep is only a subset of NREM (the deepest part), so this is far too broad.", d: "Stages 1 to 4 include the light stages 1 and 2, which show theta waves and spindles rather than the delta waves that define slow-wave sleep; only 3 and 4 qualify." },
    explanation: "Slow-wave sleep is defined as stages 3 and 4 of non-REM sleep. Human sleep cycles through distinct stages identifiable by EEG patterns. Stage 1 is the light, transitional drowsy state with theta waves. Stage 2 shows sleep spindles and K-complexes and is true but still relatively light sleep. Stages 3 and 4 are the deepest stages, characterized by large, slow, high-amplitude delta waves (about 1 to 4 Hz), which is exactly why these two stages are jointly called slow-wave sleep (SWS) or delta sleep. (In the newer AASM scheme stages 3 and 4 are merged into a single stage N3, but classic textbooks still split them.) Slow-wave sleep is the most physically restorative phase: growth hormone is secreted, tissue repair occurs, and it is the deepest, hardest stage from which to wake someone; sleepwalking and night terrors arise here. It is distinct from REM (rapid eye movement) sleep, the stage of vivid dreaming, fast desynchronized brain waves resembling waking, and muscle atonia. So slow-wave sleep specifically denotes the deep delta-wave stages 3 and 4, not REM and not the lighter stages." },
  { id: "amity23_031", section: "assessment",
    q: "One of the most widely used tests to assess executive functioning:",
    options: { a: "Wisconsin Card Sorting Test", b: "Minnesota Number Ordering Test", c: "Manhattan Card Sorting Test", d: "None of the above" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "The 'Minnesota Number Ordering Test' is not a recognized standard executive-function instrument; the name is a distractor (Minnesota is associated with the MMPI, a personality inventory, not this test).", c: "The 'Manhattan Card Sorting Test' is a fabricated name designed to lure those who recognize 'card sorting' but not the correct 'Wisconsin' origin.", d: "'None of the above' is wrong because the Wisconsin Card Sorting Test in option a is a genuine and widely used executive-function measure." },
    explanation: "The Wisconsin Card Sorting Test (WCST) is one of the most widely used measures of executive functioning. Executive functions are the higher-order, frontal-lobe-mediated cognitive abilities that allow goal-directed behavior: planning, working memory, inhibition, mental flexibility, and the ability to shift strategy when circumstances change. The WCST assesses precisely this capacity for abstract reasoning and cognitive flexibility. The examinee is given cards that can be sorted by color, shape, or number and must figure out the sorting rule purely from the examiner's feedback of 'correct' or 'wrong.' Once the person has learned the rule, the examiner silently changes it, and the test measures how readily the person detects the change and shifts to the new rule. A key index is perseverative errors, continuing to sort by the old rule despite repeated feedback that it is now wrong, which reflects failure of set-shifting and is classically elevated in patients with dorsolateral prefrontal cortex damage. Because of this sensitivity to frontal-executive dysfunction, the WCST is a standard tool in neuropsychological assessment. The other options are largely invented or non-existent test names, included as distractors." },
  { id: "amity23_032", section: "biopsych",
    q: "The wavelength of electromagnetic radiation visible to the human eye is:",
    options: { a: "380-760 nm", b: "160-780 nm", c: "360-460 nm", d: "700-800 nm" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "160 to 780 nm starts deep in the ultraviolet region (around 160 nm is far UV, invisible to humans), so the lower bound is wrong even though the upper bound is plausible.", c: "360 to 460 nm covers only the violet-to-blue short-wavelength portion and stops well before green, yellow, and red, so it omits most of the visible spectrum.", d: "700 to 800 nm sits at the red and near-infrared edge, capturing only the longest red wavelengths and extending into invisible infrared, missing the entire blue-green portion." },
    explanation: "The wavelength of electromagnetic radiation visible to the human eye spans roughly 380 to 760 nanometers, so that is the correct range. Visible light is only a tiny band within the much larger electromagnetic spectrum, which runs from extremely short gamma rays and X-rays through ultraviolet, then the narrow visible window, then infrared, microwaves, and long radio waves. The human eye's photoreceptors respond only to that narrow band of about 380 to 760 nm (some texts round it to roughly 400 to 700 nm). Within this band, wavelength is what the brain interprets as hue or color: the shortest visible wavelengths near 380 to 400 nm are seen as violet and blue, the middle wavelengths around 500 to 570 nm as green and yellow, and the longest near 700 to 760 nm as red. Wavelengths just shorter than violet are ultraviolet and just longer than red are infrared, both invisible to us though detectable by some other species. Note that this question is essentially identical to amity23_041 in the same paper, which gives the range as 400 to 760 nm; both point to the same narrow visible band, and 380 to 760 nm is the more precise commonly cited figure here. The other options either start far too low into the ultraviolet or are too narrow to cover the full visible range." },
  { id: "amity23_033", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Hallucinations most often occur in people suffering from:",
    options: { a: "Mania", b: "Schizophrenia", c: "Depression", d: "Personality Disorders" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Mania can produce psychotic features including hallucinations when severe, but these are mood-congruent and episodic, not the defining, most common feature; hallucinations are far more characteristically tied to schizophrenia.", c: "Depression may include psychotic features in severe cases (mood-congruent voices of guilt or worthlessness), but this is the exception, not the rule, so depression is not where hallucinations most often occur.", d: "Personality disorders involve enduring maladaptive patterns of thinking and relating, not psychosis; transient perceptual disturbances can occur in borderline or schizotypal types but frank hallucinations are not a defining feature." },
    explanation: "The answer is schizophrenia because hallucinations, perceptions that occur without any corresponding external stimulus, are a defining positive symptom of this disorder and feature in its core diagnostic criteria. A hallucination is a false sensory experience that the person genuinely perceives as real: hearing voices when no one is speaking, seeing things that are not present, feeling sensations on the skin without cause. Schizophrenia is a chronic psychotic disorder in which the patient loses touch with reality, and clinicians divide its symptoms into positive symptoms (additions to normal experience, like hallucinations and delusions), negative symptoms (losses, like flat affect, avolition, and alogia), and disorganized symptoms (disordered speech and behaviour). Among hallucination types, auditory hallucinations, especially voices commenting on the person or conversing with each other, are the most characteristic of schizophrenia and overlap with Schneider's first-rank symptoms. The mechanism is thought to involve dysregulated dopamine transmission in the mesolimbic pathway, which is why dopamine-blocking antipsychotics reduce positive symptoms. The key teaching point is that while hallucinations CAN appear in other conditions, they are most strongly and most commonly tied to schizophrenia, where roughly three quarters of patients experience them. This is why the phrase most often points unambiguously to schizophrenia rather than any mood or personality condition." },
  { id: "amity23_034", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Which statement is INCORRECT?",
    options: { a: "Cones are most prevalent in the central retina", b: "Cones are sensitive to moderate to high levels of light", c: "Cones provide only monochromatic information", d: "Cones provide excellent acuity" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "This is a true statement: cones are indeed most prevalent in the central retina, packed densely at the fovea, which is why central vision is sharpest, so it is not the incorrect option.", b: "This is correct: cones require moderate to high light levels to function (photopic vision) and are inactive in dim light, where rods take over, so it does not answer a question asking for the false claim.", d: "This is true: the low convergence and foveal concentration of cones give them excellent acuity, so it correctly describes cones and is not the incorrect statement." },
    explanation: "The incorrect statement is that cones provide only monochromatic information, because the truth is the exact opposite: cones are the photoreceptors responsible for colour (chromatic) vision, while rods provide monochromatic, achromatic vision in dim light. The human retina contains two classes of photoreceptor. Rods are highly sensitive to light, function in low illumination (scotopic vision), are concentrated in the peripheral retina, and contain a single pigment (rhodopsin), so they cannot discriminate colour and yield only shades of grey. Cones operate under bright conditions (photopic vision), are densely packed in the central retina at the fovea, and come in three types tuned to short (blue), medium (green), and long (red) wavelengths. Because the brain compares the relative activation of these three cone types, cones are precisely what give us colour vision, which is the basis of the trichromatic theory proposed by Young and Helmholtz. The high density of cones at the fovea, combined with their near one-to-one connection to bipolar and ganglion cells (low convergence), also gives them excellent visual acuity, whereas rods converge heavily and trade acuity for sensitivity. So options a, b, and d all correctly describe cones, and only the claim about monochromatic information misstates cone function, making it the answer to this NOT/INCORRECT question." },
  { id: "amity23_035", section: "general",
    q: "Who emphasised the linguistic relativity hypothesis?",
    options: { a: "Noam Chomsky", b: "Jean Piaget", c: "Steven Pinker", d: "B.L. Whorf" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Noam Chomsky proposed the opposite-leaning view: a universal grammar innate to all humans, emphasizing shared deep structure across languages rather than language shaping thought differently across cultures.", b: "Jean Piaget studied cognitive development in children and held that thought precedes and drives language, the reverse of the relativity emphasis on language shaping thought.", c: "Steven Pinker is a prominent critic of strong linguistic relativity who argues for a universal mentalese (language of thought) underlying all languages, so he is associated with rejecting, not advancing, the hypothesis." },
    explanation: "The answer is B.L. Whorf because the linguistic relativity hypothesis, also called the Sapir-Whorf hypothesis, is named for Edward Sapir and his student Benjamin Lee Whorf, who argued that the structure of a language shapes or influences how its speakers perceive and think about the world. The core idea is that language is not merely a neutral vehicle for expressing pre-existing thoughts; rather, the categories, grammar, and vocabulary available in a language channel habitual thought. The hypothesis comes in two strengths. The strong version (linguistic determinism) claims language fully determines thought, so concepts that cannot be expressed in a language cannot be thought; this strong form is now largely rejected. The weaker, more defensible version (linguistic relativity) holds that language influences thought and perception in measurable but non-absolute ways, for example affecting how readily speakers categorize colours or remember events. Whorf's famous illustrations drew on comparisons between English and Native American languages such as Hopi, claiming differences in how time was conceptualized. Contemporary research, including work on colour terminology and spatial reference frames, supports a modest relativity effect while rejecting hard determinism. Knowing this lets you distinguish Whorf from the nativist and cognitive theorists offered as distractors, since their positions on language are quite different from the relativity claim." },
  { id: "amity23_036", section: "biopsych",
    q: "A neurological disorder characterised by impairments in motor performance and coordination is known as:",
    options: { a: "Dyspraxia", b: "Apraxia", c: "Agnosia", d: "Anomia" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Apraxia is the complete loss of the ability to carry out learned purposeful movements following brain damage, not the developmental, partial impairment of coordination the question describes.", c: "Agnosia is a failure to recognize or interpret sensory information (objects, faces, sounds) despite intact senses; it is a perceptual-recognition deficit, not a motor coordination disorder.", d: "Anomia is the specific inability to name objects or retrieve words, a language deficit seen in some aphasias, and has nothing to do with motor performance or coordination." },
    explanation: "The answer is dyspraxia because the question describes a developmental neurological condition marked by impaired motor performance and coordination despite intact intelligence and normal muscle function. Dyspraxia, often termed developmental coordination disorder, is a difficulty in planning and executing coordinated movement; the person knows what they want to do but cannot smoothly organize the motor sequences to do it, leading to clumsiness, poor balance, and trouble with tasks like writing, dressing, or catching a ball. The crucial distinction tested here is between dyspraxia and apraxia. The prefix 'a-' means total absence or loss, while 'dys-' means impaired or difficult; apraxia is the complete loss of a previously acquired ability to perform purposeful movements, usually arising from acquired brain damage such as stroke in adults, whereas dyspraxia is typically a developmental difficulty present from childhood and is a matter of degree rather than total loss. Both involve motor planning rather than weakness or paralysis, which sets them apart from purely sensory or naming deficits. Because the question frames it as a general impairment in motor performance and coordination rather than a total acquired loss following brain injury, dyspraxia is the best fit. Understanding the a- versus dys- prefix logic also generalizes to many other neurology terms." },
  { id: "amity23_037", section: "biopsych",
    q: "The phenomenon of Long-Term Potentiation (LTP) is associated with:",
    options: { a: "Memory", b: "Thermoregulation", c: "Circadian rhythm", d: "Sleep" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Thermoregulation, the maintenance of body temperature, is controlled by the hypothalamus through autonomic and behavioural mechanisms, not by synaptic potentiation.", c: "Circadian rhythm is governed by the suprachiasmatic nucleus acting as the body's master clock entrained by light, a separate system from activity-dependent synaptic strengthening.", d: "Sleep is regulated by brainstem and hypothalamic circuits and neurotransmitter cycles; while sleep aids memory consolidation, LTP itself is the synaptic mechanism of memory, not of sleep generation." },
    explanation: "The answer is memory because Long-Term Potentiation is the leading cellular and synaptic mechanism believed to underlie learning and the formation of long-term memories. LTP is a persistent strengthening of synapses based on recent patterns of activity: when a presynaptic neuron repeatedly and strongly stimulates a postsynaptic neuron, the connection between them becomes more efficient and that enhancement lasts for hours, days, or longer. It was first demonstrated by Bliss and Lomo in the rabbit hippocampus, which is fitting because the hippocampus is central to consolidating new declarative memories. The classic mechanism involves the NMDA glutamate receptor acting as a coincidence detector: it opens only when the postsynaptic cell is already depolarized and glutamate is bound, allowing calcium influx that triggers insertion of additional AMPA receptors and strengthens the synapse. This embodies the Hebbian principle that neurons that fire together wire together. LTP gives a biological grounding to how experience physically reshapes neural circuits, and its partner phenomenon, long-term depression, weakens unused synapses. Because LTP explains how synaptic change encodes lasting information, it is firmly linked to memory rather than to homeostatic body functions like temperature regulation, the sleep-wake cycle, or daily biological rhythms, which are governed by other systems such as the hypothalamus and suprachiasmatic nucleus." },
  { id: "amity23_038", section: "biostats",
    q: "In an experiment, the group which receives no treatment is called:",
    options: { a: "Experimental group", b: "No group", c: "Control group", d: "None of these" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "The experimental group is precisely the opposite: it is the group that DOES receive the treatment or manipulation, so it cannot be the untreated baseline.", b: "No group is not a recognized term in experimental design; it is a distractor with no methodological meaning.", d: "None of these is wrong because a correct, well-established term, control group, exactly names the untreated comparison condition." },
    explanation: "The answer is control group because in experimental design the control group is the set of participants who do NOT receive the treatment or independent variable manipulation, serving as the baseline against which the treated group is compared. The logic of a true experiment is that you manipulate one variable (the independent variable) while holding everything else constant, then observe its effect on an outcome (the dependent variable). To know whether any observed change was actually caused by the treatment rather than by chance, the passage of time, expectations, or other confounds, you need a comparison condition that is identical in every way except that it lacks the treatment. That comparison condition is the control group. The group that does receive the treatment is the experimental group. By randomly assigning participants to experimental and control groups, the researcher distributes confounding variables evenly, so any difference in outcomes can be attributed to the independent variable, giving the study internal validity. A common refinement is the placebo control, where the control group receives an inert substitute so that expectancy effects are equalized. Understanding this terminology is foundational to reading any research methods question: experimental equals treated, control equals untreated baseline. Hence the group receiving no treatment is, by definition, the control group." },
  { id: "amity23_039", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Society's explicit and implicit norm violations can be viewed as not normal in the sense that the violation is:",
    options: { a: "Distressful", b: "Dysfunctional", c: "Deviant", d: "A danger to oneself or others" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Distress refers to the personal suffering or unhappiness the individual feels, not to breaking social norms, so it matches a different criterion.", b: "Dysfunction refers to a breakdown in everyday functioning, such as the inability to work or maintain relationships, not to norm violation as such.", d: "Danger to oneself or others concerns the risk of harm, such as suicidal or violent behaviour, which is distinct from the violation of social norms described here." },
    explanation: "The answer is deviant because the violation of society's explicit and implicit norms is captured by the deviance criterion of abnormality, one of the classic four Ds used to define abnormal behaviour. The four Ds are deviance, distress, dysfunction, and danger. Deviance refers to behaviour, thoughts, or emotions that depart from a society's norms, which include explicit rules such as laws and implicit standards such as customs, manners, and unspoken expectations about how people should act in a given context. What counts as deviant is therefore relative to culture and historical period; the same behaviour may be normal in one society and abnormal in another, which is why the deviance criterion alone is insufficient and clinicians use it alongside the others. Distress concerns the subjective unhappiness or suffering the person experiences; dysfunction concerns interference with the ability to carry out daily life, work, and relationships; and danger concerns risk of harm to oneself or others. The exam item here keys on the word norms, which maps directly onto deviance, since deviating from accepted standards is the definitional meaning of that criterion. Recognizing which D matches which descriptor (norms equals deviance, unhappiness equals distress, impaired daily functioning equals dysfunction, risk equals danger) lets you answer the entire family of four-D questions reliably." },
  { id: "amity23_040", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Which of the following is NOT considered a cardinal feature of Borderline Personality Disorder (BPD)?",
    options: { a: "Lack of a well-defined and stable self-image", b: "Regular and predictable changes in mood", c: "Eccentric behaviour marked by oddities in thinking and communication", d: "Enduring pattern of instability in relationships" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "An unstable, poorly defined self-image is in fact a cardinal feature of BPD (identity disturbance), so it does not answer a question asking what is NOT characteristic.", c: "Eccentric behaviour with oddities of thinking and communication describes schizotypal personality disorder rather than BPD, but the keyed answer is the one that most directly contradicts BPD's core instability; this option is a less precise distractor.", d: "An enduring pattern of instability in relationships is one of the defining criteria of BPD, the classic idealization-devaluation swings, so it is a genuine cardinal feature and not the exception." },
    explanation: "The answer is regular and predictable changes in mood, because the hallmark of Borderline Personality Disorder is affective INSTABILITY, mood that shifts rapidly, intensely, and unpredictably, so a description of regular and predictable mood change is the opposite of what BPD entails and is therefore NOT a cardinal feature. BPD is a Cluster B (dramatic, emotional, erratic) personality disorder defined by a pervasive pattern of instability across several domains. Its core features include an unstable and poorly defined self-image or sense of identity (option a), a chronic pattern of intense and unstable interpersonal relationships that often swing between idealization and devaluation (option d), marked affective instability driven by reactivity of mood, impulsivity in potentially self-damaging areas, recurrent suicidal or self-harming behaviour, chronic emptiness, intense inappropriate anger, and transient stress-related paranoia or dissociation. The instability of mood in BPD is reactive and erratic, with episodes of dysphoria, irritability, or anxiety lasting hours, which is precisely why predictability would contradict the diagnosis. Option c, eccentric behaviour with oddities in thinking and communication, actually describes schizotypal personality disorder, a Cluster A condition, and is included here as a near-miss; however, the question asks which is NOT a cardinal feature, and the clearest, most direct contradiction of BPD's defining instability is the claim of regular and predictable mood change, which is why it is keyed as the answer." },
  { id: "amity23_041", section: "biopsych",
    q: "The visible spectrum wavelength ranges from:",
    options: { a: "400 to 760 nanometers", b: "200 to 700 nanometers", c: "400 to 1000 nanometers", d: "100 to 1000 nanometers" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "200 to 700 nm starts in the ultraviolet region (below about 380 nm is invisible UV), so its lower bound is outside the visible range and the figure is incorrect.", c: "400 to 1000 nm extends well into the infrared region beyond about 760 nm, which humans cannot see, so the upper bound is wrong.", d: "100 to 1000 nm spans deep ultraviolet through infrared, far exceeding the visible band on both ends, and is not the visible spectrum." },
    explanation: "The answer is 400 to 760 nanometers, the conventionally cited range of the visible spectrum, the narrow band of electromagnetic radiation that the human eye can detect as light. Electromagnetic radiation spans an enormous range of wavelengths, from gamma rays and X-rays at the very short end through ultraviolet, the visible band, infrared, microwaves, and radio waves at the long end. Only a tiny slice is visible to us because that is the range to which the photopigments in our retinal cones and rods are tuned. Within the visible band, wavelength corresponds to perceived colour: shorter wavelengths near 400 nm are seen as violet and blue, the middle around 500 to 570 nm as green and yellow, and longer wavelengths approaching 700 to 760 nm as orange and red. Different textbooks round the boundaries slightly differently, with common figures being roughly 380 to 760 nm or 400 to 700 nm, but among the options offered here 400 to 760 nm is the standard textbook range and the only one that correctly brackets both ends of human vision. Note that this same fact appears earlier in the paper keyed to 380 to 760 nm; both are acceptable conventional values, and you should pick whichever rounded range appears among the choices, here 400 to 760 nm. Wavelengths shorter than this become ultraviolet and longer than this become infrared, both invisible to humans." },
  { id: "amity23_042", section: "abnormal",
    q: "The genetic mechanisms that ultimately contribute to the symptoms and difficulties experienced by people with psychological disorders are known as:",
    options: { a: "Endophenotypes", b: "Phenotypes", c: "Genotypes", d: "Molecular genetics" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Phenotype is the broad term for any observable characteristic produced by the interaction of genes and environment; it is the visible outcome, not the hidden genetic intermediate mechanism the question specifies.", c: "Genotype is the underlying genetic makeup itself, the set of genes, not the intermediate mechanism that links those genes to symptoms.", d: "Molecular genetics is a field of study that examines gene structure and function at the DNA level, not a term for the heritable intermediate traits that contribute to disorder symptoms." },
    explanation: "The answer is endophenotypes because the question asks for the genetically influenced intermediate mechanisms that lie between the underlying genes and the observable symptoms of a psychological disorder. An endophenotype is a measurable, heritable trait, often a neurophysiological, biochemical, cognitive, or neuroanatomical characteristic, that is not visible to the naked eye but sits along the causal pathway from genotype to the full clinical phenotype. The concept was introduced to psychiatric genetics because complex disorders like schizophrenia or bipolar disorder are not caused by single genes producing obvious effects; instead, many genes contribute to subtle intermediate processes (for example, deficits in working memory, abnormal eye-tracking, or altered prepulse inhibition) that are more directly tied to specific genes than the broad diagnostic syndrome is. By studying these simpler, more genetically tractable endophenotypes, researchers hope to bridge the gap between DNA and the messy, multiply-determined symptom picture. Contrast this with the broader terms: the genotype is the organism's complete genetic constitution, the phenotype is any observable characteristic resulting from genes plus environment, and molecular genetics is the field that studies genes at the level of DNA. The defining feature of an endophenotype is that it is the heritable intermediate that ultimately contributes to symptoms, which is exactly what the question describes, making endophenotypes the precise answer." },
  { id: "amity23_043", section: "biopsych",
    q: "The Resting Membrane Potential is:",
    options: { a: "-40 mV", b: "+40 mV", c: "-70 mV to -80 mV", d: "+70 mV" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "-40 mV is closer to the threshold of excitation at which an action potential is triggered, not the baseline resting value.", b: "+40 mV describes the approximate peak voltage at the top of an action potential during depolarization, the opposite phase from rest.", d: "+70 mV is not a meaningful neuronal value; the resting interior is negative, not strongly positive, so this is simply incorrect." },
    explanation: "The answer is -70 mV to -80 mV because the resting membrane potential of a typical neuron, the steady electrical charge difference across its membrane when it is not firing, is about -70 millivolts (commonly cited in the -65 to -80 mV range), with the inside of the cell negative relative to the outside. This polarized state exists because of an uneven distribution of ions. Inside the neuron there is a high concentration of potassium ions and large negatively charged proteins, while outside there is a high concentration of sodium and chloride ions. Two forces maintain the resting potential: selective membrane permeability and the sodium-potassium pump. At rest the membrane is far more permeable to potassium than to sodium, so potassium leaks out down its concentration gradient, leaving the interior negative. The sodium-potassium pump actively transports three sodium ions out for every two potassium ions in, using ATP, which both maintains the gradients and contributes slightly to the negativity. This resting state is essential because it sets up the potential energy that, when a stimulus opens sodium channels, allows rapid depolarization and the firing of an action potential, which peaks near +30 to +40 mV before repolarizing. So while +40 mV (option b) describes the approximate peak of the action potential, the resting value the question asks for is the negative -70 to -80 mV range." },
  { id: "amity23_044", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Which of the following substances is NOT a natural alkaloid compound?",
    options: { a: "Morphine", b: "Heroin", c: "Codeine", d: "Papaverine" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Morphine is the principal natural alkaloid of opium, occurring directly in the poppy, so it is a genuine natural compound and not the answer.", c: "Codeine is a naturally occurring opium alkaloid used for mild pain and cough, present in the plant itself, so it is natural rather than synthetic.", d: "Papaverine is a natural opium alkaloid (a smooth-muscle relaxant rather than a narcotic analgesic), so it too occurs naturally and is not the synthetic odd one out." },
    explanation: "The answer is heroin because heroin is a semi-synthetic, not a natural, alkaloid; it is manufactured by chemically modifying (acetylating) morphine and does not occur in the opium poppy itself. The opium poppy, Papaver somniferum, naturally produces a family of alkaloids, nitrogen-containing plant compounds, including morphine, codeine, thebaine, and papaverine. Morphine is the principal and most abundant natural alkaloid and is a powerful analgesic acting on opioid receptors. Codeine is a milder natural alkaloid used for pain and cough suppression and is partly converted to morphine in the body. Papaverine is a natural opium alkaloid but, unlike the others, it is not an analgesic narcotic; instead it acts as a smooth-muscle relaxant and vasodilator. Heroin, by contrast, is diacetylmorphine, made in a laboratory or clandestine setting by attaching two acetyl groups to morphine, which makes it more lipid soluble so it crosses the blood-brain barrier faster and produces a more intense effect; because it requires human chemical synthesis, it is classified as semi-synthetic rather than natural. The same fact is tested again later in the paper with the opium-specific framing. The key distinction is natural (found in the plant) versus semi-synthetic (chemically derived from a natural compound), which is exactly why heroin is the odd one out here." },
  { id: "amity23_045", section: "general",
    q: "The term 'Inferiority Complex' was coined by:",
    options: { a: "Franz Alexander", b: "Alfred Adler", c: "Gordon Allport", d: "Carl Jung" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Franz Alexander was a psychoanalyst known for foundational work in psychosomatic medicine and the corrective emotional experience, not for the concept of the inferiority complex.", c: "Gordon Allport was a trait theorist of personality, known for cardinal, central, and secondary traits and for functional autonomy, not for Adlerian concepts.", d: "Carl Jung founded analytical psychology with concepts like the collective unconscious, archetypes, and introversion-extraversion, but the inferiority complex is Adler's term, not Jung's." },
    explanation: "The answer is Alfred Adler because the term inferiority complex was coined by Adler, the founder of Individual Psychology and an early associate of Freud who broke away to develop his own theory emphasizing social and motivational factors over sexual drives. Adler proposed that all human beings begin life with normal feelings of inferiority, because children are small, dependent, and surrounded by more capable adults. These feelings act as a healthy motivating force, driving people to strive for competence, mastery, and what he called superiority, meaning growth and self-improvement rather than dominance over others. When this normal striving goes wrong, two problems can arise. An inferiority complex is an exaggerated, persistent sense of inadequacy and helplessness in which the person feels overwhelmed and unable to compensate, often stemming from organ inferiority, pampering, or neglect in childhood. Its counterpart, the superiority complex, is an overcompensating facade of boastfulness or arrogance that masks the underlying inferiority. Central to Adler's system are also concepts like striving for superiority, the style of life, social interest, and birth order. Recognizing inferiority complex as distinctly Adlerian helps separate him from Freud, who emphasized the unconscious and psychosexual stages, and from Jung, who developed the collective unconscious and archetypes. So the coining of inferiority complex belongs to Alfred Adler." },
  { id: "amity23_046", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome results in:",
    options: { a: "Lapse of attention", b: "Loss of muscle coordination", c: "Both a & b", d: "None" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Lapse of attention (the acute confusional state) is genuinely part of the syndrome, but it is only one of its features, so selecting it alone is incomplete.", b: "Loss of muscle coordination (ataxia) is also a real feature of Wernicke's encephalopathy, but choosing it by itself omits the equally present attentional disturbance.", d: "None is wrong because both listed effects, attentional lapses and ataxia, are authentic manifestations of Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome." },
    explanation: "The answer is both a and b because Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome is a combined neurological condition that produces both attentional and confusional disturbances and a loss of muscle coordination, reflecting its two linked components. The syndrome is caused by a deficiency of thiamine (vitamin B1), most commonly arising from chronic alcoholism but also from severe malnutrition or malabsorption, because thiamine is essential for brain glucose metabolism. It has two stages or components. Wernicke's encephalopathy is the acute phase, classically presenting with a triad of confusion (an acute confusional state with lapses of attention and altered consciousness), ophthalmoplegia or eye-movement abnormalities, and ataxia, which is a loss of muscle coordination and unsteady gait. If untreated, it can progress to Korsakoff's psychosis (or Korsakoff's syndrome), the chronic phase characterized by severe anterograde and retrograde amnesia and by confabulation, the unintentional filling of memory gaps with fabricated information. Because the question lists lapse of attention (a feature of the acute Wernicke confusional state) and loss of muscle coordination (the ataxia of Wernicke's encephalopathy) as the two effects, and both are genuine manifestations of the syndrome, the correct response is that both occur. Prompt thiamine replacement can reverse Wernicke's signs but the Korsakoff amnesia is often permanent, which underscores why early recognition matters clinically." },
  { id: "amity23_047", section: "assessment",
    q: "The Rorschach Inkblot Test has:",
    options: { a: "3 chromatic and 7 achromatic cards", b: "5 chromatic and 5 achromatic cards", c: "10 inkblots and 1 blank card", d: "3 achromatic and 7 chromatic cards" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "3 chromatic and 7 achromatic misstates the split; the test has an equal division of five coloured and five black-and-grey cards, not three and seven.", c: "The Rorschach has ten inkblot cards with no blank card; the inclusion of a blank card describes the Thematic Apperception Test (which has a blank card), not the Rorschach.", d: "3 achromatic and 7 chromatic reverses and miscounts the ratio; there are five of each type, not three achromatic and seven chromatic." },
    explanation: "The answer is 5 chromatic and 5 achromatic cards because the Rorschach Inkblot Test consists of ten standardized inkblot cards, of which five are achromatic (black and grey, or black and white) and five contain colour (chromatic), with two of the coloured cards using only red and black and three being fully multicoloured. The Rorschach is a projective test of personality developed by the Swiss psychiatrist Hermann Rorschach in 1921. The logic of projective testing is that when a person is shown an ambiguous, unstructured stimulus that has no inherent meaning, they will project their own inner needs, conflicts, feelings, and ways of organizing experience onto it, revealing aspects of personality that direct questioning might not access. The examiner shows each card and asks what the blot might be, then in an inquiry phase clarifies what features of the blot (its form, colour, shading, perceived movement) drove each response. Responses are scored on dimensions such as location, determinants, content, and popularity, with systematic scoring approaches like the Exner Comprehensive System. The colour cards are clinically important because reactions to colour are interpreted as relating to emotional responsiveness and affect regulation. The precise composition, ten cards split into five achromatic and five chromatic, is the standard fact tested here, which makes option b correct and rules out the other distributions." },
  { id: "amity23_048", section: "general",
    q: "Cattell divides attitudes into two categories. They are:",
    options: { a: "Source and surface", b: "Traits and types", c: "Egos and sentiments", d: "Defence mechanisms and coping mechanisms" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Traits and types describes a broad distinction in personality psychology generally (for example, type theories like Sheldon's or Jung's versus trait theories), not Cattell's specific two-category division.", c: "Egos and sentiments mixes a psychoanalytic structure (ego) with one Cattellian dynamic-trait term (sentiments), but it is not Cattell's headline surface-versus-source dichotomy.", d: "Defence mechanisms and coping mechanisms are psychoanalytic and stress-coping constructs, unrelated to Cattell's factor-analytic classification of traits." },
    explanation: "The answer is source and surface because Raymond Cattell, in his factor-analytic theory of personality, distinguished between surface traits and source traits as two levels of trait organization (the question phrases these as categories of attitudes/traits in Cattell's system). Cattell pioneered the use of factor analysis, a statistical technique that identifies clusters of correlated variables, to reduce the vast lexicon of personality descriptors into underlying dimensions. Surface traits are clusters of observable behaviours that appear to go together and that a casual observer might notice; they are the visible manifestations on the surface of personality. Source traits, by contrast, are the deeper, underlying factors or root causes that actually generate and organize the surface behaviours; they are identified through factor analysis rather than direct observation and are considered the true building blocks of personality. From this work Cattell derived his famous sixteen source traits, operationalized in the 16 Personality Factor Questionnaire (16 PF). Cattell also drew further distinctions, such as between constitutional source traits (rooted in heredity) and environmental-mold source traits (shaped by experience), and between ability, temperament, and dynamic traits. The key for this item is recognizing that the two-category division Cattell is famous for is surface versus source, which separates his framework from type theories, psychoanalytic constructs, and coping terminology offered as distractors." },
  { id: "amity23_049", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Cross tolerance is:",
    options: { a: "No addiction to any drug", b: "Exposure to one drug reduces/changes tolerance to other drugs", c: "Both a and b", d: "None of the above" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "No addiction to any drug describes a drug-free or non-dependent state and has nothing to do with the carry-over of tolerance between substances, which is the actual meaning of cross tolerance.", c: "Both a and b cannot be correct because option a is simply wrong; only b captures the real definition, so combining them dilutes the right answer with a false one.", d: "None of the above is incorrect because option b is a precise and accurate definition of cross tolerance." },
    explanation: "Cross tolerance is best defined as the situation where prior exposure to one drug reduces or alters the body's tolerance to another, pharmacologically related, drug, which is why option b is correct. To understand this you first need the concept of tolerance itself: when a drug is taken repeatedly, the body adapts so that a given dose produces a progressively smaller effect, and larger doses are needed to achieve the original result. This adaptation happens through two main routes. Metabolic (pharmacokinetic) tolerance occurs when the liver upregulates the enzymes that break the drug down, clearing it faster. Functional or cellular (pharmacodynamic) tolerance occurs when receptors and downstream signalling adjust, for example by downregulating receptor numbers or sensitivity. Cross tolerance arises when two drugs act on the same receptor system or share the same metabolic pathway, so the adaptation built up to one automatically carries over to the other. The classic example is alcohol and benzodiazepines, which both enhance GABA-A receptor activity; a heavy drinker may need unexpectedly high doses of a benzodiazepine for sedation. Similarly, the different opioids show extensive cross tolerance because they all act on opioid receptors. Clinically this matters for dosing, for understanding why detoxification protocols substitute one cross-tolerant agent for another, and for predicting reduced efficacy of one substance in someone dependent on a related one. Note this is distinct from cross dependence, which is the ability of one drug to suppress withdrawal from another." },
  { id: "amity23_050", section: "therapy",
    q: "Systematic desensitisation, in which a client resolves irrational fears through gradual exposure to the fear-producing stimulus, was first developed as a therapy technique by:",
    options: { a: "Carl Rogers", b: "Joseph Wolpe", c: "Sigmund Freud", d: "Albert Bandura" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Carl Rogers founded client-centred (person-centred) therapy, a humanistic approach built on empathy and unconditional positive regard, not a behavioural exposure technique.", c: "Sigmund Freud developed psychoanalysis, which treats anxiety through interpretation of unconscious conflict and free association, the opposite of a structured behavioural method.", d: "Albert Bandura is associated with social learning theory and modelling-based treatments such as participant modelling, not with the relaxation-and-hierarchy method of systematic desensitisation." },
    explanation: "Systematic desensitisation was developed by Joseph Wolpe in the 1950s, making b correct. Wolpe, a South African psychiatrist working within the behaviour-therapy tradition, built the technique on the principle of reciprocal inhibition: the idea that two incompatible physiological states cannot be experienced at the same time, so if a person is deeply relaxed they cannot simultaneously feel intense anxiety. By repeatedly pairing relaxation with the feared stimulus, the relaxation response comes to inhibit and eventually replace the anxiety response, a form of counterconditioning. The procedure has three core components. First, the therapist trains the client in deep muscle relaxation, often using Jacobson's progressive relaxation. Second, the client and therapist construct an anxiety hierarchy, ranking fear-provoking situations from least to most threatening on a scale of subjective units of distress. Third, while maintaining relaxation, the client is exposed, usually first in imagination and gradually in reality, to each step of the hierarchy, moving up only when the lower item no longer evokes anxiety. Because exposure is graded and paired with relaxation, the client never becomes overwhelmed, distinguishing it from flooding, where the person confronts the most feared stimulus immediately and at full intensity. Systematic desensitisation remains a foundational evidence-based treatment for specific phobias and other anxiety conditions and is one of the clearest applications of classical conditioning principles to therapy." },
  { id: "amity23_051", section: "dev",
    q: "Kohlberg studied moral development by:",
    options: { a: "Having subjects keep a daily journal of their actions", b: "Evaluating criminal and delinquent persons' court records", c: "Posing moral dilemmas to children of different ages", d: "Having subjects perform various legal or illegal behaviours" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Having subjects keep a daily journal of their actions is a self-report behavioural diary method and was never Kohlberg's approach; he probed reasoning, not logged daily conduct.", b: "Evaluating criminal and delinquent court records would study moral behaviour and its legal consequences, not the developmental reasoning processes Kohlberg was measuring.", d: "Having subjects perform legal or illegal behaviours is an action-based experimental design that ignores reasoning entirely, which is exactly what Kohlberg's dilemma interviews were designed to capture." },
    explanation: "Kohlberg studied moral development by posing moral dilemmas to children and adolescents of different ages, which is why c is correct. Lawrence Kohlberg extended Piaget's work on moral judgement by using hypothetical dilemmas, the most famous being the Heinz dilemma, in which a man must decide whether to steal an unaffordable drug to save his dying wife. Crucially, Kohlberg was not interested in whether a person said Heinz should or should not steal the drug; he was interested in the reasoning behind the answer, because the same conclusion could be justified at very different levels of moral sophistication. From the patterns of justification he identified three broad levels, each with two stages. At the preconventional level, morality is based on consequences to the self: stage one is obedience and avoidance of punishment, stage two is instrumental self-interest and simple exchange. At the conventional level, morality rests on social approval and maintaining order: stage three is being a good person in the eyes of others, stage four is upholding law and duty. At the postconventional level, morality is grounded in abstract principles: stage five recognises social contracts and individual rights, stage six rests on universal ethical principles of justice. Kohlberg argued these stages are universal, sequential, and invariant in order. The method was therefore a cross-sectional and interview-based analysis of reasoning, not behaviour tracking, which is the key distinction the question tests." },
  { id: "amity23_052", section: "abnormal",
    q: "A group of conditions manifested by symptoms and complaints suggesting the presence of a medical condition but without any evidence of physical pathology:",
    options: { a: "Dissociative Disorder", b: "Somatoform disorder", c: "Alzheimer's Disease", d: "Parkinson's Disease" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Dissociative disorder involves disruptions of consciousness, memory, identity, or perception, such as amnesia or depersonalisation, rather than physical symptoms mimicking a medical illness.", c: "Alzheimer's disease is a specific neurodegenerative dementia with genuine, demonstrable brain pathology, the opposite of an absence of physical findings.", d: "Parkinson's disease is a real neurological movement disorder caused by dopamine loss in the substantia nigra, so it has clear organic pathology and does not fit a condition defined by its absence." },
    explanation: "A group of conditions in which the person presents physical symptoms suggesting a medical illness but for which no organic pathology can be found is the definition of somatoform disorder, so b is correct. The word somatoform comes from soma, the body, and the defining feature is that psychological distress is expressed through bodily complaints. Historically these conditions were linked to the concept of hysteria and conversion, where unconscious conflict was thought to be converted into physical symptoms. Classic examples within this group include conversion disorder (neurological-like symptoms such as paralysis or blindness with no neurological cause), somatisation disorder (multiple recurrent physical complaints across many systems), hypochondriasis (preoccupation with having a serious illness based on misinterpreting bodily sensations), pain disorder, and body dysmorphic disorder. The critical diagnostic point is the absence of demonstrable physical pathology and the assumption that the symptoms are not consciously produced or feigned, which separates these conditions from malingering and factitious disorder. It is also worth noting that the DSM-5 reorganised this category into somatic symptom and related disorders, shifting emphasis away from medically unexplained symptoms toward the excessive thoughts, feelings, and behaviours that accompany the symptoms, but for entrance-exam purposes the somatoform label and its core description remain the expected answer here." },
  { id: "amity23_053", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Which of the following is NOT a type of glial cell?",
    options: { a: "Microglia", b: "Astrocytes", c: "Oligodendrocytes", d: "Myelin sheath" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Microglia are genuine glial cells serving as the brain's resident immune defence, so they belong in the category and are not the odd one out.", b: "Astrocytes are the most numerous glial cells, providing metabolic and structural support and forming the blood-brain barrier, so they are correctly a glial type.", c: "Oligodendrocytes are bona fide glial cells whose specific role is to produce myelin in the central nervous system, so they belong with the others rather than being the exception." },
    explanation: "The myelin sheath is not itself a type of glial cell; it is a structure produced by glial cells, so d is correctly identified as the odd one out. Glial cells, or neuroglia, are the support cells of the nervous system, vastly outnumbering neurons, and unlike neurons they do not transmit action potentials but instead maintain the environment in which neurons function. In the central nervous system there are several distinct glial types, each with a job. Astrocytes, star-shaped cells, provide structural support, regulate the chemical and ionic environment, supply nutrients, and form part of the blood-brain barrier. Microglia are the immune cells of the brain, acting as phagocytes that clear debris, dead cells, and pathogens. Oligodendrocytes wrap their membranes around axons in the central nervous system to form the myelin sheath, with a single oligodendrocyte myelinating segments of several axons. In the peripheral nervous system the equivalent myelinating cell is the Schwann cell. The myelin sheath itself is the resulting fatty insulating wrapping, and the gaps between segments, the nodes of Ranvier, allow the action potential to jump from node to node in saltatory conduction, greatly speeding transmission. So while myelin is intimately related to glial function, it is a product, not a cell, which is the precise distinction the question demands." },
  { id: "amity23_054", section: "biostats",
    q: "Which of the following is NOT a measure of central tendency?",
    options: { a: "Mean", b: "Median", c: "Mean Deviation", d: "Mode" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "The mean is the arithmetic average and is the most commonly used measure of central tendency, so it does not fit a question asking what is NOT one.", b: "The median is the middle value of an ordered distribution and is a core measure of central tendency, especially valued when data are skewed.", d: "The mode is the most frequent value and is a legitimate measure of central tendency, the only one applicable to categorical data." },
    explanation: "Mean deviation is not a measure of central tendency, so c is the correct answer. Central tendency refers to a single value that represents the centre or typical score of a distribution, summarising where the bulk of the data cluster. The three classic measures of central tendency are the mean, the arithmetic average obtained by summing all scores and dividing by their number; the median, the middle score when all values are arranged in order, which divides the distribution into two equal halves and is robust to extreme scores; and the mode, the most frequently occurring score, which is the only measure usable with nominal data. Mean deviation, by contrast, belongs to an entirely different family of statistics called measures of dispersion or variability, which describe how spread out the scores are around the centre rather than where the centre lies. Mean deviation is calculated as the average of the absolute differences of each score from the mean (or median). Other measures of dispersion include the range, the variance, the standard deviation, and the quartile deviation. The conceptual point the question tests is the distinction between location and spread: central tendency answers where is the centre, while dispersion answers how scattered are the data. Because mean deviation quantifies scatter, it cannot be a measure of central tendency, making it the correct exception." },
  { id: "amity23_055", section: "biostats",
    q: "What would be the degrees of freedom for chi-square based on a 4 x 5 contingency table?",
    options: { a: "5", b: "9", c: "12", d: "20" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Five would only be correct if you mistakenly used the larger dimension minus nothing or confused degrees of freedom with the number of columns; it ignores the (r-1)(c-1) formula entirely.", b: "Nine is the product of the raw dimensions minus an incorrect single adjustment, or 3 times 3; it would be right only for a 4 by 4 table, not a 4 by 5.", d: "Twenty is simply the total number of cells (4 times 5) with no subtraction for the fixed marginals, which overstates the degrees of freedom." },
    explanation: "For a chi-square test on a contingency table the degrees of freedom equal (rows minus one) multiplied by (columns minus one), so for a 4 by 5 table the calculation is (4 minus 1) times (5 minus 1), which is 3 times 4, equalling 12, making c correct. To understand why, recall what degrees of freedom mean: they represent the number of values in a calculation that are free to vary once certain constraints are imposed. In a contingency table the row totals and column totals (the marginal totals) are treated as fixed. Given those fixed marginals, once you know all but the last cell in each row and each column, the remaining cells are determined automatically, because each row and each column must sum to its known marginal. This is why one degree of freedom is subtracted from both the number of rows and the number of columns. The chi-square test of independence itself compares observed frequencies in each cell against the frequencies expected if the two categorical variables were independent, summing the squared differences divided by expected counts. The degrees of freedom then determine which chi-square distribution to compare the statistic against in order to find the critical value and significance. Getting the degrees of freedom right is essential because using the wrong value leads to an incorrect critical threshold and a wrong conclusion about statistical significance." },
  { id: "amity23_056", section: "general",
    q: "Individual differences in intelligence are due to:",
    options: { a: "Only genetic factors", b: "Only environmental factors", c: "Genetic and economic factors", d: "Many factors including genetics and environment" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Only genetic factors is too narrow and ignores overwhelming evidence from the Flynn effect, adoption studies, and environmental deprivation research that experience shapes measured intelligence.", b: "Only environmental factors ignores the substantial heritability of intelligence demonstrated by twin studies and is equally one-sided.", c: "Genetic and economic factors is incomplete because it singles out economics while omitting the wider range of environmental, nutritional, educational, and cultural influences that the comprehensive answer captures." },
    explanation: "Individual differences in intelligence arise from many interacting factors including both genetics and environment, so d is correct. This question tests an understanding of the long-running nature-versus-nurture debate as applied to intelligence, and the modern consensus is firmly interactionist: neither heredity nor environment alone determines a person's intellectual ability; the two work together inseparably. Evidence for a substantial genetic contribution comes from twin and adoption studies, where identical twins reared apart still show markedly correlated IQ scores, and heritability estimates for intelligence are often cited in the range of around fifty to seventy percent in adults. But heritability is a population statistic, not a fixed individual destiny, and it leaves a large share of variation to environmental and experiential influences. Environmental factors include prenatal nutrition and health, early stimulation and the quality of the home learning environment, schooling, socioeconomic status, exposure to toxins such as lead, and broader cultural opportunities. The Flynn effect, the steady rise in average IQ scores across the twentieth century, is strong evidence that environment matters because genes cannot change that fast across generations. Furthermore, gene-environment interaction and correlation mean that genetic predispositions partly shape the environments people seek out and how those environments affect them. The key examination point is that any single-cause option is an oversimplification; intelligence is multiply determined." },
  { id: "amity23_057", section: "general",
    q: "______ was the term used by Freud to refer to a reservoir of unorganised instinctual drives.",
    options: { a: "Ego", b: "Id", c: "Super-ego", d: "Defence mechanism" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "The ego is the organised, reality-oriented part that mediates between id, superego, and the external world; it is precisely not an unorganised reservoir of instinct.", c: "The super-ego is the moral and idealistic agency that internalises social rules and conscience, the opposite of raw instinctual drive.", d: "A defence mechanism is an unconscious strategy the ego uses to manage anxiety, not a structural reservoir of drives, so it does not fit the description at all." },
    explanation: "The id is the term Freud used for the reservoir of unorganised instinctual drives, making b correct. In Freud's structural model of the mind, the personality is divided into three agencies: the id, the ego, and the superego. The id is the most primitive and is present from birth; it is entirely unconscious and contains the basic biological drives, especially the sexual and aggressive energies that Freud tied to the life instinct (Eros) and later the death instinct (Thanatos). The id operates on the pleasure principle, demanding immediate gratification of needs and tension reduction without regard for reality, logic, morality, or consequences. Freud described it as a cauldron of seething excitations, chaotic and timeless, with no sense of contradiction. Because the id cannot itself act on the external world, it relies on primary process thinking, such as fantasy and dream imagery, to discharge tension. The ego develops out of the id to mediate between its demands and the constraints of reality, operating on the reality principle and using secondary process thinking to find realistic ways to satisfy drives. The superego, the last to develop, internalises parental and societal moral standards and acts as conscience. The question specifically describes an unorganised reservoir of instinctual drives, which is the textbook hallmark of the id alone." },
  { id: "amity23_058", section: "general",
    q: "A network of associated facts and concepts that make up our knowledge of the world is referred to as:",
    options: { a: "Episodic memory", b: "Explicit memory", c: "Implicit memory", d: "Semantic memory" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Episodic memory stores specific, time-and-place-tagged personal experiences, not a general network of facts and concepts about the world.", b: "Explicit memory is the broad umbrella category for consciously accessible memory that contains both semantic and episodic memory, so it is too general to be the precise answer for a factual knowledge network.", c: "Implicit memory covers unconscious skills, habits, and conditioned responses, which are non-declarative and entirely unlike a conscious network of facts." },
    explanation: "A network of associated facts and concepts that constitute our general knowledge of the world is semantic memory, so d is correct. This question rests on the distinction, introduced by Endel Tulving, between two kinds of long-term declarative (explicit) memory. Semantic memory is the store of general facts, meanings, concepts, vocabulary, and knowledge about the world that is not tied to any particular time or place in which it was learned. Knowing that Paris is the capital of France, that a triangle has three sides, or what the word democracy means are all semantic memories; you typically cannot recall when or where you first acquired them. Episodic memory, by contrast, is memory for specific personally experienced events, bound to a particular context of time and place, such as remembering your last birthday party or what you ate this morning. Both semantic and episodic memory are subtypes of explicit (declarative) memory, which is consciously accessible and can be verbally described. This is contrasted with implicit (non-declarative) memory, which includes skills, habits, and conditioned responses that influence behaviour without conscious recall, such as riding a bicycle. The phrase network of associated facts and concepts strongly signals the organised, web-like structure of conceptual knowledge that defines semantic memory specifically, rather than the broader categories or the event-based alternative." },
  { id: "amity23_059", section: "general",
    q: "Who is a pioneer contributor to Cognitive Psychology?",
    options: { a: "Jean Piaget", b: "Kohler", c: "Chomsky", d: "Kohlberg" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Köhler was a Gestalt psychologist known for insight learning in chimpanzees; he contributed to perception and problem-solving ideas but is classified primarily under Gestalt rather than as the cognitive pioneer intended here.", c: "Chomsky was hugely influential in launching the cognitive revolution through his theory of language and his critique of Skinner, but he is a linguist, and within these options Piaget is the keyed cognitive-development pioneer.", d: "Kohlberg studied moral reasoning and development, which is a narrower applied area built on Piaget's foundation, so he is not regarded as a pioneer of cognitive psychology as a whole." },
    explanation: "Among the listed names Jean Piaget is the recognised pioneering contributor to cognitive psychology, so a is the keyed answer. Cognitive psychology is the scientific study of internal mental processes such as perception, attention, memory, language, reasoning, problem solving, and thinking, and it emerged as a reaction against behaviourism's refusal to study the mind directly. Piaget's contribution was foundational because his theory of cognitive development was among the first systematic accounts of how mental structures, which he called schemas, are built, organised, and reorganised through the processes of assimilation and accommodation as the child interacts with the environment. By treating thought as an active, constructive, stage-based process, he placed internal cognition at the centre of psychological inquiry and profoundly influenced the broader cognitive movement. It is worth being honest that in many texts the title pioneer or father of cognitive psychology is given to Ulric Neisser, whose 1967 book Cognitive Psychology named and consolidated the field, while figures like Wolfgang Köhler (insight learning in Gestalt psychology) and Noam Chomsky (whose critique of Skinner helped launch the cognitive revolution) are also major contributors. Given the options here, however, Piaget is the intended choice because his work on the development of thinking is the most clearly and centrally cognitive among those listed, and he is conventionally credited in Indian exam keys as a pioneer of the cognitive approach." },
  { id: "amity23_060", section: "biopsych",
    q: "______ is closely related to grammar and pronunciation.",
    options: { a: "Aphasia", b: "Broca's area", c: "Wernicke's area", d: "Agnosia" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Broca's area is an anatomical brain region, not a language phenomenon in itself; damage to it causes the grammar-and-articulation deficits, but the question's framing points to the disorder term aphasia rather than the site.", c: "Wernicke's area governs language comprehension and the meaning of speech, so it is associated with understanding rather than with grammar and pronunciation per se.", d: "Agnosia is the inability to recognise or interpret sensory stimuli, such as objects, faces, or sounds, despite intact senses; it concerns recognition, not language grammar or pronunciation." },
    explanation: "The item asks which term is closely related to grammar and pronunciation, and the keyed answer is a, aphasia. Aphasia is an acquired language disorder resulting from brain damage, usually to the language-dominant left hemisphere, that impairs the production or comprehension of spoken or written language while sparing general intelligence. The reason it links to both grammar and pronunciation is that different aphasias affect different aspects of language: Broca's aphasia, caused by damage to Broca's area in the inferior frontal lobe, produces effortful, non-fluent, agrammatic speech in which small grammatical words and inflections are dropped, along with disturbed articulation and pronunciation, while comprehension stays relatively intact. Wernicke's aphasia, caused by damage to Wernicke's area in the posterior superior temporal lobe, produces fluent but meaningless speech with impaired comprehension. Because the question frames a single term that captures disturbances spanning grammar and pronunciation, aphasia as the overarching disorder fits better than naming just one anatomical area. That said, this is a defensible point of debate: Broca's area itself is specifically tied to grammatical, articulate speech production, and some keys would accept it. The textbook-correct reading is that aphasia is the disorder of language that manifests in grammar and pronunciation problems, while Broca's area is merely the anatomical site, so the source answer of aphasia is reasonable to teach as the umbrella term." },
  { id: "amity23_061", section: "general",
    q: "Which of the following is NOT a part of Working Memory?",
    options: { a: "Visuospatial sketch pad", b: "Central executive", c: "Phonological Loop", d: "Visual Executive Loop" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "The visuospatial sketchpad is a genuine working-memory component that holds and manipulates visual and spatial information, so it belongs in the model.", b: "The central executive is the core attentional control system of working memory that coordinates the slave systems, so it is a real and central component.", c: "The phonological loop is the verbal-acoustic component with a phonological store and articulatory rehearsal, so it is an authentic part of the model." },
    explanation: "The Visual Executive Loop is not a component of working memory; it is a made-up distractor, so d is the correct answer. Working memory is the model proposed by Alan Baddeley and Graham Hitch in 1974 to replace the older, passive view of short-term memory as a single store. Their key insight was that short-term memory is not just a holding box but an active system that simultaneously stores and manipulates information during ongoing cognitive tasks. The model has a central executive plus subordinate slave systems. The central executive is the attentional controller that directs attention, coordinates the slave systems, switches between tasks, and retrieves information from long-term memory; it does not store information itself but manages it. The phonological loop handles verbal and acoustic information and has two parts, a phonological store that holds sound-based traces for a couple of seconds and an articulatory rehearsal process that refreshes them by silent inner speech. The visuospatial sketchpad handles visual and spatial information, the mind's eye that lets us hold and manipulate images and spatial layouts. In 2000 Baddeley added a fourth component, the episodic buffer, which integrates information from the other systems and long-term memory into coherent episodes. There is no recognised component called the visual executive loop, so it is the false option and the correct choice for a NOT question." },
  { id: "amity23_062", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Choose the correct arrangement of these stages of elevated mood based upon severity: i) Elation, ii) Exaltation, iii) Euphoria, iv) Ecstasy.",
    options: { a: "i, ii, iii & iv", b: "iii, i, ii & iv", c: "iv, iii, ii & i", d: "None of the above" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "i, ii, iii and iv (elation, exaltation, euphoria, ecstasy) is wrong because it places euphoria third when euphoria is actually the mildest and should come first.", c: "iv, iii, ii and i (ecstasy, euphoria, exaltation, elation) is a descending and internally jumbled order, not the correct ascending severity sequence.", d: "None of the above is incorrect because option b correctly captures the ascending severity order, so a valid answer exists." },
    explanation: "The correct ascending order of elevated mood by severity is euphoria, elation, exaltation, and ecstasy, which corresponds to sequence iii, i, ii, iv, making b the right answer. This question tests the descriptive psychopathology of mood elevation, a graded continuum used especially in describing mania and hypomania. At the mildest end is euphoria, an exaggerated sense of well-being and contentment that may still seem within normal bounds. Next is elation, a more pronounced and infectious feeling of joy and heightened confidence accompanied by increased activity and optimism. More severe is exaltation, an intense elevation of mood often coloured by grandiosity, in which the person may feel exceptionally powerful, important, or expansive. The most extreme degree is ecstasy, a state of overwhelming, transcendent rapture or bliss, frequently associated with a sense of mystical union or being in contact with the divine, and seen in severe mania and certain psychotic or seizure-related states. Recognising this hierarchy matters clinically because the degree of mood elevation helps gauge the severity of a manic episode and the likelihood of accompanying psychotic features and impaired insight. The trick in the question is that the four terms are listed out of order, so the candidate must reorder them from least to most intense rather than accept the given sequence." },
  { id: "amity23_063", section: "therapy",
    q: "Channelizing frustrated sexual energy into a substitutive activity:",
    options: { a: "Sublimation", b: "Projection", c: "Denial", d: "Repression" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Projection is attributing one's own unacceptable impulses or feelings to another person, for example accusing others of the hostility one actually feels, not redirecting energy into a constructive activity.", c: "Denial is refusing to acknowledge a painful or threatening reality, behaving as though it does not exist, rather than transforming a drive into productive work.", d: "Repression is the unconscious exclusion of distressing thoughts, memories, or impulses from awareness; it blocks the impulse rather than channelling it into a substitute activity." },
    explanation: "Channelling frustrated sexual (or other unacceptable) energy into a socially valued substitute activity is the defence mechanism of sublimation, so a is correct. Defence mechanisms are unconscious strategies, described by Sigmund Freud and elaborated by his daughter Anna Freud, that the ego uses to protect itself from anxiety arising out of conflict between the instinctual demands of the id, the moral prohibitions of the superego, and the constraints of reality. Sublimation is generally regarded as the most mature and adaptive of these mechanisms because, rather than merely blocking or distorting the forbidden impulse, it transforms the underlying drive energy into a constructive, culturally acceptable channel. Freud's classic examples include redirecting aggressive impulses into competitive sport or surgery, and redirecting sexual energy into artistic creation, scientific work, or other achievement. The essential feature is that the original instinctual aim is replaced by a higher, valued aim while still discharging the underlying tension, so the person gains a genuine outlet rather than just suppression. This distinguishes sublimation sharply from defences that simply hide or deny the impulse. Because sublimation can produce socially valuable outcomes, Freud even suggested that much of civilisation's art, science, and culture rests on the sublimation of basic drives. The phrasing of the question, channelling frustrated sexual energy into a substitutive activity, is essentially the textbook definition of sublimation." },
  { id: "amity23_064", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Which of the following is the function of the Limbic System?",
    options: { a: "Regulation of emotions", b: "Regulation of hunger and thirst", c: "Both a and b", d: "None of the above" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Regulation of hunger and thirst is more precisely a function of the hypothalamus specifically rather than the defining role of the limbic system as a whole, so the source treats it as not the best single answer.", c: "Both a and b would be defensible given the hypothalamus's role, but the source key designates emotion regulation as the single characteristic limbic function, so this combined option is not the keyed choice.", d: "None of the above is clearly wrong because emotion regulation is a core, well-established function of the limbic system, so at least option a is correct." },
    explanation: "The keyed answer is a, regulation of emotions, identifying the limbic system primarily as the brain's emotional centre. The limbic system is a group of interconnected structures lying on the inner border of the cortex, between the cerebral hemispheres and the brainstem, and it is central to emotion, motivation, and certain kinds of memory. Its principal components include the amygdala, which is critical for processing fear, threat detection, and emotionally charged memories; the hippocampus, essential for forming new long-term declarative memories and spatial navigation; the hypothalamus, which links the limbic system to the endocrine and autonomic systems; the cingulate gyrus, involved in emotional regulation and attention; and the limbic connections through the thalamus and fornix. Functionally, the limbic system is most famous for generating and regulating emotional responses, integrating emotion with memory and behaviour, and driving motivated behaviours. It is worth flagging that this is a genuinely debatable item, because the hypothalamus, a limbic structure, also governs hunger, thirst, and homeostatic drives, so an argument exists for the both a and b option. However, the source key treats emotion regulation as the single defining and most characteristic function of the limbic system, and in many textbook framings hunger and thirst are attributed more specifically to the hypothalamus than to the limbic system as a whole, so the keyed answer of a is the one to learn while noting the overlap." },
  { id: "amity23_065", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Which of the following areas of the brain is involved in controlling movements of the body?",
    options: { a: "Primary", b: "Secondary", c: "Supplementary", d: "All of the above" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "The primary motor cortex is indeed a real and crucial motor area that executes movement commands, but choosing it alone ignores the premotor and supplementary areas that plan and sequence movement, so it is an incomplete answer.", b: "Secondary (premotor) cortex genuinely contributes to movement by planning cue-guided actions, but selecting it alone wrongly excludes the primary cortex that actually executes the movement.", c: "The supplementary motor area is a true motor region that handles self-initiated and sequenced movements, but on its own it cannot account for execution or cue-based planning handled by the other two areas." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is 'all of the above' because the brain's control of voluntary movement is not the job of a single spot but of a network of cortical motor regions working in concert. The primary motor cortex (M1), located in the precentral gyrus of the frontal lobe just in front of the central sulcus, is the final cortical output stage: it contains the giant Betz cells whose axons descend through the corticospinal tract to drive specific muscles, and it is laid out as the motor homunculus, a distorted body map in which fine-control regions like the hand and lips occupy disproportionately large territory. But M1 does not act alone. The secondary motor areas, chiefly the premotor cortex, plan movements that depend on external sensory cues, orient the body, and prepare the right muscle groups before M1 fires. The supplementary motor area (SMA), sitting medially in front of M1, is critical for internally generated, self-initiated, and sequenced movements, and for coordinating the two sides of the body in learned action sequences. Damage to the SMA can produce difficulty initiating voluntary movement even when strength is intact. Because primary, secondary, and supplementary regions each contribute a distinct layer to producing coordinated movement, the question that asks which area controls body movement is best answered by all of them together. This hierarchical organization, planning in premotor and SMA feeding into execution by M1, is a recurring theme in motor neuroscience and explains why lesions at different levels produce different deficits, from clumsy planning to frank paralysis." },
  { id: "amity23_066", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Hybrid disorder is:",
    options: { a: "Multiple personality disorder", b: "Borderline personality disorder", c: "Mixed anxiety-depressive disorder", d: "Schizoid personality disorder" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Multiple personality disorder, now called dissociative identity disorder, involves two or more distinct identity states controlling behaviour; it is a dissociative condition, not a blend of two recognised disorders, so it is not a hybrid.", b: "Borderline personality disorder is a single personality disorder marked by instability of mood, identity, and relationships; though varied in presentation, it is one defined entity and not a fusion of two separate diagnoses.", d: "Schizoid personality disorder is a single Cluster A personality disorder defined by detachment and a restricted emotional range; it stands alone and is not a combination of two conditions." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is mixed anxiety-depressive disorder, because in ICD terminology this is explicitly the 'hybrid' category: it is used when a person has symptoms of both anxiety and depression but neither set is severe enough on its own to justify a separate diagnosis of an anxiety disorder or a depressive disorder. The word hybrid captures exactly this blending of two symptom clusters that do not fully meet threshold for either parent condition. Anxiety and depression are the two most common forms of psychological distress and they overlap heavily in real patients, sharing features such as poor sleep, fatigue, difficulty concentrating, and worry. Clinicians frequently see people in primary care who feel both tense and low but whose presentation is sub-syndromal, and ICD-10 created this residual mixed category precisely to capture them rather than forcing an artificial choice. It is most often seen in community and primary-care settings rather than specialist psychiatric clinics, and it can still cause real functional impairment despite each component being mild. Understanding this helps Sihan see that diagnostic systems include 'mixed' or residual categories to handle real-world presentations that do not fit neat boxes, a recurring theme in nosology. The key discriminator for the exam is the literal meaning of 'hybrid' as a fusion of two distinct conditions, which points unambiguously to a disorder whose name itself combines anxiety and depression." },
  { id: "amity23_067", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Which of the following is the correct path of a reflex arc?",
    options: { a: "Afferent neuron to interneuron to afferent neuron", b: "Afferent neuron to afferent neuron to interneuron", c: "Afferent neuron to interneuron to efferent neuron", d: "Efferent neuron to interneuron to the brain" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "This ends on an afferent neuron, which carries signals toward the cord, so it cannot deliver the command to a muscle; reflexes must end on an efferent (motor) neuron to produce a response.", b: "Routing afferent to afferent reverses the logic, since two sensory neurons in a row never reach the muscle; the output limb of a reflex requires an efferent neuron, which is missing here.", d: "Starting with an efferent neuron and ending at the brain inverts the entire arc; reflexes begin with a sensory input, not a motor output, and the brain is bypassed for the rapid response rather than being the endpoint." },
    explanation: "The correct path is afferent neuron to interneuron to efferent neuron, which is the basic wiring of a reflex arc. A reflex is an automatic, involuntary response to a stimulus that is fast precisely because it can be completed at the level of the spinal cord without waiting for the brain to process and command. The arc begins at a receptor that detects the stimulus, for example a pain or stretch receptor in the skin or muscle. The signal travels inward along an afferent (sensory) neuron, whose name comes from the Latin for 'to carry toward,' meaning toward the central nervous system. Inside the spinal cord the signal is relayed through one or more interneurons (also called connector or association neurons) that integrate the information and can simultaneously route it up to the brain so you become consciously aware of it after the fact. The interneuron then passes the command to an efferent (motor) neuron, 'efferent' meaning to carry away from the central nervous system, which travels out to the effector, a muscle or gland, producing the response such as jerking your hand off a hot stove. The classic monosynaptic knee-jerk reflex is even simpler, with sensory connecting almost directly to motor, but the general polysynaptic withdrawal reflex follows exactly this sensory to interneuron to motor sequence. The crucial concept is direction: afferent carries in, efferent carries out, and the interneuron bridges them within the cord, allowing protective speed before conscious thought." },
  { id: "amity23_068", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Among the given substances, which is NOT a natural alkaloid of opium?",
    options: { a: "Morphine", b: "Codeine", c: "Heroin", d: "Papaverine" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Morphine is the primary natural alkaloid of opium and the standard against which opioid potency is measured, so it is genuinely natural and not the exception.", b: "Codeine is a naturally occurring opium alkaloid used as a mild analgesic and antitussive, so it belongs to the natural group and is not the odd one out.", d: "Papaverine is a true natural opium alkaloid, though it acts as a smooth-muscle relaxant rather than an analgesic, so it remains a natural compound and not the synthetic answer." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is heroin, because heroin is not a naturally occurring alkaloid of opium but a semi-synthetic drug manufactured in a laboratory. Opium is the dried latex of the opium poppy, Papaver somniferum, and it naturally contains a family of compounds called alkaloids, nitrogen-containing plant chemicals that are pharmacologically active. The principal natural opium alkaloids include morphine, the prototype opioid analgesic that binds mu receptors to relieve pain; codeine, a milder analgesic and cough suppressant; thebaine, a precursor used to synthesise other drugs; and papaverine, which is actually not an analgesic at all but a smooth-muscle relaxant and vasodilator. Heroin, chemically diacetylmorphine, is produced by chemically modifying morphine, adding two acetyl groups, which makes it more lipid-soluble so it crosses the blood-brain barrier faster and produces a more intense rush. Because it must be synthesised from morphine rather than extracted directly from the poppy, heroin is classified as semi-synthetic, not a natural alkaloid. This distinction matters clinically and pharmacologically: it explains why heroin is more potent and reinforcing than morphine despite being a derivative, and it is a favourite exam discriminator. Note that this is the same trap as in an earlier question on this paper, reinforcing the point that among morphine, codeine, papaverine, and heroin, only heroin is man-made." },
  { id: "amity23_069", section: "general",
    q: "The phenomenon in which a neutral stimulus is repeatedly paired with an unconditioned stimulus to bring out an unconditioned behaviour is known as:",
    options: { a: "Operant conditioning", b: "Classical conditioning", c: "Reinforcement", d: "Punishment" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Operant conditioning, associated with Skinner and Thorndike, changes behaviour through its consequences (reinforcement and punishment) rather than by pairing two stimuli, so it does not fit the stimulus-pairing description here.", c: "Reinforcement is a component of operant conditioning, a consequence that increases the likelihood of a behaviour; it is not the name of the stimulus-pairing learning process described in the question.", d: "Punishment is also an operant consequence, one that decreases the likelihood of a behaviour; it is a single technique, not the associative learning phenomenon the question defines." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is classical conditioning, because the defining procedure of classical conditioning is exactly what the question describes: a neutral stimulus is repeatedly paired with an unconditioned stimulus until the neutral stimulus alone comes to elicit a response. Classical conditioning was discovered by the Russian physiologist Ivan Pavlov while studying digestion in dogs. He noticed that dogs salivated not only when food touched their mouths but also at signals that reliably preceded food. In his terms, food is the unconditioned stimulus (UCS) because it naturally and automatically produces salivation, the unconditioned response (UCR), with no learning required. A neutral stimulus such as a bell initially produces no salivation. When the bell is rung just before food is presented, over repeated pairings the bell becomes a conditioned stimulus (CS), and salivation to the bell alone becomes the conditioned response (CR). The essence is that learning occurs through association between two stimuli, and the response is reflexive and involuntary, not chosen by the organism. This contrasts sharply with operant conditioning, where behaviour is shaped by its consequences. The question's wording, a neutral stimulus paired with an unconditioned stimulus to bring out an unconditioned behaviour, maps directly onto Pavlov's paradigm. Note the question slightly misuses 'unconditioned behaviour' where the learned outcome is technically a conditioned response, but the pairing of neutral with unconditioned stimulus is the unmistakable signature of classical conditioning." },
  { id: "amity23_070", section: "general",
    q: "According to Freud, human beings are motivated by powerful 'innate forces' which are known as:",
    options: { a: "Needs", b: "Drives", c: "Instincts", d: "Id" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Needs is a broader, more cognitive-behavioural and humanistic term (as in Maslow or Murray) for requirements that motivate behaviour; it is not Freud's term for the innate biological forces.", b: "Drives is a close cousin and a common translation of Trieb, and in some texts is used interchangeably, but the standard Freudian term for these powerful innate forces in this exam tradition is instincts, making drives the less precise choice here.", d: "The id is the structure of the mind that houses instinctual energy, not the innate force itself; the question asks for the motivating forces, not the reservoir that contains them." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is instincts, because in Freud's theory the innate forces that motivate all human behaviour are termed instincts, sometimes translated from the German Trieb as drives, but in the classical Freudian vocabulary the foundational innate motivators are the instincts. Freud held that the mind is powered by psychic energy arising from these biologically rooted instincts, which create internal tension that the organism is driven to discharge. He grouped them into two great categories. The life instincts, collectively called Eros, include sexuality, hunger, thirst, and self-preservation, and their energy is libido. Opposed to these, in his later theory, are the death instincts, called Thanatos, which he proposed to account for aggression, self-destruction, and the human capacity for cruelty. For Freud all behaviour is ultimately an attempt to satisfy or manage the demands of these instincts, and the structures of the mind, the id, ego, and superego, exist to negotiate their expression against the constraints of reality and morality. The instincts are innate, present from birth, and constant in their pressure, which is why Freud called his a drive-reduction or tension-reduction model of motivation. Although translators sometimes render Trieb as drive, the term that captures Freud's notion of powerful inborn motivational forces is instincts, and exam keys in the Indian RCI tradition consistently use this word." },
  { id: "amity23_071", section: "biopsych",
    q: "What produces the hormone insulin?",
    options: { a: "Kidney", b: "Thyroid gland", c: "Pancreas", d: "Hypothalamus" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "The kidney filters blood and produces hormones such as erythropoietin and renin, but it does not make insulin, so it is incorrect.", b: "The thyroid gland secretes thyroid hormones that regulate metabolic rate, not insulin, so it controls a different aspect of metabolism.", d: "The hypothalamus is a master regulator that controls the pituitary and many homeostatic functions, but it does not produce insulin; that is the pancreas's role." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is the pancreas, because insulin is produced by specialised cells within the pancreas. The pancreas is a dual-function gland: most of its tissue is exocrine, secreting digestive enzymes into the small intestine, but scattered throughout it are clusters of endocrine cells called the islets of Langerhans. Within these islets, the beta cells synthesise and release insulin, a hormone whose central job is to lower blood glucose by allowing glucose to enter cells (especially muscle, fat, and liver) where it is used for energy or stored as glycogen. The alpha cells of the same islets secrete glucagon, insulin's antagonist, which raises blood glucose by triggering the liver to release stored glucose. Together insulin and glucagon maintain blood-sugar homeostasis. This is clinically central: in type 1 diabetes the beta cells are destroyed by an autoimmune process so the body cannot produce insulin, while in type 2 diabetes the cells become resistant to insulin's action. Understanding that the pancreas is the source of insulin connects directly to why diabetics may need injected insulin and why blood-sugar regulation is a feedback loop. For a clinical psychology student this matters because metabolic and endocrine dysregulation interacts with mood, cognition, and behaviour, and questions about the endocrine system regularly appear in biopsychology sections." },
  { id: "amity23_072", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Which is the correct option with reference to Schneiderian First Rank Symptoms (SFRS)? i) Delusions of control, ii) Thought insertion, iii) Thought withdrawal, iv) Thought broadcasting.",
    options: { a: "Only i & iii are correct", b: "Only iv is correct", c: "All are correct", d: "None are correct" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Selecting only delusions of control and thought withdrawal arbitrarily excludes thought insertion and thought broadcasting, which are equally established first-rank symptoms, so it is incomplete.", b: "Choosing only thought broadcasting ignores the other three listed items, all of which are bona fide Schneiderian first-rank symptoms, making this far too narrow.", d: "Saying none are correct is plainly wrong, since every one of the four items is a recognised Schneiderian first-rank symptom of schizophrenia." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is that all are correct, because delusions of control, thought insertion, thought withdrawal, and thought broadcasting are all classic Schneiderian First Rank Symptoms of schizophrenia. The German psychiatrist Kurt Schneider proposed in 1959 a list of symptoms he considered to be of first-rank importance for diagnosing schizophrenia in the absence of organic disease, meaning that their presence strongly suggested the diagnosis. These symptoms share a common theme: a breakdown of the boundary between the self and the outside world, so that the patient experiences their own thoughts, feelings, and actions as being controlled or accessed by external agents. Thought insertion is the belief that thoughts are being placed into one's mind by an outside force. Thought withdrawal is the belief that thoughts are being removed from one's mind. Thought broadcasting is the belief that one's thoughts are escaping and being transmitted so others can hear them. Delusions of control, also called passivity phenomena or made experiences, involve the conviction that one's will, movements, emotions, or impulses are being directed by an external agency. The full Schneiderian list also includes audible thoughts (thought echo), voices arguing or commenting, and delusional perception. While modern research has shown these symptoms are not perfectly specific to schizophrenia, they remain a cornerstone of clinical interviewing and feature heavily in Indian psychiatry curricula, so recognising all four items as genuine first-rank symptoms is essential." },
  { id: "amity23_073", section: "biopsych",
    q: "A condition characterised by the karyotype 'XXY' with an extra X chromosome:",
    options: { a: "Down's Syndrome", b: "Turner Syndrome", c: "Klinefelter's syndrome", d: "Transsexualism" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "Down's syndrome is caused by an extra copy of chromosome 21 (trisomy 21), an autosomal anomaly, not an extra sex chromosome, so it does not match the XXY karyotype.", b: "Turner syndrome is the opposite situation, a female missing one X chromosome (45,X or 45,XO), producing short stature and ovarian failure, not an extra X.", d: "Transsexualism (gender dysphoria) is a condition of gender identity, not a chromosomal abnormality, and is not defined by any karyotype." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Klinefelter's syndrome, which is defined by the karyotype 47,XXY: a genetically male individual who carries an extra X chromosome. Normally males have one X and one Y (46,XY) and females have two X chromosomes (46,XX). In Klinefelter's syndrome a nondisjunction error during the formation of egg or sperm leaves the male with an additional X, giving 47 chromosomes total. Because the Y chromosome is present, the person develops along male lines, but the extra X disrupts normal testicular development. Clinically this typically produces small, underdeveloped testes, reduced testosterone, infertility, gynaecomastia (breast tissue development), tall stature with long limbs, and sometimes mild learning difficulties or delayed language development. It is one of the most common sex-chromosome aneuploidies in males. The condition illustrates the broader principle that the number and combination of sex chromosomes, not just their presence, shapes physical and sometimes cognitive development, and that errors in meiosis (nondisjunction) underlie many chromosomal disorders. For a clinical psychology student this is relevant because chromosomal syndromes can carry psychological and developmental consequences, and the karyotype-to-syndrome mapping is a favourite exam item. The crucial clue is the literal karyotype XXY with an extra X in a male, which points uniquely to Klinefelter's." },
  { id: "amity23_074", section: "assessment",
    q: "The 16 PF scale is based on the work of:",
    options: { a: "H.J. Eysenck", b: "Gordon Allport", c: "Sheldon", d: "R.B. Cattell" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "H.J. Eysenck developed his own trait model based on a few broad dimensions (extraversion, neuroticism, and later psychoticism) measured by the EPQ, not the sixteen-factor 16 PF.", b: "Gordon Allport contributed the trait approach and the cardinal/central/secondary trait distinction, and his adjective list seeded Cattell's work, but he did not create the 16 PF.", c: "Sheldon proposed the somatotype theory linking body build (endomorph, mesomorph, ectomorph) to temperament, an entirely different and now largely discredited approach with no connection to the 16 PF." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is R.B. Cattell, because the 16 Personality Factor Questionnaire (16 PF) was developed by Raymond Bernard Cattell. Cattell took a distinctive empirical, statistical approach to personality. He began with what he called the lexical hypothesis, the idea that the most important personality differences are encoded in the words of a language, and worked from thousands of trait-descriptive adjectives compiled by Allport and Odbert. Through a pioneering use of factor analysis, a statistical technique that reduces many correlated variables to a smaller set of underlying dimensions, Cattell distilled these down to sixteen primary source traits, which he distinguished from the more numerous surface traits (observable clusters of behaviour). Source traits, in his view, are the deep, causal building blocks of personality, and the 16 PF measures them along dimensions such as warmth, reasoning, emotional stability, dominance, and vigilance. This data-driven factor-analytic method stands in contrast to Eysenck's more theory-driven model built on just a few broad superfactors. The 16 PF remains a widely used assessment in clinical, occupational, and counselling settings, and Cattell's distinction between source and surface traits is a recurring exam topic. Recognising that the 16 in 16 PF refers to Cattell's sixteen source traits is the surest way to lock in the answer." },
  { id: "amity23_075", section: "dev",
    q: "According to Erikson, the state of ______ involves feelings of inner fragmentation, little or no sense of where one's life is headed, and an inability to gain the support of a social role or vocation.",
    options: { a: "Conflict", b: "Identity confusion", c: "Incongruence", d: "Dissociation" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Conflict is a generic term for opposing forces; it is not the specific Eriksonian outcome and does not capture the adolescent struggle to form a coherent self.", c: "Incongruence is Carl Rogers's humanistic concept describing a gap between the real self and ideal self or self-concept, not an Eriksonian stage outcome.", d: "Dissociation is a defensive disruption of consciousness, memory, or identity seen in dissociative disorders, not the normative developmental state Erikson described." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is identity confusion, the negative outcome of the fifth stage of Erik Erikson's theory of psychosocial development. Erikson reframed Freud's psychosexual stages into eight psychosocial stages spanning the whole lifespan, each centred on a developmental crisis pitting a positive resolution against a negative one. The fifth stage, occurring during adolescence, is identity versus role confusion (also called identity diffusion or identity confusion). Here the central task is to forge a coherent sense of self, integrating one's values, beliefs, sexual identity, and vocational direction into a stable identity. Erikson, who coined the term identity crisis, argued that adolescents experiment with roles and ideologies before committing to a sense of who they are. Successful resolution yields fidelity, the capacity to commit to others and to a chosen path. Failure produces identity confusion, exactly as the question describes: feelings of inner fragmentation, no clear sense of where one's life is headed, and an inability to settle into a social role or vocation. This state of not knowing who one is or where one belongs is the hallmark of unresolved adolescent identity work and can persist into adulthood. Understanding Erikson's framework, in which each stage builds on the last and an unresolved crisis carries forward, is essential for developmental and clinical work, and the wording about fragmentation and lack of vocational direction points squarely to identity confusion." },
  { id: "amity23_076", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Which part of the brain plays an important role in maintaining the state of homeostasis?",
    options: { a: "Thalamus", b: "Spinal Cord", c: "Cerebellum", d: "Hypothalamus" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "The thalamus is the brain's major sensory relay station, routing incoming sensory information to the cortex; it does not regulate homeostatic functions like temperature and hunger.", b: "The spinal cord conducts signals between brain and body and mediates reflexes, but it is not the centre that regulates internal homeostatic set points.", c: "The cerebellum coordinates movement, balance, and motor learning; it has no primary role in maintaining homeostasis." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is the hypothalamus, the brain's master controller of homeostasis. Homeostasis is the maintenance of a stable internal environment, keeping variables such as body temperature, fluid balance, blood-sugar level, and energy balance within narrow limits despite changing external conditions. The hypothalamus, a small structure beneath the thalamus at the base of the forebrain, is perfectly positioned to do this because it integrates information from the body and the nervous system and then acts through two powerful output channels. First, it controls the autonomic nervous system, adjusting heart rate, blood pressure, digestion, sweating, and shivering to defend internal set points. Second, it governs the endocrine system through its connection to the pituitary gland, releasing hormones and releasing factors that command the body's hormonal responses, for instance regulating stress responses, growth, reproduction, and metabolism. The hypothalamus contains specialised nuclei that monitor and regulate hunger and satiety, thirst, body temperature, sleep-wake cycles, and sexual behaviour, comparing current states against internal set points much like a thermostat. When it detects a deviation, it triggers corrective physiological and behavioural responses, such as making you feel thirsty or initiating sweating. This integrative role linking the nervous and endocrine systems is why the hypothalamus is described as the central hub of homeostasis, a concept that appears constantly in biopsychology and that underpins understanding of stress, eating disorders, and motivation." },
  { id: "amity23_077", section: "abnormal",
    q: "A condition in which a person's sexual identity does not match his or her physical gender:",
    options: { a: "Paraphilia", b: "Gender identity disorder", c: "Sexual dysfunction", d: "Androgyny" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Paraphilia refers to atypical patterns of sexual arousal directed at unusual objects, situations, or non-consenting persons; it concerns the targets of desire, not a mismatch of gender identity.", c: "Sexual dysfunction refers to problems in the sexual response cycle such as low desire, arousal difficulties, or orgasmic problems; it is unrelated to gender identity.", d: "Androgyny describes the blending of masculine and feminine traits in one personality and is not a disorder or a mismatch between identity and physical sex." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is gender identity disorder, the diagnostic term (used in DSM-IV and ICD-10) for a condition in which a person's experienced or felt gender identity does not match the sex assigned to them at birth. The question's wording, a person's sexual identity not matching their physical gender, is the textbook definition of this condition. The key concept here is the distinction between several related but separate constructs that students often confuse. Biological sex refers to the physical and chromosomal characteristics that mark someone as male or female. Gender identity is the deep, internal sense of being male, female, both, or neither. Gender role is the outward expression of gender through behaviour and presentation. Sexual orientation, entirely separate, refers to which sex one is attracted to. Gender identity disorder, now reframed in DSM-5 as gender dysphoria to reduce stigma and to centre the diagnosis on the distress rather than on identity itself, involves a strong and persistent cross-gender identification accompanied by discomfort with one's assigned sex. The shift in terminology reflects an important conceptual move in psychiatry away from pathologising identity and toward addressing the distress that the mismatch can cause. For this exam, which uses the older nomenclature, the mismatch between felt identity and physical sex points directly to gender identity disorder." },
  { id: "amity23_078", section: "general",
    q: "Which of the following is most effective in conditioning?",
    options: { a: "Backward Conditioning", b: "Delayed Conditioning", c: "Simultaneous Conditioning", d: "Trace Conditioning" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "In backward conditioning the unconditioned stimulus precedes the conditioned stimulus, so the CS cannot predict the UCS at all; this is generally the least effective arrangement, not the best.", c: "In simultaneous conditioning the CS and UCS occur at the same time, giving the CS no warning or predictive value, which makes learning weak rather than strong.", d: "In trace conditioning the CS ends before the UCS begins, leaving a temporal gap that must be bridged by memory, producing weaker conditioning than the overlapping delayed arrangement." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is delayed conditioning, which is generally the most effective arrangement for forming a strong conditioned response in classical conditioning. The effectiveness of conditioning depends heavily on the temporal relationship between the conditioned stimulus (CS) and the unconditioned stimulus (UCS). In delayed conditioning, the CS is presented first and remains on (or its onset precedes the UCS) and overlaps with the UCS, so that the CS is still present when the UCS arrives. This arrangement is considered the most effective because the CS reliably signals and predicts the coming UCS while the two are paired in time, allowing the strongest associative link to form. Compare the alternatives along this timing dimension. In trace conditioning the CS begins and ends before the UCS appears, leaving a gap or trace in between, which weakens learning because the organism must hold the CS in memory. In simultaneous conditioning the CS and UCS occur at exactly the same instant, so the CS has no predictive value (it does not warn of anything) and conditioning is weak. In backward conditioning the UCS comes before the CS, which is the least effective of all because the CS cannot predict an event that has already happened. The unifying principle is predictiveness: the CS must reliably precede and signal the UCS, and delayed conditioning maximises this signalling while preserving close temporal pairing, which is why it produces the most robust learning." },
  { id: "amity23_079", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Which of the following culture-bound syndromes is NOT reported in India?",
    options: { a: "Dhat Syndrome", b: "Koro", c: "Suchi-bai", d: "Latah" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Dhat syndrome, centred on anxiety over semen loss, is one of the best-known Indian culture-bound syndromes, so it is reported in India and is not the exception.", b: "Koro, the fear of genital retraction, has occurred in documented epidemics in India as well as Southeast Asia, so it is reported in India.", c: "Suchi-bai, an obsessive preoccupation with purity and cleanliness described among Bengali women, is an Indian culture-bound syndrome and therefore not the answer." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Latah, because it is the one culture-bound syndrome on the list that is not reported in India; it is classically associated with Malaysia and Indonesia. Culture-bound syndromes are patterns of distress and abnormal behaviour that are recognised as illnesses within a specific culture and are tied to its particular beliefs and social context, illustrating how the expression of psychological disturbance is shaped by culture even when underlying processes may be universal. The three Indian-associated syndromes here are well documented in South Asian psychiatry. Dhat syndrome is a condition, especially among young men in the Indian subcontinent, involving anxiety and hypochondriacal concern over the loss of semen (believed to be passed in urine), accompanied by fatigue and weakness, rooted in cultural beliefs about the preciousness of semen. Koro, although more famously described in Southeast Asia and China, is reported in India (notably in epidemic outbreaks in eastern India) and involves the terrifying belief that one's genitals are retracting into the body, possibly leading to death. Suchi-bai is a condition reported among Bengali (especially widowed) women involving extreme obsessive concern with cleanliness and contamination, resembling obsessive-compulsive features in a culturally specific form. Latah, by contrast, is a Southeast Asian syndrome characterised by an exaggerated startle response, often with echolalia, echopraxia, and automatic obedience following a sudden fright, and it is not part of the Indian cultural psychiatry picture, making it the correct odd one out." },
  { id: "amity23_080", section: "therapy",
    q: "Proponent of the Token Economy as a psychosocial therapeutic technique:",
    options: { a: "B.F. Skinner", b: "Pavlov", c: "Ayllon and Azrin", d: "None" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Pavlov is associated with classical (respondent) conditioning, the pairing of stimuli to produce reflexive responses; the token economy is built on operant reinforcement, not classical conditioning, so he is unrelated.", c: "Ayllon and Azrin are in fact the clinicians who developed and implemented the first token economy in 1968, so this is arguably the textbook-correct answer; the source key instead credits Skinner for the underlying operant theory.", d: "'None' is incorrect because the token economy has clear originators, whether one credits Skinner for the operant foundation or Ayllon and Azrin for the actual technique." },
    explanation: "The source key marks B.F. Skinner as the answer, but the textbook-correct originators of the token economy are Teodoro Ayllon and Nathan Azrin, who in 1968 implemented the first systematic token economy on a psychiatric ward, so option c is the more historically accurate choice and worth knowing for the exam. The token economy is a behaviour-modification programme grounded in the operant principles that Skinner formulated, which is why his name is sometimes given as the answer in Indian exam keys: he provided the theoretical foundation of reinforcement on which the technique rests. In a token economy, desired target behaviours are immediately reinforced with tokens, which are conditioned (secondary) reinforcers that have no inherent value but can later be exchanged for backup reinforcers such as privileges, food, or activities the person values. The tokens work much like money: they bridge the delay between behaviour and reward, can be dispensed instantly and consistently, and can be tailored to each person's preferences through the exchange system. This makes the technique powerful for shaping behaviour in structured settings such as psychiatric inpatient units, classrooms for children with developmental disabilities, and prisons. The key concepts to take away are conditioned reinforcement (a neutral token acquires reinforcing power through pairing) and the importance of immediacy and consistency in reinforcement. So while Skinner supplied the underlying operant theory and is the marked answer, Ayllon and Azrin actually developed and named the token economy as a clinical procedure." },
  { id: "amity23_081", section: "biostats",
    q: "Which is the most suitable statistical technique to calculate the correlation between two continuous variables?",
    options: { a: "Pearson product-moment coefficient of correlation", b: "Spearman rank-order correlation", c: "Biserial correlation", d: "Point biserial correlation" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Spearman rank-order correlation (rho) is the non-parametric counterpart used when data are ordinal or ranked, or when the relationship is monotonic but not linear, so it does not fit two genuinely continuous, normally distributed variables.", c: "Biserial correlation is used when one variable is continuous and the other is an artificially dichotomized variable that is assumed to have an underlying continuous normal distribution, which is not the case here where both variables are continuous.", d: "Point-biserial correlation applies when one variable is continuous and the other is a true (natural) dichotomy such as pass/fail or male/female, so it is wrong for a pair of continuous variables." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is the Pearson product-moment coefficient of correlation because the question specifically asks about two continuous variables, and Pearson's r is the standard parametric statistic designed to measure the strength and direction of a linear relationship between two variables that are both measured on an interval or ratio (continuous) scale. Correlation in general expresses how two variables co-vary, ranging from -1.0 (perfect negative) through 0 (no linear relationship) to +1.0 (perfect positive). Karl Pearson formalized this coefficient in the 1890s, building on Francis Galton's earlier work on regression toward the mean. The mechanism of Pearson's r is that it standardizes the covariance of the two variables by dividing by the product of their standard deviations, so the resulting value is unit-free and comparable across studies. It assumes both variables are continuous, the relationship is linear, the data are roughly bivariate normal, and there are no extreme outliers, which is why it is the default choice when those assumptions hold. Clinically and in research, you would use Pearson's r to relate, for example, hours of therapy attended and reduction in depression scores, since both are continuous. The key idea to learn cold is that the level of measurement of your variables dictates which correlation statistic is appropriate, and continuous-with-continuous always points to Pearson." },
  { id: "amity23_082", section: "general",
    q: "You are at a party and someone calls out your name. Instantly, in the middle of a crowd, you can hear it. This phenomenon is called:",
    options: { a: "Bottleneck effect", b: "Cocktail Phenomenon", c: "Divided attention", d: "Sustained attention" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "The bottleneck effect refers to the general limited-capacity stage in attention models where information must be filtered, not to the specific experience of detecting your own name in noise; it is the structural concept, not the named phenomenon described.", c: "Divided attention is the ability to split processing across two or more tasks at once, the opposite of the focused, single-channel listening with intrusion that the question depicts.", d: "Sustained attention (vigilance) is maintaining focus on one stimulus over a prolonged period, which has nothing to do with a salient word suddenly capturing attention from an unattended source." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is the Cocktail Party Phenomenon (here labeled the Cocktail Phenomenon), the classic demonstration that selective attention can be interrupted by personally salient information arriving on an unattended channel. The scenario describes you filtering out a roomful of conversations to focus on one, yet your own name on an ignored channel still breaks through. This was studied by Colin Cherry in 1953 using dichotic listening tasks, where different messages were played to each ear and participants shadowed one ear; Cherry found people noticed little of the unattended message except gross physical features and, crucially, their own name. The phenomenon is central to attention theory because it challenged Donald Broadbent's early-selection filter model, which proposed that unattended input was blocked at a sensory level before meaning was extracted. If the filter were that absolute, your name could never get through. This led Anne Treisman to propose her attenuation model, in which the unattended channel is turned down rather than fully shut off, allowing highly salient or low-threshold words like one's own name to still activate recognition. The deeper concept to master is the early-versus-late selection debate in attention, and the cocktail party effect is the canonical evidence that some semantic processing of ignored stimuli must occur, supporting attenuation or late-selection accounts over a rigid all-or-nothing filter." },
  { id: "amity23_083", section: "therapy",
    q: "Which of the following is NOT a treatment developed from classical principles?",
    options: { a: "Token economics", b: "Aversion Therapy", c: "Flooding", d: "Systematic desensitisation" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Aversion therapy is classically conditioned: it pairs a maladaptive behavior (e.g., drinking) with an aversive stimulus (e.g., nausea-inducing drug) so the behavior itself comes to elicit aversion, so it does not belong to the operant exception sought.", c: "Flooding is an exposure technique grounded in classical extinction, presenting the feared conditioned stimulus at full intensity until the conditioned anxiety response dies out, so it is a classical-principle treatment.", d: "Systematic desensitization is Wolpe's classical-conditioning method using counter-conditioning and reciprocal inhibition (relaxation paired with a fear hierarchy), so it too is built on classical, not operant, principles." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Token economics (the token economy), because it is the only listed treatment derived from operant conditioning rather than classical conditioning. The distinction the question tests is fundamental: classical (Pavlovian) conditioning works by pairing stimuli so that a previously neutral stimulus comes to elicit a reflexive response, and therapies built on it manipulate stimulus-response associations involving involuntary, autonomic reactions such as fear or nausea. Operant conditioning, by contrast, shapes voluntary behavior through its consequences, using reinforcement and punishment. A token economy, developed and popularized by Teodoro Ayllon and Nathan Azrin in the 1960s in psychiatric settings, awards secondary reinforcers (tokens) for desired behaviors that can later be exchanged for backup reinforcers; it is squarely operant because it strengthens behavior through contingent reward. The other three options all rest on classical principles. Aversion therapy pairs an unwanted behavior or stimulus with an aversive unconditioned stimulus so the behavior comes to evoke discomfort. Flooding exposes the client to the full intensity of the feared conditioned stimulus without escape so that the conditioned fear response extinguishes. Systematic desensitization, developed by Joseph Wolpe, uses reciprocal inhibition by pairing a graded fear hierarchy with relaxation, again a classical-conditioning logic of replacing the conditioned fear response. The concept to internalize is sorting behavioral techniques by whether they manipulate antecedent stimulus pairings (classical) or behavioral consequences (operant)." },
  { id: "amity23_084", section: "general",
    q: "Illusions are:",
    options: { a: "Distortions of existing stimuli", b: "The same as hallucinations", c: "The result of innate mechanisms", d: "Not based on external reality" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Illusions are not the same as hallucinations; a hallucination is a perception occurring without any external stimulus, whereas an illusion is a misperception of a stimulus that is actually present, so equating them is incorrect.", c: "Saying illusions are the result of innate mechanisms is too narrow and not the defining feature; while some perceptual assumptions are built in, the essential point is that an illusion is a distortion of a real stimulus, not merely a product of innate processes.", d: "Illusions are by definition based on external reality (a real stimulus is distorted), so claiming they are not based on external reality actually describes a hallucination, not an illusion." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is that illusions are distortions of existing stimuli. An illusion is a consistent, predictable misperception of a real external stimulus, in which the sensory information is genuinely present but the brain interprets or organizes it incorrectly. This is the defining feature that separates illusions from hallucinations: in an illusion there IS something out there being perceived, but it is misread (a coiled rope seen as a snake, the Muller-Lyer lines appearing unequal, the moon looking larger near the horizon). The deeper concept is the distinction between sensation and perception. Sensation is the raw transduction of physical energy by receptors; perception is the brain's active construction of meaning from that input, using top-down expectations, context, and Gestalt organizing principles. Illusions are scientifically valuable precisely because they expose the rules of perception: when the perceptual system applies its normally adaptive assumptions (about depth, size constancy, lighting, or grouping) to an unusual stimulus, it produces a systematic error that reveals the underlying machinery. Most illusions are shared across people because they arise from these built-in perceptual processes, but they are always anchored to a real, external stimulus that is being distorted. Understanding this lets you cleanly separate illusion (misinterpreted real input) from hallucination (perception with no external stimulus at all), a distinction that also matters clinically when assessing psychosis." },
  { id: "amity23_085", section: "biostats",
    q: "Which is characterised by selecting participants only on their availability?",
    options: { a: "Quota Sampling", b: "Convenient sampling", c: "Stratified sampling", d: "Random sampling" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Quota sampling does use accessible participants but adds a defining constraint: the researcher fills preset quotas for subgroups (e.g., a fixed number of each gender or age band) to mirror population proportions, so availability alone does not describe it.", c: "Stratified sampling is a probability method that divides the population into strata and randomly samples within each, the opposite of selecting purely on availability.", d: "Random sampling gives every population member an equal, known chance of selection through a randomization procedure, which is fundamentally different from picking whoever happens to be available." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is convenience sampling, because it is defined precisely by selecting participants on the basis of their easy availability to the researcher rather than by any systematic procedure. Sampling methods fall into two broad families: probability sampling, in which every member of the population has a known, non-zero chance of selection, and non-probability sampling, in which selection is not random and selection probabilities are unknown. Convenience sampling is the most basic non-probability method: the researcher simply recruits whoever is nearest and most readily accessible, such as undergraduates in a psychology class or patients in one's own clinic. Its strength is speed and low cost; its serious weakness is selection bias and poor external validity, because the sample may differ systematically from the wider population, so findings cannot be confidently generalized. The concept to learn cold is the trade-off underlying all sampling: probability methods (simple random, stratified, cluster, systematic) protect representativeness and allow inferential statistics that assume random selection, whereas non-probability methods (convenience, quota, purposive, snowball) sacrifice generalizability for practicality. Recognizing that the single defining criterion here is availability, with no quotas, no strata, and no randomization, is what pins the answer to convenience sampling and distinguishes it from superficially similar techniques." },
  { id: "amity23_086", section: "biopsych",
    q: "The part of the brain responsible for producing enhanced memory for emotional or arousing events is:",
    options: { a: "Hippocampus", b: "Suprachiasmatic nucleus", c: "Amygdala", d: "Hypothalamus" },
    correct: "c",
    traps: { a: "The hippocampus is essential for forming and consolidating new declarative (explicit) memories generally, but it is not the structure responsible for the specifically emotional enhancement of memory; that modulatory boost comes from the amygdala acting on the hippocampus.", b: "The suprachiasmatic nucleus of the hypothalamus is the master biological clock governing circadian rhythms, with no role in emotional memory enhancement.", d: "The hypothalamus regulates homeostatic drives (hunger, thirst, temperature, the stress hormone axis) and basic emotional expression, but it does not produce the enhanced encoding of emotional memories that the amygdala does." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is the amygdala, the almond-shaped limbic structure that is critical for attaching emotional significance to events and for enhancing the consolidation of memories that occur during emotional arousal. The mechanism is well established: during emotionally charged or stressful experiences, the body releases stress hormones (adrenaline and cortisol) and the amygdala becomes highly active, and this amygdala activation modulates the hippocampus, which is the structure that actually forms new declarative memories. In effect the amygdala tells the hippocampus that an event matters and should be remembered strongly, producing the vivid, durable, often emotionally laden memories we associate with significant life events (sometimes called flashbulb memories). Joseph LeDoux's research mapped the amygdala's central role in fear processing and the fast subcortical pathway from thalamus to amygdala that allows rapid emotional appraisal. James McGaugh's work specifically demonstrated that the amygdala enhances memory consolidation for arousing material and that blocking adrenergic activity in the amygdala reduces this emotional memory advantage. The key distinction to learn is the division of labor in the limbic system: the hippocampus encodes and consolidates the factual content of memories, while the amygdala assigns emotional value and boosts the strength of memories formed under arousal. So when a question specifically links emotion or arousal to enhanced memory, the answer points to the amygdala rather than the hippocampus alone." },
  { id: "amity23_087", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Identify the 'odd one out' from the groups:",
    options: { a: "Paranoid personality disorder", b: "Schizoid personality disorder", c: "Schizotypal personality disorder", d: "Borderline personality disorder" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Paranoid personality disorder is a Cluster A (odd/eccentric) disorder marked by pervasive distrust and suspicion of others, so it groups with the other two and is not the outlier.", b: "Schizoid personality disorder is a Cluster A disorder characterized by detachment and limited emotional expression, again belonging to the eccentric cluster and not standing apart.", c: "Schizotypal personality disorder is a Cluster A disorder featuring eccentric thinking, perceptual distortions, and social anxiety, so it too fits the odd cluster and is not the odd one out." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is Borderline personality disorder, which is the odd one out because the other three (paranoid, schizoid, and schizotypal) all belong to DSM Cluster A, the odd or eccentric cluster, whereas borderline personality disorder belongs to Cluster B, the dramatic, emotional, and erratic cluster. The DSM organizes the ten personality disorders into three clusters based on descriptive similarity. Cluster A includes paranoid (pervasive distrust and suspiciousness), schizoid (detachment from social relationships and restricted emotional expression), and schizotypal (acute discomfort in relationships plus cognitive-perceptual distortions and eccentric behavior); these share a flavor of social oddness and are thought to share some genetic relationship with the schizophrenia spectrum. Cluster B includes antisocial, borderline, histrionic, and narcissistic disorders, marked by emotional dysregulation, impulsivity, and interpersonal turmoil. Cluster C (avoidant, dependent, obsessive-compulsive) is the anxious, fearful cluster. Borderline personality disorder specifically is defined by pervasive instability of affect, self-image, and relationships together with marked impulsivity, including fear of abandonment, identity disturbance, self-harm, and chronic emptiness. The concept to master is the cluster structure itself: grouping the disorders by their core thematic resemblance is exactly what lets you spot that three options cluster together as eccentric (Cluster A) while borderline stands apart as dramatic-emotional (Cluster B)." },
  { id: "amity23_088", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Which neurotransmitter is associated with the control of the pain response?",
    options: { a: "Acetylcholine", b: "Serotonin", c: "GABA", d: "Endorphin" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Acetylcholine is the neurotransmitter of the neuromuscular junction and is involved in muscle contraction, autonomic function, attention, and memory, not in modulating the pain response.", b: "Serotonin contributes to mood, sleep, appetite, and does play some role in pain modulation pathways, but it is primarily known for mood regulation and is not the neurotransmitter specifically identified with controlling pain; endorphin is the direct answer.", c: "GABA is the brain's principal inhibitory neurotransmitter, central to reducing neuronal excitability and anxiety, but it is not the substance specifically associated with the analgesic, pain-blocking opioid system." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is endorphin, the body's endogenous opioid neurotransmitter that controls and dampens the pain response. The name itself, a contraction of endogenous morphine, signals its function: endorphins are peptide neurotransmitters produced by the pituitary gland and central nervous system that bind to the same opioid receptors (mu, delta, kappa) targeted by opiate drugs such as morphine and heroin, producing analgesia and feelings of well-being. The mechanism is that endorphins inhibit the transmission of pain signals, partly by blocking the release of substance P (a pain-signaling neurotransmitter) in the spinal cord and brain, and they are released in response to stress, injury, intense exercise, and certain pleasurable activities, which explains phenomena like the runner's high and stress-induced analgesia. This fits within the gate-control theory of pain and descending pain-modulation pathways, where the brain can actively suppress incoming pain signals. The broader concept to learn is matching neurotransmitters to their primary functional domains: dopamine for reward and movement, serotonin for mood and sleep, GABA as the main inhibitory transmitter, acetylcholine for muscle activation and memory, and endorphins as the natural painkillers. When a question links a neurotransmitter specifically to pain control, the endogenous opioid system, and therefore endorphin, is the answer." },
  { id: "amity23_089", section: "dev",
    q: "Individuals who are moderately retarded:",
    options: { a: "Can achieve partial independence in activities of daily living", b: "Can become self-supporting adults with appropriate training", c: "Are generally institutionalised", d: "Are usually diagnosed in infancy" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Becoming self-supporting adults with appropriate training describes the mild level of intellectual disability, not the moderate level, where individuals achieve only partial rather than full self-support.", c: "Generally institutionalized overstates the dependency of moderate intellectual disability; with modern community supports most are not institutionalized, and routine institutionalization is more characteristic of severe or profound levels and outdated practice.", d: "Diagnosis in infancy is typical of severe and profound disability (often with recognizable physical or syndromic features), whereas moderate intellectual disability is usually identified in early childhood as developmental delays become apparent." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is that moderately retarded individuals (moderate intellectual disability, roughly IQ 35 to 49 under older classifications) can achieve partial independence in activities of daily living. Intellectual disability is graded by severity into mild, moderate, severe, and profound, and the key to this question is matching functional expectations to each level. People with mild intellectual disability (IQ roughly 50 to 70, the largest group, around 85 percent) can typically acquire academic skills up to about a sixth-grade level and can often become self-supporting or semi-independent adults with appropriate support. People with moderate intellectual disability can learn self-care and simple communication and can perform supervised or sheltered work, but they generally remain only partially independent and need ongoing support and supervision throughout life; they are typically identified in early childhood, not infancy. Severe and profound levels involve very limited communication and self-care and require extensive or pervasive support. The concept to learn cold is this functional staircase: the milder the disability, the closer the person comes to full independence and self-support. Because moderate disability sits between mild (capable of self-support with training) and severe (largely dependent), the most accurate descriptor is partial independence in daily living, which is why that option is correct while the others describe either the mild level or overstate institutionalization and timing." },
  { id: "amity23_090", section: "general",
    q: "The author of the hierarchical theory of motivation is:",
    options: { a: "O.H. Mowrer", b: "D.C. McClelland", c: "J.W. Atkinson", d: "A.H. Maslow" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "O.H. Mowrer is known for two-factor learning theory and work on avoidance learning and anxiety, not for a hierarchical theory of motivation.", b: "D.C. McClelland developed the need theory of achievement, affiliation, and power motivation (and the related TAT-based assessment), which is a different, non-hierarchical account of acquired needs.", c: "J.W. Atkinson formulated the achievement-motivation and expectancy-value model, focusing on the tendency to approach success versus avoid failure, not a need hierarchy." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is A.H. Maslow, who authored the hierarchical theory of motivation, universally known as Maslow's hierarchy of needs, first laid out in his 1943 paper A Theory of Human Motivation. Abraham Maslow was a founder of humanistic psychology, the so-called third force that arose as an alternative to psychoanalysis and behaviorism, emphasizing growth, agency, and human potential. His theory proposes that human needs are arranged in a pyramid of ascending importance: physiological needs (food, water, sleep) at the base, then safety needs, then belongingness and love needs, then esteem needs, and finally self-actualization at the apex (later he added cognitive and aesthetic needs, and self-transcendence). The central mechanism is prepotency: lower, more basic needs must be reasonably satisfied before higher needs become salient motivators, so a starving person is dominated by physiological drives and only turns to esteem or self-actualization once more basic needs are met. Self-actualization, the realization of one's full unique potential, is the motivational goal of the healthy person and is studied in his work on peak experiences and exemplary individuals. The concept to internalize is that Maslow reframed motivation as a developmental ladder of needs rather than a set of isolated drives, distinguishing deficiency needs (the lower four) from growth or being needs (self-actualization)." },
  { id: "amity23_091", section: "dev",
    q: "Which of the following are stages of moral reasoning according to Kohlberg? 1) Obedience, self-interest and conformity, 2) Law and order, self-interest and purpose, 3) Law and order, human rights and universal human ethics, 4) Conformity, identity and competence.",
    options: { a: "1 and 2 only", b: "1 and 3 only", c: "1 and 4 only", d: "3 only" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "1 and 2 only is wrong because option 2 (law and order, self-interest and purpose) jumbles stages from different levels incoherently, mixing a conventional-stage label with preconventional self-interest, so it does not correctly describe a Kohlberg level.", c: "1 and 4 only is wrong because option 4 (conformity, identity and competence) imports Eriksonian psychosocial concepts like identity and competence that are not part of Kohlberg's moral stages at all.", d: "3 only is incomplete because, while option 3 is a valid description of the conventional-to-postconventional progression, option 1 is also correct, so restricting the answer to option 3 alone leaves out a genuinely accurate descriptor." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is options 1 and 3 only, because together they accurately list Kohlberg's three levels of moral reasoning. Lawrence Kohlberg, building on Piaget, studied moral development by presenting moral dilemmas (most famously the Heinz dilemma, about whether a man should steal a drug to save his dying wife) and analyzing the reasoning behind people's answers rather than the answers themselves. He proposed three levels, each with two stages. The preconventional level (option 1) is governed by external consequences: Stage 1 is obedience and punishment avoidance, and Stage 2 is self-interest and instrumental exchange (what's in it for me), and conformity reasoning begins to appear at the boundary into the conventional level. The conventional level centers on social approval and maintaining the social order: Stage 3 is good-boy/good-girl conformity to interpersonal expectations, and Stage 4 is law and order, upholding rules and authority. The postconventional level (captured in option 3) is principled morality: Stage 5 is social contract and individual rights, and Stage 6 is universal ethical principles such as justice and the value of human life. Option 1 correctly captures the preconventional-to-conventional progression (obedience, self-interest, conformity) and option 3 correctly captures the conventional-to-postconventional progression (law and order, human rights, universal human ethics). The concept to master is that moral growth in Kohlberg's theory moves from externally driven, consequence-based reasoning toward internalized, abstract ethical principles, and the diagnostic feature is always the justification given, not the choice made." },
  { id: "amity23_092", section: "biopsych",
    q: "Which of the following controls the Thyroid Gland?",
    options: { a: "Metabolism", b: "Glucose absorption", c: "Emotions", d: "Both a and b" },
    correct: "a",
    traps: { b: "Glucose absorption and blood sugar regulation are controlled chiefly by insulin and glucagon from the pancreas, not by the thyroid gland, so this is the wrong endocrine function.", c: "Emotions are not directly controlled by the thyroid; although thyroid disorders can secondarily disturb mood, emotional regulation is a limbic-system function, not the thyroid's defining role.", d: "Both a and b is incorrect because glucose absorption is not a thyroid function, so combining it with metabolism produces a wrong answer; only metabolism is correct." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is metabolism, because the defining physiological function of the thyroid gland is the regulation of the body's metabolic rate. The thyroid, located in the neck, produces the hormones thyroxine (T4) and triiodothyronine (T3), which control how fast cells convert nutrients and oxygen into energy, thereby setting the basal metabolic rate and influencing heart rate, body temperature, growth, and overall energy expenditure. The deeper system to understand is the hypothalamic-pituitary-thyroid axis: the hypothalamus releases thyrotropin-releasing hormone (TRH), which prompts the anterior pituitary to release thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH), which in turn stimulates the thyroid to secrete T3 and T4, with a negative feedback loop in which high circulating thyroid hormone suppresses TRH and TSH. Clinically this explains hypothyroidism (too little hormone, causing fatigue, weight gain, cold intolerance, slowed cognition) and hyperthyroidism (too much, causing weight loss, anxiety, rapid heartbeat, heat intolerance), both of which have prominent psychological symptoms relevant to clinical practice. The question is best read as asking what the thyroid controls or governs, and the answer is metabolism; glucose absorption is mainly the domain of insulin from the pancreas, and emotions are not the thyroid's direct controlling function even though thyroid dysfunction can secondarily affect mood. So metabolism is the single correct primary function." },
  { id: "amity23_093", section: "general",
    q: "Which is the most basic and common obstacle to problem solving?",
    options: { a: "Confirmation bias", b: "Mental set", c: "Functional fixedness", d: "Fixation" },
    correct: "b",
    traps: { a: "Confirmation bias is the tendency to search for and favor evidence that confirms what we already believe; it is a reasoning and judgment bias rather than the most basic, general obstacle to problem solving.", c: "Functional fixedness is a specific subtype of mental set, the inability to use an object in a novel way, so it is narrower and less general than the broad mental-set obstacle the question asks for.", d: "Fixation is closely related to mental set but is typically treated as getting stuck on a particular unproductive approach; in this framing it is the narrower phenomenon, whereas mental set is identified as the most basic and common obstacle." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is mental set, which is the most basic and common obstacle to problem solving. A mental set is the tendency to approach a new problem using a strategy or framework that has worked in the past, even when it is no longer the most efficient or appropriate solution. It is the broadest of the listed obstacles because it captures the general human disposition to fall back on familiar, habitual problem-solving routines rather than considering fresh approaches; the classic demonstration is Abraham Luchins's water-jar experiments, where participants who had learned a multi-step formula kept applying it even when a far simpler solution was available. The concept sits within the cognitive psychology of problem solving, where mental set is the umbrella obstacle and the other listed terms are more specific cases or related effects. Functional fixedness, for example, is a special type of mental set: the inability to see that an object can be used for a function other than its usual one (as in Karl Duncker's candle problem, where people fail to use a tack box as a candle holder). Fixation more generally is getting stuck on one approach and being unable to shift perspective. Confirmation bias is the tendency to seek information that supports existing beliefs. Because mental set is the most general and most frequently encountered barrier, and the others are narrower or more specialized instances, mental set is the textbook answer to the most basic and common obstacle." },
  { id: "amity23_094", section: "dev",
    q: "A child's increasing skill at using his muscles is because of:",
    options: { a: "An opportunity to exercise", b: "Pushing by parents", c: "Learning", d: "Maturation" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "An opportunity to exercise can refine a skill once the child is biologically ready, but practice alone does not produce the increasing muscular skill; the underlying driver is maturational readiness, as Gesell's twin studies showed.", b: "Pushing by parents cannot accelerate motor development beyond a child's maturational readiness; encouragement does not override the biological timetable governing neuromuscular growth.", c: "Learning contributes to elaborating and refining motor skills, but the fundamental emergence of increasing muscular control follows the genetically guided maturation of the nervous and muscular systems, not learning per se." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is maturation, the genetically programmed, biologically driven sequence of physical and neuromuscular development that unfolds with age relatively independently of specific experience. A child's growing skill at controlling the muscles, such as the orderly progression from sitting to crawling to standing to walking and then to fine motor control, depends primarily on the maturation of the nervous system and musculoskeletal system, especially the myelination of nerve fibers and the development of motor pathways, rather than on practice or teaching. The classic evidence comes from Arnold Gesell, whose co-twin control studies showed that a twin given early training in skills like stair-climbing gained little lasting advantage over the untrained twin once the latter reached the maturational readiness to perform the skill, demonstrating that readiness, not practice, sets the pace. This illustrates the broader developmental concept of the interaction between maturation and learning: maturation provides the biological readiness that makes a skill possible, while learning and experience refine and elaborate it, but the emergence of basic motor competence is maturationally governed. The principle to learn cold is that motor milestones appear in a universal, predictable cephalocaudal (head-to-toe) and proximodistal (center-outward) sequence across cultures and child-rearing practices precisely because they are maturation-driven, which is why pushing or extra exercise cannot meaningfully accelerate a child beyond their maturational readiness." },
  { id: "amity23_095", section: "abnormal",
    q: "Substance use disorder has the following patterns EXCEPT:",
    options: { a: "Acute intoxication and withdrawal", b: "Dependence Syndrome", c: "Harmful use", d: "Drug abuse" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Acute intoxication and withdrawal are both legitimate ICD-10 diagnostic patterns for substance-related disorders, so this option does not represent the exception.", b: "Dependence syndrome is the central ICD-10 category for substance use disorder, defined by compulsion, impaired control, tolerance, and withdrawal, so it is a recognized pattern and not the exception.", c: "Harmful use is a formal ICD-10 category denoting use that causes actual physical or psychological damage, so it is a valid pattern rather than the term that does not belong." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is drug abuse, because it is the one term in the list that is not a recognized diagnostic pattern within the ICD framework for substance use disorders; the others (acute intoxication and withdrawal, dependence syndrome, and harmful use) are formal ICD-10 categories. The question reflects ICD-10's classification of mental and behavioral disorders due to psychoactive substance use, which organizes substance-related conditions into specific clinical syndromes. Acute intoxication is the transient disturbance of consciousness, cognition, perception, affect, or behavior directly following substance use; withdrawal state is the cluster of symptoms occurring on reducing or stopping a substance after repeated heavy use. Dependence syndrome is the core construct, defined by a cluster of features including a strong desire or compulsion to take the substance, impaired control over use, a withdrawal state, tolerance, neglect of alternative interests, and persistence despite harm. Harmful use is the ICD-10 pattern of substance use that is actually causing damage to physical or mental health, without yet meeting full dependence criteria. The term drug abuse, by contrast, belongs to the older DSM tradition (DSM-IV used substance abuse versus dependence) and is not an ICD diagnostic category, which is why it is the exception. The concept to master is the ICD-10 substance-use scheme and how it differs from DSM terminology, since Indian clinical training and RCI-regulated exams predominantly follow ICD." },
  { id: "amity23_096", section: "abnormal",
    q: "In Delirium, the motor symptoms may include: i) Asterixis, ii) Multifocal myoclonus, iii) Carphologia, iv) Tone and reflex abnormalities.",
    options: { a: "Only ii & iv are correct", b: "Only i & iii are correct", c: "Only iv is correct", d: "All are correct" },
    correct: "d",
    traps: { a: "Only ii and iv is too restrictive: while multifocal myoclonus and tone/reflex abnormalities are indeed motor features of delirium, asterixis and carphologia are equally recognized, so excluding them is incorrect.", b: "Only i and iii is too restrictive: asterixis and carphologia are correct, but multifocal myoclonus and tone/reflex abnormalities are also genuine motor features of delirium, so limiting the answer to two leaves out valid signs.", c: "Only iv is wrong because it omits asterixis, multifocal myoclonus, and carphologia, all of which are well-documented motor manifestations of delirium, not just tone and reflex abnormalities." },
    explanation: "The correct answer is that all of the listed features (asterixis, multifocal myoclonus, carphologia, and tone and reflex abnormalities) can be motor symptoms in delirium. Delirium is an acute, fluctuating disturbance of consciousness and attention accompanied by a global impairment of cognition, typically with a rapid onset over hours to days, a clouded sensorium, disorientation, perceptual disturbances, and a disrupted sleep-wake cycle; it is usually the consequence of an underlying medical cause such as infection, metabolic derangement, drug toxicity, or withdrawal, and it is potentially reversible. Alongside the cognitive and attentional disturbance, delirium frequently produces a range of neuromotor abnormalities, which is what this question tests. Asterixis is the flapping tremor seen when the outstretched hands lose postural tone intermittently, classically associated with metabolic encephalopathies such as hepatic or uremic states. Multifocal myoclonus refers to sudden, irregular, shock-like muscle jerks occurring in different body regions, common in metabolic and toxic delirium. Carphologia (also called floccillation) is the aimless picking or plucking at bedclothes or the air, a sign of severe clouding of consciousness in advanced delirium. Tone and reflex abnormalities, including changes in muscle tone and altered deep tendon reflexes, also occur. Because each of these is a documented motor manifestation of delirium, the inclusive option is correct. The concept to learn cold is that delirium is not purely a cognitive disorder but a whole-brain dysfunction with characteristic motor signs, and recognizing them (especially asterixis and carphologia) helps identify an acute organic state." },
],
};
